ver believed COVID Zero, hyperventilating on a virus was going to work, because what happens, it spreads anyway. And then when you pull back, the immune system is groggy and all kinds of other viruses occur.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right, exactly.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: I think the population in China figured that out because they're rushing to stores to get Tylenol, to get acetaminophen. Here is one thing that we can believe about China. They've got a chokehold on our supply line.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that's right -- key pharmaceutical ingredients -- almost all of them come from China -- diabetes drugs, stomach medication, steroids, antibiotics, 50 percent. Tucker, 95 percent of the ibuprofen imported into the United States comes from China, 70 percent of the acetaminophen.\par \par \uc2 So if you think that them rushing to pharmacies over there is not going to impact us over here, you've got another thing coming. We already have a shortage here. That shortage is going to grow by leaps and bounds.\par \par \uc2 So for all the scientists out there who are worried about the new sub variant coming out of China, I say this, yes, they wouldn't give a damn if a new sub variant did come over here and sink us the way the original virus did, I'll grant you that. But I, this physician, is much more worried about where I'm going to get my Tylenol from, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I think that seems like a really, really smart thing to be worried about and we appreciate the heads up on that. Dr. Siegel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  of Missouri went to the Turning Point USA event in Phoenix the other day, and during his speech, he offered this advice to young men. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): Young men, let me make a suggestion to you. Why don't you turn off the computer and log off the porn and go ask a real woman on a date? How about that? Just a thought, ask her out.\par \par \uc2 Young men, why don't you be the one to do the asking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So controversial. Choose a human being over pornography.\par \par \uc2 As we said, \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  represents Missouri in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 . And he joins us tonight to explain his speech.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thanks so much for coming on. So tell us why -- I mean, you're a US senator. You know, you help negotiate trade deals, but you mentioned human relationships. Why?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Well, somebody has got to be honest and tell the truth to these young men. And then the truth is, is that what the porn industry is selling them is a total lie, and the truth is American society needs them. We need them to step up. We need them to go get married and have families and be responsible husbands and fathers.\par \par \uc2 This society is impoverished because too many young men are too despairing or too checked out on social media or porn to be doing what we need them to do as a country.\par \par \uc2 It's time to call people, to call young people in particular and young men to be something more, and Tucker, they want to be called to it. They don't want to be sold the bill of goods anymore, somebody needs to tell the truth.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, I think that's exactly right. But you're one of the very few people who said anything like this, certainly one of the very few elected officials, because it's considered really embarrassing to notice this is happening.\par \par \uc2 Why don't more people say it? It seems like it has transformed our society completely?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Yes. Well, I think that's because the liberal culture, what the message to young people is, the most you can aspire to in life is to be a consumer who sits in a cubicle in front of a computer all day, and doesn't ask any questions, who doesn't do anything meaningful with their life, and what we need to say to young men and young women, too, is just the opposite -- aspire to be something more than a consumer. And for young men, aspire to be something more than a consumer of pornography, aspire to actually create something in your life, like create a family, for instance.\par \par \uc2 That is the single greatest act of rebellion, if you like, against the liberal culture, that is suppressing people's desires, that are suppressing their potential, it is to go out and actually engage in real relationships, get married, have a family, have kids, have your own ideas and be a responsible member of society.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is what people are built to do. And again, it's what young people want to do. They want to be challenged in this way.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right, and I'm sure clips of you saying that will be everywhere later. All the cool kids will be making fun of you, cool kids who are not yet 60 and childless. Oh, I just -- I hope you keep saying it because I think it's clearly true.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's brave in the context of today.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  of Missouri, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So pretty much every big company that starts in this country, particularly the tech companies in Silicon Valley gets its funding from a venture capital firm that doesn't really represent America at all. One investor has a plan to change that, he joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Big venture capital firms have a huge amount of power in this country. They determine what companies succeed and which don't. The problem is, a lot of them truly dislike the United States. So a man we just spoke to Omeed Malik has a plan to provide an alternative. He is an investor and CEO of a new venture firm that is pro-America. He is in this business with \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  and Blake Masters. It's called 1789 Capital. Very smart guy.\par \par \uc2 We just had a long conversation with him for a new episode of "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today." Here is part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OMEED MALIK, ENTREPRENEUR: Most people have heard of kind of Silicon Valley and venture capital firms. They finance a lot of the tech companies that this country should be very proud of, I mean, most notably, is a venture capital firm that's coming up quite a bit recently in the news because of \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  is Sequoia Capital.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MALIK: It is one of the most prominent, biggest ones out there. I think their origin story is that they funded \uc2 Apple\uc2  -- \uc2 Steve Jobs\uc2 , so that's how they got started.\par \par \uc2 However, aside from FTX, \uc2 Sequoia\uc2  has launched a China-only fund where they only invest in Chinese assets. They have also raised money exclusively from China, to finance their investments in venture capital, and that just strikes me as absolutely peculiar that that's permitted and in some cases in Silicon Valley encouraged.\par \par \uc2 Now, China is very clearly our biggest geopolitical foe. And should we should not -- our private companies should not be supporting that. Right? I mean, it just seems so encouraging.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Encouraging. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MALIK: It is as if like, during the Cold War, you're investing in Soviet assets.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MALIK: If we think about how odd that sounds, but that is what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: A Bulgarian tank factory.\par \par \uc2 MALIK: Sounds like a great investment. Are they woke?\par \par \uc2 Anyway, so -- but that's really the mentality today. And so I just find that that's -- you're not going to get any answers from that cohort, right? Because that's how they think about their investments.\par \par \uc2 So our idea is, why don't you have a venture capital firm that's going to finance these newer businesses, right, that are not already established, that are going to come up and be the economy of tomorrow with a venture capital firm ourselves. And so we're going to have our own money to invest in companies that are aligned with the types of values that I think most Americans are which is to effectively have a form of patriotic capitalism, as well as an emphasis on shareholder primacy.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What will it be called?\par \par \uc2 MALIK: 1789 Capital, which yes, is the year of the Bill of Rights was put into effect.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MALIK: Some people said, oh, that's also the French Revolution, which it was but I think also that that's relevant because if we don't do this, the guillotines are going to come out anyway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Omeed Malik, very smart guy, former Democrat by the way. His explanation for why he changed is itself worth watching that conversation. It's on FOX Nation tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 We're out of time, sadly, but we'll be back 8:00 PM, the show that is the sworn enemy of lying, pomposity, smugness, and groupthink.\par \par \uc2 Have the best night with the ones you love. We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020221221eick00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Joins Union Phone Bank After Averting Rail Strike; Lawmakers Concerned With Tiktok's Influence On Elections; 54 Percent Think U.S. Has No Clear Strategy On China; United States Unveils New B-21 Raiders Stealth Bomber; Chinese Incentives For Research Funding; President Biden Proposes Making South Carolina First Primary In Democratic Presidential Primaries; Report Reveals Extent Of Fraud Involved In Distributing Loan Through Federal Government's Paycheck Protection Program; Economic Reports Indicates Low Unemployment Rate But Still High Inflation Rate\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Hillary Vaughn, Jennifer Griffin, Shannon  Bream, Rich Edson, Mark Meredith \par \uc2 7,975 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JIMMY FAILLA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Chubby chasers. You're all welcome to come. Just don't heckle the comedians like Kennedy. She's the worst audience ever.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I am the best.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: The best of me.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Oh no, I love to laugh. All right, do you do any of the casinos?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Oh, yes. I was just in Vegas, Judge. That's why I'm working off my debts.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We got to go. Have a great weekend, everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 We are coming to you live tonight from Simi Valley, California, The Reagan National Defense forum. This event brings together leaders from across the political spectrum and key stakeholders in the defense community as we look at Air Force One in the Reagan years there. That coverage of this event is straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 We are also at this hour monitoring the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed from CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  who is promising to release his report any minute now on how the company tried to suppress information about the Hunter Biden laptop scandal before he took over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . We'll have that as soon as we get it.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, President Biden is insisting his economic plan is working following the November jobs report. He's also trying to soften the blow to his core union constituency over his pushing through a deal to prevent a nationwide railway strike.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. The last 24 hours have been a lot of pomp and circumstance for the president, he shook hands with the Duke of Cambridge today. He broke bread with \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  last night but he's not looking to these allies for help. He thinks everything here is going according to plan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Without the union movement, there wouldn't be no middle class.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Surrounded by electrical union workers, President Biden is hoping to show he's still pro-union despite signing a bill today that avoided a rail strike by stripping rail workers of their right to strike for two years.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's tough for me. But it was the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president hopes today's headline is the better than expected jobs report with 263,000 jobs added last month.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Things are moving, they're moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Some see reason to be skeptical.\par \par \uc2 STEVE FORBES, CHAIRMAN, \uc2 FORBES MEDIA\uc2 : If you look at the household survey, which better indicates what small businesses are doing. That was down 138,000 jobs, gets no publicity and it was down in the month before. So, that indicates trouble ahead.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Trouble ahead in Ukraine too.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Russia has attempted to destroy Ukraine civilian infrastructure and deprive the Ukrainian people of heat, power and other critical services as winter approaches causing suffering for millions of people.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Despite all that anticipated suffering, President Biden is sticking to his plan, not to try to push Putin into peace talks.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: Only President Zelenskyy can determine if and when there's going to be a negotiated settlement and what the circumstances around that settlement would look like.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president offered a call if Putin lays down his arms and withdraws his troops, but that's not happening.\par \par \uc2 DMITRY PESKOV, KREMLINS SPOKESMAN (through translator): What did Mr. Biden say? He said talks are only possible if Putin leaves Ukraine first. Unconditionally, the special military operation continues.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: On foreign policy and domestic policy, President Biden thinks he's on a roll and he's trying to make his life easier making calls to help Democrats hold a Georgia Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: One of the things that we need, we need that 51st vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): One of the people in the room that the president was talking to can actually vote for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  because they were in Massachusetts, a 16 hour drive away from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But President Biden's services have not been requested on the campaign trail down there. So, it looks like the closest he's going to get is a call center where all the calls are long distance, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today. The Dow gained 35, came back from an early loss. The S&P 500 finished down five, the NASDAQ lost 21.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was up a quarter percentage point, the S&P 500 gained one and three -- 1.13, NASDAQ rose two.\par \par \uc2 There's increasing scrutiny tonight on the social media site TikTok. A new report indicates the Chinese run business is attempting to meddle in American politics.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Hillary Vaughn has the story tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Increasingly, people use TikTok as a new source. I think what they're really trying to do is launch an ideological war design to turn Americans against America.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tough talk on TikTok from lawmakers who say China is using the video sharing platform to try to influence U.S. elections by posting content attacking U.S. politicians.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ (R-FL): They hit Rubio, they hit Ted Cruz, they hit Abbott and the entity running these political ads is a registered foreign agent of the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Some Democrats including President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have also been targeted. Videos like this flooded TikTok in the run up to the midterm election. \uc2 Forbes\uc2  reports these TikTok accounts run by the propaganda arm of the Chinese government have millions of followers and the videos have tens of millions of views.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): I'm very concerned about what's happening with TikTok and its parent company ByteDance. The amount of propaganda and the amount of information it's sucking from young Americans.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: TikTok says it is working to clearly label these videos, "We plan to introduce our state controlled media policy and corresponding labels globally next year. The global rollout will include China state media."\par \par \uc2 China weaponizing TikTok is just one thing Washington is worried about. The Biden administration says China's threat is growing bigger as the country uses its tech influence as a military strategy.\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO\uc2 , COMMERCE SECRETARY: They're accelerating their efforts to fuse economic and technology policies with their military ambitions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): Today, London bailed on plans to let China build a giant new embassy. Their local officials had security concerns and issues with China's human rights record. It was supposed to be the biggest embassy in the country. But for now, China's London embassy will remain in a small town house, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hillary Vaughn live on Capitol Hill. Hillary, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about China and other national security matters. Joining me here at the Reagan National Defense forum, "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" anchor Shannon Bream and Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin. Good to see you guys in person, it's a nice backdrop here.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, China is going to dominate. I think the topics that they're talking about here, obviously Ukraine as well. But China, Shannon, this survey, do you think the U.S. has a clear strategy for managing its relationship with China? Yes, 27 percent. No, 54, this is the National Defense survey.\par \par \uc2 Which country do you see as the greatest threat to the U.S.? China now 43 percent. It goes back and you can see the different years, it's dominating the discussion about national security.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It is and when you have the report from the Pentagon this week to Congress that talked about the number of ballistic missiles that China is now testing, the growth of their nuclear program. There are obvious posturing with regard to Taiwan, there are increasing concerns.\par \par \uc2 But it's not just you know, a continent away an esoteric kind of threat. There are millions of people in this country who have TikTok on their phones. And we've got increasing calls from Intel, from lawmakers on both sides of the aisle who say we're giving them a window into not only collecting our data, but also trying to manipulate the way that we view politics through the lens of the CCP.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And through the lens of these military leaders, they're looking at China's action in relation to Ukraine saying is this the time that President Xi decides to move on Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, it's so interesting, Bret, because I've been coming to this Reagan National Defense forum for 10 years. When I first started, I remember when, just a couple of years ago, they tried to pivot away from the Middle East, to China to Asia.\par \par \uc2 And this year, for the first time, there are no panels on the Middle East. Nothing about Iran, nothing about Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's very interesting to see if you look at these poll numbers, how the pendulum they tried -- it tries to swing, but China and Russia and what's happening on the ground in Ukraine are completely related to what will happen in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 And so, if you listen to General Milley when he was asked about this at the Pentagon, just last week, here's what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: War on paper is a whole lot different than real war for a military that hasn't fought in combat since fighting the Vietnamese in 1979. They would be playing, you know, a very, very dangerous game to cross the streets and invade the island of Taiwan. They don't have the experience, the background to do it. They haven't trained to it yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And yet, the new Pentagon China military power report suggests that China could move on Taiwan as soon as 2027 which is the 100th anniversary of the People's Liberation Army.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Defense Secretary Austin is coming. He is going to deliver a speech but he's choosing not to do a Q&A session. He has not had a lot of interaction with reporters. You all see him at the Pentagon, but he doesn't do a lot of one-on-one interviews.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, not a lot of one-on-one interviews, but he does -- he has been doing more press briefings at the Pentagon with General Milley, particularly in light of the situation in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, I expect to hear him talking about that if this -- his speech tomorrow comes at the same time that the -- that the Biden administration is rolling out, it's the National Defense Authorization Act, Congress would be voting on that $847 billion.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of the defense contractors who are here are going to be supplying weapons for Ukraine, Taiwan, and there's going to be a lot to talk about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And there's a concern about the supply of weapons and what that means if Taiwan happens.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, their concerns that stockpiles are running low. The drawdowns that have been going, sending off the shelf weapons to Ukraine is cutting in people fear to the ability to send weapons to Taiwan if needed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" right here on this set, and you have the former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Other guests, as well as Jen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, Jen is going to be on with us on "FOX NEWS SUNDAY", our highlight guest. We're also going to have a couple of the senators who are here hammering out these conversations about how you fund Ukraine, how you get our weapon systems ready for other threats that may come forward.\par \par \uc2 So, Senators Ernst and King will be with us and senator -- excuse me, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , we talked to him as well about these reports that the DOJ is pressing him to get involved with potential criminal investigation of the president. He breaks some news on that for us and whether or not he will participate if formally asked, so all of that on Sunday plus Jen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's what we call it, deep tease.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is, see you on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Shannon and Jen, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll continue to keep an eye on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says he is going to release his report, he just said, give me 40 minutes to fact check some things, that was 42 minutes ago, on how the social media side covered up the Hunter Biden laptop scandal. It could happen any second. We are monitoring it when we see it live. You'll see it live here on Fox.\par \par \uc2 Up next from the Reagan National Defense forum, we talked to one of the key players in the aerospace industry about what's ahead for America's air defense.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 9 in Boise as the chief of the Moscow Idaho police dismisses the possibility that its investigation into the murders of four University of Idaho students will turn into a cold case.\par \par \uc2 James Frey insisting the killings will be solved. He says much information is being withheld in order to protect the integrity of that investigation.\par \par \uc2 Fox 11 in Los Angeles as the L.A. County Public Health Director says an indoor mask mandate may be required again. The county reported almost 4,500 new COVID-19 cases Wednesday 1,200 hospitalizations, 14 deaths. They haven't made it official but they're looking at it.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at New Orleans from Fox 8, one of the big stories there tonight. French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  heads to Louisiana to celebrate long standing cultural ties and discuss energy policy. It's the first visit to New Orleans by a French president since 1976.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library. This is the Reagan National Defense Forum. And joining us tonight, former Army Secretary \uc2 Eric Fanning\uc2 , who is now the president and CEO of \uc2 Aerospace Industries Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Eric, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 ERIC FANNING, PRESIDENT AND CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, \uc2 AEROSPACE INDUSTRIES ASSOCIATION\uc2 : Thanks for having me, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are exciting things happening when it comes to aerospace. Obviously, you have the unveiling of the B-21 stealth bomber tonight.\par \par \uc2 FANNING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have a picture of it, an artist rendering, but we're going to actually see the video as of later on. This is a big jump right?\par \par \uc2 FANNING: It's great. It's a -- it's a wonderful time for aerospace and defense. I'm going to head out after this actually to see the unveiling of the B-21 at the \uc2 Northrop Grumman\uc2  facility.\par \par \uc2 But everything is happening in defense, everything is happening in space. It's kind of a renaissance for aerospace and defense right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we have the Artemis rocket that obviously has launched off. And that's an exciting R&D moment too.\par \par \uc2 FANNING: Absolutely, following on the heels of the James Webb telescope that's captivating the world the images it's sending already.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of exciting things happening in aerospace and defense and space in particular right now. And that's great for us and for the future. Because it excites and ignites the passion of young people to go into STEM.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're concerned, though, about taxes, and the structure of the U.S. in -- really, in contrast and comparison to China, when it comes to R&D, to get these -- the next thing. Explain that.\par \par \uc2 FANNING: Even in comparison to Western countries. So, this year, the tax law changed so that we amortize research and development investments over five years rather than in the year of the expense. That leaves us there's only one of two Western countries. Belgium being the other that does that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, China doubled and gives you $2 for every dollar that you invest in research and development. So, they are giving a tax incentive that's 10 times what the United States gets right now. And that's billions of dollars in investment in research and development.\par \par \uc2 That's no longer available, because that capital is not available to the countries.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's kind of wonky, but it's tied to a negotiation up on Capitol Hill, and your hope as well as other CFOs and CEOs is to get them to change it.\par \par \uc2 FANNING: Absolutely. This is -- this is an important way. The tax code is about incentivizing behavior or disincentivizing behavior you don't want.\par \par \uc2 And we want this research and development. You're -- we're seeing the fruits of that now with what you mentioned, Artemis and the telescope.\par \par \uc2 And it used to be that the \uc2 United States government\uc2  spent most of the research and development dollar. It's now the private sector.\par \par \uc2 And we want to keep incentivizing that to make sure that the United States maintains its technological superiority.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, the Wall Street Journal had this U.S. CFOs ask Congress to repeal change in R&D tax rules. In a letter dated November 4th, "178 chief financial officers, primarily from large U.S. companies, said the new rules create a competitive disadvantage for American companies, will lead to job losses, and thwart it ovation. They are asking Congress to move back to immediately d