ook back on myself, I think I got a little cocky.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: There is no room in a Republican party for antisemitism or white supremacy.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): It's an honor to stand before you today as the incoming House Democratic leader.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): As the chamber knows, this is personal to me, and the first people I will call when this bill passes will be my daughter and her wife.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And the game is over. Exhale America. U.S. moving on. We will see you Saturday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: U.S. moving on, 9:00 a.m. eastern time. I want to see that game.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  hears arguments whether a Christian businesswoman can refuse to create websites for same sex weddings. Please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests, as mentioned before, former Vice President Mike pence, Iowa Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2 , and Maine Senator Angus King.\par \par \uc2 That concludes our special program from the Reagan National Defense Forum in Simi Valley, California. More coverage on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 Here is Jesse. Fair, balanced, and unafraid. And he may have some breaking news about \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . We'll see.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221203eic200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Idaho Murder Investigation; Plan to Combat Migrant Surge?; Hawaii Volcano; Georgia Election Campaigning Continues; Interview With Former Rep. \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2  (R-CA); \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to Release Details on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ; Strong Jobs Report Fuels Inflation Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 Jackie DeAngelis, Casey Stegall, Christina Coleman, Max Gorden,  Aishah Hasnie, Claudia Cowan, Kelly O'Grady, David Asman, Peter Doocy,  Susan Li, Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,170 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The jobs report out this morning coming in better than expected. That's good news. And, well, there's also some bad news. It could continue to fuel inflation and keep the Fed hiking rates.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 So, the White House really happy about today's numbers. Why shoppers may not be.\par \par \uc2 First to Susan Li the fallout on Main Street and then Peter Doocy at the White House for reaction.\par \par \uc2 But we give we give -- we begin, rather, with Susan.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, so Charles, in this case, and especially on Wall Street, this is a case where good news is bad, meaning that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  might have more room to raise interest rates.\par \par \uc2 And, as you can imagine, that will slow the economy and then depress stock markets; 263,000 new jobs in November, that's stronger than economists forecast, but less than the average for the past three months, leisure and hospitality still hiring, but nearly one million fewer jobs in restaurants and hotels than before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Health care jobs eclipsing pre-COVID levels, but then notice retail trade cutting jobs in November, which is pretty surprising, given the busy holiday shopping period. Now, it's still a pretty tight jobs market, as you can imagine. You still have two jobs available for every worker looking for one.\par \par \uc2 So, as a result, you have workers being paid more. Salaries went up 5 percent. Hourly earnings went up 5 percent from last year. That's twice as much as forecast in November, the jobless rate still holding near those 50- year lows. Now, while wages are still rising, that means inflation is still here, high inflation.\par \par \uc2 However, if you listen to the White House, they're sounding a little bit more optimistic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH, U.S. SECRETARY OF LABOR: We're starting to see signs of a little bit of inflation coming down in some ways. I think there's an opportunity for us to bring down the interest rates to continue to come down, see wages continue to rise, as they have incrementally, but also have a good strong jobs economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LI: So, that may not be the case for technology workers, those in Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , formerly known as \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  companies \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  have all been cutting thousands of jobs. Same for H.P., Snap and \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , and a reason why this has been called a white- collar jobs recession. Now, despite being down for most of Friday, the Dow Jones industrials actually managed to rally in the final hour of trade.\par \par \uc2 And that marks two straight weeks of gains for Wall Street, so something positive on this Friday heading into the weekend -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Monster reversal in these markets, something intriguing going on there. Thanks a lot, Susan.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, President Biden will crow about this number. It just beat Wall Street expectation, folks. It really wasn't that great.\par \par \uc2 But let's go to Peter Doocy at the White House with more on the White House perspective -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Charles, you guessed it, inflation still high, but this president sees plenty of evidence that his economic plan is working.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The economic report, the GDP is up even more than it was previously thought. We continue to create jobs -- lots of jobs.\par \par \uc2 And today, we've learned that the economy added 263,000 jobs in November. And we've now created 10.5 million jobs since I took office, more than any administration in history at this point in a presidency. And 750,000 of them are domestic manufacturing jobs, made in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But there are some experts that see reason for skepticism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE FORBES, CEO, \uc2 FORBES INC\uc2 .: And look at the household survey, which better indicates what small businesses are doing, that was down 138,000 jobs. Gets no publicity, and it was down in the month before. So that indicates trouble ahead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, the White House argument is basically this: The economy is so strong, it should be celebrated, but also the economy is struggling so much that courts should let them cancel some student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: One on student loans, if I can. A judge has halted your plan to relieve debt for millions of Americans that are struggling with student loans. What's your response to that?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're going to win that case. I think, in the next two weeks, you're going to see those checks going out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The checks didn't go out within two weeks, like he said, because that was five weeks ago -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Peter, you're such a stickler. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 So, more people might be working.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That's what I'm here for.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 But they really do have to work and maybe work even more to keep up with inflation. Take a look at those numbers.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to bring in FOX Business' David Asman with me now.\par \par \uc2 So, David, I just -- that's one thing that's always frustrated me with watching the market the way we do is the Wall Street consensus and reality.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Right. Right. Right. Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Oh, it's a strong number. It really is? You know what? It was 221,000 private sector jobs. That's down month over month.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Down from 700,000 in February. We're going in the wrong direction. In the meantime, inflation is still here and recession is looming.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: You know a much stronger number than that is that 75 percent of Americans think we're in a recession; 75 percent of Americans think we're in -- and these are the people that do the living and the day-to-day working in America.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: They're not the people that sit on the boards of these financial companies that look for slight moves in interest rates to decide where they're going to put their next $10 million.\par \par \uc2 That's where American economy really lies. That's what Democrats said they -- they used to talk about that as the heartland of America. Instead, they're now looking more like the old Republicans than the old Democrats.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm glad you brought that up. I mean, the stuff that President Biden fights for, student loan forgiveness, I mean, this -- these are like elitist programs.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That child tax credit they want to bring back, that went to families making $300,000, $400,000. In the meantime, blue-collar workers are really, really suffering.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And blue-collar workers, more than any other particular group in the economy, were the ones who did well under the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 So, again, the policies that we have now are policies that are very pleasing to many people on Wall Street, not so much to the people on the ground. And, by the way, of course, the main thing that's bothering people is the fact we got a winter coming.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And we may not have enough oil to heat people's homes or gas, natural gas. The fuel for heating is a real concern. We just saw a drawdown in oil, crude oil inventory. We're expecting a drop a two million.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it dropped 12.5 million.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: I mean, what's up with that? The fact is, that's even including the slowdown in China.\par \par \uc2 So, even though we have a slowdown in China, they're not getting as much oil as we thought they would, we still have a problem with oil right here. That's going to be a big problem going into the winter.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, and if it all combines, if they come off that COVID policy, and they have their demand, and we have ours.\par \par \uc2 Also, on this notion the about working, what struck me with this report is how many people are working more than one job, particularly women. And of all jobs -- of all women jobs, 5.5 percent are working two jobs. This is tough stuff, David. I mean, this is -- this is the reality that you're talking about.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And, again, to talk about the heartlessness of some of the analysis of this, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , our Treasury secretary, keeps talking about, oh, well, Americans have this buffer of savings. That's her phrase, a buffer of savings.\par \par \uc2 They have gone through that savings pretty quickly. That has -- they have talked about drawdowns. Americans have been drawing down their savings to pay for inflation. And they're using -- instead of reaching into their pocket because they don't have much money there, they're reaching for their credit card.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And the debt is going way up. So there are a lot of concerns.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to rain on anybody's parade. It was a better-than-expected jobs number. But, on the other hand, Americans aren't feeling it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But that's the gimmick, though. That's the gimmick. The real number, in reality, though, is down a lot.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I do want to alert you to White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He actually tweeted this: "Week in recap, jobs up, check, income up, check, growth up, check, inflation down, check. Rail shutdown averted, check. Gas prices down, check."\par \par \uc2 All right, so there's your checklist.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: He must be watching the show.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes. Well, what do you make of that, he asked?\par \par \uc2 What I make of that is the fact that there's -- there basically is a problem with the slowdown of the economy. I know that they're saying that the economy is growing more. We had two flat quarters, two negative quarters in the beginning of this year.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: We came up a little bit, but you put them all together -- you can't just separate them out. You put them together, we have got a flat economy, and a flat economy with 7 percent inflation is a negative ironically.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Ironically, that's why I think this market came back, because this jobs number, if you think about 200,000 people leaving the labor force, wages not keeping up with inflation, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  still keeping the pedal to the metal, a lot of people are now looking at a harsh recession, and the Fed is going to have to reverse course, and come to the rescue.\par \par \uc2 To your point, Wall Street likes that. Wall Street, what they want and what Main Street wants are two different things.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: But you don't come to the rescue of America by causing a recession. You come to the rescue of America by getting the private sector to work more, giving them more incentives to grow more, giving more Americans jobs and producing more goods and services.\par \par \uc2 And that's a way of cutting inflation. You don't have to just cut on inflation by recession. You can do it by increasing the amounts of goods and services that we have in the economy. But this administration is not willing to do that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. And that's why we get all skewed up -- all screwed up here.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: They love products. They hate the producers, as \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  often says.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Great stuff, my man. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Good stuff. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: By the way, folks, you do not want to miss David tomorrow. He will be hosting "Cavuto Live." He will be joined by Steve Moore, Lee Carter, Mark Penn and so many more.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: But you also got the rail workers union head, the guy who said he wasn't going to accept anything unless it had the seven sick-day leaves in it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: He didn't -- we didn't get that. So maybe he will go on strike. Who knows.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I can't wait to hear.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Good stuff.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I really can't. This was supposed to be the union president.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks a lot, David.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Meanwhile, folks, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is set to release the news, what he's calling the Hunter Biden story suppression. This is going to be amazing. He's gone through all of the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  data. He's going to share it with us at 5:00 p.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady has more -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: You know, I can always count on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to do something crazy on a Friday rolling into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, if we can bring up that tweet, he just said this banner that he is going to release the Hunter Biden information. It says right there what really happened with the Hunter Biden story suppression by \uc2 Twitter\uc2  will be published on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at 5:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 He followed it up with a popcorn emoji and then let everyone know there's going to be a live Q&A along with this. So we don't know exactly what they're going to release. But you're right there. It underscores this push for transparency.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has been teasing this for a while now, asserting that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  interfered in elections. And this also comes after ex-\uc2 Twitter\uc2  safety chief Yoel Roth recently shared in an interview with journalist Kara Swisher that, even though they had concerns about the authenticity of the laptop, it never got to the point where he thought the story should be suppressed.\par \par \uc2 That was really interesting when that came out. So we don't know what's going to come out. We don't really know whether it's going to be Elon answering the questions, how this is all going to work. But, Charles, I will certainly be on that Q&A. I am hoping to get a question answered. And we will certainly bring you the latest on what all this comes out, because folks in Washington have been really focusing on this.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: So there could be some bombshell news.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I can't wait. Let free speech rein. OK, fantastic. I will get my popcorn. Thanks, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And much more, of course, on this. We got Republican Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2  coming up.\par \par \uc2 And you saw \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  yesterday, right, ignore Hillary Vaughn's questions about China's crackdown. Did Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2  bring that up with him? I will ask him about that as well.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of \uc2 Apple\uc2 , take a look at this folks, brazen thieves ripping iPads right from the displays? This in a Chicago -- a California store, rather, customers looking on in shock. The suspects are still at large. So, why? Why do we still have to put up with this stuff? Why is it happening?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAM BANKMAN-FRIED, CEO, FTX: I got a little cocky, I mean, more than a little bit.\par \par \uc2 And I think part of me, like, felt like -- like we'd made it. I made a lot of mistakes. There are things I would give anything to be able to do over again. I didn't ever try to commit fraud on anyone.\par \par \uc2 Look, I should have been on top of this, and I feel really, really bad and regretful that I wasn't.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, lots of focus on what FTX founder \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  is saying.\par \par \uc2 He's been given multiple platforms to speak. But what about investors who are out perhaps billions of dollars saying?\par \par \uc2 Back with us today, crypto investor Evan Luthra.\par \par \uc2 Evan, I got to imagine you're just watching interview after interview after interview, the cavalier approach, the cavalier attitude, and then some of the things he's saying, it's shocking me. I mean, I'm sure you thought they were monitoring your money. It's felt like they had no controls, and they didn't care.\par \par \uc2 EVAN LUTHRA, FTX INVESTOR: Yes, that's what -- that's what we're realizing now, after hearing everything that we're seeing in the news, how the FTX lacked any sort of corporate controls or corporate governance. It's news to me now.\par \par \uc2 So, that is not what I expected when I started using FTX. Obviously, that wasn't how we expected they would run their business. But we're finding out all about this right now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, and to your point, though, you weren't supposed to know this. No one knew it. But there are people, right? There are checks and balances. There are people who invested a lot of money with him. There are people who said his company was valuable, like it was worth $25, $30 billion. He went on financial media over and over again. They lauded him, the \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  of crypto.\par \par \uc2 So you can see how people got sucked in. How do you feel about just the entire system letting you down?\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: It's very disappointing to me.\par \par \uc2 And it's even more disappointing that the system is still letting me down consistently. And that is what's really pissing me off, is, like, how does the media still lauding him. Why is it he gets a standing ovation in New York Times summit, when we all know that he defrauded people of $10 billion?\par \par \uc2 He can say whatever he wants. It does not change the facts. The fact is, he spent money that was not his, while, all at the same time, he kept lying about it that he's not spending in and the user funds are safe. And for this, what he's getting? He's getting -- he's not getting -- he's not in jail. He's not behind bars. He's getting a standing ovation in \uc2 New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To me, this really pisses me off. This was the media that really enabled him.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: A lot of the trust that was placed in him came from because of all these credible organizations backing him and setting up for SBF. And they're still doing it today.\par \par \uc2 And this is not really what should be the case.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: There's this element of elitism out there. And, of course, a lot of us folks look down on crypto investors to begin with. So it's sad that there's no sympathy, but we certainly want everyone to be safeguarded.\par \par \uc2 Are you confident at all that, ultimately, some form of justice will come out of this, starting with Congress, which is going to start their investigation? I think representative \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  put out a tweet today, and, again, sort of like lauding him. Hey, you have been great and straightforward. We will see you on the 13th.\par \par \uc2 Are you confident justice will prevail?\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: I am not confident at this moment, because I have not seen any sort of positive action.\par \par \uc2 The first thing that should have been done is the politicians have really should have refunded the $40 million-plus that they took in donations that came from stolen money. It is already clear that this was not SBF's major donate. This part is already clear. They can already start refunding those donations. They don't need to wait until the hearings to find out more, that they will need to hear it again and again that he did not give them donations from his own money.\par \par \uc2 This was stolen money at all times. So I think they can already start doing that. And, well, this hearing could also have happened while he's behind bars.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: Normally, there's -- people have been arrested for a lot less.\par \par \uc2 So the reason that he's still not behind bars is really surprising to me.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Evan, we will keep in touch with you. We feel for you. We really do. It's just what happened to you should happen to nobody. And I think a lot of that is embedded in this country with the elitism and how they have taken advantage of so many Americans.\par \par \uc2 And we have got to make it stop. Thank you so much. We will talk to you again soon.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, folks, \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  taking heat for refusing to take Hillary Vaughn's questions on China. What is California Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2 , who just met with him, saying? He's here.\par \par \uc2 But, first, another brazen robbery, as shoppers look on in shock, right, Claudia?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And it's more bad news for \uc2 Apple\uc2 , which once again fell prey to holiday thieves. You will see which store got hit this time, because the whole thing was caught on camera. I will have the story right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  set to release details on what he calls the Hunter Biden suppression story by \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , this at 5:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 And, ahead of that, we will ask Republican Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2  what he expects to see. He also met with \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Shocking video shows a Palo Alto, California, Apple Store getting ransacked in broad daylight, the two thieves yanking iPhones and other products from display tables, as employees and shoppers looked on.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Claudia Cowan is in Sausalito, California, with the very latest - - Claudia.\par \par \uc2 COWAN: Well, Charles 'tis the season for these brazen robberies.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, this time, it was an Apple Store, a very busy Apple Store in Palo Alto, hit by thieves on Black Friday, who didn't care that half the store began filming them looting the place. This cell phone video really is just so shocking. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 You can see two people with their faces covered and wearing hoodies calmly weaving around the display tables, ripping devices off security cables, and stuffing them into their backpacks