d for this behind the scenes. And they're feeling very excited.\par \par \uc2 Now, that being said, this still needs to be approved by the Republican secretary of state in Georgia. So it's not quite so simple, yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 I got a feeling that folks in Iowa and New Hampshire not feeling the same.\par \par \uc2 Emma, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Absolutely. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, to Hawaii now, where the Mauna Loa lava flow is getting closer to a major highway.\par \par \uc2 FOX Weather correspondent Max Gorden is there.\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, the flow of the lava has slowed considerably ever since it hit some flatter ground. It's now moving only at about 120 feet per hour, literally oozing along at a snail's pace. Still, it's about 2.7 miles away from the main east-west highway, and officials say the lava could hit in about a week.\par \par \uc2 Luckily, the lava isn't threatening any houses or the town of Kona at this hour. Now, the eruption of Mauna Loa began last week, the first time the world's largest active volcano has erupted since 1984. Since then, lava has been spewing from two fissures on the volcano. No evacuation orders have been issued, though Hawaii's governor did put out an emergency proclamation.\par \par \uc2 Sad to say they don't know how long this eruption will last. Currently, vog, or volcanic smog, is a concern for folks here. It's caused by the gases that are being emitted from the volcano and can potentially be harmful. Officials here are currently monitoring air quality levels.\par \par \uc2 The erupting volcano has become a major draw for visitors. After concerns about people parking along the busy Daniel K. Inouye Highway, an official volcano viewing area has now been set up. Those who come here say it's been remarkable to see the Earth's power on full display.\p