ical in the end, you know, and you think like -- it'll never get better, but it can get better and better people will make it better, I think.\par \par \uc2 Adam Carolla, great to see you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CAROLLA: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you're always hearing about how dangerous the right is, stochastic terrorism. They're criticizing me, that's the same as murder.\par \par \uc2 But whenever you see someone threatened in public for his political views, it's like a hundred percent a conservative being threatened by Antifa or some variation. That happened to Charlie Kirk last night. He tried to visit a college campus in New Mexico. The riot police had to be deployed. Yes, in America 2022.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Kirk joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thieves walked right into an \uc2 Apple\uc2  store in Palo Alto, California in broad daylight in one of the busiest shopping days of the year and just began stealing stuff, nobody stopped them. The footage is hard to watch. It is hard to believe.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Trace Gallagher has it for us now.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Trace.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 Palo Alto\uc2 , of course, the home to \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  and 10 miles away from \uc2 Apple\uc2  headquarters in Cupertino. The store is crowded with shoppers when two people walk in wearing masks. They then start walking between and in front of customers stealing iPhones, iPads, computers from the display cases. I mean, they are literally ripping them from their security cords.\par \par \uc2 One of the \uc2 Apple\uc2  employees hears a robber make an announcement to shoppers threatening physical violence to anyone that tries to stop them.\par \par \uc2 So under California law, that means the crime now includes force or fear, elevating it to felony robbery. And while the heist is underway, the employees are trying to keep customers away from the criminals when somebody says this. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just let them go.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Should we stop them?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just let them go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: So not only do the robbers face zero resistance, the customers just keep shopping, calmly shopping as if this happens every day.\par \par \uc2 Then again the sad truth is, it does happen every day. This very store has been hit numerous times over the years.\par \par \uc2 As far as this robbery goes, police are now looking for two Black men who fled the scene in a red \uc2 Mazda\uc2  hatchback. For the record, they stole about $35,000.00 in merchandise. Police have no suspects and I just talked to a defense attorney who told me police are likely not looking for suspects, further incentivizing the very lucrative business in California of stealing -- Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Beyond belief.\par \par \uc2 Trace Gallagher, thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: A healthy country with people like that can get shot because you can't allow that or else civilization falls apart.\par \par \uc2 Trace Gallagher, midnights, every night, he is the best.\par \par \uc2 Well, here's the story you have definitely seen before: A major college campus erupted into violence when someone tried to say things that were not approved by the people in charge, the Biden administration and \uc2 Apple\uc2 . That happened last night at the \uc2 University of New Mexico\uc2  in Albuquerque.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Kirk visited the campus and riot police had to be deployed. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: [Bleep] you. Get the [bleep] out of our campus.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Antifa, who are you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just a reporter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You're a reporter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He is a [bleep] White cowboy. That's what the [bleep] he is, right? [Bleep] cowboy? [Bleep].\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What do you think about the cops?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Cops? [Bleep] them. You know what I say, American. [Bleep] Americans, especially the cops. [Bleep] you.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting.)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, but the threat is the Proud Boys and QAnon.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Kirk is the Founder and President of Turning Point, USA. He joins tonight.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, thanks so much for coming on. This was clearly organized. A lot of the signs were pre-printed. Who were these people? Do you know?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE KIRK, FOUNDER, TURNING POINT USA: It's a good question. I wonder if they're paid and why they were spending their evenings to protest me and our Turning Point USA Chapter just to talk about the US Constitution and freedom of speech.\par \par \uc2 It is really interesting when you try to show up on a college campus and do an optional voluntary event how angry the other side gets, but this is a very important moment for people to recognize and understand that the other side, the left, the radical left, they are acting like the very same domestic violent extremists that the entire Federal government is now organized to go after and we're told that's on the American right.\par \par \uc2 Really? Go look at that footage. Is that the Proud Boys? Is that the American right? Are those conservatives? No. Those people are on the left, and I wonder who is financing them and who's behind it?\par \par \uc2 This is the third time this semester that we've done an event on this campus, the other two had to be canceled or ended abruptly because of these who people intervened. So I went there on a personal mission. Because the way -- the only way we lose right now is if we don't show up.\par \par \uc2 So I went there last evening just to send a message to every one of these people, to send a message to anyone that's watching that we're going to stand for free speech and you are not going to be able to disrupt our events or bully our students with force. And thankfully, the event went very well despite their best protest and their violence. Three people were arrested last evening.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But I noticed their signs are so familiar. "You're a racist." "You're a White supremacist." I happen to know that you argue, you even address race for a colorblind society, which is of course the opposite of the antidote to racism. Why is it always that slur?\par \par \uc2 I mean there are probably things you believe that they don't, they could attack you on those. But no, they called you a White supremacist and they always do. Why?\par \par \uc2 KIRK: Well first of all, because most people cower in fear when they are called a racist. They are almost -- they go into a place of paralysis. We are in the era of weaponized name calling, where people ask for forgiveness immediately and they apologize for no reason, just because they're called this.\par \par \uc2 But there is another reason. It is because the modern left, the postmodern left, they don't believe in speech. They don't believe you're an individual made in the image of God. They believe you are a member of a collective identity group and they believe that they can then shut us up just by calling us White supremacist and racist.\par \par \uc2 I invited every single one of them to come into our event and they could have the microphone and make their case, but they do not believe that speech is a value. All they care about is force and power.\par \par \uc2 This is the country they're trying to lead us towards. What happened in Albuquerque yesterday is not an aberration, it is a coming attraction. It is something that is soon going to happen all across the country if we don't get this right. We win when we show up.\par \par \uc2 And one last thing I'll say is despite everything the media has said over the last couple of weeks about Gen Z, the good news is that over the last five years, young people have become 13 points less liberal, according to Morning Consult. I don't think that the behavior yesterday is popular, nor do I think it's emblematic of an entire generation.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, it's just a complete dead end. I mean, it's just darkness. So I hope you're right in those numbers.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Kirk, great to see tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KIRK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So here is a story that should be leading every newscast, should be on the front page of every paper. The human species may not be able to continue because sperm counts have fallen so far, so fast that in a few years, no more babies. What is going on? And why is no one talking about it. We'll address it after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So fewer people are having children or those who are having fewer children. Why is this? We did a whole documentary on this topic.\par \par \uc2 So the assumption has always been it's cultural. As people get richer, they have fewer kids. But it turns out there may be biological reasons, chemical reasons for it, which is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 NeuroSearch\uc2  and a leading journal human reproduction update shows that worldwide not just in the West, but everywhere, the mean sperm count for men decreased by more than 50 percent from 1973 to 2018. So, if that trend continues, men will not be able to have children in say another 50 years.\par \par \uc2 So this is a crisis. How is it not a crisis? And yet the media, instead of covering it are criticizing people for having too many kids like Elon Musk, very strange. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MSNBC: A unique pro natalist movement is reportedly being led by mostly White, tech, and venture capitalist billionaires who are having as many children as possible to counteract the impact of population decline.\par \par \uc2 JULIA BLACK, INSIDER CORRESPONDENT: Somebody in the Austin Community reached out to me to suggest that Elon had more children than the public had previously realized and that this actually might be part of sort of a wider project.\par \par \uc2 And in the end, it has turned out to be a wider project to save the world. They believe it is their responsibility actually to give as much of themselves as possible to the rest of Planet Earth.\par \par \uc2 And yes, you know, there is some ego involved in that point of view for sure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the problem is, Elon Musk is having too many kids. What do we think of Elon Musk? Probably not the real problem.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carmen Messerlian is a professor of Reproductive Epidemiology at Harvard. She joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Professor, thank you so much for coming on. These numbers seem to people like me who don't really understand the science behind them, kind of shocking. Are you shocked by them? What do they mean? And what's the cause do you think?\par \par \uc2 DR. CARMEN MESSERLIAN, PROFESSOR OF REPRODUCTIVE EPIDEMIOLOGY AT HARVARD: Well, I'm not -- I'm not shocked by them, because this is what I teach in my classroom. And I had, well, I talk about sperm and about eggs and how have we mate and reproduce and make babies, and the numbers are not shocking.\par \par \uc2 We know that we live in a very complex environment where our environment is hostile and toxic to reproductive health, and this has to do with the products that we're exposed to, the environment that we're exposed to every single day. Men and men's sperm is in decline and we've been witnessing this as scientists for quite a long time.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you're stating this as fact. You teach it. Products that we use, chemicals in the air, water, and ground are making men -- well, ultimately make them unable to reproduce. Why isn't anyone doing anything about that?\par \par \uc2 MESSERLIAN: Well, I'm not suggesting not able to reproduce. Men produce 125 million sperm per ejaculate, on average. So you know, we have to consider the clinical implications of that is that you still have a lot of sperm in each ejaculate, and it only takes one sperm to have a baby, as you may or may not know.\par \par \uc2 And so yes, so the declines we can't really suggest the clinical significance of that. We do know that the concentration and count of sperm has gone down. We know that the environment plays a big role in. The number one rule, it is not related to genetics. Genetics doesn't change in 50 years, but the environment has changed.\par \par \uc2 And what we've seen is men have gotten fatter, they've gotten unhealthier. Men's health is in general decline. And as a result of that, we see measures of that in sperm. But we also see changes in men's testosterone levels. We see changes in men's sexual performance, in how they -- their moods, their energy, their rates of depression have gone up and this is bad for women, too, because heterosexual women want men to be healthy and happy, and women want to be satisfied sexually.\par \par \uc2 And if men are -- well, if their health is imperiled, then our health is imperiled, too which is really important for the kind of work that I do.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Everything you've said, as a non-scientist seems inarguable and like commonsense, and rooted in a pro-human worldview, which I appreciate. But I'm going to ask you one more time, do you think this is getting enough coverage? It seems like a huge deal.\par \par \uc2 MESSERLIAN: No, I don't think it's getting enough coverage at all and I think the part that's not getting enough coverage is the part related to how our environment is impacting your reproductive health and our evolutionary reproductive capacity, and this has to do with both men and women.\par \par \uc2 And so what are we talking about? We're talking about the food we eat, the air we breathe, the water we drink. Our environment is having a huge impact on our reproductive capacity, and men are getting fatter.\par \par \uc2 We know that, for example, high fat diets result in decreased testosterone levels, as well as decreased sperm production. So, there's a lot of factors that play a part in this. Diet is a big one, the environments another big one, lifestyle factors, smoking, alcohol consumption, marijuana use, all of these things have an important impact on the results that we're seeing now in this big meta-analysis that you commented on.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of factors have contributed to this. It's not one particular thing, but there's a lot of things men can do. We have an impact on our environment. We make choices every day, lifestyle choices, behavioral choices. Men have choice to change their diet, decrease their smoking, exercise, lose weight, choose healthier products in their day-to-day environment, personal care products have a huge burden of chemicals that I study related to things like oxalates and phenols.\par \par \uc2 BPA, for example, and DEHP are two chemical classes that we know harm men and harm male reproductions. They are anti-androgenic and estrogenic. And yes, we see these results coming out now in big studies that tell us that men's sperm is going down and we've been looking at this from an environmental reproductive point of view for quite some time. And yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I hope a lot of people hear what you just said and take it seriously. And I appreciate you coming on tonight.\par \par \uc2 Professor Messerlian from Harvard. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MESSERLIAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So there have always been rats in New York City, big rats, big Norway rats, giant rats, kind of the rats that might eat you, but now there are more rats than ever, so the city has hired a swashbuckling rat czar to solve the rat problem once and for all. Will that work? Will the rat czar be successful in New York?\par \par \uc2 Two professional rat catchers assess this rat control plan, after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It looks like \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  may have run the biggest scam in history, but he doesn't seem worried about being prosecuted for making off apparently with billions because he knows the regulators and he is the second biggest donor of the Democratic Party. So he is bold enough to go on television and seem unashamed.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, he headlined "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " DealBook Summit. This morning, he was on "Good Morning America."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAM BANKMAN-FRIED, FOUNDER, FTX: When you look at the classic \uc2 Bernie Madoff\uc2  story, there was no real business there. The whole thing, as I understand it, I think was just one big Ponzi scheme, right? FTX that was a real business.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, it was a real business. Why does that guy -- he is not an arrest? Oh, he did not trespass at the Capitol. He didn't walk into \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's office, so there is no SWAT team in his house. He is talking to George Stephanopoulos in the Bahamas. No problem.\par \par \uc2 How's he is getting away with this exactly? How is he doing that?\par \par \uc2 Well, for "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today," we sat down with a man who has participated in our finance economy and covered it and is one of the most honest people we know on those topics. His name is Max Keiser is a big Bitcoin investor, and he assessed the \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  story for us. Here is part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAX KEISER, BITCOIN INVESTOR: \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2 , the key to his empire of fraud is that he created his own play money token called FTT and he was able to create that without any oversight or any tie to anything underlying -- giving it value whatsoever, and this is a whole cryptographic scam that has been going on with the crypto market where individuals -- he is not the only one. There are many people that create these what are called alt- coins, or scam coins, and they create -- Ether is another one or Cardano or XRP, these are all coins that are just created.\par \par \uc2 And then they list these coins on each other's exchange, and then they buy them from each other to create a price and then they use the enhanced price, which now has a collateral value to go buy something like \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  did, real estate in the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEISER: Right. So, it's a Ponzi scheme. And implied in the \uc2 Sam Bankman- Fried\uc2  scam is none other than \uc2 Gary Gensler\uc2  over at the SEC, who should have been calling time on this a long ago, but we find out that he is actually involved and that there is some, what I would call collusion, and the problem is in America, you have a country that's ruled by a kleptocracy.\par \par \uc2 Every institution in America is tied to Wall Street in some way. They've all been financialized. They all use cheap money. They are all cross collateralizing each other's assets. They're all using that money to buy real assets, and they are all undermining the economy in fundamental ways, which lead to inflation, which leads to unemployment, which lead to all kinds of dysfunctions in the economy and our medical system, and all across the institutions.\par \par \uc2 It all goes back to essentially, the deregulation that happened 40 years ago, which led to the financialization and the over indebtedness, the over leveraging of the economy, and now in 2022, since interest rates are going up, that's the end of the mirage, that the bubble has been popped.\par \par \uc2 And if the FTX scandal and \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  scandal was like the last dregs of a 40-year bacchanal in cheap money, no regulation and crooked bankers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's all true, by the way and keep in mind that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made one of the people responsible for it, the former Chair of the Fed, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , the Treasury Secretary, nobody said anything.\par \par \uc2 That is Max Keiser. Full interview available right now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 So New York has a lot of problems -- New York City -- one of them is a rat problem. And the Mayor of New York says he doesn't like rats, so they have just posted the job listing for a swashbuckling rat czar. A successful applicant will have "general aura of badassery" according to the job listing. Successful applicants will also have, "virulent vehemence for vermin" and the "drive, determination and killer instinct needed to fight the real enemy, New York City's relentless rat population."\par \par \uc2 Does that sound like a plan for success? We thought we would check with the experts, Dave Schuelke, Jim Schuelke would know, they are twins,. They are professional rat catchers. Their business is called Twin Home Experts. And we are honored to have them on the show tonight. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to pick at random. Jim, will this work?\par \par \uc2 JIM SCHUELKE, TWIN HOME EXPERTS: Yes, I mean, when it comes to ratting in exterminating rats, Tucker, you've got to start somewhere. But what's going to be crucial is that you've got to be two steps ahead of these rats. You make one move, it is like playing a game of chess. The strategy has to continue to move in a positive direction. So yes, taking all the trash off the sidewalks, the streets, because when it comes to rats, they love that food and water source.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So Dave, let me ask you your brother has use the term ratter. For centuries, Europeans used terriers, small dogs as ratting dogs and they kept the rat population down with dogs. The dogs loved it and it worked. Why not try that?\par \par \uc2 DAVE SCHUELKE, TWIN HOME EXPERTS: Well, that's the thing. You know, we've been in the trenches with these rats, Tucker, and watching these rats, the intelligence behind them, we brought in dogs, we've done -- we've brought in snakes. We have done everything to be two steps ahead of them.\par \par \uc2 It can't just be dogs, it can't just be cats. It has to be a lot of different situations. So you have to take a structure, you have to look at the infestation, or even the city and you've got to really look at it to where, okay, what do we need here to combat these rats.\par \par \uc2 So it's not just one thing. So dogs work, but again, what we've learned being in the trenches is you've got to have technology, you've got to have dogs, you've got to have minks even to get these rats under control.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Minks would be considered -- so you're calling for a holistic approach to rat infestation.\par \par \uc2 Jim, let me go back to you and ask you a question. I know you're asked a lot what's the biggest rat you've ever seen?\par \par \uc2 JIM SCHUELKE: The biggest rat was in Washington, DC we caught. It was almost two and a half pounds. It was humongous.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Can you show with your hands how big a two-and-a-half pound rat is?\par \par \uc2 JIM SCHUELKE: It was about this big and the body was about this thick and the tail. I mean, it was -- it was humongous. It was shocking.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Can you tell us where in Washington it was living? You know, I don't want to hurt anyone's feelings, but where in Washington was this?\par \par \uc2 JIM SCHUELKE: It was underneath -- living underneath this deck. This lady had hired three or four other pest control companies, they were using snap traps and poisons. And we went in there, had a rodent dog and we got it right underneath that structure.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, a rat. And Dave, one last question for you. Are you ever afraid? I mean, rat that big is a threat to life and limb. Are you ever nervous around rats?\par \par \uc2 DAVE SCHUELKE: You know, in the very beginning, I was but we've gotten so comfortable being around so many of them. To be honest with you, I have a lot of respect for rats. It's unbelievable the intelligence behind this creature.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, we all go native in the end, I'm not judging you. I appreciate both of you guys coming out and giving us perspective on the ratting business. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 DAVE SCHUELKE: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 JIM SCHUELKE: Thanks for having us on.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, all of a sudden you're hearing a lot about ESG. It is being promoted by everybody. It's the idea that you invest only in left-wing operations. One man has decided to counter ESG by promoting businesses that support America. He explains his business idea next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So right now, pretty much every big institutional investor in the West is guided by something called ESG, and the idea is these are not terms that are narrowly defined. Of course, they never are. But the idea is you invest your money in companies whose values align with those of the Democratic Party, and this is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 But there is at least one person who has decided to make it easier for the rest of us to buck that trend. He has made an app that promotes companies that promote the United States. The man is Michael Seifert. He's the founder and CEO of PublicSq. He joins us to explain.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much, Michael, for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So how does this work exactly?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SEIFERT, FOUNDER AND CEO, PUBLICSQ: Thanks for having me, Tucker. It is an honor to be here. So we've been obviously pretty frustrated watching over the past few years as these major corporations that dictate so much of our lives have embraced this leftist ideology best encompassed by ESG -- environmental social governance, which really means that they're going to show you certain favor.\par \par \uc2 Companies like \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Nike\uc2 , Target, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  -- the list goes on -- they'll show you certain favor, as long as you agree with their political views. But the minute that you speak out of line with the beliefs of the regime of the day, you're at risk of your bank account being removed, you're at risk of cancellation from their platforms, or with the inability to use their services any longer.\par \par \uc2 And so what we decided to do during the COVID season, I was pretty frustrated by watching certain businesses be labeled essential and other businesses be labeled non-essential, and so often it was the major corporations like we are describing that were labeled essential.\par \par \uc2 So we decided, let's promote the small businesses that make our country special. We believe they are essential. They love our country, stand for freedom, believe in the values that have made this country so special in the first place, and would never infringe upon the rights of their consumers or their employees.\par \par \uc2 We decided, let's create a platform that highlights and celebrates those businesses. So today, now we are as of July 4th, this summer, we nationwide launched and we're the largest platform of patriotic businesses the country has ever seen, and we're just getting started.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's amazing. How do you find it?\par \par \uc2 SEIFERT: Thank you, you can head to PublicSq.com. That's the best place to get started. And it's all free for the business and the user. We have hundreds of thousands of consumers that are enjoying the app actively. We have tens of thousands of small businesses across all different industries.\par \par \uc2 So if you're fearing of cancellation from your current bank or you're wanting to buy clothes that don't lecture you about your politics while they themselves go use slave labor in China, you can head to our platform, sign up for free and within just a few minutes, you can browse all these different industries and know with a blessed assurance that you're not funding your opposition, you're funding companies that love America that make you feel proud to be an American in the process.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I would like to buy clothes that don't lecture me.\par \par \uc2 SEIFERT: Yes, don't we all.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Kind of a baseline desire for me. Michael Siefert, PublicSq. Great to see you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SEIFERT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So Stacey Abrams just lost again. Again. She was Governor of Georgia, unrecognized. She ran for Governor, lost again. She is not going back to writing soft-core porn, however. Now, she is lobbying to become an \uc2 FCC\uc2  Commissioner, the \uc2 Federal Communications Commission\uc2 . Is this true? That would mean she gets to regulate the media.\par \par \uc2 We're going to look into it for tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, We hope you have the best evening with the ones you love and now, a full 15 seconds early is our friend \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "HANNITY": I used to give you such a hard time about hitting the post. You know, you just slap me virtually in the head. I deserve it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for the extra time.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Tucker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020221202eic100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  Meets With \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  After Economic Policy Criticism; Senate Votes To Prevent Rail Strike; Methods Of FTX Cryptocurrency Under Scrutiny; \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  And \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  In Close Race For Senate Seat; Interview With Rep. Jan Schakowsky (D-IL) And Rep. \uc2 Gus Bilirakis\uc2  (R-FL) On Bipartisan Efforts To Pass Consumer Protection Legislation; Authorities Now Unsure If Four University Of Idaho Murder Victims Specifically Targeted; Congress Passes Legislation To Stop Railway Strike Which Does Not Include Paid Sick Leave For Workers; \uc2 Twitter\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Criticizes \uc2 Apple\uc2 's Control Over App Store; Congress Holds Hearings On Failure Of FTX Cryptocurrency Exchange\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Chad Pergram, Rich Edson, Jonathan  Serrie, Mike Emanuel, Gillian Turner, Christina Coleman \par \uc2 7,659 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So, next Tuesday is a big night for him. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: All right, good for him.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Judge. Thank you. Good evening and welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 The former head of the now collapse cryptocurrency giant FTX says he's trying to figure out what went wrong.\par \par \uc2 Confusion reigns in Idaho as authorities investigating the murder of four university students issued new statements about the attacks, we'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 And the Georgia Senate runoff becomes a battle of the surrogates as popular figures lend a hand to the candidates.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, the threat of a crippling national railroad strike has been averted. About two hours ago, the Senate passed a bill already approved by the House to bind all rail companies and workers to a tentative settlement reached in September but rejected by some unions. The deal does not include the paid leave provision unions wanted.\par \par \uc2 Today's action came as President Biden and the leader of America's oldest ally talked about trying to get peace in a different kind of conflict the one in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. The most animated President Biden got today was when he was trying to explain at a press conference how much he supports paid leave even though he's backing this deal for rail workers that doesn't have the paid leave they want.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I negotiated a contract no one else could negotiate.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The president's priority, avoiding a rail strike, smoothing things over with rail unions can wait.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The only thing that was left out was whether or not it was paid leave. But that other team, they're called the Republicans, they voted against it.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: A top the to do list at today's meeting, Russia's invasion of Ukraine and there's agreement between France and the U.S. not to rush into peace talks.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm prepared to speak with Mr. Putin if in fact there is an interest in him deciding he's looking for a way to end the war. He hasn't done that yet.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Side by side, things were friendly, but before French President Macron arrived today, he accused U.S. of pushing policies that could hurt Europe.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , FRENCH PRESIDENT: CHIPS Acts, Inflation Reduction Acts are both very good for the U.S. economy. But as they were not properly coordinated with the European economies, they create just the absence of a level playing field.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden isn't going back on plans to make more semiconductors in the States.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We invented the damn things.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And he says he understands Macron's concerns.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're going to continue to create manufacturing jobs in America but not at the expense of Europe.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Biden and Macron hesitated to call out China though. Part of a joint statement says the president's also expressed their respect for the Iranian people in particular women and youth who are bravely protesting to gain the freedom to exercise their human rights.\par \par \uc2 So, that's support for protesters in Iran. Nothing about protesters in China.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Ultimately, especially on the economy, America and her oldest ally seem to be doing all right.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm confident, it's my answer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): The president is going to have to change into a tuxedo shortly because he is hosting a steak dinner for the first time for his French guests. We've seen the menu, there will be lobster, there will be caviar. There will be three different kinds of American cheeses, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, behind the scenes with the menu. Thank you, Peter.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some behind the scenes information about today's Senate action and what it actually means. Here's Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The joint resolution is passed.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A Christmas crisis avoided, the Senate voting 80-15 with one present, as Congress intervened to prevent a national railroad strike for the first time in three decades. But pro labor Democrats took a loss in the effort to salvage a shaky economy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): A strike or any kind of shutdown would hurt us at a time when our economy is just starting to show positive signs of recovery.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: President Biden dispatched Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  and Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  to Capitol Hill to warn pro -labor senators about the consequences of inaction. A liberal push for medical leave could have tanked the deal.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): This amendment simply says seven paid sick days for workers in the rail industry. And I hope we could win it.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But the paid medical leave couldn't overcome a filibuster.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): If we had 60 votes in the Senate, it could possibly happen but we don't have 60 votes in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Lawmakers from both sides oppose congressional intervention in the labor dispute.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): The main thing is I don't know why we're having to do this but we are apparently, I want to make sure that we do no harm to the economy.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Some Republicans advocated another two months cooling off period.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DAN SULLIVAN (R-AK): It will give negotiators more time to get to an agreement.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But that effort failed as well. Rail workers get pay hikes in the agreement, but progressives downplayed that win for the unions.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: The workers received a 24 percent increase in their wages, sounds like a lot of money. They have not received a pay increase in the last three years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Lawmakers needed to act fast, shipments for agriculture and chemicals for safe water would stop over the weekend, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Live on the Hill, Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, a federal appeals court has halted an independent review of documents seized from former President Trump's Florida State Mar- a-Lago. This removes a hurdle delaying the Justice Department's criminal investigation into the retention of top secret government information. This is a big win for the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 The documents were seized during a search of the Mar-a-Lago compound in August, the former president's lawyers had contended the former president was entitled to have so-called -- a so-called special master. Conducted neutral review of the thousands of documents taken from the property. The Supreme Court could be asked now to review this issue.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers on Capitol Hill are looking for answers tonight after the first day of hearings over the collapse of one of the world's largest cryptocurrency exchanges. Much of the focus is on former FTX CEO \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2 . Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson has the story tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROSTIN BEHNAM\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, \uc2 COMMODITY FUTURES TRADING COMMISSION\uc2 : I think it's going to take months probably before we truly understand the extent and scope of the failure.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): That's FTX a crypto currency exchange once valued at $32 billion. It fell apart and is now short an estimated eight billion. From the Bahamas, former CEO \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  against the advice of his lawyers, he says, is now trying to explain what happened.\par \par \uc2 SAM BANKMAN-FRIED, FORMER CEO, FTX: I really deeply wished that I had taken like a lot more responsibility for understanding what the details were of what was going on there.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: This morning, the Senate committee became the first congressional panel to investigate this massive collapse. FTX may owe money to more than a million creditors from major investment firms to individuals who say they invested their life savings.\par \par \uc2 EVAN LUTHRA, FTX INVESTOR: Do you have a plan to get us our money back? Who is on your side? Well, why are you not in jail?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: FTX is in bankruptcy, Bankman-Fried is accused of transferring funds from FTX to another firm to finance risky bets. Those went bad and the whole operation collapsed. The Crypto mogul says he never tried to commit fraud.\par \par \uc2 BANKMAN-FRIED: I should have been on top of this and I feel really really bad and regretful that I wasn't and a lot of people got hurt. And that that's on me.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The FTX founder faces investigations from the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  and the Justice Department. He says he has less than $100,000 now and access to only one credit card.\par \par \uc2 Bankman-Fried was the second highest donor to Democrats during the most recent midterm cycle. He now claims he gave the same amount to groups supporting Republicans that are not required to disclose their funding.\par \par \uc2 One top Democrat says he has no idea Bankman-Fried was even his donor.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: So, the cryptocurrency people are active politically and they are trying to achieve a political end here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Bankman-Fried says he would not be surprised if he were testifying here before Congress one day. It just so happens to be a House hearing on the FTX collapse the week after next. That committee's leaders say they expect Bankman-Fried to appear, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich Edson live on the Hill. Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today ahead of tomorrow's November jobs report. The Dow lost 195, the S&P 500 fell four, the NASDAQ gained 14.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court will hear arguments in February over President Biden's plan to forgive hundreds of billions of dollars of student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, a federal appeals court denied the Biden administration's request to overturn a district judge's decision placing a stay on the program, that stay now remains in place. A ruling from the Supreme Court could be reached before June of next year.\par \par \uc2 A few moments ago, the White House released a statement saying President Biden welcomes the Supreme Court's decision to take up this case.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday, Georgia voters will decide whether Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  keeps his job. Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is engaged in a runoff for that seat.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Warnock get some high-profile assistance down in Georgia. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie tells us about it from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Democrats hope one more Georgia rally with former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  will help Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  when his December 6 runoff against Trump endorsed Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both former President Trump and President Biden have stayed away from Georgia as the Senate campaigns embrace less controversial advocates.\par \par \uc2 TIA MITCHELL, WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, ATLANTA JOURNAL-CONSTITUTION: \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is to \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , what \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  helps \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is to him.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is loaning Walker his massive get out the vote operation. But retiring Republican Lieutenant Governor Geoff Duncan says when he showed up for early voting, he saw the most disappointing ballot of his life.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GEOFF DUNCAN (R-GA): You know, I had two candidates that I just couldn't find anything that made sense for me to put my vote on, and so I walked out of that ballot box. Showing up to vote but not voting for either one of them.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: With the last day of early voting on Friday, an advanced ballots already topping one million, the attacks from both sides continue.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA: But I'm going to tell you something I found out: A werewolf could kill a vampire, did you know that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What is he talking about?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I didn't know that, so I didn't want to be a vampire anymore, I want to be a werewolf.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: A citizen complaint alleges Walker voted illegally in Georgia because he claimed a homestead exemption in Texas. Walker, who faces multiple domestic violence complaints from his past is running an ad featuring a police call to an argument between Warnock and his former wife.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Warnock thought no one was watching when his ex-wife called police to report his abuse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He's a great actor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): Police filed no charges in the Warnock case after medical experts determine there was no evidence of any injury.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, it's unlikely that Walker status as a Georgia voter is in any jeopardy. Georgia elections officials say residency is largely based on where a person intends to live, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Serrie live in Atlanta. Jonathan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 An Arizona judge is ordering local officials to certify the midterm election results by the end of the day today. The judge says Republican supervisors in Cochise County broke the law when they refuse to sign off on the vote count by this week's deadline.\par \par \uc2 Two Republicans on that county's three-member board of supervisors say they're not satisfied. The machines used to tabulate ballots were properly certified for use in elections. State and federal election officials have said they were.\par \par \uc2 Up next, while Republicans and Democrats are fighting over legislation to expand assistance to victims of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): This bill would take a few billion dollars of unspent COVID funds that Democrats have spent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Adding the Beirut bombing victims to the 911 families at this time may ensure that no one gets any justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Russian shelling has cut power to the recently liberated Ukrainian city of Kherson. This comes just days after it was restored amid Moscow's ongoing drive to destroy key civilian infrastructure as freezing weather sets in inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Kyiv's mayor is warning capital residents, they should stock up on water and preserve food to see them through a winter that could prove miserable if additional energy infrastructure is damaged.\par \par \uc2 He's also urging people to consider leaving the city if possible.\par \par \uc2 Back here in the U.S. Republicans and Democrats in Congress are at odds tonight over a bill to expand and improve federal assistance to terror attack victims.\par \par \uc2 Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel tells us what's holding up the new legislation.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Nearly 40 years after the terror attack on the U.S. Marine barracks in Beirut, which killed 241 service members, Arkansas Republican Senator Tom Cotton says Congress must help their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): I hope that all Democrats would join with us in ensuring that we take care of both the 9/11 families and the families of the Beirut Marines.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Cotton and fellow Republican Alaska Senator Dan Sullivan are proposing using unspent COVID funds to expand The Fairness for 9/11 Victims Act to include the 1983 Beirut attack.\par \par \uc2 Retired Marine Corps Brass, wrote to leaders \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  on Veterans Day, "While the evil done that day can never be undone, Congress now has an opportunity to help achieve a measure of justice for the families of those killed in the attack."\par \par \uc2 Senate Foreign Relations Committee Chairman Democrat \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  of New Jersey has concerns about timing.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ (D-NJ): I want to help them. I believe they should be helped. I just don't believe that when we finally can have justice for 9/11 families that 9/11 family should be denied, and no one will receive anything.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The House passed the 9/11 measure with an overwhelmingly bipartisan 400 to 31 vote on September 30th.\par \par \uc2 Any last-minute alteration in the Senate would require sending it back to the House to vote again. That worries 9/11 families, including Terry Strada, whose husband Tom died in the World Trade Centre attack.\par \par \uc2 TERRY STRADA, NATIONAL CHAIR, 9/11 FAMILIES UNITED: You've worked seven years to hopefully get it over the finish line, and you've dangled this in front of everyone, as it's going to be successful. And then, something like this happens. There's a lot of frustration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): Any unresolved business at the end of the term would need to start over next year in a new Congress. That pressure can force action or potentially a delay. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I will watch it. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we continue our look at the new National Defense survey coming ahead of the Reagan Defense Forum, beginning tomorrow in Simi Valley, California.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT will broadcast live from there, Friday. This evening, new exclusive information concerning how Americans feel about the threat from terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Gillian Turner is at the Pentagon tonight.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: The Fifth Annual Reagan National Defense survey finds Americans are becoming less concerned about the threat of terrorist attacks on the homeland.\par \par \uc2 Just over three quarters today say they are concerned. That's down from 85 percent five years ago.\par \par \uc2 This comes as the Pentagon announces another ISIS leader has been killed, this time, by the Free Syrian Army without U.S. help.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Central Command calls the development a blow to the terrorist group, but insists ISIS remains a threat to the region.\par \par \uc2 Today, ISIS is regrouping in Syria even carrying out operations in the open.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We do remain deeply concerned about the escalating actions in northern Syria, Iraq, and Turkey.\par \par \uc2 Turkey is now considering launching a ground invasion into northern Syria, targeting the Kurds. U.S. allies Turkey considers to be terrorists.\par \par \uc2 RECEP TAYYIP ERDOGAN, PRESIDENT OF TURKEY (through translator): Our determination to protect our borders with a 30-kilometre security strip is getting stronger with every incident we experience.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Pentagon officials are concerned this would pose a direct threat to U.S. troops in the region.\par \par \uc2 RYDER: The Secretary Austin relayed the department's strong opposition to any potential ground invasion in northern Syria. And again, the focus here is on enabling and ensuring that the defeat ISIS mission can continue unimpeded.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The Pentagon says Secretary Austin has made clear to his counterpart that the Biden administration opposes a Turkish invasion of Syria.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Moving forward, the Defense Department says that the U.S. military will conduct fewer joint patrols with the Syrian Democratic Forces. This, in order to reduce the threat of harm to U.S. service members who are in the area. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian Turner at the Pentagon. Gillian, thank you. As mentioned, tomorrow, SPECIAL REPORT will be live from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library for the start of the Reagan National Defense Forum.\par \par \uc2 We'll have a preview of what military experts are saying about big national security issues.\par \par \uc2 Saturday, you can watch the panels and speeches streaming live on Fox Nation.\par \par \uc2 Up next. This week's "COMMON GROUND" segment takes a look at consumer protection and big tech.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 Spanish police detonate a suspicious envelope at the U.S. Embassy in Madrid. It comes one day after a similar package sent to the Ukrainian embassy ignited as an employee opened the letter.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in Spain say other explosives were sent throughout the country, they have not determined who is responsible.\par \par \uc2 Cities in China are pulling back some coronavirus restrictions after government protests erupted over the country's strict zero-COVID policy.\par \par \uc2 Several locations are easing testing and isolation requirements. Beijing has allowed some people who test positive to isolate at home rather than in crowded quarantine centers.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Sydney, Australia. One of the big stories there tonight. An economist who was imprisoned in Burma is welcomed home.\par \par \uc2 Australian lawmakers gave Sean Turnell a standing ovation on the Parliament's final sitting. Day of the year, Burmese authorities arrested Turnell in 2021, accused him of violating the country's official secrets law and immigration law. He was released in mid-November as part of the prisoner amnesty.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's time now for our "Common Ground" segment. Joining us tonight, Florida Republican Congressman \uc2 Gus Bilirakis\uc2  and Illinois Democratic Congresswoman Jan Schakowsky. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 And we try to bring lawmakers from both sides of the aisle and talk about things that are actually getting done on Capitol Hill. We obviously cover a lot of the back and forth in the division. But you do come together. You have some common ground.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, just this INFORM Consumers Act that you both are working on here.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAN SCHAKOWSKY (D-IL): Absolutely. You know, \uc2 Gus Bilirakis\uc2  and I have really been able to work together to protect consumers and businesses, and just the American people from being defrauded.\par \par \uc2 And the legislation that we hope is going to be signed in any minute, we hope, into law, the INFORM Consumers Act, will actually protect consumers from fraudulent products, from dangerous products, from counterfeit products.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, too many consumers and many of us are doing more and more shopping online are finding that they aren't getting what they paid for.\par \par \uc2 And a number of businesses have found themselves to be defraud. And so, the legislation that we pass is that there -- these online marketplaces are going to have to be confirmed to be the real deal.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  can help enforce it. And they will not be able to do their business online if they are found to be not who they say they are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Congressman Bilirakis, that's important for businesses that they are losing money for all of these people that are doing fraudulent counterfeit or stolen goods out on the marketplace.\par \par \uc2 REP. GUS BILIRAKIS (R-FL): What's happening is these criminals are going into the stores and they are stealing the products and selling them on the market, in the marketplaces. We need to hold the platforms accountable. We want to prevent this retail crime that's going on because the consumers need to know where they are purchasing the item. We passed it out of the House recently, and Jan did an outstanding job. And now we've got to get it through the Senate and get it on the president's desk as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman, or Congresswoman, for people who look at Washington and say it's broken, what do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 SCHAKOWSKY: Well, I think they really ought to look at the Consumer Protection and Commerce Subcommittee, because even if we disagree on a bill and we don't pass a bill, we are really working together to find common ground. And we have found it on a number of different kinds of issues. Gus and I have sent letters to TikTok, for example, where a lot of our kids are online, about the problems that have arisen there. I think one of the most important is, and we still haven't gotten it over the finish line, but the privacy bill, the American online privacy and protection act that we want to get passed, that would make consumers feel more confident when they are online. We have bipartisan support coming out of our committee almost unanimously. It happens more than you think. At least in our committee it does.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congresswoman, what about today, and this may not be common ground with your Republican colleagues, about sick leave?\par \par \uc2 SCHAKOWSKY: Well, first of all, let me strongly say that I support people being able to gets sick leave and especially people who work on a dangerous industry like on the railroads. So, I certainly support it. I think that having a rail strike would be really unfortunate. I voted for both pieces of the legislation. And there was a separate vote on the -- extending the workers advantages. So I support -- I support that, but I am also understanding how desperate it would be if we had a rail strike right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Where are you on that, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 BILIRAKIS: Well, I disagree. See, on the consumer protection issues, we agree, and it's so very important that we pass this legislation on behalf of the American people. I don't think Congress should be intervening in this. I don't think it's their place to intervene in a dispute like this. So, I voted no.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. There are going to be things that you disagree with and on, and different -- you come from different sides of the aisle. But last word to both of you. Are there other things that you see on the agenda that potentially you two or your colleagues from both sides of the aisle could be working on?\par \par \uc2 SCHAKOWSKY: We are driven by what is really nonpartisan issues that consumers bring to us. And this whole issue of big tech and the dangers and concerns that people have when they are on the computer, they want to know that they are not going to have their information, their private information stolen, that they will have the opportunity to control it themselves. That is what is in our privacy bill. This is not Republicans or Democrats. This is the vast majority of Americans who feel uncomfortable online.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last word, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 BILIRAKIS: I agree. And this is a nonpartisan issue, the privacy act, but it's so very important we protect this information, particularly for our children who are being affected by this. And China, what they are doing with TikTok, that needs to be looked into. Jan and I wrote letters to \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2  on this particular case. They need to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congresswoman Schakowsky, Congressman Bilirakis, thanks very much for coming on Common Ground. Spread the word up there. We'll get some more people on here.\par \par \uc2 BILIRAKIS: My pleasure, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SCHAKOWSKY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BILIRAKIS: Absolutely. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can catch an extended version of this interview on my new Podcast "Common Ground" and you see all the Common Ground segments we've done there. They can be found under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewsPodcasts.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. The whole discussion can also be seen on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, serious confusion from law enforcement investigating the University of Idaho student massacre.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Border officials say at least 73,000 illegal immigrants avoided arrest in November. That number is higher than the total number of migrant encounters recorded by agents during the same time period last year. Since the 2023 fiscal year began, October 1st, Border Patrol estimates there have been more than 137,000 got-aways.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there is major uncertainty from law enforcement concerning the massacre of four University of Idaho students two-and-a-half weeks ago. Authorities now say they do not know if the victims were specifically targeted. This comes as investigators begin to receive forensic testing results. Correspondent Christina Coleman is in Moscow, Idaho, tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRACE RUBLE, STUDENT: It's definitely confusing, and we are just kind of hoping that we get some answers soon.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Moscow police threw people for a loop last night when they said they did not know if four University of Idaho students murdered more than two weeks ago were victims of a targeted attack. That was a major reversal from what they have been saying.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We've told the public very clearly from the beginning that we believe it was a targeted attack.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: But, by this morning, they reversed themselves, saying that they do indeed believe this was targeted attack. The back and forth leaving both students and local residents confused and worried.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TUCKER, STUDENT: Hearing this gives me a pause to think back and forth like, like what is our sense of safety here in Moscow?\par \par \uc2 STEVE GONCALVES, FATHER: I don't like it one bit.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Steve Goncalves is the father of Kaylee one of the four victims. Grieving his daughter's loss, he is frustrated.\par \par \uc2 GONCALVES: I know these statements take like hours and multiple individuals to review. It's not like this was something said on the side of the street within the first five hours of the case.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Meantime, the community continues to grieve. The University of Idaho held a candlelight vigil to honor the slain students. Parents of three of the victims spoke from the heart.\par \par \uc2 STACY CHAPIN, MOTHER: I want to remind you that that is the most important message that we have for you and your families is to make sure that you spend as much time as possible with those people, because time is precious and it's something you can't get back.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Madison Mogen's father is urging people to come forward if they can help with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN MOGEN, FATHER: If anyone knows anything just, please just help out and give us some closure and some -- so we can move on and get through these days a little easier.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN (on camera): And there is some progress in this investigation. Idaho state police say detectives are starting to receive some of the test results from the forensic evidence in this case. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll continue to follow this. Christina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on meetings between President Biden and the French leader, \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , plus the end of that potential railroad strike.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 6 in Milwaukee as state and federal law enforcement officials announce drug conspiracy charges against 10 people in Wisconsin accused over their alleged involvement in a possible meth ring. The filing states more than three pounds of methamphetamine and 280 grams of fentanyl were seized during the investigation. That's enough fentanyl to kill more than 100,000 people.\par \par \uc2 FOX 5 in New York City as police say seven children were injured when a school bus veered off the road in suburban Rockland County. They say the bus hit a telephone pole, a tree, two parked vehicles, then more trees before crashing into a house. None of the injuries, fortunately, were life- threatening.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  stadium in Atlanta from FOX 5, our affiliate there. One of the big stories there tonight, the college football playoff expands to 12 teams for the 2024 season. That announcement comes after the Rose Bowl agreed to amend its contract allowing the expansion to begin two years earlier than originally planned. Atlanta will host the national championship game in \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  stadium for the first 12 team tournament in January of 2025.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He just miscalculated across the board. And so the question is, what is his - how does he get himself out of the circumstance he's in? I'm prepared, if he's willing to talk, to find out what he is willing to do. But I'll only do it in consultation with my \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies. I'm not going to do it on my own.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , FRENCH PRESIDENT: We will never urge the Ukrainians to make a compromise which will not be acceptable for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  with President Biden today talking about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and whether they would sit down with him over the Ukraine situation as they get ready for the state dinner.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the railway strike averted with Senate action following up on a House bill but doesn't include what the unions wanted in there.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist and senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute, FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz, and Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist.\par \par \uc2 Leslie, for the railway action, there were a lot of people, Republicans in particular, who said this is not Congress' job to get in the middle of this. This should have been negotiated at the table. President Biden said they are going to push it down the road to fight for this paid sick leave that is not in this deal.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: They should fight for the paid sick leave. The president is a very pro-union president. Democrats are very pro- union candidates. It's the right thing to do. I would disagree that Congress shouldn't get involved, because one of the things that the president has to do is has to care about national security, has to care about the economy, has to care about transportation. And all of that is wrapped up with this.\par \par \uc2 So I think that the president did the right thing. And it's a very hard position for him to be in because he had to look at the best interest of the country but also appease the unions, which are huge supporters, and that he will also say helped him to get over the finish line to become president in the last election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jason?\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes, I also think this was the right call. And it's what the law says, that Congress is the arbiter of last resort. And I think the consequences to the economy if this didn't pass were real, they weren't being made up. And I, finally -- a lot of this wasn't about salaries, as union workers will be getting big increases in salaries. It was over these things like sick leave and so forth. I think they were asking for too much, frankly. I think the Biden administration has been quite generous in things like the Inflation Reduction Act in steering money to union labor and their priorities. So I think this was a bridge too far, and I think it was the right thing for Congress to do at the end.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also in Congress today, Howie, \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  from \uc2 Apple\uc2  was up there. Our own Hillary Vaughn tried to ask him some questions about China, to no avail through the hallways of Congress, numerous times. OK, listen to this for a second.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: -- to evade surveillance from the Chinese government?\par \par \uc2 Do you think it's problematic to do business with the Communist Chinese Party when they suppress human rights?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Valor, effort to try to get the question answered.\par \par \uc2 But meantime, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is saying, "Republican lawmakers and some tech CEOs are rallying behind \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's public attacks on \uc2 Apple\uc2 , alleging its control over its app store amounts to an abuse of power. The worst \uc2 Apple\uc2  faces from Congress is hearings that might embarrass it. The legislature has struggled and failed under Democratic control to pass any major restrictions on big tech firms or revisions to antitrust laws. Split control of Congress in the new year makes action even less likely." Musk is raising the \uc2 Apple\uc2  question.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes. And it kind of got answered now because while it's fascinating to watch Democrats pound \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  every day because they like the old rules under which you could shadow ban or suspend Republicans like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said that \uc2 Apple\uc2  is -- hates free speech. And he demonized \uc2 Apple\uc2 , and he said he was told that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  would be kicked out of the app store possibly, without giving a reason.\par \par \uc2 But then he apparently had a conversation with \uc2 Apple\uc2 's \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 , and then he tweeted, well, it was a misunderstanding and Tim was clear that he never intended to do so. In other words, Musk got it flat wrong, and he went after \uc2 Apple\uc2  without the facts. At the same time, it seems like liberals are just warning every day, Musk is ruining \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . It was awful for the last 10 years under the previous management.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Leslie, about that, the White House came out and said we are going to keep an eye out on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but yet when it comes to \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 's actions with the Chinese government, essentially helping them with the crackdown, they haven't spoken up about that at all. Here is a great interview Martha did with John Kirby did the other day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Why not say something to \uc2 Apple\uc2 , because we were just told the other day that the White House is keeping an eye on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: We have been very open about our desires to be able to see citizens communicate. And \uc2 Apple\uc2 , if this is a decision that they're making, then they should have to speak to that. But we are not -- we can't and we aren't in the business of telling private companies how to execute their initiatives.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, but \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is a private company, too. So why is \uc2 Twitter\uc2  getting one treatment and \uc2 Apple\uc2  is getting another is my question.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, these are completely two different circumstances.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I don't know. Seems similar in some ways.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Yes, but no pun intended here, apples and oranges. And the reason is \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is a social media company. If you were comparing \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , right, and we talked about \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , that would be a different thing, and that would be apples and apples.\par \par \uc2 Look, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is a private company. They have the right to do what they want. I disagree with Elizabeth Warren who says that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  can't make all the decisions. He really can. But I don't think it's good. I don't think what he has been doing, being completely transparent about, hey, you should vote for this guy in Florida, DeSantis, or you should vote for Republicans -- I don't think that's a good place for him to be because is he going to -- he is feeding into what liberals are accusing him of on my side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 But to \uc2 Apple\uc2  and to \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 , any company in the United States that has their company and has their production in China, they are going to try and appease the Chinese government, and that's why they really need to bring their factories home here. It will be better for the American people, jobs creator, and everybody will think they are wonderful.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jason?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Well, there isn't the first time that \uc2 Apple\uc2  has done this. \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  has a history of adapting \uc2 Apple\uc2  products to appease the Chinese communist strict rules over there. And he is a businessman. His bottom line is not social justice. His bottom line is to make money for his shareholders. So it's to be expected.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the real thing here is what is the alternative. Do we want a big tech industrial policy in this country where people in Congress are setting speech terms for these platforms? And I don't think we want that. I think we want the private sector fighting this out among themselves. And this is, as Howie said, very selective outrage on the part of the left because when \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was being used to see advance their agenda they had no problem with how it was run. Now it's not, or supposedly it won't be doing that, and so they now are fighting over it. But again, this is very, very selective. This is about whose ox is being gored. And so I would see it as the political theater that it is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is amazing that this free speech argument is kind of flipping on parties, and now Republicans are saying why aren't you for free speech?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Yes, and it also seems that many left leaning journalists also don't want free speech. What they would like is the old system of rules and regulations. And it's not like, and I know the \uc2 European Union\uc2  now wants Musk to do a better job of policing hate speech. He's said no permanent bans. You do something bad, you get suspended, but no permanent bans.\par \par \uc2 And it has flipped. It used to be that Democrats and liberals were the party of free speech. And now they don't seem to care very much about it as long as they get their way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime this FTX story is just so bizarre. This hearing up there on Capitol Hill just starting to get into it. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEBBIE STABENOW (D-MI) SENATE AGRICULTURE COMMITTEE CHAIR: If our bill had been law, FTX's conduct would have been illegal and could have been prevented.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL) SENATE MAJORITY WHIP: How long will it take to unpack the FTX from that mess to be able to understand exactly what happened and where it stands today?\par \par \uc2 ROSTIN BENHAM, COMMODITY FUTURES TRADING CHAIRMAN: I think it's going to take months probably before we truly understand the extent and scope of the failure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meanwhile, the former head of FTX, \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2 , is giving interviews. And surprising his lawyers are letting him give interviews, but here is one of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAM BANKMAN-FRIED, FTX FOUNDER: I wasn't spending any time or effort trying to manage risk on FTX.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's a pretty stunning admission.\par \par \uc2 BANKMAN-FRIED: What happened, happened. And if I had been -- if I had been spending an hour a day thinking about risk management on FTX, I don't think that would happened. I think I stopped working as hard for a bit. Honestly, if I look back on myself, I think I got a little cocky.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A little cocky. I just can't fully get my head around this thing. He goes from $20 billion to $100,000, and all these people lose money.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: First of all, my grandparents used to put money under their mattress, OK. Seriously. And they didn't trust banks. And this is why. Anything too good to be true is. And this was a perfect example of that. It makes me shudder that the CEO of a company said I didn't really understand the financial workings of this. I didn't understand risk management, and my bad. My bad. No, not my bad. There's billions of dollars in money that won't be recouped.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: This guy gave money to media organizations and he was lionized as the next Warren Buffet. It turns out he's more like the next Elizabeth Holmes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And gave money to candidates, too, and Democratic lawmakers and Republican lawmakers, and that part of the story is not done.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Well, it isn't done. But again, Bret, these were consenting adults who engaged in activity here and lost money. They fell for a mania, and they should bear of the consequences here. What we don't want is regulating this industry out of existence, and that is what could happen if it gets too politicized.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute. A small but mighty hero rescued his two-year-old sister as their Maryland apartment building went up in flames Tuesday evening. Eleven-year-old La'Prentis Doughty ran out of the building after noticing the fire but immediately rushed back up to the second floor when he realized his little sister was still inside. He suffered minor burns to his arm but was able to get her out. Great job.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll come to you from Simi Valley, California, for the start the Reagan National Defense Forum. See you there.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts now. A little less tanned, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221202eic100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Crypto Crash Hearing As Ex-CEO Says "We Messed Up"; Lawmakers Look For Answers On FTX Crypto Crash; Iran's Soccer Team Back Home After Loss To U.S.; Police: Still Believe Attack Was Targeted; Moscow, Idaho Tipline: 208-883-7180; RPT: China Using TikTok To Go After Us Lawmakers; China Eases Some COVID Rules After Protests; Senate Has Enough Votes To Prevent Rail Strike; Prince And Princess Of Wales Visit Boston; Trailer For Harry And Meghan's Documentary Released\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Rich Edson, Sandra Smith, Charlie Gasparino,  Alex Hogan, Christina Co