And I feel really, really bad and regretful that I wasn't and then a lot of people got hurt -- and that that's on me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is on me. Wow. This is pretty remarkable. SPF do these interviews from that penthouse that you see in this video in the Bahamas. He says that he thinks that customers of the U.S. branch of FTX, some of them could get their money back. He's been scrambling to say that he's going to be able to do that. We'll see where that goes. Sandra Smith and Fox Business Senior Correspondent Charlie Gasparino, both on top of this story from the very beginning. We're going to get to them in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first the background here today on the new developments from Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson in, on Capitol Hill. Hi, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon, Martha. Well, Congress is getting involved as it typically tends to do in these situations here. The first hearing specifically on the FTX collapse earlier this afternoon by the Senate Agricultural Committee. It has jurisdiction over the \uc2 Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2 , and that is a group that has been looking into this whole thing for a while here. The chairman of the CFTC was up here testifying earlier today, \uc2 Rostin Behnam\uc2 , he testified to senators that it's going to take months to understand the scope of this FTX failure. He says, his agency needs more authority to regulate all of this business.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROSTIN BENHAM, \uc2 COMMODITY FUTURES TRADING COMMISSION\uc2 : We had no basis or reason to sort of pierce through that entity and start phishing or asking questions about affiliate entities or non-CFTC registered entities.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voiceover): Benham also says his agency did not receive any whistleblower tips about problems at FTX. Former FTX CEO \uc2 Sam Bankman- Fried\uc2 's accused of transferring funds from FTX to another firm to finance risky bets. Those went bad, the whole operation collapsed. Bankman-Fried says he never tried to commit fraud. FTX is in bankruptcy, has $8 billion in shortfalls. And it says it may owe more money to more than a million creditors from major investment firms to Americans who say they invested their entire life savings.\par \par \uc2 Bankman-Fried freed faces investigations from the \uc2 FCC\uc2  and the Justice Department. He says, he has less than $100,000 now and access to only one credit card. Bankman-Fried was the second highest donor to Democrats during the most recent midterm cycle. He now claims he quietly gave the same amount of money or about that to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The chairman of the CFTC says that he met with Bankman-Fried 10 times over the last 14 months. Consumer groups say that was an effort to try to weaken any regulation that was working its way through the CFTC. Now, it'll be up to Congress to figure out how to sort this mess out and whether there will be more regulation. How much more of it on the crypto markets going forward. Back to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, good luck. Thank you very much, Rich Edson. So, joining me now: "AMERICA REPORTS" Co-Anchor, Sandra Smith; and Fox Business Senior Correspondent Charlie Gasparino. You guys have been all over this story. I know you just interviewed one of the people who lost $2 million in this debacle. And you sound skeptical, both of you, about what Congress is going to be able to do at this point.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, his question was -- his questions why was he delivering that Q&A with the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  from the comfort of his home in the Bahamas and not from a jail cell? I mean, if he is actually guilty of what is alleged against him, this is one of the biggest financial scams that we've ever seen --\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX BUSINESS SENIOR