rs. The group, Iran Human Rights says as many as 440 people have been killed by security forces in these months of protests. And now, after the one-zero us win over Iran, people in Iran took to the streets celebrating their loss as a moment of defiance against the regime. An activists say that security forces in Northern Iran, at one of those protests killed this man. 27-year-old, Mehran Samak.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voiceover): Security reportedly shooting him in the head for honking his horn in celebration of the U.S. win. Samak was a friend of Iranian soccer player, Saeed Ezatolahi. This midfielder pictured in this photo from Tuesday's game. Ezatolahi quickly responding posting online: "After last night's bitter loss, the news of your passing set fire to my heart. Someday, masks will fall, the truth will be laid bare."\par \par \uc2 The team traveled back to Iran late last night after a time here in Qatar of political turmoil. Many of them fell to the ground after the loss on game three, comforted by their U.S. counterparts. And their families had reportedly been threatened if the players refused to sing the national anthem as they had done in Game One.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Now, we have seen activism here at the World Cup. But many of the protesters who held on to signs those signs have been removed by security forces at the stadiums. And police even stopped one man who was wearing a t-shirt that supported women's rights. One that has now been associated with this movement and stopped that man after the latest match. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alex, thank you very much. Alex Hogan, reporting from Doha.\par \par \uc2 So, breaking late today, Moscow, Idaho police clearing up some confusion, saying that they still believe that the attack against these students was "targeted", though they have not concluded if the target was the residents or specific targets individuals within that residence.\par \pa