firmed two of the victims were found on the second floor and that two other victims were found on the third floor of that three storey off campus home on November 13.\par \par \uc2 And although police have not confirmed exactly where the victims were found in the rooms, Kaylee's father gave some insight into this last night.\par \par \uc2 He talked at that vigil very touching comments. It was -- it was very nice to hear his remarks. He explained how his daughter was best friends with Madison Mogen up until the very end.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE GONCALVES, FATHER OF VICTIM KAYLEE GONCALVES: Then they started looking at colleges, and they came here together. They eventually get into the same apartment together. And in the end, that they died together in the same room, in the same bed. And it's a shame and it hurts, but the beauty of the two always being together something that will -- it comforts us.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Just absolutely heartbreaking. And in an exclusive interview with Fox News yesterday, the father of Madison Mogen urge anyone who could help with this investigation to please come forward.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN MOGEN, FATHER OF VICTIM, MADISON MOGEN: Every time I wake up, I really remember what's going on. And it's like Madison, I don't know how you ever get used to this. It's never been --\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Life will never --\par \par \uc2 MOGEN: Go away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: And again, so many unanswered questions as officers and local law enforcement work to solve this crime, and figure out who was behind this brutal and deadly attack. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Christina.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Steve Goncalvez, who is the father of Kaylee. And he joins me now. Steve, first of all, our condolences to you and your family. We're so, so sorry for your loss, sir.\par \par \uc2 We have kids, you know, similar ages, and I can't imagine what you're going through. So, I, I thank you for being here. And as I mentioned to you earlier, our goal in talking to you is to keep this story out there, to show the tip line, which we're going to put up on the screen in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But I want to get your reaction to this confusion of it. If targeted, not targeted, maybe the people in the house, maybe it was the residence, what can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 GONCALVEZ: I don't like it one bit. I know these statements take like hours in multiple individuals to review. It's not like this was something set on the side of the street within the first five hours of the case.\par \par \uc2 That -- and these guys are professionals, they sat in a room and this is what they came out with. And they keep coming out with statements that create more questions in the answer. And then, that creates a pressure. And that's why I'm feeling like there's so many people coming and looking for answers. And I'm one of them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: One of the things that you said last night about the two of them having fallen asleep in the same bed. That was new information. Is that the case that they were actually in the same bed?\par \par \uc2 GONCALVEZ: To me, it wasn't knew. The corner and shared a bunch of information about there, there was some photos if you knew the house design, you would have seen that there was a picture of Kaylee's bed, and it was -- it was made. There was nothing in there.\par \par \uc2 So, it was kind of a process of elimination if you'd been in the home of the new -- the inside information like I knew that was my daughter's room.\par \par \uc2 So, to me it had been out, but I'm sure they're not happy with me saying that. I didn't mean to, to say something that was supposed to be on the wraps.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, of course not. Of course not.\par \par \uc2 GONCALVEZ: But I told them, you got to work with me, you got to tell me what to say, and what not to say. And