l this guy's off the streets. And the worst thing I want is another family to go through this.\par \par \uc2 Kaylee's mom said something about being fearful that he is around. That he is watching all of this. That he could even show up at a funeral service for your daughter or one of these children.\par \par \uc2 GONCALVEZ: It's pretty much a fact. He -- who does something like this and doesn't tune into the media. Who wouldn't? It's in -- it's in his 100 percent selfish interest.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, he is. And we wouldn't want to do some kind of celebration and exclude anyone. So, yes, he could easily be there. And that's a sick kind of twisted person that would do this kind of crime.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, when you talked about getting into the phone, you said we have family passwords. And you know, we were able to get in there.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything that you can share with us about what you learned there or people she was communicating with? We know there was a back and forth between she and her on-and-off boyfriend through the course of the night.\par \par \uc2 GONCALVEZ: There was a little -- there was some information, we were able to get that to the police officers. I think it definitely helped them. I know, the behavior footprint afterwards.\par \par \uc2 It did definitely get some more things in the works. But I'm a historical fact person, I can help them know, my daughter's and Maddie from past, but going forward, I'm going to be less and less relevant.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you believe that your daughter was the target? Or do you have any reason to think that it was -- that she was over someone else or that someone else was?\par \par \uc2 GONCALVEZ: I do have some inkling that there was some behavior difference. I call him a footprint. When you commit a crime you do -- you do different behaviors. I've asked permission to give any of -- and any of that out? And they told me no, it would not be beneficial.\par \par \uc2 So, I've held back on that. And I tried to keep my word.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm sorry, behavior of her or someone she knew?\par \par \uc2 GONCALVEZ: Behavior of the victims.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. All right, Steve, I appreciate you sharing this with us. And as I said, we're going to put the tipline. The tipline is up right now. It's 2008-883-7180.\par \par \uc2 And as you have said, none of these young people should fear that if they were drinking that night or involved in what most of us would consider pretty typical college behavior, that they are going to become a -- that, that's can be a problem for them.\par \par \uc2 They need to come forward with their videos, whatever's on their phone, whatever they might have picked up on, in order to continue to work to get a break in this case, which is what we all want. We all want some justice. We want to find out who did this, and that you can all find some sort of eventual peace in this.\par \par \uc2 And, Steve, we're praying for all of you and we thank you very much for being here today, sir.\par \par \uc2 GONCALVEZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, with that, let me bring in our former \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigator Bill Daly. Bill, you were listening to our conversation with Mr. Goncalvez. What do you take away from it?\par \par \uc2 BILL DALY, FORMER INVESTIGATOR, FEDERAL BUREAU OF INVESTIGATION: Well, besides being just a heart wrenching, being a parent, and you can imagine what this must be like for him and his family going through this.\par \par \uc2 But let me tell you is that he revealed a couple of pieces of information here, which were very helpful was that about behaviors and what may have happened afterwards?\par \par \uc2 And I think those are the key things. And I think when you start looking at this investigation, information that people will come up with after the fact.\par \par \uc2 Seeing somebody acted a little bit differently, maybe the movement of a vehicle or somebody didn't show up for a job, or they didn't show up for a class. Whatever happened to be, could be indicators.\par \par \uc2 You know, a lot of crimes are certainly solved when people think something is out of sorts after the fact, where somebody is different than they were before. And so I think in this case, apart from the forensics that they're going through, which is slowly we understand coming back to the police, it may very well be people from the area seeing, hearing something not too sure why is somebody out there cleaning the car so much, why are they doing this, why they didn't show up. Those are the things that I think may start to bring and this (inaudible) together.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bill, thank you very much. Bill Daly and I had a longer conversation about this case earlier today, which will be on my podcast, "The Untold Story." It's available now at FoxNewspodcast.com. So if you want to hear more about this case, and the way that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  approaches a case like this, Bill and I talked about that earlier today. Thanks, Bill. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So Fox also digging into this new bombshell report that details how TikTok accounts run by the government and Communist China are being used as propaganda to spread messages about US politics and different candidates in this country. Brian Kilmeade and a story that you will hear right here after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: China's propaganda are amusing TikTok to go after American politicians ahead of the US midterms last month, without making it clear that they were connected to any of these videos. That's according to a new report coming from Forbes today, which reports that the accounts mostly criticized Republicans for the most part and favored Democrats. Fox and Friends co-host Brian Kilmeade is here.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS HOST: Shocking.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Shocking, right? But first to business correspondent Hillary Vaughn, who gives us the 