rd number of got-aways in November.\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 We certainly are. And we are now officially in the third month of CBP's new fiscal year, as you pointed out. That started on October the 1st. And a brand-new figure at this hour, 137,000 known got-aways since that time, according to CBP. So the sector is picking right up where they left off, very, very busy, while, here in Eagle Pass in and around Del Rio, agents Say They're encountering two to three large groups every single day, accounting for at least 90 percent of all large groups that now enter the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 That's traveling across the Southern border in groups of 100 or more together. Nearly 96 percent of all fentanyl being smuggled into America also coming through Mexico, according to the data. Almost daily, CBP intercepts large quantities of that drug, among others, at ports of entry, but officials fear that they are only making a real dent in the supply, since the powerful synthetic opioid is still so readily available on the streets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL MADRIGAL, DEA SPECIAL AGENT: Before, like any type of illegal drug, was -- you had to go to a shady side of town or you had to wait on crops. Fentanyl is completely different. It's year-round. And you could buy it off your phone. You could get on social media and order pills and they could be delivered to your home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Meantime, researchers at the \uc2 University of Houston\uc2  are working on a fentanyl vaccine.\par \par \uc2 And in about 30 minutes, Texas Governor Greg Abbott will visit the lab and he will be meeting with scientists. The vaccine has shown promise in mice. And human testing is supposed to begin in a few months...\par \par \uc2 (HORN BLARING)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: ... as the train is blasting its horn back behind me. The vaccine works by preventing fentanyl from actually entering your brain, and that reduces the risk of poisoning and overdose -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 Casey, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to the White House, where President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is facing pressure over his handling of this crisis.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy is at the White House with the very latest -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Charles, the White House counts President Biden's border experience as a 2008 drive-by. We went back and checked on a trip that he made in 2008. It was very brief. He landed in El Paso, Texas, and drove basically straight north -- you can see it there -- to events in New Mexico, away from the border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Thank you, Karine. Kevin McCarthy says that he invited President Biden down to the border. Has the president RSVPed?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: We know the president has never been down to the border.\par \par \uc2 The possible next speaker says that he wants him to go with him. So is he going to?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, look, he's been there. He's been to the border.\par \par \uc2 And since he took office...\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: When did he go to the border?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Since he took office, the president, Biden, has been taking action to fix our immigration system and secure our border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Well, about a year ago, President Biden himself admitted there could be some value in taking a trip to the U.S.-Mexico border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have been there before, and I haven't -- I mean, I know it well. I guess I should go down, but the -- but the whole point of it is, I haven't had a whole hell of a lot of time to get down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There is a group of four Democratic senators who wrote a letter to the DHS secretary, Alejandro Mayorkas, giving him a deadline of today to answer a list of questions about what is going on with this administration's immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 Now we wait to see if Mayorkas writes back -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Peter, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Seventy-three thousand, that's how many known got-aways slipped past the border in November, this according to Customs and Border Protection Services. It is believed to be the highest number for a single month ever recorded. But will that number actually look tame when Title 42 goes away?\par \par \uc2 National Border Patrol Council Brandon's Judd joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, everyone's bracing for this thing to be exponentially worse, but it's hard to even fathom. Do you think we have gotten to the point now where these numbers are so mind-boggling, that they don't even have the same sort of impact, the same sort of punch anymore? It's like two million here, 70,000 there.\par \par \uc2 It's just -- it's just the numbers have gotten so big, they don't even shock us anymore.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: No, and the reason is, because it's the new norm.\par \par \uc2 The problem when you listen to what the press secretary said about Biden going to the border, the more you tell a lie, if you're not fact-checked, that lie becomes the truth. And that's the problem. And so when you look at these numbers, as the numbers continue to go up, and as the Biden administration continues to deflect, and they continue to say that the board is under control, when Secretary Mayorkas goes before Congress and says that the border is under control, if nobody fact-checks them, then that lie becomes reality.\par \par \uc2 And that's the problem that we're seeing today. The Washington Times, New York -- I'm sorry -- The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , all of these major outlets, they're not fact-checking this administration. And so the border -- the absolute chaos that exists on the border, the vast majority of the American people, they don't understand what is currently going on.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: And that's the problem that we face today. If we don't get this under control, the numbers are going to continue to rise. But nobody's going to know about it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. You know what? They don't fact-check a whole lot of things. But they don't also, in my opinion, care too much about this.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's a major issue with that as well. And the only time we have heard from those kinds of publications is when these migrants ended up on Martha's Vineyard or in New York City. Other than that, it doesn't make the news. It's not worthy of the news. And the folks who have to deal with it day to day just don't count. And I think that's a big problem, Brandon.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: No, you're absolutely correct.\par \par \uc2 And that's why that 73,000 number -- and, by the way, Charles, that's the number that we know right now. There's still another 15,000 that are -- that have not been reconciled. So that number could actually go up to nearly 90,000 people that have crossed the border illegally and have been able to evade apprehension.\par \par \uc2 That is a huge problem today. But, again, if we don't have an honest conversation -- and that's all I want to have. I want to have an honest conversation with the American people. And if we have that honest conversation, the American people will be upset. But because we're not fact-checking them, it continues to go on.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I want to go back to what you mentioned and what we started with, the White House, the claim that President Biden has been to the border.\par \par \uc2 We saw in that interview said he knew a lot about it. I mean, he's pretty good to land in El Paso and drive straight north and get -- about over a decade ago, almost two, and to get the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 And just how important is it that the commander in chief understand what's happening down there? This is a national security risk, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Let me tell you, he doesn't understand anything about what's going on, on the border. And that clearly shows in his actions.\par \par \uc2 Of course, he's going to give us the political rhetoric. He's going to say what his base wants to hear. But, in reality, he does not understand the border and the -- and the danger that it presents to the American people. If he did, he would actually do something about it.\par \par \uc2 We are going to feel all of the effects of this down the road. And one day, we're going to come back and we're going to say, this was his fault. And he had the solutions. He could have implemented the solutions, but he failed to do anything about it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow. That got-aways number is shocking. These people are deliberately evading capture. And you have to wonder why.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Thank you, Charles. OK.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, to Capitol Hill, the Senate just voting to avert a nationwide rail strike, this as a separate measure for seven days of sick leave for union workers was voted down.\par \par \uc2 To Chad Pergram, Capitol -- tracking, rather, it all from Capitol Hill. He joins us now -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL PRODUCER: Charles, a catastrophic Christmas time railroad crisis averted.\par \par \uc2 Congress intervening to prevent a rail strike for the first time in three decades, the Senate vote, 88-15, with Rand Paul voting present. President Biden dispatched Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  and Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  to Capitol Hill. They implored pro-labor senators to back off from a push for seven days of paid sick leave.\par \par \uc2 That would have tanked the deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): The amendment simply says seven paid sick days for workers in the rail industry, and I hope we can win it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But the medical leave plan could not overcome a filibuster.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): If we had 60 votes in the Senate, it could possibly happen, but we don't have 60 votes in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Lawmakers from both sides opposed congressional intervention and the labor dispute. Some Republicans pushed another two-month cooling-off period. That would give the rail lines and unions more time to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DAN SULLIVAN (R-AK): Give negotiators more time to get to an agreement. And it will not make Congress the entity of last resort in these kinds of negotiations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But that effort failed as well. Congress needed to act by the weekend. Otherwise, some firms would stop important shipments in the coming days -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Chad, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to the crypto collapse. FTX founder \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  speaking out, and investors are lashing out -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, SBF isn't in jail. He's on a media tour, it seems, Charles, but he claims he didn't commit fraud, he just made a mistake. But all the billions are still missing.\par \par \uc2 More after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EVAN LUTHRA, FTX INVESTOR: Well, why are you not in jail in the first place? This interview could have also taken place behind bars.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of questions I would have for the guy there. So -- but we do know that he has an intention to defraud us. He has done a crime. He knows what he was doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Disgraced FTX founder \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  now saying there was no fraud. Investors out billions saying there's just no way that's true.\par \par \uc2 To FOX Business' Kelly O'Grady with the very latest -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the headline coming out of his \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and "Good Morning America" interviews was, he screwed up, but he's criminally liable.\par \par \uc2 And he chose his words really carefully, saying: I didn't ever try to commit fraud. I didn't knowingly commingle funds.\par \par \uc2 Instead, SBF is blaming FTX's sister company, Alameda, for the mess, claiming he simply didn't know what was going on there, even though he's the owner. Now, being incompetent doesn't mean you broke the law. But, to be very clear, FTX customer assets were loaned out to Alameda. And the company's user agreement says it would not take user money. To SBF, that isn't fraud, it's just a mistake, as he simply didn't care to focus on the parts of the business that didn't make money.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAM BANKMAN-FRIED, CEO, FTX: Some part of it was just literal distraction. I spent a lot less time looking assets and looking at balances and positions, because that's not where revenue came from.\par \par \uc2 And so it -- I wasn't seeing it as a core business driver. Obviously, it was a core risk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Now, that's a stunning statement from a self-styled do-gooder.\par \par \uc2 And all this took center stage at a hearing today, the Senate Agriculture Committee seeking to get to the bottom of the FTX collapse, regulators sharing their authority was limited to dig into what the platform was engaged in, though, according to SBF, this all happened not because of his own -- or because of his own lack of oversight.\par \par \uc2 And get