an count the mistakes you have made, or how you have changed your story here. Yet he keeps doing it.\par \par \uc2 So what I'm thinking at this point is, he's sitting in the Bahamas. He hasn't been indicted .He hasn't been extradited. And then, of course, you have got the CFTC chairman sitting down in front of the Senate Agriculture Committee today, and they're talking about this. They're sort of pontificating about regulation, without really getting to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Who's looking at the criminality of what happened here?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 You know what? Another thing too is, they had -- they did a lot with the money, but there was one thing that stands out, a festival that they were involved in Miami in May. I mean, take a look at this thing. They pumped people's money into this. They had so much fun with other people's money, hard-earned money that folks will never see again.\par \par \uc2 This is brazen.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: This is really brazen stuff.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: That's brazen. Buying the real estate is brazen.\par \par \uc2 I have been looking at this, Charles, and -- with two different layers. The first layer would be, OK, you created a token, and you gave it a value. And that's something they can look into, how to price these assets and how to regulate them.\par \par \uc2 But any exchange, taking customer funds, spending it in this reckless way, is illegal, whether it's securities, whether its derivatives, whether it's crypto. And that's where I think the regulators missed the boat in the most egregious place of fraud here.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What about confidence that they will get it right this time?\par \par \uc2 I think another big part of this story is that the American public doesn't have a lot of faith that the regulators will figure it out or get it right. And they wonder why.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: And why should they?\par \par \uc2 To your earlier point, and how cocky \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  is acting, remember that he gave millions of dollars in campaign donations to some of the same people who were sitting in that room today, and are supposed to be overseeing this. And so you have to wonder how those people can be impartial under the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. It's so heartbreaking. It's just -- really, I get so upset with this thing. I can't wait to drill down further and further and kind of wipe the smirk off his face.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Yes, I hear you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, thanks a lot, Jackie.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: from wokeness to wakeness, a new survey of America's military leaders raising some serious alarms. Is the Pentagon becoming too politicized? General Jack Keane is here on that.\par \par \uc2 But, first, to Idaho, where something police are clarifying is increasing this murder mystery even more.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Could a potential move in Florida's Statehouse be signaling a run by Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for the White House?\par \par \uc2 And after meeting with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , why \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  is on Capitol Hill today. Hillary's on the hunt for answers.\par \par \uc2 And we're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Nearly three weeks after four University of Idaho students were killed in their off-campus home, investigators on the case maintaining the attack was targeted, but they're clarifying how.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Christina Coleman is in Moscow, Idaho, with the latest -- Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there's been a lot of confusion over this, based off of the statements that police have been saying. But, today, they are maintaining that they still believe that this was a targeted attack. Here's what they said in their new statement today.\par \par \uc2 They said -- quote -- "Our clarification last night directly address comments made by Latah County prosecutor Thompson, who said the suspect or suspects specifically looked at this residence and that one or more of the occupants were undoubtedly targeted. We remain consistent in our belief that this was indeed a targeted attack, but have not concluded if the target was the residents or its occupants."\par \par \uc2 Also, there is some progress in this case. Idaho State Police say investigators are starting to receive forensic testing results from a crime lab. Meantime, this community is still grieving. Parents of three of the victims attended a candlelight vigil last night at the university in honor of the slain students.\par \par \uc2 One of the victims is 20-year-old Ethan Chapin. Last night, at the vigil, his mother shared this advice:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACY CHAPIN, MOTHER OF VICTIM: I want to remind you that that's the most important message that we have for you and your families, is to make sure that you spend as much time as possible with those people, because time is precious, and it's something you can't get back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: There were a lot of heartfelt messages last night.\par \par \uc2 The father of Kaylee Goncalves also shared stories about his daughter and Madison Mogen. He says they were best friends up until the very end.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE GONCALVES, FATHER OF VICTIM: Then they started looking at colleges. They came here together. They eventually get into the same apartment together. And, in the end, they died together in the same room, in the same bed. And it's -- it's a shame. And it hurts.\par \par \uc2 But the beauty of the two always being together something that will -- will -- it comforts us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Also, in an exclusive interview with FOX News yesterday, the father of Madison Mogen urged anyone who could help with this investigation to come forward.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN MOGEN, FATHER OF VICTIM: Every time I wake up, I remember what's going on. And it's like -- yes, it's -- I don't know how you ever get used to this. It's never going to...\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Life will never be the same.\par \par \uc2 MOGEN: ... go away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: And, again, so many unanswered questions in this case. No word on the killers whereabouts or a motive or a murder weapon. Just a lot of questions still remain -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, it's so heartbreaking and infuriating at the same time.\par \par \uc2 Christina, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Here with me now to discuss, former D.C. homicide detective and FOX News contributor Ted Williams.\par \par \uc2 And, Ted, you have spent a lot of time out there. You have been at the scene almost from the very beginning. What are you making now of this police clarification?\par \par \uc2 TED WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: You know, Charles, you're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 I have been out there in Idaho. And I was at one of their press conferences. And I asked the first question, and that was about this targeting. And we got a very weak answer then. And I think what the law enforcement has put out there now concerning targeting is weak, because what they're saying is that, hey, they believe somebody was targeted, but they don't know if it was the house or one of the individuals.\par \par \uc2 Well, if they don't know that, who in the hell was targeted? You -- listen, when you look at what has gone on here, you have an erosion of public confidence. You need the public engaged and involved in order to try to settle -- to solve these cases.\par \par \uc2 And, sadly, law enforcement, unfortunately, has put out a great deal of misinformation. And it really is not helping in this investigation, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, Ted, I would imagine it's maybe a tough decision to decide what to share and what not to share with the public.\par \par \uc2 But, also, from what I'm getting out, there's a tremendous amount of frustration. And, to your point, if there's targeting involved, what does that look like? Because other people want to make sure that they're not a potential target themselves.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, you're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 Right now, I can tell you the public out there in Moscow, Idaho, is very, very disturbed. They are very concerned about their own safety, because they know that there is a killer or killers out there.\par \par \uc2 And when you give the public information, you cannot give misinformation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And, unfortunately, this is what has happened.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, we're hearing now some forensic tests are starting to come back. A lot of speculation on whether the killer or killers might have injured themselves. Could there be some blood samples?\par \par \uc2 What kind of a boost might we see from -- now that some of this key forensic evidence is coming through?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, it's going to take a while, I believe. And we have to just, fortunately or unfortunately, be patient.\par \par \uc2 They are going to get these forensics. They're going to examine them. They're going to try to connect the dots. They're going to try to see if there's some kind of a DNA connection. And I will tell you, since this house was sort of like what was definitively a party house, where a lot of kids partied at, they have got a hell of a job ahead of them using -- and trying to use the -- to determine anybody's DNA and was anyone involved.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Ted Williams, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, is a new survey on America's military leadership raising alarms at the Pentagon? Why General Jack Keane says it should.\par \par \uc2 And some Republicans in Florida's Statehouse may be trying to make it easier for Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to run for the White House. Florida's House speaker is here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: A new national defense survey outlining an alarming threat -- trend, rather. Less than half of Americans say that they have a great deal of trust in the U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's down from 70 percent just five years ago. The \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  institute says more people see the military becoming politicized, and that's driving the decline in confidence.\par \par \uc2 FOX News senior strategic analyst Four-Star -- retired Four-Star General Jack Keane joins us now to discuss.\par \par \uc2 This is one of those things, General Keane, that the police and the military have always been held in such high regard. When you see this, it has to raise alarm bells. Do you think the Pentagon is worried about it?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, of course, they will be.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this confirms other surveys that have been done, Charles, that are pretty similar. And it really -- it transcends administrations here. It was 70 percent in 2018. It takes a dive 14 percent by February of '21 in a Republican administration, another 8 percent in a Democratic administration.\par \par \uc2 If I was the senior civilian and military leaders in the Pentagon, I would take it very serious. I mean, after all, I mean, having spent 38 years on active-duty and still very much involved, I know how the leaders feel. I mean, they're working for the American people.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: And it's the American people who have these perceptions.\par \par \uc2 So, dig into it, find out what are driving these perceptions. Some of the survey gives you some understanding of that. Politicization is part of that. And what actions are they doing that are contributing to that perception? And do an honest assessment and come back to the American people and talk straight to them about it.\par \par \uc2 The American people deserve to get straight answers.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But, General, can they -- can they do an honest assessment and go to the White House and discuss this, I mean, have a legitimate conversation and say, hey, listen, we know there's an overarching woke agenda, but we think it's hurting us, hurting our ability to find new recruits, it's hurting our ability to be able to defend this nation?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: If they believe that is the case, and they can't go to the White House, then they don't deserve the positions they're in.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they answer to the Constitution. And the White House certainly gives them orders. And they are going to obey those orders, if they're illegal and morally correct. But they have got every right to disagree. And that's what they should be doing if they find that there's policy disagreements here.\par \par \uc2 I am encouraged by some of the things in the survey, that the American people have embraced the fact that China and Russia are clearly our enemies, Russia 80 percent embraced by the American people, 75 percent in China as well. And certainly that is a clear understanding of what is actually taking place in the world. And the American people, as usual, have got it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, again, though, some folks think that they're just -- quote, unquote - - "competitors."\par \par \uc2 And on that, let me -- let me piggyback off that, President Biden telling reporters today that he has -- quote -- "no immediate plans" to contact Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . But he is prepared to talk to him if Putin wants to talk about ending the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, I understand what he's saying.\par \par \uc2 I think negotiations, if they're going to take place, will likely not take place until there really is a bona fide stalemate, where the Ukrainians don't think they can retake any more territory and the Russians don't believe they can retake more Ukrainian territory as well. And we're not there yet.\par \par \uc2 But when negotiations do take place, it should be between the Ukrainians and the Russians. And that is a fact. And I think President Biden has had before many times that he believes that's the case, that this is all about the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 The United States obviously are players here, because we're supporting this war. We're the largest supporter in the world. And we do have a voice, and it should be heard. But the primary negotiation has got to be between the Ukrainians and the Russians.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, you have a fair amount of Republicans, as the Republicans take over the House, that are concerned about our -- the amount of money that we have poured into this war, the accountability of it, and things like that. Should we have a better accounting of what's happening with the American taxpayer dollars?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: You betcha. I mean, when we're spending American taxpayer dollars, I mean, I think the under-guiding principle the American people have is a general skepticism about it.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, of course, we -- and we learned some real lessons in Afghanistan, where we know full well that the Afghans were taken our money in corruption, and not all of that showed up in the Afghan units in terms of training and equipment.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: So, yes, there should be a good accounting here. The Congress has a right to ask for that. We are spending American taxpayers' dollars here.\par \par \uc2 Most of that money, however, is actually going to our defense industry to replenish our stockpiles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I got you.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: So, it's the American work and the defense industry that's kind of benefiting from the military assistance. That's where it's heading.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK.\par \par \uc2 General Keane, always a pleasure. Thank you so much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, great talking to you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You too.\par \par \uc2 There could soon be a move in Florida's Statehouse to set up a potential run by Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for the White House. The lawmaker behind that is here.\par \par \uc2 But, first, after meeting with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , now \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  is in a bunch of meetings on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 So, what's this all about, Hillary?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  is trying to get some face time with Republicans in the House who will soon be in power and in charge of oversight over his company. One lawmaker tells us things behind closed doors got pretty tense.\par \par \uc2 We will have the details coming up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you support the Chinese people's right to protest?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: A big tech executive, like, really big tech executive, looking to make friends on Capitol Hill today.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  CEO \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  meeting with the Republican House lawmakers, this before they officially take the majority next year.\par \par \uc2 Hillary Vaughn was there when he arrived. And she joins us now from Capitol Hill -- Hillary.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, before \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  arrived here on Capitol Hill, he cleared the air with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  in California at \uc2 Apple\uc2 's headquarters. Musk saying the two had a good conversation, and that \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  assured him that removing \uc2 Twitter\uc2  from the App Store was not on the table.\par \par \uc2 But Cook is facing even more questions from lawmakers here on Capitol Hill who have voiced their concerns about the ongoing zero COVID protests in China and whether \uc2 Apple\uc2  coordinated with the Chinese government to limit AirDrop access, a key tool for protesters.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  writing a letter to \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 , saying this: "Your company appears to be actively supporting the Chinese government's brutal crackdown. Under your leadership, \uc2 Apple\uc2  has time and again assisted the Chinese Communist Party in surveilling and suppressing the basic human rights of the Chinese people."\par \par \uc2 So we tried to get some answers from \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Mr. Cook, do you support the Chinese people's right to protest? Do you have any reaction to the factory workers that were beaten and detained for protesting COVID lockdowns?\par \par \uc2 Do you regret restricting AirDrop access that protesters used to evade surveillance from the Chinese government? Do you think it's problematic to do business with the communist Chinese Party when they suppress human rights?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: That was on -- that was when Cook was on his way to meet with lawmakers. He met with Republicans who will soon be in charge of powerful oversight of \uc2 Apple\uc2 , once Republicans take the House majority in January, one of those, Including Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): One are the things that \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  said that we're going to hold him to, he said he would be Switzerland. And we expect him to do just that, to be a tech leader of a neutral, politically neutral organization.\par \par \uc2 Free speech has to be both sides having full access. He made the commitment that he'd be Switzerland. We're going to hold him to it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And, Charles, that's what Republicans say they're going to do when they take power in the House. They're going to crack down on big tech and make sure that all political views are able to be represented -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Great stuff there, tough, tough questions. Thanks a lot, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 Now to some troubling signs for the economy ahead of tomorrow's big employment report. A new survey finds that job cuts soared 127 percent in November. That's from the month before, up a record-shattering 417 percent from the same time last year.\par \par \uc2 Economist Steve Moore joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Steve, that was the Challenger, Gray report. But there were so many economic data reports out today. All of them missed, all of them sending big red flags and red flares about where this economy is heading. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Yes, you saw the report of more and more companies talking about layoffs. Now, that's problematic, certainly.\par \par \uc2 And we didn't get a very good jobs report from the private people who are monitors of what's happening in private employment.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ADP.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So I do think the job market is slowing down. But it's still a pretty strong job market. We still have 10 million jobs open in this country. That number is down.\par \par \uc2 But we will see. I will -- by the way, I will be on FOX Business with Maria when that report comes out. I think you might be on as well.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So I'm expecting a reduction in the number of jobs that were created. And the big question, of course, that you have been asking, Charles, and that everyone is asking is, are we on the precipice of a -- of some kind of recession in 2023?\par \par \uc2 Too early to tell, as far as I'm concerned.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, when you see, for instance, today, the manufacturing data come in contracting, construction came in worse than anticipated, you have already referenced that ADP report, where 100,000 manufacturing jobs were lost.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: There was a shocker yesterday out of Chicago, a massive decline, month-over-month decline in Chicago that, ironically, in many ways, suggests the auto industry could be in more trouble than we believe.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they're mounting up. What do you need to see, Steve, to believe that we're -- that recession is inevitable?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: I'm not saying that a recession is inevitable. I think we could still potentially skate around a recession.\par \par \uc2 The big news -- the other big news that came out, this report, Charles, this week is that the Fed basically announced that they're going to do this 50-basis-point increase in rates, but then they may take a pause on that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And, of course, Wall Street reacted very positively to that, because, as you know, Charles, your friends on Wall Street are addicted to this cheap money from the Fed.\par \par \uc2 And so that -- I do see some improvement on the inflation front.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: We got a pretty good inflation number this week.\par \par \uc2 But we're still at about 6 or 7 percent inflation. That's way, way, way from where we should be at 1 to 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So -- and, by the way, you're right. Everyone loves free money until you have to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 Steve...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Exactly, including -- by the way, including consumers, right, consumers.\par \par \uc2 That's the one thing that's been strong in the economy is consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And what really concerns me is, not only do we have a national government, our federal government, that's $30 trillion in debt.\par \par \uc2 But now you're seeing -- and you have reported on this, Charles -- a big increase in consumer debt on credit cards.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And I don't know about you, but when I see that credit card bill in January after all the Christmas shopping, I'm like, I can't believe I spent that much money.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 To your point, right now, savings are at this -- one of the lowest ever, 2.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Credit card bills went up 20 percent, the debts 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 I got to go, but I do want to get a quick thought on averting this rail strike. How much of a disaster did we avert?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: It would have been a total disaster if the rail workers had gone on strike.\par \par \uc2 But I have got to say, Charles, this whole debacle is because of the runaway inflation we have seen. Prices are up 15 percent now since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came into office, so it's no wonder workers want a pay raise.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, that's -- again, that gets back to all the free money. We have to pay the price for it.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Steve, thank you so much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: A potential change in Florida's Statehouse could signal -- or could be signaling that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  may make a run for the White House.\par \par \uc2 We have got the guy who's leading the charge here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To Florida now.\par \par \uc2 The top Republican in the Statehouse there is signaling he is willing to change state law to allow Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to run for president without having to resign, if he chooses to run.\par \par \uc2 He's here now to discuss, Florida House Speaker Paul Renner.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, the plot thickens, so to speak. I remember, during the -- during the election, reporters were asking, and, of course, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ' rival asking, trying to get out of him whether or not he would be running for president. He was pretty mute about it.\par \par \uc2 But this is going to open up a whole lot of speculation. Why are you pushing this?\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. PAUL RENNER (R-FL): Well, obviously, the governor and I are focused on this upcoming session and the needs of Florida.\par \par \uc2 But the issue is this. Would our governor have to resign right out of the gate before he would actually be elected president, as would be the case with most people running for office? And we think he shouldn't. And so there's some ambiguity in the law that needs to be fixed, so that if the governor chooses to share his vision and the real leadership that we have enjoyed here in Florida with America, he can do so without having to immediately resign as governor.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I would imagine it's -- maybe it would be sort of bittersweet.\par \par \uc2 I mean, certainly, Governor DeSantis has done an amazing job. You look at how Florida came through the COVID pandemic. Everyone's moving there. Businesses are moving there. He's become a national superstar. I'm sure you don't want to lose him. But, by the same token, I think the rest of the country might want to get a piece of that action too.\par \par \uc2 RENNER: Well, I think that will be his decision.\par \par \uc2 But we sure have it great here in Florida. And he's a big reason for that. And so we would hate to lose him. But I think it's fair for him to offer his talents and his leadership to the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let me ask you about a decision made earlier today, of course, and this goes back to, of course, you, Congressman, being outspoken about this woke environmental policies, on Wall Street, in particular, Florida pulling $2 billion worth of assets managed by \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , other states looking to do the same or have done the same.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see all of this heading?\par \par \uc2 RENNER: Well, not in a good direction.\par \par \uc2 I think ESG, for those that don't know, is really driving up our cost of living. And, most importantly, it bypasses democracy. You have martini millionaires that are making decisions on major, major issues that belong in the Congress and belong in the state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 And so we pushed back. I sent a letter to our credit rating agencies. Florida has the highest credit rating you can get. And we said, look, you got to drop the ESG politics, because they're now demanding that information from us. And so you could have a situation where Florida has a lower credit rating than a state like California or Illinois, where they don't balance their budget, they don't lower their debt, and have record reserves like we do.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, environmental, social and governance, I have been raising the alarm on this for five years. I'm glad everyone is finally catching up.\par \par \uc2 Real quick, I got less than a minute ago, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2  comes back to \uc2 Disney\uc2 . A lot of people believe that they had gotten too woke. It's good to have opinions. It feels like maybe he's changing a little bit, that he's going to be focused on one thing, and one thing only, and that's fun for the customer.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 RENNER: Yes, I mean, look, no state wants somebody from out of state coming in and dictating their values to us. And that bill was really falsely maligned.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 RENNER: And they should have never been involved in that battle in the first place.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I mean, again, it's early, but I think everyone watched how much money everyone lost, how sad everyone is.\par \par \uc2 RENNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And maybe, I think, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2  will come in and do a good job.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, thank you at home. And, remember, you can catch me tomorrow. It's jobs day. It's a huge day. This market is going to be up big or down big. I will be on at 2:00 p.m., "Making Money." Again, grab a pen and pad. I'm always trying to teach you a few things too.\par \par \uc2 But, for now, here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221202eic100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Administration And \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  On Direction Of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ; Congress Intervenes To Avert Rail Strike; Politicalization Of Military Erodes Public Trust; Immigrants Continue To Attempt Crossing At U.S. Southern Border In Large Numbers; Republican Lawmakers Criticizes TikTok's Ties To Chinese Government; District Attorneys Funded By Billionaire \uc2 George Soros\uc2  Criticized For Approach To Prosecuting Crime; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  States \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Should Be Haven For Free Speech; Republican House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy Makes Bid For Speakership In Next Congress\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Peter Doocy, Chad Pergram, Jennifer Griffin, Brit  Hume, Max Gorden, Aishah Hasnie, Gillian Turner, David Spunt \par \uc2 7,792 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Here he is on the peloton. He likes to do the intervals and arms ride.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That is true.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's like a 30-minute ride and he's hit many personal records. So congratulations to you, Greg.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Trace.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF BREAKING NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Dana. Thank you. Thank you, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Bret Baier. The House goes along with President Biden passing legislation designed to stop a possible railway strike. We have exclusive results also from the Reagan National Defense forum survey, how Americans really feel about the military.\par \par \uc2 And is TikTok lying about its Chinese connections and how it can track your activity.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, the Biden administration is vowing to keep a close eye on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  because of concerns about the spread of misinformation that is raising eyebrows among many both in and out of government.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  frames his fight for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's independence as a battle for the future of civilization with references to free speech and tyranny.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest tonight from White House Correspondent Peter Doocy. Peter, good evening.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Trace. Officials here are stopping short of saying that if they see something on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  they really don't like, they will try to shut \uc2 Twitter\uc2  down.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not entirely clear what exactly the White House wants \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to do. But it is clear that the pressure from Pennsylvania Avenue is on.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's in the news a lot.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Now, because \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's former head of trust and safety admits wrongdoing when censoring the Hunter Biden laptop story.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, it's a mistake?\par \par \uc2 Yoel Roth\uc2 , former head of trust and safety, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : In my opinion, yes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: This comes as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has interfered in elections.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The fact that a laptop was discovered in the weeks before the election, that e-mails were revealed and that social media platforms including \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , actually suppressed access to a New York Post story is deeply offensive to me.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Musk is promising things will soon be different, which led to the White House saying they are keeping a close eye on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): That is offensive to me. Government's going to go after someone that wants to have free speech. What do they have to look at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  about?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Democrats see dual threats, domestic.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): I don't believe that we should be subsidizing or encouraging disinformation and distortion. I haven't seen anything from Musk so far, that gives me confidence that he understands the difference.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And foreign.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Mr. Musk is entirely dependent for the success of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , his biggest enterprise on the Communist Party in China, that's where he gets his electric batteries.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Progressives want new government regulations for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , laughing off the billionaire owner's concerns.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is doing just fine.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And despite problems with the platform at the White House, no plans to delete the app.\par \par \uc2 You're saying that you're keeping an eye on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ? Because it might not be a suitable platform. So why use it?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Look, I want to be very clear here, the president has always said and he has been very, very clear in his belief that it is important of social media platforms to continue to take steps to reduce hate speech and misinformation. And he will continue to say that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): This White House knows that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  does try to flag misinformation because it happened to them earlier this month. The White House Twitter account posted that big increases in Social Security checks are because of President Biden and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  jumped in with some clarifying information that those increases are actually because of a law signed by \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And when that happened, nobody around here blamed \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . They just deleted the tweet and reposted it with a little more context, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And moved on. We'll have more on this with the panel. Peter Doocy live in the White House. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will push interest rates higher than previously expected and keep them there for an extended period in order to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 However, Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  says the Fed may increase its key interest rate next month by only a half point after four straight three quarter point hikes.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street took the news well, the Dow surged 737 points to exit bear territory for the first time since September 26th. The S&P 500 was up 122. The NASDAQ jumped 484.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, lawmakers in the House have approved legislation requested by President Biden to avert a crippling holiday season nationwide rail strike. Big labor is against it. So are many members of both parties in both chambers.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Trace, the easy part was passing the bill through the House on a bipartisan basis, 290 to 137. Progressives supported congressional involvement once the House took a separate vote to give workers seven days of paid medical leave.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): Obviously, we never want to intervene. And it should not be the norm. And it cannot be the norm. It's an unfortunate position to be in but this is the best that we can do.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: (voice over): Republicans said President Biden should have fixed the labor dispute on his own and not dumped it on Congress.\par \par \uc2 REP. RICK CRAWFORD (R-AR): The president who has by his own declaration been the most union friendly president in history, been here since 1970. Joe Amtrak, Joe lunchbox, whatever you want to call him. He has punted this to us to deal with his colossal mistake.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: There's bipartisan skepticism in the Senate, it's a challenge to break a filibuster. Bernie Sanders wants a vote on the House medical leave provision.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): What I would say to every member of this body, Democrat, Republican, that think long and hard about how you go home to your constituents and say I voted against allowing rail workers in this country seven paid sick days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): There's worry of economic catastrophe if union strike next week that could stop shipments of food and holiday gifts. One important note on the bill today, Speaker hopeful Kevin McCarthy, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And Chad, was that an effort by McCarthy to get the votes for speaker?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, McCarthy has scored an unexpected boost from conservative radio host Mark Levin. He called the five GOP members who oppose McCarthy boneheads and saboteurs. But McCarthy challenger Andy Biggs contends there are more than five GOP members who oppose McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Does Mr. McCarthy have sufficient votes to secure that office on January 3rd, sir?\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BIGGS (R-AZ): Not where we sit today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How many folks are suppose there might be who would be opposing him at this juncture?\par \par \uc2 BIGGS: I would say somewhere around 20.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now compare the GOP turmoil to the Democrats velvet transition. Democrats tapped \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  as their leader today. Jeffrey has characterized McCarthy's situation as a "mess and chaos", Chase.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Chad Pergram live for us on the Hill. Chad, thank you. Let's bring in Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume. Brit, it's always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 I wonder if you'll pick up where Chad left off there because, you know, a lot of Republicans saying the president dumped this on Congress. Do you think he did, because while it passed the House, it's going to have a bit more iffy of a time in the Senate and the clock is ticking, Brit.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: He did dump it on Congress. Confident I think that Congress would pass this measure.\par \par \uc2 Remember, while there are -- there are about what 12 rail unions involved in this rail dispute that's led us here, eight of them, I believe, have endorsed the plan that the president negotiated or that his team negotiated, and which is attached to this bill. So, only part of organized labor is against it.\par \par \uc2 In the Senate, there's some dispute about whether it will pass. My guess is it probably will and when it does, this issue will go away. Whether the separate bill passed by the House to provide the sick leave, the paid sick leave that the railway workers want will also pass the Senate remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I'm curious Brit, just kind of moving on topics here because I'm curious if you think we ended with Chad talking about, you know whether the Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy has enough votes to become the Speaker of the House, he needs 218, a lot of people think that he's short of that. What are your thoughts on going into this process?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, look, if these handful of Republican House members who are so far refusing to back McCarthy want to have an effect, the question arises, well, who are they backing? And what other candidate for speaker in the Republican ranks has any chance, there really is no alternative candidate at this time.\par \par \uc2 So, one senses here that what's at work is in negotiation where these conservative members would like Kevin McCarthy to promise certain things that they want, at which point presumably they would go along.\par \par \uc2 It's messy, it's not pretty, it's not as smooth as what the Democrats have done as has been pointed out. But my guess is that that they'll find a way to do it in the end and the question will become what does McCarthy have to give to get the votes?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: So, it's a play and will he have to sell part of his soul to be able to get this done, Brit? Because you know, you look at the \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  and they're going to want - they're going to want a little bit of payback for their votes.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, yes, they are but it's not clear to me how much he'll have to give up. I think his soul is probably intact, Trace, that price will be something less than that.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Brit Hume, always great to see you. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you, Trace. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, the White House says the administration has expressed concerns to Turkey over threats of a possible invasion to target Kurdish U.S. partners in northeast Syria.\par \par \uc2 Turkey has threatened to invade Kurdistan for years and the U.S. has maintained contingency plans for just such a scenario.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight an exclusive first look at the latest survey from the Reagan National Defense forum that contains startling news about how you and your fellow Americans view the military.\par \par \uc2 Here's the Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We maintain the peace through our strength. Weakness only invites aggression.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): This year for the 10th annual Reagan National Defense forum, a new national defense survey, the fifth of its kind outlines an alarming trend. The perception that the military as overly politicized is contributing to a sharp decline in trust.\par \par \uc2 Five years ago, 70 percent said they had a great deal of trust and confidence in the military. Now, 48 percent agree, down 22 points since 2018.\par \par \uc2 ROGER ZAKHEIM\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2  INSTITUTE: Perception is reality that polarization whether it's something that our leaders are doing in the military or not, that is what the American people believe is happening. That goes to wokism too, this is not a function of U.S. military not succeeding in armed conflict.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Perception becomes reality when repeated by politicians.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): It seems the Biden administration is trying to destroy our military by force feeding it wokism.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Only 13 percent of respondents aged 18 to 29 say they are extremely or very willing to join the military. While 26 percent are not willing to serve at all.\par \par \uc2 The perception of Russia as an enemy reached a survey high of 82 percent, up from 65 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 And more view Ukraine as an ally, 76 percent up from 49 percent a year ago, respondents were asked about military support to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: 41 percent of Republicans saying that we've done enough is surprising. You would expect the party of Reagan to do better.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: 71 percent are concerned that Russian aggression unchecked will inspire other authoritarian regimes to invade their neighbors.\par \par \uc2 REAGAN: The United States has an obligation to its citizens and to the people of the world, never to let those who would destroy freedom dictate the future course of life on this planet.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Many are concerned that sending so many weapons to Ukraine is depleting U.S. stockpiles that may be needed for Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Three quarters of those surveyed now view China as an enemy up from 55 percent in 2018. But when asked if the United States has a clear strategy for managing its relationship with China, more than half said no, 70 percent think China will invade Taiwan in the next five years, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And Jen, the Pentagon has released a new report on China's military power, right?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: That's right, Trace. The headline is China's nuclear arsenal is growing at a faster rate than previously expected. China now has more than 400 operational nukes and is expected to reach 1, 500 by 2035. The report also finds China has conducted more ballistic missile tests in the last year than the rest of the world combined, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The report has some troubling conclusions. Jennifer Griffin live for us at the Pentagon. Jen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former Chinese President \uc2 Jiang Zemin\uc2  has died. Jiang served as president for a decade. He is credited with leading China out of isolation after the army crushed the Tiananmen Square pro-democracy protests back in 1989. He is also known for supporting economic reforms that led to a decade of explosive growth.\par \par \uc2 State media report he died of leukemia and multiple organ failure. \uc2 Jiang Zemin\uc2  was 96 years old. Up next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm just thankful for the Lord that he didn't blow it all away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Blew a lot of way though, severe weather tears through parts of the south. We will have a live report.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. At least 10 students are dead after a bomb hit a religious school in northern Afghanistan. A \uc2 Taliban\uc2  official says several others were wounded. No group has yet claimed responsibility for the attack.\par \par \uc2 Spanish officials are investigating after a mail bomb was delivered to the Ukrainian Embassy in Madrid. The Interior Ministry says an embassy security guard was injured while handling a letter that exploded when it was opened. Police say the envelope contained a homemade explosive and was addressed to the ambassador.\par \par \uc2 And here's a live look at a very dark Eiffel Tower in Paris. One of the big stories there tonight, France restarting one of its coal burning plants just months after production was halted. It's part of the country's emergency effort to keep heat and electricity on this winter.\par \par \uc2 Russia's decision to cut off natural gas to Europe has paved the way for coals come back in some regions. Those are just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. And we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, parts of the south are cleaning up tonight after tornadoes damaged numerous structures in Mississippi, and two people were killed last night in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Fox Weather correspondent Max Gorden is in Bassfield, Mississippi tonight. Max, good evening.\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX WEA