ational Weather Service has been out with teams assessing the damage trying to determine how strong these tornadoes were.\par \par \uc2 Luckily, for people living here, we should have good weather for the next couple of days. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. That's good news. Max Gorden live for us in Bassfield. Mississippi. Max, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, now, it is Democrats who are putting pressure on the Biden administration to deal with the President's border crisis.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX2 in San Francisco where police could soon have access to robots with a deadly force option.\par \par \uc2 The board of supervisors signed off on the change, with those in favor saying some of the robots will be designed to dispose of bombs.\par \par \uc2 Opponents worried the machines could be used to kill possible suspects.\par \par \uc2 KHON on Honolulu, as officials warn residents to be ready to move if threatened by lava and ash from the Mauna Loa volcano. The \uc2 U.S. Geological Survey\uc2  told those living on the Big Island, they may need to evacuate depending on the location and the direction of the lava flows.\par \par \uc2 And now, live look at New York. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX5, People magazine announcing its people of the year.\par \par \uc2 The honorees, Matthew McConaughey, Mila Kunis, Jennifer Hudson, and Quinta Brunson. McConaughey was chosen for his advocacy efforts following the Uvalde, Texas school shooting.\par \par \uc2 Mila Kunis was picked for her fundraising for Ukraine, where she was born.\par \par \uc2 Hudson and Brunson were honor for their on-screen work. And that is a look tonight live "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT, and we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Now, President Biden met with Native American leaders today for the first in-person summit on tribal affairs in six years.\par \par \uc2 He announced new plans and initiatives to help communities transition to clean energy development. One plan allocates $135 million to help certain tribes relocate from areas impacted by climate change.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, some Senate Democrats are putting pressure on the president's homeland security chief to come up with a plan to address the southern border crisis.\par \par \uc2 Alejandro Mayorkas is already being threatened with impeachment by House Republicans who will control the Chamber next year.\par \par \uc2 Axios reports the Biden administration is considering a border policy overhaul. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie has the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Senator Mark Warner now, the latest Democrat to want to know how the Biden administration plans to deal with the southern border once the pandemic-era Title 42 border policy ends.\par \par \uc2 This week, four moderate Democrats \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , Mark Kelly, \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , and Jon Tester sent a letter to DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas, requesting a detailed plan.\par \par \uc2 Writing, "We have expressed concern with DHS' preparations for the end of Title 42, especially as the situation has deteriorated at times."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JON TESTER (D-MT): I think there needs to be a plan for when Title 42 goes away. There needs to be a plan on how they're going to deal with the - - with the folks at the border.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: "Axios" reports officials are now considering barring some asylum seekers and surging prosecutions of illegal border crossings in preparation. The Democratic pressure comes as border crossings continue to shatter records. In the El Paso, Texas, sector there were nearly 2,000 migrant encounters in just the past 24 hours. That means fiscal year 2023 for this sector is up 257 percent compared to this time last year.\par \par \uc2 And a deadly 