ophy to their jobs. Turns out, several of them were funded by a well-known billionaire Democratic donor. Correspondent David Spunt shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: As crime continues to plague cities large and small, some prosecutors are being called out for progressive policies. Take Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  facing criticism after his office reported downgrading 52 percent of felony cases to misdemeanors.\par \par \uc2 INNA VERNIKOV, (R) NEW \uc2 YORK CITY COUNCIL\uc2 : He keeps doing what he is doing, and we keep living in a Gotham City.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: FOX News Digital reviewed campaign finance records in all 50 states and discovered voters just elected to new and reelected a pair of prosecutors backed by liberal billionaire \uc2 George Soros\uc2  who has spent years pushing criminal justice reform. Jackie Sartoris won in Maine to be the Cumberland County District Attorney. FOX News Digital uncovered a $300,000 payment from Soros backed groups to a group that helped Sartoris win. Kimberly Graham, the incoming district attorney in Polk County Iowa got a boost, though indirectly from Soros and his team via associated group. In Texas, Soros' money helped D.A. Joe Gonzales win another term in San Antonio, and Dallas County D.A. John Creuzot was just reelected.\par \par \uc2 Over the summer, Soros wrote in a "Wall Street Journal" op-ed, "The idea that we need to choose between justice and safety is false." Soro backed Philadelphia D.A. \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2 , first elected in 2017, is now facing impeachment by state lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KRASNER, (D) PHILADELPHIA DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Part of the Republican playbook, as you well know, is to point a finger at large diverse cities, and say large diverse city are lawless.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: A spokeswoman for Krasner declined to answer our question, but instead criticized this network's coverage of \uc2 George Soros\uc2 . We also reached out to the rest of the people mentioned in this story but are not heard back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): According to his foundation, Soros has given away $32 billion-dollar to promote justice and equality. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: David Spunt in D.C. David, thank you. And as David mentioned, Pennsylvania state government is one step closer to ousting Philadelphia District Attorney \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2 . The move comes amid criticism his progressive policies have led to a rise in crime. The state senate voted to formally accept articles of impeachment. A trial whether to remove him from office now scheduled for January. Krasner has said efforts to impeach him are political.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden versus \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . But as we go break, Fleetwood Mac vocalist, keyboard player, and songwriter Christine McVie has died. The band sold tens of millions of records during its peak commercial years in the late 1970s. Her family says she passed away after a short illness. Christine McVie was 79.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: I think the American public has spoken on this. I think our First Amendment stands up. And I think they should stop picking on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN, (D-IL) SENATE MAJORITY WHIP: I do believe in free speech. On the other hand, I don't believe that we should be subsidizing or encouraging disinformation and distortion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN, (D-MA): One human being should not decide how millions of people communicate with each other. I think a lot of people ought to be able to have input to it, and that's not what is happening right now on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's bring in our panel, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," Marie Harf, former State Department spokeswoman under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , and Brian Kilmeade of "FOX & FRIENDS" and the author of "The President and The Freedom Fighter." It's now out in paperback. Welcome to you all.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in you first, Mollie, because I want to put this tweet up on the screen. This is \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  saying, quoting here, "Why are so many in the media against free speech?" Going on to say, "This is messed up." And I think it's a fair question, because don't we in the media want robust debate?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": It's a fair question, but it's way too mild. You think about the situation with social media companies, most of them throughout the world are controlled by the left. They collude with the left to suppress news and information, to de-platform effective conservative voices.\par \par \uc2 And then you have Elon who has taken over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . It's not that he wants to turn it into a conservative version of these leftist media groups. He just wants there to be free speech and debate. And then they entire establishment, including our corrupt media, are absolutely freaking out about it. We have the president of the United States saying absolutely obscene things about wanting to go after Elon for supporting the First Amendment to our Constitution. This a massive, important story. It's an existential threat to the republic, and Elon should not be the only person out there why the media and other leftists in the regime are working so hard to fight him.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And Marie, it seems like a proper assessment because \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  really hasn't chosen sides. He's just opening the door. Peter Doocy was at the White House briefing and he asked this of \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 . Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: You are saying that you are keeping an eye on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  because it might not 