nsored on this. And you go back to the whole COVID debate, and you add some of the best medical minds, scientific minds in the world who were canceled and shut down, not because they were wrong, but because they disagreed with the popular narrative, Brian.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS HOST: A couple of things. I think we should all agree that TikTok is the problem on social media. The popularity is something noteworthy for free marketers should look at. But China controls it. We should get together, Republicans and Democrats, and decide, much to the chagrin of 12 to 18-year-old, it has got to go.\par \par \uc2 Number two, it's hard to say that I think 62,000 people that got restored to TikTok are all antisemitic and trolls. I'm pretty sure that's too broad of a swipe.\par \par \uc2 Number three, I'm pretty sure these guys were all on the same page because one phone call got the Hunter Biden story totally shelved, and anyone, including the press secretary who retweeted it, suspended. So there is a problem when they are all thinking the same.\par \par \uc2 I hearken back to 2016. Niall Ferguson came out with a book and he said -- I'm over in Silicon Valley, he's at the Hoover Institute, and he said they are so upset and embarrassed that the Trump administration, then the Trump campaign, was able to use social media, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  specifically, legally, bought time, had a strategy, was able to use that to get the White House legitimately, without Russian help. They said watch for an overcorrection. We saw the overcorrection in 2020 with the Hunter Biden story and everything else that happened since.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is looking to flatten the playing field, and he has already had some success, Trace. And here is where he had. He came out and said, why is it that app store by \uc2 Apple\uc2  controls my future, they are talking about getting rid of me? \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  today came out and said I'm a little concerned about the power of \uc2 Apple\uc2  controlling the app store that could make or break any platform in the future. So already he is changing things and flattening the playing field.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And on that note, Mollie, not to make you the decipherer of all \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  tweets, but he just tweeted, quoting, regarding his meeting with \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  of \uc2 Apple\uc2 , "Good conversation. Among other things we resolved the misunderstanding about \uc2 Twitter\uc2  potentially being removed from the app store. Tim was clear that \uc2 Apple\uc2  never considered doing so."\par \par \uc2 But if you scroll this, you look at the 60-plus major companies that are no longer advertising on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  anymore, and I wonder if there's -- I wonder if there's maybe a little bit of a healing process going on with the companies, but you look at these companies, Mollie, and a lot of them are the usual suspects, the same ones who pulled out of Georgia when voting law, right, pulling Major League Baseball All-Star game out of there and, and of course the law did nothing to suppress votes. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: We have this major problem where large corporations are aligned with the left, that they do the left's bidding. There actually is collusion between the White House and some of these social media companies where they will ask political opponents to be removed from the platform or to have certain ideas or words censored, and they get that. This is major topic that the Republican Party needs to tackle as they take over the House. All of these corporations seem to be aligned against free speech, freedom of the press, freedom of assembly, these things that are so important to Americans, and that have just kind of been frittered away in recent years.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Panel, thank you. Stand by.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Kevin McCarthy goes after the speakership of the House. And a SPECIAL REPORT salute to a hero police officer in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2 , (R-GA): We need subpoena power. And in order to have subpoena power, we have to elect Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2 , (R-FL): I think that it is impossible for Kevin to get 218 votes on the third based on my conversation with members.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Andy Biggs said he thought there was about 20, and I think he is in the ballpark.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: We either are successful together or we will fail individually, and we will not be given the possibility or the opportunity to be in the majority again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And we are now back with the panel. I want to put this up on the screen because these are the five GOP House members who oppose Kevin McCarthy. You see him right there, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , Bob Good, go down the list there. And Brian Kilmeade, to you first, because if Bob Good and Andy Biggs are correct and he is about 20 votes short, he might be able to get them, but there is going to be an awful lot of back-scratching going on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I know, but Trace, you have got to say that Republicans have to say to themselves, who else is there? Is there some stud waiting in the wings or some great leader just breaking or cutting their teeth on the Washington landscape? No. Kevin McCarthy has earned this opportunity. He is a consolidator. I think he has already showed himself, he has been tireless in earning money and raising money and recruiting candidates. There's only about -- Andy Biggs is the second highest in votes. He needs 218.\par \par \uc2 And for me McCarthy is going to get the job, but why give him the job eventually and weaken him and show so much vulnerability or make him cave to the point where he has to compromise his ability to work? Jim Jordan is on his side. That was the toughest sell along with the Freedom Caucus. \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  is on his side. I thought that was the biggest mountain to climb. And for Kevin McCarthy, I think he has earned it. Republicans are crazy to drag this out.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And they drag it out, Mollie, but Mark Levin says that the GOP needs to come together and quit this sabotage. Listen to him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK LEVIN, HOST, "MARK LEVIN PODCAST": These five have no plan, so they are sabotaging conservatives. They are sabotaging those of us who want to get the spending under control. They are sabotaging those of us who want to secure the border. They are sabotaging those of us who want to fully fund the United States military because of their antics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Mollie?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I think conservatives do need to understand that the choice isn't between Kevin McCarthy and someone more conservative. Because of that narrow majority, it's really between Kevin McCarthy and a consensus Democrat candidate. And so people are understanding, there really is nobody else who has built what Kevin has built, Kevin McCarthy has built. And so I think that will likely secure him the speakership.\par \par \uc2 There's a bigger issue, though, which is that the Republican voters are sick to death of Republican leadership, their fecklessness, their impotence, their weakness. They have no problem coming together to pass like a spending bill for Ukraine, but they can't do anything that the base actually wants or needs. They can't find the media. They can't fight their Democrat opponents.\par \par \uc2 And so whoever gets it, and the Speaker will likely be Kevin McCarthy, they need to understand that there is such deep frustration and anger in the base of the Republican Party, they need Republican leadership to act in a much more competent and focused way that benefits their voters.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, Marie, you have Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and House Minority Leader elect \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  talking about how they are willing to work with Republicans. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE DEMOCRATIC CAUCUS CHAIR: We look forward to finding opportunities to partner with the other side of the aisle, but we will also push back against extremism whenever necessary.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: If our Republican colleagues can shake off the MAGA wing of their party that is holding Republicans down, we can get a lot done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: They both hit MAGA in one form or another, and what they're saying is if they would just see things our way we would get along just fine, Marie.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I don't think that's true. Under the Democratic Congress with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as president they have passed a number of bipartisan bills that have addressed things the American people care about, like prescription drug prices, like infrastructure in that bill as well, Trace.\par \par \uc2 But look, this is a story of the Republican Party in disarray. The Republican caucus is fractured. Kevin McCarthy doesn't have the votes, but there's no one else to lead them. And if in fact he becomes speaker, which I suspect he will, he will be leading a caucus of members who don't agree on very many things, who are very willing, especially with the Freedom Caucus and other more extreme members, to vote against party leadership.\par \par \uc2 And you can say a lot of things about \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  when she has been speaker, but she kept the Democratic caucus together with a very narrow majority. And I'm not sure that Kevin McCarthy has the political chops or the ability with this raucous Republican caucus to be able to do that. And if we come into the presidential election, Trace, and we have a Republican Congress that has only investigated \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and hasn't addressed any of the policy issues that voters sent them to Washington to address, they are going to have a problem in the next election.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And just to Marie's point, very quickly, Brian, they don't have to -- they can have protest votes. They don't have to vote no. They can vote present. They could not show up. It would lower that 218 threshold dramatically. Final thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: And that was the strategy. Kevin McCarthy said if you don't want to vote for me, vote present.\par \par \uc2 But I would just say that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  barely got the job last time. They were sick of her. She said give me the job and I promise to leave, and she left. But she can't leave the stage. She's going to sit in the back and just try to pretend not to be a shadow speaker along with Clyburn and \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 . It's going to be an ugly scene. For the most part the right has gotten rid of the raucous right. The Freedom Caucus is all in with McCarthy except for a couple of guys. So I think it's going to go a lot smoother than you think. I think the bigger story is \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and Warnock, how powerful the Republicans will be at 50/50 as opposed to 51-49.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And then lastly, 10 seconds, Mollie, you think Kevin McCarthy makes it into -- he takes the gavel?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I do. And I think he actually has done a good job the last couple of years of reaching out to different groups within the Republican conference, and that will serve him well.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Mollie Hemingway, Marie Harf, Brian Kilmeade, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute. A woman in Florida is safe after a police officer jumped into action to rescue her from a sinking vehicle. Officer Kwesi Johnson went into the canal standing in waste deep water. He used a baton to smash into the driver's side window. And because of his quick-thinking, he was able to lift the woman to safety. That is great for him.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we seek common ground on consumer protection.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Trace Gallagher in Los Angeles, joining us tonight, "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT," America's late news. Have a great night. Jesse Watters right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221201eibu00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 China Ramping Up Nuclear Program?; Interview With Rio Grande City, Texas, Mayor Joel Villarreal; Border Controversy; Idaho Murder Mystery; Interview With Sen. \uc2 John Thune\uc2  (R-SD); House Passes Bill to Avert Rail Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Kat Timpf, Christina Coleman, Rich Edson, Aishah Hasnie, Chad  Pergram, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 7,960 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Today, we are here to safeguard the financial security of American families and avert a devastating nationwide rail shutdown.\par \par \uc2 TONY CARDWELL, RAIL UNION OFFICIAL: The goal is to avoid one, and it's a very simple thing. You just need to give us sick leave.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SHEILA JACKSON LEE\uc2  (D-TX): I'm standing with the unions, my locals and my national.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): I'm not voting for a deal that the workers themselves have rejected, a deal that was cut by labor bosses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Off the rails. The House passing a bill today aimed at stopping a nationwide rail strike in its tracks.\par \par \uc2 But pressure is mounting in the Senate, where some Republicans are threatening to block it, as President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is urging senators to move quickly on it.\par \par \uc2 We will talk to Senate Minority whip \uc2 John Thune\uc2  on where things stand right now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First to Chad Pergram. He's on Capitol Hill for us, where things could get thorny over the next few days -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, the easy part is done.\par \par \uc2 The House OKed the bill 292-137 with wide bipartisan support. But the hard part is passing the bill through the Senate and breaking a filibuster.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): I would say to every member of this body, Democrat, Republican, that think long and hard about how you go home to your constituents and say, I voted against allowing rail workers in this country seven paid sick days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Sanders and other progressives support a plan to give rail workers seven days of paid medical leave, but it's unclear if the Senate can shoehorn that provision into the bill or if the Senate can get 60 yeas.\par \par \uc2 Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  and the secretary of transportation, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , make their case tomorrow to Senate Democrats over lunch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Where I hope to see it going is bipartisan cooperation to move this quickly through Congress and get it to the president's desk. Look, I don't care what your politics are. America can't have a rail shutdown. There is no substitute for a well- functioning rail system in the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Some GOP members scoffed at President Biden. They say the president should have fixed the labor dispute in September.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RICK CRAWFORD (R-AR): The president who has, by his own declaration, been the most-union friendly president in history, been here since 1970, Joe Amtrak, Joe Lunch Box, whatever you want to call him, he has punted this to us to deal with his colossal mistake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Unions balked at a possible deal because it lacked medical leave. Progressives worried about interceding against unions. The bill compels unions to accept the terms.\par \par \uc2 Only eight Democrats voted against congressional intervention; 129 GOP members voted no, one notable nay, speaker hopeful Kevin McCarthy -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, Chad Pergram live on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to Minority Whip \uc2 John Thune\uc2  on how that vote is shaping up in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 So, where do things stand at this hour?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): Well, it's not real clear, Sandra. The House is acted, so the legislation is headed toward the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats in the Senate attempted to hot-line a bill, in other words, to expedite its consideration. That has been objected to. And we will see now as negotiations get under way about what the path forward might be in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But what I can tell you is, there's a lot of consternation among Republicans in the Senate that we're in this position in the first place. Republicans don't believe that this is a role for Congress. This is something that should have been worked out, negotiated, and that the administration, the president prior to the election, who punted this until after the election, is now trying to get members of Congress to bail him and his administration out for their failure to get this deal done.\par \par \uc2 So, there's a lot of conversations still to be held. And how this ultimately gets disposed of in the Senate, I think, is still an open question.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Senator, where do you stand on things? Some of your colleagues have indicated that they will not support what they see as a deal for the union bosses, rather than the railroad -- railroad workers themselves?\par \par \uc2 So, are you in favor of it?\par \par \uc2 THUNE: Well, I don't want to see a rail strike, a shutdown, for sure.\par \par \uc2 But the one thing I will say -- and this is something that's -- a lot of our members are discussing -- is the possibility that one option here would be to extend the cooling-off period for 30 days or 60 days or whatever that term would be to force the parties back to the table to get a deal that everybody could sign off on, so that Congress isn't forced to be put in a position.\par \par \uc2 I don't think, to your question about the particulars of the deal, that it's the role of Congress, I certainly am not going to support any amendments that go in and try and micromanage this deal. This is an agreement, obviously. It's been negotiated for the past three years between the railroads and the unions, and, of course, now, most lately -- lately with the Biden administration's blessing.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think it's -- I don't think Congress wants to get into the weeds and start negotiating the fine points of these agreements between management and labor.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But, as you know, this could -- this could be thrown off and there could be a full-on strike. What do you believe are the economic implications? What's at risk here?\par \par \uc2 THUNE: Well, that's a -- that's a big issue. I mean, I represent a state is very dependent upon railroads. Freight railroads are the best way to ship bulk commodities, and we have a lot of bulk commodities. We're an agricultural state.\par \par \uc2 So, the consequences of a shutdown would be catastrophic. And that has to be avoided. The question is, what's the best way to do that? And, again, a lot of our members are coming to the conclusion that the best way to conclude this is, again, to force the parties back to the table.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, we will keep watching it.\par \par \uc2 Senator, I appreciate you joining us.\par \par \uc2 THUNE: Thanks, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Senator, if you could stand by with us, because we are going to hear from one of those unions in just a second, but we want to turn to one other topic before you go, our own Peter Doocy questioning White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  on what the White House meant when she said that the White House is keeping a close eye on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: When you say that you're going to be monitoring some of the speech on there, if you see something that you don't like, would you try to shut \uc2 Twitter\uc2  down?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, look, when you -- when you talk about monitoring, it is -- I hate to break it to you, Peter, just like everybody else, we very much monitor the news. We pay close attention to everything that you all are reporting.\par \par \uc2 And -- and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's in the news a lot. And so that's what we're paying attention to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: How did you interpret that, Senator?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 THUNE: I don't know how to interpret that. I mean, it's not the White House's job to moderate the content that goes on social media platforms.\par \par \uc2 Clearly -- and I'm somebody who believes in more transparency for big tech, for social media platforms, that they need to inform customers and consumers about the use of algorithms, opaque algorithms, to influence or manipulate the content that people see.\par \par \uc2 But I certainly would not want the White House determining what is and isn't acceptable in terms of content to be put -- be put on these platforms. And that certainly seemed to be the implication of what was being said there.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Senator Thune, appreciate you joining us. Thanks again.\par \par \uc2 THUNE: Thanks, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 Four unions are demanding extended sick pay be included in any deal, which could be a tough sell in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is with one of those unions. Sean Gerie is vice president of the Brotherhood of Maintenance of Way Employees. And he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, sir, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Where are your thoughts in this moment, as you watch all the action on Capitol Hill today?\par \par \uc2 SEAN GERIE, VICE PRESIDENT, BROTHERHOOD OF MAINTENANCE OF WAY EMPLOYEES: Good afternoon, Sandra. Thank you for having me on. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 I could tell you what our thoughts are. This is not a labor dispute. That's how it's being -- it's being vilified. We're being vilified by that. It's really a health issue. It is a physical health issue.\par \par \uc2 Our members, we had several members that were sick, several members that had died during the COVID thing. We work in close proximity together. We are -- if there's five guys or six guys working and somebody comes in sick, they're all going to get sick. It doesn't just affect us. It affects the nation.\par \par \uc2 The railroad is a very dangerous industry. It's not the kind of place you can come in if you're not 100 percent. You have to be on alert. We have got trains traveling with hazardous material, trains traveling over railroad tracks that are maintained by our people that have passengers and commuters.\par \par \uc2 This is a vital issue. Our members are very frustrated, and rightfully so. And we're standing behind them and we're going to fight. We believe that anything worth having is worth fighting for. And that's what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: For so many...\par \par \uc2 GERIE: What we're asking for is fair.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What you're asking for is fair. Got it.\par \par \uc2 For so many who are hearing you right now who have loved ones that work on -- in the railroad industry all across this country, they hear you and they get it. But do you believe those in Washington understand the points that you are making? Are we getting anywhere with this?\par \par \uc2 GERIE: That's an excellent question. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 It is a bipartisan issue, by and large, I will say that. We have been talking to staffers. We have been talking to some of the legislators ourselves. People get it. They understand it; 86 percent of Americans' work force has paid sick days. Railroad workers have none. It's just -- it's time to end the hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 They were told they were essential workers during the pandemic. The bosses worked remotely from home and told them what to do. They were safe. That's not an option for our guys. And they're sick of the hypocrisy. Membership is sick of the hypocrisy. And I don't blame them, and we stand with them.\par \par \uc2 It's a fair ask. And what we're asking for is something that the vast majority of Americans have.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What do you hope happens next with the House passage of this legislation that would avert economic catastrophe if there was actually a strike?\par \par \uc2 GERIE: Let me say this. Our organization does not want to strike. That is not what this is about. We do not want to call us this economic catastrophe, as you phrased it.\par \par \uc2 What we're hopeful for is that it makes its way through the Senate, and it gets to President Biden's desk and he signs it. That's what we're hopeful for.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: hone last quick question. You said you believe that you're being vilified. Who do you believe is vilifying you over this in your industry?\par \par \uc2 GERIE: Well, to be truthful, we don't think the railroads are being very honest in what they're saying.\par \par \uc2 We feel as though they're trying to paint us out to be greedy, that we're overpaid and whatnot. That's simply not the truth. The numbers that they're putting out there are not accurate. They have cut 45,000 jobs on the industry, which is roughly a 30 percent reduction in the work force.\par \par \uc2 Our members and members of other unions out there are simply made to do more with less. There's disciplinary action if you miss so much time for taking care of loved ones. And it's really, really frustrating, to say the least; 676 percent, that is what the profits went up between 2004 and 2021.\par \par \uc2 It's not that they cannot afford to pay for the sick days. It's that they simply do not want them. They don't even want to negotiate over it, because, quite honestly, they don't feel as though they have to. I think that they feel as though Congress will ride in and give them the rescue.\par \par \uc2 And I'm hopeful that our legislators on both sides of the aisle can see past that nonsense and do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GERIE: It wouldn't be the first time in this history.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GERIE: I'm sorry?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: No, I -- we hear you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Go ahead. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GERIE: It wouldn't be the first time in the history of this country that legislation had to be passed in order to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Got it.\par \par \uc2 GERIE: We no longer have child labor, right? There's a list of things.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 GERIE: This needs to be taken care of.\par \par \uc2 And, once again, our members deserve it, and our union is going to stand and fight for them to the bitter end.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, Sean, I really appreciate you joining us. Thank you very much. And we will be watching all of this, every minute of it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GERIE: Thank you very much for having me. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You got it.\par \par \uc2 GERIE: Have a good day.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, and a quick look at the corner of Wall and Broad, because, whoa, look what happened right at the end of the trading day here. You will see it started out in the red.\par \par \uc2 And halfway through the trading day, we heard from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  chair, and those words indicated market rally, apparently, because we ended 737 points higher on the Dow. And now the Dow is officially exiting bear market territory, which meant that it was 20 percent down from its recent highs.\par \par \uc2 And there you have it, quite a rally on Wall Street, investors keeping an eye on Friday's big jobs report and what clues it will give us for another potential rate hike from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  says it's watching inflation to peak out, basically indicating that it's going to back off its aggressive stance to combat inflation. Market rallied after that.\par \par \uc2 All right, we will keep watching that.\par \par \uc2 To Idaho now and a community living in fear for weeks, as the killer in those college murders remains at large. A vigil set for tonight. Why officials are said to be watching that vigil very closely.\par \par \uc2 And with the border surge escalating, some Democrats now joining Republicans in demanding action, right, Aishah?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra. That's right.\par \par \uc2 And you know what? The White House just told our Peter Doocy that Republicans are pulling political stunts over the border, but it's not just Republicans who are worried. Coming up, I will tell you who is demanding answers by tomorrow -- next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: To Idaho now, a vigil set to honor the four students killed there. That is expected to happen tonight.\par \par \uc2 A law enforcement source telling FOX that authorities will be closely watching that vigil.\par \par \uc2 FOX News Christina Coleman is on the scene with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Why are they going to be watching this so closely, Christina?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi.\par \par \uc2 Because this killer is still on the loose. No suspect has been arrested in this case. And parents, they send their kids to school to focus on education. And there are a lot of students here focused on their finals, but they're also worried about coming across a ruthless killer. Take a listen to what some of them told me today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STARLA PAXTON, COLLEGE STUDENT: I personally live pretty close to where it happened. And I live by myself, and it's kind of terrifying. Like, every night, it gets, like, really dark here early.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don't know where this guy will be. He could be in Canada. We don't know. I really hope to find him, because, if he did something like this, and he's still alive, then who knows. He might go crazy. He might keep doing stuff like this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Idaho State Police say the collection of evidence at the crime scene is coming to a conclusion.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, cars belonging to the slain victims were towed away from the off-campus house where they were murdered on November 13. A total of five vehicles were seized from the house to be processed for evidence, as students, faculty and local residents come to terms with this quadruple homicide that has absolutely rocked this close-knit community.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAINE ECKLES, UNIVERSITY OF IDAHO DEAN OF STUDENTS: It's a sad, stark reminder that violence can happen anywhere in the world. And this is a safe community. I mean, statistics show it. It's been a long time since we have had a murder here in Moscow, over seven years, I believe. But crime does happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: And the vigil to honor the students who lost their lives in that brutal attack is set to start here in about four hours -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Christina Coleman on that for us on the ground there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Christina.\par \par \uc2 Moscow, Idaho, police say they are looking at every avenue in this investigation. What do we know so far?\par \par \uc2 The read now from former FBI Supervisory Special agent Jim Clemente.\par \par \uc2 Jim, it is hard to believe to so many, and so many of our viewers who have been following this story from day one, that there is still no suspect. And to think that that vigil will be held tonight, that that suspect, they fear, could even be there or show up.\par \par \uc2 JIM CLEMENTE, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  PROFILER: Well, Sandra, that's possible, because if this offender didn't leave town, if he was unable to leave town, then he's going to try to fit in to his normal lifestyle.\par \par \uc2 So, if he's expected to be there, he will be there, because he doesn't want to stand out. But I think there's other things that we need to have the public look for, because there was definitely pre- and post-offense behavior, that this guy must have exhibited that people -- he lives in the community. He's interacting with other humans. They must have seen this.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We're looking at the cars there that they have since removed from the house. Those were the cars of the victims.\par \par \uc2 As they close this crime scene, that is still part of it, the cars, they believe. What do you believe is ultimately going to lead them to this killer?\par \par \uc2 CLEMENTE: I believe this killer must have behaved pre-offense in a way that gave him access to that home. In other words, this was not his first foray into that home or around that home. Either he knows one or more of the victims, or he's been stalking one or more of the victims.\par \par \uc2 And that gave him the sort of confidence to overcome the fact that there are six live adult people in that -- in that residence. And that's a very high-risk crime. So he must have had the time to surveil that place, either late at night or early in the morning. He had to have the availability that night to be free to be there during the time the crime was committed.\par \par \uc2 And, post-offense, he may have been covering up wounds that he suffered on his hands afterwards. He may have showed up late for work. He may have made an excuse and left the area for a while. All these things, these changes in behaviors both before and after, those are things the public should look for.\par \par \uc2 Also, before, there was probably some stressor in his life. And that could have been loss of a relationship, loss of a job, some kind of conflict with one or more of the victims, and that made him feel emasculated, and he felt he needed to get revenge, and that's why he committed these crimes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Tapping into your expertise and background in profiling behavior of these type of people, what should people there be looking for, the people around them, if they know that somebody knew these people?\par \par \uc2 What is something that they could be looking for as this vigil