rld's largest active volcano on Hawaii's Big Island, has not happened in nearly 40 years.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden under pressure to finally fix the border from some in his own party.\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  on the hot seat today, the founder of the collapsed FTX crypto exchange about to sit down for a live interview as part of the annual New York Times DealBook Summit. No questions are off- limits. And our next guest wants answers.\par \par \uc2 Evan Luthra is a crypto investor who says he lost $2 million from the FTX collapse.\par \par \uc2 Evan, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 It is quite a remarkable moment to think that we are going to see the FTX founder sit down in this interview. This is happening a short time from now. No question is off-limits. What would you ask him?\par \par \uc2 EVAN LUTHRA, FTX INVESTOR: I would ask you, well, why did you do this, right? And what gives you the right to do this? How do you -- do you have a plan to get us our money back? Who is on your side?\par \par \uc2 Well, why are you not in jail in the first place? This interview could have also taken place behind bars. There's a lot of questions I would have for the guy there. So -- but we do know that he has an intention to defraud us. He has done a crime. He knows what he was doing. He was using -- he uses funds without permission.\par \par \uc2 I do not expect to get straight answers from an individual like that. So...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What does it tell you that he's even sitting down for this interview in the middle of this storm in the first place?\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: I think this is a big -- a big failure on the part of \uc2 New York Times\uc2  to give the gentleman like SBF a stage alongside people like \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , president of Ukraine. It's really surprising to me to see this happening in the first place.\par \par \uc2 We know what he has done. We know what actions he has taken, how he has done -- we know the crimes he has committed. He should already be behind bars .It just does not make any sense to me. And he had an opportunity to come be clean about it. He's been making cryptic tweets, typing letter by letter. It doesn't make no sense what he's doing.\par \par \uc2 So why does he get to have this stage? It kind of pisses me off to some extent.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, so, Evan, can you fill our viewers in a little bit about how you got involved in this in the first place, and now knowing that you have got $2 million at stake?\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: I got into crypto in 2014. That's when I bought my first Bitcoin. And, fundamentally, it's never been stronger to get into Bitcoin.\par \par \uc2 But one of the ways I acquired Bitcoin was through exchanges, sterilized exchanges. And FTX happened to be one of those exchanges. I started using it a couple years ago. They priced it over $12 million on the platform. And because it was a solid technology product, it was the only exchange out of -- all the top exchanges are out of China. Most of the other exchanges are from China.\par \par \uc2 This is the only one that was not from China. So I was like, OK, maybe it's good to diversify a little bit. But, I mean, the media and all the influences that were supporting it made me build more trust and break the first rule of crypto is to notch your keys, not your crypto.\par \par \uc2 And I kept it on the exchange. And then now we can't withdraw the money. So, it started with a good experience, but I believe we got played by a lot of players here. It was not what it looked -- what it seemed to be.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: At what moment did you realize that your money was gone?\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: As soon as they announced that they're going bankrupt, as soon as they announced they're going bankrupt, we found out more information, that they're using the -- they have already used the funds to buy apartments for the hundreds of millions of dollars in the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 So it already makes sense. I mean, there's no way that they could have kept this money also and then also had all these excessive money debts, spending on all these influencers and buying the stadium rights. It makes a lot of sense that they would misuse users' funds. And that is so...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And you go and look at your computer screen, and that number was zero?\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: Well, now we can't log in, so you can't even see a number anymore. It just goes to the bankruptcy page. So, there's no way to...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: No, no, no, I was asking when you we discovered that that money was gone.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: No, I mean, I could still see it on the screen, but the number means nothing, because they already spent that money.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Got it.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: So, there's over $10 billion missing that's gone somewhere, some people's pockets.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And then you tried to get that money back, and it wasn't there.\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, we really appreciate you joining us. We're out of time. We're going to be listening for the Q&A that's going to be coming up shortly, because that will be a remarkable moment to see, A, what he's asked and, B, how he answers it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LUTHRA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, protesters in China lashing out at lockdowns, that is what we do see. What -- but it's something we don't that has the Pentagon worrying.\par \par \uc2 Plus, with the border crisis escalating, is the president finally facing pressure from his own party?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The border surge not letting up. Now even Democrats are getting fed up.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Aishah Hasnie is on Capitol Hill. She's got the latest for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hi there, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Look, Senator Mark Warner just told me a few moments ago that he knows that the White House has the tools to deal with the border. And now he wants to hear directly from the administration. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): I'd like to hear what the proposals are from the White House. But, clearly, Title 42 had a lot of problems, but it was a blunt instrument, but it was relatively effective.\par \par \uc2 So the idea that you're going to go from Title 42 to nothing would be a huge mistake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, so you can hear his concern. And those concerns are mirrored by four other moderate Democratic senators, including \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  and Mark Kelly of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 They were pressuring DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas for a detailed plan of action by tomorrow. And in a letter they sent him, they wrote this: "They have expressed concern with DHS' preparations for the end of Title 42, especially as the situation has deteriorated at times. Record annual encounters have led to untenable situations," they write."\par \par \uc2 Now, Sandra, Axios is reporting the Biden administration is considering barring some asylum seekers and searching prosecutions of illegal border crossings in preparation for this policy to end. FOX News confirms that there were nearly 2,000 migrant encounters in just the El Paso Sector in the last 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 That means the fiscal year to date in that sector is up 257 percent compared to this time last year, incredible numbers there. And, on Sunday, of course, we saw video footage showing chaos near the Rio Grande River, as Venezuelan migrants there were clashing with Mexican police who were trying to clear a camp of thousands of migrants.\par \par \uc2 GOP Leader Kevin McCarthy told FOX that he personally invited 