il you get hit in the face or get punched in the face.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, at this point, yes, we need a plan. However, it is imperative that we address it and take actions to address our broken immigration system, which we have not. And, at this point, it behooves us to address it. And, unfortunately, we have not. And, thus far, we continually have these influxes.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to continue to have these influxes, by the way, because we're continuing on the same trajectory that we have for the last several decades. And guess what? We continue to see the same patterns. It's cyclical. We will have influxes a few months from today, then they're on -- but ending Title 42, without a doubt, will have deleterious consequences that I don't believe the administration is ready to handle.\par \par \uc2 And we're just going to see, unfortunately, as I mentioned, the floodgates will open.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Having experienced the border as often as you have, and having so much skin in the game, you have thought about this, I'm sure, every hour of every day, Mayor.\par \par \uc2 What do you believe is achievable bipartisan support to do something at our border, seeing what is happening?\par \par \uc2 VILLARREAL: Well, at this point, I am in favor of keeping Title 42 and remain-in-Mexico, so long as -- contingent upon Congress finally taking decisive action and addressing a broken immigration system.\par \par \uc2 Now, is that going to happen? Quite frankly, I'm not too convinced that it will. But, at this point, Title 42 is effective in managing the influxes, and so is remain-in-Mexico. However, they're not permanent solutions to our broken immigration system. And, hence, there lies the problem, because we have, yes, as the senator mentioned, it's a blunt instrument.\par \par \uc2 However, that's what we have at this time, and because of the failure of Congress to act. And neither party, by the way, has delivered comprehensive immigration reform in several