d.\par \par \uc2 On all fronts, Sandra, this is what they call unrestricted warfare. Just as I have, read what President Xi chief said in the 20th Party congress here recently. He called for rapid strengthening of their military. And he called for, of course, the demise of Taiwan as an independent entity and bring it back under them.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, we really appreciate you joining us, sir. Thank you very much, obviously, something we will be watching very closely.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Lieutenant Colonel Bob Maginnis.\par \par \uc2 MAGINNIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have much more right after this. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Go cheap or go home.\par \par \uc2 According to a new report in The Wall Street Journal, being a cheap date is now a good thing, as singles deal with price spikes.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from Kat Timpf, co-host of "Gutfeld."\par \par \uc2 Kat, I mean, I have heard a lot of stories before, but it appears the way to go now is to go cheaply because you're dating so much, right, with the online apps and everything. You don't want to spend a whole lot on one date. But is this a major plus now in the dating world?\par \par \uc2 KAT TIMPF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I take issue with the word suddenly.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I don't think that it's ever been a thing to go on a first date that's really expensive, maybe rarely, but, like, I also feel like the guy is trying to show off a little bit.\par \par \uc2 And then there's the thing that costs more than money, which is time. And you don't want to get stuck on a first date where you're at dinner, and you know, as soon as you see the person, you find them disgusting, you don't want to send another second around them. And then, if you're not disgusted enough, you have to then watch them eat. That's horrible.\par \par \uc2 So I feel like first date has always been drinks or something quick.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, it depends on where you are. But drinks can be pretty expensive.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: True, in New York, yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Think about one cocktail, one glass of wine here in New York City, and that can run a serious tab.\par \par \uc2 I want to hit this second topic with you, Kat, because it's a good one. First, the taking, now the giving, and now watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DWAYNE "THE ROCK" JOHNSON, ACTOR: When I was 14 years old, every day, I used to stop here at the 7-Eleven and steal a king-sized Snicker Bar, because I couldn't afford to buy one. That was my pre-workout.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to leave these here. If somebody looks like they're stealing Snickers, give them these, so they don't steal it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, that was superstar Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson going to a 7- Eleven in Hawaii that he used to steal Snickers bars from when he was 14 years old, and buying all the Snickers in the store was his way of making things right.\par \par \uc2 I kind of like this story, Kat. You?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes, this is why everybody likes him. This is why you don't really hear people saying that you can't stand this guy, because he's just a good guy. And that's a very sweet thing to do.\par \par \uc2 I also -- I think it's interesting that he stole the same candy every single time. I don't think I could eat the same thing every day, no matter what it was.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I have a theory on that. I think it was like the original PowerBar. It was like the original protein bar. I mean, it's got the nuts, right?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, that's caloric and could be good for a work -- I have a story. I have a really quick story.\par \par \uc2 My dad and I hiked to the bottom of the Grand Canyon and back up. I was 17 years old. We got to the bottom of the canyon, and I was handed a Snickers bar and water, and we started hiking back up.\par \par \uc2 I'm telling you, it gives you the energy you need.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: But you did it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But I did it.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Or was it just the stress?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And who doesn't like a Snickers, right, Kat?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes, if I had to do that much hiking, I would probably be filled with rage.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: So, that would be where I got my energy from.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I like to sit.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I won't say it was easy.\par \par \uc2 But kudos to Dwayne Johnson for doing that, right, making things right that many years later.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I think it's an adorable story, which is why, again, I don't ever hear anybody saying, oh, I hate that guy, which, in the age of social media, you hear people say that about almost everybody.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Hate is a strong word, we all tell our kids, right?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Kat, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 And we will see you on "Gutfeld" tonight.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you so much, everybody, for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith. You can catch me on "America Reports" at 1:00 p.m. Eastern time tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 "The Five" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221130eibu00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Twitter\uc2  vs. \uc2 Apple\uc2 ?; U.S. Advances in World Cup; China Protests; Interview With Rep. Michael McCaul (R-TX); Will Congress Avert Rail Strike?; Immigration Battle; Interview With Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\b0\par\par\uc2 Connell McShane, Charles Watson, Charlie Gasparino, Alex Hogan,  Susan Li, Adam Klotz, Gerry Baker, Chad Pergram, Aishah Hasnie, Casey  Stegall, David Spunt \par \uc2 9,119 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The border battle is heating up, as the Supreme Court takes up a pivotal case that could determine if the surge continues, and House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy tells the president today he should see that surge firsthand.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy now calling for the military to be embedded with Border Patrol to keep Americans safe.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World." We have got FOX team coverage for you top of the hour.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on the Hill, where Kevin McCarthy is demanding action, Casey Stegall on the border, where the crush of migrants shows no signs of slowing, and David Spunt on an important Supreme Court decision nearing.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Aishah.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Sandra. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the GOP leader, Kevin McCarthy, told FOX News that he did, in fact, invite the president down to go to the border with him today at the White House. And when FOX asked him what the president's response was, it wasn't a yes or no. But McCarthy said he simply dodged the request. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What was the president's response when you invited him to come to the border with you?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Well, I invited him down the border. And he said: "I'm sure you will want me to."\par \par \uc2 He asked me, what did I want to see happen on the border? And I walked through exactly what I want to see happen on the border. I want to see that the border agents were actually able to do their job and not be processing agents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, so Biden responded, I'm sure you would want me to come down.\par \par \uc2 So the invitation comes as for moderate Democratic senators are pressuring DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas for a detailed plan on what the agency plans to do once Title 42 ends on December 21.\par \par \uc2 It's coming fast. Well, the Democrats say they have got deep concerns and they have express concern with DHS' preparations for the end of Title 42, especially as the situation has deteriorated at times. "Record annual encounters have led to untenable situations," they write.\par \par \uc2 Now, at a Senate hearing earlier this month which I attended, Mayorkas told lawmakers that DHS is actually just going to stick with its same six-part plan that it unveiled back in the spring, despite record migrant encounters this year, so no new solutions from Alejandro Mayorkas at that hearing.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, he is facing, Sandra, as you know, now growing calls from the GOP on the House side, especially as the GOP gets ready to take control of the House -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Aishah Hasnie with the latest on the Hill for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to Eagle Pass, Texas, where at least 10 large groups of migrants have been spotted crossing into the United States in a 72-hour period.\par \par \uc2 Our Casey Stegall is at ground zero in all of this.\par \par \uc2 Casey, what have you been seeing today?\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, it has been an extremely busy day, an extremely busy week and a month, a year, frankly, for the people in this sector.\par \par \uc2 But, first of all, we want to tell you about a clash that actually happened a couple of days ago between some Venezuelan migrants and Mexican police. We have some video to show you of what happened when they tried to clear a camp in Juarez, which is just across from El Paso.\par \par \uc2 They tried to dismantle a makeshift migrant camp that at one point reportedly housed more than 1,000 migrants who said that they were all waiting there until Title 42 expired next month. Then they were going to cross into the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Well, chaos and anger ensued as officials cleared that site and migrants were offered bus rides to official shelters, while U.S. law enforcement monitor the situation by ground and by air. It is the same spot where weeks ago migrants did cross and assaulted American agents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VICTOR MANJARREZ, UNIVERSITY OF TEXAS AT EL PASO: If you're getting hit with people that are surrendering, right, in the local area, you have to collapse your operations to be able to deal with those in processing, which means there are going to be huge blind spots along the border that you simply can't patrol.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Meantime, agents in El Paso seizing close to 20 pounds of cocaine earlier and also three pounds of fentanyl in just the last couple of days, while agents in Nogales over the last four days seized almost two million fentanyl pills, almost 17 pounds of raw fentanyl powder, some of which was dyed purple to be manufactured into that rainbow fentanyl, which is marketed to kids.\par \par \uc2 The data shows a 435 percent increase in two years' time with the amount of that deadly drug that's being smuggled through the Southern border here -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Eagle Pass, Texas, Casey Stegall there for us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Casey.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now to the Supreme Court, where justices are expressing skepticism about the administration's migrant deportation policy.\par \par \uc2 To David Spunt at the court on what the justices are signaling -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 This was one case today, dealing with immigration. Arguments lasted about 2.5 hours, to show you how complicated this case is. And while it deals with the issue of immigration, it also deals with an underlying issue perhaps even bigger, which is simply raw power.\par \par \uc2 Now, before the justices is a 2021 \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  law put out back in 2021. It simply tells the department that -- prioritizes the removal to not only those who are considered a serious threat to public safety or cases of terrorism or espionage. There are about 11 million noncitizens in the United States as of today, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 DHS argues it just doesn't have the resources to deport them all. Texas and Louisiana are among the GOP-led states suing, arguing that the federal congressional mandatory arrest and deportation for those 11 million must happen for those in the U.S. illegally.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration says it has discretion to enforce immigration and policy as it sees fit. But it's arguing that challenging the current policy, Sandra, would actually open up the floodgates and encourage other states to sue on other similar issues.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PRELOGAR\uc2 , U.S. SOLICITOR GENERAL: Federal courts should not now be transformed into open forums for each and every policy dispute between the states and the national government on the merits.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And that was \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , the solicitor general for the United States.\par \par \uc2 This argument today comes as we continue to see record border crossings. Secretary of Homeland Security Alejandro Mayorkas has been put on notice by the incoming Republican-led House on Capitol Hill to either step down or be impeached.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): I think this is a really important case, because the Biden administration has run up the white flag when it comes to enforcing our border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And the 6-3 conservative majority of justices seemed skeptical of the government's arguments, but as this case has many different parts of this entire puzzle, we're expecting to hear something from the justices likely toward the end of the term. That will be probably mid to late June, perhaps even early July -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: David Spunt on that for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You bet.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: A lot to unpack. Let's start with Kevin McCarthy calling on the president to get down to the border and put troops there to help.\par \par \uc2 What does our next guest think of that? Lieutenant Chris Olivarez with the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 First off, what is the news that you have on this tractor trailer, a semitrailer that was found and suspected of concealing illegal migrants? What was discovered there?\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : Good afternoon, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, what we saw there is one of our criminal investigators from the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  actually located a junkyard in Laredo, Texas, where they found five illegal immigrants that were concealed inside a semitrailer. As they continued searching the area, they found 50 more illegal immigrants that were concealed inside a shipping container, seven of which were juveniles.\par \par \uc2 So if it wasn't for our criminal investigators actually locating those immigrants, who knows what might have happened. And that's a staging area where typically what they will do is, the smuggler organizations will smuggle illegal immigrants in these semitrailers, which we have seen tragedies take place, like we saw in San Antonio, and then they will move further them into the interior.\par \par \uc2 So, just a great effort and great work by our criminal investigators being out and actually locating this criminal activity that is taking place along our border.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Republican leadership in the House, Kevin McCarthy has invited the president to join him at the Southern border. This is not the first time that Republicans have called on leadership in the White House to get down there and see what's really happening with their own eyes.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it'll go?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Well, Sandra, we know for a fact that's not going to change anything, I mean, regardless if any person from the federal government comes down to visit the border.\par \par \uc2 And they should, because, technically, this is -- this should be the priority to secure our border. But, right now, they're not doing that. And they haven't acknowledged the fact that we have a crisis. But I think, in reality, the situation is, they need to implement the rule of law and actually imply consequences on these illegal immigrants that are coming across.\par \par \uc2 That would slow the flow of mass migration that we're seeing. It would stop the criminal activity. But, also, most importantly, you need to actually go after these criminal organizations and not just have a strategy or have a plan in place, but actually do something about it, have a plan of action and actually do something about it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: But I think it is important. But, again, it's not going to change anything until they have some policies in place to actually stop this mass flow of migration that we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: With no sign of the migrant surge at the border slowing anytime soon, how much is hinging on the Supreme Court decision? How much of a difference could that make?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Well, I think it's going to make a difference.\par \par \uc2 But I spoke to some of our federal agents as well, some of our people that we have -- that I have spoken with. And they have told me pretty much the federal governor right now is essentially releasing, mass-releasing immigrants into the country, and we don't know where they're at.\par \par \uc2 And these are immigrants that were not able to vet. The only way you can actually vet these immigrants is if they committed a crime, a convicted crime, in the United States. So they could have committed a crime in their home country, and we would not know about it.\par \par \uc2 And there's many that have been released, thousands that have been released into the country, and yet we do not know where they're at. So it's going to be interesting to see what plays out with the Supreme Court hearing. But, Sandra, the reality of the situation is, this situation is not going to be able to control until the federal government actually acknowledges that we have a crisis and they actually implement the policies that were working.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: This as the deadline to lift Title 42 is quickly approaching, Lieutenant.\par \par \uc2 Finally, what do you believe the impact of that will be?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Well, the impact is going to be significant.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we're already seeing daily encounters between 6,000 to 8,000 a day. Worst-case scenario, we could see double, triple the numbers on a daily occurrence. It's already tying up resources for Border Patrol agents, where we have to fill in those gaps. And just the fact that they don't have a plan in place, when Title 42 is lifted, we're going to have to fill that void with state resources, especially with our National Guard.\par \par \uc2 So, it's -- we're going to wait to see what happens, but we're preparing and we're now seeing as far as if we're going to see a mass migration or a surge on the border. But, again, it's all going to fall back on the policies, Sandra. That's what needs to be put in place right now, is policies.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Lieutenant Olivarez, you have a huge task at hand and our Border Patrol agents so busy. We appreciate you joining us right now. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We will let you get back.\par \par \uc2 And we will bring our viewers this update. It is final. The United States men's national soccer team has defeated Iran 1-0 and is advancing to the knockout round, the big star of that team right out of the gate scoring the game's only goal in the 38th minute of the first half.\par \par \uc2 The knockout stage begins this Saturday. We do not know who the United States team will yet play. But we will be watching. That's a live look there in Qatar, as, obviously, the United States will celebrate the big win.\par \par \uc2 All right, one thing leaders in Washington might be able to come together on, averting a national rail shutdown. Is Congress set to intervene? And what happens if it does not? We are on it.\par \par \uc2 And with China protests ramping up, there's growing pressure on President Biden to call on \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  to step down. Should he? The lead Republican on House Foreign Affairs, Michael McCaul, is here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We're going to head to Washington, where congressional leaders huddled with the president earlier today, a potentially crippling rail strike at the top of the to-do list.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill. He's got the latest and the details on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, the head of one rail union accused the president of not being labor-friendly, but President Biden asked Congress to intervene.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): We will act tomorrow morning, Wednesday, send the bill over to the Senate, hopefully with the biggest, strongest bipartisan vote. It is a compromise. And it is what we must do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Nearly a century ago, lawmakers deemed railroads as so important that Congress passed a law allowing it to intercede and avert strikes.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi says she has the votes to pass the bill. House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy would not say whether he'd vote for the bill. Even if the bill passes the House, it could face a Senate filibuster. There's bipartisan opposition.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Well, I think there are mixed views. And you will just have to work a little harder and talk to our members.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But do you get the sense, though, that there are people who are adamant about voting against it?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: I think some may be inclined to vote against it, and others are arguing that the -- that the economic price doing that is too great.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Members of the Squad, like \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 , oppose a bill which does not provide sick leave.\par \par \uc2 Liberals are incensed that congressional Democrats would support a plan to force union workers to return to the job.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): There's no example that I can think of outrageous corporate greed more than what is taking place with the railroad industry. It's incumbent upon Congress to do everything that it can to protect these workers, to make sure that the railroad starts treating them with the respect and the dignity that they deserve.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Firms which made chemicals will refuse to ship later this week. Water treatment plants may lack chlorine to purify H2O -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, if Congress does not get this done in time, how would a rail strike affect the economy?\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' "Wall Street Journal at Large" host Gerry Baker is here.\par \par \uc2 Gerry, what's the answer that question? How big of a hit would the economy take both here at home and globally?\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, HOST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL AT LARGE": Well, it would be a huge hit.\par \par \uc2 You heard there obviously rail is very important for the transportation of all kinds of goods, all kinds of raw materials, for things like issues -- as you just heard there from Chad Pergram, issues like the purification of water. It would be enormously damaging to the economy.\par \par \uc2 Economists were already starting to factor in a real strike into their assessments of what may happen to growth. That said, it does look very likely, again -- as you also heard from Chad and you heard there from \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , it does look very likely that Congress is going to get a -- is going to do a deal here, pass the legislation through both houses.\par \par \uc2 It does put the Democrats and President Biden in a real bind here, because it does -- as you heard from \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , it doesn't mean they're essentially letting down their union friends. The unions have been negotiating for two years here, and most of them are unhappy with the final deal that is on the table, especially because of the sick pay, a lack of sick pay provisions.\par \par \uc2 That's a classic, classic piece of Democratic, progressive, Democratic -- a Democratic measure that Democrats and that President Biden, who pronounces himself very pro-union, wants to achieve. But they're going to have to drop their union friends here if they want to actually avoid this economic disaster.\par \par \uc2 So it is -- it's a pretty difficult moment for the Democratic Party. They have got a choice between really damaging the economy or really hurting their union friends, and it does look like they're going to choose doing what they can for the economy over their union friends.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And that is a sizable chunk, the potential $2 billion hit to the economy per day.\par \par \uc2 Gerry, if you were just to -- if somebody in the street stopped and asked you what is this really about, what do you say to them? What is this really coming down to at this point?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Look, I think it's what you're -- Sandra, what you're seeing in the country more generally at the moment is, we have had these very tight labor markets for -- now for the last couple of years coming out of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 We have seen workers, even with those tight labor markets, getting sizable pay increases, but pay increases that lag inflation. So, their overall benefits are not keeping up with inflation. Their overall standard of living is not keeping up with inflation. You're seeing in many, many industries across the country and in many private companies some real pressure from unions, from workers to get better pay.\par \par \uc2 This is a -- but I think it's a -- we're at an interesting time here, because we are at exactly the point, I think, where the economy is probably about to turn down. The economy's been pretty strong over the last couple of months. But we are going to see with pressure from Federal -- from the interest rate increases by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , pressures on the housing market, financial markets generally, we're probably going to see the economy turning down.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to probably see unemployment start to rise in the next six months. So, that -- the leverage that unions have had, that workers should have had over the last couple of years to increase their pay, even when they haven't been able to increase it in real terms, that is now going to go away as the economy weakens.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: So this is a very pivotal moment for the economy. And if the unions essentially do have to back -- well, they are going to be defeated here by their own allies in the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 It's quite a significant moment in terms of the overall balance between labor and management.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Maybe you have a quick answer to this, but what does it mean for the holidays, Gerry? Because I know so many people are saying, OK, it's -- it was rough last year with the supply chain crisis.\par \par \uc2 Are people going to have a hard time getting what they need on the store shelves? A lot of the retailers we have spoken to said, no, they're -- all the stuffs already on the shelves. It's going to be the last-minute shoppers potentially that are most affected.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Because it could be the days just before Christmas that those shelves start to run empty, if this were to happen.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes. Yes, there could be disruption.\par \par \uc2 Look, December the 9th is the deadline for this deal. That's just a little over a week away.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: If, by chance, Congress doesn't manage to pass the legislation before then, then, yes, you will see some disruption. As you say, most retailers particularly have probably got most of their stocks in place by now, so they won't be adversely affected.\par \par \uc2 But the broader damage to the economy going forward, if that strike went ahead, would be significant. There would be supply disruptions. There would be disruptions of movements of things like coal to power stations. There would be significant moves, significant other utility disruptions. So it would be a real hit to the economy.\par \par \uc2 But, again, it does look like Congress is going to move to avoid it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And then economists are going to have to start looking at the retail sales that get reported and say, well, people went out shopping on the front end, fearing that they weren't going to get what they needed on the back end of the holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 A lot to take in. Thanks for going through it with us, Gerry. Thank you. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Thanks, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 Millions of Americans, meanwhile, are bracing this hour for the breakout of tornadoes in the South. We will have the latest from FOX Weather.\par \par \uc2 Then, Republican Michael McCaul on what the U.S. should be doing as China cracks down on those protesting. He will join us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Parts of the South bracing for a tornado outbreak. And with China protests ramping up, there's growing pressure on President Biden to call on \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  to step down.\par \par \uc2 The lead Republican on House Foreign Affairs, Michael McCaul, is here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Bundle up and hunker down, some major weather systems putting millions under alert across parts of the South and Northwest, nearly 3,000 flights already delayed just today.\par \par \uc2 To Adam Klotz with the latest forecast.\par \par \uc2 Adam, what are you hearing at this hour?\par \par \uc2 ADAM KLOTZ, FOX NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Well, hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 You know what? This is going to be a big storm system sweeping across the South from right now running you all the way into early tomorrow morning. And it's not just the tornado, but we do see a tornado watch now in place from Louisiana, stretching up into Mississippi, flash flooding, damaging winds, large hail all likely with this system. And it's a large area. There's a bit of a bullseye there.\par \par \uc2 But we're talking you all the way from the Gulf Coast stretching up into Central Indiana, areas where you could see some of that severe weather, but really the bullseye is largely across Mississippi, stretching just a little bit back into Louisiana and then up into Alabama, areas where you could see maybe some of the biggest tornadic activity, as much as an EF-2 tornado or higher across a large area here, again, from portions of Northwestern Alabama, stretching all the way back across into Arkansas back over into portions of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 What we're currently looking at -- and, yes, there's already some of these tornado warned storms on the ground. Everything there in that red polygon box is areas where this could be a tornado or at least a tornado-warned storm. Currently, we have begun to see these pop up. Likely only going to see more of this over the next several hours as we watch this entire system.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's not just a tornado threat. We're looking at a possibility of some very large hail from Louisiana stretching all the way through this entire region, back up in towards Memphis and even farther to the north, continuing on with that large hail. We're also looking at perhaps some very heavy rain as this system continues to make that move and, of course, damaging winds, winds up to 70 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 So even if you're not in an area where you see a tornado, winds could still be a real problem with this system. Pay attention the future track. It takes you from this evening all the way into early tomorrow morning, Sandra, very heavy rain, unfortunately. This is something we're paying attention to from now probably until at least 3:00 a.m. tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I know you will keep us updated on its movement.\par \par \uc2 Adam Klotz on all of that for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KLOTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: From the lockdowns to the crackdown, Chinese police clashing with protesters as they call for President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  to step down. Should President Biden be echoing those calls?\par \par \uc2 We will ask Republican Mike McCaul in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: With protesters in China rising up, pressure is building on President Biden to call on China's President Xi to step down.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business correspondent Susan Li has the very latest as China is beginning to lift some of those lockdown measures, Susan?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, while the White House is reiterating today the right to peacefully protest in China and support for fundamental freedoms there, you had Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  saying that that's still not enough. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: The Biden administration's statement yesterday on the Chinese people's protest was actually too tepid\par \par \uc2 But what we need are not just stronger short-term words, but stronger long term actions and strategies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LI: Now, that does include building up military, ammunition and weapons inventories for future security.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, over in China itself, we had several Chinese localities easing back on their COVID restrictions, including the city home to \uc2 Apple\uc2  supplier \uc2 Foxconn\uc2 's largest facility, Zhengzhou lifting the city's lockdown late this morning after five days of keeping residents inside.\par \par \uc2 And the tough restrictions have impacted \uc2 Apple\uc2 's production there. And that's a reason why wait times here in the U.S. are now reportedly as long as five to six weeks. China's Southern province of Guangdong, meantime, has also cut back on quarantine rules, now allowing close contacts in COVID cases to stay home instead.\par \par \uc2 And Chinese health officials are also pledging to reduce what they call inconveniences. And while protests have pressured the government to rollback on some of their COVID measures, the Chinese police have been busy limiting and identifying protesters, not only stationing a heavy police presence near known gathering sites, but also using mobile data from apps to track the movements of participants and organizers.\par \par \uc2 The Journal reporting that the police are now scanning social media accounts. And that's one of the main reasons why protesters have chosen to use a simple piece of white paper as a symbol of this movement, a blank page. It doesn't set off China's sensors and surveillance, but also a blank page that unites a common message to Beijing -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Susan, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 How should the White House be handling this situation?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Ranking Member of the House Foreign Affairs Committee Texas Congressman Michael McCaul.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What do you want to see from the White House on this? How should they be responding?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: They should be responding supporting the people of China against the oppressive Chinese Communist Party regime that is locking down their entire civilian population.\par \par \uc2 It's really fascinating to me the weak response that came out of the White House. We always support freedom and democracy over tyranny. And, in this case, communist China could not be more tyrannical.\par \par \uc2 And we see the footage of people being beaten and mothers thrown down with their children. And Reagan stood up for these people. Voice of America stood up for the people of Russia over the Soviet Union that eventually led to the collapse.\par \par \uc2 And what I see is the emperor of China, Chairman Xi, who made his case before his Congress to become the imperial president, is now an emperor without clothes. But what I mean is, the people of China, he does