But first to Georgia and both parties scrambling with the Senate run-off one week away now, early turnout shattering records. What is that signaling?\par \par \uc2 Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  is here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The Georgia Senate run-off one week away.\par \par \uc2 Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  on what he is seeing in just a moment, but first to Charles Watson both sides scrambling with just days to go.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WATSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's been a busy few days here in the Peach State, so far, more than 468,000 people casting their ballots in person during the early voting period here in Georgia. What's interesting is, more than 300,000 of those votes that were cast came on Monday alone. That shattered the previous record for single-day early vote turnout, which was set in 2018.\par \par \uc2 As you can imagine, people are keeping voting locations very busy. We have seen weights at some polling places that were as long as two hours. Now, in terms of vote share, so far, most are coming out of DeKalb, Fulton and Gwinnett counties. These are big areas in metro Atlanta, and they currently make up about a third of all votes cast.\par \par \uc2 The big question everyone's minds, though, is, who has the advantage here? Is it Warnock or is it Walker? We spoke to one expert who says that's a hard question to answer right now because of differences in when early voting actually started across the state, but she says the winning strategy will almost certainly come down to who has the best ground game and voter outreach.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDRA GILLESPIE, \uc2 EMORY UNIVERSITY\uc2 : What both campaigns need to be doing is amassing huge armies of volunteers who are actually willing to do that gumshoe work of hitting every voter personally and making sure that they turn out to vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Yes, and that's largely what we're seeing so far this week, both Warnock and Walker out on the ground trying to energize folks to not only vote, but to bring someone with them to vote.\par \par \uc2 And these two candidates haven't shied away from taking shots at each other to get the job done.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: (INAUDIBLE) The slick- talking, smooth-walking man that our father told me about, don't you trust those people.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): This is no longer about Republican and Democrat, right and left. This is right and wrong.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is crazy. It is crazy.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Yes, and it's all about ground game, Sandra. These two candidates will continue the rest of the week out on the campaign trail. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has a slate of events planned throughout the state of Georgia, as well as incoming Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , who is expected to rally with former President Obama later this week before the last day of early voting, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Great to see you, Charles Watson, on the ground there in Atlanta for us.\par \par \uc2 Charles, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, what is behind this massive turnout?\par \par \uc2 Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, sir, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 What do you believe is behind this record turnout?\par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER (R), GEORGIA SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, it's never been easier to vote in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But, obviously, voters are engaged watching this process. We have had record turnout, over 301,000 people yesterday. And that's really all over the state, Chatham County. I'm here in Tift County today in our South Georgia office. We had strong turnout here, metro Atlanta. All over the state, voters are really engaged in the process.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, so for people all around the country seeing this happened in this key race, with just days to go, what should it tell them? What is your takeaway from that turnout?\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Well, people here in Georgia know that their vote counts.\par \par \uc2 And they know this is an important race. And both sides are obviously working hard at getting their people out. Four years ago, I was actually in a run-off race myself, so I know what it's like. You're just trying to turn out your base, running hard all over the state.\par \par \uc2 So, I wish them well. I can promise you one thing. We're going to have honest and fair elections here in the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, so...\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: And that's the most important thing that people know.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Got it.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us so far about demographics with those that have shown up to vote?\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Well, if you look at the weekend, it looked like it may have favored one side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at what's happening now, it looks like it's starting to balance out on the other side. So, what we're really seeing, it's a race to the finish line. But, at the end of the day, the county election directors, they're working hard making sure that they can process all these voters that came through.\par \par \uc2 It looks like, today, we're even going to beat yesterday's number. That's how strong the vote totals look like we're going to have today. So, if it keeps up, it's going to be a very, very strong turnout election. So, we're excited about that. The counties are prepared. So are we OK.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, we saw nearly 90,000 cast ballots on Sunday. You just referenced yesterday and said today might outpace yesterday. Can you give us some hard numbers on that?\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Yesterday, we had 301,000. And looking at where we are so far today, we could very well surpass 301,000 again today.\par \par \uc2 So, this is just record territory. And we're excited, because voters are excited.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Can you give us any more information on the demographic of those votes?\par \par \uc2 You said it favored one party over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And then -- and, obviously, on the black vote as well.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Well, we look at that, but, right now, it was -- obviously, more of the urban areas had early voting locations in larger metro areas.\par \par \uc2 Now, Monday, Tuesday, all over the state has mandatory five days of early voting. All the polling locations are open. And I'm down here in Tift County. Yesterday, a friend texted me and told me how long the line was here. And people are talking about how long lines are in Chatham County, up in Cherokee County.\par \par \uc2 Voters are just engaged in this process. They understand how important this seat is. And we're making sure they have access to the ballot box, make sure they have fair and honest elections. That's what the counties are charged to do. And that's what we're doing for the counties.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Got it. OK, so you're seeing a lot of things happening there.\par \par \uc2 Let me also ask you, sir, about the timing here. Early voting goes through Friday in all 159 counties for this election. So far, you're reporting record turnout, but any other information that folks need to hear out there, as this -- as this day is quickly approaching?\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: No, people need to understand in Georgia we have photo I.D. for all forms of voting .Whether you vote absentee, it's photo I.D. Vote early, photo I.D., Election Day.\par \par \uc2 And you have a verifiable paper ballot. We actually audited a statewide race from the November race. And we are going to be asking our counties to audit this race, so a risk limit can go on of the senatorial race.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: We understand how important it is just to verify for voters, here's what the ballots said, here's what the risk limiting audit said.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: And look at the numbers. They're virtually the same. You can trust the votes here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 All right, well, we are quickly approaching that. That big day is coming up quick. I can't believe it's already -- I can't believe it's already here. It feels like we just made our way through the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, sir, for joining us. And we will see you again soon.\par \par \uc2 RAFFENSPERGER: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, tweet this. The White House catching flak after saying it we will be keeping an eye on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's social media site when it comes to misinformation.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Connell McShane is here to break it all down for us.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Connell.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Sandra. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has really spent the last couple of days going after \uc2 Apple\uc2  in a big way, the company that might matter more to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's success than the White House or anybody else when you think about it.\par \par \uc2 First, the App Store, it's seen by many as kind of a gatekeeper. \uc2 Apple\uc2  has such tight control over software distribution. And to that very point, today, Musk responded to a tweet on all the power these big tech companies have as gatekeepers. He said, it's a real problem. \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  effectively control access to most of the Internet via their App Stores.\par \par \uc2 And then there's the advertising. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  has reported that \uc2 Apple\uc2 , with its \uc2 Twitter\uc2  ad spending, is over $100 billion per year. \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  says it contributed more than 4 percent to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's revenue in the first quarter of the year. So that's kind of the context for these two \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's tweets.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Apple\uc2 ," he says, "has mostly stopped advertising on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , asking, "Do they hate free speech in America? \uc2 Apple\uc2 's also threatened to withhold \uc2 Twitter\uc2  from its App Store, but," he says, "they won't tell us why."\par \par \uc2 Now, on the first of the those two we just showed questioning whether \uc2 Apple\uc2  hates free speech in this country, we have reported this week that \uc2 Apple\uc2  is said to have restricted the use of its AirDrop feature over in China. That feature has been used in the past to get around the censors in China. So that could open \uc2 Apple\uc2  up to questions about a double standard. Those are questions not being answered right now by \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  or anyone else at \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The company has not responded to our request for comment, so we don't know for sure how much \uc2 Apple\uc2 's cut back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  ads, and we don't know its plans for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in the App Store. We do know, however, it's kicked other apps off the platform in the past because of concerns about content moderation. There is a history of this. In fact, that's what happened to Parler just a couple of years ago -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Connell McShane, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to Charlie Gasparino now from the \uc2 FOX Business Network\uc2 . He's on set with us here.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, what a treat. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: You never say that.\par \par \uc2 You're full of it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You're so full of it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It's near the end of my day, so I'm happy, happy you're here.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: It's over.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Close eye controversy. Why is this of great concern, Charlie?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, I mean, I think it's of great concern to the Biden administration, because \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is now no longer a public company. It's a private company, and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  can do what he wants with it.\par \par \uc2 And part of what he wants to do with it, quite frankly -- I don't agree with all his business moves. And, as a matter of fact, I think buying it was insane. He paid $44 billion for something that's probably worth $4 billion. So, just start off there.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You would just go to the beach if you had that kind of money, right, Charlie?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But he is a rich dude. He is a rich dude. So he can do what he wants.\par \par \uc2 But now that it's private, and it is kind of a public square, I think it's kind of -- it's a little bit -- I think that whole notion is a little bit overwrought. But it is powerful, a powerful voice of journalism, so to speak, of information flow.\par \par \uc2 The White House is concerned that it's going to start reflecting \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's political views, which...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But what did you make of the White House saying they're going to keep a close eye? Is that the role of the White House?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, they could -- this is the backdoor way into censoring \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , essentially.\par \par \uc2 The backdoor way is to use a \uc2 U.N\uc2 . agency known as \uc2 CFIUS\uc2 , the Committee for Foreign Investment in the United States, and try to use that as a backdoor mechanism to rein in \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . That is the way they probably will do this.\par \par \uc2 And that's because there are some foreign investors in \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I believe there's some Middle Eastern money, who knows, maybe Chinese money. I have no idea. But I know there's foreign investors in \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and along with Musk's majority stake. That's taken it private. And through that mechanism, they can censor it, theoretically.\par \par \uc2 They can say -- they can do what Trump tried to do with TikTok for different reasons. Remember, Trump tried to ban TikTok from the U.S. using \uc2 CFIUS\uc2 , because he thought it was a -- sort of a vehicle for the Chinese government to spy on people? They can kind of use that mechanism to go after \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and go after him.\par \par \uc2 I think, if they do that, though, it'll be such -- it's so obviously a violation of the First Amendment. I mean, it's such a cynical ploy to create free speech. But they're worried, because, listen, it's no longer a public company. It's in his hands. He can theoretically do what he wants.\par \par \uc2 He can shut it down tomorrow if he wants. But he's going to have some fun with it. And while he has some fun, you have -- have you followed his tweets about Biden lately?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very much.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very much.\par \par \uc2 And all of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's tweets, as a matter of fact.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The night side table one the other day I think was a head- scratcher.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes, with the gun. It wasn't a real gun, right? It was a fake gun.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I don't know, Charlie. Do you know?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And he had Coke -- he had \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 , not coke, OK, yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, I saw the caffeine-free Diet Coke.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: By the way, what is happening with \uc2 Twitter\uc2  then since -- since he has taken over?\par \par \uc2 He's reporting that he's getting more users, more tweets, it's more active than ever.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I mean, I think this is a tough business to run.\par \par \uc2 I think it's coming at a time when the market is correcting. He used his \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock as currency to buy this thing. It's loaded up with debt. It doesn't throw off cash flow. Put all that together, and if there was ever a company that you were saying -- going to say, predict might go bankrupt, as an LBO going -- because this is essentially a leveraged buyout -- would go back -- because, remember, he borrowed a lot, not -- didn't just use his \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock as currency.\par \par \uc2 He borrowed a lot from banks. If it wasn't for you \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , this thing would be a candidate for Chapter 11 in a second. And he's actually said that. So I think the business prospects for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  are pretty -- are pretty bad. I think he will throw everything he can at this in terms of, he will -- blue check mark, green check marks, different ways.\par \par \uc2 Maybe you can edit your tweets. He's going to try to figure out a way to improve...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The concept was just so simple, though.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But you got to make money doing it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, you got to make money. You got to make money.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And there's -- and, by the way. I don't know how you make money when so many -- I mean, you have been on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Have you seen the stuff that gets rolled at us from anonymous accounts?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, I do every day, all day.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Guys with 15 numbers after their name.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I got to wrap you.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. player who shot the only goal in today's game to beat Iran, we are being told Christian has now been taken to the hospital with an abdominal injury. This is just being reported by the AP out of Doha. I'm sure there will be more on this coming up.\par \par \uc2 Did you watch the game?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I did not watch it, but...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You were watching us. That's right. I forgot.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: ... prayers to Mr. -- prayers to the gentleman.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you very much for joining us, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to all of you. I will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221129eibt0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Air Marshals Being Assigned To Border?; Potential Rail Strike; Cyber Monday; Idaho Murder Probe; Stocks Slammed; Oil Controversy; China Protests; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  (R-LA)\b0\par\par\uc2 Casey Stegall, Brian Brenberg, Madison Alworth, Gerri Willis, Dan  Springer, Susan Li, Gillian Turner, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 8,253 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Police clashing with demonstrators in Shanghai, as massive protests break out across China over the government's zero tolerance policies and COVID lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 Some of the demonstrators demanding that President Xi must go. As a result, officials in China are easing some restrictions. It all began late last week when protests erupted at \uc2 Foxconn\uc2 , the world's largest iPhone factory. As a result, \uc2 Apple\uc2  is reportedly looking at six million fewer iPhones being produced this year, all of it sending stocks tumbling about 500 points today.\par \par \uc2 We are going to have a lot more on that story coming up, but first this: FOX on top of President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's latest oil moves putting lawmakers on edge, the administration easing some sanctions on Venezuela, while granting \uc2 Chevron\uc2  permission to resume some oil production down there.\par \par \uc2 But as Americans continue to deal with high prices at the pump, critics asking why we can't drill more here.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 Here is the reaction as Peter Doocy pressed John Kirby at the White House moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And on the sanctions relief for Venezuela...\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: ... why is it the President Biden would rather let U.S. companies drill for oil in Venezuela than here in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: That's not an accurate take of the president's view.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Earlier this month, he said, no more drilling, there is no more drilling.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The president has issued 9,000 permits for drilling on U.S. federal lands, Peter; 9,000 of them are being unused. There are plenty of opportunities for oil and gas companies to drill here in the United States. I will let \uc2 Chevron\uc2  speak for this particular issue of sanctions relief. But our expectation is, it won't be a lot of oil coming out of there.\par \par \uc2 It'll have to be shipped to the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Reaction now from Oil and Gas Association President Tim Stuart.\par \par \uc2 Tim, great to have you here today and especially to react to that from the White House, John Kirby just a moment ago saying there's 9,000 permits issued by this president that have gone unused by the oil and gas industry. Is that a missed opportunity? Is the White House getting this right?\par \par \uc2 TIM STEWART, PRESIDENT, \uc2 U.S. OIL\uc2  AND GAS ASSOCIATION: Well, Sandra, the administration views some oil as good in some oil as bad, Venezuelan heavy crude as good and Canadian crude apparently as bad.\par \par \uc2 And that's what I think we're seeing two years now some very inconsistent energy policy. To be honest with you, you wouldn't have to be lifting sanctions on Venezuela crude if you would had actually reached out to the Canadians on day one, instead of canceling their pipeline.\par \par \uc2 And so we know we can -- we can say to the administration, yes, we can produce all we can in the United States, but the reality is, is, you have got to have good ally trading partners. And I don't know why Canada is not one of those better partners vs. the Venezuelan crude.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So what is your reaction then to an administration that has not been encouraging more production here at home, has the ability to rollback regulations, and at least use rhetoric that's a little bit more friendly to the fossil fuel industry, but doesn't do that? It reaches out to Venezuela and says \uc2 Chevron\uc2  can produce more there, but not here?\par \par \uc2 STEWART: Well, again, that's been our frustration for two years now. And it has been a long two years for the oil and gas industry in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The good news is, we have -- we have -- despite the obstacles the administration has put in, the production levels are up. The rig counts are up. We have been able to work around the administration. But the reality is, they need us. And we keep telling them that. You need us and you need our U.S. production. And in doing so, that will give you a position of strength globally, and, again, so you don't need to worry about lifting sanctions in Venezuela, because you got the production members here at home.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Let me ask you something.\par \par \uc2 We have seen a dip in oil prices. They are now at the lowest price they have been this year. We're also seeing a drop in the average price of a gallon of gas -- gasoline. And we obviously need to acknowledge that that is happening.\par \par \uc2 So is that happening because we have done anything or this White House has implemented any policy that is bringing prices down? Or what do you see is the result of that?\par \par \uc2 STEWART: Well, it's a result of two things.\par \par \uc2 One, like I said, our production numbers are up, but the reality also is, is, the administration has baked in a recession that's forthcoming. I think the markets are starting to show that as well. You see what's happening in China. You see what's happening in Europe.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, is, I think the markets are beginning to price in really significantly an economic slowdown for this next year. It's good for consumers on the lower price level, but it's not good if you're worried about your job or worried about your small business.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Got it. A very strong message you're sending there from the oil and gas industry. We appreciate you joining us. Thank you very much, Tim.\par \par \uc2 STEWART: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers like Senate Energy Committee Republican \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  blasting the hypocrisy surrounding this move.\par \par \uc2 The Louisiana senator joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator, what is your reaction to the White House saying Venezuela can pump it there, but not here at home?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): OK, let's pump it in Venezuela, but let's pump it here.\par \par \uc2 Off the coast of Louisiana, there are -- there are existing rigs that you could drill off the existing rig and begin bringing oil and gas to the coast of Louisiana to be refined, lowering the price of utility bills, gasoline, helping our allies in Europe.\par \par \uc2 The administration still has not permitted those. Now, they can speak about all the permits they want. They're made -- there has to be a pipeline. There has to be an infrastructure. There has to be a way to get it to market. We can do that off the coast of Louisiana, creating American jobs. The administration chooses not to.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, a common refrain from the administration is that, to Tim's last point there, they should have allowed that oil to come down from Canada to begin with.\par \par \uc2 He was referencing the Keystone XL pipeline. And the construction was called off day one by this administration. A common refrain from the White House is, hey, well, that oil wouldn't have been used in its entirety here in the United States. It would have been sent overseas.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: I mean, who cares?\par \par \uc2 The reality is that you're going to lower gas prices in the United States, even if it is not entirely refined in the United States. By the way, the refineries in Louisiana in Texas are the most sophisticated refineries for Canadian crude. Almost all of it would have been refined here.\par \par \uc2 And if we're selling refined products around the world, that means we're helping the Europeans in their struggle against the Russian energy embargo. So, I'm not really sure what point they're making, except that they're conflicted, and that they're trying to obfuscate because they can't make the point they want to make.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Do you have national security concerns with us turning to Venezuela for help bringing down our energy prices?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: I don't really have national security concerns regarding that.\par \par \uc2 But you make the point, Sandra, that there is no kind of -- the national security, our energy security, climate policy, and our national security are all interwoven. If we want to increase the economy of our country, if we want to increase the economy of individual families, we should develop North American energy resources.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we can bring it from Venezuela. OK, that's fine. But if we want to really strengthen the pocketbook of an individual American, then why don't we produce oil and gas in our country and refine it in our country. That helps our national security, because, one, we're less subject to the whims of Russia and \uc2 OPEC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It also helps our national security because we can ship it overseas. Lastly, it helps lower emissions. It's been well-documented that refining oil and gas, say, off the coast of Louisiana is the most carbon-friendly way to do it, as opposed to importing from elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I have only got about a minute left here, sir, but can I get your response to Kirby just a moment ago at the White House, his response to Peter Doocy about these untapped leases here at home? Nine thousand permits have been issued, he said, under the Biden administration. Those are going unused. This is an opportunity for the oil and gas industry.\par \par \uc2 How do you respond to that? They have -- they have said this many times.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: It's very disingenuous.\par \par \uc2 In order to produce oil, you have got to have a pipeline that's permitted. They won't permit the pipeline. There's a whole infrastructure. The fact that the oil and gas industry is willing to do it, though, is demonstrated by, on private lands, they have basically doubled the rig count.\par \par \uc2 And so this is increasing, but it's increasing principally upon private lands, indicating, again, that, on federal lands, there's an ecosystem which they're not allowing to develop that is required if we are going to see the kind of full potential of development on federal lands.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, 10 seconds left.\par \par \uc2 Do you see oil and gas prices going up, down in the near future? Do you have any forecasts, as a senator from Louisiana?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Well, unfortunately -- well, we don't know what \uc2 OPEC\uc2  is going to do, but I see them going down principally because of demand destruction.\par \par \uc2 We're going into a recession created by the Fed's increasing interest rates in response to the Biden inflation plan. And, because of that, there will be demand destruction. That's a bad way to lower prices.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much, \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  from Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 All right, millions of Americans shopping for the holiday today, but with the threat of a rail strike looming, will you get those deliveries on time? We're on it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, protesters in China rising up after new COVID lockdowns. Why what's happening over there could have major implications for us here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: First the lockdowns, then the throw-downs. Protesters in China taking on the communist government there amid its harsh COVID policies.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Gillian Turner. She's got to latest for us on that.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 So, there are growing concerns now about the impact that these protests are having on the market as well, as the potential for disruptions to the global supply chain. These factors are fueling anxiety one of the world's largest economies could soon come offline.\par \par \uc2 The National Security Council said this just a moment ago:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: This is a -- I think a moment to reassert what we believe in when it comes to free assembly and peaceful protest. And we have done that, and we will continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 Whether it's people protesting in Iran or China or anywhere else, this is a moment for the -- for the Chinese people and the Chinese government to speak to.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: In Shanghai, the nation's largest city, there were clashes with police.\par \par \uc2 In the capital city, Beijing, protests are flaring, including peaceful student protests at one of China's most prestigious universities. All of this a first during President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's long decade -- long tenure over the last decade. It's not just a protest against some local official because he's corrupt. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GORDON CHANG, AUTHOR, "THE COMING COLLAPSE OF CHINA": It's not just a protest against some local official because he's corrupt. This is against \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and the Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 And I think that what we're going to see, eventually, let's say within a couple of years, is that the party will fail.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Tension boiled over Thursday after an apartment fire killed 10 people in a high-rise in Xinjiang province.\par \par \uc2 Some residents there had been locked down for 100 days at that point. Republican lawmakers here on Capitol Hill say the situation is barbaric.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): They're still welding Chinese people into their apartments. We have had apartment buildings burned down with people locked inside. We have toddlers being taken from their parents and put into quarantine camps.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Now, the BBC claims one of their reporters was beaten by Chinese police while covering a protest in Shanghai last night. They say they have not yet received a credible explanation from Chinese authorities.\par \par \uc2 They are allegedly claiming that they arrested that reporter to prevent him catching COVID in the crowd -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Gillian Turner live in Washington for us.\par \par \uc2 Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks, meanwhile, selling off today over China COVID worries and the economic impact of these lockdowns and protests.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk to Susan Li from FOX Business on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Susan, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, good to see you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: There was quite a market sell-off today.\par \par \uc2 LI: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Was it in part due to concerns from China?\par \par \uc2 LI: Oh, yes, absolutely, and COVID, of course, and the supply chain that Gillian just mentioned.\par \par \uc2 And we had \uc2 Apple\uc2 , of course, being the largest stock in the S&P 500 in 40 years, and they, of course, have a large assembly plant in China. And they might be impacted by six million units of the top-of-the-line iPhone 14 Pros. And for a company that's worth $2.5 trillion that's expected to sell about 90 million handsets over the holiday period, that's going to be a problem.\par \par \uc2 But I really want to talk to you about the differential between the party and the people, because, if you have ever been to China, you know that the party, yes, is communist. But the people of China are very capitalist, meaning that they want to buy their iPhones, they want to wear their \uc2 Nike\uc2  sneakers and buy their \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  latte.\par \par \uc2 And now there is an unspoken social contract in China, where, if the party, the \uc2 Communist Party of China\uc2 , keeps everyone employed and prosperous, then there are unspoken rules that the people will just -- I guess they will concentrate on buying their apartments and putting food on the table than actually going out in the streets to protest like this.\par \par \uc2 But when you're talking about three years of lockdowns, and two months of it -- you heard about the stories in Shanghai...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LI: ... where people didn't have access to food and water. I think that was too much for an economy of one billion, when they saw other economies and other countries opening up and being able to travel and have their freedoms elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Really something over the weekend, the holiday weekend here, to be watching scenes like that over there, Shanghai. That was Sunday, Susan.\par \par \uc2 There's obviously huge economic concerns, implications from these protests as they continue to emerge. You mentioned \uc2 Apple\uc2 , the impact that will have on a massive company like \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But what are you hearing are the global economic implications of all of this?\par \par \uc2 LI: Well, you know that supply chains are long and complex, and especially when you're a company the size of \uc2 Apple\uc2  trying to move 200 million handsets each and every year with virtually no inventory.\par \par \uc2 That process to change and to move, say, from China to elsewhere in the world, that's going to take a long time. But it's not just \uc2 Apple\uc2 . Think about the Nikes that get 20 percent of their sales and China or \uc2 Tesla\uc2  that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has that Shanghai factory, which is near the epicenter of the protests. Not only do they export across China through Shanghai, but they also export across Europe as well.\par \par \uc2 So there are large implications for huge multinationals. Disney Shanghai, you heard about that $5 billion park. They actually locked visitors inside until they could show a negative COVID test. So it's not just \uc2 Apple\uc2 , but I would say huge companies have a vested interest right now in China, and they're probably looking at these pictures or rethinking what they're going to do with their supply chain and production.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I mean, you look at the shutdowns, right, and then you look at the protests that are emerging in the streets there, and you have the concerns domestically here about us managing inflation.\par \par \uc2 LI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And we look over there, and that is a perfect storm for a sell-off, which we saw today to start the week, Susan.\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And the world's second largest economy, as you know, when one parts of the world sneezes or catches a cold, you know...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Exactly. Exactly. And the Fed has a huge task on their hands here at home.\par \par \uc2 Susan, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 LI: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you very much for all of that.\par \par \uc2 The probe, meanwhile, into those Idaho murders, now it's in its third week, and still few answers on who was behind it, as students return to class -- the new fear setting in.\par \par \uc2 And, later, how the crisis at the border could now be threatening safety in the skies. We will explain.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The investigation into those Idaho murders entering week three. What are we learning today?\par \par \uc2 Plus, how the border crisis could now be impacting safety in the skies? What's that about?\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: To Idaho now, where the student murder investigation is entering its third week, with no suspect named.\par \par \uc2 Students returning to campus after the holidays. Police a sense of fear is gripping the community.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Dan Springer is there for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Dan.\par \par \uc2 DAN SPRINGER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 And I'm telling you that the law enforcement has two missions right now, of course, to catch this killer, but also to make the people on this campus, the students feel safe. And I'm telling you, we're seeing a lot of students around here right now, and I'm getting the sense that a lot of them are coming back.\par \par \uc2 More on that in just a minute, but the other mission, o