ide detective Brian Foley.\par \par \uc2 Brian, what do you make of the handling of this investigation three weeks in now?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN FOLEY, FORMER HOMICIDE DETECTIVE: So, lookit, look, kids don't have enough to worry about going to school and their safety? Now they have to worry about this?\par \par \uc2 Police are really going to have to manage their communications now at this point. And they do this through three certain steps. One is to express concern. They have done that. Two is to ask for help. They have done that too.\par \par \uc2 The third thing is build confidence. And I think they have done a good job in that to this point. One, they asked for help from bigger agencies. Moscow Police Department right away decides, this is probably too big for us, and we need all the help that we can get.\par \par \uc2 If you look at that press conference last Wednesday, you saw that state trooper basically give the crime scene 101 class during his press conference. So, in the absence of case details, specific things to the investigation that they can't talk about, because those things are only known, those facts are only known to the victims, the suspects, and possibly the police, in the absence of that, he starts talking about their crime scene techniques.\par \par \uc2 Now, what that's going to do is, one, give the reporters something to nibble on, but, two, it will help the general public have confidence that law enforcement is doing everything they can to catch that killer.\par \par \uc2 And I heard your reporter say that there's a lot of police on campus. That can go both ways. That can cause fear, but it can also help people to feel safe at the same time. So it's up to them to communicate that, hey, we don't have cops out here right now because we have a threat, and we know there's something going on, but, basically, this is to make you feel safe and help the educational process get back on its feet.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I can't even imagine what it's like