out it coming up in a live report next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: To the Southern border now, where the Biden administration is reportedly moving resources away from protecting the skies to help with the surge.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Casey Stegall is in Eagle Pass, Texas, with the details on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Casey.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 A nonstop holiday weekend out here at the Southern border. That is for sure. We have personally witnessed at least 10 large groups crossing illegally, and that's just in the last 72 hours and that's just in this one spot around Eagle Pass. We're talking about over 1,000 migrants easily.\par \par \uc2 Another big group this morning, 240-plus, mostly made up of Cuban, Colombian and Dominicans. Resources now spread so thin, sources say at least 150 federal air marshals are being temporarily reassigned each month to do three-week deployments and assist agents here at the border.\par \par \uc2 DHS has brushed it off, saying it's nothing new.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, U.S. SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Every single year since 2006, I believe it is, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  has relied on the Department of Defense to augment its resources to address the challenges at the border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: But earlier this month, a \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2  flight bound for Tampa was diverted to Atlanta after a passenger had a box cutter on board, a reminder of the dangers out there.\par \par \uc2 So aviation safety experts question whether decreasing law enforcement presence on planes, especially during this busy time of year, is a smart one.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID LONDO, PRESIDENT, AIR MARSHAL NATIONAL COUNCIL: It's kind of shocking to think about that they're taking air marshals out of the sky, out of high-risk aircraft, and putting them on the border to simply do welfare checks of migrants, essentially.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: The \uc2 TSA\uc2 , or the \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2 , telling FOX in a statement that they're -- despite these deployments, they are continuing to focus on transportation security. They're doing both jobs, they contend -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you, Casey, live in Eagle Pass for us.\par \par \uc2 If the administration does not have enough resources at the border now, what happens when Title 42 ends a few weeks from now.\par \par \uc2 Let's ask National Border Patrol Council Vice President Art Del Cueto.\par \par \uc2 We see what's happening there now, Art. What about then?\par \par \uc2 ART DEL CUETO, VICE PRESIDENT, BORDER PATROL UNION: Well, look, this administration continues to rob Peter to pay Paul. They're not doing anything correctly (OFF-MIKE) and that's part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 Once Title 42 goes away (OFF-MIKE) more and more individuals that are coming in. The problem is this. They need to bring policies that are going to stop these individuals. They are going to be -- they have to detain them, bring immigration judges or assign them off to the border so they can see their cases.\par \par \uc2 But you can put as many agents from any agency you want down there, but what they're doing is releasing everyone in the United States. And that's where the problem is.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Art, make sure your microphone is pulled up a little bit closer, if you can, to make sure we can hear every word that you're saying, because it is important. Maybe raise it up a little bit on your lapel.\par \par \uc2 You look at these highly trained marshals, and they are not, Art, being asked to perform law enforcement duties down there at the border when they go do these stints. They are being asked to transport migrants to the hospital, wait for them on site, do check-in duties at DHS facilities.\par \par \uc2 This was otherwise described as basically welfare checks. Is this -- is this the best use of our highly skilled federal resources?\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: Look, that's what they have been doing with Border Patrol agents for quite some time. They remove them from one area, that you have huge numbers coming in.\par \par \uc2 They have been putting them in other areas. They been having to do transport. They been having to do Welfare checks. They have been having to do hospital watches. It's something that this administration continues to do wrong. All the while, what they want to focus on is process faster, so they can continue to release people into the United States.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We continue to see the surge come over the Southern border. We see the White House deploying these resources to address it, but still don't have it being acknowledged as the crisis that it is.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe we will get there?\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: Well, we're going to have to get there.\par \par \uc2 Look, we have been saying it for quite some time now. We're close to a breaking point or we're at a breaking point. We're beyond a breaking point at this point. This administration needs to put aside their political agenda. They need to put aside their hatred towards the last administration and start concentration -- concentrating on taking care of American citizens and the future of this country.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Art Del Cueto, thank you very much for joining us. We will keep watching all of it.\par \par \uc2 A real quick look at the Dow, as we finished the day down almost 500 points, and it was not that way to start the day. It was a gradual decline throughout the trading session, down 500 points, or 1.5 percent, concerns over what is happening in China.\par \par \uc2 That does it for today. I'm Sandra Smith. You can catch me weekdays 1:00 p.m. Eastern on "America Reports" with my colleague \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Right now, here is "THE FIVE."\par \par 