NER, FOX NEWS HOST: You will drop the hammer. Careful, because we might be calling you from the air. Arthel, it's been wonderful working with you this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 ARTHEL NEVILLE, FOX NEWS HOST: Oh, likewise, I really enjoyed it. Thank you so much for joining us. And stay tune because Anita Vogel is up next. She's in for Martha. Enjoy the rest of your Friday. Happy shopping.\par \par \uc2 ANITA VOGEL, FOX NEWS HOST: Thank you very much, Gillian and Arthel. And good afternoon, everybody. I'm Anita Vogel in for Martha MacCallum at Fox News Headquarters in New York. Right now, on THE STORY, the latest in the intensifying hunt for the murder suspect of these four University of Idaho students and what we know about a possible link to an unsolved murder in Oregon.\par \par \uc2 But first, the White House facing workers who say they are fed up as they try to avoid a railroad strike and reach a deal ahead of a December 9th deadline. And today, we see thousands of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers walking off the job in the U.S. and across the globe, demanding better wages and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We cannot tolerate the way \uc2 Amazon\uc2  operates; we must make \uc2 Amazon\uc2  pay.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Make \uc2 Amazon\uc2  pay! Make \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Pay! Make \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Pay!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Make \uc2 Amazon\uc2  pay, while all of that playing out as in inflation hovers over what's expected to be a record number of Black Friday shoppers on the hunt and hungry for the year's biggest discounts. We have Fox team coverage with Lucas Tomlinson, Garrett Tenney, and David Asman.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with Lucas Tomlinson traveling with President Biden in Nantucket. Lucas, good to see you. Suddenly, it looks like the President has a lot on his plate this holiday season with dueling looming strikes.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Anita, but that did not stop the president from doing a little shopping here in town and going out for lunch. Now, as you mentioned, there are looming threats not only from the rails, but also \uc2 Amazon\uc2  where \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers are protesting in over 40 countries including right here in the United States. Thousands walking out on the job today setting low pay poor working conditions.\par \par \uc2 President Biden appearing to tweet about the matter saying, "supporting working people is how we continue to build the economy from the bottom up and middle out. I will also continue fighting for the PRO Act to help workers unionize and achieve a $15.00 minimum wage."\par \par \uc2 When it comes to the looming railroad strike, President Biden says he isn't ready to roll up his sleeves and help quite just yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can you talk about what the holdup is, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I can't because it's familiar negotiations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Have you been in touch to both parties? Again --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My team has been in touch with all parties and rooms with the parties. And I have, I have not directly engaged yet because they're still talking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: But just a few days ago, Biden's press secretary said the exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The President is, indeed, involved directly. But I don't want to get into details at this time. But he has been involved. I just said the President has been directly involved. He's been in touch. This is a third time I'm saying he's been directly involved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Back in September, weeks before the midterm elections, the Biden administration took credit for avoiding a rail strike, Anita, but that deal is now in danger. Both sides have until early December to come to an agreement, Congressman Mike Waltz spoke earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ (R-FL): She's trying to distance the president because this deal with the railroad unions is literally about to come off the rails.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ECONOMIC ADVISER: You think things are expensive now, Julie? Wait until we have a rail strike where you can't get the goods and services. You're going to have to empty shelves in a lot of the stores.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The White House says it's not coming directly on the strikes at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  today and said the President's tweets speak for himself, Anita.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: OK. Let's hope that they both can avoid a strike, it would be the worst thing, especially at the holidays. Lucas Tomlinson. Thank you so much for that live report. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to correspondent Garrett Tenney live along the magnificent mile in Chicago where shoppers already reeling from high prices are still looking for deals this Black Friday. How're you doing there, Garrett?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Anita, a record number of folks are expected to do some shopping over the next couple of days to try and take advantage of some of the deep discounts that are out there. \uc2 The National Retail Federation\uc2  is predicting 166 million Americans will make a purchase between now and Cyber Monday. And retail spending overall is expected to go up from last year and not from folks buying more for the holidays though, but from the higher prices caused by inflation. So, folks are spending more and coming home with less. The good news is that major retailers have a lot of excess inventory they are trying to get rid of. A lot of sales have been going on for weeks and shoppers out there today are finding some deals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Like it's a very opportune way to get what you need for a fairly good price.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Honestly, I just like getting things for cheap. My little brother, he seemed to like gaming and everything, so I'm probably going to get him a gaming computer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's better than online shopping just because they can touch it, feel it, and they can take it home and not the stress of shipping on top of other fees that they need.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voiceover): So, when are you going to see the best deals? Well, according to Adobe Analytics, TVs will have their biggest markdowns today, toys will be on Saturday, clothes and sporting goods on Sunday. Cyber Monday, we'll have the biggest cuts on computers and furniture. And December 1st, we'll have the top discounts on appliances.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: In this economic climate, it is all about stretching the dollar and finding ways to do that, and that is exactly what folks are doing now looking ahead to the holidays. Anita.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes, Garret, it looks pretty busy there behind you. It looks there are quite a few people out shopping.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Yes, a lot of window shopping. We haven't seen the same bags that you may have seen a few years ago, the large ones where people are walking out with his giant haul. But a lot of folks are coming out this afternoon, and it's interesting to see the changes from just a couple of years ago.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes, good to see people out on the streets. Garrett Tenney, thank you so much live in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's bring in Fox Business Anchor, David Asman, to talk a little bit more about this. David, it's so great to see you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: Great to see you too here in New York. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: By the way, I should mention it's 50 degrees and sunny in Chicago. Very unusually nice weather for Chicago around this time. So, that might have something to do with all those people coming out.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Good shopping weather.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: OK. So, it sounds like we may have a perfect storm though heading into the holidays. We talked about those \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers going on, not going on strike, but protesting for better wages, better conditions. And now, you know, there's a looming railroad strike. I mean, how much more can the economy stand?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: That's a good question. The rail strike is the most ominous. The \uc2 Amazon\uc2  thing is happening all around the world, there are little bits, bits and pieces of strikes here and there, but nothing, nothing too overwhelming for that company. But everything we get comes in rail. I mean rail and, and these big trucks are the thing that's, that's moving stuff into our towns from wherever it's manufactured. And on both fronts, they're dealing with crises: one is diesel fuel, and the other is the possibility of a rail strike.\par \par \uc2 Rail strike, though, also carries a lot of oil. Remember that they, this administration shut down a lot of those gas pipelines in the oil pipelines. And they said, oh, well, that doesn't have anything to do with oil production, or? Well, it certainly does. And a lot of the oil that we were moving through pipelines is now moving through rail cars, it's actually more dangerous to move through rail than then through pipelines. But regardless of that, the point is, if there's a shutdown in rail, it's going to affect a lot of heating oil that people desperately need in colder climates now.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes, and going into the winter season.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes,\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: It's the worst time for this. Going back to, you know, holiday shopping --\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: -- and the economy in general, holiday shopping is supposed to be up in physical stores, 18 percent.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Well, again, Garrett pointed this out very correctly, that you have to be careful about the way the numbers are used. People who say that retail sales are up very often are including inflation in that. That is to say the unit sales, the number of actual units that are being sold, may be down. But when you factor in inflation, the actual cost of retail sales has increased. So, you have to factor all those things in there.\par \par \uc2 And people are people are looking at bargains and there's by the way, there's a toy glut, if you have kids, or I have a new grandson. And I'm happy that there's a toy glut because they're going to be some terrific bargains in buying toys. But other than that people are really nervous about what's going to happen in the future. They're worried about a recession in 2023. You even had \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , by the way, warning people not to buy stuff.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Well, I was just about to get to him. And they said online sales are going to be up about only three percent this year, and he had some interesting things to say. This is the founder of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , let's listen to him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFF BEZOS\uc2 , FOUNDER, AMAZON: Economy does not look great right now. Things are slowing down and you're seeing layoffs in many, many sectors of the economy. The probabilities say, if we're not in a recession right now, we're likely to be in one very soon. We just have to try and be reasonable about it. Take as much risk off the table as you can for yourself. You know, hope for the best but prepare for the worst.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: OK, wow. That's a little unusual coming from him. Shouldn't he be promoting shopping?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Exactly, that's his business that he's talking about and a lot of retailers were upset at what he was saying because you don't want -- I mean, they don't want retailers to delay buying decisions. But on the other hand, pretty good advice. I have to admit, I mean, if, if you're thinking of going in the hold to buy certain things. I mean, big ticket items like a car or even, even some refrigerators will put you back quite a, quite a bit these days and many people are buying them on credit. It's a dangerous moment.\par \par \uc2 First of all, interest rates are very high. Your credit will be, you'll be paying a lot of money in credit. Secondly, the fact is, is that you got to in, when a recession is coming, you got to collect your nuts before the winter is here. And we could have, we could have a recessionary year in 2023. Most economists are now saying that we are going to have a recession, which means all that, that those extra jobs that we were very happy about and they kind of buffered us from the effects of the economy. Those could go away pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Well, speaking of economists, a very renowned economist had something really interesting to say. I want to put this full screen up on the screen right now. This is \uc2 Mohamed El-Erian\uc2 , noted economist, everybody knows him. He says, "The world isn't just teetering on the brink of another recession. It is in the midst of a profound economic and financial shift." David, I got about 30 seconds left, but what do you think he means by that?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Well, he is, he's all -- we should mention, he's on the pessimistic side of that continuum of views of economists. But I think he's right about one thing: politicians and globalists with everything had been going in the wrong direction for too long. They've been emphasizing deficit spending, spending more money than governments have, which leads to higher deficits, which leads to higher inflation. And now, which leads to higher interest rates as they're trying to kill inflation by raising interest rates too high.\par \par \uc2 You just have to provide more incentives for the private sector, take less money and fuel out of the private sector. Stop doing that and give the incentives. But unfortunately, we're not hearing a lot of talk about tax cuts. In fact, the, the Prime Minister of England just lost her job, because she recommended cutting taxes. She was right. The economists who say, oh, no, no, you can't do that with everything that's going on. They, they I don't think do have it right. You need more incentives for the private sector, less fuel for bigger government.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: And more interest rate hikes coming in this country, right?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes, I think that's going to happen, I'm afraid. And that means the chances of recession are greater.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Oh boy, all right. Well, David Asman, happy holidays.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Happy Holidays! There are great bargains out there. Go out and shop for them.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: You too, my friend.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Good to see you. All right. Coming up next, what police in Idaho are saying about a possible stalker linked to one of the four college kids killed in their off-campus house. And the murder in another state that investigators are looking into for clues, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, this is such a tragic circumstance that we find ourselves in. In law enforcement, we believe we owe this to the surviving families to get this right.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: And welcome back. The manhunt for the killer of four college students in Moscow, Idaho intensifies. As questions swirl around a possible link to an unsolved stabbing in Oregon.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our investigators looking at any possible connection to an unsolved double stabbing in the Salem, Oregon area about a year and a half ago. Specifically, two people stabbed in their home in the middle of the night. Long knife, no suspect at this point. One died, one survived.\par \par \uc2 JAMES FRY, CHIEF, MOSCOW POLICE DEPARTMENT: I think -- I think, we're looking at every avenue, and we have other agencies reaching out to us with other cases and stuff that we are doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: All right. Let's bring in former homicide detective and Fox News contributor Ted Williams, just back from Moscow, Idaho and attorney Brian Claypool.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you both for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 Ted, let me start with you because you were actually on the ground in Moscow for several days. And so, you have a real sense of what investigators are doing there. One of which it seems, is trying to rule out theories.\par \par \uc2 So, now, there is this revelation of another stabbing of a couple in Salem, Oregon, not too far away from a couple of years ago. One person survived, and police, as you heard said they're looking into that.\par \par \uc2 But I want you to listen to police about the notion that one of the students Kaylee had a stalker. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRY: We have heard mentioned that Kaylee stated she may have had a stalker. Detectives have been looking into that, and to this point, have been unable to corroborate the statement, although we continue to seek information and tips regarding that report.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: So, Ted, some of the students on campus were talking about that. They've been unable to corroborate it, as you heard. So, do you think they're ruling out that theory altogether?\par \par \uc2 TED WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I don't think they are ruling out any of the theories, Anita. I think, they are following up on any and all leads off. They're following up on Oregon lead. I'm sure they are still checking out the information concerning the stalker.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that they've said very clearly at the early on in this investigation is that the perpetrator or perpetrators had a target.\par \par \uc2 It has come out as late as this afternoon that authorities lately I've told one of the fathers that one of the individuals was being stalked there.\par \par \uc2 So, if they -- well, I stand to be corrected, not be stalked, but targeted. And so, as a result of that, they know a great deal more than they are letting on to the public.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that sooner or later, but very soon that they will be able to resolve this by forensic and information that the public will be able to give them.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Wow. OK. Well, I guess that's good news that they -- you think that they might be able to make some discovery soon.\par \par \uc2 Brian, let me ask you. We are hearing today about another murder back from 1969, more than 50 years ago. Another University of Idaho student who was murdered while working in a diner nearby.\par \par \uc2 Here's a quote from the Lewiston Tribune. This is a newspaper that's not too far away that cover Moscow, Idaho.\par \par \uc2 They say: "Young Waitress' Killer Never Found: -- during Sunday evening of December 28th, after all customers had left an 18-year-old waitress Janice Lyn Foiles was preparing to close, a yet-to-be explained tragedy struck."\par \par \uc2 Foiles, a Moscow High School graduate and freshman at the University of Idaho, was found bludgeoned to death behind the cafe counter."\par \par \uc2 Now, Brian, this was a long time ago. But as a lawyer, do you rule this out? Or do you need to follow up and find out what happened with that case? No one was ever arrested.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CLAYPOOL, OWNER, CLAYPOOL LAW FIRM: Hey, Anita. Great to be back with you, and happy Thanksgiving weekend.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: Look, these other murders that you're talking about years ago, I think are a distraction.\par \par \uc2 No offence to those murders. But look, like Ted said, this was personal. This heinous murder of four girls was highly personal.\par \par \uc2 So, I doubt that this relates to a murderer five years ago, 10 years ago or 30 years ago? I think there are two things that are concerning the community in our nation.\par \par \uc2 A, the lack of transparency in the investigation. And B, do we have enough resources to investigate this? Right, Anita?\par \par \uc2 I've heard there have been over 70 or 80 interviews. I've heard there have been over 700 tips. Does this local police department have the resources to process all the tips?\par \par \uc2 Where is the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? Are they involved? What are they investigating? What is the medical forensics tell you?\par \par \uc2 For example, there was a report that there were defensive wounds. That's crucial, Anita, why? Because there's likely skin tissue under the nails of one or more of the victims. That's important because you can take that tissue, put it in a DNA database nationwide, potentially, this killer might be in that database, and you've got a match.\par \par \uc2 What about the phone records of the roommates? Have those been examined? I find it odd that a roommate calls, eight hours later says, oh, hi, I think we have an unconscious body. When they be hysterical? When they be screaming?\par \par \uc2 Was there a blood spatter on any of the roommates clothing? Have those clothes been examined? Those things need to be analyzed, Anita.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: And the community, more importantly, needs to hear about that, so, they can have an assurance that the police are really digging in deep, and that they can feel some sense of security.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Well, will they do need to know about it? Classes resumed on Monday. We do know the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is involved. So, hopefully we get some of these questions answered soon.\par \par \uc2 Ted seems to think that we will. We will follow this story and we will see. But gentlemen, got to leave it there. Thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 All right. Well, coming up next, why \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says Americans deserve to know the truth about \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's censorship of the Hunter Biden laptop story?\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , facing the mothers of soldiers serving in his war on Ukraine. What he told them, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Well, Iran's World Cup soccer team, singing a muted rendition of their country's national anthem today, after reportedly being threatened with retribution for insulting the country when they stood silent on Monday in support of protesters back home.\par \par \uc2 Iran has seen scores of demonstrations over the death of 22-year-old Mahsa Amini, who died in police custody after being arrested for reportedly wearing her headscarf the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 Those powerful protests once again spilling into the stands. One woman today seen with dark red tears painted onto her eyes. Look at that. Holding a soccer jersey with Mahsa Amini 22, printed on the back. So brave.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's war on Ukraine has killed or wounded 10s of 1000s of soldiers on both sides. And today, in an effort to fuel his propaganda war machine, Putin met with the mothers of Russian troops, some of whom, died in Ukraine, giving them this message.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): But I want you to know that, I, personally, the entire leadership of the country, we share your pain.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: In a moment, Dan Hoffman, a former CIA station chief who served in the former Soviet Union. But first, let's go to senior foreign affairs correspondent Amy Kellogg with a very latest on this.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Amy.\par \par \uc2 AMY KELLOGG, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIR CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Anita.\par \par \uc2 Well, the council of mothers and wives of soldiers, which is an activist group that was not invited to the table today said that those who were, were carefully curated by the Kremlin, "pocket mothers", and independent Russian media even identified some of them is having links to the government.\par \par \uc2 Now, this group appeared to be relatively passive, as Putin apparently attempted to lend some words of comfort, saying things like 30,000 die of alcoholism in Russia each year, implying that dying on the front is more honorable than that.\par \par \uc2 And he said we will all die one day, the important thing is how one lived.\par \par \uc2 He laid on sympathy, particularly for those who have lost loved ones in this war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: We understand that nothing can replace the loss of a son, a child, especially for a mother.\par \par \uc2 I want you to know we share this pain. And of course, we will be doing everything so that you don't feel forgotten.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: It's been said that women and quite a few took to the streets in protest after mobilization was announced this fall could ultimately prove to be real trouble for the government, if their sons, boyfriends, or husbands die.\par \par \uc2 The majority, however, so far have not questioned the war. So much is questioning why their sons have been sent unprepared to it or without proper food or equipment.\par \par \uc2 Now, Anita, this activist group confronted Putin, laid out a challenge to him on social media earlier this week, saying, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , are you a man or what? Or are you going to hide? And apparently, they were left out of the event today. They say they were blown off. And in fact, the only thing that really happened was they were trailed by security agents when they came to Moscow, labeled as extremists, and accused of being agents of the West. Anita.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Not surprising. Amy Kellogg, thank you so much for that live report in London. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right. Let's bring in now Dan Hoffman, a former CIA station chief who served in the former Soviet Union, and he's also a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dan, great to see you today. And I'm interested to get your insight on this. So the war is now in its ninth month. It is brutal, it is deadly.\par \par \uc2 And today, we saw Putin meeting with more than a dozen mothers of soldiers fighting in Ukraine, to tell them as we heard, he shares their pain. You saw them sitting around a table with tea and cakes, it all looked very calm. But how authentic do you think those conversations were? And what does this tell you, as you see this scene about independent media reports, reporting protests across the country growing.\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: That scene, I watched that in Russian and, you know, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  put on a classic Kremlin, Potemkin Village public relations stunt there. We didn't hear from any of those women, those wives and mothers who have lost their sons and lost their husbands in a war, a brutal war that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has taken to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And you think about the 100,000 casualties on each side, how Russia has reigned downhill on Ukrainian civilians. And now the war is coming home to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , his own people are not happy with the fact that they've spilled so much Russian blood and treasure. Let alone the fact that Ukraine, I mean, this is a battlefield in Ukraine, the likes of which we haven't seen humanitarian catastrophe, we haven't seen since since World War II.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: And Putin is clearly feeling a lot of pressure from his own constituents, his own military. And I would emphasize too, just from looking back at the history, the Chechen war, for example, the mothers and the wives of soldiers have traditionally played an important role on the home front, bringing some criticism to the government over the way that their sons and their and their husbands are treated with poor logistics and insufficient supplies and training, and the sort.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes, that is such a good point. And it would have been so interesting to hear from some of those women today. You know, Dan, the latest round of Russian bombing has left large parts of Ukraine, as you know, including the capital city Kyiv without water and power, right as winter is beginning.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, there's a lot of talk about how much aid the U.S. has sent to Ukraine. I want to hear from President Biden now on that topic. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I just had another drawdown. I signed another drawdown of over $300 million. And there's no time -- this is no time to walk away from Ukraine. Not at all.\par \par \uc2 Now, we had a lot of talk in this last election about whether the other team is going to continue to support Ukraine, and I still believe there's enough support there that we can continue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Dan, real quick, we're running out of time. But I want to know, given the perilous conditions that Ukraine is facing now, how much longer can they continue to put up the good fight?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: I think history has shown that, you know, attacking army can take a shot at civilian population. We saw that with the Nazis in London, in the Second World War, and the fight is going to go on. Ukraine is going to take this fight to the finish. I just wished the Biden administration would do more to shorten the war by giving Ukraine the long-range artillery and drones and air defense that they need.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes. Well, we're going to continue to hear that debate in Congress moving forward. For now, we thank you for your insight today. Thanks for coming on, Dan Hoffman.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: All right. Moving on, what the founder of FTX has planned for next week, and the lawmakers on Capitol Hill now calling for an investigation into the collapse of his multibillion-dollar crypto exchange, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  dropping a hand that he might reveal how \uc2 Twitter\uc2  handled the New York Post story about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's laptop. Responding to his suggestion that he released all internal discussions about the report. Musk tweeted, quote, "This is necessary to restore public trust." Musk has already called \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's handling of the story, quote, incredibly inappropriate.\par \par \uc2 The site locked the posts account and blocked users from sharing the report saying it violated its policy on posting hacked materials because it couldn't verify the information actually came from \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's laptop. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  changed that policy the next day, saying it would only remove information shared by actual hackers.\par \par \uc2 The former crypto CEO who may have cost his customers billions of dollars will finally face questions about the FTX crash. \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  confirms he will still do an interview at a financial conference hosted by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  next week. And the host says no questions will be off limits. Imagine that.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Edward Lawrence reporting from Washington.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: So, Anita, right now, investigators are piecing together the tangled web of money transactions across at least 107 different entities under the FTX umbrella. At the top of the pile, \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2 . Now as the onion is peeled back, there are similarities between what's happening with FTX now and what happened in the \uc2 Bernie Madoff\uc2  case. One person controlling about everything, no internal controls, and they created stories about successes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRUCE DUBINSKY, FORENSIC ACCOUNTANT: Using a small audit firm, why was a company with billions of dollars of investor money using a small unknown audit firm. Madoff used an audit firm that was in a shopping center, a small -- two-man shop. Those are huge red flags both to investors and to venture capitalist putting money into these ventures.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Forensic Accounting Bruce Dubinsky worked on the Madoff case. He's not working on the FTX case, but he went through court documents. He says it could be six to eight months before accountants know where all the money went because in some cases, no paper trail exists. Then the work starts to get the 1 million creditors back their money.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUBINSKY: But I think a lot of people are not going to get their money back. In Madoff, you had people that were elderly that were waiting to get money back from the trustee that passed away sadly. Here, you have a younger base of investors so the time horizon is greater but I think a lot of people are never going to see their money back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  is still saying he will speak at a summit next Wednesday and no questions are off limits. Anita?\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: I love to be in the audience there. Edward Lawrence in Washington for us. Thank you, Edward.\par \par \uc2 Well, what President Biden is saying about his health as the 80-year-old commander in chief considers a bid for reelection. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When will you get your annual physical, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When will you get your annual physical, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: When do you think I need it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You just had a birthday.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I've gotten my -- I will get -- part of my physical is already done, and I'll be getting it before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Well, President Biden joking about his annual physical with reporters less than a week after his 80th birthday, making him the only serving commander in chief to hit that milestone. The President's last physical was a year ago when his doctor declared him fit for duty and able to fully execute all of his responsibilities without any exemptions or accommodations.\par \par \uc2 So with that, let's bring in our panel, Gianno Caldwell, founder of Caldwell Strategic Consulting, and Doug Schoen, former Adviser to President Clinton. Gentlemen, great to see you both today. Thanks for coming in on this time (ph) --\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER ADVISER TO PRESIDENT CLINTON: Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 GIANNO CALDWELL, FOUNDER OF CALDWELL STRATEGIC CONSULTING: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: -- after Thanksgiving Friday. So we've all seen the President stumble during speeches, loses plays, he appears lost at times. What do you think the public needs to hear about him being fully able to execute all of his responsibilities? Is that reassuring for the public? And Gianno, let me go to you first.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: You know, I don't think he should be joking about this issue. I know 64 percent of Americans are concerned about his mental health, only 36 of -- percent of Americans approve of the job he's doing and only 41 percent of Democrats want to see him run again in 2024.\par \par \uc2 He is somebody who people question oftentimes, if you see him turn around and shake a hand of somebody who doesn't, it was not even near him in the missteps that he's made. For him to make a joke about it, he comes off as Bozo Joe, not the commander in chief in which Americans across the country would want to see run in 2024, or leaders across the world with respect. So I think that's pretty disappointing to see that come out of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: All right. Doug, what do you think about the President sort of making a light-hearted response there to reporter saying, yes, I'm going to get my physical next year, what did you think about it?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes. I'm less concerned with him joking about it, then him regularly releasing his medical records as virtually every president, I know, in my experience, has done. And I think if he releases his medical records, and if he's certified healthy, given the results of the last midterm election, he plans to run and probably at this point, will run.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes. Well, you know, all presidents get checked by a physician and the public --\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: -- gets to see those results because he's the most powerful leader in the world. He has access to important nuclear codes and so forth. Let's take a listen to the Press Secretary about the President's physicals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The President is in good health and maintains an active lifestyle that is from the doctor. He shared that with me. He will have a physical in the upcoming months and the results will be released in the same way that it was last year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: OK, so the President will have his physical and the results will be released. I want to go back to those polling numbers for just a minute. Let's put up this new Marist poll, finding that 54 percent of Democrats or Democrat leaning independents think someone else is the best chance for Democrats to win the White House in 2024. Doug, you know, you've been polling for years when you see those poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I know.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN Well, I think the question is who that someone else is? Right now, from the polling I've seen, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  leads the prospective field and having worked for an incumbent President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , I can tell you, it's very difficult to deny an incumbent the nomination, if he seeks it, as again, Biden appears likely to do. And at this point, he's leading \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the polls, and virtually even within the margin of error with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . So if his health is good, I think his poll numbers are just fine now.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: OK. Last word very quickly to Gianno, do you think President Biden does run again?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: I think he does. And as a Republican, I really hope that he does, because I think he will be the easiest person for Democrats to beat.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Well --\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: I mean, for Republicans to beat, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: We'll see. He proved to be pretty tough last time, so we'll see what happens. But gentlemen, thank you both so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: I appreciate. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: All right. The U.S. county with nearly 10 million people suggesting everyone go back to wearing a mask indoors. Where is it? We will tell you coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , BIDEN CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: I know sometimes when you walk in and you have a mask and nobody has a mask, you kind of feel guilty. You shouldn't feel guilty. You look terrific.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Welcome back. All right, get this folks. Los Angeles County is strongly recommending a return to masking an indoor public areas as COVID cases are on the rise on top of rapid RSV and flu infections. Dr. Janette Nesheiwat is standing by for us. But first, to Senior Correspondent Steve Harrigan with the latest on this. Hi, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Anita, this move raising a lot of alarm bells for many people, not requiring not a mask mandate but strongly encouraging people to wear masks again in Los Angeles County. And the move comes after a significant increase in local COVID infections in the area up more than 50 percent since the start of November.\par \par \uc2 And as you mentioned, it's not just COVID, the nation is dealing with it's also the flu and RSV. So many medical professionals are saying for certain groups of people, it may be time to put the face mask back on.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. WILLIAM SCHAFFNER, VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY: No one does want to be the dreaded spreader. And yes, I think with this surge in viral activity, which is true for RSV and influenza and the anticipated increase that we have in COVID prudence and being careful rather than care free, I think, would benefit everyone out there. And dust off that mask, you know? They're very easy to use and they really do offer another layer of protection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: This month, the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  commissioned a report that made that very recommendation that people should wear masks again, in public places. But keep clear, this is not a call for a mandate to wear masks and health officials once again are stressing there's a big difference between those two options.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. DEVI NAMPIAPARAMPIL, \uc2 NYU SCHOOL OF MEDICINE\uc2 : Mask is a very polarizing way to go given everything that we've been through over the past couple of years. But a mask mandate is a different type of thing. You know, mandate is different than voluntarily wearing a mask.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Rate of infection in the U.S. is right now about 40,000 cases day. That's reported cases. That is up about 7 percent over the past two weeks. Anita, back to you.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: OK, the doctor said dust off your masks. Steve Harrigan, thank you so much for that live report. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Medical Contributor and BCBoost.com founder Dr. Janette Nesheiwat. Dr. Nesheiwat, always good to see you and looking forward to hearing from you on this. Here we are heading into the winter months, COVID is on the rise and certain cities like Los Angeles are recommending wearing masks again. But I want to bring up this full screen before I hear from you on this.\par \par \uc2 This is COVID deaths in August of this year. I want to show you that 58 percent of people who were vaccinated and, in some cases also, boosted. Those are the people that passed away. So what does this say, if anything, about the need for masks?\par \par \uc2 DR. JANETTE NESHEIWAT, FOX NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, two things. First of all, we have to understand that the majority of Americans are vaccinated. So it makes sense that those who are dying or passing from it are vaccinated. We have to also remember the vaccine protection, it doesn't last forever.\par \par \uc2 So whether you have natural immunity, or even if you've been vaccinated and have your booster, you will have some protection, but that protection is not long lasting, it's short lived. And that's why, you know, the CDC, the health departments are recommending for those who are high risk to get your boosters because it can protect you. But again, it's short lived.\par \par \uc2 And let's not forget who's that high risk, those who are at high risk are those who have the underlying medical conditions, the seniors, the elderly. But, you know, when it comes to what they're doing in LA, you know, the health department, it's their job, it's their duty to monitor and assess, to look for what the prevalence of diseases in their community and it makes sense for them to make these recommendations. But we want to keep it at that, recommendations and keep it optional for all.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Right, and a lot of doctors including you I've heard say the N95 masks offer more protection than the regular cloth one. So that's an interesting point there. Also --\par \par \uc2 NESHEIWAT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: I want to take a look at what's going on in China right now. COVID on the rise there in that country, locking down again. This is a headline from the Wall Street Journal from yesterday. It says, "The larger lesson of China's COVID reckoning is that lockdowns don't work and authoritarian regimes aren't models of public health or anything else that too many Americans imagine them to be."\par \par \uc2 You know, many believe lockdowns didn't work in this country. So, hopefully, we're not going back towards that.\par \par \uc2 NESHEIWAT: Oh, yes, absolutely not. We know and we've learned locked down, these shutdowns, this social distancing, wearing the flimsy surgical or cloth mask, they are not affected. The key here is understanding and knowing your risk and taking the steps to protect yourself. We know what works. We know what doesn't work.\par \par \uc2 Anita, I have taken care of thousands of COVID patients on the frontlines these past three years of the pandemic, so I can tell you I know firsthand what works. And it's all about focusing on those who are at high risk again. You know, when I had patients who were hospitalized, or who lost their life from COVID, it wasn't your 19-year-old college student, it was - - I had patients that had heart disease, atrial fibrillation, patients who are obese and struggling to breathe or patients who were undergoing chemotherapy, for example, for, you know, colon cancer or breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 Those are the folks that we need to focus on. The lockdowns, the shutdowns, they do not work. They're ineffective and they actually cause more harm than good.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Yes. Well, Dr. Nesheiwat, thank you so much for coming on today. It's always great to see you and be safe this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 NESHEIWAT: Thank you. You too. Happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: You too. All right, breaking tonight, the World Cup game. I know you want to know this, between the U.S. Men's National Team and England just ending zero to zero. That means the U.S. controls its own destiny and has to beat Iran on Tuesday to get to the knockout rounds. Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 That is the story of Friday, November 25, the day after Thanksgiving. I hope everyone had a wonderful Thanksgiving, lots of turkey, lots of pie and lots of good food. But as always, THE STORY goes on here. Martha is going to be back on Monday, and I'm going to be back very soon.\par \par \uc2 She'll be back Monday at 3:00. "YOUR WORLD" starts right now. Have a great holiday weekend, everyone. Stay safe.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221126eibp00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Holiday Travel Forecast; Court to Rule on Mar-a-Lago Special Master; Interview With Rep. \uc2 Debbie Lesko\uc2  (R-AZ); Border Crisis; Idaho Murder Mystery; U.S. Remains Alive in World Cup; Potential Rail Strike; Black Friday Arrives\b0\par\par\uc2 Grady Trimble, Rick Reichmuth, Jeff Flock, David Spunt, Christina  Coleman, Dan Springer, Alex Hogan, Lucas Tomlinson, Garrett Tenney, Charles  Payne \par \uc2 8,143 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You're looking live at the Mall of America in Bloomington, Minnesota, as we kick off the holiday shopping season, with FOX on top of retailers under siege from shoppers looking for deeper discounts to workers looking for a better pay. And, oh, yes, potential crippling rail strike could be just two weeks away.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First to Garrett Tenney in Chicago on how this shopping day is shaping up and how some \uc2 Amazon\uc2  protests are threatening to slow it all down -- Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Charles, spending more well buying less is, sadly, going to be the new reality for a lot of Americans this holiday season as they grapple with higher prices due to inflation.\par \par \uc2 And with pocketbooks already being stretched for essentials, like food and gas, shoppers are being a lot more careful with how they're spending their dollars and are holding out for the very best deals.\par \par \uc2 A lot of major retailers started running discounts weeks ago to try to lure customers in and to clear out some of their excess inventory. So far, that hasn't really happened, though, and businesses are hoping that starts to change this weekend, when a record 166 million people are expected to do some shopping.\par \par \uc2 A lot of folks out today say they are finding the deals they have been waiting for.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We were just like, why don't we just go and see what kind of deals they might have on different things?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It would have $618. I paid $306.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And finally getting a PS5, just sacrifice some sleep to get it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels Christmassy already here. It's so fun.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Microwaves for 20 bucks and air fryers for $40, and it's like, hey, I couldn't beat that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Throwing a kink into \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's holiday shopping plans, thousands of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse workers across the globe are walking out on their jobs today in protest to demand better pay and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Rallies are taking place in the U.K., India, Japan, Australia, South Africa, and in more than 10 cities in the U.S., including one in New York City on Fifth Avenue outside an apartment building where \uc2 Amazon\uc2  founder \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  has a condo.\par \par \uc2 Analysts will be keeping a close eye on these retail numbers over the next few days, both to try to get a better sense of where the economy is now, how Americans are feeling about it with their checkbooks, and what that could look like going forward into next year -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you very much, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Retailers also bracing for a potential rail strike that could hit at the height of the holiday shopping season. And we're getting mixed signals from the White House on what's being done to stop it in its tracks.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson is traveling with the president in Nantucket, Massachusetts, with more on this -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS PENTAGON PRODUCER: Charles, we just heard from Garrett there are protests in 40 countries around the world from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers fed up with low pay and bad wage conditions, what they say, poor working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Biden appearing to respond to those protests on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , saying -- quote -- "Working people is how we continue to build the economy from the bottom up and the middle out. I will also continue fighting for the PRO Act to help workers unionize and achieve a $15 minimum wage."\par \par \uc2 Another potential sore spot for the present, a looming rail strike. President Biden says he isn't ready to pull up his sleeves and help just yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My team has been in touch with all the parties, in rooms with the parties. And I have -- I have not directly engaged yet because we go -- they're still talking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: But, just a few days ago, Biden's press secretary said something very different, Charles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president is indeed involved directly. But I don't want to get into details at this time. But he has been involved. I just said the president has been directly involved. He's been in touch. This is the third time I'm saying he's been directly involved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Back in September, weeks before the midterm elections, the Biden administration took credit for avoiding a rail strike, but that deal now appears in danger, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Both sides have until early December to come to an agreement. Congressman Mike Waltz spoke earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): She's trying to distance the president because this deal with the railroad unions is literally about to come off the rails.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: You think things are expensive now, Julie? Wait until we have a rail strike where you can't get the goods and service. You're going to have empty shelves in a lot of the stores.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Now, the White House isn't commenting directly on those protests from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , saying the president's tweets speak for themselves -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 The nation's top business group demanding that Congress intervene if necessary to prevent this strike.\par \par \uc2 Neil Bradley\uc2  is executive vice president and chief policy officer for the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, from what we have heard for, at least from the railroad side, from the union side, they say this will cost $2 billion a day. That gets everyone's attention, Neil.\par \par \uc2 NEIL BRADLEY\uc2 , EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, \uc2 U.S. CHAMBER OF COMMERCE\uc2 : Well, That's right, $2 billion a day to the economy.\par \par \uc2 It's not just store shelves that are going to be empty. You're going to have factories that won't be able to get the inputs in that they need to be able to operate. They will be closing down. Even things like the water coming out of the tap at your home depends on chlorine being delivered by rail to water treatment plants, so we cannot allow a national rail strike to occur.\par \par \uc2 I think we have reached the point where the president's deal that he took credit for back in September clearly isn't getting the approval of the final four unions. And so Congress is going to have to step in here before December 9 to avoid catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Washington has been tasked with the ability to do things when it comes to rail strikes, because they are crippling; 30 percent of cargo in this country is transported by rail. What could Congress do if the president didn't step in? Could they do something on their own to stop this or to mediate this?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Yes, Charles. That's exactly right. And it's happened before.\par \par \uc2 On 18 separate occasions, Congress has had to step in to either restore rail service after a strike or to prevent a strike from happening. The next step in the process -- the president negotiated this deal. It was voted on by all 12 unions; eight accepted it. Four rejected it. It's been accepted by the railroads.\par \par \uc2 So now it's time for Congress to act. The simplest thing for them to do -- the House and the Senate are still controlled by the president's party -- is to simply impose the deal the president negotiated in September and avoid any possibility of a strike.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: A deal, impose that. How much -- the duration of such a deal, how long would it last? These four -- apparently, the sticking point is over sick pay, sick leave, sick pay -- sick days without pay.\par \par \uc2 And, apparently, none of that's in this contract, the zero numbers. They want two weeks. To me, it seems like, from a negotiating point of view, I wouldn't be surprised to hear one week or 10 days ultimately. But if they were to intervene, and they went -- they forced these unions to go with the president's initial plan that he OKed or that he was involved with, how long would they have to live with it?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Well, the same term as the original contract that was that was agreed to.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: So, remember, this deal that was outlined was agreed to by the leadership of all 12 unions. They agreed to increase pay, 24 percent increase in pay, the largest in five decades, spot bonuses for all railroad employees, and additional sick time.\par \par \uc2 Now you have the minority of the unions, four of the 12, who are demanding more, demanding things that even the independent commission that was set out to look at and resolve these complaints between railroad and management, what they're asking for has already been rejected by everyone involved.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: And so they're just simply hoping to, frankly, be the Grinch that stole Christmas and hope that they can get more than what they're entitled to in negotiation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, there's no doubt that these numbers certainly sound sweet to a lot of people watching this and hadn't been able to enjoy similar raises.\par \par \uc2 I will point out for the audience that the chemical part of this would be 96 hours even before the official strike, which means that there's a lot of pressure out there.\par \par \uc2 We heed your call. I hope Congress is listening. I hope the president's listening. Neil, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Thanks for having me, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, shoppers hunting more than ever for steep discounts this year, as inflation is cutting into Americans' paychecks.\par \par \uc2 To Dave Dodson with the Stanford School of Business on just how much hangs on this holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 Dave, real wages have been down year over year every single month going back to last April. It's a wonder, when I see these retail sales numbers out, that Americans have been able to keep pace. Obviously, we know they're buying, they're spending more money and getting fewer goods. I mean, how long can they keep this up?\par \par \uc2 DAVID DODSON, STANFORD UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Oh, actually, Charles, I think this is even more interesting in this, because you have got these three titanic forces.\par \par \uc2 You have got inventories that are bloating among the retailers. You have got inflation. But here's the thing. All that savings that was accumulated during the COVID years is gone. So, if -- why this is so special is, if you go -- as you said, wages have not kept up with inflation, but they have risen. So it's moderated it.\par \par \uc2 This is completely different, Charles, because if you go out to buy bread tomorrow, you're buying bread at tomorrow's -- at today's prices with today's wages. When you go Christmas shopping, that's going out of your savings. And so you're buying things from last year's savings. So it's really, really going to sting.\par \par \uc2 And watching how consumers behave during this period is going to be really important to understand how Q1 and Q2 of next year is going to go.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm glad you brought up the savings point.\par \par \uc2 I have so many economists who come on this show. And they are always kind of like, hey, no big deal. There's still $1.7 trillion in excess savings, which, by the way, I feel is so oxymoronic. I never even heard the term until recently.\par \par \uc2 When I was growing up, we either had savings or nothing. So, excess savings, $1.7 trillion. But then, when you look at the numbers, it's all rich people. The bottom half of this country, they have run out of that so- called excess savings, to your point. Now we're seeing credit card debt erupt. And we know people will shop. They will lean on those credit cards.\par \par \uc2 But you got to wonder how long. Again, to your point, you start getting to the first and second quarter of next year, it feels like it's going to be in dire situation.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Oh, Charles, you hit the nail on the head, because credit card balances are $100 billion higher today than they were a year ago, which, by the way, is a record number.\par \par \uc2 You said, well, maybe we can afford that. Not so much, because delinquent payments are the highest that they have been in -- since it's been tracked over the last 10 years. So, what's happening is, you're absolutely right. All of that accumulated savings is at the very, very rich or the top part of the economic strata.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: And for the average person, the average working person, they have worked through all of that savings, and now they're on credit card debt.\par \par \uc2 And so now they're going Christmas shopping, and they're taking it out of their savings, and everything's costing 8 to 10 percent more. I think it's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: No, I agree with you 1 million percent.\par \par \uc2 So what can be done? Is there anything that can be done? The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is hiking rates, and, from what I understand, every time they have risen rates 75 basis points, it has cost Americans at least $5 billion more in interest payments, so we're paying already $26 billion, $27 billion more because of the actions the Fed has taken to slow down on inflation.\par \par \uc2 Still, there's money coming into this economy. It just seems such a dire situation. You wonder, what's the way out?\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Well, if the only tool you have is a hammer, then everything looks like a nail, right? So that's what the Fed is faced with.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing. The largest component of the CPI is shelter. And he can raise interest rates as much as he wants.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: It's not going to change the fact that you have to live where you're living. You can't move out of your apartment because interest rates went up and move into a smaller apartment.\par \par \uc2 The second biggest component of the CPI is food, 14 percent. So you're going to tell me that he's going to raise rates another 75 basis points, and I'm going to buy less bread for my family?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: No, that's not going to affect consumer behavior at all.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Dave, good stuff. We will see what happens. We will definitely want to bring you back soon. Thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Sure, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, let's take a quick look at the corner of Wall and Broad, the Dow rallying on this shortened trading day, all three major averages, though, finishing in the green for the week. It was a pretty good week.\par \par \uc2 Of course, this time next week, we're going to have the jobs report, and maybe -- we just talked about the Fed -- just maybe clues on just how much more they will be hiking in December. Of course, we will be watching.\par \par \uc2 And from Wall Street to the World Cup and one of the most anticipated first round matchups just wrapping up between the United States and England.\par \par \uc2 Alex Hogan is in Doha, Qatar, with the very latest -- Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 A nail-biting match until the very end, with neither team managing to score until the clock rang out 0-0. And despite the U.S. acknowledging that it has quite the underdog title, it came out managing to hold on to the ball for much of the game.\par \par \uc2 And it was this shot, U.S.' Pulisic, who almost managed to get a goal in the first half, but it ended up hitting the bar. Now, the U.S. will meet to beat Iran to survive this group round. And that match will take place on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's because, earlier in the day, Iran handed Wales a heavy blow, scoring not just one, but two times as the clock rang out. And, today, Qatar scored its first goal, losing to Senegal, but, effectively, this knocks Qatar out of the World Cup. The small Middle Eastern country faced a tremendous amount of scrutiny ahead of hosting the global event over claims of inhumane working conditions for the workers involved in building these stadiums and World Cup facilities.\par \par \uc2 Human rights groups say that thousands of workers have died. And, today, I sat down with a senior Qatari official who disputed many of those claims.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Is there any acknowledgement about the number of people who might have died building these stadiums?\par \par \uc2 FATMA AL NUAIMI, QATAR SUPREME COMMITTEE SPOKESPERSON: There's been a lot of figures being misrepresented in media or around the world.\par \par \uc2 But, I mean, like I would like to confirm that, sadly, I mean, the number of workers that have been -- died on our sites -- is, as work-related, three deaths.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: We are live from the FIFA Fan Festival, where you can see, Charles, thousands of people are behind me celebrating what was just, again, a very exciting, suspenseful match that we just saw the U.S. playing England.\par \par \uc2 Ad for American fans who are here in Qatar traveling all the way across the world, their World Cup dreams are still very much alive -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I got to tell you, I almost missed the show watching that soccer match. Oh, I'm sorry, football.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks, Alex.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: border agents warning these crossings will look nothing like compared to this when Title 42 goes away. It's going to be a lot worse. The question, of course, is anything being done to stop it?\par \par \uc2 But, first, it will be two weeks tomorrow, and officials still scrambling to find answers today. Who is behind the deadly stabbings of these four college students in Idaho? We will have the latest.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Still no suspects in the murder of four University of Idaho students, investigators calling on the public to continue providing tips.\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Dan Springer is at the scene with the very latest -- Dan.\par \par \uc2 DAN SPRINGER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and, Charles, no update today from police, but they have told us this week that they have received a lot of tips from the public, more than 1,000, and they have also conducted about 150 interviews.\par \par \uc2 And they say that they are making progress in this high-profile investigation. But the bottom line, 12 days in, is that police have no arrests, no suspect identified, and no major -- no murder weapon found.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  does have a mobile forensics unit here in Moscow working through a lot of physical evidence taken from the scene. There is also an elite behavioral analysis unit working the case. And it's likely they have already come up with a profile of the killer and shared that with investigators. It has not been released to the public, but police insist there's a reason for that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COL. KEDRICK WILLS, IDAHO STATE POLICE: While this is such a tragic circumstance that we find ourselves in, in law enforcement, we believe we owe this to the surviving families to get this right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And back here live, we can tell you that this campus remains pretty empty. And we will have to see if the students come back to campus when classes resume on Monday. A lot of folks have said that they're not coming back, they're not going to send their kids back until the killer was found. So we will have to wait and see.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: A memorial is planned for next Wednesday for those four victims -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you, Dan.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two weeks since these horrific murders and still no answers. What does my next guest make of the handling of this situation, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent Maureen O'Connell?\par \par \uc2 Maureen, I know that you were part of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  evidence response team. A lot of criticism over the way the evidence has been handled, the way the crime scene has been handled. I don't know how much we should be focused on that at this particular point. But, at somewhere along the line, though, people are going to want to understand how that happened, right?\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O'CONNELL, FORMER FBI AGENT: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is, some cases are wrapped up very quickly, and some take a long period of time in order to bring it to a completion. But they're doing everything they possibly can. And when you consider that the amount of evidence in one really horrific crime scene, the murder of one person, can be voluminous, imagine how it is with four different people being murdered and numerous people that had nothing -- that shouldn't even have been there traipsing through the crime scene, thereby contaminating it.\par \par \uc2 There's so much more elimination work that has to be done, the blood pattern analysis, and all this information is being fed directly to the BAU agents, who will then update their profile on this potential killer -- on this killer.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Maureen, there seem to be two areas here of confusion, concern contention. Pick any of those words.\par \par \uc2 It's the assumption, or the working assumption, that this was a targeted attack. Many at this press conference on Wednesday wondering why, if that's the case, they won't share more information. We just heard Dan Springer mentioned this behavioral analysis and that maybe they have worked up some sort of a profile. Again, they won't share this with the public.\par \par \uc2 And yet, at the same time, it feels like they're going to need more help from the public to make a breakthrough. What's your thoughts on this right here?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: It's interesting you say that, because I noticed the other day they had only received something like 700 tips. And now we're up to 1,000.\par \par \uc2 And that, to me, is a shockingly low number. And I think that number is brought about by a number of different things, one of which is all these students and so many people from the town of Moscow have up and left for the holidays, and also because they're frightened, and rightfully so.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: So, I think that that really will, in a certain way, thwart the investigation.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't mean at all that law enforcement can't reach to other municipalities and to \uc2 FBI\uc2  offices all throughout the country to go interview these people, but it just makes it take a little bit more time.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 It's just such -- to your point, it's such a horrific murder scene. And we're just not accustomed to this. Maybe we're -- maybe we want too much too soon.\par \par \uc2 Maureen, thank you very much. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To the crisis at the Southern border.\par \par \uc2 Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas refusing calls by House Republican leadership to step down. Reaction from a member of the Border Security Caucus is coming up.\par \par \uc2 And millions of Americans heading home this weekend set to hit the roads and -- roads and skies, and while bracing for a hit from Mother Nature. We're going to get the latest from FOX Weather.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The border crisis escalating. Now the head of Homeland Security is resisting calls to get going.\par \par \uc2 And if you're heading back home, some nasty weather could be heading your way. We're going to tell you all about it in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To the U.S. Southern border now. No rest for border agents this holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Christina Coleman has more on that -- Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas is not stepping down. His department is defending him by saying that members of Congress can do better than point the finger at someone else and that they should come to the table and work on solutions for our broken immigration system.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there's no slowdown in illegal activity at the Southern border. Take a look at this video from yesterday, Thanksgiving Day. Authorities say Texas State Troopers and border agents stopped an 18-year-old from trying to smuggle migrants in a neighborhood in Mission, Texas.\par \par \uc2 They located three migrants in the thick brush trying to get to Houston. And, earlier today, our photographer shot this video. He says there were about two groups of around 200 migrants that were stopped by border agents just north of Eagle Pass. Also, border agents shared this photo out of Kingsville, Texas, on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 They say their canine team sniffed out a utility truck and there were seven migrants crammed into the back of it. Also on Tuesday, border agents seized nearly 119 pounds of cocaine at a port of entry in Laredo, Texas. Authorities say the hard narcotics have a street value of more than $1.5 million.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Kevin McCarthy is absolutely right to hold Mayorkas responsible for the fentanyl pouring across the border, the cocaine you just mentioned, but also the record number of deaths of these poor people trying to cross the border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Also today, in Pennsylvania, two buses with a total of 71 migrants arrived in Philadelphia this morning. This would be the third and fourth buses of migrant sent to this Democrat-run state by Texas Republican Governor Greg Abbott, drawing even more attention to this critical issue.\par \par \uc2 The mayor's office there says city workers and nonprofit organizations helped provide those migrants with shelter, water, food and social services -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Christina, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas says he's not going anywhere after House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy demanded that he step down.\par \par \uc2 To a member of the Congressional Border Caucus on that, Arizona Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Lesko\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, so, a defiant Mayorkas. Kevin McCarthy, though, he laid it down pretty hard. Now, he was pretty serious. He drew a line in the sand. Where's this going?\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE LESKO (R-AZ): Well, I really think that Kevin McCarthy is right.\par \par \uc2 And I'm glad that he has given Mayorkas an ultimatum: Either resign or we're going to start investigations and see if it leads to impeachment.\par \par \uc2 I personally have signed onto legislation to impeach Mayorkas. Prior to that, I signed on to legislation that he resign. And I signed on to letters asking him for -- to resign. I'm a member of the Border Security Caucus in the House. And we invited Mayorkas to come to our caucus. And, surprisingly, he did, after we had written them a letter saying he should resign.\par \par \uc2 And he keeps on lying. He keeps on telling us that the border is secure. He said that again the other day in a House hearing. And it's not secure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 LESKO: Anybody knows that. Even the illegal immigrants know that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, well, I think they know probably more than anyone else.\par \par \uc2 You have so much expertise here, a member of the Border Caucus. Also, you have written extensively about this. I was reading one of your PDFs from a couple of years ago talking about securing the border, needing the wall, needing more boots, expanding technology, ports of entry, allowing -- actually following the law. I thought that was one that was sort of tongue in cheek, but true. Air assistance, intelligence.\par \par \uc2 So, there's obviously -- the Republicans have obviously thought of a comprehensive plan here. Now we throw in the potential of Title 42. Hopefully, maybe these governments can get it to stay in place. What's been the response of the White House? I mean, this is really good work that you put out there.\par \par \uc2 LESKO: Well, President Biden is -- he wants an open border. That's the only thing I can conclude. And so do most of the Democrats, because of all of their policies.\par \par \uc2 You know President Biden on day one stopped construction of the border fence. He wanted to get rid of Title 42. Now a court has said he needs to. He has gotten rid of remain-in-Mexico policy, which, by the way, if there was only one thing that Biden could do, if he reinstated the remain-in- Mexico policy, that would solve a lot of the problem that's going on right now, because, as you know, we have a humanitarian crisis, a drug crisis, and a national security crisis at our Southern border.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And those all three seem like reasons for urgency, not the sort of lax response that we have seen.\par \par \uc2 And I get it. And I get the notion that Democrats believe this works for them politically. But when you start talking about two million people that illegally enter any country, but specifically, of course, the United States, I think it's shocking. I think there are a lot of apolitical folks who aren't paying attention.\par \par \uc2 When they hear that number, when I talk to them, and they hear that number, they're taken aback. I mean, what do you make of the public, maybe just getting the public more evolved and more aware of this?\par \par \uc2 LESKO: Well, here in Arizona, this has been a big issue for many, many years.\par \par \uc2 We really -- I have gone down to the border multiple times. And I have talked to the Border Patrol officers themselves. They are so frustrated. Their morale is so low. The border is wide open. I mean, quite frankly, the president has not even come to the border. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  came once in like a photo shoot.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESKO: They don't care about the border.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESKO: It needs to be secured. I have talked to sheriffs down at the border. They have reports of women and girls being raped at the border.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESKO: And you know how the fentanyl is coming over.\par \par \uc2 This is a huge crisis. And that's why McCarthy is calling on Mayorkas to resign, or we're going to see if we could find reasons to impeach him, which I think we can.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Lesko\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LESKO: Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Meantime, folks, a decision the fate of that special master after the raid on Mar-a-Lago is imminent.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's David Spunt. He's got the latest from the Justice Department -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, whenever we get this decision, it will be significant, because if the appeals court rules in favor of DOJ, it may speed up this investigation a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Now, we're talking about the appeals court will issue whether or not a special master is needed to go through those documents taken from Mar-a- Lago by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in early August. A three-judge panel on Tuesday heard arguments from Trump's legal team about why that neutral third-party special master is necessary to sift through documents, roughly 10,000 documents taken from the former president's Florida home.\par \par \uc2 DOJ says a special master is not necessary. The three judges who will decide this case, William Pryor, a \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  appointee, and two \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointees, Britt Grant and Andrew Brasher, the trio seemed skeptical of the Trump legal team's arguments over why the special master in this case, Judge Raymond Dearie from New York, is necessary.\par \par \uc2 The DOJ under Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has already, Charles, catalogued and sifted through roughly 100 classified documents taken from Mar-a-Lago. The rest, about 10,000-plus, Judge Dearie is going through. Those are unclassified.\par \par \uc2 Now, one week ago today, Garland appointed former federal prosecutor Jack Smith to handle the entire Mar-a-Lago investigation. He's the new special counsel. And Garland did so after Trump announced he wanted to seek his old job and move back into the White House in early 2025.\par \par \uc2 We will see what happens. We're waiting on this opinion. Once we get it, we will pass it along -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you very much, David.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You bet.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Meanwhile, folks, forget weathering price hikes. Now flyers may be weathering some nasty storms as they head home.\par \par \uc2 Right, Jeff?\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Sadly so, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Out here at Philadelphia International, been a good day to travel today. It's pretty calm today. But if you're going to or from the Northeast this weekend, could be a very different story.\par \par \uc2 I will have that story when we come back in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, it's the calm before the storm, literally, with millions of holiday travelers bracing for some wicked weather and a potential travel nightmare coming home from grandma's house.\par \par \uc2 To FOX chief meteorologist Rick Reichmuth with the very latest and the forecast -- Rick.\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: Charles, I tell you what.\par \par \uc2 We have had some really bad Thanksgivings. This is not one of them. There are some troubles out there, but, overall, take a look at these temperatures; 37 degrees in Fargo is the coldest anywhere on the map here. It's really warm across parts of Florida. So if you went down to visit grandma in Florida, you have certainly won this Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 We have one little disturbance here, this down across parts of the South. That's going to be our troublemaker for the next couple of days. But, across the country's airports right now, we don't have any significant delays, except for some minor delays in the Seattle area.\par \par \uc2 Go towards tomorrow, the Northern Rockies will see that, maybe places like Dallas, Little Rock, New Orleans seeing a few delays. East Coast is looking great. It's Sunday that all that energy moves towards the east, and it's going to be a rainmaker, not a snowmaker, so that will certainly help.\par \par \uc2 Where it is, is, right now, we have got snow across parts of the Southwest. Southeastern New Mexico and far West Texas, we have winter storm warnings in effect. Take a look what happens throughout the night tonight. We're going to see this storm rotate through the area and, as it does, drop maybe three to four inches for a number of people here.\par \par \uc2 If you're on I-10, on the roads, maybe in towards I-20, be very careful, because we are going to see some accumulating snow from this little system. After that, we're going to be talking about this storm ejecting out across parts of the Deep South. But take a look at this. It's all rain, maybe a little too much, one trouble spot around the Houston area.\par \par \uc2 They had some pretty big flooding yesterday. And we're going to get some more of that today, some pretty significant rain. And then we have got this through Sunday. Take a look at this, light rain across the East Coast, nothing that's going to be too heavy.\par \par \uc2 Good thing, Charles, that our weather that we're having this week is this week, because, next week, a much more active pattern sets up, including severe weather and some pretty significant snow across the Plains. But that is not this week. So we have that to be thankful for -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: There is always something. You always find a bright spot.\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: Yes. I try.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks a lot, Rick. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: You bet.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We're already seeing more than 4,500 flight delays over the past two days. And that's even before some of the stuff Rick was talking about.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Jeff Flock at \uc2 Philadelphia International Airport\uc2  with more -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Yes, what Rick is saying, Charles, we see playing out here at the nation's airports.\par \par \uc2 It's pretty easy to stroll through here today. Not a whole lot of lines are troubled, but all eyes on Sunday. The other good thing about right now, looking for bright spots, is that there's not a lot of delay -- there are a lot of delays, but there's not a lot of cancellations.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at the number today, just 37 cancellations so far. And that -- as my old friend used to say, that ain't nothing compared to what it used to be during holiday travel, that despite the fact there's more people traveling. Yesterday, we got the TSA numbers, 1.4 million through the turnstiles.\par \par \uc2 That is a little better than it was last year. Why are things going well so far? Well, the airlines tell us it's because they have reduced their schedules, just not as many flights out there. They're not promising more than they can deliver. They're also doing better with absenteeism from their staffs. They have got people in reserve.\par \par \uc2 And they say remote work is making people spread their travel out. Everybody's not traveling on the same day because they can work from home. Still a pilot shortage, though, out there. A lot of flights have been eliminated from the schedule, not canceled, but eliminated; 324 airports around the country in the latest survey have lost flights since the pandemic started; 60 of the nation's airports have lost half their flights, and another 14 have lost all of their scheduled air travel.\par \par \uc2 That makes it tough. It drives up the cost of tickets because there's less availability. We looked ahead to Christmas. What's the average round-trip by Christmas? -- $463, that's an increase of almost 40 percent, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Good news is, you can go. Bad news is, going to cost you -- sir.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All righty.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 So, flying is pricey. So are there any deals out there for your next trip?\par \par \uc2 I'm going to bring in \uc2 Gabe Saglie\uc2 . He's the senior editor at \uc2 Travelzoo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gabe, everyone's traveling these days. I mean, everyone loves it. It's like the number one -- I think it's the number one thing adults in America do these days.\par \par \uc2 GABE SAGLIE, TRAVELZOO.COM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, I mean, really. I mean, I got folks who never talked about going places say, hey, guess where I am? I'm in Vegas, right?\par \par \uc2 So -- but now they want to know the deals, because the prices are going through the roof. You're the person to talk to about this.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Well, first of all, yes, you're absolutely right. I mean, a guy like me gets a kick out of seeing a lot of volume out there.\par \par \uc2 No one likes delays or cancellations. But I think it's a healthy thing to see the industry rebound quite nicely, as Jeff just mentioned, numbers rivaling last year. But, in some cases, Sunday and Monday of this past week, TSA checked more people through them the same dates in 2019, pre- pandemic. So, clearly, we're bouncing -- we're bouncing back.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, listen, the sales are out there. They're inevitable. Today, we have already seen a couple of airlines put out some sales. Alaska has a one-day sale today, 30 percent off flights to Hawaii from places like New York, D.C. and L.A.\par \par \uc2 Listen, Cyber Monday right around the corner. Travel deal Tuesday next week, another couple of opportunities for folks to really be on the lookout for some pretty dramatic travel industry sales. I know \uc2 Travelzoo\uc2  is putting -- we're putting out our big newsletter on Monday, Cyber Monday.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: I had a sneak peek at it, some major savings, up to 70 percent on exotic international travel.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm glad you brought that up. I got 30 seconds, right? I'm a market guy. The dollar is looking a little weak recently. It feels like, you know what, we have got a little bit of time to take advantage of just how strong it's been, particularly against the euro.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Listen, we're seeing prices to Europe drop pretty nicely over the next couple of months, really.\par \par \uc2 And, as you know, now there's that one-to-one parity with the euro. I was in Ireland just a couple of weeks ago. That helped me get that extra pint at the public, also some strength against the sterling. That's boding well for people who weren't able to afford a trip to Europe in the summer season.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: All of a sudden, a window of opportunity happening right now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Gabe, you had me an extra pint. Thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: I will meet you there.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All righty.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, with inflation eating away at household budgets, shoppers searching for any break they can get today, and we're on it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hunting for deals while dealing with inflation.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Grady Trimble is in Chicago on that -- Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 This weekend is expected to be the biggest shopping event of the year and of all time, but inflation is changing the way Americans are shopping this Black Friday through Cyber Monday. \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  says, this weekend, we will spend around $500, on average. That's up 12 percent from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 But even though we're spending more, we're actually buying less, because those dollars aren't going as far. We're getting less bang for our buck. \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  says we're buying seven fewer gifts this weekend compared to 2021. They also found more shoppers this year are relying on credit cards and buy now, pay later programs.\par \par \uc2 The folks we talk to say they're scouring for deals, looking more closely than they ever have for discounts. And, sometimes, they're striking out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The prices have gone higher, and so we're trying to look for any deals that will benefit us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But the prices, yes, they're higher, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, definitely.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And I don't know that the deals are any better this year.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have seen some 40 percents. I -- in my book, that's pretty good. I would like to see some 50 percents out there, personally, maybe 60.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You know, you have to look, and you have to browse and you find somewhere -- the coat was 40 percent off, so I got the shirt for free, practically. So it's pretty good. Sometimes good, sometimes not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Looking ahead to Cyber Monday, Adobe Analytics predicts we will spend $11 billion online this year. That's up 5 percent from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 So, even with inflation, we're still spending -- Charles, back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you so much, Grady.\par \par \uc2 Shoppers are out. Meet the guy tracking minute-by-minute sales across the country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Looking live at Mall of America, Minnesota, where Black Friday shopping continues.\par \par \uc2 So, overall, how are sales shaping up this year? My next guest is tracking it live.\par \par \uc2 Shopify\uc2  president \uc2 Harley Finkelstein\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Harley, would -- first of all, we just had your Data Stories map up. It is so groovy, my man. I dig it a lot.\par \par \uc2 HARLEY FINKELSTEIN\uc2 , \uc2 SHOPIFY\uc2 : It's pretty cool. Yes, thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It really is. It really is.\par \par \uc2 FINKELSTEIN: It's amazing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What are you seeing so far?\par \par \uc2 FINKELSTEIN: Yes, a couple of things.\par \par \uc2 So, first of all, anyone that wants to track it, that wants to track global commerce on \uc2 Shopify\uc2 , which is about 10 percent of all e-commerce in the U.S. And, obviously, we have merchants all over the world. You can go to \uc2 datastories.Shopify.com\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But in terms what we're seeing, Charles, right now, a couple early trends. First of all, this Black Friday, Cyber Monday, it just seems less frantic. There's less supply issues. There's more physical stores that are open. And it seems like the retailers and the merchants have had better capacity planning.\par \par \uc2 So it's a lot more stable in terms of what we saw last year.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 FINKELSTEIN: Last year, 2021, we saw about 47 million shoppers spent about $6.3 billion on \uc2 Shopify\uc2 . And what we're seeing right now, I can give you the numbers as we speak in real time.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, we're seeing about $2.1 million every minute on \uc2 Shopify\uc2 , and about 19,000 orders happen every minute as well. And earlier today, we actually saw $3 million happen on sales every single minute. So there are some things here. There are some bright spots here.\par \par \uc2 A couple other things that we saw. On both coasts in the U.S., East and West Coast, peak sales was midnight Pacific in California and midnight in New York. So, regardless of location, shoppers were to look for deals.\par \par \uc2 But I think the big story here -- and I heard one of your previous guests talk about it -- is this intentionality. Shoppers are really looking for a great deal. They're looking for good value. But they're also voting with their wallets to buy from brands they really, really love, as opposed to random brands.\par \par \uc2 They're going to the brands that they actually know and care about.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, so, by the way, I'm seeing more red than green, I mean, some things like toy cars up 100 percent. I have been watching this for a couple hours now, to collectible stuff is up 100, 150 percent, but a little bit more red than green in terms of overall items in general.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you real quick about that, the notion of brand loyalty and the world of inflation. So, are they saying, hey, if I can get my brand on discount, is that the ultimate combination people are looking for maybe right now?\par \par \uc2 FINKELSTEIN: Yes, that's the ultimate combination, a brand that I really, really love at a discount.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we're tracking basically across the world, but the top five product categories right now are apparel first, health and beauty second, home and garden third, food and beverage fourth, and then arts and entertainment fifth.\par \par \uc2 And when we look at some of the most popular products right now, we see the Laneige Sleeping Mask, Monos Carry-On. And, actually, the Quencher, a water bottle from Stanley, is trending as well. So you are seeing people go back to their favorite brands.\par \par \uc2 But what you said is important in terms of inflation, because there's a lot of factors that impact consumer behavior. I mean, there's inflation, there's interest rates, there's geopolitical tensions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 FINKELSTEIN: But earlier this week, we actually saw -- when the Consumer Sentiment Index was put out, we saw it higher than what was expected.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Harley...\par \par \uc2 FINKELSTEIN: So, we actually are seeing people buy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I got to leave it there, my man. It is great, great stuff.\par \par \uc2 FINKELSTEIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Congratulations. By the way, retailers love your site.\par \par \uc2 All right, folks, that will be it for me right now. But guess what? I'm with you tomorrow at 10:00 p.m. Eastern for "Cavuto Live."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221125eibp00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 DHS Fires Back As McCarthy Demands Mayorkas Resign; DHS Secretary Faces Calls To Resign Amid Border Crisis; Biden Border Policies Under Fire From GOP, Dems; Russia Pummels Power Grid, Ramps Up Strikes; Ukrainian Cities Face New Wave Of Russian Attacks; Ukraine M.P.: Pushing Hard To Get Territories Back; Report: Ex-FTX CEO "Deeply Sorry" For Crypto Crash; Report: Former CEO Says "Still A Chance" To Save FTX; FTX Collapse Draws Comparisons To Madoff Scheme; Survey: Cost Of Thanksgiving Up 20% VS 2021; Americans Struggle With Inflation For The Holidays; Idaho Police Still Without Suspect; 7 Dead In Virginia \uc2 Walmart\uc2  Shooting, Including Gunman\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Bill Melugin, Juan Williams, Greg Palkot, Edward  Lawrence, Charlie Gasparino, Grady Trimble, Robert Wolf, Max Gordon, Dan  Springer, Griff Jenkins \par \uc2 7,981 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): And good afternoon, everybody. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Martha MacCallum. Soon, a live update from Idaho police on that gruesome quadruple murder. And what they're now saying about the possibility that one of the victims had a stalker, but first, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  responding to an ultimatum from the top House Republican. Minority Leader \uc2 Kevin McCarthy\uc2  with this warning about what will happen when the GOP takes control in January. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KEVIN MCCARTHY\uc2  (R-CA): I am calling on the Secretary to resign. The Secretary Mayorkas does not resign, House Republicans will investigate every order, every action, and every failure will determine whether we can begin impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: DHS tells Fox, Mayorkas has no plans to resign and "members of Congress" can do better than point the finger at someone else. They should come to the table and work on solutions for our broken, broken system and outdated laws which have not been overhauled in more than 40 years. Mercedes Schlapp and Juan Williams in a moment. But first, to the National Correspondent Bill Melugin, he's live at the border in El Paso, Texas. Bill, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good afternoon to you as this border crisis continues, we continue to see both bizarre and dangerous methods of human smuggling some new incidents we'd like to show your view are starting with this. Take a look at this photo right here at a border patrol's Tucson, Arizona second where agents stopped a U haul work truck, they open it up and they find 14 illegal immigrants all dressed in camouflage being smuggled inside of it. That truck was padlock shut so these people had no way of getting out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voiceover): A 37-year-old U.S. citizen was driving that truck, she was arrested and charged with human smuggling, then we'll take you down to the Rio Grande Valley. Take a look at these photos. A Border Patrol stopping a utility work vehicle inside of that they find seven illegal immigrants being smuggled in the back of that work truck. That was in Kingsville, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Then, take a look at this video. Also, out of Texas this is in LaSalle County. Texas DPS pulling over a human smuggler once that smuggler stops the vehicle, five illegal immigrants go bailing out of the vehicle. That was a U.S. citizen, she was arrested and charged. She had a warrant out for her arrest. She told investigators she was paid more than $3,000 per person for that smuggling.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, take a look at these mug shots right here. Border Patrol in Del Rio sector reporting on Monday in a two-hour span, they arrested two sex offenders in two separate groups. They've actually arrested six sex offenders in that sector in the last week alone. And back out here live that Del Rio sector incredibly busy over the last week alone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): They say, they've had more than 10,300 illegal crossings and nearly 6000 known got-aways, big numbers for one sector in one single week. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And we're going to take a closer at those numbers as the show goes. Bill Melugin, live for us in the border. Bill, thank you. Let's bring in Mercedes Schlapp, Former Senior Adviser to President Trump, now a CPAC Foundation Senior Fellow; and Juan Williams, a Fox News Political Analyst. Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 Juan, I don't know how you can look at the pictures that Bill Melugin is showing us with dozens and dozens of people stuffed into the back of trucks, and then locked from the outside and not believe there is a legitimate and severe problem at the border. And yet, the DHS is literally telling Republicans to stop pointing fingers, and to do something about it. And you know, who's out -- who else is pointing fingers, Juan? Democrats. Listen to Governor-elect, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  , from Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D), ARIZONA GOVERNOR-ELECT: I don't think they're doing enough. I would love to have them visit and see firsthand the kind of support and relief that folks in these communities need from the federal government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Juan, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think that Senator Mark Kelly said yesterday, you know, Democrats need to do more and \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is repeating their thing. He also said Republicans need to stop beating up on this issue and resolve it, do something. And that's what you heard in the statement that you read, Trace, from Secretary Mayorkas' office and \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  . It's not that anyone's saying there's not a problem, there's a tremendous.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Everybody's saying they're not a problem, that's not a fair assessment. People are saying that's not a problem.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, that's not true.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: You have the DHS Secretary going before Congress time and time and time again, saying the border is secure. If that's not saying there's not a problem, I don't know what is. And I just want to say that --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Excuse, that's not saying that, no it's not.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let me just say one thing.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Wait, wait, wait -- you are misinterpreting this, Trace, because the Secretary saying we have a record number of apprehensions right now. So, obviously, you this border police, this customs force, they are doing their job, they are arresting people in record numbers, Trace. The argument here is about what to do.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Juan, they are, they are --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And when you see McCarthy go down there --\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Record numbers coming. I had been covering the border for 35 years, it was my first job in television.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I know exactly the problem, it's been going on for a long time. And Mercedes, I'll let you jump in on this.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: But to say, to say that people are not getting in this country in record numbers and they are, and walking across the border being stuffed into truck is disingenuous.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But Trace, that's not, that's not us.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: That is the problem.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Trace, that's not what anyone is saying.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And the Republicans have put --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Oh my gosh. Trace, no one's saying that.\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES SCHLAPP, FORMER SENIOR ADVISER TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: Well, you know --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: What we're saying is there's a new, there's a new stream of people coming from Nicaragua, Cuba, Venezuela, there's all new issues.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It doesn't matter where they're coming from, Juan, it's the border itself, which is unsecured. Go ahead, Mercedes.\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: Now, it's fine, Trace, I think I'm going to become the moderator now, you're doing such a great job. But look, it's not just a problem. It is a humanitarian crisis. We're talking about human lives. And the mere fact is, is that we know that President Biden and the Democrats have done nothing to stop this border crisis.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it's created a magnet, the policies that the Biden administration has put in place, the policies that they've taken away, like Remain in Mexico, for example, where they in essence, are calling folks to come into the country that has created record number of individuals coming into the country where there is catch and release and people are being able to go into the, the interior of this nation without any sort of control.\par \par \uc2 This has been caused by the Democrats. This border security issue should be a bipartisan issue. And sadly, the Democrats have failed to protect our borders and the essence --\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I just want to put these numbers up, if I can, Mercedes.\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: -- create human trafficking issues and fentanyl issues.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I want to put in, and exactly to your point, there look at these numbers. This is the encounters. You look at that two million plus encounters. The got-aways are not even included in that. And then you look at the fentanyl drug seizures -- roll over to the next one -- and you've got 14,000, 14.7 thousand pounds of fentanyl, that's enough to kill everyone in the country. That's 30 40 percent higher than last year. It is a major problem.\par \par \uc2 And I'll give you 10 seconds to wrap it up, Juan, and I hate to be, be, you know, jumping your thing there. But the Republicans have said over and over again, we need to secure the border, we need to keep building the border wall. We need to reinforce the number of border patrol agents down there. There had been solutions coming and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has never been to the border and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has done zero down at the border. Your final thoughts?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I mean, what's McCarthy doing down there? He's really running to be Speaker and he doesn't have the vote. So, he's putting on performance political art. He's not solving anything. He's not saying, yes, I have an idea. There were proposals in under Bush, under Trump, under Obama and who blocked it? It was Republicans, freedom caucus --\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: And the Democrats. No --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Never satisfied. Never satisfied,\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: The Democrats, I was there in the White House -- No, Juan, Juan, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: They're giving me, they're giving me the wrap. Go ahead, Mercedes, five seconds.\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: I'm just going to say one thing. I was there during the Trump administration. We tried to work on various deals with the Democrats. They refuse to come to the table. They don't want to solve this border crisis, in fact, they're making it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Juan, Mercedes, thank you both. Just one week after a court overturned Georgia's ban on abortion after about six weeks of pregnancy, the State Supreme Court has put it back on the books. The justices putting the lower court's ruling on hold while they consider an appeal. So, this is not the end of the legal battle. But for now, the ban on abortions in Georgia after a heartbeat is detected, is back in effects.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  blasting Russia today as dozens of rockets pound Ukraine hitting civilian targets including the maternity ward of a hospital killing a newborn baby. A member of parliament currently, currently in Ukraine will join us after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translation): We will go through this because we are an unbreakable nation.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, the "worst winter" since World War II is what Ukraine's capital city, Kyiv, is prepping for. Russia is now pummeling the country's power grid leaving millions without heat and electricity as missile attacks like this one in southeastern Ukraine continue. One of the victims, a newborn baby, according to the mayor there. Our senior affair, foreign affairs correspondent, Greg Palkot is live for us in Kyiv with more. Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Trace, yes, Kyiv and the rest of the cities across this country are in a cold dark tonight, thanks to yet another mass attack against Ukraine by Russia. According to officials here, some 70 Russian missiles were launched, around 50 were shot down but the rest got through, hitting power stations, the electric grid, plus residential sites. Take a look at what we saw on the north side of Kyiv earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voiceover): Smoke still rising from the roof of this residential building behind us, another result of another Russian missile attack. Police on the ground here trying to figure it out.\par \par \uc2 The missile was apparently targeting a nearby power facility that either one off course or was intercepted and fragments rain down. Whatever, the building was ripped open, a fire triggered: four killed, 27 hurt.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's a horrible thing that this happens in the 21st century.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I can't put it into words. Putin is from hell.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Now, yes, in the southern part of the country, Trace, a maternity hospital was struck, killing a 2-day-old baby boy, injuring his mother who was right alongside him.\par \par \uc2 Just about all the major cities, as we speak, are desperately trying to restore power, heat, and communications.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): And one more danger, Trace. Four nuclear plants in this country had to be taken off line. There were concerns that power disruptions might compromise the safety. Dangers all around.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Back to you as the news breaks. Greg Palkot, live for us and Kyiv. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now live from Kyiv is Kira Rudik, a member of the Ukrainian parliament. Kira, thank you so much for coming on. You have Greg describing what's going on there. I've been following you on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , describing what is happening there.\par \par \uc2 Give us an idea of what the energy situation looks like. And is this -- is this going to hurt any type of future negotiations or current negotiations with Russia?\par \par \uc2 KIRA RUDIK, MEMBER, UKRAINIAN PARLIAMENT: Hello, Trace. Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 Well, just a heads up, I'm on running diesel generator. So, without it, I wouldn't be able to join you today, because we don't have Internet and we do not have electricity. And we do not have running water.\par \par \uc2 But if Putin thought that being without heat, electricity, and water, would break us, or make us negotiate with him, he did not learn anything about us for the last eight months.\par \par \uc2 So