t they would not have to fight this war in five, 10, 20 years.\par \par \uc2 We need to make sure that we push the aggressor back and that we will live peacefully, and that we will be rebuilding our country.\par \par \uc2 What we have seen is that it's Russian strategy to act as a terrorist. And you remember what all the democratic countries do not do with terrorists. They do not negotiate with terrorists. They fight them. And they are standing for themselves, and this is what we are doing. We are standing for ourselves. We are standing for our country no matter how hard it is.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Sure. Yes, it is hard. Kira, listen, the best to you. We hope the energy comes back on. Thank you for joining us. We'll check back in with you and those who want more information should follow you on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as well. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 RUDIK: Thank you and glory to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, a new apology from the so-called crypto whiz kid who was living large in a Bahamas penthouse during the colossal collapse of his now- bankrupt exchange.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: While the former CEO, whose crypto collapse may have cost his clients billions of dollars reportedly says he's deeply sorry. CoinDesk reports that \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  sent a letter to his former staffers writing, quoting here, "I didn't mean for any of this to happen, and I would give anything to be able to go back and do things over again.\par \par \uc2 You were my family. I've lost that, and our old home is an empty warehouse of monitors. When it turned around -- when I turned around, there is no one left to talk to."\par \par \uc2 Here is a look at that FTX warehouse in the Bahamas last year. SBF tweeted, this photo of himself, sleeping on beanbags in the office. Of course, he also had a luxury penthouse nearby.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer says FTX spent $300 million on homes for top staffers and most of the company's assets are now missing or stolen.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER (on camera): SBF reportedly telling his former staffers maybe there is still a chance to save the company. Fox Business senior correspondent Charlie Gasparino is standing by. First, to Fox Business correspondent Edward Lawrence. He is live for us in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Edward, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good afternoon, Trace. And in that letter, he says that he froze in the face of pressure and leaks. Now, the house of cards fell leaving at least a billion dollars missing and tens of billions of dollars misused.\par \par \uc2 Now, in court, a lawyer for FTX said that the money is either missing or stolen. Now, we learned that \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  use $300 million for real estate for himself, his family, and other senior staff at FTX.\par \par \uc2 $10 billion of money from FTX customers was taken and used to prop-up Bankman-Fried's hedge fund, \uc2 Alameda Research\uc2  . That left FTX without the ability to handle customer withdrawals?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK MAHANEY, SENIOR MANAGING DIRECTOR, EVERCORE ISI: It just seems to me like this is just another mania. And, you know, we have these in the market from time to time. And this time, it's crypto.\par \par \uc2 So, we think it's very unfortunate what happened. It also seems that there's a fair amount of malfeasance involved too. I just hope we just move beyond this quickly, learn the lesson.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, a forensic accountants that I talked with, say this case has close similarities to the \uc2 Bernie Madoff\uc2  Ponzi scheme. There was almost no oversight, everything funneled through one person, and a massive lack of internal controls.\par \par \uc2 Experts say this is another warning about investing in things not fully understood.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW WHITAKER, FORMER ACTING UNITED STATES ATTORNEY GENERAL: You can see the failures looking in the rearview mirror. But I think anybody that would have invested in this on the front end should have asked some very pointed questions about really just corporate governance and control.\par \par \uc2 And anytime you're dealing with something that's offshore, obviously, that heightens the concern.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (on camera): The top 50 creditors are still owed about $3 billion. The SEC and New York state prosecutor's office are looking into this collapse. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Edward Lawrence live for us in D.C. Edward, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox Business senior correspondent Charlie Gasparino. Charlie, I want to get to more of that letter, if I can, in a little bit.\par \par \uc2 But first, at a bankruptcy hearing, FTX attorney James Bromley said the following here, quoting, "FTX was in the control of inexperienced and unsophisticated individuals, and some or all of them were compromised individuals."\par \par \uc2 Almost like, you know, what if SBF just had a couple of more years under his belt, man, none of this ever would have happened.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: I think you need more than a couple of years. Let's just ramp this forward a little bit. You mentioned that -- Edward mentioned, the Justice Department's investigating this. We're understanding that they are now reaching out to business partners, and the sort of celebrity influencers of FTX.\par \par \uc2 So, people like -- I'm not saying, Tom Brady's got the call, but people like Tom Brady, the brand ambassadors are being called by the U.S. Attorney's Office, so with his business partners. People like \uc2 Brad Katsuyama\uc2  , \uc2 IEX\uc2  , and \uc2 Anthony Scaramucci\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Again, not saying they're specifically being called, but the Justice Department right now is reaching out to them. Because they obviously don't believe that this was just, you know, I made a mistake, an $8 billion mistake, and I'm sorry. You know, they -- this is -- this is like something a little more.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And you know, here is the sort of tragedy of this disorder. That -- the definite tragedy of this. You know, in Madoff, people got some of their money back. Because they -- the bankruptcy trustee was able to claw back the ill-gotten gains that people took out of it.\par \par \uc2 It was a classy Ponzi -- classic Ponzi scheme. You paid off people with new money coming in, there was never really an investment. So, people did get their money back, at least often, what they put in.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: This is probably gone. He might have lost it on the trading markets. And bankruptcy trustees can't claw that back. That's very difficult.\par \par \uc2 But here is my point, I mean, the baffling thing about this is now you have SBF, saying, you know what, maybe we can save this thing, just pony up a little more dough.\par \par \uc2 He said the following here, "Maybe there is a chance to save the company, I believe there are billions of dollars of genuine interest from new investors that could go to making customers whole. But I can't promise you that anything will happen, because it's not my choice."\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, he sounds -- he sounds out of his mind. I mean, let's be real clear here. He went to the Middle East, I believe in October to look for money. From the Middle East, where there's lots of money.\par \par \uc2 I know people who took him around to major people at sovereign wealth funds, particularly to Saudi sovereign wealth fund. They said no. And this is October.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: So, what makes him think, now, there's a huge potential pool of money. I mean, like who? I mean, in their right mind is going to throw money at this.\par \par \uc2 And let's be real clear here. He does have technology, there is a platform there. I guess someone will buy it, but they are not buying it for $5 billion. That's for sure.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, these sort of platforms are not exactly, you know, you can't -- it's not like you can't copy, and then, we're not inventing the wheel here. They're literally -- you're literally reinventing the wheel, I should say. You know, it's --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: It's not that hard. So, I think he's -- I think he's delusional. I think he knows he's probably heading for jail.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: The U.S. Attorney's Office would like to bring a case before the end of the -- end of the year. And if no money is found, other than his physical assets, and that's not enough to cover it. This is going to go down, you know, either worse or at least at par, with Madoff, and that's pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, and he is trying to cover those bases now, Charlie, because now he's issuing apologies, right?\par \par \uc2 So, he's one -- on one hand, saying, hey, maybe give me some more money and we can fix this whole thing and the other side is, oh, boy, I'm so sorry.\par \par \uc2 That's the whole thing is. He's apologizing. He wants money. It's going to be a mess. Charlie Gasparino, great to have you on as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Anytime.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, we are now minutes away from a live update from Idaho police, as we await answers in the brutal quadruple murders of four university students. We will bring you that live with attorney Brian Claypool.\par \par \uc2 Also here, Stephen Moore and Robert Wolf, as Americans dish out more for an inflation filled Thanksgiving feast. Coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well this year's Thanksgiving feast is stuffed full of inflation. The average cost of tomorrow's meal is up 20% compared 