ht?\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: We got one more.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voice-over): OK. We got rolls.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Yes, yes. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I think it's up -- I want to say the rolls are up because I was just at the store a couple days ago, I think they're up 14% because of grain.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Good guess but even more, 22% if you can believe it. Yes. And I was going to end with the good news, which is that one item has gone down a little bit, Trace. And that's a 12 ounce bag of fresh cranberries. That's down 14% from a year ago. We're going to get this cleaned up, and then I'll send it back to you while we get Plinko set up for the next segment.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Enjoy. Happy Thanksgiving. I'm not a big fan of cranberries anyway.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Grady, thank you so much. Let's bring in Stephen Moore, founder of the Committee to Unleash Prosperity, and former senior economic adviser to President Trump and Robert Wolf, former economic adviser to President Obama, founder and CEO of 32 Advisor. He's also a Fox News contributor. Gentlemen, welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 You looked at the meal that Grady was kind of laying it out. And again, the Department of Agriculture really tried to cover all its bases. First, they came out yesterday and said, listen, prices are only up 1% year over year from last year, Steve, right? And then they say, Oh, and if they're up more than that, then just blame Putin, the Putin price hikes. So, you know, getting everything covered, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2  , FORMER TRUMP SENIOR ECONOMIC ADVISER: Yes. And, by the way, I'm one of these people who are skeptical that only cost $60 to feed 10 people for Thanksgiving. You know, that doesn't include the beer and wine, and all of that. But look,--\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: -- one of the things people are doing is buying smaller turkeys this year, because they just can't afford it. People, you know, many Americans are living paycheck to paycheck, the wage increases have been 5% over the last year, the inflation is running at 8%, it's a real problem. And I don't think -- the last thing I'd say is, I don't want to be a Debbie Downer either, but I'm not so sure that we're through with this bout of inflation. I think 2023 we could see continued price increases.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: No. And if they can't figure out this rail strike, man, it is going to be --\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voice-over): Here's how some Americans are cutting back. Americans - - one in four Americans, by the way, on this thing, Robert, plan to pass on Thanksgiving to save money. 88% are now cutting at least one dish, 57% keep the gathering small. And here is the Consumer Brand's associate VP on why prices are still going up. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM MADRECKI, CONSUMER BRANDS ASSOCIATION VP: There's so much happening across the supply chain that's contributing to those higher prices that consumers are facing in the grocery store. Right now, we have to tackle one supply chain crisis at a time, that means putting again the freight rail issue to rest. And then we can go on to the next thing and solve the next crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And, Robert, you look at some cities around the country where things are really high, and 20% is a very low number. I mean, inflation and Thanksgiving meals are pretty high. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR Well, I picked up our stuff today and it was crazy expensive. So believe me hardworking Americans out there, I feel for them. And I know it's a difficult time, during what was supposed to be an incredible festive holiday. That being said, we should not underestimate the impact of the war.\par \par \uc2 When I was president of \uc2 UBS\uc2  , Trace, we were one of the largest traders in commodities. And we know agriculture is a commodity. So let's look at the facts. Russia and Ukraine make up about a third of the exports of wheat. One of the largest and key players with respect to barley, corn fertilizer. So, of course, we were expecting this inflationary impact based on the Russia and Ukraine war. It's a factor was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 Then you add the impact on energy and you have, you know, trucks as the things that move all these goods around diesel is up, you know, pretty dramatically. And then, the last thing is consumer demand hasn't slowed. So there's no question you have in some ways, this perfect storm in a major impact is the war. So when they use the word the Putin hike, it's factual, because agriculture is a traded commodity.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Except, Stephen, he's leaving out the fact that the inflation number was going way up before the war even started. So that's one thing that has to be figured in there is, we were on this --\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Trace, (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We were on the way up, Stephen Moore, after before the war ever started.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, we had 100% inflation.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes, this October.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, wait a minute. Under Trump, over four years inflation rate is only 2%, and is 1.5% the month he left office, even during COVID. And so, you know, the -- here's what's frustrating is, yes, you're right. Why aren't we producing more energy in the United States to counteract what's happened in Ukraine. And the other thing that's interesting, because Fox reported on this earlier this week, Trace, for the first time in like 10 years, the United States is a net importer of food. Why are we producing more because prices are up?\par \par \uc2 By the way, I owe Robert Wolf these $5. I bet on this show four weeks ago that the Republicans would win the Senate. He bet the Democrats So, Robert, this money is in the mail. I just want that to be on the record.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I'm going to tap down on something else.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Man, nicely done. Gentlemen, happy Thanksgiving to both of you. Thank you for the conversation. Well done.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Today is one of the busiest travel days of the year, AAA predicts more than 48 million Americans will hit the road by car this Thanksgiving. Here in California, sales of new gas powered vehicles will be banned by 2035, they think, as the state revs up for an electric future. Fox Weather's Max Gordon is making his way up the I-5 in an electric car from LA to San Francisco. Hey, Max.\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDON, FOX WEATHER: Hey, Trace. So, we are on the road like many Americans today heading off to their Thanksgiving celebrations. I'm sitting shotgun. I've got photographer, Andy Anderson, at the wheel and we just finished charging up. And we're on our way to San Francisco, a journey of about 350 miles.\par \par \uc2 Now, a road trip in an electric vehicle is a little bit different than driving a gas-powered car as you might expect. Now the biggest difference between road tripping in an EV as opposed to driving gas-powered cars that you have to plan out your charging stops. Charging takes longer than gassing up the car, and charging stations aren't as widely available as gas stations, especially in rural areas. You also have to be mindful of weathers' impact on an EV's range. Cold temperatures reduce the number of miles an EV can travel.\par \par \uc2 Still California is charging ahead with its ban on new gas car sales by 2035. Environmental groups are applauding the move saying it will reduce emissions of greenhouse gases linked to climate change. But auto industry experts say that California still has a long way to go before the state is ready for everyone to switch over to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN BOZZELLA, ALLIANCE FOR AUTOMOTIVE INNOVATION: The \uc2 California Energy Commission\uc2  estimates when they do their calculation of an appropriate ratio for vehicles to chargers, suggests that maybe California needs a million more chargers than they have today. And so that gives you a sense of the enormity of the infrastructure challenge. In addition to that, we have to make sure that these vehicles are affordable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Now, price is, of course, a big issue for a lot of folks. The \uc2 Tesla\uc2  Model Y that we're driving today has an MSRP of around $66,000. Now, of course, there are some cheaper options out there but not a lot of economy electric vehicles on the market just yet. Trace, back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And out the door, it's going to run like 85. Max Gordon live for us on the road. Safe travels to you.\par \par \uc2 Any minute now an update from Moscow, Idaho. Police who have yet to name a suspect in the brutal slayings of for university students 10 days ago. Attorney Brian Claypool standing by live for updates, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Police are about to take the podium in Moscow, Idaho for an update on that grisly quadruple murder as they quote look extensively into whether victim Kaylee Goncalves had a stalker, one of the many reports swirling and we expect to get some clarity in moments. Attorney Brian Claypool is here, first to correspondent Dan Springer. He's live on the ground for us in Moscow, Idaho. Dan.\par \par \uc2 DAN SPRINGER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And, Trace, you said at the news conference begins at the top of the hour but not expecting any major breaking developments because the Moscow Police Department just put out a news release saying that there will be no critical updates. You know, they haven't had a news conference since Sunday, and so we've been just trying to get information from them. And what they've largely been doing is shooting down rumors and speculation.\par \par \uc2 As you said there have been some reports that one of the victims had -- may have had a stalker and police say that they have done hundreds of pieces of information that they've gone through, to look at their possibility that someone was stalking Kaylee Goncalves. And they -- after all of that, they say that they are not able to verify or identify any stalker.\par \par \uc2 As you said, we're getting our first look inside the house where the murders happened. This TikTok video shows Ethan Chapin and his girlfriend, Xana Kernodle, in Halloween costumes. And then there was a longer video showing the three roommate victims laughing and playing around. The videos were told were made just days before the murders.\par \par \uc2 As for the investigation appears police may be about done processing the scene for clues, unless more information develops. Yesterday there was no investigative activity at the house for the first time. And this morning just one cop went in -- took what appeared to be a cooler. He left 20 minutes later empty handed. We know an \uc2 FBI\uc2  profiling team is working this case. They have not released any type of profile but other experts not directly working the investigation are weighing in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LISA MILLER, NEUROPSYCHOLOGIST: There it could be the fact that the killer felt slighted by one of them, maybe on a personal level or maybe the killer was resentful of what these kids represent as being successful young people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: So again, the news conference begins momentarily at the top of the hour, but we're expecting no critical updates for the Moscow Police Department. They say that they don't have any other news conferences scheduled at this time. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Dan, back to you if there are updates. Dan Springer live for us on the same. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Attorney Brian Claypool joins me now. I mean, now the families, Brian, you've kind of watched and follow this case. They're getting frustrated. They're starting to criticize some of the police officers because as Dan was saying, there's no information coming out to anybody, even the families. And you would think that if the police had information, they would at least share it with the families. Here's Steve Goncalves, the father of Kaylee. Here's what he said and I'll get your opinion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN ANCHOR: Do you know if police believe one person in the house was the target of this attack, or all four?\par \par \uc2 STEVE GONCALVES, KAYLEE GONCALVES' FATHER: I've been told this one. But then, again, there's the big miss like, it's like purposely vague, I'm hoping but it confuses everyone, because nobody knows what that really means. Other than maybe somebody had a different kind of attack footprint.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Being purposely vague to the families to confuse maybe everybody. What do you think, Brian?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CLAYPOOL, ATTORNEY: Hey, Trace, great to be with you and happy Thanksgiving. Hey, look, this crime is like no other. It is a heinous crime and it warrants differently by law enforcement. Four young people were gruesomely murdered. That's important because public safety risk. This goes beyond just family members, Trace, (inaudible) the community. They need to know what's going on.\par \par \uc2 The university is ready to shut down. People need to know, for example -- been interviewed, why have eliminated -- suspects. It's really important. (Inaudible) looked at the footage inside the bar that they look -- did they interview all of the fraternity members to the girls were at a fraternity party. While (inaudible) the driver of the car that dropped two of the victims off.\par \par \uc2 The public needs this is -- what forensic testing has been done, was there any tissue underneath the fingernails of some of --\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Can you stop this right there (inaudible) you're kind of cutting in and out a little bit. I just want to point out a couple of things on the house, and this is important. We talked about the sliding glass doors. We talked about the sliding glass doors because we're not exactly sure which door that the suspect entered. But you can see the house is built on a slope, so one of the sliding glass doors goes in on the second floor. Two of the victims were on the second floor, two of the victims on the third floor, and the two who lived were on -- were on the first floor, they were not harmed. We'll continue to bring you updates in that.\par \par \uc2 Brian, we'll get back to you. We apologize for the bad audio.\par \par \uc2 While the man suspected of killing six at a Virginia \uc2 Walmart\uc2  has now been identified. And that is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, one body came out in a shopping cart, that from a witness to the shooting at a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  in Virginia. Seven people were killed including the gunman. Survivors say he was a store manager. The national correspondent Griff Jenkins is live in Chesapeake, Virginia with more on this. Griff, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Trace. Investigators are busy at work. I'll step quickly out of the way and you can see the heavy presence of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents here in the parking lot of that \uc2 Walmart\uc2  . We're learning now, authorities identifying the alleged gunman is Andre Bing of Chesapeake, Virginia. 31 years old, he was a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  associate since 2010, and was a overnight team leader with no criminal history. But he entered the break room and began to take lives. In the end seven total fatalities. That includes the gunman, Bing.\par \par \uc2 What we don't know though, Trace, is what was the motive? The Chesapeake Police chief says he simply doesn't have that answer yet. Take a listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can you confirm or do you know these attacks were targeted against any specific employees or this individual shooting at random?\par \par \uc2 MARK SOLESKY, CHESAPEAKE, VA POLICE CHIEF: We don't know at this time. The investigation is still ongoing, so there's no clear motive at this time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: This tragedy has left the community on edge. Here in the parking lot, we can show you a video of a memorial that has been erected, honoring the victims written on that heart is the name of Tanika Johnson. She's 22 years old. She is one of the six deceased victims. And the governor of Virginia, Glenn Younkin, by the way, ordering flags, Trace, to be lowered across the state as they mourn before Thanksgiving. Trace, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Griff, I understand we're hearing the gunman actually took his own life. Do we know if he took his own life while police were coming in or is that not clear yet?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Authorities say, Trace, he took him took his own life, self- inflicted gunshot wound before. And when they entered the building at 10:16 PM last night, they found him deceased. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Griff Jenkins live for us in Chesapeake, Virginia. Now on to another murder scene, we're talking about a press conference, this one in Moscow, Idaho in Northern Idaho. We are about to get an update and these have been rare. They haven't given an update since Sunday. Dan Springer, who's on the ground there, has just informed us that he does not believe anything monumental will come out of this, but we're hoping to get a little bit more information on maybe potential suspects.\par \par \uc2 The families are frustrated because there has been such little information. We had been reporting now for 10 days about the fact that inside that house, it was a bloody mess. There was evidence everywhere. How much and how exceptional the evidence is, is there DNA, does it point to anybody, is there a motive, was this targeted, was this random. A lot of answers -- I mean, a lot of questions very few answers.\par \par \uc2 That is THE STORY of Wednesday November 23rd. But as always, THE STORY goes on. Be sure to join me for Fox News at Night, midnight on the East, 9:00 PM Pacific. "YOUR WORLD" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221124eibn00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Alejandro Mayorkas Urged To Resign Or Risk Impeachment; First Day In Court For FTX Bankruptcy Case; Rail Looms Strike After \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Hailed Tentative Deal; Revisiting \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 's Comments Amid COVID Pandemic; Warnings Of Potential World Cup Terror Attack; Hero Emerges From Colorado Springs Nightclub Shooting As Veteran Who Tackled And Disarmed Gunman; Police Still Have No Suspect In Murders Of Four University Of Idaho Students; Chinese Government Accused Of Setting Up Stations Across World To Intimidate Chinese Dissidents; House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy Visits U.S. Southern Border; Dr. Anthony Issues Last Press Briefing At White House Podium\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Bill Melugin, Kelly O'Grady, Peter Doocy, Marc Siegel,  Dr. Marty Makary, Jennifer Griffin, Claudia Cowan, Dan Springer, Bryan  Llenas \par \uc2 7,592 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. 