ning, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. Just a short time ago, GOP Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy issued an ultimatum to DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas, resign or face possible impeachment from a Republican controlled House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): GOP Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy and the delegation of House Republicans in El Paso today touring the busiest sector along the border and meeting with frontline Border Patrol agents.\par \par \uc2 At one point during their tour, a makeshift camp of Venezuelan migrants yelled to them from across the river in Mexico. At the conclusion of the tour, Leader McCarthy held a press conference where he took aim at DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Today, I am calling on the secretary to resign. If Secretary Mayorkas does not resign, House Republicans will investigate every order, every action and every failure will determine whether we can begin impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: The GOP border visit caught the attention of the White House and El Paso's Democratic Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: McCarthy has no plan. The Republican Party has no plan. They do nothing except do political stunts.\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR (D-TX): Republicans are doing what they always do, they parachute in. They throw temper tantrums and they offer zero solutions.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: According to CBP, the El Paso sector saw more than 53,000 illegal crossings in October alone, a 280 percent increase over the year prior.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has seen multiple breaches in the border wall in this sector, breaches migrants used to sneak into El Paso illegally.\par \par \uc2 Fox's cameras have also repeatedly witnessed groups of illegal immigrants crossing and successfully evading Border Patrol here, running and escaping into residential neighborhoods, sometimes with no Border Patrol agents around, making them presumed gotaways.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, McCarthy went on to tell me that Secretary Mayorkas has until January 3rd to make a decision on possible resignation. He also said that if he is elected as speaker, and once Republicans take control in January, what he's planning on doing is actually holding a border hearings physically down here at our southern border, in an effort to force Democrats to come down here themselves and actually see this crisis firsthand. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That would be something. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Bankruptcy proceedings are underway now in the largest collapse during the short history of cryptocurrency. These come amid the question of whether the embattled CEO of FTX will be extradited from the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we're learning additional details of Sam Bankman-Fried's lavish lifestyle. Fox Business Correspondent Kelly O'Grady is in Wilmington, Delaware with the latest tonight. Good evening, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. Yes, today's hearing revealing more details in the case that the new FTX CEO is calling worse than \uc2 Enron\uc2 . One FTX lawyer characterizing the fallout as a different sort of animal and sharing this, saying: "What we have is a worldwide organization that was run effectively as a personal fiefdom of Sam Bankman-Fried. What we had was a lack of corporate controls at a level that none of us in the profession have looked at and so far has ever seen."\par \par \uc2 While lawyer shared they've now recovered a total of 1.24 billion, the bigger revelations coming in where all the billions have gone.\par \par \uc2 First, FTX highlighting that a substantial amount of assets were either stolen or missing. The company is continuing to fight off cyber-attacks that began the day it filed for bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 And more shocking, 300 million in corporate funds being used to purchase homes and vacation properties in the Bahamas for the company's senior staff, the images that you're seeing are of the disgraced founder's $40 million mansion. And with lawyers today sharing creditors will likely see just a fraction of their dollar, calls are intensifying for Bankman-Fried to be brought back from the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 The extradition treaty requires charges though, so that could be difficult given the case's complicated nature.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW WHITAKER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL: I worry having spent, you know, several years at the Department of Justice that, you know, there's very few people that actually understand cryptocurrency and these type of financial markets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: A legal experts telling us these types of fraud cases often hinge on proving implicit criminal intent and rushing charges could lead to them being thrown out, so agencies often take time to first untangle the dynamics.\par \par \uc2 That's a reality also facing creditors who are hoping to recover some of those lost funds. The lawyers here saying that it could be months before they're able to dig through it all, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kelly O'Grady live in Wilmington, Delaware. Kelly, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Now to the prospect of a nationwide railway strike at the start of the holiday season. A tentative deal from a few weeks ago has apparently fallen apart now. President Biden took credit for that agreement then, and there are new questions about his involvement this time.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has details live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. This president is an outspoken supporter of unions and this president is an outspoken supporter of trains. But so far, he is not taking the train worker union's side in this labor dispute that could bring supply chains to a crippling halt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: We have to prevent a rail shutdown.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): But we don't know how involved they are at the White House talking to unions or rail companies.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The president is indeed involved directly. But I don't want to get into details at this time.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Four of 12 unions have voted down a tentative agreement. The largest is SMART-TD whose members want more protections to improve quality of life.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY FERGUSON, PRESIDENT, SMART TRANSPORTATION DIVISION: Yes, a strike could be averted. The ball is back in the carrier's hands at this point and we're back in their court.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The White House celebrated, taking credit for stopping this strike in September.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: A tentative agreement, it was a big deal.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Now the unions may have the leverage.\par \par \uc2 FERGUSON: We sure surely want to help Santa Claus get those gifts delivered for Christmas.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: COVID no longer the all-encompassing crisis. So, the White House is saying goodbye to a retiring Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: One last time, one more time at the podium.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He's not going far, anticipating multiple congressional subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 NATIONAL INSTITUTE OF ALLERGY AND INFECTIOUS DISEASES\uc2 : I absolutely will cooperate fully and testify before the Congress. I have nothing to hide.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He's long dismissed criticism.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: They're really criticizing science. Because I represent science. That's dangerous.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Although criticism was usually about his guidance like on masks. He eventually supported mandates after starting here.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Right now, people should not be worried, there's no reason to be walking around with a mask.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And on businesses like closing bars and schools.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Right now, we have to start implementing both containment and mitigation. And what was done when you do closing the school, this mitigation.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He's unconcerned about learning loss.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: I don't think it's forever irreparably damaged anyone.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And his guidance about COVID dating back to its earliest days was inconsistent at best. Upon reflection, he makes no apologies.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: We were not dealing with a static situation. We're dealing with a dynamic situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): The presidential motorcade is staging behind us. The first family is about to head to Nantucket for Thanksgiving where the Bidens may have some potentially historic discussions because we have long been told that over the holidays, the family is going to discuss a reelection bid or not, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Peter, a couple of things. Why is President Biden extending the student loan debt pause?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Because it's something that he can control whereas he cannot control what the courts are going to do to the challenges of these student loan payment lawsuits. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It isn't fair to ask tens of millions of borrowers eligible for relief to resume their student debt payments, while the courts consider the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, now these payments are not going to resume until June 30th. That is six months later than the president said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Student loan payment pause is going to end. It's going to end December 30th. I'm extending it to December 31st 2022. And it's going to end at that time. It's time for the payments to resume.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There was no wiggle room in that statement. He said the payments needed to resume this year. Now he says they don't, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On the COVID issue, is the White House now saying that conversations with Chinese President Xi did talk about COVID origins?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Yes, it was just about a week ago we were sitting there in evening in Bali, Indonesia on the sidelines of the G20, President Biden came out to talk about his meeting with Xi, never said anything about COVID origins, never said anything about fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 This evening, a White House official reached out to say they did talk about the importance of China being transparent when it comes to COVID. And they also spoke about Counter Narcotics efforts, including fentanyl. They're filling in some big gaps to the official historical record of that meeting a week later, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Very interesting. Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight, Dr. \uc2 Marty Makary\uc2 , Johns Hopkins health policy expert, Fox News contributor, and Dr. Marc Siegel, professor of medicine at \uc2 New York University Langone Medical Center\uc2 , Fox News contributor. I wanted to get these two docs together to talk about Dr. Fauci on his last day as he leaves the White House and the White House podium. And that team.\par \par \uc2 You know, first of all, 30,000 feet to both of you, Dr. Siegel first, what do you think \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 's legacy will be?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MEDICAL ANALYST: Well, I think Peter Doocy set it up -- set it up nicely already, which is the issue is that he reversed himself a lot, that he didn't have a consistent policy, that he basically was very slow to consider the collateral damage done by lockdowns and shutdowns and we're seeing that now, not just in terms of schools, and learning problems, but also in terms of emerging viruses, immune systems that are paused, we're seeing a big outbreak of RSV and flu because we didn't consider what masks and lockdowns were going to do there.\par \par \uc2 And again, he never said a consistent policy of what the damage would be.\par \par \uc2 Bret, the other point is, and this came up at the press conference today is I'm not satisfied on the COVID origins thing. Because I've interviewed Dr. Redfield on this, who has -- who has a lot of top secret information. And they've never really put to bed the issue of lab origins at all. And Dr. Fauci has been very rigid on the lab origins issue, which bothers me a lot because he's supported an international consortium of scientists to look at gain of function research where we test a virus to see how far it can go, which may very well have been the origin of the COVID pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And so, there's a debate on that, but I don't think anyone should have ridiculed him or, you know, made chatter during the press conference today. I think he should be treated with respect. But he is definitely behind the side that never considered when the Genie is out of the bottle, what happens?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And we on SPECIAL REPORT were among the first to cover some of the early back and forth on the COVID origin story.\par \par \uc2 I want to play a little bit more of that sound bite. You heard some of it in Peter's piece on the changing science. This is Dr. Fauci today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: There was really a difference early on in the first weeks, two months of the outbreak, because we will -- we were dealing with truly a moving target.\par \par \uc2 When you're dealing with an evolving outbreak where the information you get changes from week to week and month to month, we've got to probably do a better job of when we talk to the public explain that this is a dynamic situation that could change. I've said that from this podium multiple times.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. Makary, how do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARTY MAKARY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Dr. Fauci's primary job is to fund research on infectious diseases. And the research we got was tragically late and inappropriate many times. For example, it was an airborne virus, yet, we didn't have the research to tell us it was airborne.\par \par \uc2 And so for months, people were told to wash their hands like crazy and we gave people false assurance that that would mitigate the spread.\par \par \uc2 His primary job is to fund research. We didn't see that research with natural immunity with school universal masking, and with many of the blanket recommendations like boosting young people. So, that is I think the primary failure of Dr. Fauci.\par \par \uc2 Now, he's had some contributions in public health over the last 50 years, and even during COVID, I think he did encourage high risk people to get vaccinated and warn seniors of the risk of the virus and both of those were valuable contributions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to get you both to weigh in about the risks going forward. Obviously, there's an uptick in RSV especially among children, respiratory infections. There are efforts to in some places, Dr. Siegel, put mask mandates back in. Where do you see this going forward? Look into the crystal ball.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: I don't like the mandates, and I never have. I like vaccines a lot. As you know, I'm very pro vaccine. And I think the mRNA vaccine has made a major contribution. I think it's been given to billions safely. I think their side effects are very, very low.\par \par \uc2 But you're talking about severity, decreasing severity, I want people to have immunity on board, I want them to have immunity wherever they get it from the vaccine or recent infection.\par \par \uc2 We went way too late to consider a recent infection as a form of immunity. Dr. Makary, and I've been talking about that for a year and a half already.\par \par \uc2 So, going forward, we need public health compliance without mocking people, without ridiculing people, without fear mongering, without making people feel bad. We've set vaccine compliance back with the kind of political divisiveness we've had.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. Makary, your kind of crystal ball looking forward, there's obviously still concerned with some people with long COVID as well.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: I think right now we've had a one hammer approach and that has been a COVID zero policy. And if you look at what that's done, it's resulted in significant lifelong learning loss. And we've still not really accepted the data from Europe that the schools were open, free and clear, and the kids were without masks the entire time, and they did not have significant increases in transmission.\par \par \uc2 My fear is that we've seen the politicalization of this virus because we didn't have that research that Dr. Fauci should have funded very expeditiously to answer the big questions.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, people don't trust public health officials. The new bivalent Omicron vaccine is a classic example. Only about 10 percent of Americans have received it and the data to support it is not even public except for minimal data.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. Makary, Dr. Siegel, we appreciate your time. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Happy Thanksgiving. Stocks were up on solid earnings reports today, the Dow jumped 398, the S&P 500 finished ahead 54, the NASDAQ rose 150.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  testified today before a special grand jury investigating whether then President Trump and others illegally meddled in the 2020 election in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Senator Graham's office says he spent just over two hours with the panel, answered all their questions. Graham went all the way to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  in efforts to try to avoid testifying.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , the court cleared the way for the imminent handover of former President Trump's tax returns to a congressional committee after a three year legal fight.\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court justices rejected former President Trump's plea for an order that would have prevented the Treasury Department from giving six years of tax returns to the Democratic controlled House Ways and Means Committee.\par \par \uc2 Up next, is the World Cup being threatened by Iranian backed terrorists? We'll look at the evidence and the threat stream.\par \par \uc2 And later, why is China establishing a so- called service stations, some call them police stations here inside the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The first big upset in the World Cup. Do you see this? Saudi Arabia stunning Argentina 2-1 in the opener for both teams. Really the biggest upset we've seen in the World Cup in a long long time. The U.S. is preparing to play England Friday, the American settled for a draw against Wales yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Iran has begun enriching uranium at an increasingly high purity, its latest move in the ongoing international tug of war over its nuclear program. And we have new information tonight on a story we broke last night concerning Iranian affiliated terrorists, they may try to disrupt the World Cup according to U.S. intelligence officials.\par \par \uc2 Here's Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Explosive claims from Israeli military intelligence that the powder keg situation in Iran could trigger "aggressive responses in the region and in the world, including disrupting the World Cup in Qatar".\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. AHARON HALIVA, ISRAELI INTELLIGENCE CHIEF (through translator): I am