bomb an oil tanker link to an Israeli billionaire last week.\par \par \uc2 The attack on the Liberian flagged oil tanker off the coast of Oman appears to be part of the long running shadow war between Israel and its arch enemy Iran. That has included the targeting of Israeli linked ships in strategic Mideast waterways.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest information in the investigations into mass shootings in Colorado and Idaho.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, Indonesian rescuers sift through the rubble of flattened buildings as they search for the dead and missing from Monday's earthquake that killed at least 268 people. More than a thousand people were injured. Hospitals near the epicenter are already overwhelmed. Some remote areas are still unreachable.\par \par \uc2 Six former executives of a now defunct Hong Kong pro-democracy newspaper pled guilty to a collusion charge. They were arrested last year during a crackdown on dissent after China imposed a sweeping security law in response to widespread anti-government protests in 2019.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London. One of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  welcome South Africa's president to London for the first state visit of his reign.\par \par \uc2 The trip include a formal banquet as well as talks with government leaders focused on investment and green energy.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Republicans have picked up another seat in the House. David Valadao has won reelection in his Central Valley, California, seat overcoming fallout from his vote to impeach then President Trump. The GOP now has 219 seats in the House, one over the majority 218 need to take control. Democrats have 212. That leaves four undecided at this point, one in Colorado, the Alaska ranked choice election, and two races in California, a state that as of this morning has more than a half million votes still to count this far from Election Day.\par \par \uc2 A hero has emerged from Saturday's tragic Colorado Springs nightclub massacre. He is an Army veteran who tackled and disarmed the suspected gunman, possibly saving many lives. Senior correspondent Claudia Cowan is in Colorado Springs tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FIERRO, NIGHTCLUB PATRON: We were out having a great time, a beautiful place, beautiful people. And it was fun, until this guy decided to turn our dream into a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Former field artillery officer Richard Fierro detailing the drama today on FOX when he went from partying on the dancefloor with his family into full combat mode after a gunman burst into Club Q and opened fire, tackling him to the ground from behind and pistol whipping the shooter with his own gun while a drag queen stomped on his face and other patrons pinned him down until police arrived.\par \par \uc2 FIERRO: We just started hitting the guy, man. That's all you can do. You've got to protect everybody around you. At that point everybody in that room was family to me.\par \par \uc2 COWAN: Amid the chaos, arriving officers detained Fierro who was covered in blood but soon realized he almost certainly saved lives by subduing the assailant, identified as 22-year-old Anderson Aldrich, as new details emerge about his troubled past. According to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," he was born Nicholas Brink, but after being bullied as a teenager while living in Texas, he legally changed his name and moved to Colorado. His mother also had run-ins with the law. No one from his family has spoken publicly.\par \par \uc2 But those who lost loved ones in the shooting are sharing photos and tributes to remember Raymond Vance, Kelly Loving, Ashley Paugh, Daniel Aston, and Derek Rump, the victims ranging in age from 22 to 40. At least 19 other people were injured in the shooting that has shattered this city.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 COWAN (on camera): Many are finding comfort by mourning together at this memorial outside the club as they wait for police to determine a motive behind Saturday's attack. Aldrich is now being held at the El Paso County jail and could be formally charged next week. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Claudia Cowan in Colorado Springs. Claudia, thank you. The suspect will be making his first court appearance tomorrow via video link.\par \par \uc2 A memorial service was held last night for one of the victims of the University of Idaho student slayings. This evening, we are seeing images from inside the building where the killings took place. Correspondent Dan Springer is in Moscow, Idaho, again tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAN SPRINGER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: For the first time we're getting a look inside the house where four University of Idaho students were slaughtered more than a week ago. Three of the victims who rented the house made a TikTok video imitating each other just days before their murders. Today there was no police activity in or around the crime scene which is still taped off. Investigators from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Idaho state police did briefly expand the crime scene Monday.\par \par \uc2 But there is still no suspect, no murder weapon, and no answer to the question, if this was a targeted attack, who was the killer's target and why? Former assistant \uc2 FBI\uc2  director Chris Swecker was on "FOX & FRIENDS FIRST" saying this could be a tough, long investigation.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS SWECKER, FORMER FBI ASSISTANT DIRECTOR: After over 90 interviews no one has surfaced as having a motive or the means or the capability to do that. So that suggests to me that this may have been a random or a target of opportunity if you will, which are some of the hardest crimes to solve.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: In Mount Vernon, Washington, the first victim memorial service was held. Hundreds gathered to remember 20-year-old Ethan Chapin. His mother addressed the media.\par \par \uc2 STACY CHAPIN, VICTIM'S MOTHER: They were here to honor the life and legacy of our son and brother Ethan Chapin, one of the most incredible people you will ever know.\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: Stacey Chapin also thanked Moscow police for, in her words, carrying the burden for all impacted families. Police did shoot down one of the theories some were circulating. In a news release titled "Rumor Control," police said a dog that was skinned a month ago three miles away was unrelated, and other dead dogs found were wildlife activity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER (on camera): As for information that is related to this shocking crime, police remain tight-lipped. Hopefully they will share more of what they do know at a rare news conference set for tomorrow afternoon. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dan Springer in Moscow, Idaho. Dan, thank you. We are learning just in the past few minutes that there has been some minimal police activity. Officers removed two boxes from that scene. We'll continue to follow this story.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why is China establishing so-called service stations, or police stations some are calling them, in cities around the world and allegedly meddling in elections, including here in the U.S.? We will take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YAN XIONG, CHINESE DISSIDENT: This is the USA. They have not any reason or authority to interfere in the U.S. election. That should be zero tolerance.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is accusing China of intimidation and coercion in the disputed South China Sea. She made that comment in a speech from the Philippines. Back here at home, growing concerns over what are being termed service stations opened by China in many cities, including New York. The head of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says he is concerned about what is going on inside these facilities. Republicans are pressing for answers from President Biden. Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Chinatown tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ, (R-FL): We have to wake up to the fact that this is happening. It's happening right in our backyard.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Republicans like Florida Representative Michael Waltz are demanding the Biden administration investigate China's latest efforts to spy on Chinese dissidents in the U.S. and around the world. According to the human rights organization Safeguard Defenders, China's communist regime has established at least 54 so-called police service stations in cities across more than a dozen countries as a means to threaten, intimidate, and control Chinese dissidents.\par \par \uc2 LAURA HARTH, SAFEGUARD DEFENDERS: These particular campaigns of persuading people to return against their will, forcing them return to China through fully illegal means.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Here in New York City's Chinatown, an illegal police service station is allegedly operating out of this building. The Chinese government says it's the America ChangLe Association, a nonprofit blacklisted by the \uc2 IRS\uc2  dedicating to helping Chinese nationals with bureaucratic affairs like renewing driver's licenses.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  says he is concerned and investigating.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: It is outrageous to think that the Chinese police would attempt to set up shop in New York, let's say, without proper coordination.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Yan Xiong, a U.S. veteran and famous Chinese dissident, has been the target of Chinese spies. Earlier this year the \uc2 FBI\uc2  arrested a Chinese government agent who tried to derail Xiong's his political congressional campaign through intimidation. Xiong says Chinese residents were afraid to support him.\par \par \uc2 YAN XIONG, CHINESE DISSIDENT (through translator): This is a tragic thing. They all live in the United States and yet are still afraid of CCP in China. This is proof that the CCP's arm has reached too far.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: In New York, Bryan Llenas, FOX News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel on President Biden's new reality concerning the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 First, hers' what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 436 in Charlotte where authorities say a pilot and a meteorologist from another North Carolina television station are dead following the crash of the station's helicopter next to an interstate highway. Police say no vehicles were involved in that incident. Preliminary witness accounts indicate the pilot made diversionary maneuvers to avoid injuring anyone else.\par \par \uc2 FOX 10 in Phoenix as a popular hiking destination in the Grand Canyon has been renamed. Bright Angel Trail was offensive to some people. It is now called Havasupai Gardens after the tribe that once populated the area and was forcibly removed as the park was developed.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Kansas City from FOX 4, our affiliate there. One of the big stories there tonight, a winning lottery ticket from northeast Kansas remains unclaimed two days after being drawn. The Powerball grand prize jackpot was almost $93 million at the time. It's the first time the jackpot has been hit in Kansas since 2012. Get those tickets. Check them out.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Together we bear witness to the worst border crisis in U.S. history. Now that Title 42 may end, we are bracing for a tsunami. Today, I am calling on the secretary to resign.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: What is his plan? He goes down there and he does a political stunt, like many Republicans do, that we have seen them do. But he actually is not putting forth a plan.\par \par \uc2 We have put a solution to this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The White House responding to Minority Leader now, Kevin McCarthy at the U.S. southern border calling for the resignation of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas. We should point out that President Biden has not visited the border, as pressed by the White House press secretary today.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, "THE FIVE" co-host Jessica Tarlov, and Jonathan Swan, national political reporter for "Axios." Jonathan, obviously, there is some element to this that Leader McCarthy is positioning himself to be House Speaker McCarthy, and the possibility of impeaching Secretary Mayorkas may appeal to some folk that he needs votes from. But, yet, the border crisis and the border security issue is a make vulnerability, it seems, for the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SWAN, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": It's probably the most unifying issue for Republicans across the conference. You're right, Kevin McCarthy does have a problem on his right flank. He is trying to become speaker. He doesn't have the votes right now, and he is looking for issues that can appeal to them but also not going to alienate their many moderates in his conference as well, including some newly elected ones.\par \par \uc2 But look, the border numbers started to rise precipitously after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office. That's just a fact. And they hit record highs. And this is a problem the administration has not been able to solve. So, yes, it's very fertile ground, but it's also a unifying issue for Republicans and Kevin McCarthy. It does need to be seen through the context of him trying to get the votes to become speaker.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Jessica, the pushback from the White House is that what is their plan? Their plan is security first, and then you can talk about other issues going forward. But the White House isn't really pitching hard on this issue, it doesn't seem. The president is not making a trip to the border.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": No. It hasn't really felt like it's been much of a priority, and there was, going back to the 2018 midterms where the Democrats did incredibly well, and we thought that it was going to be an election about the border and what is going on there and that the caravan was coming. And then it ended up the Democrats did just fine. And they did the same thing again in these midterms where they essentially punted on the issue, and Democrats did just fine. I would have loved to keep the House, but that wasn't in the cards.\par \par \uc2 But you do see a shift in tone. If you saw Mark Kelly give an interview to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " where he talked about this issue, and he said that Democrats absolutely do not have a grasp on what is going on at the border, but Republicans don't have a good solve for it. Just saying build a wall is not going to be solution. And then there are other Democrats like \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  who might mount a challenge to \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  for her seat in 2024 down in Arizona as well, have been having some pretty harsh words for the Biden administration in how they have been handled it. So I expect that this will be more of an issue for Democrats, but I still think, to Jonathan's point, that this is going to be something that is largely discussed on the GOP side of things.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of the GOP side, Representative Katko who we've had on on this immigration issue wrote, "Biden team flunking security test, the border, China, and more show White House not up to the job." He writes, "The administration continues to get bogged down in political posturing and the semantics of the crisis while failing to acknowledge the litany of threats stemming from criminals, drugs, and individuals on the terror watchlist crossing our border in record numbers. We must stop talking past each other on this issue." The terror watchlist, Guy, is a big issue. There's some 40 plus that were on that list apprehended in October.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: That we know of, because also in October there were most 64,000 known got-aways, and we have no idea who was included in that group of people, which is just not a sustainable situation for this country from any perspective really, from a national sovereignty perspective, from a national security perspective, from a public safety perspective, from a fairness perspective for people who are trying to come to this country the right way through legal means.\par \par \uc2 And Jessica might be right that among some Democrats the tone is shifting a little bit, but certainly not from the White House. We heard there from \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  that this is a political stunt going to the border. Apparently, any means, any effort to draw attention to the border crisis in the eyes of the White House is a stunt. And she mused, she asked allowed, what's the Republican plan? Just going back to the end of the Trump administration and those policies, which were working relatively well, that's the plan. They refuse to do it. Their policies are the dead opposite of what the Trump people finally landed on, and the results have been catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As we mentioned earlier, today was Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 's last day, we believe, at the White House podium. At least he said that. And it got heated at one point as the questions focused on the origins of COVID. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What have you done personally to investigate the origins of COVID?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Hold on one second. We have a process here. I'm not calling out on people who yell. And you are being -- you are being disrespectful to your colleagues and you're being disrespectful to our guest.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She has a question. She is asking about the origin of COVID --\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I hear the question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Dr. Fauci is the best person to answer the question.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I hear your question, but we are not doing this the way you want it. This is disrespectful -- it is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is --\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: It's done. Simon, I'm done. Simon, I'm done. I'm done with you right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, so a little bit of process back and forth. That happens sometimes. I have been in that White House press briefing room. But on the issue of COVID, when you look back 30,000 feet, Jonathan, what do you take away in this administration's dealing of it and as Fauci gets ready to leave?\par \par \uc2 SWAN: Well, Biden, one of their biggest -- if you talk to Biden officials, one of the biggest regrets that they have is effectively declaring victory over the virus last year in that speech. I think it was the July 4th independence from the virus speech. So they came in with very, very grand promises of getting the country past the virus, and then they made that big speech. And then, of course, it really hung around, and a lot of people died.\par \par \uc2 So, it's been complicated. They haven't -- it hasn't been a perfect record particularly with vaccines in terms of getting the latest technology. And it's a mixed record, but I do think they have done their best under very trying circumstances. I think the communication to the public has left a lot to be desired, particularly in the CDC. But I think that some of this has been very difficult because of the extremely polarized information landscape they are working within.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And quickly, Jessica, just listening to people and how they are going to personally deal with COVID, it is different now than it was at the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: People have moved on, right? This is as close, I think, to normal life as we are going to get. And \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 's last words were please be up to date with your shots and your boosters, and I get all that. But the core of the argument that I see playing out at least in terms of politics is, are we going to have an investigation into the origins of COVID? And that always seems like a gimme for Democrats, that we should just say yes, of course, we should understand what happened here. We knew that all western democracies are involved in gain of function research. It's not going to fall back on us.\par \par \uc2 And I think that we can say \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , that's a little ridiculous, but understanding why millions of people died of this plague seems like a good idea.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, Republicans think both of those investigations are important, but we will follow.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, but they can't get their way on everything.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's right. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 All right, it's time for "Tuesday Tweets." First up, Mike asks "If Trump doesn't receive GOP support, does he run as independent and split the ticket?" That's a really good question. It's a question that I asked in the first debate for candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  long, long ago. But we don't know what former President Trump is doing other than trying to win the GOP nomination as of now.\par \par \uc2 Next up, Julie Shera wants to know, "What is your favorite family tradition?" My family tries to do some exercise thing, like a turkey trot or something in the morning. We're going to try to do that, too.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Old Man asks, "I would like to know where Chip is. What really happens when the turkey when cameras leave?" Maybe Jesse should put on this one, and be put on this one to find out. Chocolate and Chip, those were the pardoned turkeys, I agree with you. I want to know what happens to them. Where do they go? What happens to those turkeys? We're going to look into it, or Jesse will.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us, for SPECIAL REPORT. My all-star panel podcast, you can get that, "Bret Baier Podcast." Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. Jesse, what do you think, figure out where the turkeys really go?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221123eibm00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Price For A Thanksgiving Feast Went Up By 20 Percent And There Could Be A Possible Rail Strike In The Next Two Weeks After The Union Rejects A Labor Deal; Authorities In Idaho Are Still Looking For The Suspect Who Brutally Stabbed For University Of Idaho Students While In Their Sleep; Charlamagne Tha God Talks About The Future Of The Democratic Party If They Still Support President Biden And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  In 2024 And That Trump Or DeSantis Could Win; Charlamagne Issues Grim Forecast For Democrats; Biden WH Ready To Battle GOP's Hunter Probe; All-American Tree Lighting: THE FIVE And Fox News Media Kick Off Christmas Season\b0\par\par\uc2 Jesse Watters, Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford, Jr., Dana Perino, Brian  Kilmeade \par \uc2 8,537 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS NEWS HOST: But I really appreciate this conversation with you, Hadley. Have a great Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 HADLEY HEATH MANNING, INDEPENDENT WOMEN'S VOICE POLICY ANALYST: Sure. Thanks, Charles. You too.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks. Thank you for watching us. Remember to catch me tomorrow at Fox Business "Making Money." Pretty good day for the market today, and maybe tomorrow. This is one of the best weeks generally in history. For now, here is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford, Jr., Dana Perino, and Brian Kilmeade. Five o'clock in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's managed to ruin Thanksgiving again. No, he's not inviting Hunter to your family gathering this year. And the big guy himself will be kicking back in Nantucket for the holiday. It's because the price to your feast is going to cost you an arm and a turkey leg.\par \par \uc2 The average cost of a Thanksgiving dinner is up 20 percent this year. But don't worry, Brian can afford it. Biden's goons at the \uc2 USDA\uc2  are blaming Russia for the rising cost. And if that's not bad enough, there could be crippling rail strike in December that could literally slam the brakes on the economy and cost $2 billion per day. But wait I remember Biden bragging about how this wasn't a problem anymore.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: This agreement is a big win for America. Together we have reached an agreement -- you reached an agreement that will keep our critical rail system working and avoid disruptions of our economy. And this is a win for tens of thousands of rail workers and for their dignity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The White House trying to spin their failure to avert the strike by pointing out how they kicked the can down the road.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: That we're saying is that it averted a horrible downturn in our economy by that -- that tentative agreement. It was a big deal. What it could have done to our economy would have been devastating. It was an important -- it was an important -- the tentative agreement was indeed important, again, because it stopped -- it's stopped the horrible potential downturn, again, to our economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Do you want to comment on that explanation or would you like to talk about price utilities?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: I think sometimes it's just good enough to say yeah, it didn't hold and that's unfortunate and we got to move on instead of trying to say like how great it was. I mean, it was a political Band-Aid that got them through the midterms and it gave them a Rose Garden event so that they could go out and say, oh, everybody's fine. Thank God \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's here. Thank God he's a deal maker. Thank goodness he has \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  and \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  on his cabinet, and like, crisis averted.\par \par \uc2 Just enough to get through the midterms. Similar -- similarly, the student loan crisis, right? He says I'm going to give everyone the student loan thing and then if he gets through the midterms and like actually, no, it's not constitutional, but sorry about that. They try to do it with the Saudis on the loan deal, right? They tried to ask the Saudis to not -- well, to increase their production. Saudi said no. They tried to do that, we know that they tried to do it and said can you just wait until after the election.\par \par \uc2 Well, now it's after the election and I -- it'll be interesting to see how Republicans and Democrats both play this, right? Because in the political realignment we're living in, both parties are trying to go after the working class. And so, this rail strikers, they're close on a deal. They're saying that they want to have a better quality of life. They want more time with their family.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it looks like they're getting the 24 percent raise in the deal. They say that's pennies on the dollar compared to the amount of time that they spend. So, where is everybody going to come down on this. I think that part is pretty interesting to see, but just be aware of the political Band-Aids. Oh, and there's another one by the way.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you have a student loan you don't have to pay your student loan through probably of August of 2023, but if you took out a loan for an F-150 so you could run your business, you're going to have to make your payment, sorry.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: People will need a loan for the Thanksgiving dinner. It's up 20 percent. Remember when they had that little July 4th picture where they were like we saved you three cents? Are we going to see that for Thanksgiving?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm not sure but there was a story out there trying to validate it that people are buying big chickens instead of big turkeys.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Big chicken?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right. Because chickens are, you know, it's less money. So, we'll see where that stands. But inflation is just interesting. I did not know it would end up back at \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's lap. So, we're back blaming \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . I forgot about that and Nord Stream 1 and \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2 . But there's a couple of things going on.\par \par \uc2 There's two loan forgiveness. This is ongoing. How can he do that? Just kicks it up for six more months, which is great but if you would took out a loan now when you have a freshman in college now, you're not eligible for it. If you graduate now, you don't have to pay for it. I just never -- I've never seen anything quite like this.\par \par \uc2 And this whole thing with inflation and the turkey dinner, I think it's basically emblematic of what's been going on. The President says seven percent is not a big deal because it didn't go up to eight percent, but we're all paying more for everything and it doesn't seem to matter. If this was the week before the midterms, I would say this is a big deal. But after the midterms when you see just a slight win for Republicans, I don't know what resonates.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And you know, when you go out to dinner, Harold, it's -- the food does cost a lot, but it's the alcohol bill that really gets you. And that's not a personal statement.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD, JR., FOX NEWS HOST: So, I think -- so, I think -- I think this whole notion, Brian, you -- I think you made the most important point. As we think about inflation and Biden inflation -- no, don't -- I think Dana framed this all the right way. Her reaction -- her reaction about the press secretary, I thought was right. She should just move on from it so that we -- we thought we had a deal, we don't have a deal. At least we have some framework and we hope we can get something here.\par \par \uc2 But this -- this -- for whatever reason it has not resonated politically with the country and it is not moving -- it resonates but it doesn't move them to vote for the other part. They've not assigned blame, a sufficient blame to Democrats on this. And I've struggled with trying to figure this out too.\par \par \uc2 Part of me thinks that it's because a couple of things. One, people are -- people still have some reserve cash from whatever that time may be. It could have been some of the stimulus that took place back in '20 and '21 or two, people have decided to prioritize things differently and they're not - - they're not finding themselves having to sacrifice in their lives.\par \par \uc2 Whatever it is, it's a new normal as it relates to our economy because if we are in a recession, we've never seen a recession where you're producing jobs that we're producing, where you have consumer sentiment elevated not as high as it was and people still spending money. So, something is happening in our economy and something's happening in our society that defies sort of the normal way we look at the economy and we look at supply and demand.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I think that if these numbers persist and where I do think it's fair to blame Putin or to involve him in the conversation, is that gas prices and energy prices are going to go up -- continue to go up. It's going to go up in Europe which will impact us as well. If I were Republicans what I'd be trying to get something to resonate, and if I were Democrats, it's trying to blunt it.\par \par \uc2 Domestic energy production, I don't know, it's good for him to be at the border --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We're over five million barrels a day.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Right. But that's all I would be talking about because if indeed Democrats are right and I don't want to give the Republicans about the playbook they should be playing but if Democrats are saying Putin is the reason that prices are up, then if I were McCarthy and Republicans, I'd be saying we need to increase domestic energy production.\par \par \uc2 It's good to be at the border but why not address the issue that Democrats agree is the issue and frankly Europeans know is the issue and Americans, we're going to unfortunately confront as natural gas prices continue to rise throughout the winter. And I pray and hope that we have an answer to not only that long term, but the immediate term of trying to help families pay for their energy bills.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Harold's making some sense.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: He is making some sense, but you know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came in, I always go back to this. He came in the Trojan horse. He was the moderate but he wasn't the moderate and even today one of the members of the squad wants him to run for president again. And you know, you could say that they may not yet have assigned blame, but what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to do for the next two years is blame the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 He's blaming the Republicans for the student loan thing when -- the question is whether or not it's even legal. And you know. he's blaming Putin for something as you say very correctly that we can contain -- we can take care of our own domestic energy production, all right. So, who's to blame? Biden's to blame for the fact that we went from energy efficient to not having any domestic energy.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Energy independent. You mean, energy independent.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Energy independent, to not having any now, and begging to the Saudis and then the Venezuelans and everyone else to give us oil. So, I mean, it is devastating. This rail thing is devastating. It's a Trojan horse again. I came out and I really took care of this possible rail strike that's going to cost us $2 billion a day if it doesn't go through.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: For a month.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yeah. And I -- I did I did my victory lap, but it really wasn't well and it didn't work and you really didn't close the whole thing as it relates to the loans, and you really didn't, you know, you haven't done anything for domestic energy production. So, stop blaming other people. You've been lying since you were in the Trojan horse.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : But In fairness, if everybody believed your points, you would have had a better night