d be entrusted to do this.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We call balls and strikes, too, Harold. Let's get that straight. Our strike zone is a little wider, but we do still call them strikes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Up next, pressure growing on cops to find the killer as we get heartbreaking new video of the four Idaho students who were slaughtered in their sleep.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 FORD: Heartbreaking new video shows all four University of Idaho students dancing and joking inside the shared house where they were later slaughtered. The video posted less than a month before the killing and police are still no closer to finding out who murdered them. It's been nearly 10 days since these students were brutally butchered in their beds.\par \par \uc2 The victims were out Saturday night, two at a frat party, the other two at a local sports bar. All four got back to the house before 2:00 a.m. Sunday morning. Police think they were killed three to four hours later. Two other roommates were in the house at the time and slept through the attack.\par \par \uc2 And now there are growing fears that the crime scene may have been contaminated. Authorities expanding the search and combing through the woods in a parking lot in a new area they have blocked off behind the home. An expert says catching the killer could come down to some small details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BADEN, FORENSIC PATHOLOGIST: Perhaps the deaths didn't all occur at the same time, but whoever did it could have been laying in wait and one of the things that can help determine that is the times of death and the medical examiner at the scene may be able to tell whether or not the poor victims were killed at the same time or at different times. And sometimes the perpetrator is cut by the slippage of the knife and some of the blood at the scene can belong to the perpetrator being -- be able to evaluate that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORD: Judges, this is one of the more heartbreaking, if not, the most heartbreaking story I've seen the last few years. We just have no leads here. You're experience in this space, what informs you and how do you think about this?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, you know, the whole country is focused on this because most people think it's 2022, why is it taking so long? Why don't we know what happened? First of all, I'm assuming that there's no eyewitness, none that we know of yet. So, what does that tell you? That tells you the most important evidence is the forensic evidence. The forensic evidence that will identify who the killer was.\par \par \uc2 Not only do we have from one parent that there were defensive wounds, okay, which means, you know, that the killer may have been in a fight and maybe he got cut, but we also know that the knife which is a Rambo knife was pressed into the hilt which is where the bruising came from.\par \par \uc2 So, there were tremendous screams or had to be a tremendous fight here. And yet, if you've got a local police department that is responding to the -- to a call of someone being unconscious, what does that tell you? That tells you that these kids, the two who made the call, and now we know that the call came from one of their phones, the kids on the first floor. What did they see? How much of the room did they see?\par \par \uc2 You know, why did they stay unconscious as opposed to dead when there was blood running out of the house onto the outside of the house? And where did they get this unconscious from? Why are they saying that? Are there footprints in the bedroom on the second and third floor? If there was so much blood, then what you've got is a killer who certainly is stepping in the blood as he's killing these kids.\par \par \uc2 But in addition to that, you've got the other two kids. You've got to eliminate their footprints. Then you've got the local police responding to unconscious. I guarantee you they didn't have any kind of slippers on or they didn't have any idea that this would be a quadruple serial homicide. So, they would have messed up the scene and I don't mean to criticize the local PD. They can't be expected to understand how this stuff works.\par \par \uc2 And four or them killed. Were they bound and gagged? Were they incapacitated with a drug? How does the other two kids not hear anything" How do the other two of the four dead not hear anything? I mean, I'm sure they were -- they were happy gang that were drunk, but they were at that food truck a few minutes before. They were, you know, they were capable. They weren't dropped down -- dropping down drunk.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, the one thing I want to say is that this should have been a case where the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was called in immediately. Their forensic evidence and potential is unparalleled.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : They're involved now though, right? This is for our viewers to know.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They are involved now, but it's too late. The crime scene has been contaminated. So that when we get to the trial assuming someone is ever and maybe they and not someone, it may be more than one people or one person abound and gag and stab and kill these four people who were young and would have put up a fight, then the question is the forensic evaluation that you have, the crime scene was contaminated. We don't know if it's his or hers or eight other people.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Brian?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: So, we know they eliminate the \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver or the community driver, eliminate that. The guy in the white shirt at the truck, we eliminate him. The roommates, we eliminate them. Oh, wait a second, so why are you eliminating them? Why -- what do they do to make you feel as though you're eliminated?\par \par \uc2 And to your point, is that I don't understand. They keep saying one of the phones instead of just saying a roommate used the phone and made the call. They just say the p