r \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, everybody loves their pet. But not everyone can afford to take care of their pets if they have some kind of an illness. So, this past Friday, I attended an event in Westchester. It's called -- it was a dinner for Guardian HEALS, which stands for Human Enriching Animal Lives. It's a nonprofit. There I am with my friends, Michael Capelli (PH) and Susan, and Constance Hafiak (PH).\par \par \uc2 HEALS is a nonprofit organization that helps cover the cost of life-saving veterinary care for pet owners who truly cannot afford it. They have a means test and they donate to local family vets, assuring that these pets get the care that they need. And they're looking to go national. I'm very impressed with them. You can see them at healspets.org. And they brought a dog that was going to die and they got money and the vet helped and the dog walked around.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Excellent. Excellent.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Have you ever thought to yourself, if only I could have the delicious tastes of Thanksgiving in liquid form?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, now you can. Jones has a new turkey gravy-flavored soda. Everybody, have a sip. This is kind of your --\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: No.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No? Salt and sweet. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I can't do it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I can't do it.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I like it. It's really good. It's like knockoff cream soda.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You would like it.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Yes, I like it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Where are you from again?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Why is it sweet?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I could taste the giblets.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wait, why is it sweet?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And at the bottom, you can kind of see --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's not as bad as I thought it would be.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's like -- it's like pulp in the bottom, but it's giblets.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It looks like a sample.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They're delicious. Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" Special Thanksgiving Day Quiz. Let's see a sneak peek.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY BELISARIO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL PRODUCER: When the pilgrims landed here, where did they land?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Fort Lauderdale?\par \par \uc2 BELISARIO: It wasn't a cruise ship.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, tonight at 7:00. Harold, you got ten.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: \uc2 Brown University\uc2  freshman Olivia Pichardo, a fellow New Yorker, the first woman to ever make a Division 1 Baseball Team. She's going to play her first game February 24th. Congrats to you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, women.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, women.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next with Bret.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020221123eibm00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Free Range Kids?; Idaho Murder Investigation Expands; Republicans Head to Border; Holiday Travel Rush; Congress to Get Trump's Tax Returns; Railway Strike?\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Tenney, Eric Shawn, Ted Williams, Bill Melugin, Mike  Emanuel, Peter Doocy, Connell McShane, Charles Payne \par \uc2 9,089 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Is holiday shopping about to go off the rails, as the threat of a crazy rail strike is looking all too real/\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 The White House trying to stop what could be an unstoppable world strike, threatening to keep gifts from making an under the Christmas tree this year and potentially driving prices even higher.\par \par \uc2 We have got FOX team coverage with Peter Doocy at the White House on a very real threat and Connell McShane on the real pain consumers could soon be filling.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 This tentative labor agreement between rail unions and rail bosses is held up just two months after President Biden held it up as a victory.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This agreement is a big win for America and for both, in my view.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Well, now it's in danger.\par \par \uc2 We know back then President Biden got involved behind the scenes to make things happen. Now, though, it's unclear how involved he is. But White House officials do want some credit for avoiding catastrophe in the fall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, here's what I -- here's what we're saying, is that it averted a horrible downturn in our economy by that tentative agreement. It was a big deal. What it could have done to our economy would have been devastating.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The head of the largest union to vote the deal down sounds like his members know what kind of leverage they have got threatening to strike right before Christmas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEREMY FERGUSON, PRESIDENT, SMART TRANSPORTATION DIVISION: A strike could be averted. The ball is back in the carriers' hands at this point and back in their court. It will be a difficult process, and we're going to do our best. We want to keep America moving. And we sure -- surely want to help Santa Claus get those gifts delivered for Christmas also.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But it's not just that.\par \par \uc2 And if President doesn't -- President Biden doesn't intervene, then Congress might.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BETH VAN DUYNE (R-TX): It's something totally different when you think about the number of products that go on rail, including liquid natural gas and things like chlorine that help with all of our water supplies.\par \par \uc2 This is a national security issue, not just a Christmas gift issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And it's four out of 12 unions that have now voted down the tentative agreement, the one the White House supported earlier this fall, but we are told that, even if it's only four out of 12, if one of them, any one of them decides to go on strike, then the rest are planning to go on strike out of solidarity with their union brothers and sisters -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Got it.\par \par \uc2 If a rail strike is called, businesses could see their supplies dry up.\par \par \uc2 To FOX Business' Connell McShane with that part of the story -- Connell.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this is broad economically, or potentially. I mean, you're talking about just about every industry, when you really think about it, unless there's a last-minute deal or unless, as Peter says, maybe Congress intervenes.\par \par \uc2 But just think about it in terms of numbers, size and scope, four unions not ratifying the deal. Eight have. But, again, it really doesn't matter, because they have promised not to cross the picket line, so you add this all up, all the workers, 100,000-plus, could easily go on strike here.\par \par \uc2 In terms of impact, the number that's being thrown around is $2 billion a day. A lobbying group came up with that, says that's what it would cost the economy. Another group actually said 700,000 jobs could be lost if the strike were to last a month.\par \par \uc2 Now, in terms of just a few examples of the industries to watch -- and, as I said, you can think about just about every one -- but chemical seems to be at the top of most people's lists here. So, railroads likely would put a stop to shipping hazardous chemicals, maybe a week or so before there was a strike, because the last thing anybody wants to see is some kind of a dangerous substance stranded on an unsupervised railcar somewhere.\par \par \uc2 So, you just think about things like chlorine used to purify water or something like that might be in short supply. And there are many other examples. Food, the expectation is, it would probably take a week or so until you start to notice a shortage on the grocery store shelves, peanut butter and cereal, other kinds of things that are not getting shipped where they need to be.\par \par \uc2 And the final thing is the people problem at all of this, which you can overlook. About half the commuter rail systems in the country rely at least partially on tracks that are owned by the freight railroads. And most of \uc2 Amtrak\uc2 's long-distance trains are on that network. So they could potentially all be offline as well.\par \par \uc2 Really, it's this kind of impact is why the Railway Labor Act, which is an old, old law more than 100 years -- or about 100 years old, that makes it difficult to have these workers go on strike in the first place, Charles. They try to avoid it.\par \par \uc2 But, even if they do, the final important point, I think, is that Congress could intervene. They did it in '92. There was a strike that year, but it only lasted a couple of days before the House and Senate passed a bill to end it. So we might be back in that situation again here in a couple of weeks. We will see.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow, certainly a nail-biter. Thanks a lot, Connell.\par \par \uc2 So, will strike push the economy off the rails?\par \par \uc2 Economist Steve Moore with us now.\par \par \uc2 Steve, everyone understands the importance of this, Connell underscoring way, back in the 1920s, there was legislation put into place so that this can never happen. But here we are on the eve of it happening after President Biden championed the unions. He gave them everything they want it, for the most part. And here we are.\par \par \uc2 It's a pretty precarious situation, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: You better believe it, Charles. And so much for \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  solving the supply chain problems.\par \par \uc2 If you think we have got supply chain problems now, think about how bad it would be if we had this rail strike, which we hope we can avert. Look, so much of the merchandise that we buy at stores and that we depend on, including our energy, is transported on rail. And then you have got a parallel problem at the same time, Charles, that you have talked about on your show, which is the surge in price of diesel.\par \par \uc2 So the truckers are having a hard time because they're paying so much for fuel. Now you got this potential rail strike. It looks like it's going to be a lot of gridlock ahead if this thing isn't solved.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I mean, our distillate supplies are at 1951 levels. Diesel is down extraordinarily.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I see natural gas really starting to take off as cold weather sets in, in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 It's even -- even if this strike is averted, it feels like we're in for what some are calling a double-dip inflation.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Could be.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, we have had two months of slight declines in inflation. That's the good news, Charles. But you do have these forces, including those higher energy costs that you were talking about, that are making it worse. We are -- let's put it very simply. We are not out of the woods at all on this inflation problem, especially because, as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has even said, we have authorized $4 trillion of spending over the last two years.\par \par \uc2 A lot of that money hasn't even hit the economy yet. So, when -- in 2023, when we see this deluge of federal spending, I think that's going to make inflation potentially a lot worse.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, to that point, just moments ago, the White House announced it's going to extend that pause that they weren't going to take away with student debt cancellation to June of next year, the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  saying that could cost $40 billion, to bring the whole thing to $195 billion.\par \par \uc2 These are de facto ways of pushing more money into the economy. And it really is kind of sad, because, of all the folks that need it, I'm not sure college grads in America are suffering the most right now.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, I would say the same thing.\par \par \uc2 And the problem for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is that more than one court, a couple of quarts now have basically said, this is illegal. I'm not a lawyer, Charles. I don't think you're a lawyer either. But I do know the Constitution. And the Constitution says that spending is done by the Congress, not done by the president.\par \par \uc2 And the funny thing about this, I guess it's funny, is that the way that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  tried to invoke this presidential privilege to do this was through the COVID emergency.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And what does this have to do with COVID?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Well, I mean, it's still -- they're still finding ways. And, again, it's ironic because the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is actively trying to slow down the economy, while the White House keeps pumping money into it\par \par \uc2 MOORE: They're acting at cross-purposes. The Fed's trying to take some of that surplus out of the money by raising rates.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Congress and the president keeps spending.\par \par \uc2 Job number one for this new Republican House is to get that spending down by a lot, so we can see a conquering of this inflation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That's why we have runaway inflation and recession in the same sentence. Steve, thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: OK. You too, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Meanwhile, the Supreme Court clearing the way for the release of Trump's tax returns to a congressional committee.\par \par \uc2 Mike Emanuel is in Washington with the latest -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has ordered the handover of former President Trump's tax returns to the House Ways and Means Committee. There are no noted dissents in the court's order. This is ending a three-year legal fight. It is unclear when those records will be turned over or what the currently Democrat-controlled committee will do with them.\par \par \uc2 Republicans take over the majority in the new year, and this has been quite a battle between the Ways and Means panel and the former president, lawmakers seeking Mr. Trump's personal tax records and some business records from 2015 to 2020, so just before he took office and his years in the White House.\par \par \uc2 The Trump team has argued this is all about partisan politics, Democrats going after him. Lower courts ruled that Ways and Means lawmakers had a legitimate legislative authority to review those records. Mr. Trump, as president, claimed executive privilege. The Biden administration agreed with the House committee, but waited for it to play out in the courts.\par \par \uc2 This dates back to Mr. Trump declining in 2016 to make his tax returns public, something other presidential candidates traditionally do. In 2019, when Democrats took over the majority in the House, the new chair of Ways and Means asked the Treasury Department to hand them over. It's not entirely clear the impact, with the balance of power shifting in the House and a Republican chairman duty to take over in January, but, for now, a legal setback for the former president, as we await his reaction.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats will likely have a few weeks at least to review those records and potentially issue a report based on what they find -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Mike, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now, with the border surge continuing and Title 42 ending, what a group of attorney generals are demanding.\par \par \uc2 And the holiday travel rush is on. We have got what you need to know before heading out, right, Garrett?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Charles, this Thanksgiving is shaping up to be one of the busiest for travel. And it's also looking like it'll be one of the most expensive.\par \par \uc2 We will show you just how much folks are paying this holiday season coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Holiday travel season in full swing.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 55 million people estimated to be heading out across the U.S. for Thanksgiving. And if you are one of the nearly five million flying, get ready for reduced flights and higher prices.\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Garrett Tenney is at Chicago's O'Hare International Airport with the latest on this holiday rush -- Garrett.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Well, Charles, the good news is that today is expected to be the busiest travel day of the weekend. So far, the system is holding up pretty well, no widespread delays or cancellations.\par \par \uc2 But Thanksgiving travel this year is by no means pain-free. Folks are paying more for just about everything, for the average plane ticket, $350. That's up 43 percent from a year ago. A hotel night will cost you $189, 13 percent more than last year.\par \par \uc2 Rental cars are the lone bright spot, 15 percent cheaper than 2021. Gas has also dropped a bit over the past month to $3.63 a gallon, still up from a year ago. Even with those higher prices, AAA says Americans are making travel a priority this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AIXA DIAZ, AAA SPOKESMAN: People really want to get together with family and friends. They, for whatever reason, whether it's because they haven't gotten to travel a lot during the pandemic, or they simply just enjoy it so much, they're making leisure travel a priority.\par \par \uc2 And they might be cutting back in other areas of their lives, but they're definitely wanting to get back out there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: The airlines have taken steps to try to avoid the chaos and delays of cancellations we saw this summer, hiring more pilots and staff and cutting back on flight schedules.\par \par \uc2 Combined with the higher demand, it's leading to higher prices and more crowded flights, though, for passengers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let's just say we will be living off Top Ramen for the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Holiday travel is, of course, picking back up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's not very fun, but, you know, it is what it is. As long as I get to see my family at the end of the night, it's all right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everything was pretty smooth, although this is just the beginning of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And while things have been going pretty smoothly so far, that could change later this week, when storms across the South and the Northeast could have a big impact on folks trying to get back home after Thanksgiving -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Charles, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, can airlines avoid the type of meltdowns that we saw over the summer?\par \par \uc2 Travel expert Lee Abbamonte joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Lee, we heard all those folks said so far, so good. Can it last?\par \par \uc2 LEE ABBAMONTE, TRAVEL EXPERT: You know, I'm an optimist, so I think they will do OK. I think they have kind of overcome some of the issues that they had over the summer.\par \par \uc2 You and I have spoken about this ad nauseam. And I think that, with hiring more pilots and more staff, and kind of overcoming some of the issues that they have, I think it's going to be relatively smooth. Of course, the X- factor is the weather. So we will see how that holds up. But, other than that, I'm confident that it'll be better than it has been, although the prices aren't getting any better.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I'm going to get to you on the prices, but it's so interesting, because I was just thinking about that \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  game they played in Detroit, right?\par \par \uc2 And, of course, Buffalo is unique. It's not going to just snow in most places four feet in one moment. But it does say that there sort of is that risk out there. And it's those times when these airlines are tested, because, for the most part, I don't think they have any spare capacity, right, whether it's airplanes that they parked, whether it's pilots.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they're trying to get people to work past the age of 65. I know the industry is in dire straits right now.\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: Well, like you said, you can't really prepare for the worst possible scenario like they had up in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, that's where the airlines are really tested. Again, I mean, you're kind of at the mercy of the weather this time of year, especially in the northern parts of the U.S., and, obviously, hurricanes down in Florida can have an issue.\par \par \uc2 But, overall, I think they're more equipped than they have been in the last year or so, but only time will tell.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Lee, you're the travel expert. I mean, it must be -- the skies are crowded, I know, because so many people that never really traveled before, so many Americans are really just hopping on a plane, going to Miami, gone here.\par \par \uc2 It's that YOLO, you only live once, kind of thing. But it's expensive, though. I mean, we just heard Garrett talk about that, up 43 percent in a year.\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hotels are expensive. I mean, do you find that this is just that a one -- now sort of a guilty pleasure for many people?\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: You know, I wish I had the exact answer, but, yes, it's there's no doubt about it. Prices are up across the board. Hotels are out of control. Airfares that used to be like $100 are now $500. It just makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 I'm flying to Europe just after Thanksgiving on \uc2 Turkish Airlines\uc2  to Istanbul, and tickets are more reasonable now off-season going to Europe, but around the U.S., especially the holiday times, it's very problematic.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I got less than a minute to go.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is starting to weaken a little bit. Would you say, if anyone's got Europe on your list, the checklist, make that move now?\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: I tell you what. We got beat down for the last 20 years. So I have been enjoying this last year, and I'm going to enjoy it as long as we can.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: So book as soon as you can. Lock it down. And maybe hope for the best, and maybe hedge with the dollars.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I think it was 20, 30 years ago we were at parity with the euro. We went out were there, and I tell you, we cleaned up. It felt amazing.\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Lee, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: Good talking to you, buddy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, to the border, where the migrant surge isn't slowing down.\par \par \uc2 Now Republican lawmakers are heading down, a trip the White House is shooting down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The question that we have for Kevin McCarthy, who is soon to be -- who is soon to be Speaker McCarthy, what is his plan?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Meta denying a report that CEO \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  is planning to step down next year. Their communications director, Andy Stone, calling the rumor false on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This all coming as the tech giant has seen its profits decline and its stock crater.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy leading a Republican delegation in El Paso, Texas, today. They want to see the border surge firsthand.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Bill Melugin is there with the latest -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 That's right. Leader McCarthy is set to hold a press conference here in El Paso within the next 10 minutes or so. And he has been teasing all day long that he has a major announcement he wants to make regarding DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, I caught up with McCarthy, and I asked him directly, are you planning to impeach him? Here's what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Are you going to push for Secretary Mayorkas to be impeached if you become the speaker in January?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I think we will talk a little bit about that more today. I'm going to go down, sit down with the border agents, get a firsthand knowledge of what is happening today, because I do not trust what Secretary Mayorkas has told the nation.\par \par \uc2 Every fact is what he has said is not true, from everything we have seen. But I'm going to go firsthand, get the knowledge, with a number of other members. And we will talk a little bit about more about that today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And here's what -- some of what's been happening here in this El Paso sector.\par \par \uc2 This is video we shot in Anapra, New Mexico, late yesterday afternoon in just about a 10-minute window. These were runners who were coming over the border wall and were trying to evade Border Patrol and running off into neighborhoods in the New Mexico area. You can see some of the guys wearing camouflage, some of them wearing black, obviously not turning themselves in, not asylum seekers.\par \par \uc2 We did see Border Patrol catch a few of them. We personally saw several of them appear to get away. We never saw them apprehended, some of them ending up disappearing in that residential neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 Then a couple of major fentanyl busts to tell you about. Take a look at this first photo right here, CBP at the port of entry in Nogales, Arizona, announcing they stopped a vehicle with 269,000 fentanyl pills they found being smuggled in that car. Then, over the weekend, take a look at these photos, three separate busts at the same port of entry in Nogales over the weekend, those busts totaling more than half-a-million fentanyl pills, as well -- as well as 12 pounds of pure fentanyl powder, major bust there just a few days in one single port of entry at our Southern border.\par \par \uc2 And back out here live, again, any minute now, Leader McCarthy is expected to begin that press conference here in El Paso. It's on the other side of town, where he has been teasing all day long. He's going to say something about Secretary Mayorkas. He has been facing a lot of pressure on the right to move forward with impeaching him if he's elected speaker in January.\par \par \uc2 And he personally told me today he feels that Mayorkas has been dishonest with the American public multiple times while testifying under oath that the border is secure. So we will wait and see what he has to say at this upcoming press conference, Charles.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks a lot, Bill. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, 15 Republican state attorney generals are seeking to stop the U.S. from ending Title 42 border expulsions, filing a motion in federal court Monday night. They want to keep Title 42 in place. They say it's needed to get border under control.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  Lieutenant Chris Olivarez.\par \par \uc2 Chris, it's -- your thoughts on this? I mean, listen, neither one of us are lawyers. But, obviously, you understand the other part of their argument, that something is certainly needed, and that this was -- this did have some positive impact.\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : Right, Charles? Good afternoon, sir. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 So we know, with Title 42 in place, that's really the only policy that we have to kind of somewhat sustain the situation that we're seeing on the border. But, right now, that's hanging by a thread. And we know that, come December, Title 42 is going to be gone.\par \par \uc2 And with that, you're going to see double, probably even triple the numbers that we're seeing now, the daily numbers, where we're averaging 7,000 to 8,000 daily encounters a day. So, with that number doubling or even tripling, that's going to be very -- that's really going to tie up resources that we're seeing right now with our Border Patrol agents, which means now we're going to have to deploy more state resources to fill in those gaps, those vulnerabilities along the border...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: ... because of -- we're going to see this surge of immigrants coming across, because -- and they know right now, Charles, I mean, they know, the cartels, especially the criminals, know that, with Title 42 gone, that's just more money in their pockets, because now they're going to take full advantage of that situation and exploit that and profit off of these immigrants that are coming across this border.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I mean, this is going to be a total deluge.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just -- I mean, it's already porous. It's already a joke, to be quite frank with you. The world knows, to your point. That's why they make this dangerous, arduous trip up.\par \par \uc2 Just a moment ago, Bill Melugin was talking about Kevin McCarthy saying something really important with respect to Mayorkas, maybe impeachment. I mean, what's your thoughts on just how helpful the federal government has been and their insistence that the Southern border is secure.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Well, I mean, they haven't been helpful. We have seen that. I mean, we have seen all the reporting by all the correspondents that have been on the border, especially with Bill Melugin. We see the video footage.\par \par \uc2 I mean, without a doubt, no one can dispute what we're seeing on the border, that we do have a crisis. But yet the federal government has failed to acknowledge the fact that we have a crisis, and not just so much with immigration, but with drugs. You see the thousands of pills that are coming across the border every single day and the great work that not only our DPS personnel are doing, but also our military, and as well as law enforcement as well, doing all they can to try to stop this situation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Because, again, it is the sole responsibility of the federal government to protect not only Americans, but to secure the border. And they're not doing that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, again, keep -- keep up the good work, Chris. It is mind-boggling. Every time we see those bags of pills, knowing just the destruction one could do, it blows our mind.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We'll talk to you again real soon.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Meanwhile, police in Moscow Idaho expanding their search area into the deadly stabbing of those four university students. We're going to go there live for an update next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That House GOP border delegation now speaking with reporters. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (JOINED IN PROGRESS)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: ... important for them to come see what his policies are doing and the damage that it's doing to our country.\par \par \uc2 It's an honor to be here with the agents. We just spent time with them. Our nation has never been so at risk. And their job has never been harder. Those brave men and women risk their lives every day to protect the American citizens from the consequences of illegal border crossings.\par \par \uc2 Together, we bear witness to the worst border crisis in the U.S. history. There has been more than four million illegal border crossings since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has taken office. That's not the worst part. Now that Title 42 may end, we're bracing for a tsunami.\par \par \uc2 People talk about 18,000 crossings in a day. That would mean 13 million more illegal crossings for the rest of the term of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , if nothing changes. Think about that, 17 million more illegal entries into our country.\par \par \uc2 Just last week, Secretary Mayorkas testified under oath that, yes, the border is secure. I'm here to tell you it is not. We spent the day with border agents. I asked each and every one if this border is secure. They laughed. They said it is not, that it's one of the worst points in the history of this nation.\par \par \uc2 We just went to a bridge in El Paso, where 70,000 people have come across in the last seven weeks. And the secretary of homeland thinks this border is secure. For the last two years, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats running Congress has pushed open border policies and waged baseless attacks on our border agents.\par \par \uc2 The actions of this administration have empowered cartels, drug smugglers, human traffickers, and terrorist organizations all around the world.\par \par \uc2 Just last week, U.S. Customs and Border agents off the coast of Puerto Rico came under fire in the line of duty. One agent was fatally shot, while two other agents were wounded. In March 2021, when we came down after the new administration had lifted those policies, we visited the border.\par \par \uc2 I warned, having learned from the Border Patrol agents, of the suspects on the terrorist watch lists that were coming across, the amount of fentanyl that they hadn't seen in the past now coming across. We held a press conference. We advise not just America, but to the members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Members of the Democratic Congress said I was lying, that there weren't people on the terrorist watch list. Congressman Gallego said that, he said to Armed Services. Since that time period, more than 107 terrorist suspects have been apprehended crossing this border.\par \par \uc2 Fentanyl is poisoning and killing Americans in every single community. Americans have died from overdoses. Secretary Mayorkas would like you to believe that the people committing these crimes are just in the search of a better life.\par \par \uc2 How many people have not been caught? In 42 days, the united Republican House takes control. In 42 days, we end one-party Democrat rule in Washington. A new House, Republicans will work to stop \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's assault on our laws, our borders and our border agents.\par \par \uc2 We will use the power of the purse and the power of the subpoena. Now, let me be clear. Those responsible for this disaster will be held accountable. I have spoken today again to Congressman Jim Jordan and Jamie Comer, the future chair of Oversight and Judiciary Committee.\par \par \uc2 And they have my complete support to investigate the collapse of our border and the shutdown of ICE enforcement nationwide. Under Secretary Mayorkas, the deliberate suspensions of our laws have proved deadly. Fentanyl is now flowing in record amounts.\par \par \uc2 Today, 300 Americans will be poisoned and die, tomorrow, 300 more. It is the equivalent of an airline crashing each and every day in America. But, again, he does nothing. If an airline crashed three days in a row, the entire nation would say we have a real challenge. We have a crisis. We have a disaster. We have to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 But he continues to lie to the American public. We have lost operational control of our Southern border, empowering drug cartels and human traffickers. They have fired shots at our National Guard. They put AK-47s, pointing at helicopters. They have burned and hung and raped the women and caught them on fire.\par \par \uc2 And, again, Secretary Mayorkas thinks the border is secure. He has blocked ICE and Border Patrol from enforcing our laws, while vilifying them at the same time. Do you realize what that has done? In the last 10 days, three Border Patrol agents have committed suicide, a total of 14 this year, a number we have not seen in decades.\par \par \uc2 He ended the remain-in-Mexico policy and wants to end Title 42. His actions have produced the greatest wave of illegal immigration in recorded history. Our country may never recover from Secretary Mayorkas' dereliction of duty.\par \par \uc2 This is why, today, I am calling on the secretary to resign. He cannot and must not remain in that position. If Secretary Mayorkas does not resign, House Republicans will investigate every order, every action and every failure. We will determine whether we can begin impeachment inquiry.\par \par \uc2 If Secretary Mayorkas was in charge of any company, he would have been fired by now for the failures of what he has caused. The American public deserves more, deserve better, and expect more within their government. Enough is enough. We will do whatever it takes.\par \par \uc2 This is my commitment to the men and women of the Border Patrol and to the citizens of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 With that, I'd like to introduce Congressman Tony Gonzales, who represents this area, represents more than 40 percent of the entire border and was recently reelected by more than 14 percent. But what he has done is brought more members down to the border to educate them.\par \par \uc2 And, after January 3, no longer will members have to come down to the border to tour. We will hold our hearings at the border, so the Democrats can no longer hide from the crisis they have created.\par \par \uc2 I bring you Tony Gonzales.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): Thank you, future Speaker McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 I have hosted 16 delegations at the border. I have hosted Kevin McCarthy three times. And what you see is, change is coming. And change is going to be led by the people standing right here.\par \par \uc2 It's Thanksgiving week. People are doing a lot of different things. And what are House Republicans doing? We're going to the epicenter of the border crisis. And we're highlighting two things, one, highlighting the men and women in green that are...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK, folks, we will continue to monitor this.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I do want to bring in Kelly Jane Torrance from The New York Post and Democratic strategist Laura Fink.\par \par \uc2 And just to go over this, ladies, soon-to-be Speaker Kevin McCarthy saying there was a deliberate suspension of laws on the part of Secretary Mayorkas. He's calling on his resignation, pointing out what's happening with this country, the equivalent of an airline crashing each and every day in this country, also the stress on the Border Patrol agents themselves, three dying in suicide the last week, 14 year to date.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, does he make a point? Is it time to make a change?\par \par \uc2 KELLY JANE TORRANCE, NEW YORK POST: Charles, I think it's long past time to make a change.\par \par \uc2 I think Kevin McCarthy has it exactly right. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , of course, telegraphed what his immigration policy, actually lack thereof, on the campaign trail. And, of course, on his first day, he moved to put a moratorium on deportations.\par \par \uc2 But Secretary Mayorkas has taken that and run with it. He issued his own guidance on deportations, again saying that he wants fewer of them, that resources are not going to be given to deport. And these are -- we're talking about people who are criminals who are in jail and who the country wants to deport once they're released.\par \par \uc2 He keeps claiming the border is secure. The numbers you gave, Charles, prove it is not. People are simply walking in. And cities across America are finally feeling it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TORRANCE: El Paso, of course, just struggling.\par \par \uc2 But now that we have got some migrants being bused to places right here in New York City, for example. Every city could be feeling the impact of this. And it's huge. And it's a real burden on Americans, who are already struggling through high inflation and other issues.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes, to your point, it's interesting. When these very wealthy, so-called sanctuary cities complain when they have to step up to the plate, you have to wonder about these border towns that have dealt with it.\par \par \uc2 But it is a national crisis, Laura. And it does -- you just -- I just still don't understand why it doesn't feel like a crisis or an emergency at the White House.\par \par \uc2 LAURA FINK, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, it's wild to me.\par \par \uc2 And when my colleague mentioned inflation, what Republicans ran on, and now we have -- our first press conference is at the border. And I will say this. Kevin McCarthy can throw a press conference. But what he does not offer in that press conference were any substantive solutions to the challenges that he says are a crisis.\par \par \uc2 So here we are changing the subject...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Do you believe it's a crisis?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FINK: ... what Americans are about every day and does not offer a single solution...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 FINK: ... other than wanting to fire a political opponent.\par \par \uc2 So I think that that's emblematic of the challenge that Republicans have. Now that they're governing, they have to see -- show solutions and not just indictments of their opposition.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. I mean, I think the first thing you do is, when you take over a broken system is, you fire the people that were running it. That's business 101. So that's kind of smart, I think, on his part.\par \par \uc2 I'm pretty sure, if you took over a failing business, Laura, you would fire the people that were running it, especially if they said that it wasn't a failing business.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But two million entries, it's an invasion. We have never had this many people stroll into our nation before, never before.\par \par \uc2 The got-aways are frightening, the horrible stories that the women and young girls have to take to deal with this, frightening. Finally, finally, sanctuary cities get a chance, Martha's Vineyard, and they think feign, oh, we can't deal with it. It's too much. We were just saying that, but we didn't really mean it.\par \par \uc2 So, Kelly Jane Torrance -- I mean -- Kelly Jane -- I'm sorry -- Torrance, let me go back to you, because this is something that Republicans campaigned on. It's not something that came out of left field. Of course, it is -- it is a crisis. And there were proposals. There have been proposals in the past, and I'm sure there will be new ones.\par \par \uc2 But I think we need to talk about the humanality of it all, because I think that's missing from the conversation. These are human beings who are making this trek, this dangerous trek. They're paying these coyotes. They're being abused. And we should just have a system where they can -- they can come in legally, but not have to take these sort of risks.\par \par \uc2 TORRANCE: Charles, you're exactly right.\par \par \uc2 And you have mentioned part of that human element. And that's another issue that this administration is causing. We have got a record -- we had a record number of migrants dying trying to make it over the border or shortly after they made it over the border.\par \par \uc2 You -- I'm sure viewers remember that heartbreaking image of the big truck that was found in Texas with those people dying inside.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TORRANCE: They're -- as you say, they're paying smugglers. They're having to -- and we actually ran -- New York Post ran a great piece a few days ago about it looks like they're actually putting some of these unaccompanied children to work at slaughterhouses.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TORRANCE: The secretary of labor sued a company.\par \par \uc2 So, and -- of course, and the fentanyl. With all the people coming in comes other things too, like drugs, like...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I want to apologize because we weren't ready for that -- that press conference. It went a little late -- longer than we thought.\par \par \uc2 Laura, we will give you more time next time, for sure.\par \par \uc2 Ladies, thank you both very much, Kelly Jane and Laura.\par \par \uc2 FINK: Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But we want to go to Idaho, folks, because still no suspect and no murder weapon in the investigation of those four Idaho university students stabbed to death 10 days ago. Police are now expanding the restricted area near the victims' home.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now with more on where this investigation is heading, former D.C. homicide detective and defense attorney Ted Williams.\par \par \uc2 Ted, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 You have been out there for a few days. What's your -- what's your assessment right now?\par \par \uc2 TED WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: You know, Charles, I can tell you, not only are the citizens of Idaho grieving, but we are all grieving.\par \par \uc2 We want our children to bury us. We don't want to have to bury our children. And we have got four families who are having to bury their children. And if you look over my shoulder here, that house that -- where these four young people died is frozen in time.\par \par \uc2 The investigators have been on the scene early here this afternoon. They are asking the city for more help. They are talking to residents here, trying to find out who killed these four young people. The investigation is going on. I can tell you the medical examiner has put out some information also that this was a very gruesome, gruesome murder of these four young people.\par \par \uc2 And, as a result of that, there should be a lot of forensic evidence, Charles, that they are -- that the authorities are having to work through and hopefully bringing the perpetrator or perpetrators to justice.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, Ted, as you were speaking, we're actually right now showing video on TikTok of the roommates, the victims.\par \par \uc2 And you started talking about how gruesome it is and how it's shocked the nation. It reminds me of "In Cold Blood," the -- Truman Capote's account of the murders in Holcomb, Kansas, in 1959, and just -- and it just -- it feels so gut-wrenching each day, each 24-hour period that goes by, without naming solutions.\par \par \uc2 We have read about how maybe evidence has been contaminated. Obviously, this is a small police department. But, still, many in the law enforcement community believe there should have been a much better job. And many wonder if it's salvageable at this point, the investigation.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Charles, I think it is definitely salvageable.\par \par \uc2 I think, yes, there was some mistakes that law enforcement made early on, and -- but they're trying to clean up their mistakes. While we are here talking, law enforcement is out there working, trying to find these perpetrators or perpetrator.\par \par \uc2 And I would have to believe that, at some stage, they will be successful.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The widening of the restricted area, are there any new themes coming across that you have heard, some new ideas about maybe -- about maybe where this is headed right now?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, as I said, they're asking the community for more assistance. If you saw something, say something, and call the authorities up.\par \par \uc2 As we were on the scene here yesterday when they widened on the search area, they apparently did a search of the area. They did some testing in the area. And now they have now taken those tapes down. So that area that had been expanded is no longer -- or being used as part of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Part of the crime scene?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: As part of the crime scene, yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Ted, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, we will have more right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, if you think you're making the right decisions for your kids, if you think it's tough, folks, well, what would you say about just letting them do what they want when they want?\par \par \uc2 Well, that's what some parents are doing in a trend called free range parenting. And while some supporters say that it gives kids independence, other parents are actually getting in some big trouble over it.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Eric Shawn has more -- Eric.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, you have probably heard of helicopter parenting. But now there's the opposite. It's called free range parenting, like free range chicken.\par \par \uc2 Mara Doemland's four children, they range in age from 2 years old to 9. Take a look at that video. They're running around the supermarket barefoot. Doemland says she follows the free range concept that applies looser boundaries and less restrictions to child-rearing.\par \par \uc2 But it's also landed some parents in hot water. Heather Wallace was arrested for letting her 8-year-old son, Aiden, walk home alone after karate class in Waco, Texas, last fall. So police charged her with recklessly putting a child younger than 15 in imminent danger. That is a felony.\par \par \uc2 So she took a plea deal to avoid jail time, despite an investigation by Child Protective Services that determined no abuse or negligence had even occurred.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEATHER WALLACE, MOTHER: Regardless of what it really looked like, at that time, I felt 110 percent confident that he was safe and that he could handle a half-a-mile walk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: While free range parenting is meant to encourage independence, critics, though, warn that it could have dangerous consequences.\par \par \uc2 We know about 350 children are abducted by strangers each year in our country, according to the Department of Justice. But the founder of the free range kids movement, columnist Lenore Skenazy, says children are smarter and safer than adults give them credit for.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LENORE SKENAZY, PRESIDENT, LET GROW: The whole idea is that, as parents, we know our kids best.\par \par \uc2 And if it -- if we think it's time for our kids to have some independence, to walk to school, to play outside to wait home alone for a little bit, that's up to us, and no one else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Well, Lenore is also the president of the nonprofit group LetGrow.org.\par \par \uc2 They got these cards that parents can give to their kids. The card, Charles, says: "I'm not lost or neglected. I'm a Let Grow kid." And it also includes the parents' phone number, the kid's name and all this in case the kids are stopped.\par \par \uc2 This won't get -- they hope this won't get the parents in trouble.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. In some states, it might. Others, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Eric.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So what does my next guests make of this parenting style?\par \par \uc2 Independent Women's Forum's Hadley Heath Manning joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Hadley, every time I think of a great parenting, I think of you. It's so funny, because, nostalgically, guys my age, we talk about back in the days when we have more freedom than our kids. And we rode our bikes without helmets. And we played on the monkey bars. And, sometimes, we would disappear for a few hours.\par \par \uc2 Is this the same thing?\par \par \uc2 HADLEY HEATH MANNING, INDEPENDENT WOMEN'S FORUM: Yes, I do think that, in some sense, all parenting before a certain time was free range parenting, that parents more or less told their children, go play, and expected that their children could at least make good choices, within age-appropriate boundaries, of course.\par \par \uc2 Mom and dad would check in when they think it was appropriate. And that's what the free range movement is today. I agree with the reporter who sort of paints this as a contrast to helicopter parenting. I do think the intensive parenting trend that we see today is very exhausting for parents and puts a lot of pressure on parents, when all that children really need, Charles -- and you know this -- is to know that they're unconditionally loved, to be safe and provided for, and to be taught, I believe, certain good behaviors and critical thinking.\par \par \uc2 That's the role of a parent. As long as you're doing that, there's a lot of different ways to do it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I think, though, one of the central tenets of this particular movement, at least from what I have read, though, is that the -- some of those things that you're talking about, that the parent would intervene, in terms of teaching the children certain things, that they're left to discern on their own.\par \par \uc2 You kind of go out and you discern right and wrong. And it just -- it has just a little bit more of a twist, maybe just a little bit more -- quote, unquote -- "independence" than I think many parents would think is reasonable for a young child to have at a certain age, at their age.\par \par \uc2 HEATH MANNING: Right.\par \par \uc2 And parents have to make thousands of judgment calls like this every day for every child that they have in their home. But I would just emphasize that, if you're talking about giving children more freedom and more independence -- and I believe this is one of the tenants of free range parenting -- is that freedom is the flip side of responsibility, that you have to be sure that your children are responsible enough to handle the freedom that you're giving them.\par \par \uc2 And this is true outside of parenting too, Charles. And it's something for all Americans to keep in mind. Freedom and responsibility always go together. We can't give people a lot of freedom without also expecting that they take personal responsibility.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: One of my concerns is, I feel this could be an offshoot of a movement that began about a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 And every now and then -- and, for instance, recently, I heard the Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  referred to children -- the community raising children. And there was a serious movement, and it's still going on beneath the surface, where you really don't -- the kids are not yours. They really -- I think there's a movement where they want -- legally, where children will not be yours. They will belong to the community.\par \par \uc2 And that worries me. That frightens me. Not everyone in the community has my values or even my children's best interests in mind. So I feel like this is a part and parcel of sort of that movement. While, again, I long for the nostalgic days of going out and playing and riding my bike all day, I don't know that, if we take it a step further, that now these kids belong to the community, and no longer any particular household.\par \par \uc2 HEATH MANNING: Well, I'm sure that defenders of free range parenting would say that the busybodies who report on the kids playing alone at the playground or walking home from school by themselves, that those are actually the folks who side with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They say it takes a village, and we all need to keep an eye on other people's children, instead of interesting individual parents to make those decisions about what their children should be able to handle. But I agree with you, Charles. I think the concept of the village raising the children is -- can only be successful when we think about it in terms of a society, a community, and not a government.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HEATH MANNING: Those two things are very different. We have to keep those lines very clear that, yes, when my neighbor's check in on my kids, that's a nice, kind thing for them to do.\par \par \uc2 They're not expected to raise my kids. Only I can do that. But I also don't want the government stepping in and saying, we're going to provide for your children, and we know what's best for them. You don't.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, well, I think this is something we're going to end up talking about a little bit more, because people are trying to figure different things out.\par \par \uc2 And, again, there's a nostalgic part. There's a movement now that feels like there's more crime out there, our kids are more vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 But I really appreciate having this conversation with you, Hadley. Have a great Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 HEATH MANNING: Sure. Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All righty.\par \par \uc2 HEATH MANNING: Hey, you too.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, thank you for watching us. And, remember, you can catch me tomorrow on FOX Business, "Making Money."\par \par \uc2 A pretty good day for the market today, and maybe tomorrow. This is one of the best weeks, generally, in history.\par \par \uc2 For now, here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221122eibm00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Walks Fine Line Over Latest Jobs Report; GOP Reports Alleges \uc2 FBI\uc2  Leadership Problems; Arizona And Pennsylvania Could Determine Senate Control; Pennsylvania Senate Nominees Make Last Plea To Voters; Top Issues Are Inflation And Border Security On Arizona Senate Battle; Widespread Layoffs Begin; Employees Tweet About Being Laid Off; O'Dea Trails Bennet In Narrowing Colorado Senate Race; Colorado Republican Senate Candidate Joe O'Dea Interviewed On His Campaign Against Democratic Incumbent Michael Bennet; Washington And New Hampshire Senate Races Closer Than Expected For Democratic Candidates; Republican House Members Indicate Likely Investigation Into Possible Bias And Abuse Of Power In \uc2 FBI\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Anita Vogel, Bryan Llenas, Kelly O'Grady,  Connell McShane, Shannon Bream \par \uc2 8,116 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Don't miss that.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Do you believe -- I can't even believe I'll be awake.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I think I have my money on -- well, Dana lost last sink or swim.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I've lost every time.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: She's lost every time. So, you're due.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I hope.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: All right, that's it for us tonight. We'll see you back here on Sunday at 5:00.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, with just four days left until the midterm vote, President Biden and the Democrats are trying to turn to the economic narrative and turn it in their favor.\par \par \uc2 The October jobs report shows another solid month of hiring with a slight increase in the unemployment rate. But the headwinds of inflation and skyrocketing interest rates, maybe too much to overcome and that could give control of Congress to the GOP, maybe one chamber, maybe both chambers.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. In the closing days of this campaign cycle, part of the president's pitch is an actual fairy tale as he tries to make the case that today's economic report is not too cold and is not too hot, but rather just right.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: New York Times media reports is called -- they call the report the Goldilocks report. I have my shotgun waiting for the wolf.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden talking about a strong October jobs report and saying about 261,000 jobs created while comments by Republican leadership sure seem to indicate they are rooting for a recession. The U.S. economy continues to grow and add jobs even as gas prices continue to come down.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is trusting the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to address inflation by raising rates. But officials there admitted this week, they don't know what will happen next.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm wondering, has the window for a soft landing narrowed? Do you still think it's possible?\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: Has it narrowed? Yes. Is it still possible? Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president is not ignoring higher prices.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We also know folks are still struggling with inflation it's our number one priority.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But voters appear unsatisfied, listing inflation as the most important issue to their Senate vote in three critical battlegrounds Pennsylvania, Arizona, and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN WALSH\uc2 , LABOR SECRETARY: Somewhere in the future, there might be a recession. There's still lots of positive signs.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That is not on the radar in the West Wing.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There are no meetings or anything happening like that in preparing for a recession.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: On one hand, President Biden is telling midterm voters the elections aren't about him.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Everybody talks about a referendum. It's not a referendum. It's a choice.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: On the other, he's telling midterm voters the elections are about him.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I've already been told, if they win back the House and Senate, they're going to impeach me. I don't know what they'll impeach me for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Biden stops on the campaign trail this weekend. We'll be in Chicago, New York and then Pennsylvania, where he may have just complicated \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's Senate campaign because the president told a crowd in California that he will be shutting down coal fired power plants all across America, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is broken. That is the conclusion of a scathing report by GOP lawmakers in the house. It makes some very serious allegations against Bureau leadership.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Anita Vogel has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANITA VOGEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Just days before the midterm elections, which will determine control of Congress, House Judiciary Committee Republicans have released a report claiming the \uc2 FBI\uc2  under the stewardship of \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is broken.\par \par \uc2 Unnamed whistleblowers describing the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's leadership showing a political bias against conservatives and describing the agency's Washington hierarchy as "rotted at its core" with a systemic culture of unaccountability and full of rapid corruption, manipulation and abuse.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): America is not America if you have a Justice Department that treats people differently under the law. It's supposed to be equal treatment under the law. That's not happening.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: The bulk of the report contains previously released letters from House Republicans to DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials full of complaints, from the federal investigation into the president's son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 's controversial school board memo released last year.\par \par \uc2 Garland's one page memo from October 2021 doesn't include the word parent, but parents across the country felt the documents spelled politics.\par \par \uc2 Days before the \uc2 National School Boards Association\uc2  sent a letter to President Biden, arguing some parental action should be considered a "form of domestic terrorism and hate crimes", the group later apologized.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe vs. Wade, the Republicans argue attacks on pro-life facilities are going unpunished.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 : We have seen a number of attacks against faith based organizations, pregnancy resource centers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL (on camera): There have been zero prosecutions this year of any pro- choice activist linked to any attacks.\par \par \uc2 Now, should the Republicans take back the House next Tuesday night? Look for them to bring both \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ officials before the public and frequently.\par \par \uc2 And we just received this late breaking statement from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  minutes ago which says in part, we follow the facts without regard for politics. While outside opinions and criticism often come with the job, we will follow -- we will continue to follow the facts wherever they lead. Do things by the book and speak through our work, that from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  tonight, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Anita, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's SPECIAL REPORT spotlight, we have reached the grand finale. If you've been watching SPECIAL REPORT over the last seven weeks, we've looked at key midterm races and ballot measures in 48 states across America.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we check off our final two states, Pennsylvania and Arizona and they have implications in the fight for control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, you have incumbent Democratic Senator Mark Kelly hoping to win his first full term in the toss up state and help Democrats maybe hold on to the majority.\par \par \uc2 Republican challenger Blake Masters however has closed the gap. Our latest Fox News poll has it at two points. But as you see here, it is within the margin of error. Some other polls have Masters taking the lead in this race.\par \par \uc2 A large amount of Arizona voters cast ballots early or in person and they actually have those results right after about an hour after polls closed.\par \par \uc2 Mail-in ballots can be dropped off until 7:00 p.m. local time and on election night. Those could take several days to be counted because they require signature verification.\par \par \uc2 Since 2020, Arizona's Republican legislature also eased the threshold for an automatic recount to a half percent.\par \par \uc2 Now, in Pennsylvania Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz fight to replace retiring GOP senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 . Fetterman has a three point lead over Oz in our latest poll, but again, within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 Democrats tend to do well in this -- the counties right around Philadelphia and the big cities, Philadelphia and Pittsburgh. They look at places like here Montgomery County, if you look back to 20. There you go. 2020, 62, 36. If you go up here to Bucks, it's a little bit less and there you have 58, 40.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are going to be looking a little bit further out to the West around Pittsburgh, Butler County, for example.\par \par \uc2 If you look at this, this is big Trump country, and that's where Republicans are looking to get that vote up.\par \par \uc2 Like Arizona, it could be a while before we learn the outcome of the Senate contest in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas tells us why tonight from Upper Darby.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): With just four days to go before Election Day, Senate candidates Democrat Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: And Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz are making their final pitches to voters in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: I will be the 51st vote in D.C. and to want to get all the good things that we want to accomplish, we got to eliminate the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I want to bring balance back into this equation. I want to make sure that the extreme ideas of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  aren't there.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: All indications are this race is a tossup.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of Dr. Oz?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, I'm not that crazy about him, but you know, I'm going to vote for him. You know, I like him better than the other guy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm probably going to vote for Fetterman even though I'm a lifelong Republican.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: As both campaigns try to seize momentum, tonight, Fetterman is touting an endorsement from Oprah Winfrey.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY, THE OPRAH WINFREY SHOW HOST: If I lived in Pennsylvania, I would have already cast my vote for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  for many reasons.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: She's an icon. I mean, it's unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: The late endorsement is particularly salient, given Oprah launched Dr. Oz into T.V. stardom.\par \par \uc2 In a statement the Oz campaign writes, Dr. Oz loves Oprah and respects the fact that they have different politics.\par \par \uc2 In a new memo, the Department of State ordered all counties to segregate and not count all mail-in ballots that are either missing handwritten dates on envelopes or that have incorrect dates.\par \par \uc2 DAVID VOYE, ELECTIONS MANAGER, ALLEGHENY COUNTY, PENNSYLVANIA: We don't know exactly what wrong date is. But it's not -- doesn't have the month, it doesn't contain the year. We don't know what that is. So, we're not going to make that determination without further guidance from the current state.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: It's a recipe for confusion, lawsuits and delayed results, especially if the margin of victory is small.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: It's a close race. It's a jump ball.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): And whoever wins this jump ball could control the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and with so much on the line, both parties are sending their all- stars to Pennsylvania, former President Trump, former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and President Biden will be holding campaign rallies this weekend from Pittsburgh to Philadelphia, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas at the center of the political universe in Pennsylvania. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Now to Arizona, where inflation and the border are the top issues. Fox Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady is in Phoenix tonight with the latest. Good evening, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. Yes, the Arizona Senate race is heating up even more in these final days. According to the latest Fox News polling, just two points separates the Democratic incumbent Mark Kelly from Blake Masters and the GOP candidate has room to grow with libertarian candidate Mark Victor dropping out.\par \par \uc2 And we've been on the ground talking to voters and this really comes down to inflation, border security and abortion as you said. 26 percent of registered Arizona voters say inflation is most important as they head to the polls.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to a restaurant owner right here in Phoenix who shared he may have to close down with costs so high.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, stories like that are not unique as Arizona's Capitol is experiencing the worst inflation in the country.\par \par \uc2 CPI data shows the average for U.S. city was 8.3 percent in August. Phoenix topped that at 13 percent.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Phoenix sits in Maricopa County which represents over 60 percent of registered Arizona voters. Border Security also top of mind with Border Patrol telling us the Tucson sector seeing the most getaways in the country.\par \par \uc2 Blake Masters leaning into that concern today at a GOP roundtable.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF ARIZONA: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Mark Kelly surrendered our southern border, our sovereignty to get it right over to the Mexican narco terrorists drug organizations, they're like business partners.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: But like many other Democratic candidates, Kelly is focusing heavily on abortion instead. Arizona has one of the strictest laws in the country and Kelly is seeking to turn voters against Masters on that issue.\par \par \uc2 MARK KELLY (D), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF ARIZONA: He wants a national abortion ban that is so strict that even in the case of when a woman is raped, she will not be able to make this decision herself. He thinks he gets to make this choice instead of you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (on camera): Now, voters here are incredibly passionate about all three issues but we'll have to wait until next week to see which influences their vote most. Back to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kelly O'Grady live in Arizona. Kelly, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today, the Dow gained 402, the S&P 500 finished ahead 51, the NASDAQ jumped 132.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow lost 1-4/10 percentage points, the S&P 500 was down 3-1/3. The NASDAQ fell 5-2/3.\par \par \uc2 Up next, workers at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  are getting some very bad news tonight. We'll tell you what that is, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The chairman of former President Trump's inaugural committee has been acquitted at a federal corruption trial. \uc2 Tom Barrack\uc2  was accused of using his personal access to then-President Trump, to secretly promote the interests of the United Arab Emirates.\par \par \uc2 The jury deliberated for three days before finding Barrack not guilty of acting as an unregistered agent of a foreign government, obstruction of justice, and making false statements.\par \par \uc2 The rate of deaths that can be directly attributed to alcohol rose nearly 30 percent in the U.S. during the first year of the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 That's according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , the CDC. That agency had previously said the overall number of such deaths rose in 2020 and 2021.\par \par \uc2 Companywide layoffs are underway at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  tonight. The new boss, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is expected to cut around half of his payroll at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , about 17 -- I'm sorry, 3,700 jobs in total.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some details tonight from Connell McShane of Fox Business, who joins us from New York. Good evening, Connell.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX BUSINESS CHIEF NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 You know \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is cutting back in such a big way, because its new owner says there's been a massive drop in its revenue.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE (voice-over): \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  blaming that decline on "activist groups pressuring advertisers, even though nothing has changed with content moderation," and as he says, "we did everything we could to appease the activists."\par \par \uc2 At an investor conference that was held here in New York today, Musk said much of this actually predated his ownership.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, OWNER AND CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, TWITTER: To be frank, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was having pretty serious revenue challenges and cost challenges before the acquisition talks started. And any company that is dependent on advertising has had a hard time.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Well, the big name advertisers pausing their business now, include food company \uc2 General Mills\uc2 , and automakers like \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and \uc2 Audi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  today started what appears to be a massive round of layoffs. The Wall Street Journal says that roughly 50 percent of the workforce has been hit by this. And this is a company that had more than 7,500 employees when the year began.\par \par \uc2 As for the workers, they found out their fate in an e-mail. Some impacted employees now taking to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Michelle Austin headed up public policy there, tweeting: "Woke up to the news that my time working at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has come to an end. I am heartbroken. I am in denial."\par \par \uc2 Others already are trying to push back through the courts. A federal lawsuit was filed on Thursday with a handful of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees, claiming, the company violated the law by not providing enough warning about the layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Musk, in his first week as the owner already have fired top executives, including the CEO and the CFO.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE (on camera): And he's introduced a new plan to charge $8 a month for so-called blue checkmark verification.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , should be noted is not the only social media company that's cutting jobs \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 's parent company, letting 20 percent of its staff go. And \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's parent company, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , reportedly planning to reduce staff as well. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Connell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and German Chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  are condemning threats to use atomic weapons in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Scholz warns Russia risks crossing a line in the international community by restoring to nuclear -- resorting, rather, to nuclear force. Scholz's visit to China is the first by a G7 leaders since the COVID-19 pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll talk live with Republican Colorado Senate nominee Joe O'Dea.\par \par \uc2 First, BEYOND OUR BORDERS tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): South Korea scrambles about 80 military aircraft including advanced F-35 fighter jets after tracking about 180 flights by North Korean war plans inside South Korean territory.\par \par \uc2 The North's foreign ministry calls the action an appropriate response to joint military exercises by the U.S. and South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Iran marks the 1979 takeover of the U.S. Embassy in Tehran, as its theocracy faces nationwide protests after the September death of a 22-year- old woman in police custody.\par \par \uc2 Iranian state-run television aired live feeds of various commemorations around the country. Iran's president criticized those people taking part in anti-government demonstrations, saying they are stepping in the direction of the country's enemies.\par \par \uc2 And 1000s of revelers returned to Hong Kong Stadium for a highly anticipated international rugby sevens tournament. It's the first such event since the COVID-19 pandemic began. It's part of the government's drive to restore the city's image as a vibrant financial hub after its scrapped mandatory hotel quarantines for travelers.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Back to the midterms now. One of the big races that could determine which party controls the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Let's bring in Colorado Republican Senate nominee Joe O'Dea. He was trying to unseat Democratic incumbent Senator Michael Bennet.\par \par \uc2 Joe, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 JOE O'DEA (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, COLORADO: Bret, thanks --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The RealClearPolitics Average in the polls in this race. It split by about a little 4-1/2. But RCP today move Colorado Senate to -- from lean Democrat to tossup. We're still at lean Democrat, but it is closing. What's your closing argument?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Well, I got to be honest with you. When you're talking about the issues that affect working Americans here in Colorado, that's what's moving the needle. We're on spot.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about record inflation. We're talking about record crime here, gas prices that are over the top, diesel still over $5 here in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 That's what matters to working Americans. And that's why the needle is moving.\par \par \uc2 And we feel, we built a huge coalition. We're marching forward. We're going to get over the finish line here on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, we talk -- take a look at candidates final adds just a little sliver of them. This is your closing add, a piece of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Michael Bennet is their yes man. Joe O'Dea is his own man. O'Dea is about action, secure the border, get tough on crime, attack inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Colorado has been elusive for Republicans. It's kind of blue-ish, purple to blue-ish. You think you can change that?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Well, we've got to build a huge coalition. Its Trump supporters, its GOP supporters, its Independents, and Democrats just came from a rally.\par \par \uc2 I'm up in Steamboat Colorado right now. I had five Democrats walk up to me said they'd never voted for a Republican before. They are voting with us because they know, I'm spot on, in what needs to get done in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  to represent working Americans.\par \par \uc2 Michael Bennet has dropped the ball. He's been totally ineffective, hasn't gotten anything done for our state and people recognize that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Here is a Senator Bennet at your last debate with some charges against you. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET (D-CO): This people like Joe O'Dea are getting of -- what the richest people in America, are getting away with murder. They're not paying their taxes.\par \par \uc2 On guns, I just wanted to be very clear. Joe de said he supports no gun laws beyond what we already have as a society. He is against the Red Flag Law we have in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 I don't think Joe O'Dea should be in that hospital room with her when she's got to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talking to there about abortion. Now, your response to those charges.\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Well, you know, he's just making it up as he goes. He's trying to link me to anything he can. He failed to talk about the record inflation that he has caused. He was the deciding vote on the $1.9 trillion rescue plan that sent this economy into a tailspin.\par \par \uc2 I asked him about it and it was crickets. He didn't have any answers. I said, did you regret the spending? Nada. Nothing at all. He doesn't regret it. And if we want six more years of that, that's what we're going to get with Michael Bennet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They have used the abortion issue numerous times. Explain your stance on abortion?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: I've been very clear. For the first five months, I believe that belongs between a woman and her doctor. After that, with the exceptions of rape, incest, life of the mother, medical necessity, that belongs with a woman and her doctor, and I'm going to vote that way. Michael Bennet is extreme. He has been voted for elective abortion up to and including the moment of birth, and he wants to use taxpayer dollars to pay for it. That's extreme.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You had Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  endorse your campaign there. Obviously, the former president has not done that and has said some things. "Slate" writes it this way, "A tight rope, O'Dea's effort to navigate that slim path to victory, though, has been a case study on what it is like to get hammered from both the right and the left and to have to choose every word with the utmost delicacy." Have you been choosing words with delicacy?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Look, I'm a contractor. I'm not a politician. I said what I said, and I'm going to speak my own mind. I am an independent thinker. I'm about Colorado and about working Americans here in Colorado that need a break. They need a break from this record inflation. They need a break from this record crime that Michael Bennet and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have put upon us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is inflation, do you think, the biggest thing that is driving your race, your support?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Working Americans are talking about the cost of groceries. They're talking about the cost of gas, they are talking about the cost of rent. We are just getting our first heating bills. They are up 30 percent here in Colorado. And people can't take much more of that. We have lost 10 percent of our buying power, and the cost of goods is extreme right now. They are having to make tough choices. That's why I'm going to be the next U.S. senator in November.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing. Many Democrats, including the president, are closing with the threats to democracy and election denialism. What are your thoughts on that and how effective or ineffective that messaging is?\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Look, the only threat to democracy is six more years of Michael bennet here in Colorado backing up \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's plan for our country. It is failing. If you look back 22 months ago, we had $2.38 gas, we had $3.50 diesel, and the economy was booming, wages were growing. There are a lot of people that remember that time, and that's where we are going back to when we replace Michael Bennet and I am your next U.S. senator.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow the race closely. We appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 O'DEA: Bret, thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You bet. Up next, our special midterm panel takes a look at the final weekend before election, and we'll bounce around the country.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 31 in Denver, not far from where Joe O'Dea just was, where police report at least 100 vehicles were involved in a crash this morning after the first snowfall of the season. Only minor injuries reported there.\par \par \uc2 FOX 35 in Orlando as \uc2 NASA\uc2  rolls out its Artemis 1 moon rocket. It will sit on the pad until its next launch attempt scheduled for November 14th. The mission will send an unmanned spacecraft on a month-long orbit around the moon and back.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Seattle from our affiliate FOX 13. One in the big stories there tonight and all over the country, the Powerball jackpot hits an estimated $1.6 billion, with a "b," making it the largest lottery prize in history. That translates to about $782 million if the cash option is taken. I highly recommend the cash option. The game has gone 39 consecutive drawings without a winner. Your next chance is tomorrow night. I'm not pushing it, but you never know.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Four, that's how many days we've got left, four more days. And this is big.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): You can feel it happening. There is so much momentum. Everywhere you go the red wave is washing across Virginia again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They get a little bit more excited as you get closer to Election Day, both sides do. We're going to check in with the different races around the country with reporters who are covering them. Let's bring in our special panel tonight, Jason Rantz, radio talk show host at KTTH in Seattle, Julia Manchester, political reporter for "The Hill," and Chris Ryan, "New Hampshire Today," iHeart Radio host. Thank you all for being here.\par \par \uc2 Jason, I want to start out with you and this Washington Senate race where the RCP average now has the incumbent \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  at 49.3 and the challenger, Smiley, at 46.3, and you can see the bar graph where this race has changed and really, as it comes to the end here, getting tight. And it's hard to believe, Jason, is it real on the ground?\par \par \uc2 JASON RANTZ, TALK RADIO HOST: It's absolutely real, and what is driving this are independent voters. When you're looking at some of the most recent polls, that's where the shifts are. And when you want to win as a Republican statewide in Washington state, you are going have to over perform outside of King County. So King County is where Seattle. You get about 40 percent of the vote, you actually have a shot.\par \par \uc2 However, she's not going to win, Tiffany Smiley, though King County. If she gets about 30, 35 percent and she overperforms everywhere else, that's how she wins. And since she is talking about the issues that particularly on the east side of the state people really care about. We are talking about inflation and the economy along with crime, that's how she is getting this kind of momentum. And there is actual energy on the ground that for the first time in a long time it feels like a Republican can actually win this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's interest. And one thing, Jason. What is the closing argument for each? Is \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  going to threats to democracy and abortion? How is her close?\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: Yes, \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  is talking to the Seattle voter. This is all about abortion, you're going to lose your right to access abortion, and democracy is on the line. And Tiffany Smiley is talking all about getting the crime situation under control and then bringing down the cost of just everyday life.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's head to Virginia, and Julia, I tell you, I look at these three House races, and a little inside baseball. On election night, these three House races, Virginia two, Virginia 10 and Virginia seven are going to be indications. This is Spanberger-Vega, this is Wexton-Cao, and this is \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  and Jennifer Kiggans. Fair to say that these are the canaries in the coalmine on election night to tell us how things are going?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, "THE HILL": Yes, absolutely, Bret. And starting in the second district, I would say that \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  is the most vulnerable Democrat out of these three incumbent Democrats who were first elected in the 2018 blue wave, so they have been in Congress for a while now. \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 's district in Virginia Beach area was redistricted in a way that favors Republicans.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, her opponent Jen Kiggins has really been tapping into this national environment that's favorable to Republicans, so talking about those issues we mentioned before, crime and the economy.\par \par \uc2 Moving up north to the seventh district with Spanberger, I would say Spanberger is a bit safer than Luria. Her district moved closer to Washington, D.C., and further away from Richmond, so more Democratic leaning voters. At the same time, though, she still is considered somewhat vulnerable. We know that Prince William County, for example, is somewhat of a blue stronghold. However, there are rural areas in that neck of the woods that could lean red.\par \par \uc2 And in the tenth district I would say \uc2 Jennifer Wexton\uc2  is the safest out of these three. If Hung Cao ousts here, that would be an indication of a very strong red wave. The 10th district is located just outside of Washington D.C. It's blue leaning, was redistricted in a way that favors Democrats as well. So I would say, though, we are looking at these races because Glenn Youngkin performed relatively well for a Republican in some of these districts.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I have it that Virginia Two goes GOP, it's a decent night, Virginia seven goes, it's a good night, Virginia ten goes to the GOP, it's a really, really good night for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 All right, Chris, let's head out to New Hampshire and the Senate race there, which is another one which has really close in the final days. Don Bolduc catching up to the incumbent \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , and that again, on that bar chart, on that graph, rather, is converging right at Election Day. What is the latest on the ground?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS RYAN, TALK RADIO HOST: Yes, you are absolutely right. This was the preferred candidate for \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 . The DSCC put in money in favor of Bolduc. It helped him over the finish line here in New Hampshire. But Bolduc has been able to close. The granite state is very finicky, particularly when it comes to economic issues, and there is a feeling that \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  has tied herself up a little bit too tightly to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And Don Bolduc is more of an independent.\par \par \uc2 He has his negatives, he has his positives, but overall, it feels like this race has tightened. Bolduc does have some momentum. The question is, is he going to be able to get it over the finish line? Is it more that 1.7 percent that you have, or is it more like three percent and is Bolduc a little bit too far off the race? That's what we're going to find out over the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 But Bolduc has certainly outperformed. He barely had any money. He has been buoyed by a lot of D.C. third-party groups. But does the incumbent have enough to hang on? It is going to be interesting to see how this breaks down on election night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and very quickly, the congressional race there, Republicans are poised to do well?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: So, in the two congressional races, CD-2 is a little safter, and that one is probably going to go with \uc2 Annie Kuster\uc2 . What we're going to watch really on election night is if this race is really close, the Senate race, that means that Karoline Leavitt has likely won in CD-1. If \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  is able to win by three or four percent, that means that the Pappas- Leavitt in CD-1 is going to really close. Leavitt is an individual that has been very firm in her support of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and Pappas is your typical kind of moderates Republican -- moderate Democrat who has broken with the president on a number of occasions and is trying to hang on for dear life here in what may be a red wave in the granite state, and, as others are noting, across the country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Jason, Chris, Julia, we really appreciate the time. It's great to check in in all these places.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the SPECIAL REPORT all-star panel with the Friday Lightning Round. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): Our job is to get the facts to the people, and then let's hope at some point we have a Justice Department that actually holds people accountable for their wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK (R-NY): The subpoenas are coming, that's another part of our commitment to America. And people know that the DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2 , they have a different set of standards based upon your political affiliation.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: If there are allegations of misconduct by \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees, we want to make sure that we get that information so that we can use the tools that we have to go after that conduct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In what could be a foreshadowing of a GOP House investigation of partisanship at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the House GOP put out a report, "The potential abuse of this power or even the appearance of abuse erodes the fundamental principle of equality under the law and confidence in the rule of law. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 's tremendous power is precisely why the people's elected representatives in Congress must conduct vigorous oversight, particularly in light of allegations of abuse and misconduct made to date."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  is senior political correspondent for "Axios," chief legal correspondent, FOX NEWS SUNDAY anchor Shannon Bream, and syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 . Hugh, the timing of this is right before the election, but it's still powerful what is in this report, or the allegations against the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: It fuels based turnout. I think \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  has done a fine job. I'm one of the people in the minority in the Republican Party who think he has done a fine job. I think President Trump made a good choice. But there are problems at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that people are reminded up by this report and these headline that go back to Peter Strzok and \uc2 Lisa Page\uc2 , that go back to all of the scandals of the Trump era and what is perceived as persecution of the former president that continues. So it's an important report, and it's certainly timed to get people who were very outraged by the Bureau's conduct motivated for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not only what they did, but also what they chose not to do, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, that's exactly right. And it comes up in the context now of this pro-life, pro-choice discussions and whether there have been arrests in the firebombing and arson and vandalism cases versus entire SWAT teams showing up to arrest peaceful pro-life protesters. That's just one example where even though the \uc2 FBI\uc2  statement says we don't follow the politics, and we trust them not to, that's a visualization that Director Wray has to be very aware of and how it plays publicly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, publicly, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is now a private company. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , though, dealing with a lot of turmoil, including firing a lot of people. Reuters says "Musk orders \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to cut infrastructure costs by $1 billion. Steep infrastructure cuts could put the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  website and app at risk to going down during critical events when users are rushing to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to consume and share information such as moments of crisis or major political events." We have a major political event coming up. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  just moments ago saying that 2,000 content moderators were not affected by any of these layoffs tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "NATIONAL JOURNAL": There is a lot of overreaction on all sides, but look, I think there is a concern that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  needed be shaken up and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  bought it for $44 billion. He needed to cut costs. But at the same time there seems to be an impulsive nature to a lot of the moves he made firing almost half the staff within a week of owning the company. There's a lot at play. The advertisers make a bulk of the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  revenue, and if they are not going to play ball with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , that's going to be a larger problem for the future of the social media platform.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are a lot of companies that are pulling out, Hugh. It's really interesting to watch.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: It's extraordinary. We are paying more attentions to changes in the C Suite at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  than we are to the changes in the Israeli government which is a nuclear power.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: And so it just to me is kind of crazy. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , we were talking in the green room, has done very great things with \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 . He's done great things with \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , SpaceLink. Everything he touches turns out OK. I think we will be fine, but really, it's just a company.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. So as we get ready for this election, there's a lot of midterm rhetoric, Shannon, a lot of charges that people are trying to heat things up. Here are some Democrats, including \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , talking about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Maybe you don't watch television, but if you did, you would see what I see, which are ads about crime every 30 seconds, right? No solutions. They don't care about keeping you safe. They want to keep you scared.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): This country is on track to repeat what happened in Germany when it was the greatest democracy going, elected a chancellor who then co-opted to the media.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, PRESIDENT HISTORIAN: A historian will say what was at stake tonight and this week was the fact of whether we will be a democracy in the future, whether our children will be arrested and conceivably killed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, that is scary, the Germany and the killing, that is scary. But you know, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was saying that Republicans are the ones that are making people scared and angry. By the way, you have James Clyburn on this weekend on FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We do. We're going to ask him about the comment, because any time that you invoke anything regarding the holocaust, Naziism, you get a lot of pushbacks by people who say it devalues the suffering of millions of people who lost their lives. So this isn't the first time he has used that imagery. So we'll talk with that.\par \par \uc2 He also said this week that if you don't vote the correct way, which is presumably for Democrats, it could be not only the end of the democracy here in the U.S., but the end of the world. So there's definitely some hyperbolic language.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But this threat to democracy close, does it work, do you think?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: No, and it feels like a Hail Mary pass. You hear a lot of this in the final week of an election for the party that's losing, democratically, elections and campaigns, and the fact that --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: High voter turnout in Georgia, midterm early vote is higher than it has ever been.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: The Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was in New York state trying to be bail out Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 . There is a possibility this is going to be an outright rejection of Democratic leadership, and the talk of democracy is not responsive to the concerns about economic issues, crime, and immigration that are at the top of many voters' lists.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, as we get closer to an election, we go to a place called Candidate Casino. I don't think you have been to the Casino.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You have $100 in chips, you have to put them down on control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Where do you go?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: I put them all on the Republican side, and I would have been tentative about that two weeks ago. Now I'm putting them all on there. I think Majority Leader McConnell is coming back.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Black chip. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Being neutral and partisan, I am going 50-50.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oh, so good. You're so good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm not much of a gambler. I did bet on Rich Strike at the Kentucky Derby this year, so I feel like all of my good betting karma is used up. So I'm just going to keep it neutral.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, you're excused.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: I still have it at $60 for the Republicans, $40 for the Democrats. Look, the wave is real, the red wave is probably going to sweep the Republicans to a House majority, but these Senate polls are really close.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, winners or losers. Winner first, Hugh.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Winners are McConnell and Kevin McCarthy are going to be in leadership again come the new Congress. The loser is President Biden who can only campaign in deep blue states which is a rejection of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Winner and loser, Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: My \uc2 Liberty University\uc2  football team, the flames have cracked the AP top 25, go Flames. Their one loss is to Wake Forest by one point, that's my winter. Loser, all of us who have to change our clocks again this weekend. Does anybody else hate this? There is a bill that passed in the Senate, it stalled in the House. I call on them in the lame duck session to get this beautiful, bipartisan thing to get done.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I agree. Winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Winner, Dusty Baker, the manager of the Astros, one win away from his first World Series after winning more than 2,000 games. He deserves that, even though the Astros did a little cheating a few years ago. Loser Kyrie Irving, the \uc2 Brooklyn Nets\uc2  star who posted an anti-Semitic neo-Nazi like movie on his social media feed. It took him forever to apologize. It's not a good look for the \uc2 NBA\uc2  either, that campaign on social justice a few years ago, and it took them a long time to condemn anti- Semitism.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We made it through, panel. The loser is the beautiful sunsets, they go away with the clock shift, unfortunately. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is Friday, and that means it's time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is not a referendum. It's a choice. It's a fundamental choice.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Republicans have talked a lot about inflation. They've done nothing about it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My campaign has been focused on one issue, and it's inflation, inflation, inflation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A show of hands, how many here think inflation and the economy is the number one issue in this race? And you are one of them?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am one of them, of course.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Is who will fight for you? That's the choice in this election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Somebody would target a political leader's home, target her family.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Who would you caucus with?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would not caucus with the Democrats or the Republicans. I'm going to maintain my independence.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): They Democratic Party controls the House, the Senate, the White House. Everything is worse than it was 22 months ago.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: How many of you believe that the border is secure in this group?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Show of hands.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), SENATE CANDIDATE: We've really got to get control of this. It's killing our people.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Millions more will cast their ballots in the final days leading up to November the 9th.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): If somebody told you as a democrat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is here to help you, you'd jump out the damn window because he is so unpopular.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Isn't November the 8th the Election Day. All right, well, there you go, that's one week.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, the final day of campaigning before the crucial midterm elections. And please join Shannon, FOX NEWS SUNDAY this weekend, there are her guests. We talked about them.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221105eib400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  (R-WI) Running For Reelection In Wisconsin; New York Supreme Court Orders Backpay And Reinstates All Employees Fired For Being Unvaxxed; Unhinged Student Spits At Comedian During Protest; Trans Activist Who Shows Off His Bulge In Dresses And Bikinis On TikTok Interviews Biden At The White House; Interview With Rep. \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  (R-GA) Targeted By Another Swatting Police Call\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson \par \uc2 7,519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Do I do the shows for free? Sometimes it feels like that.\par \par \uc2 That's all for tonight. DVR the show, which apparently I'm doing for free.\par \par \uc2 Tucker is always next and remember, I'm Watters and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 What's that sound you hear? Oh, it's the sound of America getting a little better. You can feel it happening.\par \par \uc2 Last night, we played you a clip from an unintentionally hilarious MSNBC Town Hall in which some pompous airhead reporter tries her best to explain to Trump voters why January 6 actually was an insurrection, an insurrection in which only the police had guns inside the Capitol, an insurrection which the police welcomed the insurrectionists inside the building to insurrect. Quite an insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the insurrection that went nowhere, where the leader was dressed in Viking horns, eating psilocybin mushrooms.\par \par \uc2 So, we thought it was pretty funny. What we didn't note though last night was the moment where that airhead reporter claims that a police officer was killed by the mob that day.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you watch television, you've heard that line so many times -- so many times that you may not even notice when people say it anymore. They've been saying it constantly for nearly two years.\par \par \uc2 "Police officers were killed, many of them." Why are they saying that? It's not true. It's provably a lie. It is in no sense accurate. No police officer was killed by the mob on January 6th. Anyone who claims otherwise is always welcome on this show live to explain who exactly was killed. What's that officer's name and how was he killed?\par \par \uc2 We don't expect any takers on that offer. So, the question is, why do they keep telling us that? Well, there's a reason. There's always a reason. They're telling us that likely, so we won't ask too many questions the next time we read about yet another January 6 protester going to jail. We'll just think "Oh, another cop killer getting what he deserves." That's what they're hoping you will think, and they're hoping that because if you knew the truth, if you knew why they're really going to jail, you'd be very concerned.\par \par \uc2 And here is the truth: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Justice Department is imprisoning people for having the wrong thoughts, thoughts that we all imagine were constitutionally protected, as all thoughts are, but don't take our word for it. That's just a right-wing talking point on our cable channel.\par \par \uc2 You can read the Court filings in January 6 cases over the past year and a half and in virtually every instance, Federal prosecutors argue in public that the political views of the defendants, not anything they actually did, not crimes they committed, but instead their personal beliefs, what they think, those beliefs are reason enough to put them behind bars.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's DOJ, for example, Army veteran Jessica Watkins needed to be detained indefinitely because she had, "extremist and violent views regarding how to address what she believed to be a fraudulent election." Oh, she was an extremist? It's a term they never explain, but that apparently is now a crime. So, we decide what extremist means and then we put you in jail for being one. That's how that works.\par \par \uc2 UCLA student Christian Secor, that was the guy who sat in \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 's chair on January 6th is now in prison, and he should be in prison, explained the Biden administration's lawyers, because he had "extremist beliefs" and a "history of adhering to extremist ideology." Really? Again, you decide what extremist is and then put me in prison for being one.\par \par \uc2 In the case of Robert Morss, that was the guy who was busted in possession of a Lego set of the Capitol, the DOJ put it this way: "It is difficult to fathom a more serious danger to the community" than someone who tried to "abort the certification of a lawful and fair election." It's the one kind of abortion they don't favor, the aborting of a fair and free election.\par \par \uc2 So, in every case, though, the defendant's real crime was not aborting an election. No. The real crime, and the DOJ says it right in the filings, is denying the outcome of the election. It was believing the wrong thing. It was not believing that a senile man who refused to leave his basement during the campaign somehow got more votes than any President in American history. If you refuse to believe that, you're now a felon.\par \par \uc2 Question the system and they haul you away. Those are the rules now as hundreds of imprisoned Republicans can personally testify if they could speak. The legal precedent has been established.\par \par \uc2 You cannot deny elections and yet, even in the face of the mounting evidence, this is now a very serious felony, there are some extremists who are not deterred. They're continuing to commit the crime of election denial and they're bold enough to do it on video. Here, ladies and gentlemen, is one of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER US SECRETARY OF STATE: I know we're all focused on the 2022 Midterm Elections and they are incredibly important, but we also have to look ahead because you know what? Our opponents certainly are.\par \par \uc2 Right-wing extremists already have a plan to literally steal the next presidential election, and they're not making a secret of it. The right- wing controlled Supreme Court may be poised to rule on giving State Legislatures -- yes, you heard me that correctly -- State Legislatures the power to overturn presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Did you hear that? Right-wing extremists will literally -- literally -- must teach that word at NPR -- literally steal the next election. That's pre-emptive election denial committed by Hillary Clinton herself.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing, probably not likely that an FBI SWAT team is going to show up at Hillary Clinton's house when she's in the shower tomorrow morning and in fact, the next time a Courthouse is attacked in Portland, Oregon, or a church is torched in downtown Washington, DC, it's unlikely that a DOJ Court filing will cite what Hillary Clinton just said as an extremist ideology that led to violence.\par \par \uc2 Why is that? Well, because in the considered opinion of the Democratic Party and the DOJ, it's not election denial when they do it. No, it's just "election affirming care." And there's been a lot of election affirming care going around these past few weeks.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, in the hometown newspaper owned by \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," \uc2 Max Boot\uc2  just announced that, we're quoting, "If the current trends hold up, Republicans are likely to take over at least the House and quite possibly the Senate, too, along with many State offices. This is how democracies die, both at home and abroad."\par \par \uc2 In other words, if you vote for the wrong person, that's not democracy, which by the way, unlike dictatorship, does not always come to the conclusion that you favor. That's not democracy. That's the death of democracy. Democracy dies in both darkness and non-liberal election outcomes. Oh okay.\par \par \uc2 "New York" Magazine isn't waiting around for the next election. Here is their latest dispatch: "There are already signs of 2020 election deniers refusing to accept their own possible defeat this year," and that's half true, of course, but "New York" Magazine isn't talking about Republicans: "Most observers are primarily worried that Trump's 2020 shenanigans may have served as a dress rehearsal for 2024."\par \par \uc2 Wait, what? So that means you have Democrats two years before the presidential election telling us that that election is going to be rigged, but that's not undermining confidence in elections? That's not an attack on our sacred norms? Not when it happens in "New York" Magazine or at \uc2 PBS\uc2 , which, by the way, you continue to fund with your tax dollars, the channel nobody watches.\par \par \uc2 The only people who watch \uc2 PBS\uc2  are the ones who are stuck in traction in the attendance, going out for a cigarette and taking the remote. But they're still broadcasting and here's what they're saying, "Nearly one in three Republican candidates for statewide office support false election claims."\par \par \uc2 Why aren't they in prison and what is the endgame of these dangerous extremists led by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Well, "Vanity Fair" figured it out. They blew the plan wide open. "And then there's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ," "Vanity Fair" reported. "He hasn't been implicated in any murders (that we know of) nor has he currently trying to kill anyone (again, that we know of). He is, however, said to be actively plotting to steal the Midterm Elections and the presidential one after that."\par \par \uc2 Did you hear that? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to steal the Midterm Elections and then the Presidential Election. How is he going to do that? And isn't predicting he's going to do that, isn't that itself a crime? We can't say. We're not \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , but we do know how he's going to do it, because \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  has been giving that away for years. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to do that as he always has done it, with help from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  himself. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLINTON - 2019: We know the Russians hacked the DNC, hacked my campaign, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Trump knows he's an illegitimate president who got illegitimate foreign help.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON - 2019: I do think that he knows that he's an illegitimate President.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY -2019: I believe he knows he's an illegitimate President.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY - 2019: You can run the best campaign. You can even become the nominee and you can have the election stolen from you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Stolen from you by Putin? We better have a nuclear war with him. Oh, we're moving toward that. Too bad every Republican leader in Washington is for it. How dumb are these people?\par \par \uc2 If you take three steps back, you will note that nothing the January 6 defendants have ever said, even the guy with the Viking horns on acid, nothing they've said is half as crazy as what you just heard \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  say.\par \par \uc2 In Federal Courtrooms, January 6 defendants are being sentenced to prison because they're not exactly sure how it is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got 15 million more votes than Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2  did. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 ? The rock star who was President and yet his Vice President was so stupid that even \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  could barely stand to be in the same room in? You're saying, he got 15 million more votes than he did. How did that work?\par \par \uc2 How did \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  get more votes than any President in American history? Is that not a fair question? No, it's not. It's a crime to ask and yet no one bats an eye when Hillary Clinton tells us that Putin is literally going to steal the election.\par \par \uc2 So, how is it that only one side is allowed to deny the results of elections? Okay, no one answers that.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  claiming that Democrats don't question election results.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a nation that believes in the rule of law. We do not repudiate it.\par \par \uc2 This is a nation that respects free and fair elections. We honor the will of the people. We do not deny it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "We do not deny it." Unless, of course, two weeks out from a pivotal midterm election, we get the sense based on polling that we're about to be spanked like the bad little girls we are, at which point we start making excuses and planting the seeds for more election denial, just as we did after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won. That's exactly what's going on now.\par \par \uc2 Even as they try to destroy the careers and tear down the character of people who ask honest questions about the last election, and that would include \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 , who is the senator from Wisconsin, the Republican, one of the very few Republicans in the entire \uc2 US Senate\uc2  who has actually improved every year that he served there, that's the opposite of the normal progression for Republicans in the Senate, in case you haven't noticed.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  is running against someone who is legitimately far out. His name is Mandela Barnes. On \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , Barnes once rang in the new year by praising -- and few politicians do this -- by praising Iran's Ayatollah because he supports BLM and we're quoting, "The first tweet of 2015 from @khamenei_ir is #BlackLivesMatter. Let that sink in. May This be a most wonderful year for you and yours," Barnes wrote.\par \par \uc2 So, he has got the Ayatollah vote. Barnes also wants to abolish ICE, defund the police, and end cash bail. That would result in further chaos and the total destruction of American society. It might be worth following up on that. But no, the media in Wisconsin are still talking about \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 's election denial.\par \par \uc2 Here's his hometown newspaper, which no one reads in fairness, attacking him the other day for it: "Election deceiver, science fabulist, billionaire benefactor. After 12 years, it's time to term-limit Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 So, again, just to be clear, Democrats are sending people to prison for questioning the last election, trying to prevent people from getting elected in the Midterm Election for questioning the last election, while at the very same time telling you that the 2016 election was rigged, the Midterm will likely be rigged and we know for a fact the 2024 election is not on the level.\par \par \uc2 How does this work exactly?\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  joins us tonight. Senator, thanks so much for coming on. Is it a little weird to be attacked by your hometown newspaper as in election denier on the very same day, the people who run the Democratic Party are preemptively denying the outcome of an election that hasn't even happened yet.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): Oh, hello, Tucker. Those are some of the better headlines I received from the mainstream media here in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's face it. What is happening here is a classic tactic of the left, falsely accuse your political opponents of doing exactly what you do. Let's face it, how many leading Democrats said that the 2016 election was legitimate? Now, they spent four years trying to overturn it, a corrupt \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation. It was the complicit and corrupt media that allowed all of this to go on.\par \par \uc2 They were fed all the false information. Why won't they expose the people that fed them false information? What about the 570 riots during the summer of 2020? Two thousand law enforcement officers injured. One to two billion dollars' worth of property damage, couple dozen people killed including two people here in Kenosha, why don't they talk about that?\par \par \uc2 No, all they do is focus on January 6, I think this has always been a massive diversionary operation against their -- from their own wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 So again operation of the left. It's a classic tactic. And unfortunately, they have the complicit and corrupt media just backing them up.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you're saying things out loud you're not supposed to say out loud. We interviewed earlier today, Dr. Robert Epstein, who is a Professor of Psychology who studies \uc2 Google\uc2  and he told us that he has never seen \uc2 Google\uc2  put its sizable thumb on the scale more aggressively than in Wisconsin right now, that \uc2 Google\uc2  is trying to lose you the race and get Mandela Barnes elected because they're so threatened by the things that you are saying.\par \par \uc2 Are you aware of this and what's your response to it?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No, I'm fully aware and I know it is his work. You know, he's talking about how \uc2 Google\uc2  uses their search to manipulate the opinion, how they only text, for example, "Get out the Vote" to Democrat supporters.\par \par \uc2 We saw \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  use his Zucker bucks, completely took over the Green Day election in 2020, and yet, this is the safest most secure election. Of course, it wasn't. There were all kinds of irregularities.\par \par \uc2 I held a hearing in December of 2020, because I was not going to scornfully dismiss the legitimate concerns of tens of millions of Americans, because observers weren't able to observe. Here in Wisconsin, we have the Legislative Audit Bureau, nonpartisan, bipartisan audit of our election, 31 recommendations, because things went wrong.\par \par \uc2 We had our Wisconsin Election Commission issue guidance that is contrary to State law; election clerks, procedures contrary to state law; and yet we're all supposed to say, "Oh, no, it was the most secure election." It wasn't.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, it wasn't. And they absolutely hate you for saying that out loud and that is why we're certainly rooting for you.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  of Wisconsin. Good luck in that race.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So Blake Masters has never been in politics before. Now, he is running for \uc2 US Senate\uc2  in Arizona. It looks like he is winning. He is one of a handful of candidates who could determine the future of the Senate and of the Republican Party. He is completely different for most Republican senators you're familiar with.\par \par \uc2 So we spent we spent -- we sent our producers months embedded with Blake Masters' campaign. He gave us remarkable access, never seen anything like it and the result is the documentary called "The Candidate: Blake Masters." It's out right now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 Before the documentary, we shot footage of Masters preparing and maybe reflecting on his debate with the incumbent, Senator Mark Kelly. Here is a preview from it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R), ARIZONA SENATE CANDIDATE: Hi. [Bleep]. How do you say hi?\par \par \uc2 Hello.\par \par \uc2 Hi, I'm Blake Masters. I'm running to be your next senator because our current senator -- it's time.\par \par \uc2 TED SIMONS, \uc2 PBS\uc2 : Good evening, and welcome to the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  debate hosted by Arizona PBS and Clean Elections.\par \par \uc2 I'm Ted Simons, and tonight, we feature a debate among the candidates for \uc2 US Senate\uc2 . The race has widely been --\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: You have to appreciate how well that went.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE BLANTON, WIFE OF BLAKE MASTERS: And also big.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Seriously.\par \par \uc2 BLANTON: You were in the middle. You got number one --\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Oh, the middle is great.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The imagery with these two short guys next you.\par \par \uc2 BLANTON: Yes. They looked tiny.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I hope it's not a contest of height.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: I hope it is.\par \par \uc2 Sir, how are you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): You doing okay?\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Yes sir. How are you?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Good.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: He was a nice guy.\par \par \uc2 BLANTON: Yes, so we can pick order out there.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Kelly, he lent me his pen.\par \par \uc2 BLANTON: I saw that.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: I had a pen, but I --\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Here you go.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Do you have another one?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: I don't understand why he wants to be Senator, because like he doesn't care about anything he, never talks about it. You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Yes, we have more work to do. Families are struggling, nobody can't afford gas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, everything sucks.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: So it's why I don't feel bad being like he's a rubber stamp for Bide. Like everything else is true.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: And we're sick of seeing Arizona families suffer just because Mark Kelly wants to fit in in DC.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: He is definitely not horrible, but I could tell he was a little flustered. I heard some wavering in his voice.\par \par \uc2 BLANTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Look, on the border, I called them out on it because I'm always going to --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He is losing it. He is [bleep] losing it.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Senator Kelly, are you honestly saying you've done everything in your power to secure the border?\par \par \uc2 One question, sir, have you done everything in your power to secure our Southern border?\par \par \uc2 It is a real question I'd like you to answer.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I've been focused on the border since day one on this job.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Well, if you've really given it your best shot, it has failed because the border is wide open. You should resign.\par \par \uc2 Because we have a wide open Southern border, so if that's the best you can do I respectfully request you resign and let's secure the border.\par \par \uc2 I thought -- I actually think one thing that he did that was effective was probably like look we all know a guy like him.\par \par \uc2 BLANTON: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I think we all know guys like this, you know, guys that think they know better than everyone about everything.\par \par \uc2 No, you think you know better than women and doctors about abortion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: First time they've done split screen. It a set piece.\par \par \uc2 BLANTON: So irritating, because you're so not that guy.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Yes. Everybody does that and I got it. It's like, hell, I don't know better than a woman or the doctor.\par \par \uc2 BLANTON: Yes, that was his best moment.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: But I do know that it's like, you can't kill an eight-month-old baby.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: A surprisingly deep look at a really unusual can