State of Pennsylvania, you'll have real concerns about the system being legitimate. We will have the whole thing tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Here is Sean.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020221026eiap00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with Jared Bernstein\b0\par\par\uc2 Bill Hemmer, Dana Perino \par \uc2 2,409 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The new consumer price report revealing inflation remains red hot, continues a trend have an effective pay cut for American workers. You're getting less with your money. Gas prices continue to climb, \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  expected to continue the rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 So what is the administration going to do to get inflation under control?\par \par \uc2 With us now, Jared Bernstein, \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  from the North Lawn.\par \par \uc2 And, sir, thank you for your time. It's good to have you on.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I wish we had better news. I'm certain that you wish this was better news.\par \par \uc2 But at the heart of this is the core inflation number. And when it comes to core inflation, that does not include energy or food. But yet that number is the highest we have seen since 1982.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Sir, is it time for this White House in the Biden administration to pivot on its economic policies?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No.\par \par \uc2 I think what we have is, the White House, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , we are acutely aware of the pressures that this causes on American families and budgets. The president started out this morning by talking about how Americans are squeezed by the cost of living, and they didn't need this report to remind them of that. But that's certainly in the report.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK, just to be clear, I asked you that question, and you said no.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, look -- and here's why I said no.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So, are you saying no change? Are you saying no change in policy?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So let me explain why.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Please.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes, let me explain why, because the policies that we're doing to help mitigate and ease price pressures are having effect.\par \par \uc2 So, for example, look at the supply side of the economy, supply chains. Shipping costs, air freight costs are significantly down. Goods are getting to ship to shelf at the same level they did pre-pandemic. And now go into this report, the one that came out this morning, and look at goods inflation. It was flat, if you take out energy.\par \par \uc2 Now, you mentioned that energy prices went up in September. In fact, according to this month's CPI, energy prices fell 2 percent in September and gas prices fell.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I stripped -- sorry -- to be clear, I stripped energy out of my question, because I was just talking about core inflation.\par \par \uc2 Continue.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, core inflation strips out energy and food. But when we're talking about squeezing family budgets, we really have to talk about energy and food.\par \par \uc2 And it is the case that, while gas prices did start to rise at the end of September, in today's report, they were down 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 But, look, nobody is denying your fundamental point, which is, this is a real squeeze on family budgets. And I think the question is, are policymakers doing what they need to do? The president released oil from the Strategic Reserve. That contributed to almost 100 day of gas declines.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned our work on the supply side, the Inflation Reduction Act, lowering medical care costs, lowering prescription drug costs, lowering the cost of clean energy.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, all of these are moving in the right direction. They take time to factor into these price indices.\par \par \uc2 And we'd like to have that -- we'd like to see that happen a lot quicker.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK, we're going to come back to medical costs, first, Dana has got a question.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I have a couple of questions here.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, the other day -- I don't want to play the sound, so we have more time with you -- the president told Jake Tapper that, if there's a recession, it will be a very slight recession.\par \par \uc2 Did you or your office give him some data that backs that up?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: That's a fairly common view among market analysts.\par \par \uc2 What the president said was that a recession is far from inevitable. And I think what he was referring to there is the strength of our job market. Look, you just don't have a recession when you have a 3.5 percent unemployment rate and you're adding hundreds of thousands of jobs per month. That's just completely inconsistent with recession.\par \par \uc2 You heard \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  talking yesterday about strong household balance sheets. Consumer spending remains solid. So I think the idea -- and we have argued about this. I think you have argued that there's a recession out there. I think that's very hard to square with the facts on the ground, particularly regarding the job market.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: That doesn't mean prices are OK. Prices are elevated, and we have to do everything we can to ease those pressures.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: We interviewed a guy last week named Ray Mikesell. He owned a cafe called Cafe Raymond. He's in Pittsburgh. He can't stay afloat. And he's really upset.\par \par \uc2 Just listen to this here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAY MIKESELL, OWNER, CAFE RAYMOND: I have been in business almost 16 years now,. To find fight them anymore and try to stay open and hire people, it's been almost three years.\par \par \uc2 Have a passion for what I do, but I just want to -- I don't want to do it this way no more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It breaks his heart, because you can't find workers. Everything is costing way too much.\par \par \uc2 He's actually having to close, leave his community, and he's going to move to Montana.\par \par \uc2 So, when you talk to somebody real like that, not just people in our bubbles...\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: ... in Manhattan or in D.C....\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: ... what's your answer to him?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: It's what I started out with, Dana.\par \par \uc2 Look, the question that I think folks need to be asking is, are the policymakers who are dealing with this global inflation problem -- inflation is actually higher in Europe than it is here -- dealing with this global inflation problem here in the U.S., are they doing what they need to do to ameliorate this problem?\par \par \uc2 And what I would say to that gentleman is, full speed ahead, 100 percent yes. It's what I told Bill earlier.\par \par \uc2 So, for example, you run a restaurant, you want your goods, you want your deliveries to get to you without delay. Well, that's something that's actually occurring now, partially due to our work on the supply side of the economy. Supply chains are much less snarled. Goods are getting from ship to shelf, from port to restaurant more quickly.\par \par \uc2 On the labor market side, we did see some promising developments relative to what the gentleman was saying. The last report on job vacancies had been coming down by a million. That's significant in terms of taking some of the excess heat out of the labor market, that gap between the workers that firms need and the labor supply.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 So, I want to play you a clip. This is not from a politician. This is from a businessman, \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 . You know him well.\par \par \uc2 We have done a 180 on energy policy in America. He's got a real problem with that. Roll this clip.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, \uc2 J.P. MORGAN CHASE\uc2 : In my view, America should have been pumping more oil and gas, and it should have been supported. We have a longer-term problem now, which is, the world is not producing enough oil and gas to reduce coal, make the transition, create security for people.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, America needs to play a real leadership role. America is the swing producer, not Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: So, look, I mean, everybody's filling up their tank, and they're wondering why repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: And you mentioned medical costs, and I was e-mailing Edward Lawrence. You know him well, works at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Medical care services in this report went up year over year 6.5 percent. And the American Rescue Plan has -- it was passed more than a year-and-a- half ago. And yet the price was still going through the roof. I mean, whether it's energy or whether it's medical costs, people feel this in their lives.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Let's talk about both of them.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes, please, go.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Totally agree with you. People feel these in their lives. It's an important point, Bill, one that we should never lose. And we don't.\par \par \uc2 This is where President -- these are table -- kitchen table issues for him in -- when he grew up. And so let's talk about both of those.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to energy, it's still the case that the price of gas is down about $1.10 from its peak, actually fell a penny today, by the way. And that's important because it relates to the release of 180 million barrels from the Strategic Reserve. And the reason...\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: But it used to be so much lower.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: But hold on, Bill. Hold on. Let me finish.\par \par \uc2 The reason I raise that is because it gets exactly to my point. Are policymakers doing what they need to do to respond to precisely the pressures you made? Yes on energy. Yes on medical costs. Again, look at the inflation reduction at; $35 a month for insulin, that's way below what seniors are paying now.\par \par \uc2 Capping prescription drug out of pocket at $2,000. You got people paying $8,000, $9,000, huge savings there. Again, ask the question, not is inflation too high? The answer to that is unequivocally yes. Are policymakers doing everything they can in this White House to help bring them down?\par \par \uc2 And I think you would have to find that the answer is yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I don't know. I mean, I don't know. I mean, we're covering this every day.\par \par \uc2 People are saying that -- like, 86 percent of them saying that inflation and the economy are their issue. But it's even deeper than that. When you are actually following people who are -- maybe they don't pay that much attention to Washington, but all they know is that they're paying a lot more for gas. Housing, they're paying a lot more.\par \par \uc2 They don't understand why all of these things aren't working for them. But they do think that whoever is the party in power, it becomes a referendum, a midterm election, on a president. It's not a choice between Biden and Trump. That might happen in 2024. And we will have that debate then.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, you just see these -- the American people feel like they are getting poorer, that we just saw that report out today, that they just -- 47 percent describing their feelings as poorer.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, look, I...\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And I know that there's a lot of numbers, but what about the heart?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes, so you're asking an economist about heart. Let me see what I can do with that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, it's -- it's -- I get -- look, not only -- yes, I get where you're coming from.\par \par \uc2 But President Biden, he approaches this with head and with heart. I mean, he -- as he would say, I literally grew up in a family where these were kitchen table issues.\par \par \uc2 I know from personal discussions with him how acutely he realizes what these pressures mean to household budgets. So, he has dispatched us to do everything we can.\par \par \uc2 I talked about the release of oil from the Strategic Reserves. You said people are paying more at the pump. Well, they're actually paying $1.10 per gallon less at the pump than they were in mid-June.\par \par \uc2 Now, that -- we have to continue to press on that. You have heard the president talk very much in this spirit. He's pointed out that the gap between wholesale and retail prices is historically high. And he's calling on American energy companies, who, by the way, are highly productive right now -- we're soon going to hit the most productive production of oil in America's history.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK. OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, he's told...\par \par \uc2 PERINO: We hear you.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: He's told these companies they need to share some of those margins, give people a little breathing room at the pump. I think that's a fair call from this president.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I think the only thing we can wonder is, we started out whether or not there was going to be a pivot on policy, and we're going to find out on November 9 if that that's the case, because you may be pushed into it based on the outcome as well.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes, well, you know, the problem is -- let me just say one other thing.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We got to go, but -- we got to roll.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I know we have to go, but the problem is, look at which way that pivot goes.\par \par \uc2 If the Republicans want to take down the Inflation Reduction Act, that's an increase in health care costs. That's an increase in clean energy costs. So, let's watch that pivot, the policy implications therein.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK, Jared, listen, thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Thank you. My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: You have been game for all our questions.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: At least it wasn't raining or super hot this time.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Well, we could go for an hour, but I don't know if we would get anywhere.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I will see you next time.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Jared, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020221013eiad00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Ohio Senate Battle; \uc2 PayPal\uc2  Under Pressure; Rail Strike Still Possible?; Date Set For Coronation of \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 ; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2  (R-KS); Migrant Wave\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Meredith, Ashley Webster, Kelly O'Grady, Brett Velicovich,  Jacqui Heinrich, Griff Jenkins, Martha MacCallum, Laura Ingle, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,569 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Here we go again, FOX on top of yet another massive wave of migrants arriving in Texas today, pressure mounting on the administration to do something about it or at least talk about it, as more buses are pouring into the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2  as a result of it.\par \par \uc2 And we, well, we're all over it with Griff Jenkins in Eagle Pass, Texas, on the crowds of migrants still streaming in, Laura Ingle on New York City's mayor now trying to slow those migrant buses down, and Border Patrol Council's Brandon Judd on how his officers are just trying to keep up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 So much to get to. Let's first get to the border in Eagle Pass. That's where you will find our Griff Jenkins.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And the Vice President Harris would just come for one day, every single day, we get these big groups here in the Eagle Pass area. It's been going on for months. Let me quickly take you to some drone footage we shot this morning. They arrived like clockwork.\par \par \uc2 This group, Neil, had 340 migrants in it from Peru, Venezuela, Colombia, Cuba, and even places as far as Sri Lanka. We were able to speak to a man from Sri Lanka. Here's a little bit of our exchange. Take a listen, Neil.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Why are you here?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We want to live here. We love -- we like U.S.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: You -- the U.S. And do you have any message for President Biden?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We thank him for accepting us for living here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And as you continue to look at the waves of migrants that have been coming every single day -- I have been here now for 11 days, Neil -- there's over 15,000 migrants have been encountered in just this area.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to one of the landowners. A woman named Magali Urbina owns a pecan orchard where migrants come every single day. She's very critical about why the vice president won't come here. Here's what she had to say. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAGALI URBINA, HEAVENLY FARMS: Well, she would see what I see. And I do not believe that the border is secure. But I would love for her to define what a secure border looks like, because maybe we have it wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And, Neil, not everyone is seeking asylum. Take a look at this photo of this gentleman. Wilmer Castro-Murillo, 44 years old from Honduras, was arrested two days ago up in Kinney County by Texas DPS.\par \par \uc2 Turns out, after a criminal check background, he has an Interpol warrant for murder out of Honduras, just all parts of this crisis down here. Finally, let me just tell you, those buses you mentioned continue to roll to New York City, particularly from El Paso. We're learning today that the El Paso government, who says they have had some 26,000 migrants released in their city, they'd like the feds to reimburse them for their efforts to the tune of $6.1 million.\par \par \uc2 Neil, we will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Griff Jenkins, at the border for that.\par \par \uc2 Well, New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  is looking for another solution to the bus after busload of migrants that make their way to his city. How about slowing the buses down?\par \par \uc2 Laura Ingle has more on this in New York -- Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, another challenge is being folded into the influx of migrants making their way here into New York City, with bus drivers from Texas seen by The New York Post getting traffic tickets near the \uc2 Port Authority\uc2 , where they are dropping off migrant passengers.\par \par \uc2 Now, a team of NYPD Highway Patrol officers were spotted pulling over and searching several buses for about an hour last Friday, according to The New York Post, which reported the buses were being combed over for any possible infractions, some drivers getting tickets for violations, including tire tread fluid levels and windshield wiper placement.\par \par \uc2 One driver was seen holding up three criminal court appearance tickets after it was all over, many drivers saying they are just doing the job they were hired to do. And some question the motive behind the traffic stops. \uc2 NYPD\uc2 's chief of department told reporters they have an obligation to keep New Yorkers safe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KENNETH COREY, \uc2 NYPD\uc2  CHIEF OF DEPARTMENT: The whole unit, our motor carrier safety unit, both within our highway district and within our citywide traffic task force, that focuses on commercial vehicle enforcement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGLE: So they're just doing their job.\par \par \uc2 Texas Governor Greg Abbott maintains his state is dealing with, of course, we have been talking about this, the unprecedented influx from what he calls President Biden's border policies and says he is forced to send thousands of migrants to Democratic sanctuary cities like here in New York, calling the traffic stops a -- quote -- "pathetic ploy" by Mayor Adams to stop Texas from busing migrants to his self-declared sanctuary city.\par \par \uc2 Now, after declaring a state of emergency last Friday, New York City Mayor Adams called the situation a humanitarian crisis that is what he calls unsustainable that will likely cost the city $1 billion by the end of the fiscal year -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for that, Laura Ingle.\par \par \uc2 That figure, just raise it by about eight times, and you have got a good idea how much states like Texas are spending to deal with the same problem.\par \par \uc2 Brandon Judd is the National Border Patrol Council president.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of these moves, at least in New York, where they're trying to slow the buses down by having these very, very long and involved inspections and citing them for a whole host of infractions? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: Well, my answer is going to surprise you. I'm actually very happy that he's doing it, because what he's doing is, he's actually drawing attention to this.\par \par \uc2 He is unwittingly becoming the lead voice for border security. He's showing everybody the reason why we have to have border security. If he's not doing this, every single time he opens his mouth or every single time he takes an action like this, it's causing the mainstream media to cover it. And when they cover that, then we're tuning into the actual outlets that are going to give us the truth.\par \par \uc2 And as long as we have an honest conversation, if that honest conversation takes place, the American people are going to be outraged on what's going on, on the border, and they're going to show up to the polls. And that's what we have to have.\par \par \uc2 That's what has to happen. We have to solve this problem at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you're quite right to say he is drawing attention this. And to be fair to \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , he had set faulted both some of the theatrics -- that's what he calls it -- out of Texas. But he's also cited the president for not recognizing the seriousness of the problem.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the vice president was saying as much last night, that we need to come up with a bipartisan solution here. Good luck with that, though. I'm just wondering, is anything really changing here?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: No.\par \par \uc2 And that's what's really frustrating, because we have given, I have given this administration three different solutions. You like to talk about solutions. I appreciate that. We have given this administration three different solutions. And they have rejected every one of them.\par \par \uc2 Two of those solutions operate within his own parameters, and he rejects it. So he doesn't want solutions. He continues to want this border crisis to continue to go on, Neil. And if \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  would actually say the right things, if he -- if he wouldn't politicize this issue, if he would put pressure on this administration to secure the border, rather than giving him funds to house these individuals, if we would just secure the border, and if he would do that, put the pressure on the White House to do that, then he wouldn't have to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 And, instead, he continues to politicize the issue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you about the immediate things that have to -- I'm sorry, Brandon -- the immediate things that you would like to see done.\par \par \uc2 They talk about finding a permanent solution for this and working on the cause of the problem. Get that. Tried that. We go back and forth with that. But the more immediate need for you is just closing the porous border itself.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would you be for resuming the wall building that began under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 But that helps with the got-aways. That helps with that individual from Honduras that has that criminal record. It doesn't help with asylum seekers. What we have to have is, we have to re-implement the remain-in- Mexico program, which we know he's not going to do. OK, that's fine.\par \par \uc2 Then surge immigration judges to the border. Hold these individuals in custody. Process their asylum claims expeditiously. And then they will stop coming, because they know that they're not going to be able to prove that they have an asylum claim. They will sent back to their countries. Then the cartels don't...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the asylum claims themselves run up, as I understand it, far more than just sort of got-aways and what have you, that that's sort of the new way to get into the country.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Claim that you need asylum. And you have to take them in, right?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Yes, and that's the problem. We're not adjudicating the cases.\par \par \uc2 Instead, we're just releasing them into the United States. And they're not showing up to the court appearances later on down the road. So that's another solution that he knows about.\par \par \uc2 You can take the criminality out of the asylum process, surge CIS officers down to the ports of entry, create a lane up to where they're only going through the ports of entry. Then you don't have to have law enforcement. You don't have to have me dealing with asylum claims.\par \par \uc2 Right now, in Arizona, 275 miles of border, we have bare-bones agents on the border right now, simply because we're constantly dealing with asylum officers. These are all solutions that he can implement, and he can implement them tomorrow. He just doesn't do it.\par \par \uc2 Biden, it appears that he continues to want this crisis to go on. How much longer do we have to continue to see these images?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I just don't remember. You're far better at this, Brandon, than I will ever be, because you're right on the front lines there.\par \par \uc2 But this asylum push is sort of like a green card, no pun intended, to get into the country and have this adjudicated here. And, as you say, it can go on for years. And I don't see too many of those cases going back. So the asylum thing has gotten to be such a big thing now, it makes up eight out of 10 of those who find their way here.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And the vast majority these claims, they don't have legitimacy, and that's the problem. These loopholes, these immigration loopholes, they're what is allowing the cartels to generate billions of dollars of profit on -- they're skirting and our laws and our loopholes. They're exploiting them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 But, Brandon, to your credit, you did warn us about this. Brandon Judd, the National Border Patrol Council president.\par \par \uc2 But this asylum thing is going to be the big issue here, because it has dwarfed all other issues when it comes to those making their way through the border and getting to stay here. We have to adjudicate those cases. We adjudicate them here. And they stay until they're resolved. And, sometimes, that can be many, many years.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, many, many problems on the energy front, and particularly the gas front, because those prices aren't declining. In fact, they're increasing, even with the promise of taking more oil out of the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. That wasn't supposed to happen. It is.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, gas prices still moving up, in the face of the president doing everything he says he can do to try to get them to go back down, as they were earlier in the year, when we had 99 straight days of declining gas prices. Now, about 19 of the last 21 days have seen them move up 17 straight days in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 The read from that was from Senator \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2 , who joins us right now, the Kansas senator, the Republican, who recently wrote the following to the president, that: "The recent decision to release another $10 million from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve is not only reckless, but it will have a minimal effect on lowering prices at the pump."\par \par \uc2 The senator kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Any reaction to that? Because it looks like he's still going to go through with the Strategic Petroleum Reserve tapping. It is getting low right now, but that this is his solution. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JERRY MORAN\uc2  (R-KS): Neil, we have been battling, I have been battling this administration their energy, I can't say policy, their energy decision, since the administration, since the president took office.\par \par \uc2 And virtually no response that I know of that ever suggests that the president is altering his course of elimination, reduction, certainly, in the use of fossil fuels. And while that's part of my correspondence with the president, the thing that set me off this week is the release of oil, petroleum from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, which the release is about a half of a day's consumption.\par \par \uc2 So it's not going to do anything, much of anything for the price. But importantly to me, and I think importantly, to the country, we live in a very dangerous world. Why in the world do we have a Strategic Petroleum Reserve? I don't think it was to satisfy the political needs of the moment.\par \par \uc2 It was to make sure the United States is prepared for some crisis. And we don't know when that crisis is going to occur. That's why we stored the petroleum away for purposes of national security. And we are now -- it is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 We are intruding upon our country's national security for purposes of a policy that doesn't exist in regard to energy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're told -- and we got first wind of it today, Senator -- that the president had made it very, very clear to the Saudis and, by extension, \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus countries, that it would look very harshly at any cut in production. As it turns out, despite that, we got the two-million-barrel-a- day cut.\par \par \uc2 And the Saudis made it very clear that they were not going to be coerced into doing anything else. What did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MORAN: Neil, how demeaning it is and how ineffective it is to beg the oil producers around the world to meet our needs, when we ought to be focused on -- if we had our energy plans in place, and we were producing more -- and, by that, I'm an all-of-the-above guy.\par \par \uc2 But you can't take fossil fuels, natural gas and petroleum off the table, if we're going to be energy-independent. And we can -- if we were that, if we were producing more, if we had -- meeting our own country's needs, we wouldn't have to be asking others for their help.\par \par \uc2 But if we did need help, they would have more reason to be helpful. That we have -- it's like we take our leverage away in dealing with other countries who are oil producers, and then we go around with our tin cup around the world, Venezuela and others, Saudi Arabia...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MORAN: ... many of those difficult places for the United States to deal.\par \par \uc2 This is not the way to protect the United States, its consumers, or to make certain that we can have an affordable pump fill-up on -- across the country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, obviously, it's a very big issue in your state.\par \par \uc2 Your Democratic opponent, Mark Holland, who we did reach out to, is also pounding this issue, but also pounding the Roe v. Wade thing, and sensing right now that there might be more public support for his position on this than was considered the case, going back to Kansas voters, deciding not to amend the state Constitution to keep abortion legal.\par \par \uc2 Is this an issue that is stronger than we know, and that, among Kansans, is more powerful than we appreciate?\par \par \uc2 MORAN: Well, I think what happened is that we're talking about a Kansas Supreme Court decision that the legislature made an attempt to -- that court decision said that it is up to -- there is no restrictions under our Constitution on availability of abortion.\par \par \uc2 And the effort by those in the legislature to change that was to say, the legislature can make those kinds of decisions. And there was certainly people who spoke in the August primary election about this topic. And it is one that is...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And they didn't want it -- that they did not want it amended, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, do you read into that you're on the wrong side of this issue, or that it's not so black and white?\par \par \uc2 MORAN: No, what I read into it is that there is individuals across the state who have opinions, strong opinions about this issue...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MORAN: ... that need to be listened to.\par \par \uc2 But there's also the desire, I think, on the part of many people to have some commonsense kind of restrictions or conclusions, that I think maybe there just wasn't to trust at that that was -- would be what the legislature and governor would decide.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Got it very much.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Senator. Very good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we did reach out to Mark Holland, the -- his Democratic opponent. Hope springs eternal that we will get him to come on. His office did seem interested. So, we will keep you up to date on that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, you might want to write this date down over Buckingham Palace, if you're looking for something to do in May of next year, the official coronation of \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 . I did not get an invite. I have the feeling Martha MacCallum did.\par \par \uc2 She's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mark it on your calendar. Westminster Abbey, be there or be square, May 6, the official coronation of now \uc2 King Charles\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How will that go down? We're supposed to hear that it's slimmed down and cheaper. Really?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Charles is already the king of England. That, I know.\par \par \uc2 But it's all official -- I think I know -- I barely know this stuff -- next May, when he is going to go through a coronation, much like his mom had done better than seven decades ago. We're told it's going to be leaner, a little meaner affair. I don't -- well, the meaner part, I don't know about.\par \par \uc2 But I know Martha MacCallum knows this stuff inside and out, who has become since, she's hopped the pond so often, a British citizen herself.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: No, no. Still American, 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Not yet. Not yet.\par \par \uc2 So, this is on. This is on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, he's talking about -- and you were reporting through all the -- Queen Elizabeth, the funeral and everything else, he wants a leaner, more streamlined royalty, but does that apply to his own coronation?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I think you're going to see a little bit of a different presentation than what we saw in 1953, when Queen Elizabeth was coronated, which was enormous. There were 8,000 people. They added extra seats inside Westminster Abbey.\par \par \uc2 They televised it for the very first time. They wanted it to be very splashy. It was a big, important moment, this young beautiful queen on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Charles has been waiting a long time for this. He's 73 years old. She waited 18 months for her coronation, because she was so young.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is going to be fairly quick. And I think that's what everyone expected, because he's been around and waiting for this. It would be silly to drag it out for a long time.\par \par \uc2 They have -- the plans are in the works.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, but he's king already.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What will the coronation do?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the coronation -- for one, they want an appropriate period of mourning after the death of the queen...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: ... and then time to prepare, although this is called Operation Golden Orb. It has been in preparation for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 And, as you saw with the queen's funeral, they do these things like clockwork.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I think it's ready to go. But you need a few months to get everything ready.\par \par \uc2 Plus, they want it to be in a good season, when the flowers are out and all of those things for the presentation of this whole thing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I was -- originally, it was going to be -- I think June was the thinking, so May 6, right in that area.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, the backdrop for this and it might be better by next spring.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But England's in a world of financial hurt.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And he obviously doesn't want to get people annoyed that this is overly ostentatious and all.\par \par \uc2 But you can't help it with a big event like this, right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, they have to do enough to make it the grand experience that people have come to expect.\par \par \uc2 And it's one of the reasons that people tune in. And don't forget, people talk about how expensive the royal family is. However, they bring in such a tremendous amount of tourism to the United Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Many would argue that they bring in more than they cost in terms of tourism. So there will be an enormous turnout for this coronation, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 And they do want to make sure that it's appropriate, given what's going on in the country, and that it's not over the top. So they have to kind of find that balance between enough pageantry to keep people interested and wanting to come to see it and not being over the top, given what's going on there with the economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I think I'm a royal expert now because I have watched "The Crown," the whole series.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And my wife and I really got into it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's very well done.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's extremely well...\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And the reports were that the queen liked to watch it on Sunday nights, and that Philip wanted nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I would imagine Charles did not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Charles not a fan.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would not.\par \par \uc2 The only reason why I mention it, Martha, is that the irony of time and how things have changed, where Camilla will, she will be at Buckingham Palace. She will -- what's her title?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Queen consort.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Queen consort.\par \par \uc2 Imagine if Diana were to be seeing this today. It's just -- everything is so weird to me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, she will be queen consort. And Queen Elizabeth wanted it that way. She wanted to give that confirmation to her. The last queen consort was the queen mother. But she was...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What if she didn't get that title? I mean, it...\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, she would have remained a princess, a princess consort, OK?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But just to -- just for those playing at home, she would never be the queen, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: If Charles passes away, it goes directly to William. She would never reign.\par \par \uc2 She is simply the wife and the queen of the king, who is in the line of succession, with William and then George and Charlotte and Louis.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How do you think Charles has handled things since assuming the throne? And, I mean, he's coming after -- his mother was a rock star and brought the country back together.\par \par \uc2 And her approval ratings were very low right after Diana's death, but she came soaring back. And how about him?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She was enormously popular, and more popular than the monarchy itself. So he has his work cut out for him.\par \par \uc2 And I think his initial reviews were very strong.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He carried himself very well throughout the course of the funeral and the ceremonies. But now he's sort of in the job.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And so the criticism, I think, will be stronger as he goes through this period and the coronation and all of that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What about Harry and Meghan? Are they going to this?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They're done. I mean -- I mean, would they be there? I would imagine they will be there. But their role is very diminished, very sidelined.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is their book still on or his book still on? All that is still on, isn't it, or no?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I -- apparently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, I don't think that the efforts to sideline the book -- I think that's part of the deal. You can come back in if you -- if you're willing to end this book.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He's not going to do that, right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But there doesn't seem to be any sign that -- no, they're getting millions of dollars for it.\par \par \uc2 The other projects seem to be trying to move forward. And they just came out with a big portrait of themselves in California...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Saw that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: ... trying to sort of assert their position.\par \par \uc2 But my experience was, people in the United States care a lot more about Harry and Meghan than people in the U.K., who are just over them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is that right? Yes.\par \par \uc2 So the idea of getting and reestablishing relationship with his son, that's still -- with William, it's one thing. And William and Kate, they're future king. But, with Harry, it's over, it seems to me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: For now, I would say. It doesn't seem as though any bridge was built during the course of -- I think there was just a respect for the queen and her desire to have her family at that very meaningful moment in the family's life.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But it doesn't appear that there's any bridge to changing that stature.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But, again, you have a very strong succession with Charles, then William, then George, then Charlotte, then Louis.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, there's no -- the heir and spare, the spare is not really necessary at this point. So...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 By the way, how did you get into knowing all of this stuff?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what? I'm just sort of a sponge for history.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I have always enjoyed British history.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm actually going to Scotland in a few weeks to tour there.\par \par \uc2 I love -- I just am very interested in it. So it's a fun assignment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm that way with municipal bonds. You just -- you can't...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You just get enough when you're studying municipal bonds, right?\par \par \uc2 I used to be like that, but I broke it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you got a life. You got a life.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's awesome stuff, so I know -- I know where you will be next spring in May. Enjoy Scotland in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 You taking the Royal Scotsman? You going to do that, or...\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, but I would love to do that sometime.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There you go. There you go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Have you done that?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I have not. I wanted to. We were supposed to do it actually before COVID hit.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And so we ended up in the Catskills. But that's a whole separate story.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will put that on our wish list.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And if you need to know anything about royalty, I can try to help you out now that I have seen "The \uc2 Crown\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Neil. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Anything I can do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm going to keep you on speed dial.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, there you go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She is so not going to do that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And understandably so.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, before we go, some sad news to share with you right now, and Martha did in the last hour. Angela Lansbury has died, of course, the star of "Murder, She Wrote." she was in the old acting style of the golden age of Hollywood. Just think about all the awards. She won five Tony Awards, at least one Grammy. She was nominated for an Oscar three different times, considered one of the most powerful actors of her time.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, on "Murder, She Wrote," she could always figure it out. She's gone on at age\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, President Biden patching in with all the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders today. And the big concern is the sudden rough turn that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has taken in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich with more on that from the White House -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We just wrapped up the briefing there at the White House. Would have loved to have gotten some more questions, any questions, rather, to the press secretary. We didn't get called on today. Unfortunately, there were no questions about whether or not President Biden would meet with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  after the Russian foreign minister suggested that the Russians would accept a meeting with Biden at the G20 summit coming up in November.\par \par \uc2 So we don't yet know if there will be a meeting of the minds on the Biden- Putin front. But in terms of the \uc2 G7\uc2  resolution, we did get a readout of the president's call. And the summary of that is basically more condemnation of what Russia, what Putin has been carrying out in Ukraine, also a vow to hold President Putin and those responsible to account for what they see as war crimes and innocent civilian populations being attacked.\par \par \uc2 But, beyond that, we did not get any answers from the president himself about how that call went today. And we also have not had any questions to the president since he made those comments about Armageddon at a fund- raiser last Thursday. He has not taken any questions from the press.\par \par \uc2 And we have heard from the National Security Council, basically tamping down any alarm over that. And in terms of saying there hasn't been any new intelligence that prompted those statements from the president, but in light of this new barrage from Russia on Ukraine, the urgent call for the \uc2 G7\uc2  meeting, and then the suggestion from the Russians that Putin might be seeking out a meeting with Biden, we would have liked to have gotten some more answers there.\par \par \uc2 The president did do an interview with Jake Tapper. And they have released a clip of that interview. The most salient point, I find, is that the president was asked to -- whether or not Putin was irrational, and he refused to say that he was irrational. So it sounds, Neil, like they're keeping things open for a potential interview.\par \par \uc2 I -- just reading the tea leaves there. He seemed not to want to insult Putin, while also saying that what he has done in this war is irrational -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. That's threading quite the needle there.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, great job, as always.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Brett Velicovich on this, the former Delta Force honcho, FOX News contributor. You do not mess with Brett. So that's why I always ask tough questions when we are on remote.\par \par \uc2 Brett, it's always good to see, my friend.\par \par \uc2 I find it interesting now that we have been waiting forever, it seems, for President Putin to respond to pressure from back home. We were thinking, though, that pressure would be from those who tired of his calling up reservists and the like, and that he might cool it. But it was quite a different kind of pressure from people who were even more warlike and conservative on this and pro-war than he is.\par \par \uc2 That worries me. What about you?\par \par \uc2 BRETT VELICOVICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, you're right.\par \par \uc2 And I think it only gets worse from here, Neil, especially as it leads up to winter. I mean, it's almost like the war is restarting again after the events of the last few days. But it also wasn't surprising to me. I think a lot of signs were pointing to these massive retaliatory strikes from Russia as a result of not only Ukrainians' tactical wins on the battlefield, but because Putin is getting humiliated on the international stage.\par \par \uc2 And he had to also address these domestic critics which were saying his invasion was failing. And so here's one of the largest armies in the world getting outmaneuvered and outgunned at an exponential rate by the Ukrainians. And the Ukrainians were at one point really starting to be able to dictate the terms of this war. And there are a lot of signs, I think, also pointing to further escalation.\par \par \uc2 I really think that, if we fast-forward this a year from now, at some point, a lot of these countries, including the U.S., are going to have to be engaged in serious nuclear de-escalation discussions. We're seeing these movements already of Russian nuclear vessels or sites that are showing more activity than normal. And Russia knows what they're doing there.\par \par \uc2 They know that, every time they move some strategic warship or a vessel, that the U.S. and \uc2 NATO\uc2  is watching, and they're doing analysis at the highest levels, and it's playing into the calculus of how to deal with them going forward.\par \par \uc2 So I think we have to be really concerned about this. We got to pay closer attention to what's happening out there, because I think it's definitely going to start affecting the lives of all Americans going forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I always think, Brett, that China could play such a crucial role here. And we do know that China has been among the countries -- I think India was with them last month, saying, we have got to find an end to this.\par \par \uc2 I'm paraphrasing here, but that their patience is wearing thin with how this is going for Russia, but still no direct intervention to say, wrap it up. But what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: Well, I think, personally, that, publicly, China's going to stay neutral, but, behind the scenes, they are tacitly supporting Russia, because they see this also as a mechanism for their strategy in Taiwan and what happens on the world stage there.\par \par \uc2 But it's clear Russia isn't giving up. I mean, the victims of these latest attacks, Neil, they were civilians. And it's just this constant barrage of civilian targets. They have made it very clear.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that's the strategy. That's the strategy, right, to do something like this, so to just go ahead and decimate them.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: Yes, they have no intention of distinguishing between military and civilian targets.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: That's their entire playbook. And they should be designated as a terror state immediately. They should be removed from the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I mean, how many more confirmed cases of war crimes do we need? We haven't even scratched the surface with the atrocities committed, especially in towns currently under Russian occupation. I saw this with my own eyes, Neil. I was just there. I was in Ukraine. I was in Zaporizhzhia.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the four areas that Russia annexed and basically claimed as part of their territory. And every single night, we'd have cruise missiles raining down on us in our neighborhood in these residential areas. I mean, one of the bridges that just got hit was a civilian bridge with tourists. This is a national park.\par \par \uc2 So when is it going to stop? The world needs to come together and stop this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, good luck with that.\par \par \uc2 Brett, thank you very, very much. We will keep monitoring it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we're monitoring what could be a delay in some Christmas gifts you have been planning to get, and all because of a rail strike we thought was done that apparently is not.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Remember that rail strike we thought we had settled, or at least the administration had? Apparently, we did not.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady with more in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, what is going on here?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: And good afternoon to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that strike is back on the table potentially. So, 56 percent of the third largest union, they voted to reject that tentative deal that was brokered by the White House. So now the two sides, they're going to go back to the negotiating table. They have agreed to avoid striking until November 19.\par \par \uc2 Note that would be after the midterms, but it would also allow Congress time when it reconvenes to step in, if necessary. Now, the big sticking point is around quality of life, specifically the number of paid sick days that those workers would get.\par \par \uc2 But, listen, if this strike happens, it could cripple the economy. So we're looking at potentially $2 billion a day at risk; 467,000 trucks would be needed per day to offset that. And we're already short 80,000 in that industry. So you could be looking at shortages, higher prices for food and gasoline, store shelves empty during that holiday season.\par \par \uc2 We're already dealing with a reignited energy crisis. So, a strike like that could turbocharge inflation. Now, the White House is telling FOX that they feel that the economy is under no immediate threat, that both sides are committed to a avoiding a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you this, Neil. We were speaking to experts today, and a source tells us that first rejection is not a good sign of votes to come. So I guess shop early ahead of this holiday season, to be safe.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Kelly, thank you, I think.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady on that.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the controversy over \uc2 PayPal\uc2  and when it comes to information not necessarily being your pal.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Webster, what's going on here?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY WEBSTER, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Look, \uc2 PayPal\uc2 's stock has fallen 15 percent over the last three days, 8 percent, you can see, so far this week, 55 percent to the downside for the year. So it hasn't been a good year. The company is still dealing with the backlash from a policy update that says it was posted in error and just doesn't exist.\par \par \uc2 However, that policy did state that, beginning November 3, the company would prohibit customers from using its services for activities that -- quote -- "effective November the 3rd involve the sending, posting or publication of any messages, content or materials that in \uc2 PayPal\uc2 's sole discretion are fraudulent or promote misinformation."\par \par \uc2 Now, the notice went on to say that anyone violating the policy would be fined $2,500 for each violation. That is very steep. In a statement to FOX Business, a \uc2 PayPal\uc2  spokesman told us that the: "AUP notice recently went out in error that included incorrect information. \uc2 PayPal\uc2  is not fining people for misinformation and this language was never intended to be inserted in our policy."\par \par \uc2 Interesting, right? \uc2 PayPal\uc2  apparently won't have to fine itself 2,500 bucks for spreading its own misinformation. But at a time when social media platforms are being criticized for their gatekeeping rules, especially by conservatives, this misstep is just adding to the suspicions.\par \par \uc2 The company has already been accused of shutting down accounts that don't match its sociopolitical beliefs, something \uc2 PayPal\uc2  denies, but no doubt the bad publicity could lead to fewer \uc2 PayPal\uc2  accounts, which have already seen a drop in growth.\par \par \uc2 And you know what, Neil? To me, it's also unclear how misinformation can actually the spread by \uc2 PayPal\uc2  or its subsidiary, Venmo. I'd like to see an example of that. I think it's strange -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And very easy, apparently, very easy to do.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Yes, apparently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ashley, thank you very much for -- Ashley Webster.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Yes. Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will have a lot more coming up, including when candidates agree on inflation, that it's a problem, and not just for one party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), OHIO SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: They borrowed and spent trillions of dollars that we just don't have. And that's thrown fuel on the fire of the inflation problem.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you think President Biden is to blame for the inflation crisis?\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Well, I think everybody's to blame.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Four weeks from today, the midterm election is on. A lot of people have already voted.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to the issues that are still hot and percolating right now, including a series of debates, and very much so in Ohio, would you know inflation is front and center.\par \par \uc2 Mark Meredith has more -- Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Both of the Senate Ohio candidates spent a lot of time talking about the economy last night, inflation, as you said, the major concern, not just here in Washington, but in places and battleground states like Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Republican candidate J.D. Vance accusing his opponent, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , of supporting massive spending here in D.C., which, in his view, is forcing Americans to pay more on just about everything.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: And that's thrown fuel on the fire of the inflation problem.\par \par \uc2 And, at the same time, they have completely gone to war against America's energy sector. And you can't do both of those things at the same time. They're each bad ideas. But when you do both of them at the same time, you're going to get record inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is trying to accuse Vance of being out of touch on the economy and everyday issues mattering -- that matter to Ohio voters. He says Vance, who worked as a venture capitalist, profited from jobs moved out of the Buckeye State.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: The problem we're having now with inflation is, our supply chains all went to China. And guys like him have made a lot of money off that. And that is exactly why the supply chains are locked up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: One fascinating part about this race is, you got \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  saying he is still proud to be a Democrat, but he is not ready to support \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for a 2024 run. He also says he's not ready to campaign with any of those national Democrats, Neil, because he's still trying to reach those moderate voters in that all-important swing state of Ohio -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: With support like that, who needs enemies?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Exactly. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much, Mark Meredith.\par \par \uc2 All right, so if inflation is such a big issue, what's happened to the abortion issue and the fallout from the passions around the Roe v. Wade decision?\par \par \uc2 Patrice Onwuka is with us right now, the Independent Women's Forum Center for Economic Opportunity, the director there. We have also got Kristal Knight with us, the Democratic strategist extraordinaire.\par \par \uc2 Kristal, end it with you and begin with you on what some had hoped in the Democratic Party to be an issue with some amount of wind at their back. And that is on bringing out the base post-Roe v. Wade and the Supreme Court. It seemed to work for a while. It might still work right now. But these other issues have become predominant for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Does that worry you?\par \par \uc2 KRISTAL KNIGHT, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, it doesn't worry me.\par \par \uc2 I knew that, once Roe vs. Wade, the decision was announced, folks would get roused up, the base would be energized around this particular issue. But it would be naive to expect that that issue would take us all the way through November, when crime is still high, inflation is high, and people are having a hard time economically in this country.\par \par \uc2 And so there are other issues that are important. I think we're seeing abortion still take a big issue -- or take place in the state of Georgia in the Senate race. But, ultimately, voters care about their pocketbook issues. And that's why they're going to hit to the polls in November.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, if that's the case, then, Patrice, so far, at least the advantage would seem to go to Republicans. That might not always be the factor here.\par \par \uc2 But how do you see this sorting out?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE ONWUKA, INDEPENDENT WOMEN'S FORUM: Well, I agree. I mean, Democrats have been in power for two years now. And they passed a recent Inflation Reduction Act that Americans know does not reduce inflation, will do nothing to deal with those pocketbook issues that my colleague here was talking about.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, it's not surprising that the Dobbs effect has lost its steam. I think, also, you have to look at the demographics. When you look at hardworking people, low-income people, middle-class families who are struggling with, what, 11 percent increase in groceries over the past year, they're thinking about what's happening here and now.\par \par \uc2 And as states have -- things have settled when it comes to the abortion issue at the state level, people are recognizing where the threat really is, and whether they have the luxury of focusing on abortion vs. focusing on filling up their gas tank, which, as we know, gas prices are rising once again.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: People are reminded, even aside from gas, Kristal, which had been declining for a while, now is back to increasing, I believe, 17 or 18 straight days, that even that argument has been taken from the president, but he keeps telling Americans we have a good job market, which, in many sense, we do, and that's different from the '70s.\par \par \uc2 But you don't want to dismiss Americans' concerns by saying that, thanks to the strong job backdrop, you don't have anything to worry about. That does appear to be a message that is ticking them off when they hear it. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 KNIGHT: Well, I think, historically, Democrats have had a hard time messaging.\par \par \uc2 And we don't have a good echo chamber where we repeat the same thing over and over again. But I think, as it relates to this inflation issue, listen, there's no perfect answer. There's no one zinger that I could say that would really identify how...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, just say, just acknowledge it. It's a problem and prices are running around like crazy. Do something.\par \par \uc2 KNIGHT: It's a problem. It's a problem.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KNIGHT: Well, yes, it's a problem.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I hear you.\par \par \uc2 So, Patrice, what do you think of that? To bash it if one thing. If you're the party challenging the party in power, you want to keep reminding folks of this, but you got to come up with solutions yourself. Are the solutions that Republicans are proposing, like cutting taxes, maybe more drilling, enough for people? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: Well, I think it's a start.\par \par \uc2 I think America -- the GOP has put out their own plan with some economic ideas included in there. It's really important, though, that people are able to keep as much of their money as possible right now. It's important that they have as many opportunities as possible and we don't have a Department of Labor instituting regulations around independent contracting for those freelancers who just want to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 So, I think you have a Biden administration and a party in power that is more than willing to push forward their agenda on climate and everything else, forgetting that it has a negative impact on pocketbook issues.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: And blaming corporate greed does not fly with Americans.\par \par \uc2 And all of these other issues that they brought up, I mean, Americans recognize that, when you give away trillions of dollars...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We'll see. We'll see. It's still early. Time -- time will tell, as they say, with three seconds to go.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221011eiab00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Crime Battle; Twitter Tussle; Final Midterm Push; Stocks Drop on Rate Hike Worries; Nuclear Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Garrett Tenney, Alexis McAdams, Charlie Gasparino,  Shannon Bream, Susan Li, David Perkins, Mark Meredith, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 8,428 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN F. KENNEDY\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It shall be the policy of this nation to regard any nuclear missile launched from Cuba or against any nation in the Western Hemisphere as an attack by the Soviet Union on the United States, requiring a full retaliatory response upon the Soviet Union.\par \par \uc2 I call upon Chairman Khrushchev to halt and eliminate this clandestine, reckless and provocative threat to world peace. Let no one doubt that this is a difficult and dangerous effort on which we have set out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You know, they say that history doesn't repeat itself, but man, oh, man, it sure does rhyme.\par \par \uc2 That was then. Armageddon now? President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  warning the risk of nuclear Armageddon is now at its highest since \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2  was dealing with the Cuban Missile Crisis 60 years ago this very month. Tensions were escalating fast between then the Soviet Union, now escalating between Russia and ourselves and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And, in North Korea, the recent long-range missile tests that seem to be happening every other day and the chance of an accident has the world on the proverbial pins and needles.\par \par \uc2 We are all over it and whether history risks repeating itself with Mark Meredith in Washington on how the administration is responding to this latest Russian crisis, Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon not changing its nuclear posturing, at least just yet, and General David Perkins on if we should all be worrying and whether we could revisit something very similar to 60 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. Glad to have you.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it with Mark Meredith with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 The White House says there is no intelligence suggesting Russia is preparing to imminently use nuclear weapons in Ukraine, but it's the president's own comments less than 24 hours ago sparking concerns about where the war is heading.\par \par \uc2 Now, the president did not speak to reporters today about Ukraine. But the White House is facing a lot of questions about some comments he made last night in New York City. The president was speaking off-camera at a fund- raiser and was quoted as saying: "We have not faced the prospect of Armageddon since Kennedy and the Cuban Missile Crisis."\par \par \uc2 Then, when he was talking about the Russian president, he said -- quote -- "He's not joking when he talks about potential use of tactical nuclear weapons or biological or chemical weapons, because his military, you might say, is significantly underperforming."\par \par \uc2 The president went on to say, if Russia were to use nuclear weapons, even a low-yield device, it would be nearly impossible for the situation not to escalate even further. The Pentagon says there's been no change to Russia's nuclear posture. But critics are now calling out President Biden for his own comments, including former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED NATIONS: It's so counterproductive. I mean, as a leader, what he should be doing is, one, you don't rile up Americans. You calm them down. You let them know you have got it under control. So it makes me worry if he's ready for this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: For its part, the White House continues to call Russia out, saying it's reckless and irresponsible behavior for what it's done so far and continue to threaten.\par \par \uc2 The president also leaving the door open for a possible meeting with President Putin next month at the G20 summit. But we're told no final decision has been made on a possible meeting. Neil, that would be quite a meeting -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: To say the least. Thank you very much for that, Mark.\par \par \uc2 To Jennifer Griffin right now on how the Pentagon is responding to all of this -- Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, the Pentagon has issued no public statements in the wake of President Biden's remarks.\par \par \uc2 But, obviously, this kind of loose talk about the possibility of nuclear war is not viewed as helpful by national security types, especially for those trying to find ways to climb down from the nuclear escalation ladder.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, here at the Pentagon, before President Biden made the remarks, Press Secretary Brigadier General Pat Ryder called the nuclear saber- rattling from Moscow reckless and irresponsible, adding, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  does not have any information that would cause the Pentagon to change its strategic deterrence posture.\par \par \uc2 General Ryder was clear the U.S. does not assess that President Putin has made a decision to use nuclear weapons at this time. However, we heard similar public assurances that Putin had not yet made the decision to enter Ukraine right up until his forces crossed into Ukraine on February 24. It's almost as though officials want to give Putin room to back down. He never does.\par \par \uc2 In a BBC interview today at the presidential palace in Kyiv, President Zelenskyy talked about the danger of loose nuclear rhetoric and suggesting it could lead to miscalculation. He said Putin is trying to numb the Russian public.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: They begin to prepare their society. That is very dangerous. They are not ready to do it, to use it, but they begin to communicate.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What, you mean prepare society for using a nuclear weapon, you think?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: It's -- they don't know if they will use or they will not use.\par \par \uc2 I think that it's dangerous even to speak about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Most people I speak to think that the focus should be less on nukes, but how badly Russia's army is faring on the battlefield and the unified international response, using economic levers, sanctions and the delivery of conventional weapons to the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 The French president had a statement filled with not-so-subtle veiled criticism of President Biden's loose talk about the likelihood of a nuclear showdown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , FRENCH PRESIDENT (through translator): I think it is especially appropriate when talking about nuclear weapons. We are a nuclear power. We have a clear and unchanging doctrine on the matter. We all must be very careful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: In other words, such talk in public is not helpful -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That is an understatement.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer, thank you very, very much for that, Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Want to go right now to General David Perkins, \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  four-star generals, at that, a FOX News contributor, very important to us to have his expertise here.\par \par \uc2 General, you're also a very good student of history, military and otherwise, and, again, the comparisons to 60 years ago do come back, because it didn't take much then for things to get wildly out of control. Fortunately, they did not. But we're reminded that a lot of slip-ups could happen. And there are a lot of potential slip-ups now. Are you worried?\par \par \uc2 GEN. DAVID PERKINS (RET.), FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Neil, I don't worry about anything. I find that's not particularly useful.\par \par \uc2 But I think it is important that we are focused. And you never underestimate your enemy. And as some of the earlier comments are, the biggest challenge with things like this are bad judgment and miscalculations and things accidentally spiral out of control.\par \par \uc2 That was the concern back during the Cuban Missile Crisis. And that's really the concern now. So that's the greatest similarity. The difference is, back then, it was really a bilateral issue, the Soviet Union and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: And this is a very multilateral issue involving Europe.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And it's also multi players, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you have the taunting that, certainly, of North Korea in the region, launching missiles, it seems, General, every other day, the most recent over Japan, China not doing anything nuclear, thank God, for the moment, but being very provocative in its actions in the South China Sea and elsewhere, and, of course, Russia, the unpredictability of potentially looking at tactical nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 So, you got a lot of players, and it doesn't take much for something to go horribly wrong.\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: Well, and that's the big concern. It just doesn't take much. That's why it's so important, as a lot of the people are speaking here, that the U.S. has got to be sort of a calming influence here and make it known that we have ways to sort of, if you want to say retaliate against a nuclear weapon, that are not nuclear, because that actually adds to our deterrence that we could make the price of using a nuclear weapon so high, and we actually could do it without us breaking the nuclear threshold, is actually much more, I think, a deterrent than saying, well, if you throw a nuke, we're going to throw a nuke.\par \par \uc2 Because then people say, well, the U.S. won't throw a nuke, so that's not a credible deterrent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, General, it's got to be at least, in my estimate, self- evident to anyone who even anticipates or talks about that, that there would be a like response. I don't even know if it's meaningful for our president or any country's leader to say we will have a like response.\par \par \uc2 But what worries me is that the fact that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  keeps mentioning this and his top lieutenants keep throwing it out there. That is what worries me.\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: Yes, I agree, Neil. We should never say, you do this, we are absolutely going to do this, because then that puts us into a corner. We have boxed ourselves in.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: There's lots of ways, back channel and other ways, to convey to the Russians, we have a broad range of capability. We have many arrows in our quiver, and we can use some of them or all of them, none of which you really could respond to.\par \par \uc2 And it keeps us below the nuclear threshold. That is credible. And that really, I think, would be a bigger deterrence than getting into an argument about who's got a bigger nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what if you're dealing with a cornered nut, General, or someone who's desperate or is already getting protests in his country because he just called up 300,000 reservists, many of whom are fleeing to the border or protesting in these reserve stations?\par \par \uc2 And he is now facing the fact that he could lose, and lose badly, in Ukraine, without the opportunity to save face or to leave with some sort of the deal that would allow him to save face. That's a desperate animal cornered, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: You know, Neil, I would say that is the biggest difference in the biggest threat compared to the Cuban Missile Crisis, because Khrushchev was not boxed into a corner and he was not grasping for a win.\par \par \uc2 Putin is. And so that needs to be part of our calculation that we have to assume that he may not be completely rational. And then who else in his sphere of influence might be rational that we could leverage?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think we can?\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: I think we have many more options than we had in the '60s. Russia is not nearly as isolated as the Soviet Union was.\par \par \uc2 And we -- again, this is now a multilateral problem for Russia, not a bilateral. And so I think we do have more options than we did. But I think our aggressor here, in this case, Putin, is in a much more dangerous position than Khrushchev was.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When I hear, General, people rationally seemingly discussing the use of limited tactical nuclear weapons, I'm fine until you get to the nuclear part after tactical.\par \par \uc2 And I worry that that's now become more common in these discussions. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: Yes, that -- I mean, I agree with you on that. There is just a psychological, a political, a cultural threshold is, once something goes nuclear, it's kind of like all self-constraint is gone.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: And most people don't differentiate between a tactical and a strategic nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 And so that's why I think having options below that threshold -- and, quite honestly, there's a lot of targets that there are better weapons than nuclear. Nuclear is not a silver bullet that solves every problem. In fact, I don't think it would solve any problem for Putin here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We need more of your kind of calm reaction to these things, because up until talking to you, I was like Barney Fife here. So you have you kind of got me off the ledge here, General.\par \par \uc2 We will see what happens. But I hope you have a great weekend. And thank you as well for your incredible service to this country, General David Perkins.\par \par \uc2 All right, more mundane concerns, money concerns at the corner of Wall and Broad, where the Dow was slip, sliding again today, and a lot of it was based on a pretty good employment number. I know that sounds a little wacky, but we continue to gain jobs in this country.\par \par \uc2 That should not be bad news. And a 3.5 percent unemployment rate should not be deemed bad news. But it's sort of reinforced the notion the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is going to keep hiking interest rates again and again and again, and nothing in today's data dissuaded them of that.\par \par \uc2 Susan Li on the importance of that.\par \par \uc2 This jobs jolt, whatever you want to call it, Susan, was palpable, and it weighed on the markets. And they were in no mood for it. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Because interest rates are going up at the fastest and the steepest rates since the 1980s, Neil, when Volcker was a Fed chair, I guess that's why we live in this weird paradigm where good news is bad, bad news is good, and 263,000 jobs is too strong. You had the wages going up by 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 And that's why Wall Street now expects the Federal Reserve to get more aggressive, of course. They expect interest rates to cross 4 percent before this year is done. But there are some cracks forming in the labor markets. I think the participation rate was a bit strange, given that we are still - - there's still a full percentage point fewer workers now than before COVID started.\par \par \uc2 Also, retail jobs dropped for the first time since May. And that indicates maybe a softening in the consumer. And, as you know, the consumer powers around three-quarters of the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, if you can update me, Susan, I guess the expectations are the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  continues to raise rates.\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think the betting is, next month, another three-quarters-of-a- point.\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They have another shot at this in December. That's assuming they wait until the meetings every six weeks. They could move before, obviously.\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that one, it looks like a half-point hike. But it could easily be three-quarter-point hike.\par \par \uc2 LI: Well, I think the expectations are, I think the Fed has already told us and telestrated that we're going to get at least 1.25 percentage points more before this year is done.\par \par \uc2 So, you're expecting three-quarters at the start of November, depending on, of course, that inflation report that we are expecting next week, and then, of course, in December, maybe another 50 basis points. But the stock market...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you would be over 4 percent, right?\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You would have essentially quadrupled rates over the past year, actually since March, when all this started. That's a big leap.\par \par \uc2 LI: And that is the fastest rate of increases that we have seen since the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 You're getting at least 4 percent more higher rates in just about eight or nine months. And the last time you saw that, of course, was during those Volcker days, when he went a full percentage point each and every meeting, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're right.\par \par \uc2 Why are you assuming that I was alive then and knew it?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You had to rub that in, didn't you?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ah, the benefits...\par \par \uc2 LI: You were paying 20 percent in mortgage rates. That's why.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, stop. The benefits of being young.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Susan, thank you, I think. But you're right about that.\par \par \uc2 Susan Li on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Again, that's the expectation. That's why stocks are down. But for the week, I might want to stress here that the Dow still had advances today. Remember, we had a Monday and Tuesday, those huge gains, about 1,600 points, so we didn't melt all of those away.\par \par \uc2 Susan, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, big debate going on in Arizona yesterday, and the pile on the president was impressive. It wasn't coming, though, from the Republican candidate. I will explain after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): When the president, when the Biden administration refused to increase oil and gas production, I told him he was wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a little unusual when you hear a prominent Democratic candidate criticizing the president. But that was an important debate in Arizona, as that contest is as tight as a tick right now.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read on the race essentially there and this notion that more Democrats are at least separating themselves from the president on things like the border and particularly on what's going on with inflation, what have you\par \par \uc2 Shannon Bream is here right now, "FOX News Sunday" host, chief legal correspondent, bestselling author. She has to rub it in and make us all feel inadequate.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I'm just proceeding here.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, great to see you again.\par \par \uc2 It is interesting that we're seeing more of this right now the closer we get to the midterms. And I'm wondering what you made that in Arizona, because, of course, that -- those issues are important. The border stuff is important. Saying that you disagree with the president on some key issue, that's an important issue to raise. How did it -- how did it resonate?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, HOST, "FOX NEWS SUNDAY": Yes, I think every state is so nuanced with these, depending on what is going on in your state.\par \par \uc2 Senator Mark Kelly knew, wisely, listen, everybody is worried about inflation. They're worried about what they're paying at the pump. It is ticking back up. With what \uc2 OPEC\uc2  did this week, it's only going to get worse. He's got to try to distance himself there.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned too the border. That's another issue that Democrats along the border states are absolutely calling out this administration. I think the word that Senator Kelly used during the debate was dumb, when this president or this administration made dumb decisions regarding the border, that he called them out on that as well.\par \par \uc2 So it's interesting, because you watch across the country where the president is a plus or a minus, by the judgment of the White House, by the judgment of the Democrat Party, because there have been people who've had scheduling conflicts, that they couldn't show up when the president was in town and campaign with him.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, you would think, would want to cling to the leader of their party, but, yes, state by state, and especially those House races, district by district, there's so much nuance. In some places, he's a plus. Some places, as you saw last night in Arizona, some of those Democratic candidates will judge he's a minus.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I always wonder, Shannon, when there is this I wouldn't call a revolt within the party, but separation among some candidates from the president, and obviously so, do they run that by the White House?\par \par \uc2 Do they let them know, not only do I not want you here, Mr. President, campaigning for me, but don't be surprised if I start differentiating myself from you on some of these issues? And I would think the White House's view, while maybe a little dinged by that, would welcome, like, anything that can get Democrats elected.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, I think it's -- I think there are probably some kind of communications like, ooh, we're not going to be able to be there on that date of the president's coming on that date. Well, what about another date? We can't be on that one either.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I think that they communicate with the advance teams and with the White House, like, this isn't going to work for us. And I think the White House gets that and they understand.\par \par \uc2 I think the president gets that and understands too. Like, I only want to go where it's going to help my party and help candidates. I think, sometimes, he's been miffed because I think he thinks the distancing by some of the Democratic candidates is based on faulty messaging, that maybe the White House hasn't gotten the message out properly as to what he really thinks or what he's really said.\par \par \uc2 And we have seen, that the president will say something, the White House will walk it back or clarify it or change it. Some candidates just don't want to get caught in the middle of that. So I think the president has some frustrations, but I think he also gets the game, and he wants to increase his party's numbers as much as possible in Congress. So he's not going to go where he thinks he'd be a detriment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Meanwhile, let me ask you a little bit about Georgia right now, not only the Senate race, but the governor's race, a lot on the line here.\par \par \uc2 And there, too, it's a delicate balance between the Democratic candidates trying to balance that with the president, and in the case of the Republican Senate candidate, just trying to avoid a whole heap of controversy. Where does that stand?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, Georgia is one of the most interesting states this time around, because they do have that split, as we see in a couple of states, where the Republican is leading in either the gubernatorial or the Senate race, and then the flip is that the Democrat is leading in the other race in the state, and that's definitely happening in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 The current governor, the incumbent Republican, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , was on with us on "FOX News Sunday" last week. He is leading \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , a rematch of their epic 2018 showdown. But the flip is true. It looks like, right now, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is struggling there in Georgia after another round of revelations that have not gone well for his campaign against the current Democratic governor -- or -- excuse me -- senator there, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He is tending to lead in most of the polls, and I'm sure we will get to new ones after these latest revelations. So we're going to have \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  on with us Sunday to be the counter now, make her case, after Governor Kemp was with us.\par \par \uc2 So, listen, she has fought for what she says is more voter access. She says there's a suppression there. She's made a lot of comments about the results of 2018. So we're going to give her a chance to clarify where she says she has been misconstrued. We will talk about that and abortion and all kinds of other things on Sunday with her.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, long before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had protested the last presidential election, she was protesting hers four years ago that the wrong person won, that it was all but it legitimate.\par \par \uc2 Has she ever dialed that back?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: When you -- when she is pressed on it about whether she ever conceded the race, she will say, listen, I know I'm not currently the governor of Georgia. I know \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , based on the rules of the election as they were run, he is the declared winner.\par \par \uc2 But she continues to maintain that there was voter suppression, there was an inability for voters to get out there and make their voices heard, and that, if all of that was factored in, and if those people had voted for her, she would be the governor. In some ways, she says she has won, because at least she's had the conversation.\par \par \uc2 But, Neil, remember, last week, a federal judge shutdown this lawsuit she's had going on for four years and said, basically, we could not find her hard evidence, that it was their duty to put it forward, that anybody was not allowed to vote or confused about where to go or stopped from actually voting.\par \par \uc2 So that's a federal judge appointed by President \uc2 Obama\uc2  who said her claims have no merit.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, I look forward to seeing you this weekend, again, on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 She gets into it all with all the players. She does it in such a nice way that the people being interviewed have no idea that she's just raked over the coals. So I think it helps that she has all this religious book writing background, because they have no idea, no idea what just hit them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Shannon, look forward to it this weekend. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: See you Sunday.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, the world's richest man and probably the most controversial company, they're seeing eye to eye, kind of, but they're still separated by a lot of money. But is that deal on or not?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the bad news is, in Florida, a lot of homes just destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Here's the worst news. A lot of folks there won't be compensated to get them rebuilt. The trouble with insurance in the Sunshine State -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When we first use that music regarding \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and the world's richest man in this battle, I didn't think it matched, because it made it sound like a drama that would quickly be resolved.\par \par \uc2 Well, sure enough, the graphics folks were right. It would not be resolved and it would be an ongoing drama, almost like a streamlined series, where you don't know what the next episode would look like.\par \par \u