ainst you. Can you imagine what the interest rate situation is going to be like at the -- in a couple of weeks, when the Fed is poised to raise rates another 75 basis points? The long bond could trade off again. That means 10-year yield spike.\par \par \uc2 That really hurts the banks' ability to finance this and essentially sell it into syndication. So, and then...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, they could just walk away, then, couldn't they? And there have been rumors that one or two have or are considering it.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: A couple have -- well, a couple -- well, I can tell you, \uc2 Apollo\uc2  walked away, but from a preferred -- buying a preferred equity piece of it, which is kind of...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what does that mean, Charlie?\par \par \uc2 It means if you -- the more walk away, potentially, the more money he -- that is, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  -- has to come up with. And the best...\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Pony up or...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... is his stock, right?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And, see, I don't -- yes.\par \par \uc2 But here's what I don't know and I don't know if anybody knows. We don't have the exact term sheets in front of us. And they're very legalistic. If he can't get bank financing, can he walk away from the deal without paying the full monty and just have to pay the breakup fee?\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's -- it's unclear if that's the case or not, so...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is demanding and the judge seemed to be saying before all of this started that that was the deal, the $54.20 deal, whatever it was, that it's drifted a little bit from that.\par \par \uc2 But there are clearly extenuating circumstances. You just mentioned a couple.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's going to compound, right?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: That's why -- these -- this is some of the $64,000 questions that have yet to be answered.\par \par \uc2 And we will probably -- we will find out within the next week, I'm pretty sure. But this is still up in the air. And it still makes me think that he's renegotiating for a lower price. I reported on your show several times over the past three months, as he's been dancing, saying he really doesn't want it, too many bots, that he's been privately telling people he wants it. He just doesn't want to pay up for it.\par \par \uc2 Is there a renegotiation of price that is still yet to come on this thing to get it done? I think -- I think everything's on the table, Neil, I mean, especially with him.\par \par \uc2 I don't know, for the life of me, if a guy -- why people want to do business with him. I know he's rich. But when you start saying that you got your -- you got funding secured on a -- taking \uc2 Tesla\uc2  private, as he did back in 2018, and you don't do anything, it's not true, and when you start saying things that are just kind of not true, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has a bot problem, I just find out...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, but you want to be associated with the -- a very rich, cool guy who starts \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  and all this other stuff and cars. This...\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Also gets you in trouble and...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Like you. You have the allure of just being Charlie Gasparino, never mind, you go out, you always leave the check for someone else to pick up.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: That's me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's you.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You know me. Well, because I'm not a billionaire, as you know, unlike you...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood. Understood.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: ... who picks up the tab all the time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, please, please. And that's the way it's going to be at the Olive Garden, my friend.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You got it. You got it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're the best. They don't come better than Charlie Gasparino in the business reporting department, bar none.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a lot more coming up, including crime. It's real and real reminders it's very tragic as well -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, finally an indictment in the killing of that Marist dad.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Alexis McAdams.\par \par \uc2 Alexis, what do we know?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, we were waiting for quite a few days here to figure out more details. We just heard from the court there in Dutchess County in Upstate New York that a grand jury voted to indict Roy Johnson Jr. in this tragic case. And this person -- we're talking about the suspect -- has a lengthy rap sheet. He was wanted in at least two states.\par \par \uc2 Police tell us he pulled out a gun and killed an innocent dad for no reason, all while officials say he should have been behind bars in the first place. So you're looking at Roy Johnson Jr. there. He was wanted on drug and gun charges out in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And then listen to this. Last year in Steuben County, New York, he was arrested and charged with eight serious counts. The DA says those charges are still pending. They range from attempted assault to possession of a weapon. But he never showed for his first court cases, so they couldn't find them, they say.\par \par \uc2 So the DA says there's still a lot of unanswered questions here. The Steuben County DA also telling us that current state bail reform laws make it hard to issue warrants and track the suspects down and says office did request a warrant, and there was some miscommunication there with the town judge.\par \par \uc2 Investigators in that small town call Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 's policies -- quote -- "ridiculous." And Republican Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , who's running for governor, says the bail reform laws are to blame. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLIN SCHMITT (R), NEW YORK CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: It's unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 Here in New York in the Hudson Valley, we're suffering from those failed policies. At the state level, we have the failed no bail law. We see the Democrats and Biden turn their back on law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Johnson Jr. was walking free when investigators say he murdered Paul Kutz. The 53-year-old was a dad of three and a loving husband. He was shot and killed while visiting his son at Marist College out in Poughkeepsie, New York.\par \par \uc2 Investigators tell me this was an unprovoked shooting. Nothing happened leading up to it. They originally, Neil, said it was over coffee, but now that's not the case. Witnesses say this person just walked into the lobby of the Courtyard Marriott there, pulled out a gun and sprayed the room with bullets.\par \par \uc2 More charges are expected. And since there was an active warrant for his arrest, Johnson Jr. was in the national database, but police said they could not track him down -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you very much for that, Alexis McAdams.\par \par \uc2 If you're beginning to notice a pattern here with some of these cases and the repetition, well, you're not imagining it.\par \par \uc2 Garrett Tenney has more from Chicago -- Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's crime report is typically the most comprehensive look at crime trends nationwide.\par \par \uc2 But, this year, there are questions about how reliable the numbers are, given that just around a half of the police departments in the country provided a full year's worth of data to the bureau's new reporting program. And nearly 40 percent of police departments didn't submit anything, which forced the agency to come up with estimates, such as a 1 percent estimated drop in overall violent crime and an estimated 4 percent increase in homicides.\par \par \uc2 But how accurate those are is hard to say, considering that some of the largest cities in the country, like Chicago, New York and Philadelphia, didn't report anything to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Violent crime in those cities is up by double digits this year, and the crime report doesn't include Chicago's totals for last year, when murders were up by 6 percent, along with increases in sexual assault, thefts, and carjackings.\par \par \uc2 It also doesn't include the higher number of murders, rapes, robberies, assaults and car thefts in New York City. And this year isn't looking any better for the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 , with overall crime up 32 percent year to date.\par \par \uc2 Today, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  said despite, arrest for major crimes hitting a 21-year high this year, the crime numbers aren't going down because recent reforms to the criminal justice system just aren't punishing criminals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KENNETH COREY, \uc2 NYPD\uc2  CHIEF OF DEPARTMENT: The reason that they're able to do that is because, right now, the only consequence that they face is spending a couple of hours in a police station, a minor inconvenience, and then they are immediately re-released and go on to steal again.\par \par \uc2 That is what drives the crime increase.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: And all of this serves as a reminder as to why crime is a top issue for many voters on Election Day, just a month away -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Garrett Tenney, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We will be talking to the former police chief in New York about what's happening nationally. And it is a problem nationally -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we talk about the crime that goes on.\par \par \uc2 And this -- in the New York area, they had something called a gun-free zone that would apply to areas in New York City that would not comply with this new standard that the Supreme Court allowed that you could carry a weapon, as long as it's a legally purchased weapon, as you walk the streets.\par \par \uc2 Now a judge essentially halting that move in the part of New York to challenge that.\par \par \uc2 Howard Safir\uc2  is the former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  commissioner, kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 I always felt that that ruling, Commissioner, was an engraved invitation to criminals to say, everyone there doesn't have a gun. Have at it. But what did you make of this judge?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD SAFIR\uc2 , FORMER NEW YORK CITY POLICE COMMISSIONER: Well, I think he's strictly interpreting the Supreme Court's decision.\par \par \uc2 And I think what New York City did was create a area that was much too wide relative to what the law is. It's OK to say you can't carry a gun into the hospital, in an airport, in the library. But to take a whole geographical area like Times Square and say that license didn't go -- owners cannot carry a gun there, I think, is going to be on what the Supreme Court intended. And I think that's why the judge ruled.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, essentially, a lot of this crime was happening in Times Square. Not all these incidents you're seeing now. Some were uptown, downtown. They're all over the place and, by the way, all over the country, Commissioner.\par \par \uc2 And that's the part that I think people can't appreciate here, these double-digits statistic rise is all types of crimes. They're not fake. This video is not a unique video. I mean, we show what we can. We have a lot more that we can. But it is what it is.\par \par \uc2 So when I hear people say, well, it's actually no big deal, and the numbers are not alarming, and this is a FOX News fixation, if only.\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: It is not a FOX News fixation. It is a public fixation. Crime is probably right with the economy when it comes to what people are concerned about.\par \par \uc2 The no bail laws, the liberal DAs, and the vilification and defunding of police is what's causing this incredible increase in crime. And that needs to change. What we're seeing now is this federal judge is probably thinking in his own mind, why should only criminals have guns? They're not going to pay attention to the law anyhow.\par \par \uc2 Now we're looking to take licensed, responsible people who have gone through the process of getting gun permits away from being able to protect themselves. I happen to agree with the judge.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I just wonder, when you hear this sort of stuff, Howard, on what basis do we release someone who's just beaten the heck out of a grandmother or a grandfather or an innocent woman? And they are quickly released, sometimes on little, if any bail at all.\par \par \uc2 Why? How?\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: Well, because you have district attorneys who are really far leftist, who are not charging what they should be charging. They're undercharging. They're complying with the no bail law. And that's the way they're charging.\par \par \uc2 And the result is what's happened recently. Somebody who was released from mugging somebody on the subway, they went out and murdered somebody. So, it's upside down, Neil. And what we have to do is, we have to gain control of our society. And we do that through assertive policing, elected officials who believe in taking care of the public, not taking care of the criminal.\par \par \uc2 And criminals need to be afraid of police. When I was commissioner, one of the most important things I made clear was, you don't have to be afraid if you're not committing a crime. But if you are committing a crime, you're going to jail and you're going to stay in jail.\par \par \uc2 None of that is happening now. We have turned this whole country upside down. And unless we change it, we're quickly going to become a country overrun by chaos.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know what I also notice, Commissioner, is that there's been a great deal of thought on the part of corporate bosses, particularly in this city, as you know, to get their workers back into the office.\par \par \uc2 And, to a man or woman, a lot of the workers say, well, besides the traffic and the scariness of taking subway or train, it's the crime. It is the overwhelming reason why people, at least in urban areas, and where this is now such a problem, is giving them pause.\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: Well, can you blame them?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: When they see -- when they see what happened just in Upstate New York recently, where an individual went to his son's graduation or an event...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: ... at his college, and two guys in a Marriott Hotel, of all places, started shooting at each other and killed them.\par \par \uc2 It -- there is reason to be afraid. And there is reason that major companies are looking to move out of these cities, so that their workers will come back. And, again, it's out of control, Neil. And it breaks my heart, because we -- and when I say we, myself and 40,000 members of the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  -- made this the safe largest city in America. And now we can't say that anymore. It is now a city out of control.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's also a city that its mayor is now saying has other burdens.\par \par \uc2 Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  has been saying that it's a state of emergency in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2  right now. It's going to cost a billion bucks, he says, to deal with this wave of migrants that come here by the busloads, and that it's a big - - a big problem. And others are saying it's going to be a big distraction, may get in the way of dealing with crime.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: Mayor Adams was a police officer for many years. He knows what needs to be done. Unfortunately, he's not doing it.\par \par \uc2 He's not supporting the police. They just reduced their budget. And he's not putting the political will of the mayor's office behind reducing crime. He talks a good game, but I'm looking at double-digit increases in crime. And the mayor and the police commissioner need to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 Cops will deal with it. But, unfortunately, what's happening is they're retiring and they're quitting because they're not getting the kind of support that they need.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know it's a separate issue, but I did notice, when he claimed this emergency, state of emergency, and this is overwhelming, and I'm thinking to myself, man, in Texas and Arizona, it's been commonplace.\par \par \uc2 And this concern for a crisis that you outline now seems a little detached from what Texans have been dealing with forever now, Arizonans as well. So I guess welcome to the reality of this. But is he -- is he behind the eight ball here? Now, all of a sudden, he has to acknowledge what these states have been going through for some time.\par \par \uc2 But does he now get into a role where he can get some change going here?\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: Well, he can get some change going if he starts policing the streets and he realizes that open borders cannot only affect Texas and Arizona, but now they can affect him and the citizens of New York.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that's not happening, Commissioner.\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: And we don't know....\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He's not doing that. And I'm just wondering.\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You can't just talk a good game about this. You have to do something. And that's the big concern.\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: Well, that's why, unless he starts doing something, his administration will be known as the administration that turned New York around back to the good -- back to the bad old days, instead of the good old days.\par \par \uc2 And, so far, I have not seen anything that impresses me. What I have seen is demoralized police, criminals running around the street. And, of course, when you have these immigrants coming in, we don't know, since they're not screened, we don't know if they're terrorists. We don't know if they're criminals. We don't know if they're rapists.\par \par \uc2 It's just adding to the problem. And it's insane.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is, indeed. A state of emergency has been in existence for a while now, long before these migrants.\par \par \uc2 Howard Safir\uc2 , thank you very much, the former New York police commissioner, kept this city pretty safe under his watch. That was then. It's very different now.\par \par \uc2 We will have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, remember when gas, oil prices looked like they were stabilizing?\par \par \uc2 Well, they're not. This week, one of the worst weeks we have seen on that front in quite some time, oil prices now over $92 a barrel, this week alone jumping about 15 percent. We have not seen anything like that in quite some time, gasoline for the 15th day running now up and moving still further up on news that \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and so-called \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus countries are cutting back on supply, to the tune of two million barrels a day.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's very good news for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . It's the currency he uses to fund the war in Ukraine, whatever its controversies, bad news for, well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the rest of the Western world. The impact here can't be overstated enough. It's walloping stocks, because they fear, the more this goes on, the more the economy gets hammered.\par \par \uc2 Today, it did, even though the market averages did manage to eke out a gain on the week, and the Dow a little bit more than a little gain. The fact of the matter is, investors are worried, shoppers are worried, drivers are worried, and average folks who buy average things are extremely worried.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be exploring it tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time and what you can do. But, sometimes, you have to know the numbers, and we will sort them all out, because they're scary. But, then again, this is October, and it is close to Halloween, a few weeks from now.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221008eia700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Celebrates Dem Inflation Reduction Act; Biden Pushes Green Energy Agenda Amid Economic Crises; Some \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  Trains Canceled Amid Possible Strike; White House Scrambles To Avoid Rail Strike; Zelenskyy: We Will Come To Our Other Territories; Ukraine Blitz Sends Putin's Forces Into Retreat; China's Xi To Meet Putin For Second Time This Year; Stage Set For Crucial Senate Race In New Hampshire; Democrat Senator Takes Aim At GOP Challenger Over Abortion; Fetterman Agrees To Debate Oz On October 25th; Mourners Line Up For Miles Along London Streets; UK Pub-Goers Raise A Glass To Remember The Queen\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jacqui Heinrich, Karl Rove, Joe Concha, Bret  Baier, Hillary Vaughn, Gerry Baker \par \uc2 8,441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TYRUS MURDOCH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, electricity only just came out. So, that's pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh, man, no comment.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I was wondering who he was referring to when he said an older gentleman. I just don't know.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: No, we're not losing you. We need you. We need you here.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks for joining us, everybody.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Thanks, Tyrus.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . THE STORY with Martha starts right now live in London, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum live from London this evening. $900 million to build electric vehicle charging stations across the U.S., President Biden touting use of that big chunk of change just moments ago, but the Environment and Climate Change rank low on the long list of voter concerns right now.\par \par \uc2 At the top, the high cost of living and inflation, which as we know, continue to soar. Karl Rove, and Joe Concha, Sandra Smith, not going to let her off the hook, she's going to stick around for us today as well, and Bret Baier also joining us this hour, as the President insists that the economy is right on track and celebrates it with James Taylor at the White House.\par \par \uc2 First, we go to White House Correspondent, Jacqui Heinrich. Hi, Jackie.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. Well, even though that new Gallup poll shows that voters are far more concerned with the economy than they are with climate. The President remains fixated on climate telling reporters ahead of this trip that he's not concerned about yesterday's hotter than expected inflation reading or the stock market sell off before announcing that $900 million for electric vehicle chargers on highways across 35 states.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: A great American road trip is going to be fully electrified, whether you're driving coast to coast along I-10 or on I-75 here in Michigan. Charging stations will be up and easy to find as gas stations are now. We're also going to invest $7 billion to make American car companies and have the batteries and other critical materials they need.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House wants half of all new cars sold in the U.S. to be electric by 2030, saying that monies from the Chips Act can produce necessary semiconductors here at home, but they can't say when those cars would actually hit the market for Americans to buy them. And then there's the issue of charging them. Energy Secretary \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  said that the U.S. should look to California as the model for clean energy.\par \par \uc2 Well, they just asked the residents not to charge their E.V.s amid a brutal heatwave. And moreover, buying an electric car is too expensive for most Americans dealing with inflation right now. One of the cars, the president test drove today, costs more than $63,000.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): The religion today and the Democrat Party is a religion to climate, they worship, the Sun God, the Wind God, and the God of Wishful Thinking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yesterday, the White House took some heat for celebrating the Inflation Reduction Act just as that new inflation reading showed a worrying increase in a key inflation metric, and then there's the inconvenient fact that experts agree that spending is not going to reduce inflation at all. Officials here are pointing to tax rebates for investments on clean energy, but spending money in order to save some money is certainly not the same thing as just bringing prices down. And prices are up across the board, including for groceries up nearly 14 percent and energy up nearly 16 percent, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jacqui Heinrich at the White House, thank you very much Jacqui. So, the White House events celebrating the Inflation Reduction Act complete with a performance of "Fire and Rain" by James Taylor. There's a little bit of that for you tonight. That'll be knocked as tone deaf, given the report that came out yesterday and the current state of the economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: There is this unfortunate split screen right now with the DOW taking a total beating down more than 1200 points. And so, it feels like it's hard to be celebratory for some people.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST, "MORNING JOE" CO-HOST: Tough day to have the big Inflation Reduction Act event as it did at the White House with this number that came out yesterday.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): I don't think any average American who's struggling to live paycheck to paycheck, thinks that's something to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, with that, we bring in Karl Rove, Former Deputy Chief of Staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and Joe Concha, Media and Politics Columnist for The Hill, both are Fox News Contributors. Gentlemen, great to have both of you with us. Karl, let me start with you, you know, great images of the President in some fancy looking E.V.s and charging stations that he wants to build across the country, is that a winner for him politically?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, a minor winner but whatever advantage you gain from that he lost by the stupid event yesterday celebrating the so-called Inflation Reduction Act on a day that inflation went up. And what did the President say, was asked, are you worried about it going up? And he said, well, it's only one-tenth of one percent? No, no, it's actually 8.3 percent year over year.\par \par \uc2 In fact, energy is up 23.8 percent, shelter up 6.2, food up 11.4. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  showed up at the thing and said, we are lowering the cost of kitchen table items for American families. Meat, poultry, fish, and eggs are up 10.6. Dairy up, 16.2. Fruit and vegetables up 9.4. I wonder what kitchen table item she's talking about? Maybe it's something that one of those luxury home goods stores in Napa Valley, because it's not the ordinary American family.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe, mostly, this Inflation Reduction Act, which doesn't really touch anything in terms of lowering costs until deep into the next couple of years, has a lot of green energy initiatives in it. It has some reductions in, in healthcare in terms of prescription drugs that also don't kick in for some time. But this is sort of the story and they're absolutely sticking with it. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's completely out of touch to Karl's point, Martha. I mean, it's, it's completely off the grid, actually, in terms of voter priorities. As Jacqui pointed out earlier in a report, when, when you look at Gallup, and you see that inflation and economy dwarfs everything else like they do in every other election pretty much. And the thinking here resides in a fantasy world as far as, oh, just if everybody could get an electric car, then we can help cure the climate.\par \par \uc2 The average cost of an electric car hit $66,000 last month. That's a 13 percent increase year over year, because there's this thing called 40-year high inflation. And we keep being told, well, electric car costs, they're going to keep coming down, but the opposite is actually happening. So, the question bears repeating, how the heck does the average person afford to buy an electric car, you look at the average median income, OK. After taxes in this country, if they worked an entire year, they still can't afford to buy the average electric car.\par \par \uc2 So, Republicans should hammer this point home, because this administration is pushing a financial impossibility for an overwhelming majority of Americans, while they're canceling oil lease, after oil lease in the Gulf, or off the coast of Alaska, stripping us of energy independence, while forcing places like Europe to depend on Russian oil. It's all bad, and this should be a central, central focus in the midterms, if Republicans are smart. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's pull up this chart, the change in CPI for major cities. And you can see that in some of the areas where we've seen the fastest growth in the consumer price index, they are states that are important, and there, where there are Senate races, you've got Arizona, Georgia, Florida at the top of that list, and it also Philadelphia is on the list as well, in Pennsylvania up 8.1 percent. But to Joe's question, Karl, what are Republicans offering in terms of an inflation reduction package that they feel would actually be effective in lowering prices in the short term?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, first of all stops are spending. We have a -- we're going to give away on, on student loans between, that's going to cost between 519 billion and a trillion dollars. We've got the so-called reflate, Inflation Reduction Act, whose price tag is variously about 435 billion are and up. I mean, stop the spending. The best, best thing the government can do is stop adding to the demand.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is too much money chasing too few goods, and we have pumped between the, the American Rescue Plan and these other things. Literally several trillion dollars of demand in the economy within basically a year and a half. So, the first thing is, stop the unnecessary spending. And tell the American people the truth, I mean, when the President United States says everything is OK, and has James Taylor out there, you know, singing "Fire and Rain."\par \par \uc2 I mean, rather than a ballot about suicide and addiction, and grief, maybe he should have done one of his other numbers, one of my favorites. It's only a "Paper Moon." It's a Barnum and Bailey world, just as phony as it can be. But it would be not, it would not be make believe if you believed in me. That's what they're trying to sell is a phony view of the world as it is. And it's going to disconnect him from the voters and they'll suffer accordingly at the polls.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Karl, the only thing that would have made that better is if you actually sing it for us. But I'm very impressed with your lyrics recall.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: It's a Barnum and Bailey World, just as phony as it can be. No, you don't want to hear me say Martha, you do not want to hear me sing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: I can listen to this all day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Maybe that, maybe that's true. Joe, you know, talk to us a little bit about what you think voters are thinking as we head into the eight weeks before the midterm here and they're watching this at the White House and they're seeing these prices go up and they're being told that they should just, you know, buy an electric car and everything will get better. But you do hear a lot of people asking, you know, what is it that Republicans would offer and we're seeing these races tighten up?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Precisely. We talk about bumper stickers on the show last week. It has to be such a clear and concise message. I mean, I get the Obama Biden campaign in 2012 a lot of credit. Very simple slogan to absorb. (INAUDIBLE) is alive and Bin Laden is dead, OK? So, you just need, basically, two sentences, something more along the lines of and I don't have it off the top of my head. But you know, are you better off than you were two years ago? Or do you want to live in a less expensive country and a more safe country. Just make it all about economy and crime, work the border and there as well.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the opioid overdoses that are happening 300 people dying a day in this country driven by fentanyl. You just have to conform it to or you just can really bring it into four basic messages that are pragmatic, common sense, and easy for people to understand. So, when they make that choice, the contrast is there. Because again, I don't know what positive things Democrats run on. And if it's the Inflation Reduction Act, even though it's a legislative win, it's a loss ultimately, in the minds of the American people because they know it won't reduce inflation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, just, just a minute, Karl, but you know, Bret's coming up, and we're going to talk a little bit about the Pennsylvania race where Fetterman is still ahead. What do you think that the bumper sticker should be for Dr. Oz in that race?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: My opponent is a far out liberal, far out left-winger who wants to tax more, let criminals out of jail, open the border, cut the defense budget, and ruin the, the energy industry of Pennsylvania to achieve sharp contrasts on any of those issues are going to help Dr. Oz defeat Fetterman because he is way out there, and he's not in the mainstream of Pennsylvania's thinking.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: And Martha, I think ultimately once --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, go ahead Joe.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: I'm sorry, yes, I was about to say, oh, once the debate happens, and it will happen now the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is even pushing Fetterman to debate before votes are passed, and that's on September 19th. The minute that he's exposed in that environment, I think, that changes the whole dynamic of that race because he's clearly struggling right now. We wish him well, but he's not there right now in terms of speech or motor skills.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Could be, we'll be watching and as I said, Bret Baier is coming up. We're going to dig into the midterm races as we are inside eight weeks right now. Karl Rove, great to have you with us. Thanks for singing for us tonight. Glad to hear your favorite James Taylor tune. And Joe Concha, thank you very much. Great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: See you soon. So, as voters continue to grapple with higher prices, many are not bringing home more money. Sandra Smith has that side of the income equation, which is a troubling story in America right now. And Bret Baier is back with a big update on a battleground race with the potential to decide the control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  narrowing in on these races that everybody needs to get a close look at. Bret's coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We have a big story breaking right now \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  canceling long distance trains starting tomorrow, in case of this possible road strike -- railroad strike that has been really percolating over the last couple of days. It says that it's trying to avoid passenger disruptions.\par \par \uc2 The talks do not involve \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  or its workers is a dispute with freight railroads. One union already rejecting a tentative deal. Under federal law, the workers are not allowed to strike until Friday. That is a big story that could be very disruptive to the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 And a quick look at this as prices on everyday expenses sore from a year ago. The amount of money that the average U.S. household is taking in remains pretty much the same, unfortunately, on the income side, according to the new Census Bureau data. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And you can be saying, hey, I got this raise. Your raise means nothing, if you had to spend it all on milk, eggs, and bacon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Your paycheck is bigger. It's just not big enough.\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, CO-HOST, MSNBC: If you're holding down a job, and you get a raise, and you're still falling behind every month. Well, you're not sitting there singing, Happy Days Are Here Again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, you're sure aren't. That's correct.\par \par \uc2 Sandra Smith, co-host of America reports joins us now. Sandra, thanks so much for staying around to talk to us this evening.\par \par \uc2 So, two really big stories here. Talk to us a little bit about this potential railroad strike. And what's the impact of these freight railroads if they -- if they do indeed go on strike.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: And the story is very much intertwined. Right, Martha? Where we just had the head of the Manufacturers' Association on with us last hour, who said this is only going to serve to potentially exacerbate this problem with inflation in this country.\par \par \uc2 8.3 percent inflation, Martha. You look at incomes that have done basically this, as you just reported for the past couple of years, while inflation is doing this. Everything that American workers are earning right now, any additional earnings, any additional income, it is getting eaten up by this inflation picture that does not seem to be improving.\par \par \uc2 And you've got this ongoing meeting happening at the White House right now, where they're trying to strike a deal. You've got \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  , the labor secretary, saying, that they're -- both sides are acting in good faith. Both parties are negotiating in good faith. They have committed to staying at the table.\par \par \uc2 Martha, these meetings started at 9:00 Eastern Time this morning. They have been in there all day. So far, also, as you just reported a short time ago, 4,900 members of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  , they voted to reject the agreement as these negotiations continue.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this could potentially bring what is already a very challenging economic situation into this country -- this could bring this economy to a standstill. We are dependent on these workers to transport goods in order to keep these prices down or at least keep them where they are. This could only serve to continue to raise prices even higher from here.\par \par \uc2 It's a tough situation, Martha. And you know, the workers, they don't feel like they're being properly represented. Even \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  acknowledging there is no sick leave for these workers, and that's a problem.\par \par \uc2 A lot of the railroad engineers are saying, this isn't about the money. And it seems the White House is making this more about the money. It's not about the money, this is about work, life, conditions. Work conditions, when they're on the job. Being able to take time off when they want to.\par \par \uc2 Something is got to get done here by that Friday deadline, or we could see this inflation problem get worse in this country for sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's so interesting, you know, you wonder about what the pressures are that are really at work here. We know that so many places have a labor shortage, they don't have enough employees right now. And that may be putting some pressure. We've seen it with pilots, that that's putting pressure on them as well.\par \par \uc2 But President Biden, Sandra, has always been very supportive of unions throughout his entire career. So, is he supporting this strike?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We'll see what ultimately happens. All we know is that they're both -- in the White House right now, both sides are trying to hash out an agreement. They know the pressure is on and this White House is facing what could be huge political implications if they don't strike a deal, and further exacerbate the inflation situation in this country.\par \par \uc2 Martha, something that stuck out to me in that report where we were talking about U.S. incomes failing to grow for a second straight year in this country, looking at the poverty rates.\par \par \uc2 Guess where we saw the poverty rate rise the most? With the 65 years old and older age group in this country, and the economist in the Wall Street Journal noting that's very likely happening, because guess what, they're living on fixed incomes, many of them.\par \par \uc2 If you're living on a fixed income in this country, and you're dealing with 8.3 percent spike in inflation, year over year, that's hardly manageable. So, it's a really tough political situation for this White House, they've got to hash out a deal somehow, because people are living through real American economic pain right now. And that's why it was tough to see that celebration at the White House yesterday for a lot of folks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, they are hopeful that they're going to bring down drug prices, but the -- you know, it's going to take a while. And that's a big issue, obviously, for that part of the -- for the population as well. That's what the White House is might be banking on in part for that demographic, but we'll see if it works.\par \par \uc2 Sandra, thank you. Great to have you with us. Good to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Amazing coverage from there. Martha. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, thank you, Sandra. Thanks. Great to see you. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, coming up, President Xi ventures out of China for the first time since the pandemic. Now, set to meet face to face with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in the coming days, as momentum in the war on Ukraine shifts, not Russia's way toward Ukraine where they've just raised a Ukraine flag over an area that was occupied by the Russians.\par \par \uc2 John Kirby joins me live from the White House right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Zelenskyy, watching his country's flag rise above the newly retaken town of Izyum today, part of more than 3,000 square miles Ukraine claims to have recaptured from Russia. Just enormous developments that are going on in this battle.\par \par \uc2 The White House calling what has happened a shift in momentum. But cautioning that war, of course, remains unpredictable. With that, we bring in John Kirby, National Security Council coordinator for Strategic Communications.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: John, great to have you with us. Thank you very much for being here today.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NSC: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I'd like to start if I may, by playing a sound bite from President Zelenskyy in Izyum. And here is what he had to say. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: We know that truth is on our side, though. So, it means that we will come. I don't know when, and nobody knows when, but we have plans. So, we'll come because this is our land, and it's our people. That's why we will come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: If you couldn't hear that, John, and I know you don't necessarily have a monitor there. But he's talking about Crimea.\par \par \uc2 And he's saying we have plans and we will come. So, is the United States supportive of their effort to retake Crimea from Russia?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Crimea is Ukraine. And we've been very clear about that. We do not recognize the so-called annexation by Russia of Crimea.\par \par \uc2 And Crimea is part of Ukraine, and Ukrainian armed forces are fighting to defend their country, their territory, and Crimea is that -- is that territory.\par \par \uc2 So, the other thing I'd say, Martha is we are not sort of telling the Ukrainians what they can go after, or what they can't. We're not -- we're not dictating the terms of their military operations then. This is their war. This is their fight. We're doing everything we can to sustain them and support them in that fight.\par \par \uc2 But President Zelenskyy, as the commander in chief, he gets to determine what the military targets are, and how he's going to defend and support his own territorial integrity.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But John, just going back in time, when the Russians invaded Crimea and took it over, we did not send any military assistance. In fact, there was a lot of criticism of the Obama administration that they were just sending meals ready to eat, and that we weren't sending them what they really needed.\par \par \uc2 So, is it the case that our stance has changed on that, that now we would we do want to militarily support the retaking of Crimea from Russia?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, there's no -- there's not a shift here. And I think it's a little unfair, the criticism of the Obama administration. They sent a lot more than just meals ready to eat. There was -- there was weapons supplied to the Ukrainians, as well as significant training efforts by the United States, the U.K., our Canadian allies, as well, and others.\par \par \uc2 But look, we're involved right now in this reinvasion, if you will, since February, by Mr. Putin. And we have been nothing but clear that we're going to continue to support Ukraine's military for as long as it takes. The President has been steadfast about that.\par \par \uc2 And we're also clear that this is their fight. This is their war, and they get to determine where their operations are going to be in, and what they're going to go after.\par \par \uc2 If they need information and context. We certainly provide that, Martha. But these are their decisions to make.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, President Zelenskyy has talked about their energy resources and using them to supply Europe. Crimea, obviously, is a strategic area for Russia. And I'm curious about the potential for great escalation if Ukraine tries to take back Crimea, and they have made, you know, obvious head win -- obvious advantage in the northeast part of the country.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is, yesterday, I spoke with the former deputy Supreme Allied Commander of \uc2 NATO\uc2  , Sir General Richard Sherriff. And here's what he said about the possibility of this escalating.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD SHERRIFF, FORMER DEPUTY SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER OF \uc2 NATO\uc2  : If Russia decides to go seriously, serious escalation, and that potentially it brings in the threat of nuclear weaponry. The \uc2 NATO\uc2  mindset needs to change. \uc2 NATO\uc2  really needs to be prepared for worst case, which is war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you agree that the \uc2 NATO\uc2  mindset needs to change considerably?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We have been from the very beginning, Martha, in fact, you and I have talked about this many times. One of the concerns and the things we've been looking at from the very beginning is not letting this escalate with Russia, not making this about the United States versus Russia or the West versus Russia, or \uc2 NATO\uc2  versus Russia, and certainly not allowing this to escalate beyond the brutality that it's already been visited -- now visiting on the Ukrainian people. Because that's not -- escalation is not in -- certainly not in our interest. It's not in the interest of our allies and partners in Europe, and it absolutely wouldn't be in the interest of the Ukrainian people who have already suffered so much.\par \par \uc2 So, we're providing them material support, we're certainly provide them information and intelligence as needed but we are not doing their targeting for them. And we are not taking upon ourselves to determine what their operations are going to look like or what they're not. I mean, this is President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  's call. Nobody benefits, though, nobody, from this war escalating beyond, again, the brutality that it's already -- that have already occurred.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. I want to shift gears for this last question that I have for you. And it has to do with President Xi meeting with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We have seen that their energy relationship has been really beneficial to Russia. They're selling -- Russia selling more oil to China than ever before. We've also seen some beneficial credit card activity that China has helped Russia to get around, the sanctions so that Russia is not suffering, that much pain in this. They've also been buying land in the United States near some military bases. Is there any change coming on the policy for Chinese investment with regard to any of this, John,\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don't have anything to speak to today, Martha, in terms of changes in our policies with respect to an investment. You're right that China and other nations continue to buy Russian oil. That's why we want to put a price cap on that oil so that Putin can't benefit from those revenues.\par \par \uc2 We haven't seen any indications that China is -- that -- well, they're not supporting the sanctions. We haven't seen any indication that they're violating the sanctions or giving material support to Russia with respect to Ukraine. Mr. Xi, sorry, and Mr. Putin can talk about their meeting and characterize it how they want. We obviously don't want to see business as usual with Russia by anybody.\par \par \uc2 And China has a choice to make. They have a choice to make about whether they're going to join the rest of the international community here and rightly condemning this action, and taking action to hold Russia accountable or not. And so far, we haven't seen them actually do anything materially to hold Russia accountable.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK but last question. Given this kind of growing relationship, which they've talked about a lot over the past several years, is it a national security threat to not restrict Chinese investment in the United States and Chinese business dealings that affect us?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think the President has been very clear, even in his discussions with President Xi, that we're mindful of unfair trade and economic practices by China. I'm not going to speak to this particular issue today. I don't have anything to announce or speak to with respect to Chinese investment here at home, but it is something that we're watching closely and monitoring.\par \par \uc2 And we, you know, we refer to China as the pacing challenge, because not just economically but from a security and military perspective. They continue to provide or present challenges and threats to us in the Indo Pacific Region and around the world that we're going to stay focused on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the President is not concerned about the Chinese buying farmland, for example, in the middle of the heartland of the United States?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No. Look, of course, it's a matter of concern. I just don't have like a policy decision to speak to today, Martha. But obviously, it's a concern and we've been nothing but honest about and consistent about our concerns with Chinese trade and economic practices.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, thank you so much. Good to talk to you. A lot of big topics going on all around the world, so, John, thank you very much. John Kirby joining us tonight from the White House and we thank him for doing that.\par \par \uc2 So coming up, I will speak to Bret Baier on what's being called MAGA's New Hampshire sweep and a big update out of Pennsylvania where Democratic Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has now responded to growing calls to debate Republican challenger, Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So there is now a declared winner in a contest that could help decide control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  , Retired General Don Bolduc beating Chuck Morse in the Republican Primary in New Hampshire. According to the AP, Bolduc at 37.1%, he will now face off against Democrat Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  with election day just under eight weeks away. "Special Report" anchor Bret Baier is standing by, but first to Fox Business correspondent Hillary Vaughn who is on the campaign trail live in Concord, New Hampshire. Hi, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, General Bolduc winning his primary is exactly what some Democrats were hoping for. Bolduc beating his more moderate opponent, New Hampshire Senate President Chuck Morse. Morse was promoted by some in the GOP as the best person to beat Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  and flip the Senate seat for Republicans. But Democrats instead spent millions trying to boost Bolduc, a more controversial candidate, thinking he would be a weaker opponent for Senator Hassan to face. But Bolduc says that strategy will ultimately blow up in Democrats' face in November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R), NEW HAMPSHIRE SENATE CANDIDATE: We have taken their arrows. We have successfully protected ourselves. (Inaudible) only thing that I call her is a bad US senator.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Hassan is already attacking Bolduc as a radical saying in a statement last night Don Bolduc is simply too extreme for New Hampshire. And she's also making abortion the center of her campaign up with a fresh ad today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Don Bolduc and congressional Republicans take control of the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  , they would push for a nationwide ban on abortion, a ban with no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: But Bolduc told me last night, loud and clear, he would not support an abortion ban as senator.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: I will support no legislation, zero legislation at the federal level. We don't belong in the abortion arena. That is the states. In this state will do the right thing by Granite Staters.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Why do you think she keeps pushing this abortion issue?\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: It's the only thing she's got.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And, Martha, former President Trump did not endorse anyone in these primaries but he is bragging about the results saying that the "Trumpist" candidates ended up winning but Granite Staters did make up their own minds and clearly chose to not play it safe and go with a moderate in this race. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting, Hillary. Thank you very much. Now to another crucial Senate race, Pennsylvania's lieutenant governor, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  , leading Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz in the new \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll that just came out. This one has Fetterman up by 10 points. If you combine voters who say they would definitely or probably choose him. He is recovering from a stroke that happened just days before his primary race.\par \par \uc2 And breaking today, he has committed to debate Dr. Oz on October 25th. That is after a lot of pressure, including some from the Washington Post Editorial Board that says that the debate needs to happen before the early voting starts next week, and that Fetterman should release his medical records. This is the race to replace Republican Senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  so this would be a pickup for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in "Special Report" anchor and executive editor Bret Baier and my co-anchor for our election coverage. Bret, great to see you tonight. Thank you for joining me. Talk to me a little bit about your read on what's going on in this Pennsylvania race with these new polls, and this commitment to debate although it's a little unclear exactly when.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, they've got a date now. And I think Lieutenant Governor Fetterman was feeling a lot of pressure, not just from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  but a lot of different news organizations saying that he had to commit to a debate he was kind of doing it nebulously, saying I will debate but there wasn't a date. Now, they have October 25th as a date he commits to with the next affiliates, and that's local affiliates throughout Pittsburgh and -- throughout Pennsylvania, I should say.\par \par \uc2 But there are stipulations that the Fetterman campaign put on it. He has to have closed captioning. He has to have two practice sessions with the moderator or moderators to get accustomed to the closed captioning. Well, just moments ago, the Oz campaign responded saying that's fine, we'll do it. But at the top of the debate, the moderator has to say that Fetterman is using this closed captioning system and that's why there's a delay.\par \par \uc2 He has to -- in the practice sessions not actually practice questions that they are going to ask on the night of the debate, which seems reasonable, and that the debate will be extended from 60 to 90 minutes because of the delay in time according to the Oz folks in this process. Now, they go on to say that they would like to base, the Oz team, in the early part of this month as those ballots are going out as the early voting starts.\par \par \uc2 And they then go on to the traditional attacks about the various policy issues that they want to hit Lieutenant Governor Fetterman on. We don't know the response from the Fetterman team yet but it seems like this is turning to at least October 25th, and maybe, maybe another.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And that's a late date, you know, in terms of when this -- how close that would be to the election, and I believe it's after the early voting starts October 25th.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Or is it right around that date?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Couple of weeks, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's after, yes, a couple of days after. So obviously that's going to be a factor. Let's put up this Monmouth poll which shows the biggest issues, most important issues, inflation still at the top, 31%, economy 30%, you can probably marry those together, illegal immigration at 14%, abortion at 13%. Fetterman has said that he thinks that women will help him win in Pennsylvania. I think that's something we're going to hear a lot. We saw at \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  's ad a lot of attention on that issue, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Definitely. And Democrats around the country are really jumping on \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  's proposal for nationwide abortion ban after 15 weeks. And every Democrat, it seems like his jumping on this issue, seeing it as ripe. It is important to point out that inflation, the economy, illegal immigration in this poll, far surpass abortion as far as priorities. And it's interesting that that kind of falls into the Oz's policy basket. We'll see how this plays out.\par \par \uc2 But clearly, Dr. Oz wants to focus on the policy differences between himself and Governor Lieutenant Governor Fetterman, specifically on crime and also on taxes, and specific policy differences left and right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, that would be obviously he's going to be questioned on that in the debate. And those issues on vary -- a lot of criminal reform that he's interested in, in Pennsylvania that is seen as highly controversial. And it's clear also that the Fetterman team, Bret, wants to ask Dr. Oz if he would support that Graham 15 week ban. That's going to be sort of a very clear boilerplate question for all of these Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It will be. And, you know, it's interesting that a lot of the conventional wisdom is that this is a really bad thing for Republicans, and perhaps it is on focusing on the issue of abortion anyway. But at 15 weeks, that is where a lot of European countries are, as far as restrictions, after 15 weeks. And if you start turning that question around, saying to Democrats, are you for abortion in 15, 17, 20 weeks, it becomes a much stickier issue when you get to the campaign.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely, I think that's absolutely right. Let's talk a little bit, before I let you go, about this breaking news, because we're just getting an update on \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  , the Labor Secretary, and these discussions that are going on with the rail unions now going into Hour 7th on this. Where is this headed, Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the Biden administration is really trying hard to avoid this strike. It is a national rail strike potentially, the first in 30 years. We're showing a picture of \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  there. \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  is not directly in the negotiation but they would be affected because of the railways being affected. And, you know, there's a possible implications down the road for all kinds of things, not only moving people but also moving a product across the country.\par \par \uc2 So, the administration is really trying double time to get this to work. But you're right, seven hours of negotiation, no breakthrough yet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President's always been very supportive of the unions all throughout his career. And this is a tough situation. We'll see what comes of it, especially given the supply chain problems that really fired up inflation early on in this process. Bret, great to see you. Thank you so much. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, bye, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, mourners are now paying their final respects. We see the lines still moving behind us overlooking Westminster Hall this evening. And many of them, thousands of them have had an opportunity to go in and pay their final respects to Queen Elizabeth II as she lay in state at Westminster Hall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She has that magic about her, much more than normal people. She's an icon of icons. So I must at least endure this camping out of respect, and when I get there I remove this and then say my prayers and say she has been an angel, angles take her home. That's what I feel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A live look tonight as mourners pay their respects to Queen Elizabeth II as she lie in state at Westminster Hall. Throughout the day, thousands of people, we've watched them streaming down the line is right below us here in the balcony that we are at. They're waiting their turn. It goes about two-and-a-half miles this line at this point. And we're being told that some people could wait as much as 20 hours, some people got in today in seven to eight hours, so we'll see how the line goes.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, Her Majesty left -- Her Majesty's coffin left Buckingham Palace for the final time, very moving ceremony. We were positioned just to the right of this screen where you see. And we saw the family members as they piled by behind the coffin and behind that imperial crown. It was just an absolutely stunning day.\par \par \uc2 That's the gun carriage that carried Queen Elizabeth's father and grandfather as well. The coffin will be in the hall surrounded by four guards and also the visual of the princes which will take place with family members standing around the coffin as well. The state funeral will be Monday morning at 11:00 am London time.\par \par \uc2 Here's the moment that this vigil began earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: See the bowmen bending their heads -- bowing their heads around the coffin today. Gerry Baker's latest in the Wall Street Journal touches on the Queen's lasting place in the hearts of the British and in the hearts of the world. He's editor at large and host of FBN's "WSJ at Large with Gerry Baker." Gerry, great to have you with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 You and I covered Prince Philip's funeral together and you were so eloquent on your take on his role in British public life. Talk to me a little bit about what you think of what we're watching play out here and the passing of Queen Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, FOX BUSINESS HOST: What is striking is that you're seeing a very -- it's very interesting, the contrast between -- you recall very well the death of Princess Diana 25 years ago, when Britain kind of went into a sort of a kind of a melt history on it, sort of melt down, very emotional. You're seeing this is a very, very calm, solemn, somber, reflection on the Queen and the role that she played. And I think it's a testament to exactly that, what was so impressive and what was so striking in the individual and unique about the Queen was just her dignity. And that she reigned with extraordinary self-restraint, extraordinary devotion to duty, to service to her country. And the country is now paying that back in its own little way with these people waiting for hours and hours to do this.\par \par \uc2 Queen Elizabeth redefined the monarchy because she had to in the 20th century. Monarchy is a very old institution, has been around in Britain for thousand years. And people are going to ask the question, what is monarchy for in the 21st century? The Queen managed to brilliantly redefine it, and I think there's going to be a question now after the Queen, what is the monarchy for after she's gone?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what do you think that looks like given \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  and Prince William behind him?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Think it's going to be difficult, to be honest with you, for them because again, there is tremendous affection for the Queen. I think there's tremendous support for \uc2 King Charles\uc2  too. I don't think there's any -- he's unpopular. I mean, he was 25 years ago with the death of Princess Diana. But he's mended a lot of that, he's healed a lot of that.\par \par \uc2 But the problem, I think, is that again, the Queen knew exactly how to play the role properly, which is the Queen has no real power. She has no real authority in politics. She can't control anything. She's a symbolic figurehead who can help bring the country together simply by being a national figure.\par \par \uc2 King Charles\uc2  , unfortunately, is one of those people who actually thinks he's got some important contribution to make in terms of the great issues of the day. He's very passionate about some of those issues. And the worry, I think, is that he will feel his conception of the monarchy, is different from his mother's. and that he should be out there involved in telling people what they shouldn't be doing, and people aren't going to like that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And William's role at this point?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, he's very popular and we'll have to see, you know, in contrast, particularly to his brother who's very unpopular right now. He will probably be given more of a role in speaking out and being more of a public figure than he's been. His wife, Kate, is extremely, the Princess of Wales is extremely popular too. So we'll have to see.\par \par \uc2 I think there's always been a thought that maybe it should go straight to William rather than to Charles, that's never going to happen. But William has a chance now to grow and to maybe redefine the monarchy in his own way too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, all right. Gerry, thank you. Great to have you with us. Gerry Baker --\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- from the Wall Street Journal. So, of course, Britain is known for its pubs. And for those here in London, many are raising a glass in memory of their beloved Queen Elizabeth. Watch some of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nestled in a narrow street in the heart of London sits the Shepherd Market, filled with cafes and traditional pubs, the passing of the Queen at the forefront of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I just thought she was an amazing woman. Absolutely amazing woman, was like -- she was like a mother to us all really. So, she was just always there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sad, very sad. She was a lovely, strong lady. We'll never see the likes of her, old school. We'll never see the likes of her again, I don't think. She's with her husband. And I think, my thoughts is that when he died, I honestly thought you wouldn't be long after many with.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: People have gathered here in good times and bad since the 1700s. And this week, many of us has been raised in the memory of Queen Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And another live look now at Westminster Hall as people continue to stream in and pay their respects, lining up by the thousands to Queen Elizabeth II. That little girl finally getting her turn to go in.\par \par \uc2 That's THE STORY for tonight. I'll see you back here tomorrow at 3:00.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220915ei9e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Inflation Reality; Queen Elizabeth Lying in State; Ukraine's Forces Advancing; Interview With Sen. \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2  (R-WY); Nationwide Railway Strike?; Stocks Stabilize\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Greg Palkot, Jacqui Heinrich, Jeff Flock, Keith  Kellogg, Gary Kaltbaum, Susan Li, Bill Melugin \par \uc2 8,131 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, at the corner of Wall and Broad, if you were expecting a big comeback for stocks today after the big 1,200- plus-point sell-off yesterday, you would be disappointed.\par \par \uc2 Stocks did manage a meager comeback, but not enough, on the heels of an inflation report on the wholesale side, in other words, the prices before you and I pay them, that remains very worrisome, just not as worrisome as yesterday's retail inflation report. The numbers are still high. The percentages are still staggering. And the worries are still out there.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World" with Susan Li on the difference in 24 hours -- Susan.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, Neil, a new reality that inflation is higher and may stay elevated for some time. So, today's wholesale prices, or the inflation that producers pay, still much higher than a year ago, sure, coming down from the previous month, but couple that with yesterday's consumer price jump and we're still close to those 40-year highs.\par \par \uc2 And that's more than four times, by the way, the level that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  thinks is healthy for the U.S. economy. And that's why we have a growing number of Wall Street now, close to a third, predicting a full 1 percentage point rate hike next week from the Central Bank.\par \par \uc2 And that would be the biggest single increase in interest rates since 1984, when Paul Volcker was Fed chair. And before yesterday's inflation report, that probability was at zero.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, you have the world's richest man, Elon Musk, weighing in. He actually thinks the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  should cut interest rates instead, and concerned that prices are going negative or in deflation, instead of inflation, because of the drop in lumber, copper oil and other commodities.\par \par \uc2 Now, no one on Wall Street is predicting a cut in rates until maybe next year. The stock markets didn't really recover much from yesterday's worst sell-off in two years. You also have more job cuts that were announced today by big technology companies. And that's why some are calling this the start to a white-collar jobs recession.\par \par \uc2 Twilio\uc2 , the cloud communications company, joining Snap, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and even \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  in laying off workers. And despite the reset that we did see in stock markets, Neil, there isn't really much panic just yet. Average investors like me and you are actually buying. They bought $2 billion worth in discounted stocks yesterday.\par \par \uc2 And that is the second biggest day in average Joes buying in so far this year in 2022.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Susan, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 And then this other development which could be moving markets, the fear of a nationwide railway strike on Friday. For \uc2 Amtrak\uc2 , it's already taken place. We're just learning right now that it has canceled all long-distance trains scheduled for Thursday, ahead of a potential freight rail shutdown. That's because \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  owns a lot of the same tracks that are affected by that freight.\par \par \uc2 The commuter rail service's heavily used Northeast Corridor will largely be spared of these cancellations, we're told. But among some of the longer routes, specifically those that have trains that go from Washington to Sanford, Florida, or the so-called Capitol Limited that serves Washington to Pittsburgh, or the routes Washington to Chicago and the Palmetto south of Washington, bottom line is, that goes as we wait to see whether a strike is coming at all.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember, this is happening ahead of this Friday development, where both sides hope to hammer out an agreement, some agreement, but they're far apart. What it will likely mean is, you will have 115,000 rail workers out on strike. Already, one key union has already said, we don't like what we're hearing, we don't like the original deal, and we're not part of it.\par \par \uc2 But they're still talking, so hope still prevails, the impact with Gary Kaltbaum and Larry Glazer, close money watchers both.\par \par \uc2 Gary, this is yet another wild card for the economy. I mean, just as we're hoping to come out of the worst of inflation, even though we don't see a lot of evidence of that, along comes of what could be a freight shutdown that will further gum up the supply chain. It's a mess. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: In an already fragile environment, if we're down for one week, think shortages, think higher prices, think spoiled crops, empty shelves, and the band will play on worse and worse.\par \par \uc2 We have already seen and, for me, surprisingly, yesterday a real bad inflation number, considering commodity inflation pretty much topped in June, and real stunning. And the market spoke loud and clear. So if you add this to it, I'm not so sure markets are going to be too happy. And I'm hoping cooler heads prevail, because, again, one week without this, bad news in this type of environment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Larry Glazer, the president said, I believe, yesterday the markets are not the economy. He obviously is trying to send a message, I don't obsess over this, with a veiled reference to his predecessor.\par \par \uc2 But the markets kind of are the economy, aren't they?\par \par \uc2 LARRY GLAZER, MAYFLOWER ADVISORS: You know, Neil, Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  warned us, as the American people, that there was more pain ahead.\par \par \uc2 For him, there could be no gain on the fight against inflation without the pain of higher interest rates, the pain that we saw yesterday in the worse- than-expected inflation report. But, of course, the economy is a reflection of sentiment. It's a reflection of how we feel.\par \par \uc2 And when tens of American -- tens of millions of Americans are behind on their utility bills, behind on rent, when you see mortgage rates doubling since the beginning of the year, of course, it's going to affect housing, which is people's single biggest asset, right? It's going to affect construction. It's going to reverberate through the economy.\par \par \uc2 You're already seeing higher auto loans, Neil, and that, of course, is going to slow the auto market. You're seeing delinquencies in autos, and you're seeing that white-collar recession that we talked about, that jobs recession. It began with technology, high-paid skilled jobs of the future that we want to be investing in. And now it's on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 Neil, you know it's bad when \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  is eliminating free coffee. I mean, that's just the tip of the iceberg. And it's going to get worse if we don't deal with this pain sooner rather than later. And it starts with Washington with a wakeup call that the spending they're doing is contributing to the pain that the American people are feeling.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I keep going back, gentlemen, to this potential rail strike here, and the fear that it's going to add to the price pressure.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, Gary, you kind of touched on it. But it's also coming at a time where a lot of retail stores are trying to tell their customers, the better part of valor might be to go out and get your Christmas presents now, because there's no guarantee they will get there in time if you wait.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you make of that, Gary, and the signal it could be Sunday?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Well, it is the signal, because, if this happens, if this is shut down for weeks, you're going to see a real rush to get things done, get holiday shopping early. It's going to move the needle. It'll get the -- I guess the economy moving better for a week or two.\par \par \uc2 But it all -- it just takes away from what happens going forward. And, again, I must say that the big word here is fragile. I think we're about as fragile as can be. Not enough talk is about savings rates are plunging, credit card usages skyrocketing, and the consumer being 70 percent of the economy. That's real trouble going forward.\par \par \uc2 And if the markets and the housing markets, which I really predicted, and it's coming down, the reverse wealth effect goes really into gear into the downside. You have a vicious cycle coming and it's going to get pretty bad if this continues and things do not get better sooner, rather than later.\par \par \uc2 So, again, hopefully cooler heads prevail, because this would be real bad news.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gentlemen, I want to thank you both.\par \par \uc2 We're getting a little bit more details on exactly why 5,000 railway workers represented by the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  rejected this.\par \par \uc2 They said that the tentative contract agreement does not do enough to recognize the sacrifices that rail workers are making. So they were among the first to reject this. They're still talking, as I say, but the question is whether Washington has to intervene to stop this.\par \par \uc2 Who better to ask than Senator \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2 , the Republican Senate -- Republican Conference chair, I should say, Wyoming Republican?\par \par \uc2 Very good to have you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There is talk right now that Washington could intervene here, that there could be a move to stop this, and that Congress could do that. It's a little murky. But how do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, I think it's important that we try to avoid this strike.\par \par \uc2 Neil, the impact on the economy is about $2 billion a day, so about $15 billion a week. It will hit supply chains. And the president ought to be very involved here. The president touts his ability as a dealmaker and he tells his influence, his push that he has with the unions and how they listen to him.\par \par \uc2 As you just reported, it's one of the main unions trying to prevent a deal that so many have already agreed to. The president ought to get involved.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right now, I'm just wondering, if we get to that point, though, Senator, where workers leave, and you have 115,000 across the country, this was thought to be at first something only you had to worry about if you were freight, were waiting for freight.\par \par \uc2 Now we know it's going to affect passengers as well on so many of these \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  lines that serve as both. I'm wondering what you think the impact is going to be.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, the impact is going to be dramatic.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about moving products from farm to the store to the markets. We're talking about moving energy. All of those things, if we don't get them moving, it's going to increase prices, add to the inflation. We already got terrible inflation numbers yesterday.\par \par \uc2 I think this is going to add to the problems that we're facing now as a nation under this administration. This needs to be resolved. I'd like to see it resolved quickly. But what people are concerned about most at home with me this past weekend in Wyoming are the cost of everything, and specifically food.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator, I was thinking of your state. It's a big energy state.\par \par \uc2 The president is still backing up that plan to freeze oil and gas leases, just as his counterpart in Great Britain, the new prime minister, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 , is talking about expanding energy production off the North Sea.\par \par \uc2 So that's something he's not open to doing. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, I think that this president and this administration and the Democrat Party, their religion is climate.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came in off and said he's going to shut down all fossil fuels. He's done it with \uc2 Keystone\uc2 . He has now frozen oil and gas leasing. He's even going after leases that have been sold to explorers for energy in the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 And it seems that he is trying to take us from energy dominance to energy dependence. Americans know what they want. We want energy that is affordable, number one, is affordable and available and reliable. But the climate extremists, the radicals that are running the Democrat Party, they don't care about any of those things, certainly not affordability.\par \par \uc2 What they want is energy that is renewable. That takes precedent over everything, no matter the cost or the consequences. And, as a result, people are going to be paying more for energy to heat their homes, cool their homes, to move their vehicles, and certainly to keep the lights on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator, finally, we talk about this inflation and this backdrop that isn't great. We have a wholesale inflation report, which is prices before they get to us, still stubbornly high. And the only game in town appears to be sir, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The expectation is that, next week, it could raise interest rates another three-quarters-of-a-point, maybe a full point. What do you think of that? Are you worried they could be overdoing it?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, that's always a possibility.\par \par \uc2 The -- you always worry, how do you calm down the inflation? How do you get those prices down? I know what I would do, and it's, I'd cut this reckless Washington spending, unleash American energy, and cut Washington regulations.\par \par \uc2 But the headline in Wyoming, to me, tells the story of inflation, and it's that food prices continue to soar. And it's the list of things that people use on a daily basis. It's bread. It's milk. It's eggs. It's meat. It's chicken, fruit, vegetables. And we're seeing prices in the last one year, Neil, that have gone up faster than in the last 40 years.\par \par \uc2 These are the things that people worry about. And, in eight weeks, they're going to be voting on that. And that's why I'm very optimistic about our opportunities to take back the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and take the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator Barrasso, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We will watch this closely.\par \par \uc2 We are also watching the ongoing talks. Right now, they're essentially being monitored, hosted, if you will, by the labor secretary, \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2 . Again, he is trying to get the two sides together, but they are very, very far apart.\par \par \uc2 And as I told you at the outset here, \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  is already moving to shut down some very lengthy passenger routes, even though this is a freight issue. As we warned you yesterday, it was distinctly possible it could spill over to those just trying to get home, albeit a long distance from their home. We will keep an eye on that.\par \par \uc2 Also keeping an eye on how the administration is responding to this and the odd things it has been doing lately to respond, maybe in a vacuum, to the reality that we're all facing. Maybe a trip today to tout electric vehicles was the latest example. We will connect, you decide.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, maybe another case of awkward timing on the part of the president of the United States, yesterday touting his inflation relief measure, at a time we learned that inflation wasn't relieving. It was still a big problem, and the market was tanking, today out in Detroit heralding electric vehicles on the same day we learned that the national average for an E.V. is north of $66,000.\par \par \uc2 If they say timing is everything, sometimes, it can be, well, everything around a disaster.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich at the White House with more -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The president test-drove a $63,000 electric vehicle and said it made him feel hopeful. But Americans might not be feeling quite the same way. A new Gallup poll showed that voters are far more concerned about inflation and the economy than they are about climate.\par \par \uc2 And those are concerns that the president dismissed, including yesterday's hotter-than-expected inflation report and the stock market sell-off, on his way out of town.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The stock market doesn't necessarily reflect the state of the economy, as you well know. And the economy's still strong. Unemployment is low. Jobs are up. Manufacturing is good.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it's -- I think we're going to be fine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: In Detroit, Biden announced $900 million in funding from the infrastructure law to build E.V. charging stations on highways across 35 states.\par \par \uc2 The White House wants half of all new cars sold in the U.S. to be electric by 2030. But they can't say when their investment in semiconductors will help those cars come to market. And then there's the issue of charging them.\par \par \uc2 Energy Secretary \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  said the U.S. should look to California as the model for clean energy. But they just asked their residents not to charge their E.V.s amid a brutal heat wave.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We invest $7 billion to make American car companies and have the batteries and other critical materials they need.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Now, even if those kinks get worked out, buying an electric car is just too expensive for most Americans dealing with inflation right now.\par \par \uc2 Republicans again calling the White House out of touch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL, CHAIR, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: It's death by 1,000 cuts for a family who is getting hit at every single level and their budget is taking a beating and they're wondering, how am I going to make ends meet?\par \par \uc2 It's not just gas anymore. It's everything. And Democrat policies caused this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president is now at a DNC fund-raiser before heading back to D.C. -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jacqui Heinrich, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Phil Wegmann right now, RealClearPolitics.\par \par \uc2 Phil, it's awkward. That's the best you could say about it. Others in an extreme case might say a tin ear, the president trumpeting his so-called Inflation Reduction Act that doesn't really quite reduce inflation. Maybe that's the hope down the road. Little evidence of it now, today going off to Detroit to tout electric vehicles, which are fine.\par \par \uc2 But in this environment and all these other pressing issues, does it look like another tin-ear moment?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEGMANN, REALCLEARPOLITICS: Well, did you see that hype video that the Texas A&M football team put out? It was right before the Aggies loss to the lowly Appalachian State football team.\par \par \uc2 And, until yesterday, I think that that ranked as probably the worst premature spiking of the football, other than what we saw from the White House yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: The president wanted to tout his Inflation Reduction Act. And what was in the lower right hand corner of the screen in every cable news segment there was? It was a stock market ticker showing how the Dow was plummeting, this, of course, after non-encouraging report on inflation.\par \par \uc2 And, look, maybe the Inflation Reduction Act five, 10 years down the road, does what it's supposed to. But I think that a lot of American consumers are not planning for the next half-decade. They're thinking about, how are they going to get through the next week or month?\par \par \uc2 And so, when you see rhetoric like this come out of the White House, it's no surprise that President Biden is underwater when it comes to his polling on the economy. If you look at the RealClearPolitics average, about 60 percent of Americans disapprove of how he's handling the economy.\par \par \uc2 They don't think that everything is fine.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I don't blame everything on him personally, maybe his staff, but someone has got to be reading the room, reading the world, reading the polls, reading the Dow, reading the economic inflation numbers, maybe telling him yesterday, you know, boss, when that CPI number came out, today might not be the day to herald inflation getting under control, further, after the big sell-off we had yesterday to be going off to Detroit to tout electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 I just wonder what's going on there, because I think of presidents like JFK after the Bay of Pigs disaster. He kind of cleared his books of any Rose Garden-type ceremonies just to make sure that the gravity of that the next day and what a disaster it turned out to be, he was not unaware of it. In fact, he later took ownership of that disaster.\par \par \uc2 The same with \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  after the 1987 stock market crash in October. He sort of cleared the decks. There were no sort of Rose Garden events to celebrate anything, outside of his saying that he still had confidence in the American economy and the markets. But there's none of that going on now here with this White House.\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: No one told the president not to invite James Taylor to play "Fire and Rain" in the -- out in the Rose Garden the other day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And of all James Taylor's songs, why "Fire and Rain"? Why not "You've Got a Friend" or -- I digress. But go ahead.\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: But, I mean, I think that the White House, they're obviously bullish because of some of the accomplishments that they have had this summer.\par \par \uc2 But, again, whether it's the Inflation Reduction Act or some of these executive actions that the president has taken, those are all things that, if they do have the intended effect, are going to have consequences down the road, not immediately. And to your point about electric cars, I mean, I think it's -- obviously, a great photo-op for the first president who's truly a motor head.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  loves cars. But that electric sports car, that Corvette is not something that the average American is going to be able to afford during the best of times, let alone now, right now.\par \par \uc2 And Jacqui teed this up really well. You have the energy secretary in California saying that that state is leading the way when it comes to clean energy. And yet Governor Newsom is encouraging a lot of his citizens who bought electric cars to cut back on charging them.\par \par \uc2 When I pressed the White House on whether or not President Biden thinks California is the way to lead, they didn't comment in the Briefing Room.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, they did not. That old line, just read the room, read the room, politicians on the left and right, or at least successful ones in the White House, have gotten that much down.\par \par \uc2 Phil, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 And you might want to just sort of read the images that we're getting out of this 10-day-long special remembrance of Queen Elizabeth II paving the way for her formal funeral next Monday, and the hope that maybe, maybe the family is coming together, including the brothers, and everything is copacetic and happy.\par \par \uc2 Enter Piers Morgan to tell us, don't believe what you'