lashing out at their family all the time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Charlie Lankston on that, The Daily Mail Femail editor, world watcher extraordinaire.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, you heard what Piers was saying. They're putting on a show. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE LANKSTON, FEMAIL EDITOR, DAILY MAIL: I mean, I agree with him.\par \par \uc2 I don't think that things behind the scenes are particularly cordial. I don't think that Harry and William are sitting down for chats by the fireside. But what I do think they are united in is their understanding that this time is about honoring the incredible life that their grandmother led, her incredible 70-year reign.\par \par \uc2 And neither of them are going to let any of their personal issues get between them really being a part of this incredible celebration of the queen's life. It's just not worth it. But what remains to be seen is whether or not that kind of joint belief in the importance of honoring the queen will continue after her funeral is done on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Will Harry release his book? In my opinion, it would make sense for him to tear it up and get rid of it altogether. But sources say to us that he's still very much planning on publishing it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that's $20 million you would blow away if you did that, and, of course, no guarantees the publisher would let him do that.\par \par \uc2 He could and we're hearing that he could add chapters or some follow-ups here in endnotes post his grandmother's death. But, even that, I don't know if that's going to heal all wounds here, if this book goes out as planned.\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Well, and if the book is as tell-all and as scandalous as we have been led to believe, even if he did write a tribute chapter or two about his grandmother, presumably, any experiences that he would write about from his time in London would include the difficult relations that have gone on between him and his brother and the rest of the royal family.\par \par \uc2 He would likely also raise the issue of him not being allowed to wear a military uniform, when his uncle, Prince Andrew, has been given special permission to wear a military uniform for the final vigil in a few days. So, I think there are even more complicated issues that could come into play, even if he decided to include chapters about the past couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, real quickly, is \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 , is he the one forcing his sons, get along right now, and I want to see playing a family party here, and you got to do it?\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: You know, I don't think it's a case of \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  saying, you have to get on.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, these two men are men. They are not young boys anymore. However, I think that the family has come together as a whole and made very clear amongst all of them the importance of these few days, that the focus remain on the queen, on celebrating her legacy, and not on family drama.\par \par \uc2 And that goes for Andrew, not just Harry.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well put. Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Lankston, The Daily Male Femail editor following these latest developments out of Great Britain.\par \par \uc2 By the way, they're expecting close to 200 world leaders to go to this event, including the president of the United States. We will keep you up to date on that.\par \par \uc2 Also keeping you up to date on what's happening at the border. By the way, it's not secure, especially when you hear what's happening with all the drugs getting by, not under control, not even close -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the vice president might be saying that things are secure at the border, but the latest evidence and video we have been seeing all this week, courtesy our own Bill Melugin, by the way, is anything but.\par \par \uc2 And now even some Democrats are 