lation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They were crowing not too long ago, Sheriff, about some fentanyl that was seized at the border.\par \par \uc2 But as I think your office had mentioned, it was but a fraction of the fentanyl that's out there and getting through there.\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: We have seen incredible increases. And it's killing our population as well. We have had a 433 percent increase from 2019 to 2021 in fentanyl-related deaths in our community, just in our community alone.\par \par \uc2 So we used to -- in 2019, we were getting just small quantities of this stuff, but now we're seizing kilos and kilos. And a kilo is worth about up to 500,000 potential overdoses. It's an epidemic. It's the worst thing that I have seen in my career.\par \par \uc2 I thought methamphetamines would be the worst that I would ever encounter, but fentanyl is just -- it's just much, much worse.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, we talk about all the migrants who get through the border, sir, but we don't talk about all the drug and fentanyl and other forces with those drugs that are sneaking them through and getting through the border as well.\par \par \uc2 Do you had any idea and how many there are?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: Well, it's -- there's three different elements that come through.\par \par \uc2 We have got not only the drugs that we're talking about, but the human smuggling and the human trafficking. The human -- what you see, those large groups of people that have been captured at the border, they have just given up. They have just walked up and given out.\par \par \uc2 What you're not seeing are, they were the diversionary techniques. So, there are drugs going across. And that's how it's coming through, is because there's too many -- too many migrants for the resources to be able to process. Social services are overwhelmed. Everyone is overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 And so that provides the diversionary tactic for the cartels.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sheriff, thank you. We need to hear what you were telling us. Very good seeing you, sir.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, giving you an update on this rail strike that is looking more and more likely by that Friday night deadline.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock on that -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: See all those shipping containers back there, Neil? There are going to be a whole lot more of them when get this strike. And it looks likely.\par \par \uc2 I will have the latest bad news in "Your World" in just a moment. Stand by.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, wait. It might not be just freight. We're learning right now that a possible rail strike could affect a lot of passengers as well.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock has the latest from Philadelphia -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  guys too, they run on the freight to rails, and so \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  now canceling its long-term -- or long-haul routes, the Zephyr, California Zephyr, the Empire Builder, all of those canceled now. And that's in addition to the freight issues.\par \par \uc2 Maybe you see the marine terminal here on the banks of the Delaware River. Go ahead, Craig, and give them a look, as we tell you the latest bad news, which is no good progress in the talks today, with the two holdout unions.\par \par \uc2 And the further bad news is that one of the unions that had reached an agreement put it to their membership, and they today rejected it. They did agree not to strike for two weeks, but the other unions say the strike is still on. And it's at the worst possible time.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at these numbers, Neil. On demand for freight last month, a widely watched index says it was up almost 4 percent, to the highest level in four years. And more is coming. It's harvest time. You know how much Corning, wheat and soybeans gets shipped on the rails these days? -- 630,000 carloads of corn, and 300,000 of wheat, 250,000 of beans.\par \par \uc2 Not able to 