s held, 3,000 miles. But, look, Russia still had about 20 percent of Ukrainian territory. But they have lost a lot of equipment. They have lost -- the Russians have a lot -- real good units have been shattered. The morale is bad on the Russians' part.\par \par \uc2 The leadership is bad on the Russians' part. And Putin needs to be concerned. But I will tell you, here's the key, though, Neil, and I really believe this. Look to the south. That's where this whole war is going to be decided, around Kherson, around Mykolaiv.\par \par \uc2 If the Ukrainians can push and they can move those offensives down there, and they cut off Crimea from the Russian offensive that started a few months ago, and they can hold on to that and then put penetrations into Crimea, then I think the Russian army falls.\par \par \uc2 And I think, after the Russian army falls with a strategic defeat, Putin falls as well. So, if I was Ukraine right now, if I was advising them, I would say, I got -- kind of got it in the north around Kharkiv. Now make a big effort in Mykolaiv and around Kherson. Try to close that gap, cut off Crimea, and then take Crimea and take a part of that nation back, Ukraine, that was given up in 2014.\par \par \uc2 I think that that's when you see the end of Putin.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think that China -- this is the first meeting between \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  since before the war.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How do you think China is handling this, or how much \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  himself is saying, all right, forget it, Vlad, find a way to get out of this?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Well, I think -- I think President Xi is very happy he doesn't speak Russian and it has to be translated.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: So he can be very circumspect.\par \par \uc2 But I think he's looking at it as an economic level. He's not helping him at all militarily. I think it was a bad handshake deal now, and Xi knows that. But Xi's got a longer game. He's helping him, obviously, economically with the purchase of oil from Russia.\par \par \uc2 But I think -- I would hope he would caution Putin about what he's engaged in, in Ukraine. But I don't think Putin really cares. But I think they're going to be a little bit of hands off with China, and -- because China is very pragmatic.\par \par \uc2 When I listened to both of them under the Trump administration -- we'd be on calls with either Putin or was Xi -- Xi was always very, very circumspect, always very pragmatic. And he was almost a little bit aloof. And I think he's going to be that way with Putin. They may be meeting, but Xi is going to kind of step back a bit and kind of tell Putin, you got a problem. You got to handle this. I'm not going to get involved with it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, real quickly, General, I always get the idea -- you're the expert -- but that Xi is almost like ashamed of the guy, that he's like, really, you -- it's gotten to this?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I think you're right. I think that's what I said about the bad handshake at the Olympics. This was a mistake. And, look, the -- I think the entire world, 95 percent of it, thought the Russians were going to roll right over Ukraine. And I'm sure that Xi thought the same thing.\par \par \uc2 He now sees a very weakened Russia. He sees a very weakened Putin. And he doesn't want to get his hands dirty with this, because he realizes the global implications of a lot of nations now pushing back against Russia, and he wants to kind of stay out of it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: May be a wise strategy.\par \par \uc2 General, thank you very much, and for your service to this incredible country. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes. Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, corner of Wall and Broad, we were up 30 points today, 30 points that was, for a while, down 200, up 200. But you will take what you can get, still, after yesterday's drubbing, not a huge comeback. We will see what happens tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220915ei9e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Gov. \uc2 Greg Gianforte\uc2  (R-MT): Migrants Climb Border Wall; U.K. Continues 10-Day Mourning Period For Queen Elizabeth; New Hampshire Holds Senate Primary; Rail Strike?; Stocks Sink; Inflation Inches Up\b0\par\par\uc2 Charlie Gasparino, Gerry Baker, Jonathan Hunt, Hillary Vaughn,  Kelly O'Grady, Jackie DeAngelis, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 9,192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let me say that again.\par \par \uc2 In America, we pay more for prescription drugs than any developed nation in the world. And there's no rhyme or reason to that.\par \par \uc2 For years, so many of us have been trying to fix this problem. But, for years, big pharma blocked Medicare from negotiating lower drug practices -- prices.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, I do not want to be a party crasher, but we interrupt this celebration for a hard, cold dose of reality, stocks tanking because inflation is accelerating, the very inflation that the president said he's made great progress addressing, heralding his Inflation Reduction Act as the boon for the economy that we didn't see in a key number out earlier today.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. And FOX on top of a sell-off the likes of which we have not seen in quite some time. In percentage terms, the roughly 4 percent hit in the Dow is the worst we have seen in two years. Ditto the S&P 500 and, again, the Nasdaq, all hit and hit hard, because inflation remains stuck. And no one can get it under control.\par \par \uc2 An 8.3 percent annualized inflation rate in the latest month, the core rate, that is without food and energy, also soaring more than 6.3 percent. This is the fourth 1,000-point sell-off for the Dow just this year. And, again, the precipitant for this was a much-worse-than-expected inflation report.\par \par \uc2 Now, the president is heralding this Inflation Reduction Act that some people said is actually making the situation worse, because, across the board, prices are going up. He's touting the prospect of eventually drug prices going down. But even if he gets that, and it is a Herculean leap, it would be but a fraction of the overall costs that continue to soar.\par \par \uc2 Keep this in mind, that, with inflation running north of 8 percent, most food and related items are running at about double, sometimes triple that. Whatever progress we have seen on gasoline and related prices has done little to affect prices almost everywhere else.\par \par \uc2 And then another startling development here and the market reaction to this, the fear that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is going to continue hiking interest rates, and now not only the three-quarters-of-a-percent hike that they see coming next week, but a growing betting among a third of investors now that that hike could be as much as one full percentage point.\par \par \uc2 Look at it this way. In just the last few months, we have gone from zero percent interest rates to, by the end of the year, likely 4 percent or more. And it could get worse than that, on fears that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is the only game in town to address spending and inflation and the ills that can cause and to keep hiking rates, maybe a lot longer and a lot higher than we ever thought possible.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be getting the latest on how this is politically falling out.\par \par \uc2 First to Jackie DeAngelis on the numbers that no one can ignore, despite a celebration that none of this is happening at all -- Jackie.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 That's right, the market taking this very badly today, the Dow finishing down 1,276 points, as investors took in that 8.3 percent inflation number for August. And they are anticipating or at least factoring in higher chances that the Fed will hike a full point when it meets next week.\par \par \uc2 Now, the CPI number ticked down year over year from 8.5 percent, but over 8 percent is still high by anyone's standards and higher than expectations for this particular report. Furthermore, the core number that you mentioned, that's the number that strips out what are considered the volatile items, food and gas.\par \par \uc2 And that number is the one that the Fed looks at. It ticked up month on month by six-tenths of a percent. That's the wrong direction for investors. Now, things that average Americans need are still costing more. Gas, for example, may be down 26 percent from the June high, but it's still up 25 percent year over year, meat prices still up nearly 7 percent, coffee 17.6 percent, airfare up 33.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 So why does an interest rate hike on the horizon spook investors? Well, it means the cost of servicing debt goes up. And the fear is, to make ends meet, companies will resort to layoffs. Now, if people start getting laid off, consumer spending lags, the economy sputters, and that hurts people. It hurts families.\par \par \uc2 But higher prices, even if they're not moving that much higher from here, are still painful for many Americans, as there is increasing evidence that consumers are cutting back on those discretionary items just to pay for their essential needs -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jackie, thank you, I think.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's review where we stand here and the fear of interest rates backing up. We have already seen a two-year Treasury note, which is a barometer for the direction of short-term interest rates in general, that is closing in at 3.8 percent. Now, some mortgages, not all, are attached to that.\par \par \uc2 But 10-year notes were also backing up. In other words, interest rates across the board, over which the Fed has control and many others over which it does not, are backing up to the degree we not seen in the case of, let's say, a two-year note -- we're showing a 10-year note right now -- but something we have not seen since 2007, in the case of the 10-year, years itself.\par \par \uc2 Now, the 10-year is an interesting one, because a lot of mortgages are attached to that. And going into today, a 30-year fixed-rate mortgage was hovering just under 6 percent. Mortgage rates have been climbing. This guarantees they will climb some more.\par \par \uc2 What worries people is, we don't know where this ends, and that the administration doesn't seem to have an eye on the problem, instead, celebrating an Inflation Reduction Act in an environment where inflation isn't being reduced at all. It's getting worse, and it could get prohibitively more so.\par \par \uc2 To \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , the former White House Economic Council chair for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kevin, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Across the board, this was a worrisome inflation report. And I'm just wondering if that's resonating, because I don't see it at the White House today.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT\uc2 , FORMER CHAIRMAN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Right\par \par \uc2 I think that what you're seeing in markets is really a response both to the inflation report and to what's going on at the White House. The fact is that getting inflation under control, it's easiest when you have got fiscal and monetary policy working together. And, right now, the administration is clearly in denial about the inflation that's spinning out of control.\par \par \uc2 But let's just put it in the simplest possible terms. If you look over the last 12 months, inflation north of 8 percent has made it so that the typical American family has to spend $460 a month more than they spent last year to get the same stuff. And if they don't have higher wages, if they don't have that $460, then their consumption goes down, GDP goes down, and so on.\par \par \uc2 And that's why we're in a recession. If you look at the numbers, we have got two negative quarters. And with inflation this high, and that kind of $460 demand on America's consumers, it doesn't look like there's any turnaround coming soon.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, I think that the market is probably 50 percent down because of the inflation report and 50 percent down because of the like, really, really Kafkaesque picture of what's going on at the White House today.\par \par \uc2 I never, ever at CEA would have allowed the president to celebrate the change in inflation on a day when the CPI was coming out. It's just a recipe for the kind of disaster we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm thinking, Kevin, if \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  had done this on October 19, 1987, when the Dow lost a quarter of its value in one day, and was ending with a Rose Garden celebration of the economy, it would be, shall we say, awkward.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, this plan is going ahead and the president is still Trumping the economy and his Inflation Reduction Act. It's just out the chute. But if it's like any other spending initiative here, it will have quite the opposite effect and the environment that he ignores, he does so at his own peril, does he not?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 And, again, to go back to the thing that you mentioned before about the Fed -- you and I have talked about this on your show recently -- that the -- I think \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  clearly signaled that he's serious about inflation. Inflation, as we see today, is not going down anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 And so I think what that means is that not only is the Fed going to give us -- I think they will give us three-quarters-of-a-point at the next meeting, but I think they're going to do that at least two more times this year. And they have to do that because...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they're the only game in town, right, Kevin? They're the only game in town.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So they're going to respond to this by continuing to hike rates, and maybe a lot more than we earlier thought.\par \par \uc2 They have already indicated a couple of weeks ago that they're more likely to stay at these levels and see how things go after that. But I'm wondering -- and you said, if you were advising a president or President Trump, when you worked for him, with a backdrop like these markets not to have an event like this president is having, I'm wondering.\par \par \uc2 This president, we're told, those around him do not focus on the markets, not very keen on obsessing with them. Many said \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was and did. But the fact of the matter is, you can't ignore them either. And you can't dismiss what they seem to be telegraphing.\par \par \uc2 What do you say?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right. I agree with that.\par \par \uc2 And I think that, if you go back and look, when I was appearing out in front of the White House during the COVID crisis, and we were talking about what we're going to do to offset the shutdowns, and signaling that GDP was going to decline -- decline by the most since the Great Depression way before that number came out, we were doing that because people, markets want to see that the Treasury secretary and the White House understand what's going on in the economy and they have a plan to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 Right now, what we're seeing is this weird counterfactual celebration about an Inflation Reduction Act that's not going to reduce inflation, while inflation is spinning out of control. And, frankly, that kind of disconnect is something I have rarely ever seen in economic policymaking.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think that we're anywhere near a bottom even today, once people really start to digest what's going on, both in that report, that the Fed has to lift rates at least probably three more times to get ahead of the curve on this, and the White House isn't going to help them at all.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I do wonder where we go from here, though, because the one thing that this economy has that going for it, and still does, not to the degree it did, is a very strong job market.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, we have been seeing more layoff announcement, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  the latest a hint of maybe a few hundred folks losing their jobs as soon as next week. But we're beginning to see it in companies like 3M. Again, the job growth is still the story, but it's not as promising a story. In other words, that is slowing.\par \par \uc2 So that gives us a bit of a cushion here. But in that strength comes a consumer that so far is still willing to spend more, but there's a limit, isn't there?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right. And you hit actually the nail on the head from some data that we haven't discussed yet that just came out.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that's happened, right -- like, so think about, like, where do you get that $460 so that you can keep consumption where it was a year ago? A lot of people don't have that kind of money. And one of the things that has happened over the first half of this year is that consumption has been held up by all of the stimulus monies that were sent to people.\par \par \uc2 But there's clear evidence in the latest data that we get from the Fed that net worth is down about $6 trillion over the last quarter and that debt, consumer indebtedness, is increasing rather rapidly. And that suggests that the cushion that people had from the money left over from stimulus checks is gone.\par \par \uc2 And, once again, if you got to spend $460 more to get the same consumption, if you don't have the $460, your consumption goes down. And that's when you're basically in recession. So we're looking at really negative numbers coming.\par \par \uc2 And the final thing is, going back to the Jackson Hole meeting, the most important academic paper there basically said that inflation gets out of control when people are convinced that the fiscal authorities, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats, but governments like them around the world, are not going to do anything about the government debt and they're just going to sit back and pray that the Central Bank inflates the government debt away.\par \par \uc2 And I think that what you're seeing today is a really, really negative celebration in that regard, because we're basically telling people, hey, we're going to spend money like crazy, and we're going to call it inflation reduction. And that's the worst possible thing that you could signal the markets right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And a number of former Democratic Trump administration consultants in administrations that were not Republicans have been saying pretty much the same.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I do want to bring your attention back, for those who just tuned in, we had about a 1,276-point falloff in the Dow today, all the major averages, including the S&P 500 and the Nasdaq, falling at or more than 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 We have not seen a single day hit like that since June of 2020 for all the averages. In point terms, this represents for the Dow, at least, the seventh biggest point drop in the history of trading. Now, again, these numbers are not like they used to be when I talked about 1987. At that time, the Dow fell about 508 points. As big a number as that seemed, it was even bigger in percentage terms.\par \par \uc2 It represented about a quarter of the Dow's value. As I said, today, it represents under 4 percent, this size falloff today. So, in percentage terms, it's eye-popping, but not as eye-popping as it was back in 1987. Nevertheless, people are looking at these developments as a sign that inflation might be running out of control, or it will take a lot longer to get it under control.\par \par \uc2 And the effect is very, very clear. We have already seen going into the day that mortgage applications are now at a 22-year low. It doesn't take much for people to stop looking around for homes or applying for mortgages or to refinance the ones they're already in. So it has a spillover effect. And that does stymie activity.\par \par \uc2 Then there's the issue of outside aberrant events over which really few people had any control, like supply chain disruptions, like a potential rail strike, and then you start talking real problems.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady on that possibility -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good to see you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, if a strike happens, we could see more supply chain issues. We could see more inflationary pressures. As of now, there are three union holdouts. That equates to about 60,000 workers. It could be over 100,000 if the rest were to strike in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 Now, to that end, railroad executives confirm that they are following protocol. They're beginning to halt transport of hazardous materials to avoid sensitive cargo like fertilizer chemicals left unattended. Now, beyond safety, a new report estimating a nationwide strike would cost $2 billion a day, require an additional 467,000 long-haul trucks on the road.\par \par \uc2 At today's White House briefing, the press secretary emphasize just how wide-reaching a strike could be to industries and Americans if it comes to that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: A shutdown would have a tremendous impact on our supply chains. It is not something that we want. It risks harming families. It risk farming -- harming businesses and whole communities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: But she also went on to tout trucking as a contingency the administration is exploring.\par \par \uc2 Now, recall that 467,000 figure I mentioned before in order to make up the rail. The industry is already short 80,000 drivers and the trucks to support the shift just don't exist.\par \par \uc2 Now, railroad industry experts warn that could wreak havoc, telling FOX Business -- quote -- "Transportation and logistics partners are not positioned to take up the slack in the first half of 2022. More than 70,000 -- 75,000 rail shipments began their journey each day. In the event of a shutdown, these shipments would sit idle."\par \par \uc2 Now, the White House's reportedly engaging in more senior level talks today to avoid a shutdown. But even if there is no strike, Neil, it really underscores the tension between these two top priorities for the administration, right? You have President Biden being very vocal about his support for these labor unions, but that right now is a bit at odds with keeping the nation's transportation infrastructure humming along -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Kelly, I'm curious. And we were talking about this on FOX Business, which, if you don't get, you should demand -- of course, you work on it, Kelly. You know that. You already demand it, you got it, and you're reporting it.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that came up is this idea with a rail strike and whether Americans, worried about shipments and all that kind of stuff getting delayed, should start their holiday shopping, like, now. What are you hearing on that front?\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Yes, I think it's a really good point.\par \par \uc2 And I certainly would urge that. I mean, we're hearing that, if they were to strike come Friday, this could cause a snarl. And we already saw what happened last year. I mean, I was actually reporting on your show last year on December 23, saying, hey, these presents, all of your presents are stuck out in the harbor, and we could see something happen again.\par \par \uc2 But, as I said, the administration is looking to avoid a shutdown. So we will see come Friday whether that is successful.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Kelly, thank you very much for that. And you were ahead of the curve on this, Kelly. We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady on a possible shipping delay on a lot of stuff that could sort of complicate and goo up the whole supply chain issue.\par \par \uc2 One other little footnote I want to make before we continue to show, an anomaly happened today that's actually widened today, the gap between a two-year Treasury note and a 10-year Treasury. Now, without getting wonky here, let me just explain this.\par \par \uc2 You get a higher rate committing it for two years than you do for 10 years. It's quite a wide chasm. And the fear seems to be that that's not a healthy environment, that people are getting near-term nervous. And that seems to signal, not all the time, but enough of the time, that we're going to slow things down, if we haven't already.\par \par \uc2 But with this gap at this rate and among those two different maturities, it almost always presages a recession. It feeds on itself. Put another way, we have an overnight bank lending rate that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will likely next week bring to around the 3, maybe a little north, percent area.\par \par \uc2 Think of that. You are paying more for your money overnight than committing it for 10 years. That is called nervousness, my friends. And it was palpable and widespread and everywhere you look today, every single sector of the S&P 500, every one, down a lot.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether this was an overreaction, probably. Whether it represents real fears that no one can deny, probably. Whether it represents concern that the administration doesn't see the severity of this problem, and maybe just celebrates the fact that it got a key piece of legislation through, probably not good timing. Probably not even close.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. You are watching "Your World."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the president has wrapped up his remarks at the White House celebrating the passage of the Inflation Reduction Act and saying it will prove good things to come, saying that: "Today offers proof that the soul of America is vibrant, the future of America is bright, and the promise of America is real."\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, he never looked to the markets while this was all being planned, because they were tumbling on a clear sort of refuting of all of that, investors in general saying that, sorry, Mr. President, the inflation problem is very real to us. And, in fact, we're quite worried about it, and selling off in droves because of it.\par \par \uc2 And, as a result, the Dow Jones industrial suffered its seventh biggest point hit in its history right now. It was the fourth 1,000-plus-point hit the Dow has absorbed this year. And, of course, if you're looking at it even in percentage terms, which might be a more fair representation, a slide of about 4 percent for the Dow is the steepest we have seen since June of 2020, the same for the S&P 500 and the Nasdaq, which has essentially been cut by a third in this swooning process here.\par \par \uc2 So it's not a very good environment and not one to say that inflation is under control, anything but.\par \par \uc2 My next guest thinks spending is part of that problem, and the president created it, and he's not helping his cause anymore with a promise to forgive student loans to the degree of up to $20,000 for some 40 million students and former students.\par \par \uc2 Greg Gianforte\uc2  is his name. You know him, the governor of the beautiful state of Montana, kind enough to join us. He's co-signed, along with some two dozen of his colleagues across the nation, that this stuff is a bad idea and has to stop.\par \par \uc2 Governor, good to have you have you.\par \par \uc2 This sell-off today might kind of hint of what you just said, enough, cease and desist. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG GIANFORTE (R-MT): Well, you're absolutely right, Neil. And thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the president is partying while the ship burns, the reckless spending. Again, you noted this call from almost half the governors in the country to scrap this student loan bailout. It's the excessive spending that's driving inflation, and it's hitting hardworking Montanans very hard.\par \par \uc2 Just this student loan bailout alone is going to place an additional $2,000 in debt on every Americans' back, whether they went to college or not and whether they have student loan or not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wondering. There are obviously some students in your state that might welcome this. I don't know, Governor.\par \par \uc2 But to your point about whether the president can even do it in the first place, there's a lot of legal challenges already building. But he's sort of doing this by executive edict, will use the debt to pay for it. He brags about the improvement that's come under his administration with lower deficits, but, essentially, we're still paying this with a credit line. It is not paid for.\par \par \uc2 So your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 GIANFORTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, Neil, this is just a political trick just ahead of an election to get attention. But the problem is, not everybody went to college. Not everybody took out loans. Many people responsibly paid those loans back. To shift the burden onto hard working blue-collar Americans to benefit the elites who went to these -- and get advanced degrees is just unfair.\par \par \uc2 And that's why we're calling on the Biden administration to scrap this political shenanigans ahead of the election to try and garner some attention.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, when you hear, Governor -- and maybe you can help me with this -- the administration obviously thinks what it's doing now, and as well with the Inflation Reduction Act, and, before that, I think he was referring to the big CHIPS legislation to help the U.S. semiconductor industry. That did have some Republican support.\par \par \uc2 But the issue seems to be that Republicans complain about all the spending, but the spending is working. Now, we will have a devil of a time seeing that thus far with these inflation numbers, and certainly in the markets. So how do Republicans in general answer that?\par \par \uc2 GIANFORTE: Well, what we're doing is working.\par \par \uc2 We have been -- in Montana, we passed one of the biggest tax cuts in history. We have been eliminating regulations. We think people should keep their hard-earned money. This spending is making every dollar in circulation worth less. We saw today the sell-off in the market is a direct result to additional high levels of inflation, above the pace of wage expansion.\par \par \uc2 So, hardworking Americans are finding their paycheck goes less far every month. We have seen some improvement of gas prices, but we're still significantly above where they have been. It shows up at the grocery store. It shows up in all expenses.\par \par \uc2 And that just makes it harder for hardworking Americans. Again, this is a trick. It's a bailout. It's a shift in the debt to people that didn't incur it. And it's just not fair.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch it very, very closely. And let us know how this goes among your two dozen colleagues right now trying to at least stop the student loan thing, because it's controversial, to put it mildly.\par \par \uc2 But, Governor, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, here, one of the things we're also following right now is the political fallout of all of this. Now, as I said, the president is convinced that this is the wind at his back, and for Democrats in general, that these programs and initiatives he's undertaking are doing the job and are going to make Americans more likely to support Democrats in eight weeks.\par \par \uc2 Then you have to look at the markets. Then you have to look at the prices at the grocery store. Then you have to look at what's happening with the supply chain problems. Then you have to start saying, who says this is good news?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Then there's the border.\par \par \uc2 Some startling video of some camouflaged migrants hopping over, rappelling down, getting into our country, and they still can't find them.\par \par \uc2 It gets worse -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the political fallout from what we just saw today at the corner Wall and Broad and some inflationary prices that are now sort of out of control, and a lot of people are seeing it no matter where they live.\par \par \uc2 Hillary Vaughn on how it's all falling out in New Hampshire, site of a key primary today, the last big one before the midterms in eight weeks -- Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, Democrats are doing the same thing here in New Hampshire's Republican Senate primary that they have been doing in other states, trying to drag down the moderate in the race to try to boost a more controversial candidate they think will be easier to beat in November.\par \par \uc2 Incumbent Democrat sitting Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  voted today. She was asked if she approves of this dark D.C. Democratic money getting in the middle of Republican primaries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): Look, I can't control what outside groups do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: A super PAC aligned with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has spent over $3 million on ads attacking New Hampshire Senate president Chuck Morse and boosting the front-runner in the polls and considered to be more controversial candidate, retired Army General Don Bolduc.\par \par \uc2 Morse is spinning that spending against him in his favor, telling me it's proof he is the bigger threat to \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  in the general.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK MORSE (R), NEW HAMPSHIRE SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I suppose I should thank them today, because it really ticked off the Republicans in New Hampshire. They don't like the Democrats meddling in our primary. And I think what they're going to see today is that worked against them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: But General Don Bolduc voted here in New Hampshire earlier today. And even though Democrats have been spending more money attacking his opponent than he has in this race, he's still not for it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R), NEW HAMPSHIRE SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I think it's a bad strategy. Don't be fooled by the politics as usual, the top-down games that they played to try and take advantage of voters.\par \par \uc2 Granite Staters are awake this time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And, Neil, I asked Senator Hassan who she would rather face in November, Bolduc or Morse. She told me it doesn't make any difference. She thinks that both candidates are extreme, branding them today as extremists -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Hillary Vaughn, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Hillary in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Sarah Westwood right now in Washington, D.C., of The Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, inflation is the backdrop for everything, if you think about it. These pocketbook issues that herald back to \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , it's the economy, stupid, it's the same issue now.\par \par \uc2 SARAH WESTWOOD, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 And, today, we saw Biden sort of reinforcing his biggest liability when it comes to inflation. And that's that he has no credibility to talk about the economy. He has been wrong about inflation, the trajectory of it, from the beginning of his presidency. Voters consistently say they don't trust him to fix this problem. They view him as out of touch.\par \par \uc2 And then the conceit of the White House to schedule an event celebrating his efforts to reduce inflation on the day the CPI numbers come out to show how out of touch his political advisers are that they didn't even anticipate the possibility that the numbers could provide such an unflattering backdrop for this event.\par \par \uc2 And, I mean, just the cherry on top was watching James Taylor play "Fire and Rain" as we watched the stock market numbers crash.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WESTWOOD: I mean, it's like your portfolio is tanking, but here's a free concert. They're just really out of touch on this one.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. I feel they could have at least sung "You have Got a Friend," but no such hope.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The one thing that's kind of interesting, though, Sarah, if you look at it, is that it's back to the credibility issue, no matter where people are politically, between this economic number and the tone-deafness, still having the celebration at the White House, then, a couple of days ago, the vice president of the United States saying that the border was secure, when we have got a record two million migrants freely crossing over, including video day in and day out that shows how freely and easily they're able to do so.\par \par \uc2 There's a huge disconnect going on.\par \par \uc2 WESTWOOD: There's a huge disconnect.\par \par \uc2 And it shows that this is a White House that thinks they can talk their way out of these problems, right? If they just find the right messaging when it comes to immigration, the border, when it comes to inflation, they don't actually have to do anything to solve them.\par \par \uc2 They have policy problems on their hands with both of these issues and a host of other ones, but they're not really talking about what they're going to do to secure the border. And their Inflation Reduction Act does very little to reduce inflation. This was a bad move by the White House today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Sarah, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I want to hop across the pond right now to Britain, where they're still mourning their queen, the latest today, as the royals go through what they have to go through. Remember, they have lost a loved one here, and yet they bear the burden of carrying the world's sympathies on their shoulder as well.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Hunt in London with the very latest -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, it is extraordinary to think that this is Queen Elizabeth's last night in Buckingham Palace behind me here.\par \par \uc2 Her coffin currently lies in the Bow Room, having been brought from Scotland in an RAF, a \uc2 Royal Air Force\uc2 , C-17 aircraft.\par \par \uc2 Accompanying the queen's casket on every step of her final journeys has been the queen's only daughter, Princess Anne, the princess royal, who released a statement today saying it was an honor and a privilege to do so.\par \par \uc2 After the arrival at RAF Northolt, about 15 miles from the palace, the hearse drove the casket through the streets of London, thousands lining those streets to say a final goodbye to Queen Elizabeth, most of them in respectful silence, a few breaking out into applause.\par \par \uc2 And then, just a couple of hours ago, the casket was met here at Buckingham Palace by members of the royal family, including, of course, \uc2 King Charles\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 And, tomorrow, that casket will be loaded onto a gun carriage and a solemn procession will take place from Buckingham Palace to Westminster Hall in the Palace of Westminster, where the queen will lie in state for four full days -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jonathan Hunt, thank you very, very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to my friend Gerry Baker, "Wall Street Journal at Large With Gerry Baker" on FOX Business 7:30 p.m. Fridays on FOX Business.\par \par \uc2 Gerry, I want to tap both your fine British background and your economic impeccable credentials here, because there are a lot of similarities with what's going on economically in Britain with what's going on here, runaway inflation, no clear plan on how to get it under control.\par \par \uc2 But at least, in Britain, there's a new government taking over, a new prime minister, so I guess hope springs eternal here.\par \par \uc2 But I am wondering about how all of this, and the death of the queen, and the fixation of the nation that, rightly so, amid all these other problems...\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, HOST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL AT LARGE": Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... how are the Brits dealing with all of it?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It's a good question, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And it is -- it comes -- as you say, we have an extraordinary situation, a new government, a new prime minister, actually -- and not a new government. She's formed a new government and, of course, a new monarch. As somebody pointed out, a week ago, the British people had a monarch who was a woman and a prime minister who was a man, and now we have monarch who's a man and a prime minister who is a woman.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: So it's an -- it is an extraordinary change.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think there are -- it could go many ways. I mean, there is a great sense of national unity at the moment. I mean, the country has been very divided. The Conservative government is very -- has been very unpopular. But, obviously, this mourning for the queen has brought the country together.\par \par \uc2 And that, to some extent, without being too cynical about it, from a political point of view, that may benefit the government somewhat, as people kind of reconnect with their national identity and maybe they will support it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do you buy, Gerry, the talk that, obviously, there's going to be a national holiday, as there should be, for a formal burial on -- next Monday, but that it's actually slowing the economy down there, to the point where people are doing little else, but, rightly so, focusing on this, to the point you could dip into a recession?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, I mean, the economy is weak already...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: ... like -- again, the U.S. economy, where the U.S. has -- the U.K. has had some pretty weak numbers over the last few months.\par \par \uc2 There is clearly going to be a suspension of spending, because people are glued to their televisions, frankly, and watching -- watching what's going. And Monday, as you say, will be a holiday for the funeral. But typically, historically, when you have that kind of thing happen, it tends to bounce back.\par \par \uc2 So, the tendency, they defer.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. They borrow from the further. Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Exactly. People just defer spending.\par \par \uc2 So I don't think it'll have that much of an immediate impact in terms of the economy. I think the larger, the much larger problems, though, are they have a huge problem with fuel prices, obviously, not quite as bad as the rest of Europe, but seeing a huge increase in natural gas prices, and a lot of people -- that powers a lot of U.K. energy, and that's going to be a huge cost for them.\par \par \uc2 The economy has been very kind of weak for a long time. Productivity has been very, very weak. You have got a new government. They're going to have to spend a lot of money. Interest rates and rising very rapidly, as, again, they are in the U.S. You have got inflation is a big problem, as, again, it is in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 It's a really -- and you have got industrial action, as they call it, strike action, coming up over the next few months. You have already seen it in the rails, in the railroads. You're going to see it in other sectors of the economy, postal workers, various others. It could be, what people are talking about, a winter of discontent.\par \par \uc2 And with a new government, which is not very popular, that could be a real challenge for them, certainly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Back in this country, of course, fears -- we had this huge sell- off today, Gerry, as you know, and fears that it's the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , the only game in town. They're going to continue hiking interest rates. We don't know for how long and how high, just that it's going to be a lot longer and a lot higher than we thought.\par \par \uc2 Or at least that was the reason given for the sell-off. Where do you think we are on that?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: I'm sure that's right.\par \par \uc2 I thought this euphoria -- I'm sorry -- inflation reduction, and what a day to -- what a day to celebrate the Inflation Reduction Act...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: ... when the evidence that we had was that inflation is embedding, unfortunately. That's the real worry here, Neil.\par \par \uc2 As you know, look, gasoline prices have been falling for several -- for several weeks, for I think 12 weeks in a row.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Now, we know broader energy prices have been reasonably stable.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that it's kind of like a relay. The baton is being passed from those energy prices to the rest of the economy, service sector. Wages are going to have to come -- people are going to want to be compensated. You have got 8 -- inflation at 8 percent. Wages are growing up -- growing about 5 percent. People are going to want more. That's going to push costs up for companies.\par \par \uc2 We're really into -- I think we really are into an embedded inflation. And the idea that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  was going to be able to push rates up maybe just to 3, 3.5 percent, and that will take care of it, I thought was always fanciful.\par \par \uc2 And now we have seen, with this core inflation number today very, very high, the very strong likelihood the Fed will -- certainty the Fed will raise rates by another 75 basis points next week.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Maybe more, some betting a full percentage.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Maybe even 100. Yes, even some speculation they make it 100, and they may need to do it again next month.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: We're going to see interest rates go well above 4 percent, I think, before this comes down.\par \par \uc2 And if that doesn't induce a recession, I find it very unlikely -- very hard to know what will. That is very, very likely to push the economy into an all-out recession, because unemployment -- and unemployment going to have to rise to bring inflation...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: ... to bring wage pressures down.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It's -- again, it's an ugly -- an ugly immediate economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And think about that, Gerry. Four months ago, we were at zero percent interest rates, if you think about it, right?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And now up to 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, and look at mortgage rates.\par \par \uc2 Mortgage rates are pushing 6 percent. A 30-year mortgage is getting close to 6 percent. That's going to kill the housing market. Let's be candid about that. It's a tough, tough few months ahead.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But when my wife I got our first mortgage, did I tell you once or twice that we got 13.5 percent, and we thought we were geniuses?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: I can beat you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: I -- my first mortgage went -- was at 10, and it was a floating rate one, and it went to 15 in the space of a year.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, Nostradamuses, we were not. Nostradamuses, we were not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Don't take any financial advice from either of these two guys. We have got nothing, yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're the best, Gerry.\par \par \uc2 Gerry Baker 7:30 p.m. on this fine network of FOX Business, which, if you don't get, you should demand. But, again, if you're smart and on top of this stuff, you're already getting it so you're just checking in to see the principal players.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, big sell-off here, and whether we, by ending at the lows of the day, whether that means we have a bumpy tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We're on top of that with Charlie Gasparino and more -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a big hit in the Dow and the S&P 500 and the Nasdaq, all because we had a big surprise, and not a pleasant one, on the inflation front.\par \par \uc2 Rather than the steady improvement many had been hoping for, we didn't see it, an 8.1 percent inflation rate that was expected for year over year, 8.3 percent. You know the drill after that. The panic ensued. The selling ensued.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino here.\par \par \uc2 You know what's kind of troublesome about this, Charlie, is, we ended near -- at our lows. That's not a good way to end the day. But what did you make of it?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes, although this market is hard to predict what tomorrow is going to bring.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I would just say this.\par \par \uc2 In the near long term, as they say, not 10 years out, but in the next two years, I mean, you got to be pessimistic here. If the Fed really is holding to its 2 percent inflation mandate, it's got a long way to go. And it would have had a long way to go even if we came in at the expectation, which was 8.1 percent, right? That was the expectation. It was at 8.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 This is really troubling. And we had \uc2 Larry Fink\uc2  on, the head of \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , the other -- the other day on FOX Business. He made a good point, that, even if energy prices are abating -- and they are -- everything else is still high, and there's a lot of structural issues with the economy.\par \par \uc2 And the administration keeps spending into the Fed's efforts of sort of trying to rein in the rapid rise in prices. So, it keeps inflating it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you about that, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 That's a very astute point, because one of the things that this administration prides itself on is it never looks to the markets, it never looks at the markets, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was too obsessed with that, and they're not.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This was one of those days where it might have been wise to be.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, Neil, this isn't a market. This is common sense. This isn't a look to -- this isn't a market call. If you have a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  that is saying, listen, I need -- we want to try to arrange what's known as a soft landing, a calming down of the economy, taking price pressures down and trying to avoid a recession, or at least a modest or a stiff recession, you can't be going out spending gazillions of dollars in an already overheated economy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there is no reason for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to be forgiving student loans. There's no reason for him to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they don't see it that way, Charlie. They just don't see it.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, who doesn't?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And they -- even though the history is such -- the administration.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, obviously.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And this isn't a left or right issue, but no spending initiative has ever eased inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, the fact of the matter is that that's just a fact. And doing this in the backdrop of this today, that would concern some people, wouldn't it?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I would say, well, wait a minute, what are you doing?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: It's concerning to \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , who's not exactly a partisan right-winger.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's former Treasury, right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: He's the former Treasury secretary, top economic guru in the Obama and Clinton administration.\par \par \uc2 And he's saying it point blank. We -- there's the wicked price pressures here. We already have an overheating economy. You're throwing fuel on the fire. And you're making the Fed's job almost impossible to do it without arranging a crash landing, not a soft landing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it's definitely -- it's definitely been complicated,\par \par \uc2 Charlie, thank you very, very much. Sorry, with all this breaking news, but thank you for weighing in on these crazy markets, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: OK.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will see how it goes tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, there's still that trouble at the border, but it's getting kind of crazy right now. Some images of migrants climbing over the wall, everyone seeing -- seeing them do it. And then they get on the other side into our country, manage to escape into God knows where. They were never caught.\par \par \uc2 It's just the latest. It won't be the last.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, does this look like a secure border to you?\par \par \uc2 FOX cameras in Arizona capturing camouflaged migrants climbing over a border wall using, a rope to rappel down, and then running into the open desert. I might point out that not a one was caught by Border Patrol. This is a sign of the times. And we're seeing this more often.\par \par \uc2 And doesn't Art Del Cueto know it, National Border Patrol Council vice president.\par \par \uc2 Art, it's amazing. But as you say and keep reminding me, this is more common than most folks think. What's going on?\par \par \uc2 ART DEL CUETO, VICE PRESIDENT, BORDER PATROL UNION: It definitely is.\par \par \uc2 Look, we're looking at over half-a-million got-aways this fiscal year so far. You're seeing the video of those that climbed over and got away. That -- you add that to 165,000 that have gotten away in Tucson sector, it being the most got-aways. Del Rio has got over 150,000 got-aways.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's just -- it's the sign of the times. And it's happening because this administration created that magnet by moving agents from one area to another, when the cartels are constantly looking at what's happening. They're looking at the traffic. The cartels are doing. This administration has created a magnet.\par \par \uc2 And it's -- there's no end in sight because no one's taking it serious right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Obviously are emboldened enough and fully aware of cameras showing their every move to do what they do.\par \par \uc2 And I'm thinking of what the vice president said a couple of days ago, that the border is secure. You're the expert. I'm not. But that doesn't look like a secure border to me.\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: You know, and you don't need an expert to tell you.\par \par \uc2 The American public has seen the video. They know what's going on. At this point, I'm at a loss for words. And I'm not at a loss for words often. But I don't know what the vice president is even thinking by saying that and to keep a straight face and to actually believe you're going to fool the American public with lies about this.\par \par \uc2 They have created the mess. The cartels and the drug individuals are the ones that are flourishing from this. And who's suffering? The United States. You're looking at these groups that have gotten away. And you have got to look back during the Obama administration, when we had so many of those unaccompanied juveniles that came across. Where are they?\par \par \uc2 No one knows where they're at now. The same thing is going to happen many years from now with these individuals that have come across and gotten away. And it's going to happen with the individuals that this administration has released into the United States by bringing back catch and release to this country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And real quickly on those being bused to points north and sanctuary cities, we really have no way to keep track of them, no matter where -- where they go, right?\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: There's really no way that they're going to keep track of them, to be honest.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: They will go to their initial court hearings, but then, afterwards, when they realize they're going to get sent back to their country, they will disappear into the shadows, like President Obama said at the time, the shadows of humanity, and just you will never hear from them again.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So this just gets worse?\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: It gets worse. And it's going to have to take real political -- individuals that really care about the country that are in office to turn this around, to stop it right now.\par \par \uc2 But, unfortunately, we're seeing no end in sight. The floodgates -- the floodgates have been opened, and no one's doing anything about it at this time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Incredible. Art Del Cueto, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Art is with the National Border Patrol Council, the vice president there. He has been warning about this and reminding anyone who is anyone that, look, we have got a problem here, and, right now, it's out of control.\par \par \uc2 All right, as we wrap things up here, some sad news we have to pass along here. \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 , the former independent counsel who led that publicized investigation of \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , he's died, 76 years old. Apparently, he died at Baylor St. Luke's Medical Center in Houston of complications from surgery, this according to his family.\par \par \uc2 You might recall that he also served as solicitor general and a federal judge during a decades-long career, but it was the investigation of the so- called Whitewater scandal that engulfed the Clinton administration and eventually led to the first impeachment of a U.S. president in more than in a century.\par \par \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2  was the one behind that investigation that prompted that entire impeachment affair, dead at age 76.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220913ei9d0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Return to Office?; \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  Becomes New British Prime Minister; Special Master Granted in Mar-a-Lago Case; Midterm Money and Messaging\b0\par\par\uc2 Abby Hornacek, Charlie Gasparino, Gerry Baker, William La  Jeunesse, Karl Rove, Andy McCarthy, David Spunt, Brian Brenberg, Peter  Doocy, Aishah Hasnie, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,568 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Nine weeks out, and the push is on, as Democrats and Republicans battle for votes.\par \par \uc2 Today, a look at the money and the messaging with Peter Doocy at the White House, where President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is touting more spending, Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill, where both sides are just scrambling, GOP pollster Lee Carter on the bread-and-butter issues that are resonating, and our Professor Brian Brenberg on four key days between now and November that could change everything.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. Hope you had a good summer weekend, the last blast, and now the blasts forward to Election Day nine weeks away.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Peter Doocy at the White House -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 We spent some time over the long weekend with President Biden out on the campaign trail as he tried to make the case that he thinks Republicans like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more than they like entitlement programs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The biggest contrast from what MAGA Republicans, the extreme right, the Trumpees, they want to go to Congress, these MAGA Republicans in Congress are coming for your Social Security as well.\par \par \uc2 Now, you -- by the way, as I said, you might think I'm making this -- some of this stuff up, it's so outrageous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He's referring there to a plan by Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  to make Congress vote to reauthorize every spending program every five years in an attempt to cut down government waste.\par \par \uc2 So, now Scott is challenging the premise of the president's claims there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): Of course, he's going to lie about it. What I believe is we got to do everything we can to preserve Medicare, preserve Social Security, make sure these are lasting programs.\par \par \uc2 That's why I believe Congress every year ought to tell the American public what they're going to do to make sure these programs survive. But the Democrats, they won't have any conversation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: For the last five days, the president's focus has been on MAGA Republicans, but, today, with the whole Cabinet gathered together at the White House, he tried to swing things back towards the economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And so this economy that works for the country from the bottom up and the middle out, as I have said from the beginning. I'm not a big fan of trickle-down economics.\par \par \uc2 I find the trickle stops fairly quickly coming down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And one way to tell that we are for sure in the homestretch of the midterm campaign, White House officials are doing something that they usually do when they're trying to get their base really, really excited.\par \par \uc2 They're going to host an event here tomorrow with Barack Obama -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bring out the big guns, right?\par \par \uc2 All right, Peter, thank you very much for that, Peter Doocy.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Aishah Hasnie right now on Capitol Hill, where Republicans are trying to get back on message about inflation, the economy. Sometimes, they have veered off course. How's that going?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there. Good afternoon to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 That's right. So Republicans primarily are still focused on those pocketbook issues, sky-high inflation, and, of course, the economy, high gas prices. And according to former \uc2 RNC\uc2  spokesman Doug Heye, he tells me that Republicans should stick to those pocketbook issues, because a new Pew poll out Neil show 77 percent of registered voters think the economy is still the top voting issue this fall.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's clear though, abortion is energizing Democratic voters, especially women, Neil. A \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis of voter registration in 10. States shows that \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  ruling, it really galvanized female voters, the biggest surge happening in Kansas, where more than 70 percent of the new voters there in the month after the Dobbs decision were women.\par \par \uc2 Here's DCCC Chair \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): Ripping away our constitutional freedom, our reproductive freedom has awakened millions of voters. And we see that in the astonishing comeback in the special elections in New York and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The GOP, though, has not had a firm position abortion as of late, so now we're starting to see Republican Senate candidates in battleground states really lean on their wives to soften their image, especially among female voters, while House Republicans are offering a different strategy altogether.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): We will lean into it. We're the pro-life party. We're going to protect the sanctity of human life. This was a huge win for the precious nature of human life, and I don't think we should shy away from it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chair \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  told the AP the GOP still needs to narrow its message on abortion, offering that the past four Republican presidents believe in exceptions, but also limitation, accusing Democrats of not having limitations on abortion at all -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you very much, Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 So, if you think that Democrats have a problem getting the word about inflation out and trying to say they're on top of it, Republicans aren't getting high marks on it either. So it's sort of like a jump ball.\par \par \uc2 Lee Carter following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Lee, what does it mean when the electorate just seems to have a pox on both parties for the time being?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER, REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: Well, I think the problem is, most Americans don't feel like the government is out to fight for them. I think they're about -- everybody feels like the government is fighting against each other, and nothing's getting accomplished.\par \par \uc2 So you have got this huge group of people that are feeling like both parties have let them down. The Republicans' messaging has really primarily been about how bad things are with inflation. And the problem there is most people already know. What they want to hear is a solution how they're going to make it better.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Democrats are trying to say that they're making it -- things are going to be OK, except that people don't feel that it's OK. So they feel like they're completely out of touch. What the Democrats do have is, they have a message on abortion. And I think it's really important that the Republicans take this seriously, because 65 percent of women are saying that abortion is the number one issue to them.\par \par \uc2 And that's a really big deal, because it was suburban women who really put \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the White House. And that's what could happen in the midterms. And I think what the Republicans need to understand is that this is not about women fighting for abortion. This is about women fighting for freedom.\par \par \uc2 Most women say that this is about them feeling like they have equal rights, protections and freedoms. In many ways, this reminds me of the mask argument, where the Democrats are saying this is all about masks and people not taking care of each other and all of this, when really what a lot of Republicans are saying, this is about fundamental rights that I should make the own -- my own decisions for myself.\par \par \uc2 That's what's happening among women right now. And the Republicans, I think, are missing the mark on how to really bring women in, saying, we're going to be the party of freedom.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I get all of that, Lee, but women also are familiar with these wallet and pocketbook issues. They see it head on, and they're aware that, while gasoline prices have come down, almost everything else has not.\par \par \uc2 They see it everyday at the grocery store. They see it every day they go out. And I'm wondering how Republicans play that and how they counter that.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, I think there's no doubt about it that the number one issue facing Americans is the economy. Nine out of 10 Americans right now feel worse today than they did just three months ago.\par \par \uc2 And some falling gas prices isn't going to change that. You're absolutely correct. But I think what Republicans need to do in this moment is not just talk about how bad it is. Talk about how good it could be if they were to take over, because I think the Republicans are really at risk of not winning the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, if I were to advise the Republicans right now, I would say, look, you need to come up with a three- or a five-point plan on what it is that you're going to do immediately should you win the midterms that is going to make the economy better, that's going to put relief back in the pockets of American people, because all of the policies that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has put forward and has been -- has claimed that they're going to make inflation better, two-thirds of Americans say, I don't think they're going to help me.\par \par \uc2 If the Republicans are the party that are going to help us get financial relief, they're going to be the party that wins the midterms.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fascinating.\par \par \uc2 Lee, thank you very much good. Catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Lee Carter following that, as only she can.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, an alert here on the markets. We are starting a fourth week on the downside for the major averages, the Dow swinging a little bit more than 173 points. I think, by now, you know the drill, fear that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  keeps hiking interest rates, in another couple of weeks, when they meet again, another three-quarters-of-a-point.\par \par \uc2 They don't see it ending. We got a separate report today that showed a little bit of strength in the services sector, which would normally be greeted as the good news it is. But now to a market that's worried about still higher interest rates, it was deemed anything but, that we're going to continue to see those rate hikes and we're going to continue to see frustration about all of that.\par \par \uc2 Brian Brenberg follows all of this very, very closely, the King's College professor in New York City, FOX News contributor, darn good instructor on all things numbers.\par \par \uc2 So, Brian, first off, on the markets, I know there are a lot of key things you're watching, including four key pieces of economic data that you're keeping an eye on.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the markets are a backdrop of concern, I would imagine, to either party, because we're melting away here. Many people argue that the run-up we had was just a bear market rally, and we're back to just the bear market.\par \par \uc2 I don't know where you stand on it, but that is not a great backdrop. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Well, it's not a great backdrop, and especially if you're the party in power, because what it says is, investors are nervous about the Fed raising rates. Why? That slows the economy down.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the whole story right now, Neil, is, how high and how long does inflation stay high, and then how bad does the recession potentially get? And right now, on both of those fronts, there's a lot of uncertainty. I'm watching prices. I'm watching all these inflation reports that are coming up, but the truth is, the prices that matter are already out there. They're already on the store shelves.\par \par \uc2 They're in the rent check. Those prices aren't going to get better come November. So that story is done. That die is cast. I think the Biden administration knows that, which is why they're trying to change the story. They're trying to change the conversation.\par \par \uc2 It's not a coincidence we have seen all these -- all this heated rhetoric, semi-fascism coming up, just a few weeks before the election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: That's a move you make when you feel desperate about the bread- and-butter issue. And, right now, the bread-and-butter issue is still inflation. I agree with Lee on that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 When you start using the fascist term, or even say half-fascist, as if that sort of takes the sting out of it, you have lost the argument.\par \par \uc2 We were just showing there, Professor, the fact that wages have been going up at a better than 5 percent clip, which is great, right, but of course, inflation running up north of an 8.6 percent clip. So the fact of the matter is, we're not keeping pace with that.\par \par \uc2 Americans know that. They see it every day at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yet it's an issue that stands only to get more problematic, the more data we get. You hearken to some inflation reports that are due out, but, before Election Day, we're going to several of them, a couple of more reads on the overall job picture in the United States, and, in the middle of that, the Fed hiking rates a lot and endlessly, it seems.\par \par \uc2 How does that play out?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes, I mean, think about that as a voter.\par \par \uc2 So here you are. You know your wages aren't keeping up with prices. That's not going to change anytime soon. Prices really aren't moderating, especially the ones people care about. And, lookit, gas prices have come down, yes. They're still really, really high.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: So that's the preexisting problems.\par \par \uc2 Now you're looking at interest rate hikes, Neil. I mean, you think about borrowing to buy something, and you're going to see potentially two big rate hikes from the Fed before the election, that really starts to get people nervous, because now it's not just expensive to go grocery shopping, but it's getting more expensive for those big-ticket items that you might have deferred on.\par \par \uc2 That is not a good backdrop for Democrats. And I just don't see them being able to turn that tide in the next two months, which means they got to do what they are doing. They got to find something else to talk about. I just don't think Americans have the luxury of doing that. Frankly, they have got to pay for this stuff day in and day out. And so I think that does really matter when you get to the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, another thing too, if you think about it, Professor -- and I don't know how your students react, but they obviously are aware of what's going on, and they see the numbers. They see it in the cost themselves to try to pay for things or get more from their parents trying to pay for things, including their own education.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering how they react to all of this. They're too young to remember. They weren't even born around the last inflationary cycle that I can remember quite well in the '70s. So they missed all of that, as well as leisure suits.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, the fact that matter is, they did benefit there.\par \par \uc2 But I'm just wondering how they are responding to this, when you bring it up in class, when they bring it up with you?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Well, it hits them hard.\par \par \uc2 I mean, college students don't have a lot of money. My students are living in New York City. So they get the double whammy of higher prices and New York prices.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good point.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: But what they -- what they want is some honesty. What they want is somebody to explain what's actually happening.\par \par \uc2 They don't want to hear slogans. They don't want to keep hearing from the bottom up and the middle out. That's not happening in this country. I'm sorry, but prosperity is not happening from the bottom up and the middle out under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And he keeps saying that.\par \par \uc2 And what they would really like -- and this gets to Lee's point -- somebody to say, you know what, here's honestly what's happening with inflation, and here's why it's a problem. And here's why, by the way, it hits those just starting their careers the hardest.\par \par \uc2 If we get a recession, that's going to hit young workers the hardest. They want honesty. They're not getting that from the current administration. I agree with Lee. The GOP needs to be out there talking much more about what they want to do to fix this and create opportunity.\par \par \uc2 I understand it starts with rolling back Biden's policies. But there's more to the story than that. They can win young voters. Everybody thinks young voters are drawn to the student debt handout. I actually don't think so. I think what they want is a real story about prosperity. They don't get that right now from the left.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's the opportunity from Republicans, if they can grasp it and get on message.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, because everyone goes to the store, right? Everyone goes out and looks at the price of things.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And when someone tells them it's no big deal or gas prices have come down and they can all relax, I mean, I think that gets them a little angry, Professor.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Great seeing you again, my friend. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: You bet, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Brian Brenberg.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, a possible distraction for Republicans all along has been the fixation on all things \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , oftentimes for good reason, what was really behind the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid of his home about a month ago. Now talk of a special master that's been approved to look through all those documents. For Republicans, the feeling seems to be anything that can give this time to not be a front-burner issue.\par \par \uc2 That side of the issue -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I think they call this hitting the ground running.\par \par \uc2 No sooner was she taking office as Britain's fourth Conservative Party leader, third female prime minister,\uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  is now saying that she is going to visit Ukrainian President Zelenskyy in Ukraine, and soon. That is just coming from her office now.\par \par \uc2 She's hit the ground running, I should say, because a number of other initiatives, including how to address the rising energy crisis in her country, where so many Brits are refusing to pay their utility bills, and looking for some government help. She plans to have a policy, she says, on that to address that, and very, very soon. We will keep you posted.\par \par \uc2 We're also keeping you posted on the fallout from a big victory for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is going to get his special master to review all those documents the \uc2 FBI\uc2  took from his home almost a month ago.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt has more on that at the Justice Department -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We are waiting for names proposed by the government and by Trump's legal team to find out who this special master will be. This person will have to go through thousands of pages of classified documents at the highest levels of classification in \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In plain English, Neil, appointing this special master will no doubt slowed down the investigation that's being led in this building, the Department of Justice. And, as you mentioned, this is a legal victory, even if a temporary legal victory, for the former president and his legal team.\par \par \uc2 This was issued yesterday by Judge Aileen Cannon. She's with a -- she's a district judge in Southern Florida actually appointed by then-President Trump in 2020. She heard arguments last week in person from Trump attorneys and federal prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 And after a nearly two-hour court session and a long weekend to think about it, she made this decision yesterday, writing: "A special master shall be appointed to review the seized property, manage assertions of privilege, and make recommendations thereon and evaluate claims for return of property."\par \par \uc2 She also goes on to say: "The government is temporarily enjoined from further review and use of any of the materials seized from Mar-a-Lago on August 8 for criminal investigative purposes pending resolution of the special master's review process as determined by this court," meaning DOJ cannot continue the investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Right now, it's on pause.\par \par \uc2 But the investigation under way by Director of National Intelligence Avril Haines to determine the classification levels of the documents will not stop. It goes on. DOJ is also working alongside Haines and can continue the classification assessment. But, in the same breath, the actual investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  slows down.\par \par \uc2 If the Department of Justice appeals Neil, a three-judge panel with the U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals in Atlanta would hear the case. Of the 11 active judges on the circuit court, six were appointed by then-President Trump.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look in Atlanta right now at the circuit court. We are waiting to see if the Department of Justice appeals. A DOJ spokesperson tells FOX News the government is looking over the judge's order and will consider the next steps.\par \par \uc2 But legal experts from across the spectrum Neil expect DOJ to appeal this ruling, because the stakes are so high here -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, David, for that, David Spunt.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: To Andy McCarthy on all of this, the former assistant U.S. attorney.\par \par \uc2 Andy, where is this going?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think, as David just said, Neil, it's going to the 11th Circuit. I can't see them not appealing.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I think we should probably at least mention is that there's always this talk around this time a year before a big federal election of the 60-day rule, which is not really a rule, so much as a -- sort of a guidance the Justice Department follows.\par \par \uc2 But they try not to take public steps in cases that may have an impact on the elections, because they don't like the perception of the Justice Department or law enforcement processes influencing the outcome of elections.\par \par \uc2 So I would have expected them to try to go dark anyhow for the next two months. We may now actually see more activity just because of these court proceedings. We will obviously be read in on those.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, in the meantime, they can't pursue this criminal investigation until all of this is resolved, one way or the other way, whether they lose in an appeals court and then the meter is running on getting this special master to go through things.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you're \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his legal team, what does that mean?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, I think that's a little bit overstated, because a court has no authority to tell the government what it can investigate.\par \par \uc2 What the court has authority to do is tell them that they can't use documents that have been collected by the government through the court's processes. Here, it was a search warrant that was the authority for the government to use these -- to use these documents.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: But that doesn't mean that other aspects of the investigation can't be pursued.\par \par \uc2 Remember, they had a pretty vigorous investigation before the search. So, I think not a lot is going to happen. And let's face it. This is not the most complicated investigation of all time. But I wouldn't expect a lot to happen in the next two months, in any event.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so all -- if it comes back, Andy, to he had government documents, he shouldn't have had government documents, that's one thing.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, this escalated and the obstruction issue came up. And, as you were reminding me when you misrepresented that, lied about that, and it turns out you did have these documents, and then when your legal team ultimately gave up some documents and said that was it, there are no more documents, when, in fact, there were documents, so there's a pattern of lies here that, intentional or not, have given the government ample ground for an obstruction case, haven't they?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, it -- Neil, I think, in broader lens, it's kind of the same decision that they had to make when they decided to escalate to a search warrant, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: They had for a while, I think, the legal and factual grounds to go seek a search warrant, but they were also quite aware that ratcheting it up like that would have a big impact on the public and the divisions in the country.\par \par \uc2 Charging former President Trump would have, by dimensions, I think, even more of an impact. So I think we're at the point in time where the Justice Department may think that they have a case they can bring. The question is it, is it prudent under the circumstances to bring it, given the conditions of the country, given what people would say about the fact that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  wasn't charged...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: ... and what it says about our two-tiered justice system? That's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, because -- I was just going to raise that issue, and other cases, Sandy Berger with documents he hid in his pants, and all the -- we can all remember various cases here. So there would be an inconsistency to going after the former president in this case.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering. The president will always go back to the argument, I was the president, and these documents were within my purview and right to have, whether he declassified them or not. There's a growing argument there whether that was the case or would have changed anything. He had them when he shouldn't have had them.\par \par \uc2 But the sheer volume of them doesn't make a difference, either, the fact that there were thousands of them?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, Neil, I think that goes to where I believe President Trump is not helping himself with his public statements.\par \par \uc2 I think, if you want a resolution of this without a prosecution, the best thing he could do right now is stop talking about it. And I think, unfortunately, he sees it -- in terms of the possibility of making a 2024 run, he sees that this is a positive in terms of his political campaign.\par \par \uc2 So he keeps harping on it. But I think, by harping on it, he makes it more likely that he gets charged.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will watch closely.\par \par \uc2 Andy, thank you again. Good seeing you, Andy McCarthy following all of that.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove is just worried about one thing. Forget about the particulars, whether it's legal or not, whether he had the documents or not, the fact that this is what Republicans end up talking about and defending, when they should be focusing on the economy.\par \par \uc2 The other side of that story that you're not hearing that you should be hearing -- next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, after Labor Day, it's the traditional back-to-work push, but, this time, really back to work, like in the offices work.\par \par \uc2 Why big investment banks are telling their workers, we're not asking anymore -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a special master, whether it will be appealed, what happens if it's appealed.\par \par \uc2 To Karl Rove, the legalities of this don't matter, so much as the distraction that he says it could mean for Republicans focused on this issue, at the expense of all others that are winnable issues for them, if they stay on the economy and stay on inflation and stay on the issues that ultimately decide elections.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Karl, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So you really come back that, despite whatever your views on this and arguing that a president can't be taking documents out of the White House, and all of that, down to the distraction it's getting to be for Republicans defending him or either commenting on it, and not on inflation that's running out of control, the big economic pocketbook issues, right?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Look -- yes, exactly. Look, since the search on August 8, what have we been talking about? We're now just shy of a month of talking about this issue. And if you're a Democrat, you love it, because the Republicans are forced to talk about Trump taking classified documents to Mar-a-Lago, instead of inflation, economy, the crisis at the border or crime.\par \par \uc2 So, if you're a Democrat, you want them to be talking about this. If you're a Republican, you really want to be talking about these things. And so, ironically enough, the decision for a special master, as you heard from both David Spunt in his excellent reporting, and then that very smart guy named Andrew McCarthy, as you have heard, this is going to slow the whole process up.\par \par \uc2 So you would -- not a lot is going to happen in the next couple of months. There may be appeals and so forth. But in terms of new information, and there's not going to be a lot. And Andy McCarthy said it was to the president -- the former president's advantage not to be talking about this issue.\par \par \uc2 To Republicans, it's also an advantage not to be talking about this particular issue, and instead talking about the things that people are talking about around the kitchen table, namely, how do they keep up with the rising prices? And how good is the economy going to be? And what's going to be done about the border and the crime in their neighborhoods and communities?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Karl, I don't have to remind you what the former president has written about you for saying what you have on this issue, and particularly the fact that you can't take government documents and bring them home.\par \par \uc2 But does that bother you at a certain level, that what you're getting back to is this notion we have got to get off this obsession, one way or the other, and speak about the economy? I mean, you would -- you would think and some people would say, well, maybe Karl's right about that, and that it's in the president, the former president's interest as well to make him less of an issue and more about the Republican Party's chances.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes, look, I understand the angst that he feels, how strongly he feels about this.\par \par \uc2 But this is a situation that's largely of his own creation. He should not have taken those documents. But now that we're in this kerfuffle over what -- what have they gotten back and how are they handled, it is better for him not to be talking about it.\par \par \uc2 When he went to Pennsylvania this weekend, he spent over 80 minutes talking about this situation, and less than half that advancing the cause of the two candidates he was there to advance and the issues that will either determine whether they get elected or not.\par \par \uc2 People are not going to vote Republican in order to affirm President Trump's view of what those documents were, namely, his property. They will vote Republican if they have a conviction that Republicans are going to do something about the inflation that's consuming our country, do something about strengthening our economy without spending a lot more money, taking it from taxpayers and turning it over to the government, and if they do something about border security and crime.\par \par \uc2 That's what they care about. And if we're going to motivate them to vote Republican in the fall, then Republican candidates need to have the airspace to talk about him, and the former president plays a big role in giving them that airspace.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he's digging in deeper, right? He criticized \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  again for supporting Alaska Senator Murkowski in her battle to keep her job.\par \par \uc2 And you always think of the possibility, should \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  run again and win, he's got to work with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . How would that go?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, that's a long way off.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: So let's focus on the here and now. And you're right.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it doesn't do any good for him to be critical of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . And it doesn't do -- it doesn't do his party any good to spend most of his time talking about himself, rather than talking about the issues that are consuming the country.\par \par \uc2 He did a lot of good things when it came to the border and the economy and energy and so forth. He ought to build on that by focusing on those issues and talking about the next chapter.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So when you hear you're a RINO -- I have heard worse -- but Republican in name only and all, on any level, does that bother you?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Not really.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, I don't need to prove to myself that I'm a true Republican. I don't need somebody else to hurt my feelings by saying I'm a RINO and having me feel sorry about myself. I have -- I have spent my adult life building and advancing the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 I was for \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  in 2004, not \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , like some people were. I have been a loyal Republican, and I don't need anybody else to tell me that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Karl Rove, very good seeing you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Neil, thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot more coming up, including the push right now to incentivize people not to own a car. I don't know, how does 1,000 bucks sound to you?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you ever get the feeling that California is not too keen on traditional gas-powered vehicles? They want them all gone, at least sales of them, by 2035.\par \par \uc2 Never mind there's been problems with the grid there and whether it can sustain all these E.V.s that they're now asking to charge up gingerly, but a separate push right now to get folks off the road by paying them to get off the road.\par \par \uc2 It gets a little more involved. Our expert on getting a little bit more involved and explaining it in English is William La Jeunesse joining us now from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 What's going on here, William?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, several things.\par \par \uc2 Number one, there are a lot of incentives, up to $15,000 to buy an electric car in California, when you add the state and the federal. But you don't have to live in California to live under our rules. In fact, 17 states already do. And now many have to confront this debate about getting rid of the gas-powered vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Why? Well, about 15 years ago, California sued and won the right under the Clean Air Act to have stricter tailpipe emissions than the federal government. So, now these 17 states are at a crossroads. They have to confront the question, will they join the electric car mandate or not?\par \par \uc2 Some will. Colorado and Pennsylvania likely will not, along with Minnesota and Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEREE DICKENS, CALIFORNIA ELECTRIC CAR OWNER: The power grid is already taxed, and then you're going to put greater demand, significantly greater demand, on it by having all electric cars, is -- I don't see that as a recipe for success, personally, at least not in that time span.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, right now, nationally, about 6 percent of new car sales are electric. California, it's 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 But under new California rules, that's going to go to 35 percent of all electric -- sales be electric cars in four years, and they will continue to ramp up. So the question is, is it doable or prudent, given that electric car prices are high and the shortage of charging stations, not to mention actually having the electricity to keep them running?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Try not to use too much electricity in those key hours. And the key hours are between 4:00 p.m. and 9:00 p.m. in the evening. Try to reduce your consumption to the extent possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, right now, Neil, we're in day six of this flex alert, basically trying to struggle to keep the lights on.\par \par \uc2 Basically, the legalese on this is that these 17 states either have to choose to be part of the federal program or the California program. There's -- the car manufacturers say, we can't meet 50 different standards in 50 different states.\par \par \uc2 So they're going to have to confront this question. And in those states where you have a mix of Republicans and Democrats, it's going to be open to debate -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And a lot of time. All right, we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 William, thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 William La Jeunesse following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  is now the new prime minister of Great Britain, and she's already completed a phone call with the president of the United States, after speaking earlier with President Zelenskyy over in Ukraine. She says she's eager to go to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 What came of the conversation with the president of this country? We will tell you -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, here we go again.\par \par \uc2 For the third time in the last six years, as my next guest says, Britain has a new prime minister, the fourth Conservative prime minister that has veered increasingly away from its Conservative roots. Now, will \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  be any different?\par \par \uc2 Gerry Baker joins us right now, The Wall Street Journal editor at large and read -- and wrote on this beautifully, very eloquently, if even a little snarkily, in The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 It's always good to see you, Gerry. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 You raise a very good point about the trend among Conservative leaders, that sort of the party eats its own, whatever is going on there. But you said that: "Bowing to the gods of ever-expanding public sector and welfare state, they have raised the tax take of the national income to its highest level in 70 years."\par \par \uc2 You mentioned other examples, but this has been happening under Conservative governments. Now, she was not always a Maggie Thatcher, right?\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, HOST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL AT LARGE": Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Quite the opposite. So, what do we have here?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, thanks for having me again, Neil. It's always a pleasure to be on.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the British, my ex-compatriots, seem to change prime ministers as frequently as some people change shirts. This is, as you say, the third time in six years they have changed it, making it the fourth prime minister in six years. That's the kind of thing that British used to laugh at other countries about. They used to have jokes that I had a great summer internship. I was prime minister of Italy for a few months.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAKER: That's basically what's going on in Britain these days.\par \par \uc2 And as I say in the column this week, Neil, the problem -- the problem has been particularly that we have had this succession of Conservative prime ministers. Conservatives have been in power in Britain since 2010. So, for 12 -- more than 12 years now, Conservatives have been in power.\par \par \uc2 But they become progressively less Conservative in the way they actually govern. So, as you mentioned very kindly, in my column, I said, yes, the tax take of the country, the proportion of income taken in tax is its highest in 70 years. That's happened under Conservative governments.\par \par \uc2 The sort of woke revolution that is sweeping this country, I mean, at least we can say, to be fair, you have got a Democratic president and we a -- we have got a Democratic present a Democratic Congress and lots and lots of Democratic-controlled states and cities around the country who are kind of pushing this woke agenda on culture and crime and policing and all this kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 It's been done in Britain under a Conservative government. We have a party that doesn't really seem to be in -- pursuing Conservative values. So, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  comes along. She wins this internal election. I have to say, it's a very strange way really to pick a prime minister. She was elected by Conservative members -- the members of the Conservative Party.\par \par \uc2 It's not like a primary, where...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: ... millions of people vote. It was 140,000 people voted.\par \par \uc2 So she wins this quite narrowly over her rival. Now, again, the promising news is, she is -- she's campaigned as a Conservative. She's been a Conservative for some time. You're absolutely right. She was once a Liberal Democrat who actually despised \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But that was -- we can forgive people youthful indiscretions. That was 20, 30 years ago, when she was a...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was a Democrat, right?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: She is now -- absolutely, very famously, yes, what was -- yes, exactly. That's right.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, so she -- she has -- like a lot of people, she's matured, become a Conservative, seen the lights, and is promising to do all kinds of things. The particular problem, of course, now, though, Neil, she faces is, Britain faces a massive economic crisis, the energy crisis that all of Europe is facing as a result of the war in Ukraine in particular.\par \par \uc2 It's got huge fiscal problems, partly because they have been spending so much money for so long, they're in huge amounts of debt. They have got the same inflation problem that the rest of the world has. And they have got very, very, very poor long-term economic performance in the form of productivity.\par \par \uc2 So she's got a lot on her hands. Their immediate challenge is going to be to spend a lot of money to help Brits through this terrible winter of energy prices, explode the deficit even more than they have already. And then she's going to promise to sort of remake the country in the mold of \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2 . It's going to be tough.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And the tax cut thing that she's still pushing, and she remains loyal to \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . She hasn't abandoned him. So we will see how all that balances out.\par \par \uc2 Gerry, great column, great job all the time.\par \par \uc2 And catch his show, "The Wall Street Journal at Large." He puts it all in perspective and just has a unique way of presenting it.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, you're back at work. Now, you working from home or are you working in the office? Because there's a real push to get you in the office. You in? You out?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, to hear a lot of New Yorkers tell it, this is not something to think about, particularly in the investment arena, where some of the biggest banks on the planet now are telling workers, we would really like you to get back to the office ASAP. We're not ordering you too.\par \par \uc2 We just think would be a very good idea.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino has been following that.\par \par \uc2 But Charlie made a swipe at younger workers, millennial workers, who he says are too cozy in their slippers and pajamas to ever leave. So we thought we'd check in with Abby Hornacek, who is certainly not lazy.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She's fully dressed right now, not in pajamas. I can't speak for the fuzzy slippers.\par \par \uc2 So, Abby, before I go to Charlie, I want you to respond to his mean, nasty, awful comments about your entire generation.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ABBY HORNACEK, "PARK'D" HOST: How dare you, Charlie? I'm so offended.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think you invited...\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: It's all true.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I think you invited me on to give a millennial perspective. But the joke's on you. I'm actually an old soul.\par \par \uc2 So I do see the value in younger workers returning to work. And here's why, because we have seen all this research done about how the productivity levels have gone up in some situations with people's jobs because they are working from home, they're working longer hours. They're cutting commuting costs.\par \par \uc2 But I think that is applying to people who have at least experienced working from an office once in their life before the pandemic, working that 9:00 to 5:00 and those five days a week. So I think it's extremely valuable for this younger generation who's just graduated from college, they're used to a culture where they can sleep in, maybe sneak in the back of an auditorium, not be seen, wear their pajamas all day.\par \par \uc2 And then they're carrying that into a virtual world. So they need to return to work,Well, experience what it's like to be in an office, learn those professional skills before they move to a virtual situation.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I don't buy any...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Charlie, you know, I knew her position.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I wanted to illustrate that to show you cast an entire generation with the same dismal brush.\par \par \uc2 Try to dig yourself out of this.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, I'm -- listen, it's not me. It's \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 . It's \uc2 Larry Fink\uc2 . It's David Solomon. It's every major Wall Street firm saying, get your rear ends back to work.\par \par \uc2 By the way, all those studies...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, but you were isolating young people like Abby who don't share that view.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, who are the ones that are rebelling?\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: It's not me. It's the young people. It's the people that have been brainwashed by woke college professors.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I don't believe any study that says working from home is more productive. You need someone screaming at you sometimes to make you more productive.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Abby agrees with you. And she is a young person.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I agree.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I'm saying, there are no studies. She said there are studies.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: There are studies.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I mean, these are like the -- these are like sort of fugazi studies that are designed...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Abby, does he sound a crotchety old man to you who is insensitive?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I'm just telling you, Neil, you need -- you see, this is why I will never be out of work.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I think Charlie needs a vacation.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You know why I will never be out of work? Because...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because you have pictures of people in compromising positions?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And I got people like Amy trying to take my job.\par \par \uc2 Oh, OK. OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Abby, I don't know how you can accept that.\par \par \uc2 But, Abby, let me ask you something. There are a lot of young people -- all people kind of liked working from home for a while. Those days are gone. Do you think that it should apply to everybody, everybody get back to the office?\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: You know, I do.\par \par \uc2 I talked to a lot of friends ahead of this because I have witnessed some of my friends do -- I won't name names.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Of course you have.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: But some of them get lazy.\par \par \uc2 But I have talked to people who have been pro-hybrid situation, because I have a very different job. You have a very different job than people who work in that office setting. Like, we get -- we have a fun job, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do we need Charlie physically here with us, Abby? Be honest.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I mean, I personally believe that part of productivity is the interpersonal relationships that spur ideas.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: See that?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes, but none of the millennial, Gen Z'ers believe in that. They're just lazy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Charlie, see that?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: They're so lazy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, here, she could have gone back to you with a nasty comment. And she chose not to. She rose above that.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Listen, they're so lazy. Well, you admit it. Everybody knows it.\par \par \uc2 You know what? The funny thing is, I write this comment in The New York Post. I thought I would get brushback. No, they are agreeing with me, everybody, even the Gen Z'ers. Yes, we know our generation is a bunch of lazy hacks. I mean, it goes down the line.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, look at the time, guys. Look at the time. We're out of time.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Oh.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But thank you both very, very much. You have to play-at-home version of "Your World," but terrific on both sides here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, see, I knew where Abby was coming from. I just knew.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220906ei960002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Cost of Dating Too High?; Election Spending; Market Outlook; Travel Turbulence; Student Loan Relief Debate; Return to Office\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne, Bret Baier, Kacie McDonnell, Madison Alworth, Mike  Gunzelman \par \uc2 8,774 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Time to get back to work, literally.\par \par \uc2 Hello everyone. I`m Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is a special Labor Day edition of YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 And from Main Street to Wall Street, more companies are bringing employees into their offices and scrapping remote work, but not everyone is on board.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get the latest from Madison Alworth of FOX Business -- Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, it`s the end of summer, and it`s the beginning of the return-to-office policy for companies across the country.\par \par \uc2 Many companies are using the holiday as an anchor for their return to office, major banks pushing for five days in the office, while others are ramping up their return, encouraging a majority of days be spent at their desks in the office.\par \par \uc2 This will have a noticeable impact on occupancy rates. Security company \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2  says we have been sitting around 43 percent occupancy for the past couple of months, with some, like New York City, seeing lower occupancy rates.\par \par \uc2 Now that more people will be returning to the office, \uc2 Kastle\uc2  says there`s an increased interest in their tracking system, and not just for security reasons.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK EIN, CHAIRMAN, \uc2 KASTLE SYSTEMS\uc2 : There`s heightened interest now for more compliance to see if people are coming in when they`re supposed to be in or when they say they`re coming in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: Companies have seen pushback from employees that do not want to end remote work.\par \par \uc2 A number of \uc2 Apple\uc2  employees have signed a petition calling the return-to- office plans -- quote -- "draconian" and arguing they do exceptional work from home. Kastle says that occupancy levels were at 20 percent earlier this year, so they have doubled, and they expect them to go up even more tomorrow -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks a lot, Madison.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring an Internet radio host Mike Gunzelman, FOX Nation host Kacie McDonnell, and retail and money watcher Hitha Herzog.\par \par \uc2 All right, Mike. I think the window is closing on these folks, particularly in tech world. This week, Snap said they`re laying off 20 percent across the board. I think that might and muscle of these folks who were able to say, hey, listen, I`m going to work from home or home or else, I think that clock is ticking down on them.\par \par \uc2 MIKE GUNZELMAN, FOX NEWS HEADLINES ENTERTAINMENT REPORTER: Maybe to an extent.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it all does come down to what sector or industry you`re in. But, Charles, I think people are still going to be frustrated. If you ask, if people would actually be honest with themselves, is there really -- are you getting that much work -- are you getting that much more work done by actually being physically into an office, if anything, I think it`s more frustrating, especially when people have to commute over an hour to get there an hour back.\par \par \uc2 I found this very interesting, Charles. On \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2 , which is still the number one place for like job recruiting and job applications, 50 percent of the applicants applied for remote jobs. And remote jobs were only 20 percent out of all though. So the demand is still there, especially with millennials and Gen Z`ers.\par \par \uc2 Now, do they have as much of a say? Maybe not. But for everybody that thinks, oh, we`re all going to get rid of remote and hybrid work, you`re out of your minds, because it`s not going away completely.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Kacie, I don`t think it`s going away completely, but I remember a year ago, it was like the new paradigm, everyone`s going to work from home.\par \par \uc2 And, slowly, but surely, more and more people are going into the office.\par \par \uc2 KACIE MCDONNELL, FOX NATION HOST: More and more people are going back.\par \par \uc2 Listen, for someone who is a serial procrastinator, and someone who is not functional at all, this is terrible for me. I try my best, and I`m just terrible.\par \par \uc2 But, no, of course, everyone`s going to be, me go back to work? I`m so productive from home. It`s probably about 50/50. You have the people who are more productive from home, like me, who can cut out that hour commute or whatever it is. You get, like, whatever you have to do done and you probably work a little bit overtime.\par \par \uc2 Then you have those people who just skate by. So I don`t know. It`s going to be very interesting. And it is interesting that, even now, people are still applying for what`s left of those at-home jobs.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hitha, of course, you cover the business angle of all of this. I`m not sure if there`s enough data in yet, but we are hearing companies say, hey, get back to work, particularly on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 HITHA HERZOG, RETAIL WATCHER: Yes, Charles, I think the greatest enemy of work from home is kind of what Kacie was saying.\par \par \uc2 It`s the fridge, the TV and the bed. However, there was a study that came out from Stanford by a professor, a labor economic professor, who did a work-from-home study, that said there`s actually really no difference between the people that work from home and the people that are in the office in terms of productivity.\par \par \uc2 With that said, as we were noting here, it really is -- comes down to the age group of the people that want to work from home vs. not. So, yes, we are seeing a lot of Gen Z and millennials wanting to work from home, ones that necessarily don`t have families, maybe are single, also people who are in retirement, maybe this is their second job, but also the people that have families too.\par \par \uc2 They want to -- they want to work from home. So the people that are single may want to go back into the office, are really pushing for that. The people that have families and are a little bit maybe coming out of retirement and have a second job, they want to work from home.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Mike, there are some intangibles also, office politics. It`s pretty well-known you have got to be in the office. You got to rub elbows. Management`s got to know who you are. It`s a lot easier when they see you walking around. Hey, Bob. Hey, how was it? How was the weekend? Great. How was the movie? Fantastic.\par \par \uc2 You really can`t do that at home.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s what we saw as far as anyone that was trying to advance their career. I think people just left the job altogether than actually got perhaps a job promotion throughout the last two and 2.5 years. If I was coming out of college or whatnot, the best advice I would give is, stop being stubborn or whatnot, actually be in the office.\par \par \uc2 That`s the best -- that`s how you fast-track getting a -- getting a raise and a promotion. But I would want to defend this though, Charles. Just because you`re working remotely doesn`t mean you`re not working as hard.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I was just on a -- quote, unquote -- "vacation." A bunch of us got a house out in Long Island. But we were still working all day long. And on top of that, just because you`re home, I mean, I`m always on call now. It`s almost an expected that you`re going to be answering phone calls, that you`re going to have to put in the time.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: So, instead of leaving at 5:00 p.m., now you`re always -- you might get an e-mail at any time. So it`s almost expected that you are going to be working.\par \par \uc2 We`re all not lazy or anything. I`m sure some people are. That can go for any age range out there. You`re still going to have to work. You still got to prove what your worth is, whether you`re home or physically in the office. I like the hybrid. Go with the hybrid.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I will say, when I first got in the Air Force, I learned people walking around with clipboards often weren`t doing much, just looking busy, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So -- and, Kacie, I have my own business too. And I got to be honest with you. I`m going to get an office again soon, because the people who are superstars working remote are superstars. But those who aren`t are just abysmal.\par \par \uc2 And it`s frustrating, because I love all of them. And -- but I don`t think -- I got -- to say that abysmal ones, I got to get them around the ones who know how to work hard.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL: Well, it definitely motivates you. You guys just were talking about that. It`s that camaraderie in the workplace. And it`s healthy to get out of the house.\par \par \uc2 We have gotten so used to staying in the house and having our comfort zone. So it`s healthy to go out. Go get lunch. It`s good for the mind. And also, I think, to your point about getting a raise or getting a promotion, it is harder when you`re in that virtual space or behind a computer.\par \par \uc2 I think I have sent maybe triple the amount of exclamation marks and e- mails over the last two-year span, just to, like, show people that I`m excited and energetic and happy about whatever assignment I have. So maybe I can turn down the exclamation marks.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, Hitha, I got 30 seconds left. You have the last word.\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: You know, 45 percent of people, according to an \uc2 Oracle\uc2  study, have not felt true happiness.\par \par \uc2 So, if you are the person that wants to work from home, I am not -- I`m with you, Charles. I have to go back into the office. I like the motivation of people and being in an office space. But if you are that person that needs to work from home, you know what? Do it. Life is short.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: My dad told me happiness was a paycheck.\par \par \uc2 Gang, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We have come a long way, baby.\par \par \uc2 All right, now about all those billions of dollars being spent on student loan handouts, the White House is saying, don`t worry about paying for. The deficit reduction will cover it.\par \par \uc2 My next guest, though, is not buying it. I want to get the read from economist Steve Moore.\par \par \uc2 Golly, Steve, every time the White House put something out, that just seems -- forget about -- listen, you`re one of the best economists in the world. But, with all due respect, you don`t have to be. The Inflation Reduction Act doesn`t make sense to the average person.\par \par \uc2 Spending $600 billion student debt doesn`t make sense to the average person. And none of it feels like there`s going to bring down the deficit or inflation.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Well, it`s almost comical, Charles, that the Biden administration is talking about how they`re reducing the deficit and debt.\par \par \uc2 When, in fact, since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came into office, if you add up all of those spending bills, the two big massive trillion-dollar plus spending bills that passed in 2000 -- 2021, and then the two or three massive bills that have already been spent this year, and then, if they put $500 billion more on that for student relief, you`re talking about -- I know these numbers are so big, people can`t comprehend them, but over $4 trillion of spending.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That`s 4,000,000,000,000.\par \par \uc2 So this amount of debt, I think, and the spending, I think, is the biggest weight on the economy, and it`s the thing that makes me most nervous about what we`re doing to the future of our country and the ability to create a prosperous society.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, to that point, politicians have always bought votes, right? I mean, that`s part of being a politician, no matter what political party you`re in.\par \par \uc2 But haven`t we come a long way from a chicken in every pot? I mean, where does it go from here?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: One of the things that disturbs me most about how we`re spending all of this money is, it`s not going for national security or the most important -- for -- it`s not even going for infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 They`re not actually building that much infrastructure. What it`s really going for is income transfer programs.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That is oftentimes going to -- let`s just put it very simply -- from people are working to people who are not working.\par \par \uc2 Casey Mulligan, an economist at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2 , says that, in many states now, when you take the free health care, the food stamps, the unemployment insurance, the brunt subsidies, all these things, you can make $80,000, $90,000 a year and not work a single hour.\par \par \uc2 And so we have got to get back to rewarding people for working. I think that`s the second priority. Stop spending so much and borrowing so much. And then let`s get back to rewarding work and making work pay, not making welfare pay.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 And that`s an interesting point. That explains in large part why you can`t find workers, particularly for these lower-paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The ADP jobs report out last week saw small businesses, one in 19 employees, they lost. They lost 47,000.\par \par \uc2 But it was an intriguing twist with the Biden administration. It`s also a transfer of cash to folks who are on the fast track of having the best lives in America. College grads have gotten the earned income tax credit for children. They`re getting money to buy E.V.s and put solar panels on their roofs.\par \par \uc2 They`re getting money to erase college debt. Why are we paying the people who are on track to make anywhere between $3 million to $5 million a year?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, that`s a good question.\par \par \uc2 And to add to that, why are we relieving debt payments, student loan debt payments, of people who make six figures? And, as you know, you have cited these statistics. Many of the people who are going to get student loan relief have master`s degrees. They`re making six figures and they just decided, hey, I`m not going to pay my loan.\par \par \uc2 That strikes people as fundamentally unfair, especially people who may not have gone to college, a small business man or woman who just started a business or somebody who is an electrician or a construction worker.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That whole policy, I think, is really cockeyed.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I think it`s actually blown up in Biden`s face politically, because people just don`t see the rationale behind it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I just -- it`s elitist. I -- it`s just the most bald-faced, elitist thing I have ever, ever seen.\par \par \uc2 Steve, thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thank you, Charles. Have a great Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, did this student loan bailout from the White House just hand the GOP a big issue for it to run on in the midterms? We`re going to debate it.\par \par \uc2 And it turns out inflation isn`t just breaking people`s bank accounts. It`s also breaking their hearts?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: So, this is just a start for you, right? It`s a step in that direction. More to come?\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): More to come on education and more to come for working families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, forget about going too far.\par \par \uc2 Democrats like California Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  says President Biden`s student loan handout didn`t go far enough. Still, they are happy to run on it and promise to deliver more if they keep the House. But Republicans are hoping that millions of taxpayers who are left out of this get to the polls and put a stop to this. Let`s debate it.\par \par \uc2 We have got Patrice Onwuka of Independent Women`s Forum and Democratic strategist Johanna Maska.\par \par \uc2 Patrice, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE ONWUKA, INDEPENDENT WOMEN`S FORUM: Oh, it`s insane, Charles, but it`s not surprising.\par \par \uc2 This is a naked vote grab for young votes and minority votes. And, ironically, these are demographics that are very likely to vote for Democrats in the November elections. However, right now, there`s a huge enthusiasm lag, particularly with young people.\par \par \uc2 And so I think this is just a way to say OK, guys, we have delivered on a promise we have made to you. We`re going to -- we want you to turn out and give us your vote. But this is not a real solution to the rising cost of college education and higher education in this country.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Johanna, I still don`t get it. I really don`t. These folks are on path. If you graduate with a regular degree, you will make about three million in a lifetime. With a doctoral, you will make $4.2 million, a professional degree $4.7 million.\par \par \uc2 High school dropouts make $29,000 a year. I don`t get it. Why are we taking money out of their pockets to give it to these folks?\par \par \uc2 JOHANNA MASKA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: So, Charles, it`s great to join you.\par \par \uc2 I would just say starting, education is not elitist. Education is crucial for our economy. And we need to find a better way than deficit financing it on the backs of the people who are trying to seek education to further their career and our economy.\par \par \uc2 And so, at the root of this, I understand that there`s an issue of -- I think the Democrats could have messaged this better. We need to do loan forgiveness. We need to have it for service. We need to get people in the job work force using their skills that they have learned in these colleges in their jobs.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is, the idea and the notion that we`re just going to continue to have more student debt pile up, it is a huge risk factor for our economy, because we have nothing to leverage if they default. We can`t take the brains of these people. It`s an economic issue if we don`t solve it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know what, Patrice? In about two months, there will be more auto loans outstanding than student debt.\par \par \uc2 This is not about the economy or economics. Again, these are going to be the richest people in America. They have been blessed. They -- every time the jobs report comes out, look at it.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Who`s making the most? Who has the lowest unemployment report? College grads. This does not make any sense to me.\par \par \uc2 But I do understand this is about modern monetary theory, free money starting with the elites. They get it first because they`re better than everyone else, and they can usher us all along.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts, Patrice?\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: Well, and the elites -- the elites are the ones who are in charge in Washington. They`re the ones who are staffing House offices and, frankly, the White House.\par \par \uc2 I think there`s an interesting report on the number of White House staffers who would benefit from the student loan forgiveness. This actually is an elite issue, in contrast to what my colleague is saying here, because you`re taking from, effectively, the poor and giving to the rich.\par \par \uc2 This is not Robin Hood. It`s the other way around. And so I feel it`s unfair when you`re seeing people who have either paid off their loans or never took out their loans, take alternative ways to get to the middle class.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: And now they have to go back and support other people who took out more than they are claiming that they can afford to pay.\par \par \uc2 But, as you have rightly said, if you`re a Ph.D., if you`re an M.D., you have the lifetime earnings that will more than allow you to pay off this student loan debt. The White House is disingenuous when they say that this is just going to be targeted at the very poor.\par \par \uc2 No, it`s actually going to be a huge redistribution plan from the poor to the rich.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Johanna, let me just...\par \par \uc2 MASKA: So, what was...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let me just -- hold on one sec, because on one hand, you want to say, the economy is amazing, unemployment rate is low, we have got all these jobs out there and, on the other hand, that people are drowning.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what America is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  overseeing right now, one that`s doing extraordinarily well economically or one where college grads can`t -- can`t -- are going homeless and can`t feed themselves?\par \par \uc2 MASKA: One that`s economically divided, which is a problem for our society.\par \par \uc2 And here`s what I would say is, we just went through a massive PPP. And I`m not saying that wasn`t necessary to fuel our businesses. But if that wasn`t a handout, I don`t know what is.\par \par \uc2 And, further, I would say, when we`re talking about...\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: You can`t compare -- no. No.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MASKA: You absolutely can.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: No, you cannot compare the PPP program, which...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MASKA: Teachers -- you can.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: No.\par \par \uc2 MASKA: But it`s teachers who actually hold a massive amount of our student debt.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I will remind audience what the PPP program was, because just so -- because this is the new talking point.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It was a program where the government shut off the entire economy because this once-in-a-lifetime unknown pandemic threatened to kill everybody in the world, so we all could not go to work.\par \par \uc2 So the government said, we will send businesses money so that they can pay employees. That`s what that program was.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: And, Charles...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: This is entirely different than someone taking a loan and saying, I will pay it back out of my earnings and then saying, no, I`m too -- my parents bought a house when they were 29, and I won`t be able to do that. Waah. I need to get money. It`s two different things.\par \par \uc2 We got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: Well, and, Charles...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Go ahead real -- last word, Patrice.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: I will just say also, this is the difference between Congress deciding to give out PPP loans and President Biden doing it by himself, very different scenarios.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Exactly. One was constitutional, and the other one a big question mark.\par \par \uc2 We will leave it there, ladies. Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 So, did you put off flying somewhere this weekend because of all the hassle and delays? Why they may be coming to an end.\par \par \uc2 And with the price spiking and the Fed hiking, what are investors on Wall Street to do? We have got you covered.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I`m Bret Baier from FOX News in Washington. "Your World" continues in a moment, but, first, this "Special Report" newsbreak.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge has granted a request by former President Trump and his legal team to appoint a special master to review documents seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  during the search of his Florida home last month. The decision came despite the objections of the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is once again going after what he calls MAGA Republicans, the president speaking to a Labor Day rally in Milwaukee earlier today. He will address a crowd in Pennsylvania shortly.\par \par \uc2 Foreign Secretary \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  will be Great Britain`s next prime minister. She was overwhelmingly selected today by the ruling Conservative Party. \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  faces immediate pressure to deliver on promises to fix the U.K.`s struggling economy.\par \par \uc2 I will be back for a "Special Report" live at 6:00 p.m. with all the day`s news in the U.S. and around the world.\par \par \uc2 Now we will send it back to "Your World."\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Spending more time and waiting longer? No problem.\par \par \uc2 Despite higher prices, travel demand remains strong. \uc2 Tripadvisor\uc2  data showing 61 percent of Americans are planning trips this autumn. But after months of mass flight cancellations and delays, how can fliers avoid travel turbulence this fall?\par \par \uc2 Travel expert Lee Abbamonte is here to help.\par \par \uc2 Lee, I need every -- I have got my pin ready. Every trip I`m taking this year has been an unmitigated disaster, every single one.\par \par \uc2 LEE ABBAMONTE, TRAVEL EXPERT: Hey, man, I hear you.\par \par \uc2 And number one tip, be patient. Bring your patience pants, as I tell everybody. It`s going to be a bit of a bumpy road for the foreseeable future. But in order to avoid -- I always tell people to check in online, try not to check bags if you don`t have to, give yourself some time, obviously.\par \par \uc2 And I always like to book flights in the morning because, if they`re delayed or canceled, then you will still have a pretty good shot at getting out later that day, whereas, if you booked the flight late in the afternoon or in the evening, and it gets delayed or canceled, you`re out of luck that day, especially if you have a big event.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Should everyone start to enroll in these sorts of programs from these airlines?\par \par \uc2 I have done it with a couple of them. And they do send me e-mails. I don`t always open them, because I think, when I get to the airport, the news is different anyway. So, I`m a little, I don`t know, cynical, if you will.\par \par \uc2 But is -- would that be a good idea as well?\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: Listen, I have TSA PreCheck, Global Entry, and CLEAR, and, honestly, it`s wonderful. I rarely spend more than five minutes in security.\par \par \uc2 Like I said, I do all the other stuff, like check in online, I don`t check a bag. But then I just walk right up. They scan your eyes with CLEAR. You get a pass go right through security. And I`m literally at the gate usually within five or 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 So, for me, there`s no better option if you fly frequently.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, CLEAR is going to love this segment, because I -- some dude roped me in about three months ago. And I have taken -- I took about four flights since then.\par \par \uc2 And I took a flight a week ago. And I said, hold up. I have got this CLEAR thing. I just whisked right in. We probably shouldn`t be telling anybody about this, Lee, honestly. It should just be between me and you. But it is really amazing.\par \par \uc2 Now, what about these clubs inside the airport, right? Because, sometimes, I get a first-class ticket. I have gone to them and say, well, we don`t really honor your first-class tickets, or I have been waiting. And I think I`m going to get on a plane, and then 73 people go before me because they`re in a 1K plan, the 3K plan, they`re in this plan, i mean, all of these different things.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure the airlines are nitpicking us, but how do we stretch our feet somewhere? And how do we make sure we get to our seat, like, before everybody else has taken all the overhead bins?\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: Well, we could do like an entire show about hacks and airlines miles and points and stuff like that.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is, the better your status on an airline, the more likely you are to, number one, get upgraded for free and board the plane first and not have to worry about overhead bin storage and stuff like that and have access to lounges.\par \par \uc2 Now, the lounges have been inundated because of the amount of people with status, especially because of credit cards. So, in order to get into some of them, you generally have to be flying business class or be a member of the...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: ... as opposed to just passing through.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I got less than a minute to go.\par \par \uc2 So we`re talking about the nitty-gritty stuff. Let`s talk about really saving some money. The best time to fly these days, the best part of the year?\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: May I tell you I just flew on Tuesday to Europe, and it was the cheapest flight I have ever seen in the last -- well, in the last, like, couple years since COVID, for sure, out to Europe.\par \par \uc2 So I always say fly midweek, fly off-times, if you can, and just be a little bit flexible. Even if you have to connect, you can generally save some serious money.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, Lee, great stuff. I appreciate it. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I have sworn off connecting. I will never connect again. I will go to where I have to go and then walk from there.\par \par \uc2 All right, see you later, my man.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ABBAMONTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The Fed ready to -- for more hiking, and investors, well, they just keep selling. What you need to know for the rest of the year to get back on track.\par \par \uc2 And if the market does have you awake at night, well, you might be tempted to hit the fridge for a late-night snack. Guess what? You`re not alone.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: For a brief moment in August, things were looking up.\par \par \uc2 Then Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  spoke, and things went down again, the Fed ready to keep hiking rates to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, what does that mean for your money for the rest of the year?\par \par \uc2 Let`s get the read from our market gurus, Rebecca Walser and Keith Fitz- Gerald.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca, the Fed is saying, take us seriously. We`re going to keep hammering away at this thing. We`re going to turn this economy down, and there`s going to be pain.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WALSER, WALSER WEALTH MANAGEMENT: Yes, Charles, and I 100 percent -- I think I`m going the bear in this conversation.\par \par \uc2 I agree. I think that he had no choice. This is what he had to do to maintain the strength of the dollar. And if we look at the true inflationary numbers, using the equation from the 1980s that we used when we had double-digit inflation in the late `70s and `80s, is, we have 15 to 18 percent inflation really and truly, and the Fed knows this.\par \par \uc2 So they know they have to be serious and they have to really get rates up. And it`s going to take a lot more pain, unfortunately. The bond market already has the worst year since the 1790s. And it`s going to get worse.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALSER: And it`s going to -- it is what it is. It`s where we are, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Keith, to that point, this is like -- there`s nowhere to hide.\par \par \uc2 That so-called 60/40 portfolio has been a disaster. Inflation hedges haven`t worked. I mean, people are saying, gosh, I did everything textbook right, and I`m still getting hammered here pretty good.\par \par \uc2 KEITH FITZ-GERALD, FITZ-GERALD GROUP: Well, that`s the problem. And Rebecca is being very generous.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: I mean, the Fed has to raise rates, but I don`t think the Fed knows what it`s doing. It`s as wrong on rates in labor as it was on transitory.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the time when you got to be simple. Is anybody giving up their iPhones? No. Is anybody stopping eating? No. Is anybody going -- stopping going to the bargain basement stores or to the big bin stores? No.\par \par \uc2 The key to what you`re going to have to do for the rest of the year is survive. And those are the big, liquid stocks that can avoid the supply chain problems, can help every dollar go further, and help you live your life.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I`m going to digress a little bit here, because, Keith, when you say the Fed doesn`t know what it`s doing, you`re talking about 18,000 Ph.D.s, a $2 billion budget, and they have just two jobs.\par \par \uc2 That`s all they have to do. They have two jobs. What`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: Well, neither of which, Charles, is equivalent to running a popsicle stand. They don`t understand how real money works.\par \par \uc2 The Fed thinks that it can raise rates because the banks have big reserves. What they do not understand, between these 18,000 Ph.D.s, is how real money works, because all of the banks that have those reserves re-loan that between seven, eight, and nine times, which means there`s only $1 in the system for every fake $9 that are out there.\par \par \uc2 So, every time they mess around with rates, you get this asymmetric response in the system. That`s why so many people are hurting. And then, on top of that, you got the Biden administration throwing more money on the fire, which might as well be jet fuel at the barbecue.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 You know, I will say, though, I think, when the carnage starts to mount, I know \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  is talking a strong game and he`s stepped up to the plate so far, but I think, when the carnage begins to mount, I don`t know, I think he will blink.\par \par \uc2 But until that moment, Rebecca, how should people try to weather this storm?\par \par \uc2 WALSER: Well, I mean, obviously Keith is into liquid equities, but I`m a commodity girl right now. I`m 100 percent in precious metals -- not 100 percent in precious metals. I`m for precious metals right now. No one should be 100 percent in anything, right?\par \par \uc2 But, honestly, Charles, I think that we are -- we`re experiencing global currency realignments that are unprecedented. We have never seen this before. We do know that the strength of dollar right now, I think the Fed has to keep the illusion that the dollar is strong. And I`m not saying that it`s not strong. It`s strong relative to the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 WALSER: But it has got massive problems. And those are going to be realized very, very soon. And so I`m definitely on the gold and silver side myself.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, Keith, and it`s -- we just did a segment earlier about student loans and that forgiveness plan.\par \par \uc2 One of the problems for the Fed is that they can`t stop the White House, right, because you have got the federal government and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is trying to slow the economy down and fight inflation. The White House keeps pumping out billions, if not trillions of dollars. It`s a heck of a fight when the other side won`t let you do your job.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: Well, this is why none of us are in Washington, is because we actually understand how real money works.\par \par \uc2 WALSER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: So, the key here, Charles, is, this is tough.\par \par \uc2 These are unprecedented times, to Rebecca`s point. I mean, there`s just no doubt about that. So the key is, don`t try to be a hero. If you play golf, keep it on the fairway. If you`re a sailor, be out in the fair weather. Don`t try to buy the scoop-it-up, low stocks. Buy the stocks that you know are absolutely going to be there in five to 10 years, the stuff that you`re going to kick yourself if you don`t own.\par \par \uc2 Some of my favorites include the big tech, which I`m content to average in over time.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: I`m content to buy \uc2 Costco\uc2 , for example, because I know people have to make every last dollar go farther.\par \par \uc2 I know that I`m going to go into the food stocks` worth, because people are not going to suddenly stop eating. So I can`t do anything about the Fed. But you know what. I can pick great companies.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: I can pick quality companies, and they`re still putting numbers up on the board, inflation or not, supply or not, Biden or not.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. I mean, you know what? The \uc2 Kroger\uc2 `s and the Kellogg`s and the \uc2 General Mills\uc2 , they seem boring...\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: There you go.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... but those stocks hang in there pretty good.\par \par \uc2 All right, so, Rebecca...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALSER: ... good.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... on top of this, on top of this, we also have higher taxes coming, the buyback tax. And I have lost track of them all.\par \par \uc2 You`re a tax expert as well. Combining all of these things up, what does it mean for the economy down the road? I mean, we could be looking at a serious recession, right?\par \par \uc2 WALSER: I mean, not to be depressing on Labor Day here, but I will just tell you, this is -- this is -- we have the Baby Boomers in the last six years of massive retirement.\par \par \uc2 By 2028, most of them are retired. We have a little bit in `29 and `30. But between the Baby Boomers retiring, and almost 9.5 percent COLA adjustment, which is what we`re looking at right now for Social Security, which we can`t afford at all, not even one year, we can`t afford it, without raising taxes, Charles, and that`s the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 WALSER: Don`t tell us you can do all this, and yet not going to charge taxes on people under $400,000. It`s just not true. Let`s get honest.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, as Neil Cavuto would say, on that uplifting note, enjoy your holiday.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: See you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All righty.\par \par \uc2 All right, so spending money on dinner, spending time at home, how inflation is taking a bite out of dating.\par \par \uc2 And for Democrats and Republicans looking to win the midterms in November, forget about following the polls and follow the money.\par