e seeing is, the president really hitting the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: He`s out. He`s been out in key states. He`s been out in Pennsylvania. He`s really trying to sharpen his message against Republicans.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And now people aren`t afraid they`re being seen with him?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: For a while, that wasn`t...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: That, of course, is a perennial issue, right? We have seen that in past midterms. That`s not a new thing. He was in Pennsylvania last week. The Democratic gubernatorial candidate was out with him. He certainly is hitting key battleground states.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things he`s trying to do is -- typically, you know, Charles, midterms tend to be a referendum on the party in the White House, right?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: So what Republicans want to do is really focus on issues that are very important voters. They want to focus on the economy. They want to focus on -- focus on inflation.\par \par \uc2 And they really want to make this a referendum on what they see as President Biden`s failings in those areas.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, of course...\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: And what Biden is now trying to do is sort of shift it to making it more of a choice and argue that he has done these things, look at what we are doing...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: ... and compare that to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, of course, the demonization of Republicans, which might be the thing that actually galvanizes them, because I don`t see or hear a central voice on the GOP side that`s -- and I don`t hear a typical message.\par \par \uc2 They have always had problems messaging, in my opinion. And so I just feel like they might have missed an opportunity to truly seize the moment, and they`re still not picking up on it.\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: Well, I think Republicans have focused a lot 