nst you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: So, it was 150 bucks for one person, and that doesn`t go well, well, you might be doing that two or three times a week. It`s too expensive.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Too much?\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Just good luck. I don`t know. I don`t have the answer.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Kacie, I tell you what, though. You got to do what you got to do, right? At least the man does, I guess.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL: You got to do what you got to do.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL: I mean, yes, I mean, if you want to -- listen, I have my cat. And if I just ended up with my cat, it would have been great. But I got a husband and I have a baby on the way. So I`m -- really, things are looking at.\par \par \uc2 But, maybe as, like, a rule, you just stick with liquids for the first few dates.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL: I always say, there`s no such thing as a second date. You know on the first date if you like them. You know when you have taken one sip of the free water if you like them.\par \par \uc2 So maybe it`s just like coffee date one, maybe two drinks for date two, and that`s it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, sounds good.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Now, if you are dating, here`s one habit that might get you dumped real quick.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hey.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s almost bedtime. Can I have another bag of chips?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: When I first glanced at it, I didn`t he had a T-shirt on. OK.\par \par \uc2 So, a new poll...\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: That`s frightening. That`s frightening right there.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I know. I was like, golly, they`re going to cancel the show after a 25-year run.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: A new poll finds that about half of millennials admit to regular eating right before bed.\par \par \uc2 Mike, are you guilty of this?\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Honestly, I am.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic -- I don`t know what it is. I`m snacking all the time. I`m going to multiple trips to like Gristedes or Duane Reade. I`m snacking all the time. But, for some reason, like, 8:00, 9:00, 10:00 p.m. comes around, I`m always going for like leftover Chinese food or pizza or ice cream.\par \par \uc2 So I`m guilty of this. However, I will eat before I get to bed. If you`re the one of those people eating like you just got off like a college bender and you`re waking up with like food all in your bed or whatnot, that`s no good.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: That is -- you are a garbage human being if you`re -- if you`re one of them.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Don`t eat in the bed, people. Just eat before you get to the bed.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Kacie, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL: Well, it said in the study, it said in the article in The New York Post that 38 percent of young adults say that eating in the bed is a dating don`t and that`s a deal-breaker.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I get it if you`re getting crumbs in the bed, but what if you`re like eating over the edge and the crumbs are falling?\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: No.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL: I don`t know. I think there`s like, where there`s a will, there`s a way.\par \par \uc2 I went on a date with a guy one time that told me -- one date, just one -- no, we had free water -- and he said that he broke up with a girl because she used the guest towels?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL: That`s a thing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL: Because I think the same thing. But then my husband used the guest towels, so I actually knew I like him.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCDONNELL: This is a news flash for people who say that that is a deal- breaker. You don`t like the person.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, we got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Right. You`re looking for anything just to break up with them, anything at all.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We could go on with these kind of dos and don`ts.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, thank you at home for joining us. Really hope you enjoy the rest of your long weekend.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, you can catch me on FOX Business weekdays 2:00 p.m. on "Making Money." And, of course, these days, it`s been real, real tough, but hang in there. We`re going to get through it together. You know I have got your back.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, good night. "THE FIVE" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220906ei9500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Student Loan Forgiveness: White House Defends Plan Amid Bipartisan Criticism; Ukraine Independence Day: Russian Forces Strike Train Station; Wartime Alliance: \uc2 U.S. Aid\uc2  To Ukraine at $13.5 Billion Under Biden; Conservative Candidates Backed by Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  Win School Board Seats Across State Of Florida; Counselor At Fairfax County Virginia Middle School Kept Working For 20 Months After Being Arrested And Convicted Of Sex Crime; State Of Kentucky Suffers More Than 2,000 Overdose Deaths Current Calendar Year; President Biden Announces Plan To Forgive $10,000 Worth Of Student Loan Debt For Those Making Less Than $125,000 Annually\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, David Spunt, Alex Hogan, Alexis McAdams,  Aishah Hasnie, Mike Emanuel, Mike Tobin \par \uc2 7,774 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: He's got a huge following on TikTok. 68,000 people follow this guy. He -- Wallygator won the contest if he wins a contest.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: He gets $10,000 price and a two-page spread in In Touch Magazine for America's favorite pet. I don't know if he'd won.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: He deserves it.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: He's somebody.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I mean, maybe second place after Percy. That's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: What exactly happens during the commercial breaks? What is the inside story?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You can maybe bribe me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can we?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Because this one's worth it?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can we get a camera there?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: No.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: No.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. I like the glasses. All right, thanks, Dana. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Brett bear.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Biden says he's canceling up to $10,000 of federal student loan debt for certain borrowers and up to $20,000 for Pell Grant recipients. According to a nonpartisan analysis, that proposal could cost upwards of $300 billion for taxpayers and disproportionately benefit the wealthy. In recent days, there has been considerable bipartisan pushback to the plan that is now going forward as the country faces record- high inflation.\par \par \uc2 President Biden today claiming the plan will have no meaningful effect on inflation. But his critics, including some former \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration officials, remain unconvinced. White House correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight lost in the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. White House officials say they have no idea how much this plan is actually going to cost because they think there are plenty of eligible student loan debt holders who are going to choose not to go online and tell the government that they want to cancel up to $10,000 in that debt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Now, it's time to address the burden of student debt.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voiceover): The President's announcement benefits 43 million borrowers including 20 million who will see their student debt totally canceled up to 10,000 in federal loans, 20,000 in Pell Grants for borrowers making up to $125,000 a year and up to 250,000 for couples. Everyone else gets an additional four-month pause.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm extending to December 31, 2022 and it's going to end at that time. It's time for the payments to resume.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  writes, President Biden's bold action is a strong step in Democrats fight to expand access to higher education. But just last year, she didn't think this was legal.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): People think that the President of the United States has the power for debt forgiveness. He does not.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The White House won't say which taxpayers are picking up the hundreds of billions of dollars to cancel this debt. The Committee for a Responsible Budget adds, "Today's announcement could add up to twice as much or more to the deficit as was just eliminated from the Inflation Reduction Act."\par \par \uc2 The President's top Domestic Policy Adviser Susan Rice explains.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN RICE, Director, U.S. Domestic Policy Council: This relief will be targeted to those who need it most.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Others aren't so sure.\par \par \uc2 KENT SMETTERS, UNIVERSITY OF PENNSYLVANIA WHARTON SCHOOL: We calculate almost 70 percent of the benefit is going to top 60 percent of the income distribution. So, this is not something that's targeting low-income households.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Former \uc2 Obama\uc2  economic adviser \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  tweets, "Pouring roughly half a trillion dollars of gasoline on the inflationary fire that is already burning is reckless. Doing it while going well beyond one campaign promise $10,000 of student loan relief and braking another all proposals paid for is even worse."\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Of all the dumb things \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done, this might be the dumbest yet. I know it's stiff competition.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The education secretary is addressing questions on fairness.\par \par \uc2 The people that already paid their student loans, they don't get anything out of this deal.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CARDONA\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF EDUCATION: Right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): So, I asked the Education Secretary how this is legal. And he explained, the White House is relying on the Heroes Act, which was passed after 9/11 and gives the education secretary the ability to do this, to cancel student debt in times of war, military operation, or national emergency. So, when there are legal challenges, the folks here are prepared to argue there is a national emergency, COVID. Right?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which is very interesting because the CDC 13 days ago kind of dialed back on COVID restrictions and guidelines. Let me ask you one thing, you spent some time in the briefing today really trying to press them how this is paid for. And the answer came back, the deficit reduction since the administration coming out of COVID. Is that what the answer is?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, yes, they're basically saying they have reduced the national deficit by over $1 trillion since they took office and that -- and that's it. That's basically their whole explanation. They don't go so far as to say that because they reduced the deficit, it's OK now to spend more.\par \par \uc2 But what I was trying to ask the press secretary today is about this idea that you canceled debt. You know, when he canceled a debt, you don't disappear it. Somebody has to pay. And I was asking over and over who is going to pay. We know it's taxpayers. They just wouldn't say which ones.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right. Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today. The Dow gained 60, the S&P 500 added 12, the NASDAQ jumped 50. More in all this with the panel in a bit. We have no reaction from President Biden tonight about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid on former President Trump's Florida compound. The response coming ahead of a highly anticipated deadline that could shed significant light, possibly transparency about the case or maybe it won't. Correspondent David Spunt joins us with the latest on this. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. The deadline for the Justice Department to propose redactions dealing with the affidavit at Mar a Lago, that is going to be happening tomorrow at noon. President Biden talked for the first time about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search. Here he is with Peter Doocy just a few hours ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Mr. President, how much advance notice did you have of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's plan to search Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I didn't have any advance notice, none, zero, not one single bit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The affidavit is a document that includes probable cause. It will explain why Magistrate Judge Bruce Reinhart signed off on allowing federal agents to search the former president's Mar-a-Lago home. The Affidavit, Bret, would have detailed information including what crime or crimes the Justice Department believes were committed.\par \par \uc2 Last week, in court, Judge Reinhart said he was leaning toward unsealing parts of the affidavit for the public to see. The DOJ warned unsealing their preliminary evidence could jeopardize their ongoing probe and could threaten the safety of witnesses interviewed by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . But over the weekend, Reinhart had some time to think about it, and in a court filing just a few days ago said, he may release some of it or none depending on the arguments made by the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 He wrote, "Releasing the affidavit could impede the ongoing investigation through obstruction of justice and witness intimidation or retaliation. This factor weighs in favor of sealing." The former president has said they're releasing the affidavit unredacted in full for the public to see.\par \par \uc2 Now, Bret, after tomorrow's deadline, another federal judge, Aileen Cannon, gave the Trump team a deadline for Friday to resubmit the former President's request for a special master. That's a neutral third party arbiter to take a look at how DOJ is actually handling this evidence. Remember, the Feds have had about two and a half weeks to look through the evidence. They see some of it labeled classified. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it's complex. We'll follow it all. Tomorrow is a big day. Also, some other news involving the Justice Department and the former president.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Correct. This is a memo from March 2019 involving the former Attorney General Bill Barr. Bill Barr recommended against charging then- President Trump with obstruction of justice during the Mueller probe. Then, Attorney General Barr agreed and publicly said nothing that Trump's contact violated the law including firing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director. Federal courts had ordered the memo to be made public. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Devastation in Ukraine tonight after Russian forces launched a deadly rocket attack on a train station today, the country's Independence Day. The lethal strike comes after warnings from President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  in recent days that the Russians were preparing a cruel assault, in his words. Correspondent Alex Hogan has that story live tonight from Kyiv. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What --\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: So, what we know about this latest attack is that 22 people now have been killed and at least 50 people are wounded after Russian shelling on a train station in the central part of the country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Alex, what else do we know about on -- this independence day, the Ukrainians are trying to push back. What exactly are they doing as far as different parts of the country in response to this Russian attack?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Well, we heard those warnings in recent days from U.S. intelligence as well as the government here urging people to stay inside. In the second largest city of Kharkiv, people are told to not leave their homes at all over fears of potential attacks on government buildings and civilian infrastructure. Here in the capital of Kyiv, people are told not to gather at all. And that is out of fear of what we have seen take place across the country today, as well as the recent attack on this train station.\par \par \uc2 There was also an attack on a shopping mall in the Donetsk region. This as people were out trying to enjoy what they could have the day. Again, this is Ukraine's Independence Day, but it also marks the six month mark since the war broke out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, Alex. President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  has been asked numerous times about how this ends, how this comes to an end. And now he's saying victory, right?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Yes, he's saying victory, not peace. He doesn't want a peace negotiation. At this point, he says it is too late for that. He will not give up until Ukraine had has managed to regain all of the territory, not only the Crimean Peninsula but the contested Donbas region where again there was intense shelling. Even today, a child killed as young as 11 years old. That lighting a fire and so many Ukrainians tonight who wanted to be out celebrating Independence Day, instead hunkered at home waiting and watching the death toll across the country only continuing to rise. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex Hogan live in Kyiv on this Independence Day in Ukraine. Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A high-ranking U.S. defense official says it appears Iran is responsible for an August 15 drone strike on a compound run by American troops in eastern Syria.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLIN KAHL\uc2 , UNDERSECRETARY OF DEFENSE FOR POLICY: Part of it was also the nature of the attacks on the 15th, the fact that they were coordinated against two U.S. facilities at the same time, the fact that we believe we have around dead rights on attribution, the UAV parts that we've collected, for example, traced directly back to Tehran.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There were no casualties in that attack. Let's talk about foreign policy and other topics with \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2 , Democratic Congressman from Washington State, Chairman of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 , and Florida Republican Congressman Mike Waltz, also on the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thanks for the time. I like to start these bipartisan interviews for some common ground and what you to agree on. You can choose your topic. Congressman Smith, first to you.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM SMITH\uc2  (D-WA): Well, I think Ukraine. Congressman Waltz and I went on a bipartisan CODEL to Kyiv about a month ago, met with President Zelenskyy, and got a tour outside of the battle of Kyiv and what the Ukrainians are doing to resist the Russian impasse. And we're very united on giving Ukraine all the support they can possibly get to fight off the Russians.\par \par \uc2 And I think President Zelenskyy is right, this war doesn't stop until Putin is stopped and pushed back. And we agree on getting all the help we can to Ukraine to do that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, I want to play a soundbite, Congressman Waltz, before we get to you on that. And it's about Ukraine from the Defense Department today about the effort to get military equipment in there and what's happening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAHL: We're trying to be very deliberate about what systems we think makes the most sense for Ukraine to have. So these also have to be systems that Ukraine itself can sustain. But I can tell you that fighter aircraft remain on the table, just no final decisions have been made about that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Fighter aircraft going in. It seems like there's just a ton of money obviously pouring in for this equipment. There's also -- and I've talked to you before about this. Is there a mission creep here from the U.S. perspective inside Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ (R-FL): Well, Brad, I think another thing that you see agreement on the Hill is that, you know, first Ukraine should have had much of this equipment in the first place, and that the administration seems to constantly be behind in terms of what they're providing versus what Ukraine needs from harpoon anti-ship missiles to the Stingers they didn't get till after the invasion despite asking for them beforehand, to the fighter aircraft, and eventually to the long-range missile artillery, the HIMARS.\par \par \uc2 We finally eventually get it to them, but I think at the end of the day, they are losing slowly. Zelenskyy is convinced that if he gets cut off from the Black Sea, he's no longer viable as a country. And you know, at the end of the day, Bret, he has to go on the offensive to regain momentum or once again, Russia will grind away if the lines solidify like they are by wintertime. There's a real fear that Russia will just annex everything they have, lick their wounds and be right back at it just a few years down the road. So it's pay now or pay later.\par \par \uc2 I think the other thing, Bret, is that the Europeans absolutely have to step up and do more, particularly Germany. They have provided a fraction of what the United States have yet they are most directly at threat. And Zelenskyy's other fear is that once we get into winter, if Russia can take the nuclear, that Zaporizhzhia nuclear plant offline, that's 30 percent of Ukraine's electricity, then they can dial back the gas to both Ukraine and Germany. You're going to see a lot more European backsliding. So, time is of the essence right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chairman Smith, I want to ask you about another foreign policy issue and that is Iran.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, I do you want to comment --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oh, yes, go ahead, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, I just -- look, we have gotten a ton of aid to Ukraine. And I don't agree that Ukraine right now is losing slowly, particularly in the last six weeks. The HIMARS systems that we've got in, the gambler's weapons systems, the 155-millimeter cannons and weapons, Ukraine is actually beginning to push back. We've seen the strikes within Crimea and within occupied -- the other parts of Ukraine that are occupied by Russia.\par \par \uc2 I think Ukraine right now is setting up pretty well for the counteroffensive that Mike talked about. So, we are getting them a lot of support. And I guess I'm a little more optimistic at this point a month after our visit about Ukraine's ability to push this fight to the Russians in the next three months.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, I want to talk to you about another topic and that is Iran. Chairman Smith, just moments ago, senior defense officials confirming reports from the region that the U.S. military fired upon two sites in Syria after receiving gunfire from those sites. This is after we saw this direct tie to Iran and the drone that was used to attack us troops and others on the ground in Syria. Meantime, the negotiations continue with Iran on the nuclear deal. What about that, from your perspective?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, two things. One, I think it's very appropriate that we push back against the Iranians anytime they attempt to or attack our troops. And that's what the response from the Pentagon and from the president was just in the last 24 hours to respond to the drone attack. You know, Iran is very active in Syria, as we all know. They're active working through Syria to supply arms to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to put pressure on Israel. It is a very complicated fight. But I think it is very appropriate that we push back against Iran.\par \par \uc2 On the Iran Nuclear Deal, you know, I certainly understand that Iran is a malign actor in the region without question, but since we've withdrawn from the Iran Nuclear Deal, in addition to continuing to be a very malign actor in the region, they've started enriching uranium at a higher level, and they're closer to a bomb now than they've ever been.\par \par \uc2 So, if there is some agreement to get them further away from a bomb, then I'm kind of at a loss for why so many people are opposed to it. Yes, we'd love to stop Iran from messing around and all these other countries, but they're doing that whether they're building the bomb or not. So, we need to focus on both those issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, I start with common ground. I'm probably going to end with a little dispute, I'm sure. Congressman Waltz, you know, Iran has obviously been implicated in trying to assassinate former U.S. officials and they're malign actors around the region, as Chairman Smith mentions. What about this deal?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Yes, you're right, Bret. This is -- this is where we -- where we do disagree. I think the last thing we should be doing right now is the Iranians are targeting American officials, have been implicated in assassinations, right now as we speak, holding American citizens hostage in Evin Prison in Tehran, and now attacking the U.S. military in Syria through their proxies.\par \par \uc2 The last thing we should be doing is releasing billions and sanction relief right into the IRGC. Also, likely lifting secondary sanctions on the IRGC as a workaround from not dropping them from the terrorism list so that they can further finance their malign activity. It's the last thing we should be doing.\par \par \uc2 But Iran is pushing right now, because they know they can get away with it. They know this administration is desperate for any kind of deal. And by the way, it's the Russians that we're using is the intermediary. And if the reporting is correct, it will be the Russians who determine if we're all in compliance to then release the uranium if they decide a future administration isn't in compliance.\par \par \uc2 So, I think for all of those reasons, we have to go back to the maximum pressure campaign and bargain from a position of strength rather than the current position of weakness.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let me --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The maximum pressure -- the maximum pressure campaign has pushed Iran to being as close to having a nuclear bomb as they've ever been. I mean, I just don't see why letting Iran get a nuclear bomb puts us in a better position in dealing with Iran. As far as the details, there's no evidence yet the Russians are going to be there. We don't know the details of it. But to cut it off and say we shouldn't even talk about it as Iran dances towards developing a nuclear weapon, we should at least try to stop that. That's all I'm saying.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last word. You've got 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: The reason that -- the reason that they're close to having a bomb is because they absolutely violated the deal as exposed by Israeli intelligence --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We walked away from the deal. We walked away from it.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: --their trove of intelligence in 2018. And that's why we absolutely walk away from it because they weren't going to uphold their end of it. I don't trust him as far as I can throw them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we're going to follow all elements of this. We enjoy these bipartisan discussions. We started on common ground at least. Gentlemen, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks, Bret. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, concerns a massive patch of farmland could become grounds for a Chinese spying operation. And later Florida's primaries are revealing a hot-button issue on the November ballot.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A North Dakota community is fighting to stop a Chinese company from opening a plant just 20 minutes from the \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2  base with highly classified material there. They say it's a matter of national security correspondent Alexis McAdams shows us tonight from Grand Forks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): This more than 300 acres of North Dakota farmland could soon be home to a massive corn mill owned by the Chinese company \uc2 Fufeng\uc2 . But the community is wary because state officials say the company has ties to the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 BEN GRZADZIELEWSKI, RESIDENT, GRAND FORKS, NORTH DAKOTA: I'm not going to say that economic development isn't a good thing but at what cost?\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: The plant would only be two 12 miles from the Grand Fork's Air Force Base, home to top secret drone technology. The community fighting back suing the city to stop the project.\par \par \uc2 GRZADZIELEWSKI: In terms of Grand Forks, its water use pollution, smell. In terms of nationally, security.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: From yard signs to very tense council meetings.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No, we did not.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: The fight against \uc2 Fufeng\uc2  rages on making its way all the way to Washington DC. North Dakota Senator \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2  says the threats are very real.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 KEVIN CRAMER\uc2  (R-ND): Stuff that I can't share but stuff that people should be concerned about.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: It's not just here in North Dakota. According to the \uc2 USDA\uc2 , Chinese owners control at least $2 billion worth of agricultural land across the country. It's low compared to other foreign countries but growing. \uc2 Fufeng\uc2  would be the largest foreign private sector investment in the history of Grand Forks. Grand Forks Mayor Brandon Bochenski says this is not a done deal until the federal government weighs in.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON BOCHENSKI, MAYOR, GRAND FORKS: If they've truly believe it's a bad project and have the facts and the -- you know, the information to back it up, we'll certainly follow, you know, their lead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): So, the question is, Bret, what is next here in Grand Forks. Right now, the federal government is conducting a review to see if there's any national security threat with \uc2 Fufeng\uc2 's plans. If they find something, that could kill the deal, something the mayor says they are prepared for at this point. They're expecting that review to be back in the city's hands in the next month. And \uc2 Fufeng\uc2  won't comment until that's back. But Bret, for now, it's all still in play.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexis McAdams live in Grand Forks. Alexis, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, primary elections, New York, Florida, Oklahoma runoff are providing significant clues into the issues voters care about the most heading into November. First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 The Italian coast guard is launching an investigation into what caused the 130-foot super yacht to sink into the Mediterranean Sea. The Guard was dispatched after receiving a distress call finding the vessel partially submerged already nine miles off the coast. Nine people were rescued there.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London, one of the big stories there tonight. A 17-year-old is now the youngest person to fly solo around the world in a small aircraft. Mack Rutherford completed his flight today after landing in Bulgaria, where his journey kicked off five months ago. The Belgian British dual national also becomes the youngest person to circumnavigate the globe in a microlight play. How about that? Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We begin tonight's midterm watch with results from yesterday's primary contest in New York and Florida. New York City Democrats picked \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  over \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2  in a Congressional primary that featured the two powerhouse committee chairs competing for the same seat. In upstate New York, Democrat Pat Tyan defeated Republican Marcus Molinaro in a special election for New York's 19th district, giving Democrats a big win in a district believed to be a bellwether for November. We'll see. The race became a referendum on abortion after the high court's ruling there.\par \par \uc2 Now to Florida, the November stage is set for two high profile races in Florida. Democratic Congresswoman Val Demings will try to unseat Republican \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , and Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  will defend his post against former Republican governor, current Democratic Congressman \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Governor DeSantis is championing a string of huge wins over candidates he endorsed for top positions in education across the state. Correspondent Aishah Hasnie has that part of the story tonight from Tampa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: Because of your energy and enthusiasm, we were able to win school board victories all across the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A conservative wave in Florida schools, 25 out of the 30 school board candidates backed by Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  won their elections Tuesday. Conservatives ran on education over indoctrination, as parents push for more control over what schools are teaching their kids, zeroing in on Critical Race Theory, which links racial discrimination to America's foundations and legal system, and gender identity lessons.\par \par \uc2 Among the biggest GOP wins, DeSantis-backed candidates flipped the three to two liberal majority in the Sarasota school board do a four to one conservative majority despite being outspent.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Even though the union put in like $1 million, our candidates swept to victory.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: These typically sleepy races turn into political battlefields after COVID restrictions and school closures and amid the governor and the Republican state legislature's efforts to reform education. Democrats tried to fight back, saying politics has no place in schools. Now they have got just two months to stymie the governor's growing influence as he fights to keep his seat.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2 , (D-FL) FLORIDA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: If he wants to run on hate and culture wars and dividing people and making people hate each other, that's his turf. It's not mine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And, Bret, the governor is facing a number of challenges in court over his new laws. Just last week a judge blocked his Stop Woke Act, calling it unconstitutional. That would have restricted race-based conversations in businesses and in schools. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie in Tampa. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 There is a major uproar among parents tonight over massive failures to safeguard children after a twice-charged sex offender was allowed to work as a school guidance counselor in a Virginia school system for nearly two years after his first arrest. Correspondent Mike Emanuel has the story from Alexandria.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: A counselor at this Fairfax County Virginia middle school was able to keep working for about 20 months despite being arrested and convicted of a sex crime. Darren Lamar Thornton was arrested near Richmond in November 2020 for solicitation of prostitution from a minor. He was convicted on March 11th of this year and put on probation. Then he was arrested a second time on June 9th for the same charge. Police notified Fairfax County public schools by phone on June 10th. He was placed on unpaid leave a week later and was later fired. The Virginia attorney general says he has many questions about what went wrong.\par \par \uc2 JASON MIYARES\uc2  (R), VIRGINIA ATTORNEY GENERAL: Why did he serve no jail time? Why is it that Fairfax decided to supply unworking email addresses even though the Chesterfield Police Department called Fairfax County and asked them, hey, there was an arrest made, we need to notify you, how do we notify you?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin says Fairfax County mishandled the situation.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN, (R) VIRGINIA: This circumstance in Fairfax County is a colossal failure.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Dr. Michelle Reid is the new superintendent of Fairfax County public schools. She started her job on July 1st. Reid wrote to parents last week, quote, "I want to make this very clear. This entire situation is unacceptable from any perspective. We are deeply concerned about how this happened in one of our schools."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): Thornton, who worked here, was required to register as a sex offender. He did so in June after the second arrest. But authorities say he lied, claiming he was self-employed. We have asked the school system for more information about its background check process, but have not heard back. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Emanuel in Alexandria. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a tragic fentanyl overdose, why the victim's father says it could happen to anyone.\par \par \uc2 But, before we head to break, hall of fame \uc2 NFL\uc2  quarterback Len Dawson has died. Dawson's unmistakable swagger in helping the \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2  to their first Super Bowl back in 1970 earned him the nickname "Lenny the Cool." Dawson's family announced the Super Bowl MVP's death through a Kansas City based television station where he starred in his second career as a broadcaster. Len Dawson was 87.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In tonight's series on the poisoning of America, a Kentucky father shares his story after losing his son to a fentanyl overdose. The incident is becoming all too familiar in the Bluegrass State. And as senior correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  shows us, officials there believe this crisis is stemming from another.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Alan Reed regularly visits the grave of his son Brandon in Richmond, Kentucky. Brandon played baseball, father and son fished together. One day Brandon got a knee surgery that required multiple surgeries. He was prescribed oxycontin, an opiate for the pain.\par \par \uc2 ALAN REED, FATHER: There's a lot of parents could tell this very same story that I'm telling right now. The end result has been catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Brandon followed familiar destructive patterns of addiction but survived until October of 2021. He bought opiates from a woman and fentanyl was part of the mix. The dealer saved him from his first overdose of the night.\par \par \uc2 REED: She revived him with Narcan. Then she drove him to get his last dose. And then later on that night Brandon took his fatal dose at his girlfriend's apartment.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: In 2021, Kentucky crossed the grim threshold of more than 2,000 overdose deaths for the calendar year. The director of the Warren County drug task force says 72 percent of those death are from fentanyl. Two years ago, his task force seized 2,000 fentanyl pills in Kentucky. Last year that number doubled. This year the number has already tripled with more than 14,000 pills seized. He blames the unchecked flow of migrants at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 TOM LOVING, WARREN COUNTY, KENTUCKY, DRUG TASK FORCE: Along with that is the drug trafficking and flow of drugs into this country winding up in communities like Bowling Green, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Kentucky's senior senator puts the blame squarely on the president.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: This dereliction of duty at the border by the Biden administration is outrageous and unacceptable. And until they get control of the border, this is continuing to rise and create an ongoing catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): The investigation following the death of Brandon Reed resulted in the indictment of 10 people, some for organized crime, two directly related to selling him the drugs. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's student loan forgiveness plan and the pushback against it. Plus, midterm politics, a look ahead.\par \par \uc2 First, what some of our FOX affiliates around our country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in New York where a police officer is in critical condition tonight after he was attacked in the Bronx Tuesday. The 48-year-old officer was repeatedly struck in the head after three men jumped out of a car and began assaulting him. Police believe the group is also responsible for a string of robberies in the city.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Dallas from our affiliate FOX 4. One of the big stories there tonight, workers at the Dinosaur Valley State Park unearthed giant dino footprints dating back 113 million years ago. The tracks were uncovered in a drought-stricken river. Officials estimate the dinosaur stood about 15 feet tall, weighed close to seven tons as an adult. Pretty amazing.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I believe my plan is responsible and fair. It focuses the benefit on middle class and working families.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN, (D-MA): My reaction is that this is a great day.\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: The president can't do it. So that's not even a discussion. Not everybody realizes that, but the president can only postpone, delay, but not forgive.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR): It's also highly inflationary, highly inflationary, at a time when we still have record high inflation. And finally, it simply encourages colleges to raise tuition, harming a new generation of students.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CARDONA\uc2 , EDUCATION SECRETARY: It's about making sure we are taking care of Americans and investing in our economy and in our people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the president's plan, $10,000 debt, student debt forgiveness for people making $125,000 or less, $250,000 for a family, and then $20,000 forgiveness for anyone with a Pell Grant. The former head of the Economic Council of Advisers for President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2 , tweeting this, "Pouring roughly half trillion dollars of gasoline on the inflationary fire that is already burning is reckless. Doing it while going well beyond one campaign promise, ($10K of student loan relief) and breaking another, all proposals paid for, is even worse." He goes on in that tweet thread.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Morgan Ortagus, former State Department spokesperson, former White House press secretary \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 , and Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist. Ari, your thoughts on this? Obviously, there are political implications here, and there's thoughts on both sides.\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Bret, I just think this is wrong on every level. Is there any one of us who would want to teach our children that when you ask for a loan, the proper procedure is not to pay it back and then expect it to be just dismissed as if money is for free? When you ask for a loan, you have a fundamental obligation to pay back that loan. And the government should not do this.\par \par \uc2 Why are they doing this one? Why not taxpayers who have loans out to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 ? The \uc2 IRS\uc2  has told a lot of taxpayers that you owe us money and we are charging you interest on it. Should the government dismiss those? What about people who owe car companies for car rentals, for their car loans? What about people who own mortgage debt? Should that be forgiven? No.\par \par \uc2 The answer is that when you apply for a loan, you pay back your loan. And for the government to be the one dismissing this is just irresponsible, and it's also a fundamental redistribution of wealth from high school students, people with a high school diploma, to college students. It doesn't get more wrong.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Leslie, I want to get your response. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , you heard her in that montage. That was a year ago, saying the president doesn't have this authority. Today she said something different, saying the authority was found. I want to read this Education Department Office of Legal Counsel memo to then Education Secretary \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2  in which they write, "We believe the secretary does not have the statutory authority to cancel, compromise, discharge, or forgive on a blanket or mass basis principal balances of student loans and/or to materially modify the repayment amounts or terms thereof," suggesting that would have to be Congress that does that. This president says this is an emergency basis through the Department of Education because of COVID, even as its CDC is dialing back on the guidance about COVID and the emergency status of it. Your thoughts, Leslie?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: The emergency status and that one word make it a game changer as to whether the president has the authority, Education Department, without Congress. I don't always agree with Ari, but I will agree with one thing. Please, please, wipe out those debts that we, myself included, owe the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But in all seriousness, the president campaigned specifically and tweeted specifically about this dollar amount with student loan forgiveness when he was elected one. Two, less than two months ago, 55 percent of the American people supported student loan forgiveness, and on top of it this specific dollar amount. As a matter of fact, Republicans, 18 to 34 years of age, also support this. So this isn't something the president is doing against the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 So let's talk about pushback. It's never going to be good enough for some in my own party on the left and others who want the slate clean or they want $50,000. Something is better to nothing, to them I say. And then on the other side of the aisle, I'm sorry, Republicans have signed off on $2 trillion to give tax breaks to the wealthy, and they didn't blink an eye on. I knew there was going to be pushback on both sides. The president is not only keeping a promise, he's trying to help those that have been hurt along this pandemic. And \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said the money they would use can infuse the economy and can help with consumerism for them to buy more food, more clothes, cars, homes, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Susan Rice, the domestic policy adviser, used that same pushback on the hypocrisy point about Republicans and tax cuts. Every time we get to an issue that we have talked about before, and student loan debt forgiveness has come up a lot, we like to go back to our archives and look to our late colleague \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  and what he may have said about this. We found one. This is from 2013.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER\uc2 , SYNDICATED COLUMNIST: It's a classic example of a compassion -- the unintended consequences of compassion. It's a good idea until you realize, you showed earlier on the show, how much tuition has increased. And the reason is you get a huge amount of money from the federal government that the colleges understand supply and demand, allow them to increase their tuition. You showed that public institutions increased tuition by 27 percent since 07, and the more expensive private colleges about half of that. So, essentially, it's inflating the budgets of the universities. Everybody understands that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I just put up a graphic here, Morgan, and that's college tuition and fees since 1980, 1,200 percent increase over that 40 year span while inflation increased only about 232 percent. So, your thoughts on all of this, Morgan?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: First, thanks for letting me go after the legend, \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2 . No pressure there, Bret.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: If I can take a little bit more cynical view of this, if you look at voters 18 to 29. In the 2020 election, about 60 percent of those voters voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . In June a poll was done of that same age group, and he was around 22 percent approval rating. So that's a 40 point drop from a key constituent group. You look at Latino voters, similarly, probably another 40 percent drop in approval rating there. Similarly, black American voters down to 60 percent approval rating there. They went overwhelmingly for Biden.\par \par \uc2 So, when you go through and you look at this from a 2020 midterm election perspective, it's clear that they are trying to throw -- throw a bone to many voters to try to get them back, to try to get them to show up at the polls. But I think it's going to backfire on them because it's going to undermine the Inflation Reduction Act, which did nothing to actually reduce inflation, and it's going to be another $1 trillion potentially of spending on the books within a matter of weeks at a time when our economy just can't take it. No commonsense economist would say that this will work.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ari, one other student debt, total outstanding student debt graphic, only 26 percent of households with student debt have a graduate degree. But loans associated with graduate degrees account for nearly 50 percent of the total outstanding student loan debt. So, just from a pure political perspective, aren't you kind of making a bunch of people mad in the same time? It's a knife-edge decision here politically, but this seems like a big group that either didn't go to college or paid off their debts.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: That's why I think this is less a partisan issue than a right or wrong issue. It's also that transfer of wealth, as the I said earlier, from people who have just a high school degree to college people and people with master's degrees. Most Americans don't go to college, Bret. Most Americans have to pay their bills. So what this does, it says to most Americans there's a sliver, better educated than you, that you are going to start to pay for the bills they incurred. It's just wrong. You don't do this to our children. It's how you buy people off, pay people off, and hopefully win a midterm election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, thank you. We'll follow it and see where it goes.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute. Overseas, a Turkish vet saved the life of this stray dog by quickly digging her out of a landslide. But when the dog kept barking frantically despite her lack of injuries, he knew something was wrong, something was up. So a pair of good Samaritans kept digging for two hours until they found seven tiny pups safe and sound. Great story.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the deadline is up for that magistrate down in Florida, the search affidavit. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220825ei8o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden's Promise Of $10,000 Student Debt Relief Gets A Warning Even On The Liberal Side That It Could Worsen The Inflation; President Biden Is Bribing Ahead Of The Midterm Election; Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  Announced He Will Step Down In December And The Republicans Vow That They Will Investigate Him If They Take Back The House And Hold Him Accountable On What Happened During The Pandemic; Eyes On 2024? Liberal CA Gov Goes Moderate On Controversial Drug Bill; Woke Revolt: \uc2 Apple\uc2  Employees Protest Against Return To Office; Squash It! People Encourage To Kill Invasive Spotted Lanternfly\b0\par\par\uc2 Jesse Watters, Rachel Campos-Duffy, Jessica Tarlov; Dana Perino,  George Murdoch \par \uc2 8,509 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LEON PANETTA\uc2  , FORMER CIA DIRECTOR: So we are in, I think for a long period of war here that, who knows what the end results will be.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2  , thank you very much for joining us. The former defense secretary, former CIA director, former White House chief of staff and much more, \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2  . And on we go, here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters along with Rachel Campos-Duffy, Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino and Tyrus. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: -- with the plan to immediately provide $10,000 in debt relief of stimulus right now.\par \par \uc2 The legislation passed by the Democratic House calls for an immediate $10,000 forgiveness of student loans. It should be done immediately.\par \par \uc2 I am considering dealing with some debt reduction. I am not considering $50,000 debt reduction. But I'm in the process of taking a hard look at whether or not there are going to -- there will be additional debt forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Are you close to making a decision on student debt?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: President Biden expected to keep his word on canceling student debt, so get ready to subsidize someone's useless liberal arts degree. He is set to announce a major decision to erase $10,000 of student loans for earners making less than $125,000 per year. That's a lot. It's a move that won't actually erase debt, but instead transfer the burden to you, the taxpayer.\par \par \uc2 Even some libs think it's a bad idea. As Obama's treasury secretary, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  , points out, quote, "Student loan debt relief is spending that raises demand and increases inflation. It consumes resources that could be better used helping those who did not, for whatever reason, have the chance to attend college. It will also tend to be inflationary by raising tuitions." Biden's White House spin team trying to downplay that narrative.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: There's been a lot of commentary raised about potential inflationary effects of debt cancellation. I think what you really have to do when you're measuring this out is look at debt cancellation, but also look at debt restart. That is the end of the pause, restarting payments. Both of those have to be factored in when you're trying to understand the fiscal impact on overall inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But Biden's plan isn't good enough for the far left. Squad member \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  sounding off, "Student debt is a nearly $2 trillion crisis. @POTUS must #CancelStudentDebt. All of it." But everybody should be seeing this for what it is, a bribe. Young voters have soured on Biden's presidency. A pathetic 21 percent of 18 to 30 year old's give him a stamp of approval.\par \par \uc2 So, is this just a quick way for Joe to win them back? Dana, I mean, if you take out a loan and if you vote for a Democrat, you might get that loan paid back by us.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: This -- it is -- we knew that this was coming because the president kept kicking the can down the road. Even he has said this is just for politics, so last January. Everybody is saying this is a bad idea except for the people who are saying, no, really, come with us. Come over to the farther left side and that we'll make a big deal -- we'll make a big deal out of it.\par \par \uc2 They're basically writing off the middle class and the blue-collar workers, completely writing them off. You know, 47 percent of Americans have a four- year degree, only 13 percent have graduate degrees, but 50 percent of the student loan debt is held by people that have graduate degrees.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Graduate degrees.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, what happens to the guy who took out a loan to get, you know, F-150 so that he could take his tools around. That debt's never going to be canceled and it doesn't get cancelled. It just gets transferred to somebody else, which is -- they're transferring the debt to the guy with the F-150.\par \par \uc2 It is also inflationary. Why are we increasing -- why are we taking -- making a move that would increase inflation at this time? Why does that make any sense? It's also something that Congress should do. So, this is an illegal move. It is unethical. It is immoral. And they're not going to get the political payoff that they're looking for. They're not going to get the P.R. spin. They're just going to go through all the policy pain and they're not going to satisfy the left and they're not going to satisfy anybody on the right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And you're probably right about that. So, Tyrus, if you got a graduate degree in philosophy and you're making $124,000 a year --\par \par \uc2 GEORGE MURDOCH, FOX NEWS CONRIBUTOR: You've been doing your research. Well played, sir.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I mean, you're going to get some money paid off.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: Look, Socrates has said on several occasions, if it floats like a duck then it's probably a duck. I'm excited at the fact that at least he's smartening up a little bit. I wish he had the backbone he did 20 years ago or even 10 years ago where he could actually say, hey, this was a bad idea, we said it. We're not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 So, at first, it was awe (ph), then it was 50, now it's 10. So, I figure by the end of next week it'll be like $17.30. So, he's just going to keep getting lower and lower and lower and then he'll say, look, look what I did guys. I got you guys 14 cents back on your student because $10,000 on a student loan isn't anything anyways.\par \par \uc2 So, that's barely going to cover the late fees because most of them haven't paid it. And the fact that it's going to the 60 percent of the earners so that the little guy once again is being forced to pay for the big guy stuff. So, what it really is it's like for his lenders, his donors, here's a little -- here's some money back and hopefully he'll get it forward, so.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: So, this was Scranton Joe. This was the middle-class guy with the lunch pail and the \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  ticket. Does he even realize that he's just helping out these graduate degrees. I'm not going to say a bad word about them.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL CAMPOS-DUFFY, FOX NEWS HOST: I -- so, I have --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I want to so badly though.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: I have a graduate degree myself. I mean, I took out --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I have (inaudible). I paid it off.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yes, and I took -- I took out loans. Sean has a law degree. He took out loans. It took us 20 years. We had nine kids. Who in Congress is complaining the most about these? It's -- AOC doesn't have any kids and she's complaining about the loan. She's agitating, trying as you said, Dana, get more people on her side.\par \par \uc2 I really think our country needs to go back to the place where your word, your handshake, your signature meant something. You signed your name. You said you were going to do it. Do it. And you know what, I will tell you this, it felt so good when we paid it off. But now I kind of feel like a chump because they're going to get it, you know.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, $10,000 is a lot. I mean, it's a lot of money if somebody pays that off. And you're right. The guy --\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: But it's not going to pay it all off.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: For some people.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: So, there's still a lot more and people still have to pay.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't mean to disparage people with graduate degrees. I --\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, actually you meant only to disparage me, but not Dana and Rachel.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: I just said I have one.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I have no graduate degrees. Everybody else in my family has graduate degrees except me, but I'm the most successful. How about that? So --\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Oh my god.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Financially successful. Emotionally, not so successful