's forever irreparably damaged anyone, but I think obviously and you -- if you go back and people selectively, Neil, pull things out about me. I was also one of the people that said we've got to do everything we can to get the children back in school.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jessica, do you think that he is, like looking at the long arc of history, to get his legacy correct?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I hope so. This is a man who's worked for six presidents and someone who until a couple years ago, when I think the demonization of this health bureaucrat went completely haywire. The stuff that circulates on social media with him. The idea that I guess Republicans think it will be a good midterm strategy to run on. All of the congressional hearings that they're going to have on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  when people are concerned about kitchen table issues and things like that, you know, I welcome it.\par \par \uc2 But when I think about Dr. Fauci and I'm not saying that things were not mishandled and Rachel Walensky has been doing the circuit as well talking about what the CDC got wrong, I'll be thinking about 2014 when he publicly hugged Nina Pham, the Dallas-based nurse who contracted Ebola, the first person here. He personally treated her.\par \par \uc2 That's something he's famous for throughout his career, or what he did in the 1980s during the AIDS epidemic when those people were demonized, treated as outcasts. He lobbied the government alongside of activists to make sure that more people with AIDS could get into clinical trials and he saved countless lives there.\par \par \uc2 So, this move to always reduce something to the last minute in time and to forget decades of service for this man I think is a huge injustice. And again, it does not mean that I think that things were perfect, but it is a wildly unfair read of this man's career and I think it is an awful and painful end to someone who has given this country that many decades of service.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: Nixon was a great president until that whole Watergate thing.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Right. It was like he blew it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's this -- but it's not really --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: It was the biggest test.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Go ahead, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: It was the biggest test and I would argue with you that COVID is a kitchen table issue. We're still feeling the effects of it. And by the way, he wasn't just the doctor in America. He had international, for all the reasons you said, he had international power and fame. A 100 million people are now in poverty because of the lockdowns that he was behind.\par \par \uc2 People -- and again, look at how the people that he hurt. It's not just children. How many old people died of loneliness? How many people didn't get to go to their grandma's funeral or hold her hand or their mother or father? His big mistake was he didn't follow the science. The science of natural immunity. And if he had recognized natural immunity which was scientific and established at the very beginning, we would be in a different place today.\par \par \uc2 But being \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  means never having to say you're sorry because the media is fueling this god-like complex that he has. This lack of humility that you just saw right there. The guy thinks he's God. He's like an authoritarian monster and he led our country on a Chinese-style approach instead of an American style where we look at science, we consider all these great scientists that we have in our country including the ones who signed the Barrington Declaration.\par \par \uc2 He tried to shut them down, shame them, censor them. That's not America and that's Chinese. And he has to be very honest --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't know. The comparison to China is ludicrous.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: It is. And his --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Were you (inaudible) in your house and they left a piece of old fish outside of your door because they wouldn't let you out? Come on. The Chinese keep people in concentration camps, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Well, he was an apologist for the Chinese and was very non- transparent with the lab and the origins and we all need to look at -- and by the way, the last thing I want to say is the reason we need these hearings that it's smart for the Republicans politically, but more importantly it's important for the country. We don't want to repeat this and we need to put him in these hearings to get to the bottom of that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And then the CDC coming out with its final report soon, as you mentioned, \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2  is doing that so we'll be able to nitpick that as well. Coming up, California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  cracking down on a radical drug policy in his state, but is this moderate move part of his plan for higher office?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: All right, liberal California getting a major reality check on the radical policies from an unexpected place. Now, Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is pumping the brakes on legislation that would have set up drug dens in three Democrat-run cities. The plan was to let addicts shoot up at supervised injection sites. And Newsom apparently is concerned it could bring a world of "unintended consequences." No kidding.\par \par \uc2 But is there something larger at play here? It's no secret \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  has been measuring the drapes at the White House and moderating his positions on controversial issues is something he's going to have to do if he's serious about 2024. The latest poll out of his state shows California voters don't want Biden to run and favor Newsom over their former senator now VP, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 So, Dana, he -- this particular bill, when he was running for governor, he said, I'm very, very open to it