oming happy hours, playing online games, like Quiplash or Warzone, binge watching Tiger King on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 . That was fun.\par \par \uc2 So many people were vegging out in front of the screens for so many hours a day. They even had a name for it, quaran-stream. And they marveled at how wonderful and near total economic stoppage was for the climate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If there is a silver lining to this crisis, it's visible in the skies above China.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Falling pollution levels brought about by lockdowns may have been shining an impact on sunshine. Wild animals are now roaming city streets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How do we make sure these pollutions models don't rebound?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. The Branch Covidians, they wanted to change the American psyche. And in many ways, they succeed it. Students are suffering from loss learning and new anxieties. Employers are suffering, because they can't find workers. And many of those who do show up, they're not happy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I recently learned about this term called Quiet Quitting, where you're not outright quitting your job but you're quitting the idea of going above and beyond. You're still performing your duties, but you're no longer subscribing to the hustle culture mentality that work has to be your life. The reality is it's not. And your worth as a person is not defined by your labor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He's oozing masculinity. Maybe it doesn't define your work. But your paycheck should maybe be defined by how much you work. But prolonged COVID shutdowns and liberal work-from-home policies, they gave a lot of workers a notion that staying home was now their right. And who can blame them after all \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  Speaker Pelosi the ultimate queen of entitlement and privilege, she has allowed house to vote from home since the spring of 2020. This proxy voting scam was supposed to expire last week, but old nan just extended it for the 18th time, of course, because of the public health emergency. That same emergency, of course, didn't stop her from traveling to Taiwan or to Italy, and it's certainly not dangerous enough to cancel that congressional baseball game.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: I love baseball. We are season ticket holders, have been forever. And this game is so great, and people are so nice. That's what makes it great. But we're competitive, and it's great to be here. Have a little fun.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They were hoping for the masks there, but no luck. Just when Pelosi had her beauty parlor opened for her blowouts during the lockdowns, remember, rules and expectations do not apply to them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think that any of your colleagues are using proxy voting as an excuse to get out of showing up to work?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2 , (D-NY): No, I don't think so. August is usually a time for us to be in our districts, be on CODELs, or if we choose to take a vacation with our families or something like that, August is usually a time for that. That stuff is usually planned way in advance, so I'm sure there were many things that people couldn't reschedule.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's what we're doing it for the last two years, yes. What an example of these people are setting for America. Grocery store workers, airline employees, they have to show up. But the people's representatives can't be bothered. The swamp strikes again.\par \par \uc2 And federal workers, you're going to be shocked to know this, I know. They don't want to show up either. The Office of Personal Management even pushed telework in their recent guidance. OPM doesn't care about how telework affects the people who are paying the actual bills though. That's you. Have you tried to call a Medicare office lately? Questions about your passport renewal? Nightmare stories there.\par \par \uc2 But again, remember the bright side, if no one commutes, the planet is going to be cleaner. Yay. But the progressives didn't anticipate the unintended consequences of their green giddiness here. What did they think was going to happen to subways and urban life when fewer people actually commute into the cities? Even a small dip in ridership on the subway could send mass transit into bankruptcy. There's also a sociological aspect to the Sweden-like obsession with work-life balance says cultural observer Malcolm Gladwell.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MALCOLM GLADWELL, JOURNALIST: It's very hard to feel necessary when you're physically disconnected. I know it's a hassle coming to the office. But if you work -- if you're just sitting in their pajamas in your bedroom, is that the work-life you want to live? If we don't feel like we are part of something important, what's the point? You're not just doing this to get a paycheck. If it's just a paycheck, it's like what have you reduced your life to?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But the left just refuses to think about any of this in a rigorous way. They just declared that a four-day workweek is smarter and better for the climate. But have they stopped to ask that with more long weakens, won't Americans just choose to travel more? How is that good for the planet?\par \par \uc2 The fact of the matter is, what they really want, really want, is for businesses to have no choice. Eventually the Angle could see government mandates that force workers to stay home, or at least a certain percentage of them. Fate crises will be cited as a reason to take away your freedoms here. It's a climate emergency, it's a racism emergency, it's a health emergency. If it means making you poorer or lowering your standard of living, gutting America's productivity, so be it. It's not like the elites are going to be inconvenienced by any of it. What they've done to our public schools, our public safety, our borders, and our energy independence, they've done to our American work ethic.\par \par \uc2 But we can turn this around, we have to, by refusing to sit back and wait for someone else to do the hard work of saving what we love about America.\par \par \uc2 And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Texas Congressman Chip Roy who is pushing to end the proxy voting that I just told you about. Congressman Roy, it's a complete scam. Even Congressman Bowman admitted that people were using it to go on vacation. So do you have any doubt that Pelosi is just going to keep extending this until she is no longer speaker at this point?\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY, (R-TX): Of course, that's what she's going to do. We've had members of Congress vote from boats in their pajamas. They show up to committee hearings on these screens that we watch wearing pajamas. Last Friday I was with my family on vacation, on a rare break with my family. And I went to the airport, went to Savannah, I flew to D.C., and I got out and I did my job. Unfortunately, 158 of my colleagues did not. That is over one-third of the so-called people's house.\par \par \uc2 And importantly, when those people go up and they vote by proxy, they are lying. If they are signing a form saying they are doing it because of COVID. But you and I both know virtually all of them, if not all of them, were lying when they said it was due to COVID. I think that's a problem, and I think that's a very bad message to the American people.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I oppose proxy voting, I think we should end it if Republicans are in charge. I also think we should pass legislation to fire bureaucrats, the bureaucrats who aren't doing the job. I introduced a bill to do that.\par \par \uc2 And finally, Republicans should defund the bureaucracy. We should fire the \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. We should fire the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents who are abusing authority. We should fire the DHS goons who are going after Border Patrol instead of securing the border. Let's do our job.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, I have to you about Wyoming. We're watching Harriet Hageman's victory speech tonight. We may have her on in a few moments. But \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  actually got up and essentially compared herself to \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , who decided it was time to -- she, like Lincoln, in a Lincoln- esche moment, had to bring the country together and put country before party, given her quixotic revenge campaign against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Your reaction to that tonight?\par \par \uc2 ROY: I haven't been able to hear it because I was getting prepared for being on your show, but I caught a little bit of it. And look, the Declaration of Independence is very clear. The government derives its power from the consent of the governed. The people of Wyoming spoke tonight. And they spoke because they want the agenda that they saw it in terms of draining the swamp. They didn't want more of the swamp.\par \par \uc2 That's I think what this boils down to. History has a way of cleansing all of the noise. And when history is judged, when we look at this moment, it's about a change. It's about moving forward. It's about not 2022 or 2024, but 2026, when America turns 250 years old. Are we going to live free or are we going to continue to fund the bureaucracy, continue to expand government at the expense of our freedom? It is time for a change. That time is now, and I think the people are speaking across the country, and this is one example of it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I hope \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is watching tonight, because the Bush- Cheney era is over. Congressman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Mass is now woke, no joke. I'm going to explain, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): This primary election is over. But now the real work begins.\par \par \uc2 The great and original champion of our party, \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , was defeated in elections for the Senate and the House before he won the most important election of all. Lincoln ultimately prevailed. He saved our union, and he defined our obligation as Americans for all of history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Abraham Cheney there, that was her concession speech just a short while ago.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Charlie Kirk, Turning Point USA founder, author of "The College Scam." Charlie, you know this is life TV when I'm changing the topic right on the fly. It's like the good old radio days. You heard \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  on the Lincoln comparison. Your reaction tonight, and we may be joined by Harriet Hageman in a moment. But Charlie, your reaction?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE KIRK, TURNING POINT USA FOUNDER: I was just chuckling. I didn't know who I was listening to at first. And I said, is that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  comparing herself to \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 ? No way, are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 Really quick, look, tonight is the fall of a dynasty. It's a dynasty that thought of themselves as an oligarch, not part of our representative republic. It's the same thing that happened in Arizona, same thing that happened all across the country. You've seen the Bush dynasty, you've seen the McCain dynasty, you see all these different types of dynasties that are challenged by the people.\par \par \uc2 And it's really interesting, Laura, the people that scream the most about democracy, they are not very good at winning elections when they are actually put in the hands of the people. And I think \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is trying to turn a loss into a victory tonight, but this is a mass repudiation of dynastic, multigenerational rule, of endless foreign wars, of bad trade deals, of just looking down on the American people. Laura, I thought your opening of the eulogy was terrific. And we will see what \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  does next, maybe wants to represent her home state of Virginia or become a K Street lobbyist. But the people are back in charge in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm glad you mentioned the Virginia suburbs of D.C., because she does represent Fairfax County, Virginia. She represents them really well because they are super liberal. But that's not Wyoming, Charlie. You and I, we hung out in Wyoming not so long ago, and that's just -- even in Teton County, it's kind of liberal there, but the rest of Wyoming is just, that's cowboy land. That's classic America.\par \par \uc2 KIRK: That's right. That's exactly right. And \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  was more focused on a January 6th Committee, going on cable television, then representing the actual needs, wants, and interests of Wyoming. And so you're going to see the media have a difficult time with this because their new narrative is that the right is antidemocracy. You see it in all their articles. It's nauseating. What could be more democratic than going into a summer primary where one candidate has ubiquitous name I.D., is the daughter of a former vice president, has more money than the opponent, and then loses by double digits? How is that not democracy at its best?\par \par \uc2 And you're right, they have a rugged independent spirit. They love liberty and Wyoming. And this is a great watershed moment, again, of people saying that they want to be back in charge to restore the consent of the governed and representative government.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Only in Cheney's brain can you get thumped and then conclude from that that you are going to be the next president of the United States, to save the whole country.\par \par \uc2 KIRK: No, not just president. No, \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 . Like OK, yes, sure.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, the next surge in Iraq is going to save Iraq, too. Charlie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And up next, the big winner of the night, we are going to talk to Harry Hageman, who just beat \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , and her first stop is right here on THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now is Harriet Hageman, the Wyoming congressional candidate who just defeated \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . Harriet, first of all, congratulations. I remember talking to you before you decided you were going to throw your hat in the ring for real, and now, you've got it, girl. You're going to be the nominee for the party, and I can't imagine you won't be the next congresswoman from the great state of Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 HARRIET HAGEMAN, (R) UNSEATED \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 : Well, thank you, Laura, it's wonderful to be back with you. We started this year ago or about 11 months ago, and here we are with all of the hard work and the support of the great people across the state of Wyoming and all of our volunteers and supporters. We've been able to succeed.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Did \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  call you and congratulate you? How was the tone of the conversation?\par \par \uc2 HAGEMAN: I haven't had an opportunity to visit with her. We have been kind of wrapped up in all of the stuff associated with what's going on downstairs with our watch party, as you can imagine. We have several hundred people downstairs ready to celebrate, who have been celebrating. And so I have not been able to even look at my phone. So I don't know if she's called or not. So I don't really have anything to report on that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's interesting. Did you happen to hear that part of her speech was a continued vow, Harriet, to make sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never steps foot in the White House after what happened on January 6th, which was, I guess not surprising that she said that, but your reaction tonight?\par \par \uc2 HAGEMAN: Well, again, I have not had an opportunity to even see or hear what she had to say because I've been kind of focused on what's going on here. It doesn't surprise me that she would revert to those same old talking points, because that's really in large part what got her defeated.\par \par \uc2 She is not focusing on Wyoming. She's not focusing on our issues. She is still focusing on an obsession about President Trump. And the citizens of Wyoming, the voters of Wyoming sent a very loud message tonight. We have spoken. And that is not what we are interested in in terms of our lone congressional representative.\par \par \uc2 Wyoming is entitled to have a representative that represents our interests, listens to us, that addresses our issues. That isn't \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , and the fact that that's where she went back to with her speech tonight I think demonstrates that she really is listening to Wyoming now, she hasn't for quite some time. And that's why we needed to replace her. We need to have a representative in Wyoming who listens to us.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And, Harriet, what I was lucky enough to spend quite a bit of time in Wyoming in July, the things that I heard most often from folks there were inability to get workers, the cost of fuel, and the cost of just basic living, rent, and food. No one was focusing on Trump. I don't think anyone I talked to, that wasn't top of mind.\par \par \uc2 HAGEMAN: No. Inflation, illegal immigration, and protection of our energy jobs are the issues that are the most important to Wyoming. And accountability. We actually expect our representatives were present are interests. I've said that a couple of times now, but that's what I heard so much on the campaign trail. So I'm excited, thank you for having us. It's a wonderful evening.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Harriet, congratulations, we can't wait to see what the future holds for you. You worked hard this campaign. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Final thoughts on, yes, a historic night, when we return.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's called the House of Representatives. It's not the house of rich kids who bought seats with daddy's money. You should speak for the people of your district. Cheney speaks for a tiny number of rich people in the D.C. suburb, that's about it. But now the cowboy state will finally have someone who represents them, not someone who lectures them, not even a pretend \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Greg Gutfeld, and the gang, and they're always hilarious, they are up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220817ei8g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Gene Sperling, Senior Adviser to President Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne \par \uc2 2,561 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Meanwhile, President Biden taking a bit of a victory lap over what he called July`s outstanding jobs number, while acknowledging some Americans may not be feeling it with rising prices.\par \par \uc2 So, where do things stand?\par \par \uc2 Gene Sperling is a senior adviser to the president. And he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Gene, thanks a lot for joining us.\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I think what made it even more suspenseful is, late yesterday, the White House press secretary suggested the number was going to be 150,000.\par \par \uc2 Did you guys take a peek at the wrong number?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: There`s no peeks.\par \par \uc2 I think there were people predicting that it would be 250,000 to 150,000. But what we saw today was the big R, and that was resilience, resilience in this labor market, another 528,000, extremely strong manufacturing growth.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it really is something. There have been 3.3 million jobs created in the economy this year. Other than last year, that`s the most in the first seven months of our economy in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So, that -- of course, as the president said, it`s not all rosy. People are still dealing with higher -- prices that are too high, even with gas prices coming down.\par \par \uc2 But there is some comfort in the resilience of the job market, the historic number of people working, the historic number of jobs, and an unemployment rate that matches the lowest in the last 53 years.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know what is interesting, though, is, just last month, we finally recovered all the jobs that were lost at the beginning of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 As I go through it, there are some parts that bother me, and I`m wondering if you can help. Since May, black Americans have lost 301,000 jobs. What`s going on where this large segment of the population is not participating in this jobs boom?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Oh, boy, is that not the case.\par \par \uc2 It`s interesting. At this time after the last crisis we had, black unemployment was at 15.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: No, no, jobs, not unemployment, because the unemployment number, by the way...\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: No.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... is skewed, because hundreds of thousands of black Americans have left the labor market, I guess because they can`t find a job.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: No.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And I`m curious as to why that`s happening.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: I -- look, that number can move around month by month, but the overall story has just been an enormously equitable recovery, with significant gains in black -- jobs for African Americans, for new small businesses for black Americans.\par \par \uc2 And, again, the unemployment rate does reflect the fact that you`re seeing -- you actually saw a historic drop in the unemployment in 2021 and over the last year for African Americans.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Hispanic Americans actually went down to 3.9 percent unemployment.\par \par \uc2 So what`s actually striking about this recovery is how equitable it has been. It has truly lifted up all...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It`s not equitable if people are leaving the labor force.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: No, I think you`re...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Listen, I know -- if someone leaves the labor force, and the unemployment number goes down, I don`t see how that`s good because, theoretically...\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: No, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... 4.5 million people could leave the labor force, and you could say, guess where the unemployment number is? Zero. We should celebrate.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: And...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But should we?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: And the key word you said there was theoretically.\par \par \uc2 If you actually look at prime age labor force participation, age 25 to 54, it went up this month.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: It is actually at about the same rate that it was in 2019, higher in 2018.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So, we have we -- I do agree with you on one thing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: I think that there`s still further we can go on labor force participation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: But, when you look at prime age workers, it`s been a strong recovery, up this month, and about exactly where we were in 2019.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It has been...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... recovery. We`re back to what we lost. And I think that is something to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 But I do worry, because the mainstream media will never talk about black people in America suffering when a Democrat is in the White House. So I thought I would take this opportunity to ask why, in the last few months, we haven`t participated.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about the household survey, because it wasn`t as good.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: I guess I`m just not agreeing with that -- with your premise there.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I`m just -- these are \uc2 BLS\uc2  numbers.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: I think African American workers have benefited enormously in this recovery.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: These are just the \uc2 BLS\uc2  numbers I`m reporting, I`m talking about. They are not mine.\par \par \uc2 Household survey, this is intriguing. Full-time workers went down 71,000. Part-time went up 380,000. Multiple workers went up 92,000, to the most ever. And we heard from Uber early this week, and they have 76 percent increase in their drivers.\par \par \uc2 So there`s no doubt people are having to do these side hustles, multiple jobs. What`s going on here with -- again, it feels like a bifurcated recovery.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: You know, I just don`t agree with any of your premises here.\par \par \uc2 For example, if you look at, like, what scholars at Berkeley and Professor Dube at \uc2 University of Massachusetts Amherst\uc2  have found, they have actually found that, in the bottom 30 or 50 percent of the work force, you`re actually seeing real income gains, even with the too high, unacceptably high inflation that we have.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Absolutely right. Teenagers have the most, and even high schools.\par \par \uc2 I`m talking about the household survey. We`re talking about two different things.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you then, since we`re on income, great wage numbers.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: But I will tell you, if you want to get into household versus payroll, the payroll survey is -- the households is a sample.\par \par \uc2 The payroll survey actually looks at well over 100,000 firms that focus on a significant chunk of the American payroll. And it has shown consistently high job growth and high job growth across the board for low-income African American, Hispanic white workers.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of your race or zip code or gender...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: ... this has been a very, very strong jobs recovery.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Now, we can talk all we want about -- and I will agree -- nobody likes the fact that global -- that we have not escaped the global inflation that is out there or the ramifications of the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to the job market, I think you`re seeing strength across the board.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let`s talk about wages. They were revised higher. They came in better than expected today.\par \par \uc2 But, again -- and you just brought it up -- this inflation, this dark cloud of inflation, like the sword of Damocles. Real wages have been down every single month since the COVID relief bill was passed, that $1.9 trillion dollars. Now, I know there`s a debate on whether or not that exacerbated this, that sparked this inflation problem.\par \par \uc2 But it is an interesting coincidence that, since then, despite getting a bigger paycheck, when people go in the store, they walk out with fewer bags. What do you tell Americans?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Well, that`s why this president says every day that reducing prices and costs for families is his number one priority, because we get it.\par \par \uc2 It is -- I mean, there is -- there are studies that show that, for lower- income workers, that many of them do actually have real income gains, even including inflation.\par \par \uc2 But look, there`s no question there`s too many Americans in the situation that you have mentioned, that they have -- they are working, many of them been able to move to better jobs. Many of them are seeing their nominal wages going up.\par \par \uc2 But they`re giving away too much of that wage gain at the grocery line and the gas pump. It`s why we`re glad to see gas prices down over 90 cents.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: We`re glad to see it under $4 in 22 states right now.\par \par \uc2 But look, when it comes to the fact that we`re not escaping -- we have not escaped the global inflation, that it`s hitting Americans at the gas pump and the grocery line, and that -- that`s a challenge.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So...\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Still said, it is good that we have so many Americans working, so many people on the job, more people -- more jobs in the U.S. economy right now than ever in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 That`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I agree a million percent. I want -- that`s why participation is the first thing I look at. And when I see it go down month after month in a strong jobs market, I think we should have more jobs.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And I think we should have more people born in this country, so we can have more jobs and we can stay on top.\par \par \uc2 Let me -- let`s get back to the inflation thing for one second, Gene. Gene, let me just...\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: But just for the record, just for the record, labor force participation for those 24 to 54, what`s called prime age, did tick up.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK. OK.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So -- it did for older workers too. So, I just don`t want you -- I just want to paint a balanced picture here. We would like to see higher labor force participation as well.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 If you want to paint a balanced picture, labor force participation, period, went down again for the second month in a row, and it`s significantly lower than it was in February 2020. That`s fine.\par \par \uc2 The inflation problem. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is out this week, big time. I mean, starting over the weekend, Neel Kashkari, he pushed back against Jay Powell`s notion of a neutral -- then you had -- I mean, what was crazy about it, the doves, the doves were hawks, right? They went out there. \uc2 Mary Daly\uc2  made some unfortunate comments.\par \par \uc2 What does the White House -- is the White House concerned that the Fed is going to go too far? We know that maybe that`s the only way we can bring inflation down. Do you want to see this Fed become very, very aggressive in bringing down inflation, so that people can actually enjoy these nominal gains?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: You know, I think you know what my answer is. It`s the same answer every single member of President Biden`s economic team is going to give, which is that we respect the independence of the Fed.\par \par \uc2 It is not our job to critique what they do. We want them to have the independence to do what they think is best to tamp down on inflation, even if those are some tough choices.\par \par \uc2 I will say, five -- we have had at least five presidents over the last 50 years who`ve chosen to berate the Fed at times of high inflation, when they`re making tough choices. We`re making the decision that we want to respect their independence.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So I will come back someday when I`m no longer at the White House, and I will give you all my thoughts.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, that`s not an area that we enter into.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let me ask you about the spending bill.\par \par \uc2 Again, we have quibbled over numbers that are in the \uc2 BLS\uc2  report. So I don`t want to debate on the inflation front. I do want to ask, though, about, historically, when you have had Democrats and fellow presidents who said you don`t raise taxes in this sort of environment. We know the names.\par \par \uc2 And what do you what do you make of that? A lot of folks in your own party believe this is another mistake, like the $1.9 trillion COVID relief package.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: You know, first of all, really, nobody`s rate is being raised.\par \par \uc2 What you`re trying to do is make sure that people are paying a basic minimum. I mean, all the corporate tax provision here does, it says is that if you report over a billion dollars to your shareholders, to the market, if you report over a billion dollars in profits to your shareholders and the market, you should pay at least 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 I think most people who hear that who are paying Social Security and Medicare payroll taxes, et cetera, would say, why only 15 percent? Well, it`s at least a basic minimum...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: ... so you don`t have the situation President Biden talks about repeatedly of 55 major fortune 500 companies with major profits paying zero taxes.\par \par \uc2 So I think there is not going to be much opposition among Democrat or Republican voters to making sure that companies that are reporting over a billion in profits have a basic minimum of 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, Senator Sinema has probably had some issues with it. We will see what the final version is.\par \par \uc2 But I got to go, but here`s the thing. You remember the AMT tax, Alternative Minimum Tax? It was a op-ed. I think it was in \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , 150 American families not paying taxes. Let`s go get them. And, of course, maybe 20 years later, 40 million people slammed by the same tax. These things can be a slippery slope, right?\par \par \uc2 They can be a slippery slope, a Pandora`s box.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: If you want to talk about -- let`s go -- I`d love -- we will come back and go into details of the tax provisions in the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let`s do that.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: And what you will see is tax incentives for tens of millions of Americans to get lower energy and get a tax incentive to do it, and to buy a whole set of consumer products.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So let`s talk -- we will talk about that.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s going to be more a talk about middle-class tax relief and making sure large corporations are at least paying a basic minimum and not getting away scot-free.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Gene Sperling, you have been fantastic. I appreciate you answering the questions.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And I look forward to speaking to you again.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Likewise.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All righty.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Take care.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220806ei850002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Buyout Saga Continues; Greg Abbott vs. Eric Adams; Interview With Sen. Dan Sullivan (R-AK); Tensions With China; Kyrsten Sinema Signs on to Spending Bill; Jobs Surge; Interview With Senior Adviser to President Biden Gene Sperling\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne, Kelly O'Grady, Gianno Caldwell, Nate Foy, Aishah  Hasnie, David Asman, Hillary Vaughn, Peter Doocy \par \uc2 9,178 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: FOX on top of jobs surging in July, 528,000 created. That was more than double what was expected, the unemployment rate dipping to 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 But with jobs moving, will the Fed be forced to keep hiking? And does Washington really need to keep spending? Senate Democrats setting up a vote on that tax-and-spend bill this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 We're going to be talking about all of this with a senior adviser to the president. Gene Sperling is coming up in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's go to Peter Doocy, who is at the White House with the latest -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And here at the White House, they think this jobs report is just another reason not to worry about a recession.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: That phrase, make it in America, the cornerstone of my economic plan. And today's report proves make it in America isn't just a slogan. It's my administration. It's a reality.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But former Obama economic adviser \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  writes: "Uncomfortably hot jobs report, 528,000 jobs added and unemployment rate falls to 3.5 percent. What worries me regarding inflation is average hourly earnings were up at 5.8 percent in June -- in July, June revised to 5.4, up from 3.8 percent. The wage moderation we were all discussing last month was simply wrong data."\par \par \uc2 But White House officials are shrugging off those fears, saying they still trust the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to adjust rates as needed to address inflation. And they claim there's more good news in 50 straight days of gas prices falling, even though they are still $1.72 a gallon more than when President Biden got here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: You want to talk to a nurse or a teacher or a firefighter if having that little bit of breathing room doesn't matter to them?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Are nurses and teachers and firefighters saying gas prices are only $4.11? Isn't it great?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I'm saying that that -- even that little bit of breathing room matters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And President Biden wanted to focus entirely on the economy. Today, we heard him talk about that. He is not addressing any of the potential trouble brewing with China and Taiwan -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Peter, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So a vote on the Democrats' $433 billion spending bill could come as early as tomorrow, this after Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  signals that she is on board.\par \par \uc2 To Hillary Vaughn, who's on Capitol Hill on where this spending push stands -- Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well over 230 economists are trying to warn Congress, do not pass the Inflation Reduction Act, because they warn that this bill actually will do the opposite, writing in a letter to Congress this -- quote -- "The inaptly named Inflation Reduction Act of 2022 would do nothing of the sort. It would create immediate inflationary pressures."\par \par \uc2 But Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is brushing off those concerns, telling me this afternoon they're wrong.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: There's a chance that these people are right. Is now the right time to do this, considering how high inflation is?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHARLES SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): They're wrong. It's obvious that it will. You're cutting prescription drug costs. We're cutting energy costs, and we are -- we are actually increasing -- we're spending $300 billion on reducing the deficit.\par \par \uc2 So, it's as plain as the nose on your face that this will reduce inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: But Senate Republicans are not convinced, saying this whole deal is misleading, telling me it does not reduce the deficit if programs are made permanent.\par \par \uc2 And they are plotting to retaliate, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  saying today Republicans are not going to make the vote-a-rama, where senators can offer an unlimited number of amendments to the bill, go quickly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): So what will vote-a-rama be like? It will be like hell. They deserve this. So, to Manchin in Sinema, I admire you at times. And I oppose you now.\par \par \uc2 And I think what you're doing is that you're empowering the most radical people in this country to tax and spend at a time we can ill afford it. And I think they need to be held accountable. And I intend to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And, Charles, it's not just Republicans looking to make major changes to this bill.\par \par \uc2 Senator Bernie Sanders is not a fan. He doesn't think it goes far enough. So he will also be trying to make his own tweaks -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hillary, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, President Biden taking a bit of a victory lap over what he called July's outstanding jobs number, while acknowledging some Americans may not be feeling it with rising prices.\par \par \uc2 So, where do things stand?\par \par \uc2 Gene Sperling is a senior adviser to the president. And he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Gene, thanks a lot for joining us.\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I think what made it even more suspenseful is, late yesterday, the White House press secretary suggested the number was going to be 150,000.\par \par \uc2 Did you guys take a peek at the wrong number?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: There's no peeks.\par \par \uc2 I think there were people predicting that it would be 250,000 to 150,000. But what we saw today was the big R, and that was resilience, resilience in this labor market, another 528,000, extremely strong manufacturing -- manufacturing growth.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it really is something. There have been 3.3 million jobs created in the economy this year. Other than last year, that's the most in the first seven months of our economy in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So, that -- of course, as the president said, it's not all rosy. People are still dealing with higher -- prices that are too high, even with gas prices coming down.\par \par \uc2 But there is some comfort in the resilience of the job market, the historic number of people working, the historic number of jobs, and an unemployment rate that matches the lowest in the last 53 years.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know what is interesting, though, is, just last month, we finally recovered all the jobs that were lost at the beginning of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 As I go through it, there are some parts that bother me, and I'm wondering if you can help. Since May, black Americans have lost 301,000 jobs. What's going on where this large segment of the population is not participating in this jobs boom?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Oh, boy, is that not the case.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting. At this time after the last crisis we had, black unemployment was at 15.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: No, no, jobs, not unemployment, because the unemployment number, by the way...\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: No.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... is skewed, because hundreds of thousands of black Americans have left the labor market, I guess because they can't find a job.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: No.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And I'm curious as to why that's happening.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: I -- look, that number can move around month by month, but the overall story has just been an enormously equitable recovery, with significant gains in black -- jobs for African-Americans, for new small businesses for black Americans.\par \par \uc2 And, again, the unemployment rate does reflect the fact that you're seeing -- you actually saw a historic drop in the unemployment in 2021 and over the last year for African-Americans.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Hispanic Americans actually went down to 3.9 percent unemployment.\par \par \uc2 So what's actually striking about this recovery is how equitable it has been. It has truly lifted up all...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's not equitable if people are leaving the labor force.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: No, I think you're...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Listen, I know -- if someone leaves the labor force, and the unemployment number goes down, I don't see how that's good, because, theoretically...\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: No, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... 4.5 million people could leave the labor force, and you could say, guess where the unemployment number is? Zero. We should celebrate.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: And...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But should we?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: And the key word you said there was theoretically.\par \par \uc2 If you actually look at prime age labor force participation, age 25 to 54, it went up this month.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: It is actually at about the same rate that it was in 2019, higher in 2018.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So, we have we -- I do agree with you on one thing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: I think that there's still further we can go on labor force participation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: But, when you look at prime age workers, it's been a strong recovery, up this month, and about exactly where we were in 2019.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It has been...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... recovery. We're back to what we lost. And I think that is something to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 But I do worry, because the mainstream media will never talk about black people in America suffering when a Democrat is in the White House. So I thought I would take this opportunity to ask why, in the last few months, we haven't participated.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about the household survey, because it wasn't as good.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: I guess I'm just not agreeing with that -- with your premise there.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm just -- these are \uc2 BLS\uc2  numbers.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: I think African-American workers have benefited enormously in this recovery.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: These are just the \uc2 BLS\uc2  numbers I'm reporting, I'm talking about. They are not mine.\par \par \uc2 Household survey, this is intriguing. Full-time workers went down 71,000. Part-time went up 380,000. Multiple workers went up 92,000, to the most ever. And we heard from Uber early this week, and they have 76 percent increase in their drivers.\par \par \uc2 So there's no doubt people are having to do these side hustles, multiple jobs. What's going on here with -- again, it feels like a bifurcated recovery.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: You know, I just don't agree with any of your premises here.\par \par \uc2 For example, if you look at, like, what scholars at Berkeley and Professor Dube at \uc2 University of Massachusetts Amherst\uc2  have found, they have actually found that, in the bottom 30 or 50 percent of the work force, you're actually seeing real income gains, even with the too high, unacceptably high inflation that we have.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Absolutely right. Teenagers have the most, and even high schools.\par \par \uc2 I'm talking about the household survey. We're talking about two different things.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you then, since we're on income, great wage numbers.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: But I will tell you, if you want to get into household vs. payroll, the payroll survey is -- the households is a sample.\par \par \uc2 The payroll survey actually looks at well over 100,000 firms that focus on a significant chunk of the American payroll. And it has shown consistently high job growth and high job growth across the board for low-income African-American, Hispanic white workers.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of your race or zip code or gender...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: ... this has been a very, very strong jobs recovery.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Now, we can talk all we want about -- and I will agree -- nobody likes the fact that global -- that we have not escaped the global inflation that is out there or the ramifications of the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to the job market, I think you're seeing strength across the board.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let's talk about wages. They were revised higher. They came in better than expected today.\par \par \uc2 But, again -- and you just brought it up -- this inflation, this dark cloud of inflation, like the sword of Damocles. Real wages have been down every single month since the COVID relief bill was passed, that $1.9 trillion dollars. Now, I know there's a debate on whether or not that exacerbated this, that sparked this inflation problem.\par \par \uc2 But it is an interesting coincidence that, since then, despite getting a bigger paycheck, when people go in the store, they walk out with fewer bags. What do you tell Americans?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Well, that's why this president says every day that reducing prices and costs for families is his number one priority, because we get it.\par \par \uc2 It is -- I mean, there is -- there are studies that show that, for lower- income workers, that many of them do actually have real income gains, even including inflation.\par \par \uc2 But, look, there's no question there's too many Americans in the situation that you have mentioned, that they have -- they are working, many of them been able to move to better jobs. Many of them are seeing their nominal wages going up.\par \par \uc2 But they're giving away too much of that wage gain at the grocery line and the gas pump. It's why we're glad to see gas prices down over 90 cents.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: We're glad to see it under $4 in 22 states right now.\par \par \uc2 But, look, when it comes to the fact that we're not escaping -- we have not escaped the global inflation, that it's hitting Americans at the gas pump and the grocery line, and that -- that's a challenge.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So...\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Still said, it is good that we have so many Americans working, so many people on the job, more people -- more jobs in the U.S. economy right now than ever in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 That's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I agree a million percent. I want -- that's why participation is the first thing I look at. And when I see it go down month after month in a strong jobs market, I think we should have more jobs.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And I think we should have more people born in this country, so we can have more jobs and we can stay on top.\par \par \uc2 Let me -- let's get back to the inflation thing for one second, Gene. Gene, let me just...\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: But just for the record, just for the record, labor force participation for those 24 to 54, what's called prime age, did tick up.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK. OK.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So -- it did for older workers too. So, I just don't want you -- I just want to paint a balanced picture here. We would like to see higher labor force participation as well.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 If you want to paint a balanced picture, labor force participation, period, went down again for the second month in a row, and it's significantly lower than it was in February 2020. That's fine.\par \par \uc2 The inflation problem. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is out this week, big time. I mean, starting over the weekend, Neel Kashkari, he pushed back against \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 's notion of a neutral -- then you had -- I mean, what was crazy about it, the doves, the doves were hawks, right? They went out there. \uc2 Mary Daly\uc2  made some unfortunate comments.\par \par \uc2 What does the White House -- is the White House concerned that the Fed is going to go too far? We know that maybe that's the only way we can bring inflation down. Do you want to see this Fed become very, very aggressive in bringing down inflation, so that people can actually enjoy these nominal gains?\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: You know, I think you know what my answer is. It's the same answer every single member of President Biden's economic team is going to give, which is that we respect the independence of the Fed.\par \par \uc2 It is not our job to critique what they do. We want them to have the independence to do what they think is best to tamp down on inflation, even if those are some tough choices.\par \par \uc2 I will say, five -- we have had at least five presidents over the last 50 years who've chosen to berate the Fed at times of high inflation, when they're making tough choices. We're making the decision that we want to respect their independence.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So I will come back someday when I'm no longer at the White House, and I will give you all my thoughts.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, that's not an area that we enter into.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let me ask you about the spending bill.\par \par \uc2 Again, we have quibbled over numbers that are in the \uc2 BLS\uc2  report. So I don't want to debate on the inflation front. I do want to ask, though, about, historically, when you have had Democrats and fellow presidents who said you don't raise taxes in this sort of environment. We know the names.\par \par \uc2 And what do you what do you make of that? A lot of folks in your own party believe this is another mistake, like the $1.9 trillion COVID relief package.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: You know, first of all, really, nobody's rate is being raised.\par \par \uc2 What you're trying to do is make sure that people are paying a basic minimum. I mean, all the corporate tax provision here does, it says is that if you report over a billion dollars to your shareholders, to the market, if you report over a billion dollars in profits to your shareholders and the market, you should pay at least 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 I think most people who hear that who are paying Social Security and Medicare payroll taxes, et cetera, would say, why only 15 percent? Well, it's at least a basic minimum...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: ... so you don't have the situation President Biden talks about repeatedly of 55 major fortune 500 companies with major profits paying zero taxes.\par \par \uc2 So I think there is not going to be much opposition among Democrat or Republican voters to making sure that companies that are reporting over a billion in profits have a basic minimum of 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, Senator Sinema has probably had some issues with it. We will see what the final version is.\par \par \uc2 But I got to go, but here's the thing. You remember the AMT tax, Alternative Minimum Tax? It was a op-ed. I think it was in \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , 150 American families not paying taxes. Let's go get them. And, of course, maybe 20 years later, 40 million people slammed by the same tax. These things can be a slippery slope, right?\par \par \uc2 They can be a slippery slope, a Pandora's box.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: If you want to talk about -- let's go -- I'd love -- we will come back and go into details of the tax provisions in the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let's do that.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: And what you will see is tax incentives for tens of millions of Americans to get lower energy and get a tax incentive to do it, and to buy a whole set of consumer products.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: So let's talk -- we will talk about that.\par \par \uc2 But I think it's going to be more a talk about middle-class tax relief and making sure large corporations are at least paying a basic minimum and not getting away scot-free.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Gene Sperling, you have been fantastic. I appreciate you answering the questions.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And I look forward to speaking to you again.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Likewise.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All righty.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: Take care.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Want to bring in our own David Asman now.\par \par \uc2 So the White House touting the strong jobs report, of course. It's a great number.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But there are concerns about inflation, legitimate concerns about inflation. And there's a lot of concerns about this spending bill and taxing bill tomorrow as well.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Well, and of course, the White House brings out their series of experts and the Republicans bring out their. Each one conflict with each other.\par \par \uc2 I must say, though, that some of there -- the people that they bring out, like Mark Zandi, for example, he over and over again have got it dead wrong on inflation for the past year-and-a-half. Back in June 2021, he wrote, by the fall or end of the year, prices will be coming back to earth.\par \par \uc2 Well, from the time he wrote that to the end of the year, inflation went up 40 percent, from 5 percent up 7, 7.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: So they -- their experts very often get it wrong.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is Americans don't want it. You look at the polls, the pop- up polls show that Americans don't believe the rhetoric that this is going to decrease inflation. Some of the people who actually support the bill say it's not going to do anything for inflation over the next five years.\par \par \uc2 Well, what are Americans supposed to do over five years, just twiddle their thumbs as they to the grocery store and can't afford to buy as much as they used to?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 I remember when Build Back Better was on the table, right? And they were doing these surveys. And some of the things that shocked me -- I'm talking polls from \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , ABC, CNN polls. People were saying they were afraid for their family's health, financial health, with another one of these packages.\par \par \uc2 Like, finally, this notion of free money, they started to realize it's not.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I mean, if I get more money, but everything else costs more, it defeated the purpose.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned to Gene -- I mean, he didn't hit it head on, because he doesn't want to -- the fact that despite wage increases of 5 percent over the past year-and-a-half or so, when you have 9 percent inflation, you're down 4 percent. And you're spending $4,000 to $5,000 to $6,000 more on gas, on food, on a whole host of things.\par \par \uc2 Now, maybe you will get tax deductions from this 