terms? That's coming up. We're going to have a debate on that later in the hour.\par \par \uc2 Also, China's war games, they're still going on. They have also cut off dialogue with the U.S. We want to find out what this is all about.\par \par \uc2 What's going on here, Aishah?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, China just keeps taking action after action after action. And while the White House calls it all irresponsible, some on the Hill don't believe that we're doing enough to push back. More on that straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: China firing up 100 warplanes and military exercises around Taiwan today.\par \par \uc2 As the dangers pile up over there, China issuing a new warning for us here.\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Aishah Hasnie with the very latest from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hey there, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Look, believe it or not, there is bipartisan support here on the Hill on how to handle China and this whole Taiwan situation. But it's not just -- it's just not jibing, matching up with what the White House is putting out.\par \par \uc2 For example, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  this morning pretty upset over reports that the administration is lobbying Democrats right now to delay passing a bipartisan bill on Taiwan that he and Democrat Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  co- sponsored that would not only send billions in security aid to the island, but also make it a non-\uc2 NATO\uc2  ally.\par \par \uc2 Senator Graham told me this is a huge mistake.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: They want us to go away. And we're not going away. They're trying to undercut congressional efforts to be more favorable to Taiwan and stronger against China, and it's the worst possible signal to send China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So the administration has also postponed another Minuteman III intercontinental ballistic missile test because of tensions with China.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, a missile test was canceled because of tensions with Russia. Senator Tom Cotton sent a letter to Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , asking how long this administration plans to allow China and Russia to dictate our missile test schedule, while Congressman \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2 , the top Republican on House Armed Services, called the delays -- quote -- "weak- kneed pearl-clutching attempts at appeasement that will only invite more aggression from our adversaries."\par \par \uc2 Even Democrat Senator Richard Blumenthal is saying that there is no cooling it now on these Taiwan tensions. And, really, there's only one option left Charles and that is to arm Taiwan to support it to deter China from moving in -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Aishah, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The read on all of this now from a member of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 , Alaska Republican Senator Dan Sullivan.\par \par \uc2 Senator, it's -- certainly, China's belligerence has been on display for quite a few years. But it feels like the message they're sending in the last few days is that we can compete with you on the battlefield. How unnerved should we be?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DAN SULLIVAN (R-AK): Look, Charles, I think that what you're seeing in the Taiwan Strait, what you're seeing in Ukraine by Russia is this new era of authoritarian aggression led by the dictators \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And they're increasingly isolated, increasingly dangerous. They base their actions on historical grievances. They're paranoid about their democratic neighbors, and they are willing to use military force against them.\par \par \uc2 What we need to do is show strategic resolve, confidence, but recognize that this new era of authoritarian aggression is going to be with us for decades. And we need to play to our strengths.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of comparative advantages, relative to the dictatorships in these two countries, a lethal military, our incredible energy and natural resources, our network of allies, our dynamic economy.\par \par \uc2 But what the Biden administration needs to do is play to these.\par \par \uc2 Let me give you a really big one. The president now in the last two years has put forward two defense budgets that are cuts, last year and this year.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: That is exactly the wrong signal to send to these dictators.\par \par \uc2 So these are the big strategic advantages we have. But we need to be wise enough to promote them and strengthen them. That's not happening in a lot of areas with this administration.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let me let me push back a little on two points you made.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: First, the isolation of these two, Putin and Xi.\par \par \uc2 There's been a lot of talk recently of this BRICS economic coalition, which was popular a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And now, apparently, Iran wants to join. Some other nations have said, hey, we'd like to join as well.\par \par \uc2 India has been buying Russian oil. Yes, they're a democracy, but they really don't seem so eager to go against China and/or Russia.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, then, on the comparative advantages, yes, we have them, but we don't exploit them. Yes, we talk about rare earth. It's really not rare.\par \par \uc2 The only reason we don't have it is because we won't drill for it. And now we're on the verge, we're on the verge of destroying our crude oil industry and handing over our entire energy independence to China, who controls E.V. batteries.\par \par \uc2 So I'm a little concerned about these things.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: I'm hugely concerned.\par \par \uc2 And you put your finger on it. Let me give you a -- you mentioned it. I mentioned it. A huge strategic advantage we have over certainly China, but even relative to Russia, is our energy resources. If you read the intel, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is very concerned about American energy dominance.\par \par \uc2 And Putin knows that that's a great way for us to undermine his power as well. So what do you have? You have an administration that's actually attacking our own energy sector. So this is what I mean. We have incredible resources. We have critical minerals.\par \par \uc2 Alaska, my great state, has critical minerals, rare earths. The same day President Biden held a critical minerals summit was the day that his Department of Interior reversed and shut down the Ambler Road and mining district project in Alaska. It makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's nuts. It's nuts.\par \par \uc2 But, sir, let me..\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: But I -- well, look, we have the power. We have the advantage. We just got to be wise enough to utilize it. This administration isn't.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: No, it's just -- I'm afraid we're going to give away something that is so precious.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to ask, and I have only got a minute or so.\par \par \uc2 West Virginia Democratic Senator Joe Manchin may be responsible for tomorrow's spending bill, but he did help Republicans pass something...\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... that President Biden apparently is no fan of. And, Senator, you were a sponsor of it. So tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: Well, this is our resolution that passed on the Senate floor in a bipartisan manner 50-47 to rescind the Biden administration's regulations they put out in April that would shut down the ability to build things.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: It was a reg from the Biden White House.\par \par \uc2 We have the power under the Congressional Review Act to rescind. And we were able to do it. Now, Biden says he's going to veto my bill if it gets to his desk.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: But I will tell you this. We had all the critical building trades in America.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Senator, I got to let you go. I'm coming up on a hard break.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to say, congratulations. I know you're a tough fighter, and we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right,Republican Governor Greg Abbott calling the first Texas bus to migrants to arrive in New York. Mayor Adams in New York City just calling it disgusting.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with Brandon Judd.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The border battle revving up, as Governor Greg Abbott touts the first migrant bus from Texas to arrive in New York City.\par \par \uc2 And ahead of a spending vote tomorrow, the new inflation warning from more than 200 economists today. It's going to scare you a little bit.\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Texas Republican Governor Greg Abbott calling this the first bus of migrants from his state to New York City.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy is outside the Port Authority bus depot, where their arrival took place earlier today -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 That bus was carrying about 50 migrants. It arrived here in New York City at 7:00 a.m. The majority of the migrants are from Venezuela, and some of them described the horrific experiences that they have had to endure before getting here. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): First border that I crossed was from Colombia to Panama. The third day, heavily armed citizens caught us. They kidnapped us for four days. There were a lot of kids and woman who weren't raped.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Charles, you can't even imagine. You can hear more about their journeys tonight at 7:00.\par \par \uc2 But you see here this was this morning. The group was met by 10 volunteers. They were given clothes, sandwiches and water. One child we know arrived with diabetes and did not have insulin. So volunteers were trying to help that child.\par \par \uc2 Here's White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  reacting to the bus' arrival today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Latest stunt from the governor, another stunt of busing -- busing desperate migrants across the country. And he's using them as a political -- as a political ploy. I mean, this is what he's been doing. And it's shameful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Charles, of course, the other city receiving migrants is Washington, D.C. This has been going on for months.\par \par \uc2 And, today, we're learning the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  has rejected a request by Mayor Muriel Bowser there. She wanted National Guard support as more migrants arrive from Texas. And we know the Governor Abbott has said over 6,500 have arrived in D.C. since April. Mayor Bowser says she plans on tweaking that request and is still asking for more help from the Biden administration, as is Mayor Adams here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 As for the migrants that arrived here today, we know roughly half of them are being bused to other cities in the country. And half of them will rely on the resources here in New York.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Nate Foy in New York City, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With me now to discuss, National Border Patrol Council President Brandon Judd.\par \par \uc2 And so, I mean, what do you make of Mayor Adams, his response to Texas over these migrants? It's rich, I would say the least. I mean, it just seems kind of nuts that these mayors, now that they're experiencing what the folks on the Southern border have experienced, or just angry.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: It's disgusting.\par \par \uc2 When you listen to what he said, when you listen to what the White House press secretary has said, it's all rhetoric. It's political rhetoric. Let's deal in facts, Charles. You and I like to talk about facts. So let's look at that.\par \par \uc2 These people were going to New York anyway. When they cross the border illegally, when we take them into custody, they have to give us addresses, where they're going to. We then have to process them under those addresses. Those addresses are in New York City. A lot of those addresses are close to Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 So once those people are released to the nongovernmental organizations who receive government grants to then transport these individuals throughout the country, once they get those addresses, then Governor Abbott is in fact facilitating that travel to those locations anyway.\par \par \uc2 That's where they were going. The problem is, is, this is being done in the light of day. So now the spotlight is on it and everybody's seeing it. Mayor Adams didn't have any problem with any of this, these people coming to New York City, as long as it was in secret, as long as it was under the cover of night. He didn't have any problem..\par \par \uc2 But now that everybody's seeing it, now that it's in the open, now he's going to come out and say something about it. And that -- again, that's political rhetoric. And it's disgusting, because it doesn't serve the American people.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But isn't it telling, though, that both of these mayors talk about the strains on their budgets, the strains on their systems, food, schools?\par \par \uc2 These are -- when other folks in other states complained about this, they were called even racist or xenophobic. And it's so interesting to me that now they are saying that -- these are large cities, by the way, with huge budgets, multibillion-dollar budgets, and you're talking small towns along the Southern border who have been dealing with these kind of strains for a long time.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Yes, they don't care as long as it's not them. They don't care as long as their citizens don't know what's going on.\par \par \uc2 Again, like I said, these people have been going to New York City. They have been going to Washington, D.C., for a long time. They have just been doing it without anybody knowing about it. Now that people know about it, of course they're going to come out and say something, because now the taxpayer in New York City, they recognize that they're footing the bill for everything that's going on.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, he's going to come out and say something about it now. But, again, look at it. Look what's happening in all of these states, in my state of Arizona, in Texas. We're constantly having to deal with this, and you never hear anything from them.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: But now that it comes to the light of day, now that everybody gets to see about it, of course he's talking about it.\par \par \uc2 And, again, that's not what politics is supposed to be for. Politics is supposed to serve the American people. It's supposed to help everybody out.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: And they don't do that. They just decry everything until it affects them.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Maria has a special on all of this at 8:00 p.m. tonight on FOX Business. And you do not want to miss that.\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , well, they're at it again in this buyout saga. We're going to give you the very latest there.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats and the president might be ready to sign the dotted spending line, but over 200 top economists signing off on a letter to Congress saying, not so fast. Find out why next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Details of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's countersuit against \uc2 Twitter\uc2  out, and then, well, the war of words is back on.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady of FOX Business has been covering this story from the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, so what do you make of this latest round, this latest back-and- fourth?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, good to see you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 I mean, my biggest takeaway is, this is going to be the business version of the Johnny Depp-Amber Heard trial. So if you go through Elon's countersuit, he's accusing \uc2 Twitter\uc2  of fraud, multiple breaches of contract, but at the heart of this, this is still always about the percentage of bots on the platform.\par \par \uc2 Now, Musk's team is saying that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has been intentionally lying on multiple instances. \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , for their part, they shared a 127-page document rebutting Musk's countersuit. They're saying it's factually inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 But, basically, they're saying he's he's trying to get out of it. Whether he's trying to get a lower price or not, I mean, Charles, Musk makes a decent point that what prompted concern is, three days after the deal gets signed, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  restates its monetizable users number and its earnings without warning them, and then didn't have to answer analyst questions because they were under the deal.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it would make me pause. Wouldn't it you?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. No, I see that. By the way, I see that all the time in the market. When a deal is announced, let's say it's $100 a share, the stock pops at $90. I take $90, because, usually after that, you get more due diligence, something -- you may get a couple of earnings reports.\par \par \uc2 And, sometimes, the deals just don't go through. So I agree with you. I think he's got a legitimate point here.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And I think this is all really going to come down, though. I mean, the judge does seem a little bit pro-\uc2 Twitter\uc2 . They got that shortened trial that they were looking for. It's all going to come down to whether he can prove the number of bots are fake.\par \par \uc2 I did think it was interesting. We finally got some info on what that was. He thinks about 10 percent of users are fake. Remember, the magic number for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: I loved this part, though, that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was throwing shade at the service that he's using, Botometer, and they said, well, at one point, they actually classified your own account as a bot.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: So, certainly, there's going to be a lot of looking into that for the judge.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Anecdotally, I think it's more than 10 percent. I don't know. I put out a tweet people don't like, and the bots come at me pretty good.\par \par \uc2 All right, so 30 seconds. The stock was up today almost 4 percent. Is there a chance that there still could be a deal?\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 If they go to trial, I mean, we have seen before this judge has ruled in the cases and made a company pay the full price. But I think there's a lot of time between now and then.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Elon has made it very clear he's still interested in a \uc2 Twitter\uc2 - like product at the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  shareholder meeting yesterday. So I think there's still -- there's still juice here.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And the folks at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  know, if this is a done deal and he doesn't have to buy, it's going down big time.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you so much. You have done an amazing job covering this.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Thank you, Charles. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, so that spending bill showdown, ahead of the vote tomorrow, an urgent plea from more than 200 economists today.\par \par \uc2 And President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  starting to rack up some wins, but with inflation still raging, how will it play out for the power -- party in power?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Senate Democrats poised to pass that new spending bill over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 But that isn't stopping over 230 top economists from sending a letter to leaders of Congress warning the bill would make inflation worse.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from Dave Dodson, lecturer at the Stanford School of Business.\par \par \uc2 Dave, why did their interpretation of this bill, why is it different than, for instance, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  or some -- or CBO?\par \par \uc2 DAVID DODSON, STANFORD UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Yes, I think the whole notion that the bill is going to affect inflation or not is crazy, frankly, because the world doesn't move that fast. And sure as heck Washington doesn't move that fast.\par \par \uc2 There's going to be some changes in tax incentives. There's going to be a minimum tax and corporate tax. But all of that is going to take 18 to 36 months to play out, where we have an inflation problem that's happening right now.\par \par \uc2 So I think the idea that it's going to move it up or down is insane. It's not going to have any impact.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 And -- right. And to that point, even the CBO says the first four or five years are going to be a treacherous, right, on the spending side, the inflationary side.\par \par \uc2 I know everyone's looking at the Fed to sort of reel this in. But it makes their job a lot more difficult when we keep spending this type of money.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Oh, yes. No, for sure.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there's three things that are driving inflation right now, $5 trillion that we threw into people's mailboxes. Then we had a scarcity of goods. So now everybody's got cash, but they can't buy it because of supply chain issues. Of course, that's going to drive prices up. And then the energy shock that happened with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Those three things are going to come down. We know that. What we don't know is whether we're going to be in this wage-price spiral, which is why actually today's jobs report is so fascinating, which I think today's jobs report is anti-inflationary.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Anti-inflationary. Why is that? The hourly wages were higher. And, to your point, it feels like -- I mean, they're talking about employers hoarding workers now because there's so few available.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: OK, so here's -- here is the key to the jobs report. It's not about job growth.\par \par \uc2 Normally, the amount of available jobs and job seekers is almost always the same number, but we had this crazy flip-flop during COVID. And we have had this period of time where available jobs greatly exceeded job seekers.\par \par \uc2 We did not create 529,000 jobs. We filled 529,000 jobs. And we filled them because employers have had to use incentives and beg people to come work. And, of course, a lot of that is driving wages up.\par \par \uc2 Today, we have about the same number of jobs that we had entering COVID, which means that the pressure is coming off employers. They don't have to beg workers like they used to.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: And so, as we fill those positions, the pressure to raise wages to try to fill must-have jobs is going to be coming down.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right. Great, great, great point. I could have used it in A block.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks a lot, Dave. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 DODSON: All right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, so President Biden racking up some wins this week. You got the jobs report today, the takedown of the top Al Qaeda leader earlier in the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Still, though, as you heard, inflation is raging big time. So how is all of this going to play out in the midterms?