aging comes first and foremost. And I think they have insulted a lot of people that way. But isn't it a great trick to be able to say this is an amazing victory when you have the White House, you control the House and the Senate?\par \par \uc2 Aren't they supposed to get these bills done?\par \par \uc2 KRISTAL KNIGHT, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I mean, they are supposed to get them done. But you also have to have a willing bipartisan Congress and Senate who's willing to help you pass your legislative agenda.\par \par \uc2 And that's not what we have seen in the first two years of the Biden administration. And so, to the president's credit, he has tried to put forth bills, Build Back Better -- he's tried to -- he's passed his a brief portion of the infrastructure bill, but, really, he doesn't have the votes that he needs to pass his key legislation that would really help Americans' economic situations across this country.\par \par \uc2 And so I think we have to understand the cards that he'd been dealt with.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let me ask you about the number one issue in this country by far is inflation.\par \par \uc2 And even with gas, Kristal, coming down, it's still significantly higher than it was a year ago. And there's signs that it's probably going to get worse. Heck, we may run out of our emergency reserve at the rate we're dumping it.\par \par \uc2 How do Democrats get around that issue? Because I don't think it's going to be alleviated come midterms.\par \par \uc2 KNIGHT: Yes. I mean, when we're in the 100-day window. I don't think that we necessarily get around it.\par \par \uc2 I think the thing that we have to do is to continue messaging it, figuring out ways that, if this bill does actually pass, that it will alleviate the economic burdens, it will alleviate some of the inflationary causes that are happening across the economy.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, we have to be straight with the American public. These prices and these price hikes are going to be here at least through November. And that's something that we have to message and make people understand that this is not an overnight issue that we're going to solve with one bill.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, you say, no, it's a 100-day window.\par \par \uc2 The spending bill tomorrow, Gianno, I'm reminded that of the old idiom, be careful what you wish for.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Right.\par \par \uc2 And I'm so disappointed Manchin signed on to this, knowing what he said in -- previously with Build Back Better that spending was going to create more inflation. So what changed? This is still spending. And the GDP growth isn't there. We're in a technical recession. I believe we're in a recession.\par \par \uc2 So, with that being the case, why would you be trying to spend more money and overheat the economy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Should there be a uniform Republican message right now, though, going into the midterm?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes, I would agree with that.\par \par \uc2 And I would say that, hey, this bill is a tax on all Americans, just like inflation has been a tax on all Americans, especially the poor and middle class, which Democrats say that they care about the most.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Kristal, I got 10 seconds. But, again, when they were polling Build Back Better, a majority of Americans said, no, thank you. We got a lot of free money, and guess what? It didn't really help us.\par \par \uc2 I was going to let you answer that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But now I got to let you go because we're running out of time.\par \par \uc2 You're both fantastic.\par \par \uc2 KNIGHT: OK.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Folks, that's going to do it for me here. Thanks so much for watching this week. And, of course, catch me every day 2:00 p.m. Eastern on FOX Business, for "Making Money."\par \par \uc2 Catch "Cavuto Live" tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. Remember, there's that 