NNEL HOST: And the pact act and as we had Tom Cotton on. Senator Cotton predicts that it will pass the Senate this afternoon. We're not quite sure in what form but we'll keep watching it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That is a big deal. We'll be watching it. Great to be with you, John. Wow, that's jam packed two hours. Thanks for joining us, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Flew by. I'm John Roberts. THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, thank you, John, and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 So, the threat level rises after that drill. Remember this drill that they did in Taiwan preparing for the eventual day that China might invade their island? That's not how China sees it, but that's how Taiwan would see it. So, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is now on the ground there defying the Chinese government. You know, the Pentagon and the White House sought to dissuade this visit, but she is now the highest ranking American official to visit the island in 25 years. This is a very significant moment in the battle with China that exists on so many economic and military fronts.\par \par \uc2 The communist nation has recently been caught, remember this, spying on our \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . They've also been buying up farmland for unknown reasons in the United States, also expanding their military bases with a stated goal of dominating the United States and making themselves the number one superpower in the world. Now, they're accusing the United States of behaving like gangsters, after Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  said any crisis that follows this visit would be "entirely on Beijing." Here's their response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUA CHUNYING, CHINESE MINISTRY OF FOREIGN AFFAIRS (through translation): The United States should and must bear full responsibility for this. The remarks by U.S. Secretary of State Blinken, you just mentioned confound right and wrong, and once again, profoundly show the hegemonic mentality and gangster logic of some American people that I can provoke you wantonly, but you can't oppose me or defend yourself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, she claims she's visiting Taiwan and that that is absolutely allowed, under the agreements that exist between the countries an exclusive lineup today, including two former CIA directors, former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , and General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2 , also Louisiana Senator John Kennedy joins us here as well as we grapple with two huge stories with regard to China and Afghanistan. But we kick things off with Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill. Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Martha. Well, I can tell you right now here on the Hill, there is a sense amongst lawmakers to really close the ranks, that's in the words of Senator Dan Sullivan earlier today really come together, unify and stand behind Speaker Pelosi and her visit to Taiwan as China gets busy possibly starting a series of military operations as early as tonight now take a look at this incredible video. Speaker Pelosi is spending the night in Taipei tonight after getting a really a rock star welcome by a sea of people in Taiwan shortly after she released a statement that attempted to send the message to Taiwan that this visit is not a provocation.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is not how China is reading it at all. According to one state media, the Chinese army will begin a series of military operations tonight. Another Chinese state media source says the Chinese military will hold live fire exercises around Taiwan starting Thursday. That's presumably after Speaker Pelosi has left that area. Now, many Republicans on the Hill are lauding the speaker's visit but they want the White House, the president to be firm with China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): This administration seems to be afraid at every step of the way of provoking our adversaries. If you've seen what's happened in Ukraine, they've consistently signaled that what we're not going to do out of fear of provoking Putin. The same thing I think is true here. We're so risk averse. We're afraid of provoking the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Martha, Speaker Pelosi is set to visit and meet the new Taiwanese president tomorrow. All eyes will be on what she says and what she does, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed. Thank you very much, Aishah. Joining me now: Republican Senator from Louisiana, John Kennedy. Senator Kennedy, always good to have you here today. I want to put up one of the statements here from China. This is one of the new statements since the visit began. Speaker Pelosi is the incumbent leader of the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 , her visit to and activities in Taiwan in whatever form and for whatever reason, is a major political provocation to upgrade U.S. official exchanges with Taiwan. China absolutely does not accept this and the Chinese people absolutely reject this and that comes on the heels of President Xi himself saying that this visit would be "playing with fire." Where do you think this is going?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): Well, Martha, I support Speaker Pelosi's trip to Taiwan unconditionally, but with an explanation. As, as petty and self- absorbed as Washington looks like, from the outside, you got to sit from the inside. This, this entire exercise began with Speaker Pelosi's ego. Now, when you're talking about a nuclear war with China, you should take your ego out back and shoot it. But that's not Speaker Pelosi's way. She knows, after the midterms, she's not going to be Speaker anymore, she may not be in Congress anymore.\par \par \uc2 So, I think she thought of this trip as a way to say well, look at me one more time. She talked to the White House, and some knucklehead either on Speaker Pelosi staff or the White House leaked it. And on top of that, President Biden spoke publicly about it and said, oh, maybe she shouldn't go. Well, at that juncture, she had to go, because weakness or the appearance of weakness, invites the wolves.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm glad the speaker went. But if she really wanted to demonstrate strength from the United States of America, she would have made it a bipartisan trip, she would brought Kevin McCarthy along, she would have brought the Secretary of Defense along. But no, it's her. It's the Speaker Pelosi's show. And the added benefit of all this is that, you know, we're not talking about inflation or crime or the open border or her husband's stock trades. And that's the way, that's the way this town works it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, I think that that's insightful. And there's probably quite a bit of truth in what you say, this is a legacy trip to be sure. The last person to do it was Speaker Gingrich 25 years ago, and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will have the recognition for doing that. But nonetheless, I want to listen to the language difference here, because I think a lot of people watch his story. And they say, well, where do we stand on Taiwan? Do we want them to be free and independent? But here's John Kirby, and what he said about that, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS AT THE NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL IN THE WHITE HOUSE: We have said that we do not support Taiwan independence. And we have said that we expect cross strait differences to be resolved by peaceful means.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the policy is that we do not support Taiwan independence. But I think it's a head scratcher. Because here's \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's statement. She says our congressional delegations visit honors America's unwavering commitment to supporting Taiwan's vibrant democracy. So, it's pretty tough to figure out how you can be a vibrant democracy under the leadership of a communist government, Senator, which one are we for?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, I know what I'm for. And I think I know what a majority of Americans are for -- they're for Taiwan's independence. Now, here's what's going on here, President Xi and China is working with, with Putin and Russia and the Ayatollah in Iran, and their ultimate goal is to have Russia dominate eastern and central Europe, have Xi and China dominate the Indo Pacific, which, of course, includes Taiwan, and move into sub-Saharan Africa, and have the Ayatollah dominate the Middle East. And they're working together. And that's not a world that is safe for America and Western values.\par \par \uc2 The fact of the matter is that we are not ready today as ready as we should be to defend Taiwan. And instead of all talking about it, that's what we make our next step. Let's game out how we would defend Taiwan, in case Xi tries to make a move, and that will likely deter, if not prevent a move by President Xi.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: These are very tense times and we know what China's aims are. And we know that they are increasing their military live drills in the coming days. And we hope that there are no accidents in the course of all of this very high tension. Thank you, Senator. Always great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: You bet. You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I just want to mention. We're waiting for some live comments by Joe Manchin, Senator Joe Manchin, obviously, he's a very important player at the moment with this new spending bill that's out there. Also, we're going to talk to \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  about what is happening in Afghanistan and what is happening in China. He's, of course the former head of the CIA and former Secretary of State. He's been intimately involved in the negotiations with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . We're going to speak to him about that.\par \par \uc2 Also, a story exclusive with General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2 , former Commander of U.S. Forces in Afghanistan on whether President Biden's deadly and chaotic withdrawal from that country left a void that has brought al-Qaeda and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  back together. As Ayman al-Zawahiri is thankfully, finally brought to justice by our fantastic American intelligence and military officials, justice for September 11th, he's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what was the leader of al-Qaeda doing in a comfortable apartment in Kabul at the home of a top \uc2 Taliban\uc2  aid? That's one of the big questions after the United States killed Ayman al-Zawahiri in a drone strike. It happened less than a year after the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took over that capital once again more than 20 years after 9/11, as U.S. troops were pulled quickly out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 We all remember the scene, Afghans desperately trying to get onto a military plane, as it took off from Kabul last August. Some of them clung to the sides and some of them fell to their deaths. Now, that evacuation led also to the killing of 13 U.S. service members in a terror attack at the airport in Kabul. Less than a year later, one of the masterminds of 9/11 ends up right back in that capital. And thank goodness to the hard work and toil and sweat of our intelligence officials, they found him and took him out.\par \par \uc2 So, General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2 , former U.S. commander in Afghanistan here with sort of a reflexive look at where we are right now given all of this. And of course, given this major victory that happened several days ago.\par \par \uc2 But first, to foreign correspondent Trey Yingst, live in Jerusalem for us. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Martha, Good afternoon, the leader of al-Qaeda, Ayman al-Zawahiri is dead, following a U.S. drone strike over the weekend in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 The wanted terrorist had a bounty on his head for $25 million.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: And he was sheltering among the civilian population in the capital of Kabul. Reports indicate he was killed with an RX9 Hellfire missile, meant to minimize unintended casualties.\par \par \uc2 This was the first Over-the-Horizon strike for the United States since the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover amid concerns that U.S. officials would have difficulty conducting counterterrorism operations in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 The compound where the strike took place is currently being roped off by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , with local journalists being denied access to gather more information on the killing of the al-Qaeda leader.\par \par \uc2 Zawahiri joined al-Qaeda in 1998, and served as \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 's deputy until he was killed in 2011. The former Egyptian doctor and Islamic revolutionary was part of the decision making process in major attacks from 9/11 to the USS Cole to American embassy bombings in sub-Saharan Africa.\par \par \uc2 His death is significant in the counterterrorism world, though the strike location raises questions about how such a high-profile individual was able to stay below the radar.\par \par \uc2 Now, days, after the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took control, President Biden had this to say about \uc2 al-Qaeda in Afghanistan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Look, let's put this thing in perspective here. What interests do we have in Afghanistan at this point, with al-Qaeda gone? We went to Afghanistan for the express purpose of getting rid of al Qaeda in Afghanistan, as well as, as well as getting \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 . And we did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The reality is al-Qaeda still exists in Afghanistan, though a shadow of its former self. The organization maintains ties with the Taliban's Haqqani Network. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey, thank you very much. And that's an important detail. It was the Haqqani, whose home he was staying in, in Kabul as well.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With that, we are very glad to bring in retired four star General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2 , former commander of U.S. forces in Iraq and Afghanistan, former CIA director, as well now a partner at \uc2 KKR\uc2 , and chairman of the KKR Global Institute.\par \par \uc2 General, good to have you with us today. What's your assessment of the meaning of this strike and the end of Zawahiri, as well as the position and what it reveals about our Qaeda and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  now in Afghanistan today?\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 DAVID PETRAEUS\uc2  (RET), FORMER UNITED STATES AND NATO COMMANDER: Well, it is a very, very important achievement by our intelligence and counterterrorism communities. A tribute to their tenacity, their creativity, their determination.\par \par \uc2 But it also reveals that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have not lived up to the Doha agreement. They committed that they would not allow international extremists to have sanctuaries on their soil. As you noted earlier, this is why we went to Afghanistan was to eliminate the sanctuary which the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  refuse to eliminate themselves in the wake of the 9/11 attacks.\par \par \uc2 But I should note that we have continually had to keep al-Qaeda from trying to reestablish various sanctuaries there over the years, including when I was privileged to be the commander in Afghanistan. And that continued even in the wake of the operation that brought \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  to justice.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, in the final few months that I was the commander there, in operation overseen by the CIA, together with our JSOC forces. So, again, a very important achievement, but one is very clear evidence that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are not leaving -- living up to the agreement that they made, and that we will have to keep very significant resources focused on Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 It's a number of us noted after the withdrawal last year, and also noted, frankly, that we wouldn't be able to conduct these kinds of Over-the- horizon operations, but that they would be much more challenging, needless to say, than when we had bases on Afghan soil. And noting as well, that those bases weren't just useful for operations in Afghanistan, but also in neighboring countries where, in fact, al-Qaeda used to have the sanctuary that is Zawahiri was believed to have been in the tribal regions of Western Pakistan prior to this.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took over joined by the \uc2 Haqqani Network\uc2  leadership, Siraj Haqqani (PH) who is now the number two in the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  regime, it's believed that it's one of the houses, that one of his individuals controlled in which Zawahiri was sheltering together with his family.\par \par \uc2 So, again, a worrisome development. And we should also note that the al- Qaeda is actually not the biggest challenge in Afghanistan at this point. It really is the Islamic state affiliate, the Khorazan group, which operates mostly in Afghanistan, but also in western Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 And we really have to keep an eye on them, because they are the ones with more international extremist objectives at this point in time.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let me ask you, you know, what's your assessment of what, you know, because we didn't learn a lot of intelligence, most likely because of the nature of this strike, and because we don't have people there on the ground. What are the chances that there will be a retaliation attack for this? What do we know about a potential successor to Zawahiri? And what do we understand about what his goals were currently, and whether or not he was able to mobilized people towards those goals.\par \par \uc2 He said, you should kill as many Americans as possible.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: He did. And in recent years, he also declared the establishment of an al-Qaeda affiliate in the Indian subcontinent. But to be sure, because he had to practice such very strict operational security, which is why we could never track him down for well over two decades prior to this, despite all the operations for he -- for which he was the brains.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he was seen as the evil brains or genius behind some of the most sensational attacks that al-Qaeda carried out, including the 9/11 attacks.\par \par \uc2 But nonetheless, his communications obviously had to be very, very careful. He didn't come up on the net, as they say very often, but clearly made a mistake here and somewhere some source network enabled us to identify him determine his pattern of life and then determine how to bring him to justice.\par \par \uc2 There are rumors of who might succeed him, it would have been Hamza, the son of \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 , had we not caught up with him a few years ago. There is a rumor that perhaps, Saif al-Adel, who's another Egyptian extremist, as was Zawahiri, who is supposedly under house arrest in Iran, that's a loose term, because they often have been able to operate from there, even though under house arrest, that he could be a candidate for this.\par \par \uc2 But clearly, al-Qaeda network has been degraded quite substantially over the years. But still, again, as always, all of these different individual entities and affiliates of al-Qaeda or the Islamic State, require additional pressure and focus.\par \par \uc2 We can never let that up, because as we have learned in the past, such as in Iraq, where \uc2 al-Qaeda in Iraq\uc2  was able to reconstitute as the Islamic State. If the pressure and focus are taken off of them, they can indeed become a major problem very quickly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and they have time. That has always been said. They're not in a hurry, and they have a mission. So, we will see.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, General Petraeus. Always good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: Pleasure, Martha. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, do you remember this comment?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Have you checked gas prices lately? Have you checked the interest rates? Absolutely, we're in a recession.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, it got us wondering how other voters across the country are feeling whether they are financially pinched by this recession. We know a lot of us are. And that there is red hot inflation out there. They are here to tell us how they are feeling about all of it, and voters across the country coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Biden facing an uphill battle with some voters when it comes to the economy. Only 17 percent, Less than a quarter think that the economy is excellent or good. And that's obviously a big drop from the 55 percent who felt that way when the president took office, and that was still in the middle of the worst part of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Three quarters say that they have been hit by recent financial hardships as a result of inflation. White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein will join us in a moment, right after we speak with voters who join us now.\par \par \uc2 Deborah Abel is a Republican from Pennsylvania. Jessica Freeman is an independent voter from Georgia. She voted for President Biden in 2020. And Scott Raymer is a Republican from Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Great to have all of you with us. Let's dive right in. Deborah, let me start with you. Do you think that the United States is on the right track or the wrong track?\par \par \uc2 And why do you think -- why do you feel either way?\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ABEL, PENNSYLVANIA REPUBLICAN VOOTER: Probably it's going to take more time than I have. But definitely on the wrong track. I'm a small business owner, I own a towing and automotive company.\par \par \uc2 At max, we're 30 employees. I just can tell you that it's never been harder to be a small business owner in this economy. I don't see real leadership here on a national level. And I think we definitely need a change.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think the president could do? What would you want him to do if you could speak to him?\par \par \uc2 ABEL: Well, obviously inflation is a real issue. I don't think there's anybody out there that's not affected by higher cost and everything in that -- in your personal life and in business. Our fuel costs are over 30 percent higher than last year.\par \par \uc2 We have a labor shortage or labor is costing more, and we are doing more with less, and it's just really difficult to be a small business today.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, before I move on, I just want to ask you a quick question because I know that the White House feels strongly that they've been able to lower gas prices a little bit in recent weeks. Are you noticing that and is it making a difference for you?\par \par \uc2 ABEL: You're -- a couple of cents? Is that what you consider lower? I mean, lower to me is lowering it back to where it was last year or the year before?\par \par \uc2 You know, we have 35 trucks, and so, diesel fuel -- we use diesel fuel. Diesel fuel is astronomically high and I think that that's a lame excuse.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ABEL: Or lame answer to say that they lowered it. Clearly he's out of touch with what it's like to actually be a small business owner. Or for that matter, even an employee who's dealing with inflation with food cost and fuel as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let me bring in Jessica Freeman from Georgia. You're an independent voter, you voted for President Biden in 2020. How would you say the President that you voted for is doing when it comes to the economy, Jessica? And thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA FREEMAN, GEORGIA INDEPENDENT VOTER: Thank you for having me. I think the economy we're in trouble. I did vote for Biden. I voted for Trump, prior to that. Man, I did vote for Biden this past election. It was the lesser of two evils in my opinion and I think we are in trouble.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you feel -- what would you like to say to the president because the White House says, you know, we're doing a lot, we're working to lower gas prices. They're pushing through another bill that they hope will make drug prices a little bit cheaper. What do you say to them when they say we've actually -- we're doing a lot.\par \par \uc2 FREEMAN: Open the reserves. Gas is based on futures. The price of gas is based on futures. In 100 years, it doesn't matter. We're all going to be driving electric cars then anyway. Open the reserves, bring down the prices, make it more affordable for your everyday Americans who have to get to and from work in order to put gas and food on the table. They can't afford to put gas in your car.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. No, I'm sorry. I didn't mean to interrupt you. When you look at, you know, the midterms are coming presidential election two years down the road. You voted for former President Trump then you voted for President Biden, you know. What do you think about the people out there? You have any idea who is attractive to you as a potential president in the next term?\par \par \uc2 FREEMAN: I'll be honest with you. I'm not enchanted by anybody. They are -- I feel like I'm coming from an independent point of view. Everybody has their own agenda. There's not the best for what for America, it's the best for what you want your party to make happen. Be it a conservative Roe vs. Wade situation or a very liberal situation. It doesn't seem to me that any of the candidates, any of the potential candidates are actually willing and able to do what is best for the people of the country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, thank you very much. Scott Raymer is a Republican from Nevada. Scott, what would you -- what's your take on how they're doing. And I would just put up this number. Our most recent poll, 55% say that the President has made the economy worse. Obviously, there's external factors here. The White House points a lot to the Russia-Ukraine war as being largely to blame for what we're seeing in inflation. Who do you blame and what's your take?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT RAYMER, NEVADA REPUBLICAN VOTER: I definitely blame Biden administration, that when Trump was in office, inflation is a little over 1%. It's gone well over 9% right now. I haven't seen any of his policies that have really made sense. They've been really detrimental to the American people. You look at the immigration policy, the cost of fuel, interest rates, and everything they've pretty much done has hurt the whole country overall. So I think it's time to make a change.\par \par \uc2 Now, I, at one time in 2008, had voted for being a longtime Republican. You know, Biden, and Obama thought we needed to change back then, then they voted for Trump. But what I see what happened with Biden and having the economy, we're going the wrong way. And I think now it's time to make a change. We have to get back on track and get people back to work, and let people start making a living just seeing more and more people having to work two, three jobs just to make ends meet. And it's tough.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Scott, when you look at the future, or is there anyone that appeals to you? You know, if you're not happy with the leadership that you see now, what do you hope for the future?\par \par \uc2 RAYMER: Sure, thank you. Being a Trump supporter, I love DeSantis. I think it's time for someone a little a little younger, a little more newer blood. You know, I'm tired of someone who's really kind of out of touch with what's going on in reality, so I'm looking for somebody with some fresh ideas, you know, creative minds. And let's bring something new in there. We don't need the same old establishment anymore. I think it's time for some change.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. I really want to thank all of you for joining us today and for, you know, sharing some of your personal thoughts with us. Deborah, and Scott, and Jessica, thank you. I hope you'll come back, you know, as we get through this midterm journey that we're about to be on over the next few months. A lot of races in the places where you live, so I hope we'll talk to you about those soon. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 RAYMER: Thanks for having us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely. Thank you, guys, very much. So very interesting, right, to listen to this and to be joined now by the White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein, has been listening in and joins us now. Jared, what's your sort of gut reaction as you listen to those voices from across the country?\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: Well, first of all, it's always good to hear voices from across the country like that. And that's one of the reasons why the President gets out every time he can to listen to voices like that and others. There's a couple of things that I want to respond to.\par \par \uc2 First of all the lesson -- the message, one, here is loud and clear from that group and similar groups like that, is inflation is unacceptably high. Now, that's a quote from the President of the United States. So we couldn't share that sentiment more closely. As you've mentioned, we're trying to do all we can to help and I know that there's different opinions about that. I do think, though, that there's a couple of fact checks I want to do. Someone said that they thought the gas price had come down a couple of cents. That's just factually incorrect. It's down $0.83 off of its peak since mid June.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But, Jared, let me ask you. Is that true for diesel fuel? Because she is in the trucking business, but that's not true for diesel fuel.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Correct. So that's the --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, how much of diesel fuel come down?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Diesel fuel also come down a lot. I don't have the number in my head, but I will get it for you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She knows with it every single day because she fills up over a tow truck.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes. So, look, hold on. Hold on, Martha. Wait a second, Martha. To say that diesel fuel has come down a couple of cents is absolutely incorrect, OK? I will get you the number of cents. It's not a couple of cents, so that's point one. Point two, you talked about inflation as Biden's problem, Americans problem, yes. This is absolutely unacceptably high from the President's perspective, but inflation is even higher in some other European countries, OK? It's higher in the UK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Why would anyone in this country same care about that, Jared?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Because if you're asking the question is this Biden's fault, you then -- which you did, you then at least have to tell the folks who you're talking to please explain why this is the case in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and the United Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's fine but here's what I would say to that. I think people in this country expect to have exceptional leadership. They don't expect to be in the same boat as other countries. A lot of those countries have socialist governments, they have other problems that they're doing with.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: OK. You're missing the point, Martha. The point is very simple.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, I think you're missing the point.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: The point is very simple.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The point is the Americans care about what their experience is when it comes to the economy.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They feel a whole lot better when they're told other folks have it worse.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, that's correct. I'm just saying that if you're trying to say that the inflation is Biden's fault, you then have to explain why Biden is not the President of the United Kingdom or the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Now, look, you made a great point, though, which is that we -- people need to see us doing everything we can to help them. And I think if they look, that's exactly what they'll see.\par \par \uc2 Whether it's prices at the pump, whether it's regular gasoline or diesel, you'll see declines that are at 3% overall for diesel, more than a couple of cents for sure, less than that but not much less than that. If you're talking about the Inflation Reduction Act, we are now working on getting across the legislative finish line, a measure that will directly lower prescription drug costs.\par \par \uc2 Now, somebody said a couple of cents in there are a little bit of help on prescription drugs, we pay two to three times more what people do in other countries. So that is more than a small decrease, health insurance coverage, the cost of clean energy. These are the kinds of things that we are working to try to address the problems that the folks that you were just speaking to face.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But those are also thing that -- and I think there's a lot of people in this country, I would say, pretty much everybody would like to see lower prescription drug prices. I know there's a lot of concern about some of the way that this bill goes about doing that, and that it could hurt some of the research and development that some of these companies are doing. But I think what you're hearing from these folks is that they want something that is going to impact more quickly. And I think one of the arguments against that is that the Fed didn't act soon enough, the administration didn't respond to the supply chain problems soon enough. We were told all of this was going to be temporary, this is from the supply chain. I remember hearing, you know, it's all going to work its way out in the next couple of months or so, nothing to worry about.\par \par \uc2 And now they say, look, what you've told us isn't necessarily our experience, and we want you to understand. What they're saying is they want a different leadership, they want somebody else.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So let's talk about some of the facts of the case. I think you raise a very good point about when these measures take effect. So one of the things that the Inflation Reduction Act will do is right out of the gate, lower the budget deficit by about $90 billion just from the minimum corporate tax alone in FY-'23 and '24 together. So that starts working right away. And that is clearly disinflationary, so we believe that will help with inflation.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to lower prescription drug prices, some of the measures including rebates to seniors and a cap on expenditures, they also get out of the gate very quickly. When it comes to lowering the coverage, lowering the cost of health care coverage, that's also something that comes directly upon signing of that measure. So some of these measures get right out there very quickly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So one of them is that by a number of measures, the Joint Committee on Taxation and the Penn research, which I know you've seen, and you've answered questions about it, say that people under $400,000 income are going to see their taxes go up. Now, I know that that the argument is that the benefits will kind of outweigh some of some of that.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you this about raising corporate taxes. What do you think the first thing those companies are going to do? When their taxes go up, they're going to look for ways to have fewer workers who they have to pay in those companies, are they not?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, first of all, let me do a real time fact check and then I'll get to your good question. Not one middle per class person filling out their taxes will find that they face higher taxes or higher tax rates because of the corporate minimum tax. It's that simple. This is a tax on companies whose profits are over a billion dollars. So to argue that this will show up on anybody's tax form is just factually --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, these are two different things.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: -- incorrect.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Different parts of the question. But small businesses, some of them will see -- those people who are personal owners of small businesses, they would see their taxes go up, correct?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, this is according to \uc2 Steve Rosenthal\uc2 , an expert from the \uc2 Tax Policy Center\uc2  who knows this area very well. "If you're not a tax cheat, hedge fund manager or corporation making over 1 billion, you're not affected. So let's be very clear. I think this has been missed in the discussion of the corporate minimum tax. It has a $1 billion threshold. No one under $400,000, in fact, will pay one penny of extra taxes on their tax form because of this measure. And I just wanted to -- if there's nothing else people remember from what I'm talking about today, please remember that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think everybody hopes you're right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: That is not a debatable point.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (Inaudible) a lot of experts who are saying exactly the opposite, that almost --\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, they're not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- we'll see some increase in their taxes. So you can --\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Not a debatable point, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I encourage everybody to read all of the pieces that we're all reading on both sides and see who makes the most sense to you. Jared, I thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And for listening to the voters across the country. And we'll speak with you soon, I hope. Take care. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: You too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  will join us exclusively as \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's Taiwan touchdown pushes some real buttons in China. Let's go to John Kirby now, excuse me, one second. Standby, former Secretary of State John Kirby speaking. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Following bin Laden's death during a US counterterrorism mission. So while he continued to pose an active threat to US persons, interests and national security. As President Biden has consistently said, we will not allow Afghanistan to become a safe haven for terrorists who might bring harm to Americans, to the homeland.\par \par \uc2 We met that commitment. This action demonstrates that without American forces on the ground in Afghanistan and in harm's way, we still remain able to identify and locate even the world's most wanted terrorist, and then take the action to remove him from the battlefield. That is the definition of this mission of when we talked to a year ago, or over the horizon counterterrorism capability. What we did this past weekend is a perfect clean example of what that capability looks like.\par \par \uc2 Now onto Taiwan, as you have all seen the Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  arrived in Taiwan earlier this morning, East Coast time. As we have said, the speaker has the right to visit Taiwan. And the Speaker of the House has visited Taiwan before without incident, as have many members of Congress over the years including this year. This trip was the Speaker's decision. Congress is an independent branch of government. You all know that. We're obviously monitoring her travel as we always do for members of Congress, and we've taken all appropriate measures to ensure the safety of her travel throughout the region.\par \par \uc2 Let me be clear. The Speaker's visit is totally consistent with our long standing One China Policy. We've been very clear that nothing has changed about our One China Policy, which is guided, of course, by the Taiwan Relations Act, the three Joint US-PRC Communiques, and Six Assurances.\par \par \uc2 We've said that we oppose any unilateral changes to the status quo from either side. We said we do not support Taiwan independence. And we've said, as I said again yesterday, that we expect cross-strait differences to be resolved by peaceful means.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have communicated this directly to the PRC at the highest levels, including in last week's call between President Biden and President Xi. The National Security Advisor, Secretaries of State and Defense, the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff have also made this very clear to Beijing in a half a dozen recent high-level conversations.\par \par \uc2 Now, we've seen a number of announcements from the PRC in just the last several hours that are unfortunately right in line with what we had anticipated and what we talked about yesterday. Now, there's no reason, as I said yesterday for Beijing to turn this visit, which is consistent with longstanding US policy into some sort of crisis, or use it as a pretext to increase aggressiveness and military activity in or around the Taiwan Strait, now or beyond her travel.\par \par \uc2 And again as I made clear yesterday, before the Speaker's travel was confirmed by her, China has positioned itself to take further steps, and we expect that they will continue to react over a longer-term horizon. I couldn't give you a date certain of what that horizon looks like, but we certainly would expect them to react even beyond her trip, including announcing additional large-scale, live-fire exercises. Of course, they've already started doing some of that today, flying across the median line . We've seen press reports of them doing that today, and using economic coercion. It's exactly in line with the playbook that we anticipated and talked to you about yesterday.\par \par \uc2 The United States will not and does not -- will not seek and does not want a crisis. We are prepared to manage what Beijing chooses to do. At the same time, we will not engage in saber-rattling. We will continue to operate in the seas and the skies of the Western Pacific, as we have done for decades. We will continue to support Taiwan, defend a free and open Indo-Pacific, and seek to maintain communication with Beijing. We'll keep doing what we are doing, which is supporting cross-strait peace and stability.\par \par \uc2 And then just real quick, lastly, Karine hinted at this at the top. The President welcomes today's announcement of an extension of the truce in the Yemen conflict. The truce in Yemen, of course, was a key agenda item during the President's visit to Saudi Arabia where he met with the King and the Crown Prince, and with leaders from across the region.\par \par \uc2 We're grateful for the leadership of Saudi Arabia throughout this truce process, as well as the sultan, as well as for the sultan and leaders of Oman who have also played an important role throughout.\par \par \uc2 Now, this truce, which is now going on five months, has brought a period of unprecedented calm in Yemen, saving thousands of lives and bringing tangible relief for countless Yemenis. Five months, which may not sound like a lot, but when you're talking about seven years of war and thousands and thousands of Yemeni lives, it counts for a lot. And now we have a chance to extend this another two months.\par \par \uc2 So we urge the Yemeni parties to seize this opportunity to work constructively under UN auspices to reach an inclusive, comprehensive agreement that paves the way for a durable, Yemeni-led resolution to the conflict. Advancing the peace process is going to require courage and dedication from all sides. The United States will remain committed and engaged in efforts to advance peace in Yemen and to bring relief to the Yemeni people.\par \par \uc2 And with that, I'll take some questions.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: OK. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Thank you, Karine. Thank you. John, how concerned is the administration right now that the -- Afghanistan has become a safe haven for terrorists?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think if you were to ask some members of al Qaeda -- ask them how safe they feel in Afghanistan right now, I think we proved to a fare-thee- well this weekend that it isn't a safe haven and it isn't going to be going forward.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What will the repercussions be for the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  harboring al- Zawahiri?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I'm not going to telegraph moves and decisions that we might make. I'm certainly not going to get ahead of anything at this point. I would just make two points.\par \par \uc2 One, the strike itself shows how serious we are about accountability. It shows how serious we are about defending our interests. And we're going to maintain, as I said, at the outset. We're going to maintain this over-the- horizon capability. In fact, I'd go so far as to say we're going to continue to try to improve that capability going forward.\par \par \uc2 And number two, we've communicated very directly with \uc2 Taliban\uc2  leaders our views of their willingness, at some level of course, to harbor Zawahiri and his family. And we have made it clear that not we believe, not we think, not we suppose, but we know that that's a violation of the Doha Agreement.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: So, obviously, John -- just to follow up on that -- clearly, this shows accountability for Zawahiri and for al-Qaeda, wherever they are --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: -- as you got him in Afghanistan. But it doesn't show accountability for what the Secretary of State described as a gross violation of the Doha Agreement. So, can you commit that there will be some act to demonstrate that they will be held accountable in some way? And how do you do that without it looking like, yes, we'll just take out one by one; you can keep allowing more in?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, again, I'm not going to telegraph punches that hadn't been thrown yet, or decisions that haven't been made yet. We're going to stay vigilant to the threat. We've made it clear to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  that we know what they did and we know who they harbored. And we know some of the steps they tried to take after the strike to cover up the evidence of it. So we're mindful of it. But I'm not going to get ahead of decisions, policy decisions that haven't been made.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's not that we take the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  at their word, but just indulge me for a second: They claim they want a relationship with the United States and with the West. They claim they want to open up and be part of the international community.\par \par \uc2 They claim they want financing. That's exactly right, Peter. So, if that's true, if that's what they really want, then it would behoove them to pay close attention to what we just did over the weekend and to meet their agreements under the Doha Agreement.\par \par \uc2 PETER, REPORTER: Without identifying them, how many other al-Qaeda individuals or leaders do you assess are presently living in Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I'm not going to get into intelligence matters, Peter. We said, even before we left Afghanistan last August, that we knew al-Qaeda was present in Afghanistan in relatively small numbers. And we know that there are still some al-Qaeda fighters in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 I would -- again, without getting into classified information here, I would say the number is not very large. And that's core al-Qaeda. There are also offshoots like ISIS-K, which we know are very active in Afghanistan right now.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that I want to say, and I know, rightly, we're focused on Afghanistan. But again, I want to take you back in time a little bit, to about a year ago when we talked about this threat in our departure from Afghanistan. We know that al Qaeda has metastasized.\par \par \uc2 Both in terms of character, now, they've got different offshoot groups, \uc2 al- Shabaab\uc2 , ISIS and ISIS has got splinter groups of its own. But they've also metastasized geographically. They're not focused as much in our in Afghanistan. They're in North Africa, they're in the Sahel, they're throughout the Middle East, and they're in Yemen.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, there are other counterterrorism threats in other parts of the world. We're going to stay focused on them all. I get that we're focused on Afghanistan right now but we're not taking our eye off the rest of the world either.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: John, something you've just said is not consistent with what we were told last year. You're saying that you've always known there was a small number of \uc2 al-Qaeda in Afghanistan\uc2 . President Biden said, what interests do we have in Afghanistan, at this point, with al-Qaeda gone?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes, I mean, in a major way, al-Qaeda was not playing -- now wait, let me finish. They weren't playing a major role in operations or resourcing or planning in Afghanistan. But, Peter, I know specifically, because I was at a different podium a year ago, and we talked about the fact that al-Qaeda had a presence in Afghanistan, but small and not incredibly powerful or potent. And I think, again, without getting into numbers, we would still assess that to be the case.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, we know that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  was harboring the world's most wanted terrorist. You guys gave a whole country to a bunch of people that are on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Most Wanted list. What did you think was going to happen?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I take issue with the premise that we gave a whole country to terrorist groups. I mean, again, I'd encourage you to ask --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  was harboring the world's number one terrorist. How is that not giving a country to a terrorist-sympathizing group, if not giving them permission to have terrorists just sit on a balcony --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The question -- I mean, Peter, the way you ask that, it makes it sound like we owned Afghanistan a year ago. It wasn't our country. It was an independent, sovereign state. And the President made a bold decision to end a war that had been going on for 20 years, because he believed then and still believes now that our national security interests are best met by meeting the threats of today, not the threats of 2001.\par \par \uc2 And we, you know, I don't want to relitigate the whole war here, but obviously, no one anticipated the Ghani government to fall as fast as it did. But we said at the time that as we depart Afghanistan, we're going to keep vigilant, we're going to stay ready, and we're not going to let Afghanistan become a safe haven for terrorists who threaten our homeland. And this past weekend, we proved that case precisely.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But -- so now that you know that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is not living up to the part of the deal that they made with the US, to not let Afghanistan be a place that terrorists feel like they can be safe, what are you going to do about it?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, that gets to Peter's question. I'm not going to telegraph decisions that haven't been made or policy initiatives one way or the other. I would just say --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Are we just waiting for some spectacular terrorist attack in the US to then say, oh, well, there's terrorists in a safe haven --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: If we were, Peter --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: In Afghanistan. Now we can go get them.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: If we were, Peter, then we wouldn't have taken the hit on Saturday, the strike that we took on Mr. Zawahiri. If we were just waiting -- this isn't about waiting, it's about watching. And we watched very closely --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Very interesting back and forth there with Peter Doocy and John Kirby.\par \par \uc2 With that we want to bring in \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , former Secretary of State and CIA director, and a Fox News contributor. Secretary, good to have you with us as always. Thank you for joining us today. You've been watching this along with us. What would you like to say about what John Kirby just said and what you're hearing in there?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, he got some things right and some things not quite right. He was right that the strike on Zawahiri was important. He was a bad guy. It's a kudos to the intelligence community during my time, and now after a lot of work done to get him and take him off the battlefield is a righteous strike against a horrible man who killed lots of Americans.\par \par \uc2 Second, it's good that we demonstrated we could conduct a strike without troops on the ground, that's good stuff. But the overstatement is that we still have the depth and capacity of intelligence that we had before we left. And that's certainly not the case and the way that we rolled out of there.\par \par \uc2 Second was respect to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . I was in the middle of the discussions with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , we never trusted them. And they continued to violate the agreement when we were in office. And for that reason, we never got everybody out. We knew that they weren't honoring the commitments that they'd made under the Doha Agreement. So we didn't do what President Biden chose to do. They knew, this administration knew that they were violating agreements and chose to leave, and the risk is therefore heightened.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, President Biden has said, you know, that Afghanistan will never be a safe haven for terror, that he will not allow that to happen. Does he have the ability to do that, given our lack of footprint there? And even, you know, understanding and respecting the over the horizon strength that we did see demonstrated here?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Boy, it's difficult to do anywhere to make such a blanket statement, say you're not going to let someplace be a haven. The question is always about how much resource and how much risk can you take down. And there's no doubt because the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have failed to live up to the commitments that they made. The risk is greater today than it was a year ago when we had the debacle of the departure from Afghanistan. I don't think there's any doubt about that. There's more risk there today, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He said, if the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  wants in a relationship, they should look at what we did. I'm not sure that there's room there for a relationship anymore at this point, but we'll see what they do there. I want to ask you --\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: The next big push will be about money, Martha. They're going to need a bunch of money. And we should make sure we don't give them a nickel.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, we've seen those kinds of exchanges in the past and we'll see what they decide to do here. I want to ask you about the large picture in terms of China. You look at this as, you know, former CIA director, former Secretary of State, what's the impact of this Pelosi trip and what do you read into the reaction to it? Does it move us or does it move Taiwan closer to a more dangerous situation? Do you think that China is planning an invasion?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Martha, I don't think there's any doubt that \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's intent on taking Taiwan. He calls it reunification. We know that it is the destruction and the violation of the sovereignty of an independent nation. Even Kirby use the language says we still need to defend Taiwan, even though he went wobbly when he talked about the history there.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese are a bit tiresome on this, Martha. I'll be honest with you. They overreact to silly things like a visit from a parliamentarian from a free nation, and threaten all kinds of silly stuff. We shouldn't be on defense here. We're doing the right thing. Speaker Pelosi did the right thing by traveling there after we were threatened and not letting the Chinese Communist Party bully us.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's plans didn't change one iota because of Speaker Pelosi's visit. I think his plans remain the same. He may use this as a pretext to mess around for a while. What we can't do is be on defense. We have to take a series of actions that demonstrate real American resolve. And if we do those things, we can push back against the CCP threats, not only on Taiwan but here in our homeland as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just have 20 seconds, but I thought was interesting. John Kirby said we will continue to manage what Beijing decides to do, roughly that's not verbatim, but roughly.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Yes, that's backwards. It should be the Chinese Communist Party that fears what America will do, that a fierce American strength economic power from the United States of America, something that can put the Chinese Communist Party at enormous risk. We should support the Chinese people. We should pressure the Chinese Communist Party and we can convince them not to behave in the very ways we've seen them behave these last handful of days, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Secretary of Pompeo, always good to have you with us. Thank you very much, sir.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Thank you, Martha. Have a great afternoon. Yes, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You too. Good to see you. And good to have all of you with us this afternoon, that wraps up THE STORY for this Tuesday August 2, 2022. THE STORY goes on. I will see you,. actually tomorrow morning with Bill Hemmer at 9:00, and then again here at 3:00. Thanks for sharing your time with us. Look forward to seeing you again tomorrow. Have a great afternoon, everybody.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220803ei8200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Sen. Dick Durbin (D-IL) is Interviewed about Democrats and the Filibuster; June Jobs Report; Biden Defends Saudi Talks; Death of \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 ; \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  Resigns; Governors Make Headlines Sparking '24 Talks; Republicans Struggling in Midterms; Interview with Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 ; Interview with Sen. \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2  (R-WY)\b0\par\par\uc2 Mike Emanuel, Ben Domenech, Juan Williams, Mara Liasson, Kevin  Corke \par \uc2 9,069 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 President Biden takes executive action to protect access to abortion as women march on Washington urging the White House to do more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's my hope and strong belief that women will, in fact, turn out in record numbers to reclaim the rights that were taken from them by the court.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice-over): Calls for the president to declare public health emergency over abortion rights -- and vowing a summer rage in response to the Supreme Court's action. We'll have a live report.\par \par \uc2 Plus, economic silver lining. A rosy jobs report, gas prices coming off record highs, but the summer travel season marred by delays at the airport due to pandemic staff cuts and pilot shortages. We'll ask Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  about the waves of disruption and new spending to improve airports across the country.\par \par \uc2 And Senate Democrats make a last push for a party line package to revive the president's stalled domestic agenda. We'll bring in leaders from both sides of the aisle, Republican Conference Chair \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2 , and Senate Majority Whip Dick Durbin to discuss the fate of the massive spending bill ahead of midterms.\par \par \uc2 Plus, shock and sadness after the assassination of an American ally in one of the world's safest countries. And another key ally resigns after a stream of personal and policy scandals.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: In politics, no one is remotely indispensable.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We'll ask our Sunday panel about any impact back here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): And, hello, again, from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 President Biden bows to pressure from progressives, taking executive action on abortion, as Women's March protesters returned to D.C., and the White House celebrates new job numbers despite growing recession fears and those high gasoline prices hitting Americans hard during summer travel.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  will join us live to discuss the major challenges we've seen this summer.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Kevin Corke live at the White House with the administration's new action following the Supreme Court's reversal of Roe - - Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Mike, a surprisingly strong jobs report and an executive action on abortion access really seemed to buoy the president with his base this week, and poll numbers show he needs it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The fastest way to restore woe -- Roe is to pass a national law codifying Roe, which I will sign immediately upon its passage on my desk.\par \par \uc2 CORKE (voice-over): President Biden this week unveiling executive actions and to push back against state legislatures and other lawmakers helping to further restrict abortion access.\par \par \uc2 This as thousands of pro-choice advocates protested in D.C., many urging the president to declare a national public health emergency as nearly two dozen states moved to ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Protests themselves have been the subject of intense scrutiny this week in Washington, following reports that an online group targeted Supreme Court Justice Brett Cavanaugh at a Washington area statehouse, forcing him to slip out the back door. The restaurant said the justice and other patrons were, quote, "unduly harassed".\par \par \uc2 The White House was less definitive.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We condemn intimidation, we condemn any violence. Peaceful protest -- people should be allowed to be -- to be able to do that that.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: In a restaurant?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: If it's outside of a restaurant, if it's peaceful, for sure.\par \par \uc2 CORKER: All of this against the backdrop of the president's low poll numbers and American's complaints about high gas prices, economic uncertainty, even flight delays scuttling their summer plans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CORKER (on camera): Meanwhile, the president as you know, Mike, is preparing for a trip to Saudi Arabia and that trip comes amid heavy criticism of the kingdom's human rights record. And with that in mind, he actually penned an op-ed overnight in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ". In it, the president said he was looking to reorient, not rupture long-standing relations with an important ally in the region -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Kevin Corke reporting from the White House -- Kevin, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Thank you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's start with travel hassles during the busy summer travel season. We've had weeks of cancellations and delays amid a pilot shortage. This comes as there are complaints from passengers they're not being treated well by the commercial airlines.\par \par \uc2 And after American taxpayers gave the commercial aviation industry $54 billion during the pandemic, can you understand why some taxpayers feel like they aren't getting their money's worth from the airline industry?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: That's right. A lot of passengers have experienced frustration, including me and millions of other Americans. Now, we've seen some improvement over the course of the summer, but still not an acceptable level in terms of performance, cancellation, and delays.\par \par \uc2 So, here's what we're doing about: we'll collaborate with airlines when they're ready to take steps that are positive and proactive, whether that's improvement in pay that are helping with hiring or flexibility in customer service.\par \par \uc2 We're also going to enforce passenger and consumer rights. And this is not new for us, as matter of fact, it was last year that I announced the stiffest fines ever imposed under our consumer protection program for airlines that were failing to provide refunds to passengers after they got stuck with cancellations. As a matter of fact, we have just concluded other 10 investigations on airlines on these issues, and have launched another 10 or so that we're going to pursue to make sure that the consumers and passengers are protected.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, I do want to emphasize again, we've seen movement in the right direction. You know, I gathered airline leaders after the Memorial Day travel weekend. We saw a really high level of cancellations and delays there.\par \par \uc2 The July 4th travel we can we just came through which had some of the busiest travel days of the year, definitely fewer cancellations and delays. The numbers I just saw from last few days, they're around 2, 2-1/2 percent, still higher than they should be. It's never going to be zero.\par \par \uc2 But we really want to see them push toward 1 percent or so with realistic scheduling, responsible customer service, and all the things airlines need to do to properly service the tickets that they are selling to passengers.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: U.S. commercial pilots must retire at age 65 by law. NBC did a study finding, quote, by 2029, not a single baby boomer will be able to legally fly commercial aircraft. As they leave, they take not just a substantial part of the labor force with them, but also decades of expertise and experience in the air.\par \par \uc2 I'm told Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is talking with colleagues about potentially raising that retirement age for commercial pilots to 67. Would you support that?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Look, these retirement ages are there for a reason, and the reason is safety. I'm not going to be on board with anything that could compromise safety.\par \par \uc2 Now, what's clearly the case is we need to cultivate, train, and support a new generation of qualified pilots. As a matter of fact, just last week, I was out with some programs that are helping to cultivate that interest in students and support them as they get their hours and get those qualifications.\par \par \uc2 The answer is not to keep the baby boomer generation in the cockpit indefinitely. The answer is to make sure that we have as many in as good pilots ready to take their place, to have a stronger pipeline. We're backing that up with \uc2 FAA\uc2  programs that support high school and college curriculums to get into aviation.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, ultimately, it will be for the airlines and those employers to hire and retain excellent talent.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, this is an issue we're seeing across the aviation sector, across the transportation sector at large -- the importance of having competitive pay, great job quality, so that we can bring in and keep the people that are going to be needed to power America's transportation sector.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The White House had a major victory last year passing a massive infrastructure package. Supply chain issues and inflation are driving up at the price tag. You've said that basically the funding rolls out over five years. We're seven months into that process.\par \par \uc2 What are you and your team doing to try to move things along?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, we have been announcing a new program every few days pursuant to that infrastructure law.\par \par \uc2 Right now, for example, we're in the middle of rolling out up to a billion dollars in airport improvements. And these are going out to airports across the country. Orlando is going to see about $50 million to help expand gates and allow them to have more capacity and accommodate more passengers. Chattanooga is going to see improvements that help passengers move more smoothly through the airport.\par \par \uc2 And we're not forgetting smaller, more rural facilities that might not be household names, but could mean a huge amount economically to those regions. The Chamberlain, South Dakota, for example, their general aviation terminal right now is a mobile home. We're going to fund them to have the kind of terminal they really need in order for flight operations to grow and to succeed.\par \par \uc2 So, we're proud of what we're doing. Whether it's this airport terminal program going to 85 airports around the country that we're working on that this week, or initiatives to eliminate railroad crossings that can be both a headache and a safety hazard in communities across the U.S., or, of course, so much funding going to fixing roads, bridges, and other facilities that people need in order to get where they need to go safely and conveniently.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Quick follow-up, how quickly people see improvements in those airports?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: So, the funding is flowing now, and obviously depends on the project a little bit. You know, constructing a whole new gate or a terminal takes longer than some of the more modest improvements. It really varies from airport to airport.\par \par \uc2 And this is an important thing to point out: we're not creating all of the designs in Washington. We know that it's local communities and individual airports or facilities that are going to know what their needs are. We're trying to trying to provide more of the funding.\par \par \uc2 But again, those dollars are going to be moving quickly. And I'll tell you, even before you see the improvements finished, you're going to see a lot of construction jobs associated with putting them in.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Will we see any projects that resemble some of the historic infrastructure investment years ago such as when the nation built the interstate highway system?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Sure. We're funding a lot of improvements for roads and bridges, too, but we're doing it in a better way I would say than what has been on the past. You know, there are a lot of lessons from choice of the `50s, `60s, and `70s. A lot of communities can point to where highway that was put in the period where they didn't pay attention to the community or listen to the community. It really cut up and separated a lot of our towns.\par \par \uc2 And so, we're also funding ways to reconnect those communities when a rail line or a highway overpass is doing as much harm as good. We're going to do it better this time.\par \par \uc2 The 2020s I think is going to be remembered as a period where we set up infrastructure that we're going to be proud of for an entire century. And I'm thrilled to be part of the process of working with states, working with cities, working with communities, working with airports and transit authorities, to get that work done.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's go to a red hot issue in Washington. Your husband tweeted after Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  left a Washington restaurant due to protesters. The tweet reads: It sounds like he just wanted some privacy to make his own dining decisions.\par \par \uc2 Is that appropriate, sir?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Look, when public officials go into public life, we should expect two things. One, that you should always be free from violence, harassment, and intimidation. And two, you're never going to be free from criticism or peaceful protest, people exercising their First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: And that's what happened in this case.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the justice never even came into contact with these protesters, reportedly didn't see or hear them. And these protesters are upset because a right, an important right that the majority of Americans support was taken away.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BUTTITGIEG: Not only the right to choose, by the way, but this justice was part of the process of stripping away the right to privacy.\par \par \uc2 As long as I've been alive, settled case law in the United States has been that the Constitution protected the right to privacy. And that has now been thrown out the window by justices, including Justice Kavanaugh who I recall swore up and down in front of God and everyone including \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  that they were going to leave settled case law alone.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, people are upset. They're going to exercise their First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: And as long as that's peaceful, that's protected.\par \par \uc2 Compare that, for example, to the reality that as a country right now, we're reckoning with the fact that a mob summoned by the former president - -\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right. Let me follow-up, Mr. Secretary.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: -- attacked the United States Capitol, for the purpose of overthrowing the election and very nearly succeeded in the preventing the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: But --\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: I think common sense can tell the difference.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: But as a high-profile public figure, sir, are you comfortable with protesters protesting when you and your husband go to dinner at a restaurant?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Protesting peacefully outside in a public space -- sure. Look, I can't even tell you the number of spaces, venues, and scenarios where I've been protested.\par \par \uc2 And the bottom line is this: any public figure should always, always be free from violence, intimidation, and harassment, but should never be free from criticism or people exercising their First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right. Let me ask you a campaign question, sir. Do you expect President Biden to run for reelection in 2024? And if not, will you run?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Yes, I do expect him to run. I'll support him.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you why he's going to have a lot to be proud of and I'm going to choose my words carefully because I'm appearing as an official and this is not a campaign related appearance.\par \par \uc2 But I'm proud to be part of this administration that has done such work to bring America back from the brink of a recession, to create record job growth, to build achievements that would not have been thought possible just a couple of years ago, like the bipartisan infrastructure law that I have the honor of working on.\par \par \uc2 You know, people scoffed at the idea that you could get anything done on a bipartisan basis in Washington. And yet, the president delivered it, and that's after years and years, multiple presidents, multiple congresses coming and going, promising infrastructure bills without any results. This president, this administration, got it done and I'm proud to be part of that.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Mr. Secretary, thank you and thanks for making time this morning.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: My pleasure, thank you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, the impact of the Supreme Court ruling on abortion and how it could change the trajectory and outcome of November's midterm elections. We will speak with two Senate leaders, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Right now, Senate Democrats are in negotiations to bring back parts of the president's Build Back Better agenda, which fell apart last year. To get it done, they're trying to find agreement without the help of Republicans on prescription drug pricing, climate spending and tax increases on the wealthy.\par \par \uc2 But Senate Republicans are pushing back and threatening to pull support from another major bill in the process. In a moment, we will speak live with the Senate majority whip, Democrat Dick Durbin of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 But, first, from Wyoming, Senate Republican Conference chair, \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator Barrasso, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Okay. So Senate Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's trying to have Democrats choose between that bipartisan bill to help the U.S. semiconductor industry versus a Democrat-only climate tax and prescription drug package. Is that because if Democrats all stick together, he has limited options?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, the American people are furious right now, and it's all about inflation. That's what this election is going to be about, and the future of our country.\par \par \uc2 Only -- 88 percent of Americans think the country is heading in the wrong direction. So what we've seen now is the Democrats see the handwriting on the wall. The president's own pollster said this is the worst environment he's ever seen in his lifetime for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So they're trying a Hail Mary pass. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said he's once again trying this Build Back Biden and trying to bring it back, rise it from the dead.\par \par \uc2 A trillion dollars in taxes, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: More government spending, piling on in the Green New Deal.\par \par \uc2 Look, does anybody actually believe that raising taxes by a trillion dollars is going to help inflation? Or the taxing the companies that produce our oil, our gas to help -- that's going to lower the cost of energy? Not a single Republican is going to support this, Mike.\par \par \uc2 And, Mike, to my friend Joe Manchin from West Virginia, whose vote is going to be necessary for this --\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: -- I would remind him that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's popularity in that state it is as low as it is in Wyoming. Only 17 percent. Joe shouldn't walk the plank for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Part of that partisan Manchin-Schumer proposals expected to include, as you mentioned, bringing in about a trillion dollars in revenue. With what we're told, half expected to go towards deficit reduction, half towards energy and healthcare spending.\par \par \uc2 Is there an economic argument to be made that now is not the time for that kind of spending?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, what we've seen is with the last big Democrat-only spending bill, it made inflation a lot worse. Democrats, the economist who worked for \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  said, don't do it. It's going to bring out all this inflation. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ignored their advice, denied that there was inflation.\par \par \uc2 The American people know what they want. Families are really hurting all across the country. When I talk to people around Wyoming in terms of inflation, they're spending $100 a week more now than they were last year just to -- just to keep up.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, they said, what do you really want? You want enough money to be able to fill your tank with gas. You want enough money that you can buy a week's worth of groceries and you want to have enough money to pay the bills at the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not going there. Oh, no. He's going to higher taxes, more government spending and attacking American energy. That is not the solution the American people are looking for, which is why only 1 in 10 Americans support the direction of the country right now.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator, let's talk midterms. Republicans believe they have a golden opportunity to win back the majority. But if candidates like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  and Dr. Oz are not winning over the voters, is there a possibility the GOP could blow this opportunity?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, I'm very optimistic about the future, election coming up in November.\par \par \uc2 The number one issue is inflation. And in all the issues that people care about, they look to Republicans as the solution on inflation, on energy, on the economy, on the chaos at the border, on crime in the cities.\par \par \uc2 And all of those nominees that you just mentioned -- and some are already the nominees, others are still in primaries that haven't been decided -- what we know is they understand their states. They understand the pain that people are having in those states.\par \par \uc2 And right now, you have candidates running as Democrats, who just this past week -- in Ohio, the Senate and the governor candidate refused to even show up or be seen when President Biden came to their state. People are distancing themselves as Democrats from this administration.\par \par \uc2 I'm very optimistic about our opportunities in November.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There are a few reports that former President Trump could announce a third run for the White House before the midterms. Are you concerned that combined with the Roe v. Wade reversal could really galvanized and energized Democratic voters ahead of the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, people will make their own decision as to what they vote on and how they decide to vote.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, when President Trump was in the White House, gas prices were half what they are today. Inflation was non-existent. The border was secure. We were energy independent.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's heading to Saudi Arabia hat in hand saying "please give us more energy" because he's killed energy in America. We need to get back to energy dominance, not energy dependence. And President Trump got us there.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Your fellow Wyoming Republican, Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who's been perhaps the most outspoken critic of former President Trump, is in the primary fight of her life.\par \par \uc2 Do you see any path to victory for Congresswoman Cheney?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, Wyoming politics is very personal, Mike. It's face to face. It's town to town.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, Liz and I disagree. I voted against impeachment on President Trump. She was for it. I voted against the partisan January 6th Commission. She's all in on that.\par \par \uc2 Now, the election isn't for another month. The travel that I've done around the state, I think she has a lot of work to do if she hopes to win the primary.\par \par \uc2 You say who's leading right now? The people that are doing the best are the people that are selling advertisements, and FOX News is doing very well.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: She has encouraged Wyoming Democrats to cross over and vote for her. Are there enough Wyoming Democrats out there to make a difference in her race?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, number one, this wouldn't be the first time that Democrats cross over and flip their registration to vote in Republican -- in Republican primaries. They're really not that many Democrats out there. And even the chairman of the Democratic party of Wyoming, said there are not enough Democrats to do that.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator Barrasso, thanks so much. Always good to speak with you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Joining us now from Illinois, Dick Durbin, the number two Democrat in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Lawmakers passed the first gun reform package a month ago, first significant gun reform in three decades. And yet, this week, your beautiful state was rocked by a mass shooting in Highland Park.\par \par \uc2 Sir, do you see any appetite for additional legislation, whether it'd be on guns or other root causes?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I certainly hope so. I hope that my colleagues will take a look at what happened in Highland Park. It's less than a week ago.\par \par \uc2 And what happened that day, of course, seven people were killed. Scores were injured by a killer who got on top of a building and took a military weapon -- now, Smith and Wesson military and police weapon -- and fired off up to 90 rounds in just a few seconds. People were scattering and running, and sad things happened.\par \par \uc2 We found a 2-year-old who is protected by his father's dying body and he was finally reunited with his grandparents because he lost both his father and his mother to a shooter.\par \par \uc2 This week, we had good news. An 8-year-old who was hit by this killer is starting to speak again. He's coming out of critical condition. Whether he'll walk again is anyone's question at this point.\par \par \uc2 The question we have to ask ourselves and I'll ask my colleagues: Is this what we want in America when people families bring out their kids for Fourth of July parade that some killer can take a military style weapon and fire off 90 rounds into a crowd of innocent people and killing so many others (ph)? I just don't think that that's consistent with America's values or its Constitution.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator, the suspect in this week's shooting evaded your state's red flag laws. Is there a loophole there to be closed?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, certainly. And if you look across the nation, we have to be much more vigilant because there are over 10 million of these military assault weapons that have been sold, some say as many as 20 million.\par \par \uc2 There are 400 million guns in America and they're arguing -- some are arguing, we need more guns in the hands of more people.\par \par \uc2 It certainly hasn't made us a safer nation. We've had over 300 mass shootings this year. I didn't know when we took this Fourth of July break that Illinois and my state would be touched personally as it has been in Highland Park.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, every senator, sadly, every senator has to face the prospect to have their own Highland Park before this is over.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: To be fair, though, if it were just about gun laws, wouldn't Chicago with some of the toughest gun laws in the country be a safer city?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Let me tell you something, quickly, Highland Park is not Chicago. It's a suburb of Chicago, a small town, a great small town. It's the first place you would have chosen to go to a Fourth of July parade with your son or grandson or granddaughter.\par \par \uc2 It's a wonderful community and to say, oh, Chicago is so dangerous. Yes, it's dangerous, but so is St. Louis and so with towns Jackson, Mississippi, and others have a murder rate as well.\par \par \uc2 It's a fact that we proliferated so many guns -- the police that I sat down with privately on a regular basis say, Senator, you just don't have any idea how many guns are out there and they're so easily accessible.\par \par \uc2 The gun industry has made a lot of money out of this, but America is not safer because of it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let me be clear. I want to express my heartfelt condolences to the people of Highland Park. And, of course to you, sir.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk policy on Capitol Hill. Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is having conversations with your colleague, Senator Joe Manchin, trying to do a slimmed down version of Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 What are the natures -- what's the nature of those conversations? Take us inside the conversations and what's left to be ironed out.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: The centerpiece, of course, is high prices -- the prices of prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 And even before this period of the economy, American families all responded that the price of prescription drugs are just too high. People sometimes can't afford the medicines they need to stay alive. They wonder why the same drugs made in America cost a fraction of what we pay for months in countries like Canada and Europe, and they wonder why we haven't done anything as a nation to bring down the cost of prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , and all of us are working towards bringing down these prices to say to people who are facing insulin dependent diabetes that the cost of their drugs and insulin will be less than not more than $35 a month, dramatic changes, bringing down prices that families struggle with every single day.\par \par \uc2 Now, Senator McConnell has stepped up and saying, I'm going to stop this effort to bring down prescription drug prices. I don't want you to do it and you shouldn't do it. And if you persist in this effort to try and do it, I'm going to stop the bill that gives us better competition with China.\par \par \uc2 The semiconductor industry is central to economic growth and job creation in this country. We've lost ground on it, and we've got to build a change that. And McConnell has said he's going to stop that bill and he's going to do it if we should violate the pharmaceutical companies' plea to us not to lower prescription drug prices.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is this, American families need help in paying their bills and they need help in lifesaving drugs being affordable. The Democrats are leading that. Senator McConnell opposes it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There are Democrats right now pressing for structural changes to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . Here now is Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): Congress, we have the - we have the possibility when we are strengthened by the repeal of the filibuster or even the change to a talking filibuster or a standing filibuster. In doing so we can codify Roe, we can codify -- and all of the other cases that the Supreme Court indicated that they would threaten. We can codify same sex marriage. We can codify the right to contraception. We can codify interracial marriage. We can do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Is she firing up the base that major changes are coming when, in fact, you don't have the votes in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I can tell you that the bottom line is, she wants to change the filibuster rule when it comes to voting rights and other issues. I support that. I think that we ought to make exceptions to the 60 vote requirement when we get down to fundamental questions like this. And it's really up to the senators, who are currently serving, the 50 Democrats. Two of them decided not to vote with us on one of these rule changes earlier. Whether they've changed their position on that since the Supreme Court handed down this decision, taking away the constitutional rights of Americans, I don't know, but I'll be asking them next week.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: As chairman of the Judiciary Committee, what do you say to progressives calling for impeaching some of the conservative justices?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I don't think it's realistic. I can tell you, there is, in my mind, a clear conflict of interest when it comes to Justice Thomas and issues related to the January 6th insurrection. His wife is actively involved politically. Going so far as to give direct advice to the president's chief of staff during this crisis. I would think that Justice Thomas should recuse himself from any decisions that relate to the January 6th episode. Already he was the single vote earlier on a case related to that issue. I think it's a mistake.\par \par \uc2 As to whether he's going to be impeached, that is not realistic. But he should show good judgment.\par \par \uc2 If this court is going to be credible, it has to be as a political as possible. Exactly the opposite has been happening.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator, a lot of headlines this week about Democratic governors, including your own \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  of Illinois. Do you think gridlock here in Washington could potentially create an opening for one of these Democrats to challenge President Biden in 2024?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I'm not looking ahead six years, I'm looking ahead to this week and whether we can come together, Democrats and Republicans, and get something done in Washington, bring down the cost of prescription drugs, deal with this issue of semiconductor chips, which has frozen economic expansion in this country. Let's get this agenda underway.\par \par \uc2 And the election, in just a few months, will take care of itself. And the one years off is certainly over the horizon from my point of view.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator Durbin, thanks very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, we'll bring in our Sunday group to discuss the seeming disconnect between voters' impression of the economy and the White House.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We are in a strong - we are stronger economically than we have been in history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  claiming the U.S. economy's strong despite inflation not seen in four decades and recession fears on the minds of many Americans.\par \par \uc2 And it's time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today" Washington bureau chief Susan Page, Fox News political analyst Juan Williams, Fox News contributor Ben Domenech, and NPR national political correspondent \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Panel, welcome. Great to have you around the table.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So, Susan, let's start here.\par \par \uc2 We have a strong jobs report this week. The White House took a bit of the victory lap. But a new Monmouth poll says only about 10 percent of the country thinks the country is heading in the right direction. What about the disconnect here?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes. Well, and it was a good job support. And the news on employment continues to be strong. Perhaps we won't head into a recession. Mixed signals on that.\par \par \uc2 But inflation just overwhelms everything. Inflation affects Americans every time they fill up their tank of their car, it affects them every time they go to the grocery store to buy something. So that is the economic indicator that just defines people's sentiment that the nation is headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 One other number from that Monmouth poll. Only 10 percent of Americans think the country is headed in the right direction. That is a devastating in historic number.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Ben, less than four months out from midterm elections, and you've got the Democrats trying to get some of the spending across the finish line. The slimed down Build Back Better. Is that putting Republicans in an awkward spot?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I don't think so. It's -- this is something I think now where you have certain things baked in for this election, and they're not going to change. The kinds of uncertainty, the kinds of questions that Americans have about their pocketbook issues, not just energy prices, as Susan said, but the other effects that they're seeing, they know - that run through our economy in lots of different, challenging ways are just things that I don't think this White House or the Democratic Party are going to be able to turn around in time to change things for November.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, you want to jump in?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think that, you know, time is short. I think everybody agrees on that. But I do think that if you look back over the last three weeks, Mike, Democrats are in a better position than we would have anticipated, not just because of the good jobs numbers that we saw on Friday. But you think about it for a second. Gas prices actually are headed down. They're still too high, but they're headed in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 In addition to that, you have to stop and think about the January 6th committee, which has had success in terms of penetrating public consciousness and public opinion. In terms of the bringing chaos, Trump's chaos to the forefront of the conversation again.\par \par \uc2 And then you think about the abortion decision. You think about the gun massacres that have taken place. You touched on that with Senator Durbin. A lot of these issues don't play well for Republicans, play well -- better for the Democrats and make the Republicans look like a radical party.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I don't believe, Juan, the January 6th committee has gotten anywhere near close enough to penetrating broader - the public conscious. I think that the numbers that you see, in fact, in response to the decision on Dobbs v. Jackson don't support the idea that there's going to be a dramatic change in terms of Democrats getting a huge vote number out.\par \par \uc2 And the gun issue, while it does play against Republicans time and again, it doesn't actually have this kind of election changing format that I think you would need to see.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think if you look at the generic numbers, just the generic numbers, do you prefer a Republican or a Democrat in November, those numbers have narrowed. Some polls even have Democrats now in the lead over the last three weeks over these issues.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's bring Mara into the mix.\par \par \uc2 So, the president's out with an op-ed this morning basically defending his meeting with Saudi leaders and saying that he's restored stability to the Middle East region.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, the op-ed didn't mention the word "oil," and that's what he was accused of, you know, downplaying human rights, which the Saudis have violated a lot. To go hat in hand to ask them to pump more oil. That op-ed didn't mention that.\par \par \uc2 But, look, the Middle East is a really important region. The president has to go over there. He's trying to tout his accomplishments. And it's a difficult position to be in because he wanted to make Saudi Arabia a pariah. I think that's the actual word he used. And instead now he's going to be meeting with those leaders.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Susan, we learned early Friday morning about the assassination of former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  of Japan while he was out campaigning. Talk about his role in terms of his relationships with U.S. leaders over the years.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Well, he met with four of them I think when they were president. He was - he built strong U.S./Japan ties. He was, I think, especially important in getting Japan to move past its World War II legacy on military fails. He built a stronger Japanese military. He took a particularly interesting stance towards China. Talked to the China -- Chinese leader, recently stood up for the rights of Taiwan, the need for others (INAUDIBLE) definitely a very powerful, the longest serving Japanese prime minister in its history. A big loss and shocking in Japan for there to be gun violence.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Ben, another major shakeup, a political shakeup, in London with \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  announcing that he will be leaving office. Here's how Fox Nation host Pirs Morgan explained it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, HOST OF "PIERS MORGAN UNCENSORED": I think \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has a massive problem with the truth. Just finally come back to home, \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . And then when he was caught, he did what \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  always does, and he tried to lie his way out of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Was \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 's departure about policy or politics?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Well. first off, let me - let me say my condolences to a former member -- editor at "The Spectator," where I am also editor at large now. So, pour one out for Boris.\par \par \uc2 He is the third prime minister in a row brought down by \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  in certain respects, which I think we should appreciate. He is -- he's an incredible figure and someone who I think certainly changed the dynamic. But part of the problem here is that Boris stopped being Boris. And by that I mean he stopped being the kind of populist that conservatives could have confidence in when it came to forming the kind of coalition that led to getting 14 million votes in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The problem for them now is that forming that coalition again, I think, will prove to be very impossible. And just one thing that I think we should appreciate about politics in the U.K. is that, you know, as much as people may speak in high Dutchen (ph) about honesty or scandal or something like this, this is such a farcical reason for him to be pushed out on so many different respects. It had a lot more to do with the failure of Covid related policies, his break with a lot of the things that he stood for before he ascended to the PM position and not to do to spending too much on parties or drapes. It would be the equivalent of getting rid of \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  for going to the French Laundry. It doesn't really track as being very serious.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, what do you make of Johnson announcing his departure, but sticking around until conservatives pick a new leader?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think, you know, Johnson's a lot -- we were -- Ben's talking about him as a populist leader. I think he's a lot like Trump in that regard. And we see that, you know, Trump is the kind of man of nine lives, always reinventing himself, trying to retain the position of power. Clearly the conservatives in Britain got rid of Johnson. Republicans, conservatives here are still locked into Trump. But I think Johnson wants to influence who might come next. His successor. Maybe he thinks he might be re-elected at some point. And he'll make a lot of money on the speaking circuit, writing, I'm sure, maybe back at "The Spectator."\par \par \uc2 But I think -\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: We'd love him. He could also come back to America.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: What - what I think he does - he will - he does want to have some influence on the Brexit deal, what comes forward with Brexit.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Sure.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: As well -- and I think this is of more global concern, the Ukraine. He's been a great friend to President Zelenskyy and the Ukrainians. And I think lots of people want to make sure that that remains in place.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, panel, we're off to a great start. We have to take a break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a string of mass shootings adding to calls for new gun reforms and change here in Washington. We'll discuss the response from some of the nation's governors leading the charge.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what the fight between \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  could foreshadow for 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You all heard what happened today. But each day we're reminded there's nothing guaranteed about our democracy. Nothing guaranteed about our way of life.\par \par \uc2 GOV. J.B. PRITZKER (D-IL): If you're angry today, I'm here to tell you, be angry. I'm furious. I'm furious that yet more innocent lives were taken by gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: That's President Biden and Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  both reacting to the horrific July 4th parade shooting but in somewhat contrasting tones.\par \par \uc2 And we're back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 So, Mara, you heard different responses from the president and the Illinois governor. Some people criticizing the way the president responded and elevating Governor Pritzker's response. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, I mean, there are some Democratic activists who wanted the president to be more fiery. Substantively, there's not much difference between Pritzker and the president. They both want gun safety laws, including, you can't buy a gun until you're 21, you have to have a universal background check. Those things are supported by huge majorities of Americans. In some polls over 80 percent or 90 percent are for those measures, and they can't get passed by Congress. And that's, I think, the real root of Democrats frustration.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: I think -- I think it's all about 2024 to.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Well, then there's that.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, I think - I think Governor Pritzker is one of several Democrats who want to run -\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: If Biden doesn't, yes.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: If Biden doesn't. Maybe if Biden does. I mean I think some of this - this - the rhetoric we're hearing now is aimed at saying to President Biden, maybe you should reconsider whether you're going to announce that you run for another term.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, you see such an ease among Democrats about what's going to happen this November and then what the prospects are looking two years down the road.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: To pick up on that, you've got Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , from California, going after Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida, running ads in Florida. Is this about raising his profile? And is he considering a primary run against President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: It's hard to imagine why the governor of California is paying campaign funds to run an ad in -- against the Florida governor in Florida unless he's looking down the road. There are both those governors, I think, have ambitions for the White House. We'll see how it goes for each of them.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , definitely annoying president -- former President Trump by being pretty open about his ambitions there.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Ben, do Florida voters give a darn about what \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's saying, and are those ads going to make any difference, or do they just make them feel better about their governor being out there in high profile?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: In the last couple of years, two years, I believe, the - look -- more than 11,000 people moved from California to Florida, bringing with them about $1.9 billion worth of business, according to the \uc2 Americans for Tax Reform\uc2 . So, I think that there's definitely some movement and it's not in the direction of California.\par \par \uc2 But to pick up on Susan's point, I do think that these are kind of signals to the White House signals to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and signals to Democratic donors that you don't have to necessarily do this again. And you saw the piece in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " this morning. You see the piece that in - that ran in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." You see other pieces that are coming out now about increasingly open skepticism that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can run again given the levels of unpopularity and the levels of difficulty that this White House has faced.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Feels like (INAUDIBLE) -\par \par \uc2 : And look at the polls in - about the number of people who don't want Trump to run again, Republicans.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Yes (ph).\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Pretty similar to the numbers of Democrats that don't want Biden to run again.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK, Juan, the more immediate political concern, balance of power up there on Capitol Hill. You've got some Republican candidates who seem to be struggling a bit to connect with voters. I'm thinking of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , Dr Oz, \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  and perhaps others.\par \par \uc2 What is that doing to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's hopes of being the majority leader in the new Congress?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think, you know, you just have to look at his behavior. Right now he seems like he's upset about the possibility of the Democrats passing this prescription drug bill that you discussed earlier in the show, and also the competitiveness bill. I think the Democrats clearly want to say, we are doing something. We can get things done. And \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's tactic over the years has been to stall, to obstruct, and he's employing that tactic now at the risk of looking hypocritical because he supports these bills in the past.\par \par \uc2 So, the question becomes, what does he think? And I think it's about just what you said, Mike, which is that he's worried now that it looks better for the Democrats with weak - with week Republican nominees running for these Senate seats. You know, the numbers in Georgia have shifted recently with suddenly Senator Warnock doing much better against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . You look in Pennsylvania, similar shifts. And I think that Senator McConnell is well aware of it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Ben, what are you hearing in conservative circles? How much alarm is there? Is -- we've seen in the past they thought they could take back the majority in previous election cycles and it didn't work out. So, are the Republicans going to squander this opportunity?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Well, they're great at it, but ideas don't run for office, people do. And that's part of the problem that they're facing here in the sense that you can have ideas of going against the Biden agenda, of emphasizing energy independence, of not, you know, embracing, you know, some of the more radical policies and high spending that has been embraced in the last couple of years. But if your - if your people who were out there on the trail vocalizing this have significant personal challenges, that makes it tougher to do.\par \par \uc2 That being said, every indication we have in terms of the overall perspective, the right track wrong track, the level of shifts that you've seen, and in some of these special elections and the like seem to indicate a Republican wave year that may, as we've seen in wave years before, end up sending a lot of people to Washington who no one ever expected to make it there.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So perhaps some races that are not getting front and center attention could be surprises. Places like New Hampshire, Colorado, Arizona?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Certainly, I think, and I think Nevada is going to be one of these races that could end up being critical in terms of determining whether McConnell is the majority leader or not.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: But, you know, there are really two models that we see. One is that you have a wave election that really sweeps away their Democratic majority in the House. I think even Democrats are pretty pessimistic. They think that is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But there have been cases where Senate candidates have been able to shield themselves to -- from a wave to some degree. And so Democrats do hope to hold on to even just a 50/50 split because think of the difference in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's last two years or second two years of his term as president if he has a divided Congress, or if he has a Congress that is united under Republican control.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And, Mara, Roe v. Wade. As a female panelist, what's the impact on the midterms?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: That's - well, that's the big question, how will these issues, like gun violence, Roe v. Wade, will they energized Democratic voters enough to close that enthusiasm gap and make a difference? We don't know that yet. I think the economy is still the number one, two, and three issue driving voters concerns this year.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Folks feeling it in the wallet when they go to the gas station, where they go to the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes. Yes, that's usually - but, look, the reaction to Roe was pretty intense. The reaction to the gun violence is intense. And Democrats are hopeful that those kinds of issues can help in -- energize their base.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, do you think Democrats hold on or do you think we're looking at a Republican Senate?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think that's up in the air. I would -- I would have thought earlier that there was more likelihood that it would lean Republican. I don't think that at the moment. But in terms of the House, this goes back to the fact that Americans have gone through the pandemic. They're upset about gas prices. And they're just angry. And we see historically they vote against the incumbent in these midterms. It happened to Clinton. It happened to Obama. It happened to Trump. And I think it's likely to happen in the House again.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, panelists, thanks so much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a final word on the week ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And that's it for today. I'm Mike Emanuel. Have a great week and we'll see you back here next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220710ei7a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Deal in Doubt?; Texas Border Surge; Interview With Sen. \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2  (R-WY); Inflation Continues; Strong U.S. Jobs Report; Former Japanese Prime Minister Assassinated\b0\par\par\uc2 Kelly O'Grady, Alex Hogan, Bill Melugin, Mercedes Colwin, Jackie  DeAngelis, Peter Doocy, Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,454 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Do the Dutch have it right? The Dutch poised to make working from home a legal right. Could it happen here? We're going to explore that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But, first, this: the economy cranking out 372,000 jobs in June. That's much better than expected. But will the strong showing mean an even bigger rate hike by the Fed just as inflation continues to eat away at wage gains?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 To Peter Doocy on how the White House is responding -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Charles, White House officials admit there are some problems with inflation, but they see those as a very, very small part of an otherwise very, very good economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today's economic news confirms the fact that my economic plan is moving this country in a better direction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The reason the president says that as he sees a low unemployment rate as above all, as top above all.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, wages are not rising as fast as prices on pretty much everything else, which Republicans say they see as a blinking red light.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NICOLE MALLIOTAKIS (R-NY): The problem is, is that every single day, Americans are hurting when they're either putting gas in their tanks, when they're trying to put food on their table, when they're trying to pay their rent or pay their energy bills.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: All of these things are out of the president's control, though. And so White House officials say those are the only things they believe is hindering their economic agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Now, remember, this price increase is due to Putin's tax hikes -- Putin's war, brutal war on Ukraine, and that's why we have seen the increase of gas prices.\par \par \uc2 That's why we have seen the increase of food.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So they're still talking about the negative impacts of the war in Ukraine, even though the press secretary says -- and I will read this just so that I make sure I get the quote exactly right -- We are stronger economically than we have been in history -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow. That's a bold statement. Peter, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, as we mentioned, wage gains, they're having trouble keeping up with inflation. In fact, average hourly earnings were up 5.1 percent in June from a year ago, but that compares to the latest inflation number, which is up 8.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Brandon Arnold is the executive vice president at the \uc2 National Taxpayers Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brandon, let me start with the one thing that the politicians like to talk about when it's lower, and that's the unemployment rate. It's a farce. It really is a farce. I hate that we use it. I hate that we in the media pick up on it. It is the worst metric out there.\par \par \uc2 I will give you an example why; 353,000 people left the labor force last month; 353,000 left. That's why the number is where it is right now. That's not exactly a booming economy, is it?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON ARNOLD, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, NATIONAL TAXPAYERS UNION: No, and that's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 We see a labor force participation rate in the low 60s. That's very, very low. Some of that is due to demographic shifts. Some of that is certainly due to spending programs that have been created during the pandemic that have effectively kept people out of the work force, because it's paid them not to work.\par \par \uc2 We need to get people back into the work force. That is a more reliable predictor of the health of this economy, which I think is actually on pretty shaky territory, despite what the president says.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's ironic about what you say, because I interview these big Wall Street folks every single day. And many of them brag about the fact they say as much as $2.5 trillion in excess savings.\par \par \uc2 I never heard of excess savings. But, nevertheless, it's all that stimmy money out there. Some of it's still sloshing around. But isn't the irony is that that's what really sparked this inflation thing? And your purchasing power gets less every single day.\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: Yes, that's absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the stimulus package that they passed at the beginning of last year put $2 trillion into the economy when the Fed was already flooding the economy with dollars. That ramped up demand for goods at a rate that was just unprecedented at a time when we're facing these supply chain constraints.\par \par \uc2 That's why we have 8.6 percent inflation right now. And that's why, despite strong wage growth, 5.1 wage growth, that's very strong wage growth, people are feeling left behind because they're losing purchasing power. They're losing the ability to buy stuff that they were accustomed to affording just a few months ago that's now out of reach for them.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, on top of all of this, we have got the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which its job is -- their goal is actually to make our wealth effect go backwards, to the take money out of our pockets, to make us less prosperous.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's really tough, when there are some signs that parts of this economy have already begun to slow. So what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: Yes, we absolutely need to see action from the Fed that would rein in some of the excessive dollars they have pumped into the economy.\par \par \uc2 We are going to see a Federal Funds Rate increase later this month. I think you can put that in the books, especially with these job numbers that suggest the economy is doing a little bit better than most people thought. I still think we're in recession territory here. We had negative economic growth in the first quarter this year. Preliminary data from the \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2  suggests that we might have had negative growth in the second quarter. We will see later this month if that's true or not.\par \par \uc2 But I think we need to proceed with a great deal of caution here. And by no means should Congress began contemplating a $1 trillion tax increase. That's just absolutely ludicrous.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You just read my mind. We're four months out from the midterms. And you know there's going to be some sort of Hail Mary from this administration. And it will include tax hikes, we heard a little bit of that today, on the rich, pay their fair share, but also pumping more money into the economy where I just said -- the experts say there's almost $3 trillion extra already.\par \par \uc2 What would that do. If they were to somehow get even more money into this economy, what would that do with this inflation picture?\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: Well, we're seeing that at the state level, to some extent, we're seeing stimulus packages passed in California and a dozen other states.\par \par \uc2 And that's the exact wrong prescription, because what it's doing is pushing more consumer demand. It's putting upward pressure on prices, when we need prices to fall, obviously. And some of the strategies that we're hearing from this Democrats -- the Democrats on Capitol Hill are really, really concerning that tax people until they're poor, and then they can't afford to buy stuff, and that will bring down inflation.\par \par \uc2 That's just an absolutely backwards mentality that's really, really unfair to Americans, especially people in the working class that are barely getting by in this economy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, bottom line, before I let you go, we're in for a tough ride, bottom line?\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: No doubt about it. We're in for a very rocky ride. And I wish we had a better, steadier hand at the tiller right now. But, unfortunately, I don't think this president is pointing us in the right direction. And this Democratic Congress, they're not much better.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's so crazy. We came into last year so ready to erupt, pick up where we left off February 2020. Did not have to be this way.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, thank you so much. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD: Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, so the question, of course, is what the impact is going to be on the upcoming midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Let's ask GOP pollster Lee Carter.\par \par \uc2 Lee, better-than-expected numbers, and, of course, you get the usual media outlets like Axios saying, what recession, Politico saying, what recession? And if anybody were able to the answer, they say, the recession that's been bogging me down for the last few months.\par \par \uc2 How far will this go toward easing some of the political pain that the president's under?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER, REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: It's going to go nowhere.\par \par \uc2 There was polling that came out this morning that was talking about, what do Americans -- what are Americans most concerned about?; 77 percent say the economy. Only 10 percent say jobs, right? Jobs is not part of this equation. What is part of what they mean when they're talking about the economy is two things.\par \par \uc2 Number one, its inflation. And, number two, it's personal finances; 44 percent of Americans are saying that they have felt the impact on their personal finances in the last month. But, I mean, in the last four weeks, almost half of Americans are saying they have had a direct impact on their savings, on their wallet, on their look at the future.\par \par \uc2 Jobs is not going to change that, because people feel fine about jobs. We have -- anyone feels like they could get a job if they wanted to.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: That's not the issue. The issue is, right now, two-thirds of Americans are having problems paying their bills.\par \par \uc2 No matter what the jobs numbers say, no matter what the White House says right now, the American people are smart. They're saying, this is not what my experience is. When 10 percent of Americans are talking about, they're having trouble paying for groceries and having to adapt their grocery shopping list, when you have 30 percent of Americans talking about how gas and energy prices are making them make changes in their financial plans and decisions, we have got a real problem.\par \par \uc2 And it's not going to be changed by a jobs number that looks a little bit better, and that -- and, still, we have got -- we have got salaries that aren't keeping pace with the inflation that we're all feeling at home.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 And we get the next inflation read on Wednesday. The estimate is, that's going to be 8.8 percent. So we might actually see a 9 percent inflation, which would shatter the last 50, 60 years.\par \par \uc2 When the White House tries to divert from all of the things that you just talked about, always bragging about how great they're doing, always bragging about their record, the greatest economy ever, that kind of nonsense, does it hurt them?\par \par \uc2 Do you see any evidence in the polls that people are also insulted, because that's adding sort of insult to injury?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Yes, I think nothing kills trust faster than a contradiction.\par \par \uc2 So if you go out there and say, look, your experience is false, it's not true, the economy's never been stronger, and your personal experience is that you're struggling more than you have in a long time, and you have less hope than you ever have, you feel like that administration is completely out of touch.\par \par \uc2 I mean, most Americans are saying -- I mean, 77 percent of Americans -- this is across Republican, Democrat independents -- are saying, we have got to do something to address the economy, and what is our number one issue right now. And this White House is saying, the economy is fine. It's never been stronger.\par \par \uc2 That gap is going to make us feel like they're completely out of touch.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: And they're going to look elsewhere for solutions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Don't believe your lying eyes when you're in the grocery line cashier.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Lee, earlier today, President Biden signed an executive order on abortion rights, and seems to be trying to make this an issue for November. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: For God's sake, there's an election in November. Vote, vote, vote, vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: How's that coming over?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, look, I mean, abortion is undoubtedly becoming a more important issue; 24 percent of Americans are now putting this in a top five issue. And that's up 14 points just since December.\par \par \uc2 So this change of in Roe vs. Wade has certainly activated a number of voters. But it isn't more important to the American people right now than the economy. The economy is still the number one issue. That's a winning issue for Republicans. I'm not sure if people are going to make the decision saying that a woman's right to choose is more important than it is to have the economy up and running and the future of our country and for our children.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Now, it's a tough call because there's a lot of women who are out there who are very, very important voters.\par \par \uc2 We looked at the 2020 election and said this was decided by suburban women voters. If those women are going to start to say look, it's not just about abortion, actually, what they're feeling is, two-thirds of women are now saying: I actually feel threatened about my rights as a woman.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just about abortion. It's much, much more. That could actually impact the election unfavorably for Republicans and favorably for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: But I'm just not sure that it's going to be enough. It's going to have -- if you look at the numbers right now, it's not suggesting that's going to be enough to make a difference for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It looks, though, that that's the path that the president's going to go down.\par \par \uc2 And for anybody looking for economic help, I guess that means it won't be coming.\par \par \uc2 Lee, always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, folks, on that note, I want to ask you. Don't forget, I have got a special, "Inflation in America," July 26. It is all happening on "Making Money" on FOX Business 2:00 p.m. Eastern time. It's the hangover edition. So send your questions to investedinyou@FOX.com.\par \par \uc2 So, what is the White House saying after a top Democrat ripped into the president's trip to Saudi Arabia to beg for oil, even though the administration begs to differ? We will get the latest.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you will get reaction from Republican Senator \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2  right later on this hour.\par \par \uc2 Also, should working from home be a legal right? One nation is about to make that happen. Can it happen here too?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, it sounds like the Dutch want to ditch that morning commute. The Netherlands is poised to make working from home a legal right.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Jackie DeAngelis has the details -- Jackie.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon to you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, life has changed in so many ways after the pandemic. And one of those is that continued trend for people to want to work at home. In the U.S., companies are struggling to get people back into the office. Some lines of work have eliminated it. Some businesses are using it as a perk to attract top talent.\par \par \uc2 But in the Netherlands, Parliament has approved legislation to make working from home a legal right. So, this is definitely first, but it still needs to pass the Senate over there. Now, currently, Dutch employers can reject a request to work at home without offering a reason or explanation.\par \par \uc2 The new laws would force employers not only to consider the request, but then give a reason if they decide to deny that request. Indeed, working from home, or WFH, as it's commonly abbreviated in text messages now, is popular in our country.\par \par \uc2 An April ADP survey found two-thirds of respondents said, if they had to return to the office full time, they would find a new job. And there are many other issues to consider as well, commute time and cost, in some cities, crime and safety of public transportation big issue, like here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Others say they just enjoy the flexibility, and that is a huge perk for them. The majority of Dutch workers are in agreement. A recent poll showed that 70 percent of those in the financial business and government sectors, they wanted some sort of a hybrid model. Only 10 percent said they preferred full-time office work. And only 20 percent said they preferred being at home the entire week.\par \par \uc2 Now, the big question for employers, of course, is productivity. When we work at home, are we producing the same work product? And the jury's still out on that one, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, I have a small business. For me, the jury is not out.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We got to get back to the office.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Jackie, thank you so much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Hey, so should the U.S. be going Dutch?\par \par \uc2 FOX News legal analyst Mercedes Colwin is with us, along with Kingsview Asset Management's Scott Martin.\par \par \uc2 Scott, I want to pick up on the productivity thing. In America, the last labor productivity report we had was down 7.3 percent. Now, there's a lot of variables in there. But you hear folks from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  all saying, we need people to collaborate face to face.\par \par \uc2 I think this would put employers at a serious disadvantage. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MARTIN, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: It does, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And you're right about the productivity numbers. They are ugly. And what a coincidence. That kind of goes in line with a lot more folks working from home over that time period, huh? So, just data, just statistics.\par \par \uc2 But I think Jackie pointed out some interesting things. I was shocked to hear that they actually do work in Europe.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: That's a new one for me. But let's say it does spill over to the United States.\par \par \uc2 We hire a lot of folks every year, Charles, and I will tell you, man, a lot of folks that we have the requirement for the job, whether it's the trading and the operations compliance at our firm, we require those folks to be in the office.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of folks -- I mean, look at the JOLTS survey, by the way, the Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey -- try to say that three times fast -- 11.3 three million jobs are out there for folks to take. But similar to what we have been trying to hiring -- trying to hire this year, Charles, these folks don't want to come into the office.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Or they want to work two days from home, then three, then four. And it keeps accelerating, and get full benefits and full salaries.\par \par \uc2 So, as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  pointed out, we want to require, at least on our firm, folks to be in the office for a minimum amount of hours or give up some of the benefits or the salary if they want to stay home.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Mercedes, the legalities of this, could the nation even force