ericans With Disabilities Act and say, I need to work from home, and for these following reasons.\par \par \uc2 They can sue under the local anti-discrimination laws. There are so many laws already on the books that can regulate working from home. It's not necessary to then clutter the courts again with other rules and regulations, and encumbering corporations that either can self-regulate, because they want that talented candidate, or, two, would be subject to discrimination laws that already exist if any of those decisions are being challenged in court.\par \par \uc2 So I can't imagine a universe that we would see these types of regulations coming to the United States. But, then again, things have been so different in the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Who knows what the future will hold.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, also these last couple of years, Scott, we saw where the stock market has sort of been a proxy for the all of this.\par \par \uc2 A year ago, all of those work-from-home stocks went through the roof, right, the Pelotons of the world, the Zooms of the world, the DocuSigns of the world. All of a sudden, they're crashing big time. Is the stock market at least saying, hey, what everyone was saying was going to be the new normal maybe won't be the new normal?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Yes, it was a little bit of a chase that was on, Charles.\par \par \uc2 But you're right. I mean, people still are staying home, which is interesting vis-a-vis those stocks. And those stocks are still in the tank.\par \par \uc2 So it's an interesting aspect. I like what Mercedes said, though. It should be a self-regulating -- like, the free market will figure it out. But that's one of the reasons, guys, that we have so much trouble filling these jobs. A lot of these folks out there that are the job seekers don't want to do the things that the employers want them to do with respect to coming to the office.\par \par \uc2 I'm curious what \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's companies are going to do, though. Let's see what their move is. And then maybe we can follow that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Of course, Mercedes, in New York, you look at things like subway use is still under 50 percent. Meanwhile, I think everybody goes to the office down in Miami. Everyone's moving down there.\par \par \uc2 So I guess it might have something to do with where you are, right? And, listen, there's some other issues that kind of influence whether we want to go to work or not. And, in New York City, I think it might be crime and just the hassle of trying to get to the office.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes?\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Oh, what? I'm sorry. You're talking to me, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, there is obviously a lot of challenges when it comes to certain jurisdictions around the country. But New York City, for one, has its own built-in challenges.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: And I think commuting is one. Traffic is crazy, as you know.\par \par \uc2 I'm sure all of us travel -- who travel into the city on a daily basis are dealing with overwhelming traffic. And the rising crime is a real issue.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: As an employer myself, I -- we employ a lot of people in New York City.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Gas prices too.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: It's a big -- it's a great point, Scott. These gas prices, $6-plus out here, trying to get into the city.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: There's a lot of things. But you know what?\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: So, all of that combined, there's a lot of challenges.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I came back to the office early because I missed my colleagues.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: And we missed you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 All right, so it's the first full day after Texas Governor Greg Abbott's order to round up and return migrants to the border. How's it going? Bill Melugin will show us.\par \par \uc2 Also, Border Patrol Union Chief Brandon Judd on that agency's findings onto the horseback investigation.\par \par \uc2 And find out what drove this brazen attack at a New York restaurant. You will not believe it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Forget about road rage. How about restaurant wage -- rage?\par \par \uc2 Find out what took these customers over the edge and why over-the-top incidents like this continue to pop up. It's mind-boggling.\par \par \uc2 And we're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The chaotic moment as a gunman is taken down after he shot and killed former Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . The 41-year-old man in custody, with questions swirling about how and why this happened.\par \par \uc2 To Alex Hogan in London with the latest details -- Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 The former Prime Minister of Japan \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  has been assassinated. He was the longest-serving prime minister in Japan. And now world leaders around the globe are speaking out, voicing their solidarity with Japan. U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that he was deeply saddened to hear the news and outraged before stopping at the Japanese Embassy in Washington today.\par \par \uc2 The president also highlighted the strong relationship between the U.S. and Japan, which was bolstered under Abe's administration. Former German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  called the attack a cowardly and vile assassination. Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  tweeted his deepest condolences to the family.\par \par \uc2 Here in London, Queen Elizabeth II saying in part: "I have fond memories of meeting Mr. Abe and his wife during their visit to the United Kingdom in 2016. His love for Japan and desire to forge ever closer bonds with the United Kingdom were clear."\par \par \uc2 Gunfire broke out earlier today in the western part of Japan during Abe's speech at a parliamentary election campaign. Bullets striking the prominent politician twice, in the back and in the neck. Doctors at the hospital were unable to resuscitate him. Police arrested the 41-year-old suspect, who is a former Japanese Navy veteran.\par \par \uc2 Now, worth keeping in mind, Japan has extremely strict gun laws and it has made it one of the safest nations in the world, with hardly ever any gun violence. That being said, this man, according to police, made this weapon at home. It was a double-barreled 15-inch musket-like weapon, a similar weapon that they found in his residence when they later searched his home - - Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Alex, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Switching gears now to the crisis at our border and a key report from the Customs and Border Patrol on agents who were accused of whipping migrants in Del Rio, Texas. We're going to actually talk to Border Union President Brandon Judd in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But, first, I'm going to go to Bill Melugin in Eagle Pass, Texas, with the very latest -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Charles, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 So, it's basically been 10 months now since this investigation was first launched into these false narrative allegations that Haitian migrants were whipped by horseback agents in Del Rio last summer. And just a couple of hours ago, CBP finally held a press release -- a press conference -- excuse me -- and released the results of their investigation.\par \par \uc2 And they confirmed they found no evidence whatsoever that any Haitian migrants were whipped or struck by these horseback agents. However, they did announce they're going to be disciplining four of these horseback agents for administrative violations.\par \par \uc2 Specifically, they're accusing these agents of working in an unsafe manner, as well as unprofessional work conduct. They highlighted -- highlighted some specifics, accusing one agent of using derogatory language and maneuvering his horse around a small child in a dangerous way.\par \par \uc2 They also accused several other agents of using -- quote -- "threat of force" to drive migrants back into the river. But, again, the highlight, they say there was no evidence that anybody was whipped.\par \par \uc2 I am told by multiple federal sources discipline proposals were sent to the agents yesterday, and those discipline proposals include up to 14 days of unpaid suspension. Take a listen to what the CBP commissioner had to say earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS MAGNUS, COMMISSIONER, U.S. CUSTOMS AND BORDER PROTECTION: OPR found no evidence Border Patrol agents involved in this incident struck any person with their reins, intentionally or otherwise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And I'm told by multiple sources that these agents will have a chance to fight back against these allegations and respond to them. It's just not going to be automatic suspensions. They will have a chance to address these allegations and give their side of the story.\par \par \uc2 It's also important to point out a lot of the media, a lot of politicians and the president himself pushed this false narrative in the first hours and days after this incident last year. CBP confirming today that narrative was false, nobody was whipped. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks, Bill.\par \par \uc2 I want to get right to National Border Patrol Council President Brandon Judd.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, a lot to get in to. First, though, this report from CBP on the agents being cleared of -- quote -- "whipping migrants." And just to remind the audience, and Bill alluded to it, at the time, President Biden said: "It was horrible to see, to treat people like they did, horses, 