par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, I love him.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I have to tell you, this was insightful for a couple of reasons. First of all, it's a clever protest, because a sex strike removes the controversy entirely because you don't need abortions, right. But it was sad, what that gentlemen pointed out, the loneliness, the estrangement that this recreational has created for this generation and the last couple, it's sad because without happy and loving couples you can't have happy and loving families.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That guy was awesome. I said to Raymond during the break, I said is there any conservative in New York at all? He's like wait, that's at the end --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Coming up. And Laura, in the heat of this abortion battle, inexplicably \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  attended a papal mass at the Vatican today. The speaker has been forbidden from presenting herself for communion in her home diocese of San Francisco due to her advocacy for abortion. Still, there she was today gladhanding with the Pope and taking communion. Laura, I've got to tell you, my phone blew up with people saw this. What we do is as important as what we say. And the Pope says the liturgy should be a source of unity, a way to protect our communion. I don't know how this image does that. In fact, I think it estranges people from whatever Catholic doctrine or belief might be.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Pelosi was not the only public figure seeking cover this week.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's right, the creator of one of most popular TV shows of all time, "Friends," Marta Kauffman, for years brushed off complaints that the show was not diverse enough. But now she has come to a new understanding.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: In an interview Kauffman says it is her internalized, systemic racism the caused the lack of inclusivity on the very popular show. Here she is discussing the problem.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTA KAUFFMAN, CREATOR, "FRIENDS": It was to a certain extent a product of the time period and of my own ignorance. There were black shows and there were white shows. There weren't a lot of shows that were interracial. I guess at the time I was thinking this is what I know. This is what I know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: In expiation for her insensitivity, Laura, over the show she created 28 years ago Kauffman is donating $4 million to \uc2 Brandeis University\uc2  to fund a professorship in African American studies. I know this story has you amped. You were a big "Friends" fan.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Here's what I don't like is going back, and you go back in time and say, no, this doesn't count, this was bad. It was a different, it was a different time. But she feels the need to publicly self-flagellate. And how much did she make on "Friends"? Hundreds of millions of dollars, right?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And in syndication.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Remember they got their last contract, Aniston and all --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It was $1 million a pop.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was $1 million for each episode. So if they're really serious about this, you've got to go more than the $4 million. That is not enough.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Look, she can spend her money any way she wants. My question is, if you want to help the black community, why are you giving $4 million to Brandeis? You want to help the black community, there are tons of great faith-based, Demario Davis's program, that actually mentor young black people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, we've got to go. They're yelling at me.\par \par \uc2 All right, he once declared he was the science, but now our good friend Dr. Fauci is back in the news for not following it. We explain with Dr. Peter McCullough who diagnosis it in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: What you're seeing as attacks on me, quite frankly, are attacks on science, because all of the things that I have spoken about consistently from the very beginning have been fundamentally based on science.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Really? But remember, Fauci is the science. But after four vaccine shots and double masking, Fauci somehow got COVID, and then this happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: Given my age, I went on Paxlovid for five days, and I felt really quite well, just a bit of rhinorrhea and fatigue. And after I finished the five days of Paxlovid, I reverted to negative on an antigen test for three days in a row. And then on the fourth day just to be absolutely certain, I tested myself again, and I reverted back to positive by the antigen test. The next day or so I started to feel really poorly, much worse than in the first go around.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We told him it doesn't work. We said that months ago. And now he's taken another round of Paxlovid. So what does the science say? The CDC and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  both say there is no evidence of benefit to a second round of the drug, let alone benefit to the first round if you've been vaxxed or have had COVID previously. We covered this months ago. But here we are with Dr. Peter McCullough again, epidemiologist, cardiologist, also author of "Courage to Face COVID-19." Dr. McCullough, what in the heck is going on here with Fauci?\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER MCCULLOUGH, MD, MPH, INTERNIST AND CARDIOLOGIST: He should go on to a community standard of care, which has been either prolonged hydroxychloroquine or ivermectin, plus steroids in combinations with other medications. Paxlovid has really struggled to join a community standard of care multidrug regimen, and he's basically a classic case example of what the CDC warned of May 24th of this year. They said, listen, those who are fully vaccinated, based on their papers, Gupta, Charness, and Carlin, that in fact if we rely on Paxlovid alone there can be rebound and get worse about two to eight days after they finish the initial course.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So he got a Pax rebound, and he is suffering from that, but I think he's going to be OK, thankfully. But Dr. Jay Varma, who is a Cornell epidemiologist in New York is warning that a possible sixth COVID wave, if you lost track, is on the way. Dr. McCullough, are vaccines making the waves more inevitable? Because you've been discussing this for some time antibody dependent enhancement going on.\par \par \uc2 MCCULLOUGH: There's three analyses by Suvermanion (ph), Campf (ph), and Bede (ph), 145 countries now, ecological analysis, Laura, it's fairly clear that mass vaccination, which has been indiscriminate, is actually making things worse. There's more cases, hospitalizations, and deaths the more populations are vaccinated. It's a mistake to indiscriminately vaccinate in the setting of a highly prevalent pandemic.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: In New York where we were, the last several weeks we have been in New York a lot, and they have these COVID-19 tents, have you seen them on the street corner in New York, you can just walk in any time. Is there any reason to even have that? Obviously, it's expensive, but they're everywhere in New York still.\par \par \uc2 MCCULLOUGH: I don't think there's any role for mass testing. Testing has only been \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved for those who are acutely ill with the virus to establish a diagnosis. They do have any role for asymptomatic or just casual testing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. McCullough, great to see you, as always.\par \par \uc2 All right, I have some great news. Women, you've wanted Freedom Matters gear, and we have it, cool, really cool new polo made in the USA. We have different colors, but I love the pink. I wore this today. And you can get the red or the white for Fourth of July. And if you order before through July 4th, you get a free koozie, one of those things for the drinks to hold the drinks. Anyway, all proceeds for the month of June are going to be donated to the Honor Flight Network, so go to LauraIngraham.com. there's cool stuff there.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we say goodbye to a true American hero. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSHEL WOODY WILLIAMS, WORLD WAR II VETERAN: I know we're classified as a hero very often, but I keep saying I'm not the hero. The heroes didn't get home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: that was Hershel Woody Williams, the last surviving World War II Medal of Honor recipient. He died this morning at age 98. He was originally rejected by the Marines for being too short, but Woody persevered, becoming a flame thrower operator during the battle of Iwo Jima in 1945. He's credited with destroying multiple Japanese pill boxes while being covered by only four riflemen. He fought for four hours under enemy fire, wiping out position after position.\par \par \uc2 And months later Woody received the Medal of Honor from President \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  for his actions. His citation reads, "Woody's unyielding determination and extraordinary heroism in the face of ruthless enemy resistance were directly instrumental in neutralizing one of the of most fanatically defended Japanese strongpoints encountered by his regiment and aided vitally in enabling his company to reach its objective."\par \par \uc2 After retiring from the military, Woody dedicated his life to helping veterans, and of course Gold Star families.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSHEL WOODY WILLIAMS, WORLD WAR II VETERAN: It is so full of miracles in my life, I can't count them all. And for those miracles to happen, it could only happen in a country like we have. It wouldn't happen in any other country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my gosh. Hershel Woody Williams, he truly loved this country, he loved America, everything it stood for and it stands for to this day, which is why we need to keep fighting to preserve the true mission of our military. It's not about gender identity or diversity equity inclusion or whatever the latest fad is. It's rather about ensuring our nation's security. First principles. So God bless you Woody and every other veteran and current servicemember tonight and always.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it is America now and forever.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220630ei6t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Yellen Admits She Was Wrong About Transitory Inflation; Mom In Vicious LA Hit And Run Talks To Tucker; Another Caravan Is Making Its Way To America; WaPo Suspends Reporter For Retweeting A Joke; Workplace Tyranny: Canadian HR Executive Tells Ottawa Truckers And Supporters They Could Be Fired; Georgia Law Lecturer Was Reinstated, But Had To Leave Due To Toxic Environment\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Charlie Gasparino \par \uc2 7,799 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST": We started this procedure. If I said something or I was about to say something that might get me in trouble, I hold her hand and she squeezes it if I'm about to go over the edge, and it's worked out so far, I have yet to be suspended.\par \par \uc2 Tucker is up next. Always remember, I'm Watters, and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT: Good evening, and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 Happy Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 It was six years ago in one of those semi-hilarious moments that has unfortunately been lost to history that \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  sat through what must have been the single most uncomfortable congressional hearing of her life.\par \par \uc2 At the time, you may remember, Yellen was running the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and she was, as she remains today, a lifestyle liberal in good standing.\par \par \uc2 So, put yourself in her position and imagine the shock she must have felt when members of the Congressional Black Caucus suggested that she, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , was, in fact, a racist, maybe even a White supremacist. What did \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  do wrong? Well, they suggested what she did wrong was she failed to harness the power of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to help African- Americans specifically.\par \par \uc2 Now, at first, Yellen seemed confused by this complaint. The point of the Fed is to keep the American economy stable. The point is not to pay off loyal blocs of Democratic voters, and she tried to explain this.\par \par \uc2 "Our powers," she said, "can't be targeted at the experience of particular groups." That was her response, but the Congressional Black Caucus did not buy it. Their message to \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  was the same as their message to you and every other person in this country of 350 million people: "Do what we say, or we will denounce you as a racist."\par \par \uc2 And finally, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  got that message. Boy, did she.\par \par \uc2 Within a year, Yellen had all but abandoned the traditional constraints of monetary policy. Instead, there she was yammering on in public about things like racial equity and environmental justice.\par \par \uc2 Now, those are issues that, unlike economics, cannot be quantified or even specifically defined. They are therefore perfect vehicles for power-hungry politicians hoping to become more powerful.\par \par \uc2 Now, Yellen was supposedly an economist. But as she turned 70, she left the field of economics completely and entered the far more familiar world of political activism.\par \par \uc2 By the time she became \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Treasury Secretary, the transformation was complete. Among Yellen's first acts as Treasury Secretary was an order changing the mission of the Treasury Department's Financial Stability Oversight Council.\par \par \uc2 Now, that Council was created by Congress after the last financial crisis, as its name suggests, to prevent something similar from ever happening again. And for about a decade, it focused on questions that might prevent another meltdown, questions like subprime lending and automated trading on Wall Street, relevant questions.\par \par \uc2 But under \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , the council turned its attention to a far more fashionable issue that she was consumed with: Global warming.\par \par \uc2 Quote: "We must look ahead at emerging risks," Yellen proclaimed, "Climate change is obviously the big one." She sent a letter to the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  telling them the same thing.\par \par \uc2 As for inflation, which traditionally was a core concern of government economists, that was the emerging risk they were worried about, Yellen just shrugged that off. Inflation? If that happens, she told us in a now-famous quote, it'll be quote, "transitory."\par \par \uc2 In the history of bad predictions from Washington, which could fill volumes, that might take first place.\par \par \uc2 In fact, since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became President the price of gasoline has doubled, and so have the prices of many other key commodities that Americans need to live, and the worst part is that not only did \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , the Treasury Secretary, fail to see any of this coming.\par \par \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , more than any other single person in America, caused it in the first place through reckless, loose money policies implemented during her years at the Fed, policies that were bound to cause inflation and did.\par \par \uc2 So, if you're mad about the current state of the economy, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is probably the first person you should blame. Why does she still have her job? It's a good question. And we might have had an answer to that because Yellen was back in front of Congress today, but this time there were no tough interrogatories from the Congressional Black Caucus. No one called her a racist. Instead, they kissed up to her.\par \par \uc2 As Yellen explained to a deeply sympathetic room of Members of Congress, the real problem with the American economy is that she and the Democratic Party don't yet have enough control over it. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY: Look, over the medium term, the critical thing is that we become more dependent on the wind and the sun that are not subject to geopolitical influences and passing clean energy credits that will boost nonrenewable is, I think, really, really critical to addressing climate change and our energy costs for households going forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So just for fun, if you have five minutes, rewind the tape, transcribe what she just said, and see if you can make sense of the verbal bouillabaisse.\par \par \uc2 What are you looking at? It's called intersectionalism. It's the new religion of government, and it allows bureaucrats when it becomes clear they have no idea what they're doing to just pivot and talk about something they know even less about. So, here you have \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  talking about geopolitical influences, clean energy credits, climate change.\par \par \uc2 What is \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  talking about? Well, she has no idea. The problem is that there are people out there who actually do understand what she's trying to talk about. Experts, actual experts, have studied the sustainability of renewable energy for years.\par \par \uc2 Michael Shellenberger, for example, found that from 2011 to 2017, the cost of solar panels dropped by more than 70 percent in this country. There are more of them. It's a supply and demand question. But here's the interesting part, during that same period as the cost of solar panels fell, electricity prices in California went up five times more than they did in the rest of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Why more solar panels? The solar panels were cheaper, but the electricity they produced was more expensive. Huh? How does that work and how is it helping you?\par \par \uc2 We know it happens because it happened in Germany at scale. From 2006 to 2018, that would be the heyday, the peak years of Germany's big renewable energy push. Electricity prices went up 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 Now, why did that happen? Well, let's compare it to a neighboring country. In France, electricity prices were much lower than Germany's even though France gets twice as much of its energy from clean energy sources as Germany does.\par \par \uc2 What? What made France so special? How'd they pull that off? Simple. Nuclear.\par \par \uc2 France gets a lot of its energy from nuclear power. \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  does not consider nuclear power a renewable. When she says renewable, she means wind and solar, which by definition are not reliable sources of energy, but they are and this is always the key, highly profitable to Democratic donors and the government of China.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is for them. Of course, Yellen didn't go into details on why she thinks what she does, because if you unpack it, if you slow down and speak clearly, it doesn't make any sense and \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  knows that. She's an activist. She gets that logic is irrelevant.\par \par \uc2 Thinking clearly? No, that gets you nowhere. Emotion is what carries the day, and that's why during today's congressional hearings, she started talking about yet another topic she knows nothing about -- gun crime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: I am also horrified by gun violence. What we've seen in recent weeks, over and over many years, and I do hope that Congress will take long-overdue action and put in place commonsense measures to reduce gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "Well, I'm horrified about gun violence." I'm sorry, ma'am, you're the Treasury secretary. Shh. Let's talk about the economy, which is imploding. Stop with your little abortion talk, your little gun talk. Not your area. Let's get back into your lane. You're the Treasury Secretary.\par \par \uc2 But of course, she doesn't want to. The goal always is to talk about anything other than the economy. Why? Because they destroyed the economy.\par \par \uc2 But there was one exception. At one point, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  was asked because it's a pretty obvious question, how exactly as the Treasury Secretary and former Fed chair, as an economist and a genius, how do you miss the inflation you caused? Here it went.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: When I said that inflation would be transitory, what I was not anticipating was a scenario in which we would end up contending with multiple variants of COVID that would be scrambling our economy and global supply chains, and I was not envisioning impacts on food and energy prices we've seen from Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So as Chair Powell indicated himself, both of us probably could have used a better term than "transitory."\par \par \uc2 I do expect inflation to remain high, although I very much hope that it will be coming down now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, so you didn't see COVID coming, even though it was here for two years and we shut down the entire economy and then made up for the gap in productivity with profligate government spending where we just printed trillions of fake dollars, but you didn't think that was going to cause inflation somehow? And you didn't think a war in the breadbasket of Europe would affect food prices?\par \par \uc2 Let's say you were a talk show host on a cable channel with no economic experience whatsoever or even the ability to balance a checkbook and it was obvious to you, but, you're the Treasury Secretary, but you didn't see it coming? Maybe you shouldn't called it transitory?\par \par \uc2 And your answer is, and we're quoting, "I do expect inflation to remain high, although I do very much hope it will be coming down." Oh, you very much hope? Is that where we are? We are very much hoping?\par \par \uc2 Tell us more about abortion and gun violence if you would. Blame COVID and Putin and call it a day. It is a joke.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Hillary Vaughn, to her credit, was not convinced. She asked \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's decision to spend trillions of dollars might have contributed to inflation because like when you have more of something, it's worth less, like your currency, which is almost worth less and here was \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Have you ever warned the White House that increased government spending could have contributed to the inflation that we're seeing today?\par \par \uc2 (Yellen waves off question and doesn't answer.)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Go away, peasant, troll, your inflation talk. I'm worried about climate change and gun violence and abortion. Leave me alone, it's not like I am Treasury secretary. Be gone.\par \par \uc2 She should be gone. This is crazy. And in a functioning country, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  would be in a retirement home somewhere writing her memoirs that no one would ever read, but that's not what's happening. She is still the Treasury Secretary, and that's not what happened today.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Democrats took turns bragging about their expensive electric cars. Worried about gas prices? Just get a \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , baby. Here is Senator Debbie Stabenow of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEBBIE STABENOW (D-MI): Well, good morning, Madame Secretary. We're so glad to have you with us. I do have to say, just on the issue of gas prices, after waiting for a long time to have enough chips in this country to finally get my electric vehicle, I got it and drove it from Michigan to here this last weekend and went by every single gas station, it didn't matter how high it was.\par \par \uc2 And so, I'm looking forward to the opportunity for us to move to vehicles that aren't going to be dependent on the whims of the oil companies and the international markets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, because electric cars don't actually use energy, so they're disconnected from the international market. Hey, Debbie Stabenow, what's an international market? Do you have any idea whatsoever? Do you know anything?\par \par \uc2 You're telling us that the oil companies are so greedy they somehow canceled their own leases for drilling. They're so greedy. They don't even want to drill oil and that's why they shut down their own pipelines. Is that right? And that's why they're talking about implementing a gas tax, because they don't want you to use oil. Right?\par \par \uc2 What's the reasoning here, Debbie Stabenow? Maybe we should contact \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 . Maybe she'll answer. Maybe she'll wave us away like the peasants we are.\par \par \uc2 What's going on here? How can this person be our Treasury Secretary? We're in deep waters. Is there anyone who can pull us out of them?\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino has spent a lot of time covering this and thinking about it. He is a senior correspondent at FOX Business and joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, I have to say I'm making fun of it because I'm not really sure what the other answer is. But you think of Treasury Secretaries as sober people who at least want to tackle the core problems of the American economy, inflation is always going to be a core problem, and she waves away the question like out of my face peasants, like, what is this?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: It was -- it was horrible. And you know, I can't think of a more inept Treasury Secretary over the past 30 years based on you know, record just what they've done and what they haven't done.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  -- maybe Nick Brady, and I'm dating myself when I bring that, right, H.W. Bush, but maybe, but you know, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is -- I mean, think of it this way, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 You're \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 . You come into office in January 2021 with an 80-mile an hour fastball down the middle of the plate, and that's called the U.S. economy. And here's why. The economy was improving greatly. You're in -- you know, people forgot this. They were all you know -- I know the election was crazy. I know, January 6 was nuts.\par \par \uc2 But the Trump economy was pretty damn strong, particularly as he left office. On top of that he delivered a vaccine, which was effective against those first two very deadly variants, less effective on omicron, but it did matter, okay. It was basically we were coming out of the pandemic, the economy was going to improve and this is what they did.\par \par \uc2 They turn around and they spent trillions of dollars on nothing, and they expected no inflation. They encouraged Jerome Powell to keep printing money when he didn't need to.\par \par \uc2 It was the very -- it was a textbook recipe for inflation that you have to be --\par \par \uc2 Listen, I have the equivalent of a minor in Econ and I could see it coming.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I mean, she is supposedly a great economist and you know and everything they've done since then, increased regulations hurts supply. Remember that cuts back supply. Higher taxes cuts back supply. Some of the crazy people they've put in office that are anti-business. Environmental mandates which prevent supply of oil.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, it's not just her it's the whole team. The word is that she is out after the midterms, that \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2 , the Commerce Secretary might replace her. I mean, Gary Gensler, the SEC Chair, but you know, these are all apparatchiks that just have no clue.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, the reason why -- what's really bad about \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , I think what's really -- she's supposed to know better. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came in there trying to be more transformative than Barack, his old boss, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . That means he spent. He didn't think what he was spending on. He just spent.\par \par \uc2 She should have sat there and told him stop. She should have known better and she obviously didn't.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's just -- it is lacking seriousness on the most basic level.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: It really is.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It really is. These are not adults, and that makes me uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino, appreciate your perspective. And as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So if you got really rich, let's just say out of nowhere, what would you do with the money? It's a hard question. A lot of people waste it. They do nothing interesting with it.\par \par \uc2 Kid Rock has been in the entertainment business and successful for like 30 years. He has taken his money and built what he calls a Redneck Paradise on a farm outside Nashville. It includes a version of the White House in which he lives, a church, a barber shop, a gas station with a garage and those little vacuums that you -- it is amazing, and he took us on a tour of it for our latest documentary series that came out today on FOX Nation. Here is an extended look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You see Kid Rock on stage and you have to wonder how does he live in his private life? Answer in a very tidy way.\par \par \uc2 Everything on the ranch is neat, clean, and orderly.\par \par \uc2 KID ROCK, SINGER/SONGWRITER: The things that drive me nuts like symmetry.\par \par \uc2 My friends will come over. I don't have my pictures bolted down, they'll just turn them a little bit just to watch me walk around straighten them all out. But I mean people that don't push their chairs when they get up from the dinner table.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KID ROCK: You know when you've got a nice glass door with some gold leafing on it and they just push the glass, just stuff -- little stuff drives me nuts, you know, I'm not going to let it bother me at the end of the day, I just kind of crack and beer and get the Windex and wipe it off.\par \par \uc2 But you know, I'm very anal person like that. I like things clean, tidy, organized and I've always been like that with everything.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's so interesting.\par \par \uc2 KID ROCK: That's probably not doing much right now for the American badass image I've been selling.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, but seems related to it. I mean you like order even though --\par \par \uc2 KID ROCK: Even though I create chaos.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 Wait so we're leaving the property?\par \par \uc2 KID ROCK: