ony I thought if I was a juror -- I haven't seen every minute of this -- but I have real questions about what she's had to say and now the jury apparently agreed.\par \par \uc2 She now says she is upset about this, and she says I seem to have lost a right I thought I had as an American to speak freely and openly. So, she is not conceding anything at this point.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, she used the me-too movement and now she has betrayed the me-too movement. You can't believe all women is basically what this case is saying. Judge, you were right from the beginning, I'm going to go ahead and say that when we first cover this you said, I don't believe that lady! Those were your words.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And you turned out to be vindicated much like Mr. Depp.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Look, here's the bottom line. For this woman to come out and say it's a setback for women, I think she needs to understand one thing. And that is that what she did was harmful to real victims of domestic violence.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And I'm sorry for saying it again, I started the first domestic violence in the nation, I have dealt with battered women for 30 years. As soon as she started it was clear that she was not the victim, she was the abuser and Johnny Depp had lawyers who could take her on this. Camille Vasquez was right on target with her.\par \par \uc2 The tape recordings where she said Johnny, are you going to be a baby, are you going to tell everybody I didn't hit you hard enough? It was difficult for this man to make a decision to put his whole life on the table, but he had nothing to lose, and when they said to him what have you lost this as a result of what she did, he said nothing short of everything.\par \par \uc2 And so, this, she tried to promote herself as part of the me-too movement. That here I am, a woman who is being abused. And she said, you know, the defense was I don't know -- we didn't know if it was about Johnny Depp. Are you kidding? Two years earlier she tried to get a temporary restraining order against him and it was in all the newspapers, but she was impossible to believe.\par \par \uc2 And I would tell the jury as a judge, if you find that one witness has falsified, as testified falsely with respect to one fact, you are free to disregard their entire testimony. This woman lied over and over again and I feel free to say that on television. She lied. She was a liar. She was the abuser and he was the victim.\par \par \uc2 And let me just say one more thing. For him to take on this burden, which is not only proof that he was defamed, that she wasn't talking about him, but she was malicious. That jury had to really see right through her to say --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- she was malicious. And they found for him $10 compensatory and $5 million punitive.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Punitive.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Punitive damages that's to punish you, Amber Heard --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: -- for taking on this guy's life and destroying his life so you could be part of the me-too movement. Don't give me this stuff, you know, with it's a setback for women. You do not represent women. You represent the worst of women who try to falsely blame men and that's the end of it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: There you go. You know, Jessica, two-part question. Johnny Depp obviously was not there. Was that a dumb idea or a bold idea in the sense that maybe who is giving her, you know, the f, big f you, you are stuck in Fairfax, I'm in London and you are going to lose. And the second question is, should \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  be impeached? Answer the first one -- second one first.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I am not going to do that. I'm going to do the first one and then say whatever I want.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: OK.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It was both a bold and a dumb idea at the same time. I thought that it didn't really have to do with stick it to Amber Heard. He obviously his lawyers did that, and he did it through the entire trial by being so composed and so open and honest and I want to add to what you were saying originally and what the judge pointed out.\par \par \uc2 He didn't actually just lay it all bear, he talked about being abused by his mother and that his mother abused his father as well.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And watching a man -- and I hate for this to have to be gendered but it is. We talk about domestic abuse all the time and it's usually in the context of women, but it is so much harder for a man, especially a man with that kind of reputation, right? He is a heartthrob, he is Kate Moss on the stand, right, and then we are all going back and looking at --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- pictures of them together --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: So right.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- to lay that out there and it reminded me of when Terry Crews testified on Capitol Hill and talked about being abused. That is not easy and I hope that that makes us all more open to considerations of how much abuse men actually endure in society, both physical and emotional and verbal abuse.\par \par \uc2 So, bold and stupid because he had fans that were camped out of that trial. People remember that woman paying $30,000 --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Every day.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- every day.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Every day.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And I think that you show up and you say thank you for supporting me and kind of completing the arc of the entire trial. But I was shocked, obviously I'm not a lawyer that seem to much higher than I expected.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I thought that he was going to win, I did not know that it was going to be $15 million. And I will be interested to see what the first movie as he gets cast in.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You know, how quickly he's able to rehabilitate --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: May be a Fox Nation special or something like that?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think that would be amazing, amazing. Yes, great, great trials of the past. I'm your host Johnny Depp.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Or pirates, you could do both thing on pirates.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Jack (Inaudible) America.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think we have the budget for it. You should see what they're paying me at Fox Nation.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I've read about that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. You know what?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Is that true?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Last word to Jesse, what does this say about love?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They hate each other.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Isn't that amazing, people can love each other and then just hate each other.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They call that a love-hate relationship, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, they do.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Have you heard that before?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I have a hate -- I have a hate-hate relationship with you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The feeling is mutual.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. All right. Coming up, gun control talks heating up in Congress as the left demands confiscation.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Bipartisan talks on how to respond to school shootings making progress on Capitol Hill. Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and Democrat Senator Richard Blumenthal trying to reach an agreement on the so-called red flag laws to stop would-be killers from purchasing a gun. But are liberals going to accept it? House Democrats have their own agenda that would raise the age to buying assault weapons to 21 and ban the sale of high-capacity magazines. And they are being encouraged by the left.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. BETO O'ROURKE (D-TX): I am still of the position that it may not be