s not interested in one of them, I don't know how it goes anywhere. But with these red flag laws, too, you have to think about due process. Some states take away the guns first when they get a report, and then you have a hearing.\par \par \uc2 But we're talking about a constitutional right. We've got to find some balance there but most people do not believe in giving up constitutional rights without due process.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Including the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, which has been pretty strong on the gun rights.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But, you know, with respect to red flags, Greg, I mean, you know, the Blumenthal and Graham, they're trying to work together on a bill that would allow for the seizure of firearms when someone is a threat to themselves or others with a court order, so it's not the automatic. They are trying to kind of, I think move to the left a little bit to try to get due process in there. What say you?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I -- I like red flag laws but the challenge about due process is that from the phone call, the phone call about Jesse being unstable, to the court hearing, to the hearing, what's being done in between them and what constitutes evidence.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This is really an obvious point, but the fact is that takes time and that's another kind of process that we will have to wait and what happens. But I do think it's worth exploring. Can this law be abused? Any law can be abused, right? But the fact is there are some laws that are abused more than others.\par \par \uc2 For example, hate crime laws. We've seen an explosion in the last, I don't know, five or seven years of hate crime hoaxes. So, people could -- let's say -- let's say Jesse is not a threat to anybody but I decide I'm going to call and say that he's been talking about suicide.\par \par \uc2 So that's the thing you have to think about. That's what happens with hate crime hoaxes. The Jussie Smolletts of the world or the people seeking attention will do stuff like that. Look at Amber Heard. I mean, she basically, you know, she took, you know, an entire movement and made it work for herself.\par \par \uc2 So, that is -- there's a red flag on the red flags but they have to be explored. I just think that everybody would focus on more than one variable because there are not -- this is not a single variable problem and that is why nobody trust \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on this because all he keeps saying is guns and that is why he didn't bring up the idea of hardening schools because that's not the variable.\par \par \uc2 He can only handle one at once and it's whatever somebody is telling him at the time. If somebody has gone up to him and said, you know, we should talk about hardening the schools that would have been the thing that popped out of his mouth but nobody said that because they are going after your guns.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Jesse, we heard the sound from Sheila Jackson Lee, Whoopi Goldberg, as well as \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2 , they either want to take away your AR- 15 or your AK-47s, or they don't you to be able to buy them. You know, this is kind of -- it reminds me of political correctness and the First Amendment. You know, we're first going to start by saying, you know, you can't say it's not politically correct, they start chipping away. Now it's like yes, we don't think you should have this gun or that gun or the other gun. But the Second Amendment is absolute, is it not?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes. So, every time they open their mouths, gun sales skyrocket.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And Biden did it too. He said the 9-millimeter thing, people don't trust him, he has poisoned the well of negotiation and that's why he is not even in the negotiation because he wants to do the assault weapons ban and get -- and the slippery slope the dope, he says, yes, maybe 9 millimeters are on the table.\par \par \uc2 So obviously he is not a serious negotiator. I put some thought into the red flag laws. You had a good point last week, evil versus crazy. Here's how you determine evil and cray. You put it into a threat matrix, and you look a few variables. You got to look at social media because animal cruelty is one of the biggest signs for people.\par \par \uc2 And if this guy down in Texas is out there holding a bloody bag of dead cats, boom, right there, that's something you look at. Also, you got to interview the school and work. Does he have antisocial behavior, is he getting into a lot of fights, has he played hooky for the entire semester.\par \par \uc2 And then you got to go to the home and interview family members. Is this a drug-infested household? You know, you can kind of tell when you go into a home and you interview the family members. Are they clueless? Do they have records, is that a problem? And then you look at clinical psychologist, have them do a whole test to see if he is borderline personality disorder, and then I'm going to have some few ideas that might not really pass legal muster. We'll call them Jesse's law.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jessica's, OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Do they dress all in black, do they listen to death metal like Gutfeld likes. Do they -- are they in church, are they're playing sports? If they're not, that's a red flag. Are they into fringe ideology?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This is -- so you are so going off the --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You're not going to pass.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Follow me here. Follow me. Are they into radical ideologies, black nationalism, white nationalism, terrorism?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's when a judge looks at the whole thing. And if 75 percent of those boxes are checked they remove the guns and then the due process comes in he can appeal while those guns are being held by --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Do you have any hobbies?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO; So, Jessica --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , if you are listening --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I ask do you have any hobbies?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I have none.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, that's why you can't -- that's a red flag. People with no hobbies!\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, guns --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's not a red flag!\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, it is!\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: How would you respond to Jesse's --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: (Inaudible) threat matrix is noted with flag.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: How would you response to Jesse's proposed legislation?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm just surprised he didn't throw in fatherless. I'm sure we were getting to that where he said anyone -- (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: When you visit the home, when you visit the home --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm not.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- you can tell.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Enough of you.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The beginning -- I just started! The beginning part of it, the bag of dead cats, if you haven't seen the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  documentary "Don't F with Cats," you really should. Because it's about a group of online folks who love cats who saw a video, a \uc2 YouTube\uc2  video of a kid abusing cats and ended up finding a murderer because they tracked this behavior.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's questionable.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's a questionable documentary.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But there is something also that needs to be added which is harassment and threats to women.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That's something that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Which are part of this kid --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Which were part of that, and --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And this young woman had reported it --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- and they did nothing about it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And did nothing. And it was interesting, \uc2 Will Hurd\uc2 , the former congressman was pointing out that there is usually also a methodology to the amount of time that you have to this. That there is a red flag that goes off two to three weeks before the actual attack is executed.\par \par \uc2 And it's interesting, I wonder if that's because, you know, it gets hyped up in these Reddit chatrooms or 4chan or wherever they are hanging out and doing this, so it seems like there is a lot of procedural work in terms of the analysis going on that maybe we could have a due process red flag law.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And I think that liberals will sign anything that happens. If you listen to Chris Murphy who represents Connecticut, where Sandy Hook took place, we want to get something done. Guns have to be a part of it as well as other variables. It's not like anyone denies the fact that there are other things that go into it, but at the end of the day --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The people do.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- at the end of the day, as someone who is certainly mentally unfit to have a gun, had a gun to carry out all of these and the access to guns is a huge part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. Coming up, President Biden furious and blaming his staff for his unpopular presidency.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: President Biden is having a hard time facing up to reality so he is taking his anger out on his staff. NBC News dropping a dime on the president with a new expose and it is a doozy. Biden is annoyed that his staff is always rushing to correct his gaffes because it undermines his credibility and his authenticity.\par \par \uc2 The president also reportedly frustrated with how badly his administration is being run. No kidding. And he says his fellow Democrats do not defend him enough in the media, even though it's overwhelmingly liberal.\par \par \uc2 Jessica, the media is comprised entirely almost of Democrats and almost always they are defending \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . What does it say when you have the president complaining about a lack of defense?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Every president complains about their coverage. It's just the way it goes --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Jessica, they should never complain. They should be grateful.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'll let them know. Stop complaining. Yesterday the inflation conversation and gas prices I think turn -- took a very serious turn with \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  giving an interview where she said that she was wrong about what was going to happen with inflation.\par \par \uc2 And now, Steve Rattner and \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , both together with the same point about the spending bill. And it's interesting because there's a division now amongst Democrats who one side feels like we should just acknowledge that the American Rescue Act had this effect that we still stand by it as policy and that the country needed it at this time, but that something was done wrong. And then the White House is more dug in on the I'm not going to admit anything.\par \par \uc2 And it'll be really interesting going forward to see how candidates are taking this because there's going to be very split. And in purple districts, you're going to see people saying I voted for this because I thought it was the right thing to do. It has had this negative effect and we need to watch what we do in terms of big spending bills going forward in the last few years before the next general election. That didn't answer your question.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No, you didn't.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But I'm glad I got it out there.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's OK. Get it off your chest. Judge, you know, you notice this NBC report and they're not talking about Joe solving problems with gas prices, no new solutions on inflation. He's just lashing out at everybody. What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, I think that what it tells me is he's not -- he's not really in control. I mean, I what I want to know is who keeps correcting him? Joe, why don't you find out who's correcting you and then let the person go if you're upset with them for correcting everything that you're saying. But if you're so clueless you don't know who's correcting you, then you pretty much deserve what you got.\par \par \uc2 I think that one of the problems is that, you know, no one -- forget about people going on television to support them, they don't even want them in their district.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I mean, and you know, this guy's got the lowest presidential improving rate -- approval ratings ever, ever. Am I wrong? I think it's ever. No one's beating him.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: So, he just beat his way to the bottom. And you know what? No one works harder anymore.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I've been there.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You beat your way to the bottom with the best, don't you, Greg? You sick son of a --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Nothing but red flags.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I like the fact that he claims he's not getting enough credit. It's like, yes, sure, gas prices are so high. But that means you can spend more time at home with your family. And think of all the money you save on travel mugs and bumper stickers. To continue the car analogy, we were sold a lemon. And if a lemon could talk, it would tell you it's your fault for buying him. But it's not the voters fault at all because they were sold the lemon under false pretenses.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: They rolled back the odometer, right? They gave it a new paint job, put on some new rims. Here's Joe just like new except he's a moderate just like Old Joe. He's great for road trips and unifying. And then it turns out everything was a con job. The irony of this whole thing is that Trump got blamed for being a salesman, right, when basically all he was trying to do was sell himself, and then when he became president, sell America to the world. That was his job.\par \par \uc2 Biden was the sale of the century, a con job orchestrated by the far left in order to get him in so they could fill him with their progressive agenda. It's probably one of the greatest bait and switches in political history, and we're alive to see it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It is a very nice time to be alive. Shannon, when you read this NBC News report and you see this one line where he says he doesn't like was administration is being run. He's the chief executive. He's the one running the administration. He can change and fire and do whatever he wants.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And apparently, that's how the American people feel as well, because in all the most recent rounds of polling, he is down double-digit with people who voted for him, young voters, Black voters, Latino voters, Independents. Like, this is going to be a nightmare for him if he doesn't turn around something before the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 The economy, everybody now admits including his advisors, is not going to get better before it gets worse. And even with this baby formula thing. Those are the kinds of things that people vote on filling up their gas tank, putting food on the table. Now, today, they're saying even with these shipments coming in, the Wall Street Journal says the shortage is actually going to get worse on baby formula and it will take months and fix that.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's right. And no popcorns.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No pop --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Don't even get started on my popcorn.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No popcorn?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Don't. Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: There's going to be a shortage.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Popcorn shortage?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I fill up my gas tank with baby formula. That's how bad it's got.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Put popcorn.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: So, when the engine gets hot, the pop -- the corn pops.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I have popcorn and I have baby formula in my gas tank.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: At the same time.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: At the same time.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a bunch of red flags.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Solving problems. All right, ahead, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is cracking down on remote workers and it could be spreading to an office near you.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Remote workers, beware. The good times of having meetings in your pajamas from the comfort of home might be over as more companies demand people back to the office. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is giving his workers an ultimatum, return to the workspace or work elsewhere. That's according to a leaked email the billionaire sent to \uc2 Tesla\uc2  employees which tells executive staff that they must be in the cubicle for a minimum of 40 hours per week or depart the company.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, what do you think the reaction to this is going to be from within \uc2 Tesla\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there's so many ripple effects. Listen, we have the numbers that 4.4 million people quit their jobs in April. We've got over 11 million job openings. So, if you would like to go -- it's like when \uc2 Netflix\uc2  said if you can't deal with things that we're going to put out, maybe you shouldn't work here anymore. I mean, there are other jobs out there.\par \par \uc2 But listen, if people have really been still at home in their pajamas, it's a pretty sweet gig if you can get it. You just can't keep it. So, I'll be interested to see what happens. But listen, I had a guy in the beginning of this on my team who was working from Puerto Rico, and he would, you know, Zoom in from the beach and I thought well, that's pretty sweet. If you could keep it, why would you want to give that up?\par \par \uc2 But I do know that the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  had an article a couple of weeks ago about how this is devastating for pets. And we should worry about our animals because they're going to be freaked out and suffer depression when we all come back to work. So, think of the animals.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Join the mental health crisis.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Jesse, so there's been a lot of evidence that efficiency did not drop during the pandemic for companies. We saw companies obviously doing quite well. Do you think that it is time to go back at least to some sort of hybrid model where -- obviously we're at work right in person here, and I know it was the preference as well to not be in remote studios, but --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No, I wanted to, again, summer remotely, but Greg drag us all back to the office. But in hindsight, that was a good move. A couple of reasons to get back to the office, gossip. Office gossip is currency. And when you use it appropriately, it can really just shoot you into the upper echelons of the executive level. Also love connections.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I knew you would say that.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You can't find love sitting home alone. Many people date in the office, many people fall in love and get married in the office.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I've heard of that.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right. Also, if you're tall, no one knows how tall you are on Zoom. As we've shown, studies will prove the tall people will excel in a corporate environment. Also, the power lunch. You're not having the power lunch at home with your parents or your son. We got to bring the power lunch back.\par \par \uc2 Also, if you isolate the country anymore, we're all going to turn into mass shooters. We saw what COVID did. If you stay home alone and just stare at a screen, people are going to get violent. We all need to get back to work.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: This is another aspect of Jesse's law, I guess.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Or they could be like Jeffrey Toobin. You know, he's done too many