uc2 PIRRO: That's great.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: That's coming up on "ONE NATION" Saturday at 8:00.eight.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And you know what, everybody should get that book because I'm in there too.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: It's great. It's great.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Did you see I was in it?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right, but it was really about Tyrus.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, OK, clearly. All right, well, that's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hey, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , how are you?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220512ei5b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Border Crisis; Search Continues For Escaped Inmate, Guard; Unrest in China Over Lockdowns; Another Russian Ship Hit?; Mariupol Evacuation Efforts; Wild Stock Swings; Interview With White House Economic Adviser Jared Bernstein\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Connell McShane, Abby Hornacek, Nate Foy, Steve  Harrigan, Jackie DeAngelis, David Perkins, Trey Yingst, Edward Lawrence,  Jennifer Griffin; Susan Li \par \uc2 8,181 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, well, here's the bad news. We lost a little war ground in the stock market again. Here's the good news. The week is over.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 And what in the world was going on with stocks this incredibly volatile week, a week where we saw the Dow move up 1,000 points, the very next day fall 1,000 points, and to see major averages skidding and skidding again.\par \par \uc2 With the Dow's losses today and the Nasdaq's losses today, we're looking at levels and a streak we have not seen for the better part of three years, six weeks in a row of these losses.\par \par \uc2 To \uc2 Susan Li\uc2  on what's behind them and what could be coming -- Susan.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, over $1.5 trillion in stock market losses the last two sessions. So that's roughly the size of Canada's economy that's been wiped out virtually in two days, the broader S&P close to its lowest in a year.\par \par \uc2 And that's despite better-than-anticipated April jobs numbers this morning. And this might be a case where good news is bad for the stock market, because the better numbers gives the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  more room to hike interest rates even higher, more than 428,000 new jobs created in the month of April. So the jobless rate did tick upwards to 3.6 percent. That's 12th month of job gains above 400,000.\par \par \uc2 But despite wages going up 5.5 percent in April, that's still less than inflation, which spiked to 8.5 percent in March, highest in 40 years. And there are also worrying signs that wage gains are stagnating, while inflation remains at elevated levels.\par \par \uc2 So that means the Fed might be forced to raise interest rates even faster. You have more than three-quarters of Wall Street predicting rates will go up by three-quarters of 1 percent at next month's June meeting. And that's despite Chairman Powell, by the way, assuring markets this week that he wasn't even actively considering it.\par \par \uc2 Now, technology stocks have probably been hit the hardest the past few days, really for this entire year, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's parent, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , both down by at least a third this year. And just one more note, Neil, on that jobs report. You know the participation rate might be key here, with the number of people in the work force actually dropping for the first time in nine months.\par \par \uc2 And it might explain why we have record job openings and historically high wage growth. But that also means that we're looking at inflation levels probably elevated still for some time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: For some time is right. Susan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Susan Li\uc2  on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Because the irony with the today's trading and the downsides and minus signs we started seeing was built on a pretty good employment report that once again has fueled the argument the underlying economy looks pretty good, so the prospect for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  continuing to raise interest rates, unfortunately, looks pretty good.\par \par \uc2 Edward Lawrence at the White House with more on that side of the story -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the president taking a megaphone when talking about this jobs report, touting it beat expectations, saying, yes, the economy's doing very well, but that megaphone kind of cuts out when he starts talking about inflation.\par \par \uc2 Now, this was a good jobs report. Overall, though, we're still down 1.2 million jobs from February of 2020 levels. Now, you can see there are some sectors that actually created jobs. Others, like leisure and hospitality, are down 1.4 million jobs from that 2020 benchmark.\par \par \uc2 The president saying the solution to all of these economic problems is government spending, like the Innovation Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Capitalist. I'm here and I believe in the free market. But I believe in the federal government's obligation to invest in America itself, invest in American workers.\par \par \uc2 And we're at risk of falling behind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, Steve Forbes is in the other camp, saying the Biden administration is actually slowing the economic growth that we're seeing and it needs to get out of the way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE FORBES, CEO, \uc2 FORBES INC\uc2 .: I think we would have made more progress if they hadn't put artificial barriers in the way, for example, on the energy front, artificially raising the cost of energy, which means businesses are being squeezed, which means they're not going to have the capacity to hire more people in the future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And stuck in the middle, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . The top man there concerned inflation could slow down spending with interest rate increases, but he hasn't seen that yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , FEDERAL RESERVE CHAIRMAN: Now, of course, given events around the world and fading fiscal policy effects and higher rates, you could see some slower economic activity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, and that's what the markets are concerned about, slower economic growth in the future. You saw those wild swings, down 400 today, up, and then finishing down, what, about 96 points on the Dow -- back here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Edward, in the meantime, the administration seems to be placing its faith in the Federal Reserve to do what it can to improve this or cut on inflation.\par \par \uc2 But outside of a spending initiative that I would imagine would go nowhere in Congress at this time, they seem to be out of cards.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. And that's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 The White House has basically said they want to push their spending agenda forward. And that's how you lower costs. They're not talking about inflation, costs. But that spending, it's been stalled in Congress here.\par \par \uc2 They also have tried to shift the inflation debate over to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , saying it lies in their purview. The Fed chairman had testified in the past before the CARES Act that no fiscal spending could be too large. Now he won't give his opinion about should there be more fiscal spending.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Edward Lawrence, thank you, my friend very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Erin Gibbs right down, the Main Street Asset Management CIO.\par \par \uc2 Erin, there are a lot of numbers in this employment report. There are not a lot of numbers to look at the averages. The one that was the most telling to me, the good news of wages running at about a 5.5 percent clip, not bad. Unfortunately, the cost of things that people want to buy with those higher wages running even higher and faster at 8.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 That's not good.\par \par \uc2 ERIN GIBBS, MAIN STREET ASSET MANAGEMENT: Now, it certainly isn't.\par \par \uc2 And I think we can delve into where the wage growth is. I think there are a lot of improvements, particularly on the -- those that are making less money, that the wage growth is higher. But, certainly, I think what we're all really hoping for is that some of these inflationary pressures will eventually subside, the supply side, and, eventually, the wage growth and the inflation will meet together.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, it does not look good.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, when you say they eventually meet together, that means what the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been doing right now, Erin -- and you have been reminding of this through this cycle that has just started, really -- is raise rates enough to at least match whatever is happening on the inflation front.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, it's got overnight bank lending rates -- it's a little arcane, but at around three-quarters to 1 percent. But we would conceivably have to get that up to 5 percent, actually more, just to be even with this. And that's going to be disruptive, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And so what the Fed is betting on is that inflation will come down, that some of the supply chain issues -- and I know they were wrong about it being purely supply chain, but that is part of it. It's not completely wage growth -- that those will be relieved, that will bring the inflation down, and as we keep rising higher overnight rates, that other inflationary pressures will diminish and slowly converge.\par \par \uc2 And that's the idea of this soft landing, that everybody has their fingers crossed that sort of your inflationary pressures lower as you increase your rates, and you don't have increase the rates up to 5. Hopefully, their target is around 3.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: And that's what everybody's fingers are crossed.\par \par \uc2 But, certainly, that's what we see, that investors are nervous that is not what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, this all happens with the backdrop of mortgages that are getting pricier.\par \par \uc2 I mean, age and perspective is everything. But for a lot of folks who didn't know double-digit anything when it came to borrowing rates, we're now up to about 5.25 percent on a 30-year fixed mortgage. So that's added to some concern that housing is going to get hit.\par \par \uc2 Do you think housing gets hit?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes. And we have seen that, a lot of those indications, both in the real estate bond market and real estate stock market, that their expectations that -- are very much so that these prices are going to come down.\par \par \uc2 Some feel that it's healthy, because some areas were considered a little too high. But, definitely, I think that the housing prices absolutely are going to take a slowdown. And we may see -- there's so much supply demand. I think still people are going to be able to build houses and sell them. Just the prices may be a little lower.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Erin, what do you tell people who look, if they haven't sold throughout this, they're looking at big paper losses? They're scared. Maybe some investors out there had bought \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2  and seen their performance just go at a torrid pace.\par \par \uc2 And they're tempted, maybe I just got to get out of this. Maybe this bear market takes hold, and I will have even less than I do now. What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: So, it certainly is particularly hard, not just for the people that have bought \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , but even investors who were in more defensive assets like bonds.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Those bond prices have come down.\par \par \uc2 So when I'm talking to investors, I'm really talking to people who have -- their safe haven is going down almost as fast a rate as their -- their riskier stocks have gone down. And so this coupling of both the safe havens and the more riskier assets is really hard on investors' nerves.\par \par \uc2 And us see that in the market. There's a lot of negative sentiment. So, for some specific stocks, I think that high-valuation tech stocks, yes, you may want to take those losses, be able to carry them and use them against your gains going forward.\par \par \uc2 For other assets that are higher-quality, I would say, look, we're just in this massively fearful environment right now. We have to wait for the Fed and inflation to kind of play out. And it is better to just hold it for the next six months, rather than come out.\par \par \uc2 So, overall, as a long-term investor, I'd say stay in. I know it's very hard, but, certainly, for -- it's very stock-specific, and in those technology stocks, I might say you might want to take those losses and use them for future gains.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, again, long term to you, because, for me -- you're very young, Erin, for me, it's like lunch on Monday. I assume you're talking a little bit longer time frame?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes, a couple years, several years, where you don't...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: You're not pulling out the cash today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood.\par \par \uc2 Erin, thank you. Have a wonderful weekend, Erin Gibbs following all these market developments.\par \par \uc2 We will be talking to White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein on all of this, what comes next and what the administration might be planning to do next.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, focus half-a-world away, where folks are obviously worried about something far more urgent and horrifying, a war that is not easing up and a battle for Mariupol that is now one for the history books -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fierce fighting continues in Ukraine, particularly around Mariupol, that has been reduced to rubble, and just trying to get people out of there, average civilians out of there, to say nothing of some 2,000 soldiers still trapped there.\par \par \uc2 Well, it's a task, a Herculean task.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst right now with the latest from Kyiv -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 We are following some breaking news today out of Ukraine, where reports do indicate that a Russian warship is on fire in the Black Sea. These reports saying this took place near Snake Island. We cannot independently confirm this information, but it would be significant if confirmed.\par \par \uc2 It does come as we are following those developments out of the southern port city of Mariupol. British intelligence officials say Russian forces plan on hammering that steel plant in the coming days, ahead of Monday, where Russia will mark Victory Day.\par \par \uc2 We did catch up with Ukraine's minister of internal affairs to discuss the situation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice-over): Five days before Russia invades Ukraine, the minister of internal affairs, Denys Monastyrsky, visits the eastern front lines.\par \par \uc2 At the time, he's joined by Denys Prokopenko, a lieutenant colonel in the National Guard and commander of the Azov regiment.\par \par \uc2 DENYS MONASTYRSKY, UKRAINIAN INTERNAL AFFAIRS MINISTER: I think we're ready for any scenario. And I see our armed force also are ready for any scenario.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Eleven weeks later, Monastyrsky is back in the Ukrainian capital working day and night, as Lieutenant Colonel Prokopenko is inside the Azov steel plant, where Ukrainian forces defend the last pocket of territory they hold in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 MONASTYRSKY (through translator): Today, he's a hero of Ukraine. Today, he's one of the main defenders of Mariupol. He's the one who organizes the defense of the Azov steel plant.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Over the past two days, more than 500 civilians have been evacuated from the steel plant through corridors organized by the United Nations. While officials in Moscow agreed to a cease-fire during this time period, Russian forces continue to bomb the plant from the air and the ground.\par \par \uc2 MONASTYRSKY (through translator): We understand there's no logic in Putin's actions. He has this thirst for victory. He has the 9th of May that's upcoming, when he needs to bring somebody's head for his people and show he's victorious. If it's not happening, he's going to make further steps to further escalate the conflict.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Ukrainian officials don't know what Russian troops will do if they are able to get further into this steel plant. They do know alongside those civilians sits 2,000 Ukrainian soldiers, who are currently taking fire from Russian military troops on the ground -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Trey Yingst in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Trey, very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to this report that Trey had of yet another Russian ship that could be sinking, hit by Ukrainian fire. Now there were a number of reports that U.S. intelligence was helping, at least on one wardship hit. Whether that appears or could be the case here is anyone's guess.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 How's the Pentagon been responding to this talk that they are very integrally involved in helping the Ukrainians do this?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby has pushed back on the reports that U.S. intelligence helped target the sinking of the warship, the Russian warship the Moskva, adding that such leaks are damaging to U.S. ability to share intelligence with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 He added that Ukraine has many sources of intelligence that does not include the United States. He said the intelligence us shares with Ukraine is -- quote -- "legitimate, lawful and limited."\par \par \uc2 Kirby said the U.S. does not get heads-up -- get a heads-up about Ukraine's daily targeting of Russian forces. Kirby did announce, however, today at the press briefing that 220 Ukrainian troops have now been trained on U.S. howitzers, radar and armored personnel carriers.\par \par \uc2 Among them, 20 Ukrainian soldiers have been trained how to use the Phoenix Ghost drones. The White House is expected to sign another $100 million in military aid to Ukraine. The Pentagon showed the breakdown today of the disbursements from the $300 million, Ukraine security assistance, announced on April 1, which includes an $18 million Switchblade drone contract.\par \par \uc2 However, this is for the 300 series model of the Switchblade, which is used for anti-personnel and light targets. Pentagon officials won't say what the holdup is for the 600 series model, which is anti-armor and experts say would be more effective against Russian tanks.\par \par \uc2 Russia claims it held simulated nuclear missile airstrikes in Kaliningrad, a city in Russia's western enclave near the Baltic Sea close to Finland and Sweden. Meanwhile, the anniversary that Trey was referring to of when Russia defeated Nazi Germany known as Victory Day is on Monday.\par \par \uc2 In the past, Russia has held large military parades in Red Square, but, this year, Russia could declare an all-out war, instead of a special military operation in Ukraine, meaning it would send more conscripts in. According to U.S. intelligence, many of their forces, the Russian forces in Ukraine, still suffer from poor morale.\par \par \uc2 It could have something to do with Russian conscripts earning as little as $25 a month. There are signs, Neil, that Russia is erecting Soviet era statues in Mariupol, even though the fighting continues there. Today, President Zelenskyy said that 500,000 Ukrainians have been deported to Russia, raising concerns at Russian efforts to -- quote -- "reeducate" them in concentration-style camps, that according to human rights groups -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 To General David Perkins right now, the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  four-star general, FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 General, what do you think \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  does to recognize Victory Day on Monday?\par \par \uc2 GEN. DAVID PERKINS (RET.), FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I mean, clearly, he's going to have to have sort of some victory or some progress to announce in a bellicose way. I think everyone understands whatever he says on May 9 is really for internal consumption. The rest of the world, particularly the West, really fully understand what's going on.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, he can claim the limited successes they have had there, what's going on Mariupol. And we have seen from his history he's not particularly tied to the truth.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 General, I wonder, though. In a way, it would remind Russians how far they as, let's say, a superpower have come. You could look back on that day when the then-Soviet Union took out the Nazis, that that was the beginning of them becoming a superpower.\par \par \uc2 And to compare those triumphs, against all odds, and the 25-plus million Russians lost in that war, and then to look at this, it's night and day. It might actually agitate Russians, couldn't it?\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: Well, that's a great point, because even if Putin were to sort of stretch his level of victory and, say, what's been going on even in the eastern part of Ukraine, as you said, you have this power that he's proclaimed as one of the sole remaining superpowers, really have had very little, if any major progress in a strategic way in Ukraine, which is much less capable, much smaller military.\par \par \uc2 And, in many ways, it is admitting that you are not quite the superpower that you would had led others to believe you are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: General, I'm curious.\par \par \uc2 What would be the difference at this point if \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  were to declare war? Maybe owing to my own ignorance, sir, I thought it was very obvious they were at war now. What would change in a sweeping declaration like that?\par \par \uc2 PERKINS: Well, for one thing, it would be admission his part that this is not this special operation that, literally, it is one nation against a another nation. So that actually could have a downside for him as well.\par \par \uc2 I guess it could allow him, at least psychologically, within Russia to mobilize greater numbers of people, increase the number of conscripts, as Jennifer was just talking about, going down there. So it could give him a temporary boost of painting a more dramatic picture here.\par \par \uc2 But I think, in the end, you could look at it is kind of an admission that, well, we couldn't accomplish the mission with a special operation, so we have had to take it up a notch.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: General, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 David Perkins, the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  four-star general, more importantly to us these days, a FOX contributor, at that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, I want to show you something out of China that is alarming. I have been mentioning how COVID cases are spiking there. This is an \uc2 Apple\uc2  factory where those who work there were going nuts. They have been sort of trapped in that facility for a while, not allowed to get out.\par \par \uc2 But then COVID cases spike up within the facility. And there's disarray and havoc among those who want those affected -- infected out, and that authorities get involved. This is playing out across the country as it deals with spikes that have prompted the government to say, stay in your homes and, if you're getting this and you're at work, stay at work.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese are now saying, in this most authoritarian of states, stop it. It's driving us nuts -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's a good example of people cracking under crackdowns that will not stop in China, this at an \uc2 Apple\uc2  factory. But it's playing out across the country.\par \par \uc2 People are sick of it, but nothing's changing.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's one thing to lock them up in their homes because of COVID spikes in China, quite another right now to do it at their place of work, an \uc2 Apple\uc2  facility here, with fellow workers going after those workers who are being -- well, experiencing these spikes in cases.\par \par \uc2 The government is insisting you stay in place, and those that try to get out or push co-workers who have tested positive for COVID out lead to, well, bedlam. And that's been playing out, again, not only in homes where people can't get out of those homes, but the cases are spiking even as they're in those homes and even as they're protesting at these facilities, like this \uc2 Apple\uc2  plant.\par \par \uc2 Jackie DeAngelis is following it all.\par \par \uc2 Jackie, what a mess.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: It is a mess, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And this new report and this video that was posted on social media out of China definitely drawing a lot of attention, indicating that some people under lockdown in that country are fed up with the government's harsh COVID restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's been over a month since cities like Shanghai, home to more than 25 million people, are under strict measures, as China continues with its zero COVID policy.\par \par \uc2 In this video, from the exterior of a technology factory owned by Taiwan's \uc2 Quanta Computer\uc2 , which makes devices for \uc2 Apple\uc2 , some workers are seen rushing through barriers, getting into scuffles with guards wearing protective gear over their comings and goings.\par \par \uc2 Now, the policy in China that's been implemented not only threatens its own economy, but also the global economy, because our economies are so intertwined. Now, one way that Shanghai has been dealing with this so far is to not completely shut down with respect to the factories.\par \par \uc2 They have their workers sleep over at the facilities. So, essentially, if you are infected with COVID, you're not taking the germs home, you're not spreading them further through the city and the community. But, as you can imagine, some people are restless. And this video shows that.\par \par \uc2 Now, all this matters because China's economy is so intertwined with ours. Experts warn that China's target growth rate, 5.5 percent, well, it could be in question because of reaction to the latest COVID outbreak. And there's also been increasing concern here at home about negative growth for a second quarter and the possibility of a recession.\par \par \uc2 Slowdown in growth in China wouldn't help our economy. It certainly wouldn't help the already strained supply chain. Now, the drastic measures in China feels possibly overblown. It's sort of is a delayed repeat, if you will, of what we saw here in 2020. And there is some fear that drastic measures like these would be very harmful on a larger scale if they continue, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jackie, thank you very much for that, Jackie DeAngelis.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, wants to take you to these latest developments in Alabama, where authorities are still on the hunt for an escaped inmate and a corrections officer, one officer that might have helped him.\par \par \uc2 The very latest right now from Steve Harrigan -- Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, they have gotten their first break in this case.\par \par \uc2 They have discovered the getaway vehicle, a \uc2 Ford\uc2  SUV, that the convict and the jail guard used to make their break from that Alabama jail just eight days ago. It was found in Central Tennessee, outside of Nashville.\par \par \uc2 But keep in mind this clue is one-week-old. The car was abandoned a week ago and only discovered in a toll lot last night. So it's a clue, but a pretty cold clue. And they're really back to square one.\par \par \uc2 As for the guard, Vicky White, her fellow employees seemed stunned at what the sheriff is calling now a jailhouse romance. And the romancer, Casey White, was serving a 75-year term for a string of violent incidents, including potentially facing the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff was asked what he would tell his former employee of the year if he had the chance to talk to her. Here's the sheriff.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICK SINGLETON, LAUDERDALE COUNTY, ALABAMA, SHERIFF: Hi.\par \par \uc2 I will be concerned for her safety until we know she's safe. This guy is -- he's volatile. I mean, he -- anything could send him off, and, at any time, he could -- he could just lose it and decide she's a hindrance to him and hurt, harm her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Casey white shot his former girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 As for Vicky White, she took $90,000 out of the bank just before this escape, and 12 days before, she sold her house for about half its value -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Steve Harrigan, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a look at the economy right now. The good news is that we're still generating a lot of jobs. The bad news is, because we are, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will keep hiking interest rates.\par \par \uc2 What is a White House economic adviser to do? We will ask Jared Bernstein - - after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the underlying economy, even with all this inflation, and maybe with that backdrop, is still doing quite well, thank you, the 12th straight month we have seen more than 400,000 jobs added to the economy.\par \par \uc2 But therein lies the problem, because the better things look for the economy, the more likely the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is going to have to keep raising rates to cool it down, because prices and inflation now kind of running out of control.\par \par \uc2 A good example is a wage component within that employment report that showed most of us have seen increases of about 5.5 percent year over year. Not bad. Unfortunately, what we have been paying out in the cost of goods has been running at about an 8.5 percent clip. So it's eating all those gains away.\par \par \uc2 To Jared Bernstein, the White House economic adviser.\par \par \uc2 Jared, that's the problem. How do you get those two to meet, the wage gains...\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... and then ease the inflationary pressures to make them worthwhile?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes, that's such an important question.\par \par \uc2 And before I offer you some thoughts about it, let me point out that there are a couple of sectors where the job market is so strong that wages are actually beating inflation, warehousing, transportation, in the leisure and hospitality sector, which added over 70,000 jobs last month. That includes restaurant workers. So there are workers who are meeting out wage gains.\par \par \uc2 But to make those wage gains more broad-based, we need to maintain a labor market that is as strong as this one, as you mentioned, 428,000 jobs last month, on average, over half-a-million jobs per month over the past few months, historically low unemployment.\par \par \uc2 So there is significant nominal wage growth. We have to do everything we can -- and I'm happy to talk about our efforts -- to ease inflationary pressures. Of course, the Fed, as you mentioned, is the first line of defense against that. And they are at it, as you know, as per this week's announcement.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think, Jared, that you and the president and the administration in general were just caught off guard, maybe flat-footed, you didn't appreciate the seriousness of this or what Americans were experiencing at the pump, going to the grocery store, and this early talk - - and you were not alone -- certainly, the Fed was of this view -- that it was transitory and would pass, and it hasn't, is the reason why people are so angry?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I would say far from it, in the following sense.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when we got here, nobody was vaccinated. Now well over 75 percent are for adults. When we got here, child poverty was going up. We intervened and helped it come down. People were facing real devastation, evictions, loss of income, an unemployment rate that was almost 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 So I think, when we're talking about the kinds of price pressures that came out of the -- of 2021, you have to look at...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wait a minute. Are you talking a national unemployment rate of 15 percent when you came in, in January of 2021?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, I'm saying that when -- unemployment had peaked at almost 15 percent. When we came in, it was, I think, around 6.5.\par \par \uc2 And so the interventions that contributed to the strong demand that persists to this day were extremely important. I think, in terms of...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you must have known, right, Jared? You must have known that your prescription at the time, if you were early on to this rise in prices, was going to be worsened by all this excessive government spending, right?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: We certainly -- we certainly knew that the fiscal interventions that we were doing was going to bring a lot of life back into the economy, and some of that was going to show up as some more heat of the...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So it was a tradeoff for you, right, the tradeoff. Get more people work, get the economy back, the tradeoff would be higher prices.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I wouldn't even put it...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But did you envision it would be this high?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No.\par \par \uc2 So, here's the thing that I have been trying to get -- so, if you look at the inflation rate at every advanced economy, it is at historic highs. They did not all do the Rescue Plan or the CARES Act or any of the other fiscal plan. The \uc2 European Union\uc2  is clocking in at 7.5 percent for their most recent March-to-March inflation rate. That's the most elevated they have been.\par \par \uc2 So the question is, what did all of these countries, including ours, have in common? It was the pandemic. And that is the triggering event here. We came in and fought off the pandemic far more effectively than the prior administration and made sure that people were getting the help that they need.\par \par \uc2 Now...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, well, Jared, Jared, wait. I mean, wait a minute. I'm not trying to cast political aspersions either way, but the prior administration did come up with the tools that would get this under control, and the drugs that would be able to get the numbers that you're talking about.\par \par \uc2 So, I understand what you're saying about how the environment improved from that period on, but it's not as if they left you empty-handed, right?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No.\par \par \uc2 And, certainly, I think the problem there was the administration of and distribution of the vaccine and bringing science to the issue, distributing it, getting the shots in arms and the checks in pockets.\par \par \uc2 And so...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But all of that, all of that came about under the prior administration.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, in every...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you had that hand. And then -- then things start improving. I get that.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do you think that now, in this environment, the recovery took hold, the jobs took off, but you're at a loss to do anything else to address it?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, I think...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Like, what can you do, besides hope the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  gets it under control?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: That's a great question.\par \par \uc2 And we are far from at a loss. We have one of the most fulsome and aggressive set of agendas and actions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Like what? One big thing you want to do.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: OK. Well, these are things we are doing.\par \par \uc2 So, we are in the ports, trying to make sure that the containers that come off the ships don't stay there too long. And, in fact, ship to shelf is accelerating at a strong rate. And we have shelf stock now just about at the level they were pre-pandemic.\par \par \uc2 We have many more...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, how is that going to help someone have cheaper pork chops or bacon or beef or cereal prices?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Oh...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How's that going to help them?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Because the transportation costs and the snarls in the supply chain very much show up as inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 So, our work in the ports will help ease some of those pressures as they start to unsnarl. We actually saw some of that in the first quarter. We're seeing some of those constraints come down.\par \par \uc2 We did the same thing with trucking. We have certainly tried to do the same thing in energy markets with the release of the Strategic Reserve.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you think it's working.\par \par \uc2 And do you think we have topped out? If you had to make a gut call, Jared, do you think we have topped out now?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: You know, I don't want to call a peak until we're clearly at the other side of that peak. Certainly, there are people in the market who are saying that.\par \par \uc2 I will say that the expectation of every forecast I have seen is that inflation is growing more slowly at the end of this year than it is now by...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it's still growing.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: ... by at least -- oh, yes. Well, look, nobody's talking about zero inflation.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think what every forecast is showing is a slower-growing inflation as we get out towards the end of this year. And why is that? One of the reason is that we have been -- one -- the Federal -- Federal Reserve is, of course, the first and foremost reason for those forecasts with inflation coming down.\par \par \uc2 But it has to do with our work at the ports, implementing the infrastructure law.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I get that.\par \par \uc2 But what you're acknowledging here is that this is going to continue through the end of the year. The pace of these increases might slow, but people paying high prices now, they're still going to be higher year-end.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, look, inflation pretty much goes up every year.\par \par \uc2 The question, does it go up at around 2 percent...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Not like this.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: ... which is the -- which is the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's target, or considerably higher?\par \par \uc2 What I think...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think it can get down to that, to 2 percent?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Not this year.\par \par \uc2 What I think the American public...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When? You think mid-next year?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Wait a second. Wait a second.\par \par \uc2 What I think the American people need to see, and what I think they will see, is inflation growing more slowly at the end of this year, while we maintain one of the tightest, most welcoming labor markets in American history. And I think that's what matters most to people.\par \par \uc2 They're actually headed into this period from a real position of strength...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: ... with strong balance sheets, with one of the most welcoming labor markets we have seen, with significant nominal wage gains and wage gains that are actually beating inflation in a couple of sectors.\par \par \uc2 What we need to do is help ease some of those pressures that we have been talking about.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: And we believe that our actions, along with those of the Fed, will help to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, knock on whatever. And you want to see it in all sectors, right, in all job categories.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: All sectors, broad-based, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jared Bernstein, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Have a good weekend, sir. Very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: You too.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The White House economic adviser, Jared Bernstein.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, to the border right now, and actually planning some drills for that day Title 42 goes away.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy has more.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, a show of force today by both American and Mexican authorities.\par \par \uc2 We got a ride-along with Texas DPS. Hovering above all of it, wait until you see what happened while we were in the air.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: To the border right now and preparing for the big day Title 42 goes away, only a few weeks off.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy right now in Laredo, Texas, with more -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 After four hours of drills, authorities from Texas, as well as the Mexican side, have now left for the day. We had Texas DPS, the National Guard, as well as Mexican police and the Mexican National Guard, all on hand today.\par \par \uc2 And we got a ride-along in a helicopter with Texas DPS. If we can pull up this video from earlier, we were providing air support -- or FOX News was there as DPS provided air support for today's drills. And while we were up there, they got a call from Border Patrol, the ground unit, about a group of runners about two miles east of the drills.\par \par \uc2 We know one person is in custody. Border Patrol is tracking footprints to try to track down the others. About a mile south of the river, there's this. Take a look at this new video of a group of 1,500 to 3,000 Haitian migrants who started gathering here last week.\par \par \uc2 DPS has received intel they may be bused to Reynosa, which is across the border from McAllen, Texas. And there's another migrant group waiting there. The Democratic judge here in Webb County, which is where we are now, worries things are going to get worse if Title 42 is lifted, as planned, in just over two weeks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE TANO TIJERINA (D), WEBB COUNTY, TEXAS: And that's what disappoints me.\par \par \uc2 That is -- those are the sleepless nights, because those Haitians, that 1,500, tomorrow, it could be 3,000, 5,000, 10,000, as we have seen in other places.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Meanwhile, today, in Eagle Pass, a group of four migrants needed to be rescued. You can see they used debris to stay above the water until Mexican authorities picked them up and brought them back to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday here in Laredo, Texas DPS tells me they had two separate groups of migrants, one group of 11 runners, another group of four runners that they detained. Five people ran back to Mexico as a result of the chase. Another busy day here today. We will send it back to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Nate Foy, thank you very much, dramatic reporting there.\par \par \uc2 By the way, tomorrow, I will have a special guest on my "Cavuto Live" show at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time, the Hidalgo County, Texas, judge, a Democrat, by the way, who's urging the president to come to the region to see for himself how bad this is and how bad he fears it could get, 10:00 a.m. tomorrow. Don't forget.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, money might not be able to buy you love, but if you don't spend a penny on mom this weekend, it might not necessarily buy you hugs or even an open door when you come to visit.\par \par \uc2 In fact, when it comes to Mother's Day, the big focus is on the big money spent. On average, on average, we spend about 220 bucks on mom for Mother's Day. That's an average. Dad, last year, about 174 bucks. So I don't know what that is saying, outside of maybe some kids are saying, I just like mom more?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Anyway, I don't know if that's the case.\par \par \uc2 Abby Hornacek is with us, FOX Nation host, Connell McShane, FOX Business correspondent extraordinaire.\par \par \uc2 Abby, when you look at this and how much we devote to Mother's Day, another poll showed that most moms just want their kids there to be with them. Money doesn't matter. A gift doesn't matter. Do you believe that?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ABBY HORNACEK, "PARK'D" HOST: You know what, Neil? I do, because my family has always emphasized the importance of homemade gifts.\par \par \uc2 Anyone can go on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , click a few buttons.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, for God's sake. Homemade.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Oh, please.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I'm trying to gain points here, Neil. Just let me, all right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Got it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: No, but, seriously, I mean, I -- how many people can say their 28-year-old daughter goes to Michaels and get some construction paper and writes mom in bubble letters with a nice message inside? That's all I'm saying.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I mean, when you look at this poll, and it says that 33 percent of Americans could take on debt just to get their mom a gift, mom just wants you to be happy. Maybe call her, show up for her, if you can spend time with her in person. I think that's great. But that's coming from a daughter, not a mother.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that's very nice.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I don't know if it applies to dads as well.\par \par \uc2 But, Connell, you read that statistic as well...\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... that dads don't get as much.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: No, no surprise there. That's the way it works out for the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 I'm already regretting this appearance. And it's just getting started, Neil.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Because I realize that, the next 90 seconds, I could say something that could either anger my wife, my mom, or, God forbid, my mother-in-law. So, I'm just hoping to get through it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I called them all. I called them all and said Connell would be on.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Now, here's my interpretation of the data, because I did read it.\par \par \uc2 The survey shows the number one gift a mom wants is a card, 36 percent, number two gift, quality time, 32 percent. And number three gift is plants or flowers, 29 percent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: What I say is that a lot of people could misinterpret that data. They could read it wrong and say, oh, cool, I will just get a card. That's the number one gift.\par \par \uc2 Wrong. The right answer is, you get all three.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: If you go all three...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ah.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: ... the top three gifts, you're golden. It's not even that expensive if you think about it. That's the way to go.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That makes sense.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Why do more moms want a card than quality time? They're like, no, kids, you don't need to come home. Is that what that is saying, 36 percent compared to 32 percent?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, women do like cards. I always find it to be redundant.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they do like the cards.\par \par \uc2 What is up with that, Abby?\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Yes, Abby.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Yes, well, it's because home...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I feel attacked. Can you guys settle down?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're to busy making your out of construction paper. I don't know.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Well, I think it takes more time, it takes more effort and thought.\par \par \uc2 I actually have a box of cards that people have made me.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I keep it under my bed. It sounds a little creepy now that I say it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: But it's true, because, sometimes, when I'm bored, I like to read them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that's very nice.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: And it's because someone actually took the time.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: That is nice.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: My mom always says the greatest gift you can give someone is your time. And I think she's right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think of that, Connell? You going to spend a lot of time with your wife and the extended family?\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: We are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Make sure all the women in your life feel special.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Trust me, we are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: A special meal was, I think -- that was the fourth answer. So we're doing that, because we're going out.\par \par \uc2 It's the -- yes, my wife will be there, my mom and my mother-in-law.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Of course.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Excellent.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: And I'm paying. So that is the plan for the weekend.\par \par \uc2 And I think...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: And, again, hopefully -- oh, look at the time, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Careful. Careful. Yes. Look at the time.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Guys, I want to -- hope it is a special day.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you're confused -- and, Abby, if you want to veer from the homemade thing, processed meats and cheeses, the gift that keeps giving.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Two-hundred and twenty-five dollars, apparently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, come on, $19.99. Just say you know me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Guys, have a great one. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220506ei560005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Economy Approval Low As Primary Season Starts; White House Briefs As Critics Blast "Disinformation" Board; Russian TV Discusses Nuclear Strikes On U.K.; DoD Briefs As Russia Ramps Up Nuclear Rhetoric; DoD Briefs As U.S. Pushes For New Iran Nuke Deal; Pentagon Briefs As Russia Targets Eastern Ukraine; US Aims To Reopen Embassy In Kyiv By End Of May; Ohio Senate Primary To Test Trump's Sway With Voters; Biden Administration Defends Disinformation Board; One Migrant Drowns, Another Rescued Crossing River\b0\par\par\uc2 Sandra Smith, Bill Hemmer, Martha MacCallum, Karl Rove, Lucas  Tomlinson, Griff Jenkins, Mark Meredith, Byron York \par \uc2 9,243 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All this for you. And Mr. Hemmer, thank you very much for helping us out today.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Great to be back with Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Good to have you. Thanks so much for joining us here on AMERICA REPORTS. I'm Sandra Smith. Be sure to set your DVR so you don't miss a minute of the show.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: I'm Bill Hemmer, now going back to 21st floor. Actually, you're going to do --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Kudlow later.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Kudlow, you got it. I'll see you tomorrow morning with Dana. Bye- bye.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Great to see both. We keep it rolling here. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum also in New York. Buckle your seatbelts because the elections get rolling tomorrow and the Ohio GOP Senate primary is going to be full of lessons and tea leaves for who holds the power these days in the Republican Party. But one thing we know already, at least looking at these polls is what Americans are fearful about, what they're concerned about.\par \par \uc2 What is nudging Independents and Democrats further right at this point. We know they're disappointed in the rising cost of nearly everything and an economy that they just don't have a lot of confidence in, take a look at the Dow -- right now down 409 points as we get into the final hour of trading. But these numbers that you're looking at on the screen right now. You are plus 14 and plus 19, in favor of Republicans when asked which party is better at handling the issues of the economy and inflation so that, that's a big indicator right there.\par \par \uc2 We're still several months away, but that's where Americans are, and that's a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 /ABC poll that those numbers come from. How about these numbers? 94 percent, pretty much the entire country says yes, I'm either concerned or I'm upset about inflation, and where it's going with that would bring in Karl Rove, former Deputy Chief of Staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and Fox News Contributor and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  Former Clinton Advisor and Pollster and Chairman of \uc2 Stagwell\uc2 , and the Harris Poll.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, great to have both of you with us today. I want to kick it off by just showing this moment from the \uc2 White House Correspondents\uc2  Dinner, you know, perhaps one of the most -- you know what, hold on, Peter Doocy has asked this question, while we're talking about that, we're going to dip over here and listen to that and then we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: -- why isn't he saying we know where those 42 are, they're in jail?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSEP PRESS SECRETARY: Well, Peter, I would really point to the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  to get more follow up details on his comments. I know he's spoken extensively at a range of hearings, and obviously on a range of Sunday shows yesterday, including on Fox.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, then, what is the President's priority? Would it be stopping potential terrorist attacks, or letting these migrants come in?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, again, I think as we noted, just a couple of weeks ago, when there was reporting about the number of individuals who were on the watchlist being stopped at the border, that that was the Border Patrol doing their jobs. That was the system working and certainly part of the role of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  is to keep our country safe, keep the American people safe, and I think that's an indication of Secretary Mayorkas' work to do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But so -- if it takes 19 people to carry out the September 11th attacks. What do you guys think 42 people on a terrorist watch list might be capable of?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Again, Peter, our -- the number one job of the president of the United States, the vice president, certainly the secretary of Homeland Security's to keep the American people safe. And I think a lot of this reporting you're referencing is an indication that they're, they're doing their jobs and doing exactly that.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And then, on another topic, does the president know that DHS is putting together this Disinformation Governance Board?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, Peter, I think I would note, and I'm not sure if this has been in your reporting yet. But this is a continuation of work that was done under the prior administration, under the Trump administration to take steps, to address disinformation address the use of disinformation, and helping smugglers prompt the movement of more migrants across to the border.\par \par \uc2 I would note that the first example given in the announcement about this was DHS has worked to understand how "misinformation spread by human smugglers that prey on vulnerable populations attempting to migrate to the United States." So, for anyone who's out there who may be concerned about the increase in migrants to the border, this is the kind of apparatus that's working to address disinformation. And again, continuing the work of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  in 2020, something we're currently applauding.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But just in terms of what the president wants out of this. Does he want the people on this board to start censoring information that is not helpful to him?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, let me be clear on exactly what this board does, or what the work they're doing does. And in their announcement, which is publicly available on the Department of Homeland Security Web site for anyone to read, it says, "The primary mission is to establish best practices to ensure that efforts to understand and respond to disinformation are done in ways that protect privacy, civil rights and civil liberties, and the right to free speech."\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But OK, there's this woman, Nina Jankowicz, who is going to be in charge of the board. She had said that she thinks the Hunter Biden laptop is Russian disinformation. So, should we look forward in the future to her censoring Internet traffic about the Hunter Biden laptop?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I think I noticed exactly what the objective of the board is including continuing the work of the prior administration and the woman you noted has extensive experience and has done extensive work addressing disinformation. She has testified before Congress, testified in Europe. She has done worked closely with the Ukrainians and has unique expertise, especially at this moment we're facing. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thanks, Jen. Following up on the question of the stockpile, and weapons, some experts tell us the Pentagon is --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So, we'll use that as our jumping off point as we bring back in Karl Rove, Former Deputy Chief of Staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , and Fox News Contributor; and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , former Clinton Adviser and Pollster and now Chairman of \uc2 Stagwell\uc2  and the Harris Poll. Gentlemen, thanks for standing by with us.\par \par \uc2 So, we, you know, we started the program with a look at inflation in the economy, and we showed how weak the president and his party are polling in both of those areas, then you have these questions from Peter Doocy, which also go to sort of the sense of security in the country.\par \par \uc2 Why are we allowing anybody on the terror watch list to be let anywhere into the country? And why are we allowing this disinformation board that some people think might stifle free speech? Karl, your thoughts on, on looking at all of that?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Let me start with the last one. Look, this is an important function of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 . But they have put the absolute wrong person in charge of it. This needs to be somebody who is above reproach, and this woman is a highly partisan individual who if she's in charge of disinformation and sorting the good from the bad, this is a woman who said that the Russians were behind the Hunter laptop, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  laptop, and that they were phony.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, wrong, wrong on all points. So, while I believe it's an important mission of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  helped guide the American guide Americans by saying this is something that's coming from Russia and or coming from a non-state actor that's attempting to unsettle the American population and bring about a decline in the trust in our institutions. They got to have somebody in charge that that we can believe. We can't believe her anymore.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mark, what do you think? Do they need to choose a different person to move this narrative along?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER CLINTON ADVISER AND POLLSTER: Yes, absolutely. And I think the entire testimony of Secretary Mayorkas has been more or less a disaster. I mean, it really, it harkens back to when they put out \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  on Afghanistan. Now, they've put out the Secretary on immigration. They have to pivot some of the policies here. They're going to have to meaningfully reduce the number of people going to and crossing the border in order to convince the American public they're dealing with the issue. And this disinformation board, it's just a gift to the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it certainly appears to be, and I think Peter's question about the number of people that it took to bring down those planes on 9/11. There's no reason why anybody -- I mean, if there's -- can think of a better reason to turn someone back at the border and say, sorry, you're not allowed to enter the country, it's having your name on the terror watchlist. Karl, I want to play this from the \uc2 White House Correspondents\uc2  Dinner. It was a joke about inflation in the United States. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH, "THE DAILY SHOW" HOST: Since people have been so hard on you, which I don't get, I really don't think ever since you've come into office, things are really looking up, you know, gases up, wrenches up food is up, everything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Big chuckle from the president there, Karl?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, you always put a president in an awkward position at these dinners. Because if he doesn't laugh, it's a story. If he laughs, it's a story. But this is no laughing matter, and the administration has got itself into difficulty by starting out this whole process by last year dismissing for far too long that inflation was transitory that was going to go away on its own. And even today, the answers that they've got have been, I think, completely inadequate.\par \par \uc2 Mark wrote a great piece in early April about how on energy and immigration and crime, education, foreign policy, the administration needed to do to make a pivot, not just in what they were saying, but what they were doing. And I think that's absolutely true, particularly when it comes to the issue of inflation. They got to recognize where it came from, and what needs to be done to stop it. And that means don't spend as much money.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, well, that's the thing. I mean, that the answer Mark is always, well, we want to pass some version of Build Back Better to put some money in people's pockets, we want to pay off your student loan. But if the gen -- if the genesis of inflation is the trillions of dollars that has been thrown into the economy, to cure COVID, it looks like independents have that message because 74 percent of them disapprove of, of the inflation situation in the U.S., Mark.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, absolutely. I mean, look, what we did in the 90s was we came out for a balanced budget, and everybody thought that was impossible. We put in a 10-year plan. It was done, I think in two. And a similarly here, the administration has to pivot its fiscal policy. It's seen as taxing and spending. Ultimately, if taxing and spending is seen as causing inflation, and that's the number one issue and you're losing that by 14-plus points, that's a disaster though. I will say that the congressional horse race is still 50/50 almost in many of these polls so the Republicans haven't closed the sale but they're leading on the issues.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, Karl, you know, I hear that a lot. People saying, are Republicans being overly sanguine about their position right now? I'm thinking I'm watching \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and some video here walking up the steps to meet the Ukraine leader. There's -- so a lot of time in politics before this midterm happens.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes, the firmament is good for Republicans, but they better execute it, and that means good candidates with good campaigns, and messages in which they lay out a comprehensive, you know, personal agenda, candidate by candidate, of what it is that they want to do about these big issues of inflation and energy and, and crime and so forth. And you got to, got to -- you can you, you can beat something with nothing, but you can beat it really bad if you've got something good.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And also, I think, on the way out here, one of the biggest wildcards is what happens in Ukraine, what happens with Putin? How does that sort of shift the attention potentially, of the American people as we move forward, \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , and Karl Rove wonderful that both of you here today, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the nuclear rhetoric is intensifying out of Russia in a number of ways. And in one instance, a lawmaker on state T.V. talked about wiping out the United Kingdom with a single strike. He's the first man speaking in this clip. The other man and the woman are Russian TV hosts in this conversation. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEKSEY ZHURAVLTOV, MEMBER OF RUSSIA STATE DUMA (through translation): One Sarmat missile and that's it. The British Isles are no more.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But we are serious people.\par \par \uc2 ZHURAVLTOV: And I am saying that seriously. They're accusing us of state terrorism. The U.K. also has nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No one will survive in this war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No one will survive in a nuclear war, says that last commentator. Former U.K. Independence Party Leader, Nigel Farage, is standing by. He has some very strong thoughts on this matter. First to Correspondent Lucas Tomlinson, reporting live from London. Good afternoon. Good evening to you, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, Russian lawmaker went on to make the following boast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The question is, can they shut it down? Dismissal can't be intercepted. Their abilities are limited. They say they can shoot it down. We will see about that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Russian threats like this to European cities in the 1980s spurred \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  deploy intermediate range nuclear missiles to Europe. Of course, the Russians would not need to launch new intercontinental ballistic missile or any ICBM to target cities in Europe, Martha, they have shortened intermediate range nuclear missiles for that already. Last night, another Russian anchor, known as Putin's mouthpiece, said Russian forces could quote plunge Britain into the depths of the sea using a nuclear tip torpedo called, the Poseidon.\par \par \uc2 Just one problem, this missile has not yet been deployed or developed, according to U.S. intelligence. That did not stop a Russian anchor from claiming the torpedo could cause a radioactive tidal wave and flood the British Isles. Here in London MI-5, the domestic counterintelligence service says the threat of Russian saboteurs launching attacks has increased. These Russian claims likely your reaction to a speech made by the U.K. top diplomat last week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIZ TRUSS, BRITISH FOREIGN SECRETARY: We're doubling down. We will keep going further and faster to push Russia out of the whole of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: And then includes Crimea and the Russian-backed separatist regions in the Donbass, the British Foreign Secretary going farther than any U.S. official publicly to date. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, very strong words from \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 . Thank you very much, Lucas Tomlinson, in London this week. So, we bring in Nigel Farage, former U.K. Independence Party leader, former \uc2 European Parliament\uc2  member and now a host at GB News. Nigel, great to have you with us today. So, I know that you have said that these are dark days for Europe, and you believe that the allies poked the bear when it comes to Russia. So, it appears that the bear is responding at least from some corners in Russia with some pretty strong language about even taking up the U.K.\par \par \uc2 NIGEL FARAGE, FORMER U.K. INDEPENDENCE PARTY LEADER: Yes, you know, I have been a critic for, you know, over a decade of endless east expansionism of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And I've always said, don't poke the Russian bear with a stick. We have made mistakes. But, you know, if I provoke you - - if, if I behave badly towards you, well, you can say some bad words back to me, or you can turn really violent on me. What I'm saying is this. None of our mistakes justify the actions that we're now seeing in Mariupol and other places like that.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, to see those appalling television shots from Russian state T.V. last night, I mean, never, never at the worst moments of the Cold War, even during the depths of the Cuban Missile Crisis. Never ever have we seen direct nuclear threats made by one side against another and it says to me, there is something now very, very wrong at the heart of the Putin regime, whether it's him, whether he's seriously ill He's obviously on steroids, there is a thyroid problem, whether his mind's been affected I don't know. But frankly, you know, we're now dealing with somebody who was totally out of control.\par \par \uc2 And yes, I know, Ukraine has its faults. That isn't the point. Russia is threatening to wipe the British Isles off the map. Can you even believe it? It's sick. It's ghastly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is ghastly. And in even during the Cuban Missile Crisis, I was reading a piece by Nikita Khrushchev's great granddaughter. She said, even during that moment, both sides didn't want to have a nuclear war.\par \par \uc2 She said, now, at this point, it appears that Russia does not want to back down -- at least, Putin doesn't want to back down.\par \par \uc2 FARAGE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He make a great point that perhaps we hope there are people around him who will think differently.\par \par \uc2 Last thought on that, Nigel, before we head to the Pentagon here.\par \par \uc2 FARAGE: Yes, I always thought that Putin was a rational player. An unpleasant man, but a rational player. I now think he's an irrational player. And it puts the world, perhaps in the scariest place it's ever been.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Dark days, indeed, as you have said. Nigel, thank you. I'm sorry, we're cut short here. It's good to have you with us, as always. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So, Pentagon Press Secretary, John Kirby has taken the podium and because of all of this talk of nuclear war, and what's going on, I want to listen in to this, and let's see what he has to say today. John Kirby.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: -- and to better defend their sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 And as you saw, the secretary and the deputy secretary met with the defense contracting CEOs just a couple of weeks ago, to talk about production lines. When we were in Ramstein, the secretary dedicated a whole session of that afternoon to talking about the defense industrial base, not just of the United States, but all those nations there, because so many other nations are contributing systems and weapons to Ukraine. And of course, you know, we'll continue to do that going forward.\par \par \uc2 So, let me take a couple from the phones here. Idrees?\par \par \uc2 IDREES ALI, NATIONAL SECURITY AND FOREIGN POLICY CORRESPONDENT, REUTERS: Hey, John.\par \par \uc2 Now, there were two more -- or two months more than two months into the war, could you talk about when the last time the secretary tried to reach out to his Russian counterpart? And I assume he hasn't been successful? What happens when he reaches out? Is it the Protocol Office reaches out to the Russian side? If they don't respond, or they pick up the phone, and they say, no, thank you. You sort of talk us through that.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Normally, you reach out through the policy channels. And you also use the defense attache, the defense attache at the embassy. No matter what the country is in question, that senior military officer is a representation of the secretary of defense inside all of our embassies. And so, they are usually the -- after working it through policy, in terms of the efficacy of doing a call. Usually, we rely heavily on the defense attache.\par \par \uc2 I don't have -- I don't have a date to give you, Idrees when the last attempt was. It's been quite some time.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, many weeks since we've -- since we've attempted another communication with the -- with Mr. -- Minister Shoigu. And there has not been much interest shown by the Russians in having that conversation.\par \par \uc2 Mike Brest, Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BREST, DEFENSE REPORTER, WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Thanks for taking my question.\par \par \uc2 With Russia's victory from today, does the Pentagon believe Russia will make a stronger push to get a definitive victory between now and then?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I can't speak for the Russian plans here, Mike. I think that is a question better put to the ministry of defense. And I think when you're asking them, I'd also urge you to find out when it is Mr. Putin is going to do the right thing and end the war. Because he can end the war right now, if he so chooses.\par \par \uc2 But as for what their plans are regarding Victory Day, I think and let you speak to them. What I can tell you is that the Ukrainians continue to fight back, to resist, to do so ably, and nimbly, quite effectively. We haven't seen the Russians make a whole lot of progress in the Donbas area, or quite frankly, in the south. And that's a result of the skill and the bravery.\par \par \uc2 And quite frankly, the kinds of capabilities that the United States and so many other countries are providing the Ukrainians in their -- in their self-defense. But as for what their plans are for later this month, I think that's something that they should speak to.\par \par \uc2 What we would urge them to plan to do is to meet with Mr. Zelenskyy, to pull their troops out of Ukraine and to end the war. And they can do that today.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Joe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On the defense secretary's meeting with his Japanese counterpart this week, what are the deliverables going to be from that meeting? And how much will parallels between Ukraine and Taiwan play into that conversation?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I'm not going get ahead of the deliverables for the meeting. I mean we'll have a good read out of it, and you guys will have access to the opening comment for that.\par \par \uc2 We're looking forward to this. This is one of the most important alliances we have around the world is with Japan. And very, very excited to have Minister Kishi come see us in person. He was -- he attended virtually, but he attended security consultantive -- the defense consultative group meeting in Ramstein, on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, I fully expect that issues regarding Ukraine and how Japan, and how the United States are going to continue to support Ukraine will be on the agenda. I also fully expect that tensions with China will be on the agenda, as you might expect.\par \par \uc2 But the degree to which there is an interplay between the two, I think we'll just let these two ministers talk before we get out ahead of that. I would just offer, if I might, and you heard the secretary has said this publicly that we got to be careful drawing too many comparisons between Taiwan and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Completely two different scenarios. Nothing's changed about our continued adherence to the One-China policy. Nothing has changed about our continued dedication to continue to support Taiwan self-defense needs through the Taiwan Relations Act.\par \par \uc2 It's a different situation. And I think we all need to be careful before drawing too many parallels.\par \par \uc2 Jen, already got you. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just a question regarding Iran in the last few weeks. Iran has said that they don't have any plans to abandon their plot to assassinate and rate retaliation for the Qasem Soleimani strike. \uc2 Mossad\uc2  also saying and that's according to the Prime Minister's Office in Israel, that a plot to assassinate a U.S. general in Germany was foiled.\par \par \uc2 And also, in the last week, China and Iran furthered their military cooperation. So, how does this Iranian provocative activity change our force protection posture when it comes to overseas deployments?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We always look at our force protection overseas, particularly in the Middle East. And we change it routinely based on whatever the existing security threat is.\par \par \uc2 I would just tell you a couple of things without getting into the specific anecdotes you cited there, and I'm not going to talk about intelligence. But nobody here at the department is oblivious to the fact that Iran continues to be a malign actor in the region.\par \par \uc2 They continue to support terrorist groups; they continue to develop a ballistic missile program. They obviously, even as they -- even as they sit in negotiations, continue to develop certain nuclear capabilities. And they are harassing shipping and clearly pose a threat in the maritime domain.\par \par \uc2 You pick it. There is an awful lot there that Iran is doing in a malign way in the Middle East region.\par \par \uc2 And that is why even as this department continues to believe that an Iran without a nuke -- and no problem in the Middle East is easier to solve with Iran -- with Iran having a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 So, we continue to support the work of our diplomats as they try to get a new agreement here on their nuclear development. But even as -- even with all that support, we still have a fundamental obligation to protect our security interests in the Middle East, and those of our allies and partners there. And that's why we still have a robust presence on the ground and at sea in the Middle East. And we're constantly reviewing that as well, whether we have that right, based on the threat.\par \par \uc2 So, look, force protection, and the security of our footprint remains a paramount concern for the secretary. And of course, he has a lot of experience in that part of the world. So, he watches this very, very closely. But we don't talk about, nor should we talk about on any given day, how it changes, because quite frankly, of Iran's multiple destabilizing activities, that, too, the threat, too, changes every day.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (on camera): John, just a follow-up. The \uc2 Mossad\uc2  said that it foiled an attack by Iran on a U.S. general in Germany. Who was the U.S. general, and when did this take place?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I'm not going to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Yes?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you. I want to ask you about North Korea. In March, the INDOPACOM enhanced their readiness level among their missile defense forces in the region and increased their intelligence collection activity near North Korea.\par \par \uc2 Could you give us an update on that? Does that enhanced readiness status remain the same?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I will just, without getting into the specific intelligence issues, we obviously -- you saw -- you saw us talk about increased ISR capabilities that we were -- that we were going to be applying in the wake of these now multiple recent tests by the North Korean. And we're -- North Koreans, and we're still doing that, and we're constantly looking for ways to get smarter and to get better information, as well as to make sure we're sharing that with the South Koreans.\par \par \uc2 OK. You in the back there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, thanks, John.\par \par \uc2 I'd like to return to Ukraine for a moment. A couple of days ago, I covered the moment when Congress just approved the Lend-Lease Act for Ukraine. And they talked about the political importance of this.\par \par \uc2 But I'd like to ask you about the military perspective. Do you have any plan or any strategy? Which equipment, which arms might be provided to Ukraine using the Lend-Lease? What will be the difference between, you know, foreign military sales or military aid? And does it mean that, for example, like it was after -- during the Second World War, that arms will go separately, and something like lorries, like Humvees, will go through Lend-Lease?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes. So, it's pending legislation. The president hasn't signed it yet. So, I'm not going to get ahead of the president here. When -- if and when he signs that, then, it becomes law, and then we'll execute it. And then, I'm sure we'll be able to talk about it in more detail.\par \par \uc2 What I can talk about is what we're doing right now. And what we're doing right now is continuing to send over the weapons and material of the last two presidential drawdown authorities, what we're calling seven and eight, which was very, very focused on artillery, particularly and some radar capabilities, as well as some unmanned capabilities.\par \par \uc2 The kinds of things that we know that they need because they've told us they need in the Donbas and in the south. And so, that's what we're focused on right now. And I just don't want to get ahead of legislation that hasn't been signed into law by the president.\par \par \uc2 Yes, (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I just kind of wanted to follow up on --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, we'll keep one here to this couple of questions about May 9, which is a significant date in Russian history, Victory Day after World War Two. And questions about whether or not that would be a significant date during which Putin would want to declare victory. He has a long way off from that on today, which is May the second.\par \par \uc2 Also, some questions about the targeting of a U.S. general, which is just starting to percolate. Two questions about that. He really didn't want to answer questions about that, as they wait for more information. And the one question is that something was thwarted, but we're going to work on that. We'll get you more information as we can, but you saw some pushback there from John Kirby at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Griff Jenkins is standing by. He is our reporter on the ground in Kyiv. And he joins us with the very latest from there. Good afternoon. Good evening to you, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good afternoon, Martha.\par \par \uc2 And what we're following are the evacuations that began out of the besieged city of Mariupol. And Martha, it's unclear at this moment, whether they're continuing or they have been stalled, amid reports of renewed Russian shelling in that city.\par \par \uc2 Now, we also have no official updates from the United Nations or the Red Cross who are conducting that humanitarian corridor where people were getting out.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice-over): It comes after President Zelenskyy yesterday announcing that the first 100 civilians, mostly women and children. Were able to get out of that Azovstal steel playing where they were trapped for days and weeks without food, water, or medicine. And there's still some 2,000 soldiers and 1,000 civilians believed to be stuck in there.\par \par \uc2 Now, this as \uc2 Ukraine's Defense Ministry\uc2  says that this video here shows Ukrainian drone attack destroying Russian boats in the Black Sea, a tactical perhaps victory of sorts for the Ukrainian forces.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Speaker Pelosi making a surprise visit over the weekend meeting with President Zelenskyy even walking out in the open with him, along with a delegation of Democratic lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 Later, she met with Poland's president and had this to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): We are giving support in a very strong way. And the Lend-Lease will not just be for Ukraine, but also for Eastern European countries who are assisting.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And today the U.S. diplomatic mission continues to move back into Ukraine, with hopes of reopening the Kyiv embassy by the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA KVIEN, UNITED STATES CHARGE D'AFFAIRS TO UKRAINE: I would say the message to Russia is you failed. Ukraine is still standing. The government is still functioning and we're going back to Lviv first, and then, Kyiv to help the government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: But the war continues. Day 68, Martha, as we mentioned, we're monitoring reports of shelling in Mariupol. But also, we have confirmed reports of missile strikes in the strategic southern port city of Odesa.\par \par \uc2 There is -- the official saying that there are dead and wounded, but no additional information on how many or what the situation is. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Port city obviously very important to Putin's goal to clear out the entire north part of the Black Sea in order to dominate it. Griff, thank you very much. Griff Jenkins reporting live from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 So, former President Trump's primary power about to be put to the test tomorrow in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: We had no inflation and we would have kept it that way. Because inflation was caused and started now it's a raging tiger, but it was caused and started by energy prices, you look at it. But now it's a raging tiger. Now you're looking at the food, you're looking at everything. Food is worse than energy. What has gone up anybody buy some bacon lately? You said no, thank you, darling.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CRYSTAL: Mercedes Schlapp, Byron York and David Rubin as the White House defends it's so called disinformation ministry when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this is the big question, it's going to be the big question all the way through the midterms, how much influence does former President Trump hold in the Republican Party and with his endorsements for particular candidates? Tomorrow's Senate primary in Ohio really kicks off this whole season, and it could answer that question at least for this Ohio candidate. Author JD Vance was behind in the polls, but shot to the top of the polls, I think he's at 23% right now, after the former president gave him his endorsement last month.\par \par \uc2 But the race does remain fairly tight Mercedes Schlapp and Byron York are standing by but first we go to Mark Meredith who lays it out for us from Mason, Ohio today. Hi, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good to see you. Later today, JD Vance, as you mentioned, the front runner in this race will be right here in Mason for one of his final campaign stops ahead of tomorrow's primary. And Vance, as you've talked about, skyrocketed to the front of the polls after winning the endorsement of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 The endorsement surprised so many people in this race because it wasn't that long ago that Vance was an outspoken critic, both of Trump as well as the MAGA Movement. We asked Vance about it over the weekend. He said he's changed his mind and he's letting voters know that it's OK to change their minds. And we've heard from some skeptical voters, they're still waiting to see where Vance is going to land.\par \par \uc2 But what's also fascinating, it was just last night that the former President was speaking in Nebraska and he appeared to mix up Vance's name with another candidate, former Ohio State treasurer Josh Mandel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know, we've endorsed Dr. Oz, we've endorsed JD, right, JD Mandel and he's doing great. They're all doing good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Certainly fascinating. There are seven Republicans running to replace Senator Rob Portman. The resumes and backgrounds all vary quite a bit, but we are watching reports that current State Senator \uc2 Matt Dolan\uc2  is seeing a late surge in supports. Dolan has been campaigning on Trump era policies but there's a big difference. He's also distancing himself from Trump directly, specifically when it comes to the claims of voter fraud. Dolan telling Fox he's glad that voters getting a second look, especially right before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATT DOLAN\uc2  (R), OHIO US SENATE CANDIDATE: I think we're getting some people leaving other candidates because they want somebody who's singularly focused and going to Washington to fight the Biden administration, fight for Ohioans and make America strong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Our recent Fox poll found a quarter of primary voters were still undecided as of a week ago, so it could still be anybody's race. Already some 51,000 Republicans have cast their ballots but we don't know what tomorrow's turnout is going to be like.\par \par \uc2 This is a primary election, Martha. It's different than a general election. The events we've seen. They're fairly small. We're not talking about thousands of people. So it really could still be anybody's race, we'll just have to find out tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And interesting that, you know, 51,000 GOP voters have already voted in this race. You wonder if they did that before or after the endorsement from the former president. Thank you, Mark. Good to see you in Ohio, where we'll be spending a lot of time over the next several months.\par \par \uc2 We bring in Mercedes Schlapp, former Trump 2020 campaign senior advisor and ACU Foundation senior fellow and Byron York, chief political correspondent for the Washington Examiner and a Fox News contributor. Great to have both of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 So, Byron, let me start with you. You've been covering this race and primaries for many years. How does this look to you and what to expect to learn from it?\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think the race in Ohio shows the influence of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and not just in the JD Vance candidacy. You did have this extraordinary jump, in the change in the polls when Trump endorsed Vance, you know, all through February and March. Vance was kind of rolling along in third place behind Josh Mandel and Mike Gibbons. And then, Trump endorses him and boom, He is in first place. So that is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 But all of the candidates in this race, including \uc2 Matt Dolan\uc2  who you just heard about, who does not buy into Trump's claim that he actually won the 2020 election. He's still pretty Trumpy and all of his other policies, and all of the candidates basically support Trump like platforms. So I think it shows that the president's influence has really stretched far beyond one candidate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, we watch Vance move up that outside lane in the first place, but it is very close. I think he's at 23. I think the next -- Mandel is I think around 19, Mercedes. You've also been watching Republican politics for a very long time. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES SCHLAPP, FORMER TRUMP 2020 CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: Well, I got to tell you, I mean, the mere fact that you had not only President Trump there with JD Vance, but you also have \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . Very popular, popular obviously Republican. Someone who -- when he goes campaigning, he has a great following. And so that is a big dynamic.\par \par \uc2 And it's interesting to watch how they really had to spend some time giving cover to JD because JD was critical of the president, where Josh Mandel then has the Ted Cruz endorsement. So it almost becomes this kind of battle between these two conservatives. The reality is, is there's one common factor. They don't want a \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  endorsement. That is really bad in states like Ohio, because the problem is, is that if Republicans are going to win in these states in a primary they got to make sure that Trump's behind them and not a rhino like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, what do you say to that, Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, that's good evidence of the President's war with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  now. And what \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  wants is 51 at least senators, Republican senators. He believes he can get them all to vote for him for majority leader.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, this is just one race. President Trump's endorsement of Dr. Oz in Pennsylvania got a lot of kind of startled looks from some Republicans, not sure how that's going to actually work out. The president kind of messed up in Alabama, so it's a longer season than just Ohio.\par \par \uc2 But in poll, most of the candidates are really Trumpy. Their platforms are very much based on President Trump's platforms. So I think that if they do do well in these races, you will see a play out what Mercedes was just referring to, which is this battle going on between President Trump and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, fascinating. And in the Pennsylvania race, you have people who worked very closely with President Trump who are supporting McCormick and you have President Trump backing out Oz. So, you know, these are fascinating and I guarantee you, most people have no idea who's endorsing anybody except they probably know the person that Trump is endorsing.\par \par \uc2 Other than that, they probably don't -- can't even name them. Thank you very much to both. Mercedes, thank you. Byron, thank you. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Getting interesting, folks. So the next -- the party, I should say rather, that perpetuated a Trump Russia collusion hoax and dismissed the Hunter Biden laptop story right before the election, now wants to police the truth and make sure that you don't get any disinformation. David Rubin on disinformation lists at the disinformation bureau, sounds like something out of Harry Potter. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: OK. there's this woman, Nina Jankowicz, who is going to be in charge of the board. She has said that she thinks the Hunter Biden laptop is Russian disinformation. So, should we look forward in the future to her censoring internet traffic about Hunter Biden laptop?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Some moments ago, Fox pressing the White House on the new "Disinformation Governance Board" at the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The primary mission is to establish best practices to ensure that efforts to understand and respond to disinformation are done in ways that protect privacy, civil rights and civil liberties, and the right to free speech.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Dave Rubin standing by but first the White House correspondent Peter Doocy just out of that briefing room where he asked that question. Hi, Peter.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Martha, we've been able to move this story forward a little bit more now. The White House is insisting that this DHS-based board is not something that is going to be censoring internet traffic, and now we have a better idea about what to expect instead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There's this woman, Nina Jankowicz,, who is going to be in charge of the board. She has said that she thinks the Hunter Biden laptop is Russian disinformation. So should we look forward to the future to her censoring internet traffic about the Hunter Biden laptop?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The woman you noted has extensive experience and has done extensive work addressing disinformation. She has testified before Congress, testified in Europe. She has done worked closely with the Ukrainians and has unique expertise, especially at this moment we're facing --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And while defending this disinformation board at DHS, White House officials are also very careful to say that this wasn't even their idea. They say that this concept came together at DHS during the last administration, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Peter, thank you very much. Peter Doocy at the White House. With that we bring in Dave Rubin, host of the "Rubin Report." And, you know, just to give people a little sample of Nina Jankowicz. This is something that she posted about disinformation and to sort of a jazzy tune from "Mary Poppins." Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NINA JANKOWICZ, DIRECTOR, DISINFORMATION GOVERNANCE BOARD: Information laundering is really quite ferocious. It's when a huckster takes some lies and makes them sound precocious. By saying them in Congress or a mainstream outlet, so disinformation's origins are slightly less atrocious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So she's talking about seeing them in public or even coming out of the mouths of people in Congress. Atrocious lies, Dave. So, you know, there's been a lot said about this. They're trying to focus it now on, oh, it's just to make sure that people don't want to cross the border and get fed disinformation and that that's a lure for them. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 DAVE RUBIN, HOST, THE RUBIN REPORT: Well, Martha, first off, it's not even 4:00 here on the East Coast, and if I knew you were going to show that clip I would have been drinking already. Look, these people, these are the architects of misinformation and disinformation. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  is the biggest paid liar in America. She cannot say anything true. If you ask \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  what her favorite color was she would say seven.\par \par \uc2 These people, and now this Nina woman, I mean, when you talk about disinformation, remember \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , he was the president for eight years a while ago. Remember if you like your health plan, you can keep it, should he be booted off \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Remember \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , if you get the vaccine, you will not get nor transmit COVID on top of the Hunter Biden laptop that they censored, on top of Trump very fine people in Russia The vaccine you will not get nor transmit COVID on top of the Hunter Biden laptop that they censored, on top of Trump very fine people and Russia collusion, and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  as a serial rapist, and Kyle Rittenhouse is a racist.\par \par \uc2 And the rest of the things that they lie about related to every big story. I mean, the machine, the mainstream corporate press lies about literally everything. And now what they're calling disinformation is just other decent people, regular citizens who can somehow see behind the curtain and see that it is not the all powerful Oz back there, but basically \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  and a really, I would say, negligent administration when it comes to defending the First Amendment trying to silence the people who voted it in.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the Wall Street Journal wrote about this today in an editorial piece. And it says Mr. Mayorkas' intentions may be nonpartisan, but refereeing political debate isn't the government's job leave that to the free exchange of ideas in the public square. The Disinformation Governance Board will promote more mistrust than it prevents.\par \par \uc2 And I think this makes an excellent point because the point is, and this is what \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is trying to return to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and we'll see if he can succeed at it, that you don't want the government to be in charge of telling people what information is real and what isn't. It smacks of things that we've seen in totalitarian governments and communist governments for decades and decades.\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: Martha, wasn't there some old document, I think it's a dusty piece of paper. We called it the Constitution. And then it had this extra set of rights, the Bill of Rights, if I'm not mistaken, that enshrined our ability to speak freely. And guess what you're allowed to lie or share misinformation? I would prefer people not to do that. I know that you try not to do that on your show. I try not to do it on my show.\par \par \uc2 But the idea that the government is going to decide what is true or not, we are actually in 1984. This is the Ministry of Truth. They're not going to police speech that they don't like if it's a lie that will further their goals. They'll be all for that lie. The lie that they will not like will be, oh say big government is bad, or the Democratic Party is not so great, or things of that nature. If it's designed to harm them, that is when suddenly they will have a problem with it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Dave Rubin, thanks for sharing your opinion with us today. And he has a new book out, "Don't Burn This Country." And we had a did a podcast earlier today that you'll hear on Thursday. So thanks, Dave. Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So as we mentioned at the top of the hour, Republicans edged out Democrats when it comes to the party that voters trust to control the border when we look at the some of these new poll numbers that are coming out. We're live there at the border right after this. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the other very hot story and topic at the White House moments ago is what happens when they lift Title 42 of the COVID restriction three weeks from today at the southern border, especially after portion of the Border Patrol has stopped immigrants on the terror watch list and knows where they are. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: There was reporting about the number of individuals who are on the watch list being stopped at the border, that that was the Border Patrol doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Homeland Security secretary says he has a plan to deal with a possible surge after Title 42 ends, and he's telling migrants do not try to get in to this country. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: Because our border is not open. What happens now is individuals are either expelled under the Title 42 authority or they are placed in immigration enforcement proceedings, and they are removed if they do not have a valid claim under our law to remain. And so, the border is not open.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Border does not open says the DHS Secretary. National correspondent Bryan Llenas live from the border in Eagle Pass Texas. Hi, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, the video we're about to show you is disturbing. It shows two migrants attempting to cross the Rio Grande behind us. You can see one man on the right drowning, literally, as his other friend there is holding on to dear life on a pillar there. Eventually he goes under and does not come back up.\par \par \uc2 This is all from our Fox News drone as well which happened to be flying at the time, capturing that surviving migrant clinging on to the pillar and he eventually floats away to a nearby bridge. The visibly exhausted and shaken man attempted to use his cell phone, and at the moment began -- that's when I started talking to the man.\par \par \uc2 I told him that help was on the way to stay tight. He said his name was LB, 42 years old and his friend Nelson had just drowned. He was 38 and they were both from Nicaragua. And eventually LB was rescued but reluctantly because he did not want to go back to Mexico. His friend's body, Nelson, floated down river with LB. Mexican authorities came over and bagged the body.\par \par \uc2 The drowning took place in front of Mexican authorities and at least to US National Guardsmen that were standing right there and they had rescue kayak boats on hand, a nearby Humvee also had floatation devices, but a National Guardsmen told me that they've been ordered not to perform any more water rescues in the Rio Grande River following the tragic death of 22-year-old National Guard specialist Bishop Evans who drowned while heroically trying to save two migrants.\par \par \uc2 And this is what we're seeing every day, Martha. The reality is, is that people are risking it all to come here in unprecedented numbers and also putting others at risk including National Guard. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very dangerous, very dangerous. Thank you very much, Brian Llenas, good to have you with us today. And good to have you with us today for THE STORY that Monday, May now, May 2, 2022. And THE STORY goes on. So we'll look forward to seeing you back here tomorrow. Hope you have a great afternoon and evening. "YOUR WORLD" starts right after this.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220503ei5200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 $30 Minimum Wage?; Pilot Shortage; Midterm Election Outlook?; U.S. Secretary of State and Defense Secretary Meets With Zelenskyy in Ukraine; Border Surge; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Buys \uc2 Twitter\b0\par\par\uc2 Brian Brenberg, Nate Foy, Chad Pergram, Griff Jenkins, David  Spunt, Bill Melugin, Gary Kaltbaum, Charlie Gasparino, Kelly O'Grady,  Sandra Smith \par \uc2 8,320 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Tweet this: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  buying \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , taking the social media company private and taking the free speech debate in America to a new level.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 We have got the market and political fallout from this deal coming right up.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's get right to Kelly O'Grady. She's live in our Los Angeles newsroom. She's got the latest on this for us.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Kelly. Big deal.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Oh, it really is, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Yes, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  now owns \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I mean, he said $54.20 was his best and final offer and he stuck to it. The dramatic news comes after a weekend of swift developments, including shareholders pressuring the board to take that deal.\par \par \uc2 Now, stockholders will receive $54.20 in cash for each share, valuing the deal at $44 billion. The purchase price represents a 38 percent premium to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's closing price the day before Musk disclosed his roughly 9 percent stake. That represents just a single-digit premium, though, to where the stock is now with how much Elon has involvement boosted the price.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the announcement, Musk emphasize his reasons for the purchase, saying -- quote -- "Free speech is the bedrock of a functioning democracy and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is the digital town square where matters vital to the future of humanity are debated. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has tremendous potential. I look forward to working with the company and the community of users to unlock it."\par \par \uc2 Now, while many are excited about his planned changes, it's been his crusade for free speech on the platform that has been driving this buyout. He disapproves of the company's moderation policies, policies that the White House says doesn't go far enough. Here's \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: No matter who owns or runs \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , the president has long been concerned about the power of large social media platforms, what they have -- the power they have over our everyday lives, has long argued that tech platforms must be held accountable for the harms they cause.\par \par \uc2 He has been a strong supporter of fundamental reforms to achieve that goal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Now, one of the most prominent deactivated accounts is former President Trump. He confirmed just moments ago that he will not return to the platform, instead choosing to remain solely on his TRUTH Social.\par \par \uc2 But other accounts, like The Babylon Bee, could be making a comeback. Now, Sandra, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  now owns \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but he has to deliver on the execution and monetization. That could be tough, given the opposition he will face inside from employees and maybe even the government, but big news today.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: A lot of critical employees, many of them, though, not giving their names when they make such comments, threatening to leave. We will see if they follow through on this now that this is a done deal.\par \par \uc2 Lots of question marks. Thanks so much for the update.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. Thank you, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read now on all this from Charlie Gasparino.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, I'm sure you have been working this deal for quite some time. Now it is done. But there's a big task ahead and high expectations to be met. What do you believe he does now with this company?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Well, first off, I'm not surprised.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, I said that it's his if he wants it. The board is just doing a dance. Here's the problem that the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  board and their advisers at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  had. For the last nine years, because I covered it, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has been looked at by everybody and they passed. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  doesn't make any money. It has lousy cash flow. So it's bad for private equity.\par \par \uc2 The only person that could really buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  -- and they -- and, trust me, they need to be bought -- was a rich gazillionaire who's willing to take a chance. \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2  wouldn't do that. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  did it.\par \par \uc2 If they said no, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  would have been sued to kingdom come, as would \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , so they had to take this deal no matter what. There was not going to be another bitter. All that private equity stuff that's out there is -- was nonsense. Private equity may fund some of the debt, but none of the equity.\par \par \uc2 Now, here's what's good about this. Shareholders are finally getting paid, OK? That's a good thing, 20 percent premium from -- or something like that from where it traded before Elon started buying the stock.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Oh, it's bigger than that. I believe it's about 38 percent since April 1 before all this, yes, started. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right. Yes, OK. You might be right about that.\par \par \uc2 But what I'm saying is decent premium from where it was trading before.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Pay any attention to people saying, oh, well, it traded at $77 last year. Remember, last year, Jerome Powell was printing money. He's not printing money anymore.\par \par \uc2 Every tech stock, all these stocks are going to sort of normalize. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  might go back down to $30. And I'm telling you, that's what they thought about internally.\par \par \uc2 Now, here's the interesting thing. What does he do with it? One of the best things about this deal is that it's private. He can do it on his timetable. He doesn't have to worry about shareholders. It's not like \uc2 Disney\uc2  is buying it and dumping money in it. It's not like -- I mean, if you think about it, Discovery bought \uc2 Warner Media\uc2  and then all of a sudden was left with this basket case of CNN+.\par \par \uc2 It had no choice but the but to whack it out and to cut costs. He's not going to have that problem right here. He's going to basically do what he wants outside the public glare.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you this. If programmers at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  don't like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , they can leave, and he will get other programmers. I'm telling you, it's...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You say programmers, because, obviously, in question is whether or not \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  decides to expose the algorithm or algorithms inside of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , how things are actually done.\par \par \uc2 And, Charlie, you and I know and anyone who uses this platform that there is an immense amount of information that is transferred on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  globally.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Obviously, a lot of people say, OK, as far as social media companies, this pales in comparison to the billions of users that, say, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has, but, still, it has control of a lot of information.\par \par \uc2 So the question is, how does he handle that?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, I think it's different. I think \uc2 Twitter\uc2  -- what makes \uc2 Twitter\uc2  unique here, it's much more efficient than \uc2 Facebook\uc2  in terms of transforming information or making -- creating actionable information for the masses.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's what Elon sees in this product. And I think he's going to make that -- he's going to open that up to different voices. We're going to see if the -- I think he will look to see if the algorithms really are biased against conservatives. I think, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to get back on, he will get back on.\par \par \uc2 I think people like Alex Berenson will get back on.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: He says he doesn't. He's already -- he's spoken to FOX Digital. He says he doesn't, but who knows. We will see. He loved it before.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Who knows. But he could change.\par \par \uc2 But, by the way, Alex Berenson will get back on. And I think all these people that were sort of -- had heterodox views. Alex had heterodox views on COVID.\par \par \uc2 The Babylon Bee, which is just funny as hell and conservative, so they're going to get back on. And I think it'll be interesting. And he doesn't have to do it with public shareholders. And here's the thing. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants a whole public -- wants once a whole social media response.\par \par \uc2 I mean, who is he kidding? Of all the things he has to worry about, he's worried about discourse on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ? We have a crisis at the border. We have all this stuff going on. We have a war. And he's worried about what \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is doing on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . That would have been the biggest...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What do you think about all those who are saying they're going to leave, whether it's employees inside the company that have gone on the record being very critical of this happening -- they're getting a nice payout, by the way -- or those that use it who don't want to use it if \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is in control of the company?\par \par \uc2 But who knows? He might not be the one to day-to-day manage it. Real quick final thought, Charlie. And, by the way, it is a 38 percent premium to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's closing stock price April 1.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That is according to the press release today.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Don't let the door hit you in the rear end as you leave.\par \par \uc2 No, it won't matter that much.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Hopefully, you're not talking to me, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And I will say one other thing.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: He's not listening.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: If you watched FOX Business -- if you watched us on FOX Business, you knew this was going to happen. Trust me on this. He did -- he played the smart, long game. And this company had no choice but to take his big, because it's a basket case.\par \par \uc2 And that's the bottom line. So, all those opposed, by the way, are working for a basket case.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Many point out what he has done with \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , and why would you question somebody who has been this smart with his moves and dealmaking?\par \par \uc2 And here we have it, deal done. We will see what's next.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, great to have you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: All right.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And good to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right, by the way, Dow finishing up 238 points, after it was down significantly through the day.\par \par \uc2 Now to the political implications.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Westwood from The Washington Examiner joining us, and also market watcher Gary Kaltbaum about more on the fallout from this deal.\par \par \uc2 Gary, I will get to you in just a second.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, to you first. Obviously, there's high political implications here, as a lot of day-to-day political dealings and communication is done through \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Big questions whether or not -- \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has been so critical of the censoring of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and its current management.\par \par \uc2 Well, does he believe that there's any room for censorship on social media? What would that look like if he believes the answer is no?\par \par \uc2 SARAH WESTWOOD, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Well, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has been sort of vocal that what he thinks should dictate who can be heard on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 He thinks that content should be moderated from a legal perspective, and not necessarily from a political perspective, which is what \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has been doing. I mean, the way that they have censored content has gone far beyond just taking down tweets that they don't like, taking down tweets that support a conservative narrative.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned before the algorithm. They have sort of configured their site in a lot of ways to suppress conservative voices and conservative content and artificially amplify the reach of liberal content. So there's a lot of different ways that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  could come in and make \uc2 Twitter\uc2  a more sort of egalitarian place for discourse.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's why you're seeing a lot of progressives sort of apoplectic today, because this is a step in the right direction when it comes to free speech. Obviously, it won't solve all the problems when it comes to big tech and censorship, but it is definitely a step in the right direction and exposed a really big problem.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Gary, there was sort of this pause on Wall Street today, because \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's stock was actually halted just over -- with just over an hour left in the trading day. It was halted pending news.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, everybody said, it's happening. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , he's finalized the deal. And, sure enough, the company came out today and announced that this deal was done, and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  stock up 6 percent today, another huge gain in the market, by the way, turned around from a significant loss after the 1,000-point sell-off on Friday, and ended up 238 points.\par \par \uc2 Fair to say Wall Street's cheering on this deal, Gary?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Whenever you see a big buyout like this, Wall Street tends to get happy.\par \par \uc2 But, frankly, the happiest people today should be the board. Usually, when you use the word bailout, it's for companies that are bankrupt. But, to me, this is a bailout of a stagnant stock in a great market for these type of stocks and a $16.7 billion gift to shareholders since the day \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  took his position.\par \par \uc2 So, if I was the board, I'd be sending flowers and chocolates to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  for this. And it speaks volumes that not one deep-pocketed company, private equity or person, came to the fore to outbid him. So, good for the shareholders and hopefully he does the right thing.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And they will get paid out $54.20 in cash for each share of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . \uc2 Twitter\uc2  stockholders, many of them obviously are employees.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, real quick final thought. We have got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 WESTWOOD: Yes, I think the White House has in a lot of ways bullied companies like \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , like \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2  into pulling down content that Democrats don't like.\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  at the helm of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , it'll be a lot harder for Democrats to use their platforms to bully \uc2 Twitter\uc2  into content moderation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Not to mention some of the other changes he's suggested he wants to make, long-form tweets, a mute button, an edit button.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps those are lower on the list of concerns for a lot of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  users, but perhaps changes he will enact.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much to both of you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 All right, from the battle over \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to the battle at our border and what the White House plans to do about it. We are on it. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: President Biden huddling with members of the Hispanic Caucus at the White House, the president facing growing opposition from members of his own party over ending Title 42, as the surge of migrants continues to escalate along our Southern border.\par \par \uc2 All of this as the body of a Texas National Guardsman is recovered from the Rio Grande after he was reported missing while trying to rescue two migrants.\par \par \uc2 We have got team coverage for you with David Spunt on how the White House is dealing with this, and Bill Melugin in Eagle Pass, Texas, with the fallout from it. We begin with David.\par \par \uc2 To you now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 The president began meeting with the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2  about 45 minutes ago. We're told that meeting is still going on right now. It's possible some of those members will come out to the microphones to speak, but no question it's likely -- and we will wait to hear what happened with the meeting -- but that the president may be getting an earful from those members in his own party specifically about the way that this administration has handled this crisis on the border.\par \par \uc2 There are numerous issues on the border. And the president is expected to be talking about those issues with some of these people, especially the real inability to get a grip on the surge of illegal immigration that happens daily. Right now, Title 42, the policy that expels illegal migrants from the United States citing COVID concerns, will end May 23.\par \par \uc2 That means there's expected to be a surge, as Bill has been reporting, at the U.S. Southern border. Several Democrats have come out publicly to warn of the surge that will come as a result of the end of the program. Pro- immigration activists have urged the White House to end Title 42.\par \par \uc2 But here's Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  when pressed about this within the last hour. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I think it's important to remember that Title 42 is not an immigration policy or an immigration authority. It's a health authority. So, yes, it was coordinated with the secretary of homeland security and through an interagency process, and there's been planning in the works for months for this possibility, that the CDC -- to take this action.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Mayorkas is going to be testifying later this week multiple times. I'm sure he will answer that question and multiple other questions and I will let him speak for himself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And this is just such a tragedy. This is Bishop Evans, a 22-year- old Texas National Guardsmen who lost his life while going in reportedly to rescue two migrants who were thought to be drowning while illegally crossing the Rio Grande River.\par \par \uc2 That's where a delegation of Republicans are today. This is all not far from where Bill Melugin is, a sad story there. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  expressing condolences.\par \par \uc2 But as far as Title 42 is concerned, I had a chance actually on the South Lawn to shout a question to President Biden about Title 42. He looked at me, did not say anything. So it's anyone's guess if the president will extend this, but there are many Republicans and Democrats now who want him to do so -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: A growing number indeed.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt live at the White House for us.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have been reporting search crews recovering the body today of Texas National Guardsmen Bishop Evans, whose authorities say drowned while trying to rescue two migrants in the Rio Grande.\par \par \uc2 Our own Bill Melugin is in Eagle Pass, Texas, for us, where the discovery was made just earlier today.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Sandra, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 And that grim discovery made just about a couple hundred yards away from where we are right now. That specialist, Specialist Bishop Evans, now the first confirmed Texas National Guardsmen to die as part of Operation Lone Star, which was first launched last March.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this procession video we were given a short time ago by Texas DPS showing the somber scene, as that specialist's body was removed from the Rio Grande more than 72 hours after he first went into it and never resurfaced. You can see his body was draped with a flag.\par \par \uc2 We're told he had spent time in Kuwait and Iraq and assisted U.S. special forces over there, but just a horrible ending to what started off as just a really tragic story on Friday morning, as he just jumped in the water to save the lives of what he thought were two illegal immigrants struggling in the water.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to what Texas Congressman Tony Gonzales had to say earlier today. This is the district he represents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): Eagle Pass isn't -- isn't an easy place to get to, but it's important. It's the epicenter of everything. It's about those that live here. It's about those that serve here. It's about those that work here. It's about Bishop Evans, who was found today.\par \par \uc2 It's about his family. It's about all the families that have been impacted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And this video you're looking at right here is just down the river from where that body was discovered, more migrants trying to cross over this morning.\par \par \uc2 But, for the first time, we saw Mexican authorities getting physical with these migrants, physically putting their hands on them, trying to take them down to the ground in an effort to stop them from crossing over into Texas.\par \par \uc2 You will see one of the women just comes flying down the hill and runs down and jumps into the water, escaping past those authorities. Ultimately, the Mexican authorities were unsuccessful in stopping anybody. Once they were all in the water, it was a hands-off approach.\par \par \uc2 And all of those illegal immigrants ended up crossing over into Eagle Pass. And they were all later processed by Border Patrol. And you have seen earlier today House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy was here in Eagle Pass taking a tour. We were embedded with him.\par \par \uc2 Here's his view on the situation, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): This can all stop and you can secure this border.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you're looking at the number of people who are dying. You're looking at the number of people who are being trafficked. You're looking at about the amount of fentanyl that's coming across. So for any community, if you think you don't know what's happening on the border, look in your own community and see who has died from fentanyl, because this is where it came from.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And McCarthy went on to point out that he said President Biden has just flat out never come down to the border, never visited the border.\par \par \uc2 And he said, how is that possible as president of the United States with decades of political experience? How can you understand the border without actually coming here to the border?\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Bill, great reporting from there today, your interview with Kevin McCarthy and all. Thank you very much for your continued reporting from the border. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 As many as 20,000 migrants are reportedly holed up in Mexican border towns waiting for Title 42 to expire in less than a month.\par \par \uc2 It's got my next guest very concerned. Chris Olivarez is with the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, sir, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 What worries you the most when this is lifted?\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : Good afternoon, Sandra. It's so great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 So, first, I want to address -- on behalf of the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 , we want off our condolences to Specialist Bishop Evans...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: ... his family, the Texas Military Department.\par \par \uc2 It was -- it's a very somber day for us, for our first responders. And I just want to point out that Specialist Evans just a true example of heroism, his bravery, his courage to risk his life to go into that river to rescue immigrants that broke the law. They entered the country illegally.\par \par \uc2 And not knowing who they are, what their backgrounds are, he still risked his life to go into that river to rescue those two immigrants. So I just want to point out that is a true hero, a true patriot, in what's Specialist Bishop Evans did in trying to rescue those immigrants.\par \par \uc2 And that's just an example of what our Texas National Guardsmen, along with our first responders, our Border Patrol agents, do every single day, by being on the front lines on the border by risking their lives. And what worries me or worries law enforcement is the fact that we still continue seeing these events play out. And now it's hit home, where we actually lost one of our own because of this situation right now, because of the fact that we have illegal immigrants entering this river, this dangerous river.\par \par \uc2 And it puts our men and women in a situation now where we have to perform these rescue operations, again, continuing to deplete those viable resources now, where we should be on the front lines trying to apprehend criminals and interdict drugs. But now we're having to perform rescue operations along with our federal partners.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps it's encouraging to you to know that there is bipartisan concern about what is happening there and a lack of plan by this administration.\par \par \uc2 This was \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  speaking to me just yesterday. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You and other Texas Democrats have shared your concerns with this White House. Are they listening?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): You know, we told them, but they said they're going to continue with Title -- lifting the Title 42.\par \par \uc2 And -- but the thing is, yes, they're listening to the immigration activists. But my question is, who's listening to the men and women in green and in blue, and, more importantly, who's listening to the border communities, the sheriffs, the landholders, the rest of the people that live on the border?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Chris, perhaps with this bipartisan support, bipartisan concern, a growing list of Democrats speaking out about what is happening there and what could be about to happen there, perhaps that will push this White House to extend Title 42.\par \par \uc2 Your final thoughts?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, that's what we're hoping for. We're hoping that this continued pressure on the federal government will force them to take some type of action, have a comprehensive border strategy in place.\par \par \uc2 And what Congressman Cuellar just mentioned right now, what he referred to, he is right on point. It's not about the activists. It's about talking to those men and women who are on the front lines every single day, our DPS troopers, our National Guardsmen, our Border Patrol agents, and also those local communities that are having to deal with this current crisis right now.\par \par \uc2 Those are the individuals that really need -- they really need to have discussions with to see what they need to combat this current crisis right now and to continue to move forward and enhance our operations. And that's what we're going to continue doing as we -- as we continue to work day in and day out on this border we're now trying to deter and stop these criminals from coming over.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Our best to you. And thank you for your service. And we will let you get back to work. Thanks so much, sir.\par \par \uc2 Chris Olivarez, thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Russia reporting a massive fire breaking out at an oil depot, this after unleashing a string of attacks on Ukrainian rail and fuel facilities.\par \par \uc2 Where things stand as this war is ramping up -- coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Inflation nation hitting your vacation?\par \par \uc2 With prices on everything from gas to food to lodging spiking, a number of Americans are cutting back. What impact will that have on the midterms?\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: New video showing fires raging at a Russian fuel depot in that same area that Russia accused Ukraine of attacking last week, this as Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  and Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  pledged more support for Ukraine in a meeting with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Griff Jenkins is in Kyiv. He's got the latest from there for us.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 And that meeting was the first high-level visit by a senior administration official since the war began. And there were two significant deliverables coming from it, first, Secretary Blinken announcing that the U.S. will indeed bring its diplomatic mission back into Ukraine, in hopes of ultimately reopening its embassy here in Kyiv in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 And, secondly, Secretary Austin says more weapons are on the way, to the tune of $700 million worth, with the goal of helping Ukraine win. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN, U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: We want to see Russia weakened to the degree that it can't do the kinds of things that it has done in invading Ukraine. So it has already lost a lot of military capability and a lot of its troops.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Hours after that visit, Russia struck deep into Ukraine today, targeting the railway systems. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 Well, it's likely, Sandra, because Ukraine uses it to move weapons to the front lines. There were five major strikes in the West in the town of Krasne, not far from Lviv, hitting the railway station there. Then, in Central Ukraine in the towns of Zhmerynka and Kozyatyn. That's significant because Kozyatyn is an important transport hub of the southwestern railway in the center of the country.\par \par \uc2 In all, at least eight -- five dead, 18 wounded. Now, it's worth noting too that that strike came right after Blinken and Austin used that same railway to travel to Kyiv for the meeting. And, over the weekend, significant strikes in the southern city of Odessa, that killed eight people, including a mother and her 3-month-old baby.\par \par \uc2 That's consistent with Russia's stated objectives of controlling that eastern Donbass and Southern Ukraine. And what we're watching now Sandra, is the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .'s secretary-general will travel to Moscow tomorrow to meet with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Now, Ukrainian officials are a little skeptical of that meeting, because the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . secretary-general does not speak for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 However, they are hopeful perhaps maybe something will come from that meeting in the way of getting Putin to open up a humanitarian corridor to get people out of that besieged of Mariupol -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Griff Jenkins, live from Kyiv for us.\par \par \uc2 Griff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So is reopening the U.S. Embassy a turning point for Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to discuss, former USS Cole Commander Kirk Lippold joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for your time, sir. Do you believe that to be the case? Could that be a turning point?\par \par \uc2 KIRK LIPPOLD, FORMER COMMANDER, USS COLE: Well, good afternoon, Sandra. And thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 I believe it is going to be a turning point, because it's really signifying that the United States is committed to a free and sovereign Ukraine, despite what Russia is doing today. And with us as the leader of the free world, that example is going to also push others to make sure that they continue their support and continue to give Ukraine what it needs to push the Russians back and out of that country.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It's all a great point, as we continue to watch the events unfold there.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see this fight going in the coming days and weeks? It's been unpredictable so far, and it's leaving us wondering how much longer this can last.\par \par \uc2 LIPPOLD: Well, that's a great question, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 I think what you really have to look at is, the United States intelligence community often knows days, if not week-plus in advance what Russia is doing. And we saw this as they pulled out from Kyiv, as they started to concentrate to go after the Donbass region and begin to focus in that area with these attacks to reconstitute and resupply.\par \par \uc2 What we are seeing is, the intelligence community now needs to get the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  to react to get the weapons that they need, the Ukrainians need, in order to be able to push the Russians back. We have been late to the party almost every single time.\par \par \uc2 While they're getting the supplies they need, they're late. They're not as effective. The Russians are making advances. And every day we delay are days that Ukrainians die as a result. So we need to do better in tying our intelligence community and the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  together to push that material to them quicker, along with our allies.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You believe that Secretary of Defense Austin's comments about Russia being significantly weakened should never have been said. Why?\par \par \uc2 LIPPOLD: I don't think he should have said that.\par \par \uc2 While that may be an objective, all that is doing is feeding into the narrative that Putin and Russia want that they are being surrounded, that they are under attack, that \uc2 NATO\uc2  is in fact trying to weaken Russia to the point that they are no longer a player on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 While it may be an objective, that is a classified statement, as far as I was concerned. It never should have been stated outright by Secretary Austin. And I think it was just unfortunate, because it feeds the disinformation campaign that Putin has been working since a decade ago in setting up what we are seeing today happen in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Sir, always appreciate this opportunity to talk to you. Thank you very much for joining us here.\par \par \uc2 LIPPOLD: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: Just how confident? Congressman Michael McCaul giving me the details on "FOX News Sunday." Check out his prediction and why he thinks the midterm elections will be a red wave for his party in November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Are Republicans going to retake the House?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): We feel very confident.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Congress is back. And so is the spending? As Americans are still dealing with inflation, Democrats expected to push for big social programs ahead of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill. He's got the latest from there.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL PRODUCER: Good afternoon, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, there's going to be a push among Democrats to get something done. Democratic Massachusetts Senator Elizabeth Warren says, if Democrats fail to deliver, they will lose in the midterms. Democrats are starting to pivot to bread-and-butter issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Got people who are in the checkout line for groceries and having to pick what they're going to send back because they can't afford to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 We have got millions of people across this country who can't fill up on a tank of gas so they will be able to get to work this week. As Democrats, we need to deliver. We need...\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But is that enough for the Democratic base? Liberals are still fighting for some version of Build Back Better, but moderates like Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  won't go for that.\par \par \uc2 Manchin only wants to tackle prescription drugs and deficit reduction. But the big social spending plan is dead. The GOP is honing in on failures by Democrats to address daily economic woes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: All they need to do is spend one day. Just go fill your car up. Just go to the grocery store. Just feel safe to try to walk the streets again. Who thought just in one year with one-party rule so much could go so wrong?\par \par \uc2 Well, don't blame us. We warned them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The GOP will still target Democrats for spending a trillion dollars on a COVID bill last year. But the biggest issue right now is getting a deal on COVID money for vaccines and tests, as well as more assistance for Ukraine -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, Chad Pergram on the Hill for us.\par \par \uc2 Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday on "FOX News Sunday," I spoke with Texas Republican congressman Michael McCaul about his party's chances in the midterms. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: We will pick up seats, all we need is five. I predict we're going to get probably at least 40 seats, because this president has been so unpopular when it comes to inflation, gas prices, in my -- the foreign policy mistakes that he's made.\par \par \uc2 And, quite frankly, Sandra, they just see him as a weak president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, with inflation raging and a whole lot of uncertainty at the border with Title 42 going away, is a new spending push going to make things better or worse for Democrats in November?\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from Democratic strategist Kristen Hawn.\par \par \uc2 Kristen, great to have you here. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN HAWN, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So it seems to be pretty obvious -- even Elizabeth Warren there painting this nasty inflation picture that most Americans are feeling in a big way in this moment.\par \par \uc2 It seems obvious to not tell more spending when, historically, in inflationary times, more spending will only cause more inflation.\par \par \uc2 HAWN: Yes, I mean, I think, first of all, there's no -- no party is the white knight here when it comes to addressing the debt and deficits that we faced.\par \par \uc2 They have continued to rise through Republican and Democratic presidents. It's very difficult to address. People are going to have to come together to do it. On the political side, I think that's right. Inflation is a difficult thing to tackle, particularly in a global economy. So it's not as easy as you would think.\par \par \uc2 But, at the end of the day, when it comes to spending, I think Manchin and Sinema have the right idea. I don't know what people think are going to get done that they can't to. They have been very honest about that from the very beginning. So I think doing some very targeted stuff on drug prices, which both parties want to do, would be smart to do, and then get that under control, get a win under our belts there in a bipartisan manner and start to address these things.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: To that point, what is a win that can be touted from this administration so far?\par \par \uc2 HAWN: I mean, I think you talk about what they inherited, it's really an unprecedented time with the pandemic, and then the global economy, with the supply chains, the difficulties we have been facing.\par \par \uc2 We talked about the bill that was passed at the very beginning of the Biden administration on a bipartisan basis to address some of the challenges that the pandemic has presented, and then not to forget about the infrastructure bill that the administration after administration has tried to pass, and that they were able to get done on a bipartisan basis.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of those things don't -- won't come to fruition just because it takes time to put them into place by the midterms. So some of it's perception. Some of it, we need to do better. But there are some major accomplishments so far.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, when it comes to Title 42, that is not perception. That is a real problem and very big challenges at our Southern border that will only be made worse if Title 42 is lifted.\par \par \uc2 And that is not just the take of Republican members of Congress. That is the take of the Border Patrol. And that is the take of many, a growing list of Democrats who are pushing back, breaking with the president on this issue. How bad politically will that hurt the president?\par \par \uc2 HAWN: I mean, I think you have got these border state Democrats who are taking a look at this.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a health policy, right? I think it really is reflective, though, of the larger need for Congress to come together and address immigration reform, comprehensive immigration reform, which is just -- which has eluded both parties all the way back to \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , who tried to do it, and members of his own party couldn't come to an agreement with him.\par \par \uc2 So I think we're seeing this flesh out right now. And it's something that we all need to take a look at, we need to figure out. And the administration needs to have a plan in place for when this when this goes away.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. All right, we will see. November is going to quickly approach.\par \par \uc2 Kristen, thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 HAWN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: More flight fright, airlines having trouble finding pilots, and it could have you finding a different way to your destination.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, your next flight might not be a flight at all.\par \par \uc2 Airlines turning to buses, instead of planes, as a nationwide pilot shortage is taking off.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' own Nate Foy is at New York's La Guardia Airport on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello again, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 United and \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  have a deal with a bus line called Landline to bring people from smaller cities with smaller airports to bigger airports where pilots will be waiting for them to take passengers to their ultimate destination. Both airlines did respond to FOX News today for a comment, saying, this is not in direct reaction to the pilot shortage, but instead just an additional service to meet rising consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 United is already offering buses between Denver and smaller cities like Breckenridge and Fort Collins. Starting June 3, American will offer buses between Philadelphia and Allentown, Pennsylvania, as well as Atlantic City, New Jersey. You see both of those destinations under 75 miles from the \uc2 Philadelphia International Airport\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to federal labor stats, the amount of pilots in the industry in total is expected to grow 13 percent by 2030. That's faster than any other occupation. The CEO of \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  says his company is taking in workers by the thousands.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT ISOM\uc2 , CEO, AMERICAN AIRLINES: We're smart about our schedule. We're going to fly and size the airline for the resources that we have.\par \par \uc2 And, this year, we have 12,000 more team members than we did last year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Until then, until they fix this problem, the shortage is going to cost you as the consumer money and travel options.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 , compared to 2019, of course, before the pandemic, flight schedules are down now at \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  13 percent. Delta is down 16 percent. And American is down 8 percent. Also, \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  is cutting up to 10 percent of its flights this summer.\par \par \uc2 Now, to hold on to the pilots that they do have and to attract more pilots, airlines are paying more, which, if you couple that with the rising cost of jet fuel, the cost of business, Sandra, is up, which means it's going to cost you more for the service.\par \par \uc2 United CEO \uc2 Scott Kirby\uc2  said last week he thinks it's going to be five years before airlines can use their full fleet in service again.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It always ends up getting passed along to the consumer, right, Nate?\par \par \uc2 FOY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you very much, Nate Foy at La Guardia.\par \par \uc2 And with inflation at a 40-year high, a Democrat running for Congress is now calling for a huge minimum wage hike.\par \par \uc2 Why Brian Brenberg says that would just lead to more price hikes. He's here and he's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): If you're going to do business out here in New York, you got to treat our people right.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): What this whole thing is about is working people standing up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That was New York Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  and Vermont independent Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  making a pitch to unionize a second \uc2 Amazon\uc2  plant, as Democratic candidate for Congress in Washington Rebecca Parson is calling for a $30-per-hour minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Some at home may be asking themselves, really?\par \par \uc2 FOX News contributor and King's College Professor Brian Brenberg joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps you're one of them.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I hope a lot of people at home are asking, really? Because that is nuts. The problem is that's the energy of the Democratic Party. If you want to understand why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done what he's done, look at who's at that union rally and look what they're talking about.\par \par \uc2 They're ignoring the issues that people across the country really have to deal with. And instead they go to their safe place, a union rally.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, lay it out for us, though, because you do have to be sensitive to the fact that inflation is hurting just about everyone.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, naturally, you want to see your paycheck go up.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Naturally, you would think, OK, it would benefit most to have wages go up. Raise their minimum. But what would be the impact on small business if you were to do that?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 It would be -- I mean, a $30 minimum wage federally, just think about that for a second. For small businesses, right now, 90 percent of small businesses say their biggest problem is they can't get a handle on costs.\par \par \uc2 Now you take their biggest line item and you jack it up by 30 -- to $30 an hour, it would be complete devastation. So this is the wrong prescription. But, again, it's the wrong prescription because the left doesn't understand or doesn't want to confront what's really happening in this country.\par \par \uc2 And the real problem is the spending-driven inflation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 A lot of pressure on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to get this right too.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Tons. And they have gotten it so wrong, which is why they have so much pressure.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It's going to be painful for sure.\par \par \uc2 Looking at the market reaction today...\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: ... there's a lot of excitement on Wall Street, a 238-point finish to the day to the upside on the Dow.\par \par \uc2 But, look, all day, it was in the red. But then here comes \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and a huge deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And markets got excited about it. Your reaction to the new deal?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Capitalism is happening. That's what I love about this.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and partners say, we don't like what's happening with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Let's do something about it. He's now got it. He will have a controlling interest to make some changes. What will he do? I don't know. That's the beauty of it. He's going to experiment. He's probably going to try a lot of different things.\par \par \uc2 But the point is, he's going to invite more people back into this platform, which means a win for freer speech.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very, very interesting.\par \par \uc2 The market excitement was interesting to me today, that Wall Street, they needed a new deal, I mean, a multibillion-dollar deal.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  basically coming in and saying, I'm going to take over a company...\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: ... that has been highly criticized by the right for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 And now we're left wondering what actual changes he will be able to implement.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: He's opening up possibilities.\par \par \uc2 The market needed possibilities. And \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is saying, let's do that. I will start with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, and there you have it; 34,000 seems to be...\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: ... the number the Dow wants to hover around here; $54.20 a share for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: A 38 percent premium before anybody knew he was going to buy the thing, that's a good deal.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: And the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  board was forced to say, you know what, it's the best deal out there. We have mismanaged this company. Maybe he can do something about it.\par \par \uc2 But if the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  board would have rejected this, it would have been a big-time lawsuit. They didn't want to deal with that. So, they gave it to Musk.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We appreciate you joining us, Brian Brenberg. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Always good to see you. You bet.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. That does it for us. Neil will be back tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 You can catch me tomorrow 1:00 to 3:00 p.m. Eastern, "America Reports."\par \par \uc2 "The Five" starts now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220425ei4p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Texas National Guard Solider Presumed Dead At Border; Biden Admin to Lift Title 42 in One Month; Judd: Migrant Caravan to Reach Border on Day Title 42 Expires; Journalist Obtains Documents of Curriculums Teaching Chicago Ages Three through Five about Gender Identity; Westminster College in Utah Offers Course on Pornographic Films\b0\par\par\uc2 Jason Chaffetz, Bill Melugin, Sean Duffy \par \uc2 7,701 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS HOST: We will all be live from Talladega, Alabama. The Talladega 500, the Superspeedway. You don't want to miss our coverage. We're going to have a lot of fun, Saturday and Sunday morning. We hope you join us.\par \par \uc2 Sean is back on Monday. Have a great night, everybody, and we'll see you tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 JASON CHAFFETZ, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Jason Chaffetz, in for Laura Ingraham and this is a special edition of "The Ingraham Angle."\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with tragic news. A Texas National Guardsmen is presumed dead after trying to rescue a female migrant crossing the Rio Grande. It's been more than 12 hours since he dove into the water and never resurfaced.\par \par \uc2 Just moments ago, we learned search teams had to cease dive operations due to the strength of the rivers current. They hope to resume operations tomorrow. The Texas Military Department saying in a statement quote, "We will continue the search for our soldier until we have exhausted all possible resources."\par \par \uc2 According to Maverick County Sheriff earlier this morning, the soldiers saw a female migrant struggling in the river. He immediately threw off all of his gear and jumped into the Rio Grande. The migrant survived and she's currently in Border Patrol custody.\par \par \uc2 We talk a lot about this border crisis and how it's led to a surge in crime. It's fueling a drug epidemic. It's literally costing us American lives. And tonight, we learned that a border crisis may have taken yet another life.\par \par \uc2 Despite all of this destruction it's doing to our country, the Biden Administration is still not doing a single thing to fix this crisis. In fact, tomorrow marks one month until Title 42 is officially lifted - one month. And then the floodgates open up even more.\par \par \uc2 You remember that caravans last year? Well get ready for more. Border agents are bracing for this. One of my next guests. National Border Patrol Council President Brandon Judd says one caravan coming from Guatemala is expected to reach the border on May 23rd, the day Title 42 expires, what a coincidence.\par \par \uc2 I really can't overstate how bad it is down there. DHS reports that more than 221,000 migrants were detained at the U.S.-Mexican border in March. And that's the highest number in two decades. And it does not include any of the gotaways. A group of 70 sheriffs are begging Congress to extend Title 42 writing, "We simply have no border left, help save American lives."\par \par \uc2 Even Biden's DHS Secretary is reportedly concerned about lifting the Trump era policy. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was recently asked about extending it. Here's how he reacted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Are you considering delaying lifting Title 42?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, what I'm considering is continuing to hear from my - well, first of all, there's going to be an appeal by the Justice Department. Because as a matter of principle, we want to be able to be in a position where if in fact, it is strongly concluded by the scientists that we need Title 42 that we'd be able to do that. But there has been no decision on extending Title 42.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Clean up on Aisle 1600, the White House quickly worked to fix the mess Joe made when he answered a question about the border with comments about public transportation mask mandates. It's no wonder why migrants think the welcome mat is out and it is. Our president can't keep his policy straight and his borders are - \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she is literally busy talking about outer space.\par \par \uc2 For what's really happening at the border we go to Fox's Bill Melugin. Bill?\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Jason, good evening to you. As there's been a lot of talk here in the U.S. about what's going to happen with Title 42 next month, we've been watching it in action here in Eagle Pass. Take a look at this video we shot here this morning as we saw multiple groups of migrants being expelled back to Mexico into Piedras Negras.\par \par \uc2 So we decided let's go on over to Mexico to Piedras Negras and talk to some of these migrants about Title 42. Sources have been telling us there are potentially 1,000s of these migrants waiting in Mexico, waiting for Title 42 to drop so they could cross without being expelled. Didn't take long to find the migrants. They were standing around all over the place, many of them telling us they had already themselves been expelled via Title 42, because they were from those Northern Triangle countries.\par \par \uc2 We talked to two Honduran migrants who both told us on May 23rd when Title 42 drops, they are both planning to cross illegally. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): On May 23rd there's another proposal from North America to help other countries, Honduras, El Salvador and Guatemala. The President has taken that measure to help us and support us in this moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): My hope is for now is that I get granted permission to cross with my son on the 23rd. That is my hope right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And Jason we're hearing that word is quickly spreading amongst migrants about May 23rd as you heard just in those interviews right there. And that's one of the reasons why DHS is projecting upwards of 18,000 illegal crossings every single day, when Title 42 drops. Just to put it in perspective for you, they're averaging about 7,000 a day right now. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Bill, thank you. Joining me now is Brandon Judd, President of the National Border Patrol Council; and Lieutenant Christopher Olivarez, Spokesperson with the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, I want to go back to something I talked about at the top of this show. And you guys put your lives on the line, but a Texas National Guardsmen is presumed dead, sacrificing his own life trying to save somebody else. Lieutenant, I want to hear in your own words, what you're hearing and what you're feeling.\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRISTOPHER OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : Right. So, of course, good evening, Jason. Of course, thanks for having me on. But what happened today is complete tragedy what's taking place for now in Eagle Pass Texas with our Texas National Guard soldier.\par \par \uc2 But that is a clear example of true bravery and the courage for these soldiers who risk their lives every single day by being on those frontlines and actually going into the river and rescue - rescuing immigrants that are coming across unlawfully, violating federal law, but they risk their lives every single day to perform these rescues.\par \par \uc2 And because of the messaging from the federal government, that right now clearly we have an open border in - of course, they're not in favor of reinstating or keeping Title 42, the message is out. So now we have mass migrants that are waiting in Mexico, caravans that are making their way to the Southern - to the Northern part of the border. And unfortunate we're going to see more events take place.\par \par \uc2 There was 19 drownings alone this month and Eagle Pass. I was there on Tuesday, there was three drownings in a single day, because of the river - the current - the strong currents that are taking place right there, right now. And because of what's taking place, unfortunately, we're going to continue seeing these events and it places our men and women, Border Patrol, our DPS troopers, our National Guardsmen in very dangerous situations where they have to risk their own lives to go into that river and perform these rescue operations.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Brandon, you've seen it firsthand. I've been on the border with you and the people - the good people of the Border Patrol. When you hear about an incident like tonight, what runs through your mind? What are the people on the ground, the men and women who serve in the Border Patrol, what are they thinking about?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL PRESIDENT: Jason, I've spoken with many agents today and we are beyond upset. We cannot believe that this president's policies are allowing migrants to put themselves in danger, go into rivers, and then force good people like this National Guardsmen to go in and risk his own life. And apparently he has now lost his life.\par \par \uc2 This tragedy falls directly at the president's feet. His open border policies are encouraging people to violate our laws. It's allowing cartels to generate billions of dollars of profit. And now one of our United States citizens lost his life going into a river trying to save somebody that the cartels put into place. President Biden needs to wake up, he needs to do his job and he needs to secure the border.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Lieutenant, we talked about the people that are apprehended, but tell us more about the gotaways, because it's an untold number. And the Border Patrol is so caught up with the paperwork and the administration of what they're having to do, because the - it is so big, the numbers are so large, it's leaving these wide open gates out there these holes that drug traffickers and everybody else are getting through.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Right, Jason, that's why Governor Abbott launched Operation Lone Star last year in March, when we saw the influx of illegal immigrants coming across, the drug smuggling, the criminals that are coming across our borders, potential terrorists that are coming across our borders.\par \par \uc2 When we talk about the gotaways, just for this fiscal year alone, for the first six months, and I'm sure Brandon can attest to this, over 300,000 known getaways. And that's only what we know about. There's much more that are getting by us that we are unable to apprehend. Border Patrol is overwhelmed. They are tied up with processing.\par \par \uc2 And that's why the State of Texas, we're now is taking unprecedented action, and providing more state assets to the Southern border to help our federal partners to try to apprehend these criminals that are taking advantage of the current border crisis.\par \par \uc2 And it all falls back on these failed border policies that we're seeing. And the fact that the federal government has neglected the American people in securing the border, and that's why again, the State of Texas along with our federal partners working together, we are stepping up and trying to secure this border.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, here's how the Biden Administration is spinning their decision to end Title 42. Quote, "With Title 42 in PLACE a significant percentage of migrants have reattempted to enter the United States illegally and ultimately face no consequences for attempting to reenter the country illegally, a White House pokes spokesperson had told The Hill. Privately the administration has argued to their allies that this is a tougher immigration stance." But Brandon, that is seems like the most desperate spin I've ever heard.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: It's a lie. Once Title 42 goes away then we're going to have to process people for titling, that's going to take even more of our resources out of the field. It's going to create even larger stretches of border that are uncontrolled. And then once we process them for Title 8, they're going to be released into the United States. It's a reward for violating our laws.\par \par \uc2 That is the magnet that is drawing so many people to cross our borders illegally, simply because they're being released into the United States never to be heard from again. We cannot reward people for violating our laws.\par \par \uc2 If we believe in the rule of law, we will put an end to this and we will stop people from putting themselves in the hands of dangerous criminal cartels, we will then be able to go after the criminality on the border, we will be able to go after the profits of the criminal cartels, we will be able to go after the drugs and all of the criminal aliens and aliens from special interest countries.\par \par \uc2 Right now, if it wasn't for a state, if it wasn't for Texas DPS, filling in those gaps that are created when Border Patrol agents are pulled out of the field, who knows what would be happening right now?\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: This tsunami that is coming north - Brandon, real quickly - tell us about what your intelligence is telling you about what's coming out of Guatemala?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Oh, we're hearing that by the day people are gathering in this group that's going to work its way up to the border. Now, they're afraid - the cartels understand that they can't have pictures like that. So once they get into Mexico, they'll break up into smaller groups and hit the border in all different parts.\par \par \uc2 Right now we're seeing increases everywhere along the southwest border. That's not what - that's not what's typical of what happens on the border.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: And Lieutenant, tell us about some of the people you are apprehending along the way, in particular, even just this last week?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Right, Jason. So, of course, we enhanced our border operations. We have state troopers that are actually working in private ranches apprehending these single adults, the ones that are avoiding detection. They're wearing camouflage clothing, they're wearing carpet on the shoes to avoid leaving any type of shoe tracks.\par \par \uc2 These are all part of those gotaways that we constantly talk about, that we don't know who they are, what their backgrounds are there. There are criminal gang members that come across, MS-13, potential terrorists, individuals that have serious criminal histories for sex crimes, murder. These are individuals that are coming across traversing through these private ranches, trying to make it further to the interior.\par \par \uc2 And Border Patrol as well as been apprehending these individuals. But we continue to try to fill in those gaps, because we know what the anticipation of Title 42 being completely rescinded, it's going to open the floodgates.\par \par \uc2 Right now we're seeing record numbers. And, of course, Brandon, has mentioned that numerous times about the individuals that are coming across, the gotaways, the criminals, the cartel members that are coming across, all the different tactics that we're seeing is a result of what's taking place right now. And unfortunately, it's going to get much, much worse, come May.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, gentlemen, thanks for the good work you do. And I hope you pass along to the men and women who serve with you how much America is behind them, because this policy is not - it's not sustainable, but we appreciate everything you're doing every day.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Jason--\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: All right. Will Biden soon have a - I got to keep going. Sorry. Will Biden have a mutiny on his hands over the border crisis, because it's not just Republicans, nine Senate Democrats are warning against lifting Title 42. And it's no surprise because these are mostly vulnerable Democrats, including Arizona's Mark Kelly, who be my next guest is running to replace.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now for tonight's retaking America segment is \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2 , he's the Arizona Attorney General and a \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  candidate. Mr. Attorney General, thanks for joining us. It is amazing how an election is just months away, and suddenly these Democrats are getting a backbone and saying, Oh, we got a problem. But you know, they weren't there before. Were they?\par \par \uc2 MARK BRNOVICH\uc2  (R), ARIZONA ATTORNEY GENERAL: No, they weren't. And that's the reason why I've been leading lawsuits against the Biden Administration in their failure to secure the border and all the lawlessness and the dangerous created in our communities.\par \par \uc2 The reality is, and you know this Jason, the