uing this. He's the richest man in the world. He sends people to space, he makes electric cars, he makes batteries or revise batteries. He's doing to this for economics. This guy, who knows his way around a balance sheet and a profit sheet. So, at the end of the day if there were not money to be made doing this, I doubt he would be doing it.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes. And Katie, isn't it fair that the founders actually believe that we didn't want a ministry of truth. We wanted to actually let the debate happen?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Listening to a Barack Obama say that the First Amendment is only a check on the state. It makes me question why he wouldn't want the First Amendment to apply to big corporations and to corporations especially that make all their money on speech. People being able to talk about things.\par \par \uc2 But then I look back at who President Obama really is. And he is a student of Saul Alinsky. And Saul Alinsky teaches in rules of radicals if you control narrative, you control information you then control the media and you control everything. And he talked about how, you know, free speech is not being able to disseminate or post clearly dangerous content.\par \par \uc2 While to the left, clearly dangerous content it's things like \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's laptop. It's things about the Wuhan lab. It's basic things about gender and biology. And so, they've shifted from actually dangerous incitement content to any kind of content that they deem a risk for their political prospect --\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: -- and that's why they have no credibility on this. But I think it's highly alarming that the president of the United States -- the former president is perfectly fine with these monster corporations going after certain speech that he certainly, just simply doesn't like.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And only one-sided.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Right.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Greg, final word?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, as yo