ork. They don't have to get dressed up. They have to comb their hair. I mean, you see them there on the streets. They're on their pajamas and get on the airplane. It's horrible. I think any excuses. If you want to go to work, get your butt to work. That's the end of it.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR: Greg?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, it's hard to judge because -- for us because we live in the city so we're kind of -- we're not -- I don't think we're representative of the rest of the country. However, I mean, the lunches that I go to like when I go to pick up my soup, or actually I had my assistant pick it up, a large soup will be $19.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And it's so -- it angers me when it's warm. So, I just throw it at her. Just throw it right at her. And that -- and then, from now on, I have hot soup.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: That's why she doesn't want to come to work?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, that's why it's --\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: She blames it on you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's not inflation. It that I'm a big jerk.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Can I say -- I wouldn't -- blizzards for my kids at Dairy Queen, it was $8.25 for medium blizzard. I'm like, are you kidding me? I mean, I remember it was like $3.25 for a blizzard. But on this one, do I --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Is the blizzard ice cream?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes, come one.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, OK.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So, I look at this and think that yes, inflation is a problem. People are spending more to drive into work. They're spending more for childcare. That's a real problem. But I think to the judge's point, people have become very comfortable being at home. They get to be by their family, they don't waste an hour going in, they don't waste an hour going back. They don't have to get ready as much in the morning. And so, I think this is a comfort play for a lot of folks who don't want to go in.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR: Katie?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: I