to the country, and they are even aware of this issue with Title 42. They're poised to come into this country, and they know that they will get away with it, and they know that they will be welcomed.\par \par \uc2 But this is the thing. And you've understood this forever. I know this. This is an issue that is not just a Republican issue. This is an issue that matters to Republican voters, but also to Democrat voters. And as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  proved to politicians in Washington, it also matters to Hispanic voters, for example, who want to have a secure border. Everybody wants to have a secure border. It's not a controversial issue.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Charlie, the DEA is warning local police about another crisis spurred by the border saying, "In just the past two months, there have been at least 7 confirm mass overdose events across the United States resulting in 58 overdoses, 29 overdose deaths, tragic events like these are being driven by fentanyl."\par \par \uc2 And the fentanyl, Charlie, as you've written about is coming from China through south of the border into the veins and into the bodies of people across this country. Many of them desperate, whether they're out of work or mental health or a combination, or they just get their drugs spiked. And it's mass carnage. I'm just going to keep saying it.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Yes. And if you talk to regular people around the country, I don't know anyone who doesn't know someone who has been affected by this poison that is pouring into our country. That you - either people in their own family, or people that they know have been poisoned by this stuff.\par \par \uc2 And if this was a smokestack that was billowing this kind of poison that was killing this - a number of people, and especially young people in this country, there would be a hue and cry. It would be a problem that would be fixed instantly.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, thank you for this piece, thank you for writing this, and we will have you back on this soon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Biden's wobbly week, and Cracker Jack goes to cracker -- you'll have to stay to find out. Raymond Arroyo has it all. Friday Follies next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This is a FOX News alert. I'm Jeff Paul live in Lviv, Ukraine. Officials say an attack of the local railway station has left more than 50 people dead and dozens and dozens of others injured. This all happening in eastern Ukraine in the Donetsk region where officials have long warned residents to get out before fighting there intensifies. Among the dead at least five children were killed great.\par \par \uc2 Officials say more than 4,000 people were at the rail station at the time of the missile strikes. Photos from the scene appear to show remnants of a rocket with the words "for the children" painted in Russian. Both the Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  and other officials say Russian forces knew what they were targeting, calling it an evil without limits.\par \par \uc2 Now local officials say among the people who were at this railway station, 90 percent of them were either elderly or children. Russia, meanwhile, has denied the attacks. For the latest updates on this developing story, log on to FOXnews.com.\par \par \uc2 Now back to Laura Ingraham.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It is Friday, can you believe it? And that means it's time for Friday Follies. And for that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, Biden has a bit of an unsteady week, and I'm putting very charitably, as we entered to the last week of Lent.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: He sure did have an unsteady week. On Tuesday the president appeared with \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , you'll remember, and we said on Monday the comparison would not go well, and it didn't, Laura. He seemed confused, utterly abandoned by the crowd. So the following day the White House sent him to a union gathering, and they must've given them a few Red Bulls. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You better stop. I'll start believing it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Please have a seat if you have one. It's good to be home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Last time I checked, everyone has a seat, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That just blew my earpiece. Wow.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I know. He's very loud. Whether it was the caffeine or some other stimulant, it gave the president lots of energy but very little clarity.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The steps we've already taken are predicted to shrink Russia's gross domestic product by double digits this year alone. Just in one year, our sanctions are likely to wipe out the last 15 years of Russia's economic gain.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I want to talk about what I'm here today to talk about, you women or men and the American union movement.\par \par \uc2 To ensure this to America, we have to have a promise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, all of this gives the impression, even when he's jacked up and he's flying high, it gives the impression the president is not in control. And you see there, and again, we're talking here about impressions, takeaways, what the audience sees and the public sees who is not particularly politically savvy or even looking at policy. There's a new ABC poll, 54 percent of those polled said the president lacks mental sharpness, 40 percent said he has mental sharpness.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What? Wow.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But you see the disconnect here. And these events I don't think help make the counterargument that the White House would like made.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Obama visit was fun for Obama, I'm sure, but it was a disaster for Biden. And sending him out to scream at the audience did not help.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Like that union address, there was virtually no coverage of this post office bill signing later this same day. This perhaps is a cautionary tale, Laura, that stimulants can take you up, but they inevitably drop you. Here is the president after being introed by a letter carrier. Note the energy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: When I said it's good to meet you, she said I know you, we've met before. I said I apologize. She said, you used to show up in Wilmington how often?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In D.C., every year.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh, in D.C., every year I'd show up at the letter carriers breakfast or lunch or dinner, I can't member which. But it reminded me why I think foreign policy is easier.\par \par \uc2 When we're dedicated and we dedicate it to the send and committed we would send to free that COVID-19 tests directly to people's homes, the Postal Service stepped up to make that happen as well. And now they've delivered over 200 -- 300, excuse me, 320 million COVID test 19 kits so far.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Good lord. Laura, this is why Trump is up five points in the national polls against Biden. But it does beg the question of how much longer this can go on, and whether his vice president is up to the task.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, you know how they say people have word salad. Well, this goes another -- this is word chopped salad. It's even worse than a regular salad, because it's chop, chop, chop. You can't know what he said.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's a word blender. None of it make sense. It's all in there, but it's jumbled around.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, he also made a comment today about his time, he was asked about this, but his time which \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 . And maybe he can't quite remember what was happening when he was vice president. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I was in the foot -- excuse, the foothills of the Himalayas with \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , traveling with him. I traveled 17,000 miles when I was vice president, I don't know that for a fact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: What does that have to do with the Supreme Court justice being confirmed? Absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He might have that on the brain, the whole Hunter Biden connection with China. What is he supposed to say and what is he not supposed to say? It's like a ball bearing in a box. It just goes, tip it over and it goes one way. And it just makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, it is baseball season, and just in time for the start of the season, Cracker Jack has a new sister, you know those little caramel coated popcorn. The parent company \uc2 Frito-Lay\uc2  has unveiled a new phase of the brand, Cracker Jill, to celebrate women and girls in sports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, Cracker Jill will be available to every sports fan, Laura, who donates five bucks to the \uc2 Women's Sports Foundation\uc2 . I kind of like the idea of Cracker Jack extending its brand to support female athletes and our girls who compete in sports. The question is, are they really? The Women's Sports Foundation that this effort supports, actually advocates biological men competing in women's sports, which as you know, is being hotly contested by female athletes. So the question is, does this advance girls in sports. Go ahead, I'll get your crack at this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Maybe they should have called it Cracker George, because George will be competing against you know Georgina in an actual female, biological female competition, or a person formally known as George will be competing. I just saw virtue signaling. OK, I played a lot of sports and was a pretty good athlete, but this adds up to nothing. It just empty virtue signaling. And in the end, girls are really strong and independent, more so than ever. And we should celebrate that. We don't really need Cracker Jack to --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Maybe Cracker Jack or Jill. Give people the option.\par \par \uc2 And Laura, we have breaking news tonight on the slap heard around the world. The Hollywood Film Academy is apparently handing down the Will Smith's punishment. For the next 10 years, he is banned from attending any Academy events, including the Oscars. That's kind of a light slap on the wrist. He had already resigned from the Academy, so they couldn't expel him. But there was talk of taking away his Oscar, but that's rarely done.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's story seems like it was a year ago now, doesn't it, already?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, thank you, great to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 And in moments we explain how a disturbing story out of our nation's capital reveals that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's promise of restoring dignity to the presidency was as much of a crock as we thought it was. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: During the 2020 campaign, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  promised to bring dignity and compassion back to Washington. And recently he has given impassioned remarks about the suffering in Ukraine where we see ruthless killing as innocents.\par \par \uc2 But so far there's been a deafening silence from him and leading Democrats on heinous, unthinkable suffering right in the nation's capital. Demands for justice are building after bodies of five late-term babies that were reportedly recovered from an abortion clinic in Washington surfaced. Now, if true, it's horrific, and obviously this demands a full investigation. But the left is so maniacally committed to the abortion industry that it will do nothing but smear those asking questions.\par \par \uc2 So where is the decency? We all recoil in the horror over the grizzly killings of innocent children in Ukraine. But where is the outcry over what's being done to these poor little babies? Not a word from the supposedly strong Catholic, Biden, and nary a peep from D.C. Mayor Muriel Bowser. After days of silence from D.C. officials, "Daily Wire" reporter Mary Margaret Olohan went to the clinic in question seeking answers. But even she never expected to find what she encountered. Mary Margaret Olohan joins me now. Tell us what you saw when you got to the clinic.\par \par \uc2 MARY MARGARET OLOHAN, REPORTER, "DAILY WIRE": Thanks, Laura. I never expected in a million years to see what I saw yesterday, and it will probably stay with me for the rest of my life. So I went to Washington Surgi-Clinic which is in Foggy Bottom in D.C. It's on the fourth floor of a big office building. And I got up there and went inside and was chatting with some of the nurses, or the abortion clinic staff that were in there. They told me I could not talk to the abortionist, Dr. Santangelo. They said he wasn't there which I have reason to believe they were lying about since the waiting room was full of patients. And when I went outside the door to leave, I bumped into a woman who told me that she was in the middle of having an abortion right then and there.\par \par \uc2 And Laura, I cannot tell you how upsetting and how moving this situation was. She was with a man who I assumed to be her boyfriend or her husband. His eyes were all right, he looked like he was crying. She was leaning on the wall kind of heaving and moaning. She was in a lot of pain. And I said what are you doing here, what's going on? And she said I'm having an abortion.\par \par \uc2 And I was really confused because it was strange language, and I looked at her and I could see that her stomach was huge and it was really firm. And I said, did you already have it, or what do you mean? She said I'm having it right now. And I'm standing there and horror looking at her, realizing that her baby is being aborted as we speak.\par \par \uc2 And right at that moment, an abortion clinic worker opened the door of the clinic, stuck her head out, and said don't talk to her, don't talk to her. And she was trying to get the people to go inside, and talking over me so I couldn't be heard. And I didn't have a lot of time, but I told them, please, talk to me. If you want to share your stories, my name is Mary Margaret, and I work for "The Daily Wire." And I didn't have a lot of time to give them much more, and then they went back into the clinic.\par \par \uc2 So I go downstairs and I get into the lobby, and I'm just standing there, and suddenly it hits me like a wave that this woman was having an abortion, she was in the middle of it. I talked to pro-life doctors who told me later that she was in the beginning stages and it looked like she was about to head in there for an induction abortion, and that the abortionist had set her up to make it easier for him to reach in there and get the baby.\par \par \uc2 And I'm walking to my car in the rain, and I just was overcome. Like, what should I have said? Should have said something differently? So I have asked her if she wanted to be there? And I'm realizing that this doctor who is aborting her baby, we know that he was recorded in 2012 saying that he would allow a baby to die if it was born alive during his abortion.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mary, this is an undercover video, the one you just referenced from 2012, where, and I don't even want to call this individual a doctor, but he explained his process. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. SANTANGELO: Hopefully we can get this pregnancy out intact, but it doesn't always happen that way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Does it ever move or anything when it comes out?\par \par \uc2 SANTANGELO: That's why I try and severe their umbilical cord first. Then we wait for that to stop pulsing. And that's when the fetus has expired, so it doesn't.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Obviously, this is a baby. Fetus is kind of a medical term, but I guess it makes them him somehow feel better just to say "fetus" like it's nothing.\par \par \uc2 OLOHAN: He does this all the time. We know that. He bungled one -- one time he was removing a baby during a miscarriage. He bungled that removal so badly that tissue from that baby ended up in the mother's lungs and she died. This man is incredibly sloppy. He's horrible at his job, and now pro- lifers are telling me that they think that he violated the partial-birth abortion act of 2003.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mary Margaret, we're out of time, but thank you so much for sharing this story, and I know it's going to touch people in different ways. But thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, up next, Mayor Pete sums up 50 years of Democrat ignorance in 15 seconds. The Last Bite explains.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mayor Pete has never been really known for his sharp political acumen, and today it was no different.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You do realize, Pete, a lot of black people feel like Democrats have kept no promises since they've been in office.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Really?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. Reverend has said that a million times.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: OK, look, I get it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What a perfect embodiment of the Democrats' complete disregard for voters they obviously take for granted.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Don't forget, set your DVR to series record so you always stay connected with us. And remember, USA made is always going to be the best. And you get your USA made Freedom Matters gear. All my proceeds, 100 percent go to charity, and this month goes to the Special Operations Warrior Foundation, a great, great cause, and it's really cool gear, too. You'll love the hoodies and everything else.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it's America now and forever. Have a great weekend. "GUTFELD!" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220409ei4800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Red Cross Convoy Headed For Mariupol Turns Back; Russia Continues Bombing Despite Pledge To Stop; Ukraine Pleads For More Military Aid; Zelenskyy Warns Mariupol May Be Target Again; White House As Russian Troops Reposition In Ukraine; Kremlin Accuses Ukraine Of Strike On Russian Oil; Russia Continues Bombing Despite Pledge To Stop; \uc2 Disney\uc2  Opposes FL Bill, Expands In Anti-Gay Areas\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Steve Harrigan, Neil Cavuto, Jennifer Griffin,  William La Jeunesse \par \uc2 9,699 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm just kind of surprised that the president says he's going to work with oil producers to ramp up production and then doesn't do anything to pay off on that.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: It is interesting. And I'm sure we'll be covering more of it as a new week begins next Monday. And I'll see you then, John. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . Have a great weekend, everyone. we'll see on Monday. "THE STORY" with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, John, and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum here is the story. So, a lot going on in Ukraine at this hour. Tensions boiling over whether Ukraine hit a fuel depot inside of Russia, which would obviously be an escalatory move. But did the Russians do it themselves? That is a big question today.\par \par \uc2 Also, the cruelty in Mariupol is making it a living hell. Then, you have the brave Red Cross workers who were turned back once again today, just hours ago. They have 42 buses, and they want to get the people who are still in Mariupol out of there, but they cannot get through the Russian checkpoints. And again, they make it impossible. And of course, they fear for their lives. They need to get in there safely in order to get people out safely.\par \par \uc2 And another big part of this story, experts talking about despite the prevailing narrative, what if \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  actually did not miscalculate? What if what we are seeing was part of the strategy all along? We'll talk about what we mean about him wanting the east part of Ukraine and bombarding areas all over in order to get that in a deal. We're going to talk about that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Also, our own Bret Baier just finished speaking with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , and we're going to bring you some new sound from that fascinating interview this hour as well. We've got Congressman and Retired Navy SEAL Dan Crenshaw, joining us in just a moment, but first to Correspondent Steve Harrigan. Good afternoon, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha, what's going on now in large parts of Ukraine is siege warfare, it's really right out of the middle-ages. This was not Russia's plan a -- their plan a was lightning raids. Those raids did not work. So, they've gone to Plan B which is basically trying to bomb and starve cities into submission to bend them to Russia's will. A siege has been going on in the city of Mariupol now for two weeks, 100,000 civilians are in that city. Alina Beskrovn is one of those who's escaped.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALINA BESKROVN, ESCAPED TO POLAND: There is no gas, no electricity, no heating, no cell phone service. We melt snow to have at least something to drink. A lot of people are just, I think starving to death in their apartments right now with no help.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Modern urban sieges really don't work that well. They're often not effective. They tie up a lot of troops that can last for weeks or even months, and when the final fight comes there is a real advantage to the defenders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPENCER, MADISON POLICY FORUM: In urban train, you take three to five times more casualties. So, even, in this situation, the Mariupol is in or any of these cities, if Russia wants to take them with how bad they are at military tasks, they have to pay for thousands and thousands of troops. 10,000 troops to take, subsidies --- 20,000.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: A lot of the Ukrainians who are under siege right now are old and frail. Some can't get out. But some have decided to stay refusing to abandon their homes, not giving in to the hunger or the shelling. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Steve Harrigan, thank you very much, Steve. Great to see you. Joining me now Anastasia Radina, a Ukrainian Member of Parliament, she met with members of Congress earlier, to appeal for more military support and she joins me now. Anastasia, good to have you with us today. We know that President Zelenskyy has just spoken with Bret Baier, we're going to play a little bit of that when we have some new sound from it. But tell me a little bit about the response that you got from Congress. What was your main goal in speaking with them? And do you think you're going to get it?\par \par \uc2 ANASTASIA RADINA, MEMBER OF UKRANIAN PARLIAMENT: The main goal was to arch the U.S. together with partners to actually provide more lethal weapons to Ukraine. We believe that our only option to establish peace in Ukraine is to win, and in order to win, we need to be getting some military assistance for not getting before. Now, we are not asking for boots on the ground, but we are asking for proper surface to air, air defense systems. We are asking for jets. We're asking for tanks. We are asking for heavy, lethal weaponry that will give us an opportunity to receive military superiority and to actually clear Russian soldiers of our land, defend our lands, and then be able to have meaningful negotiations with Russia because one cannot negotiate with a gun at his forehead and this is exactly what Russia is doing right now: bombarding our cities when it is claiming to withdraw its troops.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I'm curious what your sense was from members of Congress that you spoke with? And do you think that they want to give more lethal aid and when you talk to the Pentagon, they say: We are sending things in at a clip that we have never done before, where they -- were actually sending a lot very quickly. So, who do you think -- do you think it's the White House? Is it Congress? Where do you think that the pullback, where the resistance is coming from?\par \par \uc2 RADINA: We are hearing commitment to support with Ukraine and we are hearing that the U.S. is going to stand with Ukraine until the end. For us the end is victory. But we are urging to make this hell happen right now. The sense of urgency is that we actually needed this weaponry yesterday, we needed that the day before yesterday to help those people starving right now in Mariupol. We are very much afraid of another round of discussions; we are hearing that some things are on the table.\par \par \uc2 But our message is that things on the table do not help on the grounds that we need to bet on the grounds now. We need the West to actually stand in those values of freedom, in those values of international order and democracy and international humanitarian law and to stand in action to do more, to show that the West is really strong, strongly standing in action, in support of the values that are being proclaimed. Because this is Ukraine, who is now fighting for these values. This is Ukraine, who is now fighting to protect \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory against more crazy dictator.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, just on a specific story level, we keep hearing about these thousands, some of them children who have been, in President Zelenskyy's words, kidnapped from Ukraine and brought to Russia. And yet we're not hearing specifics about who these children are or where their parents are. Is there anything that you can do to shed light on what's going on in that situation, which sounds absolutely harrowing to everyone here in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 RADINA: The situation is actually horrible. What we know is that Russia, two people, two kids from bombarded in hospital in Mariupol, separated them from their parents and kidnap those kids to Russia. And we are now trying to trace the destinies of this kids and were not very successful. Again, Russia, kidnapped Ukrainian kids, separated them from their families, and is keeping them in Russia as hostages, as part of their genocide war against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Anastasia, if you have, I can't even imagine what those parents are going through, and we haven't heard from them. If you get any information about that story, I hope that you will contact us and give it to us because it's an absolutely terrifying situation. And we would like to share that story with more people if we can. So, thank you, Anastasia.\par \par \uc2 RADINA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, let's bring in Republican Congressman Dan Crenshaw of Texas, also a retired Navy SEAL. Congressman, great to have you with us today. You know, you listen to Anastasia, you listen to the desperation in her voice in terms of what they need. And you know, you listen to President Zelenskyy who has been great at saying we're so grateful to you. But we absolutely need more. What do you think about where we are right now on day 37?\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN CRENSHAW (R-TX): I think what's frustrating is there is widespread bipartisan agreement in the Congress that they need more and that we want to deliver more. And you know, you asked that question: where, where's the speed bump? And I think it's the administration. I don't think they're aggressive enough. I think they're making excuses for not getting weapons and a delivered fast enough. They said the 800 million would be delivered within the next two weeks. Well, why was it? Why was it delivered in the next few days?\par \par \uc2 There's not a lot of really good reasoning that they're providing to us as to why that's happening. It seems like it's bureaucratic, malas, and that's a problem. I mean, their explanation for not letting for getting in the way of Poland giving Ukraine MIGs. It was, it was highly insufficient. I mean, it talked about us being too escalatory. And that, well, you know, MiGs aren't that great. So, why do they need them? Well, they're better than nothing. And guess what the escalation has already happened. So, I think the administration needs to step up here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Congressman, we have breaking news coming from this press briefing. And I just want to mention that Zelenskyy said -- thank you very much, Congressman Crenshaw. I wish we had more time with you. He said one of his greatest fears is that Americans will lose interest in what is happening, which I think is an important point here. Let's listen into this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Second on Title 42, the announcement that it's going to be lifted on May 23rd, can you talk a little bit about why the restrictions aren't lifted sooner than May 23rd, perhaps even immediately people who want to come to the country to seek asylum will not have to wait seven weeks to do so when the CDC says that restriction is no longer necessary.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Absolutely. Well, it is always been the case and continues to be the case. This is a health directive determined by the CDC. So, let me start there. But I would also say that implementation of their decision requires an interagency process specifically leadership of the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I would note the statement that was put out by Secretary Mayorkas and it requires the implementation of appropriate COVID-19 mitigation protocols, also scaling up capacity and scaling up, you know, resources needed. And so, it was always going to be important to have an implementation period and the timeline reflects that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Next question. Is there any comment on the vote in Staten Island by \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers to --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, the president was glad to see workers ensure their voices are heard with respect to important workplace decisions. He believes firmly that every worker in every state must have a free and fair choice to join a union and the right to bargain collectively with their employer. The \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in Staten Island made their choice to organize a grassroots union and bargain for better jobs and a better life. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Just in light of the fuel depot fire in Russia. What is the U.S.'s general position on potential future attacks by Ukraine on Russian soil? Does the us believe that it is up to the Ukrainians to decide whether such potential attacks are justified? Or does it generally believe that those kinds of attacks should be discouraged because they would be seen as being escalatory?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure. Well, given we have not confirmed or commented specifically on the reports from here, and neither has Ukraine, I'm not going to get into a future hypothetical. What I would just reiterate, again, is that this is a war of aggression by the Russian leadership, by President Putin, that has left millions of people displaced homeless, has targeted civilians, hospitals, and other innocent people across Ukraine. We know who the aggressor is, that is President Putin and Russia and beyond that I don't have any comment on military tactics.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The President said yesterday that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  appears to be self-isolating that he appears to have maybe fired or put under house arrest certain advisors. Does the U.S. have a clearer sense of which advisors are currently advising Putin and who has his ear?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, the President was speaking yesterday to public reporting. So, we don't have more details to speak to at this point, point in time, but I would say, to go back to a couple of days ago, and the information that was put out by the administration, that was reflected that came through our intelligence agencies.\par \par \uc2 What we do know is that this war is not going how President Putin had planned that his intention of winning a quick war, defeating Ukraine, that Ukrainians quickly is not how it is played out. We have seen troops be demoralized; we have seen troops run out of equipment that they need on the Russian side, and that is clearly not what he had planned. In terms of who is advising him, I don't have more specifics on that front from here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And just one more on a separate topic. You're obviously not the only person in the administration recently to test positive for COVID. Can you give us a sense of whether there have been discussions lately at the White House about what would happen if President Biden were to test positive for COVID? Help us understand what working from home, quarantining, running the country would look like if the president were to get COVID?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I would first say that this wasn't exactly a question, but let me reiterate this point is that we take steps over and beyond CDC recommendations and guidelines here. I would note that, well, I tested positive, about 12 days ago, if my math is correct, I waited to return to the office until I had a negative test. And that is our protocol here or the protocol I was held to. Also, we take steps in advance of meeting the President, anyone who's meeting the President is going to have a test that day. And we also take social distancing steps in meetings when warranted.\par \par \uc2 In terms of look, we recognize that COVID-19 remains a transmissible disease, especially recent variants. And while I'm not going to get into a future hypothetical of any of our additional principles, since, since the, the second gentleman had tested positive testing positive, I would note that the President of the United States can work from anywhere can run the country from anywhere, and we have the capacity to not only prepare for that but to support anything that is needed. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thanks, Jen. Just a quick follow up on the Staten Island peril.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's obviously a big moment for the labor movement in this country, the first union at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . Has the president had a chance to speak to labor leaders? Or is he planning to issue a statement? Is he planning to talk about it at all?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I don't have anything planned to preview for you in terms of an official statement. I'm speaking, of course, on his behalf. Obviously, as you know, the president has been a longtime supporter of the right to organize, the right to -- for workers to organize and plan for better jobs and the better life. And certainly, my comments reflect that.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Jen, just a quick follow up -- just a comments from a senior Treasury official this morning that said, sort of the sanctions that have been imposed by the U.S. and its allies on Russia is sort of pushing Russia to become a closed economy. That is obviously presuming that, you know, China and others are not sort of making some of the materials that they get from the west available to them.\par \par \uc2 Has the administration noted any sort of change in stance when it comes to China, you know, and Russia? Or is it, you know, is there any evidence that they're providing, perhaps, military economic support?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Our assessment has not changed since our national security adviser spoke to this. What I would point out for you, too, as well, is the strong statements that came from European leaders, given their meeting on exactly this topic today, which certainly we share the sentiment. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Lots of urgent issues discussed here. But with the helicopter approaching, well over the House (INAUDIBLE). Is it true that you are leaving the White House to work for MSNBC?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, you can't get rid of me yet, Ed. I have nothing to confirm about my length of public service or plan service, or anything about consideration about next plans.\par \par \uc2 I'm very happy to be standing with all of you here today, after it felt like a never ending endless time in my basements quarantining away from my family. Believe it or not, I missed you a lot.\par \par \uc2 And my focus, every day continues to be speaking on behalf of the president, answering your questions, as tough as they may be, at many times. As difficult as they may be to answer at many times, and I hope that I meet my own bar of treating everybody with fairness and being equitable.\par \par \uc2 And just because this has been raised by our colleagues, but people who are observing this process, is it ethical for you to continue conducting this job while negotiating with the media?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I have always gone over and above the stringent ethical and legal requirements of the Biden administration. And I take that very seriously. And as a standard for every employee of the White House, I have received rigorous ethics counselling, including as it relates to any future employment, I've complied with all ethics requirements, and gone beyond and taken steps to recuse myself from decisions as appropriate.\par \par \uc2 And so, I hope that all of you -- I've been working with all of you for some time would judge me for my record, and how I treat all of you both in the Briefing Room and otherwise.\par \par \uc2 And I try to answer questions from everybody across the board. I know everybody in the back of the room may not always be pleased with me, but I try my best. And I will -- I will certainly continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: OK, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, NBC NEWS: Jen, given the reports, which have now been confirmed by the multiple media outlets, how can you continue to be an effective briefer if you do a back-up plans to join a media outlet?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I have nothing, again, to announce about any conversations or any future plans. And at whatever time I leave the White House, I can promise you, the first thing I'm going to do is sleep and spend time with my 3 and 6-year-olds, who are my most important audiences of all.\par \par \uc2 But I would say Kristen, that, again, I have done have taken the ethics legal requirements to the highest -- very seriously, in any discussions in any considerations about any future employment, just as any White House official would. And I've taken steps beyond that to ensure there is no conflicts.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And I understand what you're saying. But I guess the question is, how is it ethical to have these conversations with media outlets while you continue to have a job standing behind that video?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, there are a range of stringent ethical and legal requirements that are imposed on everybody in this administration and many administrations past about any conversations you're having with future employers.\par \par \uc2 That is true of any industry you're working in. And I have abided by those and tried to take steps to go beyond that as well.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And broadly speaking, is it the policy of this White House to allow staffers to have discussions even if, indirectly, with institutions that impact and affect their jobs and your job here?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, there it is the policy of this White House to ensure that anyone who is having conversations about future employment does so through consultation with the White House Counsel's Office and ensuring they abide by any ethics and legal requirements. And those are conversations that I have taken very seriously and abided by every component of.\par \par \uc2 OK, so let me go screen and we can go and then we can wrap.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Speaker Jen, can you -- report set the U.S. --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But you said you missed us.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I typically -- OK. I know, and may be that people who may have to see the president depart, and I don't want to hold anyone back. But I also happy to answer your question and a couple of others if people want to stay.\par \par \uc2 But if people need to -- are they gathering right now? Sorry to interrupt your chain of thought here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: OK. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can you confirm reports of the U.S. has been providing Ukraine with protective equipment, such as gas masks, hazmat, and other materials, because of possible chemical weapons attack from Russia?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, the United States and members of the international community have, of course, repeatedly warned about the potential for Russia to use chemical or biological weapons in Ukraine, and that Moscow is possibly planning a false flag operation.\par \par \uc2 In an effort to assist our Ukrainian partners, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is providing the Government of Ukraine with life-saving equipment and supplies that could be deployed in the event of Russia -- Russian use of a chemical and biological weapon against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It does not compromise our domestic preparedness in any way, shape, or form just for everybody's awareness. But we are providing it as we are providing a range of materials and equipment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Just one quick question on other subject, the House passed the bill today that would remove marijuana from the federal schedule of controlled substances. Every Democrat saved for two, voted in favor of this bill.\par \par \uc2 Does the president support the legislation?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first, let me say that, as the president said during the campaign, our current marijuana laws are now working. He agrees that we need to rethink our approach including to address the racial disparities and systemic -- OK -- inequities in our criminal justice system. Broaden research on the effects of marijuana and support the safe use of marijuana for medical purposes.\par \par \uc2 We look forward to working with Congress to achieve our shared goals. And we'll continue having discussions with them about this objective.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, Jen.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: OK, I'm going to wrap up in a second here. And I'll go to (INAUDIBLE). OK.\par \par \uc2 OK. So, there, you guys got to wrap?\par \par \uc2 I'm sure we will get to a -- let me -- let us get you a thorough comments on that. I will say as the president conveyed in his announcement yesterday, certainly working with the global community to ensure we are meeting the supply shortages in the global community at this time is part of our efforts to reduce gas prices for Americans, given its global oil market.\par \par \uc2 So, as part of that, and we will get to a more comprehensive. I am here and in my office, because of core question --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Jen, can I -- just a quick question on inflation.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Today, the president blamed Putin's invasion of Ukraine for not just higher gas prices, but also higher food prices. Inflation was at 7.4 percent in January before the invasion. In February, it when up 7.9 percent. Putin didn't invade until the 24th.\par \par \uc2 So, March is really going to show the impact of the invasion. That report doesn't even come out until April 12. So, how are people supposed to believe the Putin price hike is to blame for food prices going up when the timeline doesn't add up?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, here is what the president is reflecting on. One, the price of gas has gone up by approximately $1 or more, since Putin started lining up troops at the border. This is something that outside economists have spoken to as well, not just the administration. And those are just factual details about how much the price of gas has gone up. We know that's a huge impact.\par \par \uc2 And when you say inflation, people think the cost on their pocketbook and the impact on their budgets. The second piece on the availability of food, we know that different markets around the world are impacted by the lack of production in Ukraine and other -- because of the war, and we know that, that could impact global food prices.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That doesn't happen yet. This is the first time we've heard the president blame Putin for higher food prices.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I think what the president is looking at is what the impact has been in a lot of areas that are leading to price increases on people's pocketbooks and where we could see it increasing over the course of time.\par \par \uc2 I'm here in my office. I see you all. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who will replace you?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, interesting press briefing with \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  just wrapping up there. It's the first since she recovered from COVID. And it also comes amid reports that she will soon be leaving the White House to join MSNBC and you heard a number of questions pressing her on whether or not it's ethical to continue in this role.\par \par \uc2 While those talks are going on, she said that she has basically dotted all the Is and crossed all the Ts where that's concerned. But no doubt, that will get more attention in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 So, the briefing started with a number of questions about the president and his top economic advisors, and the inflation numbers, and plans to release oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, the president once again blamed Russia for rising prices. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We need to do more to get prices under control. Putin's invasion of Ukraine has driven up gas prices and food prices all over the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Joining me now, "YOUR WORLD" anchor, Neil Cavuto, watching all of this along with us today.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hello, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Obviously, there's a lot to watch. In terms of the economy. We had those numbers that came out this morning, the jobs numbers, rising prices that the White House continues to blame on Putin. And there's some blame there to be sure, but it was already on -- in that direction before this happened. So, what really stands out to you today? What do you thinking about?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it's a very strong report. But what's interesting in it, you know, this is the 11th month in a row, Martha, we've had better than 400,000 jobs added to the economy. So, it's a blistering pace, where we're now net down from the COVID heights about 1-1/2 million jobs.\par \par \uc2 No, no, no, it's about one and a half million jobs away from recouping all the jobs that were lost during COVID. So, that's the good news.\par \par \uc2 The bad news is this inflation stuff, because tucked in the report is some very promising developments on the wage front, Americans wages have gone up about 5-1/2 -- 5.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, inflation's got up at about an eight percent clip. So, whatever they're making, they're paying out of these higher costs. So, that is a big problem here.\par \par \uc2 And we're already seeing sides, Martha, that affecting people, not only their psyche, and their confidence about where things are going, but they're shopping. In the latest consumer spending period, it moved up all at about two-tenths of a percent in February. That was one-tenth the pace it was running at just the month before.\par \par \uc2 So, clearly, the impact of inflation is being felt, and we're seeing it in some of these sort of what I call the under numbers that are under reported.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Here is Claire McCaskill, the former senator from Missouri this morning, talking about what she sees as the difficulty that all of this presents for her party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CLAIRE MCCASKILL (D), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: The Democrats are really in a bind here, because everything is good in the economy, except what people remember most.\par \par \uc2 And that is what was their checkout total at the grocery store yesterday, and what did they pay at the pump two days ago?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think the impact of that is? You know, when you look at the midterms, in the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, sort of front of -- in the front window at this point, Neil?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you know, I mean, she raises a very good point. It's what you see. It's not only the gas prices you see every time you drive by one or trying to fill up your tank.\par \par \uc2 But what you're seeing again, that go way beyond just numbers, it's one thing to talk about inflation running at about an eight percent clip, but depending on what you buy. It's gone up markedly.\par \par \uc2 In the first quarter alone, consider this, oil was up about 33 percent. Gas was up at about a 25 percent rate. Wheat, so, that being cereal prices running ahead at about a 31 percent clip. Corn, 26 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, depending on what you like, and I -- you might be shocked to hear this Martha, I like pasta. And so, all of a sudden pasta prices have effectively they doubled.\par \par \uc2 And now, there are a lot of people say, oh, you can get the store brand Neil, and you can find ways to pivot around that. But there are fewer pivoting options right now.\par \par \uc2 And that's what takes people off, because it used to be, all right, pork prices are rising, I'm going to get chicken. Chicken prices started rising, I'm going to get Turkey. Turkey prices started rising, I'm going to get tofu.\par \par \uc2 By the way, no amount of increases in those things could have me move to tofu.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- a tofu pasta.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But my point is that people are tired of the pivot. They're really tired of that pivoting, and they're feeling that.\par \par \uc2 So, the irony is we do have an underlying strong economy, there's no doubt about that. No doubt about these jobs gains, but we are seeing the first cracks here, where people are saying enough, I can't deal with this.\par \par \uc2 And that could be a problem down the road because it's a self-fulfilling prophecy. In other words, you get concerned about something, you retreat, you hold back, you're not inclined to buy as much, and buy anything at all.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing that in some department store numbers, we're seeing that in some guidance, we've gotten out a host of retailers saying numbers are good now, but we're worried going forward.\par \par \uc2 You don't like to hear that, but we're worried going forward part.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and we -- you know, obviously once inflation digs in, it's very hard to shake it out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it's like getting sand in your shoes. And you know, you think you shook it all out, but it's still there months later.\par \par \uc2 So, enjoy your pasta this weekend, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Pasta is pretty reasonable. Pasta is always a good choice.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's always fine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Only when it hits $50 a box, that's when I'll stop. But it hasn't hit that yet. But right now, that's a worry. That's a big worry.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No. No, thank God. Thank God.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Neil, thank you. Great to see you my friend.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you very much, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): So, what we are learning right now, getting some new information about these reported airstrikes in Russia on an oil depot and the Ukrainian counteroffensive against Putin's forces. You know, both sides are blaming the other for what happened here. So, we don't know whether or not Ukrainians attack something inside Russia, but obviously, that would be a huge problem.\par \par \uc2 General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2  joins us next on why he says perilous times are clearly ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, breaking moments ago, Ukraine President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  speaking with our own Bret Baier in an exclusive interview that will air tonight. And here is part of that interview that we just got in. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT, UKRAINE (through translator): Today, another decision was made regarding anti-heroes. I do not have time to deal with all the traitors but they will gradually all be punished. Those high- ranking servicemen who have been prevented from deciding where their homeland is who break the military oath of allegiance to the Ukrainian people for the protection of our state, its freedom, and independence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, that obviously was from earlier today. That's \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  talking about some traitors in the ranks. And we're going to play the other sought for you in just a moment. But all of this comes as this incredible video has emerged of Russia and Ukraine, a Ukrainian hit potentially on a Russian oil depot.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, this has both sides giving accusations back and forth to the other about who did this. But this is a hit inside Russian territory, so it's a serious issue that has developed here. So, in moments, we're going to talk to General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2 . And when we do that, we'll play the soundbite from President Zelenskyy with Bret. But first let's go to National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin who's live at the Pentagon for us. Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, this oil depot strike that you're mentioning does not have the hallmarks of a Russian false flag operation based on my reporting. And if you look at Russia's reaction moments ago, mostly silence today, and tonight saying that they would now reinforce their western borders.\par \par \uc2 This was a shot across the bow at Putin telling him that his forces are porous and not safe from Ukrainian attacks. The fireball that erupted in the night sky would have also been a nice message to that effect. An oil depot needed for resupply to his forces that are already strained. The Pentagon is remaining mum on whether or not it was a pair of Mi-24 Russian- made helicopter gunships that fired on the oil depot 30 miles inside of Russia whether they were Ukrainian or not.\par \par \uc2 Satellite imagery from the area has been shrouded in cloud cover were told. Ukrainian officials would not confirm either that Ukraine Special Operations Forces were behind the strike, which would be the first known attack inside Russia by Ukrainian forces since the start of the war on February 24.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DMYTRO KULEBA, FOREIGN MINISTER, UKRAINE: I can neither confirm nor reject the claim that Ukraine was involved in this simply because I did not possess all the military information.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The U.S. is facing criticism from members of Ukraine's parliament and members of Congress that it is not sending enough lethal aid into Ukraine fast enough.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANASTASIA RADINA, MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT, UKRAINE: We are asking to abandon this artificial distinction between offensive and defensive weapons. We are defending our lands. In our situation, all types of weapons are defensive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voiceover): The Pentagon pushed back on the idea that U.S. weapons and security assistance shipments to Ukraine have slowed. In fact, Press Secretary John Kirby said weapons began flowing within days of the President signing off on the recent $850 million in lethal aid.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, THE PENTAGON: From the time he signed the order to the first shipment going on its way, it was like four days, I've never seen the Department of Defense be able to move with this sense of alacrity and speed as I have in just the last few weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Meanwhile, Russia has redirected 2000 Russian troops based in Georgia as part of its occupation since 2007. Another sign that Russia has lost more troops in Ukraine than it would like to admit. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon, thank you very much, Jennifer,\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now, Four Star Retired General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2 , a former commander of U.S. forces in Afghanistan and the former director of the CIA, now chairman of the KKR Global Institute. General, very good to have you with us today. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 I want to get your thoughts on this potential Ukrainian hit inside of Russia on that oil depot. And you heard Jennifer's reporting on what the Pentagon thinks as they look at this, although we don't have confirmation yet. General Petraeus. Can you hear me OK?\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 DAVID PETRAEUS\uc2  (RET), FORMER DIRECTOR, CIA: I can hear you. And I thought you're going to say that you wanted to hear one more report. But no, look, how can Russia be outraged if there is an attack just inside its territory on a fuel depot in Russia has been actively attacking fuel depots throughout Ukraine since the very beginning of the invasion?\par \par \uc2 They have destroyed civil and military infrastructure without any distinction just as they have struck military facilities and civilian facilities throughout the country. So, the idea that there should be outrage if indeed these two Mi-24 attack helicopters turned out to be Ukrainian is just ludicrous. It's nonsensical. And it is a continuation, of course, the kind of disinformation that Russia has sought to sow ever since the beginning of the invasion.\par \par \uc2 But you know, Martha, this is a really eventful week. This is the week that saw Ukraine win the Battle of Kyiv. This is the week that saw the Russians withdraw about 20 percent of the forces that were attacking Kyiv because they've been rendered combat ineffective. In other words, they can no longer accomplish their mission without reconstitution, without being filled back up with people and weapon systems.\par \par \uc2 And so, they're pulling those out. We believe that they're going to push those over and come into Ukraine from the east. And this is why I mentioned that this is a perilous moment as well as really a very positive moment. Again, no question that the Russians will ever take Kyiv now, and they've even pushed the artillery out of range of the downtown area.\par \par \uc2 And they're doing that to the east side of Kyiv as well, making progress outside Kharkiv, other cities, never going to let the Russians get to Odessa in the southwest. But yes, there's the concern about the southeast. The Russians helping the separatists push out from the so-called Donbas, the areas that the Russian separatists controlled within Donetsk and Luhansk Oblast.\par \par \uc2 And then also ultimately, eventually, going to take Mariupol, the embattled coast city in the southeast that is very important as well, which has become the Ukrainian Alamo, fighting to the last defender, occupying lots of Russian battalions. But when that does eventually fall, those battalions will be freed up. They can turn to the northeast. These new forces that are augmenting are going to come in from the east.\par \par \uc2 And this is why there's this urgency in President Zelenskyy and his government to get all that they can possibly get. Now, look, the U.S. has provided extraordinary amounts of weapons systems and supplies, so has other -- a number of other \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries. The problem at this point in time is that a lot of what they really want, we don't have.\par \par \uc2 We don't have the Russian caliber or the Eastern bloc heavy artillery pieces, or the rounds for it, or the multiple launch rocket systems, or some of the other Eastern bloc systems that they need, the F-300 Air Defense System which I believe our Patriot is put into Slovakia to free that up.\par \par \uc2 So, again, I think that there's been a very extraordinary job done, terrific bipartisan support by Congress to get everything in there. And just the sheer numbers in the first week alone, remember, it was 17,000 man-portable anti-tank guided missiles and 3000 man-portable air defense systems, the Stinger in that one week, and they've continued to flow.\par \par \uc2 Now, the one that we should be pressing for is the Switchblade drone. A soldier pops it up out of a -- out of a tube, it loiters. It's essentially a loitering munition, 15 or 30 minutes depending on the size of it. The operator picks a target, it dives, it blows itself up on that target. We ought to be getting thousands of those to Ukraine, not the 100 in the initial batch.\par \par \uc2 Those could be game-changers. And those could really augment these counter- attacks by the Ukrainian forces as they push further out of Kyiv, as they push further out of Kharkiv, as they oppose the Russian forces that are trying to push out of the southeast and also trying to entrap some of the Ukrainian forces that are defending against that push out of the southeast.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that's, again, the sense, the origin, the catalyst for this urgency. I agree with it. I've been a commander. You never have enough and you're always asking for more. And I think everyone is doing everything they can to provide that. And that includes our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies -- Germany, on the cost of providing an extraordinary amount of money and also of weapons and assistance.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Can we just quickly play this soundbite that we have of the interview with Zelenskyy and then -- we're short on time, General, but I just want to get a, you know, super quick reaction out of this if I can. Let's play that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Short of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , short of Article Five protection, you're saying a group of nations provides security guarantees. Has President Biden offered the U.S. as one of those countries?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY (through translator): It's hard for us to talk about \uc2 NATO\uc2  because \uc2 NATO\uc2  doesn't want to admit us. I think it's a mistake, because if we join \uc2 NATO\uc2 , we would make \uc2 NATO\uc2  much stronger. We are not a weak state. We are not proposing to make stronger at the expense of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . We are not an addition. We are the locomotive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Genera, your reaction to that.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: Well, Martha, look, you have to have sympathy for that. You have to, you know, feel, again, enormous amount of empathy for the -- for the idea. The problem is that his negotiators themselves put forward on the negotiating table that Ukraine would be neutral.\par \par \uc2 Now, it would have a security guarantee, but not as part of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and that's a very significant distinction. That's what his negotiators put forward to Russia. I'd love to see us be one of the guarantors of that. I think it's a bit premature to talk about that just yet.\par \par \uc2 The negotiators did say they want \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  permanent members. That limits it pretty distinctly. You're now talking about at our side the U.S., U.K., France, because the other two China and Russia, I presume, would not be the guarantors they're seeking.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: But I think again, yes, that that construct I think makes sense. The idea of \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership I think is one that probably that ship has sailed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it sounds a little bit like what they were supposed to get with the Budapest Agreement which pretty much fell apart in terms of protection of Ukraine's ex-NATO.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: And Russia was the guarantor.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, General. Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: That was part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: You bet, Martha. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, General. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So, when we come back, the woke World of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , that is the aim of some of the top executives at the company exposed in this video obtained by reporter Christopher Rufo. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVIAN WARE, INCLUSION MANAGER, DISNEY DIVERSITY: Our interpretation may be presenting as female that they may not want to be called princess. So, let's think differently about how do we really engage with our guests.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Columnist Karol Markowicz quit New York to move to Florida. And now she's quitting \uc2 Disney\uc2 . She'll be up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Disney\uc2  wants really woke movies for your kids. And they are saying that themselves. They don't want to be so woke though when it comes to doing business in countries like Egypt and Saudi Arabia where just being gay can put you in prison or worse. But they don't seem to have a problem with that because they're expanding their business into all of those countries.\par \par \uc2 This video was obtained by reporter Christopher Rufo and it blew the lid off this story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LATOYA RAVENEAU, EXECUTIVE PRODUCER, \uc2 DISNEY\uc2 : Our leadership over there has been so welcoming to like my like, not at all secret gay agenda.\par \par \uc2 WARE