e like canonical trans characters, canonical asexual characters, canonical bisexual characters, is to give them stories where they can like be their whole selves.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): With these people out in California who are working very high up in this company, the stuff coming out of their mouth is unbelievable that they view their programming as a way to inject a lot of these topics into programming for very young kids.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voiceover): So, this morning, America's largest gay and transgender rights group has set up a hotline for teens, parents, and teachers who feel this law is being used against them. They also want the federal government to get involved. Yesterday, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said, "Transgender youth deserve love and respect."\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, Martha, right now, this is \uc2 Disney\uc2  headquarters. Down the street is the animation studio. Of course, the battle lines are basically in Florida, but this is divided people around the country and neither side is backing down. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: William, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, New York Post Columnist Karol Markowicz who recently moved from New York to Florida. And now, she says she is quitting \uc2 Disney\uc2 . Karol, good to have you with us. You wrote an interesting piece on this. And you know, I think a lot of people are asking questions about \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I own some shares of \uc2 Disney\uc2 . I think a lot of Americans do because it's in the Dow 30 Industrial Average. But you're making -- you're making a choice here. Tell me why.\par \par \uc2 KAROL MARKOWICZ, COLUMNIST, NEW YORK POST: Yes, it doesn't feel great to have to cut out a storied American company. My kids love \uc2 Disney\uc2 . I love \uc2 Disney\uc2 . We had a family reunion at Disney World, you know, in 2019 with our whole family.\par \par \uc2 We see it as a destination. We see their movies as part of our lives. But this company has declared themselves aligned with the far left and very seriously at war with American families. American families don't want messaging to young kids about gender identity. It's just not their place. And so, I think that \uc2 Disney\uc2  has made a giant mistake here. And they're really going to see that as families make other choices as for their entertainment.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And there are a lot of other choices that they can make. It's interesting, you know, a lot of this conversation, this Zoom meeting was sparked by this law that is called the don't gay -- Don't Say Gay Law by people who don't like it. But in fact, the words Don't Say Gay or don't use that term are not in this law.\par \par \uc2 Here's a teacher in Florida talking about -- I believe he's in Florida. Yes, he is. Talking about why he is so opposed to the passage of this law, which I should just mention upfront, doesn't allow discussion about sexual gender issues up until fourth grade. Let's play this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORY BERNAERT, FLORIDA TEACHER: In order to build relationships, you've talked about your home life, you talk about what you do on the weekends. That's building community. It scares me to death that I'm not going to be able to have these conversations with my children because they're going to ask me what I did on the weekend. I don't want to have to hide that my partner and I went paddleboarding this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, he doesn't want to have to hide talking to his little kindergarteners about going paddleboarding with his husband over the weekend. What do you think about that, Karol?\par \par \uc2 MARKOWICZ: There's absolutely nothing in this law that prohibits him from telling the class that he went paddleboarding with his husband. The law specifically says instruction, OK. So, now, if he takes that paddleboarding conversation and turns it into here's a book about, you know, gender identity, and you could be a boy but you can be a girl tomorrow and all of this is really available to you. Yes, that's not allowed.But to say I went paddleboarding with my husband over the weekend, absolutely, he's allowed to say that. Nothing in the law prohibits that.\par \par \uc2 You know, I had the experience today of telling someone who hadn't heard about the law what was in it, and they were shocked that such a thing was even necessary. Who is talking to kindergarten, first grade, second grade, or third grade about gender identity or sexual orientation?\par \par \uc2 But the truth is I've gotten messages from parents all over the country that it is happening in their schools and they don't like it and they want it stopped.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the other hypocrisy here is this, you know, expansion of \uc2 Disney\uc2  +, an aggressive marketing of their products into places like Algeria and Egypt? I think we have a map. Libya, Morocco, where it's illegal to be gay and where the punishments vary from prison to worse, as I said.\par \par \uc2 So, why aren't these teachers -- I'm sorry --why aren't these executives at \uc2 Disney\uc2  speaking out about what's going on in these countries where they want to work and do business?\par \par \uc2 MARKOWICZ: Yes, absolutely. There's nothing in -- I believe that \uc2 Disney\uc2  does not care about LGBT at all. This is just, you know, sort of aligned with the far left. And the far left is not asking for them to pull out of these countries, which is really telling to me also.\par \par \uc2 They ultimately don't care. This is a political -- you know, something that could beat Governor DeSantis with and that's what this is all about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Karol, thank you. You know, I think everybody's had their eyes open to so many different things. And you do have to dig into this law and look at what it actually says. And I don't think there's a lot that's really objectionable when you really dig into what this law is talking about.\par \par \uc2 We appreciate you being here and we always enjoy your columns. Thank you, Karol.\par \par \uc2 MARKOWICZ: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I don't know about you but March Madness will be taking up part of my weekend. It's now been whittled down to the final four teams tomorrow night. First up, you've got the Villanova Wildcats, our personal favorite. We have a lot of family members that attended Villanova, including my daughter. And you got -- they're up against Kansas.\par \par \uc2 So, the Duceys are split there. You've got Peter at Villanova, Steve at Kansas. So, that's an issue for them. There's also the division between Duke and UNC on the other side of this. So, it's a great matchup. And we look forward to watching all that.\par \par \uc2 Oh, look, there they are. There they are. Coach Jay Wright can do no wrong. I understand that mentality.\par \par \uc2 OK, so, the other thing that I'm doing this weekend is "FOX NEWS SUNDAY." So, I will be hosting "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" and I look forward to seeing all of you on Sunday, watch some basketball. We'll get together and have a great show on Sunday morning. So, I hope you will join me there.\par \par \uc2 Have a great evening, everybody. And I will see you Sunday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220402ei4100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 ; COVID-19 Asylum Limits At Southern Border To End May 23; U.S. Adds 431,000 Jobs Amid Surging Inflation; Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  Interviewed On Ongoing Russian Invasion Of Ukraine; Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  Continues To Call For Weapons From Allies To Help Repel Russian Invasion; Analysts Examine Possibility Russia May Consolidate Gains In Eastern Ukraine; U.S. Jobs Report For March Shows 431,000 Jobs Added And 3.6 Unemployment Rate; Despite Strong Economy, Inflation Hurting President Biden In Polling\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Juan Williams \par \uc2 6,547 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we will have an in-depth exclusive interview with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  from Kyiv in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Today, Russian officials said Ukraine conducted its first strike on Russian territory since the war began. The Kremlin saying the attack on a fuel depot is not conducive to peace talks. Those negotiations resumed today via video link. I will ask President Zelenskyy about that attack and about those peace talks in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian military says it has retaken control of 29 settlements and the Chernobyl nuclear site where Russian troops are pulled back.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, authorities in the southern city of Mariupol say it is not possible to enter the besieged city and that it's dangerous for people to try to leave on their own.\par \par \uc2 Now, a look headlines in the U.S., news from the southern border. Three high level officials -- federal officials, sources telling FOX News more than 62,000 so-called known getaways, illegal immigrants, slipped past the border in March. That averages out to more than 2,000 per day. Sources say there have been more than 300,000 since October 1st. Known getaways are migrants seen on cameras and sensors but not apprehended due to a lack of manpower.\par \par \uc2 These figures come as officials warn of an even greater surge of illegal immigration when the administration ends its coronavirus related asylum restrictions on May 23rd. The official announcement of the repeal of Title 42 was put out today by the CDC.\par \par \uc2 Also, in that release, a notice that the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  will provide an initial dose of a COVID-19 vaccine to up to 6,000 migrants a day at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy added 431,000 jobs in March. That helped shrink the unemployment rate to 3.6 percent. It's the lowest rate since the pandemic began and just about half century level 3.5 percent that was reached two years ago.\par \par \uc2 But inflation remains a major concern for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and obviously for families across the country. We'll have the panel discussion on that later.\par \par \uc2 On the news, the Dow was up 140 today. The S&P 500 gained 15, the Nasdaq rose 41. For the week, the Dow lost a tenth of a percentage point. The S&P 500 was up 0.06. The Nasdaq gained two thirds.\par \par \uc2 President Biden touted the economic outlook while still blaming Putin for surging inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Even though we created record number of jobs we know -- I know that this job is not finished. We need to do more to get prices under control. Putin's invasion of Ukraine has driven up gas prices and food prices all over the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, to our exclusive interview with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. President, I'd like to start with this. How do you believe this war ends?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): Only with our victory, a victory of truth means victory of Ukraine and Ukrainians. The question is when it will end. That is a deep question. It's a painful question. Besides victory, the Ukrainian people will not accept any outcome.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What are you willing to agree to to get a peace deal? For example, are you willing to trade any amount of Ukrainian territory to achieve a peace deal with Russia?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: We do not trade our territory. So the question of territorial integrity and sovereignty is out of discussion. There are difficult questions regarding temporarily occupied territory.\par \par \uc2 There are principal difficulties. We are ready to put off some issues of the agenda like the Crimean issue. We have prepared to about having the Crime an issue, the occupation of Crimea should be a resolved in a diplomatic format, maybe like 10 years later.\par \par \uc2 Globally, the issue needs to be resolved such as the end of warfare and then diplomacy steps in. Diplomatically, we can find a solution because Ukraine is a peaceful, peace-loving nation, and I said this in answering to your first question. It is -- what matters most to save as many lives of our people as possible.\par \par \uc2 We do need guarantees of security from leading nations. And the Russian Federation understands that we may have argument about this or that country, but among the key states, they have to be guarantors and those can be members of \uc2 NATO\uc2  or those outside it. And we are talking about key players, considered key players of the world.\par \par \uc2 But once again, I'm saying that we want those countries that want it join this openly. In addition to the U.S., Great Britain, Germany, France, Turkey, why I named this list, this is not all of them. We would like to have China join it.\par \par \uc2 We understand that Russia needs to find a place in that agreement as well because it will be an agreement between Ukraine and Russia so that -- because they are a part of this war, they are subject of this aggression, invasion, and they need to be bound by that treaty.\par \par \uc2 And I know that several different countries want to be a member of this alliance, among the security guarantors for Ukraine. I know Israel and Ireland, et cetera. But for now, I only named those countries which have already said that they are prepared to be guarantors or those who we contacted and already exchanged some -- early drafts of the documents, about them being guarantors (ph).\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, short of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , short of Article 5 protection, you're saying a group of nations provide security guarantees. Has President Biden offered the U.S. as one of those countries?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: We talked with President Biden. We talked with him quite often, often enough. We had a conversation yesterday.\par \par \uc2 We are saying that Ukraine would like to see among the leaders of those guarantors, those who are joining this important settlement process, we would like to see the United States led by the president among those leaders.\par \par \uc2 And our part, we received a message that the United States are considering this proposition. They are considering it in a positive way.\par \par \uc2 We want to see specific guarantees. What will be our future if we now make an agreement with Russia but two years later, they come back with another war? What will happen?\par \par \uc2 Not in the next three weeks or a week, but on -- or a year, we want to know what will happen within the first hours of a new war if American intelligence says there may be an invasion starting in January? Then what - - will there be prevention sanctions? Will there be pressure on those individuals who push for escalation? What will be further steps regarding weapons? Regarding troops?\par \par \uc2 There are many questions. And we would like to have answers even if not much but some specific answers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. President, what do you think \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  wants?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  wants the worlds and Western countries -- what they give to him. Then, appetites usually grow. I think the president of the Russian Federation is not an exception if you look in the historical times. And many people have such appetites grow. The more you give them, the more their appetite grows, in terms of territories.\par \par \uc2 So, those appetites keep growing, and then you're surrounding keep saying, believe me, now is the time to take this step that surrounding usually create some kind of information bubble around the leader. And now, you live in that information environment that they created for you.\par \par \uc2 When you begin to live in such a world, then that's -- you get such information and then you try to undertake certain solutions which are terrible. Not civilized. Not democratic but some kind of authoritarian.\par \par \uc2 That's how they took Crimea. They took away our Donbas, and now, they want to take more of our territory. We will not let them because one can't do things like that. Someone has to stop it. If not us, then there will be others after us.\par \par \uc2 Europe, countries of Europe, the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , the former Soviet republics, and then, of course, Poland, Romania. Those are big plans.\par \par \uc2 So, that's what he wants. And he will get it unless he is stopped. They talk about the ambition to bring back the former Soviet countries into -- back into the Russian Federation, saying that their territory is much bigger than what they have, and it goes way outside of today's border of the Russian Federation.\par \par \uc2 So what he wants is his personal business. The question is about us. What we can allow him to get.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Our military experts believe that you want to build on recent successful Ukrainian counterattacks by expanding to kind of a counter offensive phase. That's why you're requesting all these advanced weapons, like long and mid-range air defense, combat aircraft, tanks, armored vehicles, counter battery radar, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Our people also believe that the Biden administration is resisting this in some way.\par \par \uc2 Is that true? Are they resisting that effort?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Regarding heavy weapons, everything is very simple. If the world is ready to tell the truth to their people, if we don't have heavy weapons, how can we defend ourselves? How can we fight against one of the strongest armies in the world?\par \par \uc2 Our military, our people, our soldiers are stronger than the Russian Federation. But they have a lot more weapons. So these forces are not equal and they will say, excuse me, you now have enough what you were given, and we cannot go and die for your territory.\par \par \uc2 These are serious challenges. Powerful weapons are needed to Ukrainians. Heavy weapons will give us an opportunity to talk with them at the negotiation table.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you believe that President Biden wants Ukraine to win or that President Biden and his administration fear Putin's reaction if Russia loses?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: I have faith that President Biden like any true American citizen believes and wants the truth to win. And the truth is on the side of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I believe that they want the values that make our nation closer to win. That's freedom. That's democracy, freedom of speech, freedom to choose, freedom to choose your dignified life, work, who to live with, who to love, how many children to have.\par \par \uc2 And I think that those values are close to every human being. We want simple peace for our nation. We don't want a million of quality bullet proof vests or some special brand helmets.\par \par \uc2 Just give us missiles. Give us airplanes. You cannot give us F-18, or F-19, or whatever you have. Give us the old Soviet planes. That's all. Give them into my hands, give me something to defend my country with, my state.\par \par \uc2 And if you want it and I believe that the United States, the people of the United States want that, but if this process gets longer, continues to be delayed, if we can see that the process of transferring those weapons is getting slower, then people begin to ask the question, is it really true? Maybe there is some game (ph) behind it. I don't want to believe that some partners of ours are playing games.\par \par \uc2 We want simple peace for our nation. We don't want a million of quality bullet proof vest or some special brand helmets. Just give us missiles. Give us airplanes.\par \par \uc2 You cannot give us F-18 or F-19 whatever you have. Give us the old soviet plane. That's all. Get them into my hands. Give me something to defend my country with, my state.\par \par \uc2 And if you want it and I believe that the United States, the people of the United States want that, but if this process gets longer, continues to be delayed, if we can see that this -- the process of transferring those weapons is getting slower, then people begin to ask the question. Is it really true?\par \par \uc2 Maybe there is some gain behind it. I don't want to believe that some -- some partners of ours are playing games.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if President Biden is fearing President Putin. I don't -- I believe that he doesn't. I want to believe that he doesn't because he is the leader of a big nation, a great nation.\par \par \uc2 I'm holding my fist -- that's our tradition. I don't know if you understand this standard. I hold my fist or cross my fingers so that this will never happen, that any president of the United States would fear Russia, because America for us was always a symbol of democracy, and I have faith that Ukraine is also now -- for Ukraine, you are also a symbol of freedom.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Did you order the attack into Russian soil, the Ukrainian helicopter attack on a Russian military depot across from Kharkiv, across the border in Russia?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: I'm sorry, I do not discuss any of my orders as commander-in- chief, the leader of this state, and there are things which I only share with the military armed forces of Ukraine and when they talk with me.\par \par \uc2 You need to understand that on that territory that you mentioned, you have to know, they were placing their shooting systems and firing those missiles themselves. And I know in some regions, this path to Mariupol, we have made a deal with the Russian Federation to open that corridor. They went, people went but then the Russians shot at them. And they said, no, we didn't shoot, but they controlled 140 kilometers of that road.\par \par \uc2 Our forces were not there, and they shot many of those convoys and Russians were saying, we ordered not to shoot.\par \par \uc2 So, in those temporary occupied territories, there are all kinds of things happening which do not balance. The military do looting, marauding -- it's not just Russian soldiers, we know that there are something Wagner Group fighters and there are Chechnya fighters, Chechnya guards, and some Syrians, all kinds of fighters there.\par \par \uc2 So what's happening there -- Russians don't -- might not even know fully what happens there.\par \par \uc2 What matters for us is that you and the whole world should know that we are country at war. We were attacked. That is what matters. That is the biggest tragedy.\par \par \uc2 But honestly, it's not professional to talk about it. They occupied our territory. They attacked us. It's been -- this war been going on for eight years. So, whatever happens in a certain situation, I -- it's hard for me to comment.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's history, Mr. President, on ceasefires, for example, is that he uses them to rebuild his forces, advance tactically. He always breaks the ceasefire, at least in the past, by initiating military action.\par \par \uc2 If, for example, Mariupol falls to the Russians, some observers believe that Putin may offer a ceasefire in order to stop your successful counter offenses and then have time to rebuild his forces. Would you accept a ceasefire?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Regarding an actual cease-fire, I don't think that it will be real until we have reached that -- an agreement with the leaders. Like I said, an agreement can only be made with guarantors of our security.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there may be an agreement about this or that regime for cease-fire for a day or two. For instance, to collect the wounded and the dead bodies of Ukrainian soldiers from Mariupol that's under blockade.\par \par \uc2 The President of Russia Federation may use that time in order to withdrawal his troops back to the east to strengthen his position there.\par \par \uc2 I am sure that such a strategy is likely to be considered by President Putin's military leaders. But if we do get a one-day cease-fire or some hours of cease-fire in order to be able to help our people, those people are waiting for our help. We don't care about the tactics of Russian Federation.\par \par \uc2 We will gladly accept such a cease-fire in order to help our people, that's number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, this strategy of taking Ukraine in three days or five days, this already a matter of the past. Let's not forget that it failed and we will stand defending us and we will be the first ones among those who want to finish the war diplomatically and we will always support it.\par \par \uc2 From the beginning we were speaking in favor of peace. But we are fighting on our territory.\par \par \uc2 But everybody must know that if we are pressed in a corner on our territory, we will respond the way we can in order to save as many, our lives as we can. We will answer at full strength.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And what's your final message for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: I wish him to watch television that is not Russian. To see what really is happening in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will have more with President Zelenskyy and his message in English to Americans who are watching as the war in Ukraine unfolds.\par \par \uc2 ****\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now for the second part of my interview with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your chief economic advisor says, since the invasion, February 24th, Russia oil exports have not fallen. In fact, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has received $19 billion from European countries for oil and gas. Russia's currency has shot back up in value.\par \par \uc2 Are the sanctions working or is Putin winning this -- the sanctions fight?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY (through translator): Sanctions definitely work. And Russia definitely is afraid of sanctions. It puts them out of comfort. It drops down their economy. But there's a question of how sanctions are working. We are showing and telling the United States and European leaders everybody must work together and quickly.\par \par \uc2 They must have an impact on the oligarchs, on the president of Russia and on all the parties and on the country in general.\par \par \uc2 The United States should continue working on this if the U.S. would like to have successful negotiations at the negotiation table with Ukraine. The nations of Europe and the world must feel that America is strong, and the sanctions must be appropriately strong.\par \par \uc2 If Ukraine is gone tomorrow, there will be a food crisis in the world. I was saying they'll be an artificial deficit of energy resources due to Nord Steam 2. So, the sanctions need to be pressed further.\par \par \uc2 U.S. congressmen, senators, all -- everybody must keep pressing and they must show that if you want to say that you're the leaders of this civilization, keep being that. Don't lose this initiative.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you seeing evidence that China is helping Russia militarily? Clearly, China is helping economically so is India, perhaps other countries. Do you have a message about that?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: I would like the Chinese People's Republic to be on our side but nevertheless it'll be their choice. Who can make an influence, who can impact this?\par \par \uc2 I think without direct talks, direct communication between the U.S. and the Chinese People's Republic, it will not be possible, it will be difficult to achieve the result that the world needs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A couple of quick things, if I could, Mr. President, reports are this morning that you fired two senior members of your national security team and you're quoted as a report saying, I do not have time to deal with all the traitors, but they will gradually be punished.\par \par \uc2 Is that true and can you tell us the circumstances of that?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Yes. I stripped them of their ranks.\par \par \uc2 One person was fired sometime earlier and today, he was stripped of his general rank. Another person was expelled in our Secret Service for the reasons that one of them is not inside this country right now.\par \par \uc2 And the other one was not at work, and was someplace else inside Ukraine at the time when the city was being occupied -- his city was being occupied by Russians. So I believe this was wrong and now I want our law enforcement to examine this further.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you know how many assassination attempts you've survived?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But there have been some?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: There are things which is difficult for me to count. My intelligence says there were such attempts, and we saw information about some arrivals of planes. There were other details.\par \par \uc2 But listen, I'm alive, I'm not wounded. I'm intact. So it's hard for me to talk about this. So many people have died in our country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to have you clear something up for us, and this is -- these reports about the Azov Battalion that is said to be Nazi affiliated organization operating as a militia in your country, said to be committing their own atrocities.\par \par \uc2 What should Americans know about that unit, about those reports?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: So, Azov was one of those many battalions. They are what they are, they were defending our country. And later, I want to explain to you. Everything from all the components of those volunteer battalions, later, were incorporated into the military of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Those Azov fighters are no longer self-established group. They are a component of the Ukrainian military. Back in 2014, there was situations when our volunteers were encircled and some of them did violate laws -- laws of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And they actually were taken to court and got prison sentences. So law is above all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I wonder if I could ask you to respond in English. World attention has been key to Ukraine's resistance strategy, what happens when interest wanes? Are you worried that the West has a short attention span?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: That is a very big problem. And it is not about interests to -- for the Ukraine, for all our soldiers, for peace, for democracy -- that it couldn't be, you know, very popular or not, frankly, very popular because it's counting all the people's lives, yes.\par \par \uc2 And that's why I do believe that if we want to stop this war, people want to win -- to win this aggression of Russian Federation, to win in this war, because that is -- that's -- it's not only Russian-Ukrainian war or war for our independence. I believe, it will not be, but it's a world war.\par \par \uc2 Even information that began already, that is the war of principal things for the freedom, life of all across (ph). If you know it will -- I don't believe that we'll lose, but -- always but, you know?\par \par \uc2 If the world will not be so strong and Russia would go through Ukraine to another countries and will -- you know, that is -- that is the biggest struggle for the world. That you will see how other dictators or, you know, all other countries, big countries in the world will change their principles and will do the same.\par \par \uc2 That will not be the last war in the world. It will be the first, the first of those future biggest wars in the world. That's -- I think so.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We had hundreds of people write in on social media and a lot of questions dealt with you. How you're holding up? How was your family? They wanted to feel you personally, how you were getting through this war and how your family is doing.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: I'm OK. I do what I can. And each morning I'm thinking that I don't do enough. And that is the biggest problem for me. But that's good that I don't have so much free time to think about all this -- you know, all these things.\par \par \uc2 And I don't have any time to think about me, and it's a pity that I don't have time to think about, you know, spending time with my family, that is a problem because that is the love of my life. That is my -- I mean, that's the thing that I want a lot of -- that I have the many things (ph) I have. And that's why I think we fight, all our Ukrainians --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last --\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: -- because we saw a lot of things, we want our children to be with open eyes and to see it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, you were elected at 41, younger than any of our presidents here in the U.S. Three years ago, you were starring in a Ukrainian sitcom. You had a successful career as a comedian and an actor. You were even on the "Dancing with the Stars," Ukrainian version.\par \par \uc2 But now you're being described around the world as this Churchillian figure standing firm against Russia. Short of experiencing war, could you ever anticipate the intensity of focus, the purpose that this war demands of you and where you are right now?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY (through translator): God chooses what we can endure, and I think this was not by chance. I know that we will stand through this. And my partners today are the people of Ukraine. And I am now working 24/7 and I fulfill my work as much as I can efficiently.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. President, we appreciate your time on behalf of "Special Report" and FOX News.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Thank you so much, it was a great honor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Our thanks to President Zelenskyy, his team.\par \par \uc2 Our thanks to my team for turning that around. It was a tough edit today.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: If we can see that the process of transferring those weapons is getting slower, than people begin to ask the question: is it really true? Maybe there is some game behind it. I don't want to believe that some partners of ours are playing games.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: He doesn't want to believe people are playing games with him and not giving him the weapons because they really -- they really want the war to end and a deal being made even though that deal likely if it ended now would be very unfavorable to the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: General Jack Keane reacted to that portion of the interview with President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel -- and ta-da, in studio, Juan Williams is FOX News analyst. Ben Domenech, editor of the transom.com and host of the "Ben Domenech podcast on Fox News Radio. That's not him, by the way. That's Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is Ben Domenech aforementioned.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it's been a while since we have been in the studio. So, we are getting the kinks out.\par \par \uc2 Ben, let me talk to you about this interview and what you took away from it.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, "THE BEN DOMENECH PODCAST": Well, there were a couple of things. One, it really stood out to me the question that you asked pointedly about the China relationship here and Zelenskyy lacking really an answer other than to say that the United States needed to be part of that conversation, enabling that conversation to happen.\par \par \uc2 They are the elephant in the room. We saw Lavrov go and meet with his counterpart in China this week, along with his counterpart in India. The truth is that Russia, it would not be doing what they were doing without that backing and the permission coming from their biggest backers in China.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy didn't really have an answer for that. He also though was pressing the case as he has been doing repeatedly for heavier weaponry. They want to add steel to the pines of the porcupine here. They know that is something that they need in order to have any real prospect of negotiated peace.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of negotiations, he also, Juan, said he security guarantees from a host of nations. This is outside of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , in Article 5, but including the U.S., which, by the way gave a security guarantee in 1994 when they gave up their nuclear program but didn't really hold in there.\par \par \uc2 But, what about that? They are not to that point, obviously. It's interesting to hear him talk about the nations he has reached out to and agreed to.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Right. He said that President Biden was considering it in the interview with you.\par \par \uc2 What struck me there was that he said he is open to the negotiation and with specifics. I think made news there when you said, you know, the Russians had agreed to allow some refugees to go and now he thinks that they have been firing at those refugees and that is, he says he wouldn't tell you, Bret, as to any of thinks direct orders as commander-in-chief but suggested, at least I took that, that that's why you saw Ukraine firing today at that Russian military depot.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, didn't definitively answer that but also in the way he answered it kind of.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I thought he gave it away.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing was the assassination business. I thought, again, you made news there. The suggestion that, yes, there had been assassination attempts and that he would have to get rid of people, a general, I think he said to you, and people in his secret service.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Matthew, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTEPRISE INSTITUTE: Putin has failed achieve his initial goal which is toppling Zelenskyy's government. Now the danger is that the conflict will freeze and Putin will be able to occupy more territory than he had before the invasion and the West will lose interest in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 My takeaway from your interview, Bret, was that Zelenskyy needs more help. He needs more weapons and he needs more intelligence so that he can change the facts in the ground, forcing a negotiation where he will have the upper hand.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think there is this feeling, Ben, that Russians may be losing but they may also regroup and try to fortify and dig n different places and just not leave until the west gets bored with this and moves on.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: You could refer to the Russian generals who have died to this point as being just a first wave. The way the Russians work historically, they frequently have bad first years then they figure things out. They regroup and they come back.\par \par \uc2 And to Matthew's point, I really do think that is a circumstance that Zelenskyy fears as well and knows in the undercurrent here that that's a real risk. I'm glad you asked him that question. I think it's one that has to be answered not just at this end of Pennsylvania Avenue, but at the other end as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Okay. We are going to have more on this about the interview. Also we are going to touch on the U.S. economy where they are with the news out of the U.S. after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: I am not prepared to fulfill what Russia demands at the negotiation table. I do not want to give them anything of what we own. We don't want any of their land. We don't need -- we don't ask for the life of their people. Let them go home.\par \par \uc2 Heavy weapons will give an opportunity to talk with them at the negotiation table.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Zelenskyy. We're back with the panel, Juan, Ben and Matthew Continetti.\par \par \uc2 Down the row here, any other thoughts about the interview and where Ukraine is right now?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think it is interesting he said no land concessions. We have been following all week the Russian assault on the east and the thought that they might just say, OK, we're going to stop here. Clearly, Zelenskyy is not buying that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matthew?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I was struck by his answer on sanctions. You pointed out some strengthening in the Russian economy since the war started. He defended sanctions. He's right. But sanctions are not enough, he needs more tools.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Seem to me the subtext of the interview was him asking the audience of one in the White House to stop thinking about this as the former head of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee and start thinking about it as the commander-in-chief and the leader of the West.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Speaking of the U.S. here. A jobs report out today, March jobs report, and being touted by the president, 431,000 jobs added, unemployment rate down to 3.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Big picture good, solid economy.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, Juan, inflation is still a major concern for families and for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Correct. And so, you know, you don't need any rose colored glasses. As you say, it's a very good report, but looking to the future. It looks a little bit cloudy because of inflation as you said. Gas prices still an issue, and supply chain disruptions because the war goes on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matthew?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Biden's numbers on the economy are dragging down his overall approval rating to dangerous levels. And until inflation abates, his ratings will continue to fall.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ben?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: As long as this war goes on, you're going to see increases in food prices that will extend into the summer and could upend political regimes in some of the poorest and most unstable parts of the world.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, on the southern border, you have this surge of already immigrants coming across. And known getaways and they are tracking all of these record numbers. And Title 42 announced officially today that it's going to go away, May 23rd. A lot of people, Democrats and Republicans, Juan, are saying this is going to be a big, big moment.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It's going to be a big, big problem. So, the question is how do we deal with it? What position can you take? You can take away 42. But then do you have people remain in Mexico?\par \par \uc2 The administration is going to have to impose some kind of order here. We don't know what that plan might be. To my mind, where is the Congress? Why can't congress, you know, Republican and Democrat, say here is immigration reform. It's so critically needed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  saying we are already facing an unprecedented increase in migrants this year. And that will only get worse if the administration ends the Title 42 policy. We are nowhere near prepared to deal with that influx.\par \par \uc2 We got the announcement today though, Ben. It's happening.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: It is happening. And the people who should be most worried about it are the two senators from Arizona in particular, Mark Kelly and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . This is something that they have been warning the administration about, to not do this. Unfortunately, it's happening and we're going to see the ramifications.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think, Matthew, that immigration goes up the chain important issues as we head towards November?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think so. It's already where we saw Biden's erosion last spring when the crisis began as a result of him reversing the Trump policies on the border. Biden needs a change n attitude on the border, on energy, and defense.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But doesn't seem like change is coming, does it, Juan?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Not -- well, we are going to see what happens with immigration, not going to still. But in terms of inflation and energy, I think he is taking steps. He is trying to deal with the supply chain, trying to right now consider, you know, even a tax holiday on gas.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's good to have you guys back in studio.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Up next, "Notable Quotables" right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know it's Friday? It's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: For God's sake, this man cannot remain in power.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you now regret saying that?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not walking anything back.\par \par \uc2 I was expressing the moral outrage I felt. I wasn't then nor am I now articulating a policy change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What happens when interest wanes? Are you worried that the West has a short attention span.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: That is a very big problem.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE: We have a sacrosanct commitment to Israel's security.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Israel will do anything we need to stop the Iranian nuclear program. Anything.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ADVISER: Billionaires paying 8 percent. That's a problem for this president.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: To release of 1 million barrels per day, a six months bridge to the fall.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): You are the assistant director of \uc2 FBI\uc2  cyber. I want to know where \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's laptop is. Where is it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sir, I don't know that answer.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): No apologies from the mainstream media. We were right. They were wrong.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Didn't hurt a bit.\par \par \uc2 JOSE ANDRES, CELEBRITY CHEF: Everybody is doing something. Everybody has a reason to serve and this gives me hope for humanity.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We no longer say "ladies and gentlemen, boys and girls". We say "dreamers of all ages".\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Somebody needs to tell will smith because it was his first Oscar, when people say act like you've not been there before. They don't mean prison.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS ROCK, COMEDIAN: That was the greatest night in the history of television.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's all one week. Monday on "Special Report," former \uc2 Google\uc2  CEO \uc2 Eric Schmidt\uc2  on his effort to rouse the U.S. to tech's biggest struggle yet, competition with China.\par \par \uc2 Tune in this Sunday, "FOX News Sunday," Martha McCallum will be hosting this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight.\par \par \uc2 Thank you to my staff for a very busy day with that interview that was logistically challenging but happy to get to you.\par \par \uc2 That is it for this SPECIAL REPORT. Fair, balanced and still unafraid.\par \par \uc2 "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts now.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220402ei4100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Lifting His Own Chair Saying The Economy In His Administration Is Great And It's Making Great Strides On Almost Everything; The Biden Administration Is Trying To Convince Americans That Shifting To Electric Vehicles Will Save Americans At Least $80 Of Their Monthly Gas Expenses; CDC Is Out With A Report Confirming That COVID Lockdowns Had Affected Teenagers, But Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  Doesn't Want To Admit That He's Wrong About All The Mandates; Oscars Producer Now Explains Why Will Smith Was Not Arrested By LAPD Cops After He Slapped Comedian Chris Rock; Oscars Slap Revelations, Awards Producers: Chris Rock Declined To Press Charges; Minivans Are Cool Again: Millennials Trading In Luxury Cars For Modern Vans; Return-To-Office Dress Code: Workers Wearing Blazers With Hoodies, Dress Sneakers\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Jesse Watters, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dagen  McDowell, Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,249 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: And how far does it expand now? We'll explore that. Don't forget, in the meantime, Bret's upcoming interview with the president of Ukraine, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . What he has to say about American support about global support, coming up.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, I'm Jesse Watters along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dagen McDowell, and Greg Gutfeld.\par \par \uc2 It's 5 o'clock in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 President Biden playing the part on April fools. Boasting about what he calls a booming economy. But little does he know the joke is on him. Biden claims everything is going just great, but even the left-wing media won't defend him.\par \par \uc2 Politico warning about Biden's sophomore slump with the president failing on just about every single issue. You have to give Joe some credit. Despite his dumpster fire presidency, he sure knows how to spin things.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: It's very clear, Americans are back to work. Our policies are working, and we are getting results for the American people. We need to do more to get prices under control. Putin's invasion of Ukraine has driven up gas prices and food prices all over the world.\par \par \uc2 It was the previous administration's whose reckless policies and mismanagement led to the record budget deficits. In my administration, let's getting the deficit under control.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And sadly, it gets worse. The Biden administration continues to prove how clueless and out of touch they really are. Like their solution to the energy crisis, telling everybody just to go out and buy electric cars that they can't even afford.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We can take advantage of the next generation of electric vehicles, that a typical driver will save about $80 a month from not having to pay gas at the pump.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRANHOLM\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF ENERGY: I know that electrical can be expensive at the -- at the dealer, but it's one of the reasons why the president wants to see those tax credits to bring down the price at the dealer so that people can buy electric vehicles and don't have to ever worry about going to fill it up at the gas pump.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Even if all of the oil we use in the USA were made in the USA, the price of it is still subject to the powers and dynamics outside of the USA.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Dagen, why can't we do both? Why can't the administration officials come out and just say we'll do a little bit of fossil fuel, we'll do a little bit of green, and everybody say, fine, let's go?\par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Because they're ideologues, and they have no connection to real-world consequences or facts. So, when it comes to people and their pain instead of, well, you know, I feel that this is hurting you, it's suck it up.\par \par \uc2 That's why ideologues dictating policy is so dangerous and harmful, number one. They could come out with a giant plan. But this is the policy on energy is as incoherent as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 I don't -- Andy Lipow, my go-to guy on energy analysis said the Strategic Petroleum Reserve release is a one-time measure to bridge a supply disruption. It does nothing to get more supplies out of the ground to replace Russian supply. That was in The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 So why is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  castigating and vilifying and putting the hammer down on our own energy producers and then he alienated Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Stop backing and funding the war on the Iran-backed Houthis by Saudi Arabia. Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates and Kuwait combined, have enough spare production to offset the oil and then some that's come off the market because of Russia. So --\par \par \uc2 (