mean, given what Mr. Putin has done, but in terms of hard-nosed, negotiating, you know, sitting down and with, with pen and paper and mapping it out what it's going to look like long term. No, we're not at that point right now. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Assistant Secretary (INAUDIBLE) told the House panel today that the DOD is working with Slovakia to identify the requirements for meeting Slovakia's need in case they provide their S-300s to Ukraine. Could you please detail the work and of that work a little bit to know what are you guys doing with the --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The secretary said as much when we were in Slovakia, (INAUDIBLE). So, that's absolutely right. We are doing that. I'm not going to get, I'm not going to detail from the podium consultations we're having with an individual nation state about capabilities that they might be willing to provide Ukraine and what the offset might look like. But we are absolutely having discussions with them about that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: General Walters, responding to a question today acknowledged that the U.S. pulled out its destroyers out of Black Sea, just before the Russian invasion. And he added that U.S. should return to the Black Sea as soon as possible. What was the calculus of pulling the ships out of Black Sea then? And do you have a plan or any discussion in the department to send back some ships to the Black Sea?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think, look, we move ships in and out of the Black Sea routinely. It -- that's, it's not unusual. I mean, we don't, we don't keep them permanently in the Black Sea. But we absolutely have moved them in and out as, as needed. And it was a, it was deemed a prudent decision to do it in the, in the as we continue to see, an invasion is more imminent, to, to make it very clear to everybody that the, that the United States was not interested in, in enforcing a conflict by, by some posture decision that we're making.\par \par \uc2 So, it was the prudent thing to do at the time. And I don't have anything to announce with respect to if and when U.S. ships will go back into the Black Sea. We watch and monitor this every day. And we'll make the best decisions based on what's in the best interest of our national security and that of our allies and partners as well. Over here, in the back there. Kelly?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thanks. I know you can't go too much into this. But how dangerous is it that Putin is not being informed by his advisers on what is happening on the ground? And are we in touch with the top Russian military leaders? Yes, I know, you had said that they had declined to engage just last week.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes, no, no conversations to speak to yet. We maintain our willingness to have those discussions. But it's a two-way street. The Russians have to be willing to pick up the phone and, and thus far they've not been willing to do that. Obviously, it understandably, would be an issue of concern, if for not just our European allies and partners, but certainly for, for Ukraine if, if Mr. Putin is misinformed or uninformed about what's going on inside Ukraine. It's his military. It's his war. He chose it.\par \par \uc2 And so, the fact that he may not have all the context that he may not fully understand the degree to which his forces are failing in Ukraine, that's a little discomforting, to be honest with you. And it's certainly one outcome of that could, could be could be a less than faithful effort at negotiating some sort of settlement here. If he's not fully informed of how poorly he's doing, then, then how are his negotiators going to come up with an agreement that, that, that, that that is enduring? Certainly, one that respects Ukrainian sovereignty. The other thing is, I mean, you don't know how a leader like that's going to react to getting bad news. So, yes, it's, it's disconcerting. Felicia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hey, John, thanks for taking my question.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Any notable observations or assessments since it came up yesterday on Ukrainian counter attacks? I'm wondering if you had, like efforts to take city back. And I'm wondering if you have some sort of, I don't know, top line assessment about how they're doing overall?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don't have anything today in terms of specifics. Just broadly speaking, Felicia, we've seen them counter attack and retake territory to the west and to the northwest of Kyiv. And as we see some Russian troops reposition and move away. We have seen indications that the Ukrainians are moving forward, as you might expect them to do. Where it's really notable is in the South.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when we were talking early on, you remember these are sort of three lines of access that the Russians have used: north, east and south, generally speaking. And in the south, they had made the most progress in the first couple of weeks. And now, the Ukrainians are, are literally clawing back some of that, some of that territory, particularly just to the northwest of Crimea, so the Kherson area and, and the positioning of the Russians around Mykolaiv, which they now have lost a lot of footing there.\par \par \uc2 So, we've seen the Ukrainians are very tough in trying to get back territory there in the south. Now, Mariupol, again, I don't need to tell you this, because you guys are all covering it. But that obviously there is a lot of heavy fighting going on right there. We think that there are some Russian forces that are very, very close to city center. Ukrainians are fighting very, very hard. It is the town is just being shellacked with airstrikes and bombardment, as we talked about earlier, both sides fighting very, very hard over Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 From a geographic perspective, clearly, it's very important to both sides because of where it's located right there at the, at the southern sort of end of the of the Donbass region.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thanks. Three today, that's OK.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You had three yesterday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I know.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I counted.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: First, thank you. A clarification. You'd said that there are more airstrikes in the Donbass and increased activity there. And you'd said that there were still airstrikes in the Kyiv area and that they had pulled back, would you call it a decrease in activity in the Kyiv area? Are there still, is there a significant, significant reduction in airstrikes? Like how has that changed or has it changed, other than, aside from the 20 percent that kind of pulled back?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don't, I don't, you know, we weren't counting missile strikes on Kyiv. So, I can't give you an honest answer in terms of like whether it was more today than yesterday. What I would just say is generally, the city continues to be under threat of air strike and bombardment, we are continuing to see that. So, even as the Russians reposition some of their troops -- and before that, before they started to reposition, they were in the defensive crouch basically. They weren't advancing from the ground anymore in the city. But even with all that, we continue to see that the city is very much under threat from airstrikes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. Thank you for -- secondly, how many missiles has Russia launched into Ukraine at this point?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I couldn't give you an exact number. But it's, as I said yesterday, it's definitely more than a thousand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. And then finally, I apologize if I missed this, but when you were talking about the (INAUDIBLE), do you know where they're coming from? General Townsend had told \uc2 VOA\uc2  earlier that there was recruiting going on in Africa. Can you tell us where they're coming from specifically, is it Syria?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I couldn't give you, I couldn't give you the roster. And you know, where every single employee of the Wagner Group are coming from? All I can tell you is that we do think they are going to prioritize the Donbass area that we have indications that they'll, that they plan to put a thousand or so of their contractors in Donbass.\par \par \uc2 Where they're all coming from, I don't know. We have seen indications that the Wagner Group is recruiting in places like Syria, in places like Northern Africa, Libya, but how many are of that thousand or so or were there -- I just, I don't have that level of detail.\par \par \uc2 The point is, that it's just another example of how Mr. Putin is going to is going to throw more energy into trying to, to occupy the Donbass. Now, to what end? We're not exactly sure. Certainly, so far, he has not been successful in that either. But, but we believe that there, that they, they are going to prioritize that part of the country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, did you say Wagner is recruiting in Libya to fight in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I say, we've seen them -- say, we've seen indications that they want to recruit out of places like North Africa and Syria. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Specifically --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I said Libya, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To fight in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes. Mike?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Question about the 20 percent. Refitting a mechanized unit is a significant logistics challenge acquired to maintenance munitions, POL -- are you seeing any examples of, of that happening with, you know, the support elements coming in to refit? Or are you just assuming at this point that will be refitting.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Too soon. No, as I said, we've only just --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What are the specifics about that?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes, we've only just beginning, beginning to see this repositioning. It's a minor percentage of the total force that they had to the north and northwest of, of Kyiv. And we believe the intent is to is to, in many of these cases, to bring them into Belarus, so they can be refit and resupplied. But, but I, you know, it's just now starting Mike, so I don't really have anything more specific to speak to. And look, Mike, I mean, we may never have that level of detail for you. I mean, it's, you know, we do the best we can to tell you what we're seeing, we're not going to see everything. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How close? How close are they going back from it, you say the repositioning probably --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, I mean, look, I mean, again, they, they were, again, Mike -- we're only just beginning to see this. So, we assessed in -- that the closest that they their advanced elements got to the city center of Kyiv was about 15 to 20 kilometers. So, it's the -- it's, it's the forces that were arrayed around the (INAUDIBLE) airfield that we believe those were the ones that had begun -- they're the first ones to begin this movement north. But again, I couldn't tell you where they are on the road right now or where exactly they're going to where, they're going to end up. Tony.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you give a status report of the, the latest tranche of stingers, javelins, and switchblade drones that the President authorized?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We -- what I can tell you is that material is, is getting into the region every single day, including over the last 24 hours. We are in the first half a dozen shipments of what will probably be around 30 or so of this $800 million that the President signed out. So, it's already moving into the region. I don't have an inventory list for you, Tony about, you know how many are on every flight. But we're doing the best we can to prioritize the kinds of material that we know the Ukrainians need the most. And so, obviously, that includes some of these anti-armor and anti-air defense systems. But I don't have a, I don't have a shopping list for you today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are those little drones starting to be --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: They have not shipped in yet, but we think that that will, that that will change relatively soon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I need to ask you about this acute, the use of the word "acute." I know it's I don't want to be cute. But you've mentioned a few times here. Maybe I ask you this, General Van Herrick, the Northern Command Commander said five days ago that, "Russia is the primary military threat to the homeland. And their focus on targeting the homeland has provided the model other competitors are beginning to follow."\par \par \uc2 And then General Walters last year about a year ago this month said, "Russia is the primary threat. It's the existential threat against the United States." And I'm just asking you acute sounds like a step down from existential and primary threat, almost a deliberate attempt to de-emphasize Russia and overemphasize China, this is going to come up as an issue. So, I'd like you to address it.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, it's a fair question. I mean, it's not about de-emphasizing Russia and nobody's de-emphasizing Russia. We've said many times here, certainly, you and I have talked about this many times. I mean, nobody's taking the Russian threat lightly nor should we. Now, yes, they've underperformed. They've made pretty big mistakes in Ukraine, planning and execution wise. Of course, they've also faced a much stiffer resistance than they anticipated.\par \par \uc2 But nobody's shortchanging the, the kind of threats that they continue to pose and you can see it for yourself. My goodness, just take a look at the video and the imagery coming out of Ukraine. And you can see the damage that that this military is capable of, of exerting and causing. But the secretary has been nothing but completely candid from literally day one, that the pacing challenge in his view for this department is China.\par \par \uc2 In the manner in which, the expense at which China continues to modernize their capabilities, continue to bully their neighbors coerce other nations in the region, and to continue to expand the militarization of the Western Pacific. That cannot be ignored, nor will it be by this department.\par \par \uc2 I know everybody wants us to rack and stack. We've been very honest, China remains the pacing challenge. Russia, we still as assessed as an acute threat. I understand the interest in the -- you know, in the adjectives here, but it would be imprudent for us not to try to characterize the threat that Russia poses, and to try to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 And again, look at the budget. I mean, my goodness, how many budgets have you reported on? Take a look at it, and you can see that the kinds of capabilities that we're investing in, are not just going to help us with the pacing challenge of China, but they will also help us deal with the threat that Russia continues to pose.\par \par \uc2 One mentioning that the Nuclear Posture Review cancels the sea launch nuclear cruise missile that that the Trump administration pushed. That doesn't sound like beefing up to deter against Russia, you're cutting a nuclear weapon. And what -- what's the rationale for that cut?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I'm not going to talk about the specifics of the Nuclear Posture Review, it remains a classified document, Tony. So, I'm just not going to go there.\par \par \uc2 But -- I'm not going to go there, except to say, look at -- go look at the budget we just submitted. And look at the billions of dollars that we committed, and more than 30 I think billion dollars, just to keep modernizing the triad.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you.\par \par \uc2 I should have figured you'd have the exact figure more than me. But, nevertheless, more than $30 billion to modernize the triad. Nobody's sloughing off or taken for granted, the fact that we need a strong strategic nuclear deterrent, and that we're willing to invest in keeping it strong. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): OK. We're going to hop out of this for a moment. Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby will join us in just a little bit. So, we'll get some more questions from him, then.\par \par \uc2 But joining me now on set is retired four star general Jack Keane, Fox News senior strategic analyst, and chairman of the Institute for the Study of War. General. Great to have you with us in the studio today. Good to see you. As always.\par \par \uc2 Let's look at the big picture. We just watched John Kirby talk about the movements of the Russian military. And what we're sending, what we're not sending things are on the way haven't arrived yet.\par \par \uc2 Give us the big picture. What is the mission here? What's the U.S. strategy right now?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST (on camera): Yes, you know, this is a great question. That I think, fundamentally, the United States underlying strategy in dealing with the war before it began, and after it began, is not to provoke Putin.\par \par \uc2 It's an unstated strategy, but it is operating every single day. Before the war, we delayed armaments to Putin in March. And in the fall of 21, we -- they didn't get the stingers until January of 22. We came up with this lame idea, doing sanctions only if there was an invasion, as opposed to before the invasion, again, not to provoke Putin. All of those are not to provoke Putin. And the reason is fear of adverse consequence.\par \par \uc2 And then, since the war began, no one expected the Russians to perform so poorly, and Ukraine's to actually beat them in ground combat. And they have, and now there's actually an advantage -- an opportunity to win -- as a chance to win. It's not a given.\par \par \uc2 And we have no strategy to do that. Why? Because we don't want to provoke Putin, that -- I don't like saying this, but the facts are, we don't want Putin to lose. We don't want Putin to lose, because we're afraid that if he loses, there will be a provocative reaction on his part.\par \par \uc2 So, it explains why we're not all in on helping Zelenskyy win who is begging for all of that help. And where is the statement from the president? He's had many opportunities since the war turn to Ukraine's favor.\par \par \uc2 Listen, our mission in Ukraine now is to help the Ukrainians defeat the Russian army and crushed them. And, by the way, there's no better deterrence for the \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries than for the Russian army to be crushed in Ukraine. And we're going to give him everything he needs to see this through.\par \par \uc2 There's no guarantee. Where is that conversation and no statements? We are hiding behind the fact that Putin where -- there's palpable fear in this administration, that if Putin loses, he'll do something horrible.\par \par \uc2 And when you let that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But if he win, (INAUDIBLE) he do something more.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. The fact -- the fact -- we believe that. I certainly believe that. I certainly believe, at some point, he would encroach on \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Listen, once the administration has got to get their head around, is war is fundamentally a test of wills, and leaders have got to step up and overcome their fears of adverse consequence.\par \par \uc2 And then they have to find the spine to have the perseverance to deal with all the obstacles and all the impediments that are in their way, preventing them from succeeding.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of that is has to do with who you are. Mostly what's in your heart is what I'm talking about here. And we just don't seem to have it. And that's why, Zelenskyy is so frustrated, and I believe, because I had a great source to told me that up -- two weeks ago, the administration was pressing Zelenskyy to make a deal.\par \par \uc2 Cut a deal when he is doing counter attacks, when he's pushing the Russians back, when whole Russian units are being decimated. We're asking him to cut a deal. Can you imagine his frustration when he -- went when he got that phone call?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, you're saying that the dynamic started to change. Russia started to lose. And Zelenskyy started to have an edge, and we didn't change our reaction to that reality.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: No, and we're not listening to him. He -- now, he wants tanks. Why does he want tanks? He doesn't want tanks to stop a Russian assault. He wants tanks to lead in a counter offensive, to take territory back, to destroy Russian units.\par \par \uc2 He wants the MiGs to provide cover for him in doing that, and he wants the air defense systems to protect those MiG's, who is supporting those ground attacks that he's conducting. And he is getting none of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that sends a pretty clear message. And as you say, you believe that this administration is OK with Putin not losing, essentially. They're okay with that.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: They flatly are on that -- on that path. They don't want him to lose, and they want a deal. And it's such a lost opportunity. And where is that -- where is the passion arising out of you, watching 1,000s of people being killed and literally murdered by this barbarism that's on display by the Russian army?\par \par \uc2 Let's go out there and destroy that barbarism once and for all. And Zelenskyy has the will to do it, and his troops have got the skill and the will. Give him the means to do it, and also give them the moral support.\par \par \uc2 I mean, where is -- where is the patting on the back, tell him, look, we're here. We're with -- we're with you. What else do you need?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: General Jack Keane, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have you with us, sir.\par \par \uc2 We'll get reaction to some of what the general said from John Kirby shortly.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon spokesman will join us in just a little while. Also, Fox correspondent Lucas Tomlinson back from Ukraine, and here to share his story of war from the frontlines.\par \par \uc2 And our exclusive with Poland's ambassador to the United States. What does he say about us strategy and what we're doing, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAREK MAGIEROWSKI\uc2 , POLISH AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED STATES: We can see now a very disturbing and upsetting process of, I would call it, the restalinization of this country. We are going back to the 50s, actually, in terms of personal freedoms, freedom of expression, and the general repression, and growing reprisals against the civil society in Russia.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So today is a sad day for the Fox News family. The funeral services for Pierre Zakrzewski, a member of the Fox team who lost his life while he was doing his job gathering news in Ukraine this month.\par \par \uc2 Those services were held in his home country of Ireland yesterday. Pierre covered almost every international story in and out of the war zones for Fox throughout his prestigious career.\par \par \uc2 That strike also took the life of 24 year old Sasha Kuvshynova, who was serving as a consultant, sometimes called a fixer, with our team in the field, and the -- also, of course, Ben Hall, our correspondent who survived and we send continued prayers for his recovery.\par \par \uc2 We're reminded of course of the danger of covering the war from the frontlines that is taken on by journalists all over the world and our beloved team as well, of course.\par \par \uc2 And one member of that team is correspondent Lucas Tomlinson, who recently returned from covering the war in Ukraine. And he joins me now. He's also served in the military, in the Navy and in Afghanistan, as well.\par \par \uc2 So, you have spent time in dangerous places as well, Lucas. It's good to have you back, and it's great to be here in studio with you.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: It's great to join you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we'd be remiss not to start with your thoughts on Pierre, and on Sasha, and on Ben. You've worked with all of them, obviously, in the field.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: While, I knew Pierre a short time in Ukraine, but he's the type of guy who you became best friends with almost instantly. I think people at home can understand that.\par \par \uc2 You know, first night, I was in Ukraine, we went out to dinner together, the whole group. And, you know, by the end of the meal, you felt like you were best friends. I sat right next to him. And he was incredible, and hear stories he was embedded with Ahmed Shah Massoud, the great guerrilla fighter in Afghanistan, who took on the Soviet Army and fought very bravely.\par \par \uc2 The fact that, you know, really Pierre hadn't missed a scrap in the last two decades, at least, I mean, from Sudan from, you know, Libya, you know, not just Iraq, Afghanistan, of course, where he spent, you know, tons of time with our correspondents.\par \par \uc2 But just really, you know, from Gaza to Libya, just an incredible man, intrepid and more than just a photo journalist. I mean, he was like the team leader in Ukraine. It was only a few weeks we intersected in Kyiv, but you did what Pierre wanted you to do.\par \par \uc2 And you got the sense early on that not only is had he done this many times, Martha, but he knew what was best. And he'll be bravely missed, he'll be incredibly missed.\par \par \uc2 And also Sasha, you know, there's the role of the fixture, the consultant, also the interpreter.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: I mean, somebody who can get you an interview with President Zelenskyy who can also just get anything done. I mean, that's, I think the role and, of course, while praying for Benjamin Hall and a dear colleague, dear friend, and he's incredibly brave man.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed. You know, that bravery across the board. And I think people you know, watch you guys overseas and think that you're -- that it's impervious, you know, that no one will ever get hurt, and we learned that very tragic lesson.\par \par \uc2 And we're so proud of them and so proud of you in your coverage.\par \par \uc2 What -- now that you're back and you listen to John Kirby, because you're the Pentagon correspondent for us, and you understand the stuff better than anybody. What do you make of what Kirby has been saying about where we are right now in this conflict?\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: What we heard today also on Capitol Hill about this notion of this, you know, intelligence failure, did the US military, the intelligence community underestimate the ability of the Ukrainian forces to fight, and did they overestimate the Russian forces ability to make war. Because by all intents and purposes, if these \uc2 NATO\uc2  estimates are to be believed between 7,000 and 15,000 Russian soldiers been killed in Ukraine, that high watermark of 15,000, that's as many as the Soviet Union lost in the Afghan war in the '80s.\par \par \uc2 So when you look at intelligence failure, you know, let's look back on history during World War II, Ambassador \uc2 Joseph P. Kennedy\uc2 , the father of the president's son, he told FDR and the secretary of state that he thought Hitler would win the war. He advised, you know, the United States to make peace with Hitler. I mean, it's almost outrageous in retrospect. So we look at conflict and we, you know, the intelligence community clearly doesn't always get it right.\par \par \uc2 And back to what John Kirby said today about -- I thought he got it right when he mentioned how this withdrawal of Russian forces is not a retreat. It's not even a full withdrawal. It's really a refit, rearming. Remember, these ceasefires in the Russian government is in negotiations right now with the Ukrainian government. The Russians want the ceasefire. So this has been what they've been doing for years, for decades. What do they do when there's a ceasefire, gives them time to rearm, bring in reinforcements.\par \par \uc2 We heard today about a thousand mercenaries coming in. You know, you got it right today but certainly for the Biden administration, what more can they do to help Ukraine defeat the Russians.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lucas, thank you. It's great to be with you. And I would encourage everybody to listen to the podcast that we did. We had a great conversation, much longer conversation. So that was a real luxury to have that time with you and I do appreciate it. So I'd ask everybody to download the "Untold Story" with Lucas Tomlinson as well. We'll be seeing a lot more of you. It's great to have you on set.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, Lucas. So Poland's ambassador to the United States says that the world didn't listen to warnings about Putin's imperial ambitions, which he made very crystal clear. My exclusive interview as the Kremlin keeps bombing Ukrainians, and Poland keeps calling for more weaponry to Ukraine and peacekeepers to move into the country as well when our breaking coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think the American government should revisit that question of closing the skies that Zelenskyy wants so much?\par \par \uc2 MAREK MAGIEROWSKI\uc2 , POLISH AMBASSADOR TO THE US: \uc2 NATO\uc2  country is key delivering cutting edge weaponry right now to Ukraine. And, of course, it's a race against time because we do understand that the Ukrainians need many more weapons right now in order to stave off the Russian aggression.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So as you have been hearing this hour, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  clearly not cutting back on the attacks on Ukraine, regrouping in a way stepping up in some areas moving more over towards the Donbas Region. The Ukrainian president, Zelenskyy, said this today about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: Russia is deploying new forces in our terrain to try to continue destroying us, destroying Ukrainians. We have to do more to stop this war. And first and outmost is weapons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Poland's ambassador to the United States \uc2 Marek Magierowski\uc2  joins me now. Ambassador, thank you very much. It's good to have you with us. I know you have a lot going on these days. And we all wish it was for different reasons, but we do appreciate you being here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: Thanks a lot for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you listen there to President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  talking about what we see as a stepping up of attacks in Ukraine. And we know that Poland has urged \uc2 NATO\uc2  to do more with peacekeeping forces or with the MiGs, transferring MiGs to Poland. Here's an exchange earlier today with the \uc2 NATO\uc2  allied supreme commander on Capitol Hill. Let's watch this if you would.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So it's your best military advice that the United States or its allies should not provide fighter jets to Ukraine at this time. Is that accurate?\par \par \uc2 TOD WOLTERS, COMMANDER, US EUROPEAN COMMAND: It is my best military advice that the US doesn't, and it's also my best military advice that we allow nations to independently make their decision about what they would like to offer the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you say to that, ambassador?\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: Well, approximately two weeks ago, my government proposed to the American administration to transfer those aircraft, the Soviet made fighter jets to the German -- another is in Germany. And to put them at the disposal of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , because hypothetical transfer of these airplanes to Ukraine should be a unilateral decision, and for a unanimous decision of all \uc2 NATO\uc2  member states.\par \par \uc2 So it was very -- pretty important for Poland not to make the decision unilaterally. And, of course, that decision and that proposal was turned down by the American administration. We have to move on right now and try to deliver as many weapons as possible to Ukraine, in order to help the Ukrainians defend themselves and repel that barbaric aggression on the part of the Russian Federation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Given this escalation, do you think that that needs to be revisited? Do you think the American government should revisit that question of closing the skies that Zelenskyy wants so much?\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries keep delivering, cutting edge weaponry right now to Ukraine. And, of course, it's a race against time because we do understand that the Ukrainians need many more weapons right now in order to stave off Russian aggression. But still, I believe that and I can only echo the words of for \uc2 NATO\uc2  secretary general, and many other Western political leaders. We do not need a military confrontation with Russia right now, but we are ready to equip the Ukrainian Armed Forces with everything that we have at our disposal, again, in order for the Ukrainians to defend themselves more effectively.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Poland has repeatedly been more aggressive, ending coal shipments, wanting to use these MiGs, wanting to talk about sending in peacekeepers when you see what's happening in Mariupol and in Chernihiv. So, what is your message to our government? Do you want to see a stronger - -\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: Yes. There's is another aspect to this, to this clash between the West and Russia, namely the economic sanctions and the punitive measures which have been taken by many European countries and the United States. I think that we should, above all, close all the loopholes in the sanctions package. For example, the Polish government decided yesterday unilaterally to block all imports of oil, gas and coal from Russia, in stages, until the end of this year. And all the Polish government, for example, the energy security has always been of paramount importance. And we are very sensitive in this particular area.\par \par \uc2 And I think that many other countries in Europe should follow this decision, and should also think more comprehensively about how to retaliate, economically and commercially against the Russian aggression in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Obviously, Poland is on the frontlines for \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and so close to what's happening across your border in Ukraine. In terms of the training of Ukrainian soldiers that's happening in your country, how would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: I think John Kirby described it and explained it pretty clearly, and explicitly it's not about training. It's about instructing the Ukrainian, the Ukrainian side and the Ukrainian -- our Ukrainian partners who gets those weapons, which are, you know, probably has become a hub for delivering those weapons to Ukraine. And they get those weapons. And this is what John Kirby said earlier today, they aren't being instructed somehow how to operate --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Why it's such a concern about the word training? If they're getting weapons in there, these American soldiers are showing them how to use them. Isn't that training?\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: I don't have much more knowledge about that, Mr. Kirby. So I can -- as I said, I can only quote him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We know that our Jake Sullivan said that there was no training US troops training Ukrainian troops, but so your understanding is that they are --\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: This is my understanding.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That they are, how would you say, explain it.\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: They are just -- I believe that they just instructed how to use the Stingers and Javelins, and so on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So, you heard the President on the trip that you were on, just got back a couple of days ago, say for God's sakes, he cannot remain in power, referring to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And then he said, these are my personal feelings. This is not a policy call for regime change. Does Poland believe that there's should be regime change?\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: I perfectly understand his personal feelings. It was an expression of moral outrage. Again, perfectly understandable given the fact that just a few hours before his appearance at the Royal Castle in Warsaw, and that speech, he had met with Ukrainian refugees in one of the reception centers that had been set up in our capital. And I do understand how emotional that encounter was for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 So he must have felt pretty much agitated and upset by what he had heard about the plight of the Ukrainians who had arrived in Poland over the last couple of weeks, all those stories of people who are essentially driven out of their homes deprived of their property who lost their beloved ones during the war. So I understand this expression.\par \par \uc2 And the one thing I'm sure of his -- that even if Putin does remain in power, he cannot remain impure. And I have been saying this repeatedly that I expect the international community to try him, not only him but also his cronies or his aides, who had led Russia to invade Ukraine. We need an international tribunal in order to try and to sentence all those war criminals who have committed unspeakable atrocities on Ukrainian soil --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Unspeakable, pretty sure.\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: -- over the last four weeks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And Poland has been enormously generous with taking in millions of refugees. But would you say to the Russian people that they should want another leader or does Poland want to see new leadership?\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: Of course, it's up to the Russians to elect their political leaders. It's not for the West to decide who should rule Russia. Although we can see now a very disturbing and upsetting process of, I would call it re-stalinization of this country. We are going back to the '50s actually, in terms of personal freedoms, freedom of expression, and the general repression and growing reprisals against the civil society. In Russia, it's really terrifying. The processes that are now going on in Russia.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think that Putin is using refugees as a political weapon against your country, because putting another 2 million people into your country could weaken your economy? It's a burden to bear and I know you bear it willingly, because ...\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: It obviously appear ...\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But do you think that's part of his aim?\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: It's obviously -- it was predictable that would be a wave of refugees fleeing Ukraine to Poland as the biggest country in the region, and a country in which 1.5 million Ukrainians had lived before the war. So there was fertile ground for the absorption of new migrants, new refugees into Poland.\par \par \uc2 So far, we have been doing fairly well. There was that remarkable outpouring of solidarity and sympathy towards our Ukrainian brethren. Now, more than 2.3 million people have already been admitted into Poland, overwhelming majority of them hosted by Polish (inaudible) families in our homes. About a week ago, the Polish Parliament approved a bill which essentially facilitates the integration into the Polish society.\par \par \uc2 For example, about 170,000 Ukrainian children have already been incorporated into the Polish schooling system, about 17,000 Ukrainian women have already been officially employed. So there is also an ongoing integration into the labor market. So it's -- we have already taken some measures and some important steps in order to help those Ukrainians integrate into the Polish society.\par \par \uc2 Of course, when you talk about education, there is obviously a shortage of teachers, but we are now training people who are basically bilingual, those who speak both Polish and Ukrainian or Russian, for that matter, in order to help them, to help those Ukrainian kids to, again, to become more integrated into the Polish society.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we know we hear from so many Ukrainians that they would like to go home, but you're giving them --\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: And that's why they stayed in the program because, of course, re-emigrate from Poland to other countries. Some of them have travelled to Germany, to the Scandinavian countries, to France, some of them also to the United States. But I believe they still hoped that this war will end shortly, and that they will be able to return to their home, to Lviv, to Kharkiv, to Kyiv. And that's why they stay in Poland.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we are more than happy, willing and ready to host them and to absorb, to admit even more refugees from Ukraine because we owe it to them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ambassador, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much. Good to have you with us today, sir.\par \par \uc2 So let's bring in Bret Baier, anchor and executive editor of "Special Report." He will have an exclusive interview with Ukraine's President Zelenskyy on Friday. And Bret, we all look forward to seeing that conversation. It's good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX ANCHOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So today, there was a lot of conversation on Capitol Hill, about the strategy here, about how we're doing this, some behind closed doors, some in open testimony. What are you hearing about potentially pressure on the White House to change strategy? What's the feedback?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are a lot of different elements on Capitol Hill. A lot of congressional delegations want to go to Ukraine, and that's in itself a logistical challenge. They have a two-week recess coming up and there are a lot of them gathering to try to go over there. There were some into Poland, they now want to go into Lviv. They want to meet with Ukrainian officials. And that's a heavy lift for folks in security and everything else.\par \par \uc2 But I think there is a criticism here about how the White House is handling this. Today, the President with his call with Zelenskyy offering $500 million more in aid, direct aid, but there's a sense of what Jack Keane was talking about of, making a stand if Ukraine is going to win or not. Not that the US is going to be fighting on the ground, but we are doing more directly to help Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And you saw some push back I think from \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2  at the White House saying we're doing all that we can. And that's really the question up on Capitol Hill, are we?\par \par \uc2 MAGIEROWSKI: Yes. That was an interesting point from General Keane saying, you know, where's the passion to support? There have been multiple opportunities for the President to say, you know, we will do whatever it takes to make sure that you that you succeed in this mission and yet we have never really heard that kind of decisive statement from the President.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When he got decisive, he said that this man can have power, and then there was a three-day walk back, which kind of diluted what was a kind of firm moment for this president, although diplomatically was kind of something that caught world leaders by surprise. So, how it's been handled, how it's been kind of jumbled on a number of fronts has really raised some eyebrows. I thought it was great that you were asking the ambassador about the training, as the President described it, and what John Kirby said at the Pentagon, but there's no clear answer there either.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, why is there such reluctance to call that what it is? Because clearly, we have given weapons and there were Green Beret trainers that were even in Ukraine for a period of time before the war started. They were pulled out before the war started. So, why this reluctance to own, yes, this is the way we've chosen to help. And, of course, we're going to give it 100%.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, sounds like rating (ph).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is TR training.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So we were doing it before, what is the provocative nature? What is the line in which they're so scared that this is the thing? I mean, it was MiG-29s before. Now, is it training Ukrainian troops in Poland? I don't know.\par \par \uc2 But the ambassador also talking about Polish accepting these refugees at some point that is going to be overwhelming for that country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And they're going to have to get to a point where something else happens that a valve helps them out because they're going to be in tough spot.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. They've been incredibly generous as we said, but now they are asking the rest of the \uc2 EU\uc2  to step up and do more. Bret, thank you so much, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see you tonight at 6:00, and we'll look forward to your interview on Friday with President Zelenskyy very much. Good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So here now to answer some of those questions is Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby joining me now. Good to see you, Mr. Kirby. Thank you very much for coming today.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: You bet. Glad to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know you've answered a lot of questions already. But -- so I want to start by playing this. This is a soundbite from a conversation that I had with General Jack Keane, retired four star, just a few moments ago. And it's a pretty strong statement. So I want to get your response to what he had to say. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACK KEANE, FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYS: Now there's actually an opportunity to witness a chance to win. It's not a given. And we have no strategy to do that. Why, because we don't want to provoke Putin. I don't like saying this but the facts are we don't want Putin to lose. We don't want Putin to lose because we're afraid that if he loses there'll be a provocative reaction on his part.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you say to his description of what -- it seems to him, is the overwhelming strategy which has been just not at any turn to provoke \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes, sure. First, I want to say how much I respect General Keane and known him a long, long time, and no more dedicated public servant. So, I -- with all respect to General Keane, I would respectfully disagree. This isn't about being afraid of anything. And it's not being about -- certainly not being afraid to provoke Mr. Putin. We do have a strategy. We are helping Ukraine defend itself by providing yet another $800 million worth of security assistance, as well as bolstering \uc2 NATO\uc2 's eastern flank to deter any Russian aggression against the eastern flank of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But I think we can all agree, when you look at Mr. Putin and the forces he has arrayed available to him, and the fact that he is a major nuclear power, I think we can all agree that escalating this conflict even more than it already has, and making it about the United States versus Russia is not only not good for our national security, it's not good for the Ukrainian people to have two nuclear powers potentially slugging it out in their skies or on their ground.\par \par \uc2 I think the President was exactly right not to want US troops involved in the fighting in Ukraine. But what we are doing is it much more than we were doing before in terms of making Ukraine harder for Mr. Putin, just swallow.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So -- but it goes back to the same question of what is the mission? What is the US mission in Ukraine? Is it to give them enough to win?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is it the goal to give them what they need to beat \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  on the battlefield? Is that the goal?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes. So I would say, and I'm glad you brought that up, because I meant to in my last answer. Mr. Putin has in many ways already lost, right? He has failed to take Kyiv, the capital city, his largely strategic objectives of capturing major population centers and subjugating Ukraine, and taking away their sovereignty. He's failed in all of that.\par \par \uc2 And now, we're seeing reports that he is starting to reposition his troops outside, moving them outside of Ukraine, bringing him back so he can maybe read apply them elsewhere in Ukraine. And now they're talking about reprioritizing on the Donbas. So in many ways, Mr. Putin has already failed on a lot of the lines of - and a lot of his objectives.\par \par \uc2 And as for our strategy, we don't have a mission inside Ukraine, Martha, but we do have two major goals here. One, to make sure that Ukraine can continue to defend itself and they are. And that's not by accident, Martha. It's not just the stuff that we're sending over there. It's the training that we've done for the last eight years with the Ukrainian Armed Forces, the Brits, the Canadians, us as well as other allies.\par \par \uc2 And number two, Martha, and this is not unimportant, is to bolster the \uc2 NATO\uc2  deterrence and defense capabilities on the eastern flank. We've added more US capability in terms of aircraft in just the last couple of days. And we're going to continue to do that as required to make sure that Mr. Putin knows \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory is not something he's ever going to be able to threaten.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we know that President Zelenskyy has been grateful for everything that he has gotten. We know he talked to the President this morning, but we also know that he's asked over and over again, they need more. They need more to defend the country and to achieve their goal, which is to drive \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  out of Ukraine. So I will ask you this way, is the United States OK with Russia at the end of all of this, getting the Donbas Region?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, no, not at all. And we've said from the very beginning, even before Mr. Putin decided to invade Ukraine, that Ukraine's sovereignty and national territory needs to be respected. Now, look, whatever negotiations Mr. Zelenskyy and Mr. Putin come to, that's between them. The United States is not inserting ourselves in a peace process here. But we've said from the very beginning, that Ukraine sovereignty must be respected.\par \par \uc2 So no, it's not OK with the United States. If Mr. Putin gets to grab a piece of territory, we've never observed their illegal (inaudible) of Crimea.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we're not encouraging the president of Ukraine to accept losing some of that territory. We're not currently doing that.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, ma'am. No, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. And we will not stop aiding them until they achieve that goal?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: President Biden has been nothing but clear that we are going to continue to support Ukraine's ability to defend itself as fast as we can for as long as we can.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. John Kirby, thank you very much. Always good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You too, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So a short time ago, the White House would not confirm reports that COVID-19 asylum restrictions will end in May, which would potentially count the number of migrants flooding across the southern border to surge even more. By the end of this week, the head of the US Border Patrol agency says that we will reach 1 million migrants crossing our border in just the last six months. Those are record breaking numbers, of course. Correspondent Bill Melugin is back on the border near Eagle Pass, Texas with more. Hi, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Those staggering numbers you just mentioned show we are well on track to hit 2 million illegal crossings this fiscal year because we're already at 1 million in six months. And keep in mind, we haven't even gotten to the busiest months down here at the border, those spring and early summer months. But in the meantime, it is non-stop down here.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this video we shot here in Eagle Pass, Texas, where we are constantly witnessing river crossings, migrants just broad daylight walking right in front of border patrol and Southern Texas National Guard, and illegally entering into Eagle Pass, Texas at all hours of the day. And border patrol sources are telling us if and when Title 42 finally drops, you can expect things down here to get a whole lot worse. They have been using Title 42 to immediately expel migrants back to Mexico on the basis of public health.\par \par \uc2 We'll show you what it looks like. If we can pull up this video here, we've seen Title 42 in action today. Every 30 minutes or so, one of these border patrol vans pulls up on the bridge and you see migrants get out, and they are walked back down the bridge back into Mexico. That is then being expelled back into Mexico and Title 42 has been used 1.7 million times since the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 And earlier yesterday, a bipartisan delegation of Texas lawmakers sent a letter to DHS and \uc2 HHS\uc2 , urging them not to end Title 42. They say it'll cause chaos, writing in part, "If the CDC were to rescind the Title 42 order at this time, border patrol facilities and local communities would be forced to absorb at least double the current number of migrants likely with catastrophic results."\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, elsewhere along the border plenty of action. If we can pull up this photo, what we're looking at is a failed human smuggling attempt that happened in the Rio Grande Valley at the Falfurrias checkpoint. Border agents stopping a trailer with 86 illegal immigrants locked inside, mixed in with rotten produce. And then lastly, if we can pull these two mugshots, border patrol reporting they arrested two convicted child sex predators one in Arizona, one down in the Rio Grande Valley.\par \par \uc2 Back out here live, if Title 42 does get rescinded in the coming days or months, border agents tell us you can expect a surge on top of a surge down here at the southern border. We'll send it back to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No doubt. Bill, thank you very much. Bill Melugin in Texas.\par \par \uc2 So that's THE STORY for this Wednesday March 30th from Washington, DC. THE STORY goes on. We'll be back in New York tomorrow at 3:00. Thanks for joining us, everybody. Have a great evening. "YOUR WORLD" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220331ei3u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Speaks With China's Leader As Putin Wages War; Russia Ramps Up Missiles Strikes To West And South; Putin Attends Big Rally As Troops Attack Ukraine; Russian Air Strikes Intensify In Ukraine; White House: We Made Decision To Ban Russian Oil; White House Briefs After Speaking To Xi About Putin's War; Biden Warns China On Helping Russia; Putin Stages Massive Pro-Russian Rally In Moscow; Schwarzenegger Makes Passionate Plea To Russia; Fox Talks To Wife Of Engineer At Seized Nuke Plant\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Mike Tobin, Keith Kellogg, Jacqui Heinrich,  Aishah Hasnie, Rich Edson \par \uc2 9,739 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: These things available as well.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: That's going to be a lot of cars; second, a lot of juice from the grid.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes. John, great to be with you this week. We will be back here Monday morning. Thanks for joining us, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Thank you. We, as always, we pray for all of the people of Ukraine in what they're going through. THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, John, and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum right now on THE STORY. We are waiting for some reaction from the White House. We got a little bit of a readout, not a ton of information in here. But there's the podium. This is delayed, the briefing, a busy morning obviously at the White House about a less than two-hour discussion with President Xi Jinping of China and President Biden at the White House.\par \par \uc2 And the President warned of consequences, we're told, if China helps Vladimir Putin militarily or to evade the sanctions that have been put in place as he wages war in Ukraine. China has refused to condemn officially this invasion, calling it the Ukraine crisis instead of a war. In Ukraine, its foreign ministry saying after the call, "All sides need to jointly support Russia and Ukraine in having dialogue and negotiation that will produce results and lead to peace. The U.S. and NATO should also have a dialogue with Russia to address the crux of the Ukraine crisis and ease the security concerns of both Russia and Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Retired \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  Major General Michael Repass, who has worked as a trainer and a senior adviser to the Ukrainian Armed Forces since 2016. General, great to have you with us today. Thank you for being here. So, let's get started with your reaction to what we've got so far from this readout. From the Chinese side and from the U.S. side, the Chinese saying that conflict and confrontation are not in anyone's interest. They don't want to see an escalation, in other words. What's your reaction to what we've heard so far, General?\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. MICHAEL REPASS, RETIRED U.S. ARMY: What we've heard -- so far, what we've heard is that the Chinese are walking very tight rope on sanctions, and they want to avoid being sanctioned by not only the United States, but the rest of the world. So, this is a very big deal, that they continue to walk this tightrope and pull this act so very concerned about where they're headed with this. I'm very concerned that they, they continue to side with the Russians on it. We have a lot at stake this internationally known United States with the Western world and the economy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you believe that China will give military support? There have been reports of, you know, some sort of back discussions between Russia and China, and we all remember the statement that they put out, saying that their friendship was unlimited right before the Olympics. You know, how do you think China is viewing the sanctions that are on Russia right now, and the world galvanizing response to what has happened in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 REPASS: Well, China is definitely taking note of all that. They have their eyes on Taiwan. And they know that whatever happens to Russia, Visa, the Ukraine is probably coming their way, should they actually move on Taiwan. So, they're taking note on how this goes down. They're watching the processes, the watching who's playing, and in the interim, they're going to start putting pressure on each one of these nations, before they do anything in Taiwan. So, they are getting their playbook together, based on what is happening to Russia.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I think it's interesting, I don't see any discussion of biological or chemical weapons use and any discussion between the two, and maybe it was there, and it's not in this readout, but there'll be questions about it. I would imagine at the briefing when that gets underway, about a potential agreement or pressure that should be put on Russia not to use these kinds of weapons, or they could face consequences from both their new buddy in China and also from the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 REPASS: Yes. So, that is going to be a game changer if chemical or biological weapons are brought to the game here. Nobody's going to stand by and let that happen. Let that go on punished. There'll be serious repercussions from the international community on that much worse than anything has happened so far on the economic sanctions primarily. I don't think that the international community will stand, stand by or sit on your hands and allow a chem or bio instrument to go down in in Ukraine. It's just not it's not acceptable on any level. China has to pay very close attention to what the not only United States but the international community says these are red lines that we're not going to cross but I agree with it, it's interesting that that hasn't been a point so far.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, China has problems of their own right now. They have a big COVID outbreak, if we're to believe their numbers is probably the worst that they've had since the beginning, because they did such an intense lockdown. But as we've seen, you know, once you release that, eventually it finds its way around every population. They've also got financial problems that are very oppressive, real estate issues that they're dealing with, do you think China's really in a position to say, yes, we're going to, we're going to help you out, we're going to send you weapons, we're going to make sure you win this fight in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 REPASS: You hit the nail on the head there, Martha. China has got their own problems. You know, in the past decade or so, when they've done this mass urbanization, where they move the peasants off the, off the arable land, into these mega cities that they built, they actually built the cities on the little arable land that they have. And they're incapable of feeding themselves. So, all their food has to be imported now. You throw that on top of the real estate and all the other factors that you've thrown in there. And if I -- a rational Chinese person would not believe that they want to anchor themselves to a sinking ship, that is Russia. We're watching Russia go down as a player on the international stage. They're headed down a road that they cannot recover from. So, China doesn't want to be part of any of that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let me ask you about this language on Taiwan, because this is very interesting. Obviously, there's a, an a parallel between China's ambition to bring Taiwan back into the fold completely into the China fold. And what we see with Putin's desire to make Ukraine part of Russia, once again, so this is from the, the White House side, about what they said about Taiwan. It said the President reiterated the United States policy on Taiwan has not changed, and emphasize that the United States continues to oppose any unilateral change in that status quo. Now, let's pull up what came from the Chinese side from one of their spokesmen on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . This is what they said, "President Biden reiterated that the United States does not seek to have a new cold war with China, to change China's system or to revitalize alliances against China, and that the U.S. does not support Taiwan independence or seek a conflict with China." Little different.\par \par \uc2 REPASS: Yes, we're definitely apart as two nations there on that, that's going to be up to the diplomats and the senior leaders to work that went out. I can see the difference; I think everybody can see that. That is China, incrementally, edging into the discussion on Taiwan here. They're inching farther and farther towards their strategic objective of integrating Taiwan into their fold and making it part of mainland China there. So, that's, that's the language to support your view on that it may not be the language that actually was discussed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Major General Michael Repass, thank you very much, Sir. Great to have you with us today. I hope you'll come back soon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 REPASS: Take care, Martha. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we're going to go live to the White House as soon as that gets underway and to Ukraine, as Vladimir Putin claims that his so-called Special Operation -- did you see the rally that he gave today? This was astonishing. And what he had to say about how great things are going and how the soldiers are pulling together with a brotherhood, and that they're going to prevent the genocide, he says that's going on in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN, PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translation): It is precisely to save this suffering from this genocide. That is the main reason for the convincing motive and purpose of the military operation that began in the Donbass and Ukraine, precisely for this purpose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we have some new video just coming in of the heavy assault that has been ongoing on Mariupol, reports of active machine gun battles there in the street today as we learn that more than a thousand people are still in that basement in the shelter that was below the bombed theater. The drama theater in Mariupol. It's just an unbelievable, otherworldly sight to see what has happened in that city.\par \par \uc2 The mayor says that 80 percent of their residential buildings are either damaged or destroyed. And this, this is the scene in Lviv this morning. Dark black smoke fills the sky after what is being called a sophisticated weapons attack near the airport, which is thought to be an attack on an airplane parts area that perhaps would make it difficult if those MIGs were sent in to maintain them and to you know, fix them with parts.\par \par \uc2 So, they've really been going after some of these areas, the, the air strips and the, and the airport parts facility that obviously those are those are military targets as opposed to the civilian targets that we've seen so much of, and that's where we find Mike Tobin, who is in Lviv which had been somewhat exempt from some of this and now we're seeing bombs dropping in that area as well. Mike, good evening to you. What can you tell us from where you are tonight?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Well, there is some comfort in the western part of the country, Martha, that the targets have largely been military targets. But as you mentioned, it's relatively peaceful here. That piece was shattered at dawn this morning by four Russian cruise missiles. The targets of those cruise missiles was Lviv state aircraft repair. What they do, there is customize the MiG-29 for use by the Ukrainian Air Force, and that's what the Russians were after when they struck out there.\par \par \uc2 Maksym Kozytsky is the regional administrator for the Lviv region. He says there were six cruise missiles that were launched from long range bombers over the Black Sea.\par \par \uc2 Two of them were intercepted and route to the target, four made it to their target.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian Air Force says these cruise missiles are capable of flying low, did in fact, fly low, and that's why most of them were able to evade the Ukrainian air defenses.\par \par \uc2 The Air Force says they were the Russian X-555 cruise missiles, each with a price tag of about 1 million.\par \par \uc2 You talk about down south in Mariupol. It is a desperate situation there at that theatre. The Donetsk Regional Drama Theatre, which was being used as a bomb shelter.\par \par \uc2 Despite the markings that children were present in that facility made clear to an aircraft. It took a direct hit from an aircraft.\par \par \uc2 Initially, there were some reports that people were getting out of there. What we know now from Serhiy Taruta, who is a politician down there that the rescuers who are trying to get to that location and get people out of the rubble were fired upon.\par \par \uc2 So, they can't even rescue the people who are trapped inside of that bomb shelter. And the other thing we know from Serhiy Taruta is that there were two floors.\par \par \uc2 You had people who were down in the basement, They may have been largely protected. You don't know, but you also had people on the first floor so the worst is feared.\par \par \uc2 Mariupol is an apocalyptic scene. You mentioned that the mayor said 80 percent of the housing there is destroyed. They have resorted to using mass graves and Mariupol, because they simply have too many dead people and they can't handle the logistics.\par \par \uc2 The people who are getting out are saying that the humanitarian corridors really don't exist anymore. But the people are so desperate to get out. They are leaving under fire, quite literally running for their lives. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mike, thank you very much, Mike Tobin in Lviv this evening.\par \par \uc2 So, let's bring in retired Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg, former national security adviser to Vice President Mike Pence, and now co chairman for the Center for American Security, and of course, a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 General, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET.), FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I'm really struck by what Mike Tobin is reporting about Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I want people to just think about that for a moment. So the people who are going to try to dig people out of the basement of that bombed area are being shot at.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And then you've got -- I mean, it's a split screen that you couldn't make up, right?\par \par \uc2 So, on the other side of the screen, you've got Vladimir Putin walking around in his big down jacket and his turtleneck, saying that he's saving Ukraine from Neo Nazis. So -- and genocide. So, it appears unless, you know, that the genocide that's happening is against the people of Ukraine, by -- on the part of Russian airstrikes and the soldiers who are carrying those airstrikes out as well, sir.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes, Martha, look, you're seeing classic examples of war crimes 101 when you see stuff that's happened in Mariupol. But look, here is what's interesting to me overall. The big picture of it is he's had a real problem trying to take the primary target, which is Kyiv, and eliminate the government. He stalled out.\par \par \uc2 So, what's happening right now, he's refitting rearming, and he's probably going to plan B in the north at least, because plan A failed. That was to take Kyiv immediately, eliminate the government.\par \par \uc2 And plan B is going to be to be rubble Kyiv, do we go to the west? Do we -- Do we go to street to street fighting in Kyiv? Do we just settle for the eastern part of the country and other western part of the country? At the same time, he is trying to make those decisions? We need to make decisions here in the West.\par \par \uc2 And those decisions are do we put an umbrella and Iron Dome over the western part of Ukraine to make sure those cruise missiles don't get in, those aircraft don't get in?\par \par \uc2 Do we -- do we start activating our intelligence community, our Title 50 group for -- to go into unconventional warfare or covert activities to support the Ukrainians?\par \par \uc2 Because the way it looks to me, watching Putin today with that rally, this is a fight to the finish. He doesn't want a diplomatic offer him. And I don't see one starting either. I think there should be one. But I don't see one going there.\par \par \uc2 He is doing this to the end game. And what he is doing in Mariupol and other places is absolutely horrific. And this is -- we're going to keep seeing scenes like this for the next one week, two weeks, three weeks, or four weeks until this battled complete.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think that Ukraine -- Ukrainian forces can win this fight? I mean, take us forward in this process.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think that is a potential outcome here?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes, I do. I think -- and here's what I mean by a win. Is the -- I think they can lose go to a stalemate that he asked to negotiate, and a stalemate to Putin is a loss.\par \par \uc2 The other is Putin may win this overall by sheer numbers, but it's a Pyrrhic victory. And what he has shown to the world, his military isn't that good. His command and control isn't that good.\par \par \uc2 It shows what kind of a person we -- we've all known him to be. But he's shown in public. We've seen what kind of nation Russia is, and it's going to be a pariah nation.\par \par \uc2 So, overall, could he win physically at the end. Yes, but he's going to lose the long fight because Ukrainians are proud people, Ukrainians are not going to quit. Zelenskyy is not going to quit, unless they take him out, eliminate him.\par \par \uc2 So, over the long term, I think there is at least a 50-50 shot that Ukrainians can win this. I think, to me, personally, and professionally, I think it's better than that.\par \par \uc2 But they've got a shot at this. And we just need to give them as much, the very least, moral support and equipment support as we can as they go forward. So, we have some tough days in front of us?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: General Keith Kellogg. Thank you, sir. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 The general is going to stand by with us, because joining me now is someone who grew up and went to school in Mariupol. His parents are currently stuck in that city, Ukraine parliament member Dmytro Gurin joins me now. Dmytro, thank you very much for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to put up on the screen, this really stunning image in Lviv, which is a -- they've assembled baby carriages, to represent the more than 100 children who have already been killed in these attacks. And I would imagine that including what happened in Mariupol, they are going to need to add strollers to this very poignant tribute to these children, sir.\par \par \uc2 DMYTRO GURIN, UKRAINIAN PARLIAMENT MEMBER: We don't know what's going on in Mariupol. I think that they will have much more killed children than this cradles in Lviv, just because we don't know at all how many people that in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 I think that the real numbers are like 10 times more than that was officially published because that 2,500 killed is just the bodies that were collected on the streets.\par \par \uc2 But we don't know about people died in their apartments. And, for example, in in the house of -- where I grew up and lived 15 years, and where my parents lived. Like seven people died from burns alive.\par \par \uc2 Like, and it's just neighbors of my parents, they burned dead that moment. And we have much more killed children, much more -- several times more than this number that we know exactly now.\par \par \uc2 And I heard the president --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're looking at -- I'm sorry, there's a little delay, so forgive me. But I -- tell me how your parents are doing? And have you been able to talk to them? And what are they explaining to you about what it's like.\par \par \uc2 GURIN: The last time I heard them, one minute, the day before yesterday, and it was really bad connection. And I didn't know what they told. But I heard the voice of my mother and the voice of my father. So, they're still alive. And that's how good news looks like now in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But I'm hurt, you know, the previous comment of your guest was had that Russia changes tactics and changes strategy in this war. And he's really -- he's right. But to actually change this tactic, two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 When the first week of war, it was a conventional war army gets an army and they have seen and, by the way, all the world have seen that they cannot beat us on the battlefield. That it's not a second army -- second powerful army in the world, it's just a bad joke, not an army.\par \par \uc2 So, they have a really, or they have a lot of rockets or missiles, a lot of tanks, but they are moral, they -- you know, they are soldiers. It's a joke.\par \par \uc2 And they change the tactics, and they decided to kill civilians. To equal resist, let's kill them all. And last two weeks, they are just destroying our cities and killing our civilians. And in Mariupol, we clearly see that they are planning and making already the plan -- the plan to start to hunger, just to enforce the opposition in diplomatic processes.\par \par \uc2 So, just to -- you know, to make us to make the diplomatic process more easy with us, because people, it's the most important thing for us. And Ukrainian lives is the most important.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes,\par \par \uc2 GURIN: And, of course, when several 1,000 people per day will die, because there is no water. And then, several days people in Mariupol will start dying from dehydration.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GURIN: And in the two weeks, from hunger. And that's what --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, it's clear that -- yes. Yes, it's people starving, and not allowing humanitarian convoys to get in. Obviously, this looks like war crimes to anybody who looks at what is happening here.\par \par \uc2 Dmytro, I'm so sorry for what has happened to your city. I don't know what it's like to look at these pictures of where you grew up, and not -- and not recognize the place that you see.\par \par \uc2 And these bombed-out buildings, it's just absolutely extraordinary. Just a quick thought. And then we're going to go to the White House, sir.\par \par \uc2 GURIN: It's a -- they destroyed 15 years of my past, my school, my university, everything around my house, just everything. 30 percent of buildings are destroyed totally. And 80 -- 50 percent more hardly damaged. And there is no Mariupol anymore.\par \par \uc2 We have to build a new city on this place.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm so sorry. Dmytro. Thank you for being with us today. And stay well. And we will keep in touch. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 GURIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's go to the White House now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): The press secretary is at the podium. And this is getting underway, obviously, an enormously historic discussion with the Chinese leader today between our president and President Xi. So, let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: He will also reaffirm our ironclad commitment to our NATO allies, and to defend every inch of our NATO territory -- of NATO territory.\par \par \uc2 Then, he will join a scheduled European Council summit to discuss our shared concerns about Ukraine, including transatlantic efforts to impose economic sanctions on Russia, provide humanitarian support to those affected by the violence, and address other challenges related to the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Later in the day, he will attend the G7 meeting called by Germany to further discuss with our allies and partners, the consequences we are imposing on Russia for its war of choice.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more announcements, I'm certain on the trip in the days ahead. I don't have additional details. We've also, of course, invited our national security advisor, who always enjoys engaging with all of you, to join us to preview the trip early next week.\par \par \uc2 So, we're just figuring out the date and the time, depending on when the charter leaves. With that sequence, you pick us up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No update on a Friday schedule?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Don't have any updates at this point in time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Onto the president's call this morning with President Xi, does the White House now have a sense of whether or not China has decided whether to assist Russia backfill its supplies and sort of fulfill its request for material? (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I'm not going to give an assessment of that from here. What I can tell you is that the majority of this call, as I think you heard it, you saw in the readout, and you heard, I think on the call we just did, but was focused on Russia's unprovoked invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The president spent -- the vast majority of the nearly two hours was spent with the president outlining the views of the United States and our allies and partners on this crisis, including a detailed overview of efforts to prevent and then respond to the invasion, how we got here, steps we've taken, where we've gone and why.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, as was also noted in the readout, but let me just reiterate, he also conveyed and describe the implications and consequences of China for provides material support to Russia.\par \par \uc2 But again, I'm not going to provide any additional assessment from here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The president has tasked the efforts such as fill up the Western alliances, sort of thing. Democracies versus autocracy. One very large -- the largest democracy in the world is in fact, buying Russian oil right now at a discount, expanding its purpose and taking advantage of the situation.\par \par \uc2 Does the White House have any response to India's purchases there? And it says the president's plan to reach out to the Indian government tries to get that decision reversed?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, we have not -- well, we made a decision about banning the Russian import of oil, every country has not made that decision. And we recognized that, and they have different economic reasoning as to why different countries do, including some in Europe.\par \par \uc2 We have been in touch, of course, with Indian leaders at a range of levels, not through the president. If that happens, we will of course provide that readout and information to all of you.\par \par \uc2 But what we would project or convey to any leader around the world is that the world -- the rest of the world is watching where you're going to stand as it relates to this conflict, whether its support for Russia in any form as they are illegally invading Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE) COVID we find -- saw this week, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  request to the UA for --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A fourth -- a fourth -- a fourth shot or second boost against seniors and Moderna last night was just for all adults that (INAUDIBLE). Does the federal government have the money right now to purchase the doses needed to give everyone the boosters? Should that -- should be FDA and the CDC cleared that?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, just to reiterate some of the pieces of where the process is. So, the CDC who does recommend fourth doses for the immunocompromised. Well, it hasn't been, of course, as you noted, approved for others, and they're running a vigorous data driven process.\par \par \uc2 And right now over 100 million Americans have been boosted, millions more have not done so and are eligible to do so and need to get boosted. But to go to your question, we've been clear, we need additional funding from Congress. You've heard me talk about that, a fair amount in here, including for the possibility of a fourth dose or a variant specific vaccine, so we would indeed need additional funding to ensure that can be widely available. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: During the readout of this call, a senior official said that the President was really sort of laying out his assessment of the situation to President Xi, that he was making clear the implications of certain actions. But that President Biden wasn't making specific requests of China, why not given the stakes here?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Because China has to make a decision for themselves about where they want to stand and how they want the history books to look at them and view their actions. And that is a decision for President Xi and the Chinese to make.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Given the growing concerns about China possibly aligning with Russia, would you say it's more concerning at this point that they would help Russia resupply, help Russia with military equipment, or that they would offer economic support to help Russia evade sanctions?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Any of those would be concerning to us but I'm not going to give a rank order assessment of which we're more concerned about.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And you don't have a sense that China may be leaning more in one direction or another?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Not an assessment I can provide from here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And just sort of a logistical question, you know, that the Chinese got out a readout of the call fairly quickly --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Before it was even done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was three-and-a-half --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Remarkable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- before your readout. Should we read anything into the time that it took to get your side of the story?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, it was a lengthy call, as you all know, two hour call is, of course, a lengthy call even with translation and what typically happens. And this time is a little longer than typical, but is there's a, you know, a meeting to discuss what happened during the meeting and assess what kind of information we can provide publicly. Obviously, we want to do as much as that as possible to all of you, but we also want to ensure we're protecting the diplomatic channels and conversation. So it was an effort to do exactly that. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. Just wanted to ask -- so, you know, we've spoken to US and European diplomats who say that there's still not exactly an agreement on what the red line would be that, you know, what, when China would face consequences. Can you say a little bit more about those discussions, and whether there is agreement with G7 allies and others who joined in about what the trigger is and what sort of consequences?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I enjoy, I would say, you know, the movement of China to align with Russia or to -- yes the movement of them to align with Russia or their proximity of moving closer together is certainly of great concern to us, as we've expressed. And we are not the only country that has expressed that concern, including many other members of the G7 have expressed exactly that concern.\par \par \uc2 So this is part of the discussion has been, an ongoing part of the discussion, expect it certainly would be when the President goes to Europe next week. But we're not in a place at this point to outline the specifics. We're still discussing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And then just to follow up on that. Initially, I think going into the call, we were expecting that there would be discussion about Iran and North Korea as well. Did the two leaders get around to those issues and are there any closer in terms of alignment on those issues? And then just to your first point or your response, one of the issues that has separated the US and China over -- in recent years, has been disputes over trade and nuance of tariffs. So would you anticipate that China would face a larger trade war if -- or additional sanctions and tariffs if it does proceed to align closer, more closer?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, we have a range of tools that could be considered and sanctions are certainly one tool in the toolbox, as they are for other countries as well, even if we -- as we have not outlined specific consequences, and we'll communicate those directly to China and, of course, with our European partners and counterparts. The call was focused as the readout conveyed. The vast majority of it was on Russia's unprovoked invasion of Ukraine. But also, the issue of Taiwan was raised by the Chinese and the President reiterated that US policy on Taiwan has not changed. That was really the vast majority of the focus of the call.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, there are a range of issues and topics we discussed with the Chinese. And as a part of this call, there was an agreement on ensuring there was an open line of communication and that the discussion would continue in this critical period ahead, of course, at lower levels. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: During the two-hour call, did President Xi ever refer to it as an invasion?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We'll let President Xi and his team outline any more specifics about his component of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why not reveal if he called it an invasion or call it a war --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Because we don't, as a policy, speak for other countries. They can speak for themselves.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yesterday, Secretary Blinken said the administration was concerned that China is considering answering Russia's request for more military equipment. After this two-hour call, does the White House still have that concern?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We have that concern. The President detailed, you know, what the implications and consequences would be if China provides material support to Russia, as it conducts brutal attacks against Ukrainian citizens civilians, and obviously that is something we will be watching and the world will be watching.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So that concern hasn't gone away following the call?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Obviously, actions are a key part of what we'll be watching.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And Russian Foreign Minister Lavrov says that Russia will view any weapons shipments into Ukraine as "legitimate targets." How was the President Biden just authorized $800 million in more security assistance a.k.a. weapons potentially going into Ukraine. So what is the President's response to that comment?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, that's a threat that he has made before. I would just note, Foreign Minister Lavrov. I would note that just in terms of how it works, without getting into too many logistics, of course, there are no US troops operating inside Ukraine. Our forces are in NATO territory, and so our NATO forces in NATO territory. So as we're talking about the operations of the movement of convoys, and the movement of assistance, those are not the bodies that would be moving those assistance within Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So beyond that, though, I would say, of course, we watch closely what the actions are, the continued escalatory actions of the Russians, and, and we will watch closely if they follow up on that threat. I would remind you all that all of the convoys are not just moving military assistance, many of them are moving humanitarian assistance as well, food and other aid that is getting to people who have been injured through this brutal invasion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I guess the question is how concerned are you about being able to get all $800 million, that assistance that's obviously so critically needed into Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, we continue to have the means of getting that assistance in and we have effectively been doing that in recent days. So we will continue to work through those channels.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, Jen. Did President Biden directly ask President Xi if he intends to help Russia?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, he made clear what the implications and the consequences would be if China provides material support to Russia as it conducts brutal attacks against Ukrainian cities and civilians, and that made clear what the consequences would be should they do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But did he asked, do you intend to help Russia, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I think making clear what the consequences are, made clear we don't want them to do it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So can you offer any explanation for why the US still has concerns after this call?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Because we'll continue to watch until we see what actions they take or don't take.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And following up on Andrea's question, does the administration view any distinction between financial support and military support for Russia? Or will the consequences -- what are the consequences regardless of what kind of --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We would watch closely and there we would be prepared for there to be consequences, but we will look closely on what it is and we would align with our partners around the world.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And it'd be escalatory consequences like we've seen with Russia?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Again, I'm not going to get into a hypothetical at this point in time. We'll have those discussions directly with the Chinese and directly with our European partners.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And one more, short of getting any guarantees from China that they are not going to help Russia, and short of China condemning Russia for its actions. What is it about the call that President Biden thinks went well as he said to one of my colleagues?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I think it was an opportunity for the President to convey directly what our detailed efforts have been, what we have seen on the ground, how we have responded to the invasion, why do we have the significant level of concern that we have and also to convey very directly leader to leader what the implications and consequences would be if Russia provides material support. And that leader to leader engagement and opportunity for there to be a continuing discussion, certainly the President sees as a positive development. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, Jen. A couple of days ago, you described Javelins and Stingers as defensive weapons, and MiGs and planes as offensive weapons. So, how do we classify the armed drones that we're sending?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Any -- I would remind everybody that Russia is invading Ukraine not the other way around. Ukraine is not invading Russia. They are not going into a foreign country and invading that country. And what we're sending to Ukraine is security assistance, all of it and weapons that they are effectively using to defend their country against Russian aggression. And that is how all of this material would be categorized.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: In that sense then, wouldn't MiGs also be defensive since Ukraine is defending itself from attacks from Russia?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, again, this is all assessments made by our Defense Department but I would tell you that as it relates to the transfer of fighter planes from a US base in Germany into contested Ukrainian airspace, where our military and intelligence community determine the benefits provided to Ukraine's defense are low and the risks of escalation are high. That is how we assess that. But also those type of planes would be a different category of military assistance.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: It seems like we're splitting hairs over semantics, in terms of how we're classifying some of these weapon systems. And obviously, the President's gotten some criticism from both sides of the aisle for even when he takes action, sometimes doing it after there's been a lot of political pressure, for instance on imposing the sanctions, on sending the Stingers and the Javelins, on banning Russian oil. So if there's any chance that the US is going to facilitate the transfer of MiGs in some way, why not just do it now?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I think we've outlined pretty clearly why we've made the decision not to do that, including the fact that they have multiple squadrons of planes that they can utilize. And the fact that most importantly, the types of assistance that we've been providing anti-armor, anti-tank, anti-missiles, is what is effective in fighting this war on the ground. That is why we've provided that assistance.\par \par \uc2 None of the weapons we've sent to Ukraine could be used to launch an invasion of another -- of a country like Russia. That's a fact. That's why we call them defensive weapons. That's why the Defense Department calls them defensive weapons. And I would remind everybody that they are the country that are under attack.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: I want (inaudible) Iran talks. This week, the President said that we are crippling Putin's economy with punishing sanctions that are only going to grow more painful over time. But the State Department said that the administration would not sanction Russian participation in nuclear projects that are part of the JCPOA. So, how does the administration justify calling for such strong sanctions on Russia but giving them a pass when it comes to Iran? Is that hypocritical in some sense?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: There's absolutely no pass given. They're not getting additional assistance or sanctions relief of any sorts. There have an implementation role. They've had an implementation role in the past as it relates to the Iran nuclear deal, and that is certainly what we would be talking about in this regard. But for clarity purposes, there's absolutely no, no additional sanctions assistance that Russia would be receiving that is outside of the Iran nuclear deal.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So -- but just carving out what's already there and giving it protection, that it will not be sanctioned?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, to explain to you or for those of you who have covered it back in 2015, you may remember this. That there was -- there have been roles in the past that Russia has played in terms of the movement or the protection, the nuclear protections in some capacity of -- as we're implementing the Iran deal. And that's what we're really referring to, that's part of the discussion and the deal.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: This is a new deal that we're talking.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It's the similar implementation components. Its implementation and discussion around a similar deal that was discussed back in 2015.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: And then, the director of National Intelligence Annual Threat Assessment last month said that Iran has threatened to kill former and current US officials in retaliation for the US killing the IRGC's leader Qasem Soleimani. So did those threats constitute terrorism?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I would just remind you all that where we are. One, I'm not going to confirm or get into specifics about any threats through intelligence channels. But what where we are right now -- well, where we are right now is that not only has Iran's nuclear program advanced but their behavior in the region and beyond has gotten more aggressive, including by the Islamic Revolutionary Guard.\par \par \uc2 So the notion that the actions of the past administration pulling out of the Iran nuclear deal has cut down on the actions or the escalatory behavior of the Iranian Revolutionary Guard is inaccurate. They've actually, the Iranian government has actually doubled their budget or something like that. So the notion that the approach that the status quo is effective and making us safer just doesn't bear out in any form of facts.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So they should not then -- the IRGC be delisted from the terrorist blacklist?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: There's an ongoing negotiation. I'm not going to get into specifics of it but I would just note that the status quo where we stand has done nothing to make us safer in any regard. In fact, the Islamic Revolutionary Guard has only been strengthened. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Jen, I want to follow up with you on the call. You have -- from the podium and in the readout, it says the Presidents describe the implications and consequence --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: OK. We'll keep a close eye on that. Interesting exchange there on Iran and on the classification of offensive and defensive weapons in terms of what we're willing to supply the Ukrainians with. Moments ago, the White House released this photo of President Biden's call with President Xi Jinping of China. Obviously, a virtual call and that was the setup.\par \par \uc2 While back in Moscow, President Vladimir Putin held a huge rally for himself at an enormous stadium, showing that the people are galvanized behind this war in Ukraine. He quoted at one point from the Bible to justify what he calls this special operation that's going on in Ukraine. And he vowed victory against the promise of a world without Nazism. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN, PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translation): People who lived and live in the Donbas also did not agree with this coup against them. Punitive military operations were immediately organized, and not a single one of them was immediately loaded into the blockade was subjected to systematic shelling from cannons and aircraft strikes. This is what genocide is all about. It is precisely to save this suffering from this genocide. That is the main reason for the convincing motive and purpose of the military operation that began in the Donbas and Ukraine precisely for this purpose.\par \par \uc2 You know, I come to mind words from the Holy Scriptures, no more love as if someone gave their soul for their friends.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So very interesting moment there, at the rally that Vladimir Putin held. Let's bring in \uc2 James Carafano\uc2 , senior fellow for \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  and security and foreign policy analyst. James, great to have you back on the program. It's good to see you. What goes through your mind as you watch that argument being made by Vladimir Putin to that enormous stadium full of people, that all he's doing with this special mission is stopping a genocide that was underway against Russian speaking people in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARAFANO\uc2 , SENIOR FELLOW, \uc2 HERITAGE FOUNDATION\uc2 : Yes. Well, you know, at some point, propaganda becomes so illegitimate that the propaganda itself becomes the greatest indictment of the regime. You know, people continue today to watch "Triumph of the Will," and the film about the Nazi Olympics, which were intended by Hitler to promote what they were doing. And we watched them because we realize that this is the greatest indictment of what Hitler was trying to do.\par \par \uc2 So, what Putin is doing now from a propaganda standpoint is a complete disaster. It's actually he's indicting himself. And the fact that he has to do these rallies and makes these speeches, this is not where Vladimir Putin wanted to be today. He did not want to be sitting around trying to explain to his people yesterday, why Zelenskyy was still standing and addressing the \uc2 American Congress\uc2  and the German Bundestag three weeks after this war should have been over.\par \par \uc2 So I think this actually reflects a leader that is in trouble as opposed to one that's in charge.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He clearly felt, that's such a great point. Because there's Zelenskyy, speaking to the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , the \uc2 UK Parliament\uc2 , he's going to Japan in a couple of days. So he needed a counterpoint of strength to show. And in terms of the back and forth wars of winning over the hearts and minds here, I want to play this from Arnold Schwarzenegger, who obviously was a huge movie hero to people in Russia. His father fought on the side of the Nazis in World War II and Schwarzenegger said that it broke his father, his participation in that. So here's his message, I want to get your thoughts on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD SCHWARZENEGGER, ACTOR: The Russian soldiers listening to this broadcast, you already know much of the truth that has been speaking. You've seen it with your own eyes. I don't want you to be broken like my father. This is not the war to defend Russia. Your lives, your limps, your future futures are being sacrificed for senseless war contempt for the entire world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So it seems as if this was produced on a channel that might get through to some people in Russia, how big of an impact or something like that have do you think?\par \par \uc2 CARAFANO: Actually, what's really had an impact, which has really, really made a difference, not just in Russia but around the world, is Ukrainian social media. They have been devastating, absolutely, completely devastating on the Russians and on showing the rest of the world what's really going on here. So they have actually taken Vladimir Putin to school on how to talk to the world and convince people about where justice really is.\par \par \uc2 People are going to write books about in the middle of this war, the Ukrainians throwing together an information campaign, which has been completely devastating the other side, with no resources and no capability while they're being shot at. That's impressive.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is stunning. It is stunning and it is impressive. I want to ask you before I let you go about this conversation between President Xi and President Putin. What -- I mean, President Xi and President Biden, I should say. So what's your takeaway from the conversation and what we've heard about what they talked about?\par \par \uc2 CARAFANO: You know, you guys made me sit in this chair and have to listen to that press conference, and what a bunch of nonsense. I felt like -- that was like -- that was torture. Listen, this was laughable. This was a meeting that accomplished nothing. Biden did it just to look presidential. He got nothing out of this. Xi did it because it gave him a chance to poke this. But this is reflective of all these conversations going on between all these leaders. They're just talking to each other.\par \par \uc2 None of them have a plan. The plan ended three weeks ago. Everybody is making it up as going along. And I think we shouldn't be buried about our President as being the worst. He is just having conversations to prove that he's relevant. That is nonsense.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What would be a good takeaway? What would you have said to President Biden, you need to come out of that phone conversation and be able to say that you told President Xi X, Y.\par \par \uc2 CARAFANO: Look, he did -- having the conversation with Xi was stupid, because there was nothing you could get out of it. If you want to send them a threat, put it in a letter and send it to him. Don't give the other guy a stage to make you look like a Yahoo. This is, literally, this is statecraft malpractice.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow. \uc2 James Carafano\uc2  with some criticism there, good to see you this afternoon. Thank you very much. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 CARAFANO: Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So nearly 6.5 million Ukrainians do not have a place to live in their own country. They have been displaced in their own homes. More than 3.2 million others have made it out, women and children mostly of the country. One of them, a wife whose husband was supervising Ukraine's largest nuclear power plant when it came under attack by Russia, she calls it one of the most terrible nights of her life. Her stunning interview with Fox, with correspondent Aishah Hasnie who is near the border with the rest of that story.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha, good afternoon to you. Her name is Katarina. She was in hiding when we found her. She tells us that she hasn't spoken to or she hasn't seen her husband for about three weeks because he chose to stay behind to protect Europe from a nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 So Katarina's husband whose name we are not disclosing to protect him, supervises the department in charge of cooling the nuclear reactors at this power plant in Southeast Ukraine. Katarina fled with her children days before the Russians attack that power plant, so she was watching with the rest of us as the Russians fired rockets at the compound while her husband was still inside.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATARINA, WIFE OF NUCLEAR POWER PLANT WORKER (through translation): It was the most terrible night of my life. It would be a huge tragedy for Europe and not just for our city. If something happens there, even Poland will not be able to save its people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So Katarina says Russian soldiers are walking around right now inside the plant with guns, but she says her husband is safe. The \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  though, Martha, is gravely concerned about these staff members, these employees being unable to make decisions without Russian influence. And Katarina says her husband is a hero not only to Ukraine but the entire continent of Europe because he is staying even though he's now separated from his family. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Remarkable. Aishah, thank you very much. Aishah Hasnie reporting from Poland.\par \par \uc2 So, the US inches closer to a new Iran nuclear deal that sweetens the deal in some ways for Moscow.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER US AMBASSADOR TO THE UN: You can't sleep with the devil or the devil will own you. I will say that to the last breath that I have. And the fact that Biden continues to talk to China, talk to Iran, talk to Russia, talk to the Iranians, like it's ludicrous. And all he's doing is making the world more dangerous and he's making the US look more weak.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So just moments ago, new reaction from the White House to where the Obama era Iran nuclear deal stands, as critics argue it will provide billions of dollars in sanction relief for both Iran and to Russia. Senior national correspondent Rich Edson reports live from the State Department. Hi, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon, Martha. And now Israel's foreign minister and prime minister are warning the United States against removing Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps as a foreign terrorist organization. This is what they write just earlier today, "The IRGC is a terrorist organization that has murdered thousands of people, including Americans. We refuse to believe that the US would remove its designation as a terrorist organization to do so in exchange for a promise not to harm Americans."\par \par \uc2 Sources briefed on the consideration say the Biden administration has made no decisions on this yet. Last weekend, the IRGC launched a dozen missiles into Northern Iraq, just missing a US Consulate building in Erbil. The IRGC said it was targeting an Israeli facility. A US Foreign Terrorist designation basically makes it illegal to conduct business with that group. All of this while the US and its European allies are working with Iran, Russia and China to revive a nuclear agreement. The White House argues the Trump administration's withdrawal has only made the IRGC more aggressive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The status quo where we stand has done nothing to make us safer in any regard. In fact, the Islamic Revolutionary Guard has only been strengthened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Though opponents of a new nuclear agreement with Iran would argue that the United States here, in entering into a deal, does not touch the IRGC at all. This is strictly about Iran's nuclear program, and so there would be no change in behavior in theory, those argue, if the nuclear deal will revive and Iran could still have more money they say to fund its IRGC and its ballistic missile program. Martha?\par \par \uc2 If the nuclear deal were revived and Iran could still have more money they say to fund its IRGC and its ballistic missile program. Martha\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Rich, thank you very much, Rich Edson at the State Department.\par \par \uc2 So that is THE STORY for this Friday, March 18th. THE STORY goes on. We will see you back here on Monday at 3:00. Have a good weekend, stick around.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220319ei3i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Gas Prices Higher; Manhattan DA Reverses Crime Policies; Judge Blocks Youngkin's Mask Order; China and Russia Plotting?; President Biden Touts Strong Jobs Numbers; Interview With White House Economic Adviser Jared Bernstein\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Meredith, David Lee Miller, Mike Gunzelman, Kat Timpf, Nicole  Valdes, Phil Flynn, Lucas Tomlinson, Jacqui Heinrich, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 8,388 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 February 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: President Biden touting today stronger- than-expected jobs report, while acknowledging prices for food and energy are just still too high. He says hiking spending on his social agenda will bring those prices down. But will it?\par \par \uc2 We will ask Jared Bernstein with the \uc2 White House Council of Economic advisers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hello and welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First to Jacqui Heinrich. She's live at the White House. And she's got the very latest\par \par \uc2 Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, the president said this report makes it clear that COVID no longer has the same grip on the economy that it used to. They pointed to upward revisions for unemployment data in both November and December during a COVID spike, and this huge, way-greater-than-expected January jobs report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our country is taking everything that COVID has to throw at us, and we have come back stronger. I'm pleased to report this morning what many of you already know, that America's job machine is going stronger than ever, fueling a strong recovery and opportunity for hardworking women and men all across this great country.\par \par \uc2 America is back to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Four hundred and sixty-seven thousand jobs were added last month, the biggest gains in leisure and hospitality, 151,000 hires there, and also 108,000 more in bars and restaurants, showing that people are vacationing, they're dining, they're drinking, despite COVID fears. And that is all driving hiring.\par \par \uc2 And it ran out Biden's first 12 months in office with a record-breaking 6.6 million jobs. That's the strongest of any president's first year in office. Unemployment ticked up slightly to 4 percent. But that's largely because of labor force participation growing to its highest rate since February 2020, climbing to 6.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 And wages also climbed to 5.7 percent in January from the previous year. That's nearly double the pre-pandemic average.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of those gains there in wages have been eroded by rising inflation. It's the hottest in nearly four decades. Everyday prices for things like food and gas have been going up, gas at a seven-year high today.\par \par \uc2 But the administration here at the White House does not seem to have any new plan to bring down gas prices.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN DEESE, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: We need to keep that wage growth up and we need to get prices down. And that's actually what our focus is.\par \par \uc2 It's important that we're able to do that in America from a position of unique economic strength, because economic growth is as strong as it is. And that's not true of other developed countries around the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Just yesterday, \uc2 OPEC\uc2  again rebuffed the president's request to increase production to mitigate some of that inflation. He's been asking since January and getting the same answer -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, Jacqui Heinrich live from the White House on that, thank you.\par \par \uc2 How do you balance this strong economy with surging prices?\par \par \uc2 With us now, Jared Bernstein, a member of the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jared, great to have you here. And it's good to get some answers from the White House on this.\par \par \uc2 So, I will start off by asking you about the jobs report.\par \par \uc2 First off, expectations were set by, I believe, the economists at the White House advising the president that this was going to be a pretty bad report because of the spread of Omicron in January. It didn't turn out that way. How do you explain it?\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, \uc2 WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS\uc2 : Well, as the great famous economist Yogi Berra once said, predictions are hard, especially about the future.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: It is -- look...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But that's what economists are paid for, so...\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, look, it is extremely difficult to forecast during the kind of environment we're in now, with great uncertainty around the pandemic. We knew that, in fact, eight million people were absent from work this week, unable to get to work because they were either sick or taking care of people who are sick.\par \par \uc2 Even so, we managed to add 460,000 jobs. Now, here's the thing, Sandra. We don't focus on one month of job growth, precisely because of these noisy ups and downs in this pandemic environment.\par \par \uc2 Over the last year, you just said the numbers yourself, 6.6 million jobs is president's first year, unprecedented historic job growth, over the last three months, 540,000 jobs per month.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: That is a breakneck pace. And it is an underlying trend. And that's what's so important about it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But what is also a trend is sinking poll numbers with this presidency, in particular with his handling of the economy.\par \par \uc2 So, based on everything you just said, you would expect that he's polling very well. He is not. And a lot of the reason for that, we are told, is because of inflation. And we know the average American family is dealing with skyrocketing home heating bills and energy prices.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: They're paying more at the gas tank -- or to fill their gas tank. They're paying more at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 So, explain this. Why is the president touting this strong economic growth and a jump in wages, as you see there...\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: ... hourly earnings up 5.7 percent, when inflation is up 7 percent? It's not keeping pace with that. So, you can make the case that real wages are actually down.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: It's precisely the way the president is talking about this that's going to get us out of this mess.\par \par \uc2 He is pointing out that we have to do two things simultaneously. This is a walk-and-chew-gum moment. We have to make sure that we maintain the tightest job market in history with job gains that we haven't seen in any first year of any president, at the same time, at the same time that we do everything we can to ease price pressures.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Now, you talked about...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, let's dig into that.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, wait. Let me a number that you -- let me give you a number you didn't cite.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: You talked about wage gains in the private sector, correct, 5.7 year over year.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the 80 percent of the work force that's blue-collar and manufacturing or non-managers and services, so middle- and lower-paid workers, their pay went up 6.9 percent, just about at the rate of inflation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That's still not keeping pace with inflation.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, the point is that we have to keep those wage pressures going, while we do everything with the ports, ship to shelf, make sure that the trucking sector is getting goods to -- from warehouses...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: ... warehousing and transportation, strong job gains back to above its pre-pandemic level.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I don't hear gas prices, because, right now, you're looking at an eight-year high for the price of oil, topping $90 this week. And you're looking at prices at the pump up more than a buck from a year ago, at a seven-year high.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, I talked to Brian Deese in the 2:00 hour. He's advising the president. I didn't hear anything new about what we're doing to bring those gas prices down.\par \par \uc2 What's the plan?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, the plan is to make sure that forecasts for lower prices, including gas prices, comes out the way it's predicted.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me tell you that prediction. Virtually every forecast we can find and -- you can go look at this yourself -- I know you have access to all the market data -- has not just the price of energy coming down, but the price of inflation overall easing towards the end of the year. In fact...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, that's not just going to happen. What's the plan?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: In fact, in fact, the expectation is that prices are growing half as fast at the end of this year as they were next year.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You just pooh-poohed forecasts.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, the plan to make sure we get there -- the plan to make sure that forecast comes true is to do everything we can to keep goods getting through the ports as quickly as possible, ship to shelf.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a metric that says...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That's not going to help gas prices. How do you bring gas prices down?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: There is a metric that explains how well shelves are stocked. It is typically 91 percent before the pandemic. Now it's 81 percent.\par \par \uc2 In terms of gas...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Jared, with all due respect, I think you're ignoring the question. What are we doing to increase oil production in this country today?\par \par \uc2 We are by the day becoming more reliant on foreign nations for their oil, when we were very recently energy-independent in this country.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Oil is a global price.\par \par \uc2 Now, a few months ago, the president released 50 million gallons from the Strategic Reserve. And, certainly, that helped matters. If that's something we have to look back again at, we will.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: How did it help? Oil and gasoline prices have only gone up since then.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, in fact, prices fell by about 10 percent a gallon around that period.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: They're now above that level.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Gas prices -- yes, gas prices are going to go up and down, as they always were. This is a global price.\par \par \uc2 What we can do is make sure that the American worker has the best earnings opportunities that they have had in generations. And that is precisely what this labor market is delivering.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think people hear no plan. And that's worrisome.\par \par \uc2 Again, with due respect, I don't mean to cut you off, but I asked a simple question. What is the White House plan to bring oil and gasoline prices down for the American consumer?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: And I'm giving you what I believe is a simple answer.\par \par \uc2 We got here with a less-than-1-percent vaccination rate. We said we're going to make sure that we get the vaccination rate up to 75 percent for adults, where it is right now, and make sure that checks in pockets and shots in arms get us to the other side of this crisis.\par \par \uc2 We had -- and I don't hear you talking about this. And I'd like to hear it. We had GDP growth last year of 5.7 percent, real, inflation-adjusted, inflation-adjusted.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I have acknowledged it. And I have said it could be even higher if we actually got out of the way a little bit.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, look, it can be always be higher.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But I have certainly acknowledged that growth is good.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: That's the highest growth rate in almost -- that's the highest growth rate in almost 40 years.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The point...\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So you're pulling out one variable, one global price, and saying, look only at that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Whoa. One global price that American families deal with every single day.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I'm telling you there's a more too look at here.\par \par \uc2 Consider the job market. Consider the fact that there are 11 million job openings, an historical record. So let's look at the full record. Let's look at real gains in GDP. Let's look at wage growth of almost 7 percent.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Is that an acknowledgement that bringing gas prices down is not a priority for this White House?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: It is -- the priority for this White House is to make sure that working people share broadly-based real wage gains.\par \par \uc2 And the way they're going to get there is to continue the president's program of implementing the Rescue Plan, shots in arms, checks in pockets, continue the kind of breakneck job growth that we have seen today, and wage gains that for 80 percent of the work force at this point are close to the rate of inflation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Now, that rate is expected to come down over the rest of this year, and we're going to do everything we can to ensure that it does.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. Is there a plan to increase oil production -- that's a simple question -- in this country?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: There are definitely plans to increase production in the private sector, both in this country and in other countries.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. So we will dig into more details on that the next time I talk to you.\par \par \uc2 But I want to focus on inflation in other areas, like food prices, because this is a real problem. I mean, we talk to everyday people all the time, and they talk about the pain when they go to the grocery store. It is a real problem.\par \par \uc2 What we have heard from this administration -- and it's almost shocking at times, because I have seen no evidence that this is the case. Maybe you have some. But there is a blaming of the companies that produce some of these goods. There's the blaming of the meat companies for the high prices.\par \par \uc2 We have seen the blaming of the energy companies for high oil and gas prices. Do you have some sort of evidence that these companies are colluding to raise prices to the American consumer?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, the point that the president has made consistently, particularly in the meatpacking discussion, is that when 85 percent of meatpacking is controlled by four firms, that's a heavily concentrated industry.\par \par \uc2 And when the absence of competition is present like that, we tend to see price levels that are pretty inflated. There's a lot of research to support that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But that is an accusation of collusion. Is that the allegation you're making?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I have never heard the president make an accusation of collusion. So, let's not put words in his mouth.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: To suggest that they're all -- but to suggest that they're all getting together to raise prices, wouldn't that have to be the case?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No. No. That's not correct.\par \par \uc2 The idea is that, when there's that kind of concentration in an industry, it doesn't mean people are sitting around a table in a cabal and colluding. It means that there's not enough competition.\par \par \uc2 And, typically, when there's not enough competition, somewhere, there's a barrier to entry. Now, what's interesting here is, a minute ago, you were you were getting all over us on the oil side. Now, when we're talking about trying to increase competition in this sector of the economy, that's very much exactly what we're trying to do.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Competition is good. It's capitalism. I was just asking...\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: It is.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: For them to all be raising prices at the same time, in an anti- competitive environment, they'd have to be in on it.\par \par \uc2 That's the only point I'm making.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, look, there are lots of different -- there are lots of different sectors raising prices right now. That's one of the reasons inflation is so elevated.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: All we're saying is that if you look at industries with a lot more competition, there tends to be less price pressure.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So there's that correlation between concentration and elevated price levels. And the president intends to try to bring those down on behalf of the American people.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 I really appreciate the discussion. I hope you come back on. Thank you, Jared.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: My pleasure. Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right.\par \par \uc2 And a quick look at the quarter of Wall and Broad, pretty quiet day for the Dow, but another really good week for your money, all the major averages closing higher for the second straight week, with investors able to shake off worries about inflation and those expected rate hikes that will result.\par \par \uc2 And some volatility in the tech sector as well involving \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and more, so we will be watching for that.\par \par \uc2 Now that we know what Russia is planning, what is China plotting, as these two team up? Why General Keith Kellogg says we have got to do more to take these threats down.\par \par \uc2 And none of it helping the price of oil, now topping $92 a barrel, pump prices at a seven-year high. Is there any relief in sight?\par \par \uc2 Plus, one major airline making a move that has flight attendants worried we will see more of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sit down! Don't you dare touch...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Don't you dare touch...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Let the Games begin.\par \par \uc2 Russia's President Putin and China's President Xi pushing back against the U.S. in Beijing today ahead of the Winter Olympics kicking off.\par \par \uc2 What does it all mean for the Russia-Ukraine standoff? The last time China hosted the Olympics in 2008, Russia invaded Georgia as Putin was also in Beijing to watch the Opening Ceremonies.\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Lucas Tomlinson is in Kyiv, Ukraine, with more.\par \par \uc2 Everybody's trying to read the tea leaves here to see what's coming next.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS PENTAGON PRODUCER: Good evening from Ukraine, Sandra. That's right.\par \par \uc2 And the last time the Russian army invaded Georgia, it was about half the size of the Russian army that's now massed on Ukraine's border. Now, earlier today, Secretary of State \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  spoke to his Polish counterpart.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Prefer that Russia choose the path of diplomacy and dialogue, but if it does not, we are fully prepared for the alternative.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  met his Chinese counterpart on the sidelines of the Winter Olympics beginning in Beijing today, a diplomatic shot across the bow to the United States, who launched a diplomatic boycott over concerns about Chinese human rights abuses.\par \par \uc2 And the U.S. will not be sending any officials to the Games. In a joint statement issued between Putin and Chinese President Xi, both leaders expressed their opposition to \uc2 NATO\uc2  enlargement, a not-so-subtle message to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, FOX was given rare access to attend live-fire training by Ukrainian forces in an abandoned city outside Chernobyl. Ambush, sniper, advanced first aid, firefighting and search-and-rescue highlighted the day, all in preparation for a potential urban warfare should Russian forces invade.\par \par \uc2 I asked Ukraine's defense minister if he was worried.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: How concerned are you about a potential Russian invasion from Belarus?\par \par \uc2 OLEKSIY REZNIKOV, UKRAINIAN DEFENSE MINISTER: I'm not concerned. I'm not concerned.\par \par \uc2 I'm very, very, very sure that Ukrainian armed forces are ready to deter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: And speaking of deterrence, \uc2 NATO\uc2  says, yesterday, a pair of F- 15 Strike Eagles launched from Estonia intercepted four Russian fighter jets over the Black Sea -- excuse me -- the Baltic Sea.\par \par \uc2 The Russian jets were not squawking their modes and codes. They needed to be identified visually -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, Lucas Tomlinson live from Ukraine for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, with China watching, what should President Biden be doing? The read on that now from former National Security Adviser to former Vice President Pence. General Keith Kellogg joins us now.\par \par \uc2 General great to see you. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 So China is going to be watching this closely. What should Biden be doing?\par \par \uc2 GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET.), FORMER PENCE NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Well, thanks, Sandra. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 Look, he ought to be watching what he's doing on the grand strategy, is Putin, because he's really doing a great job with it. Putin is doing it.\par \par \uc2 Look, Putin goes to Beijing, starts the Olympics, has a meeting with President Xi that's televised to all of Russia. And they come out with a joint communique that says they're going to support each other without any conditions going forward. So what did Putin do?\par \par \uc2 He just shored himself up against any sanctions the West is going to apply, because that's the world's second largest economy, and they're not going to support it. And then, last week, what does Putin do? He meets with \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , the prime minister of Hungary, who's a \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally. In a bilateral in Moscow next week, he meets with Macron of France, who's going to Kyiv, and then into Moscow.\par \par \uc2 And then \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2 , the chancellor of Germany, says he doesn't want to push sanctions, heavy sanctions on Russia, regardless of what happens. And Moscow is prepared to invade. They have got their forces they need if they want to go in.\par \par \uc2 Here's what Biden should not have done. Biden should not have made this a \uc2 NATO\uc2  issue. It's a European issue, not a \uc2 NATO\uc2  issue. With all of the allies in \uc2 NATO\uc2  now going to Moscow and weakening \uc2 NATO\uc2 's position, it actually weakens \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If we had said to everybody there, this is a European issue, you solve it, and keep \uc2 NATO\uc2  in reserve. It would have been -- it would have helped things out a lot for us.\par \par \uc2 Remember, of the 30 nations, if Ukraine wants to be admitted to \uc2 NATO\uc2 , all 30 of them have to agree. And, right now, they're not going to get 30 votes to come into \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And what it has done, it has weakened Ukraine's position and weakens Washington's positions.\par \par \uc2 And what it is set up, it has set up Putin perfectly. If he wants to invade, he will. He's got the capacity right now to do it. Interestingly enough, he invaded after the 2008 Olympics into Georgia, and he followed into Crimea after the Sochi Olympics.\par \par \uc2 It sort of it seems to be a benchmark for him if he wants to go. He's given us a master class. One thing about a grand strategist, if you can win something and succeed at something without fighting, you are without peer in strategy.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Really interesting analysis, as well as on the timing of a potential invasion.\par \par \uc2 General, given how the Pentagon's describing Russia planning this false flag operation, it is so detailed in nature. Just how soon could this happen?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: I think it could happen overnight.\par \par \uc2 And, first of all, the first indicator you're going to get if there's any conflict in Ukraine, they're going to hit them with a massive cyberattack right off the top, because that's how they do it. They did it in 2008. They do this in 2014. That's how militaries -- modern militaries do it. Then they follow with what's the Russians call their VDV, which are their special operators and airborne forces.\par \par \uc2 If they're worried about a false flag, what the president should do, he can declassify anything he wants to. I would declassify that and immediately show the American people what's really out there, because there's a lot of skepticism about it.\par \par \uc2 This goes back to Colin Powell going to \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  before we invaded Iraq, said there's weapons of mass destruction. And people saw it and said, no, wasn't really true, didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 And then yesterday, \uc2 Ned Price\uc2 , the State Department spokesman, was pushed on that, and he couldn't really respond to it. So the response should be from the administration, let me show you what I have. This is the reason we say that, because it is a good indicator they will come forward with a false flag, but they don't have to.\par \par \uc2 They're prepared to go on a moment's notice if they want to. They don't need a false flag operation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: General, I will ask you a question I asked Admiral Kirby when he joined us from the Pentagon yesterday, same time yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe that the administration was sending any sort of message to Putin, to Russia with that most recent raid by U.S. special forces in Syria as any sort of deterrent?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: No, not really, Sandra because that operation has actually been planned since 2019, when -- in 2019, when Baghdadi named al-Qurashi -- and that's one of his nom de guerres, the names that he uses -- as his successor, he basically signed the guy's death one, because, in 2020, we designate him a state-designated global terrorist.\par \par \uc2 And we put a -- Mike Pompeo put a $10 billion bounty on his head back in 2020. The message we're sending to terrorists around the world, you can run, but -- and you can try to hide, but we're going to find you. And we actually did that. That's a continuation of efforts out there.\par \par \uc2 That's something we kind of do all along. It's kind of an offset that we work with. And that was -- been in the planning for a long time. And the reason we put a $10 million bounty on his head in 2020 was, $10 million, it opens up a lot mouths out there with people giving them this information.\par \par \uc2 This guy was a dead man walking as soon as he was designated a successor. If somebody said to me, you want to be the new leader of the ISIS caliphate, I would say, no thanks. Go to my neighbor over here, because it's not a long-term job.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: General, switching gears, the Pentagon today saying the suicide bomb attack at Hamid Karzai International Airport in Afghanistan that killed 13 U.S. service members and injured at least 170 civilians was conducted by a single attacker and not followed by enemy gunfire.\par \par \uc2 What was your reaction to those new details?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes, well, I wasn't surprised by it.\par \par \uc2 That's kind of the normal, the way they did it out there, out on these kinds of attacks. And we actually made a bad shot when we actually killed some civilians, but we thought it was in response to it. These kind of reports don't surprise me, because there's a fog of war out there.\par \par \uc2 And I used to tell the president and the vice president, I would say, there's an old axiom you need to follow. The first report is always wrong in warfare, and you should understand that. So it's not surprising.\par \par \uc2 Look, Sandra, very candidly, it was a debacle in Afghanistan. It's something happened that shouldn't have happened. We should have been put in that position as the United States. Biden put us in that position. He got some -- I think he got some good advice that he just didn't follow. And we ended up losing 13 great Americans, plus hundreds of civilians as well.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And you joined us all throughout that time, General. We appreciate you joining us this evening. Thank you very much, sir.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Thanks, Sandra. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 The Arlington -- to Arlington, Virginia, now, I should say. A judge temporarily blocking Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin's mask order there, ruling in favor of seven school districts. Youngkin had issued mandate making masks optional, as you all remember. We're going to have more on that as we get it.\par \par \uc2 And to a major storm now putting flights on ice, more than 8,000 cancellations in just the past 48 hours, as this monster storm stretches from Texas to the Northeast. We're going to have the very latest for you.\par \par \uc2 And Manhattan's liberal DA taking back some of those soft-on-crime policies after taking enormous heat. But is it too little too late?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The move from a major airline that has flight attendants worrying. This is how their next flight could be looking.\par \par \uc2 We report, you might want to hide.\par \par \uc2 Back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: With pressure mounting on Manhattan DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  from police and other groups, the DA issuing a memo reversing two of his most controversial policies.\par \par \uc2 Robberies involving a gun or knife will now once again be treated as felonies.\par \par \uc2 To David Lee Miller in New York City with reaction to that reversal -- David Lee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, the city's new district attorney, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , is now responding to criticism that he has been soft on crime.\par \par \uc2 Today, he told his fellow prosecutors that he is essentially changing course in a number of areas. The question now, is this going to be enough to appease his critics?\par \par \uc2 Soon after his election, Bragg told his staff to only seek jail time for the most serious crimes. Today, coinciding with a pro-police rally denouncing Bragg's policies, he released a letter that takes a tougher stance on violent crimes.\par \par \uc2 Bragg's critics have called for him to be ousted from his new job.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MCMANUS, BRONX COUNTY CONSERVATIVE PARTY: He needs to be removed from office, resign or withdraw his policies. He's corrupting the office of the district attorney by imposing his personal ideology into the office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Now, coinciding with that rally, Bragg released a letter to his staff that spelled out how prosecutors should charge some defendants now.\par \par \uc2 One portion reads -- and I quote -- "A commercial robbery with a gun will be charged as a felony whether or not the gun is operable, loaded or a realistic imitation."\par \par \uc2 He also went on to say the use of a knife or a weapon during a commercial robbery would also be treated as a felony. Other cases, he wrote, will be decided based on the aggravating or mitigating circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the latest statistics show that crime is surging in New York, comparing last January to a year ago. Overall crime up 39 percent. Auto theft has increased 92 percent. Robbery and felony assault increased 33 percent. The one statistic that shows a decrease is murder, the murder rate down 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 And, lastly, Sandra, in New York is not unique here. A number of other major cities are also seeing spikes in crime, among them, Washington, D.C., Chicago and Philadelphia -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And many more.\par \par \uc2 David Lee Miller in New York for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Gas prices, meanwhile, surging to a seven-year high. Why the pain at the pump may be just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 And another winter storm leaving drivers spinning, literally.\par \par \uc2 And FOX Weather's Nicole Valdes is in the thick of it with what everyone is dealing with -- Nicole.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, both the ice and the snow from that storm impacting millions of Americans on the road and at the airports, even at home.\par \par \uc2 So, how long will this problem persist? Well, we will explain that coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Breaking news out of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 A circuit court judge in Arlington has ruled against Governor Glenn Youngkin's executive order on mask mandates. He wanted to make them optional for schools.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Mark Meredith. He's got the latest from there.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 This all began with an executive order that the new governor signed into law right after taking office in January. And then, almost instantly, there was a lot of criticism from some school boards around the state that said they wanted to challenge this.\par \par \uc2 The governor's executive order was basically allowing parents and students to say if they wanted to wear masks on campus, basically to block these schools from mandating that kids would have to wear masks. And then, this week, we saw seven different school systems throughout the state of Virginia, take this executive order to court, as you mentioned, in Arlington, Virginia. That's just outside D.C.\par \par \uc2 And they were asking the judge for this temporary restraining order for enforcement of this executive order. And, late today, we have learned that the judge has sided with those seven school systems, arguing that there were a number of different reasons here, one being, of course, the public health interest, this judge believing that this would be a detriment to those kids that are still in school right now that may be worried about their own public health.\par \par \uc2 And there's also been a concern about what has been included in Virginia's laws ever since last year, when schools went back, and whether or not they would have to follow CDC guidance, which, of course, encourages masks.\par \par \uc2 The governor has long said that he does not believe that this is about banning masks altogether. He just wishes that there would be a choice in all of this. We have not yet heard from Governor Youngkin's office. This only broke a few minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 But I would imagine he will have a lot to say, because this is an issue that he campaigned on heavily. We heard from the lieutenant governor earlier in the week on FOX. And she said they are not going to stop fighting for this -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And we will keep watching it.\par \par \uc2 Mark Meredith, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: You bet.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Now to the scene out of Kerrville, Texas, icy conditions stranding hundreds of vehicles for hours, the blast also putting air travel on ice, brutal conditions forcing 3,700 flight cancellations today.\par \par \uc2 That's on top of 5,000 frozen out yesterday.\par \par \uc2 To FOX Weather's Nicole Valdes in Paducah, Kentucky, with the very latest from there.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Nicole.\par \par \uc2 VALDES: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, it is truly a travel nightmare, impacting millions of Americans across the country, as this winter storm really brought everything to the table. We're talking sleet, freezing rain, and snow, so really a compounding problem.\par \par \uc2 And the story here in Kentucky really is no different. We know of at least 80-weather related crashes reported in the state as of the last few days, and roads in 12 different counties forced to close, including at one point Interstate 24, as cars just slid off of those icy roads.\par \par \uc2 Now we also know, in other areas, there are trees down, power lines, even semitrucks blocking traffic. And, on top of that, we still have hundreds of thousands of people without power across the country. If we're focusing on Kentucky, we know of at least three school districts that have canceled classes for today.\par \par \uc2 And the transportation cabinet telling us at one point they had every single plow, every single salt truck and what they call snow fighters out on the road trying to either treat them before that snow came down last night or try to move all of that ice off the roads.\par \par \uc2 Now, it is worth mentioning that this is a state that is still reeling and trying to recover from the destruction of several deadly tornadoes that just carved through many of these communities back in December. And now they're just feeling the impacts of this ice storm, which, of course, could be setting them back even further.\par \par \uc2 Now, you will notice the sun is out now, which, of course, should start to melt that ice, if it hasn't already. But, as that might solve some problems, you can imagine it might create others. Not only are we looking at the potential for flooding and even more traffic woes, but we could also see more power outages, if that ice that starts to melt off those lines causes those lines to touch there, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, Nicole Valdes on that.\par \par \uc2 Our best to all those who are struggling through this. We hope they get through it safely. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, with temperatures coming down, home heating bills have been going up, with natural gas up more than 20 percent this year alone, and just this week, gas and oil prices both surging to seven-year highs, with no slowdown in sight.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from FOX business contributor and our go-to oil expert, \uc2 Phil Flynn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Phil, it's always good to see you. Especially good to end the week with you.\par \par \uc2 PHIL FLYNN\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We don't like what we're seeing with these energy prices. It's not -- it's not great. We just had Jared Bernstein on from the White House. We asked what I thought was a very simple question. What is the plan to bring these energy prices down?\par \par \uc2 I didn't hear anything new from him to do that. But something has to be done, because this is a lot of pain for the American people.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: You're absolutely right, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 And the White House has had a very disjointed energy policy from day one. And I call it a cancel culture energy policy. They wanted to cancel the U.S. energy industry. We're not going to talk to those people. Oh, we're about green energy. We're just going to put more regulations, more taxes, let them deal with it.\par \par \uc2 Well, guess what? The American people are dealing with it, Sandra. You know that. We're seeing unprecedented pain from drivers, from people with their heating bills. And you look at these situations like we have in Texas and around the country. More people are feeling the pain.\par \par \uc2 And all these wage gains I have been hearing about are going into their gas tank.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And then -- but that is fact, I mean, the gas tank, the grocery bill, the home heating bill. You got wage growth 5.7 percent. That would be great if inflation wasn't up 7 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, everything more that folks are making is going towards these bills. What is the solution? What can we do tomorrow to improve this situation, Phil?\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: I would send a message to the world that the U.S. energy producer is coming back. We're not in retreat anymore.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden administration has failed to do that. In fact, they have said that the U.S. energy, oil and gas, is in retreat. And that sends a message. Words matter. Investment dollars matter.\par \par \uc2 And they can't make up their mind whether they're for natural gas or against natural gas, right? We hear from the Biden administration that they want to cancel pipelines. They want to encourage other countries to export more natural gas to Europe in case there's a war, but yet then we have Senator Warren leading a group of suitors saying, we want to ban U.S. natural gas exports to keep prices low here.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't work that way.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You know, Phil, and you and I had a conversation offline the other day about the amount of oil that we could have been bringing down from Canada through that XL Pipeline that was then -- the building of it was canceled, would be about the same as the amount of oil that we are now buying from Russia, which is significantly more than we were buying just a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 I'm out of time, but, Phil, we will continue the conversation. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, so a lot of pain for consumers, but President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  riding a wave of big wins this week.\par \par \uc2 How does pollster Lee Carter see all this playing out?\par \par \uc2 And are we about to see a lot more of this? Why flight attendants are worried.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Some big wins for the White House this week, both here and abroad.\par \par \uc2 The successful January employment report showed a surge in hiring, and the successful raid in Syria taking out the ISIS leader. But the administration is still dealing with inflation that is not slowing and a debt that keeps growing.\par \par \uc2 Which factors have a greater impact on voting?\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from GOP pollster Lee Carter.\par \par \uc2 Lee, it's great to spend a Friday with you.\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER, REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: Great to...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, so, talk to us.\par \par \uc2 What is going to impact voters the most, as the Biden administration takes a couple of victory laps this week?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: I think what's impacting people the most is, frankly, the impact on our pocketbooks right now,.\par \par \uc2 People are feeling really negative about the economy, but not about the economy and jobs as it was in 2016. What people are worried about right now is the economy and inflation. People are having trouble paying their bills. We were just talking about the gas prices. It's out of control.\par \par \uc2 Five out of six Americans are saying they're worse off today than they were just a year ago. And these are issues that are impacting every person that's living in this country.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, there were absolute victories for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  this week, and we should be very, very appreciative of the fact that they were great -- great jobs numbers. The victory against ISIS is another big win. But when it comes down to it, when you look at people, what they're concerned about, they're concerned about the inflation, and they're concerned about the economy. They're concerned about crime.\par \par \uc2 And on all of these measures, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not doing well. In fact, he's lost 30 points of support on all three of those issues. And those are the issues that are most important. So might this be the turn in momentum? Perhaps, but I don't see it really having a huge impact on his polling numbers overall.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You mentioned momentum.\par \par \uc2 I -- we all sure hope that this pandemic is coming to an end soon. And we do have some new numbers to report here out of the AP just a moment ago. Lee, the U.S. death toll from COVID-19 just topped 900,000, propelled in part by the wildly contagious Omicron variant.\par \par \uc2 And while we acknowledge that -- the great loss that this country and around the world has been -- suffered, we do look at the fact that things are starting to get better. And you look at that jobs report today, and the expectations were so low because of the spread of Omicron during the month in which the jobs report came out.\par \par \uc2 But it does look like things can start to get better, Lee.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: It does look that way.\par \par \uc2 And, look, only 50 percent of Americans right now approve of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's handling of COVID. He promised he was going to put an end to it. They didn't feel like, when the surge happened, that we were prepared with testing, that we were prepared with all of the things that we needed.\par \par \uc2 They didn't feel that we got through Delta the way they should. And so there's a lot of people who still have concern. Things could turn the corner now. I hope they do. The question is, is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  going to be able to capitalize on that? Are Democrats going to be able to capitalize on it?\par \par \uc2 Or have the tides shifted away from the message of the Democrats, was very much, let government intervene and help us out of this mess, to the Republican message, which is very much about let's have individuals make the decisions that are best for them and their families, and let's get back to living?\par \par \uc2 And so I think it's going to be a big question about where the momentum shifts right now as we go into the midterms. I'm not sure that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's up for capitalizing on this moment. He certainly came to New York this week, had a big opportunity to talk about crime. And I think that was a big miss as far as the speech. there was nothing new there. There was nothing to take away.\par \par \uc2 And next week, we're not going to remember a single thing he said, and no one's going to feel any safer than they did after his visit. And I think that was a big miss on his part.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And Joe Gamaldi said the same thing, missed opportunity, when we had him on following it.\par \par \uc2 "New York Post" cover today, "Blah, blah, blah. Sleepy Biden comes to New York City to talk about crime, but it's all blah, blah, blah."\par \par \uc2 Lee Carter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Good to see you. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Great to see you. You too.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, next up, the move from a major airline that has flight attendants worrying more fists will be flying.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The booze is back, \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  returning in-flight alcohol sales after a two-year absence. Flight attendants not thrilled.\par \par \uc2 Are these guys?\par \par \uc2 Host of "Sincerely Kat" on FOX Nation, Kat Timpf. And Internet radio host Mike Gunzelman is here.\par \par \uc2 So, Kat, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Excited that booze are -- back