er for that. You know, today was one of the moments when you don't get to control goes to the podium and she spoke truth. You know who else needs to answer for that? Mayor Adams.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Adams has a, a honeymoon period where he can fulfill what people thought he would be or he can go along with what's been happening so far which is a demoralization. This is pro-criminal. This isn't when Donald Trump used to say law and order, he was criticized for simplifying it, except that's exactly what we've taken for granted when our streets were peaceful. And people back the police and the people who shot cops stayed in jail.\par \par \uc2 That's law and order to include the extension our southern border, which used to mean something and today does not. The things we took for granted, we can't take for granted anymore because of the radical policies of the left. Let's pray -- and Joe's right, let's pray people in cut through the media go to the ballot box and vote for something different.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: You shoot a cop, and you're walking the streets -- we have issues.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Pete Hegseth, Joe Concha, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Also, breakthrough today for the first time, a clear posture from the nation's top brass on a possible Russian invasion of Ukraine, as Moscow gives new indication it's gearing up for bloodshed, Republican Congressman and Combat War Veteran Michael Waltz after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. MARK MILLEY, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF CHAIRMAN: If Russia chooses to invade Ukraine, it will not be cost free. In terms of casualties, or other significant effects.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Are you seeing this? A storm that could explode into a bomb cyclone. Yes, there is such a thing. Is about to hit the Northeast, potentially impacting more than 75 million Americans with up to two feet of snow in some areas.\par \par \uc2 Fox Weather Will Nunley is one of those spots, it's Cape Cod, Massachusetts. I love Cape Cod, Will, but this, apparently, is not the time to be there.\par \par \uc2 WILL NUNLEY, FOX WEATHER, MULTIMEDIA JOURNALIST (on camera): Yes, hang on, Trace. We are absolutely in the impact zone. This beautiful landscape you see all around me is going to change dramatically over the next 12 to 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 This is the area that we're talking about seeing 18 to 24 inches of snow. It's going to be incredible. When we talk about all the different hazards that are at play for an area like this, we're talking about 70-mile-per- hour wind gusts possible here on the Cape.\par \par \uc2 We have coastal flooding concerns. Then, we have those snowfalls coming in. Those gigantic snowfall totals. Also, in the Boston area as well seeing this winter storm warning for that area as well.\par \par \uc2 And so 74-75 million people right now as we speak, under some type of winter weather advisory or winter weather warning. It's time to get off the streets. We're seeing a lot of people though. Long lines at gas stations filling up their cars, getting ready for this. A substantial impact for this area.\par \par \uc2 Another concern that we're going to be tracking closely here is the amount of power outages that are possible. And you have those 60 to 70 mile per hour wind gust -- oh man, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Power outages' another concern on top of the bitter cold and incredible snow totals again.\par \par \uc2 So, everything you're seeing right now going to be covered possibly feet of snow by the time that we are here in the morning.\par \par \uc2 Fox News, Fox Weather, all lined up with the correspondents throughout this area to keep you covered. Make sure that you do download the free Fox Weather app. We're going to be sending out alerts and staying with you throughout the storm to keep informed. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Back to you as the news breaks. Will Nunley live for us on Cape Cod. Will, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The other big story, big breaking story of the day; A rare joint appearance from the nation's top military leaders affirming an attack against one NATO ally is an attack against all.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. MARK MILLEY, CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: It does feel different. In terms of what we've seen in the past of Russian exercises, et cetera. But this is larger in scale and scope and the massing of forces than anything we have seen in recent memory. And I think you'd have to go back quite a while into the Cold War days to see something of this magnitude.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, as we learn, Russia has reportedly expanded its massive military buildup on Ukraine's border to include medical materials presumably, to treat casualties.\par \par \uc2 In moments, Congressman Michael Waltz who warns Putin is trying to rebuild the Soviet Union, and Ukraine is not the end. But first, to correspondent Mark Meredith, he is live for us at the Pentagon. Mark, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Trace, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Today, we did hear from Pentagon, the officials who outline not only where things stand right now, but how dire and deadly things could get if Russia were to ultimately invade Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The top military brass saying they believe President Putin of Russia has yet to make a decision either way on a potential invasion, but those same officials say they are closely watching every move those Russian forces make.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN (RET), UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: He clearly now has that capability. And there are multiple options available to him, including the seizure of cities and significant territories.\par \par \uc2 Any Russian attack or further incursion into Ukraine, what not only it might conflict, it would also violate the bedrock principles of national sovereignty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice-over): All week long, including today, we've seen military supplies arrive in Ukraine from several countries including the United States. The Pentagon rejecting critics who say that the aid has been arriving too slowly.\par \par \uc2 They point to the recently approved $200 million in aid that was done back in December, which is allowing Ukraine to receive everything from grenade launches to ammunition.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Pentagon is also outlining what U.S. troops are on alert to deploy to Eastern Europe. But they are just on alert at this point. They have not been activated. Those men and women would be coming from across the country, and providing aviation, medical, and military logistical support, many of them either coming from Fort Bragg, North Carolina, or Fort Campbell in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): And one thing we heard over and over again today from Pentagon officials is that they believe a diplomatic approach here, a diplomatic solution is still possible.\par \par \uc2 But it was also very interesting to hear as they outlined what kind of a war could look like Ukraine. 40 million people living in that country. And of course, the sacrifices and that just the direness of all of this, what would happen if Russia were to invade? Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, they say diplomacy is possible, but never have seen a build-up like this. Mark Meredith live for us in the Pentagon. Mark, thank you, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Congressman. Michael Waltz joins me now. He is a former Green Beret commander and serves in the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for coming on at this time. I just want to ask you about the troop buildup we're talking about as Mark mentioned there, 8,500 troops from Fort Bragg, from Fort Campbell. Are you surprised that they have not been deployed as of this hour?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): No. If you -- if you understand what these troops are actually intended for, they are part of what's called a NATO Response Force which is a rapid reaction force that NATO has kind of on paper, but it's never fully been exercised. And all of the countries that are part of NATO contribute troops to this thing, or they promised troops to it.\par \par \uc2 That's what these troops were alerted to do. The problem with it, is that the NATO Response Force has never actually been used. And it takes a unanimous consent from all 30 NATO countries to be able to actually activate and utilize it.\par \par \uc2 And I don't see the Germans getting on board, the Turks, or a number of other countries. So, again, you know, I think we need much more of a bilateral support between the United States and Ukraine, rather than whole reliance on NATO, because they're compromised because they're completely dependent on Russia, particularly Germany, the largest economy for its energy needs.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, and that's a fair assessment. On the other side of that, we'll talk about Germany in a minute. But the other side, Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin was talking about the troop buildup from Russia, listen to this, and I'll get your response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN, U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: As we look at that number of troops, and that amount of hardware that's in the border region, get far in a way exceeds what we typically see them do for exercises. And so, it's very concerning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It is concerning. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, I think the additional piece is, that's concerning on the build-up isn't just the numbers, it's the types of troops, many of them are from the Russian reserves and National Guard and their sole purpose is occupation duty. So, that does lead us more and more towards a full invasion mode.\par \par \uc2 What I would have liked to have seen today from Secretary Austin and General Milley was one, a statement that we're going to accelerate lethal aid. And the types of lethal aid that we're sending just aren't good enough. They need surface-to-air missiles, they need anti-ship missiles. They need more sophisticated weaponry, and they've been asking for it. I was out in Ukraine a month ago. They've been asking for it for months.\par \par \uc2 So, we need a greater sophistication in what we're providing. And then secondly, a clear statement that after Russia invades, we will continue to support a Ukrainian resistance.\par \par \uc2 Again, that's not boots on the ground. That's supplies and resources and lethal aid. It's only if Putin decides that this could turn into a bloody quagmire for him, that he's not just going to roll through there and with an easy invasion.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: That we make it clear, it's going to be incredibly difficult militarily, not just economically, do I think we could -- we could alter his calculus.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: And hopefully, deter him from invasion in the front -- invading in the front end, rather than focusing on how we're going to respond on the back end.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. They're certainly getting more than blankets, but some say clearly not enough. The New York Post writes the following here. "It looks like Moscow simply wanted a pretext to invade not real negotiations. The ball's in Putin's court. Maybe he'll figure out something the West is willing to grant, and call it a win. He may go ahead guessing that the alliance will fracture if it has to fight." And there is a real chance the alliance, especially Germany, may very well fracture. Your final thoughts.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, look, I mean, the Germans have not lived up to their commitment of two percent of their GDP to spend