mala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Would someone who was an attorney general of the large state and who served with many key Senate votes be an attractive candidate to the president for an open Supreme Court seat?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I see what you did there, Peter, but the president has every intention, as he said before, of running for reelection and for running for reelection with Vice President Harris on the ticket as his partner.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): And this gives Biden a chance to do something Reaganesque after all. Reagan promised during his campaign to nominate a woman to the Supreme Court and he did. Biden promised to nominate a Black woman and he can but you just got a taste of it from \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  there, officials around here are not answering even the most basic questions about this with a yes or a no, they are acting like there is no open seat on the Supreme Court until Breyer says so, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you. Let's bring in Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT", Shannon Bream. Good evening, Shannon. So, who are the front runners?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, confirmation today as Peter just reported there, Bret, that the president will stick to his vow to nominate the first African American woman to the Supreme Court bench.\par \par \uc2 So, these are the names that we have been tracking. Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson sits on what's considered that stepping stone to the Supreme Court. A very powerful D.C. circuit. She was nominated and confirmed to that court less than a year ago but prior to that, she sat on the federal bench at the district court level since 2013. She's got the resume, went to Harvard undergrad and law school and she clerked for Justice Breyer.\par \par \uc2 She sailed through her last Senate confirmation just months ago, 53 to 44. Now, that includes votes from Republican Senators Graham, Collins and Murkowski.\par \par \uc2 Interesting tidbit, she's also related to former House Speaker \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  by marriage. Judge Jackson is 51.\par \par \uc2 The other front runner at this point California State Supreme Court Justice Leondra Kruger. She has served in that position since 2015. She is a former Obama Justice Department lawyer. She's actually argued 12 cases before the Supreme Court, but she's also well versed in the inner workings of the Supreme Court because she too was a clerk. She clerked for Justice \uc2 John Paul Stevens\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After attending Harvard undergrad and Yale Law School, she's viewed in her current job as a moderate, often called a swing vote. She is 45 and has young children.\par \par \uc2 Another federal judge who recently went through this Senate confirmation process is Judge Candace Jackson- Akiwumi. She's in her early 40s, serves on the Seventh Circuit Court of Appeals again, an impressive resume, Princeton and Yale.\par \par \uc2 She's only been in her current position less than a year. The Senate confirmed her by a vote of 53 to 40, again, the same GOP votes. Both her parents have served as judges. She also has worked both in private practice and then later, as a public defender for several years.\par \par \uc2 Now, those three potential nominees getting the most attention tonight, all of them Bret, considered young by Supreme Court standards, meaning they could give the president a legacy on the Supreme Court for decades to come, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, what about the timing of all this and how this all played out today?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and you'll notice, Bret, we still don't have an official announcement from Justice Breyer or from the court. Numerous sources told me today he was not intending to make the announcement today.\par \par \uc2 However, the announcement was described to me this way as eminence. Justice Breyer had firmly decided already he was ready to step down at the end of the term, that is not in question. I'm told he did not feel political pressure but was ready to wrap up his career on the bench. And clearly, like everyone else in Washington, he is aware of the current climate.\par \par \uc2 Now, I've gotten multiple descriptions of how he's been feeling today when this news broke, but overall, more that he was surprised by the news coming today, because this is not how he had planned it. But he has been at peace with a decision which had already been made, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But bottom line, Shannon, Democrats are probably going to move pretty quick, even maybe before this term ends to start this whole process, judging by what we heard from Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  today?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, that would make sense. I mean, they know the seat is going to be open probably late June, early July. You want to get that confirmation done, if you can before the new term starts October 3rd, but there are a lot of political considerations in play here because of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But yes, they could definitely start the process of a nomination, of meeting the senators, of vetting all of those things before Justice Breyer actually steps down from the seat if and when he does so.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we'll see you tonight, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Senior administration officials say \uc2 NATO\uc2  will not rule out expanding further into Eastern Europe. The Biden administration responded in writing today to Russian demands for security guarantees. The Kremlin has yet to react publicly. But one Russian senator says the response is unacceptable. We'll have a live report from the State Department shortly.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we follow up on our in-depth report Tuesday in the search for the origins of the coronavirus, what was said and written privately versus what was said and written publicly.\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  was asked today whether China will ever pay a price for the cover up and whether President Biden is committed to getting into the bottom of the coronavirus origins question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: What about sanctions for China at any point for misleading the world about the early days of the global pandemic?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We had used sanctions as a tool as it relates to our concerns about the behavior of a range of countries, including officials in China.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The answer is that we did -- I did raise the question of transparency.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is there a reason your press staff was unaware of that? And what did you say to the Chinese president?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And they weren't with me the entire time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Joining us this evening are former CDC Director \uc2 Dr. Robert Redfield\uc2 , who's currently AM LLC Senior Medical Advisor and former White House Chief of Staff to President Trump, \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thanks for being here. I know both of you saw that piece yesterday looking in some of the e-mails what was said privately in the earliest stages of the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with you Mick and your role as kind of running point to getting the task force -- the Coronavirus Task Force together as White House Chief of Staff. Some of these immunologists in these e-mails were expressing real -- not certainty, but definitely thinking that this came from a lab, the Wuhan lab. One of them saying an accidental release or natural event 70-30, accidental release. Was any of that brought forward in the earliest meetings from Dr. Fauci or Dr. Collins?\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: No, it wasn't. Keep in mind, we started having the COVID meetings what became the COVID taskforce later on, we started having these meetings in mid to late January when COVID was sort of making its presence known and at no time -- at no time did Dr. Fauci who was at every single one of those meetings ever raised the possibility that this could have been something that was manufactured, in fact, the exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 Early on in the discussions, in the meetings, he made it very clear that there was no evidence that it was manmade, and that he believed without reservation, that it was a naturally occurring phenomenon, probably from that wet market from that wild animal market in Wuhan. And we never went back to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 He was so convinced and so convincing, that it was not manufactured, that it was not manmade, that we never really revisited it again. So, I was surprised to see the e-mails when they came out here in the last couple of days.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Dr. Redfield, Dr. Fauci has responded to multiple questions about this saying he still believes that most virologist in his words believe that this was evolutionary, a jump from animal to animal to human.\par \par \uc2 You're a virologist who does not believe that, who thinks it's more likely, most likely that it came from an accident from a lab. What was your take from the story that we had last night?\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT REDFIELD, SENIOR MEDICAL ADVISOR, AM LLC: You know, Bret, I thought he did an excellent job. I was, you know, most disappointed in why it's important is that it just reflected that the approach that the leaders of the scientific community took was really not scientific.\par \par \uc2 A science loves rigorous debate of different hypotheses and use that debate to try to get to scientific truth.\par \par \uc2 Instead, what happened here was there's appears to be a consensus among a number of individuals, that they were going to portray the point of view that there was really one -- only one credible perspective here and that's what it was evolutionary.\par \par \uc2 I've said before, that I don't think it's biologically plausible that it came from nature, where you had a virus that went from bats to some intermediate animal to humans, and then was one of the most infectious viruses we've seen.\par \par \uc2 I've told people that SARS went from a bat to a civet cat to humans. And now, 18, 19 years later, there has been less than 10,000 cases in the world. MERS in 2012 13 from a bat to a camel to humans, less than 10,000 cases nine years later.\par \par \uc2 These viruses that come from nature don't immediately become highly infectious for men, this virus had to be educated in how to infect men with greater efficiency.\par \par \uc2 And so, therefore, I think, really, the evidence really points. If you want to have a rigorous scientific debate that this virus emerged from the laboratory and I'm disappointed that science doesn't embrace science. Science embraces debate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Dr. Redfield to that point, I mean, you have these doctors, Kristian Andersen, Robert Garry, number of others expressing privately in e-mails that they really think it can't happen naturally.\par \par \uc2 But yet, just days later, they write this report that is actually provided to Dr. Fauci and Collins for suggestions and editing that makes it definitive. And then that is referenced in the White House as the definitive word. Why do you think that that happens?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: You know, I think one of the challenges is that they believe that somehow, they were helping science by not having science implicated as potentially being the source of the pandemic to begin with.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, this is what I've said before, rather than try to package something in a way that, you know, prevents people from thinking what you don't want them to think, I think it's just much better to tell the truth. And I don't think that was done here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They're trying on Capitol Hill to get to the bottom of this Mick, Representative Jim Jordan, who's on the Oversight Committee, the ranking member tweeted this January 27th, 2020: Fauci learned that his agency had been indirectly funding the Wuhan lab. January 31st, 2020: Fauci learned the virus looks engineered. Over the next two years, Fauci works behind the scenes to squash the COVID lab leak theory.\par \par \uc2 For the people who say, why does this matter? This whole story of the origins? Why does this matter as we're fighting this pandemic currently? What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 MULVANEY: Well, it matters for a couple of reasons --\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Yes, Bret, I think it's --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, let me start with Mick.\par \par \uc2 MULVANEY: Yes, I put my whole (PH) budget director had on it, and I want to know if our taxpayer dollars are being used to do this, not just because it's good fiscal policy, but because that also possibly could have given us more leverage to get more information out of the Chinese. If they were using our money, it gave us another avenue to possibly put pressure on them.\par \par \uc2 But more importantly than that, I put the old chief of staff head on and I'm wondering why none of this was shared with the President of the United States. As we're sitting there in the Oval Office in late January and early February, trying to formulate policy.\par \par \uc2 The president should have known about these other opinions, they should -- he should have known about the possible financial connection between taxpayer dollars and the research that was being done, should have known about the connection between Dr. Fauci and Mr. Collins about what was happening and none of that -- none of that was disclosed to the President of the United States. And that is unconscionable when you are a senior adviser to the most powerful man in the world.\par \par \uc2 So, the process broke down. Could we have done a better job in response to it? Possibly, we'll never know. But I do think this screams out for the type of investigation that Jim Jordan is talking about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And again, we've asked for all these questions from Dr. Fauci, Dr. Collins, \uc2 NIH\uc2 , and haven't gotten a specific response.\par \par \uc2 And very quickly, Dr. Redfield, do you think we'll ever find the true answer here?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Yes, Bret. I think, again, if we can get back to focusing on science, and let science do what science does, and have that rigorous debate, I think we will.\par \par \uc2 And I do think it's really important to add what Mick said. The real issue here is a policy decision about gain in function research. You know, I've called for a moratorium on gain-of-function research. I think the broader society needs to debate that, to determine if it's necessary, and if so, how to do it safe and responsibly?\par \par \uc2 I do not think it's something that a group of scientists should decide on their own. There is a very serious possibility that this pandemic actually was a result of science.\par \par \uc2 Science doing in gain-of-functional research, and with an arrogance that they felt that it there could be no bad outcome from it.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it's critical to get to origin. It's not just some hypothetical exercise because it really is going to form the basis, i think, for a global scientific policy to reevaluate this concept of gain-of-function research.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gentlemen, we appreciate your time. We hope to continue to look for answers. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MULVANY: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: later in the program, how the coronavirus pandemic is affecting American families.\par \par \uc2 And up next, a live report from the state department on the major disagreement between the U.S. and Russia that's fueling the Ukrainian conflict.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , says he does not expect supply chain issues to be worked out by the end of this year. Powell tells \uc2 Fox\uc2  Business, the economy is not making much progress right now. He says he expects some progress in the second half of the year.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is signaling it will raise its benchmark interest rate as soon as March. This is seen as a key step in reversing its pandemic-era low rate policies, blamed by some for escalating inflation.\par \par \uc2 Well, stocks were mixed today on that news. The Dow lost 130. The S&P 500 was down seven. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  gained three.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", a spokesman for the state department says Russia's \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline project will not move forward if Moscow invades Ukraine. That's a red line there.\par \par \uc2 This comes as one Russian officials expressing disappointment over the U.S. refusal to guarantee we'll not further expand \uc2 NATO\uc2  in that region.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there is growing pushback against Russian leadership here in the U.S. With all of that, state department correspondent Benjamin Hall has the latest live from the state department. Good evening, Benjamin.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, good evening, Bret. And suddenly, that's far more definitive than we've heard so far about \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ned Price\uc2  saying that if Russia invades Ukraine, that will not go ahead. And we don't know if it's to do with the discussions that Germany had been involved in. But certainly, a big move, particularly at a time when the administration is trying to also focus on the diplomatic path.\par \par \uc2 HALL (voice-over): As Russia continues its apparent march to war, and as weapons continue to arrive in Ukraine, the U.S. is doing everything it can to keep diplomacy alive. Today, delivering written responses to Russia's concerns.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: We hope and expect that Russia will have the same view and will take our proposals seriously.\par \par \uc2 HALL: The delivery of a document had been agreed when Secretary Blinken met Russian Foreign Minister Lavrov last week, and it laid out where the U.S. hopes agreement can be reached.\par \par \uc2 Limits on military exercises, force posture in Ukraine, arms control and missile deployment. Off the table, however, is any discussion on \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: I can't be more clear \uc2 NATO\uc2 's door is open, remains open. And that is our commitment.\par \par \uc2 HALL: The Kremlin has yet to respond. But within hours it was rejected by one Russian senator. Saying, "The U.S. response to Moscow's security proposals cannot satisfy Russia, it cannot be accepted.\par \par \uc2 Even Secretary Blinken admits Russia may not be negotiating in good faith.\par \par \uc2 HALL (on camera): Have you considered that Russia is just buying time until they're in a place to invade Ukraine? Making you jump through hoops like hand delivering written responses to questions that you've answered time and time again in the past.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: You may well be right that Russia is not serious about this at all. But we have an obligation to test that proposition to pursue the diplomatic path.\par \par \uc2 HALL (voice-over): But there is a growing sense that Russia has gone too far already. And the time to act is now.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): And Putin deserves to be sanctioned now. He's put over 100,000 troops on the Ukraine border. We're having to withdraw American citizens and government officials because of the provocation.\par \par \uc2 HALL: The Pentagon and the White House both say they're ready for any outcome.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We have to be ready in case this happens very, very soon.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: They have the ability also to make the decision to de-escalate, to bring troops back from the border.\par \par \uc2 HALL: But as troops continue to mass, the administration is hoping their letter will do the trick.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Right now, the document is with them and the ball is in their court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HALL (on camera): Bret, in a further sign of deterioration in relations between the two, today, over two dozen Russian diplomats here in the U.S. flew back to Moscow. The ambassador saying it was at the request of the state department, although it's more to do with a long simmering dispute about how long diplomats can stay in each country.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, they weren't due to leave yet. And the timing at a time when tensions are so high is certainly very worrying. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Benjamin Hall, live at the state department. Benjamin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how the coronavirus pandemic is affecting American families like yours.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Emergency officials in Spain's Canary Islands say 319 people including 59 women and 24 children have been rescued from boats trying to reach the archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean.\par \par \uc2 Activists say at least 18 Migrants have died. Most of the rescued are believed to be from Northern and Central Africa.\par \par \uc2 U.S.-backed Kurdish-led forces say they have wrestled control of the last section of a prison held by Islamic State militants. They report freeing a number of child detainees used as human shields. The defeat ends a week long assault by the extremist on one of the largest detention facilities in Syria.\par \par \uc2 And this is a lot of look at Paris. One of the stories, not really big, but it's a story. French fashion house Chanel shaking up conventions by sending a horse onto the catwalk during its Haute Couture show. The horse was ridden by a niece of Prince Albert of Monaco. She is a competitive show jumper, and a Chanel brand ambassador.\par \par \uc2 Not every day on the runway.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Tonight, we take an in depth look at life in America during the coronavirus pandemic. Families are grappling with vaccine and mask mandates. Inflation causing meals to break the bank, making drives to school the most expensive in years, and a dramatic rise in violent crime across the country.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, correspondent Gillian Turner reports on a day in the life of American families dealing with this pandemic and many challenges.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Alexis, you ready?\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It's another busy morning for Kenny (PH) and Briana Howard (PH), full time working parents raising an active 8-year-old daughter in Northern Virginia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Purpose on schools. Thanks, mommy.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The morning routine making waffles and strawberries, then school lunch.\par \par \uc2 BRIANA HOWARD, VIRGINIA PARENT: Is your backpack heavy?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS HOWARD, VIRGINIA DAUGHTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Followed by a quick turnaround out the door.\par \par \uc2 B. HOWARD: You want to put your gloves on?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Mom and daughter walk to the bus stop. And after Alexis boards the bus, Briana has a minute for a cup of coffee before starting a full day at her job in healthcare from her dining room table.\par \par \uc2 She says both her husband and daughter Alexis contracted earlier variant of coronavirus, and have come out of it more resilient.\par \par \uc2 B. HOWARD: We are taking the necessary precautions, but I don't want to stop living our lives in fear of COVID, either.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But not everyone is as upbeat. Suzanne and Anthony Lucio, based in Chicago, both began working from home full time when the pandemic hit. They say their kids, Gianna, 12, and Roman, five, were forced into remote learning, and the threat of getting COVID has upended their lives ever since.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE LUCIO, ILLINOIS PARENT: It's what we base our activities on. Everything in our lives is based on COVID.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY LUCIO, ILLINOIS PARENT: I got the sniffles, or I got a headache, is this COVID? So you are on high alert all the time.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: President Biden recently called COVID one of the most formidable enemies America has ever faced.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Omicron has now been challenging us in a way that it's the new enemy.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: For the Gragg family in Denver, fear of getting the virus is still very real now two years after COVID first hit the U.S.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER GRAGG, COLORADO PARENT: I have been collecting at-home tests so that we don't have to go out in a shortage and look for them. I feel like I need to test all the time.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Heather Gragg says daily life now is more complicated than it used to be, and it would be helpful if the federal government and states came up with some clearer guidance.\par \par \uc2 GRAGG: There isn't some sort of protocol that everyone recognizes as this is how we do it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Suzanne Lucio agrees, and says now with school open again getting the kids out the door every morning seems infinitely complex.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE LUCIO: When I wake up in the morning, I have to get the disposable masks out for my children. I have to make sure that I order the four-ply disposable masks.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It's not just what kids wear to school that's changed. It's how they are learning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: May I have a cookie, please?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Parents are worried about the effects remote learning has had on their kids.\par \par \uc2 GRAGG: She