 would love for the day to come to just see her smile while she is performing.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Kids even younger than the Howard's daughter may not even remember life pre-COVID.\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE LUCIO: My five-year-old doesn't really know a world without COVID. He is used to wearing a mask. He doesn't know what a play dated is.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Parents of small children like the Lucios say keeping their kids healthy during COVID is what matters most. But for parents in some pockets of the country, the threat of COVID is coupled with concerns about crime as the pandemic has coincided with a dramatic spike in violent crime.\par \par \uc2 KENNY HOWARD: There's carjackings over here, just people walking up armed robbing people.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: For the Carney family of Amherst, New York, the crime spike hits close to home.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GELMAN CARNEY, NEW YORK PARENT: Over the course of the last 18 months, being the wife of a police officer has been an incredible challenge.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Sarah Gelman Carney's children are twins who will soon head off to college. She is optimistic.\par \par \uc2 CARNEY: Hopefully they know what to do and they adapt quickly.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Adaptability is a skill families have been forced to learn during the pandemic. Making hard choices has become just part of the routine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I found a school that was really just running things as cautiously as they could. They still let the kids just be themselves.\par \par \uc2 KENNY HOWARD: We kind of backed off a little date nights, to save extra money.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: For the Howards, less evenings out means more evenings at home as a family.\par \par \uc2 BRIANA HOWARD: We can have leftovers tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Something all these families agree on, they want their kids to be OK.\par \par \uc2 BRIANA HOWARD: I think every mother wants the very best for their kid, and that's what my husband and I want.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Looking at the future, the moms, the dads, even the kids we talked to say they want more than anything for life to get back to normal. They say that might not be next month or even next year, but they all have a lot of hope. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Great job. Gillian Turner in Arlington, Virginia. Gillian, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, for the second time in a week, another mass release of illegal immigrants into the U.S. by the Biden administration is caught on camera. We have that exclusive video.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have another FOX News exclusive tonight from the southern border. It involves the practice of releasing large numbers of illegal immigrants into the U.S. Correspondent Bill Melugin is on the border in Brownsville, Texas, tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Adult male migrants have their chains and cuffs removed as they're released from ICE custody in Brownsville today. The men were then escorted into a parking garage where a black tarp was set up to obstruct public view, and they were discreetly taken away by a nongovernmental organization which will facilitate their travel.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the day, we saw more single adults being released from ICE custody, including this large group of predominantly adult men who are being walked to the bus terminal by the NGO. Many of these men tried to hide their faces from our cameras, and now ICE is responding to FOX News' reporting on the mass release of these adult migrants, telling us in part, quote, "ICE focuses its civil immigration enforcement priorities on the apprehension and removal of non-citizens who pose a threat to national security, public safety, and border security. Individuals deemed suitable for release are released in coordination with local partners and are subject to reporting requirements."\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, FOX News reported hundreds of adult men were dropped off by federally contracted buses, some with ankle monitors. The men were assisted by an NGO and given travel packets to go wherever they want, scenes that have since gone viral and drew some strong reaction.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR): When someone crosses our border illegally, their final destination should always be turning them around and sending them back to their home country.\par \par \uc2 TOM HOMAN, FORMER ACTING ICE DIRECTOR: Not only are we sending them to their final destination, the U.S. taxpayer is paying for it. We're paying for those air flights. So once again, this administration got caught lying to the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And, Bret, the Biden administration's restarting of the Remain in Mexico policy is off to a very slow start. Brand new DHS data shows that for the month of December, out of approximately 179,000 migrant encounters, less than 300 of those migrants were put into the program. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin in Brownsville, Texas. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on Justice Breyer's retirement from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  and the situation in and around Ukraine. First, here is what some of our \uc2 FOX\uc2  affiliates around the country are covering tonight. \uc2 FOX\uc2  6 in Milwaukee where a sheriff's deputy is hospitalized after being shot several times in the arms and torso during a foot pursuit. Authorities are trying to determine whether a man who died from a self-inflicted gunshot wound as officers closed in was the same person who shot that deputy.\par \par \uc2 FOX\uc2  2 in San Francisco as San Jose becomes the first city to require liability insurance for gun owners. It's also instituting an annual fee between $25 and $35 to go towards firearm safety education and services. Opponents say the measures violate their Second Amendment rights