y hunkered down in general posture. You can see compared to other presidents, this President has done just a nine of these opportunities. And as I said, only one of them was like what we're going to get today, a formal news conference in the East Room at the White House. You can see the other presidents at this point in their presidencies.\par \par \uc2 And they gave many more opportunities for the press in this kind of environment. We're going to bring all of this to you live, special coverage and analysis great team aligned over the next two hours, Karl Rove, Mercedes Schlapp, and Juan Williams are going to kick us off in just a moment. Plus, Fox team coverage, Edward Lawrence, Jonathan Hunt, Mark Meredith, and we begin with White House Correspondent Peter Doocy, who has had a front row for this presidency and the candidacy. He will be in the room this afternoon ready to ask his questions. He's no stranger to pressing President Biden every opportunity that he has received to do so here's a look back at some of those moments over the past year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In May, you made it sound like a ticket to losing the match forever.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: That, that is true at the time.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Do you think that that might incentivize more people to come over illegally?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you guys keep sending that garbage out? Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There's this new op-ed where you talked about COVID, we are going to beat it back. Are you no longer going to shut it down?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, we got to beat it back before we shut it down. No, no, wait a minute, I'm asking you a question. I said, is that accurate? The best of your --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Do you think that people have an issue with pulling out of Afghanistan or just the way things have happened.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think they have an issue that people are likely to get hurt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Peter, a pretty impressive persistence on your part that we've seen over the course of this year. And I think that you have had a unique relationship with this president from the campaign, where we did see him at home and in the basement a lot over the course of that campaign. Your thoughts as we get ready for this news conference that clearly comes at a pivotal point for the President.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: You saw it right there. Martha, this is a president who takes things very personally, he can be emotional at these events. He can be playful, but most of all, he can be informative. And it is great to have these daily briefings. And it's great when they bring in administration officials to talk to the press, but none of them are making the decisions.\par \par \uc2 And whether you like Joe Biden or not, he is a historical figure. He is somebody who came into office at a time that this nation was dealing with a once in a century pandemic, the economy was terrible. I have heard from a White House official in the last hour or so they really want to focus on economic growth. And they want to focus today on the number of people who they have been able to vaccinate in the last year.\par \par \uc2 But we are told to expect to hear President Biden at some point, do something that he doesn't often do admit that there is still more to do and that he knows it especially with especially concerning COVID-19. We don't know much more about what to expect. But that's, that's what we got with t- minus 60 minutes to go, Martha.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. So, what's the format? How many reporters do you expect to be in there? How long do you expect the President will take questions? Any clarity on that? Yep. Peter.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The last one of these there were 30 reporters in the East Room socially distance and he called on 10. So, a third of them. I usually when he goes into these events, his staff have prepared a list of people for him to call on a list of people that they want him to call on. Fox has never been on that list dating all the way back to the campaign in the transition and the first year here at the White House. But as you just saw right there, sometimes he veers off the list and so we hope to get on there on the board with him because there, there is a lot to ask yes and it's going to be tough to cover even if it is a full hour.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we'll be watching it as Tim to stern. And we'll be watching you and hoping that you get that opportunity, Peter, because I'm sure you have some good questions for the President. And it's clearly an important moment for me should want to take questions from all across the board today.\par \par \uc2 So hopefully, there'll be a lot of back and forth that will be helpful to the American people. At this point, Peter, thank you. Always great to see you, Peter Doocy. White House correspondent who will be watching very closely this afternoon. Joining me now Karl Rove, former Deputy Chief of Staff to President George W. Bush and a Fox News contributor, Mercedes Schlapp. No stranger to White House press conferences as former Director of Strategic Communications under President Trump and Juan Williams, Fox News, political analyst.\par \par \uc2 So let's go straight down the line. Great to have all of you with us, Carl, your thoughts on what this President has to do today? He has very weak poll numbers anywhere from 33 to 40 percent of support in this country. And he has created an environment where you have plus seven GOP in terms of the way people declare themselves and their party preference has gone from plus seven dem, to plus seven GOP?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, let's put this in context. No president in a deeper hole, as Joe Biden finds himself in today is going to be able to dig it out with one news conference. His best hope is, is it on the big issues of the day COVID and the economy. He strikes a set of notes that cause people to say, well, you know what, I thought the country's going in the wrong direction. But he seems to have his hands around some of that. Let's give him a little bit more time.\par \par \uc2 But the President's in lousy shape. I mean, just take a look at the job approval. He started out at 5636 56 Approved 36 disapprove, 41.53 today; economy 53.34 When he came into office 4054 today, and COVID He came in nearly seven out of every 10 Americans was approving of the job he was doing last year. Today it's split 45.48. And as a result, people are sour. They think the country is going in a wrong direction by a huge margin. And that's not healthy for the incumbent president. Yes, I'm not going to be able to solve it in one news conference.\par \par \uc2 GUT: No, certainly not. But as you say, it really depends on what the stories are that come out of this hour, and whether or not you can legitimately say after watching this back and forth, that he is changing course, Mercedes, or that he seems to be, you know, finding some, some strength on this issue or that issue. I mean, he's got to put down some markers, and some points today that start to turn that dial a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES SCHLAPP, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Right. Well, there's no question that the word you're going to start seeing more and more is the word reset is the White House in reset mode, are they ready to enter 2022 With a strong agenda, I'll tell you, the White House, especially the comms team, and Karl knows this, they need to be nervous, because they're setting such high expectations around this press conference, there's something to be said, if you're doing multiple press conferences during the year, you're able to have lower expectations of what the President's performance will be.\par \par \uc2 But this White House has tried so hard to keep President Biden in the basement and not allow him to really have that engagement with reporters. And in essence, I think it's actually hurt President Biden, on top of that, with a long list of failures coming out of this White House, from the Afghanistan withdrawal to the chaos at the border, to not being able to get a handle on this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, with regard to how accessible he is. Let's look at call for number four here, in terms of what we see a lot of in these really short, informal Q&A Is that he does they often end like this. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not supposed to be having this press conference. You guys are bad. I'm not supposed to be answering all these questions. I'm supposed to leave, but I can't resist your questions. I'm not I'm not supposed to take any questions, but go ahead. I'm not going to get your gas down now. I'm sorry. I'm going to just last question I'll take and I said, I'm really going to be in trouble.\par \par \uc2 Was he in trouble with one who's in charge of whether or not he can take all the time he wants? He's the president of the United States?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think Carl and Mercedes White House veterans would know, he'd be in trouble with the staff that's trying to impose some discipline on the message coming from the White House. And so, they asked him to try to limit some of those interactions. You know, to me, I was just listening.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, I agree with Karl, I don't think that this press conference is intended to turn everything around, you know, yes. The President is low in terms of his approval numbers right now. But remember, Martha, he's much higher than President Trump was after one year, he's almost as high as Bill Clinton was in the approval numbers after one year.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't think there's a death knell, you know, the smell of finality around a guy who is one year into his presidency. He's he'll be here for four years. And I think that you also I have to and I hope that he does this in the press conference start to brag a little bit about unemployment rate being so historically low about GDP being so historically high; about corporate profits being out the ceiling, if they're so high.\par \par \uc2 You know, and so many shots in arms when he came into office, I think less than one percent of Americans had been vaccinated, and there was no plan for distribution. Yes, you know, he's got to let the American people do it and stop, and we push back, push back, if you will, against the press, that seems like the light to thrash and constantly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I mean, that that's changed in just the past couple of weeks. I mean, you know, there came a point with these numbers when it became, I think, impossible for the mainstream press to, to avoid the reality that there were a lot of things going wrong here, when you go up to Capitol Hill three times, and you try to convince your own party to get on board with you.\par \par \uc2 And three times it blows up, that that's not a good place to be in Mercedes. I just based on what wine just said, I'm wondering what it was like when you would say to President Trump, I'm sorry, sir, we can't take any more questions. Now, what happened when you did that?\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: Well, that strategy didn't work. We know that President Trump, engage with the reporters, he would answer every single question he was on afraid of these reporters. Quite frankly, I think President Biden seems to be just afraid, which I just don't understand.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you, Juan, I don't know what he's going to brag about when you've got inflation cutting into people's wages, when people are stressed out because of high energy prices, empty shells, and high gasoline prices. I mean, there is not much to brag about coming out of this administration, they have lived in a bubble for the past year. And as Karl notes this, the first year is critical. Once you start losing your political capital in the first year, it's hard to regain it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Karl, one of the things that strikes me for any president is that you have to strike that balance. And I think you can look at a lot of Churchill moments during World War II, where he was realistic with people about what they faced, and yet was able to imbue optimism. At the same time, it's a very difficult line to walk. But it's one that I think all presidents strive towards.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes, hard to do in this moment. He shouldn't do with all due respect to, Juan. Juan, suggested he won't say look at all the great economic news, the economy is going and blow and jobs are being created. And it's all me most people look at the recovery of the economy, and say that's the great American economy, deciding that it's going to have to live with COVID. It's not the actions of government.\par \par \uc2 One made a good point that other presidents President Trump in 27 at the end in 2019, 2018, the beginning of 2018. And President Clinton at the beginning of 1994, both had lousy numbers, I would also remind one, their party's got shellacked badly in the following elections, and granted to the President Biden may recover by, by 2024.\par \par \uc2 But frankly, with all due respect to him, I don't think the Democratic Party is going to nominate an 82-year-old, which is what he would be in 2024 for president so he's in a hole, he not going to get out of it quickly. And, and the worst way to try and get out of it is to say things that don't strike the American people as being reasonable and claiming credit for the economy and jobs.\par \par \uc2 As Mercedes accurately pointed out, their biggest concern is I when I go to the grocery store, it's costing me more, and at the pump, it's really tough.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you got to meet people where they are and meet the moment as it is in the country, and then find a way to be uplifting, and show a way out. All of you. Thank you very much. Juan, great to see you; Mercedes, as well. And Carl, great to have you with us as well. We'll see y'all later.\par \par \uc2 So, we are about 45 minutes before this news conference. Brett there, Bill hammer and Katie Pavlich with their thoughts on what to be watching here. of top concern to the voters of course is the economy. When you look at any list of what care what they care about the most President Biden has taken a 13-point hit over the last seven months on the economy, according to Fox Business polling, can he begin to pivot to solutions minutes from now. That is the work before him.\par \par \uc2 Also, Republican Senator John Kennedy has some thoughts on the president, the economy and the upcoming A&A He's here in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We expected at the top of his comments today, the president will likely declare wins where the economy is concerned. A White House official telling Fox that the economy grew at the fastest rate in decades. But voters do see also what's happening to their shrinking wallets. And so, inflation must and we expect will be addressed here.\par \par \uc2 The store shelves, also, obviously a problem. You've got the Walmart CEO this week comparing what's going on in our supply chain to the Soviet Union. And what the shelves look like they're in the 80s.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator John Kennedy blames the spending spree by the White House for inflation. He is here in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, to Fox Business correspondent Edward Lawrence joining us live at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Edward.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we're likely to see fired-up president, more with the energy he had from that Georgia speech. But he's very defensive when it comes to his handling of the economy. The president likely to highlight the fact the economy is brought back 6.9 million jobs added back into it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (voice-over): Still, there are more than 600,000 more unemployed than pre-pandemic levels. He will also point to the unemployment rate at 3.9 percent, as well as wage growth, much like his press secretary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We saw the most dramatic change in our economy of anywhere in the world. It was the biggest year of job growth in American history. And it was the direct result of actions taken by President Biden and Democrats in Congress, including the American Rescue Plan.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: However, when you look under the surface you see inflation at a 40-year high rising faster than wages, making about everything more expensive, and workers can afford less. Republicans calling him out.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): The worst inflation in 40 years, seemingly endless pandemic, soaring homicides, a border crisis, and Russia flirting with war in Europe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (on camera): And President Joe Biden had nothing on his public schedule yesterday and today to prepare. We'll have to see exactly how many questions he will answer.\par \par \uc2 And Martha, I will mention, he did this morning talk with a bipartisan group of senators about Ukraine. But again, still preparing for two days for this event today.\par \par \uc2 Well, Ukraine, obviously a very pressing issue right now, and no doubt, on his mind. Edward, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, joining us now, Louisiana Republican Senator John Kennedy. Great to see you, Senator. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We're about 40 minutes away from this news conference getting underway, and there's a lot of issues on the president's plate.\par \par \uc2 But you heard what we're likely to hear at the beginning here, and that is that this has been the biggest year for job growth. Jen Psaki has cited that quite a bit, and we expect that the president will try to, you know, put some wins on the board at the top of this to impress that upon people.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): The American people may be poorer, under President Biden's administration, but they're not stupid. They see, Martha, what you and I see.\par \par \uc2 A fair assessment of the Biden administration after one year is that it runs a tight shipwreck. I mean, let me count the ways this administration has mismanaged COVID, it's mismanaged Congress, it's mismanaged the border, it's mismanaged inflation, it's mismanaged securing our streets, it's mismanaged foreign policy, including but not limited to Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And just last week, the president traveled in Georgia to participate in racial demagoguery. I mean, how many roads must a man walk down before he knows he is lost?\par \par \uc2 In terms of the economy, the figure that the president needs to be looking at is not only employment but the labor force participation rate. And that is how many of our people in America who can work are working?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Let me say a word about inflation. We are experiencing both cost- push and demand-pull on inflation. It's a direct result of President Biden's policies. He tries to blame inflation on, on everything, but what it is? His own policies. He's blaming it on corporate greed was his latest scenario.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I wouldn't be surprised next, if he -- if he says inflation originated in the bat, you know? It's not my fault, it originated in the bat. And we all know the cause of inflation, and he needs to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, you have 10 million unfilled jobs in this country. You just touched on this a moment ago. What would you like to hear from the president in terms of inspiring Americans to get back to work and to fill that labor participation number and get it to be much, much higher?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: We're not going to get people back to work until two things happen. Number one, the president's got to get control with COVID. They -- this administration has done an abysmal job in terms of testing, they've done an abysmal job in terms of therapeutics, and they've done an abysmal job of telling people the truth. That the CDC stands for the center for disease confusion. And Dr. Fauci has lost all credibility.\par \par \uc2 They are also not going to be able to solve this economic problem until they stop spending billions, trillions of dollars to pay people not to work. Those who can work should work. Welfare for the able-bodied should be temporary.\par \par \uc2 And we're not going to get the labor force participation rate back as long as he -- and we're not going to get inflation down, as long as this president keeps shoveling billions and billions of dollars out there that we don't have. So, that's up --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's so interesting. Just on your last comment, it reminds me of President Clinton after he got a drubbing in the midterms came forward to the American people and said that basically welfare, as we know it, is ending and that he wanted to create a program where people -- Welfare-to-Work program to really reform entitlements in this country.\par \par \uc2 We're a long, long way from that message. I wonder if there's any sort of similar resonance coming if there is a wake-up call in the midterms or maybe before.\par \par \uc2 Senator, I want to thank you very much for making time for us today. It's always good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 So, in 17 of our cities across this country, homicide rates broke records in 2021 pretty substantially in double digits and most of them.\par \par \uc2 As President Biden and cities coast to coast grapple with rising violent crime, Lawrence Jones has covered it firsthand in these cities. And he joins us with his take next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the rising crime across this country is one of several crises that have been a drag on the Biden presidency. So, will he have new answers? Will he address this in a way that we haven't heard before when he is questioned about it minutes from now?\par \par \uc2 17 U.S. cities broke records for homicide across the country in 2021. Foxnews.com. put together this list going through the records from local police departments and gathering all of that data.\par \par \uc2 "FOX & FRIENDS" enterprise reporter Lawrence Jones has covered this crisis across the country. He has talked to the people who are most affected by it. And he joins us in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, to chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt in Los Angeles where police now have a suspect in the stabbing death of a UCLA student who was at her afternoon job at a furniture store when a complete stranger walked in and stabbed her to death in the middle of the day.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, right now an urgent searches underway for 31-year-old Sean Laval Smith who police believes stabbed 24- year-old Brianna Kupfer and left her dying in the store where she worked just minutes after she had texted a friend saying someone inside the store was giving her "bad vibes." Just about 30 minutes after that, Smith was at a 7/11, seen here in this security camera video, calmly walking in, paying for what looks like a vaping pen, and equally calmly walking out.\par \par \uc2 Now, Smith is believed to be homeless and has a long criminal record in several states. The most serious incident we've uncovered so far happening not here in California but across the country, in South Carolina, where Smith was charged with firing a weapon into a car where a man was sitting with his toddler son back in 2019. He apparently left the state after being freed on bail there in South Carolina. Another example of what critics say is a soft on crime approach in many cities, which is putting too many criminals like Smith back on the streets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN MCKINNEY, LA COUNTY DEPUTY DISTRICT ATTORNEY. It's just heartbreaking and infuriating. And I can tell you that from prosecutors to police officers, we're looking for a better day for sure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The Los Angeles police chief meantime says there needs to be a greater focus on the twin crises of homelessness and mental health.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHEL MOORE, LAPD CHIEF: The fact of the matter is, is that over the weekend, if an individual is suffering a mental health crisis, they're, likely, the only resource they had was 911 and a police officer or firefighter, and that's wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now, Sean Laval Smith is meantime somewhere out there. Police say they are getting a lot of tips. In particular, they've gotten several in the Covina Area. That is about 30 miles east of where Brianna Kupfer was killed. The police are warning, though, members of the public obviously not to approach Sean Laval Smith if they see him because he is quite clearly armed and very dangerous. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So that manhunt underway for this suspected killer. Let's hope that he does not act again before they're able to close in on him. Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in "Fox and Friends" Enterprise Reporter Lawrence Jones, who's covered the crime surge on the ground, most recently in Chicago, Lawrence, you know, you look at all of those mug shots, I think there were about 10 of them on the screen starting in 2010, and I tried to put myself in the place of this girl's family. Can you imagine if you see this and you realize that he's been taken into custody? What is it, 12 times 12 times he's been arrested. And I'd be asking as any family across this country would be asking, how is it possible that this person was free on the street?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, "FOX AND FRIENDS" ENTERPRISE REPORTER: He's free because there's a new standard in our justice system. It started off talking about inequity and talking about -- let's talk about when civil liberties are being violated. But now it's very clear that the Democrats and the progressive movement don't want anybody incarcerated. I mean, that is what they believe that no one should be locked up.\par \par \uc2 The criminal justice system, especially when it comes to incarceration, it is meant to protect society from people that risk a threat to them. You know, many people talk about rehabilitation, which I support that, but that's really not the goal of incarceration. It is to protect citizens. And the Democrats just don't believe that. You let the show talking about there's a manhunt, why do we even care about the manhunt if they're just going to release them back on the street?\par \par \uc2 You know, it's very clear of the strategy now of the left -- and, you know, they don't care about the families. I've covered stories for over the last three-and-a-half years of families that have been heartbroken. These DAs, especially Gascon, or when you talk about Kim Fox when they decide to release these people. Do you know, Martha, they don't even reach out to the families to talk about it. They just decide it unilaterally --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They're probably embarrassed.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. Forget about that law.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They don't want people to know what they're doing. I'm going to put another picture on the screen, which we have shown on this show many times. This is 21 children under the age of 18 in New York City. These are more faces, we've been showing you the face of this young woman in California. These are also people who were too young had the life snuffed out of them. And this is, in many ways, the more common story that you see across this country, Lawrence, which you know all too well about these young people who do not have, you know, a ton of stories done about it when it happens to them.\par \par \uc2 But these are families who are suffering the same way this young woman's parents are suffering right now. And there's just too many people in this boat.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. I encourage these DAs to sit down with some of the families. Talk to them when they're grieving, go into the yard where the blood is still staining the grass. Go talk to the family that child has been shot in the head, and they're crying because they're trying to figure out how to reconstruct the skull of these young people so they can have the proper burial. I know it's graphic but I think the country really needs to see what these families are going through.\par \par \uc2 And so, often the mainstream media, they just decide not to cover it. I mean, I've been covering it for three-and-a-half years, begging the press to show up. The other week I was in Chicago, actually, it was this week. I was there Monday, in the middle of covering the story. It was a shooting right down the street, the police that was part of the scene, the security detail rush through it. You know, the guy that he shot, he was a robber as well. So people are just shooting each other because they realize there is no consequences in this new criminal justice system.\par \par \uc2 George Soros went out there with a concerted effort to put all these DAs in office. Eric Holder, who used to be the attorney general for the country, supported him in this effort. These are people that are lawyers. They understand the law they've just chosen. They want a new system, and they don't believe in these current laws that are on the books. They're doing it unilaterally.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I would encourage them to keep their eye on this picture as well, these 21 children under the age of 18 in New York City. And this is just, you know, this is just one group. These groups are all across the country, these young people who have lost their lives. And I don't I don't know who these DAs think they are protecting if they're not protecting these children and their families across the country. It is absolutely incredible work.\par \par \uc2 There are some unconfirmed reports out there that they may have apprehended Brianna Kupfer's killer. So we're not going to go with this yet but there are some encouraging potential news on that front. And we'll continue to stay on top of it. Lawrence, thank you very much. Great reporting throughout the course of the story.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you this afternoon, thanks for being here. So President Biden is just about 20 minutes away, 24 minutes away from holding this second formal press conference if he's on time, if he starts right at 4:00. His approval numbers as we've been telling you, and as you see really everywhere, they are very low at this point. This happens in presidencies. What can be done at this point and can he say anything when he's pressed on some of these issues to turn this around?\par \par \uc2 Very tough situation in the midterms as you look ahead for Democrat. Bill Hemmer and former RNC chair and former White House Chief of Staff Reince Priebus coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So as reporters sit in the East Room, pen in hand and ready to go, the President seeks to lift himself and his party's outlook in the coming year. One network says that their numbers are not pretty at this point, and that is NBC. NBC News writing, "Over the last 30 years, the NBC News poll has given us a good idea of the President's party is headed for an exceptionally good midterm election or shellacking or somewhere in between. And right now the arrows are pretty much pointing in the shellacking territory for President Biden and the Democrats."\par \par \uc2 In moments, Reince Priebus, former White House Chief of Staff for President Trump and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , former Clinton Advisor and Pollster but first to "America's Newsroom" Co-Anchor Bill Hemmer. Bill, great to have you with us as we head into the afternoon and we wait for this news conference, which I know you'll be watching every minute of along with us your thoughts on what to expect here?\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. I guess I don't expect many surprises, Martha. I guess that's why they play the games, right? That's why we have to watch.\par \par \uc2 However, here's one thing I'm keen on. David Axelrod was on CNN earlier today, early this morning, and he made a point about admitting mistakes, and not so much that you have to come out and said, hey, we screwed this up. But you could come out and you could frame it in a way, and the President could do this on his own or perhaps prompted by a reporter, to say this is what we have learned. And I think that -- I think it'd be a significant headline, Martha, I also think it would be a good way to get into the head of the President and understand what changes might be coming.\par \par \uc2 Because he's got three more years, and as Mitt Romney said, over the weekend last week, and he said 52 weeks of bad weeks. And if you're on that staff, you're in the West Wing, and you're the President, you do not want that record to continue in that direction.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, based on those NBC polls, and we've seen a lot of numbers that point in this direction. Of course, we've got a long way to go before the midterms, really, but I think a president can make this decision to pivot now or after the midterms. And I am reminded when you talk about that quote from David Axelrod, that he remembers what it's like to take a shellacking. And I think in fact, that word was coined by President Obama after his midterms. Here's a look back at what President Obama said after his midterm election. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: I'm not recommending for every future president but they take a shellacking like I did last night, you know, I'm sure there are easier ways to learn these lessons. But I do think that, you know, this is a growth process and an evolution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting, right?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes. I remember that day and it was a stunner when Republicans took 63 House seats. Not to say that's the prediction for this coming midterm in 2022. However, I've talked to a lot of people in Washington, a lot of people have crunched the numbers. I look at districts. You have close to 80% of the redistricting decided, Martha. It's going to take another month, month-and-a-half before Florida and New York, through the bigger states outstanding check in with their maps.\par \par \uc2 And so, around the first week of March things are going to be pretty settled. This is what I hear. And granted this point, you ask a different person, you'll get a different evaluation.\par \par \uc2 But there are those in the Republican Party who believe anywhere between 45 and upwards of 70 seats are in play for them to win. I'm not saying that's going to happen. And in fact, I think as we get closer to November, the analysis will really fall around the 45 number closer to that 45, 50 than the 70 number.\par \par \uc2 However, I think this is stunning, Martha. Republican strategist believe that any district that Joe Biden won last year by 12 points or less is in play. And I just find that extraordinary a year into this presidency, that you can consider districts with that bit of a margin to be up for grabs in November next year.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, when you've got a 14 point swing from Dem leaning to Republican leaning in the country, that sounds like a pretty logical number. We'll see where it goes as we say a long time ago. Bill, thank you very much. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, also here a great panel with a lot of experience in the White House in these kinds of moments. Reince Priebus, former White House Chief of Staff for President Trump, now president of the Michael Best Law Firm, and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , former Clinton advisor and pollster, now chairman of the \uc2 Harris Poll\uc2 . Great to see both of you today.\par \par \uc2 Reince, as you look at this moment, it's always a big moment for President when he is one on one with the press. They can ask follow up questions in this kind of environment. What do you expect?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER TRUMP WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, I agree with Bill. I think it's probably going to be more of the same.\par \par \uc2 Here's the problem that Joe Biden has. He's losing his brand. And when you lose your brand, you've got a big problem. What is his brand? His brand was that he was going to be the unifier. Even when he won the Democratic primary, a lot of the party weren't happy with Joe Biden being the nominee, but they held their nose and they went with it because they thought, well, he is the guy that's going to be able to portray this unified message that's going to beat Trump.\par \par \uc2 And then, when he won in November, the week after he called for unity. When the Electoral College was counted, he said, he called for unity, and healing. And then on the 20th, in January, in inauguration, he actually tied George Washington and using the words together and unify, and unity, more than any other president except George Washington. So when you lose your brand, it's very difficult.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of the things that Bill Clinton did after he got killed in 1994, which actually the numbers are better now than they were in '94 for Republicans, he basically came on said, you know what, I am going to pivot to the middle. I'm going to govern the way that I said I would, the way I did in Arkansas. And I'm going to just -- I'm telling you right now, I'm going to be the moderate that I promised you I was.\par \par \uc2 And so, maybe that's where Joe Biden can go but rebuilding and recapturing a brand that you lost is really hard in politics.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Mark, you've been sort of calling for this from President Biden since early November for him to make this kind of pivot. I guess the question is, can he do it? Can you do it successfully?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER CLINTON ADVISOR: Well, could he? Absolutely. I think there are many things that President Biden could do. He could convene a bipartisan group to negotiate his legislation and stop doing one party solutions. I think he did pardon all the non-violent protesters from January 6 and really reach out --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that would be a big deal. That would be a very different tune than we heard from him on Jan 6, that's for sure.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Obviously it would be very different tune. You know, just as President Clinton called for a balanced budget, which was a very different tune at the time, it would take a different tune. Look, for today's press conference, President Biden has to first show he's all there. He's in charge. He can answer these questions civilly and carefully, and with some contemplation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think he's all there, Mark? I mean, do you think those assessments of his abilities are overstated or understated?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, I think that's for the American people to judge. I think that's why the President needs to go out more. And I think that's the first level of judgment. The second level of judgment is, is he going to reach over and go back to being what he campaigned on or not. And if not, the American people are going to give him a shellacking.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Reince, when you hear these numbers, 45 to 70 seats, do you think that Republicans are getting over their skis at this point? We're still 10 months away.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: Well, I would suggest for Republicans to go take the under. It's really hard nowadays because they're just not that many moderate seats out there. I mean, if you think about 2018 and 2010. And \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , he knows what I'm talking about 90% of everyone elected to the US House was safe. So 90% of Republicans are safe 90% of Democrats are safe. There's not that many out there to get. But certainly Republicans are in the driver's seat, they should win the majority. I would keep the rhetoric at that.\par \par \uc2 As to this Biden situation, part of the problem here is that he intentionally did things through executive orders on the border, on gas and oil, on the economy that helped create the situation we're in. So while I do agree, he needs the pivot to save himself. It's very difficult based on the intentional conduct of this Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, tomorrow is always another day. We will see. Reince Priebus and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , great to have both of you with us today as we head into this news conference now, just about 10 minutes away. Gentlemen, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I want to get this in before -- we don't have any two minute warning or anything like that, so we're just waiting on the White House. But this is breaking news. As we mentioned moments ago, we have now learned that Brianna Kupfer's suspected murder has been arrested. Let's check back with chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt who has been on this breaking news for us over the last hours. He's live in Los Angeles once again. Hi, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Hey, Martha. That arrest apparently made a short time ago in Pasadena. That is about 15 miles north and east of where 24-year-old Brianna Kupfer was killed last Thursday, stabbed as we now know by a man identified by police as Sean Laval Smith, a-31-year-old man with a long criminal history.\par \par \uc2 We are now being told sources telling our producer, Alexandria Hernandez, that Sean Laval Smith was arrested at a bus stop in Pasadena. Police have told us all along that he was a homeless man who frequently used public transit. They said they were getting a lot of tips. It appears that one of those at least has now paid off. The alleged killer of 24-year-old Brianna Kupfer arrested in Pasadena this afternoon. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, good news, some good news, at least for the people of California and for the family. Now we'll see what happens to this person now that he is in custody. And we'll watch it closely. Very tough story, Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, as you all know who've been with us throughout this hour, we are waiting for President Biden to take the podium in the East Room of the White House. He will do something he hasn't done in 10 months, which is face reporters in a formal press conference, Q&A at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue because of COVID restrictions. This will look a little different than in years past, about 30 reporters in the room, including Peter Doocy. A scaled down Q&A may be somewhat of a relief, perhaps, to President Biden as he enters year two of his presidency, but he obviously will face some stiff questions we expect in this crowd because, you know, really, there's been a large sort of skepticism that has become more pervasive, I guess, across the press over the course of the last couple of weeks. So we expect this to be interesting.\par \par \uc2 His ratings, as you know, are at an all time low. They hover between 33% and 40%, basically. Bret Baier is standing by with us as we get ready for this to get underway. But first, let's go to correspondent Jacquie Heinrich, who is live at the White House. Good afternoon, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon to you, Martha. Yes, there is no denying that this administration is really just haunted by losses over legislation, Supreme Court battles. You've got inflation, high crime supply chain issues, foreign policy challenges and, of course, COVID and has given this president its critics a lot of fodder to work with.\par \par \uc2 But according to the White House, one of their biggest pitfalls has been on messaging and ineffectively selling the wins that the President has accomplished. They just sent a reminder list to the press highlighting what they called record firsts in jobs, the unemployment rate, expanded access to health care, staffing diversity, infrastructure, investments, and more. And this is something that the President plans to highlight today and also over the course of the next year in a victory tour of sorts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He intends to really take his case to the American people over the course of this year. He will be out in the country talking about how the things he's proposed are going to make a difference in people's lives. And people are going to hear from him directly on that across the course of 2022.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: In a separate list we just got, dubbed results delivered for working families also highlights the administration's huge vaccination campaign. Of course, that's been a top White House priority but COVID has really been a double edged sword with record breaking case numbers and, of course, the Supreme Court swatting down mandates.\par \par \uc2 But part of this broader strategy going forward is to focus more on showcasing the President's wins and make his bill negotiations the Democrat in fighting that has stalled his agenda less visible. The White House reportedly no longer releasing details of their talks with law lawmakers, reportedly to shed this problematic image that the President is the legislator in chief amid criticism. He's approaching the presidency more like a member of the Senate where he served for 36 years and less like the commander in chief getting things done.\par \par \uc2 And that means we are not likely today to hear a roadmap to the legislation that the President wants to get passed. And that's going to be interesting, because it's going to be everyone's biggest question with the Senate set to tee up a doomed effort on voting rights in the filibuster just hours from now, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting. Jacqui, thank you very much. Joining me now as we wait for the President, Bret Baier, Chief Political Anchor and Anchor of "Special Report." Bret, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 We are told that we're going to get a 10-minute, roughly, speech at the top of this from President Biden. And now we have a view of the podium in the East Room as he gets ready to walk in there. We expect probably about an hour of Q&A. And you can hear the messages that have been sent out through Jacqui's Heinrich report about the wins and the victory tour, and the effort to kind of change the narrative here. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHIEF POLITICAL ANCHOR: Definitely. I mean, this is the 10-minute speech, you're going to get a teleprompter speech from the President that likely will have all of those elements that Jacqui just touched on. And also the message that Ron Klein was delivered to reporters today, the White House Chief of Staff, that President Biden was elected to a four-year term, not a one-year term.\par \par \uc2 Now, he's got some uphill battles to fight before the midterms and expect, like you're hearing the communications team say, that the President will travel the country. And, you know, he passed that bipartisan infrastructure bill, one of the legislative successes that they had, expect him to be standing in front of some bridge, some road, some thing that is being built to say this, we did get done.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of negatives here and a lot of things that are going to be asked about, not only domestically but foreign policy wise. This is the second time he'll hold a formal news conference on US soil and it's the sixth official news conference since taking president.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's interesting, I think about what James Carville said. He said that the Democrats were too whiny. And he said you need to focus on the stuff that you have achieved and forget about the other stuff. And then, I hear this plan to change his posture from being a legislator and former senator because that hasn't gone too well. In three efforts to go up on Capitol Hill and wrangle his own party to his side on major legislation, that BBB and this voting reform, it's failed.\par \par \uc2 So it's no doubt, no surprise I guess, I should say, Bret, that they want to go, OK, we're not doing that anymore.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it's pretty ironic too, Martha, that this is happening on the day that the voting reform effort is going to fail miserably, just by the numbers in the Senate. And, you know, he's going to have to feel that question as well.\par \par \uc2 You're right. He is the executive. He is not a senator anymore and he has not done when he said on the campaign trail, which is figure out a way to get the Senate to work on both sides and come up with things that people agree on first, instead of what they disagree on. That is not the legislative push that they've made. They've done a hat tip to the progressive side. And that's really hurt in moderates and independents around the country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, there's a lot of criticism early on of the press, you know, asking him about his favorite flavor of ice cream and things along those lines. But that's changed in recent weeks. I think it's been become impossible for any reporter covering this presidency to ignore the numbers that are out there right now and to ignore Afghanistan, and inflation, and COVID, and these other issues, testing.\par \par \uc2 Here's just a sampling of sort of how that change has happened and what it sounds like on some of -- in some of the media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Every time I think the White House stuff they've hit bottom, there seems to be a new bottom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Joe Biden has had a very tough week with setbacks for his agenda.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 2022 is not exactly off to a good start for the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Now he's in the midst of what one famous children's book writer called a terrible, horrible, no good, very bad time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Which raises the question what the tone will be like in this room with that as a backdrop, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think a lot more forceful than we saw in the first formal press conference, a lot more -- there's a lot more substance to ask about, really negative things that this White House is facing, not only at home but abroad. I think you're right that Afghanistan really did start that drop where people started to realize big picture that the credibility that Biden touted on the campaign trail was not transferring into the White House, at least not up on Capitol Hill and across the pond.\par \par \uc2 I think that they have a lot to choose from. I'm sure Peter Doocy has got a full folder full of possibilities for him to ask questions. And I know when I was in those rooms, you know, you're kind of mapping things out. You have A, B and C, and you see where you fall when you get that question.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He has not been officially called on in this environment. He's managed to pipe up and get his question in, which is what everybody will be trying to do. And we're going to be watching it closely. They've just sort of come in and out of the room and put some things at the podium, so we may be getting a little bit closer to this.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Medi