 not bother the guests. He does not get crazy. He's totally focused on the screen.\par \par \uc2 Now, haven't you always wondered whether or not a dog can see what's on television? Look at that, an iPhone. I'm going to go home tonight and try it on Ted.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: There you go.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Who's Ted?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, who's Ted?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Ted, Red, and Stella. Ted is not a -- he's a four-legged -- a four legged dog.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Some dude. Some guy you picked up.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Four-legged.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: A four-legged dog.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Where are we? Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, you know how I like to check in on the goings-on in Russia. Well, let's look at Germany. What the heck is going on in Germany? This is what's going on.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Some German woman picks up this massive bird. And what does she do with it? She's just going to drop it right into the river. She's going to pick it up. She's going to drop it right over that little thing and just drop it right in the river. The swan was disoriented. I don't know the German word for disoriented but it was very disoriented. And now it's back in the -- in that lovely body of water. But that's what they do in Germany. Get some disoriented birds and they throw them in bodies of water.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You are -- you are a fearless culture critic, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, Geraldo, 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Time for Geraldo's Geraldo news Geraldo. At the Rivera house, morning coffee is served with the side of love. It all started one morning I decided to leave a love note for Erica on her coffee which I make in the morning. Now, it's a regular thing.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Cute.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: On days when I don't have radio or TV, I sleep in, she makes the coffee. But I tried to send her a little note.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's so cute.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: And 21 years together, wonderful.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Peter, take notes.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That' wonderful.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They're not going to (INAUDIBLE), Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, I do that -- when I'm in D.C., I do that to Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220113ei1c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Parents Describe Impact Of Pandemic On Their Kids; Impact Of Remote Learning On Kids' Mental Health; Chicago Parents Accuse Teachers Of Illegal Strike; SCOTUS Debates Mandate Issued Before Omicron Surge; Fox Presses White House On COVID Testing; \uc2 Taliban\uc2  Orders Store Owners To Behead Mannequins; Five For Fighting Song Highlights Afghan Struggle; Baldwin Says He Is Cooperating With Shooting Probe; Firefighter Rescues Baby From Deadly New York City Fire\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Gillian Turner, Garrett Tenney, Karl Rove, Peter  Doocy, Trey Yingst \par \uc2 7,812 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: The pilot was the only person on board when his engine failed. Police rushed him to the hospital; the LAPD credited the officer's heroism, and quick action for saving the pilot. Remarkable story there. John, great to be with you for these two hours -- it flew by. Thanks for joining us, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith in New York.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: That officer should never have to ever buy another drink ever again. And I'm John Roberts. "THE STORY" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, John and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum at Fox News Headquarters in New York, the White House reacting moments ago to the mental health crisis that is facing kids as over 5,000 schools in the United States who have shut their doors on their students.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The President's view is to that school should be open across the country and more than 95 percent are across the country that the mental health impact on kids of not having schools open is, is very harsh and hard and he does not want to see schools closed across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what will he put behind those words, is the question for the White House right now. The \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  and three other large health agencies have declared a national state of emergency in the United States and children's health stating this. We're caring for young people with soaring rates of depression, anxiety, trauma, loneliness and suicidality that will have lasting impacts on them. Long after the pandemic is over folks. $122 billion has gone to schools to provide money for mitigation measures, testing -- where did that money go? And safety. The focus on the White House from the White House and from the CDC has clearly been on vaccines. But what about this other huge national emergency health issue facing this country.\par \par \uc2 These parents lost their children to suicide during the pandemic, and they know the heartbreaking damage firsthand.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DARA FALCO, LOST SON TO SUICIDE DURING PANDEMIC: The after-school activities again, like sports, that was his community, and that's what kept him involved.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And that was gone.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Had COVID not happened, or our country's response to COVID had been better, that he would still be alive today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Correspondent Gillian Turner has more for us. Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. So, experts say grief, anxiety and depression children have endured as the result of the school closures and forced isolation is now boiling over one mother tells Fox News her high schoolers behavior change dramatically during the month his school shut down. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLANCA TAPAHUASCO, BALTIMORE COUNTY PARENT: He was failing. He fell into a deep depression. There was not really a lot of communication between us. He was wandering, pacing, really almost. It was not a not a good scene, you could see that the anxiety was bubbling over.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice over): A mother of a 9-year-old describes a similar pattern of withdrawing and then lashing out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He doesn't want to go outside doesn't want to talk to anyone and just isolates himself. We're still working on his combative behavior. He, he would have daily meltdowns, daily breakdowns screaming crying.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Suicide attempts among adolescents are rising sharply most acutely among 12 to 17-year-old girls by 51 percent. Boys, though are not immune.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He looked at me in my face one night before he went to bed. And he said, I'm really just waiting for you to fall asleep so I can kill myself. He was nine.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Child psychologist say it is normal for children to feel intense negative emotions when they're separated from their peers.\par \par \uc2 DR. CAROL VIDAL, CHILD PSYCHIATRIST: So, it's part of their development actually want to be out with people their age, and they learn they learn about what their identity is. They, they engage them, they learn about themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Experts tell us a lot of parents' report crying and disruptive behavior in young kids and then increased violence among adolescents. If you or your child needs help, you can call the suicide prevention hotline Martha the numbers on the screen there. It's 1-800-273-8255, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Gillian, thank you very much. Dr. Jeffrey Gardere is a Psychologist and Associate Professor and Course Director of Behavioral Medicine at Toro College here in New York City. Doctor, it's always good to have you on. You know, I guess the question becomes, you know, when you're looking at this risk benefit, right? We have schools that are shut down or some schools that are going remote and balance that against what you so that's, that's the threat of COVID. Then on the other side, you've got the threat of isolation and mental illness. How do those weigh for you? What's more important at this point?\par \par \uc2 DR. JEFF GARDERE, PSYCHOLOGIST: Well, there's no easy answer to that, Martha. Certainly, we want our kids to be safe. But we also know that through the pandemic, we've seen that our children have lost an average of five months in mathematics and four months with regard to reading, and those numbers are even higher in impoverished parts of the various cities of America. So, certainly, it's a very difficult decision and why I think you see, many of the schools are saying, yes, we must do this in a more hybrid fashion. If we close, we do it temporarily, and try to get the kids back as quickly as possible. But again, the health of the children, the safety of the children is what's most important.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, they need socialization, they need time together, I think we need therapy programs in schools. And, and one of the things that you just touched on in the loss of learning, that increases anxiety in kids, when they're nervous that they're left behind, or that they're going to get in school, and they're not going to be able to keep up.\par \par \uc2 And here's some students who scored proficient or above over the course of 2021, that proficiency level in math fell by 14.2 percent. And in reading and language arts by 6.3 percent. Those are big drops. So, my question is, for teachers across the country, if you're, if your students aren't meeting that proficiency level, shouldn't we consider holding them back, so that they have time to catch up and then advance to the next grade when they're ready?\par \par \uc2 GARDERE: I think many teachers and school administrators have talked about that. I think the alternative, what they found is perhaps to have summer classes, perhaps to have the classes outdoors, hoping that perhaps there will be less of a spread of COVID during the warmer months, that is very important. But you bring up also a very incredibly important point.\par \par \uc2 And that in seeing those drops in scores, we're also seeing that high schoolers now tend to more likely drop out of school. And as well, many students who thought about college now are so disillusioned by feeling that they've lost so much in school, lost that school spirit, they're not even thinking about college at this point. So, this is a major, major issue. It is a state of emergency with regard to the mental health of our children, and this is something that we now have to give all of our resources towards.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, so what would you say to the Department of Education, because they've been pretty quiet on this issue of making sure that kids are proficient before they move on? Or making sure that we work now. It's January, on a summer school program for this summer? Why aren't we hearing specifics about that, and as a psychologist, what would you say to the department of education about what they need to do?\par \par \uc2 GARDERE: Well, I would certainly tell the department of education that they have to bring in every kind of academic program that can help children with regard to catching up, and then after catching up being able to stay proficient in these grades. But also, as part of that, Martha, they have to look at bringing in mental health services, and integrating them every single day into the programs that these kids have. Because it's not just the loss in academics, as you pointed out, but also the loss of the social skills that you address. Schools are not just about reading, writing and arithmetic, they're also about finding a safe place to be able to find who they are, and be able to develop their personalities and individuation, and all of that much of it has been lost. So, department of education in schools across the country really have to look at the mental health component being combined with the academic component.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, we can all, we can all say that that's what they really need to do. But it need -- that message needs to come from the top, we need a national program, catch up our kids academically, which I think leads to more confidence, which then helps them with the social side as well. But we need to have a national program to catch up our kids. I don't know if there's a better name for it than that, but I'd love to hear about it from the Department of Education, what their plan is to make sure that we don't let these kids fall by the wayside. Jeffrey Gardere is always good to talk to you. Thank you very much for being here today.\par \par \uc2 GARDERE: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, a story exclusive with the attorney who is taking on the \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  , alleging that its strike is not only harmful to kids and to the obligation these teachers have to educate them which is their job, but he says it's also illegal based on their own contracts. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, how about this? Some parents in Chicago are now launching a lawsuit against the teachers' union, accusing them of holding an illegal strike. The Union voted to switch to remote learning last week canceled school has now gone into the fourth day. We're in the second week of school cancellations there in a moment. I'm going to speak with a lawyer for the parents who are suing but first of course Monique Garrett Tenney, who is live in Chicago with more on this force. Hi, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hey, Martha, so seven parents filed this lawsuit against the \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  to try and get the courts to force teachers back into the classroom. The lawsuit claims that teachers are carrying out an illegal strike by refusing to show up to their classrooms to teach in person as the district has mandated.\par \par \uc2 It states that parents of children of \uc2 CPS\uc2  students will be harmed by \uc2 CTU\uc2  strike because their children will be denied schooling and they will be forced to arrange childcare because their children are unable to attend school. Earlier today, our photographer caught some of that frustration folks are feeling towards the union on camera when several teachers were outside of high school protesting the district safety measures and a woman pulled up to give them a piece of her mind.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Shame on you! Remote learning is not an answer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We care deeply about your children.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No, you don't. You'll be in there just like all of us we have to work. OK? Stop treating our children like (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I care deeply about your children.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: And this weekend, Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  said the outrage from parents is her best leverage in this standoff with the \uc2 CTU\uc2 . In this morning, the union's president accused Lightfoot of bullying teachers and refusing to let them back into the classroom by compromising to make a deal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don't like bullies, we don't like tyrants. We're not going to be bullied or pushed into a corner. But we're asking for some basic common sense safety measures.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: To be clear, the union wants its teachers to be back in the classroom. It just doesn't want students there until the number of cases in the city have gone down or until they get a deal that includes more robust testing. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Garrett, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Jeffrey Schwab, senior attorney for the Liberty Justice Center that filed the lawsuit on behalf of Chicago Public Schools' parents against the teachers union. Jeffrey, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 $5 billion, went to the state of Illinois for basic common sense safety measures that we just heard the union president talking about.\par \par \uc2 Let's play this -- let's play this parent. This is a Chicago parent who's on the side of the teachers union, and then we'll get your response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE, CHICAGO PUBLIC SCHOOLS PARENT: I have a kindergarten and a first grader. First grader does not attend school, medical reasons.\par \par \uc2 Since 2019, since COVID started, it's been hard situation. My 5-year-old, she is the one that goes in-person, and I do not think it's the safest place to send her. I really think students should be virtual.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, that's both sides of the story from our reporter, Garrett Tenney, and from that woman who you just heard from, what do you say?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY SCHWAB, SENIOR ATTORNEY, LIBERTY JUSTICE CENTER: Well, in this case, we filed a lawsuit because the \uc2 CTU\uc2  has unilaterally decided, for all 340,000 kids in Chicago that they can't go to school. And so, what we're saying is, you don't have that option. Let the people decide, let the parents have the input, let teachers have input, and let the school district decide how it wants to handle it.\par \par \uc2 The schools can decide on an individual basis if they want to. Parents can decide on the individual basis. But the union can't decide for everybody.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Who is the boss -- you know, who is the boss here? So, they are saying, we're not striking, we just won't go to work.\par \par \uc2 And then, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  , the mayor says, you know, you could be fired if you don't show up.\par \par \uc2 So, who is going to actually push that, you know, when push comes to shove? Who's going to -- who's going to take it to that level?\par \par \uc2 SCHWAB: Well, that's why we filed this lawsuit because the law and the contract are clear that the union can't go on strike.\par \par \uc2 And the problem is that nobody is enforcing the law or the contract against the union.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHWAB: So, that's why our parents -- our seven parents of \uc2 CPS\uc2  kids have stepped up, and they've said enough is enough. Over the last year and a half, the union has threatened to strike or engaged in strikes, and now it's doing it again. It's keeping kids out of school. It's unilaterally deciding for everybody what they want, without parent input, without student input, without \uc2 CPS\uc2  really --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you have any indication from the -- from the mayor that she's actually going to go to follow through and fire these teachers for not showing up at work?\par \par \uc2 SCHWAB: Well, no. And, in fact, the union -- or the \uc2 CPS\uc2  and the mayor have had plenty of opportunities to try to stop the union from engaging in this illegal strike tactic, and they haven't over the last year and a half. That's why we've had to file this lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, I looked it up today, contractually the teachers in Chicago have to teach -- they have to have 176 days of teaching. So, are -- is there -- is part of this negotiation discussion. They've now lost four of those. Right?\par \par \uc2 So, when do they plan to make those up? And will they be held to that? Because that's what these students -- that's the promise that we make to the students, 176 days of education.\par \par \uc2 SCHWAB: Yes, and I hope that they will have to make these days up. But in the meantime, the kids are at home, and they're not learning. They've already been at home for the two-week break that's been basically extended for another week.\par \par \uc2 And they need to be back in school, as your last segment shows they need to see their friends and to socialize, and to be with other kids. And they're not -- they're not getting that opportunity because \uc2 CTU\uc2  has taken the law into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, I don't understand how this happens. Like, you know, in most cases, if you don't show up for your job over a certain period of time, you're going to get fired.\par \par \uc2 So, I think everybody watches this, and they know the rules that apply for the rest of the -- of the country, and they don't understand what's going on. And people can say, we hope -- you could say we have hope that they're going to make up these days, but you know, either they have to get 176 days of education or they don't. It seems like there's just so much wiggle room and it seems like the students are the ones who are really getting hurt like that woman yelling at the people on the side of the street expressed.\par \par \uc2 So, we'll follow this case and we'll see where it goes. Jeffrey Schwab, thank you very much. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 SCHWAB: Thank you. Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, if activist \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2  is going to join us in a moment on a really important story about the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  's new crackdown in Afghanistan. And attorney Brian Claypool is here, also, when THE STORY continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): There are fewer than 3,500 current pediatric hospitalizations from COVID-19. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL: Yes. We also know that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The number is not 100,000. It's roughly 3,500 in hospitals now?\par \par \uc2 WALENSKY: It -- yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Bret Baier trying to clear things up with the CDC director because of something that Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  said during arguments on President Biden's COVID mandates. We're waiting for a ruling from the Supreme Court on the vaccine mandates and whether or not they will hold starting today at businesses with at least 100 workers, employees have to get vaccinated, or wear masks. A testing requirement for the unvaccinated set to kick in next month.\par \par \uc2 Here is what Justice Sotomayor said that Bret was pressing the CDC director on about the COVID cases in the U.S. and children getting sick. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have hospitals that are almost at full capacity with people is severely ill on ventilators. We have over 100,000 Children, which we've never had before -- in serious condition, and many on ventilators.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not true. Not true. The number is 3,500. And that's on the high side, according to the numbers that we looked at.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Karl Rove, former deputy chief of staff and senior adviser to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , co-founder of American Crossroads, and a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Karl, the question that I put to a couple of Supreme Court experts is, is she required to make clarification, make a correction on this. Because this is part of the Supreme Court record and people all across the country heard it, and it's completely false.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. Well, it should be well advised to do so in whatever opinion she renders on this case. But yes, her credibility is a little tattered today, because it's not only are there not just 3,500 children in the hospital, but the number of those that are on ventilators is -- the CDC had suggested Dr. Walensky, that there might be zero on ventilator. She was not aware of any that were on ventilators.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that's right. And that is problematic. The other thing that's problematic when you look at this case, and of course companies all across the country are watching this really closely.\par \par \uc2 There's a piece in The Wall Street Journal today. "Omicron makes Biden's vaccine mandates obsolete," which is very interesting. And it's put out by the doctor who discovered HIV who won a Nobel Prize for that, and a constitutional scholar.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it says one preprint study found that after 30 days, the \uc2 Moderna\uc2  and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccines no longer had a statistically significant positive effect against Omicron.\par \par \uc2 After 90 days, it went negative. For example, vaccinated people were more susceptible to Omicron infection. Based on data from Denmark and Canada, vaccinated people at higher rates of Omicron infection than unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is just enough to make your head spin.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But his point -- the overall point of this, Karl, is that why are the justices ruling on a vaccine that really treats the earlier stages of COVID when 95 percent of what people have now is Omicron. And we have no idea yet how powerful the vaccines that they're talking about mandating are against this Omicron.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: You're absolutely right. Dr. Montagnier also quoted -- the CDC itself, which said, we don't know if there's any efficacy of these current vaccines against Omicron.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: And look, the Supreme Court is dealing with two questions. And the issue that the very that the Nobel Prize winner and the constitutional scholar brought up is, is it essential to curbing the pandemic? Because last week, if you listen closely to the Supreme Court, they kept -- the advocates of the mandates kept referring to a case called Jacobson versus Massachusetts settled in 1905, in which the Supreme Court held that there was a right -- the right to refuse medical treatment could be overruled, if it was necessary to spread to stop the spread of a contagious disease -- its contagious epidemic.\par \par \uc2 So, this is one question. is it essential to curbing the pandemic? And the point made in the Wall Street Journal article is we don't have any evidence that the current vaccines would do anything to stop it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: But here is the more fundamental question. The case involving Massachusetts (INAUDIBLE) in 1905, it involves the ability to the government of the right of an individual to refuse treatment. There's a more fundamental question that was -- we discussed last week, which is, does the government have the legal authority to do this?\par \par \uc2 Where in statute was the intention or the expressive authority granted to the federal government to take this kind of an action, and there was clearly a lot of skepticism on behalf of most of the members of the Supreme Court that the \uc2 OSHA\uc2  law passed in 1970, was intended to be of this far- reaching power to the federal government.\par \par \uc2 We'll know soon enough. But my suspicion is they're going to strike down the ability of the \uc2 OSHA\uc2  mandate, every company will have 100 more employees has to have everybody vaccinated, they might uphold the ability of the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to require health care workers funded by the federal government to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's really stunning. And as you point out, even if you have a more liberal interpretation of what should happen under an emergency circumstance, you have to prove that the remedy that you are -- that you are mandating to people has been scientifically proven to be effective.\par \par \uc2 We don't have that here.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now, we don't have it yet. It may be, we don't have it yet.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: No, it take -- well, in fact, we've got evidence to the contrary, because there are 50 different changes variations in this latest Omicron variant that don't -- that are ways to get around the safety, the security provided by the vaccines, and we know that for a fact.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people -- that this is important, you know, these are the facts as we have them and it's really important. Especially for the spring court justices to be aware of what's going on with all of this and what the actual numbers are.\par \par \uc2 Karl, thank you very much. Always great to see you. Karl Rove joining us this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking moments ago, Fox pressing the White House on its plan to distribute 50 million COVID tests, and whether by the time they arrive, omicron might be over.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: The President talks about winter of severe illness and death. At the rate you guys are going, these tests are not going to be available until spring. So will you admit that these free tests you guys promised are not going to be ready until after the omicron surge?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will show you the answer to that question. Dr. Ben Carson is standing by listening to all of this. And he's going to weigh in. The Biden administration also has been prioritizing race when it comes to administering COVID therapies, does he think that that is a fair and effective way to address the virus when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fox pressing the White House on how the Biden administration plans to get millions of free COVID-19 test to Americans during a record surge of cases. In a moment, Dr Ben Carson who served on President Trump's COVID-19 task force, but first to White House correspondent Peter Doocy. Hi, Peter. Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Thank you, Martha. Good to be back. And a few minutes ago, a big admission in the White House briefing room, the Biden administration still has not finalized the contracts for those 500 free tests that they -- 500 million free tests that they have promised to send out to any American who wants them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President there, on television, talking about a winter of severe illness and death. While he's saying that publicly, why weren't you guys doing more to prepare for the winter?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, Peter, everyone decides where they're going to go get a test. And we make a range of options available. You can also purchase tests online. Later today, we'll have more details on how 150 million Americans who have health insurance can get reimbursed for tests. We'll have more details on that later. The 500 million tests I noted and I give you a little bit of an update on the timeline. We'll have more on the website later this week.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: A few minutes ago on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , an administration official revealed that January 15th is when some Americans are going to be able to get reimbursed for tests by their insurance. But as she talks about Americans deciding where they are going to go for tests, the CDC's guidance right now is that if you either test positive or think you might have COVID, or think you might have been exposed, you're supposed to stay home for five days. And so for a lot of Americans, receiving a test in the mail is the only way to see if they are actually sick. But that's not happening, at least not the way the administration has promised for a few more weeks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Peter. Peter Doocy at the White House drilling down on the question of when those tests are going to arrive in the mail. Dr. Ben Carson served on Trump administration's COVID-19 task force, he's also former secretary, the former secretary for the Department of Housing and Urban Development. Dr. Carson, always good to see you. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 BEN CARSON, FORMER WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 TASK FORCE MEMBER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this Omicron situation, you know, people are waiting for tests, what is your indication? What do you think is going to happen in terms of how -- when this is going to level off, and you think those tests are going to arrive in time to serve that need?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Well, it's quite clear that the tests are not going to arrive in time. Maybe that's not such a bad thing. Because, you know, the more tests you have, the more positive people you're going to have. And if you don't have a good plan for what you're going to do with those positive people and just add more to the confusion.\par \par \uc2 You know, what we really need to be thinking about is what is actually happening with this virus? It's clearly getting weaker and more contagious, which means it's going to spread widely throughout our entire society. What are we going to do about that? Are we going to be afraid of it? Are we going to hunker down and hide from it? Or are we going to use the knowledge that we have in order to be able to live effectively with it.\par \par \uc2 We have some very effective therapeutics. We need to be concentrating on early therapy, and making it available for people. It should be the same kind of urgency that we had in terms of getting out the vaccine. That's what needs to happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. I just want to go back and ask you. So are you suggesting that people shouldn't test and that they shouldn't -- it doesn't matter if they know if they're positive or not by the first comment that you made there?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Well, I'm saying that we need to be more measured in who were testing, to just go out widely and test everybody when you have something that is spreading this fast, and you don't have a plan to deal with it. All you're doing is adding to the confusion.\par \par \uc2 Yes, certainly, people who are at risk, those are the people we should be saving the test for. And people who have to interface with a lot of people who perhaps have high risk. Those are people who should be tested. Let's not be just indiscriminately testing people and then throwing up our hands when we have so many people who are positive and not knowing what to do with them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it would be great if you could, you know, apply to receive a task based on the people that you are exposing yourself to or elderly members of the family, or people who might be vulnerable for different reasons. And then you could specifically target it to that population. It's an interesting idea.\par \par \uc2 Now, with regard to treatments, what do you think about these efforts to decide based on race who should be first in line for treatment?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: You know, I grew up, I've been around long enough to remember going to Tennessee when I was six years old, and seeing the white and colored drinking fountains, and seeing the dogs and the hoses being used on people, and suffering all kinds of insults on the base of your race. But I also remember the people, the millions of people of all races who worked so hard to get rid of discrimination.\par \par \uc2 And here we are trying to bring it back, that's unbelievable. It truly is unbelievable. The fact of the matter is, yes, members of certain minority communities do have higher incidence of diabetes, and hypertension, and other types of underlying conditions that increase their risk. But they have those anyway. You don't have to throw race into this. All you have to do is treat the people who need to be treated. And probably a higher number of those individuals will be treated because they have a greater incidence of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. If you target the conditions, not the color of people's skin, then you're serving everybody who needs to be served based on the conditions that they have, and not the color of their skin.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: I guarantee that the virus doesn't go around looking for people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No kidding.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: And deciding whether --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's amazing that we're even having these conversations but that's where we are. Dr. Carson, it's always great to see you sir. Thank you very much for coming by.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. So this is an interesting image, right. They're sawing the heads off of female mannequins at the request of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  . So what's going on there, that's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  's latest target is mannequins, and Afghan journalist says that militants were told -- told store owners in one province to chop the heads off of all of the mannequins, saying that it's against Islam to worship idols. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  also detaining a Kabul University professor who confronted a senior official on live TV. Journalist, activist and Nobel Peace Prize nominee \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2  is here but first a correspondent Trey Yingst live in Tel Aviv. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. Life under \uc2 Taliban\uc2  rule really continues to deteriorate for the Afghan people. Shop owners in the province of Herat, were told to behead mannequins. All of this related to what some \uc2 Taliban\uc2  members decided was a sinful idol representation.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Ministry of Vice and Virtue reportedly issued a decree last week banning people from worshiping anyone or anything other than Allah. The government office, formerly known as the Ministry of Women's Affairs, initially told shop owners to remove mannequins completely but settled on removing their heads. This tribal interpretation of the Quran and Islamic law is not representative of the Muslim world, but is the new reality for Afghan civilians who fear speaking out against the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 One prominent professor from Kabul University is now under arrest after criticizing the group in control of this country. Law and political professor Faizullah Jalal was taken into custody over the weekend for speaking out against the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  , sparking concern from around the world.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a lot of bad news coming out of Afghanistan but we do have one piece of good news to report, a small baby who was separated from his parents during the evacuation process back in August was reunited with family members this week after he was found. He was currently in the custody basically of a taxi driver that picked him up from the airport, and he was again reunited with his family. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we all remember that moment. Trey, thank you very much, Trey Yingst. Let's bring in \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2 , Iranian journalist and activist, author of the "Wind in My Hair: My Fight for Freedom in Modern Iran," and a nominee for this year's Nobel Peace Prize. Masih, welcome, always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 MASIH ALINEJAD\uc2 , IRANIAN JOURNALIST AND ACTIVIST: Thank you. Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what is your reaction to what's happening in Herat? And the beheading of these mannequins, what's the meaning of this and what does it tell us about the bigger picture?\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: Martha, to be honest, from the first time when I saw the video of the shop owners behaving all the female mannequins, it just took me to my country, Iran. So we, people of Afghanistan and Iran, share the pain we experienced this. And I have to say that, yes. You see that the mannequins are being beheaded but in reality, in Afghanistan and Iran, under Sharia law, if you say that you don't want to be a Muslim anymore, if you criticize the prophets, if you criticize this Islamic laws, and for the crime of apostasy and blasphemy, you will be beheaded in reality as well.\par \par \uc2 So that actually breaks my heart that people, even don't realize that this is happening in 21st century. Right now, all those women in Afghanistan who had the chance to have part in please government, parliament, media, now they are all being pushed back behind the curtain again. But, of course, women in Afghanistan are brave enough and taking to the street, protesting and saying that no one can keep silent, but they are calling the rest of the world to hear their voices as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you have dedicated your life to making people aware of the oppression that happens in Iran, and also pointing out what's happening in Afghanistan under the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  . John Ondrasik, who is a musician with Five For Fighting, posted a video on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  against the US withdrawal from Afghanistan and the atrocities that have been left behind. It got pulled down from \uc2 YouTube\uc2  . They've now reinstated it. He did an interview with Dana Perino earlier today, and here's what he said, Masih.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ONDRASIK, FIVE FOR FIGHTING FRONTMAN: Is abandoning our allies, our citizens a political message? No. It's a moral message. What About the greatest women's rights decimation in our history that we are complicit in. Lesbian and gays Afghans are being hunted and their partners beheaded in front of them. Children are being sold for food. So where are all these people who like to stand on their soapbox and preach to us about their moral compassion?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, where are they in this country, where all the people who fight for freedoms for some of the groups that were just mentioned in this country, and why aren't they more outraged? Why don't we hear this every day from those same individuals about what's happening in your country, and Afghanistan and other places?\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: To be honest, it is very heartbreaking. And I always call it like, this is a betrayal. Not only from Biden administration, it is a betrayer for all of those politicians around the world who are witnessing that how people are being beheaded in Afghanistan, how people are getting shot in Iran, how people are suffering from not, you know, having basic freedom. But at the same time, like the whole world, abandoning them.\par \par \uc2 Martha, I have -- I'm in touch with a lot of women in Afghanistan with a lot of people in Afghanistan. They really angry with American government and they cannot believe that overnight, all the rights are being taken away from them. And now no one is talking about all those women or those young girls being banned from going to school. Can you believe that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, no.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: In the 21st century, and people only, like, you know, just asking for the rights, and they're being abandoned by the whole world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Where is the movement? Where's the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , I remember, bring back our girls when the Nigerian school girls were abducted. You know, it was just this national kind of outcry, right? So where is that for? What's going on?\par \par \uc2 I think some people don't want to look back at the situation Afghanistan because it's not too comfortable in terms of the way that we abandon them. Masih, I got to leave it there. Thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: Sure, Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll talk more on it on the podcast together and everybody can look for an extended version of this interview there. Thank you, Masih.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: Thank you so much for having me. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. Actor Alec Baldwin's new rant after he reports -- after there were reports that he was refusing to cooperate in the investigation into Halyna Hutchins' death. We'll show you what he said next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEC BALDWIN, ACTOR: They can't just go through your phone and take, you know, your photos or your love letters to your wife or what have you, I really don't know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alec Baldwin responding after investigators said that they still haven't had a look at his cell phone, which they put a warrant out for last month as part of the investigation into the shooting death of cinematographer Halyna Hutchins on the set of Baldwin's movie, which he was acting in and producing, the movie "Rust," in October. The actor insists that he is cooperating.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Any suggestion that I am not complying with requests or orders, or demands, or search warrants about my phone, that's a lie. We are 1,000% going to comply with all of that. We're perfectly fine with that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's bring in attorney Brian Claypool. So, Brian, how do you interpret what Alec Baldwin says there?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CLAYPOOL, ATTORNEY: Hey, Martha, good to see you again. Hey, look out Ringling brothers, there's a new circus in town. The circuses this Alec Baldwin investigation is the Alec Baldwin search warrant. He is not 1,000% cooperating. He's resisting a court order.\par \par \uc2 A search warrant is a court order issued carefully by a judge in New Mexico, Martha. They send it over to New York. Technically speaking, he does have a basis to fight it initially. But most people who aren't celebrities wouldn't have fought it. They'd say, OK, will comply with this. Instead he's forcing them to issue a new search warrant.\par \par \uc2 But, Martha, there's something deeper here too. Why is there no internet no internal investigation of the Suffolk County prosecution? Why haven't they gotten a new search warrant issued from New York to defeat this jurisdictional issue? Why is it three and a half weeks out? That needs to be looked at.\par \par \uc2 Last point I want to make is, he said on that \uc2 Instagram\uc2  video, it looked more like a commercial for Splenda. But he said on that video, oh, I want the truth, all the truth. If you really want the truth, dude, then give up your phone, like any other person in the United States would have to do so that we can get to the truth.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, here. Here's another part of that, that's in that \uc2 Instagram\uc2  video. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: I've spoken to the sheriff's department multiple times. I don't have anything to hide, you know what I mean. The facts as I see them are what I've stated on the record.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Obviously, that's on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  . That was from the interview he did with George Stephanopoulos. But, you know, he's been very forward in speaking out the whole time, right. But he's clearly trying to control the narrative because he still hasn't turned over that phone.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: That's right, Martha. And the optics for Alec Baldwin are awful because you're right. He's been trying to create a script from day one, right? He gives the interviewer initially where he said he did pull the trigger. Then it gives another interview with "Good Morning, America" saying no, I didn't really pull the trigger. And now the police want the third interview. The third interview meaning what's on that phone. He arguably, Martha, that phone is the most critical piece of evidence other than the gun. He needs to give it up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I can't understand what's taking so long. And as you point out, we should look into the Suffolk County AG Office and see what's going on there and why they haven't issued another warrant that New Mexico says they need. Brian, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: That's right. You bet. Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You too. Take a look at this picture, everybody. What a horrific fire this was over the weekend. This firefighter rescuing a baby from that fire, the deadliest in 30 years in New York, 17 people were killed including eight little children. The numbers changed after this New York Post went to press. And he wasn't only hero firefighters, some of them ran out of oxygen and they kept fighting through the inferno to save as many people as they could.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say it was a malfunctioning space heater that started that fire, and we pray for all those families involved. Terrible tragedy here in New York.\par \par \uc2 That is THE STORY on this Monday. I will see you back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220111ei1a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Justices Debate Enforcement Of Biden Vaccine Mandates; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Remarks On December Jobs Report; Jobs Report: 199,000 Added In December, 400,000 Were Expected; Lawmakers Ponder Fix To 1887 Law Electoral Count Act; Judge Sentences Men Convicted In Ahmaud Arbery Murder; Chopper And Canines Track Camouflaged Migrants; Chicago Parents Push To Get Kids Back In School; Health Expert Weighs In On Vaccination Boosters For Those 12 To 15 Years Old; Democratic and Republican Electoral Strategies for 2022 Midterms Examined; U.S. Economy Adds Fewer Jobs than Expected by Economic in December of 2021; U.S. Unemployment Rate Falls to 3.9 Percent; Supreme Court Hears Arguments Concerning Biden Administration COVID-19 Vaccine Mandates.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Shannon Bream, Jacqui Heinrich, Chad Pergram, Steve  Harrigan, Bill Melugin, Garrett Tenney, Marc Siegel, Morgan Ortagus, Harold  Ford Jr., Mark Meredith \par \uc2 7,562 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 January 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I highly suggest that you listen to it, download it at FoxNewsPodcast.com.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, Geraldo, if you can crack open the frame for the play girl. Greg says he wants to see the centerfold.\par \par \uc2 All right, just crack that center open.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Doesn't even fold out.\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE: It's beefier than the cover.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a Supreme Court showdown, the fate of President Biden's vaccine testing and mask mandates are now in the hands of the nine justices of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  after hearing arguments today.\par \par \uc2 Today's three-hour 40-minute debate focused on two issues, if the government can force private companies with more than 100 employees to mandate the vaccine or masking and weekly testing in the alternative and whether healthcare workers at facilities that receive federal funding must be vaccinated. A decision with the potential to impact nearly 100 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent, Anchor of "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" Shannon Bream starts us off tonight, kind of reading through the tea leaves of the oral arguments and the questions from the justices. Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 These fast-tracked cases at their core are really this, a test of just how far the executive branch's power can or cannot reach into Americans lives.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Seven of the eight justices in the courtroom were masked for today's arguments. Justice Gorsuch, the lone holdout, as Justice Sotomayor elected to dial in from her office.\par \par \uc2 First up, on the docket today, a request to stay the Labor Department's emergency regulation requiring employers with 100 workers or more to mandate vaccines or require unvaccinated workers to wear masks and test at least once a week.\par \par \uc2 Justices Breyer, Sotomayor and Kagan made clear they support the mandate. But some of what Justice Sotomayor had to say in defense of the measure, raised eyebrows.\par \par \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: Those numbers show that Omicron is as deadly and causes as much serious disease in the unvaccinated as Delta did.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARTY MAKARY\uc2 , PROFESSOR, JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY: That's not true. Omicron is more mild. We have laboratory evidence of that. We have epidemiological evidence of that.\par \par \uc2 SOTOMAYOR: We have over 100,000 children, which we've never had before in serious condition and many on ventilators.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: That's not true. We have not had 100,000 children in serious condition in U.S. hospitals according to the CDC.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: At the center of today's arguments was the issue of whether the federal government has the power to do via regulation. What numerous members of the Biden administration said it would not do, including White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , who has said both that the federal government would not enact and mandate, and that it was not the role of the federal government to do so.\par \par \uc2 Justice Gorsuch making this observation.\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: It appears that the federal government is going agency-by-agency as a workaround to its inability to get Congress to act.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The second case weighed whether the administration can mandate vaccines for workers that healthcare facilities that get federal dollars via Medicare or Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kagan who sided vaccination is the best way to stop the spread of COVID 19 added this:\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: You have to get vaccinated so that you are not transmitting the disease that can kill elderly Medicare patients, that can kill sick Medicaid patients.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that seems like a pretty basic infection prevention measure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): With both mandates, the opponents cited studies showing one to three percent of the workforce will quit if forced to choose between vaccination and their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Employers who joined the case argue that while they're already struggling with labor shortages, a walkout on that scale would create a nationwide economic problem.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, by the way, I expect a decision within days, I'd say a couple of weeks at the very most.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, it's always dangerous Shannon to read through the tea leaves and the questions because justices can be posing one way and really think some way else. But when you look at how that matches up, I mean, does it tilt one way right now?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You know, I think the court could split on these two cases. I think there's more of a flavor for striking down the OSHA mandate, the one for the employers with a hundred or more.\par \par \uc2 I think there is going to be reticence and more hesitation among the justices to strike down the mandate that deals with health care workers but I don't think we're going to have to wait long to find out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Shannon, as always, thanks. We'll see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 President Biden appears to be changing his tune tonight, telling reporters we can contain COVID. This of course after months of pledging to defeat the virus as a candidate and then president-elect and president.\par \par \uc2 It comes as today's jobs report showed unemployment falling to 3.9 percent. But the U.S. economy only adding 199,000 jobs, far short of the expected 400,000.\par \par \uc2 Here's White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Biden bucking calls from former members of his own health advisory team to embrace a new normal living with COVID.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're going to be able to control this, the new normal is not going to be what it is now, it's going to be better.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But amending his pledge to shut down the virus with a new caveat.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Having COVID in the environment here and in the world is probably here to stay but COVID as we're dealing with it now is not here to stay.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The comments come as coronavirus muddles yet another jobs report, December is making for the weakest of the entire year. 199,000 jobs added falling far short of the 400,000 forecasters expected. Worse yet, the data was collected ahead of the Omicron wave spooking economists.\par \par \uc2 The president glossed over the bad turning instead to the good, unemployment falling to 3.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The sharpest one year drop in unemployment in United States history. We have added 6.4 million new jobs since January of last year, in one year. That's one of the most -- that's the most jobs in any calendar year by any president in history.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Also working on her messaging, CDC director \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , reportedly seeking media training since the fall after a series of missteps, prompting criticism that continued this week over confusion on testing guidance, and on what basis it was formed.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CDC: This is hard, and I am committed to continue to improve as we learn more about the science and to communicate that with all of you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House appears somewhat closer to making good on the 500 million free tests it promised the first of many contracts signed and a deal in the works to ship them via the Postal Service.\par \par \uc2 But the administration wants to avoid getting caught flat footed again, officials at Health and Human Services and the Office of Management and Budget reportedly calculating how much future funding might be needed for therapeutics and vaccine distribution. Although the White House says it's not seeking any more money from Congress right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): We've also learned the State of the Union is scheduled for March 1st. Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  inviting the president for that joint session of Congress and the president accepting that invitation and it will also serve to measure the state of the pandemic because questions remain about whether lawmakers and guests will be invited, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The CDC director, by the way, will be my exclusive guest on "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" this weekend, one of three (PH).\par \par \uc2 Stocks were off again today, the Dow lost five, the S&P 500 fell 19, the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  dropped 145.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow sank three-tenths of a percent. The S&P 500 was down 1-9/10, the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  4-1/2.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some analysis on the U.S. economy from Charles Payne of Fox Business. Charles, good evening.\par \par \uc2 You heard the President there. What would you make of the jobs report and its impact on where we stand?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, \uc2 FOX BUSINESS NETWORK\uc2  HOST: Listen, Bret, I always say I understand the president is always selling it and trying to point to the most important things that can sell the public. But we do have to start with the top line number. And that's the estimate.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  yesterday up their number, they thought we were going to see 500,000. That one -- I want to say that because you're, you know, considered the smartest firm on the street, and we got 199,000.\par \par \uc2 In fact, over the last six months, we have missed the consensus estimate by 300,000 jobs. That's the average miss, where are those people? What's going on?\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate is fickle. Because when people leave the labor force in that count, and I'll give you an example, we saw Hispanic unemployment dropped last month to under five percent. Unfortunately, 96,000 Hispanic workers left the workforce.\par \par \uc2 In all honestly, I would rather see people coming into the workforce and getting jobs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. So, you're mentioning the miss and obviously, you got 199,000 to 400,000. But you had these folks looking into this, pointing out that the unemployment rate is lower today than it was during every month in the 1970s, 80s and 90s. That is significant in and of itself, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, again, though, Bret, you know what? If we had 10 people looking for jobs and nine quit, they would say that was good news. But those are still nine people not working.\par \par \uc2 So, using the unemployment rate, the so-called U-3 unemployment rate is the worst thing. I don't know how we ever started doing this. I think it's an atrocity. I think the media has to stop using it, certainly as a key point.\par \par \uc2 Because when 96,000 people leave the labor force, and your unemployment rate goes down, that is not good news.\par \par \uc2 Now, here's the good news. Wages didn't go up. Unfortunately, they're not keeping up with inflation, blue collar wages up 5.7 percent, that is a big number. That's a really hefty number.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, in November, the last week we got an inflation, it was up 6.8 percent. And I think this is why you have this really unfavorable job numbers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, quickly, just for people sitting at home, the Fed, the spigot is going to be turned off now pretty soon and whatever this looks like, whether it's a quarter point raise, what does that mean? And is the market already kind of baking all of this in?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The market is baking it in. They might be over baking it in. They're doing more than turning off the spigot, they're going to try to reverse the spigot.\par \par \uc2 They added trillions of dollars into this economy, trillions. And they're going to try to take some of it back. It's a delicate game. They tried it before and they failed, because, you know, we get used to all of that money, free money sloshing around.\par \par \uc2 So, that's why the market was really off this week. I'm not sure what the Fed's next move is going to be and afraid they're going to botch this thing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles Payne, as always, thanks for coming on. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks. You too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're receiving New details tonight about a potential bipartisan agreement on reforming one piece of the presidential election law. But the resolution may not ever get off the ground. Democrats want the broader voting rights legislation passed.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram shows us tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Bipartisan lawmakers are exploring whether fixing problems with a 19th century presidential election law could avert mayhem when Congress tabulates the Electoral College on January 6, 2025.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): The electoral count fix would be a good thing for one day every four years.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The Electoral Count Act of 1887 dictates that Congress settle Electoral College disputes, but it's packed with loopholes\par \par \uc2 SEN. SUSAN COLLINS (R-ME): Is vague and ambiguous and -- contributed to some of the confusion on January 6?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Those loyal to former President Trump train their attention on then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . C