m Chicago.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Many Chicago parents are growing frustrated their kids aren't in school for a third straight day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY BLUMA, CHICAGO PARENT: I'm frankly quite livid that we're back in the situation in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE PRESTON, CHICAGO PARENT: This is just extremely difficult for our children. I think this is just simply the \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  throwing their weight around so that they can make sure that they achieved what they ultimately want to achieve, and that's to won city government outright.\par \par \uc2 TENNY: Nationwide, more than 5,200 schools which received billions of dollars in federal funding to reopen have canceled in-person classes this week due the surge in number of coronavirus cases, according to data tracking service, Burbio.\par \par \uc2 But a new report from the prominent \uc2 Children's Hospital of Philadelphia\uc2  is pushing back on some of those decisions, and says based on evidence that infections and most children are mild, "we support in-person education, even in times of significant community transmission."\par \par \uc2 Despite those recommendations, which are echoed by the CDC and the White House, teachers in several cities are now following Chicago's lead.\par \par \uc2 Today in Oakland, more than 500 teachers called in sick to protest in- person learning. Forcing around a dozen schools to cancel classes. And teachers in San Francisco held their own sick-out yesterday to demand more testing and better mask.\par \par \uc2 Students at one high school said their building was almost empty after nearly 900 teachers called in sick city-wide.\par \par \uc2 JAZMINE KEEL, HIGH SCHOOL STUDENT, CHICAGO: All of the classroom doors were closed. There was barely even any -- enough subs to take attendance.\par \par \uc2 KATRINA CABILLO SACAYROS, TEACHER: To show the district that there are certain things that we need in order to keep our schools safe and open.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): Here in Chicago, negotiations continue between the teachers' union and the city. The two sides do appear to be making some progress, and today Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  said she hopes to have a deal in the next day or two to have kids back in the classroom next week. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Garrett Tenney live in Chicago. Garrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in FOX News contributor Dr. Marc Siegel. Dr. Siegel, how important is it for kids around the country to be in school?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's hugely important, Bret. Good evening. There has been multiple reports last year of harm that was done to children because of school closures, and even the surgeon general report reflected that, in terms of mental health, in terms of physical health, in terms of depression, suicidal thinking, socialization issues, screenings, immunizations of other kinds besides COVID. As you know, kids get all kinds of vaccines. Those were put in jeopardy. Nutrition, even eye care, all of this in jeopardy because of school closures.\par \par \uc2 It's essentially that schools remain open for the health of the children. And even their ability to play, a big report came out of "Lancet," Bret, that showed that worldwide when kids were out of school, the decreased mobility where they weren't even able to move around or play caused tremendous damage to their mental health.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: CHOP, \uc2 Children's Hospital of Philadelphia\uc2 , put out those guidelines. You are a proponent also of boosters for the teens 12 to 15.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Yes, Bret, I'm certainly a proponent of boosters for anyone in higher risk. But the troubling is we have an obesity epidemic in the United States, so over one half of kids are overweight or obese. So there's a lot of preexisting conditions.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is, a study just came out yesterday that shows clearly that the booster works against Omicron. So I want to decrease spread in schools. You talked and Garrett talked in his report about how everybody is trying to close. We don't need to give them the excuse to close. If we have the boosters, and they are being used, along with other mitigating strategies, it's going to be less outbreaks and then the schools can remain open. And I definitely want all teachers to be vaccinated and boosted as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you are also right about this controversy about Moderna and developed by \uc2 NIH\uc2  that won't share its technology with the world. What about that?\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Bret, as you know, I have been critical of Dr. Fauci, and I have praised him at times. I don't understand why the \uc2 NIH\uc2  is not involved in this. There is a trips advisory globally, the United States said that they were going to back giving the technology away to other countries. But meanwhile, Moderna, which was developed by the \uc2 NIH\uc2 , is not exporting either its technology, its infrastructure, or its vaccines. Millions of vaccines are being thrown away here.\par \par \uc2 And if people out there wonder what we are doing with these variants, well, Omicron came from Africa. And Delta came from India. If we could vaccinate the world, and not necessarily export all the vaccines, although, again, we are wasting a lot, we could definitely decrease the risk of further variants emerging. It is so important. We are part of the global community. I want \uc2 NIH\uc2  to be at the front of this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. Marc Siegel, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, new details on Democrats and Republicans messaging strategies heading into this year's midterms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN WALLIN, POLLSTER: We have prices increasing by five, 10, 20, 30 percent on basic American staples, household items. Those are the things that win elections.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden is in Colorado meeting with families who are impacted by the Marshall fire there. Lots of homes destroyed completely. Colorado Congressman \uc2 Joe Neguse\uc2  and Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  joining the president in touring the destruction there. The fire burned more than 6,000 acres in less than 24 hours. State officials labeled it Colorado's most devastating wildfire. Nearly 1,000 structures were destroyed in the blazes and two people remain unaccounted for. The cause of that fire is still under investigation.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's SPECIAL REPORT focus on the midterms, we are getting a clearer picture of just how Democrats and Republicans plan to portray themselves and their rivals as we near this year's midterm elections. Correspondent Mark Meredith is in Washington looking at both parties' political playbooks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Voters can expect to hear a lot more about January 6th between now and November. The Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee says Democrats are going to remind voters just how House Republicans have spent the last year trying to gaslight the American public and just how dangerous they would be if they were in charge of Washington.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Last time was just a test run.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The progressive activist group Priorities USA is launching two ads in battleground states reminding voters about the attack. But Republicans say labeling them as extremists will backfire by outraging about half the country.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS\uc2 , (R) FLORIDA: This is their Christmas January 6th, OK? They are going to take this and milk this for anything they could to try to be able to smear anyone, whoever supported \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: An Associated Press 