ope they will.\par \par \uc2 And also, we must be honest, people who do want to work, it is a shrinking number, but some do and there is very good news for them. Wages are up dramatically, particularly at the bottom end. Some fast food workers are making 18 bucks an hour plus signing bonuses, and that seems like something to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 But of course, no one in Washington is celebrating. This is a victory for Americans who don't have college degrees, and that means a defeat for them.\par \par \uc2 So in Washington, they are rethinking of ways to use mass immigration to shaft unskilled workers out of their recent gains, quote, "Immigrants could fix U.S. labor shortage," read a recent headline in VOX. Of course, it did.\par \par \uc2 The head of Domino's Pizza agrees he'd like to pay his employees less, and he wants to use newly imported foreign workers to do it. How does the White House feel about this? Of course, Biden is on board with it. He wants more Democratic voters from foreign countries.\par \par \uc2 So what you're looking at is the perfect alignment of malignant motives. So, expect the borders to stay open.\par \par \uc2 But what no one in Washington is thinking about and what we should all be thinking about is, why is this happening in the first place? Why are fewer people choosing to work, and have been for a long time, by the way? This is not a new trend.\par \par \uc2 Our labor markets have been getting sicker for generations. Look at this chart. This is one measure of it.\par \par \uc2 This shows the labor force participation rate of American men, it goes back to 1950, seventy one years ago. What do you notice? The line goes straight down.\par \par \uc2 Now, here is another chart, similar but different. This is the labor force participation rate of women in America and that line moves in the opposite direction. So as you can tell, for 70 years, we have been losing men in the workforce.\par \par \uc2 Now, many call this progress on the assumption that anything that is bad and degrading for men must be good for America, but it is not progress. It's a problem.\par \par \uc2 The average men defines himself by his job. Yes, men and women are very different, extremely different, whole societies are built their differences, every society is, and the thing about men is they kind of need to work. They tend to fall apart when they're unemployed. Not all of them do, but enough of them do that we should be worried when we see trends like this.\par \par \uc2 But our leaders aren't worried. Heedless, as always, the consequences of what they do, they have used COVID to accelerate that trend. Here is one example.\par \par \uc2 Who was hurt most by vaccine mandates? Well, the group least likely to get vaccines that, of course, is working class men.\par \par \uc2 What percentage of "New York Times" reporters have obeyed Joe Biden and had been injected with the vaccine? Well, probably around a hundred percent. Among men who work in oil fields? That number is closer to 10 percent. Massive difference. Massive, disparate effect, like a lot in American life.\par \par \uc2 There is actually a class war in progress here, just beneath the surface. When you fire people for not taking an injection, that by the way, doesn't work, you are not hurting the private equity people, you're hurting blue collar people, and maybe that's why they're doing it.\par \par \uc2 But either way, here is the core point. The incentives that we have constructed in this country over a long period of time to be fair, but accelerating recently have a very specific effect. Those policies reward people who don't want a job, and they punish people who do want a job.\par \par \uc2 What they do is they degrade work. They strip it of its inherent meaning and that is a problem in a country that is running out of things that have inherent meaning. They're telling you that your religion means nothing, your patriotism means nothing, and your family means nothing