ious that something else was happening in the country. There was some other alternative narrative going on regarding opportunity.\par \par \uc2 The point is, that was 13 years ago, and there were 2.3 open positions -- 2.3 million open positions. Today, it is 11 million -- 11 million -- 12 million people quit their jobs in the fall. So you're right to wonder, what is going on? And is there an explanation that goes beyond money?\par \par \uc2 And the answer is, yes. It's our whole relationship with work.\par \par \uc2 So look, it feels very political because everything today is very political.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: The skills gap shouldn't be though. Look, one quick point. All my buddies on the left tell me that the reason for all this opportunity is because employers are fundamentally rapacious and greedy. And all my buddies on the right tell me all of this opportunity as a function of workers, being universally lazy and shiftless. They're both painting with too broad a brush. The answer is, as you said, are a bit more complicated.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I think that's exactly right, but at its heart is the question of national character. Is this a country that respects labor? And I don't mean organized labor, but maybe that, too, but the actual labor, the act of creating something that didn't exist before, the act of improving something that was broken. Like, do we respect that?\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Well, I think part of the fault in our stars is that we come to resent the very things we rely upon. So, you know, when the power goes out for three or four days up here where I live, people shake their fist at the linemen because they're frustrated, but the linemen are trying to get the power back on. So no, we don't really respect it.\par \par \uc2 In fact, skilled labor all too often reminds the rest of us of what we are fundamentally lacking. The thing that scares me more than anything. And the reason I started the foundation, when I did was 13 years ago, for every five tradesmen that retired, two replaced them. This is what Abraham Lincoln called a terrible arithmetic.\par \par \uc2 And if that was the only thing going on today, we'd have cause to be concerned. But to your earlier points, it's not just the boomers retiring earlier, it is a real fear of COVID that's been infecting us. It doesn't matter whether the fear is justified or not. It's there. It's real.\par \par \uc2 The class thing that you mentioned, whether it's true or not, we've been told that the reason we're unhappy is that our bosses make too much and so forth and so on. It's a big complicated hairy mess.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It is, but we should care. We should care and solve it. And I know that you do. Mike Rowe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Anytime.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We should tell you, Mike Rowe, who was of all things an accomplished opera singer before he became what he is today. He just recorded a new song with the country music star, John Rich. It's called "Santa has got a Dirty Job," which seems a little spicy. It has reached number one for multiple days in iTunes. You can download your copy today. Proceeds benefit Mike Rowe Works and Folds of Honor.\par \par \uc2 Well, Kyle Rittenhouse went to Kenosha last August because rioters were burning the city into the ground .He worked there. Why were re they burning it? Because the people in charge of our country did nothing to stop the violence. In fact, they encouraged it.\par \par \uc2 We thought the story was interesting enough to make a documentary about it. We have. It is called "The Trial of Kyle." It premiere today on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 We took a very close look at who exactly was responsible for the deaths in Kenosha that day. Here's a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: The chaos in Kenosha was not accidental.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a movement, I'm telling you. They're not going