pose of all of this as Kyle Rittenhouse later realized is demoralization and pacification.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "The Trial of Kyle" is out now. You can watch the first four minutes before even committing to a membership. Just go to tuckercarlson.com, and if you like it, you can get a FOX Nation subscription for free at the website and watch the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 Again, it is tuckercarlson.com.\par \par \uc2 So the State of Florida where everyone has died of COVID, CNN has told us in fact has one of the lowest COVID infection rates in the nation. How did they manage to do that without forcing everyone to get a shot they don't want?\par \par \uc2 Well, the Surgeon General of Florida will be here to explain that to us, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We all seem to assume that getting hysterical about COVID reduces COVID. But is there any evidence for that? Joe Biden is a lot more hysterical about COVID than Donald Trump was and yet many more people have died of COVID under Biden than under Trump.\par \par \uc2 Florida has rejected vaccine mandates, masking. We're told everyone in Florida was going to die because of that. But the state now has one of the lowest rates of COVID in the country. Meanwhile, COVID cases are spiking in New York, which has vaccine passports and has for months. Everybody is vaxxed in New York, triple vaxxed, and they have all got the 'rona. What is that?\par \par \uc2 Dr. Joseph Ladapo is the Surgeon General in the State of Florida. He joins us to assess. Doctor, thanks so much for coming on. So is there like -- as a physician, as someone who tracks public health for a living, is there a connection to how hard you hyperventilate about a disease and the incidence of that disease?\par \par \uc2 DR. JOSEPH LADAPO, FLORIDA SURGEON GENERAL: You know, I would say that in this country, it appears there is a relationship between those two. And the hysteria does seem to induce additional cases, along with additional measures that didn't work the first time, but you know, we're going to do them again anyway.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So as an epidemiological matter, if something doesn't work, and you continue to do it, is that proof of insanity?\par \par \uc2 LADAPO: You know, I might leave that question to my psychiatrist colleagues. But my opinion would be that, you know, it's not healthy.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, it's not. It's definitely not healthy and it degrades reason, which is scary. So what is the state of play with COVID in the State of Florida?\par \par \uc2 LADAPO: Well, you know, Tucker. We have first of all -- I want to acknowledge Governor DeSantis's leadership. So here you have a guy who never spent a day in Medical School, but he managed to basically implement a more effective public health program for the coronavirus than any other state in the country.\par \par \uc2 And you know, what does that mean? Well, we've made vaccines widely available. We prioritize seniors. We've stepped away from mandates, they only have brought division and just really increased politicization of the pandemic. And he's made monoclonal antibodies available.\par \par \uc2 So early treatment, the idea that you know, this message that we heard nationally, that you should just go home and wait, if you're high risk and contract coronavirus. It's terrible medical practice and it's terrible advice. And it resulted in a lot of people dying who didn't need to die.\par \par \uc2 So here, Governor DeSantis led the state in helping create monoclonal antibody sites and encouraging clinicians and people who live in Florida to seek therapy.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: If you get monoclonal antibodies within the first four or five days, I mean, your chances of dying from corona are much reduced. Are they not?\par \par \uc2 LADAPO: You know, Tucker, what you're saying is, it's so simple, that what is -- it is hard to recommend something that that is so simple and is true