my tongue, but he is on this story -- Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi Tucker, a New York Metro Transportation Authority Manager was caught on camera pulling into his Brooklyn office building with an inflatable blow up doll in his passenger seat, "The New York Post" reports.\par \par \uc2 Giulio Di Virgilio claims the doll was for the company and not in attempt to ride the HOV lane with what appears to be a passenger. Di Virgilio is a General Superintendent with the MTA Buses Department, paid $122,000.00 per year. He is a former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  cop who has been with the MTA for over a decade, a source told "The New York Post."\par \par \uc2 When "The Post" confronted Di Virgilio about the doll, he said it's for the company asking, "Have I ever lied to you?"\par \par \uc2 Inflatable dolls and dummies are a common scam to skirt HOV rules. A doll called Carpool Kenny is even available online for about $15.00. One of Di Virgilio's colleagues told "The Post" it was weird when they saw the doll and his car and asked why anyone would have it except to cheat the rules.\par \par \uc2 A union official said it's unbecoming of a manager begging the question what else he might be doing in secret? The MTA says they are investigating. We'll keep you updated -- Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Man, there's just an epidemic of creepiness in this country, I have to say. I'm not going to ask you to comment on that, Matt Finn. Your job is just to report it, but thank you for doing it.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the National Archives just released more than a thousand pages of newly declassified documents relating to the Kennedy assassination. Gerald Posner is investigative journalist who's looked through those documents. He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Gerald, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 GERALD POSNER, INVESTIGATIVE JOURNALIST: Thanks, Tucker. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So first, let me just ask you, there are still documents that haven't been released. Am I right in saying that?\par \par \uc2 POSNER: Yes, I mean, it is astonishing. You have 15,000 documents approximately haven't been released. I mean, these documents were sealed before you and most people who now cover the case were born. So we have decades worth, we're waiting for them.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So what -- now, you wrote a very famous book called "Case Closed," which is a great book, arguing that, you know, the Warren Commission was essentially right in its assessment. But what could possibly -- there has got to be some reason to keep these things classified.\par \par \uc2 POSNER: Yes, look. Part of it is the bureaucracy of the Intelligence Services. So, they won't give up on documents, if they think there's something that they want to keep secret, even if it happens to be the name of a former agent or a way that they spied on embassies from Cuba and the Soviets years ago, so they lock those up.\par \par \uc2 They keep them confidential, they fight against the release. And I always thought that it would take a President like Trump, who was President in 2017, when this issue came up to say to them, no, I'm not buying the old excuses for confidentiality, release them all.\par \par \uc2 They convinced him to hold on to them. And then they came up, you know, in October of this year with Biden, who is sort of, you know, been there long enough in Washington that he is comfortable with those Intel Services. So again, most of them stay sealed.\par \par \uc2 What are they going to tell us in the end? It's not a smoking gun of a conspiracy. But I do think, Tucker that it could be hiding the evidence that the C.I.A. in particular had information that Oswald was potentially dangerous, might act out and they didn't anything a month before the assassination when he had a job on the route that the President's motorcade was going past.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, Pearl Harbor. Can I ask you? So you just went through the documents that have been released, really quick, what jumped out at you? Anything?\par \par \uc2 POSNER: What jumped out at me is this is not the way to do a document release because they're releasing documents without telling you what had been secret before. So, you get a new document in some places like "The New York Post" and "The Daily Mail" ran headlines, it said, by the way, Oswald was in touch with a KGB agent a month before the assassination. True. That's what one of the documents said. But that's old news. It was actually released in '98.\par \par \uc2 What was new in that document was one sentence about the C.I.A. spying on foreign embassies. So unless you go back to the old documents and compare them, you don't know what is new. The archives can do a much better chance of making this simpler for transparency's sake for historians.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And our journalists could do a better a better job. I mean, on many levels, but yes.\par \par \uc2 POSNER: I think that's true, but as you know we were talking about before, most journalists weren't even born when Kennedy was killed. So, they aren't the first, they aren't in the weeds like I am on the case. So it's hard for them to know what's new versus what's old news.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, that's totally right. We're glad you are. Gerald Posner, great to see you tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 POSNER: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, really an amazing story that's gotten virtually no attention. A \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  veteran has served in Iraq and Afghanistan rotting in jail tonight for the crime of defending himself and his family. It is very straightforward case of self-defense, but he's still in jail. Why is that? We will speak to his lawyer, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: One of the saddest things with the Kyle Rittenhouse trial is that it became political immediately and people broke into their predictable sides and that