ond.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They never can get -- just like Hillary. Hillary can't get past the fact that pretty much no one wanted her to be president, and these people can't get past the fact that some of their better dramatic days are perhaps in the past.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's big stars on a nostalgia trip. But the material, this is not exactly Klugman and Tony Randall relaunching "The Odd Couple" on stage, OK, which I saw in London and New York. That was different. This is weak material, and A list don't make it better. The commercials were the best part, Laura. The best thing I can say about it is there were no politics from producers Jimmy Kimmel and Norman Lear, and that itself might be an accomplishment.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That is. And it's also all too rare, Raymond, that -- there's this disturbing poll, by the way, from "Axios" showing that young Democrats despise -- they don't disagree with Republicans, they despise the other party. Not a quarter Democrats polled would not befriend someone who voted for the opposing presidential candidate, 71 percent wouldn't date someone who voted differently. Well, I kind of agree with that. What gives?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, let's not tell our friends Mary Matalin and James Carville about this poll.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's one couple. You always go back to that. One couple.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, because I think people have to overcome the ideology. Ideology and humanity are two different things. And it's important for people on different sides of the aisle to speak to each other and engage each other so they can find common cause, which is something you've advocated for years. So I am disturbed by this.\par \par \uc2 But this is why this Jussie Smollett story is so important. He created a false hoax to divide people racially and politically, and the young are watching and listening. He should go to jail for many years for that. It is obnoxious what that man tried to pull off.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Raymond, we know you and Rebecca are on opposite sides of the political spectrum in your house.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You mix it up really politically. Come on. All right, Raymond, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And Elon Musk is not just one of those most successful businessmen and the world, he has become something of an active and often astute political observer, well, on most things. In moments, we're going to play some of the recent comments he made that are getting some attention. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: One of the chief reasons billionaire mogul Elon Musk has gathered somewhat of a following among conservatives has been its ability to challenge the liberal orthodox that dominates his field. Now it's this refusal to shy away from controversy that was on full display yesterday at a "Wall Street Journal" summit. First, he took on Biden's Build Back Better fiasco.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, TELSA CEO: I would say, honestly, I would just can this whole vote. Don't pass it. That's my recommendation. The government is a corporation in the limit. It's also a monopoly. Why would you want to give a corporation with no competition that can't even really go bankrupt more money?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And then he correctly diagnosed a current societal able.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, TELSA CEO: One of the biggest risks to civilization is the low birthrate and the rapidly declining birthrate. And yet so many people, including smart people, think that there are too many people and think that the population is growing out of control. If people don't have more children, civilization is going to crumble. Mark my words.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You don't have to be a math genius like he is to know that, but he's right. And while many conservatives no doubt love to hear Musk tweak the elites and the media sycophants, it's important to remember where the Tesla CEO falls short. For instance, before he was against government largess, he took billions from taxpayers to prop up his business empire. And perhaps most troubling in the long term, Musk has shown an alarming deference to the CCP.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, TELSA CEO: My frank observation is that China spends a lot of resources and efforts applying the latest digital technologies in different industries, including the automobile industry, making China a global leader in digitalization. Tesla will continue to expand investments and R and D efforts in China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It looked like a hostage tape to me, didn't it? It didn't look like he believed what he was saying. It was reading.\par \par \uc2 I get it, though. Musk wants the Chinese to buy a lot of Teslas and whatever else he invents, but given what they are doing on a daily basis, human rights atrocities, the threat to America's future and independence, liberty, maybe it's time he embraces the person we heard in those first two soundbites. It's all about freedom and independence. Success can grow from consistency and principles as well.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is someone with a keen sense of who Elon Musk is. Curt Mills is a contributor at the "American Conservative" and "UnHerd." Curt, it's fair to say that Musk is really a fascinating person. That sounds trite to say it. He's obviously brilliant. What is your sense on his -- this dichotomy about American freedom and independence, free market, and then kind of being soft on China?\par \par \uc2 CURT MILLS, CONTRIBUTOR, "AMERICAN CONSERVATIVE": Look, I think he is just merely the tip of iceberg. If you are really analyzing this as a sickness that's throughout the U.S. business community, whether it's Apple, \uc2 Google\uc2 , Sequoia Capital if you go through November of last year, or of this year, was exposed as investing heavily in the Chinese semiconductor industry. Where to begin. I think if Musk wants to show himself as a real patriot, he would potentially consider drawing back his investments in China. But I hardly view him as the worst actor, although it is disappointing to hear.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, he just has so much, obviously, going for him in so many ways that he doesn't need them now. Right? So when you don't need them, that is the ultimate power. So why not now make a real difference?\par \par \uc2 He also went after economic central planning. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, TELSA CEO: It does not make sense to take the job of capital allocation away from people who have demonstrated great skill in capital allocation and give it to an entity that has demonstrated very poor skill in capital allocation, which is the government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He was referencing the Build Back Better thing there, but I guess he could be referencing in a way China and state run capitalism. But again, he has benefited, as many corporate CEOs have, on government deals and largess. But I guess he is attacking socialist tendencies as well.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: From the babe's mouth. What I will say is where Musk does compliment on China, I think it's always worth caveating that of course China has done things that have been very impressive over the last 40 years. They've really raised the standard of living for their own people. But they've often done it on the backs of, frankly, the U.S. industrial base. And I think it would be very helpful for leaders like Musk and other people in business who are far, far more in bed with these guys to caveat that and to start reinvesting at home.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, 5 million manufacturing jobs lost since the beginning of China's opening up. And so that is insane as well.\par \par \uc2 When we think about this, again, we think about him and the role in American society, where does this leave the Democrats and Musk? Really quickly.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Just to be clear, a lot of people look to Elon Musk, and I think especially with a Democratic White House and, frankly, with Trump off \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , this is sort of like, you can look at Musk as a young Trump. People are really looking up to him, and what he says matters. And I think on something like the China challenge, which is in my view unprecedented, he can really show leadership.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, I think you just said it perfectly. Curt, great to see you tonight. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 And up next, Biden by the numbers. Oh, can't wait. Up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What am I doing here. I'm going to lose track here.\par \par \uc2 Today, the average price you are playing here in Kansas City is below $2 a gallon, $3 a gallon, it's down to $2.90 a gallon, 20 percent down, cents from a month ago. We're going to keep at it to ensure the American people are paying their fair share for gas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Gutfeld.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020211209ehc800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Suspect's Parents Face Involuntary Manslaughter Charges In Michigan School Shooting; Growing Concern Over Russia Military Buildup Near Ukraine; U.S. Warns Russia As Tensions With Ukraine Escalates; Rep. Michael Waltz (R-FL), Is Being Interviewed About The Threat Of China To The United States; White House Blames COVID For Disappointing Jobs Report; Americans See China As Security And Technological Threat; U.S. Businesses Prioritize Access To Chinese Consumers; CDC Director Recommends American Get Vaccinations And Boosters For COVID-19 To Combat Spread Of Delta And Omicron Variants; U.S. Economy Added 210,000 Jobs In November, 2021; November Jobs Reports Lower Than Expected; Some Americans Continue To Resist Getting COVID-19 Vaccine\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jennifer Griffin, Chris Wallace, Steve Harrigan,  Benjamin Hall, Peter Doocy, Kelly O'Grady, Marc Siegel, Mike Waltz, Mollie  Hemingway, Harold Ford Jr., Kimberley Strassel \par \uc2 7,856 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 December 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: The best part, no one was injured at all. I have driven a lot around Chicago. I cannot imagine.\par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Listening to the Nugent (PH) too loud. You don't know a car is stuck at your truck.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's what it is, the music is too loud, exactly what it is.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's what happened to me.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The music was too loud. I wound up right under a truck.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's it for us, see you back here on Monday, everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We're coming to you tonight from the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library in Simi Valley, California, site of this weekend's National Defense Forum, a conference of defense leaders taking place against the backdrop of an increasingly belligerent and active China, a demanding Iran unwilling to bend for a new nuclear deal, and an aggressive Russia massing troops along its border with Ukraine. We'll have much more about activities here throughout the program.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, a story that's getting a lot of attention in this country at this hour, the parents of the teenager accused of killing four students and wounding several other people at a Michigan high school this week are now facing serious charges, criminal charges themselves.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement has been unable to locate that couple. Authorities launched a manhunt for them this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 But late this afternoon, their attorney said they are returning to the area for arraignment.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan has details tonight from Oxford, Michigan. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Those parents are facing charges of involuntary manslaughter. And there's some real uncertainty about what exactly they're up to.\par \par \uc2 According to local law enforcement, they have fled the scene and they are being pursued by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2 . Their attorney, however says, they simply feared for their own safety. They left but the attorney said they will be coming back.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff blamed the failure to keep tabs on the parents on poor communication with the prosecutor's office.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BOUCHARD, SHERIFF, OAKLAND COUNTY MICHIGAN: We didn't learn of the charges until we were called by the news media this morning. So, we would have liked to have maybe done some other things last night.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Some disturbing details learned about the 15-year- old suspected shooter, the first alarm came one day before the shooting. That's when a teacher noticed him searching for ammunition online during class.\par \par \uc2 The second alarm, much bigger, that's the day of the shooting. That's when a teacher found a drawing the 15-year-old had made. A drawing that showed someone being shot with a semi-automatic handgun and the troubling caption read: the thoughts won't stop, help me.\par \par \uc2 After that, a meeting was called with the parents, the suspected shooter and school administration officials. The decision was made at that meeting to allow the sophomore to return to class.\par \par \uc2 Two hours later, he came out of the bathroom without his backpack but with a handgun and began methodically firing. Shooting 11 people at point blank range, killing four of them.\par \par \uc2 During that meeting, no one asked did he have a weapon and his parents did not volunteer anything about the gun they had just bought him for Christmas.\par \par \uc2 The mother, when the news of a school shooting became public, texted her son, Ethan, don't do it.\par \par \uc2 At several points, the prosecutor expressed frustration that this was a tragedy that could have been prevented.\par \par \uc2 KAREN MCDONALD, PROSECUTOR, OAKLAND COUNTY MICHIGAN: But the facts of this case are so egregious, reading this document, looking at it, reading the words: help me, with a gun, blood everywhere, this doesn't just have impact me as a prosecutor and a lawyer, it impacts me as a mother.\par \par \uc2 The notion that a parent could read those words and also know their son had access to a deadly weapon that they gave him, is unconscionable and I think it's criminal. It is criminal.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The prosecutors --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The prosecutors said the investigation will continue and she hasn't ruled out charging school officials with criminal negligence, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan live in Michigan. Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, President Biden says he is putting together a set of comprehensive initiatives to make it difficult for Russia to move against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The top U.S. military officer meantime says the country has a lot of concern over Russian troop activity near the Ukrainian border. Are those two countries one step closer to all-out war? And what would the U.S. do about it if so?\par \par \uc2 Here's State Department Correspondent Benjamin Hall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Russian troops is still gathering at the Ukrainian border with new estimates putting the number at 115,000. And the U.S. is warning Europe to brace for an invasion.\par \par \uc2 General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  saying today, there's enough out there now to cause a lot of concern.\par \par \uc2 On the diplomatic front, yesterday's meeting between Secretary Blinken and Russian Foreign Minister Lavrov led to no concrete paths forward.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We had I think a very direct, very candid non-political exchange of views. It was serious. It was -- it was sober.\par \par \uc2 HALL: President Biden however insists he's putting in place everything he can to discourage the Russians.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- the most comprehensive and meaningful set of initiative to make it very, very difficult for Mr. Putin.\par \par \uc2 HALL: If there is one thing he hasn't done, reimpose sanctions on the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline. Sanctions that President Trump put in place.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): President Biden waive those sanctions in the national interest. I don't know how that's in the national security interest of the United States to allow Putin to complete his pipeline into Russia making Europe dependent on Russian, you know, oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 HALL: The pipeline would strangle Ukraine, cutting off billions of dollars in revenue. And the Senate is now pushing for a vote to reintroduce sanctions before they will pass the National Defense Authorization Act.\par \par \uc2 It has also emerged that at least 10 senior Democrats, including Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and Connecticut's Richard Blumenthal have accepted the maximum campaign contributions from a \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  lobbyist who has himself been paid $8.5 million to lobby for the pipeline.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALL (on camera): And meanwhile, the Ukrainians are also preparing for war. Although, their Defense Minister said today he thought the most likely time for Russian invasion would be the end of January.\par \par \uc2 There are many other people here in the U.S., though, who fear it could come a lot sooner than that, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Benjamin Hall at the State Department. Benjamin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: The United States and our allies and partners are deeply concerned by evidence that Russia has made plans for significant aggressive moves against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Despite uncertainty about intentions and timing, we must and we will prepare for all contingencies while working to see to it that Russia reverses course.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Secretary of State there about the Russia massing of troops along Ukraine's border. Let's talk about this weekend's National Defense Forum here.\par \par \uc2 Joining me, Fox News Sunday anchor Chris Wallace and National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer, always this forum seems to happen with some big events that are happening around the world. This one really urgent it seems talking to military leaders about what Russia is doing.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Well, the senior defense officials that I've spoken to do point to what's happening on the border with Ukraine and say there is a qualitative difference in the type of troops and the type of weaponry they're seeing from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and the Russian troops there -- that are there.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this is the second time he's done this. Last spring, he had about 100,000 troops on the border, he backed off. And those who I speak to say that it's not clear that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has taken a decision yet about whether to actually cross the border into Ukraine. And that's why you're seeing this flurry of activity. And it happens with this backdrop.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  talked about peace through strength. Right now, the U.S. military, the White House is trying to get some degree of deterrence to deter \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The question is, what leverage do they have at this point?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned Reagan, and we often in these times, in this place with this Air Force One behind us, Chris, reference \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's comments. This is from March of 1983, where he's talking about the Soviet Union, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In your discussions of the nuclear freeze proposals, I urge you to be weary of the temptation of pride, the temptation of blindly declaring yourselves above it all, and label both sides equally at fault.\par \par \uc2 To ignore the facts of history and the aggressive impulses of an evil empire, to simply call the arms race a giant misunderstanding, and thereby remove yourself from the struggle between right and wrong, and good and evil.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talking about the evil empire there and, you know, you look at what's said today, it's much more nuanced about dealing with Russia, dealing with China.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh, absolutely. I was there that day in Orlando, Florida as he was speaking to the evangelicals. He said that Russia was the focus of evil in the modern world and the evil empire. Talk about drawing bright red lines between the U.S. and Russia.\par \par \uc2 And I was also thinking of exactly that line, peace through strength, because what made \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  standing up to Russia credible was the thought he would do whatever it took to propel Russia.\par \par \uc2 As I was listening to \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  there, he spoke with all the passion of somebody reading a telephone book.\par \par \uc2 Look, we're not going to go to war with Russia even if they invade Ukraine. They're not a member of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , Article Five doesn't, apply an attack against one as an attack against all.\par \par \uc2 But you at least have to put that in the back of Putin's mind, maybe it would happen. And instead, the E.U. and the U.S. are talking about economic sanctions, political sanctions.\par \par \uc2 Putin has made it clear back from 2014 when he invaded Crimea, that's not going to stop him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you add to that, Jen, the unification, the efforts between China and Russia together almost standing up to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, imagine, Bret that if you had a deal between President Xi and President Putin that each would move Xi on Taiwan and Putin on Ukraine and simultaneously, what would the U.S. military do?\par \par \uc2 But to follow up on what Chris said, I think what the U.S. is trying to do, and you heard General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  today, they're trying to use General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  as the one to say, don't underestimate us, just because we pulled out of Afghanistan, don't think that we won't use -- all options are on the table.\par \par \uc2 However, what they haven't done and what they need to do is they need to move into what's known as the nuclear option in terms of sanctions. Take them out of the SWIFT banking system, which is what the U.S. did with Iran. Cut them off from financing in the secondary debt markets. Sanction the oligarchs, Putin's cronies.\par \par \uc2 The question is, was that message delivered by \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned Iran briefly. It seems Chris, like those talks are falling apart. A lot of people said it wasn't going to happen to begin with. But it seemed like the administration desperately wanted these talks again, but Iran is not bending.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Well, you know, part of the problem is the situation has changed so dramatically since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pulled out of the Iran agreement.\par \par \uc2 Iran is up to 60 percent enrichment of its uranium, which is pretty close to the 90 percent weapons grade. They've got a whole phalanx of advanced centrifuges.\par \par \uc2 So, to put that genie back in the bottle and say, well, we can go back, they really can't go back to 2015. They'd have to negotiate a whole new agreement. And that's new hardline president of Iran is saying, if you want to do that, you're going to have to lift all the sanctions first, which is a nonstarter for the U.S. It doesn't look like it's going to go well there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You'll be hosting "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" from here. You're hosting a Q&A, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A panel on weather sitcom (PH) in the Middle East. Everyone wants to talk about Asia and China, but the Middle East is still there as Chris just outlined.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And I'll be talking to the Defense Secretary tomorrow, live on air. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, President Biden is under the weather, so is the U.S. jobs picture, we'll bring you that this month.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 11 in Los Angeles where 14 people are arrested for a series of smashing grab robberies.\par \par \uc2 Police say there were 11 such robberies reported throughout the city from November 18th to November 28th. All 14 suspects have since been released and are back out on the streets due to California's zero bail policy.\par \par \uc2 Fox 9 in Minneapolis where a 12-person jury is seated in the trial of a white former police officer charged with fatally shooting a Black man in April.\par \par \uc2 Kim Potter claims she mistakenly fired her gun instead of her taser resulting in the death of 20-year-old Daunte Wright.\par \par \uc2 She's charged with first degree and second-degree manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look from Dallas, our affiliate Fox 4 there. One of the big stories there tonight, some of America's greatest heroes head to Hawaii to mark 80 years since the attack on Pearl Harbor.\par \par \uc2 About 60 World War II veterans, some of whom survived the actual attack were given a water cannon salute before it takeoff. For many, it's their first time back to Pearl Harbor since that tragic but historic day.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , 40TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Nations do not just trust each other because they are armed. They are armed because they distrust each other. If this globe is to live in peace and prosper, if it is to embrace all the possibilities of the technological revolution, then nations must renounce once and for all, the right to an expansionist foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  speaking about tensions between the U.S. and the Soviet Union during a Moscow University speech in 1988.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Biden deals with a disappointing November jobs' report, amid growing concern over the newest coronavirus variant. The economy added only 210,000 jobs last month, less than half of what economists had expected. However, the unemployment rate dropped to 4.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes amid worry, the president's pandemic policies could stifle an economy already struggling with skyrocketing inflation and a supply chain crisis. And after his presentation today, there was also concern about the president's health.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy has details tonight, live from the North Lawn.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Officials around here are blaming previous COVID strains for the slow economic recovery as they brace for a new COVID strain that they know almost nothing about.\par \par \uc2 And in this piece, you're going to hear \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as you have never heard him before.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: America is back to work, and our jobs' recovery is going very strong.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But short with just 210,000 jobs created last month, less than half what was projected.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): They were supposed to be 550,000 jobs, especially during a seasonal hiring period, much less than that.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Unemployment fell to 4.2 percent. Although some experts see certain pandemic era policies causing major problems.\par \par \uc2 ED RENSI\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT, MCDONALD'S USA: And look at the restaurants I worked with, and I see how very, very difficult it is to get people to come to work. It's just too easy to stay home, and it says so much about Biden's leadership.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Biden's Cabinet isn't sweating it.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2 , UNITED STATES LABOR SECRETARY: I mean, it doesn't actually disappoint me because I'm looking at the picture -- the whole picture here.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And they think the whole picture is --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: All told in the first 10 full months of my administration, the economy is created 6 million jobs, a record for a new president.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president didn't sound like himself today, speaking with a raspy voice and persistent cough.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Your voice sounds a little different. Are you OK?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm OK.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): And in a rare public statement, the president's doctor insists, he doesn't have COVID, instead, "As is readily apparent, President Biden is experiencing some increased nasal congestion this week. This can be heard in his voice and he is feeling the colloquially well-known, frog in one's throat."\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have this as a 1-1/2-year-old grandson who had a cold, who likes to kiss his pop.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president may not have COVID. But officials caution Omicron cases will soon multiply.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There are half a dozen we're aware of here in the United States. That will grow and increase.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And that comes with a subtle but important change in the president's pandemic promise.\par \par \uc2 It seems like the administration is starting to soften some of the language. There's this new op-ed where you talk about COVID, and we are going to beat it back. Are you no longer going to shut it down?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, we got to beat it back before we shut it down. Look, it's going to take time worldwide. In order to beat COVID, we have to shut it down worldwide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): So, the White House physician insists Biden's new baritone has nothing to do with the new COVID variant. And for now, he is not making any changes to his schedule. In about two hours, he will fly for the weekend to Camp David then he's going to come back on Sunday for an event at the Kennedy Center, celebrating Lorne Michaels and Bette Midler among others. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were down today. The Dow lost 60. The S&P 500 was off 39. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  plunged w96 today. For the week, the Dow fell almost one percentage point. The S&P 500 lost 1-1/4. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  was down 2-2/3.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what the next cold war might look like.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. Pope Francis tells migrants he has a responsibility to tell the hard truth about the suffering of refugees. He says many are held in conditions he compares to those in Nazi and Soviet camps.\par \par \uc2 The Pope met with migrants today in Cyprus, some of them will be among 50 to be relocated to Italy.\par \par \uc2 France says it will sell fighter planes and combat helicopters to the United Arab Emirates, the UAE. It's part of a multibillion-dollar effort to boost military cooperation.\par \par \uc2 The fighter planes contract is the largest ever for French export. It follows the collapse of the multibillion-dollar submarine contract with Australia that ultimately went to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And the International Space Station is forced to change course in order to dodge a piece of space debris. Its orbit dropped more than 300 yards to avoid a close encounter with a fragment from a U.S. launch vehicle that was sent into space in 1994.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REAGAN: We in America have learned bitter lessons from two world wars. It is better to be here ready to protect the peace than to take blind shelter across the sea rushing to respond only after freedom is lost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Present \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  speaking at Normandy. On the 40th anniversary of the D-Day invasion.\par \par \uc2 You know several of us from Fox News are participating in this weekend's National Defense forum right here at the Reagan Presidential Library. It's awesome. Awesome to see Air Force One as the backdrop here.\par \par \uc2 One of the important topics obviously is China's emergence as a high tech innovator and an economic force, and also a threat.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady is here tonight to tell us what a Cold War with Beijing might actually involve.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Well, the next Cold War is going to look a little bit different. OK? So, the adversary is China and the playing field is tech. And with President Biden declining to push China on a myriad of issues from COVID to they're mounting aggression towards Taiwan, Americans are getting concerned.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (voice-over): So 52 percent of Americans see China as the biggest national security threat. That's down from just under 40 percent In February of this year, so it's a big jump.\par \par \uc2 73 percent of respondents said that they fear that the U.S. is going to fall behind China technologically. And unlike the U.S., China controls its tech leaders. That allows them to easily leverage innovations across military and civilian sectors to bolster its defense arsenal, and it's investing in advanced surveillance tech, machine learning, decryption capabilities, and has even tested a hypersonic weapon, which will help them use space to evade American missile defenses.\par \par \uc2 Now, Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin voiced concern over China's growing military power while in South Korea this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN (RET), UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: We have concerns about the military capabilities that the PRC continues to pursue. And the pursuit of those capabilities increases tensions in the region.\par \par \uc2 And I would just point out that the hypersonic capability is a capability, but certainly not the only capability that the PRC has a capability to develop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Now, on top of that, China's growing economic might means that U.S. businesses prioritize their access to Chinese consumers. Dozens of firms and public figures overlook atrocities or have been forced to publicly apologize when they do something that Chinese government doesn't like.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY (on camera): So yes, big focus on tech, and China this weekend. And with China's emergence as a high-tech innovator, that poses threats, both economically and security-wise to us, so maybe a key issue for Americans come this next midterm election. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in former Green Beret, Pentagon advisor, and current Florida congressman, Mike Waltz.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Hey, Good to be with you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's great backdrop, right?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Yes, it's pretty incredible.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's talk first about China, coming off that piece from Kelly.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the threat. You know, we heard -- we saw this meeting between President Biden virtual meeting and President Xi. And there was a lot of talk about straightforward competition and not conflict.\par \par \uc2 But there are other people characterize it differently that China has big broader goals.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, it's not just other people that characterize it differently. It's the Chinese Communist Party themselves. Listen to President Xi's speeches. He's telling his country to prepare for war. He's talking about replacing the United States as a global leader.\par \par \uc2 And you know, the path that they're on right now, their Navy is larger than the \uc2 United States Navy\uc2  now. They're launching more into space than the rest of the world combined.\par \par \uc2 They're making huge investments in technology that the military can use. But I think the bigger piece, Bret, and this is the real tragedy of it all is they are doing it with our money.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at the investments that company, you know, hedge funds like \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . And frankly, every American out there that when they see made in China, that dollar is going right into their military-industrial complex that our soldiers, sailors, and marines may one day have to fight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Whenever we're talking about China, Taiwan and what China does with Taiwan comes out. And it seems like they're getting more and more aggressive. Here is the security of state talking about support for Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We are resolutely committed to Taiwan, to making sure it has the means to defend itself. I hope that China's leaders think very carefully about this and about not precipitating a crisis that would have, I think, terrible consequence for lots of people, and one that's in no one's interest, starting with China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you have a sense, or do you know what the Biden administration would do if China invaded Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: We've gotten mixed messages, from Biden himself and from his White House. But I think what we need is some real clarity. It if the Chinese take Taiwan, they will essentially control the economies of Japan, South Korea, partially Australia. They will control about 50 percent of the world's GDP and 40 percent of the world's trade. And that is something that the United States cannot live with.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're in these briefs. Do you think it's getting closer?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: It absolutely is getting closer. The Chinese are developing the capability to take Taiwan militarily. I think they would prefer not to have to do that, where Taiwan will just capitulate. But Xi has been clear, one way or another, they are going to, what they call, reunify, which is forcibly taking Taiwan. What is our response going to be? What is the Japanese response going to be? We need the same clarity that we heard from Reagan, that the Chinese Communist Party is an evil entity that's oppressing its own people and seeks to export that oppression around the world, and that America will make a stand.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But as far as the timeline, does Capitol Hill --\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, look, they have the Olympics coming out, what I have dubbed the "genocide Olympics" and have called for a full boycott of the Olympics. That will be a massive propaganda platform for them. We need to remind folks that Putin invaded the Ukraine just two months after the Sochi Olympics in 2014. So that will be a big piece for the Chinese. Xi will get reelected in their Chinese Communist Party this next summer, and I think between summer and when they know Biden may be out of office will be a very dangerous window that they may make a big move.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned Putin, and the current situation with Russia along the border with Ukraine, it seems like it's getting more aggressive by the day.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: It is. It's incredibly alarming, Bret. They have over 100,000 soldiers massed on Ukraine's border. Bret, I think at this point it's not a matter of if, but just when, and how far does Putin go? And it is no coincidence that he's doing it after \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  comes online, because he knows when he controls Europe's gas, he can fracture Europe and sideline them in terms of sanctions or any type of military response.\par \par \uc2 And again, we need that clarity that the United States will support the Ukrainians, could send stingers an air defense which the Ukrainians have been asking for, and other type of lethal assets. But instead we have seen some diplo-speak, and as Chris Wallace said, Blinken looks like he's reading from a teleprompter, not standing up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, Ukraine technically is not a part of \uc2 NATO\uc2  so it doesn't fall under Article Five.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And there's a lot of concern about the U.S. getting in another war, possibly being a proxy war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Sure, absolutely. But we have to understand, too, that if Ukraine goes, next will be the Baltic, which our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies. Next could be -- it's a slippery slope. Next could the Balkans. Again, it's no coincidence that Putin went after the Ukraine and Crimea under Obama. The same team is now around Biden. \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  is in place where they can fracture \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And they can also starve the Ukraine of gas.\par \par \uc2 So this all is happening in the context after Afghanistan, and we know the Russians watched that closely, and the Chinese are watching now what we do in response to the Ukraine. So these dominoes can begin falling in a way that is detrimental to freedom around the world and certainly to U.S. interests.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This would be a big topic, all these things here at the National Defense Forum this weekend. Congressman, thanks.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, Dr. Marc Siegel talks with the director of the CDC about the Omicron variant. And we go to break, President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  at Normandy in 1984 addressing the bravery of American troops on the 40th anniversary of D-Day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: You were young the day you took these cliffs. Some of you were hardly more than boys but the deepest joys of life before you. Yet you risked everything here. Why? Why did you do it? The men of Normandy had faith that what they were doing was right, faith that they fought for all humanity, faith that a just God would grant them mercy on the beachhead, or on the next.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's Air Force One here at the library.\par \par \uc2 A vigil is being held tonight for a \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  graduate student killed last night in a stabbing. Another person was injured. The man accused of the crimes is a suspected gang member with 16 prior arrests out on parole. Authorities said the suspect was arrested in Central Park while menacing another man with a knife.\par \par \uc2 As the U.S. braces for the effects of the Omicron variant, scientists in South Africa say reinfections among people who have already battled COVID- 19 appeared to be more likely with this new strain. Tonight, FOX News contributor Dr. Marc Siegel talks with CDC Director \uc2 Dr. Rochelle Walensky\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: South Africa has been under an Omicron variant spotlight and seen an upsurge in cases, with over 11,000 cases over the past stay alone, most of them mild or moderate so far. But a new study suggests an increase in immune evasion or reinfection with Omicron.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , CDC DIRECTOR: Really important science that is starting to emerge. These early data are suggesting that in fact prior infection may not protect you as well against Omicron as it had for prior variants. Certainly, we have also heard some anecdotal data, as you suggest, that younger people are presenting, and when they do so, with milder symptoms. And that would be true for all the variants of SARS-CoV-2, so that very may be true for Omicron as well.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: In my interview Dr. Walensky, she stressed the importance of boosters for all variants, not just Omicron, but especially Delta, which remains widespread in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 WALENSKY: The thing that we really need to understand right now is that we have 90,000 cases a day here in this country, and over 99.9 percent of those cases right now are still the Delta variant. And we know what we need to do to protect ourselves against the Delta variant. We know our vaccines work. We know that getting boosted will bolster that protection.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Dr. Walensky recognizes the importance of holiday travel and gatherings, and she said that testing remains an important part of their strategy to protect Americans from the disease. She talked about how far we have come and how it can help us all cope.\par \par \uc2 WALENSKY: Really importantly, I want to say we know the tools, we have the tools, we are in a much better place for the holidays of 2021 then we were in the holidays of 2020. And we know how to do this safely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL (on camera): Walensky told me that her jobs is to keep us safe from COVID, and by following CDC advice and regulations, she said Americans can have a safer holiday season. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. Marc Siegel. Doc, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, panel on President Biden's disappointing jobs report and his pandemic response, plus Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 As we go to break, President Reagan's famous demand to Soviet leader from berlin in 1987.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: There is one sign the Soviets can make that would be unmistakable, that would advance dramatically the cause of freedom and peace. Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This year we can reflect on an extraordinary bit of progress. Our economy is markedly stronger than it was a year ago, Americans back to work, and our jobs recovery is going very strong.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's 210,000 on nonfarm payrolls, a huge miss.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There's no doubt that the Biden economic agenda is getting people back to work.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2 , LABOR SECRETARY: We still have a ways to go when we think about getting people back to work, but our recovery is happening, and it's happening in a strong manner. You talk to CEOs, you talk to people around the country, they feel good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A froggy sounding President Biden today talking about the jobs report. His voice was low, so was the jobs are part. The numbers, the estimates falling half -- under half of what was expected, 550,000 expected, 210,000 jobs added.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Mollie Hemingway, senior editor at "The Federalist," Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, CEO of Empowerment and Inclusion Capital, and Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal."\par \par \uc2 Kimberley, what about these numbers, what they tell us about this economy?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": So they tell us that policies matters, and right now the one that are coming out of federal government are having a bad effect on the economy. On the one hand, you've got all these people who are not going back to work because they are still getting transfer payments from the government, whether that is the expanded child tax credit, whether it's rental assistance. On the other hand, you have businesses that for the last 10 months of this administration have been getting hit with a flood of new regulations, which act as taxes, and there is actual taxes proposals that are in the offing that Democrats are offering. So they are pulling back. And we're going to need to see some big changes out of D.C. to really get this economy moving again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: So first of all, thanks for having me on, and happy Friday. Six million new jobs have been created since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been president. If you look at the 210,000 job number, I think Kimberley would agree this is the largest labor participation that we've had in some time, maybe since COVID has affected us. We would love a bigger number, but I think if you look at how the market responded today, the trading market, there is a belief that the Fed will maybe accelerate its tapering efforts -- tapering tendency, which suggests that maybe the economy is stronger than we think and inflation is higher.\par \par \uc2 I think the early part of the show, Bret, and where you are, the fact that we're up competing with China on tech, military, human rights, those are the things that I think that are in the back of the minds of so many Americans.\par \par \uc2 And if I can say one last thing, if we want to do our part as everyday Americans and ensure our economy remains open and schools remain open, those of us are healthy enough to get vaccinated and who have no religious exceptions to the virus, we should get vaccinated, because the best thing for China would be for us to stop working again, for us to close down the economy, for us to close down the schools and impair our ability to compete.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are closing down some jobs, Mollie, because there are some people who do not want to take the vaccine, and they are standing up to the mandates, one way or another. And that's affecting some different areas of the economy. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: And we saw a group of Republicans just tried to shut the government down because they don't think people should have to get vaccinated or get tested. That's bananas and crazy.\par \par \uc2 What we are working to try to do right now is implement these mandates, which are vaccinate or test, either option, very reasonable for businesses around the country.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER MARSHALL, (R-KS) SENATE HEALTH COMMITTEE: This is still America. We still have certain God-given constitutional rights. And despite all of our efforts to educate people, to encourage them to get the vaccines, 20, 30 percent of Americans are just not going to do it, certainly not if they're getting it shoved down their throat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you are seeing this pushback, Mollie.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, SENIOR EDITOR, "THE FEDERALIST": It's worth remembering that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that his case for being elected was that he would shut down the virus, that he had a plan for doing so. We have now experienced more deaths from coronavirus under President Biden up than we did under President Trump, and that's with President Trump having provided a vaccine. So it's really an unbelievable failure on the part of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that he claimed he had some great plan for solving everything and it turned out he didn't really have a plan.\par \par \uc2 And he keeps on just coming up with new measures that don't really work or new ways of forcing things that don't work. And it makes people lose even more trust than they had in these federal policies that were ostensibly supposed to take care of this. The fact of the matter is that this vaccine has been widely available to everyone who wants it for some period of time. There is some resistance to doing it, but that doesn't explain why there are all these other policy failures that the Biden administration is overseeing.\par \par \uc2 They cannot blame the people for their own policy failures or their own inability to take the boot off the neck of the U.S. economy or otherwise keep this economy from coming back. But these job numbers weren't supposed to be that great as they were, half-a-million jobs, and to fail to meet that, which is becoming a bit of a pattern, is very bad signs for Democratic leadership.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you're seeing some Democrats, including Joe Manchin, who we talk about a lot, having a problem with some of these mandates and trying to deal with that legislatively, working with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Winners and Losers, Kimberley, let me start with you, winner and then loser?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: So my winner is the \uc2 Women's Tennis Association\uc2  for its announcement that it is suspending tournaments in China and Hong Kong in response to the censorship of Peng Shuai, who made accusations against a Chinese communist officials. This is a very bold thing to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And your loser?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Oh, and my loser. OK, and my loser is the Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  who use the abortion case oral argument this week to score her Republican colleagues, suggesting they were acting out of political motivations, and really questioning the legitimacy of her own body. Not a good look for a justice. They should stick to the legal points.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Harold, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : My winner is division one college athletics. For the first time in 20 years, I should say, the time in 20 years, the highest number of division one athletes are graduating in four years. Congratulations to them. And my loser are those who coddle repeat offenders. It's time to fully fund the police. It's time to repeal bail reform. And reform it if indeed you want to help nonviolent offenders. But leading violent offenders out repeatedly is not safe for any community in America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Mollie, wrap us up, winner and loser.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: My winner, like Harold, college football fans. There are some great matchups this weekend between Alabama and Georgia for the SEC championship, Michigan and Iowa for Big Ten. So we have seen some great football this year, and that will continue.\par \par \uc2 My loser is the media, who continue to paddle the Russia collusion lies that they participated in by downplaying the role that the dossier played in their own reporting, and also in claiming that there is some semblance to this Russia collusion conspiracy theory that they have foisted on the country for so long.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thanks. Make it a great weekend. SEC football, there's nothing like it. Go Dogs. I'm partial.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's Friday. You know what that means, time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's called the Omni-cron (ph).\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: We're not going to shut the government down.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Please wear your masks when you're indoors and public settings around other people.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president is somebody who follows the recommendations and the advice of the CDC.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: They are really criticizing science, because I represent science.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think the ego is really expanding beyond what I saw.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Who is president? Fauci?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's a point of time when my triceps got pretty jacked. So that was a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The trigger wasn't pulled. I didn't pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It hurts to even say it. It's embarrassing, but I understand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What about all the children who ran screaming, hiding under desks? The charge of terrorism reflects that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You students, this community stands with you. They are here for your families, they are here for you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Families that won't stay home that electric vehicle will never have to worry about gas prices again.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to thank Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , where are you? There you are.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will this institution survive the stench that this creates?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Abortion is a hard issue, and when the Constitution does not take sides, it belongs to the people.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I can't promise that every person will get every gift they want on time. Only Santa Claus can keep that promise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One week.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow and 3:00 p.m. eastern you can watch my interview with Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin from here at the Reagan Library. Don't want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid, a couple seconds over. "FOX NEWS PRIMETIME" hosted by Pete Hegseth starts right now. Hey, Pete.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020211204ehc300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Fourteen Suspects That Were Apprehended By Police In California Are Back On The Streets Again After LAPD Announced That They Are Being Released With No Bail And No Charges At All After Robbing Businesses Across The City; The Democrats Are Getting Slammed For Trying To Pat President Biden On The Back Over A Two Cents Decline On Gas Prices; Alec Baldwin Told ABC's George Ste