s. They are incentivizing crime by with no bail and all of these measures. That's why you have the criminals.\par \par \uc2 We are allowing the media, the White House to redefine crime is like a weather pattern. Like, you know, you go out there, be sure to bundle up. Dress like a hobo. Right? Because there's a 70 percent chance of smash and grab. Thirty percent chance of felonious assault. So, it's up to you and it's out of your hands.\par \par \uc2 I am so -- I feel so hopeless about this because it's been going on for quite a while. I am thinking about joining the dark side. Why bother being a law-abiding citizen and pay all these taxes and go shopping when you can just smash and grab? I mean, why am I going to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  online when I live around the corner from the store? I could just totally smash them windows. take some -- just join.\par \par \uc2 If you can't beat them, join them. There should be an app for solitary people like me who can meet up with other solitary looters. Because that's my problem. I don't know, like I don't have a lot of friends. I don't have a mob that can like come with me while I go trash other people's private property.\par \par \uc2 So maybe we could do -- we could do an app for that. The bottom line is we have redefined law and order as systemically racist so anytime you enforce the law, you're going to be called a racist. Which is the most effective method for undermining society. We are watching society implode.\par \par \uc2 I feel absolutely hopeless. That kid. That Columbia kid was murdered, was murdered by a guy that's been in and out of jail. I mean, maybe his parents have some influence. Maybe with the home invasion in Beverly Hills, will that wake-up people? You know, a music legend murdered, right? While her husband was sleeping or her husband was sleeping. She was murdered. Why -- how -- doesn't this wake Pelosi up? Don't some of these people travel in the same circles?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, I mean, it shouldn't have to happen that way. That's the point.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. But you know what, not until hell comes to their house --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: -- will they wake up.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, it's coming pretty close.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Straight ahead, Democrats getting roasted for a ridiculous attempt to brag about the Biden economy.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL (on camera): Democrats getting ridiculed for their pathetic attempt at trying to give President Biden a pat on the back despite soaring gas prices. The Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee proudly tweeting out this cherry-picked chart showing gas prices dropping by $0.02 over two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Even The Washington Post thinks it's lame, saying it might be the worst defense of the Biden administration yet. What jackass thought this was a good idea and should said jackass be fired?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I think I should -- we should refer to Jessica to make the one great point about how this all started. So, I don't take it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: As though as the jackass?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: No. Because I don't want to --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No. No, she already talked about it on America's Newsroom but what is funny about this is it was a joke from (Inaudible). He's a super lefty and has -- I very much enjoy his Substack, I would say. And he did it as a joke. The DCCC took it seriously, it's been retweeted by Ron Klain. It's really bad y- axis.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Like, it's insane.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: More like why not access.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, why not.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So that's a nice little distraction to the whole thing. There were some good things. The 4.2 percent unemployment rate is good. I imagine that the job numbers will be revised like it has been for the past few months because (Inaudible) just doesn't sink up with it. I think we are at an average of 588 a month during the Biden administration which is good. I know we're coming out of a pandemic. But that is the origin of the chart.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The thing is the administration has these allies like the DCCC. That's basically the campaign arm that tries to get Democrats elected to the House of Representatives. And you know, maybe somebody thought it was cute. There's no quality control, whatever.\par \par \uc2 And it seems harmless, right, but it absolutely undermines all the credibility. Everyone from The Washington Post on down basically said this is so embarrassing. You should never be allowed to do this again.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Now almost as embarrassing as the White House talking about the July 4th your barbecue is going to $0.16 cheaper than it was a year ago.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: So, Greg, they don't know what to do.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: See, I actually appreciate this kind of thing because it's a great lesson in data manipulation. We are living in -- we're drowning in data and almost all of it is B.S. Because it's when the data comes out, it ends up being manipulated in a way that it can't be trusted.\par \par \uc2 So, you see what this chart did, is that it zeroed in on the smallest increase and created a line that appears to be dramatic when in fact it's just a tiny little thing. For example, let's say you hear about a study that says if you take the conclusion from the study is if you take this pill, it will cut the rate of illness in half. Right?\par \par \uc2 But then you read up. And it was like, it went from two people in 10,000 to one person in 10,000. So cut in half but it's essentially meaningless. That's how you deal with data. And you -- the best thing you can do when you take statistics courses, learn how to read stuff, take an economics class --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: