ack as your majority leader.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Right. Cocaine Mitch feels like he never went away from me. History is a very good predictor of what's to happen, and that's why it's been so important that Democrats get through these big pieces of legislation. You know, we have Build Back Better, if it gets through the Senate, the American Rescue Plan, and then the Bipartisan Infrastructure Plan which is a win for both sides there.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats are definitely nervous about this. You know, three key categories of focus, independents, if you look at the results out of New Jersey and Virginia, that's a big problem. I'm looking at where the Hispanic vote is going. That's trending much more conservative whereas the Asian vote is actually drifting more Democratic.\par \par \uc2 And then looking at what's going to go on with those moderate Republicans and the white college-educated men specifically who did come over to vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  after Trump and seemed to be shifting back.\par \par \uc2 I am hopeful that people can just message on the policies and also show the empathy and the concern that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won on in 2020 because yes, COVID was obviously a huge part of that, but the attitude was even more important which was I hear you, I see you, I feel what you're feeling.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: OK, Sean, as the former member of Congress here. Here's the thing about the Infrastructure Bills where I push back, that even if these this next one does pass, it's another couple trillion dollars into the economy. You have \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  laughably saying that no economist says that it will add to inflation. Adding these trillions, of course, you know, plenty of economists are saying that. And if you take Econ 101, you know that.\par \par \uc2 But even if we look at the infrastructure bill in terms of roads and bridges and power grids, right, those things will take many, many years to either upgrade or fix or add, right? And once it is upgraded -- let's say - - in other words, no one is ever saying, you know, my power hasn't gone out for a while. The power grid is working pretty well or boy that bridge didn't collapse, I just drove over it, I got to thank \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for that.\par \par \uc2 In other words, I don't think these will even have a positive impact even when they're passed and even by the time they're even built because people expect their government to have these things running well anyway.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Well, the problem is Democrats didn't reform the way that we get approvals for these projects, so they won't happen for 10 years, so people won't see the results of them.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Right.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So, Jessica, I would agree -- I would disagree with you in that it's not the messaging of the policy, it's the policy itself that people don't like. And when you look at things that people care about, they care about the economy, they care -- they care about inflation. When you look at borders, inflation, crime, COVID, energy, withdrawal from Afghanistan, Democrats have nothing to run on.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  focuses on which is the weather, and he's also focusing on race, those don't even hit the list of things that people care -- things that people care about. And so, if you want to get elected, actually listen to people. What do you care about? What do I need to fix? And the policies are going to make inflation much worse which is going to make the election results in a year from now that much harder for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: It is amazing, isn't it, Dagen, that the president within his own party, only 36 percent of Democrats want him to run again in 2024. You never saw that with any previous presidents before, I don't think, at least in my lifetime, anyway. 64 of the country overall doesn't want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to run again. So, my question is, what -- who's the ticket because your plan B is \uc2 Kama