st.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Who's number one?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Number one is the 1619 Project which is the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ' actual book. So, you can help us get to number one.\par \par \uc2 JONES: There we go.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: If you go buy this as a president, if you have someone who loves Fox, that's a good book and you love Christmas.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: That's a good --\par \par \uc2 JONES: That'll be my part --\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: That's a good pitch.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- is to learn my colleagues.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: I have my own pitch. Go to shop.foxnews.com now for our Black Friday sale. Look at all of these fabulous five swag.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: This FIVE socks here. Look at these.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: We have ornaments --\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: This is amazing.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: -- proud Americans scarf. I will even put this on.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I could use these.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: You get 20 percent off sitewide, no code necessary now through November 28. Some great items. Look at this hat, right? I have a huge head. This makes my head look bigger, but Jessica --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't think it's --\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: I got a big noggin.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And there were those --\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Merv Griffin told me I had a big head.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't want to fight with Merv but I don't think you have a big head.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Wait, wait, there's dog leashes here too. You got a dog leash and dog collar collars as well.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes, dog collars as well.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Go ahead, Jess.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: The animals are great.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Animals are great, even without Greg here. So, the Kiss Cam is usually for kissing but it was used for something a little bit different. On November 24, \uc2 Orlando Magic\uc2  fan James Bailey and thousands of others in attendance found out from his wife that he was pregnant -- well, she was pregnant -- that they were pregnant -- I should say that -- on the Kiss Cam. Obviously, overjoyed, brought his parents there. There was a great quote from him. We haven't even eaten our turkey yet and it's already the best Thanksgiving ever. Very sweet moment --\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Excellent.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- in a public place.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Joey?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Listen, an elementary school in Tennessee found out that they had an extra attendee in the class the other morning. This is a buck walked in. Listen, this deer is on a rut right now, which means deer are hot and heavy. They're ready to go find a partner make other little baby deer. So, he was on the warpath looking for doe. And he found his way into a classroom.\par \par \uc2 Oddly enough, this happened in Tennessee. And word is, that deer raised the grading curve up 10 points. So, good job there is a Georgia fan. Listen, go to bootcampaign.org.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: I'm not -- I'm not standing on your side with that one. Joe?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: All right, Mamma Mia. Look at this video. I mean, it's insane. I play Mario Brothers at home with the kids on the Nintendo Wii. This kid literally does like a double jump in the air. He defies logic. That -- we didn't manipulate this in any way shape or form.\par \par \uc2 That's off the coast of Panama City, Florida where I may or may not have spent my first spring break out in a place called Sharky's which was tremendous. But yes, I mean that is a real video and that's a teenager who don't have his name but he just got on national television for doing something probably not sober.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: You don't have any scientific explanation for how that happened? There has to be some scientific --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, the boat went down.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The guy jumped and the boat went don't. I'm not -- I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: No -- so, there must be here a wave and he's popping up at the top the wave.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That makes sense.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And then the wave drops. But look --\par \par \uc2 JONES: I hope you guys get me some socks for Christmas here.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: That does it for us. We will see you on Monday. You have a wonderful weekend. Happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020211127ehbq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 U.S. Considers Sending Military Aid To Ukraine; All Three Defendants Found Guilty Of Arbery Murder; McMichael Guilty On All Nine Charges; Eight- Year-Old Boy Is 6th Victim Killed In Parade Crash; San Francisco Shoplifting Suspects Face Felonies; Roddie Bryan's Attorney To File Motion For New Arbery Trial; All Three Defendants Found Guilty Of Arbery Murder; Biden In Nantucket For Thanksgiving Holiday; White House Defends Biden's Holiday Plans; Biden Stays At Billionaire's Home Amid Economic Crisis; Food, Energy Prices Soar Ahead Of Thanksgiving; U.S. Considers Sending Military Aid To Ukraine; Fear Of Russia-Ukraine Invasion Grows\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, Mercedes Colwin, Jacqui Heinrich, Liz Peek,  Jennifer Griffin, Jillian Mele, Jeff Paul, Andy McCarthy, Jonathan Turley,  Mike Tobin, Jonathan Hunt \par \uc2 7,608 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 November 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: So calling the family you know, this year, finally, we can get together and celebrate and be thankful.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS HOST: Indeed. Sandra you and yours a happy Thanksgiving and thank you for having me. It's been a great week.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Absolutely. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I'm Trace Gallagher. "THE STORY" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS HOST: Thank you, Sandra and Trace. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Gillian Turner in for Martha MacCallum today. Right now, on THE STORY, a jury has just found all three defendants guilty in the murder, murder death of Ahmaud Arbery. Travis McMichael, who fired the shots that killed Arbery found guilty on all nine counts, including malice murder.\par \par \uc2 His father, Gregory McMichael, guilty on eight of the nine counts, and Roddie Bryan who filmed the killing, guilty on six of the nine counts against him. The jury reached their verdict and just 11 hours and 30 minutes. They asked to listen to the 9-1-1 call and rewatch the real time video of Arbery's death before making their decisions.\par \par \uc2 Just a moment ago, President Biden weighed in on the verdict saying, "Nothing can bring Mr. Arbery back to his family and to his community, but the verdict ensures that those who committed this horrible crime will be punished." Let's jump right in here. We're going to bring in our legal panel.\par \par \uc2 We've got Mercedes Colwin, Criminal Defense Attorney and Fox News Legal Analyst, as well as Bob Bianchi, he's a Criminal Defense Attorney and Former Head New Jersey County Prosecutor. Thanks to you both, for being with us on our leading story this afternoon. Bob, I'm going to start with you. What do you think? Lay it out for us.\par \par \uc2 BOB BIANCHI, CRIMINAL DEFENSE ATTORNEY: I tell you what, it was very complex jury charts for them to read. They had to go through a number of counts, and they only deliberated 11 hours, which is a former homicide prosecutor on a case like this with a self-defense claim where you have to deal with terms like reasonableness.\par \par \uc2 I thought it would take them a little bit more time, especially when it came down to the third defendant, Roddie Bryan. There was the one where most of the legal analysts were looking at it and say, did they -- the prosecutors establish enough of a causal connection that Roddie would actually be convicted?\par \par \uc2 And what I found very interesting about this case, Gillian, is that when you -- sometimes when you get a verdict back, the verdict can be inconsistent in what we would use that term, or they do a thing called jury nullification, which would be worrying of the prosecutor where they say, yes, he's guilty of the crime, but we really don't want to convict them under the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, the jury was not persuaded at all by the passionate, defense attorney's arguments that these guys are only protecting the community. And their verdict was very consistent with regard to the level of culpability or responsibility of each defendant. So, Travis got hit with everything. He was the one who was the shooter, his father wasn't the shooter, so they got him with the felony murder, and so on and so forth as we go down.\par \par \uc2 Last point, before we get to my good friend and colleague, Mercedes. Felony murder is the prosecutor's ace card. I always love having a felony murder in my pocket, because I may approve that a person intends to kill a person, but if somebody dies during a felony, then they're going to get the same sentence, a life sentence. So, that was a great tool the prosecutors ever used.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Right, no difference really, in terms of sentencing between felony and malice, murder. And Mercedes, look ahead for us, pull out your crystal ball. The next step here, in terms of waters of this trial, is now the judges got to decide if these three men are going to be eligible for parole, right? That's kind of the big thing we're tracking next.\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES COLWIN, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Well, sure, Gillian, I mean, certainly. Thank God, that jury got it right. We look at the evidence, it was overwhelming. You had the videotape, and frankly, anything that the defense attorneys could have pulled out to try to defeat that videotape fell flat, which is why the jury didn't deliberate very long, but they were very careful in their deliberation.\par \par \uc2 So, what's ahead? Well, in order for these individuals to be even eligible for parole, they have to serve a minimum of 30 years. While you're talking about Greg McMichael, he's 65 years of age, what that may met. And, frankly, that might be the extent of his life. By the time he's eligible for parole. He may have already passed at that point.\par \par \uc2 So, there's 30 years that they need to serve. There will be obviously appeal. I was unsure and I'm sure Bobby feel the same way I do -- I was floored at how the defense tried to dehumanize Mr. Arbery. I was, I was so angry when they focused on his toenails, what are you doing? I mean, if you are that's one way to inflame a jury to dehumanize a murder victim.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Bob, why I love being on panels with you, you're an amazing lawyer. You are the Chief of Homicide Bureau, and you know, as a prosecutor, the fatal flaw for any defense attorney to do in a homicide case is to dehumanize the victim. Can't do that. Have respect for the process. That was really, really problematic and vile.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Do you think -- quick question here for Bob. Do you think that, that actually clinched the deal for the prosecution here? Do you think that kind of misstep by the prosecution? Do you think it clinched the deal for the defense, excuse me, that misstep by the prosecution dehumanizing Arbery in that manner in the middle of this trial, defended his mother, she was sitting right there she audibly gasped probably offended the jurors?\par \par \uc2 BIANCHI: Yes, walked out of the courtroom a couple of times. I think the comment especially about the toenails was extremely gratuitous and dehumanizing and outrageous and ridiculous. And when you're a lawyer in front of a jury, you have to resonate with that jury.\par \par \uc2 It's important that they believe you, believe in you and who you are. The rest of the attacks in Ahmaud Arbery in terms that he was up to no good and the community was nervous and a fear, I think that was all in fair play by the defense. But the answer your question directly?\par \par \uc2 No, I don't think the jury is going to be completely find somebody guilty because the defense attorney made a misstep like that. That's just what the evidence showed in the case. And by the way, may I add shows the difference detail of two different trials between Arbery and Rittenhouse, and the point of that is the person who is being pursued by the mob is what we call -- well, I used to say the person on the ground is the victim and the guy standing up is called the defendant.\par \par \uc2 And in both of these cases, the jurors were saying in, in Rittenhouse all the narratives we received that he brought a weapon overstate lines into Kenosha who where he had no connection, not accurate. All of that he was there provoking the incidents, every one of those is not accurate, that he wasn't trying to retreat, not accurate.\par \par \uc2 And those videos showed in Rittenhouse, and Rittenhouse was trying to get away just like in this trial, it's showing that Ahmaud Arbery for five minutes he's trying to get away and that's the reason why the juries are coming up with these kinds of verdicts.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: A lot less room for misconceptions when we have a half a minute long video. Mercedes, sorry to cut you off there. We've got to leave it here. Thanks to both of you, Bob and Mercedes, really appreciate your time this afternoon. Talk to you soon.\par \par \uc2 BIANCHI: Happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: My pleasure. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I want to bring in Congressman Buddy Carter. He's joining us by phone right now. He's a Georgia Republican who represents the district where this trial was held. Congressman, thanks so much for joining us. What's the mood like in your district?\par \par \uc2 REP. BUDDY CARTER (R-GA): Well, the mood is on. I think that this is over with, you know, we live in a free and fair society. And one of the cornerstones of that is the right to a trial by a jury of your peers, and that happened. That's fundamental to a free and fair society, and today justice was served.\par \par \uc2 And I want to thank the jury, I want to thank you judge all the legal counsel for, for their commitment and for their part in making sure that that this this came to fruition and that they found the truth. You know, a young man died here and let's don't forget that, and it's a very sad and unfortunate situation.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And despite vindication for Arbery's family now, and the relief that they are -- some of them are feeling, it's not going to bring him back. There's a tremendous amount of sadness, the tragedy of his death remains a fact of life. What are your constituents telling you about the trial? How do they feel after you know having this their town be in the spotlight over the last two weeks and near before that?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, I have to tell you, I could not be any prouder of the first district and specifically of Wayne County and Brunswick, the way that they handled this. Yes, they close faster than they should have, but you didn't see destruction, you didn't see rioting.\par \par \uc2 What you saw was a group focused on, on making sure that the fundamental right to a trial by a jury of your peers was followed. And I credit the leaders in Wayne County and in Brunswick, the African American pastors, and all of the elected officials for their role in making sure that this happen.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Congressman, we're going to leave it there. Thanks so much for joining us this afternoon. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We're going to have more on those guilty verdicts with Former Federal Prosecutor Andy McCarthy and Law Professor Jonathan Turley in just a moment. We have just heard reaction to the trial from President Biden, he's in Nantucket this hour, he's spending Thanksgiving at the home of billionaire family friend David Rubenstein.\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk about how that's playing with his critics. Ordinary Americans are still confronting soaring gas and grocery prices this Thanksgiving. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: What message does it sent to the middle-class Americans, President Biden says that he's trying to help who are struggling this week to cover the cost of the most expensive Thanksgiving ever. That the President is going to take a few days off at a billionaire's compound in Nantucket.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, President Biden's approval rating is continuing a downward slide in the latest NPR/Marist poll, 42 percent of Americans approve of the job he's doing now. That's the lowest since he took office, fueling much of the drop his handling of the economy. When asked what concern the most Americans said inflation by a wide margin. Jacqui Heinrich is in Nantucket, Massachusetts where the President's spending Thanksgiving at multimillion dollar compound. Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Gillian. Good to see you. The President's critics say that his stay at a billionaire friend's Nantucket compound with the economy in such a tough place shows that he's out of touch with the problems that are facing working class Americans. The same day that the President tapped the nation's emergency oil stockpile to mitigate soaring prices amid crippling inflation, he headed to Nantucket to this $30 million estate owned by private equity billionaire, David Rubenstein.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the \uc2 American Farm Bureau Federation\uc2  found the average cost for Thanksgiving dinner of 10 people reached an all-time high this year. That's up 14 percent from 2020. Turkey per pound is up nearly nine percent; potatoes are up 20 percent; onions 32 percent; cranberries, a little over two percent, and lettuce is also up nearly 12 percent.\par \par \uc2 And the White House touted separate data from the Department of Agriculture showing a smaller increase, but the prices were still up, five percent. And they deflected questions about what message the president's trip is sending to Americans struggling to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I don't know if you've cooked a turkey before, but a 20 pound Turkey is a pretty big turkey. I think we can all agree. They're about $1 more.\par \par \uc2 So, not to minimize that. Any increase in prices is something the president is concerned about. As is evidenced by his announcement today --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Some, into new talking points on the economy and inflation today, the White House touted improvements in supply chain bottlenecks and availability of goods at two big retailers, and also small businesses ahead of Black Friday.\par \par \uc2 But there was some ominous language around causes of inflation, saying a deeper cure to congestion at ports will take years. Also calling out, "ocean shipping cartels" as a cause for price hikes and inflation.\par \par \uc2 President also touted lower unemployment claims but said there's work left to be done for the economy's back to normal. Once again, he pitched his $1.75 trillion social spending plan as a solution to that problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Now, that bill would be paid for in part by raising taxes on corporations and the very wealthy, just like the billionaire whose home the president is spending Thanksgiving at. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Jacqui Heinrich, joining us from Nantucket. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Kevin Walling, he's the former Biden campaign surrogate and Democratic strategist. Also, Liz Peek, she is a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 I want to get both of you to weigh in on this. Take a listen to what President Biden said just before he took off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's important to maintain perspective about where our economy stands today. The fact is, America has a lot to be proud of. We're experiencing the strongest economic recovery in the world. Even after accounting for inflation, our economy is bigger, and our families have more money in their pockets than they did before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Liz, you can't fault somebody for having wealthy friends. But is it fair to be skeptical of somebody who tells you that everything is wonderful before they jet off to a $30 million mansion to spend the Thanksgiving weekend there when tens of millions of Americans are still struggling to put their lives back together in the wake of the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 LIZ PEEK, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, look, let's first of all agree that any president deserves to be able to take a vacation.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes, very important.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: That said, I just think this is another sort of terrible political blunder on the part of Joe Biden. Not only is his polling crashing, but as you point out, people are really concerned about the cost of everything, and here he is doing something so lavish, so high-end, that it really kind of reminds us that Joe Scranton, as long time ago, his entire sort of presence or pretense about being one of the people.\par \par \uc2 Here is what his polling says about that, which I find really amazing. Only 39 percent of Americans think that Joe Biden, according to a morning console poll think -- feels about them, or cares about them.\par \par \uc2 How does anyone not understand that doing what he is doing, palling around with David Rubenstein, he's great guy, I think very smart, very entertaining. But all of this is just sends wrong message.\par \par \uc2 And I think this White House is so politically tin-eared, and they don't get any better. I mean, this this is just not one more example.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Kevin, a lot of us are really sick and tired of talking about optics, but it is everything in politics. Some may argue that it's even more important than policies themselves.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you say about this? The president, you know, doing sort of staying at this lavish compound over the Thanksgiving weekend. Jen Psaki saying in that clip that Jacqui just played well, turkeys are only $1 more expensive. What's the big deal? What are you -- what's everybody upset about? Is it tone deaf?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING, FORMER CAMPAIGN SURROGATE FOR JOE BIDEN: Yes. Yes. I don't think it is, Gillian. You know, listen, that the right-wing media is going to attack the president if you staying at a best western or a holiday, and they're always just going to attack him no matter what he does.\par \par \uc2 He's staying with friends in Nantucket, something that the Biden family has done since the president lost his first wife, when he went up there with his boys. So, I agree with Liz that he's entitled to some time off.\par \par \uc2 And actually, I actually prefer it this way, where he's not charging the American people millions of dollars to secure him at his property. The Secret Services and paying hundreds of thousands of dollars, like they did with President Trump for just golf carts alone.\par \par \uc2 So, I much prefer the president staying with friends. And you know, damn the optics of this. And let's have the president get some time off and regroup as he tackles this crisis with regards to the high oil costs, as he tackles a crisis with regards to immigration, and as he's doing that actively from the White House.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right now, Liz, to be fair here, the economy -- the picture writ large is not all doom and gloom or we're looking at being on track to grow 5.7 percent this year. Average earnings up 3.5 percent. Unemployment at just 4.6 percent with 6 million jobs between January and October, just some of the numbers there. The sore spot, the rub, and all of this, though, of course, being inflation.\par \par \uc2 Liz, I'll throw that over to you.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Yes, so, I think a couple of things. The economy is not in bad shape at all, in fact. And it shouldn't be. We've had unprecedented monetary stimulus and unbelievable amount of fiscal stimulus, the balance sheet of the Central Banks in Europe and the United States are double what they were a year ago. And all of that has led to inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, if you had hundreds of billions of dollars being injected into the economy and unprecedented monetary stimulus, and we weren't growing, that would say something incredible.\par \par \uc2 But the -- but the really horrific aspect of all of this is Americans think we're in a recession, Americans think -- if they don't think we're in a recession, we're about to be in one. Consumer sentiment is in the basement.\par \par \uc2 So, all of this progress that we've made, the very fast snapback from the recession has -- yes, been very positive.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: That's why the stock market is hitting new highs. It is not helping average Americans as much as it should be because of high prices.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: That's something the president is not dealing with.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We got to leave it there. Kevin, I owe you one. I'll get you back next time. Thanks to you both. Happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Happy Thanksgiving to you.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Happy Thanksgiving, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, a potential armed conflict may be brewing between Russia and Ukraine. This is prompting a brand new warning from the state department about the unusual military activity.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what the White House is saying about criminal justice reform in the wake of the deadly Christmas parade rampage in Wisconsin? That's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN CRENSHAW (R-TX): This is a really sickening tragedy, the person who did it is a really sick individual. And I think why it's infuriates so many people is because it was so preventable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back to THE STORY.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Embassy in Kyiv, today, warning American citizens about unusual Russian military activity near the Ukraine border.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is now considering sending military advisors and extra weaponry to the country. Fears really ramping up that Russia may be planning an incursion into that country.\par \par \uc2 National Security correspondent Jennifer Griffin, joins us from the Pentagon with the latest. Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 Well, as the Pentagon is weighing whether to send more lethal aid and trainers to Ukraine, all eyes here are focused on Vladimir Putin's next move.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice-over): 100,000 Russian troops remain in formation on the border with Ukraine, I'm told. Preparations for a very strong U.S. response should Russian forces cross into Ukraine are underway.\par \par \uc2 General Mark Milley, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs spoke to his Russian counterpart yesterday and to his Ukrainian counterpart twice in the past week.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has shared intelligence and maps with European allies that show a buildup of Russian troops and artillery to prepare for rapid, large-scale push into Ukraine from multiple locations.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State Tony Blinken announced additional sanctions that could target the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: We don't know what President Putin's intentions are. But we do know what's happened in the past. We do know the playbook of trying to cite some illusory provocation from Ukraine or any other country, and then using that as an excuse to do what Russia is planning to do all along.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Putin has leverage and may halt gas supplies to Western Europe to allow him to make his move into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HERBST, FORMER UNITED STATES AMBASSADOR TO UKRAINE: I don't think Moscow believes a construct with no risk. They understand that this is a inherently dangerous proposition.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): U.S. officials have warned the Belarus migrant crisis is an example of how Putin can weaponize migrants trying to enter Europe via Poland and divert attention away from the Russian military buildup on Ukraine's border. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Jennifer Griffin from the Pentagon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's bring in retired colonel, Daniel Davis. He served 21 years in the Army, deploying into combat zones four times during his career. He is now a senior fellow and military expert for Defense Priorities.\par \par \uc2 Colonel, thanks so much for your time today. So, you say --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. DANIEL DAVIS (RET.), DEFENSE PRIORITIES MILITARY EXPERT: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You bet. So, you say that the Biden administration's number one responsibility here is to sort of make sure that they don't cause any harm, that they don't do anything to escalate the tension here. What does that look like? What are their real sort of options on the ground?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes. Right now, the absolute overriding priority needs to be the preservation of U.S. national security and our economic interests throughout Europe. Because what we can't do is to see all these things that Jennifer Griffin just talked about that are indeed happening, and there are 100,000 troops on their border.\par \par \uc2 There, Putin does have a history of limited military incursions into bordering states. So, there's legitimate reason to be concerned. But what we have to avoid is inflaming that further and potentially getting sucked into a war that is not of our making, that we have no security interest in, because that could be catastrophic for our interests.\par \par \uc2 So, we want to limit it, but we don't want to exacerbate it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: What are U.S. interests here? I mean, we've heard some people argue that Ukraine is not officially a member of NATO. So, we are not obligated by treaty to jump in to defend them. What are our specific interests? Why is it in our interest to get involved?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes, and I'll just flat out tell you, it's in our interest not to get involved. Because we don't have any security interests at stake. Meaning, that even if the worst-case scenario happened and Russia took Ukraine, we are still not threatened because we will still be able to deter them from anything else.\par \par \uc2 Because, considering right now, Ukraine has no defense treaty obligations with anyone, so this will remain a bilateral issue between those two.\par \par \uc2 But right on the other side of Ukraine are four NATO countries that are all backed by Article 5 guarantees and the US nuclear umbrella. Russia knows that. They would never try to go in beyond that because they know it would be a suicide for them to do so.\par \par \uc2 So we want to prevent him from going any further. But, look, what we have to recognize is sometimes the Russians will make decisions that we don't think are rational, but are rational to them, and that they may risk potential near term problems with the US and NATO to secure their national interest on their borders. And Putin has been very clear he feels that way.\par \par \uc2 MELE: Take a quick listen to Ned Price, State Department spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: We have underscored our rock solid commitment to Ukraine's sovereignty, to Ukraine security. And we've also made clear, both with our allies and with the Russian Federation that any escalatory or aggressive moves by Russia, would be of great concern to the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELE: So that's the official line, very much in keeping with you what you just laid out. Tell us what's going on behind the scenes. What is Tony Blinken and Defense Secretary General Austin often saying to their counterparts offline?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Well, I certainly -- I don't have any inside information to what they're saying but I sure could tell you what I hope they're saying. I hope that they're saying, hey, we will take lots of non-kinetic action against you that your interest will not be solved by taking any parts of Ukraine. But if you move anything beyond that, that we have treaty obligations. And you can be sure we're going to be meeting those treaty obligations because if they went beyond that, it would be in our national security interest.\par \par \uc2 So they want to make absolutely clear there is no room for misinterpretation for Moscow, but this is a tough situation and we may not be able to keep Putin from moving.\par \par \uc2 MELE: All right. Colonel Davis, thanks so much. Happy Thanksgiving to you.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Thanks and same to you.\par \par \uc2 MELE: You bet. All right. So reaction is pouring in this hour after those three men were convicted and found guilty of murdering Ahmaud Arbery in Georgia. Andy McCarthy and Jonathan Turley are on tap, they'll join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELE: Back to our top story this afternoon. Hundreds of people are gathering outside the courthouse in Brunswick, Georgia. They are hugging, crying, cheering. Just a short while ago, the judge read the guilty verdicts for Travis and Greg McMichael, as well as William Bryan for the murder of 25-year-old Ahmaud Arbery.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jeff Paul is there in Brunswick. He's got the latest on the ground. Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Jillian, a very emotional time here in Brunswick, Georgia on multiple levels. You have some people who are in somewhat of a celebratory mode, saying that the jury got it right in this case, and you have other people who have been very exhausted throughout this whole process. And there's truly a sense of relief because in their minds, when I spoke with them, they said you look back historically there are some trials that they don't believe got it right. But in this case, they do believe that people are being held accountable in the killing of Ahmaud Arbery.\par \par \uc2 Now we know that all three men were found guilty on multiple charges. You look at Travis McMichael, that's the man who physically shot Ahmaud Arbery, found guilty on all nine counts. His father, Greg, found guilty in eight of the nine counts. And then, the man who filmed a portion of the shooting, William "Roddie" Bryan found guilty of six of the nine counts, including multiple counts of felony murder.\par \par \uc2 And as soon as that verdict was announced in full, there was a huge celebration outside the courthouse. Cheers, hugging, and for the family of Ahmaud Arbery, most importantly they say, justice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WANDA COOPER-JONES, AHMAUD ARBERY'S MOTHER: Now, Quez, would you know him as Ahmaud, I know him as Quez. He will now rest in peace.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Amen.\par \par \uc2 COOPER-JONES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Now the judge says sentencing could happen within a few weeks. But looking at the severity of all the counts and charges that the three men were found guilty on, it's likely they will spend the rest of their lives in prison. Jillian.\par \par \uc2 MELE: All right. Jeff Paul in Brunswick, thanks so much. Let's bring in now Andy McCarthy is a former federal prosecutor and a Fox News contributor as well as Jonathan Turley. He's a constitutional Attorney George Washington law professor and Fox News contributor. Good afternoon to you both.\par \par \uc2 Andy, the prosecutor Linda Dunikoski here said in her closing arguments a reiteration of the case she had been building all along. Which was essentially, you can't claim self-defense if you're the one that started it. Take a quick listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 , PROSECUTOR: You can't play self defense if you are the unjustified initial aggressor, meaning if you started it. Who started this? It wasn't Ahmaud Arbery.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELE: So this was the crux of the matter from the prosecution's perspective. The jury obviously agreed. Do you think there was any debate about that?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No, I don't think so. I think that it's interesting that the week after we looked so long and hard at the Rittenhouse case where there was so much talk about provocation, Jillian, and here we get the flip side of it where there really was provocation by the person who turned out to be the assailant, Travis McMichael. I thought Ms. Dunikoski did a great job on that.\par \par \uc2 But I also thought that a very interesting thing. At the end of her summation, her rebuttal summation, the last thing she said to the jury, which is something you really want the jury to carry away. She said, my recommendation to you is to work from the bottom of the indictment. That is to work from the lowest charges up to the top, which is not always the way that we think of how juries work through cases. But by designing the indictment, the verdict sheet that way. But what ended up happening was the felonies that were the predicates for the felony murder counts would have been the first things they turn to.\par \par \uc2 So if they were able to convict on those easier counts, then a momentum kind of builds toward the end. So that by the time they get to the last count, everybody's already been convicted of at least two felony murder counts. So I just thought it was a smart way to go about it with this particular case. And the way the verdict came out, it shows that the jury made a discriminating appraisal between the three different defendants.\par \par \uc2 MELE: So it's really sort of a series of escalating sort of interlocking charges filed against each of the three men here.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, I want to talk to you about the defense here. Some unusual moves, Ahmaud Arbery's mother audibly gasped in the courtroom during closing arguments yesterday when this was said. Take a quick listen to Laura Hogue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURA HOGUE, ATTORNEY FOR GREG MCMICHAEL: Turning Ahmaud Arbery into a victim after the choices that he made does not reflect the reality of what brought Ahmaud Arbery to Satilla Shores, in his khaki shorts with no socks to cover his long, dirty toenails.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELE: Ever heard anything like that?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. No, I haven't. You know, it did rhyme in part about the Rittenhouse trial where the prosecutor actually elevated people like Mr. Rosenbaum to a hero status, and while describing him as engaging in rioting to a Kenosha jury. But this is far more serious because it wasn't just gratuitous. Many people took it as a very racist 