nedy\uc2 , Jr. Why are they trying to censor \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2 , Jr.? He is a kook, he is crazy. No, he's actually one of the smartest and most articulate chroniclers of the erosion of our civil liberties in this country.\par \par \uc2 He has written an amazing new book and we're grateful to sit down and talk with him at length for a brand new episode of "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today." Here's part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT KENNEDY, JR., AMERICAN ENVIRONMENTAL LAWYER: That's why we had the revolution so that we could criticize the government and we put that first and yet, it's gone. Then the next thing they go after, the other half of the First Amendment, freedom of religion.\par \par \uc2 They closed every church in this country for a year and they keep the liquor stores open, by the way without any hearing, without showing any science, without notice and comment, rulemaking, no discussion no debate. They keep the liquor stores open as essential businesses.\par \par \uc2 Now, I have no problem with that with keeping the liquor stores open, but the liquor stores are not in the Constitution. The churches are and you know, and we shouldn't be able to close those lightly without having a debate about it.\par \par \uc2 Then they go after property rights. They close a million businesses without due process or just compensation. That's a violation of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 It was just a doctor who had never treated a COVID patient saying one week, masks don't work and a month later, everybody putting them on, and not citing one study to justify that change. It was government by diktat and so during that first year, we literally got rid of every amendment to the Constitution except the Second Amendment, it's the only one that's left.\par \par \uc2 And you know what I tell people is, you know we have to -- we have to love our freedom more than we fear a germ. We have to.\par \par \uc2 And you know, even to -- and I would even, you know, remind people that there is only -- even if this was the deadly disease that they say it is, there are worse things than death and there was a whole generation and that may sound cold and people get mad at me for saying it, but we're lucky that there was a whole generation of Americans in 1776 who said it would be better to die than to not have these rights written down.\par \par \uc2 And they gave us that. They gave us that gift of that Bill of Rights, and in one year at the bidding of a doctor because he was telling us, you know you need to do this, and orchestrated fear and you know all of the weird stuff they did with the numbers, which is not what public health is supposed to be doing. In one year, all of those rights have been taken away from us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: One of the most moving and interesting and enlightening conversations we've ever had. It went on for over an hour and it's very much worth listening to.\par \par \uc2 By the way his new book is about \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  and it's amazing. Just can't recommend it highly enough. Anyway, it's all on foxnation.com. We recommend it.\par \par \uc2 Well, there is still a lot to assess from the Kyle Rittenhouse trial. It's gone to the jury apparently. The Judge, we told you, dismissed the weapon charge brought against Rittenhouse today.\par \par \uc2 Professor Jonathan Turley has been watching that trial carefully. He joins us with reaction.\par \par \uc2 You can head by the way over to tuckercarlson.com during the break to get merchandise we have on there, which apparently according to producers is pretty great and highly fashionable. It's under "shop."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, the prosecution spent most of the Rittenhouse trial undermining its own case and its closing arguments today was no different than that. How badly did it go? Well, take a look at this picture.\par \par \uc2 At one point the prosecutor pointed an AR-15 directly at jurors with his finger on the trigger, which newsflash, you're not supposed to do. Keep your finger off the trigger, son.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the closing argument from the defense, while not perfect went a little more smoothly. The defense attorney, Mark Richards chose his words very carefully. He explained that Kyle Rittenhouse has the right not to be quote "molested" by Joseph Rosenbaum.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK RICHARDS, DEFENSE ATTORNEY: He has as much right to go there as anybody else in the City of Kenosha and be unmolested by the likes of Joseph Rosenbaum, Kelly, and Joshua Ziminski.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Jonathan Turley is a Professor at \uc2 George Washington University Law School\uc2 . He joins us today having watched the trial. Professor, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So, I've got a million questions but I don't have time for many. Here's the first. So we learned today that actually, it's legal in the State of Wisconsin for a 17-year-old to have a long gun, as Rittenhouse did. Why did we not find that out to the last day of the trial, this charge stood until today?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: That's an excellent question. I wrote a column that actually question why this count was allowed into the trial. I'm baffled how the prosecutors got an indictment on this count.\par \par \uc2 From my view, this has no applicability to the case. This was not a short- barreled weapon. You can determine that with simple measurement. And then the other alternative conditions are not met by him. And so, I'm not sure what the prosecutors said that got the indictment. But the court eventually did throw that out.\par \par \uc2 But part of the problem here is that an opening statement is like an oral contract with a jury. They take it very seriously. When you say you're going to show something, they expect you to show it. When you say that, you know