awyer, that's certainly the way it seems to me, so it's nice to have that instinct confirmed.\par \par \uc2 Professor, thanks so much. Jonathan Turley with us tonight. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We are out of time, but our full interview with Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., which we cannot say enough good things about is up now on FOX Nation. Absolutely worth watching. You can watch it free along with all of our shows, the "Originals" and the "Today" shows for 90 days by going to tuckercarlson.com.\par \par \uc2 And of course, we'll be back tomorrow, 8:00 p.m., the show that is the sworn enemy -- total sincerely -- of lying, pomposity, smugness, and groupthink.\par \par \uc2 We have a surprise for you ladies and gentlemen, The Great \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  rips the reins from our hands and ably rides off into the 9:00 p.m. starting now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020211116ehbf00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Democrats Make Spending Push Amid Inflation Spike; Impact Of Biden's Spending Agenda On Middle Class; BLM Activist Talks To Fox After NYC Riot Warning; New York City Mayor-Elect Adams: Won't Be A City Of Riots; Rittenhouse Deliberations Expected To Start Monday\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Newt Gingrich, Bryan Llenas, Andrew McCarthy \par \uc2 7,707 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: Sean and Rachel and a number of us here at Fox News, including myself and Sandra Smith, tell their stories about Christmas and, and recount some of their favorite Christmas traditions. It's a great book. Hope you check it out.\par \par \uc2 ANITA VOGEL, FOX NEWS HOST: A Nice Christmas keepsake.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Good being with you today.\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: You too, John, happy Friday. Thank you for joining us. I'm Anita Vogel in for Sandra Smith today, and I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . "THE STORY" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, John, and Anita. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum in New York. And here's the story that is unfolding right now: President Biden huddled moments ago with his cabinet on the talking about this trillion-dollar infrastructure package that as Axios puts it may be "his only remaining opportunity to show voters that his party can deliver before the midterms."\par \par \uc2 Here's what the President said just moments ago, saying that the spending agenda, this is the human infrastructure part of the bill will help in America's competition with China, and claiming that it will help bring down prices that you see at the stores. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're doing it right. We know what it will mean, we know what it will mean. It will create millions of new jobs and will grow the economy and will win the world economic competition that we're engaged in the second quarter of the 21st century with China and many other countries around the world. And we're going to almost see ease and I say yes, ease, lower inflationary pressures on our economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, Former Speaker of the House, Newt Gingrich, joins me now, he is the author of the new book "Beyond Biden," and is a Fox News Contributor. Newt, always good to have you with us. You know, let's jump right off from there. From what the President just said moments ago, he said that this Build Back Better Program, which is a very large extension of benefits programs that would be given from childhood to the elderly. He says that this will cut inflation, and that it will make us more competitive with China. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, Biden considering he wants to just under any relationship to reality, the fact is, when you get for example, $50 billion in this bill, to \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  with no reforms, all you're doing is throwing money into a system that desperately needs to be modernized, and there's no pressure in the bill to do it. When you throw billions of dollars into New York City, with no reforms, you're going to build the most expensive structures in history, it makes \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  happy, it makes the construction worker union happy. But it's not going to lead to better infrastructure. We desperately need very deep reforms of our bureaucracies of the length of time it takes for us to do things.\par \par \uc2 None of that was in this bill. And I think we have to recognize, these are people believe that if you throw more and more money into broken structures, something is going to work. What it means, of course, is you're just going to have more and more expensive failures. And as I think it's important to recognize that leading Democrats have come out and said, more spending in an inflationary environment, he's just going to increase the pressure for more inflation. So, people are already finding food costs going up, rent going up, gasoline going up. There's nothing in this bill, that's going to bring that down, despite the President's nice words.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the President made a promise to the American people that if, if they make under $400,000 a year, their taxes would not increase. And I just want to remind people of a previous president who made a similar promise, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE H.W. BUSH, 41ST PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Read my lips. No new taxes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, taxes did end up going up and the president, the 41st President of the United States was not re-elected. Now, the \uc2 Tax Policy Center\uc2  has done an analysis of this. And there are a lot of different ways to look at it. We just heard \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  defending it, but part of their analysis shows that for that group, yes, it would those -- their taxes would increase a slight amount, in fact, and that some millionaires in New York and Los Angeles that the salt tax change would actually cut the taxes of those millionaires. So, what's going on here?\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Well, look, I mean, the Democrats are paying off their billionaire allies. They're under huge pressure from Democrats in New York, California, New Jersey, because those states are getting hammered, because the high taxes used to be sheltered behind federal deductibility. And when the Republicans took away that deductibility, all of a sudden, these billionaires realized how much they really were paying for local government taxes. So, it's a big break for the very, very wealthy, I think up to $90,000 per person. Compare that to what the average person is going to get, but dig more deeply.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is a tax. Inflation is a function of the government putting more money into the system than there are goods and services. So, the Biden inflation tax is if you buy a car, if you buy food, if you pay rent, if you pay for gasoline, if you try to heat your house in winter. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has already raised the cost of living for you. And in fact, the real income of Americans dropped by over one percent over the last year, because wages are not going up as fast as inflation. So, anybody who lives a real life, I mean, it may be a joke, people hiding in the White House, or it may be a joke to the Secretary of Energy, who broke up laughing when she was asked what her plan is to lower the cost of gasoline.\par \par \uc2 But remember, there's a fight underway right now, to close the largest pipeline into Michigan, which carries I think, 60 percent of the gasoline used in Michigan, plus some gasoline for Indiana and Ohio. If they close that pipeline, and further raise the price of gasoline, people are going to think they're just crazy, and totally out of touch with normal, everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a good segue because we're about to talk to \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  of Michigan. Newt Gingrich, always good to see you. Thank you very much, Sir. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joining me now Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2 , Member of the Congressional Progressive Caucus. Congresswoman, always good to have you with us, thank you for being here. Just to respond to what Newt Gingrich was just saying there. What is your stance on even the consideration or the study of potentially not keeping that Line 5, running and functioning in the new plan that was, that was scheduled for it?\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): So, like all things that we talked about, they're very complicated. Newt Gingrich is a friend and I respect him, but disagreed with most of what he just said. It's a very complicated issue. We also don't want to see any kind of oil pipeline spill into the Great Lakes, which was 20 percent of the freshwater in the world. There have been several pipeline strikes, there are alternatives about how that energy will be delivered. It's complicated, not talking points.\par \par \uc2 And anybody who's worried about inflation as, as Newt Gingrich or anybody else. It's, it's real but it's being caused by a lot of things, including labor shortages for the past year, a supply chain that we've got to focus on and make more resilient. And the fact of the matter is COVID for the last 18 months has forced a lot of people to leave the workforce, we are still trying to figure out how to get women back into it because of childcare issues. It's a complicated issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, do you think -- so let me play this from the Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , because she made an argument that \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  made yesterday. Watch us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So, when we can get Build Back Better passed, and we are optimistic that we will, the American people will see cost actually reduced around some of the most essential services that they need to take care of their basic responsibilities, including issues like childcare --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't know how most Americans take this argument, but that, but what she's saying and what \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  said yesterday, is you know, it doesn't matter that all of your other costs, your gas prices are going up, food prices are going up because we're going to pay for your childcare. It's like, it's almost like saying, you know, there's a leak in your roof, but we're going to put a drain under it. But the leaf is going to -- the roof is going to keep leaking. Why is that? How does that sit? Do you think with most Americans?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: I think it's one factor, defects and (INAUDIBLE) we have. Look, everybody's looking for people to do jobs in every -- there is one reason why jobs aren't being filled. A lot of people can't afford their childcare. So, when you get people back in, I think that fixing our roads and bridges, here's a reality, our infrastructures equal to any third world country. Presidents, Republican and Democrat, for the last few decades have been trying to fix our infrastructure, the infrastructure bill, we have to have ways that we're going to spend the money. I want to say to the former speaker who's a friend, we got to make sure that when it gets out, it's spent wisely. I've said my staff, I'm going to track every dollar --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you have confidence that that will happen? I mean, you know, I think a lot of people just look at this trillion-dollar infrastructure bill, they go, oh, my gosh, OK, here we go. This pet project here, that pet project there, are we actually going to see, you know, potholes disappear and all of our roadways get better? I mean, that, that I think that is such a real concern that people have. You know, in New York, you pay $18.00 to go through the Lincoln Tunnel to get from New Jersey to New York, those are supposed to be the funds that help to support the roads. And yet it's like, you know, driving through a third world country. So, do you really think that money is going to be spent efficiently Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: I have to not only do I want it to be spent efficiently, my job and the job of Congress, and that is to make sure that these donors get where they need to go and that they are and that people hold us accountable for doing that oversight. And I, I've made it very clear to people, we've allocated this money to the States, we're not just giving it to the state. We got a responsibility to make sure it's getting where it needs to go to and how it's spent.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I think all Americans hope that you can do that. Good luck with that. I think it's going to be a very tall order. It's a trillion dollars that's going to be going all over the country. And we hope that the benefit of it is seen quickly and effectively because people want the country to be efficient, and I think that's one of the big concerns that we see right now that it just doesn't feel that way. So, we'll see where it goes. Congressman, thank you very much. Always good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Be safe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  activist threatens violence not seen since the riots that erupted in American cities last year and the rulings now being weighed by the judge in the Kyle Rittenhouse trial, that really could have a huge impact on what happens with this jury. We're going to get you up to speed on that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fox News in a one-on-one interview with \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  leader -- with a \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  leader in New York, who warned that there would be riots and bloodshed in the streets of New York City if the next mayor, \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  moves forward with his plans to reinstate the undercover police division in the New York City police.\par \par \uc2 But as you can see in today's New York Post cover, mayor -- the mayor-elect \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , who was a former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  captain is clearly not backing down on wanting to make the city safer.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent Bryan Llenas, reporting live here in New York. Hi, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Well, look, I pressed Hawk Newsom about what exactly it is that he wants from New York City's mayor-elect \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 ? And he said very clearly that ultimately, they do not want to see these plainclothes police officers on the streets. And that is a red line for them.\par \par \uc2 The units were dismantled in 2020, following last summer's violent protests. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAWK NEWSOME, LEADER, BLACK LIVES MATTER, NEW YORK: When these plainclothes police officers come back into the streets with a mentality that they're going to eradicate crime, they're going to make a mistake and they're going to kill someone. When that happens, there will be a repeat of George Floyd. It's simple mathematics. It's not about Hawk making threat.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice-over): This all stems from a contentious meeting, Newsome and his organization had with Adams that was live streamed on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  Wednesday. In the meeting, they pressed Adams about defunding and auditing the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  and making promises. While unsatisfied with Adams response, Hawk said this later.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: They think that they're going to go back to the old ways of policing that we are going to take to the streets again? There will be riots? There will be fire, and there will be bloodshed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, Adams, a former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  captain, called those comments silly, and he also said that he is going to stand by his campaign promise to make sure that those undercover cops are put back on the streets to fight the surge in crime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR-ELECT OF NEW YORK CITY: This city is not going to be a city of riots. It's not going to be a city of burning. This is going to be a city where we are going to be safe, we're going to have effective policing that's not heavy handed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: And mayor-elect Adams brought up an interesting point. He said it was telling that violence in the city and crime in the city was never brought up in their one-on-one meeting that was live-streamed, nor was the issue of plainclothes policing. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we know that crime and homicide has gone directly higher since the disbanding of that unit. And we had an interview with a former New York City police detective who said that that was exactly what would happen when that unit was disbanded.\par \par \uc2 And we see the numbers that support that. So, we'll see where it goes from here. Bryan, thank you very much. Interesting interview. Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 So, a verdict is expected soon in the trial of Kyle Rittenhouse. He's facing seven charges of shooting and killing two men and injuring a third during the unrest in Kenosha last summer.\par \par \uc2 A judge today said that he will consider secondary charges in some of these incidents. And he's going to give instructions to the jurors on Monday -- second-degree charges, I should say.\par \par \uc2 And is also considering a request by the defense to toss out the case after what's been a very emotional and dramatic week. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THOMAS BINGER, PROSECUTOR: Everybody that you shot at that night, you intended to kill. Correct?\par \par \uc2 KYLE RITTENHOUSE, ACCUSED, KENOSHA UNREST SHOOTING: I didn't intend to kill them.\par \par \uc2 COREY CHIRAFISI, DEFENSE ATTORNEY: It wasn't until you pointed your gun at him, advanced on him with your gun, now your hands down, pointed at him -- that he fired. Right?\par \par \uc2 GAIGE GROSSKREUTZ, SOLE SURVIVOR SHOT BY KYLE RITTENHOUSE IN KENOSHA, WISCONSIN: Correct.\par \par \uc2 JAMES KRAUS, PROSECUTOR: And we did have you read over your statement, right?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN DEBRUIN, FREELANCE PHOTOGRAPHER: Correct.\par \par \uc2 KRAUS: And we asked if you knew anything beyond that statement?\par \par \uc2 DEBRUIN: Correct?\par \par \uc2 KRAUS: We didn't ask you to change it.\par \par \uc2 DEBRUIN: Yes, you did.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: Pardon me?\par \par \uc2 BINGER: That was before the testimony.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: Don't get brazen with me.\par \par \uc2 I was astonished when you will begin your examination by commenting on the defendant's post-arrest silence. That's basic law. It's been basic law in this country for 40 years, 50 years. I have no idea why you would do something like that.\par \par \uc2 CHIRAFISI: The defense is going to be making a motion for a mistrial.\par \par \uc2 RITTENHOUSE: That what I -- that's why I run.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: We're going to just take (INAUDIBLE) break, anyway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, joining me now, former federal prosecutor and Fox News contributor Andy McCarthy. What a week it has been in this trial, Andy. Your thoughts on what we saw today in terms of the instructions and what you expect when we get into closing arguments on Monday?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Yes, it's been very contentious, Martha. And it's really a shame that I think what most people think they know about the case a lot of it comes through political prisms rather than, you know, a very highly wrought factual emotional scenario that plays out in the facts of the case\par \par \uc2 What happened today with the jury instructions is pretty standard. It's odd, I think that the judge is still not at the point of making some rulings that the lawyers would want to know before they begin their summations next week.\par \par \uc2 But it's pretty standard, especially in murder cases that the judge will instruct the jury on what's known as lesser included offenses. The idea being that a murderer is kind of a greater charge and subset within it are kind of lesser charges.\par \par \uc2 And the jury will sometimes find that the lesser charges are more easily provable than the greater ones. That happens in most cases, but I think this case really comes down to self-defense. It's not one of those ones where the jury is likely to split between say, first-degree and second- degree murder, it will be guilty or not guilty.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, two things really point towards that. One of the moments that we just played when the person who was wounded in this -- in this incident, admitted that he pointed his gun at Kyle Rittenhouse first, and threatened him before -- or, he pointed his gun at him before Kyle Rittenhouse fired.\par \par \uc2 You know, some people felt that at that moment, that part of these charges could have been dismissed by this judge.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is that to you the most significant moment in this trial? Or does it really rely on the video itself and how the jurors interpret it?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, I also think, Martha, when a defendant testifies, the jury always that's a big moment in a trial.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: If the defendant decides to take the stand, jury wants to see the defendant look him in the eye and say, you know, I either -- I'm not guilty, or what I did, I did with an innocent heart. And that obviously happened in this case, as well.\par \par \uc2 But you're right to seize on that moment, which is captured in video as well as very vivid testimony where you have this guy, Grosskreutz, who is actually pointing a gun at Rittenhouse's face at the moment that Rittenhouse shoots because that's really what it comes down to.\par \par \uc2 There's not a question here about what Rittenhouse his intent was, even though that, you know, that questioning has been highlighted in some of the coverage. The question is, did he have the right under the circumstances because he wasn't an assailant. He was the one who was being preyed upon? Did he have a right to defend himself?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now, quick question before I let you go. This is an image from a video that was tweeted out by then-candidate Biden, in which, he -- the video essentially calls, Kyle Rittenhouse a white supremacist. His mother mentioned the word, defamed, in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Let's listen to this real quick, and then just get a quick thought from you on the other side, if I may.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WENDY RITTENHOUSE, MOTHER OF KYLE RITTENHOUSE: President Biden don't know my son, whatsoever. And he's not a white supremacist, he's not a racist, and he did that for the votes. And what he did to my son, he defamed him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quick thought on that, Andy?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, I'm going to say the same thing I said when President Trump was president, if you're going to be the chief executive of the United States, your job is to protect people's rights to a fair trial, not to make demagogic comments that make it harder for justice to be done in this country. It's wrong, no matter who does it, and it's condemnable for Biden to have done that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes, it's a great point. I mean, it needs to come from the very top down the message that people are innocent until proven guilty. And that to cast aspersions on anyone, especially who's headed towards a trial from that high office is something that should not be done.\par \par \uc2 Andy, thank you. We'll be watching. And as I know, you will be with great interest on Monday when these closing arguments get underway.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, just how close the Biden administration was -- how closely they were working with members of the \uc2 National School Board Association\uc2  to target fired-up parents. That's a big question. We'll talk about it, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking today, the Republican who almost became New Jersey's next governor has conceded.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Jack Ciattarelli says he does not see a reason for a recount after coming within three points of winning in a race that nobody thought he had even a shot at, and it's a state that President Biden carried by 15 points.\par \par \uc2 Ciattarelli says he does not consider the campaign a failure because he helped the GOP to make big wins in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACK CIATTARELLI, FORMER MEMBER, NEW JERSEY GENERAL ASSEMBLY: We leaned into down-ballot races and the results were the greatest election night for Republicans in 30 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  becoming the New Jersey's first democratic governor to win reelection in more than 40 years in the Garden State.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): So, the school board group that compared some parents to domestic terrorists and a letter to President Biden worked on that letter with the White House for a "weeks".\par \par \uc2 That's according to an e-mail that Fox News has now obtained. An executive with the \uc2 National School Boards Association\uc2  wrote to its board of directors, "In talks over the last several weeks with White House staff, they requested additional information on some of the specific threats, so the letter also details many of the incidents that have been occurring."\par \par \uc2 Five days after the group sent that letter, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  announced that the Justice Department would help local law enforcement deal with "threats of violence" against school board members.\par \par \uc2 Garland told Congress that he did not talk to the White House before releasing his memo. No response yet from the Justice Department about this newly surfaced memo.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Marc Thiessen, former chief speechwriter for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and an \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  scholar. Marc, always good to have you with us. So, you know, we remember that.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That testimony from \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and he was pressed on whether or not he had any interaction with the White House because people thought it was kind of strange that this letter came from the \uc2 National School Board Association\uc2 , I think on a Friday. And then, boom, Monday, the Department of Justice says we're going to put the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , have a look at investigating these parents. Do you believe the attorney general that there was no coordination between the White House and DOJ that produced this letter from \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Not at all. I mean, it's obvious that there was. I mean, first of all, he said that there was no coordination between DOJ. He never spoke to the \uc2 NSBA\uc2 . The president of the \uc2 National School Board Association\uc2  says in these emails that she had been in contact with the Justice Department as well as the White House. So, you know, she was talking directly to people on his staff.\par \par \uc2 Now, maybe she -- I'm sure she didn't speak to Garland himself, most people don't get the call of the attorney general and they haven't pick up the phone, but she was talking to the people who are involved in setting this policy for sure. And we already know that the \uc2 NSBA\uc2 , which is by the way disavowed its letter and apologize for his letter. Garland hasn't disavowed or apologize for his memorandum.\par \par \uc2 They were coordinated. They were giving edits to the work to the \uc2 NSBA\uc2 . The White House was telling them to add the list of different incidents, none of which Garland says (inaudible) list of incidents inspired him to write the memo, he says, none of those is a federal crime. And then he also said during this testimony that, you know, to distance himself from the letter, he said, well, I didn't mention domestic terrorism or the Patriot Act in my letter\par \par \uc2 But when they released the letter, there was an accompanying statement said that he intended to form a task force that involved the National Security Division of the Justice Department. The \uc2 National Security Division\uc2  was created by the Patriot Act. So even that was untrue. So Garland is really, you know, he's trying to distance himself from them, from this, but this is a mess of their own making.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, I mean, it's obvious, and you put it so well, Marc, that in order for the Department of Justice to open investigation and to employ the services of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and other national security agencies. You would have to have evidence that you believe a federal crime was committed that would evoke the need to employ those institutions into an investigation.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And to push it out of the local arena, where the local law enforcement could deal with these very sparse incidents that they had evidence of, which do not seem to be systemic or widespread across borders. And the local law enforcement has every ability to take care of them. So I think this is worth continuing to dig into, to know exactly what the communication was. And I think we're getting closer to it. Marc. Thank you very much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So Russia and China are both increasing their military aggression on nearby countries right now. It's a big deal. They're putting dueling challenges in effect. And that is in the mix where the President is concerned. How will he respond to what's going on, on these borders with Russia and with China. He's got a big meeting with President Xi coming on Monday. Former national security adviser \uc2 Robert O'Brien\uc2  joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So inflation a big problem here at home, but the Biden administration is also dealing with dueling threats right now from China and from Russia, as they both ramp up their military presence on the world stage, and really creating a test situation for President Biden. The White House announcing a high stakes virtual meeting between President Biden and Chinese President Xi that will happen on Monday evening, we are told. Here's the White House just a short time ago being pressed about what may or what should be discussed at that meeting. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Does he plan to bring up the COVID-19 origins with President Xi, given that China has been blocking investigators from getting access to information that's critical to that?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: That is a remaining concern, and there will be a broad range of topics that will be discussed. And the President certainly not going to hold back on areas where he is concerned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not really clear yes or no on that. Ambassador \uc2 Robert O'Brien\uc2 , former national security adviser under President Trump. Always good to have you here, Ambassador O'Brien, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O'BRIEN\uc2 , FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER UNDER PRESIDENT TRUMP: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What did you think of that answer about whether or not the President would press President Xi on COVID origins?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, you know, it's not unusual not to get the topics out of the conversation with a foreign leader before it happens. But, you know, we'll have to see if the President actually raises the COVID origins. We know the Chinese will respond very harshly to that. And I'm not sure that's the tone that the President's looking for in this meeting. So we'll have to wait and get a readout afterwards to see if he actually raises the COVID origins with the President Xi.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What would you say if you were advising the President? What are the most important things with regard to China? And then, we'll talk about Russia in a moment. What do you think he really has to get to in this meeting to make his points?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, it's a maximum in international affairs that weaknesses provocative. And unfortunately, the US is perceived as being very weak right now by both Xi and Putin. They watched the debacle in Afghanistan. They're watching the catastrophe that's playing out on the southern border. They see Secretary Kerry literally begging for a climate deal from the Chinese who didn't even bother to attend the summit. So both Xi and Putin think they've got an opportunity here. They smell weakness and they want to exploit it.\par \par \uc2 I think the important thing is for the President to reiterate the statements that Secretary Blinken and National Security Adviser Sullivan have made with respect to Taiwan, the South China Sea and America's interests in the region that we're not going to leave the Indo-Pacific, that we're staying there, that the Chinese aren't going to run us out. And we're going to stand by our friends and democratic partners in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 We just can't have a Chinese invasion of Taiwan that would be a disaster geopolitically. And it'd be heartbreaking for the people of Taiwan, a democracy of 30 million people to go the same way of Tibet, and Xinjiang, and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed, it would. It's very interesting what's happening with Russia, because you've got Russian troops massing on the border of Ukraine again, and then you have this situation in Belarus. And, of course, Ukraine, you know, wants to be part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but then it Poland is part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . So what they're allowing is migrants to cross into Belarus. And then, Belarus, with the backing of Russia, is basically saying, we're going to push these, all of these migrants over to the Polish border, and press into Poland. It's creating a really tenuous situation.\par \par \uc2 So Poland is a \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally, of course, but they're sort of a sneaky way of trying to sort of destabilize Poland.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We saw this happen with Turkey in some earlier crisis, where the poor refugees and migrants are weaponizing. Your heartbreaks for the migrants, even though many of them are military age males that were selected to create the maximum law disruption in Poland, you feel terrible for them being out on the frontier. But the Russians understand that it's going to be very difficult for the United States, which is not defending its own border, which isn't sending troops to defend our southern border where we basically have an open border and maybe the same type of immigrants and migrants from the same countries are flooding across our southern border.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be very hard for the Biden administration to actually send troops in to support the polls who want to defend the integrity of their own country. So it's a very clever move by the President Putin and he's put Biden administration in a real bind using, you know, one of the foreign policy mishaps that that's taking place in our country right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, as you point out, we've had Afghanistan sort of open the floodgates and made us appear weaker. I think that that's a fair assessment. And we'll see whether or not anything substantial comes out of this meeting on Monday night with President Xi. Ambassador O'Brien, thank you so much. It's always good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Thank you. We'll hope for the best. Yes thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We do and we hope for the best. Thank you very much, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 So coming up, a new lawsuit filed by the chief electrician on the set of "Rust" what claims that he was almost struck by the bullet that killed cinematographer Halyna Hutchins, who he says gave Alec Baldwin the gun with that live round inside of it, which we've discussed here on this program. Another answer to that coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A crew member who was on the set in Alec Baldwin's movie "Rust" is filing the first lawsuit that we know of after the shooting that killed cinematographer Halyna Hutchins. Serge Svetnoy says that he was right next to her when she was shot and that he held her as she lay dying. I'm going to speak with his lawyer in just a moment about that case. But first, to correspondent Christina Coleman, who joins us live in Los Angeles. Hi, Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. In this lawsuit, Serge Svetnoy says the bullet that killed her Halyna Hutchins narrowly missed him. He was the head electrician on this western film. Now, this is the first known lawsuit filed in connection to the October 21st shooting. It states that Svetnoy suffered injury including severe emotional distress from this incident. Svetnoy says gunpowder and other residual materials from the weapon struck the right side of his face after that deadly shot was fired.\par \par \uc2 He alleges that multiple people fail to properly check the gun to make sure it didn't have any live rounds, including the first assistant director Dave Halls, producer and actor Alec Baldwin, and our armorer Hannah Gutierrez Reed. Svetnoy says he was close friends with Hutchins and they work together on numerous films. He says this was the last picture with her onset, and that he held Hutchins as she was dying.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERGE SVETNOY, CHIEF ELECTRICIAN, "RUST" I still cannot believe that she wasn't longer here. My (inaudible) her mother crying in my arms asking how to survive (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Svetnoy also gave a blistering account of what he says happened the day of the shooting. On \uc2 Facebook\uc2  he wrote, yes, I was standing shoulder to shoulder with Halyna during this fatal shot that took her life and injured the director Joel Souza. I was holding her in my arms while she was dying. Her blood was on my hands.\par \par \uc2 Svetnoy went on to say the shooting is a result of unprofessionalism and negligence writing, the negligence from the person who was supposed to check the weapon on the site did not do this. The person who had to announce that the loaded gun was on this site did not do this. The person who should have checked this weapon before bringing it to the set did not do it.\par \par \uc2 Now, Santa Fe detectives are still investigating how live rounds ended up on the set. And so far, no one has been charged in this shooting Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Christina, thank you very much. Joining me now Gary Dordick, the attorney for Serge Svetnoy. Gary, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, this is a very tough situation for your client and devastating to lose a friend like that in the midst of a professional work environment. I'd like to play a soundbite for you. I spoke to Dave Halls' attorney, and I asked her the question about whether or not she could confirm that Dave Halls handed the loaded gun, the loaded pistol to Alec Baldwin, and here's what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I need to just nail something down here, did Dave Halls hand the gun to Alec Baldwin? That's a very straightforward question. Did he hand the gun that was used and that killed Halyna Hutchins, did he hand it to Alec Baldwin or not?\par \par \uc2 LISA TORRACO, ATTORNEY FOR DAVE HALLS: That's why we haven't spoken out for over a week, because I want to make sure that I've got the facts right. I have received information from crew members that the armorer handed it directly to Baldwin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think about that, Gary? And can your client shed light on exactly who handed the gun to him and who had custody of that gun all the way through the sequence of events?\par \par \uc2 GARY DORDICK, SERGE SVETNOY'S ATTORNEY: I don't know that he can specifically address the custody of the gun because he was focused on his part on the film. But all of these claims coming out so long after this tragedy, why would it take Hanna Gutierrez Reed lawyer three weeks to say that it was sabotage. Now, almost a month later, they come out and say we never handed him the gun. That had been stated previously extensively. I believe that's what Mr. Baldwin had claimed. And now they have a different story. It all sounds pretty suspect to me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But your client, he would be so important I would imagine, to the testimony and to the investigation, because it does matter whether Hannah Gutierrez Reed picked up the gun, checked it, looked in it to see if there was alive round, obviously, would be very important, and whether or not Dave Halls did that. How much of that can your client shed light on? How much of it did he actually witness?\par \par \uc2 DORDICK: I don't know that he can but I think it matters only for the criminal case, right? If you're trying to criminal case and that standard, which is much higher than the civil burden of proof, which is beyond reasonable doubt, versus more likely than not. In terms of more likely than not negligence, they're all extremely negligent, careless and reckless. These guns are unattended. These guns were left laying in the dirt. They had live ammunition on the set. Why would you ever have live rounds on a movie set?\par \par \uc2 And I do believe it's incumbent on Alec Baldwin to check the gun. They're saying well, he had no responsibility to check because somebody said it was cold or safe. That needs to change. It's very easy to tell a dummy or fake or blank bullet from a real one, if anyone looks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, there were three people (inaudible), clearly. You know, with regard to the live rounds, there are stories about people doing sort of shooting practice with live rounds the day before, did your client see any of that happening?\par \par \uc2 DORDICK: He didn't see that happen. But being on a movie set, which is sort of a small group of folks spending a lot of time together, he had heard that that had happened. He had also been involved in hearing that there were two prior accidental discharges. He was also on the set when they fired the gun and they were supposed to have a blast for a scene and it didn't go off at all. So you have them not going off when they're supposed to, going off when they're not supposed to, and then you have a bullet in a real gun that's not supposed to be there.\par \par \uc2 It was a disaster of events. Mr. Svetnoy, at one point, had to take the armorer side and say, hey, there's a gun here in the dirt. It's been here for a long time unattended. Can somebody please take care of this gun and put it away? You leave guns unattended and that's when problems happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Thank you very much, Gary Dordick. We'll continue to stay on this. We hope you'll come back as this moves forward. Good to have you here, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DORDICK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the big admission just made by one of the media outlets about Trump-Russia collusion reporting and the Steele Dossier as this thing continues to unravel. The very latest coming up next, stick around.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: While we continue to follow this story and breaking just a short time ago, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  now correcting and removing large portions of two articles about the Steele dossier on former President Trump, that was after the arrest of the Russian source for the dossier as part of Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's investigation.\par \par \uc2 The Post says that Igor Danchenko's indictment and new reporting by the paper, raised questions about the involvement of another man that it had identified as a Steele source. Fox News has learned another Durham indictment references President Biden's National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan, without naming him in those documents. Correspondent Alexandria Hoff is live reporting on this for us this afternoon from Washington. Hi, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Yes, Jake Sullivan is the first person directly associated with the Biden White House mentioned in the special counsel's findings. Once a close aide to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , he was referred to as a foreign policy advisor within the indictment of Michael Sussman. Fox News sources clarified Sullivan's identity.\par \par \uc2 The White House have remained silent on the matter and there's no indication at this point that he's a target and \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's probe. Sussman on the other hand is one of two charged with lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  . According to the indictment, the Clinton campaign attorney and the other man, Igor Danchenko, had failed to tell top \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials who they had worked for. And Durham had said that if they had been honest in that information that \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials could have treated the information they come forward with w skepticism. Martha,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, Alexandria Hoff. So breaking right now, the Justice Department has just said that two charges have been filed against former Trump advisor \uc2 Steven Bannon\uc2  for failing to comply with the House subpoena for the committee investigating the events of January the 6th.\par \par \uc2 And that is THE STORY for this Friday. We'll see you back on Monday. I'll see you on THE FIVE, coming up. See you then.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020211113ehbc00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Fox News' "The Five" Celebrates Veterans Day By Honoring And Paying Tribute To The Men And Women Who Served In The Military; Left Wing Media And The Democrats Ridicule Kyle Rittenhouse On His Trial For The Shooting In Kenosha; Consumer Prices And Gas Prices Are Up And President Biden Blaming Americans For Buying Too Much Stuff; Biden: Many Don't Truly Understand Supply Chain; Worthy Of A Book Deal: Americans Think Their Life Is Good Idea For A Novel; The Perfect Bedtime: Study: Going To Sleep At 10PM Is Best For Your Health\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Jesse Watters, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dana  Perino, Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,202 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: And the defense's argument that anything that happened that night including the death of two individuals and one other who was severely wounded, that it was all self-defense. The details continue. That will do it here.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 THE FIVE is outside on Fox Square for Veterans Day paying tribute to all of our heroic men and women who've served. We're going to be honoring veterans on set later in the show. But first, the United States of America is the greatest country in the world. A place where you can do anything and you can be anything.\par \par \uc2 A place where a better tomorrow is not just a hope but a guarantee. But freedom isn't free. And on Veterans Day we must pay tribute to those who sacrificed so much so we here at home don't have to sacrifice at all. Millions of brave men and women have fought to protect our nation.\par \par \uc2 They come from all different backgrounds and creeds. They put themselves in harm's way without question, without prejudice, without terms, knowing full well the dangers they face may cost them their lives. Yet they answer the call.\par \par \uc2 They wear their uniforms proudly and put country over self. They stand strong to defend our flag ensuring our liberties remain intact. Our veterans who time and time again find themselves in the midst of impossible situation proved that anything is possible.\par \par \uc2 When things got bad in Afghanistan, it was veterans groups that rallied together to keep their sacred promise to never leave anybody behind. They refused to give up and they got people out when no one else would. Our veterans showed compassion during chaos. They prove that no act of kindness is too small and exemplify what it truly means to be selfless.\par \par \uc2 The courage of our veterans cannot be forgotten. We must honor them. We must respect them. We must thank them. We must always remember them because our veterans are not just the best of America, they are America. Greg Gutfeld, your thoughts on Veterans Day.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: I thought that was a fantastic monologue.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you. It's Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, yes. Can we do a side by side of me and Jesse for a minute here? Why does he have all of the flags?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: My fellow Americans.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This is clearly -- this is clearly -- you -- this is deliberate. You get all the flags. Look at me.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I served, Greg. I served and you did not.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, that's true. By the way, he's right. We're talking about all these -- the veterans of all these wars. We cannot forget the war on Christmas. Jesse Watters was selfless volunteer and veteran fighting the war on Christmas while I was home drinking eggnog. You were out there preserving my freedom to hang mistletoe in public restrooms.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: And we thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. But I want to say this.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Because a lot, you know, Fox -- when Fox talks about Veterans, it's not lip service. We actually follow through with everything and I think we've hired a lot of veterans. And so I mean, its advice to employers that when you hire a veteran, you have like a no -- you have zero risk of failure.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Because when you -- when the vet arrives to your place of work, fully packaged, like all the ingredients are there. It's like when you buy one of those prepared meals that has everything. So I wrote down, do like veterans have the secrets to life already solved. They have a -- they keep a routine that begins early in the morning.\par \par \uc2 Anybody who does that succeeds. They have impeccable personal grooming. I don't know one sloppy veteran. They have manners and they have discipline which is on short supply these days and they appreciate hard work. They're not scared of it.\par \par \uc2 So in this era of like the TikTok generation it's hard to come by these qualities but the veterans has it in spades.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That is a great point. I'm about -- I'm going to go hire a veteran right now for "WATTERS' WORLD."\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You're not going to make them drive you home. That is just disgusting.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That veteran might whip you into shape.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I need it, Dana. Especially on the grooming side.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Your parents would be so proud.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: They would. Your thoughts on this very special day?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I was thinking about how this day comes just before Thanksgiving every year. And when you think -- and when you go around the table at Thanksgiving, what are you thankful for? For all of us, I think as Americans that should be number one.\par \par \uc2 Your health, your family, yes of course, your employment, but the fact that there are people in this country that volunteer to defend us. And they go there with no politics. And they go there with kindness, bravery and courage. That really is the meaning of Thanksgiving to me.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Judge Jeanine?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: Hi.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: How are you doing today?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, we're working on my hearing tonight.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Okay, well I heard -- yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Listen, you know, I have to tell you, what Greg really brought something to my mind about hiring a veteran. And that is, you know, we're talking about affirmative action and there's been all those arguing about, you know, we should have it, we shouldn't have it.\par \par \uc2 You know, maybe we should have affirmative action for veterans. I don't care what color they are. I don't care what nationality they are. They are the ones who put their lives on the line. They are the ones who decided, you know, that they come back with maybe one or two limbs or three limbs less than fewer than when they started.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, I'm sitting here now, I'm wearing my dad's pin from the Navy. And it says obedience, zeal, and I don't know what the third one is -- oh, fidelity. And it has his name on the back. It says Nasser Leo Ferris, 1945.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's amazing.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And I remember when I was a kid I used to wear his uniform. And we always had the American flag hanging in front of our house and I still do to this day. And my dad was on the first ship to Nagasaki in the Navy and he saw the plume and he died early as a result of, you know, seeing the plume, he had a head and neck cancer from it. \\\par \par \uc2 But, you know, to this day, both he and my grand-dad fought in World War II. I believe in veterans. I believe in America. I am so hurt at what is happening today when people don't recognize the significance of these people who go -- our children, our brothers, our parents our sisters and do this for America.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Jessica?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I echo everyone's sentiments and adding to Greg's list, team players. Veterans know how to work in a team in an increasingly isolated culture where people are kind of out for themselves or not thinking about your neighbors or someone who you'd be working with.\par \par \uc2 And the skills that veterans need to be thought when they go into employment in the civilian world are no big deal. You can teach them to program. You can teach them to do whatever it is that you need. The stuff that they have learned before is far more valuable.\par \par \uc2 And one thing that's really nice about Veterans Day and just talking about the subject, is it's unifying across the political aisle. There was a new study out from YouGov that showed everybody over 70 percent says we don't do enough for our veterans when you think of what they suffer for mental health crisis, the homelessness crisis. Making sure that they got great health care that they deserve after serving, it's nice to all be on the same side.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But I'm into one thing about the unemployment. There's a strange thing with veterans -- say you're active duty military and you're going to transition out. For some reason, many of them don't think that they have skills that could be useful in the private sector.\par \par \uc2 So we're telling the employers hire veterans. What we should also say to you, you do have the skills. And just reach out to one of these groups that can help you figure out how -- basically, if you figure out how to prevent a bombing of a major sensitive unit, then you can actually pretty much do anything in the private sector. So, just take heart and look forward to great opportunities when you return.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: If you bombed the unit, send me the resume. I want to talk to you. THE FIVE will continue to honor those who serve and we're going to introduce you to some very great veterans later on in the show.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a self-defense expert takes the stand in the Kyle Rittenhouse murder trial as the media can barely hide their hatred for the teenager.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Another dramatic day of testimony in the Kyle Rittenhouse murder trial after prosecutors fumbled the case in epic fashion. Rittenhouse's defense resting their case today after bringing a self-defense expert to the stand along with this eyewitness who said the first man killed was being physically aggressive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANK HERNANDEZ, SELF-PROCLAIMED "PROFESSIONAL COMMENTATOR" FOR REAL AMERICA'S VOICE: -- he led the charge into the gas station. He was getting physically aggressive. He appeared to be attempting to start a physical altercation with people in the gas station to the point where he was telling one of the individuals with the rifle, "Shoot me, N-word, shoot me, N-word," clearly attempting to start a physical altercation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Meanwhile, Democrats are throwing innocent until proven guilty out the window. New York Congressman Hakeem Jeffries tweeting "Lock up Kyle Rittenhouse and throw away the key." And the media isn't doing any better.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY BEHAR, THE VIEW HOST: That acting job of the crying. I can't even look at it. That is one of the worst acting job I've ever seen.\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MORNING JOE HOST: This judge is an absolute joke. He's been a joke from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: This is white privilege on steroids.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY TOOBIN, CNN CHIEF LEGAL ANALYST: What kind of idiot 17-year-old gets a giant run and goes to a riot. He has no license. He has no training. He thinks he is going to scrub graffiti off with his AR-15. I mean, the stupidity of this. It's like what could possibly go wrong. The good news for Kyle Rittenhouse is that he's not on trial for being an idiot.\par \par \uc2 AREVA\uc2  MARTIN, CNN LEGAL ANALYST: On cross-examination what we saw was I thought a very rehearsed Kyle Rittenhouse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right. Let's take it from there. You know what's amazing about all that. You get this guy, Jeffrey Toobin. I don't know how he gets on television after what he's been through. But he actually comes out and says this kid has no training. He crossed state lines with a gun. He doesn't even know what the facts are.\par \par \uc2 Number on, the kid is a -- he's a police explorer. He's got training with a gun. He didn't cross state lines with the gun. He's not even listening to the evidence. So, this hate, where does it all come from?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think it comes from the fact that two people ended up dead. That a kid did borrow his friend's gun. I believe that's how he got the AR-15. He said that he wanted that gun because he thought it looked cool. He went to a riot that was going on about the shooting of a black man that was shot seven times (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Holding a knife. Holding a knife trying to attack a woman, but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm explaining where this --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm sorry, but we've been through these facts. We know these facts.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But they -- but that is the --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It has nothing to do with the case. It has nothing to do.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It does have to do with the case.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right. If you -- well then you have to include the fact that that the two people dead, one was a pedophile who anally raped children. Another one is a serial domestic abuser --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So, he should be dead because of the --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Absolutely!\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't -- no, no.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Guys, guys, I'm going to stop you both. I am the judge here. The bottom line is this. The -- Rosenbaum -- there is testimony not only from the defendant himself, the accused. There is testimony from Drew Hernandez.\par \par \uc2 All agree that Rosenbaum came after Kyle Rittenhouse. There was testimony that Grosskreutz came at him with a gun pointing at his head. That's self- defense. And then Hoover came at him with a skate board which is deadly physical force, knocked him in the neck with it. Again, he had the right to use it. So forget all that other stuff. On the facts, this kid should not have been indicted.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Should he had been indicted, Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Not on that charge. Just carrying a gun maybe --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, I named three of them.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- but the murder charges, absolutely not. Take the politics out of it, judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Every single lawyer in the country that's seen the trial or looked at the video has come to the exact same conclusion. It was a weak case from the jump. The prosecution did a very bad job presenting the case and it looks like it's heading to an acquittal on these bigger charges.\par \par \uc2 Even my liberal mother says, Jesse, you know, he is not a boy scout. I said mom, I texted her right back. It's clear self-defense. No comment after that. She know its self-defense.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's clearly self-defense.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS