ip that they had over the years.\par \par \uc2 But first, National Court -- National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin with a look back. Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Well known for his integrity, a soldier for 35 years. He'll be remembered for the Powell doctrine when he was Chairman of the Joint Chiefs during the first Gulf War, which stated the U.S. should only use military force if it has clear and achievable objectives with public support, sufficient firepower and an exit strategy.\par \par \uc2 He famously told then Secretary of State Madeleine Albright that U.S. troops should not be used as toy soldiers when she wanted to use the military in the Balkans.\par \par \uc2 His Pottery Barn rule, you break it, you own it was a tough lesson the U.S. learned the hard way after the invasion of Afghanistan and Iraq after 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Born to Jamaican immigrants in 1937. He wrote in his autobiography, mine is the story of a black kid of no early promise from an immigrant family of limited means who was raised in the South Bronx.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Above all, never lose faith in America. Its faults are yours to fix, not to curse. America is a family. There may be differences and disputes within the family. But we must not allow the family to be broken into warring factions. From the diversity of our people let us draw strength and not see weakness.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: He joined ROTC at the City College of New York in 1962. He was one of more than 16,000 military advisors sent by President John F. Kennedy to Vietnam where he served two tours and was injured.\par \par \uc2 He became Defense Secretary Caspar Weinberger's military assistant, he rose through the ranks and as Lieutenant General was made national security adviser under Ronald Reagan, after the Iran-Contra scandal.\par \par \uc2 Oversaw the invasion of Panama and removal of corrupt dictator and drug lord Manuel Noriega as Secretary of State under George W. Bush despite deep skepticism about the Iraq invasion. He presented the purported intelligence suggesting Saddam Hussein had WMD to the United Nations, which he later said he regretted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN, DEFENSE SECRETARY: We lost one of the greatest leaders that we have ever witnessed. I feel as if I have a hole in my heart just this learning of this. And who will be, quite frankly, it is not possible to replace a Colin Powell. We will miss him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Colin Powell passed away this morning at Walter Reed, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jennifer, thank you very much reporting from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove served with Colin Powell in the Bush administration as the president's senior advisor. He is also Fox News contributor of course.\par \par \uc2 Karl, good to have you with us. You and General Powell worked together there from 2001 to 2005, I believe was the timeframe. Your thoughts on this really surprising news that we all saw across the wire this morning?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's just sad day. This was a remarkable human being who led a great life. And his memoir is entitled an American Journey. And it was an American journey born to immigrants, first generation American, sort of wasn't finding his way in college, was an engineering major, dropped that for geology and then ended up finding his home, so to speak by joining the ROTC.\par \par \uc2 And rose to a combat veteran of Vietnam, White House fellow national security adviser to President Ronald Reagan, named by George H.W. Bush as the youngest Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, and then became the first African American Secretary of State in 2001 under George W. Bush. (INAUDIBLE) making him until Barack Obama's election in 2008, the highest ranking African American in our government ever.\par \par \uc2 And he had great integrity. He loved this country deeply. He loved the American military. He was a brilliant man who brought all that he could to the service of our country.\par \par \uc2 I think it's an interesting point in his memoir, he wrote, I have led a great life. And he had and he has.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He certainly had and you recount his rise very, very well, Karl. I know you guys had a personal relationship as well.\par \par \uc2 He thought, you know, you're the political guy at the White House. And at times, you had sort of different approaches to things. Give us a little sense of your personal relationship.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, I could know when he was irritated with me and look, I very rarely got into his -- into his lane. But occasionally, I had to sign off on appointments and he once recommended somebody who while they might have been suited from the -- from their background, had nonetheless denounced the president of the United States as being an idiot after the election, just simply before the election. And so, I raised objections to it.\par \par \uc2 And I could tell when General Powell -- Secretary Powell was irritated with me, because when he would come into the Oval Office receiving in the hallways of the West Wing, he'd barked at me, drop and give me 20, soldier.\par \par \uc2 So, one time we're in this discussion about this potential nominee, and he knew I was blocking it. So, I happen to be coming out of the Oval Office and he happened to be coming in. The president had something else he needed to do for a couple of minutes so I was supposed to keep him company. And he barked at me as soon as he saw me, drop and give me 20.\par \par \uc2 Well, I was just irritated enough that I dropped and gave him 20. And as luck would have it, you know, he was not -- he was hoping I was not going to be able to do it. So, he literally got down on his knees and put his fist on underneath my chest, so I had to bump off his fist. And he was just hoping I wouldn't do it.\par \par \uc2 And as luck would have it, the White House photographer came cruising in the office at that moment. So, I have the photograph of me finishing my 20 pushups at the direction of Secretary Powell. That was the last time he ever said to me drop and give me 20, soldier.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're not able to get it up on screen, unfortunately. But I've seen the picture and I hope everybody will get a chance to see it. It's a great photo.\par \par \uc2 And yes, he's down there on the ground underneath your chin, making sure that you -- that you're doing those -- very impressive, Karl, 20 push-ups in the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 You know, I do want to play this because I've read in some of General Powell's writings, he said that he believes that it would be a prominent paragraph in his obituary what happened with his U.N. testimony.\par \par \uc2 And he spoke openly about regretting that testimony on WMD later in his life. Here's just a little bit of that at the United Nations back in '03.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 POWELL: Every statement I made today is backed up by sources, solid sources. These are not assertions, what we're giving you are facts and conclusions based on solid intelligence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What are your thoughts on all of that, Karl, today?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, look, it's natural to have a regret about it. This is what we knew at the time. What he knew at the time, what the world community knew at the time, and part of the reason that we felt that he had -- that Saddam Hussein had a WMD was because he wanted us to know that. He thought that the image of him having WMD kept him safe at home, that it discouraged his enemies in the region from attacking him, that it kept us out of his -- out of him.\par \par \uc2 And remember, 14 resolutions of the United Nations had been passed, demanding that he live up to the terms of the surrender agreement and make accounting for all that material. And he thumbed his nose at the world community. So, that's what we knew.\par \par \uc2 But in retrospect, it turned out not to be true. Now granted, he was waiting for the moment that he thought the West would lose interest. He was taking money out of the oil for food program. He was diverting net to keep together the network of experts and scientists and infrastructure to really un-constitute these programs at the earliest available moment.\par \par \uc2 But if you look -- if we'd known that it was not accurate, we would have found other ways to constrain him. And I think the Secretary felt very particularly coming from the military background that he did, he felt very strongly about that.\par \par \uc2 And look, I understand the sense of regret, because if we -- if we known what the real truth was, we wouldn't have taken the actions we did, we would have found other ways to constrain him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't know if you remember your last interaction with Colin Powell, he pulled away from your party over the last several years, voted for -- voted against John McCain, voted against President Trump. Your thoughts on that part of his story? He said he didn't blend on either party in a not so long-ago interview.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: You know, he had become disenchanted with the drift of the Republican Party. But you know what, you could still have cordial relationships with him.\par \par \uc2 I saw him at a -- at a lunch at Vernon Jordan's house in Washington, D.C., had a wonderful visit with him, by accident ran into him over in all places at the airport in Jamaica, I was giving a speech and he was coming back from I think a short vacation.\par \par \uc2 And this was a man who always had a smile on his face when he greeted a friend and who -- you could have disagreements with him but he didn't take it personally and that was the way he was. He came out of the military. He came from little (PH).\par \par \uc2 He talks about in his memoir very movingly about having grown up in relative modesty. He had a gang -- he had a gang. His gang was two blacks, two Lithuanians and one Puerto Rican and those were the -- those were the young kids that he ran around the Bronx with.\par \par \uc2 And he lived essentially the American dream, and how can you not be optimistic and upbeat if you've come from the relative poverty of his youth and go -- and go in to the highest levels of our government and service to our country.\par \par \uc2 He delighted in the fact that he was a soldier. And he delighted in the fact that he came not from West Point, but then he came through the ROTC unit at the City College of New York and rose to the highest rank in the United States military. That for him was an expression of what America was about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely, and in the post-Vietnam period when he rose to leadership, he really, I think elevated a new respect for the military that had disintegrated dramatically in this country.\par \par \uc2 And I heard someone say earlier today that whenever he had the opportunity in his leadership positions, he always made sure that people got to speak to the members of the military directly, so that they could see for themselves what incredible young men and women they were and are. And I think that is, you know, such a huge point to his credit as well, that he was humanized the military, and elevated them in American -- in the new American mindset in a way that really took a beating after Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 Karl, thank you very much. It's great to have you with us today. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Thank you, Martha. Well said.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 So, the passing of this General and this American icon, as we have been seeing, he was fully vaccinated as -- and died of complications of COVID.\par \par \uc2 So, it raises questions about that in terms of breakthrough cases. We're going to talk to the former CDC Director \uc2 Robert Redfield\uc2  about that, and other things, the origins of COVID which still continue to elude and evade and are extremely important to stay on. So, we'll talk to him about all of that.\par \par \uc2 And breaking right now, a flight evacuating Americans out of Kabul has now landed in Qatar. Fox News was on that plane. We're going to take you inside their story because we're not leaving those people behind.\par \par \uc2 And as they leave, we're going to bring their story to you. Two exclusives coming up that you don't want to miss. I'll be right back after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This story developing right now, we continue to stay on Americans who are getting out of Afghanistan. This latest group on a flight out of Kabul, Qatar reports that it was the largest Qatari passenger flight from Afghanistan since U.S. troops left at the end of August.\par \par \uc2 So, a significant moment in the promise to not leave them behind. Foreign Correspondent Trey Yingst was on the plane and he joins us now live from Doha, Qatar. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Martha, good afternoon. 365 people were evacuated from Afghanistan and brought here to Doha. Today, we were on that flight and on the runway in Kabul talking with these civilians, not only Afghan citizens, but other foreign nationals, including Americans.\par \par \uc2 We talk with one man who lives in Virginia, he holds a U.S. passport, and another man who has a Green Card holder, a legal permanent resident who lives with his young family in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 There was a shared sense of relief among this population of people that was able to make it out of \uc2 Taliban\uc2  controlled territory. They understand the security situation on the ground in Afghanistan is quickly deteriorating with groups like ISIS operating more freely, and they also know other people are being left behind.\par \par \uc2 Here's what it looked like on the tarmac earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): It's the 18th of October and we are back in \uc2 Taliban\uc2  controlled Afghanistan where evacuation flights are continuing. This story is not over, there are thousands of American allies still stuck in the country, along with U.S. citizens and Green Card holders, their safety and security is a major concern for the international community.\par \par \uc2 ATLLAH NOUREY, GREEN CARD HOLDER: (INAUDIBLE) excited, and we are feeling great to return back to our home, and our business and work, also for the kids school.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): You can really hear the sense of relief in people's voices at the airport as they got on that plane and made it out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Now, we also talked with a \uc2 Taliban\uc2  official, the acting Foreign Minister for the Taliban who downplayed reports of people being trapped inside the country. We continue to hear these open-ended and empty promises by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  that they will allow people to exit freely, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey Yingst on the ground for us in Afghanistan today and now in Doha as we continue to cover the promise that all of the people who were left behind would eventually be taken out of Afghanistan. Trey Yingst, thank you very much for that reporting. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So, coming up right here, General Jack Keane, and also former CDC Director \uc2 Robert Redfield\uc2  as the Biden administration faces new provocations from China, we still have unanswered questions about the origins of COVID. What happened in the lab and what might be coming next. All of that ahead of high stakes meetings between the two leaders of the United States and China, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: China has reportedly tested a new weapon that basically could get around the missile defense systems that we have spent decades building. This is a huge cutting-edge development in military science. It's a hypersonic missile, it flies low, relatively low, it flies five times the speed of sound, and it can essentially be steered changing directions to evade the efforts to stop it.\par \par \uc2 And this is -- this around the global test -- around the globe test I should say, apparently took the United States intelligence according to reporting by surprise that China have the capability to do this.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Senior Strategic Analyst General Jack Keane reacts to this in a moment, but first, the background with Correspondent David Spunt. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. We know that Secretary Lloyd Austin was in the country of Georgia just a few hours ago and he was asked specifically about this reporting. He declined to get into any details about the reporting.\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials are declining to talk about it. They're actually denying most of the report and that just gives you an idea of where things are right now on a global front when it comes to this specific report from the Financial Times.\par \par \uc2 It came out late Saturday, and it talks about what allegedly happened in August. According to the Times, China tested a nuclear capable hypersonic missile by launching it into low orbit space.\par \par \uc2 The Times reports that multiple U.S. Intelligence sources were just completely caught off guard.\par \par \uc2 Hypersonic missiles different than traditional ballistic missiles and the fact they can be precisely controlled even when fired into space. It makes them much more difficult to detect.\par \par \uc2 White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki was asked about this just a couple of hours ago, listen.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We've made clear our concerned about the military capabilities that the PRC continues to pursue. And we have been consistent in our approach with China. We welcome stiff competition but do we not -- we don't -- do not want that competition to veer into conflict.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): According to the Financial Times, this hypersonic missile missed its target by about two dozen miles. That sounds far but according to experts, it's not far at all, Martha, it's actually pretty on point. And as technology develops and time goes on, things will get even more precise when it comes to these hypersonic missiles, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed, David Spunt at the White House. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Gen. Jack Keane, Fox News Strategic -- Senior Strategic Analyst, former Chief -- Vice Chief of Staff for the army and chairman of the Institute for the Study of War.\par \par \uc2 General, good to have you here today. I do want to ask you about Colin Powell's passing of course, but I want to start with this hypersonic technology, which we've reported on here before and it's something the United States and Russia and China are all working on. How much of a game changer is this technology in terms of warfare?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Well, hypersonic missiles so is advanced technology, that there is no system out there that can defeat it as of yet. And what makes it so dangerous is not just the speed because we have ballistic missiles that actually travel faster, but speed is a factor.\par \par \uc2 But it's the fact that ballistic missiles travel on a predicted path and a controlled path. Hypersonic missile can zigzag all over the place, change altitudes, and are very dangerous, even at the conventional level.\par \par \uc2 Because the Chinese aren't advanced of this compared to where we are, which is very frustrating. Chinese right now can reach our carrier battle groups in the Pacific region and take them down in mass, given the lethality these weapon systems and the fact that we really don't have a defense against them to include debate -- our basis, and that is why a number of us have been issuing sort of a clarion call here, wake up, pay attention to the erosion of our military capability that happened because of the 9/11 wars. And we're trying to catch up as quickly as we can but we're not there.\par \par \uc2 And China clearly has military advantages over the United States that we must take seriously, Martha and now they're introducing nuclearized hypersonic weapons if the Financial Times article is accurate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, I can't -- you can't underestimate the importance of this headline, this story, and it feels like we're focused on so many things in this country. But we don't seem to hear -- I'm listening to the White House talk about this this afternoon. And Jen Psaki saying, well, we've made clear our concerns with the Chinese government that we don't want anything that veers into a conflict situation. I mean, what do you think about their posture on this?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, this kind of nice words that we use to describe China, which I think in their minds, they are in a cold war situation, they won't use that. They clearly want to dominate the Pacific region and actually replace the United States as a world leader. How do we know that? Because they've said it time and time again.\par \par \uc2 And we ca -- we keep trying to find nice rhetoric so we don't aggravate the Chinese. But the fact of the matter is, is we are behind, and we have to catch up.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's some good things the administration is doing, strengthening our alliances out there, that is very good what we're doing. And we are attempting to up gun our military situation, but we have to do it with more of a sense of urgency, and we need more funding to be able to do this.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the priorities that China has in the upcoming budget, they're not anywhere near what they should be, given the seriousness of the situation that we're facing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's such an important message at a time but a quick thought before -- on General Powell, Sir, before let you go.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, he's a towering figure in American life and history. I think he's an American icon respected all around the world and all of us had such high regard for him and this -- he oozed leadership, he was -- he was gifted natural leader. He had an unflappable temperament I think what should endeared him to many people.\par \par \uc2 And if Colin Powell was in the room, he had this commanding presence that you knew he was there, not just because of his rank, but that was something he had all of his life. He started in ROTC. Out of humble beginnings, I can identify with that myself. And you found something that he had an aptitude for, and he loved and cherished it.\par \par \uc2 And he excelled at it, and how we have reaped the benefit of Colin Powell's life and his contribution to the United States, his patriotism, his competency example he set for others, and certainly the breakthrough he's made in this country as an African American, oh my, I will miss him significantly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. As you say, you both held from a great part of New York in the Bronx, two guys who really did well in life and serve the country. Thank you so much, general. It's always good to see you, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. Great talking to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You too. So will President Biden press China's leader on COVID origins when they meet in the coming weeks? And the other frightening thing that you heard here on this show, what could be next from these labs in China? Former CDC Director \uc2 Dr. Robert Redfield\uc2  weighs in exclusively on the story, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There are extremely rare cases of deaths or hospitalizations among fully-vaccinated individuals. That has been the case even before the death of Colin Powell's. And unvaccinated person has more than 10 times greater risk of dying from COVID-19 compared to a fully-vaccinated person.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Just short time ago at the White House, they answered questions about vaccine efficacy following the death of fully vaccinated former Secretary of State Colin Powell who died from complications of COVID-19. He was 84 years old and his spokesman has confirmed that his history included multiple treatments for multiple myeloma, I should say, a cancer of the blood cells.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Robert Redfield\uc2  was the director of the CDC at the beginning of the pandemic and is a well-known world renowned virologist. Dr. Redfield, good to have you with us today. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REDFIELD\uc2 , FORMER DIRECTOR, CDC: Thank you, Martha. Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So your take on General Colin Powell, obviously, very sad development today, the loss of this American hero. Also the circumstances and what you think of them given where we are now with this virus.\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Well, Martha, first to his family, a great, great, great American had the privilege to serve with him when I was in the \uc2 US Army\uc2 . It's really important, obviously, this issue of maintaining immunity. I think we've not seen the urgency that we need to see in particularly the vulnerable to get them re-immunized. I hear a lot of times people feel it's a rare event that fully vaccinated people may die. I happen to be the senior advisor to Governor Hogan in the state of Maryland in the last six to eight weeks. More than 40% of the people that died in Maryland were fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So we really do need to get vulnerable people, currently those over 65 and those 16 to 65 with comorbidities to get boosted, hopefully the FDA and CDC will act urgently to expand that to the Moderna vaccines and, of course, the J&J vaccines. But very, very sad, we've lost a lot of individuals in this period where their immunity has waned.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: And in my view, it was really unnecessary. We could have been more aggressive in getting our recommendations to get booster vaccine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's an important reminder to everybody who's in that category to do just that. The last time you were here, doctor, you talked about the Delta variant, which seems to be on the decline, at least at this moment in history in the United States. But you said that you were worried that there could be something even more lethal coming. And I spoke with Dr. Quay, Dr. Steven Quay, and he talked about a separate virus that he's very concerned about, that they have been using and working on in the Wuhan lab. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVEN QUAY, FOUNDER, ATOSSA THERAPEUTICS: What I found there was that the lab was contaminated with NEPA viral research. That's about two years ahead of SARS-CoV-2. Now, NEPA virus is 80% lethal. So this is not, you know, if you didn't like the pandemic that was just 1% lethal, this thing they're working on in December 2019 has an 80% lethality. It needs to be stopped.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we still don't have any answers, definitive answers on whether or not this started in the lab. I know you believe it did. You have said that Dr. Quay's, unequivocally, he believes that it did start in that lab. What do you know about what else they're working on there? And what are you concerned about going forward?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Well, Martha, I don't have any insight information on their current activities. But I do feel and I have written in the Wall Street Journal several weeks back that I do think we should have a moratorium on gain of function research. In the old days, we only worried about natural evolution. That's how these new pandemics emerge. But we're in a new year now, you know, since 2012, with the research that was published on modifying bird flu to make it infectious for man, I advocated that that research not be published in the public domain, but it was.\par \par \uc2 And so now, we have a whole other scenario now that new pathogens for man can actually emerge from the laboratory. I think with COVID-19, I've always argued that it's just more I think scientifically supported that it emerged from the lab. If you look at SARS, in 2003-2004, it went from bat to a civet cat, to humans but it never learned how to go human to human. And there's been less than 10,000 cases today 18 years later.\par \par \uc2 Same with MERS, it went from a bat to a camel, to humans but never learned how to go to human to human. This virus immediately understood how to go human to human. And I think as a consequence, we're at risk for variant after variant, after variant, after variant because this virus has learned how to be an efficient replicator and transmitter among humans, where SARS and MERS, we really haven't seen a lot of areas.\par \par \uc2 So I do think this research where people are trying to make pathogens that are more pathogenic for humans. Under the argument that they think they need to do that to be prepared to defend ourselves against these pathogens with developing countermeasures, I think it's flawed. And I think we really need to pull back from this type of research right now. And there can be a much broader public debate.\par \par \uc2 I did mention that in my time as CDC director, I had to close down Fort Detrick, because we did regular inspections and we found that some of the containment procedures were not being followed by the book --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean --\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: -- rather than just let it go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a really an extraordinarily important point. And, you know, you look at the research that's going on around the world, it's hard to understand why people aren't talking about this, why this isn't top of mind between the president of United States and in this conversation that he's going to have with President Xi in China. There should be a global moratorium, according to what you're saying, on all of this research and accountability in terms of shutting it down if we want to protect the population.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Redfield, I hope you'll come back. Thank you very much. Good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Thank you, Martha. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You too. So, after two months of paternity leave, Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg says that the supply chain disaster is actually an outgrowth of President Biden's economic success. Sandra Smith on all of that when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Look, part of what's happening isn't just the supply side, it's the demand side. Demand is off the charts. Retail sales are through the roof.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The supply chain crisis that has ships and cargo containers backed up at US ports could continue into next year, we're now told. that's the word from the Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg. He also says that the crisis was a result really of President Biden's success because retail sales are through the roof.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: If you think about those images of ships, for example, waiting and anchor on the West Coast, every one of those ships is full of record amounts of goods that Americans are buying, because demand is up, because income is up, but because the President has successfully guided this economy out of the teeth of a terrifying recession.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's bring in Sandra Smith, Co-Anchor of "America Reports