ng with them.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" tonight. John -- Jonathan Hunt at the Port of Long Beach, California, with the return of some buying limits at some stores due to a supply chain crisis.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie at Capitol -- at the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  where Congress is now officially raising the debt limit, just kicking the can down the road to December.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with White House correspondent Peter Doocy, starting us off the North Lawn with what the president saying about the economy tonight. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The Biden plan for the government to try to spend its way out of a jobs crisis might not be working, but officials here are spinning this report as good news because jobs were added and not lost.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Jobs up, wages up, unemployment down. That's progress.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): But a miss. 194,000 jobs added short of the 500,000 forecasts.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'd like to see it faster, and we're going to make it faster.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president still sees a silver lining.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: For the first time since March of 2020, the American unemployment rate is below five percent.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That's due to 183,000 people dropping out of the labor force.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2 , UNITED STATES LABOR SECRETARY: I think that a lot of people are thinking about their work-life balance.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There could be another reason.\par \par \uc2 GROVER NORQUIST\uc2 , FOUNDER, AMERICAN FOR TAX REFORM: Biden's telling people, I'm going to give you several $1,000 or several $1,000 a month, and we're going to give you all these benefits and nobody asked you to work or even look for work. How much do you look for work?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The White House argues the Biden plan is working.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We certainly don't see things as darkly as you do.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Some Republican lawmakers say they have reason to be concerned.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Small businesses, even some of the large corporations that I'm talking to out there, everyone is afraid of the hammer that is going to drop from Washington, D.C. in raising their taxes, and they're afraid to make investments.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden is asking people not to believe what they see on T.V.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Turn on the news and every conversation is a confrontation. Every disagreement is a crisis. But when you take a step back and look at what's happening, we're actually making real progress.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: COVID is still a hurdle for many returning to work, as the president claims he's trying to get a first-hand look at overcrowded hospitals.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As a good friend had called and he had rushed his significant other to the emergency room. Things were so backed up, I call the desk receiving nurse and ask what the situation was.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He never finished the story. He's also never talked about doing anything like this.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): How often does President Biden call around trying to help his friends cut the line?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: That certainly was not his intention. He was not trying to do that. He was checking in on a friend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: No more details about that available and also no plan to change course. Officials here believe the best way to help the economy recover is for Congress to spend a few trillion more taxpayer dollars and for the federal government to more strictly enforce COVID vaccine requirements so that, in many places, if you want to get a job, you first have to get a jab. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter, how's the White House responded to a request by President Trump to shield certain documents relating to the January 6th episode?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Officials here are saying, no. They're not going to do it. This January 6th, committee up on Capitol Hill requested about 50 documents President Trump and his lawyers claimed that they were subject to executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 But officials here looked at it and they said no, those documents are not subject to executive privilege anymore. You're not the president, so this is likely going to wind up in court soon. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, we'll see where those hearings go on the Capitol Hill riot, January 6th. Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is issuing the first-ever presidential proclamation of Indigenous Peoples Day. It will be observed Monday along with Columbus Day, which is established by Congress.\par \par \uc2 The president also wrote an acknowledgment of what he called the painful history of wrongs and atrocities that many European explorers inflicted on tribal nations and indigenous communities.\par \par \uc2 The uncertainty over the debt ceiling is over in Washington, at least, for a few weeks. But many congressional Republicans say their leaders caved in to the Democrats in allowing a temporary extension until early December.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie reports tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Senate Republicans finally realized that their obstruction was not going to work.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In a shocking move, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  trashes Republicans on the Senate floor as they help him avert a debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The motion is agreed to.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Despite the chastising, he could not have done it without Republicans. Even Democratic Senator Joe Manchin couldn't hold back his horror holding his head in his hands and finally walking out in protest.\par \par \uc2 The drama unfolded as a Senate voted to raise the nation's credit limit by nearly half a trillion dollars to keep the country from defaulting just until December. Schumer struck a deal with Minority Leader Mitch McConnell to avoid the tricky process of reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 In the end, 11 Republicans joined Democrats to pass the stopgap. Including Senator Lisa Murkowski, who told Fox Republicans were angry with Schumer.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LISA MURKOWSKI (R-AK): There is a -- there was a fair amount of frustration, yes.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Drama aside the temporary fix has major political ramifications. Many Republicans following former President Trump's lead see this as caving to Democrats when they had all the bargaining power.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I don't understand what we're folding here at the end. This is a complete capitulation. I'm upset with us because we had a strategy to make them pay a price to raise the debt ceiling, and we blinked.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Still, while the debt crisis has been averted, for now, Democrats are simply kicking the can down the road to December 3rd, when they'll have to also figure out how to keep the government-funded. The move, prolonging their chaos on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, the House reconvenes on Tuesday to take up this short-term solution. But again, we are right back to square one on December 3rd, and it does not appear that Republicans will have forgotten Senator Schumer's words quite by then. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, just look at Senator Manchin's face behind Senator Schumer's speech. We'll talk about that later. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The days of struggling to find toilet paper and bleach wipes like we experienced at the start of the pandemic may actually be returning. A supply chain crisis is forcing some stores to set limits on how much you can buy. White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  says the administration has formed a task force to look into this situation. That crisis begins at the nation's ports.\par \par \uc2 Chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt shows us tonight from the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2  in California.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Almost half of all cargo coming into the United States via sea arrives at the twin ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach.\par \par \uc2 But right now, billions of dollars worth of that cargo, be it electronic goods, sneakers, or canned food, are floating outside the port aboard some 60 vast container ships being forced to wait an unprecedented 10 days to dock.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GENE SEROKA\uc2 , EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PORT OF LOS ANGELES: It's like bringing 10 lanes of freeway traffic into five right here at the port.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Supply and demand is not the problem. Americans are buying things in record numbers China and other Asian nations are making things in record numbers.\par \par \uc2 SEROKA: It's more cargo than we've ever seen in our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: But there simply aren't enough truck drivers to get goods to American store shelves. And the domino effect means the cargo containers can't offload. So, they sit in dock and the backups outside the port get longer.\par \par \uc2 SEROKA: Folks have to rise to the occasion, flex capacity, and their workforces to move cargo out.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The cargo crisis is turning Halloween shopping into a horror show for 10s of millions of Americans. Some stores are reintroducing pandemic-era buying limits and holiday gift shopping will be impacted too.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: DESMOND, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, EXTERNAL AFFAIRS, THE TOY ASSOCIATION: Our message right now is get out, and if you see something that your child has on his or her list, pick it up now to make sure you'll have it for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Without offering specifics, the White House said today, President Biden is doing everything he can to tackle the crisis.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: There are a range of issues we're working to -- the point is: We're working to address them on several paths and on several fronts, and I can't make a prediction of when it will be concluded. It is just a top priority of the president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): And just to give you an idea of the scale here, each modern cargo ship carries from 10 to 24,000 of those huge containers. That's enough on average to hold 120 million pairs of sneakers, 250 million iPads, or 700 million cans of beans, multiply that by 60 for all the ships waiting offshore currently, and that's how much cargo is just floating out there. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's a lot of beans. Jonathan Hunt in Los Angeles, \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 . Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were down. The Dow lost nine today. The S&P 500 fell eight. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  dropped 74. For the week, the Dow gained 1-1/4 percentage points. The S&P 500 was up three quarters. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  finished ahead one-tenth.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  nuclear-powered submarine damaged in a collision with an unknown underwater object in the South China Sea last weekend has arrived in a port at Guam.\par \par \uc2 The Navy is not talking about the circumstances of that collision, or the extent of the damage, the USS Connecticut. About 11 crew members sustained injuries described as ranging from minor to moderate. \uc2 China's foreign ministry\uc2  is expressing what it termed serious concern about that incident.\par \par \uc2 BREAKING TONIGHT, at least 46 people are dead in northern Afghanistan from a suicide bombing at a Shiite Muslim mosque. The region's ISIS affiliate is claiming responsibility, while the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  struggles to control the country.\par \par \uc2 Many Afghans who cooperated with the U.S. during the 20-year war fear for their lives. As President Biden's promise of safe passage remains for them unfilled. National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin is at the Pentagon tonight in a warning there are some images in this piece that are going to be graphic.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): For former Green Berets like Matt Coburn who served five tours in Afghanistan, the war is not over. He and other veterans are still working tirelessly through private groups to rescue their translators, and elite commandos like the one we spoke to at Fort Dix, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Others were left behind and are now being hunted and tortured by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . Images are sent to them almost daily via WhatsApp, showing the torture endured by a female Afghan soldier trained by the U.S.\par \par \uc2 This soldier in Kunduz province was executed. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  were looking for his commando brother.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Secretary of State Blinken is leading the continued diplomatic efforts to ensure a safe passage for any American, Afghan partner or foreign national who wants to leave Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Those efforts are slow going. Thousands of Afghan interpreters and commandos are hiding and waiting. They think the Biden administration is coming to save them.\par \par \uc2 Afghan commandos kept fighting the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  until the very end, even serving to shepherd Americans to the Kabul airport helping them escape. They didn't have time to apply for SIV visas to save themselves.\par \par \uc2 The state department says it has evacuated 105 American citizens since August 31st. But says it is difficult verifying manifests for private charters.\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE\uc2 , SPOKESPERSON, UNITED STATES DEPARTMENT OF STATE: Despite the -- our best vetting and vetting to the best of our ability, the manifests were not accurate.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: A consortium of 25 veteran nonprofits came together to form Welcome U.S. to show their support for resettling Afghans in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 NICK PALMISCIANO, VICE PRESIDENT, SAVE OUR ALLIES: What I found is that the Afghans that are in our country are so grateful to be here. They want to race to become productive members of society here.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Former Army Ranger Nick Palmisciano is part of Welcome U.S. and recently visited fort McCoy where 12,000 Afghans are still waiting for resettlement.\par \par \uc2 PALMISCIANO: This coalition is the veteran community saying this is the thing that we can affect regardless of anything else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: A recent A.P. poll found that 72 percent of Americans say they favor the -- favor settling Afghans in the United States. But many of these veterans groups say that if the state department and the Biden administration doesn't move quickly, it may be too late. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jen, that's happening every day, right? I mean, these, these operations.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: These operations are ongoing. There are many, many groups that are involved. And there are many, many people still in danger. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Unbelievable. Jennifer Griffin, her reporting from the Pentagon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, taking on the cartels that smuggle hundreds of illegal immigrants across the southern border. Our Fox News team goes on patrol with Texas National Guard troops.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 10 in Phoenix with the public funeral for a federal agent killed in Arizona. Michael Garbo was fatally shot while questioning a passenger on an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train. A second DEA agent and a Tucson officer also were injured.\par \par \uc2 Fox 7 in Austin, Texas where the city expects a new arrival soon. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says Tesla is moving its headquarters to the state capitol. The move comes after a previous threats to leave California due to COVID restrictions that forced one plant to close.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at San Francisco from our affiliate, Fox 2. One of the big stories there tonight. The Blue Angels soar through the Bay Area skies as part of Fleet Week. Air shows, ship tours, and music continue throughout the weekend to cap-off the tradition, honoring the Armed Forces.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Los Angeles County sheriff says he will not enforce the county's vaccine mandate in his agency. Alex Villanueva made the statement during a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  live event Thursday. The county ordered all employees to be fully vaccinated by October 1st.\par \par \uc2 The Attorney for the family of Gabby Petito says its priority is bringing the woman's body back to the state of New York. Petito's body is still in Wyoming, where it was discovered last month.\par \par \uc2 The manhunt continues for her former boyfriend, Brian Laundrie. The attorney for his family said, today, there were no further plans for his father and mother to join law enforcement in searching nearby Florida parks. North Port police say reports of a recent campsite found this week in the reserve were not true.\par \par \uc2 They add they found no phones in Gabby Petito's van, which Laundrie drove to Florida without her.\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors will not file charges against a white police officer who shot a black man in Wisconsin last year. Officer Rusten Sheskey shot James Blake during a domestic disturbance in Kenosha in August of 2020.\par \par \uc2 The shooting left Blake paralyzed from the waist down, it sparked several nights of protests. The Justice Department says there's not enough evidence to prove Sheskey used excessive force or violated Blake's federal rights.\par \par \uc2 State prosecutors also declined to file charges after video showed Blake was armed with a knife.\par \par \uc2 We have another Fox News exclusive tonight from the southern border. Our team went out with Texas National Guard troops as they try to battle cartel smugglers who transport hundreds of migrants across the Rio Grande.\par \par \uc2 Cartels, by the way, authorities are saying or they are getting increasingly brazen. Correspondent Bill Melugin reports from Roma, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's too easy to get across right there.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It's Thursday evening in Roma, Texas. And we're embedded with the Texas National Guard in one of the most active cartel hotspots all along the border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And when you're looking with thermals over there, look like Christmas. I mean, it's all lit up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: While the sun was still up, we began seeing smugglers bringing illegal immigrants across the Rio Grande on rafts.\par \par \uc2 Some were turned back by National Guard soldiers at the shoreline. As night fell, the smugglers and the rafts started coming nonstop, over and over again. Full of illegal immigrants, with more than 100 others waiting on the Mexican shoreline.\par \par \uc2 The smugglers in a playful mood, with no fear, telling us they are raking in the money.\par \par \uc2 They unloaded hundreds of migrants, mostly family units from Nicaragua. One man telling us he paid $12,000 to get his family across. It's a scene that plays out in Roma every single night.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And directly across from us here in Roma is the Mexican city of Ciudad Miguel Aleman. It is an absolute hotbed of cartel activity, with frequent gun battles between the Gulf Cartel and the North East cartel, always fighting for control of this city in its prime location for both drug smuggling as well as human smuggling.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): National Guard soldiers gave us this video showing smoke billowing from the city two days ago after they heard gunfire and explosions from a cartel gunfight.\par \par \uc2 Last night in Roma, we witnessed tracer rounds from a suspected cartel machine gun being fired from Mexico into the United States. The soldiers telling us the rounds went over one of their observation posts. The circumstances behind the gunfire are unknown.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And today, U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , DHS Secretary Alejandro, Mayorkas and Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , all traveled to Mexico, where they held some high level security talks with the Mexican governments.\par \par \uc2 Both sides agreed to try to start repairing relations and also agreed to work on implementing a new security agreement. Noticeably, absent from those meetings though, was border czar \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin along the border in Roma, Texas. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the ethical questions surrounding the big money sales of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's art work.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. Finland suspends the use of the Moderna COVID vaccine -- COVID-19 vaccine in males under 30. They will be given the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine instead.\par \par \uc2 Health officials there cite the increased risk of heart inflammation in young men and boys. The rare side effect has prompted similar restrictions in Sweden, Norway, and Denmark.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  and the U.S. today said the benefits of the Moderna vaccine outweigh the risks.\par \par \uc2 They are twins, they are cute, and they're sporting new names. Meat 3-1/2- month-old Xiao Xiao and Lei Lei. They are panda cubs in a Tokyo zoo. The names mean the light of dawn turning brighter, and a bud blossoming to a beautiful flower. Welcome to the world. We love pandas here.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are growing concerns tonight about the ethical ramifications of the sales of artwork produced by President Biden's son, Hunter. There's a report several the works have gone for huge sums of money.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins has that story tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It's good to be an artist if your last name is Biden. The New York Post reports the president's son sold five art prints at $75,000 each to unidentified buyers to the Georges Burgess gallery before Hunter's first art exhibit opened at Milk Studios in Los Angeles, where the artist entertains 200 celebrity guests and prospective buyers, including L.A.'s Mayor Eric Garcetti, who was nominated to be U.S. ambassador to India.\par \par \uc2 The White House ethics chief for former President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  says this crosses the line.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD PAINTER, FORMER CHIEF, WHITE HOUSE ETHICS: This is an ethics problem for the president. You cannot have the impression that the executive branch will be doing favors for anyone who does business with the president's family.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: It's prompted questions at the White House this week that Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  has largely dodged.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: That is run by the gallerist and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's representatives that the White House provided suggestions for. I'd refer you to the gallerist for questions about the event and as well as the representatives of Mr. Garcetti sin terms of his attendance.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And it comes after the top Republican on the House Oversight Committee launched an investigation last month into Hunter's art sales.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has a history of trying to sell influence to foreign nationals, influence to his family name. And if you look at what's going on in the art world now, unfortunately, the art world has become a place for massive money laundering.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And another show is in the works lawyers are reportedly busy vetting potential patrons for an upcoming New York City gallery show this coming spring.\par \par \uc2 We reached out to Hunter's attorney and have not heard back. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we'll follow it. Griff, thank you. Tonight, another look at what many people feel is a media double standard when it comes to coverage of President Biden versus the way President Trump was portrayed by reporters.\par \par \uc2 This evening, how President Biden's recent jabs at the media are being handled by the very people he's targeting.\par \par \uc2 Here is Fox News media analyst and host of Fox's "MEDIA BUZZ," Howard Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: President Biden intended it as a joke with the press.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm learning how to fly drones, guys, so be careful.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Now, that might have been seen as tasteless crack just weeks after a U.S. drone strike killed 10 innocent Afghan civilians, yet the press let it pass.\par \par \uc2 But the media often pounced on President Trump for his satiric lines.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Will we be president in 10 years? Only if we add a couple of terms.\par \par \uc2 You can't joke, because if you joke, they take it away.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Are the two presidents covered by different standards? While Biden hasn't called journalists idiots and fake news as his predecessor did, he has taken his share of swipes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The Indian press is much better behaved than the American press do.\par \par \uc2 You could not answer questions because they won't ask any questions on point.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: That one prompted protests to his press secretary.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I don't think it was meant to be a hard cut at the members of the media.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: But on some occasions, Biden has gone right at an aggressive reporter.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you don't understand that, you're in the wrong business.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Though in that case he apologized to CNN's Kaitlan Collins.\par \par \uc2 When Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , who has been at odds with the White House, was followed into the bathroom by liberal activists.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need solutions the Build Better Back plan. We have solutions that we need.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Biden seemed to brush off the harassment.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: I don't think they are appropriate tactics, but it happens to everybody. The only people it doesn't happen to are people who have Secret Service standing around them. So it's -- it's a part of the process.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: But almost no one in the press challenged that answer as they might have if a liberal lawmaker was the target. And when the president used a digital Rose Garden back drop to address business leaders.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So let's vote and end this mess today.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Reporters didn't call him a reality show president. That was the former guy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ (on camera): There have been periods when the press has been tough on this president, especially over Afghanistan and the Haitian migrant crisis. But much of the time he gets plenty of leeway. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, Howie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Please join me Sunday night for the premier of a special documentary. It's called "To Rescue the Republic, President Grant and a Nation in Crisis." It's based on my new book which comes out Tuesday. Here's an excerpt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Grant had dominated the national scene for more than a dozen years. Yet some of his contemporaries called the 1876 election his greatest contribution to the republic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Grant's adversary James Blaine said there was no greater evidence given of his command, even his time on the battlefield, than the measures that he took during that presidential electoral crisis to avoid the violence and the damage that would have been done by those who would have been happy to subvert the system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Our special premiers Sunday night 10:00 p.m. eastern here on FOX News channel. It will be available after that on FOX Nation. Again, the book comes out Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on disappointing job numbers and the president's struggles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: When you take a step back and look at what's happening, we are actually making real progress.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT, (R-FL): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has no concept, the Democrats have no concept of how an economy works.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today's report has the unemployment rate down 4.8 percent, a significant improvement from when I took office and a sign that our recovery is moving forward even in the face of a COVID pandemic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The smallest job gains in nearly a year. Gas prices are at a seven-year high. Inflation is up. The president is struggling to get the rest of his Build Back Better agenda passed through Congress. Do you see a need at this point to course correct?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I certainly don't see things as starkly as you do. We've created an average of $500,000 a month. We are at a faster rate of economic growth, a lower rate of unemployment than in quite some time. That's progress. That's moving exactly in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it's not exactly in the right direction, if you look at the estimates about the jobs numbers. If you take a look at the estimates for September jobs report, 194,000 jobs added, 500,000 was the estimate. Yes, the employment rate is down 4.8 percent. It was scheduled to be 5.1 percent, but a lot of people still out of the market, the jobs market. And there is concern from Republicans that this administration is doing exactly the wrong thing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT, (R-FL): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is killing this economy. And by the way, that vaccine mandate, what's it going to do? More people are going to get fired, unemployment will go up so people lose their job. And on top of that we will have fewer supplies, which means higher inflation. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has no concept, the Democrats have no concept of how an economy works.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about this? Let's bring in our panel, Bill McGurn, columnist for "The Wall Street Journal," \uc2 Charles Lane\uc2 , opinion writer for "The Washington Post," and "Washington Post" columnist Marc Thiessen.\par \par \uc2 Marc, your thoughts on this report and where the administration stands on the economy?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": Yes. Only a president who considers our Afghan withdrawal an extraordinary success could try and sell this jobs report as good news. The president said the trend is positive. No, it isn't. Just look at the objective numbers. In July the economy created over a million jobs. Then in August, it created 366,000 jobs. And then now it created 194,000 jobs, which is way below the 500,000 job expectation. He said the unemployment rate went down to 4.8 percent, first time it's been below five percent. That's because more than 180,000 people left the workforce last month.\par \par \uc2 So the idea that this is an extraordinary success is a fantasy, and a president who, once again, is making statements that are directly contrary to the lived reality of Americans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chuck, at the same time, a lot of Americans are feeling prices going up. If you look at the price of oil, for example, the U.S. crude oil price topped at $80 a barrel, the highest since 2014, oil benchmark jumping more than two percent today. Trading at $80.09. That's eventually going to be felt across the board, and people are feeling it in different places when it comes to inflation.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES LANE\uc2 , OPINION WRITER, "WASHINGTON POST": One of the things that would have been a bright spot in this report was that nominal wages continue to grow. But, of course, higher privacies undercut that.\par \par \uc2 I would just say in response, a couple of points to what Marc said. Yes, this jobs report underperformed. But there were a couple of things going on. One was the Delta variant causing a lot of schools to be disrupted. And government employment actually is the weak spot in this report, not the private sector, which created 350,000 jobs, give or take. And also hurricane Ida in the Gulf. So I think there were a couple of anomalies that I don't think can you really blame on President Biden for causing that.\par \par \uc2 I think longer term, Bret, the jobs problem here is kind of a labor shortage. There is now an all-time record, 1.5 job opening per unemployed worker. And we really have to get our arms around that as a country if we're really going to finally put the crisis behind us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's a massive problem. Just talking to small businesses, restaurants, all kinds of places around the country. They cannot, Bill, get people to work.\par \par \uc2 BILL MCGURN, COLUMNIST, "THE WALL STREET JOURNAL": Right. And look, you can't judge an economy on a single jobs report. But when you combine this with inflation, look, everyone can feel it. My wife goes to the grocery store, comes back with one bag of groceries, it's over 60 bucks. My daughter goes to the gas pump, everyone is feeling it. And what are we talking about? What is the big issue? Two bills of spending that are more than $4 trillion. And we're going to pretend that this is going to have no effect on jobs? No effect on inflation? I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in danger of heading for \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  territory with stagflation, a stagnant economy with higher inflation. And I think it's a real danger.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen, and you have to add in the choices when it comes to oil and gas and what has happened in the administration up until now on that front. And we can talk about all of that another day.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to focus on this. The speech that Chuck Schumer gave, Marc, after he gets this deal raise the debt ceiling to December, he punts the deal. If you look at the video, or the picture of Joe Manchin, Democrat from West Virginia, behind Schumer, really with his head in his hands, shaking his head. Eventually he just shakes his head, says something, gets up and walks away. There was a lot of pushback especially for the 11 Republicans who voted with Democrats who do this to get it to December. There you see Manchin walking away.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, I just don't understand the Democrats' strategy here. So they have two senators they need to get their agenda through. They need them for the reconciliation bill and for everything else. And Joe Manchin's number one priority has been restoring civility and bipartisan in Washington. And so the Republicans make a concession and allow them to increase the debt limit temporarily so they can get their ducks in order, and Chuck Schumer brings out the fire thrower, flame thrower. Joe Manchin looks at him and he walks away in disgust. They are pushing Joe Manchin away. It's the same thing with Sinema the other day when she was being followed into the bathroom and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says that's part of the process. They should be courting these people. They literally have their agenda, the fate of the Biden domestic agenda in their hands. They should be trying to do things they want, not offend them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And, Chuck, quickly, there's nothing to say that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  couldn't say to Joe Manchin, hey, listen, we don't think that was a good deal either. You would be welcomed in our party. He is not going to do it. He says he wants to be a Democrat the rest of his life. But Sinema, the same way. They're one vote away from it being Majority Leader Mitch McConnell.\par \par \uc2 LANE: Well, what's striking to me about this is the different strategies of Manchin management, you might call going on here. McConnell let it be known that he cut this deal on the debt ceiling to ease Joe Manchin's predicament over the filibuster, to save him from a tough problem there. And on the other hand, you have Chuck Schumer alienating him. It does seem that one is trying to catch -- believes that you catch more flies with honey than with vinegar, and Manchin, I think, was dramatic in the way he responded.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I agree. We'll see where this goes, and we'll follow it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday lightning round.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Any loss is an enormous tragedy, and our heart goes out to the families who lost loved ones.\par \par \uc2 YLVA JOHANSSON, EUROPEAN COMMISSIONER FOR HOME AFFAIRS: On this terrorist threat from Afghanistan, I must say that my assessment is that the alert level is not high enough.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ, (R-FL): We are on a road to another 9/11, unfortunately, with what Biden has done in Afghanistan, that we are far less safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A mosque attack in Afghanistan as there are still Americans and allies of America trying to get out of that country. We are back with the panel. Bill, it's off the front pages, but it is still a huge store, Jennifer Griffin digging into that effort that's continuing every day today.\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Yes, I congratulate Jennifer because I think the Biden administration, the cynical bet was that once reporters are out there and U.S. troops are out of there, no one would know what's going on in Afghanistan. And we see everyone could have anticipated this. This is unfolding exactly how you would expect the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to behave. And the most shameful thing is we still don't have from the administration a number of how many Americans they left behind, or even how many Americans they think they left behind, not to mention the thousands of Afghans who were by our side and loyal to us during the war.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Chuck, it is tough get those numbers. We were at about 100 Americans for a long time. And others have come out, and still they are estimate estimating 100. Well, obviously it was more than that at the beginning.\par \par \uc2 LANE: There's also a real problem with Afghans who worked with the United States or its affiliated organizations who have been having a terrible time getting documents that they need to leave the country, either by land or by air. That mosque attack by the Islamic State is a horrific event and shows, by the way, that the people who worked with us and who were left behind are just not under threat from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , who, by the way, are kind of acting with some restraint, but they also face the threat from the Islamic State.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Marc, I want to turn topics to this disparity between the coverage Biden gets and the coverage Trump got from a media that even when they are getting ripped on do things a lot differently.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: No, they do. Just imagine if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had had the series of self-inflicted wounds that the president -- this president has had from Afghanistan to the southern border. To the drone strike taking out innocent civilians, to having his COVID booster plan overturned by an \uc2 FDA\uc2  panel to being the first president in American history to go to Capitol Hill and ask his own party not to vote for his -- one of his top pieces of legislation. This is an administration that has had the longest string of self-inflicted wounds I have seen in my lifetime, and they don't get raked over the coals for it like the Trump administration did.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. All right, quick lightning round. Bill, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: The winner, I think, is Kyrsten Sinema who has made all her critics and people harassing her look very small, including \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who refused to really condemn these people following her into the bathroom, and Bernie Sanders who refused to sign a condemnation unless it included criticism of her position.\par \par \uc2 And I think the loser of the week, very unfairly, are these ordinary moms and dads trying to get accountability from their public schools. And the National School Board Association asked if they might be considered domestic terrorists, and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said, well, we will have the \uc2 FBI\uc2  take a look at them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, winner and loser, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 LANE: Well, my winner, the other guys on the panel may not like it, is \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  for getting her global tax, corporate minimum tax through. That is definitely a victory for her.\par \par \uc2 My losers are the two people who are on trial and convicted of bribery and other charges in the Varsity Blues case. That's Gamal Abdelaziz and John Wilson. They say they are going to appeal. But my gosh, that was an awful scandal, and there's still more trials to come.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Marc, I owe you a winner and loser, believe it or not. We are running out of time.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Don't worry.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm going to put it on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . How about that?\par \par \uc2 When we come back, "Notable Quotables.". Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's Friday, time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The job of the Justice Department is to address criminal conduct.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You are attempting to intimidate them. You are attempting to silence them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When I was at the Justice Department, it was focused on going after MS-13. Now, apparently, it's going after parents of 13-year- olds.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The school board and everyone else has come out and said it's not taught. It's racist. It's a dog whistle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If Bill and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  had a son, it would be \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2 , and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would be his uncle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We can gather for Christmas or just too soon to tell.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's just too soon to tell.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Kids who are bullied on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , the bullying following them home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I heard so many people say they are afraid to get vaccines because they would have to get on a subway and they are afraid of being assaulted.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We need a build back better plan right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need solutions, the build back better plan.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don't think they are appropriate tactics, but it happens to everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thanks to Secretary \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , millions of Americans think that the president is now on a day pass from a nursing home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was almost 5 million a day between DOD and DHS.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To not?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To not build the border wall.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's kind of like \uc2 Geico\uc2 . It's so easy a caveman can do it.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: To oppose these investments is to be complicit in America's decline.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: The majority didn't have a plan to prevent default, so we stepped forward. [opening bell]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: FOX News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Twenty-five years, and that was one week.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Marc Thiessen chose for his winner Joe Manchin for walking out of that speech with Chuck Schumer, and his loser, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  for everything dealing with the parents of school students.\par \par \uc2 Monday, on SPECIAL REPORT, Panama's foreign minister says tens of thousands of Haitian migrants heading to the U.S. southern border. Griff Jenkins is on the ground there in Panama with that story.\par \par \uc2 And again, please join me Sunday night 10:00 p.m. eastern time for the premier of our special "To Rescue the Republic, President Grant and a Nation in Crisis," based on my new book, "To Rescue the Republic, Ulysses S. Grant, the Fragile Union, and the Crisis of 1876," which comes out Tuesday. This program will also be available on FOX Nation starting this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And this weekend House Minority \uc2 Whip Steve Scalise\uc2  will be the guest on FOX NEWS SUNDAY along with an exclusive interview with Democratic Delaware Senator Chris Coons.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. "FOX NEWS PRIMETIME" with Ben Domenech starts right now. Hey, Ben.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020211009eha800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The New Jobs Number Report For September Fell Short Than What's Anticipated, And It's All Coming Down After President Biden's Poll Number Goes On A Nosedive; Tesla's CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Announced That His Company Will Be Moving To Austin, Texas But Won't Totally Abandon The Plant In California; Parents Are Pushing Back Against The Biden Administration's Use Of The \uc2 FBI\uc2  To Stop Them From Doing What They Think Is Right For Their Kids; Liberal City Exodus: Companies, People Abandoning Dem-Led Cities; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's Trigger Warning: Company Will Alert You About Intense Conversation; Study: Video Games Just As Healthy As Jogging\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Jesse Watters, Kennedy Montgomery, Richard Fowler,  Sandra Smith, Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,397 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): We're grateful for you. We're grateful for you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST (on camera): Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters along with Kennedy, Richard Fowler, Sandra Smith, and Greg Gutfeld.\par \par \uc2 It's five o'clock in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 President Joe Biden's presidency is in a tail spin. And the bad news keeps piling up for the commander in chief. His spending a (Inaudible) and Democrats are divided over how to spend these trillions.\par \par \uc2 Biden's poll numbers are cratering to an all-time low, inflation ramping up with prices surging and now add the jobs disaster to the list. Only 194,000 were added in September, far below the estimates anticipating a half a million jobs would be added. It's the worst jobs report of his presidency so far. But don't worry. Biden says he's making real progress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Today's report as the unemployment rate down to 4.8 percent. A significant improvement from when I took office. The monthly total has bounced around. But if you take a look at the trend, it's solid.\par \par \uc2 Things in Washington, as you all know, are often noisy. Turn on the noise and every conversation is a confrontation. Every disagreement is a crisis. But when you take a step back and look at what's happening, we're actually making real progress. Maybe it doesn't seem fast enough. I would like it faster. We're making consistent steady progress, though.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS (on camera): And just yesterday Biden was bragging about the unvaxxed being fired.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: More lives are being saved. Let's be clear, when you see headlines and reports of mass firings and hundreds of people losing their jobs, look at the bigger story.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, he's saying it's good that unvaxed Americans were laid off. Kennedy, the recovery is stalling. That's clear. This was a v and then it started to fade. And there's millions and millions of jobs still open, about 11 million and Americans really aren't trying to fill them.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY, FOX BUSINESS HOST: Well, the White House can't make sense of it. And I think one of the biggest things they have going against them is they're being completely disingenuous with the messaging. And it's actually OK to let people know that sometimes things aren't great.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, for this administration, a lot of things are going wrong and that's showing up in the polls. You know, the \uc2 Quinnipiac\uc2  poll that we showed, every single measure the president is underwater. The economy, coronavirus, immigration.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Taxes.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Afghanistan, inflation, it's all bad for him. And you know, things like inflation, Sandra will tell you, that's not getting better anytime soon. And even the fed which was doing the same thing the White House does, which is try to paint this short-term rosy picture, now they have to admit that it's a long-term problem. And I just, I get really sick of sitting through briefings from the president and from \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  and the communications team where they essentially are lying to people.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes. What about what you said, Richard, if you have a problem in this recovery, why doesn't the president just say listen, we've slowed it down, here's what I'm going to do to address it, just be patient. We got this. Instead he just spins and lies and that's why people don't trust them.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Well, listen, I tend to agree with Kennedy here. I think that the White House could have come out today and said, listen, this is not the best jobs report and that speaks to why we need the Build Back Better plan. And I think that he could have made a pivot to talk about how this one -- the one thing -- the one thing we saw on this jobs report were a lot of the losses of jobs were two women. Right?\par \par \uc2 A lot of women lost jobs in this report which speaks to the fact that, you know, a lot of women aren't going