ight or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS HOST: Always great to work with the Sandra. I'm Trace Gallagher in for John Roberts. "THE STORY" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, Sandra. I'm so glad that Trace got his wine in time for the weekend. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. So, here's THE STORY. The Biden administration looks at the much weaker than expected us jobs report and says, "We still have work to do."\par \par \uc2 The President says we still have work to do to fix this economy, you've got about 194,000 jobs that were added last month. The number was expected to be 500,000. That's a serious shortfall. You've also got about 11 million open available jobs in the U.S., and an odd reluctance from some American workers to get back at it.\par \par \uc2 So, the administration is blaming the pandemic, a number of things for the reasons for this, why people haven't gone back to take those jobs. But critics say, that vaccine mandates are a factor here, and expanded unemployment benefits are keeping Americans home. You heard Dan Henninger from the Wall Street Journal just talking about that.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, the President made brief remarks on the report, he quickly left the room. He didn't take any questions on the economy or the report, but he insists progress is being made.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Jobs up, wages up, unemployment down. That's progress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here now, Andy Puzder, former CEO of CKE Restaurants and author of "It's Time to Let America Work Again." So, he's a perfect person for this topic. And Mollie Hemingway, Senior Editor at The Federalist and a Fox News Contributor, great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 So, Andy, you know, obviously the President is going to try to put this in the best light possible when he looks at these numbers. But he says it's progress. And he likes the fact that the unemployment number is lower, what's your take?\par \par \uc2 ANDY PUZDER, FORMER CEO OF CKE RESTAURANTS: Well, number one, we missed the numbers two months in a row by a lot. And in fact, the, the economist, the experts were baking in the COVID problems that the President talks about. So that's not the problem. The problem is and the President went through a number of programs that he's proposing that will create more jobs, and that will give benefits to people that aren't working.\par \par \uc2 Well, the problem is we don't have a jobs shortage. As your numbers showed at the beginning of the show, we have almost 11 million job openings, and only about 7.7 million people unemployed. So, the problem isn't a shortage of jobs; the problem is a shortage of workers. So, if the programs you're proposing are government spending to create jobs, we don't really need to create jobs, we have the jobs.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing you're spending on is discouraging people from working by providing benefits to people who don't work. You're actually hurting the problem. You're enhancing the problem of too many, of not enough workers. You know, we don't have a job shortage. We have a worker shortage. And I think it would be very helpful if the, if the White House would come to grips with what the problem is that we're facing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Mollie, they point to a number of things, some of which Andy just touched on. They talked about the number one factor they said is fear of coronavirus, in terms of why people don't want to go back to work or I've chosen not to go back to work yet.\par \par \uc2 Vax mandates for some people are creating an issue in keeping them out of the workforce, childcare worker shortages, because a lot of childcare centers don't have enough staff.\par \par \uc2 So, that goes in -- you know, it's a circular problem when it goes around to that. That's a really -- you know, COVID has wreaked havoc up and down the line, when you look at it that way on the economy, on psyche, in America, on everything.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: That's exactly right. The response to the COVID pandemic has so disrupted what had been this very functioning engine of our economy in so many different ways. You still have schools that are not operating at full capacity, that puts a burden on childcare, people haven't find difficulties and having childcare so they can't work.\par \par \uc2 You have vaccine mandates that are making and other issues that are making people not want to work or take certain jobs that are there. We don't we haven't even really understood all of the disruptions that we, that we are now experiencing as a result of our response to the pandemic. But it is clear that we are experiencing a very sluggish recovery.\par \par \uc2 It is reminiscent of what was happening during the Obama era. And not totally surprisingly, because President Biden as Vice President Biden shares some of the same views on how to, how to thwart economic growth and he's trying to implement those. And so, this is the direct result of a lot of the policies that he has enacted.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mollie, I'm going to jump in for one second, we're going to head back to the press briefing. Stay with us. We want to get your reaction to this. She's talking about gas prices right now, Jen Psaki. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- at home with American companies and instead asked OPEC for more production where that production is perhaps not as environmentally regulated. Is there any consideration perhaps being given to, to this keeping in mind, rising gas prices?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, we are in touch. We are not a member of OPEC, as you all know, we are in regular touch with OPEC and we have also raised issues of supply in meetings that members of our national security team and others have had in recent weeks, as I've confirmed from here.\par \par \uc2 We, of course, want to address the short-term supply issues. One of those issues, as we know, was related to Hurricane Ida and the impacts in the region which we took steps to address, certainly at the time. But our view to go back to your original question is also that well, we need to take steps to address short-term supply issues.\par \par \uc2 We need to also keep our eye on the long term and the impact of the climate, the crisis that is we are in the middle of and ensure that we are continuing to encourage the production and rise of renewables and the clean energy industry, which is exactly what the President's proposals would do. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As the January 6 committee looks to enforce some of the subpoenas that it has been issuing for witnesses, will the president direct the Justice Department?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, we just wanted to hear her response to that. Andy Puzder, obviously, the increase in gas prices for Americans is an inflationary issue. We know that we have seen the end of the X.L. pipeline was one of the very first things that the President did. We also know that Nord Stream II is supplying from Russia to Germany, which is another issue here. So, you've got a lot less in terms of future U.S. energy production in our pipeline, so to speak.\par \par \uc2 And now, we're asking OPEC, gee, can you loosen the spicket a little bit? She clearly, once again, here she doubled down on, on their perspective on the White House, which is that the bigger emergency is climate change. And so, working on alternative energy forums is more important than figuring out a way to lower gas prices in the U.S. at this point. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 PUZDER: Well, I hate to hit you with a question, but how on earth does it help prevent carbon emissions if we use OPEC's and Russia's oil instead of our own oil? That doesn't make any sense whatsoever? If we're using the oil, you get the same carbon emissions. We're the third largest energy exporter in the world, our production capacity is down about 15 percent from where it was pre-pandemic, if we need more oil now to keep prices down.\par \par \uc2 And we do, inflation's look, inflation is raging. Part of that is energy prices. It's all due to Biden's policies. If we want to bring those prices down, simply encourage American energy producers to produce more energy put in the Keystone Pipeline, open up the federal lands, and stop begging Russia and Saudi Arabia to give us oil because that really doesn't solve the problem at all. It just makes us pay more for our energy cost.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mollie, what's your take?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's just amazing to think back to the 2020 campaign, when President Biden was saying that he wouldn't severely restrict energy supply. Made it one of the first things that he did when he took office. And now, you have Jen Psaki, not just what we just heard, but she did this just the other day, admitting that they care more about their own personal beliefs on climate change than actually managing energy policy in a way that makes sense.\par \par \uc2 After killing, the Keystone Pipeline, then they have repeatedly gone to OPEC asking for more supply have been shut down. This is just -- it's again, this is, these are the byproducts of actual policy decisions that this administration has made. And they're now reaping the consequences, and it's not going particularly well for them, and it's really not going well for the country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Andy, I want to ask you another -- I want to go back to our initial topic here for a moment at the Child Tax Care, the Child Tax Credit, which is another check in the mail, this is not something that you deduct when you do your taxes. It's an actual physical check that arrives in the mail, which is unprecedented in terms of this kind of child tax credit in the United States.\par \par \uc2 By one account in the Wall Street Journal says that it would lead to 1.5 million fewer workers, 2.6 percent of all working parents. So, that's another element here, where families are making that decision while we're getting this check. So, we're not going to go back to the workforce. What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 PUZDER: Well, number one, Joe Manchin just last week was saying this doesn't make any sense. Why would we have this child tax credit and not have a work requirement, the kind of work requirement that Bill Clinton and Newt Gingrich added to the U.S. code and really had a tremendous impact and getting people back to work, particularly women back in the workforce, and increasing the labor participation rate?\par \par \uc2 It was a spectacular idea and, and a great idea. We should be helping people, but encouraging them to work. And if you want to get child poverty down, this isn't something that people talk about much. But in 2019, the child poverty dropped to a 50, almost a 50-year low at 14.4 percent. That was down from the 18 percent rate that President Trump inherited when he took office.\par \par \uc2 So, if we have economic growth, if people are working, if people have jobs, if we encourage people to work, you're going to see things like child poverty decline without, without all of this government largess that encourages people not to work. This is, this is all very counterproductive and I don't understand that it's, its policy. I told somebody today I wanted to cry after I heard the President's press, press conference, it was just so bad.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Mollie, I want to get your thought on another moment for the press conference. Peter Doocy was questioning Jen Psaki about, you know, it kind of reminds me of what we saw in Del Rio, because it's a very clear visual, right? When you look at what's going on with these containers off the coast of California, you don't need, you know, an economics degree to see that everything is stacked up and nothing is moving. And you ordered something six months ago, and it's not here yet.\par \par \uc2 So, it's very clear to all of us as we look at that, what's going on? And her answer was that they have a task force and that they have a range of steps that they're taking, hosting industry leaders to talk about this. But the fact of the matter remains, there's a huge backup here. And you know, if government is so good at handling things, why aren't we seeing a little bit more action on this front?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, our government clearly is not very good at handling problems that face Americans. This is an issue that we knew again at the onset when the lockdowns were first enforced, two weeks to, to slow down everything that was expected to have shipping disruptions. And so, this should have been worked on for many months now that they're finally taking it seriously is good, but there's, let's hope they have a good plan. It's complicated.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It'd be great to see the Transportation Secretary, Pete Buttigieg, you know, front and center, here's the plan, here's what we're going to do here. We haven't seen that yet. Mollie, thank you very much, Andy Puzder, thank you very much. Great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 PUZDER: Thanks Mollie.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the mom who months ago stood up to her school board about the teaching that she was hearing coming back from school, which was that America is a racist country. She just got a standing ovation when she said this, about this new information that the DOJ wants to target some of the parents at these meetings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUISHA KING, MOTHER: I really think at this point, the only thing to do is have a mass exodus from the public school system. That's it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quisha King only here when THE STORY continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A mom and activist from Florida getting a standing ovation from the crowd after she called for dramatic action as the Department of Justice bumped scoreboard battles to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for investigation. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING: I mean, you're at home wanting to make peanut butter and jelly sandwiches for your kids. And the \uc2 FBI\uc2  could be knocking at your door because you might have said the wrong thing at a school board meeting. These people, they're not, they are serious. They want to silence us and shut us down. I really think at this point, the only thing to do is have a mass exodus from the public school system. That's it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And everybody was up on their feet in that room just seconds later. Quisha King had a lot more to say, she's a mom of two, and a member of Moms for Liberty, a group standing up for parental rights at all levels of government. Quisha, thank you so much. It's great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, you -- so were you surprised when, when you said you know, we need to have a mass exodus, and you said that it should be organized, we all have to do it at the same time and everybody was up on their feet.\par \par \uc2 KING: I was very surprised. We've all been talking about it. And I really didn't think, you know, that I was saying anything that we've kind of already been talking about. But I guess it just at this moment in time, I guess it just really resonated with a lot of parents and you know, just concerned citizens. And so, we've been working all morning we are -- we're getting this together, we're organizing, we are pulling our children out of these indoctrination camps.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that's what I wanted to ask you about. Because it was sort of a call, a call to arms so to speak, or call to, to fight together. And you said it's going to really make a big statement if a lot of people do it. And it's coordinated at the same time. So, how are you going about organizing that?\par \par \uc2 KING: Well, right now we're trying to figure out what the most concerns are that parents have? Is it transportation, you know, getting your child to this new school? Or is it you know, is it that you want to homeschool, but you're maybe not the best person to homeschool, your, your, your child and you need a teacher?\par \par \uc2 You know, or can we get a group of parents together, a group of kids together, and we have, you know, a retired teacher or something. So, right now, we're trying to figure out what the concerns are, and find solutions for those concerns, so that way, you know, there's no excuse, and then we can just do it all together.\par \par \uc2 And, and say goodbye, because at this point, they're not hearing us, they are not, they're not following what we want for our own children. They have basically just tried to silence us, and I think that at this point, when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  gets involved, I think that we've been pushed so far aside that that's the only thing left to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, it's interesting because we've already seen about three percent of public-school children leave the public school system since the pandemic began, which is a big number and no doubt it's getting the attention of public schools and teachers unions across this country.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to point out that sort of initially you got involved in this because you didn't like what your children were being taught that America is a racist country, essentially. And that was shocking to you. I think it's shocking to a lot of parents. You know, I'm wondering, when you read this letter from the Department of Justice, which says: "Threats against public servants are not only illegal, they run counter to our nation's core values, they -- those who dedicate their time and energy to ensuring that our children receive a proper education, in a safe environment deserve to be able to do their work without fear for their safety." What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 KING: Yes, I mean, there is no -- I don't know any parent that condones acts of violence. But at the same time, I'm not seeing parents being violent at these school board meetings. So, the premise of the memo, the letter is, in my opinion, false, and we are still the, we have the authority over our children to decide what is best for them.\par \par \uc2 I get that they are elected officials, but that's the thing, they are elected officials, they are elected by the public. They are public service members. We are the public; they work for us. They're supposed to be implementing curriculum and ideas and, and, and things into the school that we decide that is best for the children and for our children.\par \par \uc2 And when they have so drastically decided that they don't care what we think anymore, and we are basically just supposed to send them our tax dollars and just let them do whatever they wish with our children, I find that, I find that to be absurd. And of course, we're going to stand up and push back.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to our children. These are our most precious resources. They are given to us by God. And we are supposed to be good stewards over our children. Of course, we're going to push back when they start, you know, pushing all of these radical ideas upon our children.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, Quisha, thank you very much. You know, some states like West Virginia have portability with your school tax dollars for school choice. And Florida, I think, has talked about that as well because you should be able to take the money that you put in for school taxes, if you decide to make a different choice, you should be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 Quisha, I hope you're going to keep us posted on this movement, because I would really love to hear the next steps as we go forward. Thanks, Quisha. Good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 KING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, joining me now is Christopher Rufo, he's Director of the Discovery Institute Center on Wealth and Poverty, and he's been all over this issue for a long time. Chris, thank you very much. It's good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with you, if I may, by pulling up this letter from Senator McConnell, to Attorney General Merrick Garland, because I think this whole issue also has focused a lot of attention on, on Merrick Garland, and what kind of Attorney General he is and whether or not the jurisdiction to go after parents.\par \par \uc2 And McConnell says, "Parents absolutely should be telling their local schools what to teach. This is very basis of representative government, they can do they do this both in elections and is protected by the First Amendment of the Constitution, while petitioning their government for redress of grievance telling elected officials they're wrong is democracy, not intimidation." What do you say about that, Chris?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER RUFO, DIRECTOR, DISCOVERY INSTITUTE CENTER ON WEALTH AND POVERTY: I think that's absolutely right. And we have to understand that this entire saga is predicated on a verifiable lie. In the original \uc2 National School Boards Association\uc2  letter, asking the Justice Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to start cracking down on parents, they created this this myth that there is somehow a, a rash of cases of violence and intimidation and harassment all over this country.\par \par \uc2 But I actually went through the report citation by citation. And they only actually provided one single example of a simple battery in Illinois, which is condemnable. Obviously, people should be protesting peacefully. But it's hardly the justification for a nationwide dragnet by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , on domestic terrorism grounds.\par \par \uc2 The thing is predicated on a lie, and it's being used to suppress speech. And it's being used, frankly, to run interference for the School Board Association and the Teachers Union, who are begging the federal government to intervene after now a year of failure after failure, after failure, to educate our kids to open schools, and to get rid of the divisive ideologies such as Critical Race Theory.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, well, it's clearly an attempt to chill dissent and to keep people home because they might be worried: well, I don't -- what if I get caught up in something at that school board meeting? I'm not sure I really want to do that. And as you point out, I went through a lot of it too, and there's, there's almost nothing there.\par \par \uc2 And any whenever someone steps out of line, or breaks the law, or assault someone or harasses someone, their local law enforcement is equipped to deal with that as they should. That is wrong. And there is already something in place to take care of that. So, Chris, thank you very much. Chris Rufo, good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 RUFO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, President Biden is going to meet with Xi Jinping by year's end, under pressure to demand some answers from Beijing on the origins of COVID, which they say they very much want to do, the White House has said. So, one researcher says that science is closing in on this issue, and it leads directly to the lab leak theory. He has done an intense amount of work on this question. He's one of the foremost experts on it in the country. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So far, the Biden administration has put very little pressure on China over the question of how and where the pandemic was unleashed because just this week, National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan talked with a top Chinese diplomat. The word COVID-19 never came up in the official readout that we went through.\par \par \uc2 Now, the pressure is on President Biden. He is going to meet virtually with Chinese President Xi Jinping to address one of the world's biggest mysteries: What happened in Wuhan that led to the deaths of millions of people around the globe?\par \par \uc2 Dr. Steven Quay joins me now, he's the co-author of "The Origin of the Virus," the hidden truths behind the microbe that killed millions of people and he's the Founder of Atossa Therapeutics. Doctor, it's great to have you with us. Thank you much for being here.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN QUAY, CO-AUTHOR, "THE ORIGIN OF THE VIRUS": It's great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You are obviously on the scientific side, and a lot of your research and work was part of the invest -- became part of the investigation that was done by David Asher. And he's very frustrated with the administration's aggressiveness on this issue, lack of aggressiveness on this issue. And he wrote this, this is David Asher. He says if we could break down the Iranian nuclear weapons program with our allies, including our Israeli partners, if we could break into North Korea's missile and space and nuclear programs multiple times, why can't we get inside the bloody Wuhan Institute of Virology? Seriously," he asks. What do you say to that, doctor?\par \par \uc2 QUAY: You know, David is a colorful fellow but he's exactly right in the sense that all of the answers, the absolute answers are inside that institution now. My work has shown that we don't need to get inside the institution to answer these questions. I mean, I think that's an important thing that people realize. I think there's a lot of kicking the can down the road.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, after two years, we've done a lot of studies and all of the evidence points directly toward the laboratory. There's no evidence that supports a natural origin at this point. And so, I think we're done at this point with the investigation,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You think you're done? You think you can conclusively say that it absolutely leaked from the lab in Wuhan?\par \par \uc2 QUAY: Yes. I mean, I think there's no animal after studying 80,000, that's a one in a million probability. The virus is absolutely pure to beginning as opposed to having a lot of diversity that you would expected, that's a one in a million possibility. And there's no infections in humans in 10,000 blood samples that were taken. Again, we were expecting about 400. That's also a one in a million probability.\par \par \uc2 So the three things in a zoonosis, the animal, the virus and the human all point toward a laboratory accident. But I think the additional piece that I want to be sure you emphasize here is, I have gotten inside the Wuhan Institute of Virology in patient specimens in December 2019. What I found there was that the lab was contaminated with Nipah viral research, that's about two years ahead of SARS-CoV-2. Now, Nipah viruses 80% lethal, so this is not, you know, if you didn't like the pandemic that was just 1% lethal, this thing they're working on in December 2019 has an 80% lethality. It needs to be stopped.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you -- are you -- you know, we know from the investigation that David Asher was part of that -- he said there was a military presence in the lab. When you talk about a virus, you're talking about another virus that they are working on, and you have evidence of that. That is 80% lethal. What is -- is there any scientific reason for concocting something like that other than to kill people?\par \par \uc2 QUAY: Well, I mean, you're studying that virus because it's 80% lethal. It's like Ebola. It's called Nipah virus. And so, there would be legitimate reasons to obviously prevent that from becoming an epidemic. But what we did was we took patient samples that were delivered to the (inaudible) in December 2019. That data was run through a sequencing machine, US made sequencing machine. And the raw data was put up in the US, GenBank at the NIH.\par \par \uc2 We went down and teased around inside that. We found SARS-CoV-2, so the patients were sick with that. But there's laboratory evidence of synthetic biology with synthetic biology handles attach to Nipah virus, and the only reason you're doing that is you're moving the Nipah virus genes around just like we believe -- we've had in this article for two or three years. So this is their next project, if I could call it that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, I just have to ask you again, is there any -- so when you say it's their next project, is there any scientific health-oriented reason for this project or do you think this project is a project of bioweapons?\par \par \uc2 QUAY: Well -- so look, I don't want to get down in the weeds but there there's something called pseudo viruses that can be used to study Ebola, can be used to study Nipah, and they have zero potential infectivity. So you can, I believe, you can answer all the questions you need with this pseudo virus.\par \par \uc2 What this was, was a large, this is 1/3 of the entire genome of the Nipah virus being moved around somewhere via synthetic biology. If they complete a synthetic version of Nipah and then have a lab accident, like I believe SARS-CoV-2 was, we then have a virus that has a much higher lethality. It's just -- we really need to focus on stuff that is kind of dangerous research.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean I don't think you can emphasize that enough. Dr. Quay saying we really need to stop what is currently going on at the Wuhan lab with this other more lethal virus. Sir, I hope you'll come back. This is a really important story, especially in the larger scheme of what's going on with China. Dr. Steven Quay, thank you very much. Good to speak with you, sir.\par \par \uc2 QUAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's frightening. All right, we're going to stay on that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, here's another thing to be worried about is \uc2 Instagram\uc2  tearing down your teenagers self-esteem, and what to do about it with the star of Netflix important documentary on just that called "Social Dilemma." We'll talk to him next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRISTAN HARRIS\uc2 , PRESIDENT, CENTER FOR HUMANE TECHNOLOGY: We evolved to care about whether other people in our tribe think well of us or not, because it matters. But where we evolved to be aware of what 10,000 people think of us, we were not evolved to have social approval being dosed to us every five minutes. That was not at all what we were built to experience.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, FACEBOOK WHISTLEBLOWER: TikTok is about doing fun things with your friends. Snapchat is about faces and augmented reality. Reddit is vaguely about ideas, but \uc2 Instagram\uc2  is about bodies and about comparing lifestyles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow. That's quite a statement. One in five teenagers says that \uc2 Instagram\uc2  makes them feel bad about themselves and that they can't stop watching it or can stop using it. That's according to \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's own research that number part of that. The company says that a greater number of teens report positive feelings, but a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  whistleblower this week said little is done to stop \uc2 Instagram\uc2 's harmful attack effects on teenagers.\par \par \uc2 Tristan Harris\uc2  joins me now. He stars in the Netflix documentary, "The Social Dilemma," and is the president of the Center for humane technology. He has also briefed members of Congress and tech leaders about the dangers of social media. Tristan, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think that Frances Haugen in that statement sums up \uc2 Instagram\uc2  pretty well?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: You know, I think she does but it's really not about her. It's about \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's own research as you said, right? I mean, the important thing about this story, because a lot of attention has been put on her, it's that this is \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's own internal research, their own documentation. They specifically said in the research, we know that \uc2 Instagram\uc2  is uniquely worse than Snapchat and TikTok because, as she said, they focus on bodies and lifestyles. They profit from keeping kids engaged all the time.\par \par \uc2 And I think the other thing that Frances said in her testimony is, you know, typically, if you're a high school student, you can go home to escape the kind of the bullying and the comparison in that whole thing. But in this case, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  profits from creating kind of a 24/7 feeling of other people are watching me, commenting on me, I have to respond other person's comments. If I don't, they're going to get upset.\par \par \uc2 So kids don't have an escape from this. And \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , the company that owns \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , they know this. And so that's what is so significant about these findings. It's just like big tobacco. It's not that these harms exists, they know that these harms exist and they chose not to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We showed that the big tobacco hearing and it really is eerie when you look at those professions that nicotine does harm your health. This is a side by side comparison of magazine covers from 2010 and 2021. Both pictures of Mark Zuckerberg, who was sort of heralded as this, you know, young genius in 2010, Person of the Year by Time Magazine. And now in 2021, "delete \uc2 Facebook\uc2 " is right over his face.\par \par \uc2 But my question is, you know, is the tide turning here? And can Congress really do anything to change it? Or is it going to have to come from individuals and from parents, and from restructuring the way that we handle this technology as humans?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, it's such a great question. It's going to take a whole of society response. And obviously, parents also have a responsibility. But the thing that I saw on Tuesday for the first time, and I've been working on this topic for eight years non-stop, this is my only focus, is how do we create humane technology and recognizing the business models that are at stake. For the first time on Tuesday, I saw a bipartisan consensus.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of senators and Congress members, they have teenage daughters. You know, actually I talked to them and when I talked to them about all the issues, national security, China, what social media is doing to polarization. When I talk about teenagers, that's when their heart stops, because they see this in their own home. And it doesn't take rocket science to know that.\par \par \uc2 So I think that, you know, we're going to have to take some pretty radical action, I think Apple has a role as well, because they can set up better controls for parents. But really, it's just this business model that maximizes engagement and whatever sort of creates the most reactivity in society. And so, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  wants to claim they're showing you a mirror, but they're really showing you a funhouse mirror that's very warped, that that reflects back the things that got the most reactivity. That's that core thing across all of these apps that we have to change.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes, I think you're so right. I have three kids who grew up with all of this stuff. And FOMO, fear of missing out, to me is such a sad thing. And it's really says a lot because fear of missing out, when you're looking at someone's picture, what they look like, or what the party they went to, or the vacation that they took, it is very toxic for society. And I think that's why it's resonating on both sides of the aisle, because everyone's seen that sad look on their kid's face when they look at their phone, which is just heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for all the things that you've done.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Thank you so much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. No, you're right. That is the key thing. We'll talk again, I hope. Thank you so much, sir. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So it has been more than two weeks since we learned that Gabby Petito was murdered, but we still don't know exactly what happened. Renowned pathologist Dr. Michael Baden finds that peculiar and he's going to tell us why after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Police say that they did everything they legally could to keep an eye on Brian Laundrie before he vanished more than three weeks ago. They're not giving any details or saying how he got away after coming home to Florida without his girlfriend, Gabby Petito. The coroner in Wyoming ruled her death a homicide more than two weeks ago. So why do we still not know the actual cause of death?\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Dr. Michael Baden, a forensic pathologist and Fox News contributor who helped investigate President Kennedy's assassination, and he also testified at the OJ Simpson trial. Dr. Baden, good to see you. Good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 I'd like to start by playing a piece of this interview that Laura Ingle did with Gabby Petito's stepfather Jim Schmidt on their understanding of what's going on. Let's watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIM SCHMIDT, GABBY PETITO'S STEPFATHER: We were holding out hopes that they would do the autopsy and release her but they had to bring in a lot of expert, forensic pathologists and people to come in and do the autopsy. And they felt it was important to hold on to her remains for the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Dr. Baden, they bring in a lot of expert forensic pathologists, what do you read into all of this?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BADEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think that's extremely unusual to still be holding on to the body, and not allowing a proper disposition by the family of the remains. In order to know that it's a homicide, you first have to know what the cause of death is. That would have been known on the day the autopsy was performed. The autopsy performed, the cause of death and then the manner of death is determined at that time, why they delayed and then only released the homicide as the manner of death is itself very unusual.\par \par \uc2 And it isn't a matter of hiding something from the perpetrator, because the perpetrator knows what the cause of death is. It's the public doesn't know. The coroner knows, the police know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  knows the cause of death. And there's no reason to hold on to the body whatever has to be identified in the body. DNA, trace evidence, tissue samples can all be done at the time of the autopsy, that's why we do an autopsy. And holding on to the body, not releasing the cause of death, but knowing that it's a homicide from the autopsy is very, extremely unusual.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, I mean, just covering these stories at all feels very unusual to me as well, why they wouldn't, you know, originally, they said it would take two to three weeks, they were going to do toxicology on the body. And then, I also heard a detail that keeps sticking in my mind that they found a piece of her clothing nearby, and that helped them to identify her.\par \par \uc2 BADEN: Well, number one, the clothing is kept but the body is released, but whatever clothing was on the body would have been kept by the medical examiner and by the police. So that there's no reason to keep the body and I have not heard of any agency. I think the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is being overly cautious about it. But if they do the proper autopsy, and save the proper tissues and laboratory materials, there's no reason to hold on to the body.\par \par \uc2 The fact that the toxicology isn't done yet does not affect the cause of death. That's just the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. A lot to think about you left us with. Dr. Michael Baden, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BADEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to see you, sir. Thank you for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 So another California company relocating their headquarters to Texas where everything is bigger and the cost of doing business is cheaper. That story is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Elon Musk says that he's relocating Tesla's headquarters from California to Texas. He already moved to Austin himself after protesting COVID lockdowns that put production at his California factory on hold. But he says there's another reason for this move. And the company is not completely leaving the state. National Correspondent William Lajeunesse reporting live for us from LA. Hi, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LAJEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Martha, Tesla will continue manufacturing in the Bay Area, not far from their battery plant in Reno, Nevada. But Tesla's headquarters, its executives and its corporate taxes will now be paid in Texas. And it joins 74 other companies that already left California this year, including \uc2 Hewlett Packard\uc2  and \uc2 Oracle\uc2 , about half of those going to Texas. News of the move brought a huge applause from employees and others yesterday at Tesla's annual shareholders meeting.\par \par \uc2 Why is it leaving? Well, according to CEO Elon Musk, cost of housing, long commute, quality of life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, CEO, TESLA: It's tough for people to afford houses and let people have to come in from far away, and so it's a -- we'll take -- we're taking it as far as it as possible but it's -- there's a limit to how big you can scale in the Bay Area.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAJEUNESSE: Musk also says Texas is more attractive to the pool of workers he needs than San Francisco, in Bay Area, where homelessness and crime make living less than ideal. Other site California's aging infrastructure, taxes in the 500 state agencies that regulate business, raising production costs when Tesla is facing emerging competition.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The highest income tax rate in California right now is about 13.5 percent. And as you know in Texas, the income tax rate is zero. And that has created an incredible competitive advantage to states like Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAJEUNESSE: While this move is about what is good for Tesla, there is a political backdrop, right? With a running battle between red state Texas and blue California, and what kind of policies are best to live and do business. So some will see this, Martha, as a ding on Governor Newsom and a win for Governor Abbott. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fascinating to watch. William Lajeunesse, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So that is THE STORY of this Friday, October 8, 2021. But as always, THE STORY goes on, so we will see you back here on Monday at 3:00. "YOUR WORLD" starts right now. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020211009eha800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Angle Warned Blue States Would Be Left Behind; High Employment In Blue Cities Cripples Economy; Biden's Climate Crusade Set To Devastate Economy; Teachers Fight Back On Vaccines; New York City Requiring Public School Employees to Obtain Vaccination for COVID-19; Profane Chant Regarding President Biden From Crowds Across U.S. Caught On Video; Health System In Louisiana Requiring Employees And Employees' Spouses To Get Vaccination For COVID-19; National School Board Association Asks Biden Administration To Use Patriot Act To Investigate Those Possibly Threatening Teachers Over Controversial Curriculum\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Larry Kudlow \par \uc2 6,984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY, FOX NEWS HOST: Hearts be troubled. Laura Ingraham is next. We'll see you back here, tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Laura Ingraham. This is THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from Washington tonight. Now the CDC says the COVID vaccine doesn't actually stop the spread of the virus. OK? So, why are we banning unvaccinated teachers from their own classrooms? We talked to one teacher who's taking her case to court and domestic terrorists. That's what the National School Board Association wants to label parents who stand up for their kids. Plus, Biden insults the American people and they're talking back, Raymond Arroyo has it in Seen and Unseen. But first, big blue cities block America. That's the focus of tonight's angle.\par \par \uc2 Well, you can't say we didn't try to warn them. It's becoming more obvious by the day that these power hungry governors and radical mayors are endangering their state's futures. And will eventually see their states