te for it if it comes to them.\par \par \uc2 We will keep monitoring that. Stay with us. You're watching "Your World."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, apparently, the buck doesn't stop at $1 at the \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2 , indicating right now it has to push up things a little bit, maybe $1.25, $1.5, $2, but the \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2 , an iconic symbol, raising its prices.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the search continues right now for Brian Laundrie.\par \par \uc2 And they are searching out the more unusual types of tips that are coming their way.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Serrie has more from North Port, Florida, on this -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 In fact, this afternoon, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  returned to the home of Brian Laundrie's parents. Agents wouldn't say what they were doing there, but the Laundrie family attorney tells us that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was gathering some additional personal items of Brian Laundrie.\par \par \uc2 Steven Bertolino tells us these items will assist dogs in their search for Brian and -- quote -- "There is nothing more to this."\par \par \uc2 Investigators were also seen photographing the Laundries' camper trailer. Pinellas County authorities say the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is looking at security camera video from Fort De Soto Park, where Brian and his parents went camping about five days after he returned home alone from his trip out West with his girlfriend, Gabby Petito.\par \par \uc2 Bounty hunter Duane "Dog" Chapman led a private search team to several islands near the park, but found no conclusive traces that Brian was there. The Laundrie family's attorney confirms Brian purchased a new cell phone on September 4, but says it's not a burner.\par \par \uc2 In a text to FOX News, Steven Bertolino writes: "Brian left that phone at home the day he went for a hike in the preserve on September 14, 2021. And the \uc2 FBI\u