1 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, you're looking live.\par \par \uc2 The president of the United States is in Queens, New York. Remember, he is on this tour throughout the Northeast, in New Jersey earlier today, and now in Queens, New York, 52 deaths of the Northeast. Queens has that sort of ignominious position of being where most of the deaths occurred. Most of the people who did end up dying drowned in their basements or in their cars.\par \par \uc2 The president is getting an assessment of that and the needs of the community. He has also talked increasingly in this emergency statement for both New York and New Jersey that federal help and maybe tens of billions of dollars worth of that help is on the way.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 And what we're chronicling here is a president who is trying to tell those affected by a storm that went way beyond the Gulf Coast that, I'm here and so is the federal government. He will be speaking very shortly about the kind of aid he is talking about.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from Alex Hogan, who was with him earlier today in Manville, New Jersey -- Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 It is such a painful day for people in this community who are starting to pick up the pieces after losing pretty much everything that they own. Now, as you mentioned, the president was here today in Manville, New Jersey. He met with state and local officials touring this area and meeting many of these residents.\par \par \uc2 He talked with people, stopping by to give them hugs at some point. And he also spoke with one woman who talked about what it was like for her when she came home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Probably around 2:30, 3:00 Thursday, the house was on fire.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank God you didn't come back.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. Thank God we were not near the house. Nobody in the area was able to come back yet, because it was still -- the waters were so high.\par \par \uc2 So, we're extremely grateful that we were able to -- all of us in the community, we were all able to not be here when this happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  says that he wants federal aid for six more counties on top of the six that are already approved. And the White house says that President Biden is open to applying a disaster declaration to more counties.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden is now in Queens, New York, where he's witnessing the damage there and meeting many of the residents. He will meet with Governor Hochul, Chuck Schumer and New York City Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 ; 13 people died in New York City, 11 of them in lower-level basements; 27 people died here in New Jersey, most of them swept away in their cars, drowning in those vehicles, because of the floodwaters.\par \par \uc2 Here in Manville, the water levels reached the second story of many of these homes, and residents had to go to that top floor to be rescued by police, who save them by boat. Many of these streets look very similar. There are cars, one after another, that are totaled, and every sidewalk looks just like this.\par \par \uc2 Every inch of the ground is covered with personal belongings. These are desks, these are beds, appliances, like washers and dryers and fridges, as people continue to load things out of their home, a very difficult, devastating day for them, again, losing everything that they have.\par \par \uc2 And now on top of this, the National Weather Service is saying that most of New Jersey could see one to two inches of rain on Wednesday. And that could cause potential flooding to an already overly saturated ground -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Alex, thank you very much for that, Alex Hogan following all of that in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we are learning a little bit more about the emergency funds that the president is requesting. This urgent spending request, as it's called, goes for $14 billion in aid to respond to natural disasters that actually occurred before, before Hurricane Ida, as well as $6.4 billion to pay for the ongoing relocation of tens of thousands of Afghans that partnered with the United States during the war in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 But you can bet, with reports of up to $10 billion dollars in extra funding requests to help those post-Hurricane Ida, these are going to be some pretty eye-popping sums.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove with us, FOX News contributor, former White House deputy chief of staff, bestselling author.\par \par \uc2 Very few, Karl, will stop or turn down a president who's asking for emergency funds after a disaster. But more than half of this money is prior to that disaster. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, either bad planning or an attempt to take advantage of a crisis by getting a bunch of money for other things.\par \par \uc2 And I think we need to look carefully at the details of the requests for monies that are not connected with this emergency. If they were legitimate expenditures for previous emergencies, then the question arises, why didn't the president and why didn't his director at FEMA alert the public and indicate that the cupboard was bare and ask Congress for the money then?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The Afghanistan situation is still part of that mix.\par \par \uc2 So we're out of there, but we're not out there, the funding and all this other stuff that he's calling for, $6 billion. It's a moving target, I grant you, and all of this at a time with the controversy -- and you're quite familiar, Karl -- about whether the State Department is blocking Americans getting out, sticking to forms, paperwork and all of that, or has nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 But it's a bumpy process. And I imagine it's going to get bumpier. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: I think so.\par \par \uc2 The administration doesn't think so. They have sort of basically gone to the press in what is, I think, an incredibly cynical way, and said to the press, we're not going to talk about Afghanistan anymore. That's going to be over at the State Department. And we think voters are going to forget this by the 2022 election. And, instead, we're going to be focused on the infrastructure package, the American jobs package, human infrastructure, free community college, free this, free that, free that, because we think voters will reward us at the polls in 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 Incredibly cynical. They cannot avoid talking about Afghanistan. Afghanistan is going to take continue to be part of our national dialogue. I was looking at a list of the members of the new government. The acting prime minister is the head of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  leadership council. This is the group that sanctioned Al Qaeda being in -- Afghanistan being a safe haven for Al Qaeda and attacking the United States.\par \par \uc2 The deputy prime minister is Abdul Baradar, who is the Taliban co-founder and was a -- the Pakistanis considered a terrorist. The prime minister has been designated a terrorist by the United Nations, and the interior minister is one of the Haqqani family, which the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is trying to get and there's a bounty on his head. And the defense minister is the founder of -- is the son of the founder of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , who's now deceased.\par \par \uc2 But the son is intimately tied up in this. Do we think these people are now going to say, OK, we're going to open for American tourism, and what can we do to establish a consul and in the United States in order to have good diplomatic relations, and, oh, incidentally, yes, we have got economic opportunities here, we want Western companies to come in and partner with us?\par \par \uc2 No, they're going to be -- they're going to be focused on revenge killings. And we're going to have more stories like we had in the last couple of days of a policewoman being shot in front of her family in a provincial capital by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . They're not going to be able to stop talking about it.\par \par \uc2 And they ought to talk -- end the talk about this being this political and we're looking at it only through a political prism.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, we're also looking at it through a financial, as you know, Karl.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And keep talking about leverage and the billions that we can control and freeze through our \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and Treasury, and that the impetus to unfreeze it is that you, \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , have to continue helping on all of this.\par \par \uc2 There's very little indication in the past that money has been an incentive for them to do much of anything, but what do you think?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Look, I don't think so either. In fact, it is a demonstration of weakness.\par \par \uc2 We ought to be saying to them, that was the money that belonged to the legitimate government of Afghanistan. You overthrew it by force. That money's gone. And that would be our greatest leverage on them, because, look, they have their own source of money. They have the illegal drug traffic.\par \par \uc2 And now we have made them into the largest weapons supplier to terrorist groups in the world by leaving behind billions of dollars of equipment, small arms, artillery pieces, vehicles, hundreds of millions of rounds of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 So they're going to have their own sources of money. We shouldn't be basically saying to them, hey, we got a bunch of money, and you can hold us hostage for whatever you want, because, as we have demonstrated already under the previous administration, we can be taken to the cleaners in negotiations, and the new administration is even more likely to be taken to the cleaners.\par \par \uc2 So we got a bunch of money. Figure out ways in order to get us to give you money by providing roadblocks to becoming a normal nation and acting with respect to the lives and values of your people.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is -- this is like telling them, bend -- I'm bending over, kick me, is the way the administration appears to be dealing with the Afghan government on the question of money.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Timing is everything too, Karl, right? I mean, here we are on the 20th anniversary. You were there. You were with President Bush at the time.\par \par \uc2 Could you ever have fathomed -- and we were still days away from targeting Afghanistan and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  there -- that they would be back in power, and with a vengeance, two decades later?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: No, because we gave it to them.\par \par \uc2 For 20 years -- think about this, two-thirds of the people of Afghanistan are now under the age of 25. So all they know is what they have seen in the last 20 years, which is a government trying to become responsive to its people, a growing economy, incredible opportunities for women that didn't exist before to get an education and to pursue a career, respect for human rights, a measure of personal liberty that they'd never seen before.\par \par \uc2 And we did so for the last several years. We ended our combat role six years ago. Our last death was 18 months ago. And we had 2, 500 people in the country. And we were able to keep the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  at bay by providing contractors to keep the Afghan air force in the air and by, if need be, attacking the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  by raining hell down from above.\par \par \uc2 And we unilaterally gave that up and gave the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  a victory. And they're celebrating in the streets of Afghanistan. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is, not ordinary people, but the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is, and talking about how they have humiliated the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 That will embolden our enemies, and more -- make it more likely that they will be making attacks from the safety of Afghanistan against us and our allies. Al Qaeda struck us on September 11. The only reason they could was that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  allowed them to use Afghanistan as a refuge.\par \par \uc2 In the negotiations of the previous administration, they never got the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to agree to condemn Al Qaeda. Three times, the Department of Defense under the last administration and this administration have confirmed that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and the Al Qaeda are working hand in hand.\par \par \uc2 And if we think that anything is going to happen in Afghanistan, except to becomes a sanctuary for terrorist attacks on us and our allies, we're kidding ourselves.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think that was a mistake, negotiating with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , even now and in the prior administration?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we -- think about it. The deputy prime minister, who really is the leader, Baradar, was in a Pakistani prison, because even the Pakistanis identified him as a radical Islamic terrorist. And we were the guys, under the negotiations of the previous administration, who got the Pakistanis to let him out of prison, so he could lead the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  negotiations.\par \par \uc2 And one of his first demands is, 5,000 \uc2 Taliban\uc2  warriors needed to be let out of the Afghan jails, and, in return, the Afghan -- the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  would give up 1,000 Afghani hostages. And guess who a lot of those 5,000 battle- hardened \uc2 Taliban\uc2  warriors who were in Afghan prisons? Guess where they ended up?\par \par \uc2 They ended up in the attack on provincial capitals, and ultimately Kabul that brought down the Afghan government. So it was a mistake, in my opinion, to negotiate. And the only good thing in that agreement between the \uc2 United States government\uc2  and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  was that the American withdrawal was supposed to be conditions-based.\par \par \uc2 And one of those conditions was that they should not attack provincial capitals. But when we got rid of Bagram Air Force Base and cut our numbers down to 2, 500 people, it became impossible to continue to make that a conditions-based agreement with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , because they violated the conditions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: And there was no real response by our government.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Karl Rove, thank you, my friend. Very good catching up with you and also remembering 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of which, we are going to be concentrating not only on the new Afghanistan -- in fact, we will be doing that right when we come back -- but something we're doing this week, and we're going to be doing this on all my shows on FOX News and FOX Business leading up to our Saturday special coverage on the 20th anniversary of the attacks, is remembering what happened then, and down to what we were doing 20 years ago each day this week leading up to the attacks.\par \par \uc2 For example, do you know what the biggest story was 20 years ago today? The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  had approved \uc2 Chevron\uc2  buying Texaco. It was a huge oil merger. That was bigger than big. At the same time, we learned that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  was giving up its quest to ultimately break up \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And a new fellow had just taken over the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just a couple of days prior. His name? Robert Mueller. This week, that's the kind of stuff we look at each day, every day, going back 20 years to the day, what was happening leading up to that day.\par \par \uc2 We will also go back in our own records, our own coverage to let you know, what were the worries, what were the concerns, especially after that attack? Were we ready for it? Hear it in the voices and the people at the time? Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A lot of people came very close and saw up quite a bit yesterday. And I'm wondering, among the survivors, the friends of the survivors, how that affects going back to business?\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK, GRANT THORNTON: Well, it has to, even if you're just watching these horrible images on TV. How can you just go back to business as usual?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do people tell you, Diane?\par \par \uc2 SWONK: Well, one of the things that it reminds me of is, I have been thinking about I never delineate between the economy and my life.\par \par \uc2 And this can't be more wrapped up in both. I have been asked the economic impacts of this. And, ironically, we have talked about the rebuilding. And rebuilding is a stimulus. And it's something you don't want to talk about in sad times.\par \par \uc2 But I think what you have to think about is, we have been through crises. Remember the Cuban Missile Crisis, when John F. Kennedy was killed. The economy continued. Life goes on. The sun comes up the next day.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's the way it's going to be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Our secretary of state is on the ground in Qatar now working to see what we can do to get flights up and operational, not just from Kabul, but from other regional airports in the country, because we know that, while that's also a part of getting individuals out who want to leave, including the remaining American citizens, it's also the way that a range of assistance will get into the country.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, but the bugaboo in this whole situation is how you go about that process. What role, if any, can the State Department really play, besides processing paperwork and that sort of thing, that would expedite this process?\par \par \uc2 If anything, such a task complicates it.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon on where all this stands now, getting our people out.\par \par \uc2 Where are we?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Well, hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Statements by the State Department and \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  that those with American travel documents are being allowed to leave by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  don't match the reality on the ground. Now that the U.S. is no longer in Afghanistan, they are relying on the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to be the TSA, and they don't want to give permission for charter flights to land at U.S. military bases in Qatar, for obvious security reasons.\par \par \uc2 We were given access to those evacuated on the last U.S. flights now being processed at three U.S. military bases in Europe, Ramstein, Sigonella, Italy and Rota, Spain, where the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Customs and Border Patrol and NCIS had agents overseeing the screening of about 17,000 Afghan evacuees.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. MARK MILLEY, CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: They're getting their names registered. They're doing the biometrics. They check their irises. They do their fingerprints. They take a full facial photo.\par \par \uc2 They run that against the 20 years of databases that we have in the interagency.\par \par \uc2 I'm very comfortable that these folks are being properly cleared through the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And they're being checked about three times before -- with their biometrics before they even get to the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: That's right, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We saw the biometric screening process firsthand.\par \par \uc2 We learned that anyone flagged for drug or terror ties, anyone on a U.S. watch list, is sent to Camp Bondsteel, the U.S. military base in Kosovo. We learned that the thousands of Afghans who have been sent to the U.S. have been screened three times before arrival.\par \par \uc2 Most of the people we interviewed had some connection to the U.S. in Afghanistan, like this young boy, Yousef Abdullah, whose father worked for the U.S. Drug Enforcement Agency. His father is already in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLEY: Tell me how you got out.\par \par \uc2 YOUSEF ABDULLAH, FATHER WORKED FOR DEA: My father's was language inside the airport.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: He's inside the airport?\par \par \uc2 ABDULLAH: Yes, he was inside the airport. And he -- that's why we can't get into today airport and we are now here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Many of those we met had been guards at the U.S. Embassy. They showed us their documents.\par \par \uc2 Some were among the 600 Afghan Special Forces who helped guard the Kabul Airport in those final days, including this interpreter named Sher Mohammed, who we interviewed in Sigonella.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHER MOHAMMED, AFGHAN INTERPRETER: I feel very, very happy because we're safe right now. And when I was in Kabul in Afghanistan, I was very afraid of -- afraid of being captured by \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Right now, I'm very happy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: This brother and sister were students. One has a full ride to the \uc2 University of Kentucky\uc2 , Louisville. We interviewed many students like this 21-year-old from American University of Kabul who left her entire family to escape.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEHRIA GHAFOORI, AMERICAN UNIVERSITY OF AFGHANISTAN STUDENT: It was so difficult to leave my family behind and come. But I had -- I had no choice. My family wasn't there for -- they wanted to come with me, all of my family, but they couldn't make it, because \uc2 Taliban\uc2  would -- just started gunfire, and suddenly everything was so messed up.\par \par \uc2 So, my family just told me, like, you just go. Save your life. It's all right. We will stay here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We have also learned the CIA alone helped the State Department and DOD to evacuate more than 30,000 Afghans who had helped the U.S. over the years or were known Afghans flagged by trusted sources.\par \par \uc2 Now, with no U.S. officials on the ground, it's much more difficult, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: To put it mildly, as you said.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jennifer Griffin, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 To Lieutenant General Richard Newton, a former U.S. Air Force assistant vice chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 General, always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Do you think we're going to get all these people out?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. RICHARD NEWTON (RET.), \uc2 U.S. AIR FORCE\uc2 : Well, good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 As Jennifer reported a lot of heart-wrenching, also some heroic stories there. Again, right now -- and I'm generally an optimistic person. But, again, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  hold all the cards, Neil. They own the territory, . They own the messaging.\par \par \uc2 They're doing, by the way, a magnificent information operations campaign. And they hold in enemy hands our American citizens. But let's also focus on the tens of thousands of \uc2 Taliban\uc2  -- or Afghan citizens who've been by our side for the last 20 years or so.\par \par \uc2 I was just reading -- one of my sources sent me a note that said that if -- the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  will turn you away if you don't have your proper documentation, or they will take the documentation away from you.\par \par \uc2 They own all the cards here. We have picked up and left. And so, again, we have no leverage.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what do we do? I mean, it seems like just to get these few people who did get out, and it was like an administrative nightmare -- some were pointing fingers that the State Department. The State Department said it had nothing to do with this.\par \par \uc2 If you have that kind of difficulty with just a few individuals. And, presumably, you have 100 or so, maybe more Americans who want out, tens of thousands of Afghans who want out, that doesn't look like it's happening, General.\par \par \uc2 NEWTON: Well, and, if it's happening, it's very slowly.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I think it's good that we have got our secretary of state and secretary defense in the region. I think that's good.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 NEWTON: But this administration needs to remain laser-focused on the challenges we have here.\par \par \uc2 And the State Department needs to take a little bit of the strings off of it in terms of allowing some of the private organizations, some of the veteran organizations we have been reading about that have been assisting with, again, some of the evacuation efforts.\par \par \uc2 This is not just something that's going to go away in a few days or a couple of weeks. This is going to be painstakingly, I think, through days, weeks and now months, Neil. But we can't leave any of our Americans behind at all.\par \par \uc2 And I think we take advantage of all leverages that we may have. It could be other nations, friends, allies, or maybe pseudo partners. We take advantage of all non-governmental organizations that may remain back in Afghanistan. We start working, obviously, the capitals there in the region that I hope that the secretary of state's doing.\par \par \uc2 But, again, I would tell the American people that this is one where we have got to buckle up. This is going to take a long time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood.\par \par \uc2 General, thank you very much, and more for your service to this country, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 You think about it, where were you 20 years ago this week? I bet most people couldn't find Afghanistan on a map. It's tough, right? It's now so with us. But back then, who knew? That was before the attacks, right? And then came the attacks, and then came searching for the victims of those attacks, including a young son who couldn't believe that his father had gotten out of those towers and was safe and sound.\par \par \uc2 But he wanted to go back and make sure everyone he worked with was too. He didn't make it. His son on each and every anniversary joined us to remember.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What goes through your mind, at the risk of sounding trite, on a day like this five years after the fact?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's not what goes through my mind, Neil. It's what goes to my heart. There's such an absolute connection between the people down at Ground Zero.\par \par \uc2 And the absolute feeling I get is that some of that horror is being replaced with resilience.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, a quick message from Kathy Hochul, the Democratic New York governor, who is asking the president just behind her.\par \par \uc2 New York needs help, a lot more help, and the president saying, as he did in New Jersey, help is on the way, billions of dollars worth of help -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the president making clear right now as he gets ready to speak to New Yorkers, after speaking to New Jerseyans, after seeing the Ida damage there, that more help is on the way.\par \par \uc2 He has already asked Congress for an emergency funding for disaster aid that actually includes help for Afghan refugees to the tune of more than $16 billion. We will get into that in a second and hearing from the president of the United States shortly.\par \par \uc2 But that money, at present, does not include any jobless benefits. They have formally expired today.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock following the fallout for a lot of businesses who want a lot of those folks losing those benefits. And the sooner, the better, I guess, right, Jeff?\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: That's what they think, Neil. And we hope they're right about.\par \par \uc2 John Alianello of MiniMoves, which is as a company outside Chicago, a moving business, you hope these benefits going away mean more people coming back to work. These guys here, you were able to hire, but a lot of people you needed to hire, you couldn't.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ALIANELLO, MINIMOVES: Yes, that's true.\par \par \uc2 With the pandemic and the unemployment and getting the benefits made it difficult this summer to hire new people, train them and get them inserted into our operation.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: But here's a question. Wall Street Journal did some analysis of the states that ended the unemployment benefits early. They found that there was no additional boost to hiring.\par \par \uc2 You worried about that?\par \par \uc2 ALIANELLO: A bit worried about it. We're pretty confident, though, in the Chicago area, we bring them in here and train them and get them suited and get them ready for our -- for our fall and winter business.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALIANELLO: But we are concerned about it.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Yes, it's job creation frustration, Neil.\par \par \uc2 As you know, the numbers in the jobs report last week, we thought we were going to get 700,000 jobs. We got 200,000-plus.\par \par \uc2 You had to do what to get people to stay here and work for you?\par \par \uc2 ALIANELLO: Bonuses, incentives, higher incentives, recruiting bonuses, lunches, a lot of outside activity just to retain the people and keep them engaged.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Cut it into your profit, or did you pass it on?\par \par \uc2 ALIANELLO: Cut into our profit. But that's OK. We want to make sure the business is there, and we can service it.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 This is a tremendously labor-intensive business, Neil, as perhaps you can tell. Obviously, any time you have been through a move, these boxes don't pack themselves. These couches don't wrap themselves up in cellophane. They need workers. Millions of them are needed around the country. And perhaps - - no offense to those folks that really needed the unemployment benefits, the extra benefits -- maybe this will be a spur to hiring some of those folks -- sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you mention a very good point, Jeff. We had the exact same jobless benefits situation in the prior month, when jobs soared, as in this latest month, when they did not. So there's got to be another culprit that we're missing here.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Jeff, Jeff in Broadview, Illinois, on that.\par \par \uc2 I want to explore that a little bit more with Steve Moore.\par \par \uc2 Let's say the benefits situation isn't entirely the story, Steve. Let's say its reluctance about the COVID spikes, people who are anxious about returning to work, things like that. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  CAMPAIGN ADVISER: No, there's no question that some of the reluctance of workers to go back to work is because of the Delta variant, no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 And that's one of the reasons we had a disappointing jobs report. But the evidence has been pretty crystal clear that these states -- I just disagree with The Wall Street Journal analysis. I think it's pretty clear in talking to employers around the country that the additional benefits has really made it very difficult to get these workers back, just as you heard that business owner you had on.\par \par \uc2 There was a survey just taken last week. Half of small businesses in the country say they cannot get the workers back. We have 10 million job openings in the United States. And we have got eight million unemployed people. How do we get those eight million unemployed people back into those jobs?\par \par \uc2 The good news is that, starting basically today, those extra unemployment benefits are going to go away. I do think that will be an impetus to get people on the job.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, like I said, we had the same, almost exact benefits situation the prior month, when things are soaring. So you're quite right.\par \par \uc2 I guess it's a jump-ball at what is really doing this, but what if it's people just leery, period? And like I said, it could be they're reticent about people unvaccinated, or their employees, future employers, if they're looking for work, who are demanding all of this stuff, and they're just sitting tight, holding tight.\par \par \uc2 How much of an effect or lag could this have?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, here's the thing.\par \par \uc2 It's not just unemployment benefits that have increased. Just a week or two ago, Neil, the president expanded food stamps. We're providing people with $300 per-child per-week cash benefits. We're providing people with rental assistance. So the whole panoply of all these benefits have really incentivized people not to go back to work.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, you know who this is most unfair to? It's the people who have been working for the last year, the nurses, the transportation people, the people in that factory we just saw who are working. And in some cases, Neil, they actually are getting less money than the people staying home on their couch.\par \par \uc2 I don't see how that's fair. I don't see how it's good for the economy. And, by the way, the longer people stay unemployed, because many people are unemployed now for 18 months, the worse it is for their families, for themselves, for the small businesses.\par \par \uc2 I just think we got to get back to, as much as possible, business as usual, and reward people for working, not reward people for not working.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Steve, we're waiting momentarily to hear from the president. He's in New York today, as I told you. Last week, he was along the Gulf Coast of Louisiana, assessing the damage there, now in New Jersey earlier today, the damage there, in New York, and promising a lot more aid, in fact, emergency requests, where it looks like to me -- and I took off my socks to include counting on my fingers and toes -- $16 billion in more funding.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, look, in cases where you have a national emergency or a natural disaster like this, we do come in and provide aid. But what we ought to be doing is saying, OK, if we have to spend, say -- I forget, what was the number, did you say $16 billion -- then we should cut other government programs and make room for that.\par \par \uc2 That's the thing that the government never does. We just keep borrowing and borrowing and borrowing. And while we're talking about this $18 billion, of course, the president is pitching a $4.5 trillion spending bill, which will massively increase our national debt.\par \par \uc2 I have big problems with that. I think, for the future of this country, and the future of our finances, it's a very problematic proposition.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, one of the things that president has been pushing is this notion that, I'm here, I'm with you, I'm going to stand by you. And, by the way, we have this $3.5 trillion human infrastructure package that will address a lot of this.\par \par \uc2 If he stopped at just the bipartisan package, he might have a good argument to make. But some of this other stuff, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, don't forget the big story from I guess it was Friday was Senator Joe Manchin of West Virginia saying, I believe in the pages of The Wall Street Journal, I can't go for $3.5 trillion.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Too much, right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: We got to put a pause on it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 I wonder what that could mean, though.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mean, Joe Manchin could be setting a different bar here, but it might be only $2.99 trillion. I don't know what's going on here.\par \par \uc2 I do know, in the end, he invariably and almost always votes with the Democrats or is setting up the smaller number.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: He does.\par \par \uc2 Well, you're going to have him on your show to answer that question.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MOORE: I don't know what's going through his mind. But I'm hoping that he will stick to this principle.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think all Americans, Democrats and Republicans, agree $4.5 trillion of new spending is going to be financially ruinous. And, by the way for that amount of money, we could just eliminate income taxes and payroll tax for a full year.\par \par \uc2 I mean, imagine, Neil, how much the economy would grow if we told every business in America you don't have to pay taxes for a year. It would be cheaper than all of this new spending. It comes out, by the way, if my math is right, $4.5 trillion, we have got a little over 110 million households in this country, that's like $40,000 per household.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wondering, too, it's going to be tough to get over the end zone, to your point.\par \par \uc2 But that could also mean the tax hikes that are ostensibly meant to pay for this, they could be delayed as well. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, the Democrats have a big problem, Neil, because their numbers aren't adding up.\par \par \uc2 And so they have to go to something called the Joint Tax Committee and the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  to get the final numbers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And guess what? They are saying that your numbers don't add up. You're way short on your revenues.\par \par \uc2 Now Democrats are looking at a whole panoply of new tax increases, including one, did you see this one, Neil, you might be taxed on the number of miles you drive in your car.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that would be a tough one to get through everybody.\par \par \uc2 As we wait for the president, this is a chance for him also to pivot from Afghanistan. Easier said the done. How do you think that's going?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, of course, if I were president, I'd want to pivot away from the catastrophe of Afghanistan too.\par \par \uc2 But, here on the home front, things aren't going so well either. I mean, we have got a border crisis that you have been reporting on pretty regularly at FOX News. You have got a problem of rising prices and inflation. You have got the small business problem of not having workers available.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And then you have this new outbreak of COVID.\par \par \uc2 Show me something that's going right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There's a lot on the plate, a lot on the plate.\par \par \uc2 The president will talk about that and his plans to financially help, well, everyone in the storm's path out after this. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (JOINED IN PROGRESS)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: ... by saying, I wish every American could walk down this alley with me to see and talk to the people who have been devastated, just talk to them.\par \par \uc2 None of them were shouting or complaining. Every one of them were thanking me as if it was something special -- I mean it sincerely -- that I was here and hoped that we'd be able to do something.\par \par \uc2 This is America, where I am standing right now. These are the people...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: ... whether it was in Scranton or Claymont or anywhere around the world, the country, who built this country.\par \par \uc2 And it's about time we step up. They're always the first ones that are hurt and the last ones that are helped. But that's not going to happen this time. The group I have standing with me led by Chuck Schumer and your -- Congresswoman, is this your district?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER (D-NY): \uc2 Grace Meng\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh, it's Grace's district.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I want to thank her personally for her gumption, the way she's fought and hollered and fought so hard for all the people in this alley. I really mean it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, thank you, thank you. But that goes for everybody here.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And, look, folks, I want to thank governor for -- and Leader Schumer and Kirsten -- I shouldn't -- I should say Senator Gillibrand, and Congresswomen Meng and Maloney and Meeks and Mayor \uc2 de Blasio\uc2  for being here.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's not -- how can I say this? Sometimes, some very bad things happen that have a tendency to bring out the best in a people and a country.\par \par \uc2 And I think what people are seeing across this country, from the wildfires in California in the far West, which I'm heading to in a couple days, all the way to down in Louisiana in the Gulf, where I was a couple days ago, to New Jersey and Pennsylvania, a lesser extent, Delaware to a lesser extent, and New York, people are beginning to realize this is much, much bigger than anyone was willing to believe, and the whole segment of our population denying this thing called climate change.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But I really mean it.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes -- my mother used to say, out of everything bad, something good will come of it if you look hard enough for it. Well, I think we have all seen, even the climate skeptics are seeing that this really does matter.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just whether or not people who are just trying to get by in these homes in these alleys here, working their butts off, do well. It's people in high towers along the shore who find that, as this rain and all this change takes place in the groundwater, the buildings are actually beginning to tilt, 100-story buildings. This goes so far beyond what anybody is willing to speak to up to now.\par \par \uc2 We just finished surveying some of the damage in the neighborhood here in Queens. And earlier today, we were in the Raritan Valley in New Jersey, which also got badly, badly hit.\par \par \uc2 Walking these neighborhoods, meeting the families and the first responders, seeing how folks are doing after this destruction and pain and another devastating storm, is an eye-opener.\par \par \uc2 The people who stand on the other side of the fences who don't live there who are yelling that we are talking about interfering with free enterprise by doing something about climate change, they don't live there. They don't live -- they don't understand.\par \par \uc2 And, last week, right here, in so many other communities, these waves crashed through the streets here, testing the aging infrastructure, and taking lives.\par \par \uc2 More lives were taken here than down in Louisiana. Let me say that again. They had over 20 inches of rain. They had 178-mile-an-hour winds, gusts, and more lives were taken here than down in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 And you all saw the harrowing images of stories and families trapped in flooding basements, and struggling to survive.\par \par \uc2 Well, you didn't have to -- just go along this valley. Talk to the people. I'm sure the press has done that. My message to everyone grappling with this devastation is, we're here. We're not going home until this gets done. I really mean that.\par \par \uc2 We're not leaving. We're going to continue to shout as long as it takes to get real progress here. Folks, we have to take some bold action now to tackle that the accelerating effects of climate.\par \par \uc2 If we don't act now -- I'm going to be heading -- as Chuck knows, as the senator knows, I'm going to be heading from here to Glasgow in Scotland for the COP meeting, which is all the nations of the world getting together to decide what we're going to do about climate change.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , the former secretary of state, is leading our effort putting it together. We are determined.\par \par \uc2 We are determined that we are going to deal with climate change and have zero emissions, net emissions by 2050, by 2020, make sure all our electricity is zero emissions. We're going to be able to do these things, but we got to move. We've got to move, and we've got to move the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 It's not just the United States of America. And so, folks, this summer alone, communities with over 100 million Americans, 100 million Americans call home, have been struck by extreme weather. One in every three Americans has been victimized by severe weather, the hurricanes along the Gulf, the East Coast up through this community.\par \par \uc2 And I saw the human and physical costs firsthand, as I said, in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 But, Governor, you called, \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 , so many of the leading -- Governor Murph, so many leading with urgency and action, are -- said, enough, enough.\par \par \uc2 And there's not a single request I'm aware of -- there may be something -- that we haven't signed off on, that we haven't signed off yet. And here's the deal. The New York Fire Department, the \uc2 New York Police Department\uc2 , the Sanitation Department, and other first responders, they're leading with incredible, incredible courage.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Two line men have been killed trying to make sure we -- and, folks, the evidence is clear. Climate change poses an existential threat to our lives, to our economy.\par \par \uc2 And the threat is here. It's not going to get any better. The question, can it get worse? We can stop it from getting worse. And when I talk about building back better -- and Chuck is fighting for my program, our program on the Hill -- when I talk about building back better, I mean you can't build it to what it was before this last storm.\par \par \uc2 You got to build better, so if this storm occurred again, there would be no damage. There would be. But that's not going to stop us, though, because, if we just do that, it's just going to get worse and worse and worse, because the storms are going to get worse and worse and worse.\par \par \uc2 And so, folks, we've got to listen to the scientists and the economists and the national security experts. They all tell us this is code red. The nation and the world are in peril. And that's not hyperbole. That is a fact.\par \par \uc2 They've been warning us the extreme weather would get more extreme over the decade. And we're living in real time now, where we can look around the records and the ruins and the heartbreak from so many communities to feel it. Just don't understand. You can feel it. You can taste it. You can see it, precious lives lost in Louisiana, New Jersey, New York, families living in shelters, subway stations flooded, decaying infrastructure pushed beyond the limits, lives and livelihoods interrupted once again.\par \par \uc2 We're working closely with the governors and mayors and members of Congress and community leaders. On Sunday, I immediately approved the disaster declaration of Governor Hochul to rush federal assistance to where it was needed here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, President. Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: FEMA's working intensively with State and local officials assessing the damage and mobilizing resources.\par \par \uc2 One of the things I want to thank Chuck for, as leader of the Senate, he has helped mobilize state, local and federal. When they're all working together, that's when things happen positively.\par \par \uc2 The health and human services secretary is working with the state to ensure folks on Medicare and Medicaid get the emergency care they need. They're going to make sure it's equitable, so that the hardest hit, including lower-income folks, communities of color and the elderly and the most vulnerable, get help and get it first.\par \par \uc2 They are the ones in the greatest need. And there's much to be done, and we're working around the clock in all these critical needs and areas.\par \par \uc2 Look, I say to anyone who can hear this, or this is broadcast, if you need help, please go to disasterassistance.gov or call 1-800-621-FEMA, 1-800- 621-3362. We can get you help now. We can get you help now.\par \par \uc2 And I know these disasters aren't going to stop. They're only going to come with more frequency and ferocity. And, as I said, I'm working in Congress to pass two important pieces of legislation that this man here is honchoing through the Congress for me.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The bipartisan plan to modernize our physical infrastructure, our roads, our bridges, our power transmission, our distribution lines.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: How many bridges I just went through in New Jersey that had been overflown by the river? The river has gone higher than the bridges, having done damage to them.\par \par \uc2 My Build Back Better plan with key investments in -- to fight climate change, cutting emissions and make things more resilient, each dollar we invest, every dollar we raise, a city block by two feet flood-proof power stations, sanitations, reduction in the buildup of kindling in our forest, installing electrical lines underground, rather than overhead, saves us $6 for every single dollar we spend to do those things.\par \par \uc2 Because, the next time disaster strikes, the flood is contained, the fire doesn't spread, as widely, and power stays on, not to mention those investments save lives, homes and create good-paying union jobs.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Yay. We like union jobs.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I hosted -- I hosted 56 heads of state in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And I pointed out -- we were talking about climate change. And I said, I think of one word when I think of climate change, jobs, good-paying jobs. Each of these things requires a good-paying job, not $7 or $12 or 15, but $45, $50 an hour, plus health care.\par \par \uc2 That's what is needed. And so, folks, it will also -- and Wall Street, not too far from here, acknowledges, if we spend the money and these things, we're going to grow the economy, increase employment.\par \par \uc2 The fire in Oregon sent smoke all the way to the Atlantic. The storm in the Gulf, as you've now figured out, can reverberate 10 states away. Supply chains and crop production get interrupted, driving up costs, devastating industries all over America.\par \par \uc2 This is everybody's crisis, everybody's crisis.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And let me just say again, the fact is that the damage done on the West Coast, which I will be heading to, they've already burned five million acres to the ground.\par \par \uc2 That's bigger than the state of New Jersey, if I'm not mistaken, five million acres. And you see it by the smoke that ends up coming over the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 Folks, we're all in this. It's about time we stopped the regional fights and understand helping somebody make sure there's no fewer fires in the West warrants helping people in this alley make sure they're not flooded.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, it's not just the flooding. I will end with this. Not just the flooding. Flooding ends up overrunning sanitation systems. And it causes disease, and people get sick. And it's serious, serious business. So we got a lot of work to do.\par \par \uc2 Again, it's good-paying jobs. We can put the economy back on a path to real growth. But, in the meantime, we're going to save a whole hell of a lot of people's lives and we're going to save a whole hell of a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 God bless you all. Let's get this done.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Mr. President, look at that nice kid with the American flag.\par \par \uc2 Say hello.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: How are you, pal?\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: What's your name? He can't hear us.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: How old are you?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Seven.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Seven.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh, you're getting old.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's cute.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Is that nice?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And, by the way, the neat thing about America, every time we end up with a problem going into a serious circumstance, we come out better than we went in.\par \par \uc2 That's because we're so diverse. That's America. Be proud of it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Amen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: God bless America.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And don't jump.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: All right.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Great job.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you so much. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 I didn't get to mention all of the names.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, still monitoring that and see if he says anything.\par \par \uc2 My producers will continue to monitor the president of the United States. But he uses this opportunity after the storm in the aftermath of Ida, which has killed 52 people in the Northeast, that this is now, more than ever, a chance to address climate change, and that it will pay for itself.\par \par \uc2 Well, the numbers simply don't add up at this point. He's talking about raising taxes on the well-to-do and corporations. But that covers roughly a little bit more than half the cost of the $3.5 trillion package that others have put at closer to $5 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Be that as it may, that's a debate in Washington perhaps for another day. The president wants to make it a more immediate day.\par \par \uc2 For my next guest, it's just the issue of protecting and helping people right now.\par \par \uc2 Kristin Palmer is a New Orleans council member who has been following these developments, and also what's been a devil of a time helping the elderly and those who had to evacuate senior living facilities.\par \par \uc2 Council member, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 How are things looking right now?\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN PALMER (D), NEW ORLEANS COUNCILMEMBER: Well, we're looking -- we're looking great, if you look at the way our infrastructure held ground when Ida hit us. And I think we did very well.\par \par \uc2 I think it showed that those investments paid off. But where we actually saw a loss of life was in the aftermath, quite frankly, and not being able to handle the amount and the time it took once the power went out. It really showed our vulnerabilities in a lot of these living facilities.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you also have to deal with COVID, which was a big problem. And so it's obviously less of a problem now.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that's a lot to juggle, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 PALMER: It is.\par \par \uc2 And we have done this before.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: I mean, last year, we had seven named storms that came through during COVID. And we have shown our resilience time and time again,\par \par \uc2 I think what we're seeing now is the quickness of the storms have prevented us from being able to evacuate, because with contraflow, when you try to evacuate an entire city, it has to be done in stages, especially when you have low-lying parishes. So, with that into consideration, then we have to really start looking at, how do you make a plan for the aftermath and loss of power for long periods of time?\par \par \uc2 Because what we saw in New Orleans is that you have these things called independent living facilities.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: And, of course, you can be independent if you're in a wheelchair on the third floor and you have power and you have an elevator.\par \par \uc2 What you -- you're not independent once the power goes out. And so we lost someone in my district, the very first reported death, and he was on the second floor, but did not have access to oxygen, did not have access to an elevator. And, quite frankly, there was no management on site. And nobody knew what was going on in this independent living facility.\par \par \uc2 And so it's something that's tragic that shouldn't have happened. And we really need to start evaluating what we consider independent living, and ensuring that, when these things happen, when we have loss of electricity, how do we protect our most vulnerable?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you think about it, and you know the weather forecast better than I do in your neck of the woods, but I think I heard rain, and I think I heard threats of more flooding.\par \par \uc2 How do you prepare for something like that?\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Well, the biggest thing, quite frankly, is the heat index too, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: As you're seeing that climb up, this past -- this past week, we have had a heat index over 106.\par \par \uc2 And that can, again, really affect our vulnerable populations. And so you do need to be able to make sure that we have the ability either to evacuate them post-storm or to ensure that we have things such as generators that can be functional up until 96 hours post-storm to ensure that our people can actually survive it, because that's what we're really talking about, right, is what happens post-storm at this point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, power, has it returned?\par \par \uc2 PALMER: We are now over 75 percent together with our storm...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: ... in terms of our electricity, so that's doing well.\par \par \uc2 But you can -- again, it's something that comes in phases. You can't just flip a switch and the entire city comes back on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I understand.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: So, you will see in different parts of town, we will have the electricity on faster than others.\par \par \uc2 Again, when we talk about hardening the system long term, I think it's really important to talk about burying the power lines and how do we make our infrastructure more resilient, and, at the same time, though, paying attention to how do we make sure that people can live and stay independent within their homes at -- in the aftermath of a storm such as this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good point.\par \par \uc2 Council member, thank you very, very much. You're doing the yeoman's work here. Keep at it. And thank you.\par \par \uc2 That'll do it.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210908eh9700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Afghan Refugees; Do Christmas Shopping Now?; Inflation Fears; Biden's Poll Numbers Sink; Extended Unemployment Benefits End\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Lawrence, Phil Flynn, Mike Emanuel, Gerri Willis, Jackie  DeAngelis \par \uc2 7,756 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Afghanistan COVID, the border, we're taking a look at the job President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing as his poll numbers are dropping and what it means for America. That's coming up.\par \par \uc2 But, first, it's Labor Day, and more Americans are getting back to work and still dealing with the sticker shock at the store.\par \par \uc2 Hello, everyone. I'm Edward Lawrence, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is a special Labor Day edition of YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 And FOX on top of the labor market trying to get back to full employment, consumers trying to stay on top of the price spikes that don't seem to be slowing down.\par \par \uc2 And we have got you covered with Jackie DeAngelis on what's going down at work and Gerri Willis on what's going up at the stores.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's go to Jackie on jobs -- Jackie.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: This has been the moment that everyone has been waiting for, the time when the economic recovery is on track post-pandemic, where pandemic unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 Today, 7.5 million workers who have received boosted unemployment checks will be cut off, according to \uc2 The Century Foundation\uc2 . Now, a group of people have already lost their benefits, 9.4 million people as of mid-July. These were people who were not traditionally eligible for aid, like freelancers, who got it because of the crisis.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Labor says that more than 72 percent of Americans got one form of assistance or another. Recall that in, March of this year, the American Rescue Plan extended benefits through Labor Day. Now, Democrats insisted more aid was necessary at that time, while Republicans questioned why reopening wasn't enough to end benefits.\par \par \uc2 And here we are now, many businesses struggling to hire workers, forced to raise wages as inflation is palpable. The question at this moment, will the expiration of these benefits force people back to work? Early data from the Census Bureau indicates that people cut off from benefits didn't rush back to find jobs.\par \par \uc2 Still, others say, yes, this will encourage people to return to the job marketplace, but that higher wages that drove inflation could be here to stay. There's also some concern that the spikes in COVID variants could create some difficult circumstances yet again and what the government response to that would be.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, so thanks, Jackie.\par \par \uc2 So, with those extended benefits running out, and bosses looking to hire, we're looking at a record 10.1 million job openings. Will we see more people back in business?\par \par \uc2 GOP strategist Lauren Tomlinson maybe thinks so, thinks that's the right move. She's joined by Democratic strategist Ari Aramesh and CPA and money analyst Dan Geltrude.\par \par \uc2 Lauren, ladies first. Let's start with you.\par \par \uc2 So do you believe these extension of these benefits, was that purely a political move?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN TOMLINSON, GOP STRATEGIST: I think it was.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came in and people elected him at the beginning because they wanted him to give confidence in the economy. They wanted to return to normalcy. They wanted control over the pandemic. It was the one of the top reasons why he was elected.\par \par \uc2 And to be frank, he did the -- even though we weren't in the middle of shutdowns anymore, he extended the unemployment benefits for partly that reason, to give people a sense that he was doing something for the economy, for the American people while we were in the middle of this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 However, now with vaccines available, with remote options available, with mitigation measures, we're not in the same place that we were a year ago, and these unemployment benefits are propping up a work force that needs to be out there actually earning wages and contributing to the economy again, because it's creating an inflation -- inflationary situation.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: And it's not -- driving consumer prices up. And people need to get back out and we need to actually return to normalcy, which is what people in the work force.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right. I want to ask about this.\par \par \uc2 So, Dan, for you, an estimated 7.5 million people will lose their benefits when the federal emergency benefits do end. So, Dan, do you see these people then going back into the work force? Or are we going to see an uptick then people needing some social program help?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL GELTRUDE, GELTRUDE & COMPANY: I think that we should be seeing people going back to work.\par \par \uc2 Look, Edward, if you look on Main Street America, everyone is trying to hire. And what's ironic about these programs for assistance, you know who got the greatest level of assistance? Corporate America. I will tell you why.\par \par \uc2 Because, as wage inflation hit, what happened was the large corporations were able to absorb those costs. The businesses on Main Street were not. So, the result here is businesses on Main Street going out of business. And let's look at corporate earnings on Wall Street, going through the roof.\par \par \uc2 So, President Biden wanted to help the little guy, but he really hurt small businesses.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, the big seemed to get bigger there.\par \par \uc2 So, of the 24 states that ended the extra unemployment insurance by the federal government, according to the \uc2 BLS\uc2 , 19 of them had an unemployment rate below the national average. Ohio was at the national average, 5.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, Ari, does this show that those extra benefits then were holding back job growth in this last month, month-and-a-half? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ARI ARAMESH, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: No, that's not what the data suggests.\par \par \uc2 In the report he just played, in that package, we just saw that, even though when people go off unemployment benefits, they don't necessarily look for work. It doesn't translate into a flow into the job market.\par \par \uc2 Look at what's happened in the past year, year-and-a-half. First and foremost, since President Biden took over, you see a lot more confidence in the American worker, American economy. The unemployment rate has dropped down to 5 percent. But there are still segments of the economy, single mothers, African-Americans, they're about 8.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 People have to stay home to take care of their kids. They're heavily dependent on some of those benefits. With the schools sort of still not sure when they're going to open, if the kids are going to go back to school, a lot of those single parents still need that $300 or $400 check.\par \par \uc2 So it's not necessarily people sitting at home waiting for handouts. It's people who really need them. And, again, the data shows that people who were affected by mass unemployment were not high-income earners and not top sort of education categories -- or educated categories.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARAMESH: Were the income earners, and those are the people most in need.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, on the one hand, we need more hiring. On the other hand, these social safety nets...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: It's -- a lot of this, though, is, you're seeing the government sort of expand through all this.\par \par \uc2 I'm sure Lauren has something to say about this.\par \par \uc2 You have a response to some of that, Lauren.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Well, we already have programs in place to help those at the very bottom, even in -- like you were just saying, these are real groups that need help. And they would need help in normal times and -- or in the pandemic, because their situation has changed.\par \par \uc2 But that's why we have food programs. We have unemployment assistance. I mean, this -- what we're talking about is ending the inflation of the unemployment programs, that extra $300 a month, the reason why people who are on unemployment right now are earning $32,000 a year if you annualize it.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's what -- that's the part that needs to end because it's not -- we need to still encourage people to get back to work. And schools need to open. I mean, that's just -- that's -- the child care part of this is huge, and it's unfair for single mothers, and it's unfair for families to continue doing this.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, we can't keep prop -- the government can't keep propping up people. We have to get them back into the work force and get back to normal as soon as possible if we're ever going to move past this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, talking about that getting people back into the work force, Dan, I want to pose this to you. We had 3.5 percent unemployment in February under the last administration. Can we get back there, do you think?\par \par \uc2 GELTRUDE: Well, we should be able to get back there, because if you think about this, logically, what really has changed?\par \par \uc2 Before the pandemic, everyone was working, inflation was under control, economy was humming along. Now fast forward to where we are today. We have wage inflation. We have a lot of people unemployed, and businesses can't find people.\par \par \uc2 The math doesn't add up. Where have all these people gone? Now, I understand that there are single moms and people who can't get back to work. And those circumstances exist. And I agree that the government needs to help.\par \par \uc2 However, there has to be a lot of people that are choosing to be unemployed. And unemployment should not be a career option. If you can work and you're healthy enough to work, go back to work.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Dan, Ari, Lauren, have to leave it there. Thank you, guys. A big debate. Lots to chew on there.\par \par \uc2 So, moving on, no jab, pay up, \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  charging unvaccinated workers a monthly $200 health care fee. This with more companies modeling financial penalties for employees without a shot.\par \par \uc2 So, what's the potential legal fallout from all this?\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from attorney Brian Claypool.\par \par \uc2 So, Brian, first of all, is it legal to be able to just tack on these fees if you're unvaccinated?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CLAYPOOL, ATTORNEY: Hey, Edward. Happy Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 This is a colossal heavyweight COVID fight between employers like Delta and other employers, employers across the country who are going to do the same thing. In the early rounds of this legal battle, Delta and other employers are winning. They have the upper hand.\par \par \uc2 And here's why. Most employees across the country are what's called at will employees, which means they don't have a written contract. And that means that employers such as Delta and other employers can basically do whatever they want.\par \par \uc2 They can assess these additional fees for failure to get vaccines. And guess what? If the employees of Delta don't -- or don't want to pay those fees, don't want to get vaccinated and aren't happy about it, they're likely to lose their jobs. And that's the way the groundwork is right now, Edward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, what about the basic question here? Is your employer even legally allowed to ask if you're vaccinated? I mean, there's so many rules around health care.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I mean, let's be clear for your viewers. I mean, there's no game plan or playbook for this. We haven't had a pandemic like this in a century. So this is novel for the court systems as well.\par \par \uc2 But, clearly, employers have to be mindful of public safety and the safety of their other employees. Think about this. It's a double-edged sword for employers like Delta. If they don't do anything about COVID. If they don't require vaccinations, if they don't assess these financial penalties, then what happens?\par \par \uc2 Employees go to work, they don't get vaccinated, and then others get sick. And then employers like Delta are going to face a mountain of civil lawsuits. So, it's basically pick your poison.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Then they're on the hook, right.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: And the employers are picking the poison of public safety.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 But, still, let's take it a little bit further from an employee standpoint. Can an employer actually fire you for refusing to take a vaccine? Do you think there's legal ground for that?\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I do believe there's legal grounds for that, as long as an employee doesn't have, for example, a legitimate medical exemption. And I use the word legitimate because it can't be, oh, I'm afraid of the consequences of a vaccine. They would have to show history, for example, of not having gotten any other vaccines throughout their lifetime, or show that they have had some serious medical implications from a prior vaccine.\par \par \uc2 But, short of that, Edward, I got news for you. If employers out there want to fire you because you're not vaccinated, absent that legit medical exemption, you're going to be out of a job if you don't get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, in the last 30 seconds we have -- and I know it's hard for a lawyer to stick to 30 seconds -- but what -- might be too soon for them to get a vaccine safely, if some people have had COVID?\par \par \uc2 So, these rules don't take any of that into consideration, that, if they have got the antibodies already, and maybe they can't get a vaccine for some reason, or religious reasons, is there anything to be said about that?\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: Well, look, yes, religious reasons could be an exemption to, but, again, got to be legitimate. Can't be, hey, my pastor said he or she doesn't believe in these vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 It's got to be a hardcore religious belief. And I -- me being a Christian, I don't really see that anywhere in the Bible. So I think that's -- you're going to be hard-pressed to make that argument as well.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: And to your other question, I mean, it's going to be hard to implement whether you had COVID or not.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, employees are going to have to weigh it. Do I get a vaccine, keep my job or risk losing my job if I don't get it?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, Brian, I appreciate that. A lot to think about. A lot of new legal paths with COVID. I appreciate it. Thanks for the debate.\par \par \uc2 Now, to pain at the pump, pain at the produce aisle. Will shoppers be getting a break anytime soon? We will dig deeper.\par \par \uc2 Also, the president took a hit in popularity as the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took control. Now will the Democrats' spending agenda suffer because of it. We're on it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, the administration would like to turn the page after talking about the aftermath of Hurricane Ida, talking about two spending packages Democrats want, anything, anything but Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 A recent USA Today/Suffolk University poll showing why, President Biden's approval rating at 41 percent, mainly due to the botched Afghanistan withdrawal. Numbers like this could cause a problem for the president's domestic spending agenda.\par \par \uc2 Let's find out why from The Washington Examiner's Kaylee McGhee White.\par \par \uc2 So, is it a problem?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE MCGHEE WHITE, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: It is definitely a problem, not to mention the fact that one of the Senate Democrats, Joe Manchin, already came out and said that he wasn't going to support it anyway. So that obviously throws a big wrench in Biden's plans.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, the unpopularity on the Afghanistan withdrawal is going to affect the rest of Biden's agenda. If he does not have public support, he's going to be hard-pressed to find other Democrats who will then support him, because ultimately they have to answer to their constituents.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And you see that job approval rating 41 percent, other polls in the same range. ABC News as a poll out that says 59 percent of Americans disapprove of the handling of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 But does foreign policy, does a foreign policy blunder really translate into votes on the other side come the election next year?\par \par \uc2 MCGHEE WHITE: If a foreign policy blunder becomes a national security crisis, then, yes, absolutely. And, unfortunately, that is exactly what Biden has created.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the immediate effects of this are obvious. We still have more than 100 Americans stranded in Afghanistan, and a lot is going to depend on what happens to them over the next few weeks or months.\par \par \uc2 But the long-term consequences of this withdrawal are going to be serious. We have basically handed a terrorist organization, a regime, a stronghold in the Middle East that is now being directly funded by China. And that will affect our national security moving forward. There's no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But, Kaylee, voters seem not to have a long memory. So, in 2022, are they really going to remember this?\par \par \uc2 MCGHEE WHITE: Again, I think a lot depends on what happens moving forward, if a direct threat does emerge, if there are reports of Americans who are now stranded in Afghanistan, of them being kidnapped by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  or being held as hostages.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot more that is going to happen from this. So it's not just going to be a weeklong news cycle. So we will have to see you know what happens. But I don't think that this is going to be something that the Biden -- the Biden administration is ever going to live down.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now, in polling, almost all the polls show that the president, President Biden, is winning when it comes to COVID. And that's one of the reasons he was able to beat former President Trump, is that the numbers on COVID were big.\par \par \uc2 If the Delta variant sort of takes hold -- and there's a new one coming, Mu -- if either of those take hold and things get worse related to COVID, is that going to affect this president?\par \par \uc2 MCGHEE WHITE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Recent polls already show that American voters' confidence in the Biden administration's handling of COVID is sinking as well. And a lot of this has to do with kids going back to school, having to re-up mask mandates. People are losing confidence that COVID actually is a thing of the past. Clearly, it's not.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of people want to be done with it. Some don't. But everyone can agree right now that this is not where the U.S. wants to be. And the Biden administration is responsible for that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, then, Kaylee, how much of that poll number, 41 percent approval rating, how much of that do you think is also the inflation that people are seeing when they go to the pump, when they go to the grocery store?\par \par \uc2 You can really see the stuff you buy costs more, and wages are going up, yes, but inflation is going up more right now. How much is that -- of the loss of support is Afghanistan and how much of it is domestic, is the inflation?\par \par \uc2 MCGHEE WHITE: The issues that affect voters' bottom line and their wallets are the number one biggest motivator for many Americans.\par \par \uc2 And so it is going to be a really big deal if Biden does successfully push through his $3.5 trillion spending bill, because what is that going to do to the economy? Probably not anything that's good. And that's why Manchin is objecting to it, and some other Democrats, including Kyrsten Sinema, have raised concerns as well.\par \par \uc2 We're already dealing with inflation. If you push through another $3.5 trillion spending bill, that will inevitably get worse.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes. And there's one -- at least one Federal Reserve district president saying that we don't need any more spending.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you, Kaylee. We're going to have to leave it right there. I appreciate it on this Labor Day, coming out.\par \par \uc2 So, many experts starting to say, do your Christmas and holiday shopping, guess what, right now. It's not just inflation that's playing a role of Grinch for the holiday gifts.\par \par \uc2 We report, you might want to change your shopping plans. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: I'm Mike Emanuel from FOX News in Washington. "Your World" continues in a moment, but, first, this "Special Report" news break.\par \par \uc2 About 1,000 people, including many Americans, are stuck in Northern Afghanistan tonight. They're on charter flights awaiting clearance to leave. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is said to be blocking their departure.\par \par \uc2 There are new developments in a bizarre murder case in South Carolina. It involves a lawyer whose family members were killed and who apparently has suffered an attempt on his life. We will have a report at 6:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Crews in Louisiana have restored power to nearly 70 percent of New Orleans and all of Baton Rouge after Hurricane Ida, but other places may have to wait weeks for electricity. Also today, a heat advisory and flash flood watch are in effect in parts of Louisiana ravaged by the storm.\par \par \uc2 I will be back at 6:00 p.m. Eastern for a live "Special Report."\par \par \uc2 Now back to "Your World."\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Prices keep spiking, and shoppers keep feeling the pinch for just about everything at the store.\par \par \uc2 To Gerri Willis with the latest on inflation that seems to be anything but temporary right now -- Gerri.\par \par \uc2 GERRI WILLIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Ed, you have got that right, a significant uptick in prices year over year, especially in categories like food, housing and new and used car and truck prices.\par \par \uc2 The Consumer Price Index up 5.4 percent, that's the most since 2008. Let's dig into the details. First off, heading to the grocery store? Well, steel yourself. Doughnuts in July cost 2.8 percent more than they did in June. Crackers are higher by 3.7 percent and -- in just a month. And if you want to grill, hey, hot dogs are up 4.8 percent in four weeks, steak and rib prices are 4.4 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 On a year-over-year basis, prices for food consumed at home rose 2.6 percent, with meats, poultry, fish and eggs rising the most, 5.9 percent. And you won't get a break by eating out. Prices rose 6.6 percent for fast food and 4.3 percent for full-service restaurants, those hikes the steepest since 1998.\par \par \uc2 We also saw continued spikes for cars and trucks both new and used. New vehicle prices were up 1.7 percent or 5.4 percent in the last three months. And while used vehicle prices cooled to an increase of just two-tenths of a percent, it comes on the heels of a 7.3 percent increase in each of the previous three months, year over year, those prices up a staggering 41.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Now, housing prices showing no signs of cooling either. The CPI report has shelter prices up 2.8 percent in the last year, but prices for housing in major cities spiked 18.6 percent. That's according to S&P Case-Shiller's monthly report released last week. And, by the way, that's the biggest increase since 1987.\par \par \uc2 And, Ed, as I send it back to you, we will get updated inflation numbers next Tuesday, but I don't have to tell you probably that the prices are only expected to go higher.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Oh, thanks, Gerri. I might have to stock up on my doughnuts, 2.8 percent, if that keeps going. I wonder how long they will stay in the freezer?\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Gerri. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So how about this for some coal in your stocking? A new report saying prices' spike and uncertainty from the Delta variant could put a major damper on the holiday shopping season, keeping people from buying and keeping some key gifts from getting to the shelves.\par \par \uc2 Retail watcher Hitha Herzog joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Hitha, a lot for shoppers to deal with as we head into the final few months of the year. I want to ask you first about inflation. So, will the fact that prices are rising faster than wages mean, yes, people are making more, but the stuff that they're buying is even more expensive?\par \par \uc2 Will that slow down spending this year? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HITHA HERZOG, RETAIL WATCHER: It's certainly going to have an impact on the way people are spending.\par \par \uc2 In the past, especially when it comes to holiday, shoppers are pretty agnostic when it comes to price, because it's the holiday season. People are a little bit looser with their wallets. They want to celebrate. They're in -- they're in gift-giving mode.\par \par \uc2 But in this instance, especially as these prices get higher, as Gerri just pointed out, food prices, these general staple items that we have in our pantries and our refrigerators, are rising. So, people are going to be a little bit more aware about what's going on.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So I want to ask you about supplies, and we have had supply a crunch. There's a chip shortage. Almost everything we buy has a chip nowadays.\par \par \uc2 There's other shortages also of the stuff that we make, the products we -- the stuff that's needed for the products that we buy. Are you seeing goods, or do you believe that goods will start selling out, and then we're going to have those longer delays? Is that going to happen earlier this year?\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: Yes, I have an alarming statistic for you. It's actually a number that I was looking at as of August 30.\par \par \uc2 So there are 44 container ships that are just hanging out in Los Angeles and Long Beach. These are the two ports that are on the West Coast, 44 container ships. So those container ships have different types of goods, mostly the stuff that's coming in to start stocking the shelves for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 So we're seeing CEOs such as the CEO of Target just on the earnings call the other day, say, oh, we have about $2 billion worth of holiday merchandise coming in. The issue is, the retailers don't know what are in the containers. So they may have $2 billion worth of merchandise, but they don't know if it's $2 billion worth of men's socks.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: So if you are excited about potentially not getting what you want for the holiday, this is something to be -- to be concerned with.\par \par \uc2 And I also have another statistic too equally as alarming. I was poring over this \uc2 Oracle\uc2  report. And it said that purchases of apparel, women and children's apparel, was up 50 to 70 percent in Q1 of 2021.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: That was compared to 2020 last year. So, if you're in market to get those sweaters, apparel for the holidays, you have to do it now.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, exactly. That's what I was going to do.\par \par \uc2 Two things real quick in the last 30 seconds we have. When should we start buying? And then the price spikes, is that really going to affect how much, the total amount people are shopping for the holidays?\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: You have to start buying now. I can't reiterate this enough. I'm not trying to be alarmist, but you have to do it now because of the shortage. The supply chain shock is certainly an issue.\par \par \uc2 And with the rising prices, what's going to end up happening is that, if you delay longer, you're going to start to pay more for those items that are in short supply.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 Hitha, I appreciate it. Thank you for your insights on this. I better get my list and make that right now. Thank you. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, if markets are worried about inflation -- we talked about inflation here -- and COVID, they have a funny way of showing it. Will the green keep flowing for the rest of the year?\par \par \uc2 We will debate it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, inflation, COVID, for Wall Street, none of it seems to matter, as the stocks continue to set a record pace this year. But there is still plenty of uncertainty out there for you and your money.\par \par \uc2 So let's sort it all out with our market gurus, Keith Fitz-Gerald and Erin Gibbs.\par \par \uc2 Erin, ladies first.\par \par \uc2 So, how does the market keep going under the weight of all this inflation and the shortage of supplies of the things that we make? How does this keep going?\par \par \uc2 ERIN GIBBS, GIBBS WEALTH MANAGEMENT: So, one of the ways it has been going in the past is, we have actually seen a rotation in leadership, where it's been stocks that do well with inflation vs. growth stocks, and that switch.\par \par \uc2 So you see the market just pretty much -- it's been -- when you look at the charts, and, overall, in the broad markets, it is pretty stable, but there's been a fair amount of turmoil underneath. Now, going forward, how do we keep going? It's really all about betting that the consumer and consumer discretionary stocks come back.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of spending going on to next year, as well as industrials, that our manufacturing really comes back online, and we see that increased manufacturing, and that is what's really going to drive us to keep up that growth rate within next year.\par \par \uc2 And they...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But I want to follow up, Erin, just one thing on manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 You're talking about a huge -- if someone orders a refrigerator, sometimes, it could take four or five months, a dishwasher six months to get. Are people actually still spending on those items? Or is the length of time going to dissuade people from going and making that big purchase?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Well, so the bets within Wall Street art that we're going to get past the supply chain issues around the second quarter. So, I'm talking a little farther ahead.\par \par \uc2 Within the fourth quarter, it's less dependent on industrials. And it's really about, for the markets, we're looking 12 to 18 months ahead, not what you're sort of being very frustrated with right now. And that's what's pushing the market forward.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Keith, I remember that you have been skeptical at some of the valuations that companies have right now.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned at all about the regular investors that sort of flooded the market with a lot of money, but these businesses and some of the business plans maybe just don't justify the stock price?\par \par \uc2 KEITH FITZ-GERALD, MONEY MAP PRESS: Well, that's an interesting point, because I think the market is clearly split.\par \par \uc2 The individual investors who have latched on to the winners and stayed there have done very, very well. The biggest problem is that most investors simply don't own enough of those stocks. So, they're placing the wrong bets at precisely the right time.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So where are the right bets then?\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: Well, the right bets are things that are going to improve our world, no matter what happens with COVID and no matter what the Fed does next.\par \par \uc2 Both of those things set us up for a rocky road, albeit path of least resistance still is higher into the tail end of the year, if we can overcome the variants and if the Fed manages to thread the needle and avoid upsetting a taper tantrum.\par \par \uc2 So, to me, it's technology, it's defense stocks, it's key health care players and, of course, the financials, because as rates rise, as purchasing power moves, those are things that are going to come forward.\par \par \uc2 And to Erin's point, as industrials come online after that cycle continues, that will continue to power it too.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Erin, it seems that we have been drunk off great profits. We have seen huge, huge earnings from some of these companies.\par \par \uc2 We invest, so investors are getting used to this 40, 50 percent profit gains. What happens next year, as some of this sort of starts to level out a little bit?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Right.\par \par \uc2 Next year should look like a normal profit growth year. About, right now, we're expecting around 8 to 9 percent profit growth, so no more double digits. And, again, part of that is reopening, so travel, entertainment being fully open and then getting back to normal on that side, as well as manufacturing coming back.\par \par \uc2 So anything that can derail us getting back to normal by, let's say, the second half of next year could push those profits down, and we might see a little more shakiness within the markets. And, also, interest rates are extraordinarily important about both for the housing boom, as well as financials. And that's another thing that we constantly have to look at that could either keep us going or really pull us back next year.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, Keith, with the last 30 seconds here, say I'm a retail investor. Say I'm just an average Joe? What's the signal I'm looking forward to know when the big guys, the institutions are going to start to sell off some of this stuff? Because there's no way the green can keep going.\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: When they tell you to buy.\par \par \uc2 What they're trying to do is, there's an expression called shake the money from the trees. If you start seeing a rash of reports saying this is going to go -- bull markets don't end because they're good. Bull markets end because somebody else tells you they're even better.\par \par \uc2 So, FOMO steps in. And that's your signal to say, hey, I need to -- I need to do something here.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Keith, Erin, we will leave it there. Hopefully, we can all make some more money as we go into the holiday seasons here.\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, we're all just starting to get a little relief at the pump, just starting. Then came Ida.\par \par \uc2 The impact of the pump -- on the pump of the storm could linger. And it could be with us for a while.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. We will debate it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, Ida slamming pipelines and platforms in the Gulf. Some refineries could be offline for weeks. So what's the potential impact on gas prices now and going forward for the fall?\par \par \uc2 The only guy to talk oil with, analyst \uc2 Phil Flynn\uc2 , keeping track of it all. He joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Phil, so do you see the possibility at all of gas prices falling in the near term?\par \par \uc2 PHIL FLYNN\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Ed, probably not. In fact, I think we're creeping higher right now. We're probably going to be leaving, I mean, which is pretty amazing, because, this time of year, you and I both know we love to see a break and gasoline price when winter comes.\par \par \uc2 But this year is different. We have a lot of things in play right now. And, obviously, the ability to come back from Hurricane Ida is the main reason for our bullish forecasts on gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 Out in the Gulf of Mexico, they're doing a fantastic job trying to bring production back online. But it's almost impossible because of all the damage that we saw in Port Fourchon in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: And so it's making it more difficult to bring it back online.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, we are now more reliant on foreign sources of oil than we were under the last administration. \uc2 OPEC\uc2  had a meeting lacking the usual drama. They decided to gradually increase the supply.\par \par \uc2 Will that be enough to outpace demand and bring some of those prices down?\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: No, it won't.\par \par \uc2 And it's funny. I heard the Biden administration congratulate \uc2 OPEC\uc2 . Great job, guys, raising production. But if you look at their own numbers, Edward, the amount they're raising is still going to leave us globally in a supply deficit. So we're depending on \uc2 OPEC\uc2  to give us more oil. They're giving us more, but not enough.\par \par \uc2 It's time that we allow the U.S. producers to do their thing, start raising production, and start acting as a competitor against \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , instead of a floor mat to \uc2 OPEC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 So, as we're paying more now, about $1 more, than we were a year ago for a gallon of gas, a regular gallon of gas, the administration now says that they're going to possibly use the strategic reserves to keep gas prices down. This is from the initial shock of Ida.\par \par \uc2 But that's not a long-term solution, is it? And will it even have an effect?\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: No, it's not.\par \par \uc2 In fact, it will have an effect because there's some refineries right now that need that oil. \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2 , for example, says, hey we can start the refinery, but we don't have feedstock to run through it. So this is the right thing to do. So they're doing the right thing as far as that goes.\par \par \uc2 But over the long run, to try to use the Strategic Petroleum Reserve to manipulate prices for political purposes, which is what I'm afraid they're going to do in the future, that's the wrong thing to do. And so, if they start to do that, they will ultimately fail on that plan.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, if we look, Phil, at the price of oil, what is the lag time as we see oil drop for that to trickle down to gas actually dropping?\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: The oldest saying in the gasoline business is, it goes up like a rocket, but it falls like a feather.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: So it takes weeks sometimes for prices start coming back down.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this. Right now, we're expecting prices to creep up into the end of the year. So, we're not looking for a big break in price anytime soon. The price of oil is going to continue to be strong. \uc2 OPEC\uc2  still controls the price. They're not going to let it fall. And if they don't let it fall, that means you and I are going to be paying a higher price at the pump.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Well, Phil, thank you.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to tell Neil not to have you back into you have good news to tell us on that front.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: \uc2 Phil Flynn\uc2 , thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: See you next year.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, the U.S. is taking in thousands of Afghans after the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  seized control of the country. How resettlement agencies are helping these refugees thrive and survive here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, tens of thousands of Afghan refugees fleeing \uc2 Taliban\uc2  control are arriving in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 My next guest is helping them find safety and stability in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 John Moeller Jr. is the president and CEO of Inspiritus, a resettlement agency working with Afghans in Atlanta and Savannah. He joins us now.\par \par \uc2 John, so this seems like an overwhelming task. I mean, these people literally did not pack anything. They just ran for a U.S. plane. How do you start?\par \par \uc2 JOHN MOELLER JR., CEO, INSPIRITUS: Edward, it's a scary thing to watch happen around the country and around the world and in Afghanistan. Our hearts are hurting for these folks.\par \par \uc2 But we have a 40-year record of responding to these kinds of crises around the globe and resettling refugees. We are the local affiliate of Lutheran Immigration and Refugee Services. And we have been resettling Afghan refugees for 40 years.\par \par \uc2 And we have literally resettled hundreds of them, 378, to be exact, here in Atlanta and in Savannah. And the Atlanta and Savannah communities...\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But, John, what do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 I mean, what do you tell -- they have literally lost everything. How do you talk to them? What do you tell them? What do you say?\par \par \uc2 MOELLER: Well, that's a great question.\par \par \uc2 And we're fortunate to employ Afghan staff, individuals and former refugees themselves, medical doctors, who have come to the U.S. and now work on our team. And they know exactly what it's like to have to flee for your life. These former refugees themselves understand what it's like to arrive and have to learn a new language, a new culture, and have to start all over again.\par \par \uc2 Our staff understand what it's like to have to leave a country, not because you want to, but because it's a matter of life and death. And so these staff are uniquely qualified to provide that long welcome and to meet those folks at the airport in some cases, but to just bring them in and wrap them up in community love.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And something just struck me.\par \par \uc2 And if you're watching this, how do I, how would I approach someone like this? What should I say to them to help this process for them, if we see them out and about at the shopping -- at a shopping mall, a grocery store, whatever?\par \par \uc2 MOELLER: You know, that's exactly -- it's just Atlanta and Savannah are the most welcoming communities.\par \par \uc2 These are places where people come from around the world to settle. And so I think it's just a matter of that warm greeting, that warm welcome. And, frankly, these folks are so grateful to reach safe shores, to reach safety. And, universally, they're grateful.\par \par \uc2 And I think we ought to express our gratitude to them. We're the grateful ones, right? I mean, these are the individuals who've paid a tremendous price helping and assisting our U.S. military forces. So it's our gratitude that we get to express that and to get to see them thrive here on American soil.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Do you keep them together in clusters, so they speak the same language until they learn -- the ones that don't know English learn English?\par \par \uc2 MOELLER: Great question.\par \par \uc2 So we want to see these folks have a nurturing -- and Atlanta has that kind of community. We have got a huge Afghan community right here in Atlanta, and Savannah has the same kind of community. And those folks get to share common meals. They get to experience community with folks they recognize and might know from their prior life.\par \par \uc2 But that's exactly what folks need to kind of get that good start.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: How many people are you expecting to help?\par \par \uc2 MOELLER: Well, we just received our first family this week, a family with five children, four daughters.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm a dad of daughters. I can't imagine going through what they have been through to get their entire family out. We're expecting literally dozens more in the coming weeks. These are typically large families. And these families do so well here in Atlanta and in Savannah.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the families that we work with, particularly in Savannah, that resettlement site has a 100 percent self-sufficiency rate within six months. So these families are doing incredibly well. And in Atlanta, it's more than 90 percent within six months are completely self-sufficient.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, I'm curious.\par \par \uc2 They have been through the vetting process already. But does your group also look for maybe some radical folks that may have infiltrated who actually need help?\par \par \uc2 MOELLER: You know, that's a great question.\par \par \uc2 So, we only work with Afghan refugees that have been approved by the federal government. So, we don't get into the vetting process. We understand that the vetting process and the approval process is thorough. And, in fact, we have done nothing but work with the most incredible individuals and families, former doctors, engineers, truck drivers, intelligence officers and their families.\par \par \uc2 So we're working with the cream of the crop.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: John, some really great work that you're doing. And I know those folks need a lot of help. We're getting tens of thousands of them here in the U.S. as they run through this process here.\par \par \uc2 And the community of Georgia, that's a perfect community for it, that it's a great people that are there.\par \par \uc2 John, I really appreciate you for coming on. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOELLER: Oh, Edward, we appreciate the opportunity to tell the story.\par \par \uc2 These folks are heroes, and they deserve a hero's welcome here in the U.S. And thanks to our donors and supporters for giving it to them.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And, John, I got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 That's where the end of -- this is the end of the Labor Day, ending with a labor of love for those people who helped our military in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Neil will be back tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Let's enjoy the rest of your holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 "THE FIVE" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210906eh9600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Thirteen American Troops Dead, Biden Fires No One; GOP Must Rein In Bloated Pentagon Spending; Biden Lays Groundwork For New Refugee Crisis; The Biden Admin's Transparency Problem; Kirby: It's Not The Time To Release ISIS-K Names; Officer Who Shot Ashli Babbitt During January 6th Capitol Riots Gives Interview Explaining His Actions; Hurricane Ida Devastates Parts of Louisiana; President Biden Gives Press Conference On Government Response To Hurricane Ida; New Study Indicates Natural Immunity Effective Against Delta Variant Of COVID-19\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham \par \uc2 7,499 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY, FOX NEWS HOST: Laura Ingraham takes it from here. And my heart struggles, how are you?\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm OK, Hannity, but we're going to pick it up where you left off. There's a lot. There's a lot to cover. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 I am Laura Ingraham. This is THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from Washington. The Biden's administration's mix of sheer incompetence and outright arrogance is laid bare. We're going to document all of it tonight. And we begin with "Building Back Weaker," that's the focus of "Tonight's Angle."\par \par \uc2 Now, of all the heart wrenching photos I've seen over the past few weeks, this one hit me right in the gut. Troops paying tribute to their brothers and sisters who died last Thursday, knowing full well that it could have been one of them. And then the somber scene at Dover during the dignified transfer, one that's been carried out 1000s of times, during the past 20 years of combat in Afghanistan and Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Parents, wives, husbands, children, even, lining up to greet the caskets. For many, the grief was too much to bear. Now, each life lost of the 13 and all of the others, all of them, every single one mattered. They were all tethered together in a strong, patriotic spirit of sacrifice. Many of them came from families with long histories of military service.\par \par \uc2 Today, I had the great privilege of speaking on the phone with a mom whose son died last week. Every one of these young people were exceptional human being she said, Every single one. Please let the people know that.\par \par \uc2 But now, the sadness for many of these families is mixed with rage, and justifiably so.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATHY MCCOLLUM, MOTHER OF FALLEN MARINE: Genius child, an IQ of 156. He could have done anything he wanted to do in the military, and he chose to be a marine sniper. But I never thought in a million years he would die for nothing, for nothing because a feckless, dementia-ridden piece of crap decided he wanted a photo op on September 11. Every Democratic that's listening, you did this to my son to prop up a disgusting human being.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, there's a lot to be angry about. The deadly idiotic handling of our Afghanistan withdrawal will be a permanent stain on America's foreign policy and our military leadership. When the predictable chaos started to unfold at the airport, the Biden team was off on summer break.\par \par \uc2 We learned this weekend that the Secretary of State was in the Hamptons with the fancy people. Biden was with Jill, and no cabinet officials at Camp David. The press secretary had a vacation autoreply set up on our White House e-mail. Apparently none of them thought something important might be happening in Afghanistan, while they were out having fun. It's disgraceful.\par \par \uc2 This isn't the junior varsity team. This is the team of people who couldn't make JV. And sadder for our troops and their families is that the kids cut from the JV squad are actually in charge of military planning as well.\par \par \uc2 Most Americans are not familiar with General McKenzie in charge of our Central Command. He's one of the most powerful military figures in America today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS: After 20 years of war, you've served there, you've now watched the last troops leave, you've lost troops in recent days, how did it feel leaving Afghanistan to the very group that you overthrew 20 years ago - the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GEN. KENNETH MCKENZIE, COMMANDER OF THE U.S. CENTRAL COMMAND: I was very conflicted, actually. But I would tell you, I was pretty much focused on the task at hand. I'll have days ahead to actually think about that. I'm sure I will do that in the future. But right now I'm pretty much consumed with the operational task at hand. That's a huge question, and I am going to be thinking about that in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It seemed kind of like he was smiling at one point. Maybe it's just me, that's what I saw. But that's it? That's all he has to say? He's had years to think about this. Imagine this guy leading your son or daughter into battle. And the generals on TV, by the way, are just as useless.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GENERAL MARK HERTLING, CNN MILITARY ANALYST: This has been a very difficult 20 years. I was in the basement of the Pentagon - the other situation room when this war started. Watching our allies and the U.S. forces fight this conflict over the last 20 years with the ups and downs, has brought a whole lot of thought and emotion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What does this mean? A whole lot of thought and emotion, what does that mean? The hard reality is, we lost. The U.S. military was given a mission to accomplish. It was given a series of missions actually. And under this military leadership over the last 20 years it failed to carry them out. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  won the war and we're retreating in disgrace. That's what happened. Even though none of our made for TV generals want to say so.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we all love our troops. No one blames them. The best, the bravest soldiers, they can't be expected to succeed with weak and ineffective military leaders. Abraham Lincoln had to fire a whole series of generals until he found a team that would lead the army of the Potomac to victory.\par \par \uc2 We never found a general who could win in Afghanistan and so we lost. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  one, and we're going to have to live with those facts. Now, believe me, I do not regret the pullout. Others do. It's long been clear that the Pentagon is simply incapable of advancing U.S. interests in the Middle East. We gave the generals every chance. The American people have spent more than $6.5 trillion on the war on terror and has little to show for it.\par \par \uc2 American soldiers, reservists and Special Ops, all were asked to do three, four, some of them five tours of duty. There was enormous sacrifice and suffering with huge financial costs to the American taxpayers. And in the end, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is left in charge, controlling more territory. And now with bragging rights - see the celebratory gunfire, laughing on camera? That they drove out and defeated what used to be regarded as the most powerful military in the world.\par \par \uc2 Americans deserve a full accounting of the mistakes that were made from Bush to Biden. Trump was really the only president who really wanted to end the war in a manner that wouldn't be an embarrassing disaster, but he never had a secretary of defense who agreed with him or even respected him.\par \par \uc2 The problems were widespread, with the military often closing ranks to cover them up. Many of the moral and ethical lapses of military leadership were blatant, they felt invulnerable, a lot of CEOs do as well, we know that. And no one should be surprised. After all, there's been precious little accountability for their failures in combat operations. So why would they have to be accountable on anything else?\par \par \uc2 They rely on our love and admiration of the enlisted men and women and their bravery to insulate them at the top from answering for years of mismanagement, misrepresentations and failures. Conservatives say that we shouldn't keep increasing the budgets of departments like education or HUD without tangible results and metrics being met. So why shouldn't the Pentagon be held to that same standard?\par \par \uc2 We have to demand better. Our country is in real danger. Our military isn't the Peace Corps or a refugee transportation service. We love these photos, cradling babies, handing out water bottles, it's wonderful humanitarian work, and we applaud it. But in essence, it's not the purpose of the U.S. Military. We need generals who can win wars. And we should never tolerate the type of failure we've seen over the past two decades.\par \par \uc2 Finally, we can't allow the Biden team to use the collapse of Afghanistan as yet another excuse to circumvent our immigration laws. The families of the fallen and the American taxpayers deserve to know why the lives of certain Afghans were prioritized over the lives of Americans still in the country?\par \par \uc2 The Biden Administration has proven itself completely untrustworthy and incompetent. And now, we have no reason to believe that they're going to handle the relocation of Afghans any better. Americans are very compassionate. We're very generous. But Americans also have the right to insist that our laws be followed. We should be told where the refugees will eventually be placed, by what legal authority they're here, and how their sizeable expenses will be covered.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the Biden people don't want accountability for their failures. They just keep saying, now's not the time to point fingers. Well, they're wrong. This is precisely when we should be demanding answers. We've already waited too long. we've wasted too much money. We've lost too many lives. Our patience is that an end, and that's "The Angle."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton who served in Afghanistan. Senator, what can you all do to demand accountability on behalf of the vast majority of Americans who are disgusted by the past few weeks?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Laura, I share your sentiment that my patience has tapped here. For the last two weeks my office, like so many congressional offices, was focused on trying to get American citizens out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden broke that promise. He promised that \uc2 Taliban\uc2  we'd be out by August 31st. He promised the Americans in Afghanistan, he'd stay to get them out. He kept his promise to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . He broke his promise to Americans.\par \par \uc2 Now that all troops are out of Afghanistan it is the time to demand answers, to hold accountable all those officials who created the terrible conditions that we've seen in Afghanistan for the last several weeks. Some of these things are very simple questions to which there are knowable answers.\par \par \uc2 What did the military recommend to Joe Biden about Bagram Air Base? Who decided to close Bagram Air Base? What were the arbitrary troop numbers that were kept that at the end that led to the closing of Bagram air base? There's a lot of very simple, but very critical questions to be asked and their answers are knowable. They're sitting on a piece of paper somewhere in the Pentagon, in the White House and it's time for Congress to demand those answers in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 It's also time, as you say, to ensure that we are conducting rigorous oversight of the resettlement of refugees in this country. Look, we all want to protect and welcome those Afghans who served alongside American servicemembers. We've heard from so many people in my office, service members and veterans, who vouched personally for their translator or their interpreter.\par \par \uc2 But we evacuated more than 125,000 Afghans at last count. Many of these had very little, if any connection, to our service members over the last 20 years. Look, I'm glad they're out of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's grips. But they have to qualify under American law before we admit them into this country. They can't be a threat to this country.\par \par \uc2 And like the Trump Administration kept many Syrian refugees in Jordan, leveraging our relationship there, that's probably what we're going to need to do with most of the Afghan refugees who are in places like Qatar, Turkey, or Albania or other countries. I'm glad that they're out of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's grip, but it's not our responsibility to welcome every single one to the country if they do not contribute directly to our military forces there over the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. Well, we keep hearing things about Afghanis who are at risk, need to be out of the country, well, that's millions of people. That's not 120,000 people. We're talking several 100,000 or millions of people. And I again, everyone feels terrible for everyone suffering around the globe, but there are laws in place.\par \par \uc2 And Congress has a responsibility to make sure the laws are either changed, that refugee caps are lifted, and pretty much anyone can come here and live here, if they're Afghani and they don't want to be there. That law has to be written, passed by Congress. They have to live with it in the next campaign, and Biden has to sign it into law. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Yes, Laura, part of our oversight, we'll be looking backwards at the failures that created these conditions in Afghanistan, but an important part of our oversight in Congress is exactly what you suggest, ensuring that the Biden Administration is following the law.\par \par \uc2 Those Afghans who served alongside of American troops, who have those troops vouching for them, so many of whom we've heard from over the last two weeks, are welcomed to our country. But, again, more than 125,000 Afghans were evacuated out of Afghanistan--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: They're in places that may not have American standard of living, but at least they're safe from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, one thing them--\par \par \uc2 COTTON: That doesn't mean that we have to bring every single one to America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, one of them is already - "The Washington Times" reporting on one Afghan who was convicted of rape, and deported and then made it back to America on one of the evacuation flights. Again, I just don't trust these people. To vet people properly or to handle this properly, there has to be rigorous oversight.\par \par \uc2 Let me get to one comment from General Mckenzie, that stuck out today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I will tell you, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  had been very - very pragmatic and very business-like as we have approached this withdrawal. They were actually very helpful and useful to us as we closed down operations. They established a firm perimeter outside of the airfield to prevent people from coming on the airfield during our departure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, we have 13 Americans dead and we have the CENTCOM Commander praising the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . Your response.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Yes, well, if the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  was such a good security partner that was carefully vetting everyone who got close to our troops at the airport, I don't understand how a suicide bomber got through their outer perimeter to our inner perimeter. These are the kinds of questions that Congress is going to be probing in the days ahead to figure out exactly how we got into the situation where we had to cooperate with a barbarian group of outlaws in Afghanistan, simply to get American citizens out of the country.\par \par \uc2 And now that we're actually sort of like acting as if that's something that we can be proud of, or that we should even celebrate, that we cooperated with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . We should have never gotten in that position from the beginning. And it all goes back to Joe Biden's terrible execution of this decision to withdraw our troops from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator Cotton, thank you. And Biden and his team's staggering ineptitude has Afghan war veteran sounding the alarm. That includes my next guests who wrote recently in "The New York Times," "When the president spoke on Thursday night after the attack on the Abbey Gate, he nodded at sacrifice. That's fine. But what matters is to truly understand the value of service. If you do, you don't send troops to die on a poorly planned and poorly executed mission. Failing to understand the value of service results and incompetence."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is retired Marine Elliot Ackerman. He served five tours in Iraq and Afghanistan, has received both the Bronze and silver Stars. Elliot, your reaction to just what we heard today from General McKenzie, understanding what happened last Thursday to 13 of our bravest young men and women. Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 ELLIOT ACKERMAN, RETIRED U.S. MARINE: Well, I think individuals who wear the uniform, as well as us as citizens, have the right to expect a certain amount of competence in the execution of these decisions. And what we see in the last two weeks has been nothing less than incompetence.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people have trotted out for a long time, is this going to be Saigon? Is it not going to be Saigon? This is way worse than Saigon. I mean - but you just look at a map, and at least Vietnam had hundreds of miles of coastline. Afghanistan is a landlocked country. So why would you begin shutting down all the airports like Bagram, Kandahar, Herat and leave us with only a single point of eagerness in Kabul?\par \par \uc2 So this has been mismanaged from the very beginning. And frankly, as Senator Cotton mentioned, fallen to veterans, these journalists, activists to try to get Afghans out in an extremely ad hoc manner. And with regards to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , I mean, if they're so organized and pragmatic - we just saw the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  flying a Black Hawk helicopter, above Kandahar with a dead body hanging from its bottom. Now are those our pragmatic partners? I think this whole thing is absurd. And, frankly, it's the most shameful thing I've seen in my adult life.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Were you at the airport yourself as you were helping some of these evacuations that were underway?\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: No, I've been I've been doing this - basically on the side along with dozens of other folks who have deep connections to Afghanistan. Coordinating on Signal, online with - I've had - I have - if you look at my notes, I had notes from hundreds of backends, many people I don't even know just begging for help. This is like watching 1000s of people thrown into a black hole all at once.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I had a former Marine - a combat ops soldier - excuse, I'm botching it, but a former Marine was over there, Elliot, and he was working morning, noon, and night, and he's an old friend of mine. So he's calling me going. It's so much worse what's happening on the ground. He's at the airport for only 24 hours. But he's back in another country helping.\par \par \uc2 He said it's so much worse than it's being shown on television. He said, like, he didn't even have words for it. He said it's - something bad is going to happen. And of course, two days later, that's what happened to our troops. Elliott, final thoughts. What next?\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: I think when what next is that - America is less safe based off of the - particularly the execution of this evacuation. I think our credibility with our allies and partners outside of Afghanistan needs to be rebuilt. And I think this country needs to do some real reckoning or how we got into this position where we are negotiating from a position of weakness with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . I mean, these people are thugs and barbarians. And we need to remember who we are, and we're the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Do you agree Elliot that there needs to be a reckoning and reform that follows. Maybe we need more civilian leadership at the top of the Pentagon, maybe we need a new approach, because obviously 20 years of warfighting with the greatest army and greatest troops around and all this technology, and we weren't able to win.\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: Well, I think, ultimately, we need to ask ourselves what is winning? I mean, the United States has a very specific role in the world. And if we walk away from Afghanistan - we saw this story before. In 2011, all the troops came home from Iraq, and they wound up right back there 2014, because we had a foolish policy. We need to grow up.\par \par \uc2 There are people in this world who want to do us harm. And we need to accept that. And so ultimately, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  doesn't talk about winning - winning the war against crime. It takes continued vigilance, and I'm sorry, it requires the same thing internationally.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And we need to bring the people along with us. So politically we have to make sure that people of the country are with us in every single conflict. Elliot, your piece in "The New York Times" was stunning. And congrats on all your success and what you're doing and writing and everything else. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 A Biden approved drone strike killed a family of 10, including seven kids, and we keep - being told it's ISIS, but the administration won't give us any details. Why is this? Glenn Greenwald has reaction in moments. And Jonathan Turley was alarmed by an admission from the Capitol Police officer who shot Ashli Babbitt. He's here to tell us what it is.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GEN. HANK TAYLOR, JOINT STAFF DEPUTY DIRECTOR FOR REGIONAL OPERATIONS: Significant secondary explosions from the targeted vehicle indicated the presence of a substantial amount of explosive material. We are aware of reports of civilian casualties and we take these reports very seriously and we are continuing to assess the situation.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We take efforts - take steps from the United States to avoid civilian casualties in every scenario, and probably more than almost any country in the world. I can't speak to you or confirm the numbers or cases of civilian casualties in this case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She can't, but "The New York Times" is reporting that 10 civilians, including seven children were killed by a drone strike in Kabul. Now, the Biden Pentagon assures us that this strike and others neutralized ISIS-K terrorists, who they of course, can't tell us anything about or identify. So what's going on here?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Independent Journalist Glenn Greenwald. Glenn, no one in the media seems too concerned about what looks like to be another intelligence failure. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 GLENN GREENWALD, INDEPENDENT JOURNALIST: It's pretty bizarre. I mean, right from the beginning there was a huge question lurking, at least for me, which is a month ago, Joe Biden assured everybody that there was very little chance that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  would overrun the Afghan National Security Forces and would take over the country.\par \par \uc2 Of course, that's exactly what happened five weeks after he gave that assurance, which seems to suggest that if they weren't lying, they know very little about what's happening in this country we've been occupying for 20 years.\par \par \uc2 And then suddenly, we're told that they were able with pinpoint precision twice in a row to identify the location of two different ISIS-K planners or potters or commanders, which seems very inconsistent with the fact that they seem to have no idea what's going on in this country. They wouldn't give us the names of who these supposedly important people were. And they just asserted that there were no civilian casualties.\par \par \uc2 And unlike in the Trump years, when all these media outlets loved to say Trump says this with no evidence, Trump falsely claims - all their skepticism evaporated overnight, and they just repeated the fact that we got our target, they were no civilian casualties. And there was never any evidence for it and no questioning of it at all.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the Pentagon is just they're not going to release the names - the identities of these terrorists. And it doesn't look like we're going to learn much more about it than we already have. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: There's probably a time when we can talk to you about the names, that's not the time right now. We are still dealing with, as we saw from last night's rocket attacks, very real, ongoing threats. And I think we're doing what we believe to be the prudent thing with respect to the release of information. We're giving you as much as we can, and as close to real time as we can. But we're not going to be able to give you everything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Glenn, this is the new trick. They say, well, now's not the time. Now's not the time to demand accountability, now's not the time to ask questions, now is not the time to give us any identities. But the fact is, it's never a good time.\par \par \uc2 GREENWALD: Look, this is one of the most major national security events, obviously, in the last decade or two, if not, in our lifetimes. People have different opinions about whether we should have left or shouldn't, how we should have done it. But we all have the right to know what actually is going on, what happened in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And remember, the media during the Trump years made a big flamboyant showing of the importance of transparency. "The Washington Post" actually adopted a motto, specifically for the Trump era, that read "Democracy Dies in Darkness."\par \par \uc2 And so here's John Kirby, the Pentagon's spokesperson telling you that you have to believe them when they tell you that they killed the really important people involved in these attacks. But you can't even know who these people are. Why not? Why can't we know that? We're just being asked to accept their word on blind faith.\par \par \uc2 And every time this happens, Laura, where you go and you kill an entire family, just wipe them off the face of the planet - young children - it isn't that anybody questions whether or not we have the right to do a retaliatory strike after that hideous attack in Kabul? Of course we did. But every time that happens, it generates so much anti-American hatred and anti-American sentiment around the world.\par \par \uc2 We need to know, the very least, whether it was actually a legitimate strike or whether it was just something that was done for Biden to get rid of a bad news cycle and to prevent people from saying that he's weak. And the lack of interest on the part of the media is amazing in that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, there's a lot of kids - there's a lot of kids who are dead. And I think we should know whether our drone strike actually killed them. It's basic question. Glenn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to another tragic killing, the shooting of Air Force vet Ashli Babbitt by a Capitol Police officer whose identity has finally been revealed. His name is Lieutenant Michael Byrd, and of course, he recently got the all-star treatment on NBC News. But during this interview, he said something that caught our attention.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. MICHAEL BYRD, OFFICER WHO SHOT ASHLI BABBITT: It was later found out that the subject did not have a weapon, but there was no way to know that at that time. And I could not fully see her hands or what was in the backpack, or what the intentions were. But they had shown violence leading up to that point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But wait a minute, the media told us Byrd's actions was exonerated by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and \uc2 Capitol police\uc2 . How can that be with what he just said? Raising red flags.\par \par \uc2 Here with me now is Jonathan Turley, \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law prof and FOX News contributor. Jonathan, I know you watched this closely. Your reaction to this, his statement? And does it blow up the whole he was exonerated narrative?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, \uc2 GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY\uc2  LAW PROFESSOR: It was an astonishing interview. I wrote about it in "The Hill" because he basically went through the list of what you are not allowed to do as the basis for lethal force. He said I didn't see a weapon, I didn't know her intent, I didn't know what was on her back, I shot her. And obviously, all of us can sympathize in being in a closed room in the middle of a riot. Babbitt was engaged in criminal conduct, she was illegally trespassing, she was disobeying police orders, there was a great deal of damage, and she was going through a window at that time. So all of that is criminal conduct, but you can't just shoot criminal trespassers. You can't shoot people damaging government property. You have to show an imminent threat to yourself or to others.\par \par \uc2 And here you have an officer who actually says I really couldn't see her hands, I didn't know if she was armed or not. According to that interpretation he could have shot the next 20 people through the window. He could have shot hundreds of people that day on the Hill. But only one officer did so, that was Lieutenant Byrd. All the other officers, including officers who were attacked, who were injured, refrained from using lethal force. And I say that with a great deal of respect, because we demand of the officers a level of restraint and courage that most of us couldn't muster. It's a really difficult job. But what he described in the interview falls way outside of the standards for the use of lethal force.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, outside the parameters. Here is more from the NBC interview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Based on your training, In that case, does it matter that the person was armed or not?\par \par \uc2 LT. MICHAEL BYRD, OFFICER WHO SHOT ASHLI BABBITT: According to law, it does not. I know based on my training and my policy, what I did was appropriate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jonathan, all fine according to his training.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yes. Yes, I don't know where that comes from. I've heard from officers around the country who couldn't believe the interview, because it is true that you can use an authorized level of lethal force with someone how is unarmed if you believe they were unarmed and it was reasonable. We've had cases were officers thought someone was armed, maybe they threw a weapon away seconds before. We had a case like that in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 But he was describing something very different. He was saying he didn't have any idea whether she was armed. He had no idea what her intent was. All he knew is she was coming through a window against police orders. And under that argument, you could have shot hundreds of people in Seattle, Portland. There were police stations that were taken over. People went through those doors. Under this standard, every one of those protesters in Portland and Seattle that went into those government buildings destroying property --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Could have been shot.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: -- could have been shot. Does that track? Right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No. It's imminent bodily harm. Someone coming through a window, you're essentially shooting blind when you can't see if there's any weapon. Jonathan, your piece was so important, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, if you thought Biden's Afghanistan response has been a disaster, wait until you see how he is handling the hurricane so far. I'm talking about Ida. "Seen and Unseen" with Raymond next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we expose the big cultural stories of the day. And joining us for hurricane Ida edition our own personal Ida refugee, FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo who joins us from Florida. Ray, we're glad you're safe. But the president has been staging pressers, I've noticed, to assure everyone that he has this whole situation in hand, or his administration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And the hard week of the FEMA team, all of whom are gathered behind me, many of whom are gathered behind me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it was 20,000 employees. Laura, what's 20,000 give or take the handful behind him? But this presser ended in classic Biden style.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not supposed to take any questions, but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, on Afghanistan --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not going to answer Afghanistan now.\par \par \uc2 (CROSS TALK)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you, thank you. I know there's no group of Americans who know how dangerous it is more than you guys do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: First of all, he seems lost. He's lost.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He's coming, he's going. He can't find the exit.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Where is he going?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Exactly. But Laura, who is telling the president there are questions he's not supposed to answer? I want to see that list. Why is the president of the United States being controlled this way? He should come out and tell us the facts. He said he would shoot from the shoulder, give us the truth. We don't know what to believe it these press conferences.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And today Biden was at it again, Raymond. He was making even less sense, if that's possible, during a press conference call with governors and mayors who are all affected, of course, by the hurricane.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I know you've got a lot to manage in your states, but the fact is that -- and we know hurricane Ida had the potential to cause massive, massive damage. And that's exactly what we saw. And I'm here with my senior advisor and a boy who knows Louisiana very, very well, and New Orleans. And Cedric Richmond.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: My boy who knows Louisiana? This is a former congressman, our African American congressman from Louisiana. Why is the president calling him "boy"? And why is no one concerned about this, to say nothing of the syntax, ah, ah, ah? This is a sad display, Laura. And at a time when people are hurting, this does not engender confidence.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: My favorite, though, Raymond, is the mask hanging from his ear because that's always a sign of someone who is fully in control. Was that a mask hanging down? That's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He seems completely programmed, Laura, and unable to even read his own 25 point font script.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Now, Cedric, who are we going to next? Is Mayor Cantrell on? Mayor Cantrell, are you on? I don't think she was able to get connected.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think so, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: They can't even get the Zoom call right, Laura. The reason Cantrell isn't on the line is because we in New Orleans are not going to have electricity restored for at least three weeks. And by the way, we had failures, electric failures throughout the hurricane when the pumps didn't work. That is after you, the American taxpayer, paid $14 billion to increase and upgrade our levees and our pumping system. They still didn't really work.\par \par \uc2 Laura, the most galling thing about all of this, all day I've been watching all the networks -- they are focused on Afghanis and bringing them to the United States. We can evacuate Kabul, but we can't evacuate our own people from the path of a natural disaster. They worry about Afghanis at risk. I'm worried about Americans and New Orleanians and Louisianians at risk tonight, and it seems no one is worried about them now that the storm has moved northward. They are hurting, and they are dying in New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But Raymond, don't worry, because LaToya Cantrell has it all covered. Raymond, thank you for everything, and we will pray for the people of New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 A bombshell new study confirms what this show has been telling you for months -- recovering from COVID could give you far better protection that a vaccine. My medicine cabinet breaks down the incredible new research, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A groundbreaking new study from Israel finds those with natural immunity were up to 13 times less likely to contract the Delta variant then those who were given two jabs of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Dr. Jay Bhattacharya, Professor of Medicine at Stanford University, and Dr. Harvey Risch, Professor of Epidemiology at the Yale School of Public Health. Dr. B., this flies in the face of the political and scientific establishment who are pushing now vaccine passports. Why do they continue to ignore natural immunity?\par \par \uc2 DR. JAY BHATTACHARYA, \uc2 STANFORD UNIVERSITY\uc2  EPIDEMIOLOGIST: It really does not make any sense. We've known for a very long time, at least since the middle of last year, that natural immunity was quite effective. We knew that basically because we saw so few documented reinfections. As the public health community continues to deny the efficacy of natural immunity, it really undermines confidence in them. And vaccine passports make absolutely no sense in light of this. Frankly, it might even be safer to be in a room filled with unvaccinated, COVID recovered patients that it is to be in a room filled with vaccinated patients. It's incredible. I really think this is amazing study, but even more amazing is that continuing denial of natural immunity by the public health community.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Former FDA commissioner \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 , who is on the board of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , is quick to dismiss natural immunity.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 , FORMER FDA COMMISSIONER: I'd be careful about concluding that natural immunity is more robust than the immunity conferred by the vaccine based on that one study. I think the question of whether one is better than the other, frankly, from a policy standpoint, isn't that material. I still think the balance of the evidence tips the favor of the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 The spike protein in the vaccine is stabilized, so it makes a better target for the immune system than the spike protein in natural infection where the virus tries to hide it from our bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Risch, take it away.\par \par \uc2 DR. HARVEY RISCH, YALE SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH: Well, that one study is now no longer the only study. A study from Qatar came out a couple days later that showed exactly the same thing in some 800,000, 900,000 people showing that vaccine immunity wanes over three to six months. And you can't substitute actual data for theories, and Dr. Gottlieb has his theories, but the actual data show otherwise.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Dr. Bhattacharya, Dr. Fauci has spoken out about the booster shot now, and apparently this is going to become the norm. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: It is entirely conceivable that when all is said and done, the standard regiment will be a three dose shot for the MRNA and a two dose shot for J&J.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But Dr. Bhattacharya, does the antibody response not ultimately wear off after three? Or does it become permanent?\par \par \uc2 BHATTACHARYA: We have no data whatsoever on the efficacy, the necessity of boosters. Actually, one thing, the vaccines, they will protect you even if the antibodies decline, so if you're elderly and you have not had COVID, the vaccine will protect you against severe disease and death. That's a good thing. There is no data on the boosters. I have no idea what Dr. Fauci is talking about, because no data has been released at all, clinical data, demonstrating the efficacy of the boosters.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Dr. Risch, on that same topic, Israel's version of the vaccine passport we've been talking about for now a year-and-a-half almost, it's already hitting an expiration date because starting October 1st, the green pass will expire six months after the holder received the second or third dose, meaning those without a booster will no longer be eligible for this green pass, Dr. Risch, once six months have passed since their second dose. So where does this all end?\par \par \uc2 RISCH: It doesn't end. The vaccine passports is a useless idea. And in Israel they have already seen 15 cases of people who have had boosters and have gotten COVID after the boosters, after the third shot. So this doesn't end, it just keeps percolating around with trying to make policies over something that's meaningless. It just doesn't work.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Dr. Bhattacharya, aren't we now at the point where we have to get used to living with COVID? The vulnerable getting vaccinated, the younger people are almost fine with COVID unless they have an underlying condition, take vitamin d, zinc, all the things that have worked, and take better common sense precautions at this point?\par \par \uc2 BHATTACHARYA: Laura, we have no technology to stop the spread of COVID. Lockdowns do not stop the spread of COVID. They cause devastating harms. The vaccines, they can protect you against severe disease, but they will not stop the spread of COVID. And no many how many boosters you do, we do not have a technology that will stop the spread of COVID. You're absolutely right, Laura. We have to learn to live with the virus.\par \par \uc2 We've made so much progress. we're better at treating it. We have a vaccine that can protect the vulnerable against severe disease. We should be celebrating good news as opposed to this dread with the vaccine mandates and violating people's rights. It just doesn't make any sense.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's not working in Australia or New Zealand. They keep getting more cases the more they lock down. Gentlemen, thank you most.\par \par \uc2 And Blinken pulls a Biden at today's briefing. The Last Bite explains.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: As the U.S. retreats from Afghanistan, Secretary Blinken flees from the press poodles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Thanks for listening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He said thank you for listening. That was a weird ending. Remind me again about how he is supposed to go toe to toe with the CCP. We're in trouble if that's it for our diplomatic corps.\par \par \uc2 And don't forget, set your DVR so you always stay connected with us. And finally, never forget to show that you understand what's at stake by wearing your Freedom Matters gear. We've got a lot of reaction to the gear out in California. A lot of people wanted it. I said you got to go to LauraIngraham.com to get it. It's all for charity, and made in the USA, which we love.\par \par \uc2 I want you to always remember, I'm going to say this every show, America now and forever.\par \par \uc2 Greg Gutfeld takes all the magic from here. Make sure to watch.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020210831eh8u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Storm Targets Florida; Disney Beats Earnings Estimates; Restaurants Dealing With COVID Restrictions; Border Battle; U.S. Troops Heading to Afghanistan; Inflation Rising; Office Return Delayed\b0\par\par\uc2 Amesh Adalja, Rick Reichmuth, Charles Payne, Jonathan Hunt, Lucas  Tomlinson, David Lee Miller, Susan Li, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,102 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 August 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, about those plans to return to work, well, a statement from \uc2 Facebook\uc2 : Put it back, a lot, not a week, not two weeks, not a month, not even this year.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  announcing a short time ago that it's going to delay its in person return until at least January of next year, following the likes of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . But that's just for those looking to start next year. There are already scores of companies that have indicated an immediate pickup in activity, in person activity, as soon as Labor Day that is off. Delayed at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  and CNN and NBC and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  and Apple and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and a host of others, sending a signal that is whining as we speak that the getting back to work in person isn't going to happen anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 We are on top of that and on top of inflation. You probably know better than anyone what it's costing you to buy everything from groceries to, well, you name it, gasoline and all of that. Inflation at the wholesale front is now running at a clip of almost 8 percent year over year.\par \par \uc2 So, you have been seeing wages go up in the vicinity of 4 percent. But the things you want to buy, even on the wholesale level, are double that.\par \par \uc2 What to make of all of that and the trend that this could be setting with \uc2 Susan Li\uc2  with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Susan.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, so that wholesale price tells us it's not just consumers, but companies are also paying a lot more to make their products.\par \par \uc2 And you can bet, if companies have to pay more, consumers will have to pay more. Producer prices, as you mentioned, jumping about 8 percent of July, wholesale prices, as you want to call them. And that is the fastest increase since 2010. And that's just a day after consumer prices, what you and I are pay, coming in at 5.4 percent, still the highest rate since 2008.\par \par \uc2 Now, chip shortages, lack of labor, also the COVID impact on supply chains, that's being blamed for the higher prices that companies are shelling out for. And numerous items have gone up in price for both companies and consumers. Steel accounted for a fifth for the surge in producer prices.\par \par \uc2 You also have energy prices going up by the most in four months. Airline tickets have jumped for consumers. Same for food, coffee and cars. So, as a result, companies are hiking prices on the products that they sell. So, from diapers to \uc2 Clorox\uc2  wipes, and you have beer prices going up because a can and barley costs more, Yogurts, ice cream, burrito, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and even washing machines.\par \par \uc2 Now, the spread of the Delta variant means that consumers are going to go out less and take less flights. So that means prices might ease off, something that we have heard from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and Chairman \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 , who have all said that they anticipated these price increases to be temporary.\par \par \uc2 But our FOX News poll tells us that more than 85 percent of Americans are concerned about inflation, with 70 percent of American households saying grocery prices and grocery price increases are causing hardships in the household. So are gas prices at the pump, which are at the highest in seven years.\par \par \uc2 And you know, Neil, that Americans vote with checkbook issues like the economy and their well-being.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Susan Li\uc2 , thank you very, very much for that.\par \par \uc2 We should also point out that, as that was again spiking, I'm talking prices, so too all of these COVID cases that are now prompting a number of companies to reassess where they stand and where this economic recovery for a lot of them stands.\par \par \uc2 David Lee Miller\uc2  following it very, very closely at La Guardia International Airport -- \uc2 David Lee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, as the number of new COVID cases continues to increase because of the Delta variant, the number of airline passengers is likely to decrease, costing the industry millions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 The country's third largest airline, Southwest, now says it is seeing already a drop in the number of passengers that are booking flights, as well as more cancellations. In a revised estimate sent to the Securities and Exchange Commission, Southwest says it expects revenue for August to be down between 15 to 20 percent compared to pre COVID-2019.\par \par \uc2 And the airline's projection for the month of September, that's even more dire, revenue down by as much as 25 percent. The airline did have some good news. Travel demand for the Labor Day holiday remains -- and I quote now -- "healthy."\par \par \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2  also expects its business to be impacted by the virus. Company guidance released last week said -- and I quote -- "We have noted softening in the level of bookings over seasonal norms that we believe is directly related to the increased COVID-19 case numbers associated with the Delta variant."\par \par \uc2 An economics professor who follows the industry says a small reduction in the number of business travelers will have a big impact on the airline's bottom line.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATRICK GOURLEY, UNIVERSITY OF NEW HAVEN: Most air travelers in the United States domestically are actually leisure travelers, but most of the profit that an airline next is actually from business travel.\par \par \uc2 That's because business travelers are much more likely to book last minute, which then sees higher fares.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLER: And, according to the TSA, the number of travelers in just the last few weeks is trending down. But, significantly, it is nowhere near its lows at the height of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And later this hour, we should learn just how the virus and the Delta variant is affecting the theme park and movie industry, when \uc2 Disney\uc2  reports its quarterly earnings -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you very much, \uc2 David Lee\uc2 , on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Well, obviously this reverberates, the spike in cases and how company after company, public institution after public institution, even the U.S. Health Department advocating mandates or vaccines for its entire staff.\par \par \uc2 This is a trend. We have already seen the likes of \uc2 Google\uc2  and Amtrak Walmart, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , \uc2 Cisco\uc2 , \uc2 Tyson Foods\uc2 , \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , a host of others now no longer asking, but demanding that their employees be vaccinated, or, at least in the case of the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2 , you can't step on the trading floor.\par \par \uc2 It is a widening sort of a move on the part of companies to get ahead of the spikes. And it does appear to be early on getting more people to get vaccinated. But is it enough?\par \par \uc2 Larry Glazer is watching very, very closely, because, whether you have been vaccinated or not, this is going to have an impact on you and the economy, maybe the markets themselves.\par \par \uc2 Larry, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 This is really I think, at the center of an up or down market, up or down economy. Inflation is one thing. It's a big worry. But this whole virus and its stubbornness and the increase in cases, that could upset the whole apple cart, couldn't it?\par \par \uc2 LARRY GLAZER, MAYFLOWER ADVISORS: Look, Neil, there's no doubt that today's record-setting inflation data comes at a time that's critical, because Washington has considered major spending packages that are contributing to these problems.\par \par \uc2 And it's no surprise for working families in this country, who've been facing rising costs in food and fuel and everything else, just about everything they buy on a daily basis for months. So they don't need Washington's data to tell them what they already know.\par \par \uc2 The concern here is what's around the corner and the inability to address the COVID concerns not just in the U.S., but also in China. Many businesses we talked to are very concerned about shipping challenges going into the important holiday season.\par \par \uc2 So we know China's having massive shipping challenges getting goods and services, getting products out. That's going to affect the U.S. consumer. So that's going to increase shipping costs. It's going to be passed through to consumers just at a time that they're already facing other inflationary concerns.\par \par \uc2 Now, we know today's COVID data is going to put a damper on any kind of business travel that's coming up, which means profitability for the hospitality sector and all those other areas. But we're also concerned about the rest of the economy.\par \par \uc2 And I think most consumers realize that certainly some inflationary costs are going to be temporary. We can't expect used car prices to go up at the rate they have been at, but other costs are just getting going, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Rent costs are going through the roof. So it's a critical time. We need more support here. We need the business community to step forward.\par \par \uc2 And the business confidence is starting to lag because they're worried about the direction that the economy's taking.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's interesting that you joined at the hip, and wisely, my friend, this idea of a flare-up in cases, flare-up at prices, that they are joined at the hip here.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: That's right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But there are other issues, like a flare-up in serious government spending. That's more than a flare-up. It's record FDR New Deal on steroids-type levels here.\par \par \uc2 But I'm wondering. The fact that the investment community takes this in stride most of the time, they are in that camp that says, yes, maybe this will be transitory. Now, you and I can remember enough other inflationary spikes. They tend not to be transitory. That doesn't mean they necessarily have to be like we experienced in the '70s. But they just don't, poof, go away.\par \par \uc2 What are you looking for?\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: That's right.\par \par \uc2 Well, look, Neil, the initial price increases that we saw, which were due to supply disruptions because of the reopening economy, those may have been temporary. Lumber prices went up and they have started to come back down.\par \par \uc2 The next round of price increases are much stickier. They're much more long-lasting. Home prices, record increase. I wouldn't want to be looking for a house right now. Rent increases dramatically, not even showing up in the inflation data today. That's going to be a concern for working families looking to rent. And they're getting -- you're getting crickets out of Washington.\par \par \uc2 What they're getting is, they're getting a trillion-dollar spending package. And that's the warmup act for the $3.5 trillion or whatever ultimately comes through. And, again, that will only contribute to more inflationary spending.\par \par \uc2 So what the economy is really concerned about, what the stock market's really concerned about, more importantly, the bond market, they're worried that it's actually going to lead to slower future growth. And that's what we're seeing play out today. They're worried that, in the future, the government spending is going to crowd out the private sector.\par \par \uc2 And most consumers in a recent Gallup poll are saying they are seeing these inflationary pressures affecting their daily lives, and it's eating up all the stimulus money that they already got. So I think that's the concern going forward, and it's not going away anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Higher prices are here to stay because of spending and because of supply challenges, especially because of COVID right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the supply challenges, I could see.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If you consider some of the other factors here, we're coming off the mat, right?\par \par \uc2 The year-over-year comparisons, we were in the middle of pandemic.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The economy was essentially in park. It was stopped.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, any activity after that obviously will be, on a percentage basis, noteworthy.\par \par \uc2 So, these retail and wholesale inflation numbers we got, they are from those levels. Now, the administration has been arguing, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and other experts have been saying that is going to peter out, even out. The numbers and the comparisons year over year, even month to month, will start to stabilize. Do you buy that?\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Yes, so it's all in the comparisons, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And I remember getting into this business. Someone gave me a book. It's, how do you lie with statistics? And we do our own proprietary research at Mayflower Advisors. I took my son out for chicken wings, right? We sat at the table, and they said, don't look at the price on the menu. There's a 20 percent surcharge in chicken.\par \par \uc2 We call that the Glazer chicken wing indicator. And even a teenager knows that can't be good when the price of chicken wings is 20 percent higher on the menu. And I think that's what consumers realize. They don't need to look at Washington's data. They know what's going on. Their costs are rising faster than their incomes are rising. That's going to be a problem.\par \par \uc2 And it's going to be with us for some time. And we have got to grow the economy and the private sector, not rely on government to bail us out here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch very, very closely.\par \par \uc2 One of the things you look for, we haven't seen it yet, to Larry's point, is when people capitulate or give up. They stop buying the chicken wings. They stop buying this stuff. They start going to alternative either meats or, heaven forbid, vegetables. But we're not at that point yet.\par \par \uc2 I only put this into perspective that is warranted here. We are up smartly on the price front. And the notion that it is just going to, poof, disappear, that does not appear to be the case.\par \par \uc2 And the notion, by the way, in Afghanistan, we were going to quietly leave and everything would be OK and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  would have an uphill battle taking over the country, something changed along the way. The very latest on why the administration is looking at bringing 3,000 troops into Afghanistan, largely to help get our own personnel out.\par \par \uc2 But could there be something more?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, before all our troops exit, bringing in 3,000 more to help with that exit.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson at the Pentagon with more on this sudden, well, it appears like an about-face -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS PENTAGON PRODUCER: Neil, that's right.\par \par \uc2 Three infantry battalions, some 3,000 soldiers and Marines and all, will be deploying to Afghanistan in the next two days.\par \par \uc2 Here's how Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby described the mission.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: This is a temporary mission with a narrow focus.\par \par \uc2 As with all deployments of our troops into harm's way, our commanders have the inherent right of self-defense. And any attack on them can and will be met with a forceful and an appropriate response.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: This comes at the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have been on a lightning offensive in the last week.\par \par \uc2 Ten days ago, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  controlled no provincial capitals. Today, they control 11, the latest, Herat, Afghanistan's third largest city on the border with Iran. U.S. officials say Kandahar City, the second largest city and spiritual home the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , is in the process of being conquered. Ghazni, just 80 miles south of Kabul, fell today.\par \par \uc2 The State Department ordering thousands of Americans out of the country, reducing its U.S. Embassy staff to something very small. In addition to the 3,000 troops coming in, the alert brigade at Fort Bragg is heading to Kuwait. That's 3,000 more soldiers; 1,000 others are going to Qatar.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: -- combat mission?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: This is a very narrowly focused mission of safeguarding the orderly reduction of civilian personnel out of Afghanistan, and that's what we're going to be focused on.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: It's not a combat mission?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Lucas, I have already described this mission now three times.\par \par \uc2 We're mindful that the security situation continues to deteriorate in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: When I asked John Kirby if the plan was still to evacuate and pull out all U.S. troops from Afghanistan by the end of the month, even with this deployment, Neil, Kirby said that's still the plan -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, even these extra soldiers, it's -- everyone pulls out by the end of the month. That's a couple of weeks away.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: That's a couple weeks away. That's what Kirby said.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, got it. All right, Lucas Tomlinson.\par \par \uc2 Now for some friction closer to home, what's happening at the border, some interesting developments on that front.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Hunt in Brownsville, Texas, with a certain visit that got a big controversial -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The homeland security secretary, Alejandro Mayorkas, was here today. He wanted to tout to local leaders and law enforcement that the Biden administration is doing everything it can to tackle this migrant surge.\par \par \uc2 But one of his duties while he was here was to announce the figures for apprehensions in July; 212, 672 was the figure the secretary announced. And you have seen from our pictures them surging across that border all day and all night every day and every night. And that 212,000 figure represents a fivefold increase over July 2020.\par \par \uc2 The secretary also met while he was here with some local political leaders. They let him know exactly what they think. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HENRY CUELLAR (D-TX): The men and women in green and blue need help, bottom line. They're strained. They're overworked. They don't need just a pat on the back. They actually need the resources.\par \par \uc2 PETE SAENZ (D), MAYOR OF LAREDO, TEXAS: Whatever system they're using is broken. It needs fixing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Now, after that meeting with those local leaders, Secretary Mayorkas arrived here in Brownsville, Texas, to hold a news conference, in which he admitted that this really is a uniquely difficult time for law enforcement and the U.S. Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, U.S. SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: The situation at the border is one of the toughest challenges we face. It is complicated, changing, and involves vulnerable people at a time of a global pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And he said that the Biden administration is doing everything it can to deal with this outbreak, this growing number of COVID-19-infected migrants who are coming across the border.\par \par \uc2 And, as you also know, Neil, I spoke with the incoming chief of the U.S. Border Patrol yesterday. And he told us that it is one of his nightmares, he said that the thought of an agent contracting COVID-19 from a migrant and then him as a result having to, a she put it, hand a flag to a family is what keeps him awake at night -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jonathan, do we know the number of COVID cases, a solid, reliable number, because I know it's all over the map, of those who are at the border who have -- who had the virus?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The bottom line, Neil, is, no, there is not a single, solid, reliable number.\par \par \uc2 And, obviously, politics comes into this. And you talk to one people with a certain political perspective, they're going to say one number. You talk to other people with the opposite perspective, they're going to say a different number.\par \par \uc2 But the secretary admitted today that COVID-19 infections among migrants is a major problem. And that is why he says they're putting new practices into place to try to deal with it. So, whatever the number, everybody is admitting it is something that needs to be tackled, and needs to be tackled urgently, most of all, to protect those Border Patrol agents who are out there every day and every night trying to humanely and professionally deal with these migrants coming across the border -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So true.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Hunt, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And Brandon Judd right now, the Border Patrol Union president.\par \par \uc2 Brandon Judd, if their job isn't difficult enough, they risk getting COVID, these days, whether you're vaccinated or not. What do you think of this new development?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: I'd like to take a step back and address what Secretary Mayorkas said, that he said that this is complicated issue.\par \par \uc2 In reality, it is not a complicated issue. This is an issue that can be fixed very quickly. It was an issue that we have seen fixed time and time again. This administration just refuses to implement the policies and the programs that are necessary to address this problem.\par \par \uc2 As long as we continue to release people into the United States. As long as we continue to reward people for violating our laws, we're going to continue to see these numbers go up. I -- in my wildest dreams, I would have never thought that an administration would have allowed the border to get to the point that we're at now.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't even seem like there's a cap. It doesn't -- even if we go to 300,000 apprehensions, it doesn't seem like this administration is going to address the issue. So we can expect to continue to see COVID enter the United States by illegal means, and that is scary for the American public.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What's interesting, too, I know they had set up a facility, an enclosed facility, or at least an enclosed geographical area, that would handle these COVID cases.\par \par \uc2 That is already too limited for the number of cases that they have. And I'm wondering, with the surge continuing, and migrants continuing to move toward the border, how can agents just keep up with this?\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's one thing to sort it out that adjudicate these cases, try to handle what you can, separately deal with those who are contracting the virus, but it's almost moving pieces on a chessboard. You can't keep up with it.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: We're not keeping up with it.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the number of agents that we have in the field, when you look at the number of got-aways, we're not keeping up with anything that is happening on the border. That means there are dangerous products that are coming across. The drugs are coming across. They're killing our children in our rural areas, in our suburbs and in our inner cities.\par \par \uc2 All of this is happening directly on the border. And it's all because we're not implementing those policies and programs that would be effective to secure the border. And, again, we have discussed this over and over and over again, Neil. This is an easy solution.\par \par \uc2 It just matters whether or not this administration is going to push back against their base. And, right now, it's not doing it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Apparently, it got pretty tense behind closed doors. What did you learn? What did you find out?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I know you weren't physically there, but that it got I wouldn't say nasty, but back to my original term, tense.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: It did get intense. He visited with agents in McAllen at the 6:00 muster this morning.\par \par \uc2 I got briefs on that as it was ongoing. And it was very intense. The Border Patrol agents were very upset with Secretary Mayorkas. They were upset with Chief Ortiz. One agent even asked him, how would you rate your success?\par \par \uc2 And, of course, he tap-danced around that question, because his success has not been good to this point. And I wonder, because I know Secretary Mayorkas. I know that he knows what needs to be done. I know that he's giving the administration the advice that they need. I just don't think that the administration is listening to the secretary.\par \par \uc2 And so he has to be the face that goes out there. And he has to take the brunt of the vitriol that's taking place. But it does fall on him. He is the secretary. He needs to step up to the plate and he needs to do his job.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you're right. He did seem to be threading the needle on that.\par \par \uc2 Brandon Judd, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, we are following developments on COVID. Haven't forgotten about that. We're telling about the spikes in cases. We're telling you about all the cities and states now that are requiring that you have proof that you have been tested for COVID or at least tested negative and that you have proof that you have been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 They're telling and demanding restaurants do the same, not only in places like New York, but in Los Angeles, where restaurant owners like this familiar face have already been dealing with grief, and now more grief.\par \par \uc2 She's back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANGELA MARSDEN, OWNER, PINEAPPLE HILL SALOON & GRILL: I'm losing everything. Everything I own is being taken away from me. And they set up a movie company right next to my outdoor patio.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Could \uc2 Disney\uc2  save the day?\par \par \uc2 The company is out with some numbers that are off the charts, handily beating revenue and earnings estimates, and seeing strong continued growth in its theme park business. That could be a good sign.\par \par \uc2 We're on it -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Angela Marsden became an iconic symbol for the frustration of restaurant owners dealing with regulations and requirements and lockdowns and mandates that were certainly inconsistent, sometimes outright hypocritical.\par \par \uc2 Now she's facing the same pressure again, the Pineapple Hill Saloon owner out of Los Angeles facing a requirement that she's got to check that patrons who come into her establishment have been vaccinated. I'm sure she has plenty of time for that.\par \par \uc2 Kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Angela, good to see -- good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Man, one more headache. One more headache for you.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of this?\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: Ay yi yi yi.\par \par \uc2 Well, I take it very personally. It's kind of a double whammy for me, because I carry an EpiPen, and I cannot get the vaccine. So that is -- on a personal level, has definitely create a lot of anxiety.\par \par \uc2 But I want to correct what you said. It's not just restaurants. It's restaurants, bars, spas, theaters, some retail, and they're looking at grocery stores.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: So, on a financial -- I mean, there's so many levels to this.\par \par \uc2 On a financial level, as a small business owner, number one, we have an employee shortage. I can't find enough staff to keep the service going here. Now I'm going to say, well, you have to be vaccinated? I have certain employees that have had it before, and they feel their immune system is good enough. Do I have to let them go? How do I find new people?\par \par \uc2 And then they want us to be the sheriff? And I'm supposed to, what, put a vaccine checker at the door? And if somebody gets mad and punches my employee in the face, what do I do?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I have homeless in my parking lot. It takes two to three hours to get a police officer here. And they won't even take them. So, I don't understand. Not only that. From a different perspective, I can't have kids in my bar, but I have talked to my fellow restaurant owners. One of them is like, well, what do I do with people who have children? Children aren't vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Does that mean I can't have families come inside with their children?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good point.\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: I mean, it's just there are so many--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Angela, what if you don't do it? What if you don't do it?\par \par \uc2 I have talked to many New York restaurant owners who've been telling me, I simply don't have the time. I can't play the police. I barely have people enough to do the job they're supposed to do, let alone play a bouncer here.\par \par \uc2 What if you don't do it? What's the punishment for restaurant, bar owners, other who don't?\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, well -- well, we don't know.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: They're in a 14-day process right now trying to figure out how they're going to enforce this.\par \par \uc2 And, typically, what they do is, they will start fining us through the public Health Department. And then they will pull our public health permit, as you know, with Tinhorn Flats, that now has a fence around it.\par \par \uc2 So I don't know how they're going to enforce this or what they're going to do to punish us for not enforcing it. I just really -- Neil, I really want to urge the board of supervisors right now to please, please think of not just the financial repercussions on the small businesses, but the humanitarian repercussions to the community, the division that this is creating.\par \par \uc2 I mean, 50.3 percent--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What are your customers saying to you, then? What are they saying? Are they worried about this? Are they worried that you won't, will follow this? What?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: No, my customers aren't happy about it.\par \par \uc2 So my customers that come in that are vaccinated, they're like, well, I'm vaccinated. I don't care if my friend here isn't. And the ones that don't feel safe, they sit outside,\par \par \uc2 I'm not anti-vax. I want everybody to know that. I just can't get the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: And I also have been looking into a new system that I'm putting into my bar that's FDA-approved, and it has bipolar ionizing system that kills COVID 99.99 percent of the time in air, so that we don't have to divide people.\par \par \uc2 I have teachers freaking out. I have a friend of mine that works for the city, she went into a panic, although the teachers just won. They were going to sue. So now they don't have to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't know. I don't know where they're going to go with this. But it's not going to create anything good for the community, in my opinion, you know?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And you just thought you dodged a bullet. And then now here comes another one.\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you will handle it, Angela. I know that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're a pretty strong person. And your people are pretty loyal.\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: I will.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That, I know.\par \par \uc2 So, keep us posted on this. Hang in there.\par \par \uc2 MARSDEN: Well, I want to take care of my community.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you, Neil. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you, Angela Marsden, the Pineapple Hill Saloon owner.\par \par \uc2 These guys just can't get out of their own way. Just as they're getting their act together, boom, something like this happens.\par \par \uc2 We are on top of that. We're also on top of those \uc2 Disney\uc2  earnings I was teasing you. The Mouse House is out with some way-better-than-expected numbers.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  is sort of like a proxy on the American consumer and whether they will splurge to have fun and do fun things. Apparently, that is still on.\par \par \uc2 Charles Payne on why he's not surprised -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you think about this, a lot of people going to \uc2 Disney\uc2  parks, they were limited capacity. You had to wear masks. That's actually increased in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 And yet business was booming at the Mouse House. There was such strong revenues and money being made, absolute profits. The stock is jumping in after-hours trading. And maybe that will help the markets in the belief here that you cannot slow that consumer penchant to want to get out and have fun, something that my buddy Charles Payne, the host of "Making Money" on FOX Business at 2:00 p.m. Eastern time, has been reminding me about again and again.\par \par \uc2 He has great faith in you, the people, to have some fun.\par \par \uc2 So, Charles, what do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Right now, just the initial reaction, Neil, we had the screen up. You can see it's up after-market.\par \par \uc2 I should remind people, the last few times they reported, the stock got hit pretty good for the next week. Listen, you and I both parse word. So listen to what the CEO had to say three months ago. He said they saw encouraging signs across our businesses. This what he had to say in this report: "We see more encouraging signs of recovery."\par \par \uc2 Stronger, more confident. It's reflected in these numbers, to your point, the park numbers. Listen, parks -- between parks, hotels, cruise ships, in 2019, that was 36 percent of their business. It's crucial. It's critical. It's coming back. It's reflected in the stock.\par \par \uc2 And I think, to your point, also, it sends a great message about overcoming -- it is a proxy. It is a really good proxy at this time, when we're still dealing with the COVID and, of course, the new Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm wondering too. With so many businesses now putting off in person return to work -- \uc2 Disney\uc2  not part of that, although it's talking about maybe vaccinations for its workers and doing things in stages -- I'm wondering how that impacts this economic success story, people -- sort of the great opening out and all of a sudden, in the middle of that, the delayed opening up.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I think it will be just delayed, if it hurts it at all.\par \par \uc2 You know one thing that's really intriguing? And, of course, the postmortem on this is going to be written, rewritten and relitigated for decades to come. Right at this very moment, I find it really compelling that the United States is -- with this Delta variant, we're still doing business a lot better than those countries that were lauded for the harsh shutdowns that they went through last year, particularly China and Australia.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Now they're going through harsh shutdowns again because, really, honestly, they never learned to live with it. So a single case can shut down an entire shipyard.\par \par \uc2 In China overnight, the second largest shipping container site in the world was shut down. So that was -- when you go through it and you do it that way, that's the only way how to do it. They're going to have really tough economic repercussions. You just had a wonderful restauranteur on. She's an amazing story.\par \par \uc2 She's trying to work through it. We have learned to work through it to a certain degree. And I think that's why -- part of the reason we have seen this amazing recovery vis-a-vis the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, your confidence in mankind is always justified.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Charles Payne, thank you very, very much. Good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks. Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, only one thing that could get in the way maybe for Disney World in Florida is this storm, although it's not as bad a storm as it was. I think now Fred is a tropical depression, but still a potential threat for the Sunshine State.\par \par \uc2 Rick Reichmuth on that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Rick.\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, definitely we're going to be getting some rain out of this. I think rain would probably be our biggest problem. That said, the storm still has plenty of time over water, very warm water. And we have seen storms really strengthen pretty quickly in most recent years.\par \par \uc2 There's where the storm has been. Center of the storm now just to the north of the Cuba coastline, the southern Cuban coastline. That said, take a look at the radar image here, and there's really nothing happening around the center of the storm. It's been the case all day long, though, when you look at the visible satellite, you certainly can still see this rotation there.\par \par \uc2 But until you start to get some storms right back around the center, you're not going to see much of a strengthening storm at all.\par \par \uc2 So, our tropical models that we look at, all of them bring this into the Gulf, at least most of them, into the eastern parts of the Gulf, the western side of Florida, eastern side of Florida. Not going to be talking about a landfall from the storm, but I think we are going to be talking about a pretty significant amount of rain, especially across South Florida. So, the Miami area, we're going to watch that.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at the future radar. This is over the next, say, about 36 hours. Notice we do start to see this get a little bit better organized here. That is Saturday morning early, so late tomorrow night into Saturday morning a storm just to the south of Florida and then likely spending some time just off of the west coast of Florida before making landfall somewhere around maybe the Panhandle of Florida.\par \par \uc2 That would be sometimes Sunday night into Monday. One other, Neil, I just want to point out, we talk so much about where a storm makes landfall. We have talked a lot about Florida. Folks across parts of the Southern Appalachians need to be watching this as well. I think we're going to get a lot of rain there, some spots, maybe five to seven inches. That will also cause some pretty significant inland flooding -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Rick Reichmuth on all of that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, this is one reason why you get vaccine hesitancy among folks. They hear news, particularly on one vaccine, that makes them pause big time -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You wonder if they made a bigger deal out of this than is warranted, but it did provide some ammunition to those who are leery of getting vaccinated, a study that shows at least \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 's vaccine with some of these Delta variants isn't nearly as effective as was once believed, maybe only 42 percent effective.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Amesh Adalja\uc2  us right now.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, should we be worried about that?\par \par \uc2 DR. AMESH ADALJA\uc2 , \uc2 INFECTIOUS DISEASES SOCIETY OF AMERICA\uc2 : No, we shouldn't be worried about it, because you have to remember there's a couple of caveats to that study.\par \par \uc2 First of all, it hasn't been peer-reviewed. But the efficacy that they're looking at is against any type of positive test, not against severe disease, hospitalization and death. And when you look at what really matters, the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine stacks up very well when it comes to those three serious outcomes, the whole reason why we actually embarked on a vaccination campaign.\par \par \uc2 So I think it's important to remember what they're actually looking at isn't what's most important when it comes to this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it's that vaccine, as I understand, Doctor, that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is most likely to approve next month, not Moderna's, for example, that has a 72 percent efficacy rate against this variant. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  was the first vaccine on the market. They have accrued the most data. They're the only one actually ready to be approved, because the \uc2 FDA\uc2  requires six months of data.\par \par \uc2 I don't think that really makes a difference. I think we have to really keep emphasizing to the public that what matters about these vaccines is not their efficacy against minor breakthrough infections, but against severe disease.\par \par \uc2 And by that measure, all of these vaccines stack up very well against each other. And I would recommend any of those three to anybody that hasn't been vaccinated right now. And I think we do need to have this vaccine approved as soon as possible, because I think it will spur many people who have been on the fence to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, this latest finding notwithstanding -- and, to your point 42 percent is a hell of a lot better than zero percent, if you have never been vaccinated -- but more Americans may be responding to this pressure.\par \par \uc2 We're hearing these alarming stories about people hunkering down again and businesses having their operations limited, proof of vaccinations, all the rest, that they are responding. Vaccination rates have been rising. How significant, do you think?\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: It's very significant that we're seeing people now being prompted to get vaccinations -- vaccinated because they're seeing what the Delta variant can do.\par \par \uc2 The Delta variable find you if you're an unvaccinated person. It will infect you. It will disrupt your life. It will disrupt your business. And the easiest way to prevent it from happening is to get vaccinated. So I'm glad to see that some people are actually seeing with their own eyes what the Delta variant is doing, and then taking action to protect themselves and to improve their lives.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully, that trend continues and we can get ahead of it in some of those states where vaccination rates have been poor. The higher the vaccination number is in this country as a whole, the quicker this pandemic is in our rearview mirror.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Dr. Amesh Adalja\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, it's not been good seeing inflation, right, again, nothing like we had in the 1970s, but it is the kind of thing that is not going away. And that has some Democrats fearing that also means neither are their problems for the midterms and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Are they making things worse with yet more money?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, are you worried about inflation?\par \par \uc2 Join the club; 86 percent of Americans say they are. I don't know whether the 14 percent are even going outside to shop for things. But 86 percent of us are.\par \par \uc2 Susan Crabtree of RealClearPolitics.\par \par \uc2 If I'm president of the United States, if I'm the party in power, I'd be worried about that.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN CRABTREE, REALCLEARPOLITICS: Oh, yes. And they are worried about it.\par \par \uc2 This is what I have been calling the Democrats' catch-2022 on inflation. They have this window of opportunity where they still have their House and Senate majorities, the majority in the Senate just barely. And so they're going to use this opportunity to push through as much spending and these big pet projects that they have always wanted to do, like community college, free community college and paid family leave, push them through right now.\par \par \uc2 But the result of that, it actually might come back to bite them in the 20 -- the '22 midterms, which are just right around the corner next year. That's if inflation stays long term. We saw this with the Fourth of July recess. The Democrats got hit really hard, vulnerable Democratic congressional members in their districts during town halls.\par \par \uc2 They got asked these questions, and there was ads from -- Republican ads saying, we can't afford these increasing gas prices. Backyard barbecues are more expensive. We can't afford this. And Democratic -- a top leading Democratic strategist said those were the most effective Republican ads they have seen. And we can expect more this recess.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, it's interesting. They have been positing this -- that is, some Democrats, not all, Susan -- as saying, look, this link between going deeper into debt and yielding inflation, if that were the case, then interest rates would have been a lot higher than they were.\par \par \uc2 We have gone through 10 years with Republican and Democratic presidents alike. Inflation has been very, very low. Interest rates have been darn near zero. There are other factors to that. I get that.\par \par \uc2 But some Democrats I talk to say, this is going to, especially if they get the $3.5 trillion measure, lead to a boom in the next year that's going to offset what other potential inflationary pressures are there. What do you make of that argument? Because Republicans claim just the opposite. One side has got to be wrong here.\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: Senator Mike Lee is calling this like an inflation bomb, these bills that the Congress is passing, this $3.5 trillion bill that Democrats want to pass.\par \par \uc2 There are about six Democrats, House Democrats that are opposing this bill. And House -- Nancy -- Speaker Pelosi needs three of them. So, yes, there's a lot of concern on their side about this. And you have had President Biden say, oh, this is just like reopening a pipe that's frozen. And there's spits. And it needs to get -- the economy needs to get running again. And then it'll be -- it's just really short-term inflation.\par \par \uc2 But you don't see that. You have the Federal Reserve chairman coming out a couple of weeks ago saying, actually, this is longer than we expected. It could be longer.\par \par \uc2 So, if it goes into next year -- I mean, if it's short term, I think Democrats survive this, no problem. It doesn't become an issue next year. But if we still have inflation in the summer after Labor Day, after summer, people are going in their cars, enjoying their summer vacations next year, when, really, people start paying attention to the midterms, I think it's going to be a serious problem for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you're right. Then all bets are off.\par \par \uc2 And to your point, Susan, I follow the math. I'm a herd here. It's money in, money out. And Americans are making more money. Wage gains are running at a 4 percent clip year over year. Not bad, the strongest we have seen in about nine years. Unfortunately, their expenses, the cost of things have risen at a 5.5 percent clip, if you look at wholesale inflation, close to an 8 percent clip.\par \par \uc2 And that's going to sink in real quickly. Do you think this lasts beyond the midterms?\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: Well, beyond the midterms, I think it's interesting.\par \par \uc2 I think you're getting -- Democrats are caught in this catch-2022, because it's -- because you have, like, people like \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  who's very vulnerable, in a traditionally conservative district in Orange County, California. She's trying to say that the child--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, it could last. It could last for them.\par \par \uc2 I'm jumping on you. It could last. You're--\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: Yes. It -- there you go.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: It could last.\par \par \uc2 But -- yes, yes, it could.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: But we will see. We will have to see, wait and see.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We call these hard breaks, Susan.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Susan Crabtree.\par \par \uc2 Now it's time for "The Five."\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210812eh8c0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 NY Gov Andrew Cuomo Resigns, Effective In 14 Days; Senate Passes $1 Trillion Infrastructure Bill; Biden: Afghans Have To Want To Fight; Mask Mandate Debate; Google Workers Staying Home Could Get Pay Cut-Rpt; Tyson, \uc2 Shake Shack\uc2  Late Companies To Hike Prices\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Bryan Llenas, Peter Doocy, Charlie Gasparino, Steve  Moore \par \uc2 8,134 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 August 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: That's THE STORY of Tuesday, August the 10th 2021. But THE STORY goes on. So we will see you back here tomorrow at 3:00. "YOUR WORLD" with Neil starts now.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: You'd have almost missed it. Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  of New York, tucking his resignation in the final minutes of his remarks where he indicated right now that he was challenging some of the women who were challenging him on outrageous comments and behavior.\par \par \uc2 But as it turned out, the governor gave up the fight. And now 14 days away from being a former governor. We'll be talking to another former governor, Republican George Pataki, what he makes of the changes now in the financial capital of the world, New York state because no matter how you slice it, the new lieutenant governor, by the way, a woman and the first the state has seen in its history will be pulling the levers of power and extreme influence.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto and this is YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 And what an incredible day right? Were something as big as a trillion dollar plus infrastructure passed with bipartisan support, and yet it takes a backseat to the drama in Albany.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas, with the very latest on how it played out and how it might continue playing out. Hey, Brian.\par \par \uc2 BRYAS LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey Neil. Well this morning began with Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  is outside counsel, really delivering a blistering attack via PowerPoint against each and every one of the accusers and the Attorney General's report. Then the Governor came on TV, and he apologized for making people feel uncomfortable and if he had offended them, while at the same time discrediting the most serious allegations made against him. And then he decided to resign, blaming politics. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDREW CUOMO\uc2  (D-NY): I am a fighter. And my instinct is to fight through this controversy, because I truly believe it is politically motivated. The best way I can help now is if I step aside and let government get back to government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: It is a stunning fall for one of the Democratic Party's biggest stars earlier, President Biden was just asked about this and well, he had very brief comments. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: I respect the Governor's decision and I respect the decision he made.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Earlier, Rita Glaven the outside counsel for the governor attacked Lindsay Boylan, the first accuser to come out publicly against the governor. And this is what Boylan now says about the resignation. From the beginning, I simply asked that the Governor stop his abusive behavior, it became abundantly clear he was unable to do that, instead attacking and blaming victims until the end. It is a tragedy that so many stood by and watch these abuses happen.\par \par \uc2 The big question now is whether or not impeachment will continue here in Albany. Democrats and Republicans some expressing that there is an appetite to continue with the impeachment investigation that has been ongoing now for five months into multiple different scandals, the nursing home, COVID- 19 deaths, the pull -- the COVID-19 preferential testing for his family and whether or not he used state resources to write his $5 million dollar pandemic book.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to one Assemblyman a Democrat on the Judicial Committee, Phil Steck, and this is what he had to say about the prospect of impeachment moving forward.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHIL STECK (D) NEW YORK STATE ASSEMBLY MEMBER: I think it's too early to tell. I think we'd have to see what the consensus was of the Democratic majority in our conference. I'd be interested and I've conveyed this to the Committee Chair of having our meeting on Monday and discussing the issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: If they did move forward with impeachment, it could bar the governor from running for re-election next year. So that is why that is a big deal. Of course, people do want accountability for those other scandals. Bottom line is Kathy Hochul, the lieutenant governor will now become the governor in 14 days time, the first woman to be governor of the state of New York. Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bryan Llenas, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We've got George Pataki, a member and the three term Republican governor of the great state of New York kind enough to join us now. Governor, great to see you again.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of everything that happened?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE PATAKI, (R) FMR NEW YORK GOVERNOR: Get out some bizarre. First of all, I think it was inevitable that the Governor was either going to be impeached, or he was going to resign. And they didn't resign out of a sense of remorse or acknowledgement that he had this hideously toxic work environment. It was because he wanted to do the right thing and that's just not the case. And he did because he would have been impeached.\par \par \uc2 The Attorney General's report was done thoroughly professionally in a balanced way by an elected Democrat. And then he says it was all politics. It wasn't politics, it was his behavior, not just with the 11 women who, whose credibility was upheld by the Attorney General, but on the nursing homes and the abuse of state resources for its family, for the book, so many other things. And it was just time it was long passed on forgotten or fallen leaf.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, obviously, everything changed, the Governor when his own party turned on him better than seven out of 10 assembly and then state legislators of the Democratic bent, said he should go that really changed it, didn't it? It's one thing to fight Republicans quite another your own party.\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: He would have stayed. If he thought he had the slightest chance of not being impeached, there's no way he would have gone. But ultimately, when the Democrats realized that, and I don't think they want us to impeach him, I think they just finally concluded that they had to do it. Or they would look like they were covering up. So, this is this isn't a day for celebration, this is really a day for looking at the future and hoping that we can do better next time.\par \par \uc2 Neil, I just have to say I heard President Biden's comments. I mean, that was a little bizarre to have so little to say. But anyway, we can move on here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it was talking about what he did as a governor, the 10 and a half years versus the accusations of these (INAUDIBLE). It was a little weird. But I do want to get your take on maybe when you have outlived your usefulness. And I was discussing this with you earlier, this notion of, you know, three and you're getting on peoples nerves. I'm talking about three terms and seeking a fourth one, whether it was getting long in the tooth. And obviously these were very different circumstances.\par \par \uc2 But you experienced that running. In fact, you unseated \uc2 Mario Cuomo\uc2 , the governor's father was a giant at the time you were little known, a mayor of Peekskill, New York, but maybe the governor then talking to the governor's father, \uc2 Mario Cuomo\uc2  had sort of pushed it too far. Is there such a thing like after three terms, you're setting yourself up for people to say enough already?\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: Yes, I think there is that people do get tired of it. But on the other hand, it really comes down to whether or not your policy is working. You know, and in the case of \uc2 Mario Cuomo\uc2 , his policies didn't work. We were the most dangerous state in America, highest tax burden, one of the 11 on welfare. So yes, there was fatigue, but there was also the failure of his policies. And whether \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , if he had not gone through this and just resigned, if he would have run against, I think he would have been beatable just because of his policies.\par \par \uc2 Look at what's happening in New York, the crying through the roof, the school is falling apart, the mentally ill homeless roaming the streets. We don't know what's going to happen to the school system a few weeks. So, I think his policies were such that it would be better, but he saw the light and resigned in the face of impeachment. But I think either way, it would have been gone at the end of the year of next year because its policies have been such a fad.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now the Lieutenant Governor takes over and 14 days that she will fill out the remaining, what 15, 16 months of the governor's term, but there are a lot of people angling for that job. But what do you see happen?\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: Neil, this is New York. I know that I think while we're talking here, there are at least 1,000 politicians from every corner of the state on the phone with their advisors and others say what do you think and I run for governor. So Kathy Hochul will be the governor. I have no doubt that she will face primaries, particularly from the far left of the Democratic party that the socialists when -- you see many faces a primary with \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  who was a horrible mayor, but may try to get a pro