IDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That was \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 . He is the Prime Minister of Hungary.\par \par \uc2 We came here this week to see what they've been doing to secure their borders and it seems like a pretty happy little country and how did they do that? It was worth getting outside where you grew up and see how the rest of the world does things. People in Washington had a heart attack that we would dare do that.\par \par \uc2 We could give you a million examples, but we just got this one. Someone just sent this to me. This is a tweet from one of the dumbest ladies at "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." I'm going to read this slowly because I don't have my glasses on, but it's good because you're going to want to catch every line and see how deeply this amuses you, quote: "In Orban's Hungary, more than 90 percent of the media is controlled by the ruling party. Businesses are physically and legally harassed if they don't toe the party line. Elections are manipulated. Party leaders are mysteriously rich."\par \par \uc2 Does that sound like any place you know? Does that sound at all familiar to you? It sounds like my country, weirdly. That's not even true about Hungary, but whatever.\par \par \uc2 Douglas Murray has spent time in Hungary, knows the Prime Minister, thought a lot about Europe, wrote one of the best books ever on Europe, "The Strange Death of Europe," and one of our favorites, of course. He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Douglas Murray, thanks so much for coming on. So, I'm sure that there are things you don't like about Hungary ...\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS MURRAY, AUTHOR, "THE STRANGE DEATH OF EUROPE": Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: ... or Orban. You don't live here, I noticed, but the portrayal of Hungary as like the worst place on Earth, it seems very important to a lot of the people running our country to say that. Why is it so important to them?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes, I noticed this first of all, really in 2015 when I noticed that Hungary had become the sort of whipping boy of the E.U. and indeed of the American left, and there was a reason for that, which was that that year \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2 , the Chancellor of Germany opened the borders of Europe and said, "Come" to the world's migrants from North Africa and Middle East.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  was the only European leader, or at least the first European leader to say no, we don't want millions of people who we don't know to walk into our country.\par \par \uc2 And put that into some perspective, that year, almost a million people walked illegally into Hungary. Hungary tried to get them through the country and to Germany as fast as possible, but just put that in American terms.\par \par \uc2 Hungary with a population of 10 million people, one million illegals coming in in a number of months, that's equivalent to something like 33 million illegal migrants coming through the southern border of the United States in a matter of months. That's three times the estimated number of illegals currently in the U.S. coming in in a few months.\par \par \uc2 So, Hungary said, no, we can't cope with that. And after that, the demonization of the country and the demonization, not legitimate criticism, but the demonization of the Hungarian people started in earnest and it has never stopped since.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, I mean it almost sounds listening to the President speak, people at the State Department that they would like regime change in Hungary. In fact, it doesn't almost sound that way, it sounds that way.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes. Yes, and it's very sinister because there is no evidence that \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  has rigged the elections. You may not like \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , a lot of people in Europe, a lot of people in America don't like hi.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: But there is no evidence that he has rigged the election, so he is the legitimately elected leader, repeatedly elected leader of Hungary, and this extraordinary decision to just sort of decide that if you don't like somebody, if they are a conservative leader that basically you can advocate regime change in a European democracy, which let's remember in the 20th Century was very nearly erased repeatedly by fascists and then by communists. That country suffered so much in the 20th Century.\par \par \uc2 And in the 21st Century, when Hungarians hear people in Washington and elsewhere talking about basically them not having a legitimate country and outside powers having to intervene, they get very worried and I think they're right to feel worried and they're right to feel sore about the treatment that they get.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's actually making me mad just hearing your characterization is so precisely true. That it really is embarrassing as an American to hear it.\par \par \uc2 Douglas Murray thanks so much for that, I appreciate it. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: It's a great pleasure. Likewise.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you. That's it for us tonight and for our week here in Budapest.\par \par \uc2 But before we go, a few people to thank.\par \par \uc2 We have spent a happy week at the Matild Palace Hotel in Budapest. We have been broadcasting from there. They just opened, by the way, along the Danube. It is a great place, we can recommend it very strongly.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, thanks to our team. We were not just here for TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT. We are excited to show that we are putting together for our FOX Nation show on "Tucker Carlson Originals" that's coming very soon.\par \par \uc2 So, thanks to our producers who came with us on location: Senior producer Charles Couger, Tom Fox, Lexi Ciccone, Tom Reed, our "Tucker Carlson Originals" photographer and editor, Kyle Rothenberg, photographers Frank Merola, David Tresnowski, and of course, Justin Wells, who runs the show.\par \par \uc2 We would also like to thank, by the way, the people of Hungary for being so nice to us.\par \par \uc2 We will see you back from America Monday night, 8:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020210807eh8600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Inflation Fears; Vaccine Mandate On Broadway; Interview With State Sen. Brad Hoylman (D-NY); \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  Under Fire; Border Crisis; COVID Restrictions\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Mike Gunzelman, Kat Timpf, Lydia Hu, Bryan Llenas,  Bill Melugin, Jackie DeAngelis, Susan Li \par \uc2 8,188 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, we connect, and you decide.\par \par \uc2 COVID restrictions that are ramping up that just as job creation appears to be slowing down, and this as we just get word that they're putting the brakes on this year's in New York auto show. This economy might not be on autopilot.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 What is going on with a recovery that seems to be getting bumpy, as the number of new COVID cases are making a lot of people pretty grumpy, not only about going back to school this fall, if that is still on, but increasingly about doing anything like we used to be able to do, say, in the last couple of weeks in city after city, state after state where restrictions are coming fast and furious?\par \par \uc2 We have got Jackie DeAngelis talking about the big job impact of all of this, and also Susan Li on how some companies are feeling the pressure from this and higher prices that they're trying to pass along to you, but might not be succeeding for long.\par \par \uc2 All right, I want to first begin with you, Jackie, on this employment report that as people wondering, wait a minute, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, before I get there, Neil, I want to talk about the COVID cases, because that's how we're going to get to how these numbers impact the market.\par \par \uc2 COVID cases rising in the country because of the Delta variant, and states and cities are handling this surge very differently this time around. So, in Florida, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  says that he's not going to shut things down or impose a mask mandate. He believes the surge of cases is a seasonal outbreak, also adding that lockdowns have failed time and time again throughout the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, in Louisiana, starting today, people aged 5 years and older are required to wear a mask indoors regardless of their vaccination status. Here in New York City, vaccine mandates for indoor dining, the gym and other businesses. You're going to need to prove that you had at least one dose for these activities coming September 13.\par \par \uc2 Now, Mayor \uc2 de Blasio\uc2 , he shared the CDC's sentiment on masks for vaccinated people indoors, but he did not reinstate the mask mandate. And it looks like the strategy here is to push people to get those shots.\par \par \uc2 Now, all this uncertainty, bring it back to the market with the virus, some of this key data coming out having an impact on Wall Street, the stock market lower today after a big miss in the ADP private sector report. This is teeing up Friday's jobs report. Now, the ADP numbers showed 330,000 new positions were filled. But it's a sharp decline from that 680,000 that we saw in June and the lowest since February.\par \par \uc2 Right now, this is the big concern for the market and its participants. It's the Delta variant and does it derail the trajectory of reopening plans and employment across the country, Neil?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jackie, thank you very much on that.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Susan Li right now, because this is coming at a time, you think about it, Susan, when investors are apparently cleared to think, all right, things are all going to be going back, the companies are in good shape, their earnings are strong, and yet more than a few of them have been citing these inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 What is going on?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, you know it. That's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, over and over again, we have been hearing from some of the biggest companies in the world, complaining about higher prices that they have to pay for labor, fuel and raw materials. So, companies being companies, they will protect their bottom line, so they're going to pass on those costs to consumers, \uc2 Clorox\uc2  being the latest.\par \par \uc2 Also, you have beer brewers, alcohol makers, food companies, consumer product brands, and it felt like, on every earnings call, you had executives being asked about inflation and responding by saying that prices were going up.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Clorox\uc2  wipes are going to cost more in the future. Diaper prices have jumped over the past year. You have Budweiser beer being more expensive, \uc2 Nestle\uc2  ice cream, Smirnoff vodka, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  coffee, and Chipotle burritos.\par \par \uc2 Now, a lot of these companies say that they need to pay their workers more in order to attract and keep talent these days. So, the Philadelphia Federal Reserve said that nearly three-quarters of companies in their survey say that wages went up this spring, which is, of course, stoking inflation.\par \par \uc2 So the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's favorite inflation gauge rising at the fastest pace in 30 years in June from a year ago. And government officials, as you heard, Neil, that includes the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , say that this is all just temporary, transitory, as they say. We heard that from Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  today, the former Federal Reserve chair saying that she believes monthly inflation will come down by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 Now, Yellen making those remarks while trying to sell that $3.5 trillion climate and anti-poverty package, along with another trillion-dollar infrastructure deal. And, Neil, that's a lot of money that a lot of people say will increase prices even further if it's passed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and that's what some of these companies are worried about, that there will come a point where customers will say, all right, we're not going to keep paying these. And then they're in a heap of trouble, as are their customers.\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you very much, Susan Li, for that.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Edward Gomez. He runs the Da Noi Restaurants, the owner there, a big Italian eatery.\par \par \uc2 And he has to go ahead and comply with these new restrictions in New York that everyone has to be tested negative for COVID, preferably be wearing a mask when they go in. It's just going to be a mess and for him some food for thought. His headaches, of course, aren't over yet.\par \par \uc2 Edward, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Now, these are still a week or so away. But they're going to change the way you do business. How do you police people on whether they have been vaccinated or not or whether they're doing everything according to the city's wishes?\par \par \uc2 EDWARD GOMEZ, OWNER, DA NOI RESTAURANTS: Well, that's the big question right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: Right now, we find (AUDIO GAP) staff to begin with? So now it's like an extra job that we have to make sure that people, when they come in, we have to kind of guard the gate and make sure that if they are or if they aren't.\par \par \uc2 And if they aren't, we will have to turn them away. So it creates a big obstacle for us. And we're already struggling to begin with, and still waiting for any type of relief from the federal or state government, rather than just the debt that they threw on us.\par \par \uc2 But...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The vaccine requirement, it's one thing for the city to recommend it.\par \par \uc2 It's another for you to play, on top of everything else you have to do, the police to check that your customers are, and they might mete out punishments if some of those customers have snuck in and are indeed lying to you or misrepresenting whether they have been vaccinated or not.\par \par \uc2 Then you're on the hook for that, I guess, right?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: Yes, that's what they say.\par \par \uc2 And the truth of the matter is, like, I'm not even qualified enough to watch over these people, that we're running around (AUDIO GAP) where, at times, I'm cleaning toilets in between shifts, because there's nobody here to do any work.\par \par \uc2 It's really become something that is just daunting. And it's almost like we have to pack up and leave.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you don't need this grief.\par \par \uc2 Edward, I'm curious too. I mean, obviously, you're quite an attraction. People love the food. And I'm wondering, what are your customers saying these days? Of course, this isn't immediate. It could be coming. The mayor promises, if everything is hunky-dory and cases subside, all of this goes away.\par \par \uc2 But that doesn't mean that for at least a few weeks it's going to be the rule. What are they saying? Are they saying, hey, I might not come -- come back, I might wait until this blows over? What?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: Well, it's -- everybody's up in arms.\par \par \uc2 I try not to -- I try to deflect a little bit because I try to bring happy times, rather than stress everybody out. But, at the same time, but it's my main concern, because we're really trying -- we're struggling to stay afloat as is.\par \par \uc2 And after almost 19 months of being used as a yo-yo, back and forth, open, close, open, close, and us being in Staten Island, New York, it's a little bit even more politicized. So, I try to take a backseat to it and just try to accommodate everybody as best I can.\par \par \uc2 But it really is -- it's just the mismanagement from the top down. And I feel like we're being used as whipping posts.\par \par \uc2 I don't know why they don't go after the...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it certainly seems that way.\par \par \uc2 Yes, well, hang in there. I know you're great with the garlic. Maybe that could wipe out a lot of the anger that people are feeling. It does that for me.\par \par \uc2 But hang in there, Edward, and keep us posted.\par \par \uc2 Edward Gomez runs the Da Noi Restaurants.\par \par \uc2 Let's get Sarah Westwood's read on this for The Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, I don't know if you had a chance to hear that whole conversation. Here's a restaurant owner how has a devil of the time just getting staff together, reopening to the requirements of the city and the state, and now this added burden of checking out all his customers and making sure they have been vaccinated. He doesn't need this.\par \par \uc2 SARAH WESTWOOD, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Well, that's right.\par \par \uc2 And the pressure, the upward pressure on wages is just one of many factors right now that's contributing to inflation. That's hurting Biden politically. It's creating a vulnerability for him in terms of his ability to get his massive spending package through Congress.\par \par \uc2 And it's one of many reasons why businesses are struggling right now. So, from a macro perspective, though, the rise in cases caused by the Delta variant and the renewed restrictions in cities, that could actually serve to soften the impact of inflation in some ways. The Biden administration has been blaming this on overheated demand related to the sudden nature of the reopening and a supply chain that just hasn't been able to catch up.\par \par \uc2 So, in some ways, from a macro perspective, that could alleviate some of the pressures. But for individual businesses that have already been struggling for more than a year and are now trying to claw their way back, facing the fact that the cost of their raw goods, the cost of transportation, the cost of labor is all going up, and then now facing renewed restrictions, it's just a really difficult for business owners like your previous guest.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know what is kind of interesting?\par \par \uc2 When I was looking at the story yesterday, Sarah, about the rising price thing, and then, of course, the rising spikes and what's happening on that price front, that this was seen as an opportunity for the president, rather than a damaging issue, that it will necessitate and justify still more spending.\par \par \uc2 I think they might have that upside-down. But what did you think of that? Because even a paper like The \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 , which looks at the global economy, is saying, this presidency is vulnerable to inflation, and with it the global recovery. What did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 WESTWOOD: Yes, I think that's absolutely true. I read that story as well.\par \par \uc2 This is a huge weakness for President Biden and the Republicans are sort of relentlessly beating this drum and tying directly the inflation that we have seen to the size of President Biden's stimulus package. You also can see that the Biden administration's messaging on this has completely shifted.\par \par \uc2 They have gone from saying that they don't expect inflation to accompany this massive package that they passed, to saying that they believe it's just a blip due to the fact that Americans had money in their pockets right when everything opened up to the due to the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 And now you have the Treasury secretary saying these high prices could be with us through at least the end of the year. And you're seeing the Biden administration reframe this argument related to their spending package.\par \par \uc2 Now they're saying that it's necessary to offset some of the high prices that American families are paying because they want to subsidize child care. They want to subsidize housing in some cases, and all these other social programs, that, if you look at the overall picture, it'll save American families money.\par \par \uc2 Republicans right now, though, are unified in arguing that this big spending is what's causing inflation. And there's signs that Americans could be buying that. There was a Morning Consult poll last week that said 59 percent of voters, not just Republicans, but all voters, blamed Biden's economic policy for inflation.\par \par \uc2 So that message is breaking through. And moderate Democrats, along with all lawmakers, will be going back to their districts for the August recess. They're likely to get an earful from constituents about inflation and the rising cost of living right now.\par \par \uc2 And so they could come back even more reluctant to support that massive spending package.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, Sarah, we're hearing from more and more of these consumer products companies that are saying, whether you're talking Kraft or \uc2 Clorox\uc2 , there is a point of resistance.\par \par \uc2 And we're beginning to see it out of customers who won't just keep paying these higher prices, serious headwinds. I'm wondering if that's reverberated yet on the administration, or they're just not buying it.\par \par \uc2 WESTWOOD: Well, I think that's certainly why you are seeing the Biden administration start to talk about this in a different way.\par \par \uc2 They are seeing polling that shows that more and more Americans are concerned about this. Polling shows that Americans are no longer really listing coronavirus as their top concern. They're starting to list their ability to pay their bills, their ability to get a job as things that they're more concerned about.\par \par \uc2 And this is a real weakness for the Biden administration. If people stop being willing to pay these prices, if they stop viewing them as a temporary byproduct of a recovery that started more suddenly than I think people expected, that's going to be a huge problem for the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 And then, in addition to everything we have talked about, they're going to have a credibility problem on their hands, because the same people who have been telling everyone not to expect inflation, and then to expect inflation to go away, are then going to be trying to argue people out of caring about the fact that they're paying higher prices for everything from food to clothes.\par \par \uc2 And so there's a lot.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WESTWOOD: This just a mine field for the Biden administration right now, as they're trying to pass one of the most ambitious pieces of legislation that we have seen in recent years.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sarah, thank you, Sarah Westwood of The Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 We're continuing to follow that. And, by the way, when we talk about vaccines and getting them out there right now, how about a company setting a standard like this to incentivize workers to do so? \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  is saying it will give employees 1,000 bucks to get the COVID vaccine.\par \par \uc2 That's a start. More after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, more controversy at the border, not so much with the growing throngs there, but now talk the administration will provide vaccines for those who are gathering there. And to say gathering might be an understatement.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Bill Melugin in McAllen, Texas -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 We have got some developing news we'd like to report to you. Take a look over my shoulder right now. You will see an emergency tent compound that was literally built here by the city of McAllen overnight. This was not here last evening. This was literally built in the overnight hours.\par \par \uc2 And it's being used to house illegal immigrants who have been released from Border Patrol custody. The city says they have to do this because Border Patrol has been releasing so many illegals in their city that some of them are being stranded out on the streets and they have got nowhere to go. So they are now building this compound to house some of those folks.\par \par \uc2 They say it has to be done. And they're being very critical of the federal government. Take a look at this drone video we shot this morning. This is when we got out here about maybe 7:00, 8:00 this morning.\par \par \uc2 When we first got word about this tent compound, there weren't a whole lot of migrants here just yet. It was the early stages of being built. But what we're being told is this had to happen because there are so many immigrants on the streets of McAllen released from the feds.\par \par \uc2 And we witnessed this happening on Monday. Take a look at this video we shot in downtown McAllen on Monday. We watched over and over and over as Border Patrol buses were dropping busload after busload of immigrants at a charity in downtown McAllen, just releasing them by the busloads.\par \par \uc2 We watched hundreds of them being released. Well, that Catholic charity can only take in so many people, they are well over capacity. And because they are so full, some of those folks didn't have anywhere to stay. They're wandering the streets of McAllen. And that's why the city says they had to build this.\par \par \uc2 But they put out a scathing statement towards the federal government. Take a look at this, saying in part -- quote -- "The city intends to demand relief from the federal government for the alarming number of migrants that are being released into the city of McAllen and that as these immigrants are released by the federal government -- as these immigrants are released, the federal government does not test them for COVID-19. A third party tests them for COVID and 19 once they are dropped off by Border Patrol."\par \par \uc2 And some shocking COVID numbers for you right here. Take a look at this, the city of McAllen reporting that immigrants released by the federal government, there have been 7000 COVID-positive migrants released into the city of McAllen just since the month of February, more than 1, 500 of which were just in the last week alone.\par \par \uc2 So, as you come back out here live, you can tell from that statement there the city of McAllen incredibly frustrated with the federal government. They are now having to build their own tent compound here to deal with this overflow. As they say, the federal government is just pumping illegal immigrants out onto their streets. Many of them have COVID.\par \par \uc2 And they say the federal government has to change something, because they're having to foot the bill for this now. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bill Melugin, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 All right, the latest on Governor Cuomo in New York. Fully three-quarters of the Democratic powers that be in New York want him out of there. And, increasingly, average New Yorkers are saying pretty much the same.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  should not be -- have anything to do with politics.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm not too sure, because he came out and stated that he didn't do it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think he should resign immediately.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Allegations are one thing. Proving it is another.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Three out of four New York lawmakers want Governor Cuomo out, including a Democratic state senator we will be talking to who says essentially this: The sooner, the better.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, they can talk all impeachment they want to in New York state. Right now, if they're pressuring the governor to call it quits, he is not giving them any ammunition, at least not now, saying that he is going to stick in his job and do what the people elected him to do.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas is following this in New York -- Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, yes, look, 74 percent of New York state lawmakers, mostly Democrats, believe Cuomo should go.\par \par \uc2 But if the governor does not resign, the Democrats would have to impeach him. A simple majority of Assembly members is needed for that to happen to draft the articles of impeachment. Then, two-thirds majority of state senators and seven judges on the court of appeals would then have to vote to find him guilty.\par \par \uc2 And during that impeachment trial, Cuomo has to step down. It's only been done once before, this entire process, in New York in 1913. And, look, all of this as for district attorneys for Albany, Westchester, Nassau counties and Manhattan have now requested evidence gathered by the attorney general's office be turned over for criminal investigations into the governor.\par \par \uc2 The Albany DA is continuing its investigation into the allegations made by an unnamed executive assistant who says Cuomo groped her seconds before asking to take this selfie in the executive mansion in 2019. She claims Cuomo put his hand under her blouse in a separate incident months later.\par \par \uc2 The Westchester DA is investigating claims made by a state trooper that Cuomo personally asked that she joined his security detail and then sexually harassed her. Former aide Charlotte Bennett said this about Cuomo's video apology and bizarre kissing montage from yesterday designed to show that he's just a touchy-feely kind of guy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLOTTE BENNETT, CUOMO ACCUSER: And I don't want an apology. It's not necessary. It's fake. And his propaganda video was not only uncomfortable and inappropriate, but downright weird and unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Despite calls for impeachment right now, Neil, one Republican on the Judiciary Committee said: "We can't let emotions drive us."\par \par \uc2 It will take about a month for them to complete their ongoing impeachment investigation -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bryan, thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 To \uc2 Brad Hoylman\uc2  right now, a Democratic New York state senator.\par \par \uc2 Senator, it doesn't look like the governor has any intention of resigning. Do you think he should be impeached?\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. BRAD HOYLMAN (D-NY): ... for the attorney general's really comprehensive report.\par \par \uc2 To me, this is probably the tip of the iceberg. I mean, you have seen patterns of this kind of behavior relayed throughout this entire report. I cannot really fathom the hypocrisy that the governor's undertaking when he, for so many years used to advertise near state and his administration as a leader in human rights and rights for individuals who have been sexually abused, LGBTQ people.\par \par \uc2 And then this. It's a national embarrassment. The governor must go. I think the Assembly has enough information to act on the attorney general's report, bring those articles of impeachment to the Senate as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If you had to wage whether your colleagues would vote on something like that, how do you think it will go?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HOYLMAN: Let me just say this.\par \par \uc2 This is not political, as the governor would argue. This, if anything, is the most apolitical act I have witnessed and all of my time in Albany. There's virtually no support in the state legislature for Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 . And when I say no, I mean zero.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You mentioned the attorney general, Senator.\par \par \uc2 And she has been among those listed as a potential gubernatorial candidate herself next year. Now, of course, that would put her in the unusual position of putting out this report and then, as Governor Cuomo seems to see it, capitalizing on that.\par \par \uc2 How do you feel about that, if she were to run for governor?\par \par \uc2 HOYLMAN: Well, I guess we will cross that bridge, she will cross that bridge when we get there.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, I don't think anyone can question the integrity of her investigation and the investigators, the comprehensiveness, the professionalism, and the conclusions that are supported by reams of evidence.\par \par \uc2 So, look, for Governor Cuomo to say that this is political, he himself led something at the time which was known as Troopergate to investigate one of his predecessors in office.\par \par \uc2 So, the difference here, I think, is that we almost seem to be unanimously supporting the attorney general, if not in reality. Again, I don't know of a single legislator who has spoken up in support of Governor Cuomo.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Senator, keep us posted.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very, very much, Senator \uc2 Brad Hoylman\uc2 , a Democrat in the New York state Senate. Could be a very influential player in all of this.\par \par \uc2 We will keep you posted if we hear any statements and reaction to calls, repeated calls we have had into Governor Cuomo's office. They have not been returned.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, the Great White Way is back in business. A new Broadway show, brand-new, first since the pandemic, it's called "Pass Over," but you first better hand over proof that you have been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 We have the very, very latest right now Lydia Hu who on that -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, after more than a year, Broadway is coming back, but with those new restrictions in place. So, will the crowds return? We're asking and getting some insight.\par \par \uc2 That's coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we are just learning for the White House right now that the president at this point is not planning to lift international travel restrictions, but developing a policy to transition to a new system.\par \par \uc2 We don't know what that new system will be. But we're learning as well that the plan will require nearly all foreign nationals traveling to the United States to be fully vaccinated. Again, the issue of timing and details, we just don't have right now, but, again, a requirement that will probably raise some issues abroad that those who want to make their way here better be fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Let's get a read on all this from Dr. Syra Madad of the New York City Health and Hospitals Special Pathogens Program senior director.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, what do you think of this, cracking down on those who come here, making sure they're vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 DR. SYRA MADAD, NEW YORK CITY HEALTH AND HOSPITALS: Well, I think we're certainly in a period in the pandemic, both here domestically and internationally, where cases are continuing to increase, especially with the hyper-transmissible Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 So, only way we can layer on more protection to ensure we have less community transmission or less virus coming into the country, the better.\par \par \uc2 And so these mitigation measures of ensuring people are vaccinated or tested before they enter the country certainly provides that layer of protection that's needed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Doctor, how do you feel also that these mask requirements even for those who've been fully vaccinated? They're not too pleased about it. A restaurant owner was telling me he's not too thrilled about it either. But what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 Are they that much of a risk that they should, indeed, be wearing masks?\par \par \uc2 MADAD: Well, I think, first, masks totally work. And they're part of the mitigation strategies and layers that we have in our toolbox to prevent the spread of COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 Now, the best and primary strategy is certainly to get vaccinated. And with the news of the Delta variant being more transmissible and, in uncommon events, vaccinated, fully vaccinated people are able to transmit the virus to others, that's where that mask guidance now comes into play in areas with higher substantial community transmission to wear that mask indoors.\par \par \uc2 So I do think that is the right recommendation, given that we're seeing increased spread. But I think it's also important to note that the large majority of transmission that is happening is among unvaccinated communities.\par \par \uc2 So there's two things. First. the primary goal is to get vaccinated and, if you're not vaccinated, to continue wearing a mask and apply the risk reduction techniques to protect yourself and those around you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're learning as well, Doctor, that maybe as soon as next month, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  will go ahead and approve I believe \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 's vaccine. Don't know about some of the others that are out there.\par \par \uc2 Do you think this will make a big difference? Do you think people who heretofore have been a little concerned about taking a treatment that has only been granted emergency approval, that many more will get vaccinated, if it has a nod from the \uc2 FDA\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Can you hear us?\par \par \uc2 MADAD: Yes, I do think so.\par \par \uc2 Sorry. I think the audio is going out a little bit.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 MADAD: But I do think that getting that full approval of the Pfizer COVID- 19 vaccine will encourage people that are unvaccinated to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 In fact, some of the surveys, for example, the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  has shown, in terms of individuals attitudes, about three out of 10 are stating that they are more prone or more likely to get the COVID-19 vaccine if it has a full approval. So I certainly do think it's going to make a dent in the overall vaccination rates.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, I think that many different organizations, institutions, agencies like health care systems and other employee -- employment agencies will probably start mandating COVID-19 vaccines once they have that full approval.\par \par \uc2 And I think we have collected so much great data, real-world data over the past seven months after clinical trials to show that these vaccines are safe and effective, and they can help prevent severe illness, hospitalization and death.\par \par \uc2 So it's high time that we get that approval, and that people that are reluctant to get vaccinated to protect themselves and those around them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Syra Madad, thank you very much. We will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 To Lydia Hu right now looking at Broadway reopening, the first show to kick things off since the pandemic, new show to kick things off, but there are some requirements -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 HU: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the patrons that are going to be seeing the premiere of "Pass Over," a play here at the August Wilson Theatre tonight, they will have to demonstrate that they have been vaccinated with proof that they have received those shots. And they must also wear a mask in order to gain entry to the theater.\par \par \uc2 But it's not just here at August Wilson Theatre. It is all 41 Broadway theaters across in New York City. A particular challenge for Broadway right now is tourism, because usually about 65 percent of the audience are made up of tourists.\par \par \uc2 And with tourism down across the city, the question is whether or not there's enough of a draw from a local audience, the tristate area, to make up for those ticket sales.\par \par \uc2 Just a little while ago, Neil, I talked to a Broadway producer who pointed out that, because "Pass Over" is a play, that's going to help with ticket sales, because that usually caters to more of a local audience, a New York City-based audience, rather than, say, a musical.\par \par \uc2 But, still, there's still the added pressure of the cost of just reopening. Just to bring a single production like "Pass Over" back online and make it available, it costs between $1 million and $4 million. And that's on top of the new vaccination requirements and mask mandates that are an added cost.\par \par \uc2 But still, Neil, not every precaution is being taken.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLOTTE ST. MARTIN, PRESIDENT, BROADWAY LEAGUE: We are not socially distancing. And that's been one of our commitments from day one. The financial model for Broadway just doesn't work with socially distanced audiences.\par \par \uc2 So it's one of the reasons why we had to wait this long.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: Now, the Broadway League says, Well, generally, about 75 percent of tickets need to be sold out in order to support a performance.\par \par \uc2 Anything short of that means that they are in the hole. And that could be problematic if you see that over the next coming weeks. I did look up ticket sales for tonight's performance, which is at 8:00 p.m. And it does appear, based on what I'm seeing online, that at least 75 percent of the tickets have been sold, maybe a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 So that is promising news for this particular show. We will have to see if that continues to happen in the weeks to come, as these requirements are enforced. Also, something else to look out for, the musicals are coming back online in September with shows like "Hamilton" and "The Lion King."\par \par \uc2 Of course, as I mentioned, those draw more of a national and international crowd. And, as we know, those tourists are not back here in the city. So it could be more of a challenge to get those tickets sold and see an audience in those theaters -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 We do want to be entertained, though. That's pretty clear. Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of wanting to be entertained, our parent company, FOX Entertainment, is out with earnings and revenues that handily beat estimates in the fourth quarter, coming in at 65 cents a share on an earnings-per-share basis, as we like to say, revenues close to $2.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 Those shares are not really moving much in after-hour trading, but they have appreciated about 20 percent since the beginning of the year, about 31 percent in the last 12 months. We're on top of that.\par \par \uc2 More after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, when we worry about inflation, it's the companies that pass that along to you. So you have seen it really at a Kraft Heinz and \uc2 Clorox\uc2  and some of these other big players, who've noticed that you will still buy when they raise their prices, because prices are going up for them as well.\par \par \uc2 But they seem to be pushing out a string right here, indicating, in the case of \uc2 Clorox\uc2 , certainly in Kraft Heinz, that these are worries down the road, not so much for Hostess, out with some numbers that beat expectations, but still saying that the inflationary pressures that are there, they're there. They don't miss them, and they're not ignoring them. But they expect people to still buy lots of goodies from them, even if they do go up.\par \par \uc2 Hitha Herzog here, a retail analyst extraordinaire, on what he makes of this.\par \par \uc2 You know, some companies are in the Hostess mind-set that, look, prices go up, we could pass it along, no problem. We don't like it going on and on like this, but we can deal with it. Others not so much.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see this going, Hitha?\par \par \uc2 HITHA HERZOG, RETAIL WATCHER: Yes, it really depends on the company, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And if you're looking at it by a company that produces product such as Hostess in these individual multi-package packages, it's going to be different than, let's say, a major retailer like a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  or a Target.\par \par \uc2 So what Hostess is doing is -- you're absolutely right. They did mention the fact that there are inflation headwinds. However, they're innovating and they're offering other products. So, if you look at the study that came out from \uc2 Oracle\uc2  back in May, it was talking about how across the board consumers were buying snacks like crazy during the pandemic, everything from glazed donut sticks to cereal-flavored coffee creamer.\par \par \uc2 Mostly, the millennials were doing that. But Hostess is really capitalizing on that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Not just millennials. Not just millennials, young lady.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you about that. There are ways companies can hide these increases right, Hitha? In other words, I have always noticed this phenomenon, I get a bag of chips and there aren't as many chips in the bag. But the bag is roughly the same price. They do things like that, right?\par \par \uc2 But, I mean, what other stuff do they do?\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: Right. It's called shrinkflation. So that's exactly what you're talking about. Price of the good goes up. So they have the same packaging, but you have less of that product.\par \par \uc2 You also have companies that include these fees, right? Some companies will tack on fees. God knows what those are for me. In the case of Uber, you might look at your Uber and say, oh, my gosh, well, here's a fee for -- who knows what that's for?\par \par \uc2 But, also -- and we're going to pivot back to Hostess for a second. They were talking about this innovation that they were doing. Well, they would maybe have a specific package like a single serve cupcake or doughnut, that maybe they will add in something extra, or they will repackage it so it seems like you're getting more for the price. But actually the price has increased and you just feel like you're getting more product.\par \par \uc2 So there are different ways that these companies try to hide the inflationary price increase. But let's be honest. If you're going out and buying doughnuts, you're probably price-agnostic. You just want that doughnut.\par \par \uc2 Neil, I was reading through the earnings report right now. I'm not going to lie. I may be going and getting a Twinkie in a second.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, real quickly, right?\par \par \uc2 I do want to get your take, though, on how severe this inflation is. Normally -- and you and I have discussed this. You know, inflation remains an issue if people continue paying more for goods, and goods especially they like or they find particularly attractive or delicious. And it stops or slows when they stop doing that.\par \par \uc2 Some companies have indicated they have run into resistance by consumers who say, all right, that's enough, that's enough, but not many. There are a few. But where do you see this going?\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: I think that we are going to certainly see those inflation headwinds affect all products and all product categories, mostly because, Neil, there's also another -- other factors, such as there's a lack of materials.\par \par \uc2 So you think about what's going on with some of the computers and the electronics, you don't have those microchips. They're still in short supply and demand. So, or I should say increase in demand, but short supply.\par \par \uc2 So when you have that, and also when things are -- products are being imported, and when you have a limited supply of things getting imported, that's also going to have an impact on that inflation. So while I think that, for the most part, consumers are price-agnostic when it comes to an iPhone or doughnuts or very specific things, there's also other factors that are going to be involved there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 All right, and I know you. You're very thin and fit, Hitha. I cannot see you eating Twinkies. Me, I don't think any viewer would be stunned to hear that I would pay top dollar for them.\par \par \uc2 Hitha, great seeing you. Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 HERZOG: You too, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime, a lot of people like working at home. They want to continue working at home.\par \par \uc2 And you won't believe what they told some folks what they would give up to make sure they could continue doing that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, what would you give up if you wanted to work from home full-time?\par \par \uc2 A lot of folks want to do that. A lot of their bosses aren't too keen on the idea, but 65 percent would be happy to take a 5 percent pay cut. More than one out of three would give up their 401(k). People, I have got to talk to you about that. More than half would give up social media for one year. As if. More than half would give up \uc2 Netflix\uc2  for one year and half would give up \uc2 Amazon\uc2  for one year just for the liberty of working at home.\par \par \uc2 Mike Gunzelman here, the Internet radio host, Kat Timpf, "Sincerely Kat" host on FOX Nation, FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Welcome guys.\par \par \uc2 Kat, that's a lot to give up. Would you give any of that up to maintain a couple of days still working from home? Maybe all those days?\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE TIMPF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No.\par \par \uc2 I mean, certainly, I don't really have the option of working from home right now. But for some jobs, some people who think that they can do their job just as well from home, and have been doing it from home, they're saying, why I got to go back?\par \par \uc2 Look, this -- these lockdowns and being out of the office went on for a long time. And a lot of people adjusted their lives accordingly, and some drastically.\par \par \uc2 I will say that I personally know two or three people who I follow on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , who I know or who I met who are now fully living in vans on purpose, so they can drive around the country and, like, appreciate nature or whatever it is they're doing, and work alone.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I'm not that into nature.\par \par \uc2 And work from their vans or coffee places. People like that, they're like, hey, I love nature. I love my van. I don't want to go back.\par \par \uc2 Some people made changes, and this lifestyle suits them and they just don't want to go back to how it was.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Gunz, what would it take to claw you out of your parents' basement?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: By the way, you're not in your parents' basement.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just curious. What would it take? What would it take?\par \par \uc2 Oh.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MIKE GUNZELMAN, FOX NEWS HEADLINES ENTERTAINMENT REPORTER: I actually -- I want to get out.\par \par \uc2 I have that personality where I thrive in an office setting. People see me in the hallway, they're like, yo, Gunz. I'm like, what's going on? Like, I need that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're a very social guy. That's true. You are.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: These walls are getting smaller and smaller around me, Neil. I need to get out.\par \par \uc2 But I can't understand why some people are kind of hesitant to completely return. You look at like the technological advancements. Before the pandemic happened, the average person wasn't using Zoom or Slack. Now it's become routine, a normal part of our lives. So technology has made it easier.\par \par \uc2 And then also, like, what about those that are starting to feel stressed about 60 minutes, 90-minute commute back and forth twice a day? Like, that can kind of be a bummer. Like, why would people want to go back to that?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: And then also the parents aspect. Parents might want to be home with their children still, especially if they're young.\par \par \uc2 So, you combine all that and it's like, I will take a 5 percent pay cut, for those that want to. Not me, though. I need all the money I can get, Neil. Come on now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No. No.\par \par \uc2 Yes. I understand. You're a capitalist at heart. I admire that.\par \par \uc2 So, Kat, you're a busy capitalist, too.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You got all these hit shows you're on. You're doing a lot of different things. So, obviously, it's not easy for you to say, all right, I will do it all from home. I get that. But\par \par \uc2 a lot of people want a balance with that. Can I have one or two days at home and all that? What do you think of that? Because some companies are trying to meld the two. And some are going so far as to say, all right, you can keep it remote for a long, long time, maybe permanently.\par \par \uc2 Where are you on this?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: All right.\par \par \uc2 Well, I mean, for me personally, obviously, as somebody who took off two days for her own wedding, I might be the wrong person to ask. But I love coming to work. And, again...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That was one day too many, young lady, one day too many.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes, I know, right? I can't believe I didn't get fired.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I didn't really like that, yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: But, like, again, I'm coming in on a show where we sit there. We talk. We all have to be in person or it's a lot more difficult.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: But if you're someone who answers e-mails for a living, or -- I don't know what normal jobs are.\par \par \uc2 Banks, spreadsheets, whatever the normal jobs are that people have, and you're sitting at your computer anyway, you might say, what's the difference if I do it at home?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. You're right. It depends on the field. It does. You're right.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gunz, like what would make you do it from home all the time? What would you give up? I know you're not keen on that. But what would you give up?\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: I mean, I love social media. So I wouldn't give that up.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: But it would have to be it would have to be a lot in order to keep you home in all honesty.\par \par \uc2 But I do believe -- I thought the hybrid schedule was what we'd be going to. But with this variant, I think we're going to keep doing remote for a longer time, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think you're right.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: I think that's a big thing happening.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I think you're right.\par \par \uc2 Guys, I want to thank you both. I didn't mean to jump on you.\par \par \uc2 But we got some news on the NIH director, Francis Collins, who wants to talk to us tomorrow about some of these spikes in cases and the kind of thing that could keep a little bit of this virtual work style going for a while.\par \par \uc2 Just how long is a while? We will find out tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 "THE FIVE" now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210805eh8400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Scarlett Johansson Suing \uc2 Disney\uc2 ; Robot Workers?; Interview With Cochise County, Arizona, Sheriff Mark Dannels; Vaccine Mandates Dividing Unions?; CDC Warns of Delta Variant; Inflation Spiking\b0\par\par\uc2 Jonathan Hunt, Bret Baier, Steve Harrigan, Charles Payne, Susan  Li \par \uc2 7,785 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Inflation spiking and now more signs you will be paying more.\par \par \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2 , the maker of well-known products like Pampers and Tide, warning, costs are growing up so fast and sales are going down. So expect to begin hiking prices, the company does, later on this fall. And \uc2 P&G\uc2 , well, they're not alone. Companies behind products like Budweiser, Evian water, \uc2 Nestle\uc2 's ice cream, and Baileys Irish Cream aren't set to either be hiking prices or looking to hike them.\par \par \uc2 All this as lawmakers are eying more than $4 trillion in new spending that could push prices even higher.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' \uc2 Susan Li\uc2  keeping track of all of these price hikes -- Susan.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, big business have been warning us that prices are going up. And if they have to pay more, you can bet that you will have to pay more.\par \par \uc2 From Tide maker \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2 , to \uc2 Nestle\uc2 , \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 , yogurt maker Dannon, Chipotle, Budweiser brewery, A.B. \uc2 InBev\uc2 , even Barbie maker \uc2 Mattel\uc2 , over and over again, the company executives are pointing to some of the highest price pressures that they have seen in years. And the numbers prove it.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's key gauge of inflation jumping 3.5 percent in the month of June from last year. And that is the fastest gain in 30 years, going back to 1991. Now, it's not only input costs, like aluminum, plastic and corn going up, but also wages, according to the companies.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 FedEx\uc2 , \uc2 Nestle\uc2  all pointing to wage pressures and having to possibly pay more in order to attract workers. In fact, McDonald's CEO says that workers are coming back faster in the states that have ended the $300-per-week jobless benefits.\par \par \uc2 And 74 percent of companies surveyed by the \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2  say that wages just went up in the past three months, with the median increase in wages of maybe 4 to 5 percent. But here's the good thing is that we also learned this week that despite having to pay higher prices for wages and commodities, corporate America is still making a ton of money, thanks to that D.C. money printing that you just mentioned.\par \par \uc2 The five biggest tech companies in the world reported a combined profit of -- get this -- close to $75 billion. That's almost the entire value of \uc2 General Motors\uc2 . And they did it in three months. Charles, American capitalism is a great thing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It certainly is.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So how long can folks keep, though, absorbing all of these higher prices?\par \par \uc2 With me now, Democratic strategist Marjorie Clifton and Republican strategist Noelle Nikpour.\par \par \uc2 Noelle, people are complaining. I mean, I saw an NPR poll, an NPR poll that the number one problem the number one issue, by far, was inflation. Deep second was wages. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 NOELLE NIKPOUR, REPUBLICAN FUND-RAISER: Yes, well, Charles, I mean, look, this is what's happening. You have got inflation in every area.\par \par \uc2 I was just talking to a friend of mine earlier that's got this company called Fortress Anchors. And they're telling me that they can't find laborers, that they have had an increase in like raw materials. You're seeing it in every space. I mean, you have got the housing markets.\par \par \uc2 Forget about getting a pool in your backyard. There's a two-year wait, bicycles, everything. It's affecting every aspect, every avenue.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hey, by the way, it's Laura Fink who's with us, not Marjorie.\par \par \uc2 I apologize, Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA FINK, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Hey, no worries.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But are Democrats going to have -- are Democrats going to have to apologize for these prices going higher, especially if they get their wish and they ram through another $4 trillion in spending?\par \par \uc2 FINK: Well, I don't think it's the Democrats or the Republicans' fault that prices are going higher. It's called a pandemic. And it's a once-in-a- lifetime experience, we hope.\par \par \uc2 Now, we know that those price increases have come down in certain areas. We saw it down 60 percent lumber. So that means homebuilding is going to increase. We're going to see some improvements there. And a lot of these -- a lot of economists are saying these are temporary.\par \par \uc2 And wages have gone up. I listened to that last report. Wages have gone up and the purchasing power of consumers is up. That helps. You would be hard- pressed to find a working family that's upset that they're making more money for doing the work and making a living wage maybe for the first time in decades.\par \par \uc2 So that's all a good thing. When these temporary price increases go down -- and, by all accounts, all of these companies expect sales to increase, even though \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2 , we talked about, they expect 2 to 4 percent increases in sales, and that's over a year where they made record profits.\par \par \uc2 Now, that means the economy isn't going to slow down anytime soon. We're recovering from the pandemic. We're putting one foot in front of the other. We see wages increasing. That's good for working America.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FINK: And we're going to see that continue.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, well, here's here's the thing, Noelle.\par \par \uc2 I want to go back to you on this. There are a few things.\par \par \uc2 And I hope you're right about everything you just said. But real wages have been down. And the same report today said that real income down 3 percent in June, down 4.2 percent in May, and down 5.8 percent in April. That's the insidious nature of inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, when you're sitting at your kitchen table, Noelle, and, yes, you just got to raise in your wage, and you wonder why you came home from the grocery store with fewer groceries, that explains it, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 NIKPOUR: Yes, that's exactly why I was shaking my head, because they may have higher wages. You may go home with a bigger paycheck. But it's a wash, Charles.\par \par \uc2 It's a wash, because when you go to the grocery store, and you have got to pay more for your rice and your cereal and your milk and whatever you want to buy, it's a wash. So that higher wage is going into inflated prices, because, after all, all the stores have got to pass this down to the consumer.\par \par \uc2 So they're paying more wages. It's costing more for them to order because of the demand and the shortage of workers to make the products. So you're passing that -- the money down to the consumer.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 NIKPOUR: So even though they have a higher wage, it's being passed on to them. So they're not really gaining.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Do you worry, Laura, that there could be resentment?\par \par \uc2 In other words, this last COVID package, $1.9 trillion, extended unemployment benefits, there have been a whole lot of federal government money that's gone -- gone into households, and they spent it, to your point. They have enjoyed it. And a lot of that does push up inflation and make costs go up.\par \par \uc2 For the person who's working, who hasn't said, hey, listen, I'm not going to work and just accept these benefits, but still, because so many others have, the prices for me to make my ends meet every single month have gone up, do you worry that could be some sort of resentment out there?\par \par \uc2 FINK: Well, I worry the -- that is just a little bit, I would say, incorrect. It's not the actual benefits that are driving up inflation. It's its supply chain problems, which we have seen over and over again.\par \par \uc2 Here's what I would say. That $200-plus tax credit that is landing in American families' bank accounts starting now and will continue, that is a Biden plan. And that's going to help alleviate some of these short-term costs that I think are legitimate. I acknowledge that.\par \par \uc2 And I also think, when you look at the long term benefits of something like the bipartisan infrastructure bill that just cleared its first hurdle this week, those are long-term investments that are going to create a foundation in our economy, going to create career paths over time.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 FINK: That's not dumping the money right away. That's over years.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 FINK: ... to save our roads, our bridges, our airports, and to create career pathways, real careers with real good wages for tens of thousands of Americans.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And to your point, Laura, it's real infrastructure, right, something we could all applaud. So, hopefully, we will get that over the finish line soon.\par \par \uc2 Ladies, thank you both very much. Have a fantastic weekend.\par \par \uc2 NIKPOUR: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: By the way, folks, cruise ships, cruise line ships getting clobbered after six passengers aboard a \uc2 Royal Caribbean\uc2  cruise ship tested positive for COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 Now, four of these guests were vaccinated. \uc2 Royal Caribbean\uc2  reportedly expanded expanding its testing now for all crews five nights or longer that leave the United States. And, in the meantime, these cruise line stocks were down sharply on that news.\par \par \uc2 Well, new documents from the CDC painting a dire picture of where we stand in the fight against COVID. Our own Bret Baier has an exclusive interview tonight with CDC Director \uc2 Dr. Rochelle Walensky\uc2 . Bret, by the way, will be here with a preview of that interview.\par \par \uc2 And two sides coming together to demand action the border, but is the White House listening?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): The people who live in your district are literally under siege.\par \par \uc2 REP. HENRY CUELLAR (D-TX): Something has to change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE, WHITE HOUSE PRINCIPAL DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: The president has always said he's going to follow the science and he's going to listen to public health experts. That's what we do, right?\par \par \uc2 That's why we are all following the CDC guidance on masking. And so that's our North Star.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, that North Star is setting off some serious fireworks.\par \par \uc2 New CDC documents showing how the organization came to change its mask guidelines.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Steve Harrigan in Atlanta with the very latest -- Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, the CDC releasing data today to backup some of its policy recommendation changes mainly concerning those people who are fully vaccinated, first, that fully vaccinated people should continue to wear masks in certain conditions, and, second, that fully vaccinated people can easily spread the Delta variant of COVID.\par \par \uc2 This as hospital rates continue to climb across the U.S. Most of those hospitalized for COVID, though, are unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. WILLIAM SCHAFFNER, DEPARTMENT OF PREVENTIVE MEDICINE CHAIRMAN, VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY: More hospitalized patients with COVID, with serious COVID. And here's the bottom line. Well over 90 percent of Those patients are unvaccinated. It really is unusual to see a vaccinated person need hospitalization.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The CDC average right now for seven days of hospitalization stands at about 3, 500. That is up 32 percent from just one week ago.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the overall picture, it's only about a fifth of the hospitalizations of the peak during last January -- Charles, back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you very much, Steve.\par \par \uc2 We're going to actually be talking with Bret Baier shortly. He has an exclusive interview with CDC Director Walensky tonight.\par \par \uc2 But, first, I want to ask Dr. Bob Lahita what he makes this. He is the health director of St. Joseph's Medical Center.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Lahita, it's -- again, it gets back to a couple of things here. The -- from the very beginning, obviously, we all want to follow the science. It just seems to be moving either extraordinarily rapidly, or there just have been a lot of mistakes with messaging, and that's got a lot of folks unnerved and worried.\par \par \uc2 DR. BOB LAHITA, ST. JOSEPH UNIVERSITY HOSPITAL: Yes, Charles.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a -- this Delta variant is very, very infectious. This is a big surprise. But it comes out of India. And it's -- there's 1,000 times more of a viral load in a patient, whether you're vaccinated or not.\par \par \uc2 The interesting thing is that you can still be symptomatic and be vaccinated. There were 35,000 symptomatic cases among 162 million patients that were vaccinated. If you're not vaccinated, then you have a big problem, because this various -- this Delta variant is very specifically doing major damage to the nasopharynx.\par \par \uc2 That's the nose in the back of the throat, and also goes into the lungs, and really causes respiratory failure more rapidly. And this is in the unvaccinated. If you're vaccinated, as you heard, you're not likely to wind up in the hospital at all, but you may have symptoms.\par \par \uc2 And so we're -- what the president is saying is to wear a mask to prevent you from infecting others and preventing them from infecting you. And that is in closed spaces.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: I don't think you need to wear a mask in the -- out in the open.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, the headline today that caught everyone -- that got everyone's attention, the CDC internal report saying that the variant, the Delta variant, is as contagious as chicken pox.\par \par \uc2 It also goes on to say more transmissible than MERS, SARS, Ebola, the common cold, seasonal flu, and smallpox. That certainly got my attention. What does it mean, though, in real life?\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: It means in real life that this is a very, very sticky virus that replicates rapidly. It sticks to the receptors in your mucous membranes of your nose. It even gets in your eyes.\par \par \uc2 And it replicates quickly, 1,000 times more powerful than the Alpha variant, which we saw last year. So the fact that we're all vaccinated, or most of us are, is a good thing. The people who aren't vaccinated, I worry about, . I worry about my patients, my family. I worry about everybody that has not had the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Professional golfer Jon Rahm has now pulled out a two tournaments for a positive test, and he has been vaccinated. He was on his way to winning one of them and more recently right now the Olympics.\par \par \uc2 Some are wondering, so, if you have the vaccine, if you have been vaccinated, can you also still be something of a -- could that make you a carrier as well? This question is starting to pop up. And I think it probably needs to be nipped in the bud or answered quickly.\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: Yes, that -- you're right on target, Charles. That's everybody's concern.\par \par \uc2 You can be vaccinated, you can carry the virus, you have no symptoms. Roughly a third of the people who are infected with this variant don't have symptoms at all. So think about this. They can infect little children in an -- the unvaccinated children between the ages of 12 and, say, 6 months, they can be infected by an adult who has no symptoms, who may be at a barbecue. And that could be a problem.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: So that is the danger here and the reason for our concern about wearing masks and wearing masks in groups.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Dr. Lahita, thank you very much. Always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And, again, our own Bret Baier has an exclusive interview tonight with CDC Director \uc2 Dr. Rochelle Walensky\uc2 . Bret, though, will be here first with a preview of that.\par \par \uc2 Also, no testing and free movement for migrants at the border, despite COVID-19 cases surging. Meet the Arizona sheriff who is sounding the alarm.\par \par \uc2 And vaccine mandates are now dividing unions. Coming up, we have got two local leaders on opposite sides of the mandate debate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Fired up and now teaming up to take on the border crisis, as it's become a health crisis. We have got the very latest.\par \par \uc2 And don't be surprised to see more of these at a restaurant near you. We have got the scoop next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: New York City will require vaccinations or COVID testing for municipal employees starting mid-September. The mandate is dividing unions across the city.\par \par \uc2 My next guest says that the order ignores workers' civil liberties.\par \par \uc2 New York City's Local 2507 Union President Oren Barzilay joins me now. He represents EMTs, paramedics and fire inspectors across the city.\par \par \uc2 Oren, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 With the damage that COVID did to New York City, the shutdown, and now with the Delta variant rising the way it is, you still are resistant to mandatory vaccinations. Why?\par \par \uc2 OREN BARZILAY, PRESIDENT, NEW YORK CITY LOCAL 2507 UNION: I just want to be clear first. We're not telling people not to get the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 All we're saying is that it should be an individual decision. People should have a right of what's being injected into their body. You know, there's so much misinformation that's given by one agency, a federal agency or a city agency. They're constantly contradicting each other.\par \par \uc2 I haven't said this before, but I had two men -- two members who died within four days after getting vaccinated. I have countless others who have serious medical reaction and have serious medical conditions today because of the vaccination.\par \par \uc2 They're not sharing all the truth about the side effects of this vaccine. They're glorifying all of the positive things, which is great.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARZILAY: You know, 99 percent effective. That's something that they take pride of.\par \par \uc2 And -- but we don't know if that's the truth. And if it is 99 percent effective...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Oren, let me just jump in one second, if I can.\par \par \uc2 Just two things then. Number one, are you -- it's a coincidence that these two gentlemen died after taking a vaccine? You're not sure if the vaccine was the cause of their deaths? You're not saying that for a fact right now?\par \par \uc2 BARZILAY: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK.\par \par \uc2 The paramedics and EMTs, front-line workers, they connect the public to the hospital system. Are you concerned about the public relations aspect of it, if they are resistant to taking a vaccine? Certainly -- and I'm in the public. If I know someone who's a paramedic and they haven't taken a vaccination, I'm going to question its effectiveness.\par \par \uc2 BARZILAY: I understand that.\par \par \uc2 But you know what? Now they're telling us, if you're vaccinated, you can still carry the infection and you can spread it to somebody else. So what's the difference? If you're vaccinated, and you can contaminate somebody else, how does that impact us? What will make a change?\par \par \uc2 When we were out there -- and we were the only ones out there that were told to go in and out -- when we were tested positive and had no symptoms, they told us, oh, just got back out there. It's fine. Don't worry about it. Just go do your job. Save -- save our city. And don't worry about it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARZILAY: And this is when -- again, when we were the only ones who were allowed to go out there, when we were testing positive.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Oren, I got a minute to go. I got a minute to go.\par \par \uc2 Believe me, I salute -- I salute the work you did. It was amazing, the EMTs and the paramedics.\par \par \uc2 Let me just ask, though, with a minute to go, is there something that can be done on the federal level, on the city level that would make you feel better, that will assuage some of your concerns, particularly about the miscommunication?\par \par \uc2 BARZILAY: If they told us that they will make sure our medical bills, and we will remain on payroll, should we have some sort of side effect from this vaccine, then they will put a lot of people's mind at ease.\par \par \uc2 But we're not going to be kept on payroll, and then not going to cover our bills. And, as a matter of fact, yesterday, the mayor announced that if we want to be COVID-tested, we have to pay out of own pocket. How is that reasonable?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, something is going to have to happen.\par \par \uc2 Oren, I'm sorry. I apologize. I'm out of time.\par \par \uc2 I appreciate your candidness. And I hope someone in charge is paying attention to what your concerns are. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BARZILAY: OK. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I have got to switch gears a little bit here, because not everyone, of course, is opposed to the requirements for vaccinations or testing.\par \par \uc2 New York City Teamster Local 237 Union president Gregory Floyd says that the mandate is a responsibility. He actually represents 24,000 city workers.\par \par \uc2 Gregory, thanks for joining.\par \par \uc2 I'm not sure if you heard your colleague Oren, but he brought up some serious concerns. And I'm really surprised that perhaps members of your own union aren't -- don't share these concerns, the mixed messages, the fact that it's emergency approval for the vaccine. Some people are not happy that it hasn't received an official approval just yet and some of the other mixed messages.\par \par \uc2 Are any of your members saying, hey, Gregory, it might be responsible, but we're not ready yet?\par \par \uc2 GREGORY FLOYD, PRESIDENT, NEW YORK CITY TEAMSTER LOCAL 237 UNION: Well, thank you, Charles, for having me on.\par \par \uc2 And let me clear something up. I'm not opposed to the mandate to have vaccinations and/or testing. That's responsible.\par \par \uc2 What I was opposed to is the way the mayor rolled this out. That's why there are a lot of concerns. Had we had a conversation before the announcement, all of these fears could be cleared up.\par \par \uc2 One, no, you do not have to take the vaccination. You should, but you do not have to. However, to work for New York City, you have to be tested. Now, where are the testing sites, who's going to pay for it, when they will be tested, all of those things should be made clear in the policy.\par \par \uc2 That's where the fear comes in. So, I am not opposed to his policy. I'm opposed to the way we rolled it out. That's why we have so many concerns.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Have you expressed these? Have they gone up the chain of command?\par \par \uc2 FLOYD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Obviously, Gregory, that this is -- this is a tough situation and time is of the essence.\par \par \uc2 FLOYD: Yes, we are in discussions right now.\par \par \uc2 Now, here is what is perplexing. Some of the policies won't take effect until mid-August. And most of the policy will take place in September. This is July 30. What is wrong with taking the rest of this week, next week, to iron out whatever concerns we have before you announced the policy?\par \par \uc2 It (AUDIO GAP) rollout. The questions would have been answered before people's fears take place. Now that you have people scared, you have pandemonium. And that's never a way to operate. So everybody is dug in on their own ideas.\par \par \uc2 Me, personally, my family and I, we have taken the vaccination. We have taken it in March and April.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 FLOYD: We are comfortable with the vaccination. That's our personal concern.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK.\par \par \uc2 FLOYD: And we see a lot of other people.\par \par \uc2 And when I heard my colleague say that people...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FLOYD: ... carrying the disease.....\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Gregory, I'm sorry. I have got to jump in here.\par \par \uc2 But you have represented your union well here, and you have articulated the issues. And, again, just like your counterpart Oren, I hope someone up there is listening to these legitimate concerns.\par \par \uc2 And I appreciate you coming on. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I want to now bring in my colleague Bret Baier. He is, of course, the anchor of "Special Report." And he's got a very special interview tonight, in fact, an exclusive at 6:00 p.m., with CDC director \uc2 Dr. Rochelle Walensky\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, Bret, so much to get into with the director. What are you trying to get out? What are the main focuses for you with this interview?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 I tell you, the main focus is what we're hearing from a ton of people, is, they want to hear the science, the data behind these decisions. A lot of people made the tough decision for their family to go ahead and get vaccinated, thinking that that was the thing that was going to clear them from having to wear masks and to be able to do things that they wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 And now kind of going backwards in a number of communities, being told they have to wear masks even if they're vaccinated. We want to get into the data with the new information that the CDC -- they didn't put it out, but it leaked out -- of what they thought about in these different circumstances where vaccinated people caught the Delta variant, COVID, in greater numbers.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the big numbers of the breakthrough disease, the breakthrough people that had vaccines and then had troubles or went to the hospital or died, they are minuscule when you compare them to the 163 million people across America who've been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So that's number one.\par \par \uc2 But, Charles, we received dozens and dozens of questions, a lot of them video questions from viewers. And you're going to see a number of them tonight, asking their own questions from families across the country with real concerns about going forward, what their kids are going to do in school. And they're emotional about it and upset.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And right before you -- I'm not sure if you were listening -- the union head in New York City of the paramedics and EMTs, they're frustrated. These are paramedics. And they're saying, hey, the way this is being cobbled together, we don't want to participate just yet. We have -- we lack confidence.\par \par \uc2 So, if they're in the system, and they lack confidence in what they're hearing, how could the CDC or this administration expect everyone to feel - - just to come on board?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. I heard that interview.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's really telling and eye-opening that they're on the front lines in New York, going to help people who are in trouble. And those people are, for the most part, all vaccinated. Of course, there are some decisions that we have seen in medical personnel who have chosen not to take the vaccine. And you wonder why they're making that choice.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So those are all questions for Dr. Walensky's tonight.\par \par \uc2 She's given us a lot of time. She said she will answer what we give her. And if you all or your viewers have any questions, feel free to @BretBaier on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or \uc2 Facebook\uc2  or \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: One of the -- from that leaked internal report you referenced, one of the parts that caught my attention is that the -- this Delta variant is more transmissible than MERS, SARS, Ebola, the common cold, seasonal flu and smallpox.\par \par \uc2 Two things. That's a heck of a list. And some of these things have never been cured. And so it gives you a real uneasy feeling, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, it does.\par \par \uc2 And what -- when do we get back to normal? This is the Delta variant. What happens when the next variant comes? Are we going to be able to be nimble enough to not have to go backwards each time? And that's some of the questions from people around the country.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And there are businesses that are fearing shutdowns, frankly. They're fearing the economic impact.\par \par \uc2 And we talked about this yesterday, Charles. No matter what it is, there will be some economic impact to all of these policy decisions that are being made. And if it affects schoolchildren and people with kids in school, it's going to be big.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And we're also hearing from restaurants who are saying they cannot take another blow, those who have survived.\par \par \uc2 Bret, while I have you here, I want to quickly switch gears to another story, the Justice Department saying the \uc2 IRS\uc2  must release former President Trump's tax returns to Congress. How big of a deal is this?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, for Democrats who've been wanting to get their hands on those, it's a big deal up on Capitol Hill. What's in them and what they will tell, we don't know.\par \par \uc2 From what we have seen them stories that leaked out, there was a lot of real estate tax deals that, by the book, but that's how the New York real estate tax structure worked for developers there. They're going to be looking with a fine-tooth comb.\par \par \uc2 And now the Treasury Department -- the DOJ says the Treasury Department should hand over those taxes to Capitol Hill. Democrats feel like there's something in there. And that's the reason the Trump people fought so hard to prevent that. But I think they're going to look forward.\par \par \uc2 And I bet you will see a few committee hearings on this topic.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I got a feeling we will. But what we're all looking forward to is your interview.\par \par \uc2 Folks, make sure you don't miss it, Bret's exclusive interview tonight at 6:00 p.m. Eastern time with CDC Director \uc2 Dr. Rochelle Walensky\uc2 . And get your questions in. There's still time.\par \par \uc2 Thanks a lot, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Hey, with the COVID cases now up on the border, we have got Democrats and Republicans teaming up. The question, of course, is, will the White House listen up?\par \par \uc2 And we have got a border sheriff to help us out. He's coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: It is not rational that I can't go to Canada because it may hurt the United States' COVID problem, and completely ignore a border that's on fire when it comes to COVID.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: I have been very respectful, very supportive of the administration, but something has to change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: South Carolina Republican senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and Texas Democratic Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  teaming up to take down issues at the Southern border, both sides slamming the administration for not addressing the migrant surge there.\par \par \uc2 Cochise County, Arizona, Sheriff Mark Dannels has been facing the front lines of this from the very beginning. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff, we have talked so many times this year about this situation and just how overbearing it's become. But now, with the spike in COVID cases, describe what it's like now.\par \par \uc2 MARK DANNELS, COCHISE COUNTY, ARIZONA, SHERIFF: Well, here we go again, Charles. Thanks for having me on the show.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to what we're addressing on the border, it is not getting better. And I appreciate the senators and all our federal leaders for stepping (AUDIO GAP) troublesome down on the border. It's a crisis on our border.\par \par \uc2 The politically neutral stats show that, record high numbers, 20-year historical numbers that we're addressing down here. And I just learned yesterday, Charles, that Mexico has made the decision not to accept other than Mexican citizens being deported back to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 And we were told by a briefing I had this morning with our local Border Patrol that those caught that are not from Mexico that can't be expelled are going to be released, I say released into our communities for us to address.\par \par \uc2 What you see in Texas is now going to be along the entire Southwest border. Once again, we're either in a health pandemic or we're not. This is a public safety issue and a national security issue. And it's troublesome down here.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, President Biden at this very hour 24 hours ago underscored that this is a health crisis. And everyone agrees, which makes us somewhat puzzling to us, but it has to make it very frustrating to you.\par \par \uc2 What is the federal government saying? What are law enforcement folks on a federal level telling you with respect to help, to COVID testing, and to making sure if someone is positive COVID that the right actions are taken?\par \par \uc2 DANNELS: Well, let me say this, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Since this health pandemic started, and since we have had the surge, the combination of both, the one thing that's been consistent is the lack of intervention or lack of concern on the Southwest border by this administration to address it.\par \par \uc2 You look at Dr. Fauci, you look at this administration saying we need to mask up, we need to make sure we're being protected, the variant is increasing in this country, what you don't hear is saying, let's take care of our Southwest border.\par \par \uc2 Right now, when these -- starting today, these people are being released in our communities. We are now impacted on this. I met with our emergency service coordinator. I talked to the governor this morning on this, my governor here in Arizona. Once again, nobody is talking to border communities. Nobody's talking to our border governor.\par \par \uc2 I know Texas Governor Abbott is in the same position talking to the sheriffs in Texas. This is just not (INAUDIBLE) frustrating. And I'm telling you, it is very troublesome for us trying to address this issue on the border, which is a federal mandate, not a local mandate.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: This news you heard today with respect to Mexico only accepting Mexican citizens, when others folks are relocated, other migrants from different countries, have they relocated some in your community?\par \par \uc2 And if they have, are you getting the resources to take -- to help take care of those folks?\par \par \uc2 DANNELS: Well, let me just say this.\par \par \uc2 What we have been getting in our county is the unaccompanied minors. We have a center here for the children. We have been getting those. When it comes to the adults, no, we have not. What we get in my county primarily is the 20-, 30-year-old single male adults that are covertly smuggled.\par \par \uc2 I get about 350-plus a day coming through my region of the state and my county. Second to that is, now with the -- pretty much, if you're not from Mexico, you're going to be released (AUDIO GAP). Now we're dealing with not just the covert smuggling. Now we're going to be dealing with these folks being dropped off in our communities for us to address, which poses a health pandemic and a public safety pandemic.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sheriff, I'm going to -- you're coming in and out. You were just breaking up a little bit too much.\par \par \uc2 I hope that the esprit de corps holds up. We appreciate you coming on.\par \par \uc2 And something's got to change. When you hear a Democratic congressman who's on the front lines expressing the kind of emergency that he's been expressing, talking Representative Cuellar, I think everyone should be listening.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So, folks, your table is ready. And so is your robot. One California restaurant's solution to the worker shortage? Well, we will tell you.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, that employee shortage, worker shortage, still slamming businesses big time, but one California restaurant having so much trouble finding workers that, well, they now employ a robot to serve guest.\par \par \uc2 So, how's it working out?\par \par \uc2 Sugar Mediterranean Bistro general manager Ana Ortiz joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Ana, first of all, golly, you have gotten a tremendous amount of publicity for this. It is a brilliant idea. But it wasn't a publicity stunt, was it? I read what you said that this was actually a lifesaver. Walk us through how you thought of this. And how's it going?\par \par \uc2 ANA ORTIZ, GENERAL MANAGER, SUGAR MEDITERRANEAN BISTRO: It's going amazing. It is a lifesaver. It's helping us so much serving tables, running the food to the table, when we don't have enough employees to go to our tables.\par \par \uc2 We can also send the robot to the tables that's already done. And we pick up all of the trays and we send it directly back to the dishwasher. And it's just amazing. We just unload it. It cuts the job like right in half or 75 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Are you already benefiting financially? In other words, I'm not sure how much it would cost for a robot like that, but has it already made a big difference economically and that this was a great economic decision?\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: I think it is a great economic decision, not because it is very cheap to use it, but because it is pulling a lot of people in. It is getting a lot of attention from the public.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: So, everybody is coming in and being served by the robot.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Any -- has the robot gotten any big tips yet?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: Nobody has tipped him yet. Poor little thing.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let me ask you about this, the new guidelines that are coming out from the CDC.\par \par \uc2 The restaurant industry took it harder than any other industry out there. It's -- I say kudos to every one of you who have stayed open through it. But now you have got another wave to deal with.\par \par \uc2 What are your concerns, now that more mandates are coming back?\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: We are very concerned about the new rules that CDC is going to set up for restaurants, especially for us. It was really difficult during the whole pandemic, because we were basically closed for a whole year.\par \par \uc2 We survived with takeouts and with the support of our customers. But we lost all of our employees. We were, like, behind on a lot of different things that we have to pay. And we have to catch up with that.\par \par \uc2 And, fortunately, since we have been open, it has been really good for us. And then now that we have these robots, like I said, everybody's coming in. So it's helping us out our lot, and especially because nobody wants to work, unfortunately. So it's a huge help.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We're out of time.\par \par \uc2 I know 700,000 people quit that industry the last time they took a look at the numbers. A lot of people don't want to work. There's controversy about that. Stockton has some universal basic income. You're making it work as an entrepreneur.\par \par \uc2 We salute you. And send me one of those sugar palomas, if you can. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  it to me, because I went and look at your menu. It looks phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you so much. And congratulations.\par \par \uc2 ORTIZ: I would love to. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 Well, a new kind of streaming war, folks, actress Scarlett Johansson suing \uc2 Disney\uc2  over its release of Marvel's "Black Widow."\par \par \uc2 So, where's all of this going? And what does it mean for the future of your moviegoing?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP, "BLACK WIDOW")\par \par \uc2 SCARLETT JOHANSSON, ACTRESS: I was an Avenger. I made mistakes and a lot of enemies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP, "BLACK WIDOW")\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE PUGH, ACTRESS: You're thinking about moving, but you're going to wait until the interest rates go down.\par \par \uc2 JOHANSSON: That's not my story.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Mickey, Scarlett has a bone to pick with you.\par \par \uc2 Actress Scarlett Johansson suing \uc2 Disney\uc2  for breach of contract by releasing the film "Black Widow" to its streaming service, Disney+, at the same time it was released in theaters. Now, the Mouse House firing back, calling the lawsuit sad and distressing and pointing to the global effects of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Will we see more battles like this? And what does it mean for the future of movie theaters?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss, The Daily Mail's editor Femail Charlie Lankston and FOX News correspondent Jonathan Hunt.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, I don't breathe the rarefied atmosphere of multimillionaires, and unless \uc2 Dogecoin\uc2  goes to 10,000 suddenly, I'm not about to.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: But like it's hard to feel sympathy for anybody in this.\par \par \uc2 Can \uc2 Disney\uc2  afford to pay Scarlett Johansson what they would have had in the contract had it gone straight to theaters? Could they afford to give her $20 million more? Yes, they could. Scarlett Johansson, is $20 million enough? I don't know. What's she going to lose out on here?\par \par \uc2 Is the yacht anchored off of Saint-Tropez are you going to be 10 foot shorter next summer? I don't have sympathy for anybody. But I do understand Scarlett Johansson. She believes she had a contract, which said it was only going to the movie theaters, and the back end would have been a heck of a lot more in terms of dollars if it had just done that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So she has a point. I just -- it's hard to feel sympathetic for all these millionaires talking here.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. No, it's the -- I'm with you on that.\par \par \uc2 But, Charlie, I do remember that Monday, the headlines, \uc2 Disney\uc2 , all of these new subscribers that they got. And I'm thinking, if I'm Scarlett Johansson's lawyer, not only do I want money from the theater stuff I missed out on, but from the recurring revenue from all of these folks now that you have got on Disney+.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, from a legal point of view, it sounds like she might have a case. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE LANKSTON, FEMAIL EDITOR, DAILY MAIL: Well, they do actually mention that in the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Her lawyer says, not only are you in breach of contract, or, in our opinion, you're in breach of contract, but we also know that stock in \uc2 Disney\uc2  went up after you released the figures saying that "Black Widow" was one of your biggest releases ever. They had a whole new slew of subscribers.\par \par \uc2 All of these Marvel fans who were desperate to see the movie didn't want to go to a theater all signed up for Disney+, so that they could watch it when it was released. And that has helped \uc2 Disney\uc2  to profit tenfold, not just in sales of the movie itself, but also in the long-term subscriptions that they have now gained because of their decision to release this movie online.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 I want to...\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: So, while I agree I don't think it's that easy to be sympathetic towards millionaires, I do think that she has a reasonable case.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I want to share \uc2 Disney\uc2 's response to the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 And it reads -- quote -- "The lawsuit is essentially sad and distressing in its callous disregard for the horrific and prolonged global effects of the COVID-19 pandemic. \uc2 Disney\uc2  has fully complied with Mrs. Johansson's contract. And, furthermore, the release of "Black Widow" on Disney+ with Premier Access has significantly enhanced her ability to earn additional compensation top of the $20 million she has received to date."\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, beyond this, though, do you think that -- listen, there's a big debate over, will we ever go back to movie theaters like we once did?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: I hope that we do for a variety of reasons.\par \par \uc2 But this certainly changes the game, to a large degree, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 The COVID pandemic has changed the way we all watch movies. And I think a lot of people, the bottom line is, they won't go back to theaters. And some of the theaters in my neighborhood here in L.A., for instance, Charles, have said that they are not reopening.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, it's a very different landscape we're seeing, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, we got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 LANKSTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Now, some lawmakers now pushing back against COVID restrictions coming back.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace had a message for Speaker Pelosi after the House reinstated a mask mandate. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): ... in the tunnels this morning, socially distant from anybody else.\par \par \uc2 I had COVID. I have had two vaccinations. I'm washing my hands. I'm even wearing my mask inside the chamber, but I'm not going to wear it anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 So, Madam Speaker, come and get me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Congresswoman Nancy Mace will be joining me tomorrow on "Cavuto Live" at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Also, make sure -- we're going to go back to the border for that.\par \par \uc2 "The Five" starts.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210730eh7u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Worker Shortage; Interview With Jackson County, Texas, Sheriff A.J. Louderback; Masking Guidelines; Big Spending on Security For Mayors; Interview With \uc2 Sen. Thom Tillis\uc2  (R-NC); Infrastructure Negotiations\b0\par\par\uc2 Kat Timpf, Mike Gunzelman, Liz Peek, Charlie Gasparino, Alex  Hogan, Peter Doocy, Lydia Hu, David Spunt, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 8,290 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 July 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Inflation? What inflation?\par \par \uc2 With prices spiking for everything from gasoline and used cars to fruits and vegetables, even Oreo cookies, Democrats are reportedly pushing the message that their more than $4 trillion in proposed spending is a way to help families and that a nearly 5.5. percent spike in inflation is just temporary. But is it?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I`m Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we will speak to Republican Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  about all of that.\par \par \uc2 But first to David Spunt. He`s reporting live from Washington. He`s got the very latest from there.\par \par \uc2 Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Big story. Democrats are looking at the temporary spending burden for millions of Americans as, well, temporary. They believe that this is a marathon not a sprint, and, ultimately, the higher spending will help more Americans than hurt them.\par \par \uc2 Citing a private White House presentation obtained by NBC News, Democratic lawmakers are encouraged to focus on how the president`s $3 trillion-plus human infrastructure plan helps out working families by lowering costs, instead of getting people back to work or maybe even helping the economy.\par \par \uc2 The Consumer Price Index, measuring what people pay for goods and services, as you mentioned, increased by 5 percent from about a year ago. The president is counting on a bipartisan infrastructure plan to eventually help the economy. Republicans are continuing to make inflation an issue, arguing that the president and Democrats` spending in Congress is reckless and ultimately that increased spending will hurt where it counts, at the grocery stores and at those gas pumps.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is at its highest in the past 13 years. Economists, though, are concerned it won`t just disappear either in the next few weeks or few months. The ultimate message from Democrats, stay the course, stay calm. The spending more overall will actually cost less for working families.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, Sandra, as you know, the clock is ticking, Democrats and Republicans set to leave for the August recess. So both sides are hoping to get something done as soon as possible -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, David Spunt live in Washington for us.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, will massive government spending lower costs for Americans, as the White House is suggesting, or will inflation ultimately win out?\par \par \uc2 With us now, economist Steve Moore and Democratic strategist Laura Fink.\par \par \uc2 All right, welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 LAURA FINK, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: This seems to be just one of the hottest debates in Washington right now, because, Steve, can we continue all this spending in the face of this rising inflation?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Well, look, you don`t have to have a Ph.D. in economics to understand if you print more money -- and that`s $4 trillion worth that President Obama wants -- I mean, President Biden wants, on top of the $2 trillion already this year, and on top of the $4 trillion budget that we have, that this is going to cause much higher prices.\par \par \uc2 In fact, I got to tell you, Sandra, when I watched \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in that town hall meeting, when he said this is going to lower inflation, I almost dropped off my chair. And, look, Americans know this is not complicated.\par \par \uc2 You have put more money out there -- and, by the way, none of these programs have any kind of work requirement -- you`re going to have higher inflation, not lower inflation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It`s a great point, because, as these unemployment checks continue to go out, the work requirement is a big question, right, Laura, because we have seen many of these states where they continue those extended unemployment benefits that a lot of folks still are not going to 