at they`re at low risk for COVID. And those who are at higher risk have had that opportunity to be vaccinated. And so, at this point, we wish that those government officials would be addressing our kids` mental health, because it is in crisis from these mask mandates and these prolonged restrictions.\par \par \uc2 We have been advocating in every way that we can. I have spoken at all the school board meetings, the county board of supervisor meetings, asking for mask choice for our kids. And, unfortunately, in California, it looks like the only way to move forward with this is to actually sue, to let them breathe, and to be able just to see our children`s smiles again.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What do you believe? What are your expectations going into this filing this lawsuit against the governor there?\par \par \uc2 MCKEEMAN: Well, it is shocking and then kind of crazy to me, as a parent, that we are taking this on. But we are very hopeful.\par \par \uc2 I was part of the school reopening lawsuit that was successful in reopening our schools. We`re working with that same legal team. And we`re asking for an emergency temporary restraining order. And we know that, if that was granted to us, then that would immediately keep the state from enforcing their mask mandates, which, if a child wants to or needs to wear a mask, they still could.\par \par \uc2 It would just allow those kids that are at the point where they need to unmask, they want to unmask to be able to do that for school in the fall. So we`re just waiting to hear when our hearing date is and hoping that that`s very soon.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And that`s such an important point, because you are clearly stating you`re not anti-mask or against masks. You`re simply saying that should be the choice of those students when they return in the fall, knowing that so many have had the opportunity to be vaccinated, including the teachers, of course.\par \par \uc2 We did reach out, by the way, to the \uc2 California Department of Public Health\uc2  for a statement, and they provided this to us: "CDPH does not comment on litigation. But the data and science is unequivocal. There`s no substitute for in person instruction. And California`s COVID-19 prevention strategies are the best way to fully open our schools while protecting students and staff. The state`s guidance, which aligns with the CDC and the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2 , is how we keep our kids safe and start the school year fully in person."\par \par \uc2 Sharon, there are some parents that we hear from who say, whatever it is, just get them back in the classroom. Masks, no masks, whatever it is, we want them in class, we want them in person, full learning, and they will take whatever comes their way, including masks.\par \par \uc2 To them, you say what?\par \par \uc2 MCKEEMAN: Well, we are partnering with Reopen California Schools to bring forward this lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what they have been all about. I was part of the reopen schools movement. But in that reopening schools lawsuit, the judge ruled that the kids have not only a right to an education, according to the California state constitution, but an effective, quality education.\par \par \uc2 And so what we`re asserting and what we`re hearing from teachers and our students is that they`re not able to engage in an effective education when they can`t see each other`s faces, they can`t have that communication. Also, this lawsuit is addressing the asymptomatic testing and the close contact quarantines that are keeping our kids out of school.\par \par \uc2 They`re keeping our kids away from that in person education that the CDPH has acknowledged is a priority. And, also, the CDPH, they`re not aligning with the CDC. So all of these government agencies, they shouldn`t be parenting. They shouldn`t be doing the school boards` jobs. And they can`t even agree with each other on what`s the best thing for the kids.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes. And it`s the constant moving of the goalposts that`s really hard for parents as well.\par \par \uc2 And we want our kids, who have lost out on so much learning in some areas of this country, want them back in the classroom, especially learning. And, obviously, masks are -- looks like they`re going to be part of it.\par \par \uc2 Great to have you here. Thank you very much, Sharon, one of those parents speaking out for the kids.\par \par \uc2 MCKEEMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And to a similar story in New Jersey now.\par \par \uc2 Seven high schoolers are among 20 people suing the governor there over that state`s classroom mask mandate.\par \par \uc2 FOX News` correspondent Alex Hogan is in West Orange, New Jersey, reporting on that for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of this is different from some of the cases that we have seems centering around freedom of religion. This one is arguing freedom of speech and freedom of expression.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE) people involved in the case, including seven students here in New Jersey, students that say they`re taking this matter into their own hands. Their lawyer says mask requirements violate kids` rights to interact with each other.\par \par \uc2 Chicago, Boston, Atlanta, and New York City schools are all announcing that they will require face coverings in class this fall. Here in New Jersey, Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  says that he will leave that decision up to individual districts.\par \par \uc2 The class-action lawsuit names Governor Murphy, the head of the State Department of Education and the state commissioner of the Department of Health. Families involved say that they fear long-term social development effects on their kids, as children head back to the third school year that will have been impacted now by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLY FORD, NEW JERSEY PARENT: How is it that everybody around now doesn`t have to wear a mask, including kids in sports now, but kids still have to wear masks? They`re the only ones that are still paying the price and they`re the ones that suffer from it the least.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Now, we have reached out to everyone named in this suit, and they say they will not comment pending litigation.\par \par \uc2 As far as this group behind the lawsuit, they have already raised about $48,000 to support this file against the school and the governor -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Alex Hogan reporting live from West Orange, New Jersey, for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: If companies don`t add flexible work policies, do they risk losing employees? One Wall Street firm is banking on it.\par \par \uc2 But first to the Southern border, where Texas law enforcement is trying to get in front of the migrant surge there by doing something critics say the administration refuses to do. What is it?\par \par \uc2 A Texas sheriff will tell us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Looking live at the Southwest border, where things are not slowing down, so local law enforcement is stepping up.\par \par \uc2 Texas officials have begun arresting migrants on trespassing charges. Authorities say 10 have been made so far.\par \par \uc2 So, will this help stem the flow of drugs and human smuggling throughout the state?\par \par \uc2 Jackson County Sheriff A.J. Louderback joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff, welcome, and good to see you.\par \par \uc2 A.J. LOUDERBACK, JACKSON COUNTY, TEXAS, SHERIFF: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, how far will this go in helping stem that problem, do you believe?\par \par \uc2 LOUDERBACK: Well, when you have an administration that has rendered the law meaningless -- and, by that, I mean not enforcing the law.\par \par \uc2 You can follow the law. But if you just don`t enforce it, then you have the same -- the same issue here. We`re hoping that at least Governor Abbott has stepped up more than any governor the United States has ever done on trying to put some kind of process and stabilize the border to some degree.\par \par \uc2 So we applaud that. The infancy of the program right now, so we`re -- the numbers are still low. And there`s -- there will be some migrant movement, I`m sure, to try and thwart those measures that the governor has put in place. But at least it`s the effort that counts here. And\par \par \uc2 that`s what we`re most proud of here, as Texas law enforcement, where we have our governor fighting for us and fighting for our citizens here, with the federal administration that could care less.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Sheriff, what about the demographics of these arrests that have been made so far? It`s my understanding that all so far have been single adult men?\par \par \uc2 LOUDERBACK: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 But you have to understand that, yesterday, there was a group of 400 massed -- 400 massed at to Del Rio gates. Those were not just single males in that particular group. Those were Haitians and Venezuelans. You`re looking at 4,000 people in the Donna facility right now that`s supposed to hold 800.\par \par \uc2 None of these numbers are going down. They`re simply going up. They`re more and more people coming. This administration has created the largest catastrophic issues on our southern border that`s ever happened before in the United States.\par \par \uc2 You`re looking at the massive resettlement of the United States right before Americans` eyes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Sheriff, in just the short time that I have left here, as far as resources, do you have the resources you need to continue making these arrests?\par \par \uc2 LOUDERBACK: Well, certainly, no -- no Texas sheriff has the kind of resources to prevent the kind of invasion that`s happening right now.\par \par \uc2 The state of Texas is stepping up and spending billions of dollars to try and help us in this respect. And our hats are off to the legislative -- the legislators in the state and the leadership of this state led by Governor Abbott.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Sheriff, we really appreciate you joining us. Our best to you as this crisis obviously continues at the Southern border. Appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 LOUDERBACK: Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We will follow back up with him.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, employers are facing a new labor test, as they try to get workers back in the office.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino has the very latest on that.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden`s poll numbers are dropping as prices are spiking. Is there a connection?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Want to work from home? Then work for us.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business learning that Wall Street firm \uc2 UBS\uc2  is using its flexible return-to-work policy as a recruiting tactic to poach brokers from \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  calling for most of its workers to return to the office full time.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business` Charlie Gasparino has the scoop. He is joined by FOX News contributor Liz Peek.\par \par \uc2 So, Charlie, I feel like it`s something everyone`s talking about. Are you going back to work? How long have you been back to work? But this is becoming a major recruiting tool. So what is \uc2 UBS\uc2  doing here?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I can`t wait to get back to work. Actually, I`m at work right now.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Good. Good for you.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But, listen, I like telling stories through anecdotes. I was taught to do that when I was a senior writer at The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 And this is an anecdote here of one instance. But it`s interesting. And it`s becoming much more typical. \uc2 UBS\uc2  is a brokerage firm. It`s seeking to grow. It wants to poach high-producing brokers -- those people that handle rich people`s money -- from other places like \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And one of its recruiting tools, in addition to maybe more money, but another recruiting tool is, guess what, guys? If you come to -- and gals -- if you come to work for us, if you leave \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  -- that`s the biggest brokerage firm, considered the sort of gold standard in the brokerage business -- well, we will let you work from home or do whatever you want. Or, if you want to come in, you could come in as well. It`s up to you.\par \par \uc2 It`s very flexible if you`re a broker dealing with clients.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And, Liz, that`s because \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  doubled down on wanting its employees back to work.\par \par \uc2 James Gorman\uc2 , the head of that company, says: "I fundamentally believe the way you develop your career is by being mentored, by watching, experiencing the professional skills of those who came before us."\par \par \uc2 He is huge on getting people back to the workplace, but is what \uc2 UBS\uc2  is doing evidence that perhaps these firms should be more flexible?\par \par \uc2 LIZ PEEK, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think that`s right.\par \par \uc2 This is an argument raging on Wall Street, Wall Street is on fire. Like everyplace else, they`re trying to compete for workers right now. And so this is one thing that \uc2 UBS\uc2  can do.\par \par \uc2 But I would note, Sandra, \uc2 UBS\uc2  is not as highly regarded a platform as \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  or \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  or Goldman, all of which have really have taken a much harder line on people coming back to the office. They can probably do that and still keep most of their people.\par \par \uc2 But, certainly, the other firms who are allowing a little more flexibility are appealing to the new generation on Wall Street. There is a new generation. And they are talking about lifestyle. And part of that has to do with flexibility and being able to work from home.\par \par \uc2 The thing is, we have seen over the past year that people can be very productive doing that. So, they have a pretty good case to make.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: There`s a question how long that can happen.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Because so many of those folks learned in the office, on the job, with mentors.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And everything...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Hold on, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 I want to get to inflation, though, switching gears.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: OK.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Price spikes.\par \par \uc2 Everybody`s talking about this as well, a new Gallup poll showing President Biden`s job approval rating dropping to 50 percent last month.\par \par \uc2 So, Liz, you have a new column out that is making the case that President Biden is not taking inflation seriously. So are these new poll numbers dropping because prices are spiking?\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Well, I think consumers and voters, the same, are recognizing and connecting the dots between these enormous spending programs.\par \par \uc2 Biden wants to spend another $4 trillion, and he`s arguing that that will bring prices down. Sandra, I don`t know anybody who thinks that`s actually realistic. And so, yes, I think people are beginning to worry about the cost of everyday goods going up.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, the White House has changed its messaging. They`re no longer talking transitory, because guess what? It isn`t going to be transitory. They`re going to be looking at...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It`s been around a while already, right?\par \par \uc2 All right, Charlie, you weigh in.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, I will just say this.\par \par \uc2 We had really good growth during the Trump years. And we didn`t -- we didn`t have the massive increases in prices that we`re having now. And \uc2 David Einhorn\uc2  of \uc2 Greenlight Capital\uc2 , the hedge fund manager...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: ... just came out with a report -- with his annual report.\par \par \uc2 It`s a quarterly report to investors.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I got 10 seconds, Charlie.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And he basically laid out why this is not transitory, because it`s affecting everybody. And you have zero percent interest rates.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It is real.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Zero. Zero.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Those price spikes are real. No, it`s true. We will see what the Fed does next.\par \par \uc2 Great to have both of you.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, as employers struggle to find workers, one group that`s usually on the hunt for summer jobs is leaving millions of them open.\par \par \uc2 Kat and Gunz on where these workers are this summer. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Restaurants, hotels, amusement parks all reporting labor shortages.\par \par \uc2 And while these type of job openings are usually a big draw for teenagers looking for summer work, a new report by global outplacement firm Challenger Gray & Christmas shows that number -- the number of teen jobs added in June was at its lowest since 2015.\par \par \uc2 So what`s keeping teens from more of these jobs?\par \par \uc2 Internet radio host Mike Gunzelman and FOX News contributor Kat Timpf joins us now.\par \par \uc2 OK, for now, I will go first to Gunz.\par \par \uc2 Gunz, you`re joining us.\par \par \uc2 Why are teenagers not applying for these jobs? Why are they not filling these positions?\par \par \uc2 MIKE GUNZELMAN, FOX NEWS HEADLINES ENTERTAINMENT REPORTER: Well, it`s kind of funny.\par \par \uc2 I was actually at the Jersey Shore this past weekend. And a lot of the locals were saying that it`s been hard to try and find lifeguards or people to work the boardwalks or board games this summer. But I kind of had a different opinion maybe they Kat and others, because I don`t think that this is because teenagers are lazy.\par \par \uc2 It`s not like they`re getting stimulus checks like adults are that just don`t want to go to work because, hey, they`re making money at home. I think that this is more so the local governments and board of eds have scared and frightened kids.\par \par \uc2 You think all year long they could go -- they couldn`t play sports. Many of them couldn`t go to outside graduations. They couldn`t have their prom in May.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: But now a couple weeks later, in June, they`re all of a sudden supposed to go out and work as if everything`s fine?\par \par \uc2 I think this is a byproduct of what the -- this fear factor that`s been going on from the governments or from the board of ed.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Kat, is Gunz letting them off too easy? And why aren`t these teenagers applying for jobs? Apparently, the outplacement firm that did this survey says that the Delta variant is to blame.\par \par \uc2 It`s making it hard for some people in some areas of the country where there`s a spike in cases. That is keeping teens out of these mostly in person positions. Is that what you see from your view?\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE TIMPF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Look, I don`t think teens are all lazy. I think, if anything, I would never say that because I`m pretty scared of teens.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know. I see teens out. And I`m like -- I just feel like they`re going to make fun of me. They`re always on skateboards. They always look cool. Very scared of teens.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: But I really think, honestly, that is probably part of it.\par \par \uc2 Really, if you -- people are really still concerned about this and the Delta variant., And, also, maybe some of these teens aren`t vaccinated. I know 16 and up, that is available to you. But if you`re still a teenager living at home, that`s going to be a decision that you`re going to make with your parents. Maybe you`re still concerned about that.\par \par \uc2 And it does seem like a little bit of mixed messaging. As Gunz put it, OK, no proms, no school. You haven`t been in school for over a year, but go out and work.\par \par \uc2 I`m really not sure, because it`s not like there`s really much to do. There`s -- I mean, there`s restaurants are open. Some areas are more open than others. But travel is still a little questionable.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Gunz, we`re -- we are -- we depend on that group of teenagers in the work force, because many of them do fill some of those minimum wage- paying jobs, whether it`s at restaurants or small businesses. And they make up a good chunk of our economy.\par \par \uc2 So, if they`re not working, can this economy fully recover, especially when we see signs like this all over the country?\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Well, I mean, this is what we are going to have to deal with, that many restaurants are dealing with this.\par \par \uc2 The fact is that, listen, teenagers want money. Like teenagers, I really don`t think have a problem working. It`s not like they`re working 40 hours a week, 20, 25 hours. They want all the money they can get.\par \par \uc2 But this is the situation that we`re in, because this is what the government -- or this is just what`s happened to us, that we`re kind of hesitant to fully go back to working at 100 percent, especially the younger you go, with no vaccine for 15-year-olds.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, it seems that they`re also attributing this to the difficulties navigating virtual school, the COVID precautions that we talked about, distancing from friends, potentially missing major milestones.\par \par \uc2 A lot of making up to do right, Kat?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: There was a lot of lost living.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Right.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Kat, I believe I will see you on "Gutfeld" tonight.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Oh, absolutely. Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: We`re going to have the best time.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Excellent. We will indeed.\par \par \uc2 I will see you then.\par \par \uc2 Gunz, great to see you. Thanks for being here as well.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Always.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It is always fun to join you. And thanks to Neil for allowing me to fill in for him.\par \par \uc2 I will be back here tomorrow, with Republican Senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  sounding the alarm over the Fed and inflation.\par \par \uc2 And, as always, you can catch me and \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  on "America Reports" weekdays live from 1:00 to 3:00 p.m. Eastern time. We`d love to have you. Tune in. We will see you there tomorrow, and I`ll see you on "Gutfeld" tonight.\par \par \uc2 "The Five" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210726eh7q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Milley Compares Trump and Supporters to Nazis; Gen. Milley was Undermining the Trump White House; Conservative Woman is Running for Congress on a Message of Unity; LA County Says the Vaccinated must Wear Masks and Requiring Indoor Mask-Wearing Again; South Carolina A.G. Challenging College Vaccine Mandates; MSNBC Remained and its Evolution Continued; Kamala Harris Has Reputation for having Toxic Workplace; Drug Overdose Deaths Hit Record during Pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Trace Gallagher, Marc Siegel \par \uc2 7,969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: All Access Live as we break down the biggest moment from the Student Action Summit.\par \par \uc2 It all starts Saturday afternoon on foxnation.com. Sign up for free using the code, SAS. SAS at FOX Nation and sign up for free.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching "FOX News Primetime." Tucker Carlson, up next.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 Here's a story we'd completely forgotten about because there are so many.\par \par \uc2 Just before last November's presidential election, two former Army officers wrote an open letter to the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, a man called Mark Milley, who still has the job. The letter became public. It was published on a left-wing blog called Defense One, and within minutes was all over the internet.\par \par \uc2 The authors of the letter had a direct order for Mark Milley, who strictly speaking, did not report to them quote, "If Donald Trump refuses to leave office," the letter began, "The United States military must remove him by force and you must give that order." You must remove the President by force -- that was a little shocking. What country is this?\par \par \uc2 Even the usual power-mad partisans in the news media began to wonder if that was really a good idea. Slate.com, of all places, reminded its readers that no matter how orange Donald Trump might be, military coups generally turn out to be unwise.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon had to go on the record as opposing it, too. The rest of us could keep civilian control of our government and what a relief that was.\par \par \uc2 Within days, the story just kind of receded. Another weird footnote to a weird four years, but if you paused and thought about it for a second, you had to wonder: Where did that idea even come from? Did two former U.S. military officers really just suggest removing the President of the United States by force of arms? Since when do American military officers talk like that or think like that? And do a lot of them have views like that?\par \par \uc2 We pushed that consideration from our minds, but we shouldn't have.\par \par \uc2 Now, we know that Mark Milley himself is the sort of person who considers military coups entirely within the realm of possibility. A new book written by reporters at "The Washington Post" who cover Mark Milley reveals that the current Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff is a legitimate extremist. He is the last person you would give power to if you could possibly help it.\par \par \uc2 In the book, Milley describes Donald Trump and the millions of people who supported him as the moral equivalent of Adolf Hitler. As thousands of Trump supporters peacefully gathered in Washington for what they assumed was a constitutionally protected political rally, the kind we've had for hundreds of years, shortly after the election, Milley likened them to brown shirts. That is the paramilitary wing of the Nazi Party.\par \par \uc2 Trump's complaints about voter fraud, Milley explained to his advisers out loud were actually calls for genocide. "This is a Reichstag moment," Milley said. "The gospel of the Fuhrer." Those are quotes. Think about that.\par \par \uc2 So, your grandfather joined the U.S. military to go overseas to risk his life to fight the Nazis. Now, the head of the U.S. military calls you a Nazi for having your grandfather's political views. What do you think of that? Keep in mind that Mark Milley is a man the media tells us is a deep intellectual, someone who reads books and stuff, not just Wikipedia.\par 